/* EN-переводы контента. window.I18N = {cards,terms,fp,prin}. */
window.I18N = {"ui":{"__проба__":"__probe__"},"cards":{"jc-eqhash":{"q":"You overrode equals but not hashCode. Why does the object get \"lost\" in a HashMap?","a":"A HashMap first locates the right bucket by hashCode. Different hashCode → different buckets → your equals is never even called, and the object isn't found.","d":"The contract: equal objects MUST have equal hashCode. Breaking it is the most common bug with collections.","note":"The symmetric trap: if you override hashCode so equal objects collide in the same bucket but forget equals, the object lands in the right bucket but the Map still treats it as different (using the default ==). Rule: always override equals and hashCode TOGETHER. And never use a mutable key in a HashMap/HashSet: change a field after put, the hashCode changes, and the object gets \"lost\" in the old bucket.","hook":"Found the building, but never got past the door: HashMap heads straight to the wrong street (bucket) and never looks for you there.","more":"HashMap places an element into a **bucket** whose index it computes from `hashCode()`. On lookup it first goes to the bucket by the key's hash, and only then compares candidates inside the bucket via `equals()`.\n\nIf `hashCode()` isn't overridden, two objects that are \"equal by content\" get **different hashes** (Object's hashCode is identity-based). They land in different buckets, and your `equals()` never gets a chance to run — the object is \"lost\".\n\n- Rule: if you override `equals`, you must override `hashCode`.\n- Contract: equal objects must produce the **same** hashCode (the reverse is not required).","quizCorrect":"Lookup goes by hashCode to a different bucket, so your equals is never called","quizWrong":["HashMap ignores equals and compares objects only by ==","Without hashCode the object isn't added at all and put silently fails with no error at all","equals without hashCode throws an exception on key lookup"],"picCap":"key1.equals(key2)=true, but different hashCode → not found"},"jc-eqeq":{"q":"What does == compare for two objects, and what does equals compare?","a":"== compares references — whether it's the same object in memory. equals (when overridden) compares content.","d":"For String/Integer this is a source of endless bugs — see the cards on the string pool and the Integer cache.","note":"Trap: == on Integer sometimes \"works\" because of IntegerCache — values -128..127 are cached, so Integer.valueOf(100)==Integer.valueOf(100) is true, but with 200 it's already false. Same with autoboxing in collections; never rely on == for wrappers — use equals or a primitive int.","hook":"== asks \"is this the very same person?\", equals asks \"do the twins look alike?\"","more":"For objects, `==` compares **references**: whether two variables point to the same object on the heap. It doesn't care about content. For primitives, `==` compares the values themselves.\n\nBy default (from Object), `equals()` does the same thing as `==` — it compares references. But it gets **overridden** to compare by content (as in String, Integer, your own DTOs).\n\n- For strings and any objects, compare with `equals`, not `==`.\n- `equals` must be consistent with `hashCode`, otherwise collections break.","quizCorrect":"== compares references (same object), equals compares content","quizWrong":["== compares content, while equals compares references in memory","Both compare content, the only difference is performance","== calls equals under the hood, so the result is always the same"],"picCap":"== — same object? equals — same content?"},"jc-erasure":{"q":"Why are List<String> and List<Integer> the same thing at runtime, and why can't you do new T[]?","a":"Generics exist only at compile time (type erasure). In bytecode the type parameter is erased to Object (or its upper bound); at runtime it simply isn't there.","d":"That's why there's no T.class, no instanceof List<String>, and no array of generics.","note":"Erasure breaks overloading: two methods foo(List<String>) and foo(List<Integer>) won't compile — after erasure they're the same foo(List). And an \"unchecked\" cast of (List)obj to List<String> passes with no error — the ClassCastException fires later, at the point where you actually read an element.","hook":"A generic is a label on a box: at the warehouse the label is torn off, and all the boxes look identical.","more":"Generics are a **compiler** tool for type checking. After compilation the type parameter is **erased** (type erasure): `List<String>` and `List<Integer>` both become a plain `List`, and `T` becomes `Object` (or its upper bound, if one is declared).\n\nThat's why there's no type information at runtime: you can't do `new T[]`, `T.class`, or `instanceof List<String>` — the JVM simply doesn't know. Erasure was chosen for **backward compatibility** with old pre-generics code.\n\n- Workarounds: pass a `Class<T>` parameter, or `(T[]) new Object[n]` with a suppressed warning.\n- Pitfall: overloads that differ only by their type parameter won't compile.","quizCorrect":"Type erasure: the type parameter is erased to Object and isn't present at runtime","quizWrong":["The JVM keeps the type but forbids arrays of generics for safety","The types are present at runtime, the compiler just hides them from reflection calls made at runtime","List always stores Object, so generics are pointless even at compile time"],"picCap":"Type parameter erased to a raw List — both are identical"},"jc-strpool":{"q":"Why is \"a\"==\"a\" true, but new String(\"a\")==new String(\"a\") false?","a":"String literals are interned in a shared pool — it's one object. new String always creates a new object on the heap, so the references differ.","d":"Compare strings only with equals. new String() is almost always a code smell.","note":"Concatenation of constants (\"a\"+\"b\") is folded by the compiler into a literal and goes into the pool, while concatenation with a variable (s+\"b\") creates a new object on the heap — that's why == on such a result unexpectedly returns false. Compare strings only with equals.","hook":"A literal is one shared park bench — everyone sits on it. new String means you dragged in your own private bench.","more":"The compiler puts string **literals** into a shared *string pool*. The same text `\"a\"` refers to a single object in the pool, so `\"a\" == \"a\"` compares a reference to itself — `true`.\n\n`new String(\"a\")` forcibly creates a **new** object on the heap, bypassing the pool. Two such `new`s produce two different objects — the references don't match, so `==` is `false`, even though the content is the same.\n\n- Compare strings only with `equals()`.\n- `intern()` returns the reference from the pool if you really need `==` (in practice you almost never do).","quizCorrect":"Literals are interned in a shared pool (one object), new String creates a new one","quizWrong":["Literals are compared by content, while new String is compared by reference and nothing else","new String is always compared against null, so it returns false","The JVM caches only single-character strings; long strings also return true"],"picCap":"Literals — shared pool, new String — a new object"},"jc-intcache":{"q":"Why does Integer 127==127 give true, but 128==128 false?","a":"Autoboxing caches Integer in the range -128..127 — it hands back the same object. Beyond that it creates new ones, and reference-based == breaks.","d":"The Integer.valueOf cache. Lesson: compare wrappers with equals, not ==.","note":"The trap isn't only ==: an Integer in a collection can throw NPE on unboxing if the value is null (Map.get returned null and it's assigned to an int). The lower cache bound (-128..127) can't be changed; the upper one can be raised via -Djava.lang.Integer.IntegerCache.high — but you must never rely on that in code.","hook":"Loose change up to 127 sits in a shared till and is handed out as the same coin; anything bigger gets a freshly printed bill every time.","more":"Autoboxing (`Integer x = 127`) calls `Integer.valueOf()`. That method keeps a **cache** of ready-made objects for the range **-128..127** and reuses them. So two `127`s are the same object, and `==` gives `true`.\n\nFor `128` there's no cache: `valueOf` creates a **new** object each time. The references differ → `==` is `false`, even though the values are equal.\n\n- This is a classic trap: the code \"works\" on small numbers and breaks on large ones.\n- Always compare wrapper types with `equals()`, or unbox to `int` first.","quizCorrect":"Autoboxing caches Integer in the range -128..127, beyond that it creates new objects","quizWrong":["int overflows at 128, so the comparison breaks","== for Integer always compares values, this is a bug in a specific JVM build on that particular machine","Only even numbers are cached; 127 ended up in the cache by chance"],"picCap":"Integer.valueOf cache -128..127: one object"},"jc-checked":{"q":"Checked vs unchecked exceptions — what's the difference and when to use which?","a":"The compiler forces you to handle checked exceptions — they're expected external failures (IO, network). Unchecked (RuntimeException) are programmer errors (null, index) and you're not required to catch them.","d":"Don't wrap everything in checked. And remember: by default @Transactional does NOT roll back on a checked exception (see spring-tx).","note":"Trap: you can't throw a checked exception inside lambdas / the Stream API — functional interfaces (Function, Supplier) don't declare it, so the compiler forces you to catch it inside the lambda or wrap it in a RuntimeException. This is a real reason functional code pushes out checked exceptions, not just \"fashion\".","hook":"Checked is the umbrella handed to you at the entrance with a signature (rain is expected). Unchecked is you tripping over your own doorstep.","more":"The compiler requires **checked** exceptions to either be handled (`try/catch`) or declared in `throws`. Their meaning is *expected external failures* the code can actually react to: file not found, network dropped, database unavailable.\n\n**Unchecked** (subclasses of `RuntimeException`) don't have to be handled. These are usually **bugs in the code**: `NullPointerException`, array index out of bounds, an invalid argument — you fix them rather than catch them.\n\n- Don't swallow an exception with an empty `catch` — that hides the problem.\n- Anti-pattern: wrapping everything in checked exceptions; modern style leans more toward unchecked.","quizCorrect":"The compiler forces you to handle checked; unchecked (RuntimeException) it does not","quizWrong":["Checked are caught at runtime, unchecked at compile time","Unchecked must be declared in throws, checked are optional","The only difference is the hierarchy: checked are faster, unchecked slower at the moment they are thrown"],"picCap":"Checked = external failure. Unchecked = your bug."},"jc-twr":{"q":"What does try-with-resources guarantee that a plain try/finally doesn't?","a":"It automatically closes resources (close()) in the correct order, even on an exception, and properly suppresses secondary errors from close. In a manual finally it's easy to forget close or lose the original exception.","d":"Any AutoCloseable: connections, streams, files.","note":"Ordering catch: resources in try-with-resources are closed in the REVERSE order of declaration, and if close() throws, that exception is suppressed — the main exception from the body isn't lost, but it's easy to miss in the logs. You can retrieve suppressed ones via Throwable.getSuppressed().","hook":"The tap shuts off by itself the moment you leave the bathroom. Do it by hand, and one day you'll walk away and flood the neighbors.","more":"`try-with-resources` takes resources implementing `AutoCloseable` and calls their `close()` automatically when leaving the block — whether normally or via an exception. Closing happens in the **reverse order** of declaration.\n\nThe main benefit is correct exception handling: if both the body and `close()` fail, the **original** exception stays primary, and the close error is added as *suppressed* (`getSuppressed()`) instead of overwriting it.\n\n- In a manual `try/finally` it's easy to forget `close()`, leak a resource, or lose the primary exception.\n- The downside — extra nesting and more verbose code.","quizCorrect":"Auto-closes resources in the right order and suppresses secondary close errors","quizWrong":["Guarantees that no exceptions occur inside the block at all","Rolls back all resource changes, like a transaction, on error","Closes resources only on success, leaving them open on an exception until the garbage collector runs"],"picCap":"try-with-resources closes for you; manual finally is on you"},"jc-hashmap-resize":{"q":"How does a HashMap grow, and what is the load factor?","a":"When it's filled past the load factor (0.75) of capacity, the HashMap doubles the number of buckets and rehashes all entries (resize) — an expensive operation. If you know the size in advance, set initialCapacity.","d":"If a chain in one bucket reaches ≥8 and the table is ≥64, it turns into a red-black tree (treeify): O(log n) instead of O(n) with a bad hashCode.","note":"initialCapacity is set as \"how many buckets\", not \"how many elements\": to fit N without a resize, use capacity = N/0.75 + 1. And HashMap isn't thread-safe — a concurrent put during resize in old JDKs (before 8) could loop the bucket's linked list and peg the CPU at 100%.","hook":"The wardrobe is 3/4 full — you grab one twice as big and move EVERYTHING over. If you know the volume, get the big one up front.","more":"HashMap stores entries in an array of **buckets**. The `load factor` (default `0.75`) is the fill threshold: as soon as the number of elements exceeds `capacity * 0.75`, a **resize** kicks in — the array doubles.\n\nResize is expensive: a new array is created, and **all** existing entries are rehashed and redistributed. Putting 10,000 elements into a default map (starting at 16) triggers several such doublings.\n\n- If you know the final size, set `initialCapacity` with headroom: `new HashMap<>(expected / 0.75 + 1)`, so no resize happens at all.\n- 0.75 is a tradeoff: lower → more memory and fewer collisions; higher → saves memory but means longer chains and slower lookups.","quizCorrect":"When filled past the load factor (0.75) the capacity doubles and all entries are rehashed","quizWrong":["The load factor is the maximum number of collisions in a single bucket","HashMap grows one bucket at a time, with no rehashing","When the load factor is exceeded, old entries are evicted like in an LRU cache without any rehashing"],"picCap":"Know the size — set initialCapacity to avoid a resize"},"jc-arr-linked":{"q":"ArrayList vs LinkedList — when to use which?","a":"ArrayList is an array: index access is O(1), but inserting/removing in the middle shifts elements. LinkedList is a linked list: fast insert/remove via an iterator, but index access is O(n) and cache locality is poor. In practice ArrayList is almost always better.","d":"LinkedList is rarely justified. Don't pick it just \"because there are lots of inserts\" — measure first.","note":"LinkedList loses even at \"insert in the middle\": to insert by index via list.add(i, x) it first walks LINEARLY to the node (O(n) traversal), so amortized ArrayList with memmove is usually faster. O(1) insertion is real only if you already hold a ListIterator at the spot; remove(Object)/contains are O(n) for both, and traversing a LinkedList thrashes the cache due to heap-scattered node jumps.","hook":"ArrayList is books on a shelf: pull one from the middle and you have to shift the rest. LinkedList is a string of fairy lights: easy to splice in, but you find a bulb one at a time.","more":"ArrayList is a **plain array** inside. Index access is `O(1)`, elements sit contiguously in memory (great cache locality). The downside: inserting/removing in the middle shifts the tail, and growth copies into a new array.\n\nLinkedList is a **doubly-linked list** of nodes. Insert/remove at an already-found position (via an iterator) is `O(1)`, but index access is `O(n)` (you have to walk from an end), and each node is a separate object with overhead and scattered memory.\n\n- In practice **ArrayList is almost always faster**, even where LinkedList \"wins on paper\": predictable memory beats asymptotics.\n- LinkedList is appropriate mostly as a `Deque`/queue with edits at the ends.","quizCorrect":"ArrayList — index access O(1), almost always better in practice; LinkedList — fast insert via iterator, but O(n) access","quizWrong":["LinkedList is always faster because it doesn't shift elements on insert and keeps every element in a contiguous block of memory","ArrayList can't insert in the middle, only append at the end","LinkedList uses less memory because it has no references"],"picCap":"ArrayList: get O(1); LinkedList: insert via iterator"},"jc-failfast":{"q":"What is fail-fast and where does ConcurrentModificationException come from?","a":"An iterator remembers the collection's modCount. If the collection is modified during iteration NOT through the iterator, it throws ConcurrentModificationException immediately. That's \"fail-fast\": fail right away instead of silently corrupting data.","d":"To remove during iteration, use only iterator.remove() or removeIf(). This is not a thread-safety guarantee.","note":"Fail-fast isn't guaranteed: modCount is checked on a best-effort basis, and under concurrent access a CME may not fire at all while the data is silently corrupted. Removing during iteration is safe only via iterator.remove() or removeIf().","hook":"You're counting people in a line and someone cuts in — you lose count and shout right away, instead of silently tallying nonsense.","more":"A collection keeps a counter of structural changes — `modCount`. When an iterator is created, it captures the current value in `expectedModCount`. On every `next()` it compares them.\n\nIf the collection is modified **bypassing the iterator** during iteration (e.g. `list.remove()` in a plain for-each), `modCount` drifts, the check fails — and the iterator throws **ConcurrentModificationException**.\n\n- This is **fail-fast**: better to fail loudly right away than to silently skip or duplicate elements.\n- The name is misleading — it catches not just concurrency but any modification during iteration. Fixes: `iterator.remove()`, `removeIf()`, iterating over a copy, or concurrent collections (`CopyOnWriteArrayList`).","quizCorrect":"The iterator checks modCount; if the collection changes outside the iterator it throws immediately","quizWrong":["The exception is thrown only under concurrent access from different threads","Fail-fast automatically rolls back collection changes during iteration","ConcurrentModificationException occurs when the collection's capacity overflows and its internal table"],"picCap":"Modified outside the iterator → modCount diverged → fails on next()"},"jc-map-impls":{"q":"HashMap vs LinkedHashMap vs TreeMap — what's the difference?","a":"HashMap — no order, O(1). LinkedHashMap — keeps insertion order (or access order — the basis of an LRU cache). TreeMap — sorted by key, O(log n), backed by a red-black tree.","d":"Need order — Linked/Tree; need speed — Hash.","note":"TreeMap compares keys via compareTo/Comparator, NOT via equals — if compareTo is inconsistent with equals, two keys \"equal\" by equals will be distinct in a TreeMap (and vice versa). LinkedHashMap-as-LRU is enabled by the accessOrder=true constructor plus overriding removeEldestEntry.","hook":"HashMap — a pile of socks. LinkedHashMap — in the order you put them. TreeMap — arranged alphabetically, but arranging takes longer.","more":"All three are `Map` implementations, differing in order and structure:\n\n- **HashMap** — array of buckets + hash. Key order is **undefined**, operations average `O(1)`. The default choice when order doesn't matter.\n- **LinkedHashMap** — a HashMap plus a linked list over the entries. Keeps **insertion order**, and in access-order mode moves a touched element to the end — a ready-made basis for an **LRU cache** (via `removeEldestEntry`).\n- **TreeMap** — a **red-black tree**, keys are always **sorted**, operations `O(log n)`. Provides range queries (`subMap`, `firstKey`).\n\nThe price of order: LinkedHashMap — extra memory for references, TreeMap — log n instead of O(1) and a required `Comparable`/`Comparator`.","quizCorrect":"HashMap — no order O(1); LinkedHashMap — insertion/access order; TreeMap — sorted by key O(log n)","quizWrong":["TreeMap keeps insertion order, while LinkedHashMap sorts by key and rebalances the tree on every insert","All three are O(1), the only difference is thread safety","LinkedHashMap sorts by value, not by key"],"picCap":"Three Maps: no order · insertion order · sorted"},"jc-hashtable-chm":{"q":"Why is Hashtable obsolete and ConcurrentHashMap needed?","a":"Hashtable synchronizes every method on the whole object — threads queue up. ConcurrentHashMap locks only a node/bucket, so it scales under load. Hashtable is legacy.","d":"Hashtable and ConcurrentHashMap don't accept a null key/value (unlike HashMap).","note":"ConcurrentHashMap atomicity applies to individual operations but NOT to compound check-then-act like `if(!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k,v)` — another thread can slip in between. Use atomic `putIfAbsent`, `computeIfAbsent`, `merge`. And beware: inside a `compute/computeIfAbsent` lambda you must not touch the same map (recursive update of the same bin) — in Java 9+ this throws IllegalStateException, before that it could silently deadlock.","hook":"Hashtable — one toilet per floor, a line at the door. ConcurrentHashMap — a separate stall for each person.","more":"**Hashtable** wraps every method (get/put) in `synchronized` on the whole object — that's one shared lock (monitor). While one thread is inside, the rest wait in line, even if they're working with different keys.\n\n**ConcurrentHashMap** splits the lock: only the needed **bucket/node** is locked (in Java 8+ — synchronization on the chain head + CAS operations). Reads are almost always lock-free. That's why it genuinely scales across many cores.\n\n- Pitfall: ConcurrentHashMap **doesn't allow null** keys or values — deliberately, otherwise you couldn't tell \"no key\" from \"value is null\" in a concurrent setting.\n- Hashtable is kept only for old code; for new code use CHM or a plain HashMap under external synchronization.","quizCorrect":"Hashtable synchronizes methods on the whole object; ConcurrentHashMap locks only a node/bucket and scales","quizWrong":["Hashtable isn't thread-safe, so it was replaced by ConcurrentHashMap, which synchronises whole methods and is therefore slower","ConcurrentHashMap is fully lock-free thanks to immutability","Hashtable doesn't allow null values, while ConcurrentHashMap does"],"picCap":"Hashtable: 1 lock for everything. CHM: a lock per bucket"},"jc-hashset":{"q":"How is HashSet built internally?","a":"HashSet is a wrapper over HashMap: elements are stored as KEYS, and the value is a shared dummy. Hence the same requirements for correct equals/hashCode.","d":"TreeSet → TreeMap (sorted), LinkedHashSet → insertion order.","note":"`HashSet.contains`/`remove` first locate the hashCode bucket and only then compare via equals — so an inconsistent equals/hashCode (equals true but different hashCode) yields \"the element is there but cannot be found\". Also `Collectors.toSet()` does NOT guarantee the returned Set's concrete type (may not be HashSet) or its mutability — don't rely on it.","hook":"HashSet is a HashMap where the guests are seated in key-rooms, and everyone's suitcase holds the same empty placeholder.","more":"**HashSet** internally holds a plain `HashMap`: your elements become the **keys**, and the value is a single shared placeholder constant (`PRESENT`). `add(x)` under the hood is `map.put(x, PRESENT)`, and a presence check is `containsKey`.\n\nThe key consequence: uniqueness and lookup speed are entirely determined by the elements' **equals/hashCode** — exactly like map keys. A broken hashCode → everything in one bucket → O(n) instead of O(1); a broken equals → duplicates \"leak\" in.\n\n- Pitfall: if you put a **mutable** object into a Set and then change fields that affect hashCode, the element \"gets lost\" — it ends up in the wrong bucket and you can no longer find it.","quizCorrect":"HashSet is a wrapper over HashMap: elements are stored as keys, the value is a shared dummy","quizWrong":["HashSet stores elements in a sorted array with binary search over that same array on every lookup","HashSet is a linked list of unique values","HashSet uses a TreeMap internally to guarantee uniqueness"],"picCap":"Elements are the keys → correct equals/hashCode required"},"jc-stream-lazy":{"q":"Why is a stream \"lazy\" and single-use?","a":"Intermediate operations (map/filter) do nothing until a terminal one (collect/forEach) is called — until then only the pipeline is built. And a stream can't be reused: after the terminal operation it's closed.","d":"Need a second pass — create a new stream from the source.","note":"Because of laziness, side effects in map/peek may not run at all if the terminal operation short-circuits (findFirst, limit, anyMatch). Never put business logic in peek — it's meant only for debugging and may be skipped by the optimizer.","hook":"The conveyor sits idle until you press \"Start\"; press it, and the parts roll through once — you can't run a second pass.","more":"A stream is built as a **pipeline**: each intermediate operation (map/filter) only records what needs to be done and returns a new stream. No actual traversal happens — not until you call a **terminal** operation (collect/forEach/count). Only that triggers the run, and elements flow through the whole pipeline one by one.\n\nLaziness enables optimizations: e.g. `filter(...).findFirst()` stops at the first match without traversing everything.\n\n- Pitfall: a stream is **single-use** — after the terminal operation it's closed, and calling it again throws IllegalStateException. Need another pass — create a new stream from the source.","quizCorrect":"Intermediate operations wait for the terminal one; after it the stream is closed and not reusable","quizWrong":["It caches the result after the first pass and is therefore reused indefinitely","Each intermediate operation runs immediately, and the terminal one just prints the total straight to the console","Laziness means the stream runs asynchronously on a separate thread"],"picCap":"Lazy: computes only on the terminal op. Single-use."},"jc-stream-collectors":{"q":"What does collect(groupingBy) do?","a":"A terminal operation collects the stream into a structure; groupingBy builds a Map<key, List<element>> — like GROUP BY in SQL.","d":"There are downstreams: counting(), mapping(), partitioningBy().","note":"Collectors.toMap throws IllegalStateException on duplicate keys — you need a third argument, a mergeFunction. And groupingBy by default returns a HashMap with unpredictable order; for a null value in toMap you'll get an NPE (HashMap.merge dislikes null values).","hook":"You sort socks into drawers: reds in one, blues in another — key → pile.","more":"`collect()` is a terminal operation that gathers the stream's elements into a ready structure (List, Set, Map) via a **Collector** object. `groupingBy(keyFn)` groups elements by key and returns `Map<K, List<T>>` — a direct analog of **GROUP BY** in SQL.\n\nThere's a second argument — a downstream collector: `groupingBy(x->x.type, counting())` yields `Map<Type,Long>`, and with `mapping`/`summingInt` you can aggregate right away.\n\n- Pitfall: the values are a mutable List by default; the key must not be null (NPE). For counts/sums use a downstream rather than building lists by hand.","quizCorrect":"Collects the stream into Map<key, List<element>> — like GROUP BY in SQL","quizWrong":["Sorts the stream by key and returns a sorted List","Removes duplicates by key, keeping one element per key","Counts the number of elements for each key, returning Map<key, Long> for every key"],"picCap":"Stream → groups by key, like GROUP BY in SQL"},"jc-parallel-stream":{"q":"What makes parallelStream dangerous?","a":"It uses the shared ForkJoinPool.commonPool for the whole application; blocking/IO tasks in it starve the entire pool; ordering and side effects break. It only pays off on large CPU-bound data.","d":"For IO — never parallelStream; use your own pool.","note":"The default commonPool size = number of cores minus 1, and it's shared across the whole JVM — one heavy parallelStream blocks parallel streams elsewhere in the application. To isolate it, run a parallel stream inside your own ForkJoinPool via submit().","hook":"You call all the office cleaners to wash your one cup: while they're washing it, no one else can work.","more":"`parallelStream()` splits the data and spreads it across the threads of the shared **ForkJoinPool.commonPool** — one per JVM. If there's a blocking call inside (IO, network, DB), you occupy the shared pool's threads, and the **whole application** starts to slow down, including other parallel operations.\n\nParallelism only pays off when there's a lot of data, the work is purely **CPU-bound**, and the source splits well (array, ArrayList).\n\n- Pitfall: order isn't guaranteed, and side effects and shared mutable state break (races). For IO use plain threads/executors, not parallelStream.","quizCorrect":"It uses the shared commonPool for the whole app; blocking tasks starve the entire pool","quizWrong":["It's always slower than a plain stream because it creates a thread per element of the set","It creates a separate thread pool for each call, exhausting memory","It's guaranteed to break the result of any operation, even pure CPU-bound ones"],"picCap":"One commonPool for all; IO starves the pool. CPU-bound only"},"jc-stream-reduce":{"q":"map vs reduce — what's the difference?","a":"map transforms each element 1→1 (the stream stays a stream). reduce folds the whole stream into ONE value (sum, max, concatenation).","d":"","note":"reduce requires the accumulator to be associative and stateless, otherwise on parallelStream() the result becomes non-deterministic; and the three-arg reduce(identity, accumulator, combiner) additionally requires the combiner to be compatible with the accumulator (combiner.apply(u, accumulator.apply(identity, t)) == accumulator.apply(u, t)) — violating this contract silently yields wrong sums only under parallel load.","hook":"map — a sticker on every apple. reduce — all the apples into one jar of jam.","more":"`map` is a 1→1 transformation: N elements in, N out, the stream stays a stream (e.g. strings → their lengths). It changes the content but doesn't \"collapse\" it.\n\n`reduce` is a fold: it takes a starting value and an accumulator `(a,b)->...` and folds the whole stream into **one** value — a sum, a maximum, a concatenation.\n\n- When to use which: map — when you need a new stream of different values; reduce — when you need a single result. They're often combined: map first extracts numbers, then reduce/`sum()` adds them up.","quizCorrect":"map transforms each element 1→1; reduce folds the whole stream into one value","quizWrong":["map folds the stream into one value, while reduce transforms elements 1→1 each time","Both return a stream, the only difference is that reduce runs in parallel","map filters elements by a condition, while reduce sorts them"],"picCap":"map: stream→stream · reduce: stream→1 value"},"jc-optional-chain":{"q":"How does Optional eliminate a cascade of null checks?","a":"map/flatMap/orElse build a chain: the value either flows onward, or the \"empty\" branch yields a default — without nested if (x != null).","d":"Optional is for return values, not for fields/parameters.","note":"Optional is not Serializable, and an Optional-typed field/parameter breaks frameworks (Jackson by default serializes it as {\"present\":...} without the Jdk8Module, JPA/Hibernate won't map it to a column); also orElse(buildDefault()) evaluates its argument ALWAYS — even when the value is present — so for an expensive default you must use orElseGet(() -> ...).","hook":"An envelope inside an envelope: you open them in a chain, and if it's empty you pull out a backup note — without a pile of \"what if it's empty?\".","more":"`Optional` is a container for 0 or 1 value. `map`/`flatMap` apply a function only if a value is present, otherwise they pass \"empty\" straight on — the null checks are tucked inside. At the end `orElse`/`orElseGet` gives a default, or `orElseThrow` throws an exception.\n\nflatMap is needed when the function itself returns an Optional (otherwise you'd get a nested `Optional<Optional<>>`).\n\n- Pitfall: Optional is for **return values**, not for fields and parameters. Don't call `.get()` without a check — that's NPE turned inside out. orElse always evaluates its argument, orElseGet does so lazily.","quizCorrect":"map/flatMap/orElse build a chain: the value flows on, or the \"empty\" branch yields a default","quizWrong":["Optional automatically replaces all nulls in the code with empty strings during evaluation of the chain","It wraps NullPointerExceptions and silently swallows them","It requires explicit ifs at every step, just with shorter syntax"],"picCap":"present → map/flatMap; empty → orElse, without nested ifs"},"jc-funciface":{"q":"What is a functional interface?","a":"An interface with exactly ONE abstract method — which is why it can be implemented by a lambda. It can have any number of default/static methods. @FunctionalInterface is a compiler check.","d":"Runnable, Comparator, Predicate, Function are functional.","note":"An interface with one abstract method stays functional even if it overrides a public method of Object (equals/toString/hashCode) — those don't count as abstract for SAM. That's why Comparator with its equals() is a valid functional interface.","hook":"A socket with one slot: the lambda-plug goes straight in. Two slots — you can't tell where to plug in.","more":"A functional interface is a contract with **exactly one abstract method** (SAM, single abstract method). That's precisely why the compiler can infer which method a lambda implements: there's only one, nothing to confuse it with.\n\nMeanwhile the interface can carry any number of `default` and `static` methods — they have implementations and don't change the abstract count. `@FunctionalInterface` adds nothing; it just asks the compiler to **verify** the single-method rule.\n\n- Examples from the JDK: `Runnable`, `Comparator`, `Function`, `Predicate`.\n- Subtlety: methods from `Object` (e.g. `equals`) don't count as abstract and don't violate the SAM rule.","quizCorrect":"An interface with exactly one abstract method — it can be implemented by a lambda","quizWrong":["An interface with no methods at all — a pure marker for the compiler to recognise it as such","An interface whose every method must be static","Any interface annotated with @Override, regardless of method count"],"picCap":"One abstract method → can be a lambda"},"jc-lambda-anon":{"q":"Lambda vs anonymous class — what's the difference?","a":"A lambda has NO this of its own — this refers to the enclosing object (in an anonymous class, this is the anonymous object itself). A lambda captures only effectively final variables and doesn't spawn a separate .class per instance.","d":"An anonymous class can have fields and several methods; a lambda — one.","note":"A lambda capturing this holds a reference to the enclosing object — a lambda stored in a long-lived field/listener will prevent the enclosing object from being GC'd (a leak). An anonymous class with capture has the same problem plus its own hidden ref to the enclosing instance.","hook":"A lambda is a guest in someone else's house: it says \"I\" (this) meaning the host. An anonymous class has its own house: \"I\" means itself.","more":"The key difference is the **semantics of `this`**. In an anonymous class, `this` is the nameless object itself. A lambda has no `this` of its own: it inherits the enclosing method's context, so `this` refers to the **enclosing object**.\n\nBoth capture only **effectively final** variables (the value doesn't change after assignment).\n\n- The implementation differs: an anonymous class compiles to a separate `.class` ($1, $2...), a lambda goes through `invokedynamic`, with no new file per instance.\n- Pitfall: if you need `this` of the handler object itself, or a non-static field via `this`, a lambda won't do — use an anonymous class.","quizCorrect":"A lambda has no this of its own — it refers to the enclosing object; no separate .class per instance","quizWrong":["In a lambda this refers to the lambda object itself, like in an anonymous class declared inside a method","A lambda can capture any local variables, including mutable ones","An anonymous class doesn't create a separate .class, while a lambda does"],"picCap":"λ: this = enclosing. Anon: this = itself"},"jc-default-methods":{"q":"Interface default methods and the \"diamond\" conflict?","a":"A default method provides an implementation right in the interface (so you can add methods without breaking implementors). If a class implements two interfaces with the SAME default method, the compiler forces you to override it and choose explicitly via Interface.super.method().","d":"This is how Java gives you multiple inheritance of behavior, but not of state.","note":"Conflict resolution rule: a class beats an interface, a more specific interface beats a more general one, otherwise it's a compile error and you pick manually with Iface.super.method(). A default method cannot override an Object method (equals/hashCode/toString) — the compiler forbids it.","hook":"Two navigators shout different turns — the driver decides whose voice to follow: A.super.m().","more":"A `default` method carries its implementation right in the interface. It was introduced to **add methods to existing interfaces** (for example `stream()` on `Collection`) without breaking thousands of implementors — otherwise all the old code would stop compiling.\n\nThe \"diamond\" problem: a class implements two interfaces with the **same signature** default method — the JVM doesn't know which one to take.\n\n- In that case the compiler forces you to **override** the method in the class and choose explicitly: `InterfaceA.super.method()`.\n- Priority rule: an implementation in the class/superclass always beats any default from an interface; a conflict only arises between equally-ranked interfaces.","quizCorrect":"The compiler forces you to override the method and choose explicitly via Interface.super.method()","quizWrong":["The JVM picks the method from the first interface in order — no need to override","The code won't compile at all: two default methods with the same name are always forbidden by the language","The method of the interface declared later in the implements list is called"],"picCap":"Two identical defaults → the class chooses explicitly"},"jc-marker-iface":{"q":"What is a marker interface?","a":"An interface with NO methods (Serializable, Cloneable) — just a flag/tag: code or the JVM checks instanceof and changes behavior. Nowadays usually replaced by annotations.","d":"Serializable means \"I can be serialized\".","hook":"A marker interface is like a \"fragile\" sticker on a box: empty inside, but the handler sees the tag and treats it differently.","more":"A marker interface is **empty** — no methods, no constants. It doesn't define behavior; it puts a **tag** on a type that is later read via `instanceof` or reflection, changing logic accordingly.\n\nExamples: `Serializable` (the JVM allows serialization), `Cloneable` (without it `clone()` throws `CloneNotSupportedException`).\n\n- It's an old idiom from a time when annotations didn't exist yet.\n- Today the same role is usually played by **annotations** (`@Entity`, `@Override`): they carry metadata more flexibly and don't pollute the type hierarchy.\n- The advantage of a marker over an annotation is that it provides a real type, visible to the type system at compile time.","quizCorrect":"An interface with no methods — a flag/tag by which code or the JVM changes behavior","quizWrong":["An interface with a single abstract method, implemented by a lambda","An interface whose methods all have a default implementation","An abstract class with flag fields that marks a type as serializable for the mechanism"],"picCap":"A tag with no methods → instanceof decides behavior","note":"`Serializable` is not just a passive flag: on deserialization the JVM does NOT call the serializable class's constructor (it instantiates via `ObjectInputStream`/`Unsafe`), but it DOES call the no-arg constructor of the first non-serializable ancestor — missing it throws `InvalidClassException`. And `Cloneable` is useless without overriding `clone()`, while the default `Object.clone()` is a shallow copy."},"jc-static":{"q":"What does static mean?","a":"It belongs to the CLASS, not to an instance — one for everyone. A static field is shared, a static method has no this (called via the class), a static block runs once when the class is loaded.","d":"Static fields are a common source of leaks and races in multithreading.","note":"Static initialization is lazy and thread-safe (the JVM guarantees it runs once under a lock) — the \"holder\" idiom for a lazy singleton is built on this. But a cyclic dependency between the static blocks of two classes yields a partially initialized class with silent null/0 instead of an error.","hook":"static = a shared fridge on the floor: one for all residents. Non-static = a personal thermos each person holds.","more":"When the JVM loads a class, it allocates **one** memory area for the static fields and runs the static blocks — this happens once for the entire lifetime of the application. Instances aren't involved here: static lives at the level of the class itself.\n\nThat's exactly why a static method has no `this` — there's no object for it to refer to, and the correct way to call it is via the class name (`Math.max`).\n\n- Pitfall: a shared static field is **global mutable state**, a source of bugs in concurrency.\n- Use it for constants and stateless utilities, not \"just so it's convenient to reach from anywhere\".","quizCorrect":"Belongs to the class, not an instance — one field/method for everyone, called via the class","quizWrong":["Creates a separate copy of the field for each object instance","Means the field cannot be changed after initialization (like final) and belongs to the instance","Forbids inheriting the class or overriding the method"],"picCap":"Static field count — one per class, visible to all objects"},"jc-abstract-iface":{"q":"Abstract class vs interface — when to use which?","a":"Abstract class: shared state (fields) + constructor + partial implementation, only ONE inheritance. Interface: a contract with no state (constants only) + default methods, you can implement MANY. \"is-a\" with shared code → abstract; \"can-do\" contract → interface.","d":"You inherit from a single class but implement as many interfaces as you want.","hook":"An abstract class = a parent (one family, shared blood). An interface = a driver's license: you can hold many at once.","more":"An abstract class is an **incomplete parent**: it has fields (state), a constructor, and some ready-made methods, but it can't be instantiated. There's a single parent because Java doesn't allow multiple inheritance of classes — otherwise state would conflict.\n\nAn interface is a pure **contract** of \"what an object can do\". There's no state (only constants), you can implement any number of interfaces at once, plus default methods for shared behavior.\n\n- Use an abstract class when there's shared code and fields within one family of types (\"is-a\").\n- Use an interface when you describe a capability attached to different, unrelated classes (\"can-do\").","quizCorrect":"Abstract class: shared state + constructor, you inherit one; interface: a stateless contract, you implement many","quizWrong":["No difference — neither can be instantiated, pick either one","An interface can have state fields, while an abstract class cannot","You inherit from any number of abstract classes but from only one interface, which is the opposite of how it used to work before Java 8"],"picCap":"Shared code → abstract; contract → interface","note":"Default-method trap: if a class implements two interfaces with the same default-method signature, it does NOT compile (the diamond) — you must override explicitly and call `Interface.super.method()`. Also default methods can't access fields or synchronize on state, so an interface with defaults can't replace an abstract class where shared mutable state is needed."},"jc-reflection":{"q":"Reflection — what is it and what are the downsides?","a":"Access to classes/fields/methods at RUNTIME by name (Class.forName, getDeclaredMethod). Spring/Hibernate/Jackson rely on it. Downsides: slow, breaks encapsulation (reaches into private), not checked by the compiler — errors surface at runtime.","d":"That's why Quarkus moves away from runtime reflection toward build-time.","note":"Since Java 9+ (modules) and strict encapsulation, setAccessible(true) on someone else's package throws InaccessibleObjectException without an explicit opens/--add-opens — a common cause of Hibernate/Jackson failures when migrating to a new JDK.","hook":"Reflection = a lockpick: you open any class by name without a key. Slow, reaches into private, and you find out something's broken only after you've already broken the door.","more":"Reflection reads the class metadata the JVM keeps at runtime and lets you find and invoke a field/method/constructor by **string name**, even a private one. DI and ORM are built on this: Spring creates beans, Jackson maps JSON, Hibernate populates entities — all without knowing your classes at compile time.\n\n- Downsides: **slower** than a direct call, bypasses encapsulation (access to private via `setAccessible`).\n- The main pain: the compiler doesn't check names — a typo in a method name only shows up at runtime, plus IDE refactoring breaks.","quizCorrect":"Access to classes/methods at runtime by name; the downside is it's slow and errors surface at runtime","quizWrong":["It's compile-time type checking — it speeds up the program","Access only to public members of a class, encapsulation can't be broken in any way from outside the package","A mechanism for automatically copying objects via clone() at runtime"],"picCap":"A string name → an object at runtime. Convenient, but 3 downsides."},"jc-annotations":{"q":"What retention policies do annotations have?","a":"SOURCE — only in the source, stripped by the compiler (@Override, Lombok). CLASS — ends up in the bytecode but isn't visible at runtime. RUNTIME — accessible via reflection (@Autowired, @Entity). Set via @Retention.","d":"For a framework to see an annotation at runtime, you need RUNTIME.","hook":"A receipt at the checkout: SOURCE — tossed right away, CLASS — sits in your pocket, RUNTIME — stuck on the fridge, always visible.","more":"Retention sets **how long** an annotation survives. The compiler and JVM discard it at different stages depending on the policy, so as not to carry unnecessary metadata.\n\n- **SOURCE** — needed only by tools/the compiler (`@Override`, Lombok), doesn't make it into the `.class`.\n- **CLASS** — sits in the bytecode but isn't visible at runtime via reflection (the default if not specified).\n- **RUNTIME** — stays accessible while running, read by frameworks (`@Entity`, `@Autowired`).\n\nPitfall: if a framework doesn't see your annotation — you almost always forgot `@Retention(RUNTIME)`.","quizCorrect":"SOURCE — only in the source, CLASS — in the bytecode, RUNTIME — accessible via reflection","quizWrong":["PUBLIC, PRIVATE, and PROTECTED — by annotation visibility level","COMPILE, LINK, and EXECUTE — by project build stages","All annotations are always accessible at runtime, there are no other options to configure them further"],"picCap":"@Retention: where the annotation lives — source / bytecode / runtime","note":"RUNTIME annotations are not inherited by subclasses by default — reflection sees them on a subclass only if the annotation itself is meta-annotated with `@Inherited`, and even then `@Inherited` works ONLY for class-level annotations, not methods/fields. That's why Spring uses its own `AnnotatedElementUtils`/`findAnnotation` instead of `@Inherited` to also traverse interfaces and meta-annotations."},"jc-error-exc":{"q":"Throwable: how does Error differ from Exception?","a":"Throwable → Error (fatal, from the JVM: OutOfMemory, StackOverflow — DON'T catch it, you can't recover) and Exception (checked — handle it; RuntimeException — a bug in the code). Throwing an Error is technically possible but a bad idea.","d":"Error is a separate branch so that catch(Exception) doesn't accidentally swallow something fatal.","note":"Don't catch Throwable/Error \"just in case\": after catching an OutOfMemoryError or StackOverflowError, you'd continue running in an inconsistent state. A separate insidious case — catching Exception swallows InterruptedException, breaking proper thread interruption (you need to restore the flag: Thread.currentThread().interrupt()).","hook":"Error — a house fire (put it out? no, run). Exception — spilled coffee: a checked one you wipe up, a Runtime one is your own clumsy hand.","more":"`Throwable` is the common root. The **Error** branch signals a failure of the environment itself (out of memory, stack overflow) — there's nothing to fix in the application in such a state, so you shouldn't catch Error.\n\nThe **Exception** branch is about application-logic problems. Checked exceptions are ones the compiler forces you to handle or declare (external failures: file, network). RuntimeException (unchecked) is usually a **bug in the code**: NPE, array index out of bounds.\n\n- Pitfall: a broad `catch(Throwable)` or `catch(Exception)` swallows both Error and bugs, masking real problems. Catch narrowly and on purpose.","quizCorrect":"Error — fatal JVM failures (OutOfMemory), no need to catch; Exception — recoverable problems","quizWrong":["Error is checked, the compiler requires handling it; Exception is always unchecked and need not be caught","Error can't be thrown via throw, but Exception can","Error and Exception are synonyms, the difference is only in the name"],"picCap":"Throwable: Error don't try to put out, Exception handle it"},"jc-catch-order":{"q":"Is there an order to catch blocks?","a":"Yes — from SPECIFIC to GENERAL. If you put catch(Exception) before catch(IOException), the code won't compile: the second block is unreachable.","note":"Multi-catch (A | B) doesn't allow related types in one block — only ones unrelated by hierarchy, otherwise you get a compile error about redundancy. And catching a bare Exception for \"robustness\" is dangerous: it swallows RuntimeException bugs that should have failed.","hook":"A queue at the doctor's: first the specialist (IOException), then the GP (Exception). Put the GP first and the specialist is never called.","more":"Java checks catch blocks **top to bottom** and picks the first one matching by type. So a more general type must come after a more specific one.\n\nIf you put `catch(Exception)` before `catch(IOException)`, IOException will always be caught by the first block — the second would become **unreachable code**, and for checked exceptions that's a compile error.\n\n- Rule: from the leaves of the hierarchy to the root.\n- An alternative when handling different types identically — multi-catch via `|`: `catch (IOException | SQLException e)`, where order doesn't matter.","quizCorrect":"From specific to general: subclass before superclass, otherwise a compile error","quizWrong":["From general to specific: catch(Exception) is placed first in the chain of handlers","Order doesn't matter, the JVM picks the right block itself","Alphabetical order by the exception class name"],"picCap":"An exception descends through catch blocks top to bottom: specific → general ✅"},"jc-map-notcoll":{"q":"Why doesn't Map extend Collection?","a":"Collection is about a group of SINGLE elements (add(e)). Map stores key-value PAIRS with a different contract (put(k,v), access by key). These are different abstractions, so Map is a separate hierarchy.","d":"But keySet()/values()/entrySet() give Collection views onto a Map.","hook":"A basket — you put apples in one at a time (add). Lockers with keys — you store a \"key → item\" pair (put). Different furniture.","more":"`Collection` describes a group of **single** elements: its core contract is `add(e)`, `iterator()`, `contains(e)`. Map is built differently: it stores **associations** key→value, access goes by key (`put(k,v)`, `get(k)`), and `add(e)` simply makes no sense here.\n\nThat's why Map was placed in a separate hierarchy — you can't fit it onto the Collection interface without ugly compromises.\n\n- That said, Map gives \"collection\" views: `keySet()`, `values()`, `entrySet()` — those are Collection/Set, and you can iterate over them.","quizCorrect":"Map stores key-value pairs with a put(k,v) contract, not single elements add(e)","quizWrong":["Map was added to Java later than Collection, so the link wasn't made","Map can't be iterated while Collection can, hence the different hierarchies in the JDK","Map does extend Collection, just through an intermediate interface"],"picCap":"A single element vs a key→value pair","note":"`entrySet()` returns a live view of the same Map, and the `Map.Entry` handed out by the iterator is valid only during the current iteration: calling `entry.setValue()` mutates the Map itself, and after `next()` the old Entry's behavior is undefined. So you mustn't keep references to iterated Entries, and structurally modifying the Map while iterating `entrySet()` throws `ConcurrentModificationException`."},"jc-immutable-howto":{"q":"How do you make a class immutable?","a":"1) make the class final; 2) all fields private final; 3) no setters; 4) defensively copy mutable constructor arguments (defensive copy) and also hand out a copy, not a reference to an internal field.","d":"A record does almost all of this, but you still need a defensive copy of mutable fields by hand.","note":"Without a defensive copy, immutability \"leaks\": if a List was passed to the constructor and the reference stored directly, the caller can keep mutating the internals through its own reference. Also, a final field guarantees visibility without synchronized only with proper publication — a partially constructed object (this escaping the constructor) breaks visibility.","hook":"Like a bank safe: seal it (final), put a copy of the document inside, hand out a copy too — the original can't be touched.","more":"An immutable object **doesn't change state** after creation — this gives thread safety without locks and safety as a key in a HashMap.\n\nRecipe: the class is `final` (so a subclass can't add mutability), all fields are `private final`, no setters. The key point is the **defensive copy**: if a mutable object (List, Date, array) came into the constructor, copy it inward, and through a getter also hand out a copy.\n\n- Pitfall: returning `return this.list;` directly — and the outside can change the internals through that reference. A leaked reference kills immutability.","quizCorrect":"A final class, private final fields, no setters, a defensive copy of mutable data","quizWrong":["It's enough to declare all fields private, setters can stay","The class must extend java.lang.Immutable","Marking fields transient is enough to make them unchangeable from the outside of the class"],"picCap":"On the way in and out we make a COPY — the original never leaks out"},"jc-prim-wrap":{"q":"Why have primitives if there are wrappers (and vice versa)?","a":"Primitives (int, double) — the value right in the field/on the stack: fast and with no memory overhead. Wrappers (Integer) — objects on the heap: needed where a reference is required (collections, generics) or null.","d":"Autoboxing between them silently spawns objects and NPEs — see Integer cache.","hook":"A primitive — cash in your pocket (right there, instant). A wrapper — money on a card: there's a reference number, and it can be \"empty\" (null).","more":"A primitive (`int`, `double`) stores the **value itself** right in the variable — on the stack or inside an object. That's compact and fast, with no heap allocation and no dereferencing.\n\nA wrapper (`Integer`) is a full-fledged object on the heap with a reference. It's needed where an object is required: generics and collections (`List<Integer>`, you can't have `List<int>`), and the ability to store **null** as \"no value\".\n\n- Pitfall: autoboxing hides the cost — millions of Integers are born in a loop.\n- And it's dangerous: comparing wrappers with `==` compares references, not numbers — you need `equals` or unboxing.","quizCorrect":"Primitives are fast and overhead-free; wrappers are needed for collections, generics, and null","quizWrong":["Wrappers are faster than primitives, so primitives are obsolete","Primitives can go into a collection, but wrappers can't","There's no performance difference, the choice is purely stylistic and a matter of team convention"],"picCap":"int — the value in place; Integer — an object by reference","note":"Integer cache by default only caches -128..127, so Integer a=127; a==127 is true but 128 is already false — yet the upper bound is tunable via -XX:AutoBoxCacheMax / -Djava.lang.Integer.IntegerCache.high, so code that worked on one JVM breaks on another. Never rely on == for wrappers."},"jc-identity-state":{"q":"Identity vs State of an object?","a":"Identity — \"which object it is\": its reference/address, compared via ==. State — \"what's inside\": the field values, compared via equals. Two DIFFERENT objects (different identity) can have the same state.","d":"equals/hashCode are about state; == is about identity.","hook":"Two identical passports with DIFFERENT numbers: the data inside matches (state), but each has its own number (identity).","more":"Identity is **\"which exact object\"**: its place in memory/its reference. The `==` operator compares precisely identity (whether two references point to the same object or not).\n\nState is the **contents**: the field values. It's compared by `equals` (if it's overridden by value). So two different objects with the same fields: `equals` → true, but `==` → false.\n\n- Pitfall: comparing strings or wrappers via `==` — you're checking identity instead of meaning, and you catch flaky bugs.\n- And the rule: if you override `equals`, you must override `hashCode`, otherwise HashMap/HashSet break.","quizCorrect":"Identity — reference/address, compared via ==; state — field values, via equals","quizWrong":["Identity is compared via equals, and state via ==","Two objects with the same state always have the same identity and match as objects","Identity is hashCode, and state is the object's toString"],"picCap":"Identity = address (==), State = fields (equals)"},"x-java-pecs-wildcards":{"q":"Why do we need ? extends T and ? super T (PECS)? Why can't you add an element to List<? extends Number>?","a":"PECS: Producer Extends, Consumer Super. extends — you read from the collection (it produces), super — you put into it (it consumes). In List<? extends Number> the compiler doesn't know the exact type (List<Integer>? List<Double>?), so add is forbidden — except null. Reading as Number is allowed.","d":"<? extends T> — an upper bound: the type is unknown but definitely a subtype of T → safe to read as T, can't write. <? super T> — a lower bound: you can put T and its subtypes, read only as Object. Example: Collections.copy(dest, src) — dest is super, src is extends.","hook":"A supplier hands out (extends-read), a consumer takes into the warehouse (super-write).","more":"Generics in Java are **invariant**: `List<Integer>` is not a `List<Number>`. Wildcards soften this. `? extends T` — \"some unknown subtype of T\": you're guaranteed to get a T out, so it's a **producer** (a source). `? super T` — \"some supertype of T\": it's safe to put a T in, so it's a **consumer** (a sink).\n\nWhy `add` is forbidden in `List<? extends Number>`: the compiler only knows \"a list of something that is a Number\", but not the exact type. Put an `Integer` in there — but it might actually be a `List<Double>`, a type-safety violation. So adding is forbidden (except `null`), while reading as `Number` is fine.\n\n- The **PECS** mnemonic (Producer Extends, Consumer Super) tells you which wildcard to use in a method signature.","quizCorrect":"The compiler doesn't know the exact list type (Integer? Double?), so add is unsafe","quizWrong":["Number is an abstract class, its instances can't be created at all","extends makes the list immutable (read-only) at the JVM level","Numbers must only be put in via super, while extends is just for strings and characters"],"picCap":"extends = you take out, super = you put in","note":"Because of type erasure, a wildcard bound can be bypassed via reflection or an unchecked cast, but with consequences: `((List<Number>) extendsList).add(x)` compiles with a warning but blows up with ClassCastException not at add, but later — when the owner of the real `List<Integer>` reads it. Another subtlety: `List<?>` (unbounded) is NOT `List<Object>`, you still can't put anything but null into it, and `Arrays.asList` returns a fixed-size list, so even a `List<? super T>` from it throws UnsupportedOperationException on add."},"x-java-comparable-comparator":{"q":"Comparable vs Comparator — what's the difference and when to use each? Why is compareTo via subtraction (a - b) dangerous?","a":"Comparable — the \"natural\" order inside the class (a single compareTo). Comparator — an external order, you can have many different ones without touching the class. a-b is dangerous: for large ints, overflow gives the wrong sign → sorting breaks. Use Integer.compare(a,b).","d":"A class implements Comparable to be sortable \"by default\" (TreeSet, Collections.sort). A Comparator is passed from outside: comparing(...).thenComparing(...).reversed(). Contract: compareTo must be consistent with equals, otherwise TreeMap/TreeSet \"lose\" elements.","note":"The main subtle bug is violating the compareTo contract (not transitive / not antisymmetric): Collections.sort throws \"Comparison method violates its general contract!\" only on certain data, in production and not on all of it. NaN and -0.0 break naive double comparison — use Double.compare.","hook":"Comparable — a passport (one, yours). Comparator — rulers from the side (as many as you want).","more":"`Comparable` lives **inside** the class: a single `compareTo` method that defines the one \"natural\" order (strings — alphabetical, numbers — by magnitude). `Comparator` is **external**: a separate object, and you can make as many as you want (by name, by date, descending) without changing the class itself. Handy for foreign/immutable classes.\n\nWhy `a - b` is dangerous: the contract only requires the sign of the result, but for large `int` values the subtraction **overflows**. For example `Integer.MAX_VALUE - (-5)` goes negative → the order inverts → sorting breaks or fails.\n\n- The fix: `Integer.compare(a, b)` (or `Long.compare`) — no subtraction, no overflow. Even handier is `Comparator.comparingInt(...).thenComparing(...)`.","quizCorrect":"For large values int overflows and gives the wrong sign, breaking the sort","quizWrong":["Subtraction is slower than Integer.compare due to autoboxing on every comparison","a - b always returns 0, so sorting doesn't work","The compiler forbids arithmetic inside compareTo"],"picCap":"inside one — outside many"},"x-java-string-immutable-builder":{"q":"Why is String immutable and what's StringBuilder for then? What's wrong with concatenating strings in a loop via +?","a":"String is immutable: pooling, thread safety, a safe HashMap key, cached hashCode. But s + x in a loop creates a new object each time → O(n²) and garbage. StringBuilder accumulates in one mutable buffer → O(n). A single s+x outside a loop the compiler optimizes itself.","d":"Every \"modification\" of a String returns a new object, the old one is garbage. In a loop that's quadratic in character copying. StringBuilder — a mutable char[] with amortized growth (not thread-safe; StringBuffer is synchronized, slower, almost never needed).","note":"StringBuilder is not thread-safe (unlike StringBuffer), but Buffer is almost never needed in production — a builder shared between threads is a design error anyway. And substring since Java 7u6 copies the characters (earlier it shared the array with the original and held a large string in memory — the classic old leak).","hook":"Concatenating with + — rewriting the whole sheet from scratch. Builder — appending to the bottom of the same sheet.","more":"`String` is immutable on purpose: a single object can be safely shared between threads without locks, kept in the **String pool** (literals are reused), safely used as a `HashMap` key (the value and `hashCode` won't \"drift\"), and the **hashCode is cached** once.\n\nThe price of immutability: every change is a new object. In a loop `s = s + x`, each iteration creates a new string and copies all the accumulated text → complexity **O(n²)** and tons of garbage for the GC. `StringBuilder` writes into one growing `char[]` buffer → **O(n)**.\n\n- Nuance: a single `a + b + c` outside a loop the compiler assembles via `StringBuilder` (or invokedynamic) itself — no manual optimization needed there. The pain is only in loops.","quizCorrect":"Each iteration s + x creates a new String object, copying everything anew","quizWrong":["+ is synchronized, and locks in a loop give quadratic complexity over the length","String caches hashCode, and recomputing it in a loop gives O(n²)","Each + triggers garbage collection, which runs in O(n)"],"picCap":"On the left every += spawns a new garbage object; on the right one buffer grows in place"},"x-java-stream-collectors-vs-reduce":{"q":"Why is toList()/collect a mutable reduction, while reduce must be \"pure\"? Why is reduce with new ArrayList dangerous in parallel?","a":"collect does a mutable reduction: each thread has its own container, then the combiner merges them — safe. reduce requires an associative function with no side effects on shared state. If reduce mutates a shared ArrayList — a race in parallelStream and data loss.","d":"The identity in reduce must be neutral (x op identity == x), and the accumulator must be associative, otherwise parallel gives a different result. To collect into a collection always use collect(Collectors.toList()/toMap()), not reduce: collect is designed for thread-safe merging of parts.","note":"Even groupingBy on a parallel stream is safe only with a concurrent collector (groupingByConcurrent) and an unordered stream; a plain groupingBy in parallel works but merges maps via merge — more expensive. A mutable accumulator in reduce is a race and data loss.","hook":"collect — each cook on their own board, then the dishes are combined. reduce into a shared list — a crowd at one board.","more":"`collect` is a **mutable reduction**: the framework gives each thread its own container (`supplier`), accumulates elements into it (`accumulator`), and then merges the containers (`combiner`). There's no shared state between threads, so in parallel everything is correct — races can't arise by construction.\n\n`reduce` is designed for a **pure** fold: the function must be associative and side-effect-free. If inside `reduce` you mutate a single shared `ArrayList`, in `parallelStream()` several threads write to it at once → a **race condition**, lost or corrupted elements, sometimes an exception.\n\n- Rule: accumulating into a collection — use `collect(Collectors.toList())`. Leave `reduce` for values (sum, max), where there's a neutral element and the operation is associative.","quizCorrect":"Several threads write to one list at once — a race and data loss","quizWrong":["reduce in parallel always throws ConcurrentModificationException","The ArrayList in reduce is recreated for each element, losing capacity","reduce forbids using any collections, only numbers"],"picCap":"Each thread accumulates into its own container, then the results are merged into one"},"p-lean-on-compiler":{"q":"You need to refactor, but there are no tests yet. How do you \"lean on the compiler\" and not break things?","a":"Make only mechanical, reversible micro-steps: Extract Method, Rename, Introduce Variable, Move — ONE at a time, recompiling after each. \"Lean on the Compiler\": deliberately break the signature — the compiler lists ALL the call sites that need fixing. Don't mix extraction with logic changes.","d":"Preserve Signatures — copy the parameter list verbatim when extracting a method, so you don't make a manual mistake. The goal of these tiny steps is to reach a point where a seam appears, then write a characterization test, and only then refactor \"for real\". IDE refactorings are trusted, manual edits are not.","hook":"You shut off the water at one tap — all the leaking pipes reveal themselves by dripping. You fix them down the list.","more":"When there are no tests yet, your safety net is the **compiler and the types**. Make only mechanical, reversible micro-steps: Extract Method, Rename, Introduce Variable, Move — strictly ONE at a time, recompiling after each.\n\n**\"Lean on the Compiler\"**: you deliberately break a signature (change a type, name, parameter) — the compiler lists ALL the call sites that need fixing. So it works as a \"what else is affected\" checklist instead of a manual search across the project.\n\n- The main rule: do NOT mix extraction/moving with a logic change in one step — otherwise it's unclear what exactly broke.\n- Limitation: it only catches what the types see; dynamics, reflection, and behavior it won't verify.","quizCorrect":"Deliberately break the signature so the compiler finds all the call sites","quizWrong":["Turn on the strictest compiler flags for checking","Rely on compiler optimizations instead of manual refactoring of the code itself","Use a static analyzer to find errors"],"picCap":"The compiler = a checklist of edits: you break the signature → you walk through all the errors","note":"\"Lean on the Compiler\" is powerless against overloads and autoboxing: changing a parameter from int to Integer (or removing one overload) produces no compile error but silently resolves to a different overload/to boxing, and behavior quietly changes. That's why \"trust only IDE refactorings\" matters most where overloaded methods and varargs exist — there the compiler is not a checklist but a trap."},"jc-transient":{"q":"transient and serialization — what does transient do?","a":"A field with `transient` is NOT saved during serialization — Java skips it. When the object is restored from the stream, that field gets the default value: null (for objects), 0 (for numbers), false (for boolean). Used for passwords, caches, and references to non-serializable objects.","d":"Serialization (`Serializable`) turns an object into bytes to write to disk or send over the network. By default ALL fields are written. `transient` marks a field as \"skip\". Why: 1) security — don't write a password/token to a file; 2) derived data — a cache can be recomputed, no point storing it; 3) technically — the field references an object that isn't itself Serializable (otherwise NotSerializableException). On deserialization the constructor is NOT called, so a transient field stays at default; you can restore it manually in `readObject()`. The `transient` analog in JPA/Hibernate is a different topic (exclusion from DB mapping), don't confuse them.","note":"transient together with default serialization requires manually restoring the field in readObject (or it stays null/0 forever). A common trap: people mark a field transient, then are surprised by an NPE after deserialization because they didn't add readResolve/readObject.","hook":"Luggage onto a plane: carry-on (fields) you check in, but transient is a \"do not load\" tag — it arrives as an empty slot.","more":"During serialization Java walks the object's fields and writes them to the stream. Fields with **transient** are skipped by this walk — they simply aren't in the bytes. On deserialization the constructor isn't called, so the skipped fields get the **default values** of their type, not what you set in an initializer.\n\nWhy: hide **secrets** (passwords, tokens), avoid carrying an easily computable cache, and bypass fields whose types aren't Serializable themselves (otherwise — NotSerializableException).\n\n- Pitfall: after restoration a transient field often needs to be **re-initialized** by hand (for example, in readObject), otherwise you get null/0 where you expected a value.","quizCorrect":"Gets the default value: null, 0, or false","quizWrong":["Is restored from the stream like a normal field","Takes the value from the default constructor","Throws NotSerializableException on read"],"picCap":"A transient field drops out on write → it's not in the stream → after read = null"},"jc-var":{"q":"var — what is it and where can you use it?","a":"var is \"compiler, guess the type yourself\". You write `var x = ...`, and Java looks at the right-hand side and substitutes the type. Only for local variables and only with immediate assignment. The type doesn't change afterward — Java is still strict.","d":"`var` (Java 10+) is syntactic sugar: local-variable type inference (LVTI). The type is computed from the initializer at compile time and fixed forever — this is NOT dynamic typing like in JS/Python. The real type remains in the bytecode (String, ArrayList, etc.). Not allowed: class fields, method parameters/return, `var x;` without initialization, `var x = null`, a lambda without an explicit type. Allowed: in for/for-each, in a regular assignment.","note":"var infers the most CONCRETE type, not the one you expect: var x = new ArrayList<>() gives ArrayList<Object>, and var s = condition ? \"a\" : 1 — a common supertype. With var you can't do var x = null and you can't use var in fields/parameters/method return.","hook":"var — like \"the same as that one over there\": the seller sees the item and puts a price tag; the price doesn't change afterward.","more":"**var** enables local type inference: the compiler looks at the right-hand side `var x = new ArrayList<String>()` and substitutes the exact type itself. This happens at compile time — in the bytecode the type is explicit, there's no dynamics like in JS.\n\nJava stays **strongly typed**: the type is fixed at the moment of declaration and never changes.\n\n- works only for **local variables** with immediate initialization\n- not allowed on class fields, method parameters, and you can't do `var x = null`\n- pitfall: `var` reduces readability when the type isn't obvious from the right-hand side (for example, the result of a factory method) — there it's better to write the type explicitly","quizCorrect":"Only for a local variable with initialization","quizWrong":["For class fields and method parameters","Everywhere — it makes Java dynamically typed at last","For a method's return type"],"picCap":"The compiler infers the type from the right-hand side and fixes it forever"},"jc-switch-expr":{"q":"switch as an expression (Java 14+) — why is it better?","a":"The old switch is \"forgot break — fell through\". The new switch (Java 14+) is an expression: you write case L -> result, it returns a value, with no break and no fall-through. Shorter, reads top to bottom, harder to make a mistake.","d":"case A, B -> ... — one arrow for several labels. For several lines you need a block { ... yield value; } — yield returns the result from the block. The key thing: switch now produces a value that can be assigned to a variable or returned from a method. For enum and sealed types the compiler requires exhaustiveness (cover all variants), otherwise you either need a default or get a compile error — this catches forgotten cases before running.","note":"A switch expression must be exhaustive (for enum/sealed the compiler requires covering all branches or a default) — that's its plus. But if you use the old colon syntax inside a switch expression, fall-through is preserved; with the arrow -> there's none.","hook":"The old switch — like a staircase with no railing: didn't stop (break) — you fly down. The new -> takes you straight up by elevator to the right floor.","more":"The classic switch is a set of statements with **fall-through**: forget `break` — execution leaks into the next case, a source of silent bugs.\n\nA switch expression (Java 14+) with the syntax `case L -> ...` returns a value that can be assigned to a variable. There's no fall-through, `break` isn't needed; for a code block the value is returned by `yield`.\n\n- the compiler requires **exhaustiveness**: all branches must be covered (or a `default`), otherwise an error — a forgotten case won't slip through unnoticed\n- you can group labels: `case MON, TUE -> ...`\n- pitfall: the old `:` syntax with fall-through is still legal in the same file — don't mix styles within one switch","quizCorrect":"yield value;","quizWrong":["return value;","break value;","case value;"],"picCap":"The arrow -> returns a value; no fall-through and no forgotten break"},"co-race":{"q":"What is a race condition in plain terms?","a":"Two unsynchronized threads touch the same data, and the outcome depends on who got there first. Example: both read counter=5, both write 6 — one increment is lost.","d":"Cured with synchronization (a lock), atomics, or immutability.","hook":"Two hands reaching for the last cookie: whoever's faster eats it; one touch is wasted.","more":"A race condition is when correctness depends on the **order/timing** of thread execution, not just on the logic. Several threads read and write shared data without synchronization, and the result becomes non-deterministic.\n\nThe classic case is a lost update: `count++` is not a single operation but three (read, add, write). Two threads read 5, both write 6 — instead of 7. One increment is **lost**, even though both \"fired\".\n\n- The nasty part is that the bug is **flaky**: it shows up under load, but disappears under a debugger.\n- Cured with synchronization (lock/synchronized), atomics, or by making the data not shared at all (immutability, local copies).","quizCorrect":"Unsynchronized access to data by several threads, where the outcome depends on order","quizWrong":["Processes racing for CPU time in the OS scheduler","An error where a thread crashes with an exception due to running out of memory in the middle of a run","A situation where a thread waits too long for a lock to be released"],"picCap":"Both read 5 — one increment is lost","note":"Trap: even a lone read/write with no write-race can be \"broken\" by visibility — without volatile/synchronized one thread may forever see a stale field value (the JIT caches it in a register), and a while(!flag) loop never ends. This isn't a classic race condition but a memory-visibility / happens-before problem, and a lock fixes it too."},"co-volatile":{"q":"What does volatile guarantee, and what does it NOT?","a":"It guarantees visibility (another thread sees the fresh value) and prevents reordering around the variable. It does NOT guarantee atomicity: i++ is a read-modify-write, and volatile won't save it.","d":"For counters use AtomicInteger, not a volatile int.","note":"Before recent Java versions a volatile long/double could be read in \"torn\" halves — volatile itself fixes that, but ordinary (non-volatile) long/double writes are still not atomic on 32-bit JVMs. And the main trap: a long counter, even when volatile, still can't be incremented (++) — you need AtomicLong.","hook":"Volatile is a shared notice board: everyone sees the latest. But \"erase and rewrite\" done by two people at once overwrites each other.","more":"`volatile` solves two memory problems. **Visibility**: a write is immediately visible to other threads (without it, the value may get stuck in a core cache/register). **Ordering**: it forbids the compiler and CPU from reordering operations around the variable (part of happens-before).\n\nBut this is about a **single read / single write**, not about compound actions.\n\n- `i++` = read-modify-write, three steps: another thread can slip in between them, and volatile is powerless here — you need an Atomic or a lock.\n- Good use cases: a `volatile boolean running` flag to stop a thread, publishing a reference to a ready immutable object (the double-checked locking pattern).","quizCorrect":"Visibility of the fresh value to other threads and prevention of reordering","quizWrong":["Atomicity of the i++ operation across multiple threads running on different cores","Mutual exclusion, like a mini-synchronized","That the value will never end up in the CPU cache"],"picCap":"volatile: visibility yes, atomicity no (i++ is RMW)"},"co-sva":{"q":"volatile vs synchronized vs AtomicInteger — when to use which?","a":"volatile — visibility of a single variable only, no atomicity. synchronized — mutual exclusion of a whole section (visibility + atomicity, but it blocks). Atomic — atomic operations on a single variable without blocking (CAS).","d":"Atomic is faster than synchronized under contention.","hook":"volatile is an \"open/closed\" sign on the door. synchronized is one restroom with a lock and a queue. Atomic is a turnstile: click and through you go.","more":"This is a ladder of cost and capability. **volatile** is the cheapest, giving only visibility of a single variable, with no atomicity for compound operations. Use it for flags and publishing references.\n\n**AtomicInteger/AtomicReference** — atomic read-modify-write via **CAS** (compare-and-swap, a hardware instruction) without blocking. Ideal for counters and non-blocking updates of a single variable.\n\n- **synchronized** (or Lock) — mutual exclusion of a whole block: atomicity + visibility, but threads queue up.\n- Rule: one variable and a simple operation → atomic; several related fields or a complex invariant → synchronized; just \"see the fresh value\" → volatile.","quizCorrect":"When you need to atomically protect a whole section of several actions","quizWrong":["When you only need visibility of a single variable","When you need an atomic operation on a single variable without blocking","When maximum performance under high load matters"],"picCap":"1 variable — Atomic; a whole section — synchronized","note":"volatile does NOT make a counter-style operation safe and does not cover arrays: `volatile int[] a` makes the reference volatile, not the elements — a write `a[i]=x` is not visible to other threads. For individual elements you need AtomicIntegerArray or a VarHandle, and even a compound `count++` on a volatile field needs Atomic/synchronized because the increment is not atomic."},"co-hb":{"q":"What does happens-before mean in simple terms?","a":"If action A happens-before B, then all memory changes from A are guaranteed visible in B. These are the rules by which the JVM decides what one thread sees from another.","d":"unlock→lock, volatile write→read, Thread.start, etc. establish happens-before.","note":"Common mistake: 'I used volatile/synchronized — so it's visible'. Visibility only comes from a MATCHING pair: the write and the read must go through the SAME monitor/volatile variable. A write under one lock and a read under another (or none) does NOT create happens-before.","hook":"A relay race: until you put the baton in the next runner's hand, they won't run and won't see what you've done.","more":"happens-before is a formal visibility guarantee in the Java Memory Model. If A happens-before B, then **all** memory changes made before A are guaranteed visible to the code in B. Without such a relationship, the JVM/CPU is free to cache and reorder — and a thread may see stale data.\n\nThis is not about wall-clock time, but about an **established order** between actions.\n\n- Basic rules: unlocking a monitor → acquiring it; a volatile write → its read; `Thread.start()` → the thread's code; the thread's code → `join()`.\n- Practical meaning: for a reader thread to be guaranteed to see the data, the writer and reader must be linked by such a pair (lock, volatile, atomic, concurrent collection).","quizCorrect":"If A happens-before B, memory changes from A are guaranteed visible in B","quizWrong":["That action A always runs earlier than B by wall-clock time","That two threads start strictly in the order they were created by the scheduler","That method A is called before method B within one thread"],"picCap":"A happens-before B → changes from A are visible in B"},"co-pool":{"q":"Why can't you spin up new Thread() by hand under load?","a":"A thread is expensive: ~1 MB for the stack + the cost of context switching. A thousand requests = a thousand threads = out of memory / thrashing on context switches. You need a pool that reuses a limited number of threads.","d":"ExecutorService / ThreadPoolExecutor with a sensible size and queue.","hook":"Why hire a thousand couriers one time each? Keep a crew of ten and run them in circles.","more":"Every platform thread is an OS thread: it gets a **stack** (~512KB–1MB) and the kernel spends resources scheduling it. Physically, only as many threads run as there are cores; the rest wait their turn.\n\nThat's why \"a thread per request\" under load hurts twice: thousands of stacks eat up memory (OOM), and the scheduler thrashes on **context switches** instead of doing useful work.\n\n- A pool (ThreadPoolExecutor) keeps a **limited** number of threads and reuses them, putting tasks into a queue.\n- Pitfall: an unbounded queue masks overload and piles up tasks until OOM; for blocking IO in a pool use a sensible size and backpressure, or virtual threads (Java 21).","quizCorrect":"A thread is expensive (~1 MB stack + context switches), thousands of threads eat up memory","quizWrong":["The JVM forbids more than 100 threads and throws an exception","Every new Thread() automatically creates a memory leak in the GC that is only released when the program exits","Threads can't run in parallel without an explicit pool"],"picCap":"A thread is expensive (~1 MB + switches). 1000 → OOM. Solution — a pool.","note":"The default Executors.newFixedThreadPool/newSingleThreadExecutor use an unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue — tasks pile up until OOM, and with an unbounded queue maximumPoolSize is never reached (no threads beyond core are ever created). Build a ThreadPoolExecutor by hand with a bounded queue and a deliberate RejectedExecutionHandler (e.g. CallerRunsPolicy for backpressure)."},"co-chm":{"q":"Why is ConcurrentHashMap better than synchronized(map) under load?","a":"synchronized locks the ENTIRE map on every operation — threads queue up. ConcurrentHashMap locks only an individual bucket/node, so many threads work in parallel.","d":"Don't confuse it with Collections.synchronizedMap — that has a single lock on everything.","note":"ConcurrentHashMap is atomic per individual operation, but get-then-put is a race. Use computeIfAbsent/merge/putIfAbsent. And don't call operations on the same map for another key inside computeIfAbsent — you can hit a deadlock/IllegalStateException (recursive update).","hook":"One shared lock for the whole building — everyone waits. A lock on each apartment — they all enter at once.","more":"`Collections.synchronizedMap` wraps every method in a single shared lock — on each operation the whole map is locked, threads go strictly in turn, and under load this is a bottleneck.\n\n`ConcurrentHashMap` breaks up the locking: only a specific **bucket/node** is locked (in modern versions — via CAS and synchronization on the head node). Different keys are modified in parallel, and reads are almost always lock-free.\n\n- The price: iteration gives a \"weakly consistent\" snapshot, and compound actions aren't atomic — `if (!contains) put` hits a race.\n- For atomic compound operations use the built-in `computeIfAbsent`, `merge`, `putIfAbsent` rather than a pair of calls.","quizCorrect":"It locks only an individual bucket/node, not the whole map","quizWrong":["It abandons locks entirely and works only via CAS","It keeps a separate copy of the map for each thread","It locks the whole map, but does it faster thanks to JIT optimizations"],"picCap":"One shared lock vs per-bucket locking"},"co-deadlock":{"q":"How does the classic two-lock deadlock arise?","a":"Thread A holds lock1 and waits for lock2, thread B holds lock2 and waits for lock1. Both wait forever. The cause is acquiring the same locks in different orders.","d":"Fix: always acquire locks in one global order; tryLock with a timeout.","hook":"Two people stuck head to head in a narrow corridor: \"you move first\" — and they stand there forever.","more":"A deadlock is a mutual, eternal block. Thread A holds lock1 and waits for lock2; thread B holds lock2 and waits for lock1. Neither releases its lock until it gets the other's — both hang forever.\n\nThe root cause is a **different order** of acquiring the same locks by different threads (one of Coffman's four conditions — circular wait).\n\n- The main cure: always acquire locks in a **single global order** (for example, by resource id).\n- Alternatives: `tryLock` with a timeout and rollback, reducing the number of locks held simultaneously, non-blocking structures. Diagnosis — a thread dump: the JVM writes outright \"Found one Java-level deadlock\".","quizCorrect":"A holds lock1 and waits for lock2, B holds lock2 and waits for lock1 — acquired in different orders","quizWrong":["One thread takes the same non-reentrant lock twice","A thread holds a lock and goes into an infinite computation loop that never releases it and never yields the processor","Too many threads wait at once for one shared lock"],"picCap":"A holds lock1, waits for lock2; B — the reverse. Circular wait.","note":"On Java 21+ virtual threads hide deadlock differently: pinning inside synchronized (before JDK 24 / JEP 491) keeps the virtual thread bound to its carrier and can starve the whole ForkJoinPool — this is not a classic lock cycle, and `jstack` won't print \"Found one Java-level deadlock\". That's why ReentrantLock is preferred over synchronized for virtual threads."},"co-vt":{"q":"Why is a virtual thread (Java 21) better than a platform one for blocking IO?","a":"You can have millions of virtual threads — they're nearly free. When blocking on IO, a virtual thread \"unmounts\" from its carrier, and the carrier serves others. You get reactive-level scalability while writing ordinary blocking code.","d":"Doesn't help CPU-bound tasks. The target stack for your new services.","note":"Virtual threads don't speed up CPU-bound code (there are still as many carriers as cores) — the win is only on blocking IO. And you can't pool them: the point is to create a new one per task; ThreadLocal on millions of vthreads is a memory leak.","hook":"A waiter doesn't stand at the table while the guest chews — they move on to others. A virtual thread does the same while waiting.","more":"A virtual thread (Project Loom) is a lightweight thread managed by the JVM, not the OS. You can keep **millions** of them: the stack is stored on the heap and grows as needed. They run on a small pool of OS **carrier** threads.\n\nThe trick is in blocking IO: when a virtual thread hits a network/disk call, the JVM **unmounts** it from the carrier, and the carrier immediately picks up another virtual thread. The carrier doesn't sit idle.\n\n- Result: you write ordinary **blocking** sequential code, but get reactive-level scalability — without callback hell.\n- Pitfall: `synchronized` around a blocking call and native/JNI calls \"pin\" the thread to its carrier — replace them with `ReentrantLock`.","quizCorrect":"There are millions of them and they're nearly free; on blocking they unmount from the carrier","quizWrong":["They perform IO asynchronously, without blocking the call itself and rewrite blocking code into a reactive pipeline","They run on the GPU, offloading the main CPU cores","They automatically rewrite blocking code into reactive code"],"picCap":"Blocking code — reactive-level scalability"},"co-tl":{"q":"Why is ThreadLocal dangerous in a thread pool?","a":"A thread in a pool is reused. If you don't call remove(), the ThreadLocal value leaks into the next request on the same thread (someone else's data / memory leak).","d":"Always remove() in finally. @Transactional and the security context are built on this.","note":"remove() in finally is mandatory, but there's a second trap: the default value via withInitial is computed LAZILY on the first get(), already on the pool's executor thread — overriding initialValue doesn't prevent leakage between requests, you need remove() specifically.","hook":"Didn't wipe the cafeteria tray — the next person gets your sauce. ThreadLocal without remove() is the same.","more":"Under the hood, **ThreadLocal** stores the value inside the thread itself (Thread.threadLocals — a Map keyed by the ThreadLocal object itself). In a pool (executor, tomcat threads), threads don't die but are returned and reused for the next requests.\n\nSo the stored value stays \"stuck\" to the thread. The next request on the same thread sees **someone else's data** (e.g. the previous user's userId) — that's both a security bug and a memory leak if the ThreadLocal holds something heavy.\n\nRule: always clean up in **finally** via `remove()`. That's why in frameworks (Spring Security, MDC) the context is set by a filter on entry and removed on exit — don't rely on the garbage collector.","quizCorrect":"Without remove() the value leaks into the next request on the same reused thread","quizWrong":["ThreadLocal is visible to all threads at once and breaks data isolation between separate tasks","The thread pool ignores ThreadLocal, and the value is always lost","ThreadLocal blocks the thread until all other tasks complete"],"picCap":"ThreadLocal without remove() leaks into the next request"},"co-atomic":{"q":"Why is AtomicInteger better than int when incrementing from threads?","a":"int++ is a read-modify-write — two threads overwrite each other. AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet() does it in ONE indivisible step via CAS (compare-and-swap) at the CPU level — without locks and without a race.","d":"CAS: \"swap if the value is still X\"; on mismatch → retry (busy-retry).","hook":"int++ is like a register with no queue: two people grab one receipt. AtomicInteger is a turnstile: one per click, nobody gets overwritten.","more":"**int++** under the hood is three operations: read the value, add 1, write it back. Between \"read\" and \"write\" another thread manages to slip in with its stale value — and one increment is lost.\n\n**AtomicInteger** does the same thing with a single atomic CPU instruction, **CAS** (compare-and-swap): \"write the new value only if the current one still equals what I read\". On mismatch — retry in a loop. So no locks and no lost updates.\n\n- Pitfall: each operation is atomic on its own, but not their combination. `if (atomic.get()==0) atomic.set(1)` is already a race — here you need `compareAndSet`.","quizCorrect":"incrementAndGet() is atomic via CAS, whereas int++ is a read-modify-write with a race","quizWrong":["AtomicInteger uses a synchronized lock on every increment","int++ is atomic on its own, AtomicInteger is just easier to read","AtomicInteger stores the value in a volatile, which alone eliminates the ++ race entirely"],"picCap":"int++ = 3 steps (race) · Atomic = 1 indivisible CAS","note":"AtomicInteger guarantees atomicity and visibility but not fairness or freedom from starvation: under high contention busy-retry CAS on a single cell degrades (cache-line contention), and LongAdder/LongAccumulator with striped cells is often faster for counters. There's also the theoretical ABA problem — solved by AtomicStampedReference."},"co-pool-reject":{"q":"What happens when more tasks arrive at a pool than it can handle?","a":"Extra tasks pile up in the pool's QUEUE. When the queue is full too — the RejectedExecutionHandler kicks in: by default AbortPolicy (throws an exception); there's CallerRunsPolicy (the caller runs it itself), Discard, and others.","d":"That's why a pool is configured with a size, a queue type/size, and a rejection policy.","note":"The default Executors.newFixedThreadPool uses an UNBOUNDED LinkedBlockingQueue — the RejectedExecutionHandler will never fire, the queue grows until OOM instead of backpressure. For real backpressure you need an explicit ThreadPoolExecutor with a bounded queue and CallerRunsPolicy.","hook":"The pool is a cafe: tables taken → people wait in line at the door; the line is full → security says \"Stop!\" (an exception) or carry your own tray.","more":"A pool has two capacities: **worker threads** and the **task queue**. First, tasks take free threads, then they pile up in the queue, and only when both overflow is a task rejected.\n\nWhat exactly happens is decided by the **RejectedExecutionHandler**:\n\n- **AbortPolicy** (default) — throws `RejectedExecutionException`.\n- **CallerRunsPolicy** — the calling thread runs the task itself; this is a natural brake (backpressure) on the source.\n- **DiscardPolicy** / **DiscardOldestPolicy** — silently drop the task.\n\nThe main trap is an **unbounded queue** (`Executors.newFixedThreadPool` on a `LinkedBlockingQueue`): rejection never happens, but memory grows until OOM. Better to set a bounded queue and a deliberate handler.","quizCorrect":"Tasks pile up in the queue; when the queue is full the RejectedExecutionHandler fires","quizWrong":["The pool automatically creates new threads without limit while there's enough memory in the process heap","Extra tasks are silently lost without any reaction by default","The calling thread blocks forever until space frees up"],"picCap":"First the queue, then — rejecting tasks"},"x-co-pinning-vthreads":{"q":"What is virtual-thread pinning and why does synchronized break it?","a":"A virtual thread normally unmounts from its platform (carrier) thread when it blocks on IO. But inside synchronized it gets stuck (pinned): it can't unmount and keeps the carrier busy. Many such cases — and the carrier pool starves, the vthreads' benefit vanishes.","d":"In Java 21 pinning happened on synchronized and in native calls. As of JDK 24 (JEP 491) synchronized no longer pins — the vthread unmounts even inside a monitor. But before JDK 24 the fix is: replace synchronized with ReentrantLock around blocking IO. Diagnosis: -Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full.","note":"As of Java 24 (JEP 491) pinning on synchronized is removed — a vthread no longer pins its carrier on blocking monitors. Before 24 the rule 'replace synchronized with ReentrantLock' applies; pinning remains only on native calls (JNI) and inside methods with a synchronized block in the native stack.","hook":"A guest took the whole parking lot for himself and sleeps in his car: the spot is taken, others have nowhere to park.","more":"A virtual thread runs on a **carrier** (platform thread). When the vthread hits blocking IO, the JVM **unmounts** it: its stack is parked, the carrier is freed for other vthreads. That's the whole saving — thousands of vthreads on a handful of carriers.\n\n**Pinning** is the situation where unmounting isn't possible. The classic case is blocking inside `synchronized`: the monitor is bound to a specific thread, so the vthread sticks to its carrier and keeps it busy for the duration of the block. Many such cases — the carrier pool **starves**, and parallelism collapses.\n\n- Cure: replace `synchronized` around IO with `ReentrantLock` (it doesn't cause pinning). In Java 21 you catch it with the `-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads` flag; in recent versions (JDK 24+, JEP 491) the `synchronized` problem is largely resolved.","quizCorrect":"Inside the block the vthread can't unmount from the carrier and keeps it busy","quizWrong":["synchronized is entirely forbidden in virtual threads at the JVM level since Java 21","Every synchronized creates a new platform carrier thread","A virtual thread permanently loses its stack on synchronized"],"picCap":"synchronized + blocking IO = carrier stuck"},"x-co-double-checked-locking":{"q":"Why is double-checked locking broken without volatile, but works with it?","a":"Without volatile, another thread may see a NON-null reference to an object that isn't fully constructed yet (the constructor was reordered). It gets a half-built object. volatile adds happens-before: the constructor's field writes are visible before the reference is published.","d":"The JIT/CPU may reorder 'allocate memory' and 'write fields' relative to publishing the reference. A volatile write inserts a barrier: everything before it is visible to whoever read the volatile. In practice it's simpler: the holder idiom (static nested class) or an enum singleton — both are thread-safe without explicit volatile.","note":"The volatile read in DCL must be cached in a local variable (read the field once), otherwise two volatile reads add an extra barrier on the hot path. And for a simple singleton DCL isn't needed at all: the holder idiom (a static nested class) is simpler and without volatile.","hook":"You hung an 'open' sign before stocking the shelves — the customer walked into an empty shop.","more":"Under the hood, the JIT and CPU are free to **reorder** operations: `instance = new Obj()` is actually three steps — allocate memory, run the constructor, write the reference to the field. The reference write may happen BEFORE the constructor finishes writing the fields.\n\nAnother thread checking `if (instance != null)` without synchronization sees a non-null reference — but to a **half-built** object with default field values. It gets garbage or an NPE inside.\n\n- `volatile` introduces happens-before: everything written before publishing the volatile reference is guaranteed visible to whoever reads that reference\n- Without it the code \"looks\" working and fails rarely, under load and only on weak memory models (ARM) — that's why the trap is insidious","quizCorrect":"A thread may see a non-null reference to a not-yet-built object (reordering)","quizWrong":["Without volatile the != null check always returns false","Two threads are guaranteed to create two instances without volatile","synchronized without volatile doesn't block the second thread at all at all on entry"],"picCap":"volatile = barrier: build first, publish after"},"x-co-false-sharing":{"q":"What is false sharing and why do two independent counters slow down?","a":"The CPU pulls memory into cache in lines of ~64 bytes. If two threads write to DIFFERENT variables that happen to lie in the same cache line, the cores constantly invalidate each other's cache. There's no logical conflict, yet speed drops several-fold — 'false' sharing.","d":"Cured with padding so that hot fields live in different lines. In Java 8+ there's @Contended (jdk.internal.vm.annotation, needs -XX:-RestrictContended). The classic example is LongAdder, which deliberately spreads its cells across lines, so it scales better than AtomicLong under high write contention.","note":"@Contended (jdk.internal.vm.annotation) works only with the -XX:-RestrictContended flag or in JDK classes; in ordinary code you have to do the padding by hand. The JDK already pads LongAdder/ConcurrentHashMap counter cells — that's why LongAdder beats AtomicLong under high contention, precisely by eliminating false sharing.","hook":"Two neighbors at one desk: each writes their own thing, but they jostle elbows and get in each other's way.","more":"The processor doesn't move memory one byte at a time — it works in **cache lines** of ~64 bytes. Coherence (MESI) is maintained at the level of the whole line, not the individual variable.\n\nIf two counters lie next to each other and land in the same line, then a write by thread A marks the line invalid on core B — even though B writes to a DIFFERENT variable. B is forced to re-read the line from L3/memory. There's constant **ping-pong** of the line between cores, even though the data is logically independent.\n\n- Cured with padding/alignment (in Java — `@Contended`, the JVM flag `-XX:-RestrictContended`) to spread variables across different lines\n- Typically burns in counters, ring-buffer indices, per-thread fields in one array","quizCorrect":"They lie in the same ~64-byte cache line, and the cores invalidate each other's cache","quizWrong":["The JVM stores all counters in one volatile field under a shared lock for the whole time","The garbage collector moves the counters and breaks their alignment","Both counters share one CPU register, waiting for it to free up"],"picCap":"Different data, one line = cores fight over invalidation"},"x-co-completablefuture-compose":{"q":"Why CompletableFuture, and how does thenCompose differ from thenApply?","a":"CompletableFuture chains asynchronous steps without blocking the thread. thenApply takes the result and transforms it (T→U). thenCompose takes the result and returns a NEW future (T→CF<U>) — for chaining asynchronous calls, otherwise you get a nested CF<CF<U>>.","d":"thenApply = map, thenCompose = flatMap. By default the then* run on the same thread that completed the previous step (or ForkJoinPool.commonPool for the *Async variants). Under load, pass your own executor to thenApplyAsync(fn, pool). Catch errors via exceptionally/handle, otherwise they get swallowed.","note":"thenApply/thenCompose without an explicit Executor run on the thread that completed the previous stage (often the common ForkJoinPool or the completing thread) — blocking code there stalls the whole pool. For blocking steps always pass your own Executor (thenApplyAsync(fn, pool)).","hook":"thenApply = peel the potatoes. thenCompose = send a courier who fetches the result themselves.","more":"`CompletableFuture` is a container for a result that will appear later, plus a set of callbacks. You describe a **pipeline** of steps, and the thread doesn't block waiting — the continuation runs once the result is ready.\n\nThe difference is in the function's type. `thenApply` takes `T -> U` (a pure value transformation). `thenCompose` takes `T -> CompletableFuture<U>` — that is, the next step is itself asynchronous.\n\n- If you return a future inside `thenApply`, you get `CF<CF<U>>` — you'd have to unwrap it manually\n- `thenCompose` \"flattens\" the nesting itself (analogous to `flatMap` on Stream/Optional) — use it for chaining async calls, and `thenApply` for synchronous processing of the result","quizCorrect":"thenCompose returns a new future (T→CF<U>), avoiding a nested CF<CF<U>>","quizWrong":["thenCompose blocks the thread until completion, thenApply doesn't","thenApply works only with numbers, thenCompose — with any types","thenCompose runs synchronously, while thenApply always runs on another thread"],"picCap":"Apply = map, Compose = flatMap (no nesting)"},"co-executor":{"q":"ExecutorService, Runnable vs Callable, Future?","a":"ExecutorService is a crew of workers (a thread pool). You throw it a task via submit() and get a ticket — a Future. Later you call future.get() — it waits until the work is done and hands you the result. Runnable works silently (returns nothing), Callable returns an answer and can throw an exception. Done — be sure to shutdown(), otherwise the crew doesn't disperse and the program hangs.","d":"submit(Callable) returns a Future immediately without blocking — the task runs in the background. Blocking happens only on future.get(). Runnable.run() returns void and declares no checked exceptions; Callable.call() returns a value of type T and can throw a checked exception (inside the Future it's wrapped in an ExecutionException, thrown on get()). shutdown() forbids accepting new tasks and lets current ones finish; shutdownNow() tries to interrupt. Without shutdown(), non-daemon pool threads keep the JVM alive.","note":"shutdown() doesn't interrupt already-running tasks and doesn't wait for them — you need shutdownNow() + awaitTermination. And submit() swallows exceptions inside the Future: if you don't call get(), a failed task dies silently, with no stack trace in the logs.","hook":"The pool is a crew. submit = order + ticket (Future). get() = you wait at the pickup window. Left? Close your shift: shutdown().","more":"`ExecutorService` separates **what** to run from **how** (on which thread). Inside there's a thread pool and a task queue: `submit()` puts the task in the queue and immediately returns a **Future** (a receipt), a free thread picks it up and runs it. `future.get()` blocks the calling thread until the result is ready.\n\nWhy a pool: creating a thread per task is expensive, the pool reuses them. The interface difference: **Runnable** returns nothing and throws no checked exceptions, **Callable** returns a value and can throw an exception (which flies out of `get()`).\n\n- Pitfall: without `shutdown()` non-daemon pool threads keep the JVM alive — the program won't terminate.\n- `get()` is blocking — for long tasks use the variant with a timeout.","quizCorrect":"Callable returns a result and can throw a checked exception","quizWrong":["Callable runs faster because it doesn't use threads","Runnable returns a Future, and Callable doesn't","Callable can't be passed to ExecutorService.submit() at all, only Runnable is accepted"],"picCap":"Throw a task into the pool → get a Future → .get() waits for the result. At the end, shutdown()."},"co-sync-tools":{"q":"CountDownLatch / CyclicBarrier / Semaphore — which one when?","a":"Three tools for \"wait/let through/limit\":\n- CountDownLatch — you wait until N events happen, then move on. One-shot.\n- CyclicBarrier — you gather N threads at one point and release them all together. Reusable.\n- Semaphore — you hand out N \"passes\": only N may access the resource at once, the rest wait.","d":"CountDownLatch: a counter from N down to 0, countDown() decrements, await() blocks until zero. It does NOT reset back — you need a new object. One waits for many OR many wait for a start.\n\nCyclicBarrier: N threads call await(); when the last one arrives — the barrier opens for everyone at once and automatically resets for the next cycle. Optionally a barrierAction runs once on trip. The threads wait for EACH OTHER.\n\nSemaphore: N permits. acquire() takes a permit (blocks if there are 0), release() returns one. Classic — a pool of N connections / N parking spots. The fairness flag gives a FIFO queue.\n\nKey difference: Latch and Barrier synchronize a MOMENT, Semaphore limits a COUNT.","note":"CyclicBarrier, on a timeout/interrupt of ONE thread, breaks the whole barrier (BrokenBarrierException for everyone else) — a common pain. Semaphore is non-fair by default: starvation is possible; and release() can be called more times than acquire — it silently hands out extra permits.","hook":"Latch = wait for all guests then leave; Barrier = runners at the start, waiting for each other; Semaphore = N parking spots.","more":"All three from `java.util.concurrent` solve the problem of coordinating threads, but in different ways.\n\n- **CountDownLatch** — a down counter: threads wait on `await()` until others call `countDown()` N times. Once it counts to zero, the gate is open forever. **One-shot**, can't be reset. Typical: \"wait until N services have loaded\".\n- **CyclicBarrier** — a rendezvous point: the first N−1 threads wait, the N-th opens the barrier for everyone at once. **Cyclic** — after a pass it counts again. For iterative phase-based computations.\n- **Semaphore** — a pool of permits: `acquire()` takes a pass, `release()` returns it; if there are no passes — the thread waits. It limits **simultaneous** access to a resource (a connection pool).\n\nPitfall: forgot `release()` on the semaphore in `finally` — permits leak and everything stalls.","quizCorrect":"CyclicBarrier","quizWrong":["CountDownLatch — it's one-shot, doesn't reset","Semaphore with 5 permits","A synchronized block on a shared object"],"picCap":"Latch: wait for N events (once). Barrier: gather N and release together (repeatable). Semaphore: let only N through at once."},"co-cow":{"q":"CopyOnWriteArrayList — when to choose it?","a":"CopyOnWriteArrayList is a list for \"read often, write rarely\". On any write (add/set/remove) it COPIES the entire array and swaps the reference. In return, reading is lock-free and very fast. The iterator works on a \"snapshot\" of the array and never throws ConcurrentModificationException. Classic: lists of listeners/subscribers.","d":"A write holds a shared lock and does an Arrays.copyOf of the whole array — O(n) memory and time on every insertion, so it's not suitable for frequent writes (use ConcurrentHashMap-based structures or an ordinary list with synchronization). A read reads a volatile reference to an immutable array, so it doesn't block. The iterator fixes the array at the moment of creation: it won't see new writes, and the iterator's remove() throws UnsupportedOperationException. Ideal when there are few subscribers and the list changes rarely but is traversed often (event listeners, observers).","note":"Every write copies the whole array O(n) — on large or frequently-changing lists this kills performance and memory. The iterator works on a snapshot: you will NOT see changes made after its creation (you read stale data).","hook":"A guest book: reading list (\"often\") is free, but adding an entry = rewriting the whole book from scratch.","more":"`CopyOnWriteArrayList` stores data in an array marked `volatile`. On any modification (`add`/`set`/`remove`) it **copies the whole array** under a lock, modifies the copy, and atomically swaps the reference. Readers work with the old array without any synchronization — hence the very fast reads.\n\nWhy: for a **many-reads, few-writes** scenario this is cheaper than locking every read. The iterator takes a **snapshot** of the array at the moment of creation, so it never throws `ConcurrentModificationException` and doesn't see changes made afterwards.\n\n- Pitfall: frequent writes = constant copying of the whole array — **O(n) per write** and pressure on the GC, so it's contraindicated here.\n- The iterator doesn't support `remove()`/`set()` — it's over a snapshot.\n- Classic use: lists of event listeners/subscribers.","quizCorrect":"Read often, write rarely (a list of listeners)","quizWrong":["Write often, read rarely","You need frequent random insertions in the middle","A large list with equal read and write loads"],"picCap":"Reading is cheap, a write copies the whole array"},"g-co-forkjoin-steal":{"q":"What is work-stealing in ForkJoinPool and why is it needed?","a":"Each thread has its own double-ended task queue. It takes its own tasks from one end, and if its queue is empty — it steals someone else's from the other end. That way nobody sits idle, and load balances itself across cores without a shared bottleneck.","d":"fork() puts a subtask into the local deque (LIFO for its own — hot cache), stealing goes FIFO (old, large tasks — fewer conflicts). The common pool is used by parallelStream() and CompletableFuture by default: don't block it (especially with blocking IO), or all the app's streams will stall.","hook":"A supermarket checkout: a freed-up cashier calls people from someone else's long line, nobody stands around for nothing.","more":"**Under the hood:** each worker thread has its own **work-stealing deque** (a double-ended queue). The thread puts its own subtasks (from fork) and takes them from one end (LIFO — cache locality). When its own queue is empty, the thread goes to someone else's and **steals a task from the opposite end** (FIFO — it takes the \"largest/oldest\" one, which has more work).\n\n**Why:** automatic load balancing without a single shared queue — no global lock and no bottleneck, the cores don't sit idle.\n\n- **When to use:** for recursive divide-and-conquer (`RecursiveTask`) and parallel streams. **Pitfall:** blocking operations (IO, locks) in tasks choke the pool — the thread is busy, there's nothing to steal; for blocking there's `ManagedBlocker`.","quizCorrect":"So as not to sit idle when its own queue is empty — load balancing","quizWrong":["To guarantee strict FIFO execution order of tasks","To free memory by deleting others' tasks","To raise the priority of its own tasks over others' in the queue ahead of the rest"],"picCap":"T2 is idle → steals a task from T1 at the other end","note":"The common pool's default size is available cores − 1, so on a 1-2 vCPU box (a typical container with low CPU limits) parallelStream() may end up with parallelism=1 and run sequentially — and Runtime.availableProcessors() in older JDKs ignored cgroup limits. For guaranteed control set java.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool.common.parallelism or pass your own ForkJoinPool via .submit(() -> stream.parallel()...)."},"g-co-cf-combine-exc":{"q":"thenCombine vs exceptionally vs handle in CompletableFuture?","a":"thenCombine — waits for TWO independent futures and combines their results. exceptionally — catches an error and substitutes a fallback result (not called on success). handle — is invoked ALWAYS: it receives (result, error) and decides itself what to return.","d":"exceptionally sees only success→skip, error→fallback. handle/whenComplete fire on both outcomes (whenComplete doesn't change the result, only a side effect). Common gotcha: an exception inside thenApply is wrapped in CompletionException — unwrap it via getCause(). thenCompose for dependent chains, thenCombine — for parallel ones.","hook":"Assembling a burger: you wait for the patty AND the bun (combine); if the patty got burnt — you put in a spare (exceptionally).","more":"**thenCombine:** a combinator for **two independent** futures that run in parallel. It waits for both successful results and combines them with a BiFunction. If either fails — the combination fails.\n\n**exceptionally:** an error-only handler. On the success path it's NOT called, the result passes through. On error it receives the Throwable and substitutes a fallback value — analogous to a catch returning a default.\n\n**handle:** is invoked **always** with a `(result, throwable)` pair — one of them is null. It decides itself what to return and can turn an error into a value or vice versa.\n\n- **Pitfall:** `exceptionally` gives no access to the result on success; `handle` gives both, but you have to manually check `throwable != null`. For a transformation with error handling, use `handle`.","quizCorrect":"Only if the previous stage completed with an exception","quizWrong":["Always, both on success and on error","Only if the result is successful, for post-processing of the value","Only when the future is cancelled via cancel()"],"picCap":"combine joins two; exceptionally substitutes on failure","note":"If a future is already complete when you attach thenApply/handle, the callback runs synchronously on the thread that called thenApply, not on a pool — so an \"async\" chain can unexpectedly execute on the main/HTTP thread and block it. When you need guaranteed offloading, use the *Async variants (thenApplyAsync) with an explicit Executor instead of relying on where the callback \"should\" run."},"g-co-stampedlock":{"q":"How is StampedLock more powerful than ReadWriteLock?","a":"It adds optimistic reads: you grab a \"stamp\", read WITHOUT locking, then validate(stamp) checks whether anyone wrote in the meantime. If not — the data is valid for almost free. ReadWriteLock takes a real lock on every single read.","d":"Three modes: write, read (pessimistic), tryOptimisticRead. Optimistic reads barely slow down writers. Downsides: NOT reentrant (re-acquiring = deadlock), no Condition; on a failed validate you must fall back to a real readLock. After an optimistic read, always copy fields into local variables BEFORE validate.","note":"StampedLock is NOT reentrant (re-acquiring from the same thread is a deadlock) and does not support Condition. After an optimistic read, always copy fields into local variables BEFORE validate() — if you read them afterwards, you can observe a half-updated state.","hook":"You copied your neighbor's answer, then glanced over to check whether he changed it. He didn't — great; he did — you redo it honestly.","more":"**Under the hood:** `tryOptimisticRead()` doesn't block — it returns a **stamp** (a version of the lock state). You read fields into local variables, then call `validate(stamp)`: true if no one took the write lock during your read. If false — the data may have changed, so re-read under a regular `readLock()`.\n\n**Why more powerful:** an optimistic read writes nothing to the lock's memory → no contention between readers at all. `ReadWriteLock` mutates a reader counter (CAS) on every read, which becomes a bottleneck on many cores.\n\n- **Pitfalls:** StampedLock is **not reentrant** and has no Condition support. Between read and validate, copy data into local variables — don't run logic off them. Don't naively upgrade read→write — only via `tryConvertToWriteLock`.","quizCorrect":"Lock-free read followed by a validate(stamp) check","quizWrong":["Reentrancy: you can acquire it recursively","Automatic read→write upgrade with no checks on the stamp","Built-in Condition for wait/signal"],"picCap":"read optimistically → check the stamp → ok or re-read"},"g-co-aba":{"q":"The ABA problem in CAS and how it's fixed?","a":"A thread reads value A, gets preempted; another changes A→B→A. The first does CAS(A→…) — it succeeds, since the value is \"A again\", even though everything actually changed underneath. The fix is a version/stamp: compare not just the value but a change counter too.","d":"Dangerous in lock-free stacks/queues on references: the top is \"the same\", but the node has already been reused. AtomicStampedReference adds an int stamp, AtomicMarkableReference adds a boolean mark. Every change bumps the stamp, so ABA won't pass the CAS. For primitive counters ABA is usually harmless — it matters for pointers/memory reclamation.","note":"A common misconception is that GC fully prevents ABA: it protects against memory reuse but not logical ABA — e.g. in a lock-free queue a node can validly be removed and re-added, same reference but changed semantics. Also AtomicStampedReference internally boxes the (reference+stamp) pair into a Pair object on every update, creating allocation pressure — overkill for counters; the stamp is meant for pointers.","hook":"A door tag still says \"closed\". Someone opened and closed it — the tag is unchanged, but everything inside the room was rearranged.","more":"**The gist:** CAS only compares the current value. A thread read A, stalled; another managed A→B→A. The first does `CAS(expected=A)` — it passes, since the value is \"A again\". But the world changed: e.g. a node from a freed list got reused, and the reference now points \"somewhere else\".\n\n**Why dangerous:** it most often hits lock-free stacks/queues on pointers, where the same address can be reused.\n\n- **Fix:** `AtomicStampedReference` (value + version counter) or `AtomicMarkableReference`. CAS passes only if BOTH the value AND the stamp match. In managed languages with GC, ABA is less often fatal (a node stays alive while referenced), but it's still logically possible.","quizCorrect":"AtomicStampedReference — CAS checks both the value AND the version stamp","quizWrong":["Wrap the CAS in a synchronized block and drop the compare-and-swap loop entirely","Replace CAS with a volatile write","Use a ThreadLocal for each reference"],"picCap":"the value went back to A — a naive CAS is fooled"},"g-co-blockingqueue-types":{"q":"ArrayBlockingQueue vs LinkedBlockingQueue vs SynchronousQueue?","a":"Array — a fixed-size ring buffer, one lock. Linked — node-based, optionally unbounded (backpressure is lost!), two locks (faster under load). Synchronous — capacity 0: hand-off, put waits until someone takes.","d":"The queue choice in ThreadPoolExecutor drives behavior: an unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue → maxPoolSize is ignored, risk of OOM. SynchronousQueue + a large max = a thread per task (like cachedThreadPool). PriorityBlockingQueue — by priority, unbounded. DelayQueue — elements come out on a timer. Always bound the queue under load.","note":"The main prod pitfall: a LinkedBlockingQueue with no explicit capacity is unbounded — backpressure is lost, the producer fills memory until OOM, and the consumer can't keep up. For pools/queues under load, always set capacity explicitly.","hook":"A parking lot with fixed spots (Array), an endless dirt lot (Linked — you'll fill the whole yard), and a hand-off relay with no warehouse (Synchronous).","more":"**ArrayBlockingQueue:** a fixed-capacity ring buffer, **one** ReentrantLock for put and take. Bounded — gives natural backpressure. Memory is allocated upfront.\n\n**LinkedBlockingQueue:** linked nodes, capacity optional (defaults to `Integer.MAX_VALUE` — practically unbounded!). **Two** locks (head/tail) → higher throughput under concurrent put/take, but a node allocation per element.\n\n**SynchronousQueue:** capacity 0, no storage. `put` blocks until another thread does `take` — a direct hand-off.\n\n- **Pitfalls:** an unbounded Linked in a pool = risk of OOM, backpressure lost. Synchronous is the basis of `newCachedThreadPool` (a task is handed straight to a thread or a new one is created).","quizCorrect":"maxPoolSize is ignored, tasks pile up → OOM, no backpressure","quizWrong":["It's slower than ArrayBlockingQueue because of its single lock","It doesn't support a blocking take()","It loses FIFO order as it grows"],"picCap":"array — fixed; list — grows; sync — no buffer"},"g-co-sleep-vs-wait":{"q":"Thread.sleep vs Object.wait — what's the difference for the lock?","a":"sleep does NOT release the monitor: you sleep while holding the lock, and no one can touch the data. wait releases the monitor and joins the wait queue until a notify wakes it. sleep is about time, wait is about a condition/coordination between threads.","d":"wait/notify can only be called inside synchronized on the same object (otherwise IllegalMonitorStateException). wait ALWAYS goes in a while(condition) loop — protection against spurious wakeups and against the condition changing between notify and reacquiring the lock. sleep can be called anywhere, interrupted by interrupt → InterruptedException. notifyAll is safer than notify (no lost signal).","note":"wait/notify must be called strictly inside synchronized on the same monitor (otherwise IllegalMonitorStateException), and wait ALWAYS in a while(condition) loop — because of spurious wakeups and because another thread may change the condition between notify and the actual wakeup.","hook":"sleep — you fell asleep in the stall without sliding the latch (everyone has to wait). wait — you stepped out, freed the stall, and wait outside.","more":"**Thread.sleep:** a static method of Thread, simply puts the thread to sleep for a duration. The monitor is **NOT released** — if you're inside `synchronized`, you sleep holding the lock, and no one else can touch that data. About time, not coordination.\n\n**Object.wait:** called only while holding the object's monitor. It atomically **releases the monitor** and joins the wait-set until another thread calls `notify`/`notifyAll`. After waking, it competes for the lock again.\n\n- **Pitfalls:** `wait` always inside a condition-check loop (`while(!cond) wait()`) because of spurious wakeups. `sleep` for pauses/polling — but holding a lock while sleeping is almost always a bug. The modern alternative for coordination is `Lock`/`Condition`, `CountDownLatch`.","quizCorrect":"wait releases the object's monitor, sleep does not","quizWrong":["sleep releases the monitor while wait holds it","Both release the monitor, the only difference is the time unit","wait can't be interrupted by interrupt, but sleep can"],"picCap":"sleep sleeps holding the lock; wait releases the monitor"},"g-co-livelock-starvation":{"q":"Livelock vs starvation vs deadlock — how do they differ?","a":"Deadlock — everyone is frozen, waiting on each other, 0% CPU. Livelock — threads keep actively reacting and yielding to each other, but make no progress (CPU 100%, output 0). Starvation — a thread is alive but is forever denied a resource: greedier/higher-priority threads keep cutting in.","d":"Livelock: two people in a hallway step the same way to pass each other, and again in sync — the classic naive retry with identical backoff. Fixed with a randomized delay (like Ethernet CSMA/CD). Starvation: unfair locks, low priority, a heavy writer in a ReadWriteLock without fairness. Lock(true) = a fair queue, but slower.","note":"","hook":"Two people in a doorway step the same way, over and over (livelock). Deadlock — both stand frozen. Starvation — you're always waved to the back of the line.","more":"**Deadlock:** threads cyclically hold and wait on each other's resources. Everyone stands still, CPU ~0%, no progress at all. The classic — two locks acquired in different orders.\n\n**Livelock:** threads are **active** and react to each other, constantly yielding (like two people in a hallway stepping the same way). CPU is high, but no useful progress — the state just spins.\n\n**Starvation:** a thread is able to run but systematically **doesn't get a resource**: higher-priority or \"greedy\" threads keep cutting ahead, or an unfair lock.\n\n- **Fix:** deadlock → strict lock-acquisition order / `tryLock` with a timeout. Livelock → randomized backoff. Starvation → fair locks (`new ReentrantLock(true)`), but fairness lowers throughput.","quizCorrect":"In livelock the threads are active (CPU busy) but make no progress","quizWrong":["Livelock is just another name for deadlock","In livelock the threads are fully blocked and asleep on a monitor until interrupted","Livelock only occurs with no locks at all"],"picCap":"frozen / thrashing pointlessly / forever passed over"},"g-co-tlrandom":{"q":"Why is ThreadLocalRandom faster than Math.random under load?","a":"Math.random and a shared Random keep a single seed in an AtomicLong — every thread fights to CAS that seed on each call (contention). ThreadLocalRandom gives each thread its own seed: zero contention, the number is generated locally and fast.","d":"Don't create a new Random per thread by hand — TLR does it right (avoids false sharing via padding, lazy). Call it via current(): ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(). Not suitable for cryptography (predictable) — use SecureRandom there. In Java 17+ there's RandomGenerator/RandomGeneratorFactory as a generalized API.","note":"You can't store ThreadLocalRandom.current() in a field and reuse it from another thread — current() must be called each time on the right thread, otherwise the whole point is lost (shared seed → contention and overlapping sequences).","hook":"One shared remote for the whole family — a fight. Everyone with their own remote — no one waits.","more":"**Under the hood:** `Math.random()` and a shared `Random` keep a single seed in an `AtomicLong`. Every `nextInt` does a `compareAndSet` to update the seed. With many threads this is a **shared hot spot** — the CAS keeps failing and retrying (contention + false cache sharing).\n\n**ThreadLocalRandom:** the seed lives in fields of the Thread itself (essentially thread-local). Each thread runs its own generator with no atomics and no shared memory → **zero contention**, the number is computed locally.\n\n- **When:** always prefer `ThreadLocalRandom.current()` in multithreaded code over a shared Random. **Pitfall:** you can't share an instance between threads and can't set a fixed seed for reproducibility. For crypto — only `SecureRandom`.","quizCorrect":"Each thread has its own seed — no CAS contention over a shared seed","quizWrong":["It uses the CPU's hardware random generator","It's cryptographically strong and therefore faster than the shared one","It precaches all future numbers in an array"],"picCap":"shared seed = a fight; each with its own seed = quiet"},"g-co-phaser":{"q":"Why Phaser when there's CyclicBarrier and CountDownLatch?","a":"Phaser is a barrier for multi-phase tasks with a dynamic number of participants. Unlike CyclicBarrier (fixed count) and Latch (one-shot), here you can register/deregister parties on the fly and pass through many phases with a single object.","d":"register()/arriveAndAwaitAdvance() — you arrive and wait for the phase to end; arriveAndDeregister() — you leave and decrement the count (for dynamics/termination). Override onAdvance(phase, parties) to stop the cycles. Handy for iterative algorithms (waves of computation) where the worker count changes each phase. A more complex API — reach for it only when you genuinely need dynamics/multi-phase.","note":"","hook":"A staged hike: at each rest stop we wait for everyone, but someone falls behind for good (deregister), someone catches up (register).","more":"**Under the hood:** Phaser keeps a count of registered parties and the current phase number. Each thread calls `arriveAndAwaitAdvance()` — once all have arrived, the phase goes +1 and everyone wakes at once. You can call `register()`/`arriveAndDeregister()` right during the run.\n\n**Why so:** CyclicBarrier fixes the participant count in the constructor, and Latch is one-shot. Phaser removes both limits — dynamic parties + an arbitrary number of phases with a single object.\n\n**Pitfalls:** the party count is 16-bit (max ~65535). For deep fan-out use a tiered Phaser. Rarely used — only when you genuinely need both phases and dynamics.","quizCorrect":"The participant count can change dynamically and you can pass through many phases","quizWrong":["Phaser is one-shot, while CyclicBarrier is reusable and cannot be reused after the first cycle","Phaser can't wait, it only counts down","Phaser works without threads, synchronously"],"picCap":"many phases + participants join and leave dynamically"},"g-co-condition-lostwakeup":{"q":"Why Condition instead of wait/notify, and what's the gotcha with two conditions?","a":"ReentrantLock gives several Conditions on one lock: a separate \"not empty\" and \"not full\" queue. You can wake exactly the right ones (signal on the right condition) instead of everyone. With wait/notify there's one queue — you have to notifyAll and wake extras.","d":"await/signal are called under lock() (otherwise IllegalMonitorStateException), just like wait under synchronized. The main gotcha: mixing up signal/signalAll with two conditions leads to lost wakeups and hangs — you wake the wrong queue. await in while(condition) is mandatory. signalAll is safer but less efficient. This is exactly the bounded buffer pattern.","note":"await() must be called only while holding the lock and always in a while(condition) loop — just like wait. And with separate conditions it's easy to slip: signal on 'notFull' won't wake those waiting on 'notEmpty', so you must signal exactly the condition whose predicate became true.","hook":"One turnstile, but two bells: one for those coming in, another for those going out — you call the right crowd, not everyone.","more":"**Under the hood:** on one `ReentrantLock` you create `notFull` and `notEmpty` via `lock.newCondition()`. Each Condition has its own wait queue. After a put the producer does `notEmpty.signal()`, after a take the consumer does `notFull.signal()`. You wake exactly the one waiting for the right event.\n\n**Why so:** with `wait/notify` there's a single queue per object. `notifyAll` raises both producers and consumers — the extras wake up, check the condition, and go back to sleep (spurious load).\n\n**Pitfalls:** `await()` always in a `while(condition)` loop — protection against spurious wakeups. `signal` without holding the lock — `IllegalMonitorStateException`.","quizCorrect":"Several separate wait queues on one lock — targeted signal","quizWrong":["It doesn't require holding the lock when calling await","It fully protects against spurious wakeups without while around it","It runs faster because it doesn't use the monitor"],"picCap":"two wait queues on one lock — we wake them selectively"},"sp-cfgproxy":{"q":"Why does a @Bean method called directly from a @Configuration return the singleton instead of a new object?","a":"Spring wraps the @Configuration class in a CGLIB proxy. The proxy intercepts calls to @Bean methods and returns the already-created bean from the container instead of running new again.","d":"Turned off via proxyBeanMethods=false (lite mode — but then inter-bean calls create new objects).","note":"The substitution works only in @Configuration (proxyBeanMethods=true). On @Component / @Configuration(proxyBeanMethods=false) there is no proxying — a direct call to a @Bean method creates a NEW object bypassing the container, and you end up with two different instances of a 'singleton'.","hook":"Calling a @Bean is like phoning the front desk: you get handed the same person on duty, not a fresh clone.","more":"Spring doesn't use a class annotated with **@Configuration** directly — it generates a **CGLIB subclass** (proxy) of it. All @Bean methods are overridden in that proxy.\n\nWhen one @Bean method inside the config calls another, the call doesn't go to your code but to the proxy interceptor: it first checks the container — is the bean already created? If so, it returns the existing one instead of doing `new`. That's how the **singleton** contract holds: one instance per context.\n\nGotcha: with `@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)` (lite mode, recommended for startup speed) there is no proxying — a direct call to a @Bean method really does create a new object. If you rely on singleton semantics between methods, inject the bean as a method parameter rather than calling it by hand.","quizCorrect":"Spring wraps @Configuration in a CGLIB proxy that intercepts @Bean calls","quizWrong":["Java caches the method result thanks to the @Bean annotation","@Bean methods are static, so they always return the same object","Singleton is guaranteed by the JVM itself for all configuration methods and for fields"],"picCap":"The proxy returns the bean from the container instead of doing new"},"sp-bpp":{"q":"Through what mechanism is the 'magic' of @Autowired / @Transactional applied?","a":"Through a BeanPostProcessor — a hook Spring calls before and after the initialization of every bean. That's where dependencies are injected and beans are wrapped in proxies with advice.","d":"Understand the BPP and you understand where almost all of the container's 'magic' lives.","hook":"A wardrobe room at the entrance: every bean passes through and gets dressed in its proxy costume before walking out on stage.","more":"**BeanPostProcessor** is an interface with two hooks: `postProcessBeforeInitialization` and `After...`. Spring calls them for EVERY bean at creation time, between instantiation and being ready for use.\n\nThis is exactly where the 'magic' lives: `AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor` injects dependencies marked with @Autowired, and the AOP infrastructure (`AnnotationAwareAspectJAutoProxyCreator`) **wraps the bean in a proxy** with the right advice (@Transactional, @Async, etc.) during the After phase.\n\nPractical takeaway: all the declarative functionality isn't 'the compiler knowing about @Transactional' — it's ordinary processor beans. That's why these features don't work on objects created via `new` outside the container: there's nobody to attach the proxy.","quizCorrect":"Through a BeanPostProcessor — a hook before and after bean initialization, where the proxy is also applied","quizWrong":["Through reflection right at the moment the controller method is called, replacing the class implementation on the fly","Through a bytecode agent that rewrites classes at JVM startup","Through an annotation processor at project compile time"],"picCap":"BeanPostProcessor: before init — DI, after init — proxy","note":"A BeanPostProcessor is itself instantiated EARLIER than ordinary beans, so injecting a normal bean into a BPP (especially via @Autowired) can force that bean to be created too early — before other BPPs run — and it won't get @Transactional/AOP proxying. Spring even logs \"is not eligible for getting processed by all BeanPostProcessors\"; keep BPP dependencies minimal or lazy."},"sp-circular":{"q":"How does Spring resolve a circular dependency, and why does constructor injection break it?","a":"For field/setter injection Spring puts a 'half-baked' early reference into a cache and finishes resolving it later. For constructor injection the object can't be created without the full dependency — the cycle is unresolvable and an error is thrown.","d":"A cycle is usually a sign of poor boundaries. Better to break it than to patch it with @Lazy.","note":"The early reference is placed in the cache BEFORE proxying. If a bean is part of a cycle and must be wrapped in an AOP proxy (@Transactional/@Async), the raw object without the proxy gets injected — Spring detects this and fails at startup (BeanCurrentlyInCreationException) if it can't finish resolving in time.","hook":"Two people shaking hands: via a field you can offer a half-ready hand and firm up the grip later. Via the constructor there are no hands at all — no way.","more":"With field/setter injection Spring creates the bean in two steps: first an empty object (via a no-dependency constructor), puts an **early reference** to it in the cache of 'partially created' beans, and only then resolves the dependencies (via setter/field). Bean B receives a reference to the still 'half-baked' A — by the time it's actually used, A is fully resolved.\n\nWith **constructor injection** that's impossible: to call A's constructor you need a ready B, and for B you need a ready A. There's nowhere to put an early reference — the object doesn't exist yet. Spring detects the cycle and throws `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException`.\n\nThis isn't a constructor flaw but a signal: a circular dependency is usually a design problem. Better to break it (extract a third bean, an event, `@Lazy`) than to hide it behind a field.","quizCorrect":"The object can't be created without the full dependency; for fields an early reference is placed","quizWrong":["Constructor injection isn't supported in the Spring context at all","Field injection creates two separate instances and bypasses the cycle without the container ever noticing it","Spring forbids cycles only when @Lazy is used in the constructor"],"picCap":"The early-reference cache saves field/setter, but not the constructor"},"sp-scope":{"q":"singleton vs prototype scope — what's the catch with a prototype inside a singleton?","a":"A singleton is created once. A prototype is new on every request from the container. But if you simply inject a prototype into a singleton, it gets injected ONCE — you need a scoped proxy or a Provider, otherwise the 'prototype' is effectively a singleton.","d":"ObjectProvider / @Lookup.","note":"ObjectProvider/Provider.get() and a scoped proxy give a new prototype on every call, but Spring does NOT manage its destruction — @PreDestroy on a prototype bean is never called, so you must release resources manually.","hook":"You ordered 'a fresh glass every time' but they poured one at check-in — and you drink from it the whole stay.","more":"The container creates a **singleton** once at startup and keeps it. A **prototype** is a factory that hands out a new instance on every request from the context.\n\nThe catch: injection happens ONCE — at the moment the singleton is created. If you simply inject a prototype as a field, Spring asks for an instance once and wires it in forever. From then on the singleton always works with the same object — the 'prototype' is effectively a singleton.\n\nThe fix is to inject not the object but a way to obtain it each time:\n\n- **scoped proxy** (`@Scope(proxyMode=TARGET_CLASS)`) — a proxy is injected that fetches a fresh bean on every call;\n- **ObjectProvider / Provider** — you explicitly call `.get()` wherever a new instance is needed.","quizCorrect":"It gets injected once and becomes effectively a singleton — you need a scoped proxy or a Provider","quizWrong":["Spring throws an error at startup: a prototype can't be injected into a singleton bean under any circumstances","The container recreates the singleton itself on every access to the prototype","The prototype bean is correctly recreated on every call of the singleton's method"],"picCap":"Direct inject = 1x. Provider = new per request."},"sp-self":{"q":"Why does calling your own @Transactional method via this not open a transaction?","a":"The proxy wraps the bean from the OUTSIDE. When you call this.other() inside a method, the call goes directly, bypassing the proxy — the advice (transaction/cache/async) doesn't fire.","d":"A top interview gotcha. Fix: move the method to another bean, or self-inject the proxy.","hook":"Calling yourself from inside your own room — the secretary at the entrance won't intercept that call.","more":"The advice (transaction, cache, async) lives in the **proxy** that wraps your bean from the outside. Spring injects that proxy into other beans' dependencies, not the bare object.\n\nWhen method A calls `this.b()`, `this` is the real object itself, not the proxy. The call goes directly inside the instance — the proxy interceptor isn't in that chain — so @Transactional/@Cacheable on `b()` are simply ignored.\n\nA common pitfall. Workarounds:\n\n- move `b()` into **another bean** (then the call goes through its proxy) — cleanest;\n- inject **yourself** (a reference to the proxy) and call through it;\n- `AopContext.currentProxy()` — works, but couples the code to AOP, a so-so option.","quizCorrect":"The call goes directly, bypassing the proxy, so the advice doesn't fire","quizWrong":["this always points to the proxy, so the transaction opens twice one after another","The inner call inherits the caller's transaction as REQUIRED","The proxy doesn't work because the method must be static"],"picCap":"this.other() bypasses the proxy — the advice doesn't fire","note":"Self-injection as a fix breaks under spring.main.allow-circular-references=false (the default since Spring Boot 2.6) — the container fails with a circular dependency; you need @Lazy on the self field. Also recall the @Transactional nuance: even a correct external call won't roll back on a checked exception unless rollbackFor is set — by default rollback happens only on RuntimeException/Error."},"sp-proxytype":{"q":"JDK dynamic proxy vs CGLIB — when does Spring use which?","a":"If the bean has an interface — by default a JDK proxy (proxies the interface). If there's no interface — CGLIB (generates a subclass). CGLIB can't proxy final classes/methods.","d":"proxyTargetClass=true forces CGLIB always.","note":"Since Boot 2.x the default is spring.aop.proxy-target-class=true — CGLIB is used even when an interface is present. So 'has an interface -> JDK proxy' is often wrong in plain Boot; and with CGLIB injecting a bean by its interface type still works, while a final method silently loses its advice.","hook":"There's a mask (interface) — we put on the JDK mask. None — we sculpt a CGLIB double. But you can't swap out a concrete (final) one.","more":"A **JDK dynamic proxy** can only proxy interfaces: it creates an object implementing the same interfaces as the bean. **CGLIB** generates a **subclass** of the target class and overrides its methods.\n\nSpring's rule: there's an interface — it uses a JDK proxy; no interface — CGLIB. You can force CGLIB via `proxyTargetClass=true` (the default in Spring Boot).\n\nHence CGLIB's limitations: it works by subclassing, so it can't proxy **final classes and final methods** (they can't be overridden) and it requires an accessible constructor. A JDK proxy, in turn, won't 'see' methods that aren't in the interface — call the concrete class directly and the advice won't fire. That's why the advice is to inject **by the interface** rather than by the implementation.","quizCorrect":"When the bean has no interface — a subclass is generated; final can't be proxied","quizWrong":["When the bean has an interface — CGLIB always proxies interfaces rather than classes","CGLIB is used only for @Async, and JDK for everything else","CGLIB is chosen for final classes, JDK for ordinary ones"],"picCap":"Interface -> JDK proxy, otherwise -> CGLIB (but not final)"},"sp-aopcommon":{"q":"What do @Transactional, @Async, @Cacheable, @PreAuthorize have in common?","a":"They're all proxy-based AOP. The bean is wrapped in a proxy that intercepts the call and applies the behavior. So they all share the same ailment: self-invocation and final disable them.","d":"Understand the one proxy mechanism and you understand half of Spring's gotchas at once.","hook":"All four are makeup artists at the entrance. Call yourself or use a concrete final — and the makeup doesn't get applied.","more":"All four are **proxy-based AOP**. The bean is wrapped in a proxy that intercepts the incoming method call and applies behavior: open a transaction, serve from cache, hand off to another thread, check permissions.\n\nSince the mechanism is one and the same, the **weak spots are shared**:\n\n- **self-invocation** — calling your own method via `this` bypasses the proxy, the annotation doesn't fire;\n- a **final** class/method breaks CGLIB proxying;\n- it works only on beans from the container, not on `new`;\n- usually only on **public** methods.\n\nPractical takeaway: once you remember the single model 'a proxy stands on the outside', you immediately understand why any of these annotations stopped working — the diagnosis is the same for all of them.","quizCorrect":"They're all proxy-based AOP, so they break on self-invocation and final","quizWrong":["They all require a separate thread to execute","They all work via bytecode instrumentation at compile time during the project build","They all apply only to controllers, not to services"],"picCap":"Proxy-based AOP: self-invocation breaks all, final breaks CGLIB"},"sp-rollback":{"q":"Will @Transactional roll back on a checked exception?","a":"By default NO. Rollback happens only on RuntimeException and Error. Throw a checked exception (e.g. your own DomainException extends Exception) and the transaction commits with partial changes.","d":"Fix: @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class). The most expensive silent gotcha.","note":"rollbackFor is set on the annotation itself, not globally: @Transactional(rollbackFor=Exception.class). Another trap — if an inner method threw and marked the transaction rollback-only, the outer method that 'caught' the exception and wants to commit will get an UnexpectedRollbackException.","hook":"You won't drop the receipt onto a hot stove — only boiling water burns and makes you drop everything. Checked doesn't roll back.","more":"By default Spring rolls back a transaction only on **unchecked** exceptions — `RuntimeException` and `Error`. The reasoning is historical: checked exceptions are considered an 'expected' business situation the code will handle, while runtime ones are a real failure.\n\nSo your `DomainException extends Exception`, thrown from a @Transactional method, will **commit** the changes already made — you get partially applied data, which is usually not at all what you wanted.\n\nHow to fix:\n\n- `@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)` — roll back on checked too;\n- have domain exceptions extend **RuntimeException** (a common choice in Spring projects);\n- remember that `rollbackOnly` is set on the WHOLE transaction — an exception caught at the top can't save it anymore.","quizCorrect":"No, rollback only on RuntimeException and Error — checked commits","quizWrong":["Yes, rollback happens on any exception, including checked","No, by default there is no rollback on anything at all","Yes, but only if the checked exception extends IOException directly"],"picCap":"By default checked doesn't roll back — the transaction commits"},"sp-prop":{"q":"REQUIRED vs REQUIRES_NEW — what's the difference and where are the gotchas?","a":"REQUIRED (default) — join the current transaction or create one. REQUIRES_NEW — always suspend the outer one and open a separate one. Gotcha: REQUIRES_NEW commits independently, and if the outer one rolls back, the inner one is already saved.","d":"REQUIRES_NEW is handy for audit logs, dangerous for business consistency.","note":"REQUIRES_NEW takes a SECOND connection from the pool while holding the first suspended. With pool size=1 (or a shortage of connections) this is a classic self-deadlock: the outer transaction holds a connection and waits for a second one that's no longer in the pool.","hook":"REQUIRED — you ride in a shared taxi; REQUIRES_NEW — you called your own. Yours has already left, even if the shared one turned back.","more":"**REQUIRED** (default): if a transaction is already open, the method joins it, otherwise it creates a new one. Everything works as one logical unit: a rollback anywhere rolls back everything.\n\n**REQUIRES_NEW**: the outer transaction is **suspended** and a fully independent inner one is opened — with its own commit/rollback and often a separate connection from the pool.\n\nGotchas:\n\n- the inner one **commits on its own** — if the outer one then fails, the inner changes are already in the DB with no rollback (useful for audit logs, dangerous for business data);\n- two simultaneous transactions = two connections, and with a pool shortage — **deadlock/timeout**, especially if the outer one holds a row lock that the inner one touches;\n- self-invocation breaks the propagation switch the same way.","quizCorrect":"The inner one commits independently, so if the outer rolls back it's already saved","quizWrong":["REQUIRES_NEW also rolls back together with the outer transaction as one indivisible unit","REQUIRES_NEW joins the outer one and commits together with it","REQUIRES_NEW blocks the outer one until its own rollback"],"picCap":"REQUIRES_NEW commits on its own: outer failed — inner stayed"},"sp-txprivate":{"q":"Does @Transactional work on a private method?","a":"No. The proxy can't intercept private (and the call goes via this). The annotation is simply ignored, there's no transaction.","d":"Transactional methods should be public and called from outside the bean.","hook":"The proxy is the doorman at the entrance. He won't peek into a private room through the back door — no transaction.","more":"Spring wraps the bean in a **proxy object**, and it's the proxy that opens/commits the transaction around the call. It can only intercept what's called from the outside through the proxy reference.\n\nA private method fails on two counts at once: it can't be overridden in the proxy subclass, and it's usually called via `this.method()` — i.e. bypassing the proxy, on the raw object. The annotation stays, but nobody reads it.\n\n- Gotcha: a `public` method called inside the same class via `this` also bypasses the proxy — there will be no transaction.\n- Fix: move the transactional method to a separate bean or use self-injection.","quizCorrect":"No, the proxy can't intercept private — the annotation is ignored","quizWrong":["Yes, a separate CGLIB proxy is created for private methods on top of the ordinary one","Yes, but only with propagation REQUIRES_NEW","Yes, if the method is called from another bean"],"picCap":"@Transactional on private is silently ignored"},"sp-txconn":{"q":"Where does the transaction 'live' while a method runs?","a":"The connection/transaction context are bound to the current thread via a ThreadLocal (TransactionSynchronizationManager). That's why all code in one thread inside the method sees the same transaction.","d":"Move to another thread (@Async, parallelStream) and the transaction does NOT carry over.","hook":"The transaction sits in the thread's pocket (ThreadLocal): all code in one thread pulls it out of the same pocket.","more":"Spring places the active connection and transaction metadata into a **ThreadLocal** inside `TransactionSynchronizationManager`. A ThreadLocal is a 'variable that's private to each thread', so any code running in the same thread retrieves exactly the same transaction.\n\nThat's how `@Transactional`, repositories, and the `EntityManager` 'find' the right connection without passing it by hand through parameters.\n\n- Gotcha: move to **another thread** (a new Thread, `@Async`, parallelStream, reactive) and the ThreadLocal won't travel there, so the code ends up outside the transaction.\n- That's why the classic thread-bound approach doesn't work in a reactive stack (WebFlux).","quizCorrect":"In the current thread's ThreadLocal (TransactionSynchronizationManager)","quizWrong":["In the bean's proxy object itself, shared across all threads and all requests","In the HTTP request session bound to the user","In the connection pool as global application state"],"picCap":"One thread -> one TX in ThreadLocal: all the method's code sees it"},"sp-nplus1":{"q":"What is N+1 and how do you fix it?","a":"You load a list of N entities (1 query), and for each lazy association another query is fired -> N+1 queries instead of one or two. It hits performance imperceptibly.","d":"Fix: JOIN FETCH, @EntityGraph, batch fetch. Catch it via the SQL log / Hibernate statistics.","hook":"You took the guest list and then run to the kitchen separately for each one. 1 list + N runs instead of a single tray.","more":"You make 1 query and get a list of N entities. Then in a loop you touch a **lazy association** on each (`order.getItems()`) — and Hibernate fires a separate SELECT for each. That's 1 + N queries instead of one or two.\n\nThe insidious part is that with 10 rows in dev everything flies, while in prod with 10,000 rows the database falls over — the bug isn't visible in the code, only in the SQL logs.\n\n- Fix: `JOIN FETCH` in JPQL, `@EntityGraph`, or `@BatchSize` (then the N queries collapse into batches with IN).\n- Prevention: enable SQL logging and count the actual queries on a typical scenario.","quizCorrect":"For a list of N entities, another N queries fire for the lazy associations","quizWrong":["N threads simultaneously open one connection each to the DB from the shared pool","One query returns N times more rows because of a join","N+1 repeated commits of one transaction on retries"],"picCap":"N+1: list + 1 per association -> fix with JOIN FETCH","note":"JOIN FETCH fixes N+1 but silently breaks pagination: with a collection `JOIN FETCH` plus `setMaxResults`/Pageable, Hibernate can't push LIMIT into SQL (rows are multiplied by the join), so it pulls the WHOLE result into memory and paginates there — you'll see `HHH000104: firstResult/maxResults specified with collection fetch; applying in memory`. For paginating collections use @BatchSize or a two-step @EntityGraph load, not a fetch join."},"sp-lazyinit":{"q":"Where does LazyInitializationException come from?","a":"You accessed a lazy association AFTER the persistence context was closed (outside @Transactional). The association proxy tries to hit the DB, but the session is already gone.","d":"Fix: load what you need within the transaction (fetch/DTO projection) instead of dragging the entity into the controller.","hook":"You reached into the fridge after the store had closed: the association is lazy, but the session is already gone.","more":"A lazy association is loaded not immediately but on first access — and for that it needs a **live session** (persistence context). The `@Transactional` method finished, the session closed, the entity became detached.\n\nWhen you later call `getItems()` in a controller/template, the association proxy goes to the DB — but the session is gone. Hence the `LazyInitializationException`.\n\n- The proper fix: load what you need inside the transaction (`JOIN FETCH`, `@EntityGraph`) or return a **DTO** assembled while the session is still open.\n- Anti-pattern: fixing this by enabling OSIV or `FetchType.EAGER` — it hides the problem and breeds N+1.","quizCorrect":"Accessing a lazy association after the persistence context is closed","quizWrong":["The lazy association was loaded twice in one session","The entity isn't marked @Entity, so no proxy was created for that particular class","The transaction became read-only and blocked loading"],"picCap":"Lazy association touched outside @Transactional — the session is already gone","note":"OSIV (spring.jpa.open-in-view) is ON BY DEFAULT in Spring Boot and masks LazyInitializationException — it keeps the session open until the end of the HTTP response, so the problem won't reproduce in a web controller but instantly surfaces in @Async, a Kafka listener, or a test without web context. Boot logs a WARN about this at startup; set `open-in-view: false` to catch N+1 and lazy issues immediately rather than in prod."},"sp-osiv":{"q":"What is Open Session In View and why is it a controversial default?","a":"By default Spring Boot keeps the persistence context open until the response is rendered, so lazy associations don't fail. You pay with a long-held connection and hidden N+1 in the view.","d":"spring.jpa.open-in-view=false + deliberate data loading is the best practice for services.","note":"OSIV masks LazyInitializationException in dev and blows up in prod: a pooled connection is held for the entire JSON serialization, and under load the pool gets exhausted and requests queue up. In Boot it's disabled via spring.jpa.open-in-view=false (default true + a warning in the log).","hook":"The waiter doesn't leave your table until dessert — in case you ask for something. Convenient, but the table is occupied all evening.","more":"Open Session In View keeps the persistence context open **for the whole HTTP request** — while the controller works and the response is serialized. So lazy associations are loaded even in the view and no `LazyInitializationException` is thrown. In Spring Boot this is enabled by default.\n\nThe price: a pooled connection is busy for the entire rendering, not just the service's working time — under load the pool is exhausted faster.\n\n- Downside: lazy loads hide in the presentation layer, where they're easy to miss -> hidden **N+1**.\n- Deliberate alternative: turn OSIV off and load everything you need explicitly (fetch/DTO) inside the service layer.","quizCorrect":"Holds the connection open for a long time and hides N+1 in the view","quizWrong":["Completely disables lazy loading of associations","Opens a new session for each lazy association separately","Makes all transactions read-only until rendering finishes on the server side"],"picCap":"Open Session In View: the connection is held until rendering finishes"},"sp-dirty":{"q":"Why is an entity change saved without calling save(), and when is the UPDATE fired?","a":"Hibernate does dirty checking: a managed entity in an open session is compared against a snapshot, and on flush (usually before commit/query) it automatically generates an UPDATE. An explicit save isn't needed.","d":"That's why an accidental change to a managed entity = an unplanned UPDATE.","note":"Dirty checking works only for managed entities in an open session. Edit a detached object (returned from a controller, session closed) — no UPDATE is sent; you need an explicit merge/save, and merge returns a NEW managed instance, while your old one stays detached.","hook":"Hibernate is like mom: she memorized the room before cleanup, saw the mess, and wrote it down herself. No need to call save.","more":"An entity loaded in an open session becomes **managed**, and Hibernate remembers its original snapshot. Before flush (usually at commit or before the next query) it compares the current state with the snapshot — that's **dirty checking** — and generates an UPDATE for the changed fields by itself.\n\nSo a separate `save()` isn't needed for an already-loaded entity: change a field and Hibernate will notice and persist it.\n\n- Gotcha: accidentally change a field of a managed entity and an unplanned UPDATE flies off.\n- For a detached object (outside the session) there's no auto-save — there you need an explicit `merge`/`save`. The defense against accidental writes is `@Transactional(readOnly = true)`.","quizCorrect":"Hibernate compares against a snapshot (dirty checking) and sends an UPDATE on flush","quizWrong":["Any field change writes an UPDATE to the DB immediately","Spring Data intercepts setters and calls save() automatically at the end of the transaction","The entity is saved only if you explicitly call merge()"],"picCap":"flush compares the snapshot -> sends the UPDATE itself, save() not needed"},"sp-sba":{"q":"What does @SpringBootApplication consist of?","a":"It's a composition of three: @Configuration (a class with beans) + @EnableAutoConfiguration (turn on auto-config) + @ComponentScan (scan the package for beans).","d":"Knowing this = understanding how a starter 'configures everything by itself' at all.","hook":"A Swiss army knife of three blades: beans + auto-configuration + package scanning — in a single annotation.","more":"`@SpringBootApplication` is a **meta-annotation**, a fusion of three:\n\n- `@Configuration` — the class can declare beans (methods with `@Bean`).\n- `@EnableAutoConfiguration` — turn on the auto-configuration mechanism based on the classpath.\n- `@ComponentScan` — scan this class's package and nested ones for `@Component/@Service/@Repository/@Controller`.\n\nWhy: one annotation instead of three covers 99% of typical applications.\n\nGotcha: `@ComponentScan` scans **from the main class's package downward**. Put the main class in a deep or sibling package and some beans won't be found. Keep it in the project's root package.","quizCorrect":"@Configuration + @EnableAutoConfiguration + @ComponentScan","quizWrong":["@Controller + @Service + @Repository","@Bean + @Autowired + @Component","@EnableWebMvc + @RestController + @RequestMapping on the configuration class"],"picCap":"3 annotations in one","note":"Beyond 'put the main class in the root': @SpringBootApplication is configurable, but scanBasePackages/exclude on it are attributes forwarded into @ComponentScan/@EnableAutoConfiguration via @AliasFor — and if you add YOUR OWN explicit @ComponentScan on the same class, it OVERRIDES the built-in one rather than augmenting it, suddenly disabling default-package autoscanning. Use the @SpringBootApplication attributes themselves, not a second annotation."},"sp-autoconfig":{"q":"How does a starter 'magically' configure beans you never wrote?","a":"The starter contains a list of auto-configs (AutoConfiguration.imports). Each is marked with @ConditionalOnClass/@ConditionalOnMissingBean — it applies only if the needed class is on the classpath and you haven't defined the bean yourself.","d":"That's why adding a dependency gives you the setup; defining your own bean makes the auto-config back off.","hook":"The starter is a guest with tools: it pulls them out only if the needed part is on the table and you don't already have your own.","more":"A starter pulls a JAR onto the classpath with ready-made config classes listed in the file `META-INF/spring/...AutoConfiguration.imports`. Spring reads this list at startup.\n\nEach config is decorated with **conditions**: `@ConditionalOnClass` (the needed class is on the classpath), `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` (you haven't declared such a bean yourself), `@ConditionalOnProperty`, etc. If a condition isn't met, the config is quietly skipped.\n\n- Why: add a dependency — get working defaults; define your own bean — yours wins.\n- Gotcha: the 'magic' sometimes doesn't fire because a condition wasn't met. Run with `--debug` and look at the **Conditions Evaluation Report** — it shows what was enabled and why something wasn't.","quizCorrect":"Auto-configs from imports apply by the conditions @ConditionalOnClass/@ConditionalOnMissingBean","quizWrong":["Spring scans the jar and instantiates every public class as a bean","It uses reflection to create beans for all interfaces in the dependencies found on the classpath at startup","Beans are generated at build time and baked into the jar"],"picCap":"The conditions match — the bean creates itself","note":"Ordering and placement of conditions is the real trap: @ConditionalOnBean/@ConditionalOnMissingBean only see beans REGISTERED BY THE TIME that auto-config is processed, so everything depends on order (@AutoConfiguration(before/after=...)), and these bean conditions are simply unreliable on a user's @Configuration because user component-scanning runs AFTER auto-configs. Also, since Boot 2.7+ the list lives in `META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports`, with the old `spring.factories` deprecated."},"sp-propord":{"q":"What overrides what: application.yml, an env variable, a command-line argument?","a":"The 'closer to launch', the higher the priority: command-line args > environment variables/system properties > application-{profile} > application.yml. External overrides baked-in.","d":"@ConfigurationProperties (grouped, type-safe) is preferable to a scattering of @Value.","note":"In Boot 2.4+ the merge order of profile-specific files changed (spring.config.import, document order), and @PropertySource does NOT participate in this hierarchy at all — it's loaded earlier and easily overridden by anything.","hook":"Whoever is closer to the door at launch gives the orders: a command-line arg is louder than env, env is louder than yml.","more":"Spring assembles config from many sources into a single `Environment` and orders them by priority. The rule is simple: **the closer to the moment of launch and the more 'external' the source, the higher its priority** and the more strongly it overrides the rest.\n\nOrder (descending): command-line arguments > JVM system properties (`-D`) / environment variables > `application-{profile}.yml` > `application.yml`.\n\n- Why: the same artifact runs in dev/stage/prod — the external environment overrides the baked-in defaults without a rebuild.\n- Gotcha: the exact order is longer (there's also `@TestPropertySource`, config data import); when in doubt, check the actual value via Actuator `/env`.","quizCorrect":"Command-line arguments > env/system properties > application.yml","quizWrong":["application.yml has the highest priority and overrides everything external","The earlier a source is read, the higher its priority","All sources are merged, and on conflict the last alphabetically wins"],"picCap":"Closer to launch — higher priority: external overrides baked-in"},"sp-filterchain":{"q":"How is Spring Security structured at the top level?","a":"It's a chain of filters (SecurityFilterChain) in front of your controllers. The request passes through filters: authentication -> setting the context -> authorization. If it doesn't pass, it never reaches the controller.","d":"The user context lives in the SecurityContextHolder (ThreadLocal).","hook":"Turnstiles at the entrance one after another: don't tap your pass and you won't reach the office-controller.","more":"Spring Security inserts a **chain of filters** (`SecurityFilterChain`) into request processing BEFORE your controllers. These are servlet filters running in a strict order.\n\nThe request passes through them one by one: authentication filters determine who you are and put an `Authentication` into the `SecurityContext` (that same ThreadLocal), then the authorization filter checks the permissions for this URL/method. If it doesn't pass, the filter returns 401/403 itself, and the request never reaches the controller.\n\n- Why this way: security is separated from business logic and works centrally at the entrance.\n- Gotcha: filter order matters; a custom filter without an explicit position is easy to place wrong — and the check runs earlier/later than needed.","quizCorrect":"A chain of filters (SecurityFilterChain) before the controllers: authentication and authorization","quizWrong":["An aspect (AOP) wrapping every controller method with a permission check","An interceptor in the DispatcherServlet after mapping to the controller","A check in the controller itself via @PreAuthorize before the business logic of every request that reaches it"],"picCap":"Filters stand before the controllers: didn't pass — stop","note":"Spring Security 6 / Boot 3 finally removed WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter — you now declare a SecurityFilterChain bean and use authorizeHttpRequests (not authorizeRequests). Subtle trap: with multiple chains the FIRST one whose securityMatcher matches wins, so @Order between beans is critical, and authorizeHttpRequests evaluates rules top-to-bottom — a broad anyRequest().permitAll() placed above specific rules silently opens everything."},"sp-jwt":{"q":"How does a resource server validate a JWT from Keycloak without hitting the DB?","a":"The JWT is signed by Keycloak. The service takes the public key (via the JWKS url) and verifies the signature + exp/iss/aud locally. Valid — extract roles/claims, no requests to Keycloak on every call.","d":"That's the whole point of a stateless token. Revocation is a separate pain (short TTL / introspection).","hook":"A passport with a hologram: you check the seal with your own UV flashlight on the spot, you don't phone the passport office.","more":"Keycloak signs the token with its private key (asymmetric, usually RS256). The service downloads the **public key** once via the JWKS endpoint (`/.well-known` -> `jwks_uri`) and caches it. After that, on every request it computes the signature locally and compares it with the one in the token — math, no network.\n\nWhy: the signature proves the token was issued by Keycloak and hasn't been tampered with. It also locally checks `exp` (not expired), `iss` (the right issuer), `aud` (meant for us). All of this is self-contained inside the JWT.\n\n- Gotcha: keys are rotated — you need to refetch JWKS by `kid`, otherwise valid tokens start getting rejected.\n- You can't revoke a JWT early — it lives until `exp`. That's why access tokens are made short-lived.","quizCorrect":"Takes the public key via JWKS and verifies the signature + exp/iss/aud locally","quizWrong":["On every request it hits Keycloak's introspection endpoint","Looks the token up in a local session table and checks its status in the database","Decrypts the payload with a symmetric secret from application.yml"],"picCap":"The key from JWKS is cached -> verification is local on every call","note":"By default Spring maps authorities from the 'scope'/'scp' claim with a 'SCOPE_' prefix, not from Keycloak's realm_access.roles, so hasRole('ADMIN') silently fails — you need a custom JwtAuthenticationConverter/GrantedAuthoritiesConverter that extracts realm_access/resource_access and adds the 'ROLE_' prefix. Separate landmine: NimbusJwtDecoder caches JWKS but does NOT validate 'aud' by default — add a JwtValidators/audience check manually, or a token minted for another client will pass."},"x-sp-bean-lifecycle":{"q":"What happens to a bean between \"new\" and \"ready to use\"? Why do we need BeanPostProcessors?","a":"Spring creates the object, injects dependencies, then runs it through the chain of BeanPostProcessors: before init and after. It's the \"after\" stage that swaps the bean for a proxy (@Transactional, @Async). Then @PostConstruct/init. Without BPPs there'd be no AOP magic.","d":"Full order: instantiate → populate (DI) → Aware interfaces → postProcessBeforeInitialization → @PostConstruct/afterPropertiesSet/init-method → postProcessAfterInitialization (here the AutoProxyCreator wraps it in a proxy) → bean in the container. On shutdown: @PreDestroy/destroy. Important: inside @PostConstruct the bean is still the ORIGINAL, while the container hands out the PROXY to the outside world, so self-invocation in @PostConstruct isn't proxied either.","hook":"The car is built on the assembly line, but at the exit it gets SHRINK-WRAPPED — what ships is the wrapper, not the bare body.","more":"Lifecycle: Spring instantiates the object, performs **dependency injection**, then runs it through the chain of `BeanPostProcessor`s — each has two hooks: `postProcessBeforeInitialization` and `...After...`. Between them, initialization is triggered (`@PostConstruct`, `afterPropertiesSet`, init-method).\n\nThe key is that `...After...` is usually what **wraps the bean in a proxy**: the AOP infrastructure replaces your object with a dynamic proxy that intercepts calls for `@Transactional`, `@Async`, `@Cacheable`.\n\n- That's why the container holds not your `new`, but a proxy — hence the self-invocation gotcha (`this.method()` bypasses the proxy and the annotation doesn't fire)\n- BPP is the extension point of the whole framework: without it there'd be no transactions, no cache, no validation","quizCorrect":"BeanPostProcessor at the \"after initialization\" stage","quizWrong":["The bean's constructor when new is called","@PostConstruct inside the bean itself","The garbage collector during object finalization runs it"],"picCap":"BPP \"after\" swaps the bean for a proxy","note":"BeanPostProcessors are themselves created before ordinary beans and do NOT go through the full post-processing chain — so dependencies injected into a BPP often don't get proxied (the classic 'is not eligible for getting processed by all BeanPostProcessors' warning). Another trap: proxying via `...AfterInitialization` breaks injection by concrete class with JDK dynamic proxies — a field typed `MyServiceImpl` won't resolve, you need an interface or `proxyTargetClass=true` (CGLIB), and CGLIB in turn can't proxy final classes/methods."},"x-sp-qualifier-primary":{"q":"Two beans of the same type — Spring fails with NoUniqueBeanDefinition. How to resolve: @Primary, @Qualifier, parameter name?","a":"@Primary — \"pick this one by default\". @Qualifier(\"name\") — \"pick exactly this one, explicitly\". The parameter name also works as a qualifier. Priority: @Qualifier > @Primary > parameter name. If none — NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException at startup.","d":"@Primary is global and dangerous: it's easy to forget that it silently hijacked an injection somewhere. @Qualifier is local and explicit — preferable in large projects. You can build your own qualifier annotation via @Qualifier meta-annotation. For collections (List<Payment>) Spring injects ALL beans of the type — a legitimate way to gather strategies. Map<String,Payment> gives bean-name → bean.","hook":"Primary is the default waiter on duty. Qualifier is you pointing your finger: \"call that one over there\".","more":"When the type is ambiguous, Spring tries to pick a candidate by the rules. If it can't, it fails at startup with `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` (which is good: a config error shows up immediately, not at runtime).\n\nMechanisms and their **priority**:\n- `@Qualifier(\"name\")` — most explicit and strongest: \"this exact bean\"\n- the parameter/field name matches the bean name — works as an implicit qualifier\n- `@Primary` on a bean — weakest, \"the default if nothing was specified\"\n\nIn practice: put `@Primary` on the main implementation (the one wanted in 90% of places), and `@Qualifier` pointwise where the second one is needed. Don't smear `@Primary` across several beans of the same type — you'll get a conflict again.","quizCorrect":"NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException","quizWrong":["BeanCreationException due to a circular reference","NoSuchBeanDefinitionException","Silently takes the first one alphabetically"],"picCap":"Qualifier overrides Primary","note":"Injecting into `List<Payment>` collects beans in `@Order`/`Ordered`/`@Priority` order, NOT declaration order — a frequent surprise when strategies 'suddenly' change priority. And `@Primary` does NOT help for collections (all beans go in anyway), yet it silently loses to a `@Qualifier` even on the `@Primary` bean itself; and with multiple `@Primary` beans of one type Spring fails NoUniqueBeanDefinition again — `@Primary` is not 'strongest of all', it's just a tie-breaker among candidates."},"x-sp-isolation":{"q":"Transaction isolation levels: what are dirty read, non-repeatable, phantom — and where does READ_COMMITTED fit in?","a":"Isolation = how much your transaction sees the mess of its neighbors. READ_UNCOMMITTED sees even uncommitted data (dirty). READ_COMMITTED — only committed. REPEATABLE_READ — a row doesn't change within the tx. SERIALIZABLE — as if transactions ran one at a time.","d":"Three anomalies in increasing severity: dirty read (you see someone's update that gets rolled back) → non-repeatable read (you re-read a row and it changed) → phantom read (you repeat the same SELECT and new rows appear). Each level suppresses its share: READ_COMMITTED removes dirty, REPEATABLE_READ — non-repeatable, SERIALIZABLE — phantom. The price is more locking and less concurrency. Postgres default = READ_COMMITTED, Oracle too; MySQL InnoDB = REPEATABLE_READ. @Transactional(isolation=...) overrides it.","note":"PostgreSQL has no READ_UNCOMMITTED (its dirty read is unavailable and silently upgrades to READ_COMMITTED), and its REPEATABLE_READ is in fact already close to snapshot/SERIALIZABLE — isolation-level behavior depends on the DBMS, not on the JPA spec.","hook":"Roommates in a shared flat: RU — you see someone's soup right in the pot, SER — the kitchen is used one at a time, by booking.","more":"Isolation determines which **uncommitted effects** of neighboring transactions are visible to yours. The higher the level, the fewer anomalies, but the more expensive (more locks/rollbacks).\n\nThree anomalies:\n- **dirty read** — you read someone's uncommitted changes (the neighbor later rolls back — you read a phantom). Already blocked by READ_COMMITTED\n- **non-repeatable read** — you re-read the same row in your tx and the value changed. Blocked by REPEATABLE_READ\n- **phantom** — a repeated query by a condition returned NEW rows (inserted by a neighbor). Blocked by SERIALIZABLE\n\nREAD_COMMITTED — Postgres default: you see only what was committed as of each query. Cheap and sufficient for most tasks; for monetary invariants add explicit locks or raise the level.","quizCorrect":"READ_COMMITTED","quizWrong":["READ_UNCOMMITTED","REPEATABLE_READ","SERIALIZABLE"],"picCap":"The further right, the stricter and slower"},"x-sp-optimistic-lock":{"q":"Two users edit the same record at once. How does @Version protect against a lost update?","a":"The @Version field increments on every UPDATE. Hibernate sends UPDATE ... WHERE id=? AND version=?. If someone saved earlier — the version doesn't match, 0 rows are updated → OptimisticLockException. No DB locks, the conflict is caught after the fact.","d":"Optimistic locking = \"we trust conflicts are rare, we'll check at commit\". Cheap, holds no locks, ideal for the web with long user \"think\" times. Pessimistic (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE / @Lock(PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)) — the opposite, locks the row immediately, more expensive, but no retries. On OptimisticLockException you usually retry or show \"data is stale, refresh\". @Version can be int/long or a timestamp.","note":"@Version is checked only on a dirty-update of the versioned entity itself. Changing just its child @OneToMany collection (a different table) doesn't bump the parent's version — optimistic lock won't catch a lost update in associated data, you need @OptimisticLock(LockModeType.OPTIMISTIC_FORCE_INCREMENT).","hook":"A table reservation \"by menu version\": show up with an old version — \"sorry, everything changed, book again\".","more":"Optimistic locking assumes conflicts are **rare**: we don't lock the row in advance, we check on save. The `@Version` field (a number or timestamp) is incremented by Hibernate on every UPDATE.\n\nIt generates `UPDATE ... SET ..., version=version+1 WHERE id=? AND version=?`. If someone saved earlier, the version in the WHERE won't match, the DB updates **0 rows** — Hibernate sees this and throws `OptimisticLockException`. The lost update is caught without a single DB lock.\n\n- Plus: scales, holds no locks, great for the web (read → think → save)\n- Gotcha: you have to handle the exception yourself — usually re-read the data and ask the user to retry (retry/merge). You can't set the version by hand","quizCorrect":"OptimisticLockException — 0 rows updated","quizWrong":["PessimisticLockException with a row lock","A deadlock at the DB level","Silently overwrites the data with the last update"],"picCap":"The late B gets an OptimisticLockException"},"p-hex-domain-vs-jpa":{"q":"Why a separate domain model Order if there's already a JPA @Entity Order? Isn't that duplication?","a":"@Entity is tied to the DB (annotations, lazy proxies, empty constructor, setters) — it can't be always-valid with invariants. The domain model is pure Java with no DB. You pay with a boilerplate mapper, and get a domain testable without a DB.","d":"A JPA entity forces compromises: a no-args constructor, mutable fields, lazy proxies (LazyInitializationException), getters/setters — that's the road to an anemic model. The domain model knows nothing about JPA/Panache, encapsulates invariants, immutable VOs. The outbound adapter (repository) maps domain ↔ entity. Yes, the mapper is boilerplate (the downside of hexagonal), but it pays off through isolation and unit tests of the domain without Spring/DB. Don't shove @Entity into a use case.","hook":"A mannequin in a shop window (JPA — for display in the DB) ≠ a living person (the domain with behavior).","more":"This isn't duplication, it's separation of responsibilities. **@Entity** serves the DB: it needs an empty constructor, setters, lazy proxies, public fields for the annotations. Because of this it can't guarantee \"always valid\" — it can be created half-empty and changed at any moment.\n\nThe **domain model** is pure Java without a single JPA import. The constructor requires all mandatory fields, mutations only through methods that check invariants. Business rules live here.\n\n- The price is a **mapper** between Entity and domain (boilerplate), plus a bit more code.\n- The benefit: the domain is testable with unit tests without spinning up a DB, and switching the ORM/storage doesn't touch business logic. Apply it when the logic is genuinely rich; for CRUD tables it's overkill.","quizCorrect":"@Entity is tied to the DB and can't be always-valid; the domain is pure Java","quizWrong":["So the domain can be serialized to JSON without annotations","To get around the JPA limit on the number of fields in an entity","It's duplication, a separate model isn't needed — @Entity is enough with Hibernate annotations"],"picCap":"pure domain ↔ mapper (conversion happens here) ↔ JPA","note":"A non-obvious cost of separate models is losing automatic dirty checking: JPA issues an UPDATE on its own for a modified managed entity, but with manual domain→entity mapping you load the entity, copy fields, and must handle optimistic locking (@Version) yourself, otherwise you overwrite @Version and hit StaleObjectStateException or a lost update. You have to carry the version through the domain model and back into the entity on save."},"p-domain-vs-jpa-entity":{"q":"Why is a single @Entity class with business logic inside a problem, and what does separating the domain model from the JPA entity give you?","a":"When @Entity Order is also the domain object — the domain depends on JPA: you can't test it without a DB (new Order isn't enough — you need a persistence context), the DB schema dictates the domain's structure, a lazy @OneToMany throws LazyInitializationException in an unexpected place. Solution: a pure domain (Java record, zero annotations) + a separate JpaEntity + a mapper. The domain is tested as new TaxReport(...), the schema changes independently.","d":"The price is more code (entity + model + mapper); for dumb CRUD it's overkill. Use it where there's real business logic. Records (Java 21) are ideal: immutable, concise. A good reference — audit-service keeps its domain on records.","hook":"A person and their passport are different things. Don't glue a barcode to the face, create a separate document.","more":"When `@Entity Order` is simultaneously the domain, the domain depends rigidly on JPA. The under-the-hood consequences: `new Order()` isn't enough for a test — you need a live persistence context; the DB schema starts to dictate the domain's structure; a **lazy** `@OneToMany` fires `LazyInitializationException` where the business logic isn't expecting a trip to the DB at all.\n\nThe solution — split it: a pure **domain** (Java record, zero annotations), a separate `JpaEntity`, and a **mapper** between them.\n\n- The domain is tested instantly: `new TaxReport(...)` without a DB or a Quarkus context.\n- You change the DB schema independently of business rules.\n- The price is mapper boilerplate; justified for a rich domain, often excessive for CRUD tables.","quizCorrect":"The domain depends on JPA: untestable without a DB, the schema dictates the structure","quizWrong":["@Entity can't contain methods other than getters and setters according to the JPA specification","Business logic in @Entity slows down loading from the DB","JPA forbids business logic in entities by specification"],"picCap":"The domain is pure, JPA is separate, the mapper converts in between","note":"Mapping pitfall: a Java record as the domain model breaks reverse-mapping of graphs and identity — a record is immutable, so cyclic associations (Order↔OrderLine) and lazy re-loading are inexpressible in it, and equals over all fields breaks entity identity (two versions of the same Order by business id must be equal). For aggregates with identity use a regular class with equals-by-id; keep records for value objects."},"sp-stereotypes":{"q":"@Component / @Repository / @Service / @Controller — what's the difference?","a":"All four are beans (@Component during scanning). Spring finds them and puts them in the container the same way. The difference is a semantic layer label: @Repository = DB work, @Service = business logic, @Controller = web (HTTP/MVC). @Component — when the layer fits none of them.","d":"The only technically meaningful difference comes from @Repository: PersistenceExceptionTranslationPostProcessor applies to it — native DB/JPA exceptions are translated into Spring's DataAccessException hierarchy (unchecked). @Service and @Controller are, as of today, semantic markers with no special behavior (read by humans and tools). @RestController = @Controller + @ResponseBody (returns a body, not a view name). @Controller is scanned by Spring MVC for request routing. All annotations are meta-annotated with @Component, so they're picked up by @ComponentScan. You can put @Component everywhere and the app will work — but you'll lose layer readability and exception translation in repositories.","hook":"One \"bean\" passport, three job titles: cashier (DB), manager (logic), reception (web).","more":"All four are **specializations of @Component**. During component-scan Spring finds them the same way and registers them as beans in the container. So technically the behavior is almost identical.\n\nThe difference is in **layer semantics** and a couple of bonuses: **@Repository** enables translation of DB exceptions into Spring's DataAccessException; **@Controller** (and @RestController) is picked up by the web layer for request routing; **@Service** — purely a marker for business logic with no extra magic.\n\n- Use them per the layer's purpose — it improves readability and provides the right aspects (e.g., exception handling in a repository).\n- @Component — for anything that doesn't fit DAO/service/controller (utilities, config helpers).","quizCorrect":"They're semantic layer labels, the bean is created the same way","quizWrong":["Only @Component creates a bean, the others don't","Each registers the bean in the container differently","@Service creates a singleton, @Controller a prototype on every call"],"picCap":"One @Component root, three semantic layer specializations.","note":"Exception translation in @Repository doesn't kick in just from the annotation — it's enabled by PersistenceExceptionTranslationPostProcessor, which Spring Boot registers for you, but in plain Spring without Boot you must declare it as a bean explicitly, or native PersistenceException/SQLException propagate as-is. Also, default Spring Data repositories (SimpleJpaRepository) already get translation via @Transactional/proxy, so for interface-based repositories the annotation is irrelevant."},"sp-mapping-anno":{"q":"@GetMapping vs @RequestMapping?","a":"@RequestMapping — general: goes on a class/method, you set the method by hand: method=GET. @GetMapping — just shorthand for @RequestMapping(method=GET), reads more clearly. For the other verbs there's @Post/@Put/@Delete/@PatchMapping.","d":"@RequestMapping is universal: on a class it sets a common path prefix (e.g. /api), on a method — a specific path and HTTP method via method=RequestMethod.GET. If method isn't specified, the method catches ALL verbs — a common mistake. @GetMapping and friends (@PostMapping, @PutMapping, @DeleteMapping, @PatchMapping) appeared in Spring 4.3 as ready-made shortcuts: one verb, less noise, harder to get wrong. The class prefix is still usually set via @RequestMapping. In practice: class — @RequestMapping(\"/path\"), methods — @GetMapping/@PostMapping etc.","hook":"@RequestMapping is a Swiss Army knife (does everything), @GetMapping is a ready-made bottle opener: one job, but handier.","more":"**@RequestMapping** — the base annotation: it sets the path and, via attributes (method, params, headers, consumes/produces), finely tunes the mapping. Without specifying method it catches **any** HTTP verb.\n\n**@GetMapping** and its siblings are **composite** annotations that already have @RequestMapping(method=...) baked in. Less code, clearer intent, harder to accidentally open an extra method.\n\n- At the **class** level you usually put @RequestMapping with a common path prefix, and on methods — specific @Get/@PostMapping.\n- Gotcha: leaving a bare @RequestMapping without method — the endpoint will respond to both GET and POST, which is often not what you want.","quizCorrect":"It's shorthand for @RequestMapping(method=GET)","quizWrong":["@GetMapping works only on a class, not on a method","@RequestMapping can't handle GET requests","@GetMapping doesn't support specifying a URL path"],"picCap":"@GetMapping = @RequestMapping(method=GET), just shorter. Each verb has its own @...Mapping."},"sp-criteria":{"q":"Criteria API / JPA Specification — what for?","a":"User filters vary: sometimes by name, sometimes by price, sometimes by everything at once — you don't know in advance. Instead of gluing JPQL strings by hand, you build the query from objects (cb.equal, cb.like). The compiler checks types. Spring Data wraps this in a Specification — you toss it into the repository and the filters combine like building blocks.","d":"The Criteria API is building a JPQL query through Java objects (CriteriaBuilder, Root, Predicate) instead of text. Pros: type safety (a typo in a field name is caught by the compiler, especially with a metamodel), no manual concatenation and no SQL injection, easy to add conditions with an if. Con: verbose and less readable than JPQL. Spring Data JPA provides the Specification<T> interface — one spec = one Predicate; the repository extends JpaSpecificationExecutor, and specs combine via .and()/.or(). Ideal for dynamic filters (a search with lots of optional fields). For static queries @Query or derived methods are simpler — don't drag Criteria in there.","hook":"A query out of LEGO: you click together the condition-blocks you need at runtime, instead of molding a string out of clay.","more":"The **Criteria API** builds a query programmatically: through CriteriaBuilder you assemble a tree of Predicates (cb.equal, cb.like, cb.and). It's **type-safe** — an error in a field name or type is caught by the compiler (especially with a metamodel), not a runtime crash like with string-glued JPQL.\n\nThe main plus is **dynamism**: filters are assembled from the conditions that actually came from the user, without fragile ifs around string concatenation (and without SQL injection risk).\n\n- In Spring Data this is wrapped in a **Specification**: you write small reusable pieces and combine them via .and()/.or().\n- Gotcha: Criteria is verbose and reads worse — for simple fixed queries @Query or derived methods are simpler.","quizCorrect":"Type-safely assemble dynamic queries from objects","quizWrong":["Speed up queries by caching results","Replace the DB with storing objects in memory instead","Automatically create tables from entities"],"picCap":"Optional filters → assembled from objects → one WHERE","note":"Dynamic filtering via Specification easily blows up into N+1 and a cartesian product: each .and() with a join to a collection (cb.join over @OneToMany) duplicates root rows, and without root.fetch + query.distinct(true) you get a mess of duplicates and extra queries. Also Criteria's type-safety is an illusion without the generated metamodel (Entity_ classes) — with string field names root.get(\"naem\") you get exactly the same runtime failure as in JPQL."},"sp-di-types":{"q":"Constructor / setter / field injection — which is better?","a":"Use constructor (via the constructor). Make dependencies final — they can't be swapped, the object is immediately usable, you can't create it without them. You test without Spring: new Service(mockRepo). @Autowired on a field is shorter, but it hides dependencies and breaks tests. setter — only for optional ones.","d":"Why constructor is the default:\n- final + mandatory: the compiler guarantees the field is set, the object is always in a valid state (no \"half-built\" bean with null).\n- Test without a container: just new MyService(mock) — no @SpringBootTest or reflection needed.\n- Honest dependencies: a long constructor = a signal of \"too many responsibilities\" (a hint at a god class). Field injection hides this smell.\n- Spring 4.3+: if there's a single constructor, you don't need to write @Autowired over it.\n\nField (@Autowired on a field): shorter, but dependencies aren't final, the bean can be created in an invalid state, a test needs reflection/a container, it's easy to get circular dependencies unnoticed.\n\nSetter: for optional or overridable dependencies; the field can't be final.\n\nCycles: with constructor injection a circular dependency fails at startup (which is good — visible right away). With field — Spring \"swallows\" it, the problem surfaces later.","hook":"The constructor is like the ingredient list on the package: without them you can't cook the dish. Field is a stash in your pocket, nobody sees it.","more":"With **constructor injection** dependencies are passed into the constructor, and you mark the fields `final`. This means: the object can't be created without them, and after creation they can't be swapped — the state is guaranteed complete and immutable.\n\n**Why exactly this way:** all dependencies are visible in one place (if there are 7, the constructor is ugly, and that's a signal the class does too much). It's testable without Spring: `new Service(mockRepo)` — no context, reflection, or `@Autowired`.\n\n- **field injection** hides dependencies and doesn't allow `final`, breaks unit tests outside the container\n- **setter** is justified only for optional dependencies that can be re-set","quizCorrect":"Constructor: dependencies are final, the object is immediately usable","quizWrong":["Field injection: shorter and more convenient for tests and for quick prototypes","Setter injection as the main way for all dependencies","Any is equal, no difference for tests"],"picCap":"Constructor: dependencies are visible, final, the object is immediately usable. Field — hides them and hinders tests.","note":"The main real advantage of constructor injection — that a circular dependency fails at startup — was reinforced by Spring Boot 2.6+, which forbids cycles by default (spring.main.allow-circular-references=false), so field injection no longer silently 'swallows' them. But constructor injection breaks the lazy proxied self-invocation pattern for @Transactional/@Async: you can't inject yourself via the constructor (a cycle), so you need either @Lazy on the parameter or ObjectProvider — whereas field/setter self-injection used to pass silently."},"g-sp-postconstruct":{"q":"@PostConstruct runs, but @Async/@Transactional inside it somehow don't work. Why?","a":"@PostConstruct is called on the \"raw\" bean BEFORE the BeanPostProcessor wrapped it in a proxy. Aspect annotations (@Async, @Transactional, @Cacheable) live on the proxy — but in @PostConstruct you're sitting inside the real object, bypassing the proxy. So the aspects stay silent.","d":"Order: create the instance → inject dependencies → @PostConstruct (via InitDestroyAnnotationBeanPostProcessor) → then other BPPs wrap it in a proxy. Fix: move the logic into a separate bean and call its method, or listen for ApplicationReadyEvent / SmartInitializingSingleton, where the proxies are already ready.","hook":"The alarm is installed AFTER you buy the car — at the body-assembly stage it doesn't work yet","more":"**Under the hood:** Spring creates the raw object, runs the `BeanPostProcessor`s, and it's `postProcessAfterInitialization` that wraps the bean in a proxy (AOP, @Transactional, @Async). But `@PostConstruct` is invoked in the initialization phase — BEFORE that wrapping, on the bare object.\n\n**Why so:** aspects live on the proxy wrapper. Inside `@PostConstruct` you are `this`, the real object, not the proxy. Any call to your own methods bypasses interception.\n\n**Gotcha:** don't expect a transaction/async behavior in `@PostConstruct`. If you need it — move the logic into a separate bean and call it, or listen for `ApplicationReadyEvent`/`ContextRefreshedEvent` — there the proxies are already ready.","quizCorrect":"The method is called on the raw bean before wrapping in a proxy","quizWrong":["@PostConstruct forbids caching","The cache isn't configured by Spring yet","@Cacheable requires @Transactional nearby on the very same method"],"picCap":"@PostConstruct runs BEFORE proxying — aspects turn on later","note":"@Async stays silent inside @PostConstruct, but @Transactional is trickier: self-invocation never opens a transaction at all (even outside @PostConstruct), because a self.method() call goes through this and bypasses the proxy — a separate and even more common trap. Note: using TransactionTemplate/programmatic transactions inside @PostConstruct WILL work, since it doesn't rely on the proxy."},"g-sp-factorybean":{"q":"You registered a bean of type MyFactoryBean, but @Autowired MyFactoryBean fails with \"no such type\", and a completely different object gets injected instead. What's this magic?","a":"FactoryBean is a factory of beans. When you ask for the bean by name, Spring returns NOT the factory itself, but the result of its getObject(). To get the factory itself, ask with the '&' prefix: context.getBean(\"&myFb\"). Without '&' you get the product, not the machine.","d":"That's how SqlSessionFactoryBean (MyBatis), LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean etc. work. getObjectType() tells Spring what type the product will be — autowiring goes by it. In Spring Boot people more often write a @Bean method returning a ready object — it's simpler than a FactoryBean.","hook":"You ordered \"coffee\" at a café — they give you a cup, not a coffee machine. The machine is handed out only on a special request with an asterisk","more":"**Under the hood:** `FactoryBean<T>` is a special interface. By registering a bean of this type under the name `myFb`, you tell Spring: \"hand out the product of `getObject()`, not me\". So by name/type `T` you get the product.\n\n**Why so:** the mechanism is needed for complex assembly of objects that are hard to create with a normal constructor (this is how, for example, `SqlSessionFactoryBean` in MyBatis works).\n\n**Gotcha:** you get the factory itself with a prefix: `getBean(\"&myFb\")`. The type `getObjectType()` determines what's injected by `@Autowired`. If it's `null`, early type-matching breaks — Spring can't find the bean by type until full initialization.","quizCorrect":"getBean(\"&name\") with the ampersand prefix","quizWrong":["getBean(name) — it always returns the factory","Via @Qualifier(\"factory\")","No way, the factory isn't accessible from outside"],"picCap":"& gets the machine (spinning), without & — the part it produced"},"g-sp-lookup":{"q":"A singleton bean needs a FRESH prototype bean on every call. Injecting via a field is useless. How to fix it besides ObjectProvider?","a":"@Lookup. You mark an abstract (or regular) method — via CGLIB Spring overrides it and on every call goes into the context for a new instance of the prototype bean. A field is injected once at startup, while a @Lookup method — every time anew.","d":"Alternatives: ObjectProvider<T>.getObject() / Provider<T> (JSR-330) — cleaner and without CGLIB limitations. @Lookup requires the bean itself to be proxyable (not a final class/method). It's the classic fix for \"prototype inside a singleton\".","hook":"There's one water cooler (singleton), but you take a new cup each time (@Lookup)","more":"**Under the hood:** for a `@Lookup` method, Spring generates a subclass of the bean via CGLIB and overrides the method. The body is replaced with `getBean(...)` — each call returns a new prototype instance from the context.\n\n**Why so:** the problem is scope-mismatch — a singleton lives forever, while field injection happens once at startup, so the prototype \"freezes\". `@Lookup` moves the retrieval to call time.\n\n**Gotcha:** it works only on Spring-managed beans (CGLIB can't subclass a final class/method and won't override a private one). The method must have no required arguments. Essentially it's syntactic sugar over `ObjectProvider`/`ApplicationContext.getBean` — pick whichever is cleaner in your code.","quizCorrect":"Spring overrides the method, returning a new bean on every call","quizWrong":["Caches the method result forever","Makes the method transactional","Looks up the bean by the method name once at startup and returns it forever"],"picCap":"@Lookup returns a new proto on every call","note":"@Lookup won't work if the bean is created via a @Bean factory method in @Configuration — CGLIB subclassing only applies to components Spring instantiates via a constructor, so on factory-method beans the method stays unoverridden/un-invoked. In those cases use ObjectProvider, which works regardless of how the bean is created."},"g-sp-async-self":{"q":"You put @Async on a method, but it runs synchronously in the same thread. The two most common causes?","a":"1) Forgot @EnableAsync — without it the aspect isn't enabled. 2) Self-invocation: you call the @Async method from a neighboring method of the same class via this — bypassing the proxy, no aspect. Plus a gotcha: an @Async method must return void or CompletableFuture, otherwise you can't await the result.","d":"By default @Async uses SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor — it spawns a thread per call, with no pool. In prod, define your own ThreadPoolTaskExecutor. Exceptions from a void @Async are lost (caught by AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler); from a Future — they arrive on .get().","note":"The default executor for @Async is SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor, which is NOT pooled: it creates a new thread per call and under load will bring down the JVM with an OOM from thousands of threads. In prod you must define your own ThreadPoolTaskExecutor (in Boot 3.2+ you can enable virtual threads).","hook":"You ask yourself to \"do it in the background\" — but you do it right here yourself. You can only delegate to another person (the proxy)","more":"**Under the hood:** `@Async` works through a proxy: the interception puts the call into a `TaskExecutor` and immediately returns control. Without the aspect enabled or without passing through the proxy there's no interception — the method runs synchronously in the current thread.\n\n**Why so:** 1) `@EnableAsync` registers a post-processor that creates the proxy. No it — no wrapper. 2) Self-invocation: calling a neighboring method via `this` bypasses the proxy.\n\n**Gotcha:** return `void` or `CompletableFuture` — a plain object/primitive comes back empty/default. The method must be `public`. Exceptions from a `void` method are lost (caught by `AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler`), from a Future — they arrive on `.get()`.","quizCorrect":"Self-invocation via this, or @EnableAsync is missing","quizWrong":["@Async only works with void","@Transactional is missing","The default executor is forbidden in this configuration"],"picCap":"only a call through the proxy moves to another thread"},"g-sp-eventlistener":{"q":"@EventListener fired, but the DB changes the listener made after the publisher's commit aren't visible / got rolled back. What to use?","a":"@TransactionalEventListener with the AFTER_COMMIT phase. A plain @EventListener is synchronous and runs INSIDE the publisher's transaction — if it's rolled back, your work disappears too. AFTER_COMMIT guarantees: the listener runs only after a successful commit.","d":"Phases: BEFORE_COMMIT, AFTER_COMMIT (default), AFTER_ROLLBACK, AFTER_COMPLETION. Important: in AFTER_COMMIT the original transaction is already closed — if you need to write to the DB, open a NEW one (REQUIRES_NEW), otherwise the work runs outside a transaction. For background processing add @Async.","note":"@TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT) by default runs in the SAME thread after the commit, but the transaction is already closed — any save() in the listener either requires REQUIRES_NEW, or silently won't be written (no active transaction). If there's no transaction at publish time at all — the listener won't be called at all by default.","hook":"You call the courier \"deliver\" only when the payment has actually gone through, not while the cart isn't confirmed yet","more":"**Under the hood:** a plain `@EventListener` is synchronous — the listener runs in the same thread and the same transaction as `publishEvent()`. Its DB write goes in the same commit. If the publisher rolls back — the listener's work rolls back too.\n\n**Why so:** `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)` subscribes via `TransactionSynchronization` and starts only after the publishing transaction commits successfully.\n\n**Gotcha:** in `AFTER_COMMIT` there's no current transaction anymore — your DB write without `@Transactional(REQUIRES_NEW)` may not commit. If the event is sent outside a transaction — the listener won't fire at all by default (see `fallbackExecution`).","quizCorrect":"The listener fires only after the publisher's successful commit","quizWrong":["Makes the event asynchronous","Rolls back the event together with the transaction that published it","Speeds up event publishing"],"picCap":"the listener fires only AFTER the commit"},"g-sp-scheduled":{"q":"@Scheduled with fixedRate=5000 runs every 5s, but the task sometimes takes 8s — do tasks start overlapping? And why do two instances do the same thing?","a":"By default @Scheduled has ONE thread for all tasks — they don't overlap, they queue up (the next one waits). fixedRate measures from the start, fixedDelay — from the end. And in a cluster @Scheduled runs on EACH instance independently — you need ShedLock/a distributed lock.","d":"fixedRate — start-to-start (risk of queue buildup with long tasks); fixedDelay — end-to-start (safer). The single scheduler thread serializes everything: one long task delays the rest — define your own TaskScheduler with a pool. For a cluster: ShedLock, Quartz with a JDBC store, or a DB advisory lock.","note":"fixedDelay/fixedRate with a single default thread: a long task blocks ALL other @Scheduled in the application, not just itself. You need a pool: set spring.task.scheduling.pool.size or your own ThreadPoolTaskScheduler, otherwise one stuck job halts the entire scheduler.","hook":"The alarm rings on three phones at once — without \"one answers\" all three run to turn it off","more":"**Under the hood:** by default the `TaskScheduler` is single-threaded (a pool of 1). Tasks don't overlap, they serialize: until the 8-second one finishes, the next tick waits. `fixedRate` counts the interval from the start of the previous run, `fixedDelay` — from its completion.\n\n**Why so:** a single thread is a safe default, but it also slows down other `@Scheduled` tasks in the queue. Increase the pool via `ThreadPoolTaskScheduler`/`spring.task.scheduling.pool.size`.\n\n**Gotcha:** in a cluster the scheduler runs on each instance independently — the task gets duplicated. You need **ShedLock** or a distributed lock (Redis/DB) so that only one node executes it.","quizCorrect":"fixedRate measures from the start, fixedDelay — from the end of the previous run","quizWrong":["fixedRate in ms, fixedDelay in seconds","fixedDelay is multithreaded, fixedRate isn't and always runs on a single thread of its own","No difference, they're synonyms"],"picCap":"in a cluster both instances tick at once — you need ShedLock"},"g-sp-conditional":{"q":"You want your own bean instead of Boot's auto-configured one. You put @Component — but both load / a conflict crashes. How to properly \"beat\" the autoconfig?","a":"Boot's autoconfigs are decorated with @ConditionalOnMissingBean — they back off if YOUR bean already exists. The key is ordering: autoconfigs are processed AFTER user configs (@AutoConfiguration After). Declare your own @Bean of the same type — Boot will see it and won't create the default. @ConditionalOnProperty enables/disables beans by config.","d":"Hierarchy of conditions: @ConditionalOnClass (present on the classpath), @ConditionalOnMissingBean (the user didn't define it), @ConditionalOnProperty (a flag in yml), @Profile (the profile is active). Your own @Conditional — you implement Condition.matches(). Debugging: run with --debug — Boot prints the ConditionEvaluationReport (what matched/didn't match and why).","hook":"You brought your own chair to a meeting — the organizer doesn't set out the office one, seeing you already have something to sit on","more":"**Under the hood:** Boot's autoconfigs are marked `@AutoConfiguration` and processed AFTER user configurations. Inside there's `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` — the condition is checked against an already-populated context, so your bean of the same type suppresses the default.\n\n**Why so:** the ordering guarantees that user `@Bean`s are registered first, and the autoconfig only fills the gaps. Just declare your own `@Bean` of the needed type.\n\n**Gotcha:** `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` matches by type — two beans of different types won't conflict, and both will come up. `@ConditionalOnProperty` enables/disables beans by a config value. A \"both load\" conflict usually means the types didn't match.","quizCorrect":"Autoconfigs are marked @ConditionalOnMissingBean and back off","quizWrong":["@Component always has the highest priority","Boot loads user beans last","You need @Primary on each of your own beans in the configuration"],"picCap":"your bean wins — the autoconfig backs off via OnMissingBean"},"g-sp-value-spel":{"q":"@Value(\"${app.timeout}\") in one bean = 30, in another null. Plus: @Value on a static field stays silent. Why?","a":"@Value is read AFTER the bean is created (at injection time), so in the constructor/@PostConstruct of another, earlier bean the value may not be substituted yet. Injection doesn't work on static fields — Spring injects into instances, not into the class. ${} is a property placeholder, #{} is SpEL (an expression).","d":"${prop:default} gives a default if the property is absent — without it and without the property you get a startup error. #{...} — Spring Expression Language: #{systemProperties['x']}, #{T(Math).PI}, #{otherBean.field}. Gotcha: @Value doesn't bind complex objects/lists nicely — for configs use @ConfigurationProperties (type-safe, validation, relaxed binding).","note":"@Value is read before some property sources are auto-configured, plus in a dependent bean's constructor the value may not be substituted yet. For type safety and ordering, @ConfigurationProperties is better. And remember: ${} with a missing key and no default crashes startup, while #{} (SpEL) executes an arbitrary expression — injection from outside is dangerous.","hook":"${} is like \"substitute a value from the table\", and #{} is \"compute a formula right here\"","more":"**Under the hood:** `@Value` is resolved at injection time, via a `BeanPostProcessor` after the instance is created. If an earlier bean reads a neighbor's property in its constructor/`@PostConstruct` — it may not be substituted yet (initialization order).\n\n**Why so:** injection doesn't go into `static` fields — Spring works with instances, and statics belong to the class. `@Value` stays silent there.\n\n**Gotcha:** `${...}` is a property placeholder (taken from `Environment`), `#{...}` is a SpEL expression. For static, use a setter on an instance field and assign to the static manually. `null` more often means the property is absent or the placeholder wasn't resolved (no `@Value(\"${x:default}\")`).","quizCorrect":"${} is a property placeholder, #{} is a SpEL expression","quizWrong":["${} is SpEL, #{} is a property","Both read only from application.yml and from nowhere else","#{} works only with static fields"],"picCap":"${}=property, #{}=expression"},"g-sp-requestscope":{"q":"You injected a request-scoped bean into a singleton service directly as a field — at startup it crashes or always returns the same thing. How is this fixed?","a":"A singleton is created once at startup, while a request bean lives only during an HTTP request — at startup there's no request. The fix: a scoped proxy. Spring injects a lightweight proxy that, on each access, fetches the \"correct\" instance for the current request. Without proxyMode — fail.","d":"@Scope(\"request\", proxyMode = TARGET_CLASS) or @RequestScope (a meta-annotation already with the proxy). The proxy holds a reference to the current request via RequestContextHolder (ThreadLocal). So outside the HTTP thread (background @Async/@Scheduled) accessing the request bean will crash. An alternative to injection — ObjectProvider.","hook":"At the reception desk (singleton) there's a call button (the proxy) — you press it, and the attendant of THIS exact shift comes (request)","more":"A **singleton** is instantiated once at container startup and its dependencies are resolved then too. A **request bean** exists only while there's an active HTTP request, and at startup there's no request yet — so it's either a crash or a \"frozen\" single reference for the whole lifetime.\n\nThe fix is a **scoped proxy**: `@RequestScope(proxyMode = TARGET_CLASS)` (or `@Scope(proxyMode=...)`). Spring injects not the bean itself, but a lightweight proxy wrapper. On each method call the proxy goes into the current request scope and fetches the \"correct\" instance.\n\n- An alternative without a proxy: `ObjectProvider<T>` / `Provider<T>` and `.get()` at the right moment.\n- Gotcha: accessing a request bean outside the HTTP thread (e.g. in `@Async`) throws `ScopeNotActiveException`.","quizCorrect":"A proxy fetches the current request's instance on every call","quizWrong":["So the request bean becomes a singleton for the whole application","Otherwise the bean will be a prototype","To disable ThreadLocal"],"picCap":"the proxy fetches the instance of this exact current request"},"g-sp-controlleradvice":{"q":"@ExceptionHandler in @ControllerAdvice doesn't catch exceptions from a Spring Security filter (401/403) and from an @Async method. Why?","a":"@ControllerAdvice catches only what arrived inside the DispatcherServlet (controllers). Security filters stand EARLIER than the dispatcher in the filter chain — advice doesn't see their errors. And an @Async method runs in another thread without a request context — its exception also slips by.","d":"401/403 from Security are configured via AuthenticationEntryPoint / AccessDeniedHandler. For filters — a separate wrapper filter. Precision gotchas: @ExceptionHandler picks the most specific type; @ResponseStatus on the exception vs ResponseEntity; @RestControllerAdvice = @ControllerAdvice + @ResponseBody. The modern path is ProblemDetail (RFC 7807) in Spring 6.","note":"401/403 from Security filters must be handled via AuthenticationEntryPoint/AccessDeniedHandler, not @ControllerAdvice. A common trap: throw an AccessDeniedException INSIDE a controller — that one @ControllerAdvice does catch (it's already past the DispatcherServlet), while the same exception from a filter — no.","hook":"The inspector at the turnstile (the filter) catches fare-dodgers BEFORE the hall — the hall administrator (advice) no longer sees them","more":"`@ControllerAdvice` + `@ExceptionHandler` are built into the **DispatcherServlet** via `HandlerExceptionResolver`. They see only exceptions that bubbled up from controller handling.\n\nSpring Security is **servlet filters**, and they stand in the chain BEFORE the dispatcher. An authentication/authorization error occurs before the request reaches the DispatcherServlet, so advice doesn't intercept it. An `@Async` method runs in another pool thread with no binding to the request — its exception bubbles up in `AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler`, not in advice.\n\n- 401/403: configure `AuthenticationEntryPoint` and `AccessDeniedHandler`.\n- `@Async`: return a `CompletableFuture` and catch it, or set your own `AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler`.","quizCorrect":"Security filters stand before the DispatcherServlet in the chain","quizWrong":["You need @Order(0) on the advice","403 can't be intercepted at all","Security itself calls advice last, after every other handler in the chain"],"picCap":"advice catches only what made it past the dispatcher"},"jc-text-block":{"q":"Text blocks — what are they for?","a":"A text block is a string in triple quotes \"\"\"...\"\"\". You write multi-line text right in the code: line breaks and indentation are preserved automatically. No need to stick in \\n and concatenate strings with +. Handy for JSON, SQL, HTML.","d":"Introduced in Java 15. The opening \"\"\" must be on its own line, with the text below it. The compiler strips the common \"incidental\" leading indentation (relative to the closing \"\"\"), so you can align the code nicely. Inside, you don't need to escape ordinary quotes \". If you don't want a trailing newline, put \\ at the end of the line. The result is a plain String — no new type.","hook":"It's like writing a whole letter on a single sheet, rather than taping words together from scraps with \\n.","more":"Under the hood the compiler takes the text between the `\"\"\"` and inserts `\\n` at each line break itself. The opening quotes must be on their own line, and the minimal common leading indentation is **stripped** by the compiler — code alignment doesn't end up in the string.\n\nIt's designed so multi-line text in code looks like the text itself, without `+` and `\\n`. The JSON/SQL/HTML structure is immediately visible.\n\n- Gotcha: trailing spaces are stripped (use an explicit `\\s`), and the indentation level of the closing `\"\"\"` sets the indent.","quizCorrect":"Write multi-line text without \\n and + concatenation (for JSON/SQL/HTML)","quizWrong":["To make the string run faster at runtime","To automatically parse JSON into an object","To make the string mutable, like StringBuilder and could be edited in place"],"picCap":"Left — + and \\n concatenation. Right — a text block: write it as-is."},"jc-sealed":{"q":"sealed classes/interfaces — what do they give you?","a":"For a sealed class/interface you EXPLICITLY write the list of \"who is allowed to extend it\" (permits). Everyone else is out — the compiler won't allow it. The hierarchy is closed and you own it.","d":"Every subtype must pick a modifier: final (no further extension), sealed (continues the controlled list) or non-sealed (reopens the hierarchy). If the permitted types live in the same file, permits can be omitted. The main payoff is the exhaustive switch: the compiler knows ALL the variants, so a switch over a sealed type needs no default, and when you add a new subtype the compiler flags any switch that doesn't cover it as an error. It fits pattern matching perfectly (Java 21).","note":"All permitted subtypes must be in the same module (or package for the unnamed module), and each must be sealed, non-sealed or final — otherwise it's a compile error. It's precisely this closedness of the hierarchy that lets the compiler do exhaustive switch checks without a default.","hook":"A VIP list at the club door: only those on the permits list get in. The compiler-bouncer turns the rest away.","more":"`sealed` + `permits` pins down a **closed list** of subtypes right in the type declaration. Each of them must be `final`, `sealed` or `non-sealed` — i.e. you decide whether the hierarchy branches further or is frozen.\n\nWhy: the compiler knows ALL the variants up front. This enables an exhaustive `switch` over types without a `default` and protects the domain — outside code can't sneak into your model as a subtype.\n\n- Use it for ADTs/models (Shape: Circle, Square), not everywhere: for APIs meant to be extended, closedness just gets in the way.","quizCorrect":"One of: final, sealed or non-sealed","quizWrong":["Must be abstract","Nothing, no modifier needed on the subclasses","Only public"],"picCap":"permits lets only Circle and Square through; Triangle is rejected"},"jc-pattern-match":{"q":"Pattern matching — what does it simplify?","a":"Before: check `instanceof`, then cast the type by hand, then assign it to a variable — three steps. Pattern matching does it all in one line: `if (o instanceof String s)` — already checked, cast, and stored in `s`. In a switch (Java 21) you can branch by type, and the compiler verifies that all cases are handled.","d":"`instanceof Type t` introduces a binding variable `t` in the scope where the check is true (flow scoping) — it's visible in the then-branch, on the right side of `&&`, etc. In a switch over types: case labels like `case Integer i ->`, plus record patterns `case Point(int x, int y) ->` for destructuring. For sealed hierarchies the compiler requires exhaustiveness — if not all subtypes are covered, the code won't compile without a `default`. This catches missing cases at build time, not in prod.","note":"A bound instanceof variable is only visible where the check is guaranteed true (flow scoping): in if(!(o instanceof String s)) return; the variable s is available AFTER the if — that often surprises people. In a switch with patterns, null must be handled with an explicit case null branch, otherwise NPE.","hook":"A turnstile with a built-in scanner: checks the ticket, opens the gate and prints a receipt — all in one move.","more":"Before, `instanceof` + a manual cast + a new variable — three steps and a risk of mistyping the type. Pattern matching fuses them: the type check immediately **binds a variable** that's available further down the branch.\n\nIn a `switch` (Java 21) you branch by type, add `when` guards and deconstruct records via record patterns. With `sealed` the compiler checks **exhaustiveness** — miss a case and it won't compile.\n\n- Gotcha: the variable's scope depends on the condition (`&&` vs `||`); and don't forget a branch for `null`.","quizCorrect":"The type check, the cast and the variable s all at once","quizWrong":["Only the type check, you still need the cast afterwards anyway","Creates a new String object from obj","Compares obj and s for equality"],"picCap":"Three blinking steps collapse into a single line"},"jc-nested-classes":{"q":"Kinds of nested classes — what's the difference?","a":"4 kinds of nested classes: static nested — holds no outer object, like a standalone utility. inner (non-static) — holds a reference to the outer instance, sees its fields, prevents GC of the outer. local — declared inside a method. anonymous — an interface implementation on the fly. A lambda is often shorter than an anonymous class.","d":"static nested: new Outer.Nested() with no outer instance. inner: only outer.new Inner() — a hidden Outer.this reference keeps the outer object in memory (a classic leak in listeners/Runnable). local and anonymous classes capture only effectively final variables. A lambda replaces an anonymous class only for functional interfaces (a single method) and does NOT create its own this — this points to the enclosing class.","note":"A non-static inner class holds a hidden reference to the outer object — this is a frequent cause of memory leaks (e.g. a non-static listener prevents collecting an Activity/service) and serialization problems. Rule: if the inner class doesn't need the outer object — make it static.","hook":"static nested — a neighbor in a shared house; inner — a child holding mom's hand and not letting go (GC waits).","more":"The key axis is whether the class holds a reference to the outer object. `static nested` doesn't — it lives on its own. `inner` keeps a **hidden reference** to the outer instance, sees its fields, but prevents it from being garbage-collected. `local` and `anonymous` are essentially inner classes inside a method.\n\nWhy: to group tightly related code together and encapsulate it.\n\n- Gotcha: a non-static inner class is a frequent cause of memory leaks. If you don't need the outer object — make it `static`. For a single method a lambda is usually cleaner than an anonymous class.","quizCorrect":"inner (non-static)","quizWrong":["static nested","local class","anonymous class always has no reference"],"picCap":"static — on its own; inner clings to Outer; local/anonymous/λ live inside the method"},"jc-enum-adv":{"q":"enum under the hood — what is it?","a":"An enum is an ordinary class, but with a fixed list of pre-built objects. The JVM creates each constant exactly once. They can have fields, a constructor and methods — like any class.","d":"Each constant is the single instance (a singleton by its very nature). That's why an enum singleton is thread-safe: the JVM guarantees it's created once at class load, with built-in protection against reflection and serialization. constant-specific body: after a constant you write {...} and override an abstract method your own way — each constant behaves individually.","note":"An enum is guaranteed to be a singleton even under serialization and reflection — which is why it's the best way to implement a singleton (Effective Java). values() returns a fresh copy of the array on every call (costly in a loop), and ordinal() must not be stored in a DB — reordering/inserting constants shifts all the numbers.","hook":"A cafe menu: exactly N dishes, each with its own recipe; you can't add new ones, and each exists in a single copy.","more":"An `enum` compiles to a `final` class extending `java.lang.Enum`, and each constant is a static instance created **once** at class load. That's why comparing with `==` is safe, and an enum-based singleton is thread-safe out of the box.\n\nIt's a full-fledged class: add fields, a constructor, methods, even different behavior per constant (a body in braces).\n\n- Gotcha: in a `switch`, write a branch for new constants; `ordinal()` is fragile — don't tie logic to the ordinal number, it shifts when constants are reordered.","quizCorrect":"The JVM creates each constant once at class load","quizWrong":["enum constants are stored in a DB and cached","Java copies the enum separately for each thread that touches it","enum uses synchronized internally by default"],"picCap":"enum = a class with a ready, fixed set of objects, each knowing its own apply()"},"jc-method-ref":{"q":"Method references — what kinds are there?","a":"A method reference is a short lambda that just calls one existing method. Instead of x -> x.foo() you write Obj::foo. 4 kinds: Class::staticM, obj::instanceM, Class::instanceM, Class::new. Easy on the eyes — you immediately see which method is being called.","d":"4 forms:\n1) Class::staticM — a static method: Integer::parseInt instead of s -> Integer.parseInt(s).\n2) obj::instanceM — a method of a SPECIFIC object: System.out::println instead of x -> System.out.println(x).\n3) Class::instanceM — a method invoked on the FIRST argument: String::toUpperCase instead of s -> s.toUpperCase(). The first parameter becomes the receiver (this).\n4) Class::new — a constructor: ArrayList::new instead of () -> new ArrayList().\nWorks only when the lambda calls EXACTLY one method and does nothing else.","note":"Subtlety: Class::instanceMethod (unbound) supplies the receiver object as the first argument — String::length is s -> s.length(). A method reference to an object's method (obj::m) captures obj at the moment the reference is created, not at call time — if obj is later reassigned, the reference still calls the old one.","hook":"A lambda is a note saying \"call Pete\", and a method reference is a speed-dial button labeled Pete.","more":"A method reference is syntax for a lambda that merely **delegates** to one existing method. The compiler matches the functional interface's parameters against the method's signature itself.\n\nFour forms: `Class::staticM` (static), `obj::instanceM` (a method of a fixed object), `Class::instanceM` (a method of the object passed as the first argument), `Class::new` (a constructor as a factory).\n\n- Gotcha: `str::length` and `String::length` look similar, but the first calls the method on the specific `str`, the second on whatever is passed in. Confusing these is a common mistake.","quizCorrect":"s -> s.toUpperCase(): the method is called on the FIRST argument","quizWrong":["Calling a static method of the String class on every element of the stream","Creating a new String object","A method of one specific String object"],"picCap":"A single-call lambda collapses into a method reference (4 forms)"},"jc-func-interfaces":{"q":"Built-in functional interfaces — what are the main ones?","a":"So you don't have to write your own interface for a lambda every time, Java already provides ready-made ones in java.util.function: Supplier gives a value, Consumer takes one and returns nothing, Function turns one thing into another, Predicate answers yes/no, BiFunction takes two, UnaryOperator returns the same type.","d":"They all live in the java.util.function package. The main method is invoked differently for each: Supplier.get(), Consumer.accept(t), Function.apply(t), Predicate.test(t), BiFunction.apply(t,u), UnaryOperator.apply(t). UnaryOperator<T> is a special case of Function<T,T>, and BinaryOperator<T> is a special case of BiFunction<T,T,T>. There are primitive versions without autoboxing: IntFunction, ToIntFunction, IntPredicate, IntSupplier, etc. — use them in hot code to avoid wrapping int in Integer.","hook":"Ready-made \"sockets\" for lambdas: give (Supplier), take (Consumer), transform (Function), check yes/no (Predicate).","more":"They're all interfaces with **a single abstract method** (SAM), which is why a lambda fits them. They're grouped by the \"input → output\" shape: `Supplier` (0→1), `Consumer` (1→0), `Function` (1→1), `Predicate` (1→boolean), `BiFunction`/`BiConsumer` (2→…), `UnaryOperator`/`BinaryOperator` — a special case of Function with identical types.\n\nWhy the built-ins: a single vocabulary for the whole streams API and `Optional` — no need to breed your own interfaces.\n\n- Gotcha: for primitives use `IntFunction`, `ToIntFunction`, `IntPredicate` — otherwise you hit autoboxing on every call.","quizCorrect":"Predicate<T>","quizWrong":["Function<T,Boolean>","Consumer<T>","Supplier<Boolean>"],"picCap":"java.util.function: 6 ready-made lambda shapes","note":"Subtlety: the same lambda literal can implement DIFFERENT functional interfaces — the compiler picks the target type from context, and with overloaded methods this causes an ambiguous-reference error (classic: Runnable vs Callable, or Consumer vs Function for a body that both returns a value and may ignore it). Fix with an explicit cast (Runnable)() -> ... or by typing the parameter."},"jc-flatmap":{"q":"flatMap vs map in streams?","a":"map transforms each element by a 1→1 rule. If you return a stream from map, you get a Stream of streams — nested. flatMap does the same, but then \"opens up\" each inner stream and dumps everything into ONE flat stream. List<List> → stream of T: only flatMap.","d":"map(fn): Stream<A>→Stream<B>, exactly one output element each. If fn returns Stream<B>, the result is Stream<Stream<B>>. flatMap(fn): fn must return a Stream (or another stream), and flatMap joins them by concatenation into Stream<B>; the element count may grow, shrink (an empty stream drops out), or stay the same. That's why flatMap is exactly what you use to flatten List<List<T>>, split a string into words, or Optional→value (.stream() on Optional, Java 9+).","hook":"map = put a box inside a box. flatMap = empty all the boxes into one basket.","more":"`map` is a one-to-one transformation: the type changes, the number of elements doesn't. If the function itself returns a `Stream` (or `Optional`/collection), you get `Stream<Stream<T>>` — nesting.\n\n`flatMap` expects the function to return a stream and **stitches** all the inner streams into one flat one. So it's map + flattening one level of nesting.\n\n- Use it for: `List<List<T>>`, a tree, parsing a string into words — anything where one element yields zero-or-many. Gotcha: return a stream from flatMap, not a bare element; and remember that the inner streams are closed.","quizCorrect":"Stream<Stream<Integer>> — a nested stream","quizWrong":["Stream<Integer> — a flat stream 1,2,3,4 with no nesting","List<Integer> with all the elements","A compile error"],"picCap":"map keeps the nesting (red), flatMap unwraps everything into one flat stream","note":"flatMap is NOT element-wise lazy on the traversal side: the inner stream returned by the function is fully consumed before moving to the next element, and before Java 10 this broke short-circuiting — e.g., flatMap(...).findFirst() could needlessly evaluate the entire first substream (JDK-8075939). For primitives use flatMapToInt/Long/Double — a plain flatMap here causes autoboxing."},"jc-primitive-streams":{"q":"Why IntStream / primitive streams?","a":"IntStream/LongStream/DoubleStream store primitives (int/long/double) directly, without the Integer wrapper. So there's no autoboxing — fewer objects on the heap, less garbage, faster. And they give you sum(), average(), range() right away — handy for numbers. Stream<Integer> does a new Integer for every number.","d":"Stream<Integer> stores references to Integer objects: every int is wrapped (autoboxing) → an object on the heap, and summing unwraps it (unboxing). That's extra allocations and GC pressure. IntStream works with primitive int directly — the data sits \"flat\", without wrappers. Bonus: terminal operations sum()/average()/max()/summaryStatistics() and the factories range()/rangeClosed()/iterate() are already built in. Converting back and forth: stream.mapToInt(x -> x) / intStream.boxed().","hook":"Integer is a wrapped candy: you have to unwrap each one. int is an unwrapped candy: eat it right away.","more":"`IntStream`/`LongStream`/`DoubleStream` operate on **primitives** at the bytecode level — without `Integer` wrappers. With `Stream<Integer>` every value is an object on the heap, plus box/unbox on every operation.\n\nWhy: fewer allocations and less garbage, more predictable memory and speed on numeric pipelines. Bonus — numeric methods right in the API: `sum()`, `average()`, `range()`, `summaryStatistics()`.\n\n- Transitions: `mapToInt`/`mapToObj`/`boxed`. Gotcha: `average()` returns an `OptionalDouble` (empty stream), and on large values `IntStream`'s `sum()` overflows easily — use `LongStream`.","quizCorrect":"No autoboxing → less garbage and faster, has sum()/average()","quizWrong":["Supports parallelism, while Stream<Integer> doesn't and runs slower","Can store null, unlike Stream<Integer>","Automatically sorts the elements in ascending order"],"picCap":"On top, Integer wrappers breed garbage; below, bare ints flow straight into sum()","note":"Non-obvious: IntStream.range(0, n).sum() silently overflows when the sum exceeds Integer.MAX_VALUE — the result wraps around with no error. To aggregate safely, mapToLong before sum(); and IntStream.average() returns OptionalDouble, where an empty stream yields OptionalDouble.empty(), not 0.0 — a forgotten orElse throws NoSuchElementException."},"jc-collectors":{"q":"Collectors — what do they collect?","a":"A Stream is a data pipeline. `collect` is the final step that folds the stream into a finished structure. Collectors are recipes for \"where to put it\": into a list, a set, a map, a single string, grouped or counted. One call — and the stream turns into the object you need.","d":"They're all static methods of the java.util.stream.Collectors class, passed into the terminal operation stream.collect(...). The basics: toList()/toSet()/toMap(keyFn, valFn). joining(sep) glues strings together. groupingBy(keyFn) -> Map<K, List<V>>, optionally with a downstream collector: groupingBy(k, counting()) or groupingBy(k, mapping(f, toList())). partitioningBy(predicate) -> Map<Boolean, List<V>> with exactly two keys, true/false. counting() -> Long. mapping(f, downstream) transforms elements before collecting. Terminal = the stream is closed after it.","hook":"collect is the checkout: goods roll down the belt, and the cashier sorts them into bags (a list, a map, a receipt).","more":"`collect` is a terminal operation: it runs the pipeline and **folds** the elements into a result via a Collector recipe (a container supplier, an accumulator, a combiner).\n\nReady-made recipes in `Collectors`: `toList`/`toSet`/`toMap`, `joining` (concatenate into a string), `groupingBy` (a map by key), `partitioningBy`, `counting`, `summingInt`. `groupingBy` also takes a second downstream collector — grouping + aggregation in one go.\n\n- Gotcha: `toMap` throws on a duplicate key — provide a merge function; and mind thread safety with parallel streams.","quizCorrect":"partitioningBy(predicate)","quizWrong":["groupingBy(keyFn) with no downstream at all","toMap(k, v)","counting()"],"picCap":"One stream flows into collect — out come different shapes: a list, a string, a map, a counter","note":"Non-obvious: Collectors.toMap()/toList() return mutable HashMap/ArrayList, but that is NOT contractual — Collectors.toList() guarantees neither the type nor mutability nor serializability, so relying on add() is unsafe; for immutability use toUnmodifiableList()/Stream.toList() (Java 16+), for guaranteed mutability use toCollection(ArrayList::new). Another trap: toMap throws NPE if valueFn returns null (unlike HashMap.put), because internally it uses Map.merge."},"jc-assert":{"q":"assert — what does it do and what's the catch?","a":"assert condition : message — it's a \"this can't happen\" check. If the condition is false — AssertionError. BUT by default assert is DISABLED, enabled with the -ea flag at launch. So you must NOT use it to check user input (it'll just pass through) — only your own internal assumptions in code and tests.","d":"assert is for invariants: \"by here age is definitely > 0\", \"control never reaches this point\". In prod you run without -ea, and all asserts vanish (zero cost). Validate input data with if + throw (IllegalArgumentException / Objects.requireNonNull) — those always work. Rule: assert = catch programmer bugs, exceptions = react to bad data.","note":"Never put a side-effecting expression in an assert (assert list.remove(x)) — with assertions disabled (and in prod they're off by default) the code simply won't run, and behavior will diverge between dev and prod.","hook":"assert is like a pilot's \"for myself\" checklist: on during a training flight, off on a real one. You don't steer passengers' luggage with it.","more":"Under the hood `assert` is just an `if` that throws an **AssertionError**, but the JVM inserts the check only if you launch with the `-ea` flag. Without the flag the assert bytecode isn't executed at all — the line is as if empty.\n\nWhy: asserts were conceived as \"cheap insurance for development\" that can be turned off in prod without losing performance. That's their fundamental difference from validation.\n\n- **Gotcha:** you can't use `assert` to check method arguments or user input — in prod it's off and bad data slips through.\n- Use it for **internal invariants**: \"control should never reach here\", \"the list is definitely not empty here\".","quizCorrect":"By default assert is disabled (-ea off) and the check simply won't run","quizWrong":["assert is too slow for validating input","assert can only compare numbers, not strings from the user","assert throws a checked exception that must be caught in every method that works with it"],"picCap":"assert wakes up only with -ea (green); validate input via if+throw"},"jc-multicatch":{"q":"Multi-catch — what is it?","a":"A single catch block catches several error types at once via `|`. You write the handling once instead of copy-pasting. The variable e here is \"effectively final\" — you can't reassign it. And the types can't be related (parent/child).","d":"If you caught IOException and SQLException separately, the handling code would be duplicated. Multi-catch merges the branches. The ban on related types (e.g. IOException and FileNotFoundException) is because one already covers the other, making the second redundant — the compiler forbids it. The variable e is implicitly final: the compiler won't let you assign it a new value, because its actual type is the common supertype of the caught ones, and reassignment would be unsafe.","hook":"Like a bin labeled \"glass OR plastic\": you toss both into one bucket, empty it once.","more":"Under the hood multi-catch (`catch (IOException | SQLException e)`) is compiler sugar: one block catches several types, and the type of variable `e` is inferred as their **nearest common ancestor**. So inside you only have access to the methods of that common type.\n\nWhy: it removes copy-paste when the reaction to different errors is the same (log, wrap, rethrow).\n\n- The variable `e` is effectively **final** — you can't reassign it.\n- The types must not be **related** (parent and child together): then one already covers the other and the compiler complains about redundancy.","quizCorrect":"Types in an inheritance relationship: catch (IOException | FileNotFoundException e)","quizWrong":["Two independent types: catch (IOException | SQLException e) in one handling block of the method","Logging e once for both types","Rethrowing e via throw e"],"picCap":"Two error types converge into one common catch and are handled together","note":"Non-obvious: not only direct relatives are forbidden, but any pair where one type is a subclass of the other; meanwhile rethrowing from a multi-catch is smarter than it looks — since Java 7's 'more precise rethrow', the compiler analyzes which concrete types could actually have been thrown and lets you declare in throws a narrower set than the common supertype of variable e."},"jc-private-iface":{"q":"private methods in an interface — why (Java 9)?","a":"A private method in an interface (Java 9) is a hidden helper inside the interface. If two or three default methods do the same thing, you extract the common piece into a private method. It's invisible from outside, no duplication.","d":"Before Java 9 the shared code of default methods was either duplicated or moved into a separate utility class. Java 9 allowed private (and private static) methods right in the interface. They're not part of the contract: they can't be called via an implementing class or overridden. So the interface gained a hidden implementation for reuse inside its own default/static methods.","hook":"Like a private kitchen at a cafe: the patrons (default methods) order, a shared cook prepares it — but he's not allowed into the dining room.","more":"Since Java 9 an interface can have `private` and `private static` methods. It's an ordinary method in the interface body, but **invisible from outside** — implementing classes don't inherit or call it, it exists only for internal use.\n\nWhy: before Java 9, shared logic of two `default` methods had to be either duplicated or exposed as a public default (cluttering the contract). Now you hide the common piece in a private method — the contract stays clean, no duplication.\n\n- `private` methods are called from default methods, `private static` — from the interface's static methods.\n- This is about **encapsulating the implementation**, not the API: the client shouldn't know about these methods.","quizCorrect":"To extract shared code of default methods without exposing it","quizWrong":["So an implementing class can override them","To replace the contract's abstract methods","To call them directly via the interface name from outside it as well"],"picCap":"Both default methods funnel into one hidden private — no duplication","note":"Non-obvious: a private static interface method can be called from both static and default methods, but a regular (non-static) private method cannot be called from a static interface method — there is no instance. Another subtlety: private methods do not participate in diamond-inheritance conflict resolution, they are invisible to implementors, so two interfaces with an identically named private method do not clash."},"jc-modules":{"q":"JPMS — Java modules (Java 9)?","a":"JPMS (Java 9) — modules. In module-info.java you write requires (which modules you need) and exports (which packages you expose). Non-exported packages are fully hidden — not even reflection can reach them. The dependency graph is explicit, and jlink builds a minimal runtime out of only the needed modules.","d":"Before Java 9 there was \"classpath hell\": any class could see any public class from any jar. JPMS added a layer above packages — the module. The rules: requires X — you need module X; exports pkg — the package is visible to everyone; exports pkg to mod — only to the named modules; opens pkg — allows reflection (for Spring/Hibernate). Without opens, even setAccessible(true) can't break the encapsulation of a non-exported package. The module graph is built at launch: a missing requires = an error right away, not a ClassNotFoundException at runtime. jlink walks the graph from your module and assembles a JRE image out of only the actually needed modules (you can shrink from ~300MB to ~40MB) — handy for containers.","note":"Strong module encapsulation breaks reflection: for Jackson/Hibernate/Spring to reach private members via setAccessible, the package must be opened via opens (in module-info) or the --add-opens flag at launch — otherwise InaccessibleObjectException. This is the main pain of migrating to JPMS.","hook":"A module = a store: exports — a storefront for everyone, non-exported — a locked warehouse (the reflection-thief can't get in), requires — the list of suppliers.","more":"JPMS adds a level above packages: a module is described in `module-info.java`, where `requires` declares dependencies and `exports` — which packages are visible from outside. Everything you don't export becomes **strictly private** — unreachable even via reflection (`setAccessible` won't help).\n\nWhy: before modules, any `public` class was accessible to everyone, the dependency graph was implicit, and the classpath easily fell into \"hell\" from conflicts. Modules make boundaries and dependencies **explicit and verifiable** at startup.\n\n- `jlink` assembles a minimal runtime out of only the needed modules — a smaller image.\n- Gotcha: legacy libraries without a module fall into the **unnamed module**, and strong encapsulation breaks reflective frameworks in places.","quizCorrect":"No — strong encapsulation blocks it, you need opens","quizWrong":["Yes, reflection always bypasses encapsulation in Java","Yes, if you call setAccessible(true)","Only if the class is public"],"picCap":"requires pulls in another module; jlink trims the runtime down to a mini-JRE"},"jc-nio2":{"q":"NIO.2: Path and Files — why better than the old File?","a":"The old File is dumb: you ask \"did it get deleted?\" — it silently lies true/false. NIO.2 is smarter: Path is just an address (you don't touch it), and Files does the work: read, copy, move, walk the tree. Something broke — it throws an exception with the cause, not a mute false.","d":"Path is immutable — operations return a new Path, safe in multithreading. Files — static methods: readAllLines, copy, move, exists, walk (Stream<Path> over the tree). Support for symbolic links (LinkOption), attributes (BasicFileAttributes in a single system call), and proper exceptions (IOException with details instead of a boolean). Files.walk returns a lazy Stream — close it via try-with-resources.","note":"Non-obvious: Files.exists(path) and !Files.exists(path) are NOT opposites — with insufficient permissions both can return false, which is why there is a separate Files.notExists() returning a third 'cannot determine' state. Another trap: Files.lines()/walk() return a Stream holding an open file descriptor, and a plain forEach without try-with-resources leaks even on tiny files.","hook":"Path is the mailing address on the envelope; Files is the mailman who actually carries, copies, and tells you to your face if there's no door.","more":"The old `File` mixed everything into one class and reported errors with a boolean: `delete()` returned `false` — but why (no permission? no file? in use?) you can't tell. NIO.2 splits the roles: **Path** is an immutable path description (does nothing to disk), and **Files** is static operations (`read`, `copy`, `move`, `walk`).\n\nWhy: explicit exceptions (`NoSuchFileException`, `AccessDeniedException`) give the cause of the failure, not a mute `false`. Plus support for symlinks, attributes, tree traversal.\n\n- `Files.walk` / `walkFileTree` recurse through directories — via a stream or a visitor.\n- Gotcha: `Files.walk` returns a lazy Stream that **must be closed** (try-with-resources), otherwise you leak a file descriptor.","quizCorrect":"Throws an IOException with the cause instead of a mute false","quizWrong":["Automatically retries the operation until success on any error","Returns null and writes to System.err","Changes the Path object itself to a correct path"],"picCap":"The Path-address flows into Files, which does the work and honestly throws IOException"},"jc-datetime":{"q":"java.time: Period vs Duration and why not Date?","a":"Date/Calendar are legacy: they mutate in place and break in multithreading. java.time (Java 8) is immutable and thread-safe. The span between DATES (days/months/years) = Period. A span of TIME (hours/sec/nanos) = Duration. Don't mix them up.","d":"LocalDate — date only, LocalDateTime — date+time without a zone, ZonedDateTime — with a time zone. All objects are immutable: a method like plusDays() returns a NEW object, leaving the old one untouched — hence thread safety. Period measures in calendar units (accounting for varying month lengths), Duration — in physical time (seconds/nanoseconds). So Period is for birthdays/deadlines, Duration for timeouts/measurements.","note":"LocalDateTime does NOT store a time zone — arithmetic on it ignores daylight saving transitions; for \"wall-clock time with a zone\" you need ZonedDateTime. Instant, in turn, is a point on the UTC axis. Period.between counts in calendar units (a month ≠ 30 days), Duration — in fixed seconds.","hook":"Period is the calendar on the wall (days/months), Duration is the stopwatch in your hand (hours/sec).","more":"`Date`/`Calendar` are **mutable**: a method like `setTime` changes the object itself, so in multithreading and when passed by reference they're a source of bugs. `java.time` is made **immutable** — any operation returns a new object, the old one stays intact, giving thread safety \"for free\".\n\nThe key is what you're measuring. **Period** is the difference in calendar units (years/months/days), it \"knows\" about varying month lengths. **Duration** is an exact physical span in seconds/nanoseconds.\n\n- Gotcha: `Period.ofDays(1)` and `Duration.ofHours(24)` aren't equal across a daylight-saving transition — a day can be 23 or 25 hours.\n- For \"a month from now\" use Period, for timeouts and measurements — Duration.","quizCorrect":"Period — it counts days/months/years","quizWrong":["Duration — it counts days/months/years","Date.getTime() — the difference in days","Calendar.diff() — accounts for months"],"picCap":"Moving from mutable Date to immutable java.time. Period measures dates, Duration measures time."},"jc-i18n":{"q":"Localization: Locale and ResourceBundle?","a":"Locale is \"language + country\" (e.g. ru_RU). ResourceBundle is a dictionary: key → text, a separate file per language. Pick the Locale you need — get its texts and the correct format for numbers, dates, currencies. Don't hardcode strings and formats in code.","d":"Locale sets the rules: thousands separator (1 000 vs 1,000), date format (dd.MM.yyyy vs MM/dd/yyyy), currency symbol (₸, $, €). ResourceBundle looks for messages_ru_RU → messages_ru → messages (a fallback chain). Formatters (NumberFormat, DateTimeFormatter, NumberFormat.getCurrencyInstance) accept a Locale and apply the local rules themselves — you hardcode neither texts nor masks. Changing the app's language = changing one Locale, you don't touch the code.","hook":"Locale is the \"country tag\" on a suitcase; ResourceBundle is the phrasebook for that country, with local prices and date formats.","more":"`Locale` is the \"language + region\" identifier (`ru_RU`, `en_US`) by which the JDK picks formatting rules. `ResourceBundle` is a set of files `messages_ru.properties`, `messages_en.properties` with **key → text** pairs; the code fetches a string by key, not a hardcoded constant.\n\nWhy: by externalizing texts and formats, you add a new language without recompiling — just drop in a new `.properties`.\n\n- Format numbers, dates, currencies via `NumberFormat`/`DateTimeFormatter` with the right Locale — separators and ordering differ by country.\n- Gotcha: the default `Locale` comes from the system and may differ on the server/for the user — **pass Locale explicitly**, don't rely on the environment.","quizCorrect":"Stores texts by key, a separate file per language","quizWrong":["Sets the user's language and region (ru_RU)","Formats numbers, dates and currency by the region's rules","Automatically translates strings between languages"],"picCap":"A single Locale sets both texts (its own pack per language) and formatting rules","note":"Non-obvious: the real pain of i18n is not the texts but Locale.getDefault(), which silently affects String.format(\"%.2f\"), toLowerCase()/toUpperCase() and number parsing; the classic 'turkish-i' bug — under Locale tr_TR, \"TITLE\".toLowerCase() yields \"tıtle\" (the dot over i is lost), breaking key comparisons and SQL. For machine, non-UI operations always use toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT)."},"g-java-finalize-clone":{"q":"Why is clone() considered \"broken\" and what does Cloneable do?","a":"Cloneable is a marker with no methods: without it Object.clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException. clone() itself makes a shallow copy: reference fields are shared. Write a deep copy by hand. Often a copy constructor or copy factory is simpler.","d":"clone() bypasses constructors, plays badly with final fields and inheritance. Effective Java advises: instead of clone() — a copy constructor/static factory. For records there's a ready pattern via a new instance.","note":"Object.clone(), bypassing the constructor, doesn't initialize final fields properly and ignores invariants — which is why a copy constructor/factory is almost always preferred. With inheritance, clone() easily returns a subclass object typed as the superclass, or breaks if a subclass doesn't implement Cloneable.","hook":"A photocopier copies the sheet, but both keep one shared folder of photos.","more":"**Under the hood:** `Object.clone()` makes a bitwise **shallow** copy of the fields. `Cloneable` is an empty marker interface: it's checked inside `clone()`, and without it you get a `CloneNotSupportedException`. The oddity is that the copying contract is smeared between `Object` and a method-less interface.\n\n**Why \"broken\":** the copy's reference fields are shared with the original — change a list in the clone and it changes in the source. For a deep copy you must manually clone the nested objects in an overridden `clone()`.\n\n**When:** almost never. A **copy constructor** `new Foo(other)` or a static factory `copyOf` is simpler and safer — explicit control, working with final fields, no casting.","quizCorrect":"A shallow copy: reference fields are shared","quizWrong":["A deep copy of the whole object graph and all its fields","Calls the copy constructor","Always throws an exception"],"picCap":"shallow copy: one list reference shared between two"},"g-java-comparator-nulls":{"q":"comparing/thenComparing — how to sort by multiple fields and not crash on null?","a":"Comparator.comparing(A::field).thenComparing(A::field2) builds a chain: the second key kicks in when the first is equal. For null fields wrap with nullsFirst/nullsLast, otherwise NPE during comparison. reversed() flips the order.","d":"comparing takes a key extractor and calls compareTo on the key. For primitives there are comparingInt/Long/Double — without autoboxing. Important: reversed() flips the WHOLE chain before it, so mind the call order.","note":"thenComparing(A::field) with a primitive getter requires the thenComparingInt/Long/Double overload — otherwise you get extra boxing or ambiguous type inference. The comparator itself, with null keys and no nullsFirst/nullsLast, throws NPE right at sort time, not at creation.","hook":"You sort by surname; tie — by first name; tie again — by age.","more":"**How it works:** `Comparator.comparing(A::field)` extracts a key and compares by it. `thenComparing(A::field2)` kicks in **only when the first key is equal** — giving a lexicographic sort by several fields.\n\n**Null:** a null field itself crashes the comparison with an NPE. Wrap the key: `comparing(A::field, nullsFirst(naturalOrder()))` — null goes to the front (or `nullsLast` — to the end). This is needed at every step of the chain where the key may be null.\n\n**Gotcha:** `reversed()` flips the **whole accumulated** comparator, not just the last key — mind the call order. For primitives there are `comparingInt/Long/Double` without boxing.","quizCorrect":"When the previous key gave equality","quizWrong":["Always, summing the results","Only if the first key is null in the pair","Only in reversed mode"],"picCap":"on a lastName tie the comparison moves to the next key"},"g-java-varargs-trap":{"q":"Varargs: why is passing null or an array sometimes \"not what you meant\" and what's heap pollution got to do with it?","a":"varargs is sugar over an array. method(null) is treated as a null array (NPE/ambiguity), not as a single element. With generic varargs the compiler warns about heap pollution: inside, the type is erased, so a foreign type can be put in. @SafeVarargs suppresses the warning.","d":"foo(T...) creates T[] = Object[] due to erasure; writing a foreign type = ClassCastException later. Put @SafeVarargs only if you really don't write into the array and don't expose it outside.","note":"method(null) with varargs passes null as the WHOLE ARRAY (often an NPE inside), not as a single element — to pass \"one null element\" you need an explicit cast: method((T) null). Generic varargs invite heap pollution; @SafeVarargs is added only when you genuinely don't write into the array.","hook":"\"Pass as many as you want\" — but a single box is still assembled for them.","more":"**Under the hood:** `m(T... xs)` compiles to `m(T[] xs)` — an array is created at the call site. So `m(null)` is ambiguous: null is treated as **the array itself** (= `null`), not as a single null element → often an NPE. To pass one null element, cast: `m((T) null)`.\n\n**Heap pollution:** with generics `T...` creates an array with an erased type (`Object[]`). Through such an array you can smuggle a value of a foreign type, and the ClassCastException surfaces later, not at the point of error — the compiler warns you.\n\n**When:** if the method is genuinely safe (only reads the varargs, doesn't publish the array outside), put `@SafeVarargs` on the static/final method to silence the warning noise.","quizCorrect":"Heap pollution: a foreign type in the array -> ClassCastException","quizWrong":["Always a memory leak","Forced boxing of every argument passed to the method, including primitives","Loss of element order"],"picCap":"arguments get packed into an array under the hood"},"db-leftmost":{"q":"Will an index on (client_id, date) help a query with WHERE date = ?","a":"No. A B-tree is sorted by client_id first, and only within equal client_id values by date. Without a predicate on client_id, rows for a given date are scattered — you need either an index that starts with date, or a client_id predicate added to the query.","d":"The leftmost-prefix rule: an index works left to right across its columns.","note":"Postgres can still use such an index via Index Skip Scan / a separate scan, but only if client_id is low-cardinality, and it's slow and unreliable — don't count on it. Conversely, an index on (client_id, date) perfectly serves WHERE client_id=? AND date=? and WHERE client_id=? ORDER BY date — that's exactly the point of the leftmost prefix.","hook":"A phone book sorted by last name then first name. Searching by first name alone — you have to flip through everything: the leading letter doesn't match.","more":"A B-tree is a tree ordered by a **composite key, left to right**. Everything is sorted by `client_id` first, and only within a single `client_id` by `date`. That's the leftmost-prefix rule.\n\nA query `WHERE date = ?` without `client_id` can't \"jump\" to the right place: rows with that date are scattered across all the index blocks. The database would have to scan the whole index — usually a plain seq scan over the table is cheaper.\n\n- What helps: an index that starts with `date` (`(date, client_id)`), or adding `client_id = ?` to the predicate.\n- Useful nuance: the prefix also works partially — an index `(client_id, date)` perfectly serves a query that only filters on `client_id`.","quizCorrect":"No: a B-tree is sorted by client_id first, so rows are scattered by date","quizWrong":["Yes, a B-tree uses any index column regardless of order","Yes, the planner reorders the index columns to fit the query","No, because composite indexes don't work with equality at all on any column"],"picCap":"date is scattered — the index must start with date"},"db-covering":{"q":"What is a covering index and why use one?","a":"An index that contains ALL the columns a query needs. Then the database answers from the index itself without touching the table (index-only scan) — much faster.","d":"Postgres: INCLUDE columns. But a wider index means more expensive writes.","note":"In Postgres an index-only scan actually kicks in only if the visibility map says the page is 'all-visible' — otherwise the engine still goes to the heap to check the row version's visibility. After bulk updates without VACUUM, a covering index silently stops being index-only. INCLUDE columns add data to the index but don't participate in sorting/lookup.","hook":"A cheat sheet in your pocket: you don't run to the textbook, it's all right there.","more":"A regular index stores the key columns plus a pointer to the row in the table (heap). If the requested columns aren't in the index, after the lookup the database does an extra **heap fetch** — a jump into the table for the remaining fields.\n\nA covering index contains ALL the columns a query needs — both in `WHERE` and in `SELECT`. Then the answer is assembled straight from the index — this is an **index-only scan**, with no table access. No extra random reads.\n\n- In Postgres, fields needed only for output are added via `INCLUDE (...)` — they live in the leaves but don't bloat the sort.\n- The cost: the index gets wider and more expensive to write. Use covering indexes surgically for hot queries, not everywhere.","quizCorrect":"An index with all the columns a query needs — answered from the index without reading the table","quizWrong":["An index that covers every row of the table without exception without a single exception, including deleted ones","An index on all table columns so you never do a seq scan","A clustered index that physically stores rows in key order"],"picCap":"The index contains all columns → answer without reading the table"},"db-notused":{"q":"There's an index, but the planner doesn't use it. Why is that normal?","a":"If the query returns a large fraction of the table, a seq scan is cheaper than random index jumps. The planner estimates cost from statistics and deliberately chooses a full scan.","d":"EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows the choice. Sometimes the culprit is stale statistics (ANALYZE) or a type coercion.","note":"A common real reason for 'the index isn't used' isn't selection size but a type mismatch (a bigint column with a parameter passed as text/numeric) or a function over the column (lower(col), col::date), so the planner can't see the match. Another is stale statistics after a bulk load: ANALYZE helps.","hook":"You walk to the next shelf for bread instead of calling a cab — it's closer that way.","more":"The planner chooses a plan not by \"there's an index, so use the index\" but by **cost estimate**. Index access means random jumps: find the key, then go to the heap for the row. That's expensive per row.\n\nIf the query returns a large fraction of the table (roughly tens of percent), there will be too many such jumps — reading all pages sequentially (seq scan) ends up cheaper. The estimate comes from statistics on value distribution.\n\n- So the index isn't \"broken\" — it's just not worthwhile for low selectivity.\n- Gotcha: stale statistics (run `ANALYZE`). If the planner is genuinely wrong about cardinality, dig there instead of disabling seq scan.","quizCorrect":"For a large fraction of rows, a seq scan is cheaper than random index jumps","quizWrong":["The index isn't used until you run a manual REINDEX on it","The planner ignores indexes on tables smaller than 10000 rows","An index on a column with NULLs is automatically disabled by the optimizer completely"],"picCap":"Large fraction of the table → seq scan is cheaper"},"db-keyset":{"q":"Why is OFFSET 100000 LIMIT 20 slow while keyset pagination isn't?","a":"OFFSET still reads and discards the first 100000 rows. Keyset (WHERE id > last_seen ORDER BY id LIMIT 20) jumps straight to the right place via the index — cost doesn't depend on depth.","d":"For infinite scroll / large lists — keyset only.","note":"Keyset breaks on a non-unique sort key: with ORDER BY created_at there are duplicate timestamps, and rows at page boundaries get lost or duplicated — you need a composite cursor (created_at, id) and WHERE (created_at, id) > (?, ?). Also keyset can't jump to 'page 50' — only forward/back.","hook":"A bookmark in a book: you open straight to the page instead of flipping from the start every time.","more":"`OFFSET 100000` doesn't mean \"start at row 100000.\" The database still **reads and discards** the first 100000 rows in order, and only then returns 20. The deeper you page, the slower it gets — cost grows linearly.\n\nKeyset (seek) pagination remembers the last value and does `WHERE id > :last ORDER BY id LIMIT 20`. Via the index the DB jumps straight to the right point in the tree and reads exactly 20 rows — page depth doesn't affect cost.\n\n- Requirement: you need a stable, unique sort key (often composite — `(created_at, id)`).\n- Downside: you can't jump to \"page N\" by number — only forward/back from the cursor. For an infinite feed that's ideal.","quizCorrect":"OFFSET reads and discards the first 100000 rows; keyset jumps straight via the index","quizWrong":["OFFSET locks the table, while keyset works in a separate transaction","OFFSET doesn't use the index, while keyset always forces an index-only scan on any table","OFFSET re-sorts the whole table, keyset caches the result"],"picCap":"OFFSET reads everything up to the target; keyset jumps straight via the index"},"db-explain":{"q":"What does EXPLAIN ANALYZE show, and why ANALYZE separately?","a":"EXPLAIN — the plan the planner INTENDS to run (an estimate). EXPLAIN ANALYZE actually runs it and shows actual rows/time — you can see where the estimate diverged from reality.","d":"Look at: seq vs index scan, estimated vs actual rows, the most expensive nodes.","note":"Plain EXPLAIN ANALYZE in Postgres doesn't show actual page reads — without BUFFERS you can't see shared hit/read, which is often the real bottleneck; for parameterized queries the prepared-statement plan (generic plan) can differ from what EXPLAIN with literals shows. Also ANALYZE itself adds noticeable timing overhead from per-node gettimeofday — in PG12+ use EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, TIMING OFF) for cleaner total time.","hook":"A navigator's route vs the actual trip: where you hit traffic only shows up once you've driven it.","more":"`EXPLAIN` shows the plan the planner **intends** to run: access methods, joins, and estimated numbers (cost, expected rows). The query itself isn't executed — it's a forecast from statistics.\n\n`EXPLAIN ANALYZE` actually **runs** the query and adds factual data: `actual rows`, real time per node, the number of loops. By comparing expected and actual, you catch the main cause of slowness — the gap between estimate and reality.\n\n- Red flag: estimated 10 rows but 500000 in fact — stale statistics or a sneaky column correlation.\n- Careful: ANALYZE actually runs the query, including INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE — wrap it in a transaction with a rollback, or use `BUFFERS`.","quizCorrect":"EXPLAIN — a plan estimate; ANALYZE actually runs it and shows actual rows/time","quizWrong":["EXPLAIN ANALYZE rebuilds the table's statistics before showing the plan","EXPLAIN shows the plan, ANALYZE — only the total time without a plan","EXPLAIN ANALYZE doesn't run the query, it just estimates cost more precisely from the statistics"],"picCap":"EXPLAIN — estimate, ANALYZE — fact: where the planner was wrong"},"db-isolation":{"q":"Read Committed vs Repeatable Read vs Serializable — what does each allow?","a":"Read Committed — you see only committed data, but data can change between two reads. Repeatable Read — the snapshot is stable within the transaction. Serializable — as if transactions ran one after another, with no anomalies.","d":"Postgres default is Read Committed. The stricter the level, the more conflicts/rollbacks.","note":"The level names are misleading: in Postgres REPEATABLE READ is snapshot isolation and does NOT catch write skew, and in Oracle 'SERIALIZABLE' is actually snapshot isolation too. The default in Postgres/Oracle is READ COMMITTED, in MySQL InnoDB it's REPEATABLE READ. The same code behaves differently across DBMSs.","hook":"A photo of the hall: one live (people flit by), one frozen at the entrance, one — as if everyone went one at a time.","more":"The levels limit which anomalies a transaction can observe, by working with data **snapshots**.\n\n- **Read Committed** — each statement sees a fresh committed snapshot. Within one transaction, two identical `SELECT`s can return different results (non-repeatable read, phantom).\n- **Repeatable Read** — the snapshot is fixed at the transaction start; repeated reads are stable. In Postgres this is snapshot isolation; phantoms no longer appear here, but write skew is possible.\n- **Serializable** — the result is guaranteed equivalent to some serial execution; all anomalies are excluded.\n\nGotcha: a higher level means more rollbacks on serialization conflicts — you need to catch the error and **retry** the transaction. Pick the minimum level sufficient for the invariant.","quizCorrect":"RC sees committed data; RR — a stable snapshot; Serializable — as if one at a time","quizWrong":["RC locks rows, RR locks the table, Serializable locks the database","All three are identical in anomalies, differing only in speed","RR allows dirty reads, RC doesn't, Serializable allows repeatable ones reads instead"],"picCap":"The higher the level, the fewer anomalies a transaction sees"},"db-lostupdate":{"q":"What is a lost update, and how does optimistic locking catch it?","a":"Two transactions read balance=100, both write their result — one change is overwritten. Optimistic locking adds a version: on write it checks that version hasn't changed, otherwise rollback and retry.","d":"@Version in JPA. The alternative is SELECT FOR UPDATE (pessimistic).","note":"Optimistic locking catches a lost update only if ALL writers go through the same version mechanism; a bare UPDATE that bypasses it (a migration, hand-written SQL, another service) sidesteps it silently. And in JPA the @Version check happens on flush/commit — between the read and commit a race window remains, so you need a retry on OptimisticLockException.","hook":"Two people editing the same document without saving versions — whoever saves later overwrites the other's edits.","more":"A read-modify-write scenario: both transactions read `balance=100`, each computes its own value in memory (one +10, the other +20) and writes it. The one that commits later **overwrites** the other's result — one change is lost without a trace.\n\nOptimistic locking adds a `version` column. The write goes as `UPDATE ... SET balance=?, version=version+1 WHERE id=? AND version=:read`. If someone has already incremented version in the meantime, the predicate won't match — 0 rows are updated, and the application does a **rollback and retry** on fresh data.\n\n- That's how it works in JPA/Hibernate (`@Version`) — the conflict surfaces as `OptimisticLockException`.\n- A version-free alternative is the atomic `UPDATE ... SET balance = balance + 10`, computing right in the DB.","quizCorrect":"One change overwrites another; version is checked on write, otherwise rollback and retry","quizWrong":["A lost commit on a crash; fixed by the WAL journal and repeatable read","A deadlock of two transactions; solved by a timeout and pessimistic SELECT FOR UPDATE","A rollback on timeout; optimistic locking holds the row until the end of the transaction itself"],"picCap":"lost on version — we don't lose it, we retry"},"db-writeskew":{"q":"What is write skew, and which isolation level saves you?","a":"Two transactions read the same state, each is individually \"correct,\" but together they violate an invariant (e.g. both of the last two on-call staff take time off at once). Repeatable Read will miss this — only Serializable saves you.","d":"The classic example from DDIA. The tell: a decision is written based on what another transaction read.","note":"In Postgres SERIALIZABLE (SSI) doesn't block but rolls back the losing transaction with error 40001 (serialization failure) — without a retry wrapper, operations are silently lost under load. A cheap alternative to full SERIALIZABLE for write skew is to materialize the conflict via SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on a shared invariant row.","hook":"Two on-call staff each took time off separately — each thought the other was there. The hall is empty.","more":"Write skew — both transactions read the **same** state, and each individually makes a correct decision, but their combined commit breaks an invariant. The classic: a rule \"at least one person on duty\"; both read \"there are two of us\" and both take time off — ending up with zero.\n\nThe key: the transactions write to **different** rows, so there's no write conflict, and version/`@Version` on a specific row doesn't help here — each changed its own.\n\n- Repeatable Read (snapshot isolation) misses this: the snapshots don't overlap on writes.\n- **Serializable** saves you (in Postgres — SSI, which tracks dangerous read-write dependencies and rolls one transaction back). The cheap workaround is to materialize the conflict via `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on a shared row.","quizCorrect":"Two transactions read the same state and are individually correct, but together break an invariant; Serializable saves you","quizWrong":["It's a lost update from two UPDATEs; Read Committed helps","It's a dirty read of uncommitted data; Repeatable Read saves you","It's phantom rows on a repeated read; Snapshot isolation is enough to prevent it, because it already serialises the two transactions"],"picCap":"Write skew: each is \"right\" alone, together they break the invariant"},"db-optpess":{"q":"Optimistic vs pessimistic locking — when to use which?","a":"Optimistic (version, checked at commit) — when conflicts are rare: don't block, worst case a retry. Pessimistic (SELECT FOR UPDATE) — when conflicts are frequent: lock the row immediately, others wait.","d":"Under low contention optimistic is cheaper; under high contention pessimistic is more predictable.","note":"Pessimistic locking doesn't scale and easily deadlocks if transactions take rows in different orders — fix the order (e.g. ascending id). Optimistic under high contention degenerates into a storm of rollbacks and retries, eating more than it saved — then pessimistic or a queue is better.","hook":"A restroom: pull the door and walk in (rarely occupied) vs hang a lock in advance (a permanent line).","more":"These are two strategies against concurrent writes; the choice depends on the **frequency of conflicts**.\n\n- **Optimistic**: don't block anything, hope you won't collide. The conflict is caught at commit time via `version`; if caught — rollback and retry. Cheap for reads, nobody waits on anyone, but with frequent collisions it degrades into endless retries.\n- **Pessimistic**: `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` immediately locks the row. Others wait in line until the first commits. It guarantees progress without retries at the cost of waiting and deadlock risk.\n\nRule: rare conflicts, lots of reads — optimistic; a short hot row with frequent contention (inventory, balance) — pessimistic. Pessimistic gotcha: always lock rows in the same order, otherwise **deadlock**.","quizCorrect":"Optimistic (version) — when conflicts are rare; pessimistic (FOR UPDATE) — when frequent","quizWrong":["Optimistic — for frequent conflicts; pessimistic — for rare ones","Optimistic locks the row immediately, pessimistic checks version at commit","The choice doesn't depend on conflict frequency, both work at the same speed under any load"],"picCap":"Rare conflicts → version. Frequent → lock the row."},"db-mvcc":{"q":"Why doesn't a SELECT in Postgres block an UPDATE of the same row?","a":"MVCC: a change creates a NEW version of the row, the old one stays for those already reading. Readers see their own snapshot, the writer writes a new version — they don't interfere.","d":"\"Readers don't block writers, writers don't block readers.\" The cost — dead versions (see vacuum).","note":"The cost of MVCC is bloat: a SELECT doesn't block an UPDATE, but a long open transaction (or a forgotten replication slot) holds an old snapshot and prevents VACUUM from cleaning dead versions across the WHOLE database — tables and indexes swell for no reason. Watch pg_stat_activity for long-lived transactions.","hook":"A draft is rewritten into a clean copy: readers keep the old sheet, the new one is written alongside.","more":"Postgres stores **row versions** (tuples). An UPDATE doesn't edit the row in place but writes a NEW version and marks the old one invisible from a certain point. Each transaction has its own **snapshot** — a list of which versions are visible to it.\n\nSo a SELECT reads the version current as of its transaction/query start, while an UPDATE calmly creates the next version alongside. Readers don't wait for writers, writers don't wait for readers.\n\n- Locks arise only between two WRITERS of the same row.\n- The cost of this is dead versions, which need cleaning (VACUUM).","quizCorrect":"MVCC: an UPDATE creates a new row version, readers see their snapshot of the old one","quizWrong":["A SELECT takes a shared lock that's compatible with the UPDATE's write lock on the same row","Postgres queues the UPDATE until all SELECTs finish","Reader and writer work on the same row, but the writer has priority"],"picCap":"MVCC: the old version is read, the new one is written — without locking"},"db-vacuum":{"q":"Why doesn't DELETE shrink the table, and what does VACUUM have to do with it?","a":"Because of MVCC, deleted/old row versions physically remain as \"dead,\" just marked. VACUUM cleans them up and returns the space for reuse; without it the table swells (bloat).","d":"Autovacuum usually handles it, but under high load you have to watch it. VACUUM FULL rewrites the table (with a lock).","note":"A plain VACUUM returns space for reuse within the table but does NOT give it back to the OS — the file doesn't shrink. To actually reclaim space you need VACUUM FULL (takes an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock, rewrites the table — a stop-the-world on prod) or pg_repack without locking. The main risk is wraparound: if autovacuum falls behind, the database can go read-only.","hook":"You threw out the stuff but left the boxes on the shelves — they take up space until the cleaner hauls them away.","more":"Because of MVCC, UPDATE/DELETE never touch the data physically right away: the old row version simply stops being visible but stays in the file as a **dead tuple**. This is needed so transactions with an old snapshot can still read it.\n\nVACUUM walks through and marks dead versions as free space — new rows reuse it. The file size usually does NOT shrink; the space just becomes available inside the table.\n\n- Without regular (auto)vacuum — **bloat**: tables and indexes swell, scans slow down.\n- Only VACUUM FULL (with an exclusive lock) or pg_repack can return space to the OS.","quizCorrect":"Because of MVCC, dead row versions stay physically; VACUUM cleans them up and frees space","quizWrong":["DELETE only marks rows, and only a Postgres restart compacts the table","Size doesn't drop because of cache; VACUUM flushes the page cache","DELETE shrinks the table immediately, VACUUM is only needed to update statistics for the planner"],"picCap":"VACUUM frees space for reuse, doesn't shrink the file"},"db-numeric":{"q":"Why can't money be stored in FLOAT/DOUBLE?","a":"A float is a binary fraction, and 0.1 can't be represented exactly in it. Sums accumulate rounding error, and the balance \"is off by a cent.\" You need NUMERIC/DECIMAL — exact decimal arithmetic.","d":"In Java — BigDecimal, not double. The Karwin antipattern.","note":"NUMERIC is exact but slow (software arithmetic), and without a specified scale NUMERIC stores values as entered — rounding is on you. A common prod mistake is float sneaking in not via the DB but via Java: a double in code or JSON; for money you need BigDecimal all the way through, otherwise precision is lost before it's even written to NUMERIC.","hook":"0.1 in float is like splitting a pizza evenly among three: it doesn't work in binary, cents are lost. Money — NUMERIC only.","more":"FLOAT/DOUBLE are **IEEE-754**, binary fractions. Numbers like 0.1 or 0.2 are infinite in binary, so they're stored approximately. The classic: 0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3.\n\nOn a single operation the error is tiny, but in finance thousands of additions/multiplications accumulate it — and the final balance ends up \"off by a cent,\" which is unacceptable in accounting.\n\n- Use **NUMERIC/DECIMAL** — exact decimal arithmetic with a defined precision.\n- In Java — BigDecimal (not double), and set scale/RoundingMode explicitly.\n- Float is fine for metrics/physics, where approximation is OK.","quizCorrect":"A float is a binary fraction, 0.1 is inexact, rounding error accumulates; you need NUMERIC/DECIMAL","quizWrong":["FLOAT takes up too much space, so INTEGER is used for money","DOUBLE doesn't support negative values, which are needed for debts and refunds in accounting and reporting","FLOAT rounds to integers, losing cents; DECIMAL stores more digits"],"picCap":"FLOAT accumulates error — for money use NUMERIC"},"db-idxbloat":{"q":"Why do indexes also \"bloat\" and slow down over time?","a":"Each new row version requires a write to the indexes, and dead entries remain until vacuum. Plus frequent updates/deletes fragment the B-tree. A bloated index = more pages = slower.","d":"REINDEX (CONCURRENTLY) restores it. HOT updates and fillfactor reduce the problem.","note":"REINDEX without CONCURRENTLY blocks writes (and before PG12 — reads too); on prod you need REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY or pg_repack. The root of bloat is often long transactions that prevent vacuum; simply recreating the index doesn't cure it if the cause isn't removed.","hook":"A card catalog: cards get torn out and inserted, old ones aren't removed — the drawer swells, searching takes longer.","more":"An index is a structure (usually a **B-tree**) pointing to row versions. On an UPDATE Postgres often creates a new row version, and it needs a new entry in the index; the old entry lives until vacuum.\n\nPlus inserts/deletes at random spots in the tree cause page **splits** and fragmentation: pages are half-full, the tree \"breathes.\" More pages — more reads for the same lookup.\n\n- Symptom: the index outgrows the data in size, queries over it slow down.\n- Cure: REINDEX (CONCURRENTLY), regular autovacuum, sometimes HOT updates (fillfactor) so an UPDATE doesn't touch the index.","quizCorrect":"Each new row version is written to the index, dead entries remain until vacuum, the B-tree fragments","quizWrong":["The index grows from every SELECT, which caches the pages it traversed","A B-tree automatically duplicates branches for fault tolerance on every query that touches the index at all","Indexes bloat only when you add new columns to the table"],"picCap":"Dead entries pile up until VACUUM → the index bloats"},"db-selectstar":{"q":"What's harmful about SELECT * in production?","a":"It pulls extra columns (traffic, memory), breaks a covering index (you have to go to the table), and is fragile to schema changes (a new column — and mapping/order shift). List the columns you need.","d":"Especially painful with large TEXT/JSONB fields.","note":"The concrete pain of SELECT *: in a JOIN of two tables with same-named columns (id, created_at) the mapper silently picks the wrong one, plus returning bytea/JSONB/TEXT fields the application doesn't need bloats traffic and memory. In code it also breaks quietly when a column is added, with no compile error.","hook":"You ordered 'the whole menu' for one dish: they bring the extras, and if they change the kitchen, the plates get mixed up.","more":"SELECT * forces the DB to materialize and transfer ALL columns, including heavy text/bytea/jsonb the application doesn't need — extra I/O, network, and memory.\n\nMost importantly, it breaks the **covering index**: if the index columns would have sufficed (index-only scan), with `*` the DB still goes to the table (heap fetch) for the remaining fields.\n\n- Schema fragility: add/reorder a column — and positional mapping, ORM caches, and test assertions break.\n- Rule: always list the columns you need explicitly; `*` is fine only for ad-hoc debugging.","quizCorrect":"It pulls extra columns, breaks the covering index, and is fragile to schema changes","quizWrong":["SELECT * always does a full scan and ignores any indexes","SELECT * locks the whole table while reading all columns","It's harmless in itself, the only problem is network load on large rows over the wire"],"picCap":"List columns explicitly, not *"},"db-eav":{"q":"Why is EAV (entity-attribute-value) usually a bad idea?","a":"You store attributes as rows in a key-value table instead of columns. You lose types, constraints, proper indexes, and simple queries — any selection turns into a pile of joins. Flexibility is bought at the price of integrity.","d":"If you need flexibility — a JSONB column is often better than EAV. The Karwin antipattern.","note":"JSONB as an EAV replacement isn't free: until you add a GIN index (or an expression B-tree on a specific path), any filter on a field inside JSONB is a seq scan, and GIN can't speed up ranges/sorting nor give the planner honest statistics. Another trap: the '@>' operator uses the GIN index while '->>' with '=' does not, so a poorly written query silently falls back to a full scan.","hook":"Instead of a structured form — a bag of sticky notes 'key=value': flexible, but impossible to search.","more":"EAV stores data not as columns but as rows of the form (entity_id, attribute, value) in a single key-value table. It seems flexible: a new attribute is just a new row, no ALTER TABLE.\n\nBut value becomes text/varchar, and you lose **types, NOT NULL, CHECK, FK** and proper indexes. To assemble one object with 10 fields you need 10 joins or a tricky pivot — queries and plans balloon.\n\n- Gotcha: no integrity, slowdowns, no analytics, hell in the code.\n- When it's justified: truly dynamic, unknown-in-advance attributes — then **jsonb** is better than classic EAV.","quizCorrect":"Attributes as rows in a key-value table lose types, constraints, indexes; selections turn into a pile of joins","quizWrong":["EAV violates first normal form and is therefore forbidden by the SQL standard, so portability across database engines is lost","EAV is always slower because it stores data in JSON instead of rows","The only problem with EAV is that it doesn't support foreign keys"],"picCap":"EAV's flexibility — at the price of types, indexes, and integrity"},"db-polymorphic":{"q":"What's dangerous about a polymorphic FK (one column referencing different tables by type)?","a":"comment.parent_id + parent_type='post'/'photo' — you can't put a real foreign key on such a column, the DB doesn't guarantee integrity, and joins become conditional. Separate FK tables are better.","d":"The Karwin antipattern. Integrity is the DB's job, not the application's.","note":"A polymorphic FK quietly kills the planner: an index on (parent_type, parent_id) is half-empty and inefficient, and ORM eager-loading of such associations usually turns into N+1. If you really need it — separate nullable FK columns with a CHECK that exactly one is filled are better.","hook":"A key to a door but with no tag: it seems to fit different doors, but the concierge (the DB) isn't watching — you'll lose it and not notice.","more":"A polymorphic FK: one parent_id column plus parent_type ('post'/'photo') tells which table to look at. Handy for a \"comment on anything,\" but you can't put a real **FOREIGN KEY** on parent_id — it can reference different tables.\n\nSo the DB doesn't guarantee that the parent row exists: \"dangling\" references appear, and deleting a parent cascades to nothing.\n\n- Joins become conditional (on type), it's harder on the planner, indexes are less efficient.\n- Alternatives: a separate FK column/link table per type, or a common supertype parent to reference instead.","quizCorrect":"You can't put a real foreign key on it, the DB doesn't guarantee integrity, joins are conditional","quizWrong":["A polymorphic FK slows down writes because it checks all tables at once before writing a single row","It works only in NoSQL and is incompatible with relational DBs","The main problem is data duplication, while DB integrity is preserved"],"picCap":"One column for 2 tables — an FK is impossible, no integrity"},"db-read-phenom":{"q":"Dirty / non-repeatable reads and phantoms — what are they?","a":"Dirty — you saw someone else's UNcommitted data. Non-repeatable — you re-read a row and it was changed in between. Phantom — you re-read a range and new rows appeared in it. Isolation levels close these anomalies one by one.","d":"Read Committed removes dirty; Repeatable Read — non-repeatable; Serializable — phantoms.","note":"The SQL standard defines levels via these three anomalies, but real DBMSs protect differently and against more: snapshot isolation (Postgres RR) removes all three but adds write skew, which isn't in the standard at all. So 'my level closes phantoms' — verify against the specific DBMS, not the standard.","hook":"Dirty — you peeked at a draft. Non-repeatable — the item was swapped. Phantom — new items appeared on the shelf.","more":"All three anomalies are about another transaction's committed (or not yet) work wedging in between two actions in yours.\n\n- **Dirty read** — you see someone's changes before commit; they may be rolled back, and you worked with data that doesn't exist.\n- **Non-repeatable read** — you read the same row twice, between the reads it was **changed/deleted**, and the values diverged.\n- **Phantom** — you repeat a **range** query (`WHERE age > 18`), and the second time new matching rows appeared.\n\nIsolation levels close them in a ladder: Read Committed removes dirty, Repeatable Read — non-repeatable, Serializable — phantoms. The stricter the level, the more locking and less concurrency, so the maximum level is taken only when truly needed.","quizCorrect":"Dirty — reading uncommitted data; non-repeatable — the row was changed; phantom — new rows appeared","quizWrong":["All three are the same anomaly under different names","A dirty read is reading from a committed but stale replica","A phantom is when a transaction sees its own uncommitted changes before any other transaction has committed them"],"picCap":"3 read anomalies: someone's draft flickers, a value was swapped (100→200), phantom C appeared"},"db-jdbc":{"q":"JDBC, Driver, ResultSet — what are they?","a":"JDBC — Java's standard API for SQL databases. Driver — the implementation for a specific DB. Connection — a connection, Statement — a query, ResultSet — a CURSOR over the result rows (you move next() forward, usually one direction).","d":"Spring JDBC and Hibernate sit on top of JDBC and use it under the hood.","note":"A ResultSet by default pulls ALL rows into memory (especially the MySQL driver) — OOM on a large selection; for streaming you need a forward-only ResultSet + setFetchSize, and in Postgres autoCommit=false is mandatory, otherwise fetchSize is ignored. And Connection/Statement/ResultSet must be closed (try-with-resources), otherwise connections leak from the pool.","hook":"JDBC is Java's power socket, Driver is the adapter for your DB, ResultSet is a tape of rows: you flip next() forward only.","more":"**JDBC** is a contract (a set of interfaces) in Java's standard library. It doesn't execute SQL itself — that's done by the **Driver**, a separate library for a specific DBMS (postgresql, mysql) that implements the JDBC interfaces.\n\nThe chain of work: `Connection` (a live connection to the DB) → `Statement`/`PreparedStatement` (a query) → `ResultSet` (the result). **ResultSet is a cursor**, not a ready list: it points to a row, you move `next()` forward, usually only one direction and while the connection is open.\n\n- Gotcha: `Connection`, `Statement`, `ResultSet` must be **closed** (try-with-resources), otherwise a connection leaks from the pool. And always use `PreparedStatement` with parameters — that's protection against SQL injection.","quizCorrect":"JDBC — the API to SQL databases, Driver — the implementation for a specific DB, ResultSet — a cursor over rows","quizWrong":["JDBC is the driver for a specific DB, while Driver is Java's general standard for connecting to any relational database","ResultSet is the database connection object","Driver holds the query result, while ResultSet opens the connection"],"picCap":"JDBC → Driver → DB; ResultSet — a cursor over rows, forward"},"db-hikari":{"q":"Why use a connection pool (HikariCP)?","a":"Opening a TCP connection to the DB and authenticating is expensive (tens of ms). The pool keeps ready connections and reuses them: take → work → return. HikariCP is the fast default pool in Spring Boot.","d":"Pool size ≠ \"the bigger the better\": you'll hit the DB's own connection limit.","note":"The main prod trap of a pool is a connection leak: a connection is taken and not returned (forgot to close, an exception bypassing finally), the pool is exhausted and the app hangs on getConnection. Cure it with leakDetectionThreshold. And bigger isn't better: a pool larger than the DB can handle by cores/connections lowers throughput and hits max_connections.","hook":"A connection is like a cab: hailing a new one each time is slow; the pool is a lot of ready cars: get in, drive, return.","more":"Creating a connection is a TCP handshake, TLS, authentication, and session initialization on the DB server. Tens of milliseconds in total — on a short query that's more expensive than the query itself.\n\nA **pool** keeps a batch of already-open connections ready: the application takes a ready one, runs a query, and **returns (doesn't close)** it back to the pool. This also caps the number of concurrent connections to the DB — its resources are finite.\n\n- **HikariCP** is the default pool in Spring Boot, known for low overhead.\n- Gotcha: the key parameter is **pool size**, not \"the bigger the better.\" Too large a pool overloads the DB; a reasonable start is close to the DB's core count. And you must return the connection, otherwise the pool is exhausted and queries hang waiting.","quizCorrect":"Opening a DB connection is expensive; the pool keeps ready ones and reuses them","quizWrong":["The pool speeds up the SQL queries themselves by caching results","The pool is only needed to limit the number of rows in a response","Without a pool, one application can open only one connection to the DB at a time"],"picCap":"The pool keeps ready connections and reuses them"},"db-pg-storage":{"q":"How does Postgres physically store a table and an index?","a":"A table (heap) — files of 8 KB pages, rows stored without order. A B-tree index — a separate page structure: sorted keys + pointers (ctid) to rows in the heap. An index-only scan — when everything needed is in the index and there's no need to go to the heap.","d":"That's why a covering index is fast: it doesn't jump into the heap for data.","note":"An index-only scan in Postgres still visits the heap if the page isn't marked all-visible in the visibility map — which only VACUUM updates, so on a freshly-written/frequently-updated table a covering index suddenly behaves like a regular index scan. Watch Heap Fetches in EXPLAIN ANALYZE and don't neglect autovacuum.","hook":"The heap is a warehouse with boxes piled up. The B-tree is an alphabetical catalog: you look up by letter, and inside it is the shelf address (ctid).","more":"A table in Postgres is a **heap**: files split into **8 KB pages**. Rows lie inside pages without any order — a new one is just placed in the first free spot. So a lookup without an index is a full scan (seq scan).\n\nA **B-tree index** is a separate tree of the same kind of pages, where keys are **sorted**, and the leaves hold `ctid` — physical \"page + position\" pointers to a row in the heap. The query goes through the tree in O(log n), takes the ctid, and jumps into the heap for the remaining columns.\n\n- **Index-only scan** — if all needed columns are already in the index, there's no need to go to the heap at all (subject to the visibility map).\n- Gotcha: indexes aren't free — they take space and slow down writes, since every INSERT/UPDATE has to update them too.","quizCorrect":"A table — a heap of 8 KB pages without order; an index — sorted keys with ctids to rows","quizWrong":["A table is stored sorted by the primary key, so an index isn't needed","An index stores full copies of rows, so the heap isn't used","Rows in the heap are always ordered by insertion time, the index just duplicates them for lookups"],"picCap":"Looking up M: the index finds the ctid, we jump into the heap by it (sometimes the heap isn't needed)"},"x-db-deadlock":{"q":"What is a database deadlock, and why can two UPDATEs hang forever?","a":"Transaction A has locked row 1 and is waiting on row 2. Transaction B has locked row 2 and is waiting on row 1. Each waits on the other forever. Postgres notices this and kills one transaction with a deadlock detected error. The fix: always acquire locks in the SAME order (e.g. by ascending id).","d":"A deadlock is a wait cycle: A→B and B→A. Postgres periodically (deadlock_timeout, default 1s) runs a detector, finds the cycle, and rolls back one \"victim\". This isn't a DB bug — it's an application logic error: inconsistent lock ordering. Prevention: a deterministic order for acquiring rows/tables and short transactions. Retrying the rolled-back transaction is also fine.","note":"Deadlocks don't come only from explicit locks: they also arise from row order in a single range UPDATE, FK checks (lock on the parent), gap locks in MySQL, and even two INSERTs with the same unique key. Postgres kills the victim itself, but the application MUST catch the error and retry — otherwise the user sees a 500.","hook":"Two people in a narrow hallway: each waits for the other to step aside — and nobody moves.","more":"A deadlock is a **cyclic wait** on locks. Transaction A holds a lock on row 1 and asks for row 2; B holds row 2 and asks for row 1. Neither will release what it holds until it gets the other's — a permanent standoff.\n\nPostgres runs a background detector (on the `deadlock_timeout`) that finds the cycle in the wait-for graph and **kills a victim** with `deadlock detected`, breaking the cycle. The rolled-back transaction must be retried.\n\n- Primary fix: always acquire resources in **one order** (e.g. rows by ascending id) — then a cycle physically can't form\n- Also helpful: short transactions, retry on error 40P01, and `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` in the right order up front instead of random updates","quizCorrect":"Always acquire row locks in the same order (by id)","quizWrong":["Increase the transaction timeout","Raise the isolation level to SERIALIZABLE on both transactions","Run the UPDATE without a transaction"],"picCap":"Each holds what the other needs — a wait cycle"},"x-db-n-plus-one":{"q":"What is the N+1 query problem, and where does it come from in an ORM?","a":"You fetch a list of 100 orders in one query, then loop over each and hit the client with a separate query — that's 1+100 = 101 trips to the DB instead of 2. Each query is a network round-trip, so it's painfully slow. The fix: a JOIN / fetch join / batch loading by a list of ids.","d":"A classic lazy-loading issue in JPA/Hibernate: a collection or @ManyToOne is loaded with a separate SELECT on first access. In a loop this turns into a storm of tiny queries. Fixes: JOIN FETCH, EntityGraph, @BatchSize, or a manual query with IN (...). The symptom in the logs: hundreds of nearly identical SELECTs that differ only by id.","note":"A JPA/Hibernate trap: lazy associations cause N+1 silently — when you access a collection already outside the transaction (LazyInitializationException) or quietly inside a loop. JOIN FETCH fixes it, but with several collections it produces a cartesian product — you need @BatchSize or separate queries. Enable hibernate show_sql/p6spy to see the actual query count.","hook":"You bought 100 items and run to the store separately for each one, instead of one trip with a list.","more":"N+1 is born from the ORM's **lazy loading**. One query fetches the collection (1), then accessing a related entity on each element triggers a separate query (N). For 100 orders that's 101 round-trips instead of one or two.\n\nIt hurts not because of data volume but because of **latency**: each query is a full network round-trip to the DB and back (~0.5–1 ms), and they run sequentially. 100 round-trips add up to a noticeable delay.\n\n- Fix: `JOIN FETCH` / `@EntityGraph` (pull everything in one query), or batch loading `WHERE id IN (...)` (`@BatchSize`, `default_batch_fetch_size`)\n- The trap: the code looks clean (`order.getClient().getName()`), the problem isn't visible in the logic — catch it via the SQL log or query metrics","quizCorrect":"101 queries (1 for the list + 100 for the clients)","quizWrong":["2 queries — the list and a client batch loaded separately","100 queries total","1 query with an automatic JOIN"],"picCap":"A storm of tiny queries ticking one by one → a single JOIN"},"x-db-for-update":{"q":"Why do you need SELECT ... FOR UPDATE, and how does it differ from a plain SELECT?","a":"A plain SELECT locks nothing — two callers can both read a balance of 100 and both debit it. FOR UPDATE places a lock on the read rows: the second waits until the first commits. That's how you make \"read → check → debit\" atomic (pessimistic locking). Only inside a transaction.","d":"FOR UPDATE is an explicit row-level lock on read. While a transaction holds it, other FOR UPDATE/UPDATE on that row wait (plain SELECTs don't — MVCC). Variants: FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED (for queues — skip locked rows), FOR UPDATE NOWAIT (fail immediately instead of waiting). It guards against lost update without a version column, at the cost of serializing hot rows.","note":"FOR UPDATE doesn't lock rows that don't exist yet — against phantoms (a double insert on a business key) you need a unique constraint, not FOR UPDATE. The NOWAIT/SKIP LOCKED variants change behavior: without them, workers queue up behind a single row. And the lock lives until the end of the transaction, not the end of the SELECT.","hook":"You checked a book out of the library — everyone else waits until you return it.","more":"A plain `SELECT` reads a snapshot of the data and **locks nothing** — two threads will calmly both read a balance of 100, both decide \"enough\", and both debit. The classic race on a balance/quantity.\n\n`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` places a **row-level lock** on the read rows until the end of the transaction. A second thread on the same `FOR UPDATE` blocks, waiting for the first to commit, and then reads the up-to-date value. That makes the \"read → check → debit\" sequence atomic (pessimistic locking).\n\n- Works only inside a transaction (a single statement releases the lock immediately)\n- Pitfall: locks + arbitrary row order = deadlock risk; keep the transaction short and take rows in a stable order. There's `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` for queues and `NOWAIT` to avoid waiting","quizCorrect":"Locks the read rows until the end of the transaction","quizWrong":["Caches the query result in memory","Makes a copy of the rows for rollback","Automatically raises isolation to SERIALIZABLE for the session"],"picCap":"The row is held until tx1 commits"},"x-db-sql-injection":{"q":"What is SQL injection, and why does a PreparedStatement kill it?","a":"If you concatenate SQL with user input as a string, an attacker slips in ' OR '1'='1 and reads/destroys other people's data. A PreparedStatement sends the query text and the parameters SEPARATELY — the driver never confuses data with code. Never concatenate input into SQL.","d":"The root of the hole is mixing code and data in one string. A parameterized query (PreparedStatement / :named parameters) sends ? as placeholders, and the values separately over the protocol; they're never parsed as SQL. Bonus: the query plan is cached. You can't \"escape by hand\" — that's unreliable; parameterization is the only correct way.","note":"A PreparedStatement parameterizes only values, NOT table/column names, ORDER BY, or LIMIT direction — if you inject those dynamically from input, injection remains; validate such fragments with a whitelist. Concatenation \"for one harmless filter\" is a typical source of the hole.","hook":"A form with blank fields: whatever you write in is a value, not a new command stamped over the form.","more":"Under the hood the DB receives the query in two stages: first it **parses the template** with placeholders (`WHERE id = ?`) and builds a plan, then it substitutes the parameters as pure values. A parameter physically cannot become part of the SQL grammar — a quote inside it stays an ordinary character, not a \"string terminator\".\n\nThat's exactly why concatenation is dangerous: there, input becomes the query text BEFORE parsing, and the DB faithfully executes `OR '1'='1'` as code.\n\n- **Pitfall:** a PreparedStatement does not parameterize table/column names or `ORDER BY` — validate those via a whitelist.\n- An ORM (Hibernate/Panache) does this for you, but `createQuery(\"... \" + input)` breaks it all.","quizCorrect":"Sends the query text and the parameters separately","quizWrong":["Escapes quotes in the query string","Encrypts the user input","Validates input with a regular expression before the query"],"picCap":"Code and data kept apart — injection is impossible"},"p-outbox-skip-locked":{"q":"Two relay instances simultaneously run SELECT ... WHERE sent_at IS NULL. Why do you get a double send, and how do you remove it with one SQL change?","a":"Both read the same rows and both send. You need FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED: the first relay locks a batch of rows, the second skips them and takes the next. That way several relays pick the queue apart in parallel without overlap and without blocking each other.","d":"Plain FOR UPDATE would make the second relay wait for the rows to be released — losing parallelism. SKIP LOCKED skips already-locked rows and immediately takes free ones. This is the standard way to build a \"queue on top of a table\" in Postgres. LIMIT is mandatory so one relay doesn't grab everything (recall ~103k rows on rollout).","note":"","hook":"Two checkout lanes and one stack of orders: SKIP LOCKED — each grabs the top free ones, no fighting over the other's.","more":"A plain `SELECT ... WHERE sent_at IS NULL` under load is read by both relays at once — transactions don't see each other's uncommitted changes, so **both take the same rows** and both send them.\n\nThe fix in one expression: `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`. The first relay locks the selected batch; the second, on meeting locked rows, **doesn't wait** but skips them and takes the next free ones. You get concurrent draining of the queue without overlap and without mutual stalls.\n\n- Without `SKIP LOCKED` (just `FOR UPDATE`) the second relay **blocks** and waits for the first — no parallelism.\n- Pitfall: take a bounded batch (`LIMIT`) and keep the transaction short, otherwise rows are locked for a long time and throughput drops.","quizCorrect":"FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED — the second relay skips the locked rows","quizWrong":["SELECT FOR UPDATE — the second relay waits for the lock to release","SELECT DISTINCT — removes duplicate rows from the result","A SERIALIZABLE transaction fully serializes the two relays one after another"],"picCap":"SKIP LOCKED: A takes r1,r2 (blue), B skips them and grabs r3,r4"},"p-tx-repeatable-read-name-trap":{"q":"In Postgres, is REPEATABLE READ \"almost SERIALIZABLE\"? What does it NOT protect against?","a":"No. REPEATABLE READ in Postgres = snapshot isolation: it gives a stable snapshot and catches lost update on a single row. But it does NOT protect against write skew — two transactions read the same data and write to DIFFERENT rows, there's no row conflict, and the invariant is broken. Only SERIALIZABLE handles that.","d":"Snapshot isolation removes phantoms in read-only queries, but not in read-write where a write adds rows matching a read's condition. Many people think \"repeatable read ≈ serializable\" and hit a bug. The naming trap: the SQL standard calls it REPEATABLE READ, but it's actually Snapshot Isolation. For write skew you need SERIALIZABLE (SSI), or SELECT FOR UPDATE on the read rows, or a UNIQUE constraint.","note":"SSI gotcha: SERIALIZABLE in Postgres doesn't lock — it optimistically tracks rw-dependencies and throws serialization_failure (SQLSTATE 40001) at COMMIT, so the application MUST have a retry loop, otherwise you've merely turned write skew into unhandled exceptions in prod. Also SSI requires ALL participating transactions to be SERIALIZABLE: a single READ COMMITTED transaction in the mix breaks the guarantee.","hook":"Two doctors, each seeing \"someone is on shift\", both go home. Each is right individually — the hospital is empty.","more":"The name is misleading: in Postgres, REPEATABLE READ is implemented as **snapshot isolation** (MVCC). A transaction sees a consistent snapshot of the data as of its start and catches lost update if BOTH transactions touch the same row (the second gets a serialization error).\n\n- The hole is **write skew**: two transactions read a shared set of rows, check an invariant, and write to DIFFERENT rows. There's no row conflict, both commit, and the invariant (e.g. \"at least one doctor on shift\") is broken.\n\nA snapshot can't see this in principle, because there's no write overlap. Only **SERIALIZABLE** saves you (in Postgres, SSI), which tracks read/write dependencies between transactions.","quizCorrect":"Against write skew: two transactions read the same data, write to different rows, no row conflict — only SERIALIZABLE saves you","quizWrong":["Against lost update on a single row — for that you need SERIALIZABLE","Against a dirty read — REPEATABLE READ allows it","Against nothing: in Postgres it's an alias for SERIALIZABLE and behaves identically under every workload you can construct in practice"],"picCap":"Different rows — no conflict, but the rule is violated."},"p-tx-ssi-retry-40001":{"q":"You set SERIALIZABLE and forgot about errors. What breaks under load?","a":"SSI is optimistic: on a conflict it ROLLS BACK one transaction with error 40001 (serialization failure). If you don't catch it and retry, operations are silently lost under contention. You need a retry loop around every SERIALIZABLE transaction.","d":"Since 9.1, Postgres implements SERIALIZABLE as Serializable Snapshot Isolation: it tracks dangerous read-write dependency cycles and at commit aborts one of the transactions. The overhead is small (lighter than 2PL). But code that treats any DB error as fatal will drop valid operations under load. Catch SQLSTATE 40001 and retry (with backoff and an attempt limit).","note":"The retry must cover the ENTIRE transaction from the start (a new snapshot), not just a re-run of one UPDATE — otherwise you retry on top of the same conflicting snapshot. And the error can even arrive on COMMIT, not on a specific query, so the retry wrapper must encompass the commit.","hook":"A turnstile for two: one gets turned back. Don't panic — just go through again instead of leaving.","more":"Postgres does SERIALIZABLE via **SSI** (Serializable Snapshot Isolation) — optimistically. It doesn't lock up front but tracks dangerous read/write intersections and at commit ROLLS BACK the offending transaction with `SQLSTATE 40001` (serialization_failure).\n\n- This isn't a bug, it's the normal mechanism. Under high contention there are many such rollbacks.\n- If the application doesn't catch 40001 and retry the transaction, the operation simply vanishes silently, as if it never happened.\n\nThe mandatory pattern: wrap every SERIALIZABLE transaction in a **retry loop** (e.g. up to 3 attempts with a small backoff). Important: you must retry the WHOLE transaction from scratch, not a single query.","quizCorrect":"SSI rolls back the transaction with error 40001 (serialization failure); without retry, operations are silently lost under contention","quizWrong":["SERIALIZABLE takes locks and under load will deadlock forever with no errors reported back to the application at all, so nothing is ever retried","Transactions wait in a queue until they hit lock_timeout and time out","Nothing: SERIALIZABLE automatically retries conflicting transactions itself"],"picCap":"A 40001 rollback is normal: the transaction fails, retries, and commits"},"p-db-find-save-race":{"q":"Why is the ORM pattern find() + setX() + save() a race, and how do you write it correctly?","a":"find→change→save does a hidden read-modify-write. Two concurrent transactions read the old value, compute, and the later save overwrites the earlier one — a lost update. The correct way: an atomic UPDATE with the condition checked right in the WHERE.","d":"READ COMMITTED doesn't protect against lost update. Instead of read-in-code-then-write: UPDATE ... SET x=x-1 (atomic in the DB) or UPDATE ... WHERE status=$expected (precondition check in the WHERE, check rows affected==1). Alternatives: SELECT FOR UPDATE, optimistic locking via a version column, or SERIALIZABLE. Postgres REPEATABLE READ catches lost update itself and rolls back; MySQL REPEATABLE READ doesn't (important when switching stacks).","note":"JPA/Hibernate trap: @Version protects only when you load and save the entity through the persistence context — a bulk JPQL UPDATE (`update Account a set a.balance = ...`) does NOT auto-increment the version column and bypasses optimistic locking, silently reopening the same race. And a bare atomic `SET balance = balance - 10` prevents lost update, but under READ COMMITTED a separate SELECT can still read a stale value for a business check — update atomicity is not the same as correctness of a precondition read by a separate SELECT.","hook":"Two people editing one Google Sheet by pasting from an old version: whoever hits Ctrl+S later wins.","more":"`find() → setX() → save()` is the classic **read-modify-write**: the value is read into application memory, changed in code, and written back as a whole row. There's a window between read and write.\n\n- Two transactions read `balance=100`, both compute `100−10=90`, both save 90. One debit is lost — **lost update** — even though both commits are \"successful\".\n\nThe correct way is to compute in the DB itself, atomically: `UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 10 WHERE id=? AND balance >= 10`. Then the row lock and the arithmetic happen in one step inside the engine. An alternative is **optimistic locking** via a version column (`WHERE version = ?`).","quizCorrect":"A hidden read-modify-write: two transactions read the old value, the later save overwrites the earlier — a lost update","quizWrong":["The ORM doesn't wrap find and save in one transaction, so the connection is lost","save() always does a full UPDATE of all columns, overwriting other fields forever","find() caches the row in the session, so setX() changes a stale copy from the cache rather than the current row in the database"],"picCap":"Both read 100, the second write overwrites the first — one debit disappears"},"p-db-composite-order":{"q":"Query WHERE created_at >= ? AND status = ?. Why is the index (created_at, status) worse than (status, created_at)?","a":"In a composite index, after the first column with a range (>, <, BETWEEN), the rest stop narrowing the tree search — they become a filter. (created_at, status): first a range on the date -> status then only sieves rows. (status, created_at): first an exact equality, then a range over ordered leaves -> a narrow scan.","d":"Rule: equalities first, ONE range last among the indexed predicates. Access predicate (walks the tree and bounds the leaf scan) vs filter predicate (sieves already-read rows and discards them). In EXPLAIN the marker of trouble is \"Rows Removed by Filter\". Bonus for (status, created_at): WHERE status=$1 ORDER BY created_at LIMIT 20 takes the leaves already in order -> no separate Sort, pipelined, fast LIMIT. Column order is chosen for the most important queries (including their ORDER BY), not by \"selectivity\".","note":"The rule 'equality first, range last' also applies to ORDER BY: the index (status, created_at) additionally returns rows already sorted by created_at within status — eliminating a separate Sort step. But two ranges on different columns can't be efficiently covered by one B-tree in any order.","hook":"First the exact house address (equality), then floors 5 through 9 (a range) — not the other way around.","more":"In a B-tree index, columns are ordered left to right, and after the first column with a **range** (>, <, BETWEEN), the rest no longer narrow the tree search — they become just a filter over the found rows.\n\n- `(created_at, status)`: the tree is immediately sliced by the date range, and status afterward only sieves each row — you read excess.\n- `(status, created_at)`: first an exact **equality** on status (a narrow branch), and within it created_at is ordered → the range becomes one compact leaf scan.\n\nRule: **equality columns first, range last**. Pitfall: if status has low selectivity (2-3 values) or you need to sort by date, the trade-off shifts — check EXPLAIN.","quizCorrect":"After the range on created_at, status no longer narrows the tree; (status, created_at) — first equality, then the range","quizWrong":["Index columns are sorted alphabetically by name, so created_at must always come second","A range on created_at always forces a full scan, and an equality on status fully eliminates it for the planner to rely on","Column order in a composite index doesn't affect the plan; the optimizer picks the best itself"],"picCap":"A range \"closes off\" the columns to its right"},"p-db-unindexed-fk":{"q":"You have an FK but no index on it. Why does a DELETE of the parent row suddenly grind to a halt?","a":"Postgres doesn't index an FK itself. On a DELETE/UPDATE of the parent, it must check there are no referencing children — and without an index on the child FK column it does a FULL scan of the child table for each deleted row. A large child table -> the classic prod stall.","d":"The PK is enforced by the parent's index automatically, but the child side of the FK you must index yourself. Why index an FK: (1) parent->child joins go through the FK (Nested Loop -> one lookup per row); (2) the cascade/check on ON DELETE/ON UPDATE scans the children. Rule: every FK on a large table needs an index. While you're at it, clean up unused indexes (pg_stat_user_indexes, idx_scan=0): each extra one is a write tax on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.","note":"This is the most common 'invisible' prod stall: an index on an FK isn't created automatically in either Postgres or Oracle (in MySQL InnoDB it is). Besides a slow parent DELETE, a missing index on the child FK provokes lock escalation and deadlocks during cascades.","hook":"Evicting a tenant — but without a room-by-room inventory you walk the whole apartment, and so for each one.","more":"Under the hood: an FK is an **integrity constraint**, not an index. Postgres automatically indexes only PK and UNIQUE. When you delete/update a parent, the engine must make sure no one references it — it runs the check `WHERE child_fk = :parent_id` over the child table.\n\nWithout an index on the FK column, this check is a **Seq Scan** over all children, and so for EACH affected parent row. On a large child table, deleting one row takes seconds, and a batch grinds to a halt.\n\n- Pitfall: the problem is invisible in tests (little data) and blows up in prod.\n- Fix: always put an index on the FK column of the child table, especially if the parent is cleaned up/cascades.","quizCorrect":"Postgres doesn't index the FK itself; on DELETE it checks children with a full scan of the child table per row","quizWrong":["An FK without an index locks the whole child table with an exclusive lock until the DELETE finishes completely for its duration","Postgres builds the FK index automatically; the slowdown is from rebuilding it on DELETE","A parent DELETE cascade recreates all child rows, hence the long operation"],"picCap":"No index on the FK — each delete scans through all children (cells ticking one after another)"},"p-pg-hot-fillfactor":{"q":"What is a HOT update in Postgres, and why is indexing a frequently changing column (status) harmful?","a":"An UPDATE = a new tuple + the old one marked dead. HOT (Heap-Only Tuple): if the new tuple fits on the SAME page AND no indexed column changed — Postgres does NOT touch the indexes — cheap, less bloat. An index on status (changes every time) breaks HOT → every update writes to all indexes. Fix: fillfactor=80 (slack on the page) + don't index columns that always change.","d":"A HOT chain lives inside a page: the index points to the old ctid, and within the page Postgres walks the chain to the current version. HOT conditions: (1) the new tuple fits on the same heap page (you need fillfactor < 100 for slack), (2) NO column that belongs to any index changed. So for update-heavy tables: leave fillfactor headroom and don't put an index on counters/status if queries don't need it. This reduces write amplification and index bloat.","note":"Gotcha: HOT is broken by ANY index whose index expression depends on the changed column, even a partial one — e.g. a partial index WHERE status='active' formally doesn't contain status in its key, yet Postgres still considers it affected and skips the HOT update. And fillfactor only applies to new/rewritten pages: on an already-packed table you need VACUUM FULL or pg_repack, otherwise the headroom won't appear right away.","hook":"A notebook with spare lines: you add to the same page — no need to touch the table of contents. Set up an alphabetical index by status and you'll be fixing it on every little change.","more":"Under the hood: Postgres doesn't change a row in place — any UPDATE creates a **new tuple version** and marks the old one dead (MVCC). Usually this requires a write to all indexes. **HOT** (Heap-Only Tuple) is an optimization: if the new version fit on the same page and no indexed column changed, Postgres chains the versions within the page itself and does NOT touch the indexes.\n\nWhy: fewer index writes, less bloat, autovacuum cleans up more easily (HOT cleanup right on the page).\n\n- Pitfall: an index on `status`, which changes on every update, is guaranteed to break HOT — every UPDATE writes to all indexes.\n- Technique: `fillfactor=80` leaves room on the page so the new version fits nearby, and don't index forever-changing columns.","quizCorrect":"Changing a column that has an index on it","quizWrong":["Any UPDATE, even with no indexes on the table","Using a fillfactor below 100","An UPDATE inside an explicit BEGIN/COMMIT transaction"],"picCap":"Slack on the page = a cheap update without going to the indexes"},"p-pg-cic-invalid":{"q":"Why is a plain CREATE INDEX in prod a stop-the-world, and what's the danger of CONCURRENTLY if it fails?","a":"A plain CREATE INDEX takes a write lock (SHARE) — updates wait while the index builds. On a hot table that's downtime. CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY builds without a write lock (2 passes over the table, slower, more I/O), but: it does NOT work inside a transaction, and if it fails it leaves an INVALID (indisvalid=false) index. You must DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY and recreate it, otherwise it isn't used but slows down updates.","d":"Liquibase: mark the changeSet runInTransaction:false (otherwise the error 'cannot run inside a transaction block'). Flyway: a separate non-transactional migration. After a CIC failure: SELECT * FROM pg_index WHERE NOT indisvalid; — find the invalid ones, DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY, retry. To rebuild a bloated index online — REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY.","note":"After a failed CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY, the invalid index isn't used for reads, but Postgres still maintains it on every INSERT/UPDATE — so you pay for the index without getting any benefit, until you DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY. Check: indisvalid in pg_index.","hook":"Repaving a road at night without closing traffic: slower and in two passes, but if you leave it half-done, a pothole remains that you have to tear out and redo.","more":"Under the hood: a plain `CREATE INDEX` takes a `SHARE` lock — it forbids writes but not reads. While the index builds, all INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on the table **hang waiting**. On a hot table that's effectively downtime.\n\n`CONCURRENTLY` does two passes and waits for old transactions, so as not to block writes. The cost: slower, more I/O, can't run inside a transaction.\n\n- Main pitfall: if CIC fails (a conflict, a duplicate in a UNIQUE), it leaves an index with `indisvalid=false`. The planner does NOT use such an index for queries, but it's still updated on every write — so it slows things down with zero benefit.\n- Fix: find the invalid ones (`pg_index.indisvalid`), `DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY`, and recreate.","quizCorrect":"Leaves an invalid index that slows things down but isn't used","quizWrong":["Rolls back all table changes from the last hour","Blocks writes to the table until a manual DB restart","Deletes the row data that was being indexed at the moment of failure"],"picCap":"CIC doesn't block writes, but after a failure you have to clean up the garbage"},"p-pg-skip-locked":{"q":"Ten workers pick a task queue out of a table. Why does a plain FOR UPDATE line them up in a traffic jam, but SKIP LOCKED doesn't?","a":"A plain SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on the same rows: worker 2 WAITS until worker 1 releases the row — everyone queues behind the first batch. SKIP LOCKED: skips already-locked rows and takes the next free ones → each worker grabs ITS OWN batch, nobody blocks. This is the canonical pattern for a queue/outbox-relay on a single DB without a separate broker.","d":"NOWAIT — throw an error instead of waiting. SKIP LOCKED — quietly skip. The combination UPDATE ... RETURNING (or SELECT FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED + UPDATE) atomically 'claims' a batch. Downside: processing order is not strictly FIFO (skips), and you can't guarantee a row is processed exactly once without idempotency on the handler side. Ideal for an outbox-relay: WHERE sent_at IS NULL ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED LIMIT 100.","note":"SKIP LOCKED doesn't guarantee order (FIFO breaks), and with short transactions workers can compete for the tail; you need ORDER BY + LIMIT inside a subquery. The main thing — commit/delete the claimed row, otherwise if a worker crashes the task 'stays locked' only until the end of its transaction, then becomes visible again (risk of reprocessing — make it idempotent).","hook":"A supermarket checkout: FOR UPDATE — everyone in one line behind the first customer; SKIP LOCKED — you skip the busy lane and join a free one.","more":"Under the hood: `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` places a **row lock** on the selected rows. If ten workers run the same query, they all aim at the same top rows: worker 1 locks them, workers 2–10 block and wait for the first to commit. You get a strict queue — no parallelism.\n\n`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` changes the rule: already-locked rows are simply **skipped**, and the worker takes the next free ones. Each grabs its own non-overlapping batch, nobody waits.\n\n- Use for: a task queue, outbox-relay, a job runner on a single DB — without a separate broker like Kafka/Rabbit.\n- Pitfall: add a `LIMIT` (take a batch), and remember — skipped rows aren't lost, the next free worker will take them.","quizCorrect":"Skip locked rows and take the next free ones without waiting","quizWrong":["Release other rows' locks, speeding up neighboring transactions","Disable row locks entirely for speed","Guarantee strict task delivery order by primary key"],"picCap":"FOR UPDATE: workers blink while waiting. SKIP LOCKED: each takes its own row in parallel"},"p-pg-pgbouncer-txn":{"q":"Why do prepared statements, SET, and temp tables suddenly break in PgBouncer transaction mode?","a":"Each Postgres backend is a process (5–10+ MB). A thousand clients = a thousand processes = thrashing on memory and context switches. PgBouncer in transaction mode hands out a server connection only for the duration of the TRANSACTION — hundreds of clients share a small pool. But between transactions the connection goes to another client → anything that lives in the SESSION (server-side prepared statements, SET, temp tables, session-level advisory locks, LISTEN/NOTIFY) doesn't survive the transaction boundary and breaks.","d":"Fix: SET LOCAL (lives within the transaction), protocol-level prepared statements (the driver negotiates with pgbouncer), or session mode for such features (but session mode pins a connection to a client — defeating the pool's purpose). Sizing: count not clients but active queries: Σ(pool_size × replicas) + reserve < max_connections. In Quarkus/Agroal keep jdbc.max-size small per pod (5–10), not 100 — otherwise with pod autoscaling you'll collectively hit max_connections.","note":"With PgBouncer transaction mode, the Postgres JDBC driver must be switched to server-side prepared statements off (prepareThreshold=0) or to a pooler-aware mode — otherwise 'prepared statement S_1 already exists' errors under load. And session-level advisory locks/LISTEN-NOTIFY can't be used through such a pool.","hook":"Car sharing: you take the car for a trip, not for keeps. Left your stuff in the glovebox (SET, prepared)? The next driver doesn't have it.","more":"Under the hood: each Postgres connection is a separate **OS process** (5–10+ MB). Thousands of clients = thousands of processes, and memory and context switches kill the server. PgBouncer puts a lightweight pool in front: in `transaction mode` a server connection is pinned to a client only for the duration of one transaction, then returned to the pool and handed to another.\n\nThis is exactly where all **session state** breaks: between transactions you may get a different backend.\n\n- Won't survive the boundary: server-side prepared statements, `SET`/`SET LOCAL` outside a transaction, temp tables, session-level advisory locks, `LISTEN/NOTIFY`.\n- Fix: disable the driver's server-side prepared cache (or `max_prepared_statements` in pgbouncer), use `SET LOCAL`, and session features — only in `session mode`.","quizCorrect":"Things that live in the session: prepared statements, SET, temp tables","quizWrong":["Any SELECT queries that use subqueries","Transactions with the SERIALIZABLE isolation level and nothing else in that mode","Queries returning more than one result row"],"picCap":"A connection is rented per transaction (the token shuttles back and forth), not per client → session state doesn't survive"},"p-sql-null-not-in":{"q":"Why does a query with NOT IN (a subquery where a NULL crept in) suddenly return NOTHING, even though there's data?","a":"NULL is 'unknown', not a value. x NOT IN (1,2,NULL) = x<>1 AND x<>2 AND x<>NULL, and x<>NULL yields not false but UNKNOWN → the whole condition is never true → 0 rows. So one NULL in the subquery nullifies the entire result. This is the classic 'Fear of the Unknown' trap. Fix: NOT EXISTS instead of NOT IN, or filter out NULLs in the subquery (WHERE col IS NOT NULL).","d":"Related pitfalls: col = NULL is never true (you need IS NULL); col <> 'x' silently drops rows with NULL; COUNT(col) doesn't count NULL. NOT EXISTS works correctly with NULL and is usually also more optimal. Don't replace NULL with a magic placeholder (-1, 'N/A') — it breaks aggregates and types. NULL is a valid, intentional state of 'unknown/not applicable'.","note":"The same trap is broader than NOT IN: any comparison with NULL yields UNKNOWN, so WHERE col <> 'x' silently discards rows with col IS NULL, and a UNIQUE constraint treats multiple NULLs as distinct (duplicates pass). For NOT IN the canonical fix is switching to NOT EXISTS, which behaves correctly with NULL.","hook":"A list of 'who NOT to let in' where one line is smudged to '?'. The guard isn't sure of anyone — and lets nobody in.","more":"Under the hood: SQL uses **three-valued logic** — TRUE / FALSE / UNKNOWN. NULL means \"unknown\", and any comparison with it (`x <> NULL`, `x = NULL`) yields not FALSE but UNKNOWN.\n\n`NOT IN (a, b, NULL)` expands to `x<>a AND x<>b AND x<>NULL`. The last term is UNKNOWN, and `anything AND UNKNOWN` can never become TRUE. So no row passes the filter → the query returns nothing, even though there's data.\n\n- This is the classic trap: one NULL in the subquery nullifies the entire result, and the bug stays silent until a NULL appears in the lookup table.\n- Fix: use `NOT EXISTS` (it works on two-valued logic and is NULL-safe) or add `WHERE col IS NOT NULL` to the subquery.","quizCorrect":"The comparison x<>NULL yields UNKNOWN, and the whole condition is never true","quizWrong":["NOT IN automatically discards all rows when a NULL is present, per the standard","A subquery with NULL raises an error and rolls back the query","NULL equals zero and matches all numeric values"],"picCap":"One NULL yields UNKNOWN (red pulse) and infects the whole AND → 0 rows"},"db-sql-nosql":{"q":"SQL vs NoSQL — when to choose which?","a":"SQL means tables with a strict schema and relationships: use it when data is structured and integrity matters (money, orders) — you get JOIN and ACID. NoSQL means a flexible schema with no rigid relationships: use it for huge volume, speed, and easy horizontal scaling.","d":"SQL (PostgreSQL, MySQL): data is laid out in tables, relationships via foreign keys, queries join them with JOIN. ACID guarantees: a transaction either fully succeeds or fully rolls back — no half-debited money. Downside: scaling horizontally (sharding) is hard.\n\nNoSQL is a family:\n- document (MongoDB): JSON documents, flexible schema;\n- key-value (Redis): key → value, very fast;\n- columnar (Cassandra): for huge volumes and writes;\n- graph (Neo4j): relationships as first-class (social networks, recommendations).\nMany NoSQL stores give BASE instead of ACID: data converges \"eventually\" (eventual consistency) — sacrificing instant strict integrity for speed and availability. Data is often denormalized (duplicated) to read in one query without a JOIN.\n\nThe selection rule: integrity and complex relationships → SQL; extreme volume/speed/flexible schema → NoSQL. In practice they're often combined.","note":"'NoSQL = horizontal scaling, SQL = not' is an outdated myth: modern SQL offers sharding and distributed ACID (CockroachDB, YugabyteDB, Spanner, Citus), while many NoSQL stores cut guarantees under CAP — e.g., MongoDB had no multi-document transactions at all before 4.0, and Cassandra offers only tunable consistency with no true cross-partition transactions.","hook":"SQL is an accounting ledger to the penny (everything balances). NoSQL is a box of labeled bags (toss in whatever you want).","more":"**SQL (relational)** stores data in tables with a fixed schema and key-based relationships. It gives **JOIN** and **ACID transactions** — integrity is guaranteed, no partial writes. The cost: the schema must be thought out in advance, and horizontal scaling is harder.\n\n**NoSQL** is a broad umbrella (document, key-value, columnar, graph): a flexible or absent schema, data often denormalized, easy to shard across many nodes.\n\n- take **SQL** when there are relationships and consistency matters: money, orders, accounting\n- take **NoSQL** for huge volume, simple access patterns, and horizontal growth\n- pitfall: NoSQL isn't \"SQL without a schema\" — for flexibility you pay with manual integrity and data duplication","quizCorrect":"Structured data, relationships, integrity matters (ACID)","quizWrong":["When you need easy horizontal scaling out","When the data schema constantly and flexibly changes","When there are no relationships between data and only speed matters"],"picCap":"SQL: a rigid table + integrity. NoSQL: free-form documents + scale."},"g-db-gin-jsonb":{"q":"You have a JSONB column and search WHERE data @> '{\"status\":\"paid\"}'. A B-tree doesn't help. What index do you need and why?","a":"A B-tree indexes the value as a whole, but you need to search INSIDE the document. GIN breaks JSONB into key-value pairs and indexes each one. Then @> (contains) and ?| (has key) fly.","d":"GIN = Generalized Inverted Index, an inverted index (like search engines: word → list of documents). For JSONB there are two operator classes: jsonb_ops (default, indexes keys AND values) and jsonb_path_ops (paths only, smaller and faster for @>, but can't do the key-existence check ?). GIN is more expensive on writes and larger in size than a B-tree.","note":"GIN is large and slow on writes (especially jsonb_ops vs jsonb_path_ops — the latter is more compact but only for @>). To speed up inserts there's fastupdate (a pending list), but it temporarily slows reads until the list is merged. A B-tree is still needed if you search by a specific scalar data->>'id' = ? — for that you build an expression index.","hook":"GIN is like the alphabetical index at the back of a book: word → pages, not page → words","more":"**B-tree** indexes the column value as a whole and orders it by `<`/`=` comparison. For JSONB that's useless: you're searching not \"is the whole document equal\" but \"does it contain a pair inside\".\n\n**GIN** (Generalized Inverted Index) is an inverted index: it breaks JSONB into components (keys and values) and for each stores the list of rows where it occurs. So the operators `@>` (contains), `?`, `?|`, `?&` (key existence) work via the index.\n\n- The default `jsonb_ops` indexes keys and values; `jsonb_path_ops` is more compact and faster but only for `@>`.\n- Pitfall: GIN is more expensive on writes and larger in size; a point lookup `data->>'x' = ?` is better via an **expression B-tree**.","quizCorrect":"GIN","quizWrong":["A B-tree over the whole column","A hash index","BRIN"],"picCap":"GIN: a key lights up a list of rows (an inverted index)"},"g-db-brin":{"q":"A trades table with 500M rows, written in time order. A B-tree on created_at is gigantic. What's 1000x more compact?","a":"BRIN. It doesn't store a pointer to each row; for each block it remembers min/max. If the data physically goes in order (like tickets by time), a range query discards whole blocks. The index costs pennies in size.","d":"BRIN = Block Range Index. Effective ONLY when the physical row order correlates with the column value (append-only by time, an autoincrement id). If the data is shuffled — BRIN is useless (each block's min/max covers the whole range). Not suitable for a point lookup of a single row — there you want a B-tree.","note":"BRIN fully relies on the physical correlation of data with the column: if insertion order doesn't match created_at (backfill, updates, shuffled blocks) or a VACUUM FULL/reload happened — BRIN degrades to nearly useless, discarding few blocks. It needs regular brin_summarize and isn't suited for point lookups/uniqueness.","hook":"BRIN is like labels on boxes in an archive: \"box 3 = Jan-Feb\", without opening each one","more":"**BRIN** (Block Range INdex) stores not a reference to each row but a summary (min/max values) per **block range** of the table. The size is tiny, often thousands of times smaller than a B-tree.\n\nIt works only when the physical row order correlates with the column value — append-only data by time (trades, logs) is the ideal case. A range query on `created_at` discards whole blocks whose min/max don't intersect the condition, and scans only the matching ones.\n\n- Pitfall: after `UPDATE`/`DELETE` or out-of-order inserts the correlation breaks — the index becomes nearly useless.\n- BRIN doesn't speed up a point lookup of a single row; you need a B-tree. Keep it current via `brin_summarize`/autovacuum.","quizCorrect":"When the physical row order correlates with the column value","quizWrong":["For a point lookup by a unique key","When the column values are randomly shuffled across the table","For full-text search"],"picCap":"BRIN stores min/max per block, not per row"},"g-db-partial-index":{"q":"A table has 10M orders, but you only process WHERE status='NEW' (200 of them). An index over all 10M is bloated. What do you do?","a":"A partial index — you index only the needed subset with a WHERE condition. The index will be tiny (200 rows), fast, and is updated only when a row enters/leaves the condition. Ideal for queues and \"active\" records.","d":"A partial index's condition must match (or be covered by) the query's condition, otherwise the planner won't pick it. Bonus: you can make a partial UNIQUE — e.g. \"only one active contract per client\": CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... WHERE active. This can't be expressed with a plain UNIQUE constraint.","note":"The planner will apply a partial index only if its WHERE predicate provably covers the query's condition — and the constant must match literally (an index WHERE status='NEW' won't fire for status=:param if the planner isn't sure of the value). A handy side trick is a partial UNIQUE index (uniqueness only among active rows).","hook":"A partial index is a to-do list with only the \"urgent\" items, not the whole planner","more":"A **partial index** is an index with a predicate: `CREATE INDEX ... WHERE status='NEW'`. Only rows satisfying the condition go into the tree, so it's tiny and fits in cache.\n\nWrites are cheaper: a row is indexed only when it enters the condition, and removed from the index when it leaves (status changed). Ideal for \"hot\" subsets — queues, active/unprocessed records in a huge table.\n\n- For the planner to apply it, the query's condition must be **logically covered** by the index's predicate.\n- Pitfall: a predicate with a volatile `now()` won't work; use fixed literals. Often combined with partial + sorting on the selection field.","quizCorrect":"Uniqueness only among rows satisfying the condition","quizWrong":["Uniqueness over the whole table is faster to check and simpler","Automatic cascade on delete","Index compression on disk"],"picCap":"The stream is filtered by the condition → only the subset enters the index"},"g-db-expression-index":{"q":"You log in by WHERE lower(email)=?. There's an index on email, but it isn't used. Why, and how do you fix it?","a":"The index stores email as-is, but you search by lower(email) — that's a different expression, the index doesn't fit. You need an expression index right on lower(email). Then the planner sees the expression match and uses it.","d":"Rule: an index works only if the left side of the condition TEXTUALLY matches the expression in the index. Any function wrapping the column (lower(), date_trunc(), col+0, col::text) kills a plain index. The solution — either an expression index with the same function, or rewrite the query without a wrapper over the column.","note":"The expression in the index must match the expression in the query CHARACTER-FOR-CHARACTER and be IMMUTABLE — e.g. lower(email) is fine, but now()-based or timezone-dependent (col::date on a timestamptz) is not — the index won't be created or won't be applied. The planner collects statistics on the expression separately — after creation an ANALYZE is useful.","hook":"Searching a phone book by surname when the book is sorted by first name — the function reshuffles everything","more":"A plain index on `email` stores the original values. The query `WHERE lower(email)=?` applies a function to the column — for the planner that's a **different expression**, and a B-tree on the raw `email` doesn't fit (it would have to run `lower()` over all rows).\n\nThe solution is an **expression (functional) index**: `CREATE INDEX ON users (lower(email))`. The index stores the already-computed `lower(email)`, the planner sees an exact expression match and uses it.\n\n- The expression in the query and in the index must match literally.\n- Pitfall: the function must be `IMMUTABLE`. An alternative for case-insensitivity is the `citext` type or a collation. For LIKE prefixes you need `text_pattern_ops`.","quizCorrect":"A function over the column creates a different expression, the index doesn't match","quizWrong":["lower() is forbidden in WHERE","Indexes don't work with strings at all, only with numeric and date columns of a table","You need to add ORDER BY"],"picCap":"The index must store exactly the expression that's in the WHERE"},"g-db-explain-cost-rows":{"q":"In EXPLAIN you see cost=0.00..431 and rows=1, but the query actually returned 5000 rows. What does that tell you?","a":"cost is the planner's made-up units (an estimate, not milliseconds), and rows in the plan is its ESTIMATE. If it estimates 1 but the reality is 5000, the statistics are stale, the planner guessed wrong, and may have picked a bad plan (nested loop instead of hash join). Fixed with ANALYZE.","d":"EXPLAIN (without ANALYZE) gives estimates only; the query isn't run. EXPLAIN ANALYZE actually executes it and shows actual rows. Compare estimated vs actual: an order-of-magnitude gap means stale statistics or correlated columns. EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) shows shared hit (from cache) vs read (from disk), so you can tell whether the query is I/O-bound.","hook":"cost is like an ETA from a sat-nav using an old map: the road is closed, so the estimate lies","more":"In `EXPLAIN`, **cost** is an abstract estimate in arbitrary units (pages × coefficients like `seq_page_cost`), NOT milliseconds. `rows` is the **estimated** row count, derived from the `pg_statistic` statistics.\n\nAn estimate of 1 vs an actual of 5000 means the statistics are stale or skewed. On such an estimate the planner picks its strategy: at \"1 row\" it goes for a **nested loop**, which on 5000 rows is catastrophically slow instead of a hash/merge join.\n\n- Check the actuals via `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and compare estimated vs actual rows.\n- Fix: `ANALYZE` / tuning autovacuum, raising `default_statistics_target`, and for correlated columns extended statistics via `CREATE STATISTICS`.","quizCorrect":"Statistics are stale; the plan may be chosen suboptimally","quizWrong":["The query always runs fast","cost is measured in milliseconds on the planner's machine that built the plan","An index is definitely missing"],"picCap":"est vs actual rows diverge = bad statistics","note":"Main trap: EXPLAIN ANALYZE ACTUALLY executes the query, including INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE — without wrapping it in BEGIN; ... ROLLBACK you'll corrupt production data. Another subtlety: for nodes with loops>1 (the inner side of a nested loop) actual rows are shown as the AVERAGE per iteration, not the total — multiply by loops, otherwise it looks like the estimate matched while the discrepancy is huge."},"g-db-pool-size":{"q":"40 application threads, and you set the HikariCP pool to 100 connections \"to be safe\". Under load everything gets slower. Why is fewer better?","a":"A connection is a process/backend in Postgres, and a limited number of cores executes them. 100 connections on 8 cores means contention for CPU and disk plus context switching. A small pool (formula ~ cores×2 + spindles) gives less parallelism but HIGHER throughput.","d":"The classic PostgreSQL wiki formula: connections = (core_count * 2) + effective_spindle_count. A queue inside the pool beats a queue inside the DB: a waiting thread holds a transaction open, holds locks, and consumes work_mem ×N. Also, Hikari's maxLifetime must be shorter than the server-side timeout, otherwise you hit \"connection reset\".","note":"Counterintuitive: when connections run short the answer is almost always 'shrink the pool', not grow it. A big pool merely shifts the queue from the pool onto the DB's CPU/disk and hurts neighboring services through the shared max_connections. The real ceiling is the database's max_connections minus other consumers; past that you put PgBouncer in front instead of inflating the application pool.","hook":"Checkout lines in a store: 20 registers for 8 cashiers means queues and chaos; better to run 8 registers and let customers flow","more":"Each connection in Postgres is a separate **backend process**. Only the physical cores actually move them in parallel. 100 connections on 8 cores = constant fighting for CPU, context switching, and contention over disk and locks — throughput drops and latency rises.\n\nA small pool creates a queue on the application side, but the DB runs at peak efficiency: less parallelism, yet higher total throughput and more stable latency.\n\n- The old HikariCP heuristic: `connections ≈ cores × 2 + effective_spindles`.\n- Pitfall: the pool must be ≥ the number of threads that actually hit the DB concurrently, otherwise you get connection-acquisition timeouts. Long transactions hold a connection — move external calls outside the transaction.","quizCorrect":"Connections fight over limited CPU/disk, and context switches grow","quizWrong":["Postgres caps the pool at 10","A bigger pool is always faster under any load on any hardware you can throw at it","Hikari can't go above 50"],"picCap":"Fewer connections = less contention for CPU/disk"},"g-db-fk-cascade-storm":{"q":"You put ON DELETE CASCADE everywhere \"for convenience\". You deleted one client, and the DB hung for minutes and locked up half the system. What happened?","a":"CASCADE is a recursive delete across all child tables in ONE transaction. Deleting the client dragged in orders → line items → logs: millions of rows, a pile of locks, and a bloated WAL. Plus, if there's no index on the FK, every cascade does a seq scan.","d":"CASCADE dangers: (1) an unpredictable delete volume and a long transaction holding locks; (2) without an index on the FK column, each cascade step scans the entire child table; (3) triggers on child tables fire silently. Alternatives: ON DELETE RESTRICT (explicit block), soft-delete (a deleted_at flag), or deleting in batches in the background.","note":"ON DELETE CASCADE runs row by row inside the DB and is invisible in application code — monitoring and auditing often miss it, and a cascade without indexes on the child FKs does a seq scan per row. For large volumes it's safer to delete children in explicit batches (DELETE ... LIMIT in a loop) or use soft-delete.","hook":"You pulled one thread from a sweater, and the whole sleeve unraveled — and now you're holding it together by hand (the locks)","more":"`ON DELETE CASCADE` is a recursive walk over all child FKs, deleting the related rows in **one transaction**. Deleting a client drags in orders → line items → logs: millions of rows, millions of row/page locks, a swollen WAL, and a long fsync.\n\nIf the FK column has no index, each \"are there children?\" check does a **seq scan** of the child table — on large tables that's lethal.\n\n- Always index FK columns (Postgres does not index them automatically).\n- For mass deletes — do it in batches, off-peak, or use a soft delete (`deleted_at`) / archive partition.\n- Pitfall: a long transaction holds locks and blocks others' operations on the same rows — hence \"half the system hung\".","quizCorrect":"A long transaction, an avalanche of locks and WAL, especially without an FK index","quizWrong":["A cascade deletes only one row","CASCADE doesn't work in Postgres","It's always faster than a manual DELETE, because the database does the work in a single pass"],"picCap":"A cascade delete rolls down the chain (a wave of red) and wipes millions of rows in one transaction"},"g-db-window-vs-groupby":{"q":"You need to tag each of a client's trades with their total turnover, but WITHOUT collapsing the rows. GROUP BY breaks the detail. What do you use?","a":"A window function: SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY client_id). It computes the aggregate over the group but keeps ALL rows in place, appending the result to each one. GROUP BY would fold a client into a single row — here the detail rows stay.","d":"The key difference: GROUP BY reduces the number of rows, a window function does not (the aggregate goes into an extra column). Windows give you ROW_NUMBER/RANK/DENSE_RANK (numbering, \"top-N per group\"), LAG/LEAD (previous/next row — a delta over time), and running totals via ORDER BY inside OVER. A common interview question: \"the latest trade per client\" = ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY client ORDER BY ts DESC) filtered to =1.","hook":"Each student gets their own grade AND the class average written in the margin — nobody is removed","more":"`GROUP BY` **collapses** a group's rows into a single aggregated one — the detail is lost. But you need to keep every trade and append the total to it.\n\nThe **window function** `SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY client_id)` computes the aggregate over the group (partition) without folding the rows — the result is placed in EVERY row of the partition. So the detail stays, with the total alongside.\n\n- A window can also do `ROW_NUMBER`, `RANK`, `LAG/LEAD`, and running totals via `ORDER BY ... ROWS BETWEEN`.\n- Pitfall: window functions run AFTER `WHERE`/`GROUP BY` and can't be used in `WHERE` — filter on them via a subquery/CTE. For performance you want an index supporting `PARTITION BY`/`ORDER BY`.","quizCorrect":"Computes the aggregate while keeping all original rows","quizWrong":["Always faster than GROUP BY","Works only with numbers","Removes duplicates automatically from the result set before aggregating"],"picCap":"Window: all rows stay, the aggregate is appended to each one in turn","note":"Subtlety: when there's an ORDER BY inside OVER but no explicit frame, the default frame is RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW, and with duplicate sort-key values RANGE (not ROWS) sums all peer rows sharing the same value at once — the running total 'jumps' on duplicates. For an honest row-by-row accumulation specify ROWS BETWEEN ... explicitly."},"g-db-recursive-cte":{"q":"A table (id, parent_id) holds a category tree. You need all descendants of a node. A single SELECT can't do recursion. What do you use?","a":"A recursive CTE: WITH RECURSIVE. The anchor is the starting node; the recursive part joins the table to itself on parent_id, repeating as long as new rows are found. That unrolls a tree of any depth in a single query.","d":"Structure: WITH RECURSIVE t AS (anchor UNION ALL recursive_part) SELECT FROM t. Pitfalls: (1) with cycles in the data (a→b→a) the recursion loops forever — you need protection (accumulate the path and check it, or use UNION instead of UNION ALL); (2) an ordinary CTE in Postgres ≥12 is NOT materialized as a barrier by default (it's inlined), but you can force MATERIALIZED/NOT MATERIALIZED. A CTE is not a \"table in memory\", it's just a named subquery.","note":"A recursive CTE without protection loops forever on a data cycle (parent_id pointing to an ancestor) — you need either UNION (dedup) or a depth limit / path tracking with an array and a repeat check. And in old Postgres a CTE was an optimization fence (materialized) — since PG12 it's inlined, but a recursive CTE is still materialized.","hook":"A matryoshka doll: you open one and there's another inside, and so on until they run out","more":"**WITH RECURSIVE** builds the result iteratively. The **anchor** part (before UNION) gives the starting row — the node whose descendants you're after. The **recursive** part (after UNION) joins the source table to the rows already found on `t.parent_id = cte.id`, adding the next level.\n\nThe engine spins the recursive part as long as it returns new rows; when there are none, it stops. So a tree of any depth unrolls in a single query, with no fixed number of joins.\n\n- **UNION** (not ALL) prevents looping on broken data (cycles).\n- On large trees, add a depth/limit and an index on `parent_id`.","quizCorrect":"The recursion loops forever without protection against repeats (UNION / path check)","quizWrong":["A CTE doesn't support JOIN","Recursion is forbidden in Postgres","UNION ALL removes duplicates on its own during the recursion, which is why it is preferred here"],"picCap":"A recursive CTE unrolls the tree level by level (the highlight goes deeper)"},"g-db-prepared-plan-cache":{"q":"A parameterized query ran fast, then on the 6th run it suddenly got slow on the same data. What's the plan cache got to do with it?","a":"After 5 executions of a prepared statement, Postgres may switch from a custom plan (built for the specific parameters) to a generic plan (one for all values). If the data is skewed (status='NEW' — 200 rows, 'DONE' — 10M), the generic plan picks a seq scan and kills the fast case.","d":"The mechanism: for prepared statements PG first builds custom plans (accounting for the values), and after ~5 runs compares them with the generic plan's cost and may lock in the generic one (independent of parameters → cheaper to plan, but ignores data skew). Control it with plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan / force_generic_plan / auto. In JDBC this surfaces via prepareThreshold and server-side prepared statements; in PgBouncer transaction mode server-side prepared statements break entirely.","note":"The generic plan is chosen after 5 executions, but only if its estimated cost is no worse than the custom one — yet with skewed data it misses and kills the fast case. Fix: plan_cache_mode=force_custom_plan, or lower prepareThreshold in JDBC. This same effect is why 'the same query is sometimes fast, sometimes slow' with no visible change.","hook":"A barista memorized \"your usual\" and makes it on autopilot — but today you wanted something else, and they didn't ask","more":"A prepared statement is parsed and planned separately from the parameter values. For the first **5 runs** Postgres builds a **custom plan** for the specific parameters, then compares it with the **generic plan** (averaged, with no knowledge of the values) and, if the generic isn't more expensive, switches to it to save on planning.\n\nThe problem is **skewed data**: the generic plan estimates an \"average\" selectivity. For `status='DONE'` (10M rows) a seq scan is fine, and that same plan lands on `status='NEW'` (200 rows), where an index scan was needed — hence the sudden slowdown.\n\n- Fixed with `plan_cache_mode=force_custom_plan` or by concatenating the value into the query (mind the injection risk).","quizCorrect":"PG switched to a generic plan that ignores the data skew","quizWrong":["The connection cache overflowed","The index was deleted automatically by autovacuum during the run","An automatic VACUUM kicked in"],"picCap":"After 5 runs PG switches to a generic plan → degradation into a seq scan (a red flash)"},"g-java-enummap-weakhashmap":{"q":"EnumMap, WeakHashMap, IdentityHashMap — when to use them instead of HashMap?","a":"EnumMap — enum keys: internally an array indexed by ordinal, faster and more compact than HashMap. WeakHashMap — keys are held weakly, the GC evicts an entry once its key is no longer referenced anywhere (caches). IdentityHashMap compares keys by == rather than equals.","d":"EnumMap preserves the enum declaration order. WeakHashMap is handy for per-object metadata without leaks. IdentityHashMap is needed by serializers/object graphs where identity matters, not equality (two equal strings = two distinct keys).","note":"WeakHashMap clears an entry once the GC has collected the KEY, but if the value holds a strong reference back to the key, the entry is never freed (the classic leak). Also WeakHashMap is not thread-safe; for weak-reference caches in prod people usually reach for Caffeine/Guava Cache.","hook":"Enum keys — like a cabinet with numbered slots; Weak — a guest who'll get evicted; Identity — admitted by face, not by name.","more":"**EnumMap:** keys are constants of a single enum. Internally a plain **array indexed by `ordinal()`**, no hashing and no collisions — faster and more compact than `HashMap`, iterates in declaration order. Always use it when the key is an enum.\n\n**WeakHashMap:** keys are held by **weak references**. Once nothing else references a key, the GC collects it and the entry disappears on its own — handy for caches/metadata tied to an object's lifetime. Keep values free of strong back-references to the key.\n\n**IdentityHashMap:** compares keys by `==` (reference identity), not `equals`/`hashCode`. Needed where object identity matters (graph traversals, serialization) rather than logical equality.","quizCorrect":"Compares keys by == rather than equals","quizWrong":["Holds weak references to keys and to the values","Sorts keys by ordinal","Forbids null keys"],"picCap":"three specialized maps, each for its own case"},"g-java-navigablemap":{"q":"NavigableMap/TreeMap and Deque — why floor/ceiling and a double-ended queue?","a":"TreeMap (NavigableMap) keeps keys sorted and offers floorKey/ceilingKey/higher/lower — the \"nearest below/above\", plus headMap/tailMap, subMap. Deque — a queue open at both ends: addFirst/addLast/pollFirst; replaces Stack and works as either a stack or a queue.","d":"NavigableMap is ideal for range queries: tiers by threshold, nearest date. ArrayDeque is faster than LinkedList as a stack/queue and is not thread-safe (so without that overhead). Stack is obsolete (synchronized, extends Vector).","note":"TreeMap compares keys only via compareTo/Comparator and ignores equals entirely — two keys unequal by equals but equal by the comparator are treated as one; the classic trap is a comparator inconsistent with equals (e.g. on a single field), which silently overwrites entries and, in a TreeSet, makes elements 'vanish'.","hook":"TreeMap — a ruler with notches: \"give me the nearest one to the left\". Deque — a pipe, you push and pull from either end.","more":"**TreeMap (NavigableMap):** keys are stored in a red-black tree and are always **sorted**. Provides navigation: `floorKey`/`ceilingKey` (nearest ≤ / ≥), `lower`/`higher` (strictly less/greater), plus the ranges `headMap`/`tailMap`/`subMap`. Operations are `O(log n)`.\n\n**Where:** finding the nearest value, intervals, \"next by time\" — the classic fit for schedules, tariff grids, leaderboards.\n\n**Deque:** a double-ended queue — `addFirst/addLast`, `pollFirst/pollLast`, `peekFirst/peekLast`. Replaces the obsolete `Stack`: as a stack use `push/pop` (at the head), as a queue use `addLast/pollFirst`. Implementations: `ArrayDeque` (the fast default), `LinkedList`.","quizCorrect":"0 — the largest key <= 50","quizWrong":["100 — the smallest key >= 50","null — there is no exact key","50 — it fills it in itself"],"picCap":"floor: query 50 descends to the nearest key below"},"g-java-generics-capture":{"q":"Capture: why does List<?> forbid writing, and how does a helper method rescue it?","a":"List<?> — \"a list of something unknown\": the compiler doesn't know the type, so add is forbidden (except null). Capture — internally it gives that unknown a name, CAP#1. The trick: a private generic method <T> \"captures\" the type into T and works with it type-safely.","d":"Wildcard capture is the basis of swap/reordering on a List<?>. Without the helper the compiler can't prove that get and set operate on the same type. This is a common senior question about the limits of type inference.","note":"Capture inference works only one level deep: List<List<?>> does not get captured into List<List<T>>, and the helper method won't compile — the compiler cannot prove the inner ?'s are the same; nested wildcards require either recursive capture at each level or explicit parameterization.","hook":"You won't hand a stranger the key; but once he gives his name (T) — you can work with him.","more":"**Why you can't write:** `List<?>` means \"a list of some single but unknown type\". The compiler can't guarantee the element being added fits, so `add` is forbidden — except `null`. You can read as `Object`.\n\n**Capture:** while processing `?` the compiler internally assigns the unknown type a name — `CAP#1`. Error messages with `capture of ?` are exactly about this.\n\n**The trick (capture helper):** you write a private generic method `<T> void swapHelper(List<T> l, ...)` and call it from the method with `List<?>`. The `?` argument is \"captured\" into a concrete `T`, and inside the helper everything is type-safe — you can write and swap elements.","quizCorrect":"The type is unknown, compatibility can't be guaranteed","quizWrong":["List<?> is always immutable","? means an empty list","A wildcard forbids any access to the collection at all, read or write"],"picCap":"the helper captures ? into a concrete T — and the write goes through"},"g-java-final-vars":{"q":"final on a variable, a method, and a class — three different meanings. And what is effectively final?","a":"final var — assign once (it doesn't make the object immutable, only the reference). final method — can't be overridden. final class — can't be subclassed (String). Effectively final — you never reassign the variable after the initial assignment; lambdas and anonymous classes capture only such variables.","d":"A final field guarantees visibility after the constructor (safe publication in the JMM) — the basis of immutable classes. A lambda can't capture a mutating local variable because of capture-by-value semantics.","note":"final on a reference variable doesn't make the object immutable — a final List can still be .add()-ed. Effectively final: the variable can't be reassigned after initialization, otherwise a lambda/anonymous class won't capture it — the typical mistake \"mutating a counter in a lambda inside a loop\" won't compile.","hook":"final on a reference — the leash is nailed to the wall, but the dog still runs around the yard.","more":"**Three meanings:** `final` on a **variable** — assign exactly once (the reference/value is fixed, but the object behind the reference stays mutable). `final` on a **method** — forbids overriding in subclasses. `final` on a **class** — forbids subclassing (`String`, `Integer`).\n\n**Important:** `final List l` doesn't make the list immutable — you can mutate its contents, you just can't reassign `l`.\n\n**Effectively final:** a variable without `final` that is never reassigned after initialization. Lambdas and anonymous classes capture local variables **only if** they are final or effectively final — otherwise a compile error (the value is copied, mutability is not allowed).","quizCorrect":"Reassign l to another reference","quizWrong":["Call l.add(x)","Read l.size()","Pass l into a method as a parameter"],"picCap":"one keyword — three meanings"},"g-java-object-methods":{"q":"The hashCode/equals contract and why override toString — what does the JVM require?","a":"The contract: equals is reflexive, symmetric, transitive, consistent; equal objects yield equal hashCode (the reverse isn't required). Break it and HashMap/HashSet break. toString is for logs/debug; getClass and instanceof behave differently with subclasses.","d":"equals via getClass() breaks symmetry with subclasses; via instanceof — risks asymmetry. For a record, equals/hashCode/toString are generated automatically from the components. hashCode must stay stable while the object is in a collection.","note":"Using getClass() in equals breaks symmetry with subclasses, while instanceof breaks it for proxies/Hibernate entities (a lazy proxy isn't equal to the real one). An unstable hashCode (depending on a mutable field) causes the object to \"disappear\" from a HashSet after a change.","hook":"Identical keys — identical lock (hash). But one lock can be opened by different keys too (a collision).","more":"**The equals contract:** reflexivity (`x.equals(x)`), symmetry, transitivity, consistency, and `x.equals(null)==false`. **The link with hashCode:** equal objects must yield an equal `hashCode`; the reverse isn't required (collisions are allowed). Break it and `HashMap`/`HashSet` \"lose\" elements, since they first look up by bucket (hash), then compare with `equals`.\n\n**toString:** not for logic but for logs/debug — the default `Class@hex` is useless, override it for readability.\n\n**Inheritance pitfalls:** a `getClass()` check breaks symmetry with subclasses, `instanceof` is gentler, but subclasses must be careful. The JVM doesn't \"require\" anything, but collections and the contract rely on these rules.","quizCorrect":"a.hashCode()==b.hashCode()","quizWrong":["a==b by reference","Different hashCode for speed","a and b of the same getClass()"],"picCap":"equal ⇒ hashes are equal; the reverse — no guarantee"},"g-java-checked-streams":{"q":"Why can't you throw a checked exception inside stream().map(...), and how do people work around it?","a":"Functional interfaces (Function etc.) don't declare throws for checked exceptions, so a lambda inside map can't throw, say, IOException. Workarounds: wrap in a RuntimeException, do a try inside the lambda, or build a throwing wrapper function.","d":"This follows from the signatures of the JDK interfaces. Best practice — don't drag I/O into streams; if you must, define your own @FunctionalInterface with throws or collect errors via partition/a Result object instead of swallowing them.","note":"Wrapping a checked exception in a RuntimeException inside the lambda loses the type in the signature — the caller isn't required to catch it, and the exception surfaces in an unexpected place. The \"sneaky throws\" alternative (via a generics trick or Lombok's @SneakyThrows) compiles, but hides the checked exception from the compiler — a debatable practice.","hook":"A \"dirty\" checked exception won't fit into the tidy pipe of a stream — wrap it in Runtime film.","more":"**Under the hood:** `map`, `filter`, etc. take functional interfaces (`Function`, `Predicate`) whose SAM method is declared without `throws`. A lambda is an implementation of that method, so it physically can't throw a checked exception broader than the one declared: the compiler forbids it.\n\n**Why so:** the Stream API is generic and doesn't know which exceptions your code raises; adding `throws Exception` to every interface would make them unusable for non-throwing cases.\n\n**Workarounds:** wrap in a `try` inside the lambda and rethrow as `UncheckedIOException`/`RuntimeException`; or define your own `ThrowingFunction` and a wrapper that catches the checked exception and wraps it. The pitfall: the wrapper loses the exception type and breaks early termination — for I/O chains a plain `for` is often cleaner.","quizCorrect":"Function.apply doesn't declare throws for checked exceptions","quizWrong":["Streams catch all exceptions themselves","map runs on a different thread","Checked exceptions were removed from Java entirely long ago in a release"],"picCap":"a checked exception won't get into Function — we wrap it in an unchecked one"},"g-java-compact-intern":{"q":"Compact Strings and intern() — how is a String stored, and why is intern dangerous in prod?","a":"Since Java 9 a String is internally a byte[] (Latin-1 = 1 byte/char), not a char[]: it saves memory on ASCII. intern() puts the string into the pool and returns the canonical instance — but it clogs the pool and can slow things down; for deduplication prefer -XX:+UseStringDeduplication with G1.","d":"Literals and constants go into the pool automatically. A manual intern() is rarely justified (massive numbers of identical keys), otherwise it grows the pool metadata and causes contention. Compact Strings are on by default, disabled with -XX:-CompactStrings.","note":"intern() puts strings into a pool on the Java heap (since Java 7 — not native/PermGen; interned strings are GC-able). With many unique strings this bloats the heap and causes StringTable contention. To dedup equal strings on the heap, prefer -XX:+UseStringDeduplication with G1 rather than calling intern() by hand.","hook":"Compact — packed the text into smaller bags. intern — a shared shelf: handy, but pile it up and it's hard to find.","more":"**Under the hood:** since Java 9 (JEP 254) `String` stores data in a `byte[]` plus a `coder` flag field. If all characters fit into **Latin-1**, it's encoded as 1 byte/char; otherwise — UTF-16 at 2 bytes. Previously it was always `char[]` (2 bytes). For ASCII text this is half the memory.\n\n**Why:** most strings in real applications are ASCII (keys, URLs, JSON), there's no point overpaying for UTF-16.\n\n**Pitfalls:** `intern()` places the string into the native pool and returns the canonical instance — it looks like \"free deduplication\", but the pool is bounded, GC over it is more expensive, and excess interning slows down and leaks. To eliminate duplicates on the heap it's better to enable `-XX:+UseStringDeduplication` (G1) — it runs in the background and automatically.","quizCorrect":"ASCII strings are stored as byte[] (1 byte/char)","quizWrong":["Automatically interns all strings","Makes String mutable","Compresses strings with gzip in memory to save space"],"picCap":"compact: ASCII shrinks to 1 byte; intern collapses to a single copy in the pool"},"ds-acks":{"q":"What does acks=all on the producer mean, and why use it?","a":"The partition leader acknowledges a write only after ALL in-sync replicas have received it. That way a leader crash won't lose the message. acks=1 is faster but can lose data if the leader fails.","d":"acks=all + min.insync.replicas + idempotent producer = baseline durability.","note":"acks=all alone does NOT guarantee durability: with the default min.insync.replicas=1, \"all ISR\" can mean a single live replica (the leader), and its crash loses data. The real guarantee comes only from acks=all + min.insync.replicas=2 with RF=3.","hook":"The courier hands over the parcel only once all the neighbors have signed. One left? There's still a copy. acks=1: only the senior signs, he leaves, and it's gone.","more":"acks controls when the producer considers a write successful. With **acks=all** the partition leader replies \"ok\" only after the message has been replicated to all **in-sync replicas (ISR)**.\n\nThe point: if the leader dies right after, a new leader is elected from the ISR, which already has the message — no loss. acks=1 waits only for the leader (fast, but if it dies before replication the message is lost), acks=0 — no acknowledgment at all.\n\n- Durability needs the full combo: acks=all + min.insync.replicas>=2 + RF>=3.\n- Otherwise, with a single live replica, \"all\" = \"one\" and the guarantee evaporates.","quizCorrect":"The leader acks the write only after all in-sync replicas receive it; nothing is lost when the leader fails","quizWrong":["All consumers confirmed they processed the message before the ack is sent","The message is written to disk on every broker in the cluster, including non-replicas and standby followers as well","acks=all disables replication for speed, acknowledging with only the leader"],"picCap":"acks=all: ack only once all in-sync replicas have written it"},"ds-eos":{"q":"What does exactly-once in Kafka REALLY mean?","a":"Not the magic of \"delivered exactly once.\" It's an idempotent producer (no duplicates on retries) + read-process-write transactions + an idempotent consumer. \"Exactly once\" is achieved because reprocessing doesn't change the outcome.","d":"In a distributed system, only at-least-once + idempotency is honest. EOS works within Kafka→Kafka boundaries.","hook":"Not \"one receipt at the register,\" but configured so that ringing it up again yields the same total. Not magic — just the habit of not duplicating.","more":"Exactly-once in Kafka is not \"the message is physically delivered exactly once,\" but a guarantee that the **effect** of processing is visible once. It comes from three parts.\n\n- **Idempotent producer**: no duplicates in the log on retries (sequence number tracked at the broker).\n- **Transactions**: read-process-write — reading, processing, and committing the offset are atomic; readers with read_committed don't see uncommitted data.\n- An idempotent consumer at the end of the chain.\n\nIt only works within Kafka→Kafka (Streams). The moment you write to an external DB/API you need your own idempotency (dedup key, outbox); EOS doesn't reach there.","quizCorrect":"Idempotent producer + read-process-write transactions + idempotent consumer; a retry doesn't change the outcome","quizWrong":["Kafka physically delivers every message exactly once with no duplicates even when the network drops mid-send halfway","The broker stores hashes of all messages and drops any repeats itself","It's just acks=all together with automatic offset commits"],"picCap":"\"Exactly once\" = a retry doesn't change the outcome, not delivery magic","note":"EOS isn't a single flag: besides enable.idempotence (true by default since Kafka 3.0) and a producer transactional.id, the CONSUMER must run with isolation.level=read_committed (default is read_uncommitted!), otherwise it reads aborted/uncommitted messages and the whole EOS collapses. Also transactional.id must be STABLE across instance restarts — a random UUID at startup breaks zombie fencing and lets duplicates through."},"ds-idempotent":{"q":"Why MUST a consumer be idempotent?","a":"Delivery is at-least-once: on a rebalance/retry/crash before the offset is committed, the message arrives again. If processing isn't idempotent — double charge / duplicate order.","d":"Implementation: an idempotency key + an \"already processed\" check (processed_ids table / upsert).","note":"Idempotency on the order's business key isn't enough when processing has several distinct side effects (DB write + email + external API call): some of them will run again on a retry. Every side-effect must be idempotent, not just the final write.","hook":"A letter can be delivered twice. If you charge money on each one, you'll be cleaned out. Mark it \"already paid\" and ignore the duplicate.","more":"Kafka gives you **at-least-once**: the offset is committed after processing, and between \"processed\" and \"committed\" a crash, rebalance, or retry can occur. Then the same message arrives again.\n\nIf the handler isn't idempotent, a retry = charging money twice, creating a duplicate order, sending the email twice.\n\n- Idempotency = reprocessing doesn't change the outcome.\n- Techniques: dedup by business key/messageId, UPSERT instead of INSERT, an \"already processed\" check in a table, naturally idempotent operations (set, not increment).\n- This is more reliable than chasing \"exactly-once\" on the delivery side.","quizCorrect":"Delivery is at-least-once: on a rebalance/retry the message arrives again, otherwise a double charge","quizWrong":["Kafka guarantees exactly-once; idempotency is only needed for performance","Idempotency is required only of the producer; the consumer always gets unique messages exactly once each","It's a DB transactionality requirement, unrelated to message delivery"],"picCap":"Without an offset commit, one msg arrives again → duplicate"},"ds-outbox":{"q":"Why a transactional outbox if I already have a DB transaction?","a":"You can't atomically \"commit to the DB AND publish to Kafka\" — they're two different systems, one can fail after the other. The fix: within the SAME DB transaction, write the event to an outbox table; a separate process reliably reads it and sends it to Kafka.","d":"The write and the event fact commit atomically. Your migration to archive_outbox is exactly this.","note":"Outbox gives at-least-once, not exactly-once: the relay can send a message and crash before marking it 'sent'. Without an idempotent consumer on the receiving side, outbox is incomplete. Plus, publishing from the outbox can reorder events relative to how they were written unless you preserve per-key ordering.","hook":"You can't with one hand both put something in the safe AND drop it in the mailbox. You put a note IN the safe, and the mail carrier picks it up and sends it.","more":"A DB transaction protects only the write to the DB itself. But \"persist the order AND publish an event to Kafka\" involves **two independent systems**. Between the DB commit and the Kafka send the service can crash: the data is saved, the event is lost (or vice versa). A two-phase commit between Postgres and Kafka doesn't exist in practice.\n\nOutbox solves this: the event is written to an `outbox` table in the **same transaction** as the business data. Either both commit or neither — the DB guarantees atomicity. Then a separate process (a poller or CDC via Debezium) reads the table and sends to Kafka.\n\n- Delivery ends up **at-least-once** — the same event can go out twice, so the consumer must be idempotent.\n- Pitfalls: forgetting to clean up the outbox table (it grows forever) and not ordering the sends.","quizCorrect":"The DB and Kafka are different systems, you can't commit atomically; write the event to a table in the same transaction","quizWrong":["A DB transaction can roll back messages already sent to Kafka on its own once the broker has already acknowledged them to the sender","Outbox speeds up writes to Kafka by batching messages","Outbox is only needed when the DB driver is in autocommit mode"],"picCap":"Data and event commit in one DB transaction → relay sends to Kafka"},"ds-rebalance":{"q":"What happens when you add an instance to a consumer group?","a":"A rebalance kicks in: partitions are reassigned across consumers, and processing pauses during it (a stop-the-world for the group). Each partition in the group is read by exactly one consumer.","d":"That's why parallelism is capped by the number of partitions. Frequent rebalances = pain; cooperative rebalancing softens it.","note":"A common non-obvious cause of endless rebalances is not crashing instances but exceeding max.poll.interval.ms due to slow batch processing: the consumer is 'alive' by heartbeat, but the broker considers it stuck and evicts it, its commit fails, and the batch is reprocessed. Fixed by lowering max.poll.records or using static membership.","hook":"A new waiter shows up — the tables get redivided, and for a minute everyone freezes. And one table is always served by exactly one waiter.","more":"A consumer group splits a topic's partitions among its consumers so that **each partition is read by exactly one** member. When an instance is added or drops out, the group coordinator triggers a **rebalance** — recomputing \"who takes which partitions.\"\n\nIn the classic protocol a rebalance is **stop-the-world**: all consumers give up their partitions, wait for the new assignment, and only then continue. Processing in the group halts during the pause.\n\n- Frequent rebalances are painful: e.g., a slow handler can't keep up with `max.poll.interval.ms`, gets evicted, and a recompute starts.\n- Mitigated by **cooperative/incremental rebalancing** (Kafka 2.4+), static membership, and sane timeouts.","quizCorrect":"A rebalance kicks in: partitions are reassigned, and processing halts during the pause","quizWrong":["The new instance starts reading the same partitions in parallel with the old ones for a while","Messages are duplicated to all consumers in the group for reliability","Kafka automatically creates new partitions for the added instance"],"picCap":"1 partition = exactly 1 consumer"},"ds-partkey":{"q":"Why does a message need a partition key?","a":"Messages with the same key always go to the same partition → their order is preserved and they land on one consumer. Without a key — round-robin, and order isn't guaranteed.","d":"The key = the entity (orderId, clientId). Ordering is guaranteed only WITHIN a partition.","note":"Order is preserved only as long as the partition count is unchanged: increasing partitions changes how keys hash across them, and messages for one key can land in a different partition — the ordering guarantee for old keys breaks. You can't change the partition count painlessly in prod for a topic that relies on ordering.","hook":"Letters from one sender — into one mailbox and to one carrier, in order. No signature — they get scattered any which way.","more":"The producer hashes the key and takes it modulo the partition count — so **one key always lands in one partition**. Since Kafka guarantees ordering only within a partition, all events for that key are processed strictly in sequence by a single consumer.\n\nExample: the key is `clientId`. All operations of one client go to one partition, and the order \"opened account → bought → sold\" won't get scrambled.\n\n- Without a key — round-robin distribution, and order between messages is **not guaranteed**.\n- Pitfalls: skew (one \"hot\" key loads a single partition), and changing the partition count breaks the prior key→partition mapping.","quizCorrect":"Messages with the same key go to one partition — order is preserved and one consumer handles them","quizWrong":["The key encrypts the message body to protect it in transit","The key sets delivery priority: a larger key is processed first ahead of all the others in the topic","The key guarantees exactly-once delivery of that message"],"picCap":"One key → one partition → order"},"ds-cap":{"q":"CAP in plain terms — what choice are you actually making?","a":"During a network partition (P, which is always possible) you choose: either keep responding, risking stale data (AP), or refuse for the sake of consistency (CP). There's no \"ideal\" CA in a distributed system.","d":"In practice it's about the system's behavior during a partition, not an \"either-or\" forever.","note":"In practice the choice isn't a binary 'CP or AP' but PACELC: even without a network partition there's a latency-vs-consistency tradeoff (Else: Latency or Consistency). Most 'CP' systems actually provide consistency only with a quorum and degrade into unavailability rather than magically staying both C and A.","hook":"The call dropped: you either answer from memory (maybe stale), or honestly stay silent until the line is back. You can't both know and answer at once.","more":"The network in a distributed system **can split at any moment** — that's a partition (P), and there's no opting out of it. So the real choice is always between the two remaining letters once the split has already happened.\n\n- **AP**: keep serving requests, risking stale data (nodes are temporarily out of sync). Good where availability beats accuracy — a feed, a cart.\n- **CP**: refuse to respond until you're sure about consistency. Good for money and balances, where an error beats a wrong number.\n\n\"Pure CA\" doesn't exist in a distributed system — that's a single-node mode. In practice the choice isn't global: different operations of the same service can be AP or CP.","quizCorrect":"Either respond with stale data (AP), or refuse for the sake of consistency (CP)","quizWrong":["You can get all three guarantees at once with good hardware (CA)","You choose between speed and the volume of stored data","P (partition tolerance) can be turned off if the network is reliable enough between the nodes"],"picCap":"During a partition (P): respond (AP) or be accurate (CP)"},"ds-queue-vs-svc":{"q":"Why integrate through a queue instead of calling the service directly?","a":"Decoupling: the producer doesn't wait on the consumer and doesn't fail if it's down. A buffer for peaks (messages pile up, get processed at their own pace). Retries and delivery guarantees. The downside — asynchrony and harder debugging.","d":"Direct REST — when you need a synchronous response here and now.","hook":"A queue is like a mailbox: you drop the letter and walk away. The recipient isn't home — the letter waits, then they pick it up.","more":"With a direct REST call the producer and consumer are **tightly coupled**: the caller waits for a response and fails (or hangs) if the recipient is unavailable or slow.\n\nA queue puts a **buffer** between them. The producer drops a message and is immediately free; the consumer pulls at its own pace. What this gives you:\n\n- **Decoupling** — the recipient can be down, and the messages will wait.\n- **Peak smoothing** — a load spike accumulates in the queue instead of toppling the service.\n- **Retries and delivery guarantees** — the broker won't lose a message until it's acknowledged.\n\nThe price is **asynchrony**: the response doesn't come immediately, eventual consistency appears, and it's harder to trace a request's path and handle duplicates. A direct call is simpler when you need an immediate response and the load isn't heavy.","quizCorrect":"Decoupling: the producer doesn't fail if the consumer is down; a buffer for peaks, retries and delivery guarantees","quizWrong":["A queue is always faster than a direct call because it works synchronously and delivers the reply back in the very same call","A queue gives strong real-time data consistency with no latency","A queue removes the need for retries and simplifies request debugging"],"picCap":"Queue = buffer: the producer lives on even if the consumer is down","note":"A queue gives decoupling but in return almost always gives at-least-once, not exactly-once — the broker redelivers on ack timeout or rebalance, so the consumer MUST be idempotent (dedup by key / processed-id table). Otherwise the very retries you adopted the queue for turn into double charges."},"ds-consumer-groups":{"q":"How do Kafka consumer groups work?","a":"Within ONE group a topic's partitions are split among consumers — each message is processed by exactly one of them (scaling). DIFFERENT groups each get ALL the messages independently — one topic, many subscribers.","d":"Parallelism within a group is capped by the number of partitions.","hook":"A topic is a pizza. Within a group, colleagues split the slices (each their own). A new group = a whole new pizza all to itself.","more":"A Kafka topic is split into **partitions** — the unit of parallelism. A consumer group is a `group.id` label by which Kafka hands out partitions.\n\nWithin **one group** each partition is pinned to exactly one consumer, so a message is processed by just one member of the group — that's how the group **scales** by adding consumers (but no more than the number of partitions).\n\n**Different groups** are independent: every group gets the same message — the \"one topic → many subscribers\" pattern (e.g., billing and analytics read the same thing).\n\n- Pitfalls: more consumers in a group than partitions — the extras sit idle. And ordering is guaranteed only **within a partition**, not across the whole topic.","quizCorrect":"Within one group partitions are split, one consumer reads each message; different groups each get all messages independently","quizWrong":["Each consumer in a group gets a copy of every message in the topic","All groups share partitions among themselves; a message is processed exactly once across the whole cluster and never twice anywhere","A consumer group reads only one fixed partition forever"],"picCap":"Messages flow from partitions: group A splits them between C1/C2, group B takes all","note":"Every group rebalance is stop-the-world: all consumption halts while partitions are reassigned, and slow processing in one poll() exceeds max.poll.interval.ms, so the broker evicts the consumer, triggering yet another rebalance (a cascade). Mitigated by the cooperative-sticky assignor, static membership (group.instance.id), and lowering max.poll.records."},"x-ds-consumer-lag":{"q":"What is consumer lag and why must you watch it?","a":"Lag = the difference between the latest offset in a partition and the offset the consumer has already processed. Growing lag means consumers aren't keeping up with producers, the backlog piles up, data is \"running late.\" It's the key health metric of the pipeline.","d":"Lag grows for several reasons: a slow handler, too few consumers in the group (more partitions than instances), a stuck consumer (poll() not called > max.poll.interval.ms → rebalance). Fixes: more partitions+instances, batch processing, moving heavy work out of the poll loop. Monitored via kafka-consumer-groups --describe or Burrow/Prometheus (kafka_consumergroup_lag). Note: lag itself isn't a bug — spikes are normal; what's dangerous is a steadily rising trend.","hook":"A supermarket checkout: the line growing = the cashier is slower than people arriving.","more":"Each partition is a log with growing offsets. The producer writes to the \"head\" (latest), the consumer advances its committed offset as it processes. **Lag = latest − committed** on each partition; the group's total lag is the sum across all its partitions.\n\nYou watch it because lag is **a delay in time** expressed in messages: the business sees stale data even though the service is \"alive\" and error-free.\n\n- Watch the **trend**, not the absolute: steady lag is fine, rising lag means the consumer can't keep up.\n- Causes of growth: a traffic spike, slow processing/an external call, frequent rebalances, a crashed instance.","quizCorrect":"The difference between the latest offset and the processed offset","quizWrong":["Network latency between the broker and the consumer measured in messages","The number of messages the producer hasn't sent yet","The consumer's downtime during a rebalance"],"picCap":"The unread tail blinks; the gap to end = lag","note":"Lag is measured in messages, not seconds — so with uneven record sizes or key skew one 'hot' partition can have huge lag while the total is near zero, and the average metric masks it; look per-partition. Another pitfall: after a manual offset reset or starting a new consumer with `auto.offset.reset=latest`, lag shows 0 even though data was skipped, not processed — zero lag does not equal 'everything processed'."},"x-ds-rebalance":{"q":"What is a rebalance in a consumer group and why is it dangerous?","a":"A rebalance is the reassignment of partitions among the group's consumers when an instance joins / crashes / hangs. During it the whole group is paused (stop-the-world), processing halts. Frequent rebalances = a pipeline that keeps freezing.","d":"Triggers: adding/removing an instance, exceeding max.poll.interval.ms (heavy work in the loop), losing a heartbeat (session.timeout.ms). The pain: with the classic strategy — eager rebalance, everyone gives up all partitions (a full stop). Fixes: CooperativeStickyAssignor (incremental, only part is handed over), static membership (group.instance.id — a pod restart doesn't trigger a rebalance), moving heavy processing out of the poll loop, a correct commit before handing off a partition via ConsumerRebalanceListener.","hook":"Waiters dividing tables: one leaves — and for a minute everyone drops their trays and redivides the floor.","more":"Partitions are pinned to consumers via the **group coordinator**. When membership changes (a new instance, a crash, or a missed heartbeat), the coordinator triggers a rebalance and redistributes the partitions. In the classic protocol this is **stop-the-world**: all consumers revoke their partitions and wait for the new assignment — processing halts.\n\nThe danger isn't the rebalance itself but **frequent** rebalances: the pipeline gets stuck in a loop of \"pause → reassign → work a bit → pause again.\"\n\n- A common cause — processing taking longer than `max.poll.interval.ms`: the broker considers the consumer dead and kicks it.\n- Mitigated by cooperative rebalancing (incremental) and proper timeouts.","quizCorrect":"The whole group is paused (stop-the-world), processing halts","quizWrong":["All messages in the partitions are lost","Producers in the group get duplicated","Offsets are reset to the start of the topic on every rebalance"],"picCap":"C3 crashed → partitions moved, the group froze","note":"With `enable.auto.commit=true`, rebalance is a frequent source of duplicates and loss: auto-commit fires on a timer inside poll(), and when a partition is revoked, records already processed but not yet committed move to another consumer (at-least-once duplicates), or committed-but-unprocessed ones vanish. CooperativeStickyAssignor requires a rolling restart of the WHOLE group (you can't mix eager and cooperative assignors simultaneously), while static membership without a carefully tuned `session.timeout.ms` instead freezes a dead pod's partitions until the timeout expires — processing stalls longer than a normal rebalance."},"x-ds-saga":{"q":"Why use a Saga instead of a distributed transaction across services?","a":"Microservices have no shared DB, so there's no single ACID commit across all of them. A Saga is a chain of local transactions: each service commits its own, and rollback is done via compensating actions (not a rollback, but a \"reverse\" action), not a two-phase commit.","d":"2PC (XA) is bad in a distributed system: it locks resources, the coordinator is a single point of failure, and it scales poorly. A Saga gives eventual consistency. Two styles: choreography (services react to each other's events — simple, but the logic is smeared around) and orchestration (a central orchestrator/Zeebe drives the steps — clearer, but you gain a coordinator). Compensation ≠ undo: the money has already moved → compensation is a \"refund,\" not \"as if it never happened.\" Intermediate states are visible — you need semantic locks or PENDING statuses.","hook":"A hotel booking in stages: payment failed — they call and cancel the room reservation, rather than \"rewinding time.\"","more":"A two-phase commit (2PC) requires the coordinator to hold all resources locked until a shared \"commit.\" In microservices this is **expensive and brittle**: different DBs, network failures, a hung coordinator blocks everyone. So distributed ACID is almost never used.\n\nA Saga breaks the process into **local transactions**: each service atomically commits its own and publishes an event / calls the next step. If a step fails — **compensations** run in reverse order (not a `rollback`, but an explicit \"cancel the reservation,\" \"refund the money\").\n\n- The price: no isolation — intermediate states are visible (eventual consistency).\n- Compensation must be designed deliberately; sometimes \"undoing\" is harder than \"doing.\"","quizCorrect":"Via a compensating action in the service","quizWrong":["Via a two-phase commit (2PC)","Via a ROLLBACK of a shared transaction","Via a rollback through a single ACID coordinator"],"picCap":"pay failed (red) → rollback runs backward, releasing stock","note":"Compensations themselves can fail (network/service down), so they must be idempotent and retry-able with a persistent log of steps, otherwise the saga hangs in a half-rolled-back state; in the orchestration style the engine (Zeebe/Camunda) handles this with durable instance state, but in choreography you must build retry/dead-letter yourself. A separate trap is an unreachable compensation: between a step and its rollback another service already read the intermediate state and made a decision (no isolation), so the compensation must be semantically correct (refund + notification), not 'roll back as if it never happened'."},"x-ds-idempotency-key":{"q":"How do you make a REST endpoint safe against retries (idempotency key)?","a":"The client sends a unique Idempotency-Key in the header. On the first request the server executes and stores the result under that key. On a retry with the same key — it returns the stored response without performing the action a second time. Protection against retries and double clicks.","d":"Why: the network is unreliable, the client retries, and with at-least-once you can charge money / create an order twice. The client generates the key (a UUID per \"operation,\" not per retry). Server: a UNIQUE constraint on idempotency_key + storing the response body. A race between two concurrent requests with the same key is caught via INSERT with a unique index (the second fails on conflict) or SELECT FOR UPDATE. This is the application level — distinct from a Kafka idempotent producer (which is about duplicates inside the broker).","hook":"A coat check: one ticket gets you one coat; show the ticket again — the same coat, no second one appears.","more":"The idea: a repeated operation should produce **the same result** as the first time, without a side effect happening twice. The client generates a unique key (a UUID) and sends it in `Idempotency-Key`. On the first request the server atomically stores `(key → result/status)`, performs the action, and returns the response; on a retry with the same key — it returns the stored response.\n\nWhy: networks are unreliable — a timeout doesn't mean \"it didn't run.\" Retries and double clicks must not create two payments.\n\n- **Pitfall:** the key must be reserved BEFORE execution (via an `INSERT` with a unique constraint), otherwise two concurrent requests slip through.\n- Store the key with a TTL and bind it to the request body to catch \"same key, different data.\"","quizCorrect":"Returns the stored response without performing the action again","quizWrong":["Performs the action again and logs a duplicate entry into the audit table","Rejects the request with an HTTP 409 error","Creates a new record with a new key"],"picCap":"The same key arrives twice: the 1st time it ran, the 2nd — it returned the cache","note":"A classic hole is writing the idempotency key and saving the result in DIFFERENT transactions from the business operation: if the service crashes between the business commit and storing the response, a retry sees 'no key' and executes the action a second time. The key, the operation state (IN_PROGRESS/DONE) and the effect itself must commit in one local transaction (or via outbox), and a concurrent second request with the same key while status is IN_PROGRESS must return 409/wait rather than silently skipping."},"p-architecture-quantum":{"q":"What is an \"architecture quantum,\" and why are two services sharing one DB a single quantum rather than two independent services?","a":"A quantum is an independently deployable piece with high cohesion AND its own data. If two services write to one DB — they share static coupling through the schema: change a table — fix both; the DB goes down — both go down. That's ONE quantum (a distributed monolith), even though it's two processes. You split the services but not the data — and got the worst of both: network calls plus a shared point of failure.","d":"Ownership = the sole WRITER of a table (many can read via API/replica). Single ownership is the default. Common (many write, e.g. audit) — hide it behind a single write service. Joint (several write) — the heaviest: table-split / data-domain / delegate / merge the services back.","hook":"Two apartments with one shared boiler aren't two houses. Shut off the boiler — both freeze.","more":"An **architecture quantum** is an independently deployable piece with high functional cohesion AND its own data. The keyword is \"its own.\"\n\nIf two services write to one DB, **static coupling** appears between them through the schema: change a table — fix both deployments; the DB goes down — both go down. They share one point of failure and one data contract, so it's ONE quantum — the classic distributed monolith.\n\n- The symptom: \"we split the processes but not the data\" → we got the worst of both worlds: network calls AND a shared point of failure.\n- To become two quanta — each gets its own DB (database-per-service), communicating only via API/events.","quizCorrect":"Shared static coupling through the schema: change a table — fix both","quizWrong":["Because they have a single deployment process","A quantum is counted by the number of processes, and here it's one single process","They become one quantum only with shared code, not a shared DB"],"picCap":"A shared DB glues the two services into one quantum","note":"Non-obvious: a quantum is defined not only by data but by COUPLING — two services with separate DBs yet synchronously coupled (one can't respond without a blocking call to the other, shared availability pattern) also form a single dynamic quantum. Conversely, a shared read-only replica (read-only access) does not merge quanta — static coupling arises specifically from schema WRITERS."},"p-saga-three-axes":{"q":"Along which three axes do the 8 saga patterns differ, and why is \"async + atomic\" almost always a bad choice?","a":"Three axes: communication (sync/async) × consistency (atomic/eventual) × coordination (orchestrated/choreographed) → 2×2×2 = 8 sagas. Atomic across services means \"all or nothing\" via two-phase atomicity — but async by nature breaks synchronous atomicity (responses come later, there's no \"commit everyone at once\" moment). That's why Fantasy Fiction and Horror Story (async+atomic) are wishful thinking, and they're avoided.","d":"Sweet spots: Fairy Tale (sync+eventual+orchestrated) — balanced; Parallel (async+eventual+orchestrated) — speed with central control; Anthology (async+eventual+choreographed) — max throughput, but debugging hell. If you need atomic across services — you've probably split too deep. Compensation ≠ rollback: emails/charges have already happened, so make the steps idempotent.","note":"Choreographed sagas on eventual consistency are treacherous because there's no explicit orchestrator: as the number of steps grows, the 'who triggers whom' logic gets smeared across services, and tracing/debugging the full chain and the failure point becomes nearly impossible — hence the pull back to orchestrated at >3-4 steps.","hook":"Async+atomic = \"send a letter and guarantee everyone reads it at the same time.\" That doesn't happen.","more":"The eight sagas are a cube of three binary axes: **communication** (sync/async) × **consistency** (atomic/eventual) × **coordination** (orchestrated/choreographed), 2×2×2 = 8.\n\n\"Atomic across services\" means \"all or nothing\" in one moment — effectively a distributed commit / two-phase atomicity. But **async** by nature spreads responses out in time: there's no shared \"commit everyone at once\" moment, participants reply whenever. Atomic and async pull in opposite directions.\n\n- That's why async+atomic (Fantasy Fiction, Horror Story) is wishful thinking and avoided in practice.\n- The workable middle for a distributed system is async + **eventual** + compensations: simpler, more fault-tolerant, but you have to live with temporary inconsistency.","quizCorrect":"Async by nature breaks synchronous atomicity: there's no shared commit moment","quizWrong":["Async is slower than sync, so atomic doesn't finish in time","Atomic requires an orchestrator, while async can only be choreography","Async is always eventual, and atomic across services is impossible altogether in practice"],"picCap":"3 binary axes → 8 saga combinations"},"p-outbox-relay-race":{"q":"The relay sent a message to Kafka/CDA but crashed BEFORE the UPDATE sent_at. What happens on restart, and how do you live with it?","a":"The row still has sent_at IS NULL → the relay will send it again. Outbox gives at-least-once, not exactly-once. Fixed not on the relay side but with an idempotent receiver: dedup by document_id/key. So outbox without an idempotent consumer is a no-go.","d":"Only save(doc)+save(outbox) in one transaction is atomic. The send itself + UPDATE sent_at are outside the original transaction, so the \"sent but not marked\" window is unavoidable. Option: UPDATE sent_at in the same transaction as the network call is impossible (you can't roll back a packet that already left). Hence the rule: relay is best-effort once, the receiver catches duplicates.","hook":"The courier handed over the parcel but didn't log it. After the shift change he'll hand over a second one — the recipient must recognize it's the same.","more":"The relay works in two operations: \"send outward\" and \"mark sent_at.\" If the process dies between them, the row stays **sent_at IS NULL**, and after restart the relay honestly sends it again. This isn't a bug but a fundamental property: outbox guarantees **at-least-once**, not exactly-once.\n\nWhy: you can't atomically combine \"commit to your own DB\" and \"acknowledgment from Kafka/CDA\" — they're two different resources with no shared transaction. So a duplicate is inevitable in principle.\n\n- Fixed **on the receiver side**: dedup by a stable key (document_id, business key), upsert instead of insert, an \"already seen\" check.\n- Pitfall: running outbox without an idempotent consumer — sooner or later a duplicate arrives and creates a second document/payment.","quizCorrect":"The row still has sent_at IS NULL — the relay will send it again (at-least-once)","quizWrong":["The message is lost because sent_at wasn't written in time","The relay automatically recognizes the duplicate and skips the row without any locking","Kafka itself deduplicates the repeated message by content"],"picCap":"Crash between send and UPDATE → a re-send after restart","note":"An idempotent consumer doesn't save you if the dedup key is taken from the payload while the relay duplicates the message under a different Kafka key, or if after a consumer rebalance the duplicates land in different partitions — then handlers on different partitions don't see each other's duplicate. The dedup key must be a business identifier (document_id), ideally also used as the Kafka message key, so a duplicate is guaranteed to land in the same partition."},"p-outbox-polling-vs-cdc":{"q":"The relay does SELECT WHERE sent_at IS NULL on a timer. What's the downside of this polling, and what does CDC (Debezium) offer?","a":"Polling = constant queries against a hot table + delivery lag up to the poll interval, plus the table swells and needs an index/cleanup. CDC (Debezium) reads not the table but the DB's WAL log: an insert into outbox immediately becomes an event in Kafka, with no polling and almost no lag.","d":"A polling relay is simple and needs no infrastructure — for a moderate flow (as in document-service) it's enough. CDC wins at high throughput: no polling load, lower latency, but it adds Debezium + Kafka Connect to the stack (harder to operate). Choose by volume, not by \"trendy/not trendy.\" In both cases you need a partial index on sent_at IS NULL, otherwise the SELECT scans everything.","note":"CDC via Debezium removes polling but adds its own pitfalls: on a connector failover/restart, already-sent changes may be republished (at-least-once at the CDC level), plus the initial snapshot of a large table can lock or load the DB for a long time on first start. Consumer idempotency is still mandatory.","hook":"Polling — calling every minute \"any parcel?\". CDC — the carrier calls you the moment it arrives.","more":"Polling is a timer running `SELECT WHERE sent_at IS NULL`. Downsides: constant queries against a **hot table**, delivery lag up to a whole poll interval, the table swells and needs an index on unsent rows + regular cleanup.\n\nCDC (Debezium) reads not the table but the database's **WAL/transaction log** — the journal of all changes. Inserting a row into outbox immediately becomes an event in Kafka, with no polling and almost no lag, and the load on the table itself doesn't grow.\n\n- When polling is enough: low flow, a couple seconds of lag isn't critical, you don't want to drag in Debezium/Kafka Connect.\n- CDC pitfalls: its own infrastructure (a connector, replication slots), harder operations, and on errors a replay is possible — the receiver still needs idempotency.","quizCorrect":"Reads the DB's WAL log — an insert immediately becomes an event in Kafka, with no polling","quizWrong":["Puts a trigger on the table that writes to Kafka itself","Polls the table more often, cutting delivery lag to a minimum and loading the database far more heavily","Caches the outbox in memory for instant reads by the relay"],"picCap":"Polling ticks with SELECTs on a timer; CDC flies from the WAL straight to Kafka"},"p-api-composition-latency":{"q":"A composer for \"document details\" hits order-service, agreement-service, tax. Why is calling them in parallel not enough — and what must you add?","a":"In parallel, latency = max(calls); sequentially = sum(). But fan-out is fragile: one slow/crashed service drags the whole response down with it. You must add a timeout on each call + a circuit breaker + a degradation strategy (a partial response or a fail), otherwise the composer hangs on the slowest one.","d":"Parallelism saves time (max instead of sum) but doesn't save you from tails: the P99 of one source = the P99 of the whole request. The CB opens the circuit to a downed service so you don't accumulate hung threads and cascade the failure. You decide product-wise: \"a document without the tax block beats a 500\" (degrade) or \"all or nothing\" (fail). Without a timeout the thread waits forever — the connection pool gets exhausted.","hook":"Assembling a parcel from three warehouses in parallel — but you wait on the slowest. With no deadline, one warehouse holds up the entire order.","more":"Parallel fan-out makes latency = **max(calls)** instead of sum(), but the price is fragility: the composer's response is ready only when all of them return, and one slow service sets the time for the whole request, while a crashed one topples the entire response.\n\nSo a single `CompletableFuture.allOf` isn't enough. You need to bound the worst case and isolate the failure: **a timeout on each call**, a **circuit breaker** (fail fast on calls to an already-down service instead of piling up hung threads), and an explicit **degradation strategy**.\n\n- Degradation: either a partial response (tax unavailable — return the document without the tax section, flagged) or an honest fail — decided by the business, not silently.\n- Pitfalls: without a timeout the thread hangs on the slowest one; without a breaker an avalanche of retries finishes off an already-overloaded downstream.","quizCorrect":"A timeout on each call + a circuit breaker + a degradation strategy","quizWrong":["A cache of all the services' responses so you don't call them again later on","Retries with exponential backoff on each call","More threads so the calls go even more in parallel"],"picCap":"A slow tax (pulsing) drags the whole response to 2.5s","note":"A timeout on the individual HTTP call does not actually cancel the hung work: if you wrap calls in CompletableFuture.supplyAsync, the timeout (orTimeout/completeOnTimeout) only completes the Future, while the background thread keeps holding the pooled connection until the client itself returns. True fail-fast comes only from a timeout at the HTTP-client level (request/connect timeout) plus a dedicated bounded pool for the fan-out, otherwise the shared ForkJoinPool.commonPool fills up with stuck tasks."},"p-kafka-eos-side-effects":{"q":"You have exactly-once in Kafka (transactions + read_committed). Is it safe to write to Postgres and make an HTTP call inside the consumer?","a":"No. A Kafka EOS transaction is atomic only for Kafka→process→Kafka (writing to topics + committing the offset). A write to Postgres and an HTTP call are NOT part of it — they'll repeat on a retry. They still need idempotency or an outbox.","d":"A Kafka transaction wraps only the produce to topics and sendOffsetsToTransaction. External side effects (DB, REST, sending an email) can't be rolled back by the broker. So \"but I have exactly-once\" is a false sense of security: consume-transform-produce is protected, but consume→write-to-DB isn't. The fix: an idempotency key in the DB (UNIQUE) or a transactional outbox, where the DB write and the \"intent to publish\" are in one local transaction.","hook":"The \"I won't drop it\" guarantee only holds inside your own truck — whatever you unload at someone else's warehouse, you can drop twice.","more":"Exactly-once in Kafka is a transaction atomic **only within the Kafka boundary**: writing to the output topics plus committing the input offset. `read_committed` merely hides uncommitted messages from readers.\n\nA write to Postgres and an HTTP call are **not part** of this transaction — they have no shared commit with Kafka. If processing repeats (a retry, a rebalance), the external effects run again: a second row in the DB, a second POST.\n\n- For side effects you need **idempotency** (upsert by key, dedup) or a **transactional outbox**: write to your own DB and hand off to Kafka via a separate relay.\n- Pitfall: \"but we have EOS\" lulls you — in reality the guarantee covers nothing beyond Kafka, and that's exactly where duplicates pile up.","quizCorrect":"No, the transaction is atomic only for Kafka→process→Kafka; for Postgres and HTTP you need idempotency or an outbox","quizWrong":["Yes, everything inside the consumer is in one atomic transaction and won't repeat on a retry, including any external calls","Yes, if you enable read_committed, the external writes will roll back automatically too","No, but wrapping the HTTP call in a try/catch is enough to keep it from repeating"],"picCap":"EOS holds Kafka↔Kafka, while Postgres/HTTP (red) are outside the tx → duplicates"},"p-kafka-poison-hol":{"q":"One 'poison' message in a partition keeps failing forever on processing. Why does the whole partition stall, and how do you resolve it without losing order?","a":"A partition is read strictly in order: until you commit the offset of the 'poisoned' message, the ones after it aren't processed — head-of-line blocking. The fix: after N retries, move the message to a retry topic with a delay, and a hopeless one — to a DLQ, then move on.","d":"Within a partition the offsets are monotonic and processed in sequence, so a stuck message blocks EVERYTHING after it (not just itself). A plain DLQ removes the blocker. But an instant dump to the DLQ loses the chance to recover from a transient error. The mature pattern is tiered retry topics: orders-retry-5s → orders-retry-1m → … → orders.DLT. A delayed retry doesn't hold the main partition. Into the candidate message we put headers: original topic/partition/offset, exception, attempt count. The DLT needs an alert on size/lag, otherwise it becomes a silent dumping ground.","note":"A retry topic with a delay breaks the ordering guarantee for the poisoned message's key: while it spins in retry, later messages with the same key from the main partition are already processed — you get per-entity out-of-order. For strict per-key ordering, the skip-to-DLQ approach can't be used.","hook":"One stalled car in a single-lane tunnel holds up everyone behind it — tow it to the shoulder (DLQ), and traffic flows.","more":"A partition is processed **strictly in offset order**: you can't commit the next message without committing the previous one. A 'poison' message that always fails therefore **holds the entire partition** — the classic head-of-line blocking, and all clients on those keys are stuck too.\n\nResolve it without losing order: after N local retries, take the message out of the main flow — into a **retry topic** with a delay (a deferred retry), and a finally hopeless one — into a **DLQ**, then move on down the partition.\n\n- This keeps order for the healthy messages, and one broken message doesn't paralyze the flow.\n- Pitfall: the global order for that key is still broken anyway (the broken one went \"sideways\"), so the DLQ needs an alert and triage — it's not a \"folder you dump things into and forget.\"","quizCorrect":"A partition is read strictly in order: without committing the failing message's offset, the ones after it aren't processed (head-of-line blocking)","quizWrong":["The broker blocks the partition as protection until an admin removes the lock manually","The consumer group goes into a permanent rebalance because of one failing message","The offset is automatically reset to the start of the partition, and reading loops from zero, losing every offset the group had committed before the failure"],"picCap":"Poison holds the queue; the retry → DLT path frees the partition"},"p-kafka-inflight-order":{"q":"You turned on producer retries for reliability — and suddenly messages within a partition got reordered. Why, and how do you fix it?","a":"Without idempotence, a re-sent batch can land AFTER the one that follows it (multiple in-flight requests) → reordering. Fix it with enable.idempotence=true (it numbers batches, and the broker restores order) as long as max.in.flight ≤ 5.","d":"max.in.flight.requests.per.connection > 1 means: batch N could fail and get retried while batch N+1 has already been sent and persisted. The broker writes N+1 before N → order is broken, even though nothing is lost. enable.idempotence=true assigns a (Producer ID, sequence) and the broker deduplicates AND preserves order across retries when in-flight ≤ 5. This is the same machinery behind 'exactly-once per partition for producer retries'. Without idempotence, the only way to keep order is max.in.flight=1 (slow).","note":"A common mistake is dropping to max.in.flight=1 just to preserve order, sacrificing throughput. With enable.idempotence=true order is guaranteed even at max.in.flight=5, because the broker reconstructs it from the sequence number. There's no need to lower in-flight to 1.","hook":"Numbered letters: if envelope #1 gets re-sent, it can land on top of #2 — the post office's numbering (idempotence) sorts them back into order.","more":"With `max.in.flight > 1` the producer keeps several unacknowledged batches in flight. If the first batch hits an error and is **retried**, it can reach the broker **after** the following one has already been accepted — and messages within the partition swap places.\n\nThe fix is `enable.idempotence=true`: the producer gets a PID and **numbers** its batches (sequence number); the broker accepts them strictly in ascending order, rejects duplicates, and restores order across retries. Works with `max.in.flight ≤ 5` and `acks=all`.\n\n- Bonus of the idempotent producer — it also removes duplicates on broker-side retries.\n- Gotcha: setting `retries>0` while leaving `max.in.flight=5` without idempotence — order silently drifts under load, reproduces only on failures/retries, and is painful to catch.","quizCorrect":"A re-sent batch can arrive after the one following it (multiple in-flight requests); enable.idempotence=true fixes it","quizWrong":["Retries send messages to a random partition, so order is lost","The broker sorts by timestamp, and retries bump the timestamp to a later value on the broker as it is written to the log","Order always breaks with retries; only setting acks=all fixes it"],"picCap":"Retried B1 trails B2 and lands in the wrong slot; idempotence fixes the order"},"p-kafka-minisr":{"q":"RF=3, acks=all. How many brokers can you lose without data loss and without halting writes? And why is min.insync.replicas=3 a bad idea?","a":"min.insync.replicas=2 + RF=3 + acks=all → you survive losing 1 broker: data is safe and the topic keeps accepting writes. If min.insync=RF=3, losing any single broker immediately drops ISR below min → writes block (NotEnoughReplicas). Too strict.","d":"acks=all means 'ack once the write reaches all ISR members, but no fewer than min.insync.replicas'. With RF=3/min.insync=2, after one broker dies the ISR still has 2 ≥ 2 → writes continue, no data lost. With min.insync=3, any missing broker makes ISR=2 < 3 → the producer gets an error and writes stall. Another invariant: unclean.leader.election.enable=false — otherwise a lagging replica can become leader and silently drop the tail of the data. Prod set: RF=3, min.insync=2, acks=all, enable.idempotence=true.","hook":"You need signatures from at least 2 of 3 witnesses: one's away — the deal still goes through; require all three — and any vacation shuts the office down.","more":"`acks=all` means 'the producer gets ok only once every replica in the ISR has confirmed the write' (in-sync replicas — the ones not lagging). And `min.insync.replicas` is the floor on ISR size below which writes are disallowed entirely.\n\n- RF=3 + min.insync=2: ISR=3, one broker dies → ISR=2 = the floor, writes continue, data safe on 2 nodes.\n- min.insync=3: any broker dies → ISR=2 < 3 → the producer hits **NotEnoughReplicas**, writes stall.\n\nBottom line: the 'survivability' formula = RF − min.insync. At (3,3) the headroom is 0 — you can't even survive a planned restart/deploy of one broker. (3,2) is the sweet spot: both durability and availability.","quizCorrect":"Losing any single broker immediately drops ISR below min → writes block (NotEnoughReplicas); the right value is min.insync.replicas=2","quizWrong":["min.insync=3 doubles write latency without improving reliability","With min.insync=3 consumers stop reading the topic until replicas recover","It's safe and recommended — data is more durable and writes aren't affected in any way at all, which is why it is the default in every cluster"],"picCap":"min.insync=2 leaves headroom for one broker failure","note":"min.insync.replicas is a broker/topic config, not a producer one: setting it only in producer code is useless — it doesn't exist client-side, and without a topic override the broker default (often 1) applies, collapsing acks=all into acks=1. There's also an asymmetry: when ISR<min only writes block, while reads of already-committed messages continue, so \"topic won't accept writes but still serves reads\" is normal, not contradictory."},"p-kafka-schema-compat":{"q":"You're changing an Avro event schema. Under BACKWARD compatibility, who do you roll out first — producers or consumers? And what can you never change?","a":"BACKWARD (the default): the new schema reads OLD data → roll out consumers first, then producers. FORWARD is the reverse — producers first. Safe: adding fields with a default. Forbidden: removing/renaming a required field, or changing a type.","d":"The Schema Registry stores schemas, and only a schema ID travels in the message → small payload, single contract, and producer/consumer deploys are decoupled. BACKWARD = the new consumer understands both new and old messages, so you roll it out first to survive the stream of old events. FORWARD = the old consumer understands new messages → producers roll out first. FULL = any order. In practice: add a field with a default — safe everywhere; remove a field — FORWARD only; add a required field — breaks BACKWARD; change a field's type — breaks everything.","note":"BACKWARD compatibility by default only checks against the latest schema, not the whole history — for a consumer to read data from any older version you need BACKWARD_TRANSITIVE. Plain BACKWARD will let through a breaking chain of several consecutive evolutions.","hook":"BACKWARD = 'look back': a new hire first learns to read the old paperwork — so they're the one you hire first.","more":"The Schema Registry checks compatibility so old and new code versions can coexist during a rollout. **BACKWARD** means: code with the NEW schema can read data written with the OLD schema.\n\n- So under BACKWARD you roll out **consumers** first (the new ones already understand both old and new), and only then producers — otherwise new messages reach old consumers that can't parse them.\n- FORWARD is the mirror image: the old schema reads new data, so it's safe to update producers first.\n\nGotcha: you can only safely add a field WITH a `default`. Removing/renaming a required field or changing a type breaks compatibility — the Registry will reject registration.","quizCorrect":"Consumers first, then producers — the new schema reads old data","quizWrong":["Producers first, then consumers — otherwise old data can't be read","Order doesn't matter: BACKWARD guarantees compatibility both ways","Update the Schema Registry first, then producers and consumers simultaneously"],"picCap":"The compatibility mode dictates the rollout order"},"p-idempotent-sametx":{"q":"How do you make a Kafka consumer reliably idempotent? Why is 'I'll check with a SELECT first, then process' not enough?","a":"Check-then-act has a race: two duplicates pass the 'not found' SELECT at the same time → both get processed. The right way: in ONE transaction, INSERT the message_id into processed_messages (UNIQUE PK) plus the business record. A duplicate hits the uniqueness conflict → rollback, message ignored.","d":"Kafka is at-least-once → duplicates are inevitable (retries, rebalance before the offset commit). A separate SELECT followed by processing is a TOCTOU race: a second duplicate slips in between the check and the insert. The DB itself provides atomicity: a PK/UNIQUE on (consumer_id, message_id). Put the id insert, the business change, and the outbox write in one transaction — all or nothing. A repeat message_id → uniqueness violation → ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING / rollback, message silently dropped. The alternative without a separate table — a UNIQUE on the record's own business key (like tax_report_hst_usd in prod).","note":"INSERTing message_id into processed_messages only works if it's in the SAME transaction as the business record and in the same DB. If the business effect is an external HTTP call or a different DB, there's no transactional atomicity, and the dedup table won't save you from a partial replay.","hook":"At the entrance they stamp the log by ticket number: show up with the same number and you're rejected at the stamp — not by a guard who 'doesn't recall seeing you'.","more":"The problem is a **race condition**. The 'SELECT → not found → process' check is two steps with a gap between them. Two duplicates of a message arrive at once, both SELECT and both see 'empty' → both proceed and duplicate the effect.\n\nThe fix is to push the check down into the DB via a **UNIQUE** constraint:\n\n- In one transaction: `INSERT message_id` into `processed_messages` (PK/unique) plus the business record.\n- The second duplicate's INSERT hits a uniqueness violation → the whole transaction rolls back atomically, no effect.\n\nThe key is that atomicity and uniqueness are guaranteed by the DB engine, not by your code between two queries.","quizCorrect":"Race: two duplicates pass the 'not found' SELECT at once and both get processed; you need an INSERT of message_id with UNIQUE in one transaction","quizWrong":["SELECT is slow, so it's better to cache the check in application memory — otherwise the consumer simply cannot keep up with the incoming stream of messages at peak","A SELECT with FOR UPDATE is enough — it guarantees insert uniqueness","Check-then-act works if you raise the isolation level to READ COMMITTED"],"picCap":"The msg goes to an INSERT under UNIQUE: new is processed, dup hits the conflict"},"p-kafka-ack-after-dbwrite":{"q":"Consumer: ack the message first, then write to the DB — or the other way around? Why is the order critical?","a":"Write to the DB first, then ack. If you ack first and the DB write fails, the message is marked processed but its effect is gone → the event is lost forever. DB-first: worst case is a duplicate, which idempotence handles.","d":"You can't atomically commit both the DB transaction and the Kafka offset (two systems with no shared transaction). The pragmatic DDIA rule: write-then-ack. The failure window between them yields at most reprocessing (at-least-once), not loss. Calling ack() inside a @Transactional callback is a bug: if the transaction rolls back, the offset has already moved and can't be taken back.","hook":"Put the money in the till first, then tear the receipt. Tear it early and the money's gone without a trace.","more":"An ack (offset commit) is a promise to Kafka: 'I've processed this message, don't send it again'. The order determines what happens on a crash right between the two steps.\n\n- **Ack → DB**, crash in between: the offset has moved but there's no DB record. Kafka won't resend → **silent data loss**, forever.\n- **DB → ack**, crash in between: the record exists but the offset hasn't moved → after restart the message comes again → a duplicate.\n\nA duplicate is curable with idempotence (see processed_messages); loss is not. So always 'effect first, acknowledgment after': this is the at-least-once + idempotence pattern = effectively exactly-once.","quizCorrect":"Write to the DB first, then ack: otherwise a crash during the write leaves the message marked processed and the event is lost","quizWrong":["Ack first, then DB: that way the consumer won't hang if the DB is temporarily unavailable and the message can always be replayed later","Order doesn't matter with auto-commit on, the offset syncs up anyway","Ack first: redelivery is more dangerous than loss and harder to detect"],"picCap":"The correct DB→ack order lights up step by step; ack-first leads to loss","note":"With enable.auto.commit=true (the KafkaConsumer default) the \"DB then ack\" ordering is an illusion: poll() periodically commits offsets in the background per auto.commit.interval.ms, so the offset advances before the DB write on its own. The write-then-ack guarantee holds only with manual control — enable.auto.commit=false and an explicit commitSync after the successful DB transaction (in Quarkus/SmallRye, the throttled/latest strategy with explicit ack())."},"p-kafka-autocommit-loses":{"q":"What's dangerous about enable.auto.commit=true on a Kafka consumer?","a":"Auto-commit advances the offset on a timer, regardless of whether processing succeeded. Crash mid-work after an auto-commit and the records are marked read but unprocessed → loss. Turn auto-commit off and commit manually after success.","d":"The at-least-once guarantee only holds if the offset is committed AFTER successful processing. Auto-commit breaks this: the timer knows nothing about your business logic. In SmallRye — commit-strategy=throttled and ack() only after the business transaction + outbox have gone through. A poison message (always fails) goes to a dead-letter after N retries, otherwise it gets stuck on it forever.","hook":"A timer-driven cleaner marks 'done' by the alarm clock, even if you never reached the corner. The dirt stays, the checkmark's there.","more":"With `enable.auto.commit=true` the client commits the offset in the background on a timer (`auto.commit.interval.ms`, 5 sec by default) — **regardless of whether you successfully processed** the message. The commit is tied to time, not to your business logic.\n\n- Loss scenario: `poll()` returns a batch → auto-commit fires (offset already advanced) → you crash mid-processing → after restart Kafka considers those messages read and won't resend them.\n\nThe right way: `enable.auto.commit=false` and `commitSync()` strictly AFTER successful processing and the DB write. Then a crash yields reprocessing (a duplicate), not a hole in the data.","quizCorrect":"The offset advances on a timer with no regard for success; a crash after auto-commit marks records processed → loss","quizWrong":["Auto-commit commits after every message, which sharply tanks throughput and makes the consumer far slower than it needs to be","Auto-commit leads to duplicates, but it can't lose messages","Auto-commit blocks the group rebalance until the current poll finishes"],"picCap":"The timer blinks and commits blindly; a crash mid-way → the record is lost","note":"Counterintuitive: even with enable.auto.commit=true the loss happens not on a crash but on an ordinary rebalance — auto-commit flushes the offset inside poll() BEFORE returning the next batch, so if processing of the previous batch isn't finished by the next poll() the offset moves ahead. Manual commitSync() isn't a silver bullet either: when committing per-partition, make sure you commit offset+1 of the last processed record (Kafka stores 'next to read', not 'last read')."},"p-stream-event-vs-proc-time":{"q":"Why is stamping timestamps via time.Now()/new Date() in a service a landmine, and what do you use instead?","a":"Processing-time (when you received it) lies under lag, redelivery, and replay. Different instances' clocks drift by tens of ms. For comparisons in the DB, use a single clock — the DB server's DEFAULT now(), or event-time from the payload, not a client timestamp.","d":"Event time = when the event actually happened (in the payload); processing time = when the consumer received it. On a backlog replay, processing-time windows compute wrong, event-time computes right. A client timestamp from a device is especially unreliable (clock skew, NTP drift). Deadlines and counters that later get compared in the DB should always come from a single time source — Postgres's server clock.","note":"Even event-time from the payload needs a watermark / allowed-lateness mechanism for windowed aggregations: late events can arrive after the window closes and be silently dropped. And a DB-side DEFAULT now() saves you from clock skew, but not from the case where you specifically need the moment of the business event, not the moment of the write.","hook":"Everyone in the office has their own clock. Set the deadline by the clock on the front-gate wall — one for all.","more":"There are two distinct times. **Event-time** — when the event actually happened (sits in the payload). **Processing-time** — when your service got to it (`now()` in code). They diverge under queue lag, retries, and history replays.\n\n- `time.Now()`/`new Date()` reads the clock of one specific instance. Across pods, NTP wanders by tens of ms → events 'from the future' or 'from the past', breaking order and time-based comparisons.\n\nRule: for consistency, use ONE time source. For DB timestamps — a server-side `DEFAULT now()` (one clock for all records). For business logic — event-time from the payload. A client timestamp can't be trusted.","quizCorrect":"Processing-time lies under lag, redelivery, and replay, and instance clocks drift; use the DB's DEFAULT now() or event-time from the payload","quizWrong":["new Date() returns local time without a timezone; switching to UTC is enough","The only problem is the cost of the call, so it's better to cache the current time","A client timestamp is more accurate than the server's, you just round it to seconds and the difference between the two clocks disappears completely"],"picCap":"Instance clocks lie — use a single DB server clock."},"p-cb-timeout":{"q":"You put @CircuitBreaker on a call to a slow service, yet threads still hang and the service goes down. Why didn't the CB save you?","a":"Because there's no timeout. A CB counts ERRORS, and a hung call isn't an error — it just hangs. Without @Timeout the thread waits forever, the thread pool fills up, and the CB never even gets a chance to open. A timeout turns 'hang' into an error the CB can count.","d":"The rule from Release It!: a Circuit Breaker always pairs with a Timeout. The Timeout gives a fast fail (hang → exception), and the CB opens on those errors and stops hammering the dead downstream. Without a timeout both are useless — cascading failure via thread exhaustion. You also need a fallback for the Open state.","note":"An extra catch: @Timeout in MicroProfile runs the call on a separate thread and interrupts it via interrupt — if the code doesn't react to InterruptedException (e.g., stuck in a native socket with no socket timeout), the thread won't be freed. You also need a real timeout at the HTTP-client/driver level.","hook":"A breaker without a timeout is like a fuse that can't sense the wire is merely heating up rather than arcing.","more":"A Circuit Breaker trips once it accumulates enough **errors/failures**. But a hung call isn't an error: the thread just stands there waiting for a response that never comes. The CB has nothing to count, so it never opens.\n\n- Without `@Timeout` every slow request holds a thread. Under load the pool fills with 'hangers' → new requests aren't served → cascading failure.\n\nThat's why @Timeout and @CircuitBreaker work as a pair: the timeout forcibly turns a 'hang' into a `TimeoutException`, and the CB counts that error and, past the threshold, trips the circuit, giving the dependency a breather. A timeout is a precondition for the CB to fire at all.","quizCorrect":"No @Timeout: a hung call isn't an error, the CB doesn't count it and never opens, and the pool fills up","quizWrong":["A CB only reacts to downstream exceptions, but the slow service returns 200 OK","The failure threshold was set too high, so the CB just never gathered enough statistics to open at all to work with","A CB only works for synchronous REST calls, but this was a reactive chain"],"picCap":"A timeout turns a 'hang' into an error — only then does the CB fire"},"p-cb-halfopen":{"q":"The CB has opened (Open). How does it learn the downstream has recovered without sending all traffic into a dead service?","a":"Via the Half-Open state. After a delay the CB lets through a FEW probe requests. Success → it closes (Closed) and traffic resumes. Failure → back to Open and another delay. This way the downstream gets a 'breather' and isn't drowned by all the traffic at once during recovery.","d":"Three states: Closed (counting errors) → after N errors Open (instant fail without calling) → after a delay Half-Open (probes). The subtlety: if Half-Open lets through ALL traffic and the service is still fragile, you knock it down again. So you probe with a small portion. Tuning the thresholds (requestVolumeThreshold, failureRatio, delay) is the main pain: too sensitive = false positives, too lenient = no protection.","hook":"After a fire you don't switch on every appliance at once — you flip one and watch whether the breaker trips again.","more":"After the delay the CB doesn't fling the gate fully open but moves to **Half-Open** — it lets through just a couple of probe requests and watches the result.\n\nThe point: you can't 'guess' the downstream is back without sending it real traffic. But if you slam all the accumulated load onto a barely-recovered service, it falls over again. Probe requests give a gentle check without finishing it off.\n\n- Probes succeed → **Closed**, traffic resumes.\n- Probe fails → back to **Open** and a new delay countdown.\n- Gotcha: while in Half-Open, it's important to cap the number of probe requests (often 1), otherwise the probe itself becomes a mini-dogpile.","quizCorrect":"Half-Open: after a delay it lets through a few probe requests; success → Closed, failure → back to Open","quizWrong":["It pings the downstream with a background health-check and closes when it answers 200 to the probe request","It waits a fixed delay and immediately goes fully Closed, letting all traffic through","It listens for a recovery event the downstream itself publishes to the bus on startup"],"picCap":"Half-Open (pulsing) lets probes through, not all the traffic","note":"Half-Open concurrency pitfall: while a probe is in flight, all other calls must fail fast, otherwise with a 30s downstream timeout you'd let hundreds of 'probes' through during that window. In Resilience4j this is governed by permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState, and note: its Open→Half-Open transition is lazy (on the next call after wait-duration elapses), not driven by a background timer — with no traffic the CB can stay Open forever."},"p-edm-notification":{"q":"You send a 'thin' event (just an id: 'order changed'), and the consumer calls the producer back for the details. What did you quietly reintroduce into the system?","a":"Temporal coupling. A thin notification forces the consumer to make a synchronous round-trip to the producer for the data — meaning the producer MUST be alive at processing time. Producer goes down → the consumer stalls. This is exactly what you moved to events to escape.","d":"Two styles: Notification (id + 'what happened', needs a callback/RPC) vs Event-Carried State Transfer — the event carries the full state, the consumer needs no one. ECST is the default decoupling backbone: it removes the runtime dependency on the producer, and the consumer builds a local read-model. Notification is justified when the payload is huge/sensitive or the event is rare.","hook":"A text saying 'come to the cabinet' is useless if the cabinet's locked. Better to send the contents straight away — take it and use it.","more":"You sent a notification event with no data, so the consumer is forced to synchronously call the producer back for the details — and **temporal coupling** reappears: both services must be alive at the same time.\n\nThis rolls back the main benefit of events — decoupling in time. The producer goes down or slows → the consumer stalls too, even though it already received the event.\n\n- The fix is **event-carried state transfer**: put the data itself (the needed snapshot) into the event, and the consumer processes it autonomously.\n- When a thin event is OK: the payload is huge, you need freshness 'as of read time', or there are privacy requirements — then you knowingly pay with coupling.","quizCorrect":"Temporal coupling: the producer must be alive at processing time; it goes down — the consumer stalls","quizWrong":["Data duplication: the same fields are now stored both at the producer and the consumer in full on both sides","Loss of event order, since thin notifications can't be ordered by key","A broken idempotence, because the event id doesn't guarantee unique delivery"],"picCap":"A thin event = a hidden synchronous call back (red)","note":"The flip side people forget: ECST removes temporal coupling but introduces a staleness/ordering problem — the consumer builds a read-model from snapshots, and with out-of-order delivery (different partitions/retries) a newer event can be overwritten by an older one. The fix isn't just 'put data in the event' but a version/timestamp in the payload plus upsert logic 'apply only if version is greater than current'; also event size grows and sensitive fields risk leaking to every subscriber."},"p-edm-compaction":{"q":"A new service joins 2 years later. How does it get the CURRENT state of all entities without reading billions of old events?","a":"Via a log-compacted entity stream. It's a stream keyed by entity id where the broker keeps only the LATEST value per key (old ones get cleaned up). The new consumer replays it from offset 0 → quickly builds a 'table' snapshot, then tails the live stream. Deletion = a tombstone (key→null).","d":"Two kinds of streams. Event stream: every event, time-based retention → audit/analytics/sagas. Entity stream: latest-per-key, compacted → a rebuildable 'table'/materialized snapshot, this is the read-side of CQRS. Compaction stores no history, only the current value per key — which makes bootstrap cheap. That's how a system 'joins in' years later without touching the source.","hook":"Not the whole house correspondence, just each resident's current address — old entries get erased.","more":"The solution is a **log-compacted** stream keyed by entity id. The broker (Kafka) keeps only the latest value for each key and physically cleans up old versions via compaction.\n\nSo a new consumer replays from offset 0 and quickly assembles a current 'table' snapshot (one record per entity), then switches to reading the live tail.\n\n- Deleting an entity = a **tombstone**: a key→null record, which compaction eventually removes.\n- Gotcha: a key is mandatory (without it compaction doesn't work); retention is configured separately from compaction; and mind delete.retention for races with the tombstone.","quizCorrect":"A log-compacted stream keyed by id: the broker keeps only the latest value, and a replay from offset 0 gives a snapshot","quizWrong":["Request a full REST snapshot of all entities from the producer in one batch at startup","Read only the events within the retention window, since the broker already dropped the old ones by TTL and cannot be restored","Subscribe from offset latest and gradually rebuild state from new events"],"picCap":"Compaction keeps only the latest value per key","note":"Compaction subtlety: it does NOT guarantee a new consumer won't see duplicates or intermediate versions of a key — only the 'tail' is compacted (segments older than the active segment and past min.cleanable.dirty.ratio), so fresh records in the active segment stay unconsolidated, and bootstrap logic must be idempotent (last-write-wins per key). A tombstone isn't eternal either: it's removed after delete.retention.ms (default 24h), so a consumer replaying the stream later may never see the deletion at all — a 'resurrected' deleted entity."},"p-edm-schema":{"q":"You're adding a field to an event. How do you NOT break consumers that haven't updated yet (and vice versa)?","a":"Maintain compatibility. Backward (new code reads old data) — new fields must be optional with a default, don't remove/rename required ones. Forward (old code reads new data) — the old code just ignores unknown fields. Full = both at once, needed for a shared backbone where producer and consumers roll out independently.","d":"An event is a contract ('data on the outside'), not a private JSON structure. Schema registry + Avro/Protobuf/JSON-Schema, the schema id in the record for deterministic deserialization. A breaking change (removed/renamed a required field, changed a type) ⇒ a new event version/new topic, dual-publish, consumer migration, retire the old. You can't silently break the wire — things roll out independently.","hook":"A form with a new field: whoever fills it in, fills it in; whoever doesn't, gets processed anyway. But removing an old field = breaking everyone.","more":"What saves you is **schema-compatibility** discipline, because producer and consumers roll out at different times and you can't rebuild them all atomically.\n\n- **Backward** (new code reads old data): new fields optional with a default; never remove or rename anything required.\n- **Forward** (old code reads new data): add things so the old code just ignores unknown fields.\n- **Full** = both at once, needed for a shared event backbone.\n\nGotcha: changing a field's type/semantics breaks both compatibilities — that's already a new event or a new version. A Schema Registry with a check on publish catches this before rollout.","quizCorrect":"Compatibility: new fields optional with a default, nothing removed/renamed, old code ignores the unknown","quizWrong":["Bump the topic version and force all consumers to migrate at the same time","Make the new field required with a broker-side default via the Schema Registry for every old consumer reading the topic","Send both event formats in parallel until all consumers switch over"],"picCap":"Adding is allowed, removing/renaming is not"},"p-cqrs-staleness":{"q":"After writing a command, the user immediately does a GET — and doesn't see their own changes. Is this a CQRS bug or expected?","a":"Expected. In CQRS the read-model is a separate model, populated asynchronously from the write side's events. There's replication lag: the write went through, but the read-side projection hasn't caught up yet. This is eventual consistency, not a bug. CQRS does NOT give 'read your own writes' out of the box.","d":"The write-model holds invariants; the read-model is denormalized for queries and updated by subscribing to domain events → a sync gap between them. Fixes: return the result straight from the command (don't hit the read-model immediately), read fresh from the write side for critical cases, versions/ETag for 'wait for version N', or just accept the lag in the UI. This is exactly why Fowler says 'be very cautious about CQRS' — a team with no eventual-consistency experience gets burned here.","note":"The crutch of 'read from the write side after a write for read-your-writes' breaks the whole idea of CQRS and doesn't scale. The proper techniques: a version token/timestamp in the command response that the read side waits to catch up to, or an optimistic UI update from the command response rather than from the read-model.","hook":"You transferred money — the bank debited it, but the app still shows the old balance. Not a bug, the view is just catching up.","more":"This is **expected**, not a bug. In CQRS the read-model is separate from the write-model and is populated asynchronously — the projection is built from events/changes after the command commits.\n\nBetween 'command written' and 'projection updated' there's **replication lag**, so the system is only **eventually consistent**. There's no 'read-your-own-writes' guarantee out of the box.\n\n- Workarounds: on a read right after a write, temporarily hit the write side; hand the client a version/token and wait for the projection to catch up; or optimistically render the result on the front end.\n- Gotcha: if the business genuinely needs strong read consistency — maybe CQRS doesn't belong here at all.","quizCorrect":"Expected: the read-model is populated asynchronously, there's projection lag — eventual consistency, not a bug","quizWrong":["A bug: the command must synchronously update the read-model before returning the response to the client that made it","A bug: read and write should hit one DB, otherwise CQRS is implemented wrong","Expected, but fixed by enabling strong-consistency mode on the event bus"],"picCap":"The window between the write and the projection update = a stale read"},"p-stab-slow-vs-fail":{"q":"Why is a slow response more dangerous than an explicit error?","a":"An error frees the thread immediately. A slow response holds your thread busy for the entire wait. Under load every thread hangs on the slow dependency → the pool is empty → the service is dead, even though the CPU is idle. Better to fail fast than to slow down.","d":"Slow Responses propagate up the stack: a dependency slows → your threads block (Blocked Threads) → your caller hangs too (Cascading Failure). The user hits refresh → even more load. The cure: a timeout on EVERY blocking call; treat 'too slow' as an error; an SLA on latency, not just availability. Fail Fast = check health/breaker/resources BEFORE the expensive work and reject immediately.","hook":"Better to get turned away at the register right away than to stand 40 minutes in a frozen line.","more":"An error returns instantly and **frees the thread immediately** (and the connection back to the pool). A slow response keeps that thread busy for the whole wait.\n\nUnder load this is lethal: requests pile up faster than they finish, every thread/connection hangs on the slow dependency → the pool is empty → the service accepts NOTHING, even though the CPU is idle. One dependency's slowdown cascades the whole service down.\n\n- So **fail fast**: hard timeouts, a circuit breaker, concurrency limits.\n- Gotcha: your timeout must be shorter than the caller's, otherwise the client bails first while you're still holding the resource.","quizCorrect":"An error frees the thread at once, while a slow one keeps it busy; under load the pool empties — the service is dead","quizWrong":["A slow response more often leads to data loss, while an error just rolls back the transaction","An error gets retried by the client and amplifies load, while a slow response is just waited on silently by the caller","A slow response bypasses the CB and logs, so it can't be detected by monitoring"],"picCap":"A slowdown holds threads (hanging); fail fast releases them","note":"The main timeout trap in Java is that most 'timeouts' do NOT abort a stuck thread: HttpURLConnection/Socket give you connectTimeout and readTimeout, but a read between packets can hang forever, and a JDBC call ignores Thread.interrupt() entirely while still holding the pool connection. You need a separate statement/query timeout on the driver (PgJDBC socketTimeout, Postgres statement_timeout) — otherwise your circuit breaker opens but the physical thread stays blocked until the server replies."},"p-stab-dogpile-jitter":{"q":"Why is a cron at :00 on every node plus an identical cache TTL a self-inflicted wound, and where does jitter come in?","a":"When a crowd of actors does the same thing AT THE SAME instant, you get a load spike (dogpile). Cron exactly at :00, all TTLs expiring together, all clients reconnecting after an outage. The fix: jitter — give each one a random offset to spread the actions over time.","d":"Dogpile = a synchronous, self-induced spike. Typical sources: scheduled periodics, mass reconnect after an outage, simultaneous config-reload. The cure: jitter (a random offset) on every periodic action; staggered/randomized TTLs; exponential backoff ALWAYS with jitter (otherwise retries re-synchronize); request coalescing / single-flight on cache fill (one node warms it, the rest wait for the result) to avoid a cache stampede.","hook":"Everyone leaves work at 18:00 → traffic jam. Spread it over 17:40–18:20 → it flows freely.","more":"The problem is **synchronicity**: when many actors do the same thing in the same millisecond, it stacks into a sharp spike — a **dogpile** (thundering herd).\n\nClassic cases: cron exactly at :00 on every node, an identical cache TTL (everything expires at once), mass reconnect after a failure. The system handles the average load, but not the synchronous burst.\n\n- The cure is **jitter**: give each one a random offset (±N seconds on cron, randomness on TTL, exponential backoff with spread on retries).\n- The goal is to smear the actions over time and flatten the spike. Gotcha: don't forget jitter specifically on retries, otherwise clients re-synchronize themselves after an error.","quizCorrect":"Everyone acts at once → a load spike (dogpile); jitter adds a random offset that smears the load out","quizWrong":["Cron at :00 throws off NTP time sync; jitter compensates for clock drift between nodes","An identical TTL overflows the cache; jitter is a random record size for even eviction","The problem is the race for the cron lock; jitter randomly picks the leader node for the task each time"],"picCap":"One spike vs a steady flow","note":"A non-obvious self-inflicted wound is 'full jitter vs equal jitter': classic exponential backoff with symmetric jitter (delay ± random) still keeps a common center across all retries, so the herd is only slightly smeared. The AWS recommendation is full jitter: sleep = random(0, cap·2^attempt), i.e. a fully random interval from zero — otherwise after a shared outage clients re-synchronize on the very next wave."},"p-stab-backpressure-bounded":{"q":"Why must every queue be bounded, and what is back pressure?","a":"An unbounded queue grows under overload, eats memory, and eventually OOMs — you just deferred the crash. A bounded queue, when full, SLOWS/blocks the producer — that's back pressure: a 'slow down' signal travels up the pipeline instead of piling up endlessly.","d":"A full queue → one of three choices: block (back pressure), shed (drop), reject (503). Across a network boundary back pressure needs a protocol: Handshaking, HTTP 503 + Retry-After, flow control. The combo: bounded queue + Shed Load at the edge + Handshaking. The antithesis — an Unbounded Result Set: a SELECT without LIMIT, an ORM lazily loading 10M rows, an unbounded batch. Always put a LIMIT on the server, don't trust the client.","hook":"A funnel with a narrow neck: pour faster and it overflows, so you pour exactly as much as drains out.","more":"An unbounded queue under overload simply grows, eats memory, and runs out into an **OOM** — you didn't solve the problem, you deferred the crash and made it sudden.\n\nA **bounded queue**, when full, doesn't silently accept new items but slows or blocks the producer. That's **back pressure**: a 'slow down' signal travels up the pipeline to the load source, instead of accumulating forever.\n\n- This makes overload visible and manageable: you can throttle, shed (load shedding), or return 429.\n- Gotcha: blocking the producer can push pressure all the way to the client — you also need a timeout/policy for a full queue, otherwise the sender itself hangs.","quizCorrect":"An unbounded one grows to OOM under overload; a bounded one blocks the producer — that's the signal to slow down going upstream","quizWrong":["The limit is for FIFO order; back pressure is prioritizing important messages in the queue by their declared business importance","An unbounded one loses messages; back pressure is automatic retry of failed items","The limit sets message TTL; back pressure is dropping old items when full"],"picCap":"The queue is full (slots lit) → pressure back onto the source","note":"A subtlety with ThreadPoolExecutor: a bounded queue doesn't behave as expected — the pool spawns threads up to maximumPoolSize ONLY once the queue is full, so with corePoolSize<max and a large queue the extra threads never start and tasks just pile up. And 'blocking the producer' isn't built in: the default AbortPolicy throws RejectedExecutionException — for real back pressure you need CallerRunsPolicy or a custom RejectedExecutionHandler that blocks on queue.put()."},"p-saga-compensation":{"q":"Why is a compensating transaction in a saga NOT a rollback?","a":"In a DB, a rollback wipes changes as if they never happened. In a saga each step is already committed in its own service — you can't 'undo' it. A compensation is a NEW business action that logically neutralizes the past (not 'un-generate' the PDF, but mark it 'voided' / issue a storno).","d":"A saga = a chain of local transactions, each committing immediately. No 2PC, so no atomic rollback. Properties of compensations: semantic (a storno entry, canceling a booking), often NOT fully reversible (the email already went out), must be idempotent, and can themselves fail → retryability is mandatory. A saga isn't isolated: between steps others see the intermediate state (business-level dirty reads) → you need semaphores/PENDING statuses. In practice orchestration runs on Zeebe: BPMN describes the steps and compensations, and Zeebe holds the process state.","hook":"The payment already went out — you don't 'erase' the money, you make a reverse transfer (storno).","more":"In a DB a **rollback** is possible because the changes aren't committed yet — they're literally wiped, as if they never were. In a saga each step is already **committed** in its own service and visible to others; there's no 'transaction to undo'.\n\nSo a compensation is a **new business action** that logically neutralizes the past effect rather than deleting it. Not 'un-generate' the PDF, but mark it 'voided'; not erase the entry, but issue a **storno**.\n\n- Consequence: compensations must themselves be idempotent and able to fire in any rollback order.\n- Gotcha: not everything is compensable (the email already went out) — put such steps last or plan for manual handling.","quizCorrect":"Each step is already committed in its own service; a compensation is a new business action that neutralizes the past","quizWrong":["Because a rollback in a saga does run, but only at the orchestrator level, not the services'","Because a compensation rolls back all steps at once via a distributed 2PC transaction","Because a compensation only logs the error, while the real changes are rolled back by the DB itself on its own afterwards"],"picCap":"Forward by commits, back by compensating steps (not a rollback)","note":"In Zeebe/BPMN compensation is NOT an automatic state rollback: it runs only if the step explicitly declares a compensation boundary event with an attached compensation task, and fires only when a compensation throw event is raised (or an error caught by the matching handler). A common mistake is expecting a failed process to 'roll itself back': without explicitly modeled compensating tasks, Zeebe simply halts the instance as an incident, leaving the already-committed steps untouched."},"p-trace-propagation-islands":{"q":"Why does a trace_id generated in a service but not propagated into outgoing calls turn the trace into 'islands'?","a":"A trace is tied together by a shared trace_id that flows in headers (W3C traceparent in HTTP, headers in Kafka). If you generate the ID locally and don't put it into the outgoing REST/Kafka, every next service starts a NEW root span unrelated to yours. Instead of one waterfall chain you get a set of disjoint islands, and you can't see where it's slow.","d":"A span is a unit of work in one service; spans link parent-child via trace_id + span_id, forming a tree for one request. Mandatory span fields: trace_id, span_id, parent_id, timestamp, duration. Quarkus OpenTelemetry auto-instruments JAX-RS/JDBC/Kafka and propagates traceparent itself. But through Zeebe/Camunda the context is NOT propagated automatically — you put trace_id into the process variables manually, otherwise the chain breaks at the orchestrator. A local random UUID that goes nowhere is useless for a distributed trace.","note":"A common cause of 'islands' even with a tracing library in place — async boundaries: when work moves to a thread pool, @Async, a reactive chain, or a Kafka consumer, the trace context isn't carried with the task automatically and is lost on the thread switch. You need explicit context propagation via executor/messaging instrumentation.","hook":"A relay race: if you don't pass the baton (trace_id), every runner starts their own race, and there's no combined result.","more":"A trace is a set of spans glued by one **trace_id** that physically travels in the request's headers: **W3C traceparent** in HTTP, headers in a Kafka message. The receiver reads the header and makes its spans children of yours.\n\nIf you generated trace_id only locally (for logs) and didn't put it into the outgoing REST/Kafka — the neighbor has no header. It considers itself the start of the chain and spins up a **new root span** with a different trace_id.\n\n- Result: instead of a single waterfall — disjoint 'islands', and the end-to-end latency can't be assembled.\n- The fix isn't manual propagation but auto-instrumentation (an OTel agent) that injects/extracts the context at every boundary.","quizCorrect":"The trace_id wasn't propagated into the outgoing calls' headers","quizWrong":["The trace_id was generated too long for an HTTP header","Every service is required to generate its own trace_id by the standard","Kafka can't carry a trace_id, unlike HTTP"],"picCap":"No traceparent → islands instead of a chain"},"g-ds-compaction":{"q":"How does log compaction differ from retention, and why do you need a tombstone (value=null)?","a":"Retention trims by time/size and deletes EVERYTHING old. Compaction keeps the latest value for each key — the topic becomes like a 'current state' table. To delete a key, you send a message with value=null (a tombstone): compaction wipes all the old ones and the tombstone itself per delete.retention.ms.","d":"Compaction gives an 'infinite snapshot by key' — the basis for a Kafka Streams KTable and for onboarding a new service that reads the topic from the start and gets the current state, not billions of deltas. Compaction does NOT guarantee there were no intermediate versions — only that the final one is preserved.","hook":"A fridge: only the fresh carton of milk stays on each shelf, and an empty carton (null) clears the whole shelf.","more":"**Retention** is about age/volume: the broker drops whole old log segments by `retention.ms`/`bytes`, regardless of key. History is lost wholesale.\n\n**Compaction** works by **key**: a background thread walks the log and keeps only the **latest** record per key. The topic turns into a 'current state snapshot' — perfect for changelogs and a KTable.\n\n- You can't delete a key with an ordinary message — the latest value would remain. You send a **tombstone** (`value=null`): compaction wipes all old versions of the key, while the tombstone itself lives another `delete.retention.ms` so lagging consumers see the deletion, then disappears.","quizCorrect":"Send a record with that key and value=null (a tombstone)","quizWrong":["Lower retention.ms to zero","Call deleteRecords by offset","Switch cleanup.policy to delete for that topic and back again"],"picCap":"The latest value per key stays; null erases the key","note":"Non-obvious: compaction does NOT apply to the active (latest) segment — the freshest duplicate keys stay in the log until the segment is rolled by segment.ms/segment.bytes, so a consumer reading from the start can still see several versions of the same key. Also min.cleanable.dirty.ratio (default 0.5) delays cleaning, and a tombstone can disappear before a lagging consumer reads it if delete.retention.ms is too small — and the deletion gets 'lost'."},"g-ds-maxpoll":{"q":"Processing a batch took 6 minutes, and the consumer got kicked from the group even though the heartbeat thread is alive. Why?","a":"session.timeout.ms catches a dead process via the heartbeat (a background thread). But max.poll.interval.ms (5 min by default) catches 'alive, but stuck in processing': if more than that elapsed between poll() calls, the broker considers you hung, evicts you, and triggers a rebalance. Your commit then fails, and the batch gets reprocessed.","d":"Fixes: lower max.poll.records (take fewer at a time), move the heavy work to a separate pool and keep calling poll(), or raise max.poll.interval.ms. Raising session.timeout here is pointless — it's a different timer.","note":"The key trap: session.timeout.ms and max.poll.interval.ms are two independent timeouts. Raising session.timeout won't save you from an eviction due to slow processing; you must tune max.poll.interval.ms specifically (or lower max.poll.records). They're often confused.","hook":"The cashier waves a hand (heartbeat) that they're alive, but hasn't rung up a single receipt in 6 minutes — they get pulled off the shift anyway.","more":"A consumer has two independent timers. **session.timeout.ms** checks process liveness via a **heartbeat** from a background thread — yours is ticking, so you weren't deemed 'dead'.\n\n**max.poll.interval.ms** (default 5 min) measures the interval between `poll()` calls in your worker thread. Processing the batch took 6 min → the next `poll()` was late → the broker decided you hung, evicted you from the group, and triggered a **rebalance**. The partitions went to another consumer, your `commitSync()` fails (Rebalance/CommitFailed), and the batch is reprocessed.\n\n- Fixes: lower `max.poll.records`, raise the interval, or move the long work out of the poll loop.","quizCorrect":"max.poll.interval.ms","quizWrong":["session.timeout.ms","heartbeat.interval.ms","request.timeout.ms"],"picCap":"Stuck in processing between poll() → the rebalance kicks you out"},"g-ds-idempotent-producer":{"q":"How does enable.idempotence=true fix duplicates and reordering on producer retries, and at what level?","a":"The producer is assigned a PID, and every message in a partition gets a sequence number. The broker remembers the last seq it accepted and silently drops a duplicate (same PID+seq) on retry; it also rejects a gap in the numbering, so ordering survives even with max.in.flight=5. This kills retry-induced duplicates, but NOT duplicates caused by an application restart.","d":"Without idempotence, safe ordering only held at max.in.flight=1 (slow). With idempotence you can keep 5 in flight and still preserve order. It's the foundation of Kafka transactions (EOS). Dedup works within the PID session on a specific partition, not across business-logic restarts.","note":"Producer idempotence guarantees exactly-once only within a single session: a fresh PID is issued on every application restart, so messages sent before a crash and replayed after restart aren't covered by dedup. The only protection against restart duplicates is transactions (transactional.id) or an idempotent consumer.","hook":"Numbered coat-check tickets: hand in the same number twice and the second is refused, and you can't get ticket 4 issued before ticket 3.","more":"With `enable.idempotence=true` the broker assigns the producer a **PID** (producer id), and every message in a partition gets a monotonic **sequence number**. The broker tracks the last accepted seq per (PID, partition).\n\nA retry resends the same PID+seq, the broker sees a duplicate and quietly drops it (ack without writing). A gap (seq greater than expected+1) is **rejected**, so ordering holds even with `max.in.flight.requests=5`.\n\n- The guarantee lives only within the producer session: after an application **restart** the PID is new and duplicate protection doesn't carry over. For exactly-once across restarts you need **transactions** (transactional.id).","quizCorrect":"By the (PID, sequence number) pair within the partition","quizWrong":["By a hash of the message payload","By the partitioning key","By the offset of the previous record in the partition written before it"],"picCap":"A retry reaches the broker; it dedups by PID+seq and keeps order"},"g-ds-cooperative":{"q":"Eager (range/round-robin) vs cooperative-sticky rebalance, what's the practical difference?","a":"Eager is stop-the-world: on a rebalance ALL consumers give up all partitions, then everything is reassigned. The whole group sits idle during the pause. Cooperative-sticky revokes only the partitions that actually migrate; the rest keep working. Less downtime, smoother deploys. It's the default in newer clients.","d":"Cooperative runs the rebalance in two phases (revoke only what's needed, then assign). For rolling restarts this is critical: otherwise every instance restart freezes the whole group. Sticky additionally tries to give the same partitions back to the same instance (warm cache/state).","note":"The eager to cooperative-sticky switch can't be done as a one-shot restart: a mixed group running different assignment protocols is incompatible. You need a rolling migration through an intermediate config (both protocols in partition.assignment.strategy), otherwise the group won't form.","hook":"Reseating a plane: eager deplanes everyone and seats them again; cooperative just moves two passengers while everyone else stays put.","more":"**Eager** (range/round-robin) is **stop-the-world**: on every rebalance all consumers give up **all** their partitions via `onPartitionsRevoked`, the coordinator recomputes the assignment, and only then does work resume. The whole group idles for the round.\n\n**Cooperative-sticky** runs the rebalance in two passes and revokes only the partitions that actually **move** to another consumer; the rest keep being consumed without a pause. Less downtime, smoother rolling deploys.\n\n- In newer clients (Kafka 3.x, Streams) cooperative is the default.\n- You can't mix strategies within one group during migration, you need a phased rollout.","quizCorrect":"It doesn't stall the whole group, only the affected partitions migrate","quizWrong":["Guarantees exactly-once without transactions of the producer at all being involved","Eliminates rebalances entirely","Commits offsets for you automatically"],"picCap":"Cooperative moves only the migrating partitions"},"g-ds-static":{"q":"Every rolling pod restart triggers a full group rebalance. How do you stop the rebalance storms?","a":"Give the consumer a stable group.instance.id (static membership). On a short restart (within session.timeout) the broker does NOT evict the member and does NOT trigger a rebalance, it waits for the same instance to come back with its partitions. Perfect for k8s, where the pod restarts with the same ordinal.","d":"Without static membership every restart = leave group = a rebalance, plus another one when it returns. With a static id and a raised session.timeout, brief restarts are invisible to the group. Downside: if an instance really dies for a long time, its partitions sit idle until the timeout expires.","note":"Static membership has a flip side: if a pod actually dies (not a restart), its partitions sit idle until session.timeout.ms expires, and that value is usually raised for static membership. Too large a session.timeout means a long partition stall on a genuine instance failure.","hook":"A named desk in the office: out for lunch, the desk isn't handed to someone new, they wait for you to return.","more":"By default a consumer gets an **ephemeral** member.id on connect, so any leave/join means a rebalance. **Static membership** gives it a fixed `group.instance.id`.\n\nWhen a pod with the same instance.id disconnects briefly, the coordinator does **not** evict it immediately and does **not** trigger a rebalance, it holds the assignment and waits for the member to return within `session.timeout.ms`, handing back the same partitions. That way short restarts don't touch the group.\n\n- Perfect for a **k8s StatefulSet**: the pod returns with the same ordinal/instance.id.\n- Raise `session.timeout.ms` to cover the restart duration.\n- Downside: a genuinely crashed instance is noticed later (partitions hang until the timeout).","quizCorrect":"group.instance.id, to survive a short restart without a rebalance","quizWrong":["enable.idempotence, to drop duplicates on restart of the consumer after a restart","client.rack, to read from the nearest replica","sticky assignor, to get the same partitions back"],"picCap":"A stable instance.id survives a restart without a rebalance"},"g-ds-retry-topics":{"q":"How do you build delayed retries and a DLQ without blocking the main partition on a poison message?","a":"Don't retry in place. Commit immediately and move the failed message to a separate retry topic with increasing delays (retry-5s, retry-1m, retry-10m). Consumers of those topics wait and try again. After N attempts it goes to the DLQ for manual review. The main flow never stalls and the main partition's ordering is untouched.","d":"A blocking in-place retry stalls the entire partition. Tiered retry topics separate fast and slow traffic. It's important to carry trace_id and an attempt counter in the headers. Downside: you lose global ordering for the failing key, so it fits when ordering isn't critical or can be recovered via idempotency.","hook":"A letter couldn't be delivered, so it goes into the 'try again in an hour' bin, then 'in a day', then into the archive for manual review.","more":"Retrying in place (seek back, sleep in the handler) is dangerous: a poison message blocks the partition and breaks heartbeats/`max.poll.interval` and ordering.\n\nThe pattern: **commit** the offset right away and copy the failed record to a separate **retry topic** with a delay. Set up several tiers (`retry-5s`, `retry-1m`, `retry-10m`); their consumers wait out the pause and try again. After N attempts the message goes to a **DLQ** for manual review.\n\n- The main partition never stalls and its ordering is untouched.\n- Downside: global ordering for that message is lost (it's processed later), so this fits when strict ordering isn't required.","quizCorrect":"So a poison message doesn't block the main partition","quizWrong":["To guarantee exactly-once delivery","To preserve a key's global ordering across the topic as it grows","To halve consumer lag"],"picCap":"A message climbs the retry-topic tiers, ending in the DLQ","note":"Pitfall with tiered retry topics: their consumers 'sleep' until a message is ready, and if the pause exceeds max.poll.interval.ms (default 5 min) the broker evicts the consumer from the group and triggers a rebalance — instead of thread.sleep you need pause()/resume() on the partitions or a separate scheduler. And remember: with several tiers one message can be reprocessed over a span longer than the sum of all delays, so the handler must be idempotent."},"g-ds-quorum":{"q":"What does R+W>N mean in quorum stores, and why does it give read consistency?","a":"N is the number of replicas, W is how many must acknowledge a write, R is how many you poll on a read. If R+W>N, the write set and the read set are GUARANTEED to overlap on at least one replica, so a read sees the latest write. Tune R and W: a smaller W means faster writes but slower/weaker reads.","d":"This is Dynamo-style tunable consistency (Cassandra QUORUM). N=3, W=2, R=2 gives 2+2>3, a strong read. W=1, R=1 is fast but you may read stale data. Versions are resolved by timestamp/vector clock, and conflicts are handled on the read-repair side.","note":"R+W>N guarantees set overlap but NOT linearizability without extra measures: on a failed write (it landed on some replicas and got cut off) a read can return a value that 'officially' never existed, plus concurrent writes need conflict resolution (last-write-wins loses data, vector clocks/CRDTs don't).","hook":"Two of three signed for the delivery; ask any two and at least one definitely saw the signature.","more":"**N** is the number of data replicas, **W** is how many must acknowledge a write, **R** is how many are polled on a read. The coordinator waits for W acks on a write and R responses on a read, picking the freshest version among them.\n\nIf **R + W > N**, the written set and the read set must, by the pigeonhole principle, overlap on at least one node, so a reader sees at least one replica with the latest write and never gets a stale value.\n\n- Tune the balance: a small W means fast writes, but R has to grow.\n- It doesn't save you from conflicts under network partitions, versions still have to be resolved (vector clock / last-write-wins).","quizCorrect":"The read and write sets overlap on at least one replica","quizWrong":["Writes are always faster than reads","A complete absence of version conflicts","Linearizability without clock synchronization between nodes"],"picCap":"W=2, R=2, N=3, the sets overlap"},"g-ds-vector-clock":{"q":"Why a vector clock if you have a wall-clock timestamp? What can it do that a timestamp can't?","a":"Wall-clock lies: node clocks drift out of sync, so a timestamp can't tell you who really happened first. A vector clock is an array of counters [A:2, B:1, ...], one per node. By comparing vectors you can tell EXACTLY: one event precedes another (purely <), or they're concurrent (neither is <= the other) and therefore a conflict to resolve.","d":"A Lamport clock only gives a total order of 'possible causality' but can't tell concurrency apart. A vector clock distinguishes causal-before from concurrent, needed for Dynamo/Riak, CRDTs, and detecting write-write conflicts. The cost: the vector grows with the number of nodes.","hook":"Two editors change different lines of a document: clocks can't say who's 'more authoritative', they edited independently, that's a conflict.","more":"**Wall-clock** is unreliable: node clocks drift (clock skew), and with a time difference a timestamp can't honestly say which write is newer, LWW can lose newer data.\n\nA **vector clock** is `[A:2, B:1, ...]`, an event counter per node. A node increments its own element on each event and merges maxima on exchange. Comparing two vectors component-wise gives a precise answer: if every element of one is **<=** the other, there's a happened-before (causal) relation; if **neither is <=** the other, the events are **concurrent**, a conflict.\n\n- A timestamp can't make that distinction.\n- Downside: the vector grows with the number of nodes/writers; concurrent versions must be merged on the application side.","quizCorrect":"Distinguish causally-related events from concurrent ones","quizWrong":["Synchronize the nodes' physical clocks through NTP once a second","Give an event a globally unique ID","Guarantee linearizability of writes"],"picCap":"Neither vector is <= the other, a conflict","note":"Catch: a vector clock by itself does NOT resolve the conflict — it only detects concurrency; the merge (semantic merge or collecting siblings, as in Riak) is the application's job; you can't naively pick the 'larger' vector since concurrent vectors are incomparable. And the per-node vector doesn't grow unboundedly for free: Dynamo truncates by timestamp/size, which can erroneously merge genuinely concurrent versions."},"g-ds-2pc":{"q":"Why is 2PC (two-phase commit) avoided in microservices in favor of the saga?","a":"2PC: the coordinator sends prepare to everyone, waits for 'ready', then commits. The trouble is it's blocking: if the coordinator dies between phases, participants sit with locked resources in limbo until it comes back. That hurts availability and doesn't scale. A saga instead does local commits plus compensations, with no global lock.","d":"2PC gives atomicity but at the cost of CP behavior and a single point of failure (the coordinator). Under a network partition a participant gets stuck. A saga sacrifices isolation (intermediate states are visible) for availability and service independence, usually the right tradeoff in a distributed system.","note":"The main practical flaw of 2PC, beyond the blocking itself, is the participant's window of uncertainty: if the coordinator dies AFTER a participant's 'ready' but before the decision, the participant may neither commit nor roll back and holds its locks until the coordinator recovers. That (not two-phaseness per se) is what makes 2PC dangerous for availability.","hook":"A notary gathers everyone for a deal, says 'get ready', and vanishes, leaving everyone sitting with frozen money, waiting.","more":"**2PC** drives a distributed atomic commit through a coordinator: the **prepare** phase (all participants lock resources and answer 'ready') then the **commit/abort** phase. Locked rows/resources are held between phases.\n\nThe problem is it's a **blocking protocol**: if the coordinator dies after prepare, participants are stuck holding locks 'in limbo', unsure whether to commit or roll back, until it returns. That cuts availability and scales poorly across a network.\n\nA **saga** instead runs a chain of **local** transactions, each committing immediately; on failure it runs **compensations** (undo actions). There's no global lock, but you pay with **eventual consistency** and the risk of visible intermediate states.","quizCorrect":"Blocking: a coordinator crash leaves resources locked","quizWrong":["It doesn't provide transaction atomicity between participants","It requires exactly-once in Kafka","It doesn't work without vector clocks"],"picCap":"Coordinator dies, resources stay locked in limbo"},"g-ds-gossip":{"q":"How do nodes learn who's alive and who's down without a central registry? What is gossip?","a":"Gossip (epidemic): each node periodically picks random peers and exchanges its view of cluster state (who's alive, versions, metadata). The rumor spreads exponentially, all nodes converge in O(log N) rounds. No master, no SPOF, fault-tolerant and scalable.","d":"Used in Cassandra, Consul, and Serf for membership and failure detection (often with a phi-accrual detector). Downside: it's eventually consistent, the moment when 'everyone knows' isn't instant. It fits cluster metadata, not strict data consistency.","hook":"Village gossip: each person whispers to a couple of neighbors, and by evening the whole village knows, with no loudspeaker announcement.","more":"**Gossip (epidemic protocol)**: each node has a local map of the cluster. Once per period it picks **random** peers and merges its view of membership, versions, and metadata with them, like a rumor or virus. In **O(log N)** rounds the state converges everywhere.\n\nLiveness is determined via **heartbeat counters/timeouts** (often with something like phi-accrual): a node whose counter hasn't advanced in a while is marked suspect/down.\n\n**Why this way:** no master means no **SPOF**, no bottleneck, it scales linearly and tolerates packet loss. Gotchas: convergence is **not instant** (there's detection lag), and false positives can happen during network pauses/GC. Used by Cassandra, Consul, and Serf.","quizCorrect":"Spread membership/state to everyone in O(log N) with no SPOF","quizWrong":["Provide strict linearizable data consistency across the cluster","Guarantee exactly-once message delivery","Synchronize all nodes' physical clocks"],"picCap":"A rumor spreads node by node, round after round, converging in O(log N)","note":"Trap: a long stop-the-world GC pause or a network freeze stops heartbeat increments, and the phi-accrual detector will mark a live node as down — hence false failovers and flapping. That's why detection thresholds (phi threshold, gossip interval) are tuned to P99 GC and network RTT rather than left aggressively at defaults, otherwise the cluster tears itself apart under load."},"ws-camunda":{"q":"Camunda, what is it and why use it?","a":"**Camunda** is a business process (workflow) engine. You describe a process in **BPMN** notation, drawing tasks, gateways (conditions), and events, and the engine executes that diagram step by step: it calls services, waits for responses, branches on conditions, and stores the current state of every process instance. The key value: business logic becomes a visible diagram instead of being smeared across code, and you always know which step a given instance is stuck on.","d":"Camunda doesn't replace your code, it orchestrates it. A step's business logic lives in a service/worker, while the engine owns step ordering, state, and reliability (retries, history, waiting for events).","hook":"Where is request #147 stuck right now? Camunda shows it on a diagram, not via grep over logs.","more":"**Why you need it.** Picture handling a request: verify the client, approve, create the contract, send a notification. Without an engine this is a chain of calls in code where it's hard to tell where the process failed and how to restart it. Camunda orchestrates these steps: it keeps state in a DB, retries failed tasks, can wait a long time (hours, days), and an analyst sees the same process as a diagram.\n\n**Two versions, an important distinction:**\n- **Camunda 7** is the classic embeddable engine. You add it as a library right inside a Java application, processes run in the same JVM, next to your code and DB. Simpler for a monolith.\n- **Camunda 8** is cloud/distributed, built on top of the **Zeebe** engine. Here the engine is separate, and your services connect to it as workers (External Tasks / Job Workers) and pull tasks. Scales better for microservices.\n\n**Key BPMN elements:** a task (do something), a gateway (branching on a condition, e.g. is the amount over the limit?), an event (start, timer, message from Kafka). For example, Zeebe + External Tasks are used exactly in `orders-service` and `agreement-service`, the service subscribes to a task, does the work, and reports the result back to the engine.","quizCorrect":"C8 is cloud, built on Zeebe; C7 is embedded in a Java application","quizWrong":["C8 is written in Python and C7 in Java","C8 doesn't support BPMN, only code","C7 is distributed and C8 runs in a single JVM on one node of the cluster"],"picCap":"A BPMN process: tasks, a branching gateway, a token moving along the diagram, the engine keeps the state","note":"Camunda 7 gotcha: each wait state (user task, async-before/after, timer) commits a transaction, but the synchronous stretch between two wait states runs in ONE transaction, so an exception at the end rolls back ALL intermediate service tasks — including external calls already made. That's why you put async boundaries before/after non-transactional steps (HTTP, email), otherwise a retry re-executes the step."},"ws-zeebe":{"q":"Zeebe, how does it differ from Camunda 7?","a":"**Zeebe** is the process engine at the core of **Camunda 8**, designed for microservices and the cloud. The key difference from **Camunda 7**: Zeebe doesn't store state in a shared relational DB. It uses its own distributed event log (like Kafka inside) and partitioning, so it scales horizontally, add nodes and throughput grows. Camunda 7 is an embeddable library: it runs inside your Java application and writes state to a single SQL database, which becomes the bottleneck under load.\n\nThe second difference is the worker model. In Zeebe tasks are handed out via **PULL**: job workers poll the broker themselves and take work when they're ready. In Camunda 7 logic is usually executed inside the same process (PUSH/embedded delegates). PULL gives service decoupling, backpressure, and resilience.","d":"A **job worker (PULL)** is an external service that polls the Zeebe broker itself and takes a task when it's ready to handle it, rather than having it pushed in (PUSH). Gives decoupling and backpressure.","hook":"Camunda 7 bottlenecks on one shared DB. Zeebe doesn't. What's the trick?","more":"**Camunda 7** is a proven embeddable BPM engine. You add it as a dependency in a Java application (Spring/Quarkus), it keeps process state in a single relational DB (PostgreSQL/Oracle), and executes steps synchronously via delegates inside the same JVM. That's convenient for a monolith and moderate load: one transaction, one rollback, everything at hand. But that very shared DB becomes the ceiling, it's hard to shard and it limits horizontal scaling.\n\n**Zeebe** (the core of Camunda 8) flips the approach:\n- **No shared SQL database.** State is written to its own append-only log, replicated via Raft. History and search are offloaded to a separate exporter (Elasticsearch/OpenSearch).\n- **Partitions + horizontal scaling.** Processes are distributed across partitions among brokers, more nodes, more throughput.\n- **PULL job workers.** Service tasks aren't executed inside the engine. External workers (any language) activate jobs over gRPC, do the work, and report complete/fail. That's natural backpressure and loose coupling.\n\n**When to pick which.** Camunda 7 if you have a monolith/moderate load, need a transactional binding between the process and business data in one DB, and the team already knows the stack. Zeebe/Camunda 8 if it's microservices, you need high throughput, cloud/Kubernetes, and worker language can be anything. For example, Zeebe External Tasks fit the distributed services (orders, agreement, print-service), where you don't want to drag everyone into one shared engine DB.","quizCorrect":"Stores state in its own distributed log, with no shared relational DB","quizWrong":["Stores all state in one shared SQL database, just like Camunda 7 with the same tables","Doesn't store process state between steps at all","Keeps state only in the memory of a single node"],"picCap":"C7: everything in one JVM + a shared DB. Zeebe: brokers with partitions + workers pulling jobs (PULL)."},"ws-bpmn":{"q":"BPMN, what kind of notation is it?","a":"BPMN (Business Process Model and Notation) is a standard for graphically describing business processes. A process is drawn as a diagram of intuitive shapes: tasks (rectangles), gateways (diamonds, branching and merging on a condition), events (circles, start, timer, message, error), and arrows set the step order. The core idea: one diagram is understandable to both business and developers. And the engine (Camunda, Zeebe) doesn't just draw it, it EXECUTES that diagram as process code.","d":"Don't confuse BPMN with UML or a flowchart. UML describes software structure and behavior (classes, sequences), while BPMN describes the business process end-to-end. It differs from an ordinary flowchart in that it's standardized (ISO 19510) and, crucially, executable by an engine, not just a picture for a presentation.","hook":"Business and coders argue over where the 'truth' of the process lives? BPMN is the very picture the engine actually runs.","more":"**Why you need BPMN.** It used to be that a business analyst drew the process in one tool while a developer rewrote the logic in code their own way, and the two drifted apart. BPMN closes that gap: the diagram becomes the single source of truth. Business sees the steps and conditions, the developer sees an executable model, and the engine takes the same file (`.bpmn` is XML) and actually runs processes over it.\n\n**A basic alphabet of three shapes:**\n- **Tasks** (rounded rectangles), a unit of work: \"Create order\", \"Generate PDF\". There are user tasks (a human does it) and service tasks (the system/code does it).\n- **Gateways** (diamonds), decision points. Exclusive (XOR), go down one branch of several by condition; parallel (AND), go down all branches at once and then wait for them. A gateway both splits and merges flows back.\n- **Events** (circles), things that happen: process start, a timer (\"wait 3 days\"), a message (arrived from Kafka), an error (catch/throw for handling failures).\n\n**How it executes.** The engine advances a **token** through the diagram, a marker for \"where the process is now\". The token enters a task, waits for it to finish, takes the right branch at a gateway, and splits at a parallel gateway. In Zeebe/Camunda a service task is usually hung on an external task / job worker: the code subscribes, takes the task, does the work (go to legacy CBS, put a file in MinIO), and says \"complete\". That's how order and agreement orchestration in `orders-service` and `agreement-service` is held together, on BPMN diagrams over Zeebe.","quizCorrect":"Branching or merging the flow on a condition","quizWrong":["A unit of work, an executor's task in the process","A start or timer event","A process data store"],"picCap":"One flow: circle event, rectangle task, diamond gateway (branching), task, filled-circle end. A token-dot shows where the process is now.","note":"Hidden gateway trap: an exclusive (XOR) gateway with no matching condition and no default flow throws an exception and raises an incident — always set a default. And don't confuse a parallel (AND) join, which WAITS for all incoming branches, with an inclusive (OR) join, whose synchronization is costlier and requires the opening inclusive gateway to activate exactly the branches the join will later await — mismatched branches hang the token."},"ws-job-worker":{"q":"Job worker / external task, how does a service take part in a process?","a":"The service **picks up** the task from the process engine itself (pull/poll), rather than the engine calling the service. The worker takes a job, runs business logic, and reports the result and variables back to the engine. So business logic lives in the services, and step orchestration in the engine (Zeebe/Camunda). If a worker crashes without finishing a task, it times out, returns to the pool, and another worker picks it up.","d":"Push (engine to service HTTP): tighter coupling, the engine owns the service's availability and retries, and it's easy to overwhelm a loaded service. Pull (the worker polls itself): decoupling, backpressure out of the box, easy horizontal scaling. The price of pull is the need for idempotency (a job may arrive twice after a crash) and careful lock timeouts.","hook":"Who calls whom: the engine the service, or the service the engine? In this pattern it's the opposite of what it seems.","more":"**How it works step by step.** The engine reaches a service task and puts a job on a queue with a type (e.g. `generate-pdf`). Workers of that type continuously poll the engine: \"any work for me?\". One of them takes a job, gets a lock for N seconds, does the work, and calls `complete` with output variables. The engine moves the process on. This is a **pull model**: the initiative is on the service side, the engine calls nothing itself.\n\n**Why this and not push (engine hits an HTTP endpoint).**\n- **Decoupling:** the engine doesn't need to know service addresses, load balancing, or availability, the worker comes when it can.\n- **Natural backpressure:** an overloaded service simply polls less often, nobody floods it with requests.\n- **Scaling:** add worker instances and tasks are picked up in parallel, competing for one pool.\n\n**Fault tolerance, the key point.** A worker holds the lock only for the duration of the work. If it **crashes** before `complete`, the lock expires by timeout and the job becomes available again, another worker picks it up. So it matters to: make the operation **idempotent** (the same task may run twice on a retry) and not set the lock timeout shorter than the real work time. In Zeebe this is External Task / Job Worker, in Camunda 7 External Tasks; the idea is the same.","quizCorrect":"The lock expires by timeout and another worker picks up the job","quizWrong":["The task is lost and the process hangs forever","The engine runs the business logic itself instead of the worker itself","The process rolls back to the very beginning"],"picCap":"Workers pull jobs from the engine themselves; one takes a lock, runs it, and reports the result back. If it crashes, the lock expires and another worker takes the job.","note":"Pull-model subtlety: a job worker must finish before the lock/job timeout expires, otherwise the engine treats the job as abandoned and hands it to ANOTHER worker — you get double execution, and then the first worker's `complete` fails with NotFound. So set the timeout with headroom over p99 latency, make the operation idempotent by job key, and split or extend the lease for long tasks."},"de-srp":{"q":"SRP — the Single Responsibility Principle?","a":"A class should have only one reason to change. A God Class (like the 1361-line OrderServiceImpl) violates SRP — it changes for a bunch of unrelated reasons at once.","d":"Cure: split into use-case classes along the axes of change.","hook":"A Swiss army knife that also does dentistry: touch one blade and they all twitch. Every job deserves its own tool.","more":"**SRP**: a class should have **one reason to change** — meaning it answers to a single \"stakeholder\"/single axis of requirements. Not \"does one thing\", but \"changes for one reason\".\n\nA God Class like `OrderServiceImpl` (1361 lines) bundles validation, calculations, DB access and publishing to Kafka — and gets edited for a pile of unrelated reasons. Any tiny change risks breaking everything else, the tests are huge, and git conflicts are constant.\n\n- When to apply: split when you see a class being touched by different teams/for different reasons. Don't take it to absurdity — one method = one class is already over-engineering.","quizCorrect":"A class should have only one reason to change","quizWrong":["A class should expose only one public method to callers","A class should have a single state field","A class should extend only one parent"],"picCap":"One class — one reason to change","note":"A common overcorrection when \"fixing\" a God Class is anemic scatter: you extract logic into many classes but state stays in DTOs, so cohesion drops instead of rising. SRP is about a single AXIS of change (one actor/stakeholder per Martin), not about size: a 1000-line class with a single reason to change does not formally violate SRP."},"de-ocp":{"q":"OCP — open for extension, closed for modification?","a":"Add behavior with new code (a new interface implementation) without touching what already works. A growing switch/if on type is the classic smell of an OCP violation.","d":"Polymorphism / Strategy instead of branching on type.","hook":"A power socket: you just plug a new device in, no need to chisel the wall. Add plugs, leave the wiring alone.","more":"**OCP**: you extend the system by **adding new code**, not by editing what works. In practice this means: hide the point of variability behind an interface, and a new behavior variant becomes a new implementation that gets plugged in.\n\nWhy: code already tested and running in prod stays untouched — fewer regressions. A growing `switch`/`if` on type (\"what if it's a VIP client, what if...\") is the classic smell of a violation: every new type forces you back into the old method.\n\n- Pitfall: don't abstract ahead of time \"just in case\" (YAGNI). OCP pays off where variants genuinely multiply; one or two `if` branches aren't a reason to breed interfaces.","quizCorrect":"Add new behavior with new code, without editing what works","quizWrong":["Make all class fields private and sealed off","A class cannot be extended after release","The public API is frozen forever with no extensions of any kind"],"picCap":"New implementation — add it, don't touch working code","note":"OCP isn't free: you must guess the extension point IN ADVANCE and correctly — an abstraction designed for the wrong axis of variability later hurts more than a switch (you'd have to change both the interface and every implementation). This is \"premature abstraction\"; in practice it's cheaper to first duplicate/keep the if and extract a Strategy only after the second or third real variant (rule of three)."},"de-lsp":{"q":"LSP — the Liskov Substitution Principle?","a":"A subtype must work correctly anywhere its supertype is expected, without breaking its contract. The classic violation: Square extends Rectangle, where setWidth breaks the height invariant.","d":"\"is-a\" is defined by behavior, not by the structure of fields.","note":"LSP breaks silently: the compiler lets through an override that narrows the contract (throws UnsupportedOperationException, strengthens a precondition, weakens a postcondition). A JDK classic — Collections.unmodifiableList: the type is List, but add() throws at runtime, violating the List contract.","hook":"If the box says \"AA battery\", any AA must fit and work. Slip in an \"almost AA\" and the device dies.","more":"**LSP**: a subclass object must work correctly **everywhere** the code expects the base type, without violating its contract — same preconditions (no stronger), postconditions (no weaker) and invariants.\n\nThe classic: `Square extends Rectangle`. On a rectangle `setWidth` changes only the width, but a square is forced to change the height too — the invariant \"width and height are independent\" breaks, and code that computes area gets a surprise.\n\n- Pitfall: \"is-a\" inheritance by dictionary ≠ correct substitution by behavior. If a subclass throws `UnsupportedOperationException` or narrows the accepted input — that's a sign of an LSP violation, more often cured by **composition**.","quizCorrect":"A subtype works everywhere in place of its parent, without breaking the contract","quizWrong":["A subclass must override all of the parent's methods","The inheritance hierarchy depth is at most three levels in any well-designed system","Parent and subclass must have identical fields"],"picCap":"Square breaks the Rectangle invariant — an LSP violation"},"de-isp-dip":{"q":"ISP and DIP in brief?","a":"ISP — several narrow interfaces are better than one \"fat\" one: a client shouldn't depend on methods it doesn't need. DIP — depend on abstractions, not on concretions; the high level doesn't know the details.","d":"DIP is the essence of the hexagon: the domain depends on a port interface, the adapter implements it.","hook":"ISP: not one remote with 100 buttons, but a separate one for each. DIP: the socket doesn't know whether a kettle or a charger is plugged in.","more":"**ISP** (interface segregation): better several **narrow** interfaces for specific roles than one \"fat\" one. A client shouldn't depend on methods it doesn't use — otherwise it breaks on changes that don't concern it at all, and you end up writing empty stubs.\n\n**DIP** (dependency inversion): both the high level (business logic) and the low level (details — DB, HTTP) depend on an **abstraction**, not on each other. Business code knows only the port interface, and a concrete adapter is injected from the outside (DI).\n\n- Connection: DIP is the essence of the hexagon. Ports (audit-api/audit-spi in your reference service) are exactly those abstractions the core depends on, without knowing about MinIO/JDBC.","quizCorrect":"ISP — narrow interfaces; DIP — depend on abstractions","quizWrong":["ISP — one shared interface per module; DIP — depend on concretions","ISP — interfaces with a single method only; DIP — no interfaces","ISP — inherit interfaces; DIP — the high level knows the details"],"picCap":"ISP: split the interface. DIP: depend on the abstraction","note":"In DI frameworks DIP easily degenerates: an interface with a single implementation living in the same module gives no inversion — the core still only compiles together with the detail. True inversion requires the port to live in the domain module and the adapter in a separate module that DEPENDS on the domain (like audit-spi vs adapter in your reference); otherwise it's an \"interface for the interface's sake\"."},"de-comp-inherit":{"q":"Composition vs inheritance — which to prefer?","a":"Inheritance binds you rigidly to the parent and breaks encapsulation (fragile base class). Composition assembles behavior out of objects — more flexible and testable. \"Favor composition over inheritance\".","d":"Inheritance — only for a true \"is-a\" by behavior (see LSP).","note":"The fragile base class in practice: overriding a method that the base class calls internally from another of its own methods (a self-call) breaks behavior in non-obvious ways. So if you do need inheritance — either design the class for inheritance explicitly and document self-calls, or make it final.","hook":"Inheritance — born into this family forever. Composition — you assembled a team of hired specialists and swap whoever you want.","more":"**Inheritance** ties a class to its parent rigidly and at compile time: the subclass sees the protected guts, and any change to the base class can break it — that's the \"fragile base class problem\". Plus you can't build a multiple hierarchy in Java.\n\n**Composition** assembles behavior out of nested objects (delegation through fields). The coupling is through a narrow interface, the implementation can be swapped — including in tests (mocks) — and at runtime.\n\n- \"Favor composition over inheritance\": inherit when it's an honest, stable \"is-a\" and you control the base class. For code reuse and flexibility — composition almost always wins.","quizCorrect":"Composition: more flexible, more testable, doesn't break encapsulation","quizWrong":["Inheritance: less code and faster at runtime","Inheritance: it is strictly safer than composition","Composition only for interfaces, otherwise inheritance in every other case"],"picCap":"Favor composition over inheritance"},"de-strategy":{"q":"The Strategy pattern — what does it solve?","a":"Pull an interchangeable algorithm out behind an interface and plug in the implementation you need — removing the switch/if on type. The behavior is chosen at runtime.","d":"In Spring this is just different beans of one interface + selection by key (Map<String, Strategy>).","hook":"One navigator, modes \"walking/driving/cycling\" — switch it and the route recalculates. Same road, different way.","more":"**Strategy** pulls an interchangeable algorithm out behind a common interface. The context holds a reference to the interface and calls its method without knowing which implementation is plugged in — the choice is made **at runtime** (via DI, a factory, a map).\n\nWhy: it removes the sprawling `switch`/`if` on type and thereby closes the class under OCP — a new algorithm is added as a new strategy class, the old code stays untouched. Each algorithm is isolated and easy to test on its own.\n\n- When: there's a family of variants of one action (fee calculation, payment method, report format). Pitfall: if there's exactly one variant and no more are expected — it's a needless abstraction, a plain method is simpler.","quizCorrect":"Hides an interchangeable algorithm behind an interface, chosen at runtime","quizWrong":["Guarantees a single instance of the algorithm in the application for its whole lifetime","Caches the algorithm's result between calls","Creates objects while hiding their concrete class"],"picCap":"Interface + swappable implementations instead of switch","note":"A classic `Map<String, Strategy>` trap in Spring: the registration key becomes the bean name, not your domain key — when you inject `Map<String, Strategy>`, Spring fills it as beanName→bean, and your `map.get(\"VIP\")` silently returns null. Make the key explicit (a `key()` method in the interface plus assembling the map in @Configuration or @PostConstruct) rather than relying on bean names."},"de-factory":{"q":"Factory — what's it for?","a":"It encapsulates object creation: the client doesn't know the concrete class and doesn't scatter new all over the code. Easier to swap implementations and to test.","d":"It often degenerates into a DI container — which is itself a bean factory.","hook":"You tell the barista \"cappuccino\" — and he picks the machine, beans and recipe himself. You don't go into the kitchen.","more":"**Factory** hides object creation behind a separate method/class: the client asks \"give me an implementation by such-and-such criterion\" and gets an interface, without knowing the concrete class and without scattering `new` all over the code.\n\nWhy: the creation site becomes the single one — to swap an implementation, add caching or do complex assembly (dependencies, config) you change one spot. The client depends on an abstraction (works hand in hand with DIP), and is easier to mock in tests.\n\n- Distinguish: Factory Method (the subclass decides what to create) vs Abstract Factory (a family of related objects). Pitfall: in Quarkus/Spring the factory role is often already played by the **DI container** — don't duplicate it by hand without need.","quizCorrect":"Encapsulates object creation, hiding the concrete class","quizWrong":["Guarantees a single instance across the whole application","Swaps the behavior's algorithm at runtime","Assembles an object with many fields step by step"],"picCap":"The client doesn't know the class — Factory hides new","note":"A static factory method (Integer.valueOf, EnumSet.of) is not itself the GoF Factory Method — the real Factory Method is virtual and overridden by a subclass. This confusion causes wrong interview answers: a static factory can't be overridden, it doesn't participate in polymorphism and so isn't the Factory Method pattern."},"de-singleton":{"q":"Singleton — what's the catch in Java?","a":"One instance per application. A hand-rolled Singleton with double-checked locking requires a volatile field, otherwise another thread can see a half-constructed object. The safest is the enum singleton (thread-safe, protected against reflection/serialization).","d":"In Spring, beans are singletons by default anyway — your own Singleton is almost never needed.","note":"A Spring/Quarkus bean with singleton scope is NOT the GoF Singleton: it's one instance per container, not per JVM, and isn't thread-safe by itself. The main trap of a singleton bean is holding mutable state in a field (a counter, the current user): in prod, different requests from different threads clobber each other.","hook":"One company stamp for the whole office. Without volatile a colleague grabs it half-finished; enum keeps it in a fireproof safe.","more":"**Singleton** guarantees one instance per application and a global access point. The catch in Java is the thread safety of lazy initialization: with double-checked locking the field MUST be **volatile**, otherwise, due to instruction reordering, another thread can see a reference to a still **half-constructed** object.\n\nThe most reliable way is the **enum singleton**: the JVM itself guarantees uniqueness, thread safety, and at the same time protection against reflection and duplication on serialization (a plain class breaks through these loopholes). An alternative is a holder on a static nested class (lazy without synchronized).\n\n- Pitfall: global state hinders tests and hides dependencies. In Quarkus/Spring an `@ApplicationScoped`/`@Singleton` bean is preferable — the container manages the lifecycle for you.","quizCorrect":"Without volatile, another thread can see a half-constructed object","quizWrong":["A Singleton can't be made thread-safe in principle","A single instance necessarily requires a static block","The enum singleton is unsafe and unprotected against reflection attacks"],"picCap":"enum — the safest Singleton in Java"},"de-builder":{"q":"Builder — when to reach for it?","a":"When an object has many fields, especially optional ones: instead of telescoping constructors you assemble it step by step and call build(). Readable, and it yields an immutable object.","d":"record + a compact constructor often covers the need more simply.","note":"Builder doesn't give immutability and validity by itself: if build() doesn't check the required fields, you get a half-assembled object with nulls. Validate invariants inside build()/the constructor, not in the builder's setters.","hook":"Ordering a burger: bun, cheese no onion, double patty — you assembled it to taste and said \"done\". Not 10 different menus.","more":"Builder separates the **construction** of an object from its representation: you spin up an intermediate builder object, set fields in a chain (`.name(..).age(..)`), and `build()` assembles the final immutable instance and checks invariants.\n\nWhy: with 4+ fields, especially optional ones, constructors **multiply** (telescoping) and it's easy to mix up arguments of the same type. Builder makes the call self-documenting and lets you validate the object before it's created.\n\n- Pitfall: dragging a Builder in where 2-3 fields suffice is needless code. If the object is mutable and simple, a plain constructor or factory is simpler.","quizCorrect":"When there are many fields, especially optional ones; yields an immutable object","quizWrong":["When a single instance of an object is needed","When you need to hide the concrete class on creation","When you need to swap the behavior's algorithm at runtime without rebuilding the application"],"picCap":"Many fields → steps instead of a constructor, build() = immutable"},"x-de-observer":{"q":"The Observer pattern — what does it solve and where is it already built into Java?","a":"One object (Subject) keeps a list of subscribers and, on a change, notifies them all itself — they don't have to poll it in a loop. Loose coupling: the Subject doesn't know who's listening, only the interface. This is the basis of event-driven and pub/sub.","d":"In Java: java.util.Observer is obsolete (deprecated since 9), replaced by listeners (Listener), PropertyChangeListener, Flow.Publisher/Subscriber (Reactive Streams, JDK 9+), Spring ApplicationEvent. Downsides: memory leaks if you don't unsubscribe, non-obvious ordering and cascading of notifications, debugging difficulty.","note":"The built-in java.util.Observer/Observable were declared @Deprecated since Java 9 — don't use them. The pattern's pitfall: synchronous listeners make notification effectively blocking (one slow/failing observer stalls or crashes the whole notify), plus memory leaks if you forgot to unsubscribe.","hook":"A broadcast in a Telegram channel: you post once — all subscribers get a push immediately.","more":"The Subject holds a **list of observers** and a `notify()` method. On a state change it runs through the list itself and calls an agreed method (`update()`) on each. Subscribers implement a common interface — the Subject knows only that, not the concrete classes. This is an inversion: observers don't poll, the source **pushes** the change itself.\n\nWhy: loose coupling and extensibility — you can add a new listener without touching the Subject.\n\n- Built into Java: `java.util.Observable` (deprecated), event listeners in Swing/AWT, `PropertyChangeListener`, and at the reactive level — `Flow.Publisher/Subscriber`.\n- Pitfalls: memory leaks if you forgot to unsubscribe; synchronous notifications can block the source.","quizCorrect":"The Subject notifies all subscribers itself on a change","quizWrong":["Subscribers poll the Subject in a loop on a timer","The Subject keeps a copy of each subscriber and compares them","Each subscriber directly calls the methods of other subscribers"],"picCap":"The Subject notifies all subscribers"},"x-de-decorator":{"q":"The Decorator pattern — what for, how does it differ from inheritance?","a":"You wrap an object in another object of the same interface and add behavior without touching the original class. You can stack layers dynamically in any order — instead of an explosion of subclasses for every feature combination.","d":"Decorator implements the same interface as the wrapped object and delegates calls to it, adding its own before/after. A JDK classic — java.io: new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(new FileInputStream(f))). Unlike inheritance (fixed at compile time) — it's composed at runtime.","hook":"A nesting doll, or toppings on a burger: same burger, you wrap it in layers — cheese, bacon — the order is yours.","more":"A decorator implements the **same interface** as the object it wraps, and holds a reference to it. It delegates its methods to the inner object, adding behavior before/after the call. Wrappers can be nested inside one another — you get a chain of layers assembled **at runtime**.\n\nThe difference from inheritance: inheritance fixes behavior statically and breeds subclasses for every combination (buffer+cipher+compression = a class explosion). Decorator composes features dynamically.\n\n- The classic example — `java.io`: `new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(in))`.\n- Pitfalls: lots of small objects, harder stack debugging; the wrapper must honestly proxy ALL of the interface's methods.","quizCorrect":"Wraps an object and adds behavior dynamically at runtime","quizWrong":["Creates a subclass for every feature combination at compile time","Modifies the original class's bytecode on load","Fully replaces the wrapped object's interface with a new one"],"picCap":"Each layer adds behavior on top — the cost grows along the chain","note":"Decorator breaks when a client checks a concrete type or calls methods absent from the shared interface: instanceof/cast to the wrapped class fails, and the wrapper's equals/hashCode no longer match the original object (an identity problem in collections/caches). That's why JDK wrappers like Collections.synchronizedList or java.io rely strictly on the interface, and there is no standard way to extract the 'real' object from under the layers."},"x-de-dip-vs-di":{"q":"Dependency Injection vs Inversion of Control — are they the same thing?","a":"No. IoC is the general principle: you hand control of the flow to the framework (\"don't call us, we'll call you\"). DI is a specific technique of IoC: you don't create dependencies yourself inside the class, you receive them from the outside (via the constructor). This way the class isn't tied to concrete implementations.","d":"DI ≠ DIP. DIP (the D in SOLID) is about depending on abstractions — it's a design rule. DI is the technique for delivering those dependencies. Kinds: constructor (preferred — required deps, immutable, easy to test), setter, field (@Inject/@Autowired on a field — bad for tests). The container (Spring/CDI/Quarkus) is just a convenient way to do DI, you can do it by hand too.","note":"Field injection (@Inject/@Autowired on a field) is a common trap: it hides dependencies from the constructor, hinders tests (you can't supply a mock without reflection/a container) and doesn't catch circular dependencies at startup. Prefer constructor injection.","hook":"A repair job: you don't forge the key yourself (new), it's handed to you at the door — take whichever one fits.","more":"**IoC** is the broad principle: you hand control of the flow outward. Your code doesn't call the framework at the right moment — the framework calls your handlers/beans (\"Hollywood principle\"). Template methods, the event loop, and containers belong here too.\n\n**DI** is a concrete technique for implementing IoC specifically for dependencies: a class doesn't do `new` inside, it receives ready dependencies from the outside (constructor, less often setter/field). Control over building the object graph goes to the container (CDI/Spring).\n\n- DI usually pairs with **DIP** (depending on interfaces, not implementations) — but they're different things: DIP is about the direction of dependencies, DI is about the way of delivering them.\n- Pitfall: field injection hides dependencies and breaks testability — prefer the constructor.","quizCorrect":"No: IoC is the general principle, DI is a specific technique of IoC","quizWrong":["Yes, they're two names for the same technique","No: DI is the general principle, and IoC is a specific technique of it","DI is about databases, IoC is about execution threads"],"picCap":"The dependency arrives from outside, it isn't created inside"},"x-de-law-of-demeter":{"q":"The Law of Demeter — what is it and what for?","a":"The \"don't talk to strangers\" rule: a method should call only methods of its own object, its fields, its arguments, and what it created itself — but not reach deep into other objects through chains like a.getB().getC().doX(). This lowers coupling.","d":"Long getter chains (train wrecks) make code fragile: change the internal structure of B or C — and the caller breaks. Cure: add methods on the top-level object (Tell, Don't Ask). Important: fluent builders and the Stream API are NOT a violation — there the chain returns the same/same-typed object, it doesn't expose other objects' internals.","hook":"Don't reach into your friend's friend's pocket — ask your friend to hand it over.","more":"Formally a method may access only: **the object itself**, its fields, its own arguments, objects it created itself. Other objects obtained through a getter are already \"strangers\". The chain `a.getB().getC().doX()` violates the rule: the calling code knows the internal makeup of B and C.\n\nWhy: less coupling — changing the structure of B/C won't break a heap of clients. An object should **give you a result**, not hand its internals outward (\"Tell, Don't Ask\").\n\n- Cured like this: instead of pulling out the chain, add a method on the object itself — `a.doX()`, which walks inside on its own.\n- Don't confuse it with fluent/builder (`builder.a().b().c()`) — there the chain returns the same object, that's not a violation.","quizCorrect":"Call methods only of your own fields, arguments and what you created","quizWrong":["Every class must have at most one public method","Pass all dependencies only through static fields of the calling class","Every method must return a value, not void"],"picCap":"Top — a train-wreck chain, bottom — a single neighbor","note":"The Law of Demeter is about method calls, not data access: navigation chains in JPA entities (order.getCustomer().getAddress().getCity()) formally violate it, but hiding them behind methods on the aggregate is dangerous — each getter can trigger lazy loading and N+1, and the long chain over a lazy association throws LazyInitializationException outside a transaction. So blindly following LoD in ORM models masks performance traps better solved with join-fetch/projection rather than yet another 'convenient' method."},"p-ddd-one-agg-per-tx":{"q":"The rule \"one transaction = one aggregate\". Why is it violated and what does it hurt?","a":"In one transaction change only ONE aggregate. Want to change a second — do it in a separate transaction, via a domain event (eventual consistency). Otherwise you inflate the consistency boundary and run into locks and contention.","d":"An aggregate is a consistency boundary: its invariants must hold by the end of the transaction. If you edit two aggregates at once, you're essentially demanding strong consistency where it isn't needed: locks grow, versions conflict more often. Vernon: inside the boundary — true invariants and one transaction; everything outside — via Domain Event + saga/outbox. The outbox record goes into the SAME transaction as the aggregate — that's not a violation, it's part of one commit.","note":"The violation surfaces not immediately but under load: editing two aggregates in one transaction widens the lock boundary and yields deadlock/contention on hot rows. The price of the rule is eventual consistency: between two transactions the system is in an intermediate state, and the UI/consumer must tolerate it.","hook":"One cash receipt — one purchase. A second store? Go and ring it up separately.","more":"An aggregate is a consistency boundary: inside it all invariants must be true at the moment of commit. The rule \"one tx = one aggregate\" means we atomically change exactly one such boundary.\n\nNeed to touch a second aggregate — publish a **domain event**, and the second will update in its own transaction a bit later (**eventual consistency**). Yes, there's a micro-window of desync between them, and that's normal for the business.\n\n- The rule is violated for the sake of \"having it all consistent at once and immediately\" — it seems simpler.\n- It hurts via **locks and contention**: a long tx holds locks on several aggregates, the chance of deadlocks grows, throughput drops, sharding gets hard.","quizCorrect":"In a separate transaction via a domain event (eventual consistency)","quizWrong":["In the same transaction, but with a pessimistic lock on both aggregates at once","In the same transaction — that guarantees strong consistency","Via a distributed 2PC transaction across the aggregates"],"picCap":"1 tx = 1 aggregate; the second changes later — via an event from the outbox"},"p-ddd-ref-by-id":{"q":"Why does an aggregate reference another aggregate by ID (clientId), not by object (client)?","a":"You hold a clientId field, not a client. An object reference drags in loading the other aggregate, graph bloat, extra locks and the temptation to change someone else's invariants. ID is a weak link: the aggregate stays small and loads independently.","d":"An object pointer (`order.client.address`) provokes: huge lazy graphs, N+1, and editing Client from within an Order transaction (a violation of \"1 tx = 1 aggregate\"). By ID, aggregates stay small and independently loadable; showing the client's name in the UI is the job of the read model/CQRS, not of the domain graph. In JPA: instead of `@ManyToOne Client`, just store a `UUID clientId`.","hook":"In your address book — a friend's phone number, not the friend himself. Need to — you'll call.","more":"Inside an aggregate we hold `clientId` (a value), not a reference to the `Client` object. This decides what gets loaded into memory: an ID is just a number/UUID, and the other aggregate is NOT pulled along.\n\nAn object reference provokes loading the whole graph of related aggregates, extra SQL/locks and — most dangerously — the temptation to call `client.setX()` and change someone else's invariant, bypassing its boundary.\n\n- An ID is a **weak link**: aggregates load and live independently, each is responsible only for itself.\n- Need the client's data — load it separately through the repository by that ID. Pitfall: lazy ORM proxies mask heavy lazy-loading of the graph, and it \"surfaces\" unexpectedly in prod.","quizCorrect":"ID is a weak link: the other aggregate loads independently, the graph doesn't bloat","quizWrong":["An object reference is technically impossible in JPA","An ID serializes faster to JSON in an API response","An ID is needed only to avoid circular dependencies in code between the model classes"],"picCap":"Linked by ID, not by a pointer","note":"Referencing by ID breaks declarative DB referential integrity: instead of an FK constraint between aggregate tables you get a bare UUID column with no foreign key (a deliberate DDD choice — you don't put FKs between aggregates). So cross-aggregate consistency becomes eventual and relies on domain logic/sagas, and deleting a client won't cascade automatically — you need explicit checks or soft-delete."},"p-hex-api-vs-spi":{"q":"Inbound Port (API) and Outbound Port (SPI) — what's the difference and who implements whom?","a":"API — the INBOUND port: it's called by an adapter (REST/Kafka), implemented by the use case. SPI — the OUTBOUND port: it's called by the domain, implemented by an adapter (MinIO/JDBC). Both are interfaces, but the dependency arrows always point inward, toward the domain.","d":"Inbound (API): controller → interface GenerateDocumentAPI → implemented by the use case. Outbound (SPI): use case → interface StorageSPI → implemented by MinioStorageAdapter. The key is the direction of control: for SPI the domain DECLARES the contract it needs, and the infra adapts to it (Dependency Inversion). That's why swapping Postgres→MinIO = a new adapter, the domain stays untouched. Names from the audit reference: *API for inbound, *SPI/*Port for outbound.","hook":"A socket in the wall: API — where you get plugged in, SPI — where you plug yourself in. The house dictates the standard.","more":"Both are interfaces; the difference is the direction of the call. **API (inbound port)** is the entry into the application: it's CALLED by an external adapter (REST controller, Kafka consumer), and IMPLEMENTED by the use case inside the core.\n\n**SPI (outbound port)** is the exit: it's declared and CALLED by the domain (\"I need to save a file\"), and IMPLEMENTED by an external adapter (MinIO, JDBC, REST client).\n\n- The key — **dependency arrows always point inward, toward the domain**: both the controller and the DB adapter depend on the core, not the other way around.\n- That's why the core doesn't know whether Postgres or Kafka is outside — they can be swapped for mocks/another technology. Pitfall: if a type from the adapter (JPA entity, DTO) leaks into a port's signature — the dependency inversion is broken.","quizCorrect":"API is implemented by the use case; SPI is implemented by an adapter (MinIO/JDBC)","quizWrong":["API is implemented by the adapter, SPI is implemented by the domain of the application","Both are implemented by the domain, adapters only call them","API is the REST controller, SPI is the JPA repository"],"picCap":"API — entry (request inward), SPI — exit (the domain calls outward)","note":"A subtle point is where @Transactional sits: the transaction belongs to the inbound side (the use case implementing the API), not the outbound adapter, because one scenario = one transaction spanning several SPI calls. If you put @Transactional on the repository adapter, two port calls within one use case end up in different transactions and the scenario's atomicity breaks."},"p-ddd-domain-vs-app-service":{"q":"Domain Service vs Application Service — where's the business logic, and where's the orchestration?","a":"Domain Service — pure domain logic that didn't fit into a single aggregate (TaxCalculator), it does NOT know about infra. Application Service (use case) — a thin conductor: opened the tx, loaded the aggregate, called a method, saved, published an event. There are NO business rules in it.","d":"A common interview mix-up. Domain Service is a verb that belongs to no single entity, but it's still domain (stateless, no DB/Kafka). Application Service = your use case: load → call → save → publish, one transaction, one aggregate. If ifs with business rules appear in the Application Service — they leaked out of the domain (an anemic model). An infra service (emails, payment gateway) lives behind a port — that's no longer a domain service.","hook":"The chef works out the recipe (domain), the waiter carries it and rings up the order (app), the courier delivers it (infra).","more":"**Domain Service** appears when the logic is purely domain but doesn't fit into one aggregate (for example, computing tax across several entities). It operates on domain objects and does NOT know about the DB, Kafka, transactions.\n\n**Application Service** (use case) is the scenario orchestrator. Its work is mechanical: open a transaction, load the aggregate through a port, call its method, save, publish an event.\n\n- The main rule: there are NO business rules in the Application Service — only the sequence of \"conductor\" steps.\n- Pitfall: when `if`s with business conditions (\"if the client is VIP, then a discount\") creep into the use case — logic leaks out of the domain into orchestration, the domain becomes anemic, the rules get duplicated.","quizCorrect":"In the Domain Service; the Application Service is thin orchestration without business rules","quizWrong":["In the Application Service; the Domain Service only loads data","Evenly in both — the logic is duplicated for reliability","In the Application Service, while the Domain Service knows about infrastructure and about transactions"],"picCap":"logic / conductor / outside world"},"p-deep-shallow-module":{"q":"What are \"deep\" and \"shallow\" modules per Ousterhout, and why is a God Class a shallow module even though it has 1000 lines?","a":"A module's cost = its interface (what you must know from the outside), its benefit = its functionality. Deep = much benefit behind a narrow interface (Unix open/read/close hide disks, permissions). Shallow = a wide/complex interface for little. A God Class sticks out dozens of methods and fields — the interface is huge, it doesn't hide complexity. Many small classes (\"classitis\") = the sum of small interfaces, more complexity, not less.","d":"The interface isn't just signatures: side effects, call ordering, what's read from globals — everything the caller is obliged to know. Fix a God Class into deep modules with a narrow public API, not by splitting it into a pack of pass-through classes.","hook":"A socket in the wall: one hole, and behind it a whole power station. A bad module — a 100-button remote just for a light bulb.","more":"Ousterhout evaluates a module like this: **benefit** = its functionality, **cost** = the volume of interface you must keep in your head from the outside. A good module maximizes the difference.\n\n**Deep** = powerful functionality behind a narrow interface: Unix `open/read/close` — three functions hide disks, blocks, permissions, the cache. **Shallow** = a wide interface for a modest filling — you must know a lot from the outside, little payoff.\n\n- A God Class is shallow not because of its line count, but because dozens of public methods and fields stick out: the interface is huge, complexity isn't hidden but dumped on the user.\n- But splitting into a hundred tiny classes (**classitis**) doesn't cure it either: the total interface and the links between them only grow. The goal isn't \"few lines\" but a narrow interface over hidden complexity.","quizCorrect":"A huge interface outward for little benefit — complexity isn't hidden","quizWrong":["Any class over 500 lines is automatically considered shallow by that rule","Shallow — because it's reused in a single place","Due to low test coverage the benefit tends to zero"],"picCap":"Benefit/cost: a narrow interface + thick logic wins","note":"A counterintuitive corollary of Ousterhout is that a getter/setter per field makes a module shallow: they widen the interface to the size of the internal state and leak implementation details, directly contradicting \"narrow interface.\" So an anemic model of nothing but getX/setX is a set of shallow modules, and splitting a God Class into such DTO-like classes doesn't deepen anything — it multiplies shallowness."},"p-info-leakage-temporal":{"q":"What is information leakage, and why is splitting code by execution order (read→modify→write across 3 classes) an antipattern?","a":"Leakage is when one decision (file format, protocol) is known by 2+ modules at once: change it in one and you fix it everywhere, even if it doesn't show in the interfaces (a back-door coupling). Temporal decomposition — structuring by time (first read, then modify, then write) instead of by knowledge. Operations that share one piece of knowledge (the format) end up in different classes and leak into each other. Structure around KNOWLEDGE, not around the order of steps.","d":"Cure: hide the secret in one place, or merge the leaking modules. Same goes for exposing config parameters outward \"just in case\": that's a leak of responsibility, work out good defaults yourself.","hook":"A borscht recipe in two heads: one changes it, the other isn't in the loop — the soup is ruined. Keep the recipe in one book.","more":"**Information leakage** is when one design decision (file format, protocol, schema) \"lives\" in several modules at once. Change the format in one — you're obliged to fix the rest in sync, even if their interfaces say nothing about it (a hidden, back-door coupling).\n\n**Temporal decomposition** splits a program by execution order: read → modify → write = three classes. But these three steps share ONE piece of knowledge — the data format. As a result, knowledge of the format is smeared across all three, and any change leaks between them.\n\n- Structure around **knowledge**, not around steps in time: a class that knows the format both reads and writes it.\n- Sign of trouble: two modules always change together, even though the API between them is \"clean\".","quizCorrect":"Temporal decomposition: one piece of knowledge (the format) leaks into different classes","quizWrong":["Three classes are slower than one because of method calls","The single-responsibility principle is violated by definition and nothing else besides that","The JVM can't inline the methods of three classes in time"],"picCap":"Format knowledge smeared across two → gather into one","note":"Common false signal: \"two classes always change together\" is NOT always leakage — it can be a healthy decomposition around shared knowledge hidden behind an abstraction (e.g. encoder/decoder of one format). The leak is precisely when both know the decision DIRECTLY, with no shared owner; cure it by extracting a third knowledge-holder class, not by mechanically merging the two."},"p-define-errors-out":{"q":"What does \"define the error so it doesn't exist\" mean, and why does it cut complexity more than a tidy try/catch?","a":"Every throw is a new branch in the interface the caller is obliged to know about. The best strategy is to design the API so the error simply CAN'T arise. Example: delete(start,end) on an already-empty range = a no-op, not an exception. Unix unlink of an open file doesn't fail — it defers deletion. This way the number of places that even think about errors tends to zero.","d":"Order of preference: 1) eliminate the error by design; 2) mask it low down (TCP hides packet loss); 3) aggregate handling into one high-level handler; 4) for truly-unrecoverable cases — just crash, don't write never-tested recovery.","hook":"A door that doesn't slam: don't install a slam sensor, fit a door closer — the problem vanishes by itself.","more":"Every `throw` adds a new branch to the interface that the caller is OBLIGED to remember: check it, handle it, rethrow it. Interface complexity grows with the number of exceptions, not the number of methods.\n\nThe idea — **define errors out of existence**: design the semantics so that the \"error\" situation becomes a normal case. `delete(start, end)` over an empty range is just a **no-op**, not an exception. Unix `unlink` of an open file doesn't fail — it marks it for deletion and cleans up when the last descriptor closes.\n\n- This way the number of places thinking about errors tends to zero — stronger than a tidy try/catch.\n- Pitfall: don't mask real bugs (an invalid argument, a violated invariant) — those can't be hidden.","quizCorrect":"Design the API so the error doesn't arise: delete of an empty range is a no-op","quizWrong":["Catch all exceptions in one global try/catch","Replace exceptions with return codes for speed","Log the error and continue without rethrowing it upward to the caller at all, silently"],"picCap":"Fewer exceptions in the contract = fewer branches for everyone","note":"Dangerous over-application: \"define errors out of existence\" easily degrades into swallowing real bugs — returning null/empty collection/silent no-op instead of a violated invariant hides the defect until it surfaces far from its cause. Ousterhout's rule: reduce the FREQUENCY of exceptional cases via semantic design, not silence an error that already occurred (that's the \"exception masking\" antipattern)."},"p-legacy-code-no-tests":{"q":"What is legacy code per Feathers, and why is it \"scary\" to change?","a":"Legacy = code WITHOUT tests — doesn't matter if it's new or old, pretty or not. With no tests, you can't be sure your change preserved behavior. You're changing blind: \"Edit and Pray\". The cure — first cover it with tests (\"Cover and Modify\"), then change.","d":"The algorithm for changing legacy (strict order): 1) find change points, 2) find test points (where you can observe and isolate), 3) break dependencies so the class fits into a test harness, 4) write characterization tests, 5) only now change and refactor. The main effort is step 3.","hook":"Fixing wiring in a dark room by feel vs first turning on the light (tests) — then working.","more":"Feathers' definition is harsh and counterintuitive: **legacy code = code without tests**. Age, style, cleanliness don't matter. Code written yesterday with no tests is already legacy.\n\nThe logic is simple: without tests you can't prove a change PRESERVED behavior. Any change is blind, in **\"Edit and Pray\"** mode.\n\n- The cure is **\"Cover and Modify\"**: first cover the area with tests, pin down current behavior, then change.\n- The chicken-and-egg paradox: to test safely you often have to change the code a little first (introduce a seam) — that's exactly why safe refactoring techniques exist.","quizCorrect":"Any code without tests, regardless of age and quality","quizWrong":["Old code written in outdated technologies","Code without documentation and comments","Code that nobody has maintained for a long time at all"],"picCap":"Tests = a vise: without them a change is blind, with them regressions get caught","note":"Characterization-test trap: they pin down CURRENT behavior, bugs included — if the legacy returns a wrong result, the correct characterization test must pass on exactly that wrong value, otherwise you're \"fixing\" behavior before you've covered it. First pin down what exists (even if broken), then change the spec as a separate, deliberate step."},"p-seam-enabling-point":{"q":"What is a seam and an enabling point — how do you substitute behavior WITHOUT editing the place itself?","a":"A seam is a place where you can change a program's behavior without editing the code AT THAT place. Every seam has an enabling point — where you choose which behavior to plug in. The best seam is an object seam: depend on an interface, and in the test inject a fake. You change behavior AT the enabling point, not in the class body.","d":"Seam types: object seam (polymorphism/DI — choosing the implementation at construction; the best one), link seam (swapping a library at build/classpath time), preprocessor seam (#ifdef, C/C++ only). The object seam survives refactoring and needs no build tricks.","hook":"A wall socket: you change what you plug in (a lamp / a fake) without rewiring inside the wall.","more":"A **seam** is a place where program behavior can be substituted without editing the code AT THAT place. It gives you a point to insert a test without rewriting the logic itself.\n\nEvery seam has an **enabling point** — the place where you choose which behavior to activate. For an object seam, the enabling point is where it's decided which object to plug in (constructor, DI, setter).\n\n- The best kind is the **object seam**: the class depends on an interface, in prod it's the real implementation, in tests you inject a fake/mock.\n- The essence: you change behavior AT the enabling point (from outside), without touching the class body — so the change is safe and reversible.","quizCorrect":"A place where you can change behavior without editing the code at that place","quizWrong":["The boundary between two modules of the system","The place where code most often breaks on changes","The junction point of two different architectural layers inside the application"],"picCap":"Substitution at the junction, not inside","note":"Object-seam gotcha in Java: substitutability requires the method not be private/static/final — otherwise there is no seam (you can't override it), and you must introduce one via refactoring (Extract Interface, wrap a static in an instance method). That's exactly why static utilities and `new` inline in a method body are the prime killers of testability: there is no enabling point, and nothing to mock without PowerMock/bytecode instrumentation."},"p-sprout-wrap":{"q":"You need to add logic to a God class that can't be tested. How — Sprout and Wrap?","a":"Don't write new code into the swamp. Sprout Method: put the new logic into a SEPARATE new method, covered by tests, and call it from the old one. Wrap Method: rename the original, create a wrapper with the old name that calls both old and new. You don't touch the old code; the new code is clean and tested.","d":"Sprout Class — if the new code needs different dependencies or the old class can't be instantiated at all: put it in a new class, the seam = that class. Wrap Class (Decorator) — wrap the whole class with the same interface, adding behavior broadly. Trade-off: the old code stays untested, but the rot is stopped — you pay down the debt later.","hook":"You don't build into a rotten house from inside — you attach a new clean addition with its own entrance.","more":"The idea behind both techniques is not to write clean new code into the untested swamp of a God class.\n\n**Sprout Method**: move the new logic into a SEPARATE new method (or class) that you immediately cover with tests, and just call it from the old code. You barely touch the old body — one call line.\n\n**Wrap Method**: rename the original method, create a new one with the former name as a wrapper that calls both the old logic and the new. Callers notice nothing.\n\n- Benefit: the new code is born clean and tested, the risk of breaking legacy is minimal.\n- Pitfall: the God class doesn't shrink — this is a \"add safely\" tactic, not a \"clean up\" one.","quizCorrect":"Put the new logic into a separate tested method, called from the old one","quizWrong":["Split the God class into several small classes right away before writing any tests","Fully rewrite the old method with tests","Move the new logic into a separate microservice"],"picCap":"A new sprout splits off from the God class — clean and covered by tests","note":"Sprout Method tempts you to make the new method private and call it from the legacy code — but then you can't reach it directly from a test without reflection, and the whole point (testability) is lost. Make the sprout at least package-private (or extract a Sprout Class), otherwise you've just got the same untested code in a new method."},"p-api-composition-vs-cqrs":{"q":"The composer joins data in-memory. When does this stop working and it's time for CQRS with a read model?","a":"An in-memory join doesn't scale: to join orders with customers you have to pull large lists from services and join them in memory — memory and network blow up. When the query is frequent and the dataset is large, you build a denormalized read model (CQRS) that's updated by events and read in a single query.","d":"Rule: start with API Composition (simple, no event sourcing). The pain shows up on \"list/search across many entities\" — that's N calls + a heap join on every request. CQRS removes the runtime join: the read model is already denormalized, the price is eventual consistency and the cost of maintaining the projection. For rare queries composition is cheaper; don't introduce CQRS \"for the future\".","hook":"Running across three warehouses every time and reconciling by hand — or keeping a ready-made storefront. The storefront costs more to maintain but is faster at scale.","more":"In-memory composition is fine while you join **a few objects by id**. As soon as you need \"all of a customer's orders + customer data over a period\", the composer pulls **large lists** from services and joins them in memory — network traffic, heap, and response time all grow nonlinearly.\n\nHere you move to **CQRS**: build a separate **denormalized read model** that services populate via events (order created, customer changed). A read becomes a single query against a ready projection — with no runtime join.\n\n- \"It's time\" signals: the query is frequent and hot, the dataset is large, the fan-out latency doesn't fit the SLA.\n- CQRS pitfall: the read model is **eventually consistent** (update lag), it has to be built, versioned and rebuildable from events — that's a real price, not taken \"just in case\".","quizCorrect":"When the query is frequent and the dataset is large — memory and network blow up","quizWrong":["When the system grows past three services","When you need strict data consistency in the response","When the composer is written in a slow programming language without a JIT compiler"],"picCap":"Runtime join (N calls) vs a storefront pre-built from events","note":"Moving to CQRS easily turns into a hidden dual-write: the service writes a row to its own DB and emits the read-model event as two separate actions, and a crash in between leaves the projection permanently diverged from the source. The event feeding the read-model must flow through the same transactional outbox/CDC as the source write, and the projection consumer itself must be idempotent on offset/version, otherwise a rebuild after failure produces duplicates."},"p-acl-not-just-mapper":{"q":"How does an Anti-Corruption Layer differ from a plain DTO->Entity mapper, and where must it live?","a":"A mapper moves fields between models that mean the same thing. An ACL translates FOREIGN semantics (the legacy system's crooked enums, legacy ids, denormalized fields) into your domain — and back. It lives in an adapter at the context boundary; the domain sees only its own VOs and has no idea there's a legacy SP or JDBC rows outside.","d":"The point of an ACL is to localize the pain of a foreign system in one place. If the foreign model leaks into the domain (the domain operates on legacy codes), the ACL didn't do its job: you can no longer swap the source without rewriting the domain. That's why in document-service DocNumberPort/OrderDataPort are ACLs: translation into DocumentNumber/VO inside the adapter. Swapping JDBC->REST changes only the port implementation, the domain stays untouched.","hook":"A translator at the border: only translated speech is allowed into the country, the neighbors' dialects stay behind the fence.","more":"A DTO->Entity mapper moves fields between **two shapes of the same model** — the semantics match, only the form changes. An ACL solves a different problem: it translates a **foreign model** (the legacy system's crooked enums, legacy ids, denormalized/\"flat\" fields) into the clean concepts of your domain and back.\n\nThe point is to **protect the domain from leakage** of foreign abstractions: business logic operates on its own Value Objects and doesn't know there's a legacy SP, JDBC rows or another service's REST beyond the boundary.\n\n- Lives strictly **in the adapter at the context boundary**, not in core: all the conversion grime is locked in one place.\n- Pitfall: if you let foreign DTOs/codes leak into the domain, you tie business rules to the external system — swapping it later will tear apart half the service.","quizCorrect":"It translates foreign semantics (legacy enums, ids) into your domain, not just fields","quizWrong":["An ACL works only on input, while a mapper works both ways","An ACL is data validation, while a mapper is type conversion","An ACL lives in the domain, while a mapper lives in the infrastructure layer of the service"],"picCap":"The foreign model reaches the ACL, beyond it only the clean domain (VO) travels","note":"A common ACL leak is not the legacy system's domain codes but its exceptions and types: if the adapter lets a SQLException, a vendor-specific runtime, or even a jakarta-annotated Panache entity escape as a \"domain\" object, the domain is again coupled to the source even though fields are mapped cleanly. The ACL must convert errors too — translating foreign failures into the port's domain exceptions, otherwise a catch (SQLException) surfaces in the use-case."},"de-principles":{"q":"YAGNI, DRY, KISS — what are they?","a":"YAGNI — don't write code \"for the future\", only for what's needed now. DRY — one piece of knowledge lives in one place, don't copy logic. KISS — choose the simplest thing that works. All three hit the same enemy — over-engineering.","d":"Nuances so you don't overdo it:\n\n- YAGNI != \"don't think about architecture\". It's about features/abstractions nobody asked for yet. Extensibility is built in through simple boundaries, not a pre-written \"engine for every case\".\n\n- DRY is about duplication of KNOWLEDGE, not similar text. Two pieces of code that happen to look the same but change for different reasons should NOT be merged — otherwise you get false coupling. The WET / \"rule of three\": tolerate a dup up to 2-3 times, then extract.\n\n- KISS — simple for the READER (especially a junior), not \"short at any cost\". A clever one-liner is not KISS.\n\nConflicts: sometimes DRY (extract the common part) argues with KISS (keep it simple) and YAGNI (don't breed abstraction). Resolved by context: if the abstraction is needed right now and removes a real duplication of knowledge — extract; if it's \"just in case\" — don't.","hook":"A repair: fix the current leak (YAGNI), one faucet for the kitchen (DRY), grab a screwdriver, not a combine harvester (KISS).","more":"**YAGNI** (You Aren't Gonna Need It) — don't build abstractions and features for an imagined future; add them when you actually need them. Premature flexibility almost always turns out to be the wrong kind.\n\n**DRY** (Don't Repeat Yourself) — every piece of knowledge (rule, formula) lives in one place, so you change it in one spot instead of hunting for copies.\n\n**KISS** (Keep It Simple) — the simplest working solution wins.\n\n- all three hit **over-engineering**\n- DRY pitfall: don't confuse identical code with identical meaning — gluing together accidentally similar pieces is worse than duplication (premature abstraction)","quizCorrect":"Against over-engineering — needless abstractions, duplication and complexity","quizWrong":["Against slow code — those are performance optimization principles","Against production bugs — those are testing and debugging principles rather than design ones","Against poor naming — those are code style and formatting principles"],"picCap":"The three rules fire in turn and collapse over-engineering into simple code"},"g-de-value-object":{"q":"What is a Value Object and how does it differ from an Entity? Why is it immutable and id-less?","a":"A Value Object is defined by ITS values, not by an id. Money(100,'KZT') equals any other Money(100,'KZT'). An Entity has identity (id), a VO doesn't. We make it immutable: changing it means creating a new one. equals/hashCode by fields.","d":"Entity: two customers with the same name are DIFFERENT (different id). VO: two amounts of 100 KZT are THE SAME, interchangeable. A VO captures invariants in the constructor (currency not null, amount >= 0), removes \"primitive obsession\" (passing BigDecimal+String everywhere). In Java 17 a record is ideal: automatically immutable, equals/hashCode by fields. Don't confuse with a DTO: a DTO is transport with no logic, a VO has invariants and behavior (add, convert).","note":"If a VO is mutable or you forgot to override equals/hashCode by value — it breaks as a HashMap key and in comparisons (two Money(100,KZT) end up != ). Another pitfall: equals by fields must include ALL significant fields (the currency, not just the amount), otherwise 100 USD == 100 KZT.","hook":"A 100-tenge bill: which exact one doesn't matter, any equals any (VO). But your passport is the one and only (Entity).","more":"A **Value Object** is defined by the set of its values, not by identity. `Money(100,\"KZT\")` is interchangeable with any other `Money(100,\"KZT\")` — they have no \"identity\". An **Entity** has an id, and two objects with different fields but the same id are one entity.\n\nVOs are made **immutable**: a \"change\" is the creation of a new instance (`add()` returns a new Money). So it's safe to share and cache. **equals/hashCode** are computed over all fields, not by reference.\n\nPitfall: forget to override equals/hashCode — the VO breaks in Set/Map and in comparisons. Keep invariants (currency, non-negativity) inside the constructor.","quizCorrect":"It's defined by its values; changing = creating a new one, otherwise you break equals / its use as a key","quizWrong":["To save memory via an object pool","Immutable classes are faster to write to the DB and take up noticeably less space in every row of it when it is finally persisted","So Hibernate can track dirty state"],"picCap":"Entity — by id; VO — by values"},"g-de-anemic-vs-rich":{"q":"Anemic model vs rich — what's the difference and why is the \"anemic\" one considered an anti-pattern in DDD?","a":"Anemic: classes are bare getters/setters (a bag of data), all logic in services. Rich: data and rules live together, the object guards its own invariants. Anemic is bad: logic is smeared around, the invariant is easily bypassed via a setter, there's no encapsulation.","d":"An anemic model is procedural code dressed up as OOP: account.setBalance(x) can be called from anywhere with any value. In a rich model there's no setBalance — there's account.withdraw(money), which checks \"are there enough funds\" INSIDE. The invariant is protected in one place. IMPORTANT nuance: an anemic model isn't always evil — for CRUD/thin services and Transaction Script it's fine. It's evil when there's complex domain logic that was forcibly extracted into a God service (see OrderServiceImpl 1361 LOC).","note":"The main practical catch of a rich model is that JPA/Hibernate requires a no-arg constructor and field access, which drags you back toward setters and public fields. So in a rich model invariants are guarded via factory methods and private setters, not a public setter that the ORM can bypass anyway.","hook":"Anemic = a puppet ATM: anyone changes the balance number by hand. Rich = a safe: you ask to withdraw — it checks whether there's enough itself.","more":"**Anemic model**: classes are bare getters/setters, a bag of data, while all logic and rules sit in services. **Rich**: data and behavior live together, the object guards its own invariants (methods instead of open setters).\n\nThe anemic one is considered an anti-pattern in DDD: logic is **smeared** across services and duplicated, the invariant is easily bypassed — a public setter lets you set an invalid state, there's no encapsulation.\n\nPitfall: the anemic model is fine for DTOs / the transport layer — there the object should just be data. It's an anti-pattern specifically in the domain core, where there are business rules.","quizCorrect":"Invariants are smeared across services and easily bypassed via setters","quizWrong":["It's always slower than a rich one due to extra methods on the same data","Hibernate can't map classes with behavior","It can't be covered by unit tests"],"picCap":"anemic on the left, rich on the right"},"g-de-adapter-vs-facade":{"q":"Adapter vs Facade — both \"wrap\" foreign code. What's the fundamental difference?","a":"Adapter — changes the INTERFACE to the needed one (incompatible -> compatible), 1:1, you didn't choose the API. Facade — SIMPLIFIES access to a complex subsystem (many classes -> one convenient method), you hide the complexity. Adapter is about compatibility, Facade is about simplification.","d":"Adapter: you have a PaymentPort port, and the vendor has an SDK with a different method name — you write VendorPaymentAdapter implements PaymentPort. Goal — fit something incompatible. Facade: a dozen legacy SDK classes (connect, auth, call, parse) -> one LegacyFacade.getDocNumber(). Goal — hide the orchestration. In a hexagon an outbound adapter is often = Adapter + a bit of Facade. Don't confuse with an ACL: an ACL is Facade+Adapter+translation of the foreign domain's model into yours (protection from \"leakage\" of foreign concepts).","hook":"Adapter = a plug adapter for a Euro plug into a local socket. Facade = a hotel reception: one window instead of running around 10 services.","more":"An **Adapter** fits an incompatible interface to the one the client needs: it wraps one object and translates calls 1:1. You didn't choose the foreign class's API — the adapter makes it compatible (the classic port/adapter in a hexagon).\n\nA **Facade** simplifies access to a complex subsystem: behind one convenient method it hides the orchestration of many classes. You design the interface yourself — for convenience, not compatibility.\n\nIn short: **Adapter — about compatibility** (we change the shape of one interface), **Facade — about simplification** (we hide the complexity of many). Pitfall: a facade must not turn into a God Class dragging everything through itself.","quizCorrect":"When a foreign class's interface is incompatible with the one you need and you must reconcile it","quizWrong":["When you need to hide the complexity of a subsystem of many classes behind one simple entry point","When you need to cache expensive calls","When you need a single entry into a module for all clients"],"picCap":"Adapter: a plug adapter; Facade: a single window","note":"Trap: an Adapter can be class-based (extends the foreign class + implements the target interface) or object-based (holds a reference and delegates) — interviewers expect you to name the object-based one as preferred, since the class-based variant is impossible in Java when the vendor class is final and drags in unwanted inheritance. A 'two-way' Adapter (implementing both interfaces at once) is an anti-pattern: it violates ISP and usually signals blurred port boundaries."},"g-de-proxy":{"q":"The Proxy pattern — what does it solve? Where in Spring/Quarkus do you use it without even knowing?","a":"A Proxy is a stand-in with the same interface as the real object, but it adds behavior BEFORE/AFTER the call (cache, lazy loading, permissions, transaction, logging). The client thinks it's calling the real object. @Transactional, @Cacheable, Hibernate lazy associations — these are proxies.","d":"Key idea: the same interface — so the substitution is transparent. Types: virtual (lazy-init of an expensive object), protection (permission check), remote (a network call as if local), caching. In Spring AOP @Transactional wraps the bean in a proxy that opens/commits a transaction around the method. GOTCHA: self-invocation (this.method() inside the bean) does NOT go through the proxy — the annotation silently does nothing. Hibernate lazy: returns a proxy, and on access outside the session — LazyInitializationException.","note":"A Spring/Quarkus proxy doesn't intercept self-invocation: a this.method() call inside the same bean bypasses the proxy, so @Transactional/@Cacheable on an internal call simply don't fire. Also, a proxy isn't put on private/final methods.","hook":"A personal assistant with your voice: the caller thinks they're talking to you, while the assistant filters, caches answers and admits by appointment.","more":"A **Proxy** is an object with the same interface as the real one, standing in front of it. The client calls the proxy thinking it's calling the real object, while the proxy adds behavior **before/after** the call and delegates onward: cache, lazy loading, permission check, opening a transaction, logging.\n\nIn Spring/Quarkus it's everywhere under the hood: **@Transactional**, **@Cacheable**, security, **Hibernate lazy associations** — all of these are generated proxies (via CGLIB/dynamic proxy or compile-time build).\n\nPitfall: in proxy models self-invocation (calling your own @Transactional method via this) bypasses the proxy — the annotation won't fire. Call it through the bean.","quizCorrect":"Self-invocation goes around the proxy — straight to the object, only the proxy processes the annotation","quizWrong":["Spring forbids nested transactions at compile time","The annotation works only on public methods of the top bean","The JVM caches the first call and doesn't re-read the annotation on every subsequent invocation of the method"],"picCap":"The call passes through the proxy (tx/cache) to the real object — the client of the same interface notices nothing"},"g-de-template-vs-strategy":{"q":"Template Method vs Strategy — both \"vary a step of an algorithm\". How do they differ and at what cost?","a":"Template Method — the algorithm skeleton in a base class, subclasses override steps (inheritance, fixed at compile-time). Strategy — the whole algorithm in a separate object, swapped at runtime (composition). TM is simpler but binds you with a hierarchy; Strategy is more flexible and testable.","d":"Template Method: abstract class ReportGenerator with final generate() = steps fetch()->format()->save(), and format() is abstract for the subclass. Cons: rigid inheritance, you can't swap a step at runtime, it's easy to get a brittle hierarchy. Strategy: a Formatter interface passed in the constructor — you change it without new subclasses, you mock it in tests. Rule: if ONE or TWO steps vary and the hierarchy is shallow — TM is fine; if behavior needs to change dynamically or be combined — Strategy. The modern trend is to prefer Strategy (composition > inheritance).","hook":"A recipe template: \"boil, ___ , serve\". The blank (what to cook) is filled by the concrete dish. TM is the recipe in the book, Strategy is a swappable cook.","more":"**Template Method**: the base class defines the algorithm skeleton (the order of steps), and subclasses override individual steps. This is **inheritance**, the variant is fixed at compile-time by the class hierarchy.\n\n**Strategy**: the entire varying algorithm is moved into a separate object that's passed from outside and swapped at **runtime**. This is **composition**.\n\nCost: TM is simpler but rigidly binds you with a hierarchy — one subclass = one combination, hard to test steps in isolation. Strategy is more flexible (change behavior on the fly, easy to mock) but needs extra interfaces and objects. Take Strategy when the variants change dynamically.","quizCorrect":"It's tied to inheritance — a step can't be swapped at runtime, the hierarchy is brittle","quizWrong":["Template Method can't be covered by unit tests in principle without a full integration setup","Strategy always needs more classes and is therefore slower","Template Method doesn't allow shared code for the steps"],"picCap":"The skeleton fetch->format->save is fixed; only the format() blank is swapped","note":"Template Method pitfall: the overridable steps (hooks) must be protected and the template method itself final, otherwise a subclass overrides the 'skeleton' and breaks the step-order invariant; calling an abstract step from the base-class constructor is a classic bug, because the subclass field isn't initialized yet (NPE). Strategy has the symmetric trap — a stateful strategy must not be a singleton/shared across threads without synchronization."},"g-de-command":{"q":"The Command pattern — what does it solve? Why \"wrap a method call in an object\"?","a":"Command turns a request (what to do + with what data) into an object. This gives you: queueing/deferred execution, undo/redo, operation logging, retry on failure, sending over the network. The caller doesn't know WHAT is inside — just execute().","d":"Without Command: you called a method — and that's it, the operation is ephemeral. With Command: the operation is data you can save, put on Kafka, retry, roll back (undo()). Where it really shows up: tasks in a queue (Runnable is de facto a Command), a Zeebe external task = a command to execute, event-sourcing commands, the CQRS write side. IMPORTANT, don't confuse Command with Event: a Command is an imperative (\"debit the money\", may be rejected), an Event is a past fact (\"money debited\", indisputable). Interviewers love exactly this distinction as a gotcha.","hook":"An order at a cafe on a slip: the slip (Command) can be stacked, repeated, canceled. Shouting straight at the cook — can't be rolled back or saved.","more":"**Command** packages a request — *what to do* and *with what data* — into an object with an `execute()` method. The caller doesn't know the details, just runs the command.\n\nWhy wrap a call in an object: a command can be put on a **queue** or deferred, you can **log** the history of operations, **retry** on failure, implement **undo/redo** (by adding `undo()`), send it over the network. A bare method call can't do any of this — but an object holds the context.\n\nPitfall: for a simple synchronous call this is an extra layer (over-engineering). It's justified when you genuinely need a queue, cancellation, audit or retries.","quizCorrect":"A Command is an imperative intent, may be rejected; an Event is an accomplished fact, indisputable","quizWrong":["A Command is asynchronous, while an Event is always synchronous and waits for its handler to the very end","A Command is stored in the DB, while an Event is only in logs","They're the same thing, the terms are interchangeable"],"picCap":"Requests sit as objects in a queue and go off to execute() one by one","note":"Persistent/queued Command trap: the command must be serializable and idempotent — on retries from Kafka/Zeebe it runs ≥1 time (at-least-once), so without an idempotency key 'debit money' debits twice. And undo() is not a 'DB transaction rollback' but a compensating action: for an already-sent email or external API call a true undo does not exist."},"g-de-state-machine":{"q":"The State pattern — what does it solve and why is it better than \"a pile of ifs on a status field\"?","a":"State moves the behavior of each state into its own class, and the object delegates to it. Instead of if(status==NEW)... else if(status==PAID)... all over the code, the transitions and rules live next to the state. An invalid transition (cancel on SHIPPED) is caught in one place.","d":"The \"need State\" symptom: the same switch(status) repeats across 10 methods, adding a status = edits everywhere (an OCP violation). State makes each state an object with action methods that either perform a transition or throw \"not allowed\". PRAGMATISM (anti-over-engineering): for 3 statuses a full-blown State is overkill — an enum with a canTransitionTo() method or a transition table is enough. The full pattern is justified when there are many states and each has rich behavior. In practice, in Java people often take an enum state machine or a library (Spring StateMachine) instead of hand-written classes.","hook":"A traffic light: red itself knows that only yellow comes next. You don't need a guard checking with an if whether you can go straight from red to green.","more":"**State** moves the behavior of each state into its own class, and the main object **delegates** to the current state. Instead of `if(status==NEW)... else if(status==PAID)...` scattered across the code, the rules and transitions live next to the concrete state.\n\nEach state itself decides which actions are valid and which state to move to. An invalid transition (`cancel()` on SHIPPED) is caught **in one place** — in that state class, instead of being duplicated all over the code.\n\nPitfall: for 2-3 states this is overkill — an enum + switch is enough. State pays off when there are many states, each with its own behavior, and the transitions change often.","quizCorrect":"When there are few states and the behavior is simple — an enum or transition table is enough","quizWrong":["When there are more than ten transitions — State doesn't scale and the code becomes unreadable","Never — State is always better than an enum","When you need to persist the status in the DB"],"picCap":"States light up along the allowed transitions NEW->PAID->SHIPPED; the rest are forbidden","note":"Real state-machine pitfall: the actual pain isn't the switch but where transitions live and how they're persisted — if state is stored in the DB as an enum string, renaming/removing a constant breaks reading old rows, and concurrent transitions need optimistic locking (@Version), otherwise two threads move the order from one state into different ones. Classic GoF State also spawns redundant state objects if they're stateless — those should be singletons."},"g-de-composite":{"q":"The Composite pattern — what does it solve? Why are a tree of \"folders and files\" handled uniformly?","a":"Composite gives a leaf and a container a common interface, so the client works with a single object and with a group THE SAME way, without telling them apart. A tree (a folder contains folders and files) is traversed recursively through one method. Fewer ifs of \"is this a leaf or a branch?\".","d":"Structure: Component (an interface with size()/render()), Leaf (a file), Composite (a folder with a list of children, delegates the operation to the children). The client calls component.size() — without knowing whether it's a leaf or a subtree. Real examples: DOM/UI components, AST, a menu with submenus, an org structure, a cart discount from nested sets. Interview GOTCHA: type safety vs transparency — if add/remove are pushed into the common interface (transparent), they're meaningless on a leaf; if only in Composite (safe), you lose uniformity. GoF chooses transparency.","hook":"Boxes inside boxes during a move: you ask any one \"how much do you weigh?\" — it sums up everything inside itself, not caring whether it's an item or another box.","more":"**Composite** defines a common interface for both a leaf (a single object) and a container (a group). The client works with them **the same way**, without knowing whether it's a leaf or a branch with children in front of it.\n\nThe container holds a list of children of the same type and in its method **recursively** traverses them (a folder calls size() on nested folders and files). So a \"folders + files\" tree is handled with a single call, without `if(is this a leaf or a branch?)`.\n\nPitfall: the common interface leaks — a leaf has no add()/remove(), you end up throwing an exception or leaving an empty implementation. Suitable when the data is genuinely tree-shaped and operations are uniform across nodes and leaves.","quizCorrect":"Work with a single object and with a tree of groups through a unified interface","quizWrong":["Cache the results of tree traversal for speed so the second traversal costs nothing at all","Forbid nesting objects deeper than one level","Serialize the object tree into JSON"],"picCap":"a leaf and a branch — one interface","note":"Composite production trap: recursively traversing a deep/cyclic tree throws StackOverflowError, and aggregates (size()) are recomputed on every call — on large trees that's O(n) per request, cured by caching with invalidation up through parents. Cycles (a node added as its own descendant) aren't caught by recursion itself — you need an explicit check in add()."},"g-de-cohesion-coupling":{"q":"Cohesion and Coupling — what are they and why is the goal \"high cohesion, low coupling\"?","a":"Cohesion — how much the elements INSIDE a module relate to one task (you want it high: the module is about one thing). Coupling — how much a module depends on OTHERS (you want it low: fewer connections). Goal: a module that's cohesive inside and loosely connected outside — then you change locally without breaking the neighbors.","d":"Low cohesion = a God Class: one class does parsing, the DB, and notifications — scary to change, hard to test. High coupling = you touch one class and five fall apart. Interview metrics: coupling types (content > common > control > stamp > data — from worst to best) and cohesion (functional — the best, coincidental — the worst). Tie-in with SRP: SRP is about high cohesion. Tie-in with DIP: programming to interfaces lowers coupling (you depend on an abstraction, not an implementation). This is the foundation under ALL GoF patterns — they sell low coupling.","hook":"Departments in a firm: inside a department everyone's about one thing (cohesion), and between departments there are short clear agreements, not \"everyone calls everyone about anything\" (coupling).","more":"**Cohesion** measures how much the code inside one module solves one task: a class `OrderPricing` only about computing the price — that's high cohesion. **Coupling** measures how much a module knows about the internals of others: if it reaches into foreign fields and depends on 10 classes — that's high coupling.\n\nThe goal is **high cohesion / low coupling**, because that way the system splits into independent blocks. You change logic inside a module — the neighbors don't break (low external coupling), and the module itself is easy to understand and test (it's all about one thing inside).\n\n- **Pitfall:** a God Class — low cohesion (does everything) and high coupling (drags half the project).\n- A module's boundary runs where it changes for one reason.","quizCorrect":"Inside a module everything is about one task, and there are few dependencies on other modules","quizWrong":["Many small modules with lots of connections between them and a tangled call graph between them","One big module with no external dependencies","Modules are rigidly connected, but each does one thing"],"picCap":"cohesive inside, loosely connected outside","note":"Interview trap: low coupling means no unnecessary dependencies, not zero dependencies; chasing the minimum produces an anemic model and feature envy (logic leaked into the service, data stayed in the DTO) — that's also bad coupling, just disguised. And 'temporal coupling' (methods that must be called in a strict order: init() before use()) isn't caught by the compiler — you eliminate it with a constructor/builder, not with interfaces."},"g-de-dip-example":{"q":"DIP on a concrete example: what does \"both the high and low level depend on an abstraction\" mean, and where does the arrow point?","a":"Business logic (high-level) must NOT import a concrete DB/HTTP (low-level). Instead, core declares an interface-port, and the DB adapter implements it. The dependency arrow flips: the adapter depends on core, not core on the adapter. That's the \"inversion\".","d":"Naively: OrderService -> JdbcOrderRepo (core depends on infrastructure — you can't change the DB without editing the logic, can't test without a DB). Per DIP: core declares interface OrderRepository (a port, owned by core), and JdbcOrderRepository implements it in the adapter. Now core knows nothing about JDBC at all. Key: the port BELONGS to the domain, not to infrastructure (otherwise there's no inversion — just an interface for its own sake). This is the heart of the hexagon: outbound ports are declared in core, implemented outside. The DI container (Spring/Quarkus) merely plugs in the implementation — it's the mechanism, and DIP is the principle.","hook":"The wall socket (the port) is defined by the HOUSE. Any appliance (the DB adapter) adapts to it. You don't rebuild the house for every new kettle.","more":"Usually the dependency goes \"top-down\": a service calls `PostgresRepository`, i.e. the business logic **imports** the concrete DB. **DIP** forbids this: core declares an interface `OrderRepository` (a port) on its own side, and the class `PostgresOrderRepository` in the adapter **implements** it.\n\nThe compile-time dependency arrow flips: now the adapter references the interface from core, and core knows nothing about the adapter. Both levels depend on an **abstraction** (the interface), the detail depends on the policy — that's the \"inversion\".\n\n- **Why:** the DB/HTTP can be swapped (tests, MinIO migration) without editing the logic.\n- **Pitfall:** if the interface mirrors the driver's API — there's no inversion, the abstraction must be in the language of the domain.","quizCorrect":"The domain core — the infrastructure adapter implements it","quizWrong":["The infrastructure module, closer to the DB implementation itself","A separate shared module of interfaces between everyone","The DI container that wires the beans"],"picCap":"the arrow is flipped: infra -> core","note":"A common trap in Java/Spring: for the inversion to be real, the port (interface OrderRepository) must live in the core module/package and its implementation in a separate adapter module; otherwise in a single-module project the compiler still lets core import JDBC classes and DIP exists only on paper. The guard is physical separation into Maven/Gradle modules (like audit-service: audit-core separate from adapters), so a violated arrow simply won't compile."},"inf-redis":{"q":"What is Redis and what is it for?","a":"An in-memory key-value store. Used as a cache and for fast structures: counters, queues, rate-limiting, sessions. Data lives in RAM → microsecond access, but capacity is limited by memory.","d":"Persistence is optional: RDB snapshots and/or an AOF log. Single-threaded command execution model.","hook":"A notepad on your desk instead of a trip to the archive in the basement: instant, but few pages.","more":"Redis keeps all data in **RAM** and runs commands on a single thread, so operations are atomic and respond in microseconds. Besides strings it ships with ready-made structures: hashes, lists, sorted sets, sets — handy for building queues, leaderboards, rate-limiters.\n\nWhy: take load off the primary database and give predictably fast access to hot data and ephemeral state (sessions, tokens, counters).\n\n- Pitfall: memory is expensive and finite — you need TTLs and eviction. Without configured persistence (RDB/AOF) a restart can wipe data, so Redis is usually not the source of truth.","quizCorrect":"An in-memory key-value store: cache, counters, rate-limiting, sessions","quizWrong":["A relational DBMS with SQL and on-disk transactions and a full query planner","A message broker with a guaranteed durable queue","A search engine for full-text search over documents"],"picCap":"Data in RAM → access in µs (capacity limited by memory)","note":"Single-threadedness applies only to command execution: one O(n) command (KEYS *, a large SUNION, FLUSHALL on a huge DB) blocks the ENTIRE instance and spikes latency for all clients. In prod use SCAN instead of KEYS and watch the slowlog; it's the heavy command, not throughput, that usually wrecks p99."},"inf-cacheaside":{"q":"Cache-aside (lazy caching) — how does it work?","a":"The application checks the cache first. On a miss → it reads from the database and stores the value in the cache with a TTL. On a write → it invalidates or updates the key. The cache sits \"to the side\" of the main path to the database.","d":"The main pain is invalidation and cache/DB drift. \"There are only two hard things…\".","note":"A classic race: thread A reads a stale value from the DB and is about to put it in the cache, while in parallel thread B updates the DB and invalidates the cache — A overwrites the cache with stale data. It is safer to invalidate the key (delete) rather than write to it on update, and to delete the cache AFTER the transaction commits, not before.","hook":"First you check your pocket, not there — you go to the closet and drop it in your pocket along the way. Next time you pull it straight from the pocket.","more":"With cache-aside the cache is managed by the **application itself**, not by a library under the hood. Read: check the cache; on a miss go to the DB, store the result with a TTL, and return it. Write: change the DB and **invalidate** the key (delete more often than update).\n\nWhy: the cache sits \"to the side\" of the path to the DB, so its failure does not break the application — all requests simply go to the database. Only data that is actually requested gets cached.\n\n- Pitfall: between the DB write and the invalidation a race and stale data are possible. Deleting the key is usually safer than overwriting it with a new value.","quizCorrect":"Cache miss → read from the DB and store in the cache with a TTL","quizWrong":["Writes always go to the cache first, then synchronously to the DB","The cache preloads data from the DB ahead of time, before the request","All reads go to the DB; the cache only duplicates writes"],"picCap":"Cache on the side: the App talks to both the cache and the DB itself. Miss → set"},"inf-eviction":{"q":"Why does a cache need eviction (LRU/TTL)?","a":"Cache memory is finite. TTL evicts entries when their time expires, LRU evicts keys not used in a long time. Without eviction the cache fills up and starts dropping what you actually need.","d":"Watch the hit rate. After a restart the cache is \"cold\" — a spike of load on the DB.","hook":"The fridge isn't infinite: expired food (TTL) gets tossed, long-untouched food (LRU) goes too. Otherwise the door won't close.","more":"A cache is limited by RAM capacity, so when it fills up new keys **evict** old ones according to a policy. TTL removes an entry when its time expires (protection against staleness), LRU/LFU drops long-unused or rarely used entries when there is no room.\n\nWhy: without eviction the cache either hits its limit and starts rejecting writes, or eats all memory and crashes the process. Eviction keeps exactly the **hot** data in the cache.\n\n- Pitfall: too short a TTL causes an avalanche of misses, too long — stale responses. The policy (LRU vs LFU vs random) is chosen to match the access pattern.","quizCorrect":"Memory is finite: we evict by time or what hasn't been used in a long time","quizWrong":["So that cached data always matches the DB","To speed up writes by flushing the cache to disk and freeing up the memory for new data","To replicate keys across cache nodes"],"picCap":"Eviction frees space: TTL — by time, LRU — by disuse","note":"Redis's default maxmemory-policy is noeviction, not LRU: once maxmemory is hit, writes fail with an OOM error instead of evicting, and the 'cache' suddenly stops accepting new keys. For a cache explicitly set allkeys-lru/allkeys-lfu, otherwise keys without a TTL are never evicted under volatile-* policies."},"inf-stampede":{"q":"What is a cache stampede (thundering herd)?","a":"A popular key expires, and immediately a pile of requests hammer the DB at once to recompute it → a sharp load spike, and the DB can go down.","d":"Cure: a lock on recomputation (single-flight), early refresh before expiry, TTL jitter.","note":"TTL without jitter provokes a stampede by itself: keys created in one batch expire at the same time. The fix is randomizing the TTL + a single recompute under a distributed lock (or early recompute before expiry), while the rest serve a slightly stale value.","hook":"One turnstile in the subway is switched off — and the whole crowd rushes the next one at once, a crush.","more":"A stampede happens when a hot key's TTL expires: before anyone has recomputed it, **dozens/hundreds** of parallel requests all miss the cache simultaneously and all rush to compute the same thing in the DB.\n\nWhy it's dangerous: instead of one trip to the database you get a synchronous burst → a load spike, rising latency, sometimes a cascading DB failure precisely on the popular data.\n\n- The fix: a lock/single-flight (one recomputes, the rest wait), early refresh before TTL expiry, and a small random spread (jitter) of key lifetimes.","quizCorrect":"A key expires — and a pile of requests all go to recompute it in the DB at once, causing a load spike","quizWrong":["The cache overflows, and old keys are evicted before they manage to warm up","Several cache servers fall out of sync and return different values for the same key","Requests loop between the cache and the DB due to a wrong TTL, forming an infinite loop that never terminates"],"picCap":"Key expired → a crowd of misses hits the DB all at once"},"inf-rabbit-kafka":{"q":"RabbitMQ vs Kafka — the difference in model?","a":"RabbitMQ is a queue broker: a message is delivered and deleted, with smart routing (exchanges), good for tasks/RPC. Kafka is a distributed log: messages are STORED and re-read, high throughput, good for events/streams.","d":"\"Task queue\" → Rabbit; \"event feed with replay\" → Kafka. In your project — Kafka.","note":"Ordering in Kafka is guaranteed only within a single partition, not across the topic: if the partitioning key isn't set (or differs), messages for one entity spread across partitions and are read out of order. In RabbitMQ, any prefetch>1 with multiple consumers on a queue breaks ordering.","hook":"RabbitMQ is a courier: handed over the parcel and forgot it. Kafka is a library ledger: entries stay there, re-read them a hundred times.","more":"RabbitMQ is a **queue broker**: a producer sends to an exchange, which routes by rules into queues, the consumer takes the message and acknowledges (ack), after which the message disappears. Its strength is flexible routing and \"task/RPC\" scenarios.\n\nKafka is a **distributed log**: messages are appended to partitions and kept by retention, readers hold their own offset and can re-read. Hence huge throughput and event replay.\n\n- Choice: Rabbit — when you need complex routing and work-as-a-task; Kafka — event streams, many consumers of the same data, and history. These are different models, not \"faster/slower\".","quizCorrect":"RabbitMQ is a queue: delivered and deleted; Kafka is a log: stored and re-readable","quizWrong":["RabbitMQ stores a message log for re-reading, while Kafka deletes after delivery","Both delete the message right after delivery, the difference is only in the exchange protocol","RabbitMQ is synchronous and queueless, while Kafka is just a REST event bus"],"picCap":"Queue: delivered and erased. Log: stored and re-read."},"inf-es":{"q":"What is an inverted index in Elasticsearch?","a":"Instead of \"document → words\", it builds \"word → list of documents that contain it\". That's why full-text search is instant — you go from the word straight to the documents.","d":"ES is for search/analytics, not the primary DB: near-real-time and eventually consistent. In your case — in dbp-fullfront.","hook":"You don't flip through every book one by one — you check the alphabetical index: word → the pages where it appears.","more":"An inverted index stores a mapping of **word → list of documents** (with positions and frequency), not \"document → words\". On indexing, text is split into tokens and normalized (lowercase, stemming), and each token lands in this dictionary.\n\nWhy: a search by word becomes a direct lookup of a ready list of documents, without scanning all of them — hence instant full-text and relevance ranking (TF-IDF/BM25).\n\n- Pitfall: search quality depends entirely on the **analyzer** (tokenization, stop words, language). A wrong analyzer — and what you need simply isn't found, even though the data is in the index.","quizCorrect":"A mapping of \"word → list of documents where it occurs\" for instant search","quizWrong":["A mapping of \"document → list of words in it\" for fast output of its contents","A B-tree over document ids that speeds up lookups by primary key","A cache of recent search queries and their results for repeated requests"],"picCap":"You go from the word straight to the documents, without scanning","note":"Mapping and analyzer are frozen at index creation: you can't change a field's type or analyzer on an existing field — you need a new index and a reindex (usually via an alias to avoid downtime). So 'data is there but won't match' often means docs were indexed with the old analyzer, and reindexing is mandatory, not just a settings change."},"inf-docker":{"q":"Image vs container in Docker?","a":"An image is an immutable template: filesystem layers + metadata. A container is a running instance of an image with a thin writable layer on top. One image → many containers.","d":"Layers are cached, so the order of instructions in the Dockerfile affects rebuild speed (rarely changed — higher up, frequently changed — lower).","hook":"An image is a cake recipe. A container is a cake baked from it. One recipe → many cakes.","more":"An image is a set of **read-only** filesystem layers plus metadata (start command, variables, ports). Layers are cached and reused across images, so building and shipping are economical.\n\nA container is a running instance of an image: on top a thin **writable layer** (copy-on-write) is added, where runtime changes live. That's why one image can spawn many isolated containers.\n\n- Pitfall: data in the writable layer disappears with the container — for persistence you need volumes. And the thicker the image/the more layers, the longer the pull and startup.","quizCorrect":"An image is an immutable template of layers; a container is a running instance with a writable layer","quizWrong":["An image is a running process, while a container is a file on disk with its snapshot of the file system inside","An image is mutable and stores data, while a container is just a read-only template","They are synonyms: an image becomes a container only when pushed to a registry"],"picCap":"One read-only image runs as many live containers","note":"Layer cache invalidates cascadingly: changing an early instruction (e.g. COPY . . before RUN mvn install) rebuilds ALL later layers, which is why you copy pom.xml/package.json first, install deps, and only then COPY the source. Also deleting a file in a later layer doesn't shrink the image — it stays in the underlying layer; clean up within the same RUN instruction (or use a multi-stage build)."},"inf-k8s-probes":{"q":"Liveness vs readiness probe in Kubernetes?","a":"Liveness — \"is the pod alive\": failed → restart it. Readiness — \"is it ready to accept traffic\": not ready → remove it from load balancing, but do NOT kill it. Confusing the two is a common cause of restart loops.","d":"On startup/warmup: readiness=false (no traffic sent), liveness=true (don't kill).","note":"A liveness probe hitting a heavy endpoint or an external dependency (the DB) starts timing out under load, and K8s restarts healthy pods — a cascade of restarts under peak. Liveness should check only the process itself; dependencies are readiness's job. Also don't forget startupProbe for slow-starting JVMs, otherwise liveness will kill the pod during warmup.","hook":"Liveness — is there a pulse? no → resuscitate. Readiness — ready to serve at the register? busy → don't send customers, but don't fire them.","more":"Liveness answers \"is the process still alive or hung?\": on failure → kubelet **restarts** the container. Readiness — \"ready to serve requests?\": on failure → the pod is removed from the service's endpoints (no traffic), but the container is **left alone**.\n\nWhy separate them: during startup, warmup, or temporary unavailability of a dependency the pod is alive but not yet ready — it should be taken out of load balancing, not killed.\n\n- Pitfall: hanging a heavy dependency check on liveness — and a slow DB causes endless restarts. Dependencies are checked in readiness, liveness is kept lightweight.","quizCorrect":"Liveness — is the pod alive (failed → restart); readiness — ready for traffic (no → out of load balancing)","quizWrong":["Liveness removes the pod from load balancing, while readiness restarts a failed container on the very first failure","Both restart the pod, the difference is only in the check interval","Liveness checks readiness for traffic, readiness checks for free memory"],"picCap":"Liveness restarts, readiness removes from traffic"},"inf-k8s-pod":{"q":"What is a pod and why not a \"container\"?","a":"A pod is the smallest unit in K8s: one or more containers with a shared network and volumes, always on the same node. It's pods (replicas) that get scaled and scheduled, not individual containers.","d":"Usually 1 container per pod + sidecars (proxies, agents) when needed.","hook":"A pod is a train compartment: the container passengers ride together, in one car. You couple and count compartments, not people.","more":"A pod is the smallest schedulable unit in Kubernetes. Containers inside a pod share one **network namespace** (a common IP, communicating over localhost) and can share volumes, always live on the same node, and start/die together.\n\nWhy not a \"container\": sometimes alongside the main one you need a sidecar (proxy, log agent) tightly coupled to it. A pod is the wrapper that K8s scales with replicas and distributes across nodes.\n\n- Pitfall: cramming loosely related services into one pod — you can't scale them separately. Usually one main container per pod plus sidecars when needed.","quizCorrect":"The smallest unit: one or several containers with a shared network and volumes on one node","quizWrong":["It's a synonym for a container: K8s just renamed the container to a pod","It's a group of cluster nodes onto which one application is rolled out","It's a load-balancing layer that distributes containers across different nodes of the cluster evenly"],"picCap":"A pod, not a container — the unit of scheduling"},"x-inf-kafka-consumer-group":{"q":"What is a consumer group in Kafka and how are partitions split among consumers?","a":"A group of consumers sharing a common group.id divides a topic's partitions among themselves: each partition is read by EXACTLY one consumer in the group. That's how you scale reading. If there are more consumers than partitions — the extras sit idle. Each group stores its own offset independently.","d":"A partition is the unit of parallelism: max useful consumers = number of partitions. When a consumer fails/is added, a rebalance happens — partitions are reassigned, reading briefly freezes. Ordering is guaranteed only within a partition, not across the whole topic. Multiple groups on one topic = independent readers (pub/sub over the log).","note":"When a consumer joins/leaves, a rebalance happens: partitions are reassigned and during that time consumption stalls (stop-the-world in the old protocol). Processing a single message longer than max.poll.interval.ms is treated as the consumer's death → it gets kicked out, triggering a rebalance loop.","hook":"Checkout lines in a store: the queue is split among cashiers, but a single receipt is served by one cashier.","more":"A topic is split into partitions — that's the unit of parallelism. Within one group (`group.id`) the coordinator hands out partitions so that **each is read by exactly one** consumer in the group; one consumer can hold several partitions. The offset is committed **per group**, so different groups read the same topic independently (this is pub/sub over a single log).\n\nWhy: horizontal scaling of reads and ordering within a partition (by key).\n\n- **Pitfall:** more consumers than partitions — the extras sit idle; the parallelism ceiling = number of partitions.\n- That's why the partition count is set with headroom: you can't reduce it later, and adding partitions breaks the key→partition mapping.","quizCorrect":"Each partition is read by exactly one consumer in the group","quizWrong":["Each partition is read in parallel by all consumers in the group","Partitions are split evenly by bytes, not by count","One partition is shared by two consumers taking turns"],"picCap":"Records flow from partitions — each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer in the group"},"x-inf-es-text-vs-keyword":{"q":"What's the difference between text and keyword in Elasticsearch, and why do exact search/sorting break?","a":"text goes through an analyzer: split into tokens, lowercased — for full-text search. keyword is stored as one whole string — for exact match, filters, sorting, and aggregations. If a field is only text, an exact-value filter and sorting won't work.","d":"A common practice is a multi-field: one field both as text (search) and as text.keyword (filter/sort). The text analyzer splits 'Acme Bank' into ['acme','bank'], so a term query on the whole string won't find it. keyword isn't analyzed and uses doc_values for aggregations. keyword has an ignore_above limit for long strings.","hook":"text is ground meat for searching by pieces; keyword is a whole steak for exact weighing.","more":"When a field is **text**, on indexing it goes through an analyzer: the string is split into tokens, everything is lowercased, sometimes word endings are stripped. Individual words land in the index, not the original string — so full-text search finds \"phone\" inside \"buy a new phone\".\n\n**keyword** isn't analyzed at all: the whole string is put in the index as one indivisible token. This gives exact comparison, equality filters, sorting, and aggregations (group by).\n\n- Pitfall: if a field is declared only as text, sorting and an exact filter will fail or return garbage — there's no whole value.\n- Solution: a **multi-field** — store both `field` (text) and `field.keyword` at once.","quizCorrect":"text is split by the analyzer into tokens, there's no whole string","quizWrong":["text is stored in binary form and isn't available for queries at all","text always converts the value to uppercase","text is indexed only with fielddata enabled manually"],"picCap":"text crumbles into separate tokens; keyword goes in whole as one value","note":"Since Elasticsearch 7.x string fields default to text + an automatic .keyword sub-field with ignore_above:256 — values longer than that threshold are silently NOT indexed into keyword, so exact filter/sort/aggregation over it just drops those documents with no error. Also text fields have no doc_values: sorting/aggregating over a bare text field requires enabling fielddata (eats heap and risks OOM) — the correct path is an explicit keyword sub-field, not fielddata."},"x-inf-redis-persistence":{"q":"Redis is in-memory — what happens to data on restart and how do you not lose it (RDB vs AOF)?","a":"By default Redis keeps everything in RAM; on a restart without persistence the data is lost. RDB — periodic snapshots (fast restart, but you lose the last few seconds). AOF — a log of every write (more reliable, but the file is bigger and slower). Often both are enabled.","d":"RDB forks the process and writes a dump every N seconds/changes — compact, ideal for backups, but between snapshots data isn't protected. AOF appends commands to a log (fsync is configurable: always/everysec/no), and on startup replays them. everysec is the balance. For a pure cache, persistence can be disabled entirely — losing it is no big deal.","note":"AOF with fsync everysec loses up to 1 second of data, not \"nothing\"; appendfsync always kills throughput. A background RDB/AOF rewrite forks the process, and due to copy-on-write it can double RAM usage at the write peak — a common cause of Redis OOM.","hook":"RDB — a photo of the room once an hour; AOF — a video recording of everything moved in it.","more":"Redis works in RAM for speed, but can flush data to disk. **RDB** is a binary snapshot of the whole dataset at set intervals (every N seconds / M changes). A restart from RDB is fast, but everything that arrived after the last snapshot is lost.\n\n**AOF** writes every modifying command to a log. On restart Redis replays the log and restores state with almost no loss (fsync can be configured as often as every second).\n\n- Tradeoff: AOF is more reliable, but the file is bigger and grows, so it's periodically \"compacted\" (rewrite).\n- In practice, **both** are often enabled: AOF for durability, RDB for fast backups and startup.","quizCorrect":"RDB — periodic snapshots, AOF — a log of every write","quizWrong":["RDB — a log of writes, AOF — periodic snapshots","RDB stores data in RAM, AOF — on disk synchronously","RDB encrypts the dump, AOF compresses it without loss of reliability"],"picCap":"RDB takes a periodic snapshot on a timer; AOF appends every write to a log"},"x-inf-container-vs-vm":{"q":"How does a container differ from a virtual machine and why is it lighter?","a":"A VM boots its own guest OS on top of a hypervisor — heavy, takes minutes to start. A container shares the host's kernel and is isolated via namespaces/cgroups — it's just an isolated process. That's why it weighs megabytes, starts in seconds, but gives weaker isolation than a VM.","d":"namespaces give each container its own view of PID, network, mounts, hostname — it seems to be alone in the system. cgroups limit CPU/RAM. A shared kernel = less overhead, but also less isolation: a kernel vulnerability is shared by all. A VM isolates at the hardware level (a separate kernel) — safer, but costlier in resources.","hook":"A VM is a separate house with a foundation; a container is a room in a shared apartment with common walls.","more":"A VM emulates a whole machine: the hypervisor allocates virtual hardware, on top of which a **separate guest OS** with its own kernel is installed. Hence gigabytes of weight and minutes to start, but also strong isolation — each VM has its own kernel.\n\nA container doesn't boot an OS — it's an ordinary host process to which the kernel, via **namespaces** (isolation of view: its own PID, network, filesystem) and **cgroups** (CPU/memory limits), creates the illusion of a separate system. The kernel is shared with the host.\n\n- That's why containers are light and fast, and packing them densely pays off.\n- Pitfall: a shared kernel = weaker isolation; you can't run a Windows container on a Linux kernel, and a kernel vulnerability hits everyone at once.","quizCorrect":"A container shares the host's kernel without booting a guest OS","quizWrong":["A container boots a stripped-down guest OS without drivers","A container runs directly on the hypervisor without a kernel of its own","A container compresses the guest OS image in RAM"],"picCap":"A VM drags its own OS; a container shares the host's kernel","note":"A container sees the host kernel and part of host resources, and until recently the JVM by default read the host's /proc rather than cgroup limits — so on older builds (before JDK 8u191/10) Runtime.availableProcessors() and heap sizing were derived from the node's hardware, and a container with a 512MB limit hit OOMKill. Even now you should verify the image runs a JDK with cgroups v2 support (JDK 15+ is needed to read v2 correctly), otherwise thread pools and heap scale to the whole host instead of the cgroup."},"g-inf-redis-sortedset":{"q":"Redis Sorted Set (ZSET): what is it and what for, when a plain Set won't do?","a":"A ZSET is a Set where each element has a score (a number). It stores unique elements SORTED by score. Ideal for ratings, leaderboards, priority queues, time windows (score = timestamp). You fetch the top-N or a range in O(log N).","d":"Inside — a skip list + hash. ZADD adds, ZRANGE/ZREVRANGE fetch by position, ZRANGEBYSCORE by a score range. A common backend case: a rate limiter (sliding window) — score=timestamp, ZREMRANGEBYSCORE cleans up old entries, ZCARD counts requests in the window. Or delayed tasks: score=run time, ZRANGEBYSCORE now fetches the ready ones.","hook":"A tournament table: names are unique, but always lined up by points from top to bottom.","more":"A **ZSET** stores unique members, each tied to a **score** (double). Inside — a skip list + hash, so elements always lie ordered by score, while access by member is O(1). Commands: `ZADD` to add, `ZRANGE`/`ZREVRANGE` to fetch the top, `ZRANGEBYSCORE` for a range.\n\nA plain Set only stores uniqueness, without order or weight — you can't ask for \"top-10\" or \"everything in a time window\". A ZSET solves this: score = player points, price, priority, or timestamp.\n\n- **Use for:** leaderboards, rate-limiting by sliding window (score=time, clean up with `ZREMRANGEBYSCORE`), delayed tasks.\n- **Pitfall:** a duplicate score isn't an error — the member is unique, not the score; insert/fetch is O(log N).","quizCorrect":"Each element has a score, and elements are stored sorted by it","quizWrong":["ZSET allows duplicates, while SET doesn't","ZSET is stored on disk, while SET is in memory","ZSET is faster on insert thanks to the absence of sorting on write"],"picCap":"elements are kept in score order","note":"ZSET trap for a rate-limiter: members must be unique, otherwise two requests in the same millisecond with the same member overwrite each other (ZADD updates the score instead of adding a second element) and the window undercounts; use a unique member (e.g. timestamp+UUID or a counter) with score=timestamp. Also, since Redis 6.2 ZRANGEBYSCORE's logic moved into ZRANGE via REV and BYSCORE/BYLEX flags, making ZREVRANGEBYSCORE legacy."},"g-inf-redis-hll":{"q":"Why HyperLogLog in Redis if you can just use a SET and count uniques?","a":"HLL counts the approximate number of UNIQUE elements (cardinality) in a fixed ~12 KB for any volume — even a billion values. Error ~0.81%. A SET is exact, but on a million uniques it eats tens of MB. HLL — for when you need \"how many uniques\", not \"which exactly\".","d":"Commands: PFADD to add, PFCOUNT to estimate, PFMERGE to combine several HLLs. The classic: unique page visitors per day, unique IPs. You can't fetch the elements themselves and you can't delete a single one — only the count estimate. It's a probabilistic structure: memory in exchange for accuracy.","hook":"A bouncer at a club entrance: clicks a counter for \"roughly how many people\", rather than writing every name in a ledger.","more":"**HyperLogLog** doesn't store the elements themselves — by the hash it looks at the position of the first set bit and statistically estimates **cardinality** (the number of uniques). So memory is fixed at ~12 KB per structure regardless of volume: both 1000 and a billion uniques.\n\nAn honest SET stores every element; on a million strings — tens of MB, on a billion — unfeasible. HLL pays for this with an **error of ~0.81%** and the fact that you can't fetch the elements themselves or intersect two sets exactly.\n\n- **Use for:** unique visitors/IPs/requests per day, when what matters is \"how many\", not \"who\".\n- Commands: `PFADD`, `PFCOUNT`, `PFMERGE` (combine days).\n- **Pitfall:** if you need a list or an exact count — HLL won't do, use a SET.","quizCorrect":"Fetch the elements themselves or delete a single one — only estimate their count","quizWrong":["Combine two HLLs into one","Add a new element","Get the approximate number of uniques with an error of about a percent of the true value"],"picCap":"exact count vs an approximate one for pennies of memory","note":"HLL error gotcha: the advertised ~0.81% is the standard deviation, not a hard cap, so individual values can be off by noticeably more, and the count may over- or under-estimate. Critically, PFCOUNT on a single key is O(1), but PFCOUNT across multiple keys does an on-the-fly merge and becomes expensive; for recurring unions, PFMERGE into a dedicated key in advance."},"g-inf-redis-streams-vs-pubsub":{"q":"Redis Pub/Sub vs Streams: why does Pub/Sub lose messages while Streams don't?","a":"Pub/Sub is fire-and-forget: whoever isn't subscribed at the moment of publishing will NOT get the message, and it's stored nowhere. Streams — an append-only log: messages are persisted, there are consumer groups, offsets, ACKs, and redelivery of unread ones. Streams — for a reliable queue, Pub/Sub — for instant notifications without guarantees.","d":"Pub/Sub: PUBLISH/SUBSCRIBE, no persistence, no history. Streams: XADD writes, XREADGROUP reads within a group (like a Kafka consumer group), XACK acknowledges, XPENDING shows what's unprocessed (a crashed consumer → XCLAIM takes over). If you need at-least-once delivery and surviving a restart — Streams. If you need minimal latency and loss is acceptable (a live ticker) — Pub/Sub.","hook":"Pub/Sub — a shout down the hallway (whoever's away didn't hear it). A Stream — a notice board: it hangs until someone takes it down and signs off.","more":"**Pub/Sub** stores nothing: on `PUBLISH` the broker delivers the message only to those subscribed **right now**, and immediately forgets it. A consumer disconnected for a second — missed everything, reconnected — no history.\n\n**Streams** are a persistent append-only log with time-based IDs. Messages stay until deleted; **consumer groups** share the load, hold an offset, require `XACK`, and the unread (`XPENDING`) can be reassigned to a crashed handler.\n\n- **Pub/Sub:** live notifications, cache invalidation — loss is acceptable, speed is needed.\n- **Streams:** a task queue, an event log, where losing isn't an option.\n- **Pitfall:** a stream grows indefinitely — cap it with `MAXLEN`/`XTRIM`.","quizCorrect":"Messages aren't stored: a subscriber that's offline loses them irrecoverably","quizWrong":["Pub/Sub supports only one subscriber per channel","In Pub/Sub you can't publish more than 1 message per second to a single channel","Pub/Sub requires a mandatory ACK and is therefore slow"],"picCap":"In Pub/Sub a message flies by and is lost; in a Stream it settles into the log in order","note":"Streams subtlety: XACK only removes a message from the PEL (pending list), it does NOT delete it from the stream — without MAXLEN/XTRIM the log grows forever even with perfectly healthy consumers. Second: a dead consumer's message is not redelivered automatically — you need a background process with XAUTOCLAIM (Redis 6.2+) or XPENDING+XCLAIM, otherwise items stuck in the PEL hang forever."},"g-inf-redis-rdb-aof-tradeoff":{"q":"Both RDB and AOF are enabled — why, and what actually gets restored after a crash?","a":"RDB — a periodic snapshot (fast restart, but you lose data since the last snapshot). AOF — a log of all write commands (you lose at most ~1 sec with fsync everysec, but the file is bigger and restart is slower). Together: AOF for minimal data loss, RDB as a fast backup. On startup Redis loads the AOF (it's fresher).","d":"appendfsync: always (reliable, slow), everysec (balance, default), no (delegate to the OS). AOF grows → BGREWRITEAOF compacts it. Since Redis 7 — Multi-Part AOF (base RDB + incremental). Gotcha: RDB BGSAVE forks the process, and copy-on-write can double memory at the write peak. A pure cache with no valuable data — you can turn persistence off entirely.","hook":"RDB — a family photo once an hour. AOF — a dashcam recording everything in a row. Burned down — from the video you'll restore it more precisely.","more":"**RDB** is a binary snapshot of the whole dataset on a schedule (`save 900 1`). Restart is instant, the file is compact, but everything between snapshots is lost on a crash. **AOF** appends every modifying command to a log; with `fsync everysec` you lose at most ~1 second, but the file is bigger and restart (replaying the log) is slower.\n\nBoth are enabled to get the best of each: AOF gives minimal data loss, RDB — a fast backup and a base for restore.\n\n- **On startup** Redis loads the **AOF**, if present — it's fresher than the snapshot.\n- **Pitfall:** AOF grows — there's rewrite/compaction; `fsync always` is more reliable but hits latency; a pure cache with no important data — you can run without persistence at all.","quizCorrect":"AOF, since it contains the fresher changes","quizWrong":["RDB, because the snapshot loads faster","Both at once, merging the data line by line","Whichever file is smaller in size"],"picCap":"snapshot vs a log of every operation"},"g-inf-es-refresh-near-realtime":{"q":"You wrote a document to Elasticsearch, got a 201 — but search doesn't find it. Why?","a":"ES is near real-time, not real-time. An indexed document lands in an in-memory buffer and becomes searchable only after a refresh (by default a new segment is created once every 1 second). 201 = the document is accepted and in the translog (durable), but not yet in a segment open for search. A GET by id finds it immediately, but search doesn't.","d":"refresh ≠ flush. refresh opens a segment for search (cheap, in-memory). flush writes to disk and truncates the translog (expensive). Don't call refresh=true on every write in prod — you'll kill throughput with a multitude of tiny segments. For tests/strict consistency: ?refresh=wait_for waits for the next refresh without forcing its own.","note":"A force refresh on every write (refresh=true or a small refresh_interval) under load spawns tiny segments and kills performance due to constant merges. For bulk loading, conversely, set refresh_interval=-1 and refresh once at the end.","hook":"You submitted an article to the editors (201). It'll go to print in the next issue (refresh once a second), not the same minute.","more":"On indexing, ES writes the document to an in-memory buffer and to the **translog** (for durability), but the engine can search it only in immutable Lucene **segments**. A new segment from the buffer is created on **refresh** — by default once every **1 second**.\n\nSo a **201** means \"accepted and reliably in the translog\", but until the next refresh the document hasn't made it into search yet — this is **near real-time**, not real-time.\n\n- `GET /index/_doc/id` finds it immediately (it reads from the translog), but `_search` doesn't.\n- **Pitfall:** for a test, hit `POST /index/_refresh` or `?refresh=wait_for`; but refreshing on every write is expensive — frequent refreshes spawn segments and load merges, and during bulk loading refresh is better temporarily disabled.","quizCorrect":"ES is near-real-time: the document is visible to search only after the next refresh","quizWrong":["The write failed despite the 201","You need to manually rebuild the inverted index","The document is always visible immediately — so the mapping is broken in the index right away"],"picCap":"The document is accepted (201), but visible in search only after a refresh ~1s"},"g-inf-es-shards-replicas":{"q":"Primary shards can't be changed after creating an index, but replicas can. Why, and how does it hit you in prod?","a":"The number of primary shards is fixed at index creation: a document is routed by hash(id) % number_of_primaries — changing it = recomputing everything, so you need a reindex into a new index. Replicas (copies) change on the fly: they provide fault tolerance and read scaling. Got the primaries wrong — migrate via reindex.","d":"Anti-pattern: 1000 shards on a small index (over-sharding) — each shard is a Lucene index with overhead, and the cluster drowns in metadata. Rule of thumb: a shard of 10–50 GB. replica=0 means you can't afford to lose data when a node fails. A replica can't live on the same node as its primary. To change primaries — the Shrink/Split API or a reindex.","note":"Too many small primary shards (oversharding) is a common mistake: each shard is a Lucene index with its own heap and cluster-state overhead, and thousands of shards take down the master node. A replica can't exceed the number of available nodes — extra replicas hang unassigned, and the index goes yellow.","hook":"Primary — like cutting a pizza into slices while baking: you can't redo the number of slices later. Replicas — copies of the menu, print as many as you like.","more":"A document is placed in a shard by the formula `shard = hash(routing) % number_of_primary_shards`. If you change the number of **primary shards**, the formula yields a different shard for the same ids — old documents get \"lost\", so primaries are fixed at index creation; change them only via a **reindex** into a new index with the desired shard count.\n\n**Replicas** are copies of primary shards; their number changes on the fly (`number_of_replicas`). They provide fault tolerance (a node goes down — a copy becomes primary) and read scaling (search hits replicas too).\n\n- **Prod pitfall:** you provisioned too few primaries — you hit a ceiling, migration via reindex + alias switch.\n- Too many shards on a small index — overhead; rule of thumb ~10–50 GB per shard.","quizCorrect":"The number of primary shards — it sets document routing","quizWrong":["The number of replicas","The index's refresh_interval","Aliases pointing at the index under a common name for the client"],"picCap":"P — primary (count fixed), R — replica (changeable)"},"g-inf-docker-layer-cache-order":{"q":"Why does COPY of the whole project before mvn install break the cache and make every build re-download dependencies?","a":"Each Dockerfile instruction is a layer, cached by content. If COPY . . comes BEFORE downloading dependencies, any code change invalidates that layer and everything below it — Maven downloads the internet every time. The right way: first COPY pom.xml + mvn dependency go-offline (rarely changes, the cache lives), then COPY src.","d":"Layer order = from rarely changed to frequently changed. Dependencies change once a month, code — every commit. Also a .dockerignore (target, .git, node_modules) so the build context doesn't bloat and a stray log edit doesn't bust the COPY cache. For Java this maps well to multistage: a build stage with .m2, a runtime stage with only the jar.","note":"A subtlety with go-offline: it doesn't pull all transitive and plugin dependencies, so the first real build sometimes still reaches out to the network. Also, the layer cache is invalidated by ANY change to pom.xml (even the app version), and a COPY with changing file timestamps/permissions breaks the cache silently.","hook":"Packing a suitcase: heavy and unchanging items (a winter coat) — at the bottom, socks you take out every day — on top. Otherwise you rummage through the whole suitcase for socks.","more":"Each Dockerfile instruction creates a **layer**; the cache is reused only if the instruction and its inputs haven't changed. As soon as one layer is invalidated, **all the layers below are rebuilt**.\n\nIf `COPY . .` stands **before** downloading dependencies, any change in the code alters that layer → the `mvn install` below runs anew and pulls the whole internet on every build.\n\nThe right order — from rarely changed to frequently changed:\n- `COPY pom.xml .` → `mvn dependency:go-offline` (changes rarely, the dependency cache lives long)\n- then `COPY src ./src` → `mvn package -o`\n\n**Pitfall:** in a multi-module project copy all `pom.xml` files; use a `.dockerignore` so junk doesn't bust the COPY cache.","quizCorrect":"First COPY pom.xml and download dependencies, then COPY the sources","quizWrong":["First COPY the whole project, then download dependencies and build at the very end","Combine everything into a single RUN instruction","Put COPY src at the very top of the Dockerfile"],"picCap":"rarely changed — higher up; frequently — lower"},"g-inf-k8s-configmap-no-reload":{"q":"You changed a ConfigMap, but the app still behaves the old way. What's the catch?","a":"A ConfigMap injected as ENV variables is captured at pod startup — the change will NOT reach a running container, you need a restart (rollout restart). A ConfigMap mounted as a file (volume) does get updated in the filesystem on its own (with ~1 min delay), BUT the app has to re-read the file itself. ENV → needs a restart; volume → needs a re-read.","d":"A common pain point: people assume a ConfigMap is \"hot\", while the pod keeps the old ENV. Fix: kubectl rollout restart deployment, or a checksum/config annotation on the pod template (the config hash changes → the pod is recreated automatically on apply). A Secret behaves the same way. For Quarkus/Spring with a volume you need a config reload mechanism inside the app itself.","note":"A subPath ConfigMap mount is NOT updated automatically (unlike a regular volume) — the file is frozen at startup. And even with a regular volume the update lands in the filesystem, but the app must watch the file itself; the typical pattern is a checksum annotation on the pod template so that a ConfigMap change triggers a rollout.","hook":"ENV is like dictating an address to a taxi driver at the start: the address changed — get out and get back in. A volume is the GPS updating the map by itself, but the driver still has to look at it.","more":"How the ConfigMap is injected decides everything. Object through **env** (`valueFrom: configMapKeyRef`) the values are substituted into the process **once at container startup** — after that you can change the ConfigMap all you want, they won't update in a live pod. You need `kubectl rollout restart`.\n\nThrough a **volume** (`mountPath`) the kubelet periodically syncs the files (delay up to ~1 min), and the contents on disk update by themselves — **but** the app must re-read the file itself (watch/reload), otherwise it keeps the old value in memory.\n\n- **ENV → restart the pod** (or a hash annotation on the ConfigMap so the Deployment rolls itself).\n- **volume → reload needed inside the app**.\n- **Gotcha:** a `subPath` mount is NOT updated automatically — it behaves like env.","quizCorrect":"ENV from a ConfigMap is captured at pod startup and requires a restart","quizWrong":["A ConfigMap can't be changed after creation","You always have to recreate the ConfigMap itself with a new name every time","ENV from a ConfigMap updates instantly — so it's a cluster bug"],"picCap":"ENV doesn't update on the fly"},"g-inf-k8s-hpa-needs-requests":{"q":"HPA on CPU is configured, but it doesn't scale the pods. What's almost always missing?","a":"HPA on CPU measures load as a PERCENTAGE of resources.requests.cpu. If a container has no requests.cpu set — HPA has no baseline, the utilization metric can't be computed, and there's no scaling. Plus you need a metrics-server in the cluster. No requests → no percentage → HPA stays silent.","d":"HPA compares targetAverageUtilization against (current CPU / requests). Without a metrics-server kubectl top is also empty — that's the first check. Gotchas: HPA and a fixed replicas in the Deployment conflict (HPA wins, but don't set replicas by hand). The cooldown/stabilizationWindow smooths out flapping. For non-CPU metrics (RPS, queue length) — custom/external metrics via an adapter.","note":"For the JVM there's a separate trap: HPA measures % of requests.cpu, and if the app is bound by memory rather than CPU, it won't scale on CPU at all. And a limits.cpu set below the real demand causes CFS throttling — the pod stalls, but CPU utilization looks low to the HPA.","hook":"Without requests, HPA is like a speedometer with no \"100%\" mark: pedal to the floor, but the needle has nothing to measure \"how much of max\" against.","more":"HPA takes the pod's current **CPU usage** and divides it by **resources.requests.cpu** — getting utilization as a percentage, which it compares against the target (say 70%). The metrics source is the **metrics-server**, which HPA queries through the metrics API.\n\nIf **requests.cpu is not set** — there's nothing to divide by, utilization can't be computed, and HPA shows `<unknown>/70%`. No scaling, because there's no baseline for the percentage.\n\n- What's almost always missing is exactly **requests.cpu** on the container\n- The second common culprit — the **metrics-server** isn't installed/working\n- Check: `kubectl get hpa` — if the target is `<unknown>`, that's the cause","quizCorrect":"resources.requests.cpu isn't set — HPA can't compute the load %","quizWrong":["replicas in the Deployment is too high and set by hand in the manifest","A liveness probe isn't configured","CPU can't be used as a metric for HPA"],"picCap":"Without requests.cpu there's no baseline for the load percentage — the chain breaks and HPA stays silent"},"g-inf-nginx-upstream-lb":{"q":"Why an nginx upstream, and why does the default round-robin break logged-in users?","a":"An upstream is a pool of backends that nginx load-balances across as a reverse proxy. The default is round-robin (in turn). The problem: if the session is stored in a specific instance's memory, the next request throws the user onto a DIFFERENT backend — the session is lost. Fixed with a sticky session (ip_hash) or, more properly, by moving the session into Redis (stateless backend).","d":"Strategies: round-robin (default), least_conn (to the least loaded), ip_hash (one client → one backend, sticky). weight= for nodes of different capacity. Passive health checks via max_fails/fail_timeout (OSS), active ones — in Plus. The right path at scale: a stateless app + session/cache in Redis, then any node can serve any request and round-robin is safe.","note":"ip_hash breaks behind a corporate NAT/proxy (many users from one IP stick to one backend → load skew) and re-distributes everyone when a node is added/removed. The default upstream also does no health checks (those are only in nginx plus / via max_fails), so it can send traffic to an already-dead backend.","hook":"A host at a restaurant seats guests at tables (round-robin). But if your jacket is still at the first table (session in memory) — being reseated leaves you out of luck.","more":"**upstream** — a named block with a pool of backends. nginx as a reverse proxy spreads requests across them; by default — **round-robin** (in a circle).\n\nThe problem appears with a **stateful** backend: the session lives in the instance's memory. The next request, via round-robin, goes to another instance where that session doesn't exist — the user gets logged out.\n\n- Workaround: **sticky** (`ip_hash` or `sticky cookie`) — pinning a client to a single backend\n- Proper way: move the session into external storage (**Redis**) → backends are stateless, any balancing works\n- Downside of ip_hash: NAT/proxy break the evenness, and an instance failure loses its sessions","quizCorrect":"The next request goes to a different backend, where that session doesn't exist","quizWrong":["Round-robin doesn't support HTTPS at all","With round-robin nginx deletes the client's cookie","Round-robin sends all requests only to the first server in the upstream list of the group"],"picCap":"A client request reaches nginx, which hands it out in turn across the pool of backends"},"te-pyramid":{"q":"What is the test pyramid?","a":"Many fast unit tests at the base, fewer integration tests in the middle, very few slow e2e tests at the top. You catch bugs cheaply and quickly at the bottom.","d":"The anti-pattern is the \"ice-cream cone\": a pile of brittle e2e tests and almost no unit tests.","hook":"The foundation is a heap of cheap unit-test bricks; up top sit a couple of expensive e2e chandeliers. Flip it over and it collapses.","more":"The idea is about proportions by cost and speed. At the base — **lots of unit tests**: milliseconds, no DB or network, isolating logic. In the middle — fewer **integration** tests: they check the wiring (repository+DB, two modules). At the top — a handful of **e2e** tests: slow, brittle, exercising the whole system as a user would.\n\nThe lower the test sits, the cheaper and more precisely you pinpoint the cause of a bug.\n\n- The anti-pattern is the \"ice-cream cone\": a mountain of e2e tests and almost no unit tests. The suite becomes slow and flaky, feedback takes minutes instead of seconds, and bugs are caught late.","quizCorrect":"Many fast unit tests at the bottom, fewer integration tests, very few slow e2e tests at the top","quizWrong":["Many e2e tests at the bottom, fewer integration tests, very few unit tests at the top of the pyramid","Equal numbers of unit, integration, and e2e tests at every level of development","Only e2e tests, exercising the whole system at every level"],"picCap":"Catch bugs at the bottom — fast and cheap there"},"te-mock-stub":{"q":"Mock vs stub — what's the difference?","a":"A stub provides canned RESPONSES (it controls the inputs). A mock verifies that a method was CALLED as expected (it verifies behavior/interaction).","note":"The line is blurry: in Mockito one object is both stubbed (when) and verified (verify). The pitfall is over-verification: when verify checks every internal call, the test breaks on any refactoring even though the externally observable behavior hasn't changed. Use verify only for side effects that have no visible result.","hook":"A stub is a planted actor with a scripted line. A mock is a secret inspector: \"did you actually make the call you promised?\"","more":"These are two kinds of test doubles with different purposes. A **stub** controls the input: you predefine what the method returns (`when(repo.find()).thenReturn(x)`) to drive a specific branch. Afterwards you assert on **state/result**.\n\nA **mock** controls interaction verification: after the run you confirm the dependency was **called** correctly (`verify(sender).send(msg)`). You assert on **behavior**, not on the result.\n\n- Pitfall: don't verify everything — tests get coupled to the implementation and break on refactoring. Mock to check side effects (sending, writing); for data, a stub is enough.","quizCorrect":"A stub returns canned responses (input data); a mock verifies that a method was called as expected (behavior)","quizWrong":["A mock returns canned responses, while a stub verifies that a method was called with the right arguments during the run","Mock and stub are the same thing, just terms from different libraries","A stub only works with the DB, while a mock only works with HTTP requests"],"picCap":"Stub feeds responses · Mock verifies the call"},"te-testcontainers":{"q":"Why Testcontainers?","a":"It spins up a real DB/Kafka in Docker for the duration of the test — the integration test runs against a real Postgres instead of an H2 fake. Closer to prod, it catches what a mock hides.","d":"Slower than unit tests, but more honest than mocking out the infrastructure.","note":"Don't spin up a new container per test class — Docker startups will eat up minutes in CI. Use reuse (testcontainers.reuse.enable) or the singleton pattern with a static container and manual start, and clean data between tests via truncate/rollback rather than recreating the container.","hook":"You rehearse the play on a real stage, not a cardboard mock-up — what's in prod is what's in the test.","more":"The library spins up real infrastructure (Postgres, Kafka, Redis) in a **Docker container** for the duration of the test and tears it down afterward. The test runs against a real DB of the same version as prod, with its dialect and behavior.\n\nWhy: fakes like **H2** lie — different SQL, types, indexes, transactions. The test is green, but prod breaks. Testcontainers closes that gap.\n\n- Pitfall: it requires Docker and is slower than unit tests (seconds to start). Reuse the container per class/suite rather than spinning one up for each method. This is integration-level, not a replacement for fast unit tests.","quizCorrect":"They spin up a real DB/Kafka in Docker for the duration of the test — closer to prod than an H2 fake","quizWrong":["They speed up tests by replacing the real DB with in-memory H2","It's a library for writing unit tests instead of JUnit","They isolate tests by automatically replacing all dependencies with mocks before the suite starts running"],"picCap":"Test against a real DB, not an H2 fake"},"te-hexagon-test":{"q":"What do you test in a hexagonal architecture without a DB and Spring?","a":"The domain/use case — with pure unit tests using fake ports (an in-memory implementation of the port interface). The adapters — with separate integration tests. Ports make the domain testable without infrastructure.","d":"This is the main practical payoff of the hexagon.","hook":"You train a chef on dummy ingredients — without a real kitchen you can already tell whether they can cook.","more":"In a hexagonal architecture the domain depends only on **port interfaces**, not on a DB/Spring. So the core (use cases, domain rules) is tested with **pure unit tests**: you plug in an in-memory implementation of the port (fake/stub) and verify the logic without bootstrapping a context — fast and stable.\n\nAdapters (JDBC, Kafka, REST) you test separately — with **integration tests**, e.g. via Testcontainers, to verify exactly the seam with the infrastructure.\n\n- The gist: ports make the domain isolatable. Don't drag Spring/DB into business-logic tests — that turns a unit test into a slow integration test for no reason.","quizCorrect":"Unit tests with in-memory port implementations; adapters with separate integration tests","quizWrong":["Only end-to-end tests with the full infrastructure, otherwise it's pointless to run them at all","Run the domain against a real DB but replace the Spring context with mocks","The domain can't be tested without a running application container"],"picCap":"Ports make the domain testable without infrastructure","note":"An in-memory port fake easily diverges from the real adapter on semantics the interface doesn't express: unique indexes, cascades, optimistic locking (@Version → OptimisticLockException), Hibernate flush at the transaction boundary, case/collation sensitivity in LIKE — so the fake must be exercised by the same contract test suite as the Testcontainers adapter, otherwise green unit tests hide a bug that surfaces only in prod."},"te-flaky":{"q":"What is a flaky test and why is it dangerous?","a":"A test that sometimes fails and sometimes passes with no code changes (races, time, ordering, shared state). It erodes trust in the suite — people start ignoring \"red\", and real bugs drown in the noise.","d":"Fix the root cause or quarantine it — don't blindly retry.","note":"A common source in prod/CI is dependence on system time and time zone (the test is green during the day, red after midnight UTC), unsorted Map/Set ordering, and a shared DB under parallel runs. Cure it with a fixed Clock, explicit sorting, and data isolation — not with @Retry, since a retry hides the real bug.","hook":"A fire alarm that wails at steam from the shower: soon everyone waves it off — and sleeps through the actual fire.","more":"A flaky test is one that, on **unchanged code**, sometimes passes and sometimes fails. Causes: thread races, dependence on the current time/time zone, execution order, shared mutable state between tests, network timeouts.\n\nThe danger isn't the failure itself but the **erosion of trust**: the team gets used to \"red means it's flaky again\" and reruns the suite without looking. Against that backdrop a real regression bug slips through as \"just another flake\".\n\n- Cure: don't ignore it or slap on `@Disabled` — isolate state, remove sleeps/real time, fix the root cause. One flake poisons trust in the whole suite.","quizCorrect":"Sometimes fails, sometimes passes with no code changes; erodes trust — people start ignoring \"red\"","quizWrong":["A test that always fails because of a real bug in the code that reproduces on every single run of the suite","A slow test that bogs down the CI pipeline on builds","A test with no assertions that effectively checks nothing"],"picCap":"Flaky: same code, sometimes ✓ sometimes ✕ — trust in the suite drops"},"te-tdd":{"q":"What is the TDD cycle?","a":"Red-Green-Refactor: write a FAILING test for behavior that doesn't exist yet → minimal code to make it green → refactor while the tests have your back. The test drives the code's design.","d":"A side benefit: the code is testable from the start (no global state, dependency injection in place).","hook":"First you set up an empty target, then you shoot dead-center, then you clean the gun — the target keeps your aim.","more":"**Red-Green-Refactor** — three phases. First you write a test for behavior that doesn't exist yet: it **must fail** (red) — this proves the test actually checks something. Then minimal code to turn the test green. Then you clean up the code (refactor), with the green tests guarding against regressions.\n\nThe point isn't coverage but **design**: the test is the first \"user\" of your API and exposes awkward interfaces before implementation.\n\n- Pitfall: writing too much code at once for a failing test — the steps should be small.\n- Don't skip red: a test that's green from the start may be checking nothing.","quizCorrect":"Red-Green-Refactor: first a failing test, then minimal code, then refactoring","quizWrong":["First you write all the code, then cover it with tests at the end before the release","Green-Red-Refactor: first working code, then break it with a test","You write tests only after release to lock in the bugs you found"],"picCap":"Red → Green → Refactor in a loop"},"te-junit-mockito":{"q":"JUnit and Mockito — who's responsible for what?","a":"JUnit is the framework for running tests and assertions (test structure, assertEquals, etc.). Mockito creates mocks/stubs of dependencies (when/verify) to isolate the class under test from the DB/network.","d":"JUnit without Mockito — fine; Mockito without a runner (JUnit/TestNG) — no.","hook":"JUnit is the examiner with exam tickets and a grade; Mockito is the stand-in extras instead of live actors on stage.","more":"**JUnit** is the engine: it discovers and runs tests, provides the lifecycle (`@BeforeEach`, `@Test`) and assertions (`assertEquals`, `assertThrows`). It's responsible for the **structure and verdict** of a test.\n\n**Mockito** solves a different problem — isolation. It replaces real dependencies (DB, HTTP client) with **mocks**: `when(...).thenReturn(...)` sets behavior, `verify(...)` checks calls.\n\n- They complement each other: Mockito sets up the environment, JUnit runs and asserts.\n- Pitfall: don't mock what you're testing, and don't use `verify` where checking the result is enough — otherwise the test breaks on any refactoring.","quizCorrect":"JUnit runs tests and asserts; Mockito provides mocks/stubs of dependencies (when/verify)","quizWrong":["JUnit creates mocks of dependencies, while Mockito runs tests and makes assertions on the results","Both do the same thing — they're competing test-runner frameworks","Mockito is an assertion framework, while JUnit replaces DB and network calls"],"picCap":"JUnit runs the test, Mockito substitutes the dependencies","note":"Mockito can't mock `static`/`final` methods or constructors with plain `mock()`: statics require `mockito-inline` (default since Mockito 5) and `Mockito.mockStatic(...)` inside try-with-resources, otherwise the mock leaks into other tests on the same thread. Also, a spy runs real code unless stubbed via `doReturn().when(spy)`, which surprises `verify`."},"te-captor":{"q":"Why ArgumentCaptor (@Captor) in Mockito?","a":"It captures the argument that was ACTUALLY passed to the mock so you can assert on it later. Example: a service concatenated a full name and called sender.send(x) — the captor catches x, and you check that the string was assembled correctly.","note":"ArgumentCaptor captures a reference, not a snapshot: if the code mutates that same object after the mock call, the captor will see the FINAL state, not what it was at the moment of the call. For mutable arguments this gives a false-green/false-red test.","hook":"A hidden recorder in the room: the mock taped what was HANDED to it, and you rewind later to check whether the right thing was said.","more":"By default a mock checks that the call happened and that arguments match. But when the argument is an **object assembled inside** the method under test, the expected value isn't always convenient to describe in `verify`.\n\n**ArgumentCaptor** solves this: you say \"the call happened, and hand me what was actually passed.\" Then you pull the object out via `getValue()` and check its fields with ordinary assertions.\n\n```\nverify(sender).send(captor.capture());\nassertEquals(\"Ivanov Ivan\", captor.getValue());\n```\n\n- When to use: to check the **content** of an argument, especially one built from several parts.\n- Pitfall: for multiple calls use `getAllValues()`. And a captor is for checking the result, not for setting up behavior (use `ArgumentMatcher` for that).","quizCorrect":"Captures the argument actually passed to the mock so you can later assert on its value","quizWrong":["Sets the value the mock should return when the method is called","Replaces the real object with a stub across the whole test class","Counts how many times the mock's method was called during the test with each set of arguments"],"picCap":"The captor catches the mock's real argument — and you assert on it"},"x-te-param-tests":{"q":"What is @ParameterizedTest and why do you need it?","a":"It's a single test that runs many times with different input data. Instead of 5 copies of the method — one method and a list of values. Each data set = a separate run with its own result (pass/fail).","d":"Data sources: @ValueSource (primitives/strings), @CsvSource (several columns right in the annotation), @MethodSource (values from a method — for complex objects), @EnumSource. Each case is reported separately, so you see exactly which input failed, not just \"the test is red\". It reduces duplication and forces you to cover boundary cases.","hook":"One machine, different blanks: swap the part — same machine, its own result.","more":"**@ParameterizedTest** is a single test method that JUnit runs several times, each time injecting a new data set from a source (`@ValueSource`, `@CsvSource`, `@MethodSource`, etc.) into the arguments. Under the hood the framework generates a separate invocation per data set.\n\nThe point is to remove copy-paste: instead of five nearly identical methods, one method and a list of inputs with expected outputs.\n\n- Important: each data set is an **independent** run with its own pass/fail; a failing case doesn't hide the others and shows up in the report on its own line.\n- When to use: checking one piece of logic across many values (boundaries, validation, an \"input → expected result\" table).","quizCorrect":"Run a single test many times with different input data","quizWrong":["Run tests in parallel across several threads","Pass parameters into the Spring context before the test","Repeat the test until it passes"],"picCap":"One method — three runs light up in turn, each with its own verdict","note":"@MethodSource requires a static method (or non-static with @TestInstance(PER_CLASS)), and its name is given as a string — a rename refactor silently won't update the reference and the test fails with JUnitException only at runtime. Also: @ValueSource can't be used with arrays/multiple parameters, and @CsvSource by default treats an empty string as \"\" but a missing value as null only with explicit nullValues/emptyValue."},"x-te-verify-behavior":{"q":"Why verify(), times(), never() in Mockito — how is it different from assert?","a":"assert checks the RESULT (what was returned/changed — state). verify checks BEHAVIOR — that a mock's method was called and how many times. You need it when an action has no visible result: \"the email was sent\", \"it was pushed to Kafka\".","d":"This is behavior verification vs state verification. verify(repo, times(1)).save(x) — called exactly once; never() — not called at all; verifyNoInteractions() — the mock was untouched. The danger: overdoing verify makes the test brittle (coupled to the implementation, not the contract). Use it where there's a side effect that is the whole point of the test (sending, writing, publishing an event).","hook":"Not \"did the parcel arrive\", but \"did the courier ring the bell, and how many times\".","more":"Tests check two different things. **State verification** (`assert`) looks at the result: what the method returned, how the state of an object or the DB changed. **Behavior verification** (`verify`) looks at the interaction: whether a dependency's method was called, with what arguments, and how many times.\n\nMockito counts calls on a mock object, so `verify(mock, times(2))` and `never()` set the exact expected number of invocations.\n\n- Why: when an action has no visible result — \"sent an email\", \"pushed to Kafka\", \"hit an external API\". There's nothing to check but the fact of the call.\n- Pitfall: don't overdo it — a test for every internal call makes the code brittle and breaks on any refactoring. Use verify only for meaningful side effects.","quizCorrect":"That a mock's method was called and how many times (behavior)","quizWrong":["That the mock returned the correct value (result)","That the object's state changed after the call","That the mock didn't throw an exception when called from the code"],"picCap":"verify counts the calls to the mock, not the outcome"},"x-te-spring-slices":{"q":"@SpringBootTest vs slices (@WebMvcTest, @DataJpaTest) — when to use what?","a":"@SpringBootTest boots the WHOLE context — slow, but everything is real. A slice boots only the layer you need: @WebMvcTest — controllers only (no DB), @DataJpaTest — repositories only (+ embedded/Testcontainers DB). Use a slice when it's enough.","d":"Slices are faster because they load a narrow set of beans; the rest you mock via @MockBean. @WebMvcTest comes with MockMvc and doesn't start a server. @DataJpaTest by default rolls back the transaction after the test and swaps the datasource. @SpringBootTest(webEnvironment=RANDOM_PORT) — for full e2e via TestRestTemplate/WebTestClient. Rule: the narrower the slice, the faster and more stable the test set.","note":"@WebMvcTest and @DataJpaTest boot only their own layer — beans from other layers (services in a controller) must be mocked via @MockBean, otherwise the context won't start. And @DataJpaTest by default rolls back the transaction and swaps the datasource for an embedded one — to make it run against Testcontainers you need @AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace=NONE).","hook":"Don't power up the whole house just to test the doorbell — turn on the doorbell only.","more":"**@SpringBootTest** boots the whole ApplicationContext: all beans, configs, DB, web layer. As close to prod as possible, but slow and heavy — for integration checks of \"everything together\".\n\nSlice tests load only the part of the context you need. **@WebMvcTest** boots the MVC layer (controllers, filters), while services and repositories are mocked — the DB doesn't start. **@DataJpaTest** boots only the JPA layer and repositories, usually on embedded H2 or Testcontainers.\n\n- Rule: take the narrowest slice that suffices — tests run faster and localize the failure more precisely.\n- Pitfall: not all beans are available in a slice; anything outside the layer must be explicitly mocked (`@MockBean`).","quizCorrect":"Only the controller layer, without a DB or the full context","quizWrong":["The whole application context, but faster thanks to caching between classes","Only the repository layer with an embedded database","Controllers together with the real production database"],"picCap":"Full context vs only the layer you need"},"x-te-test-fixtures":{"q":"Test lifecycle: @BeforeEach/@AfterEach vs @BeforeAll/@AfterAll?","a":"@BeforeEach/@AfterEach run BEFORE/AFTER each test — fresh state, isolation. @BeforeAll/@AfterAll run once for the whole class (static method), for an expensive shared resource. Each is for cleanliness, All is for speed.","d":"By default JUnit 5 creates a NEW instance of the class for each test — fields are reset, tests don't share state. That's why @BeforeAll must be static (no instance exists yet) — unless @TestInstance(PER_CLASS). Expensive things (a container, a pool) go in @BeforeAll; shared mutable state between tests is a road to flakiness. Cleanup (close a resource, roll back) goes in @AfterEach/@AfterAll.","note":"@BeforeAll/@AfterAll by default require a static method, because by default JUnit 5 creates a new test instance per method (PER_METHOD). With @TestInstance(PER_CLASS) you can make them non-static, but then instance fields are shared between tests — a risk of test pollution.","hook":"All — open/close the café for the day; Each — wipe the table before every guest.","more":"These are lifecycle hooks. **@BeforeEach/@AfterEach** run around **each** test method: prepare fresh data, clean up state. This way every test starts with a clean slate and doesn't depend on its neighbors.\n\n**@BeforeAll/@AfterAll** fire once for the whole class (by default the method must be `static`), before the first and after the last test. They're used for an expensive shared resource — spin up a DB container, open a connection pool.\n\n- Tradeoff: Each gives **isolation** (more reliable, but slower — repeated N times), All gives **speed** (but a shared resource is easily \"polluted\" between tests).\n- Pitfall: mutable state in @BeforeAll creates hidden dependencies and order-dependent flaky failures.","quizCorrect":"@BeforeAll runs once per class, @BeforeEach before each test","quizWrong":["@BeforeAll runs before each test, @BeforeEach once","Both run before each test, but @BeforeAll runs earlier in the class","@BeforeAll works only in parameterized tests"],"picCap":"All — wraps everything; setUp pulses before each test"},"p-hex-inject-clock":{"q":"Why is LocalDate.now() right in the service a violation of the hexagon, and how do you test \"yesterday / end of month\"?","a":"now()/random()/UUID.randomUUID() in the domain is a hidden dependency on the outside world: the test is non-deterministic, and \"end of month\" can't be reproduced. Inject Clock through a port — in the test you feed it a fixed date.","d":"Time is infrastructure. A hardcoded `LocalDate.now()` makes the domain untestable and flaky (a test near the day boundary fails). Solution: a `Clock` (or CalendarPort) in the constructor; prod supplies `Clock.systemUTC()`, the test supplies `Clock.fixed(...)`. The same trick for ID generation/randomness — behind a port. In practice a CalendarPort exists exactly for this. Bonus: the business calendar (exchange holidays) also lives behind a port instead of being hardwired into the domain.","note":"Inject java.time.Clock specifically, not LocalDate — then in the test Clock.fixed(instant, zone) reproduces both the end of month and the midnight rollover. A common mistake is passing a Supplier<LocalDate> but forgetting the time zone: now(clock) and now() compute the date differently at the day boundary in different zones.","hook":"Don't look out the window at the sun — hang a clock you can set to any day in the test.","more":"`LocalDate.now()`, `Random`, `UUID.randomUUID()` pull a value straight from the outside world — that's a **hidden dependency**, invisible in the method signature. The test becomes non-deterministic: green today, red at midnight or on the 31st.\n\nYou can't reproduce \"yesterday\" or \"end of month\" — you can't set the system clock just for a test.\n\n- The solution is to inject a **Clock** (or TimeProvider/IdGenerator) as a port/dependency.\n- In prod you plug in `Clock.systemUTC()`, in the test `Clock.fixed(...)` with any date you need, and edge cases are checked reliably. Bonus: time becomes an explicit part of the contract instead of magic inside the method.","quizCorrect":"A hidden dependency on the outside world: the test is non-deterministic, the date can't be substituted","quizWrong":["now() is slow and blocks the thread on every call","LocalDate isn't thread-safe and corrupts data","now() is a violation only when called in an adapter; in the domain it's fine and does not get in the way of tests"],"picCap":"Time lives behind a port; we fix it in the test"},"p-characterization-test":{"q":"What is a characterization test and why must the first assertion deliberately FAIL?","a":"It's a test that pins down the ACTUAL current behavior (what the code does), not the desired one (what it should do). You write an assertion that's guaranteed to fail (assert == \"SENTINEL\"), run it — the failure reveals the actual value, you paste it in. Now the test is green and holds the behavior in place while you refactor.","d":"The key nuance: you pin the behavior TOGETHER with its bugs — deliberately. First pin it \"as is\", and only as a separate, visible step fix the bug. Characterize not the whole class, but only the area around your future change — a sparse grid is enough.","hook":"You chalk-outline the body on the floor: not \"how it should lie\", but how it lies now — so you'll notice if it shifts.","more":"A **characterization test** pins down what the code actually DOES right now, not what it SHOULD do per the spec. It's a snapshot of current behavior so that refactoring breaks nothing.\n\nThe deliberately-failing-assertion trick is a way to find out the actual value without reading the code by eye: you write `assertEquals(\"SENTINEL\", result)`, run it, and the error message shows the real output. You paste it in — the test goes green.\n\n- This way you document the behavior \"as is\", including possible bugs.\n- Important: if the discovered behavior looks like a bug — that's a separate business/team decision; for now the test LOCKS IT IN rather than fixing it.","quizCorrect":"The failure reveals the actual current value, which you then pin down","quizWrong":["To check that the test framework even works at all","To confirm the code contains a bug that needs fixing","It's a TDD requirement — first a red test, then a green one before any change"],"picCap":"First the failure reveals the real value, then the test goes green","note":"The deliberately-failing-assertion trick silently lies on non-deterministic values: if result contains a timestamp, HashMap iteration order, hashCode-dependent toString(), or Locale-dependent format, the pasted value freezes a random snapshot and the test turns flaky. Normalize such output before locking it in (fixed Clock, TreeMap/sorting, explicit Locale/UTC), otherwise the characterization test will fail on a colleague's CI."},"g-te-assertj-fluent":{"q":"Why AssertJ instead of plain assertEquals, and what does assertThat(...).extracting() give you?","a":"AssertJ — fluent assertions: assertThat(x).isEqualTo(...). It reads like a sentence, error messages are clearer (showing actual/expected). extracting() pulls fields out of an object/collection and checks them without loops.","d":"For collections: containsExactly (order matters), containsExactlyInAnyOrder (order doesn't matter), extracting(\"name\") to check one field across all elements. assertThatThrownBy(...) checks an exception and its message/cause in one chain. usingRecursiveComparison() compares objects field by field rather than by equals — a lifesaver for DTOs without equals.","hook":"AssertJ is a Lego kit for assertions: click-click-click in one line, instead of a heap of separate bricks.","more":"**Under the hood:** `assertThat(x)` returns a typed assertion object with a chain of methods (`isEqualTo`, `contains`, `hasSize`). Each throws an `AssertionError` with a **diff** of expected/actual — clearer than JUnit's bare `expected:<a> but was:<b>`.\n\n**Why it's better:** it reads like a sentence, the IDE suggests only the assertions applicable to the type, and you can merge several checks into one block.\n\n**`extracting()`:** pulls a field (or fields) out of an object or out of each collection element and checks them all at once, without a `for`: `assertThat(list).extracting(\"name\").containsExactly(\"a\",\"b\")`. Handy for checking one slice across a collection. Pitfall: string field names aren't checked by the compiler — prefer lambdas (`extracting(User::getName)`).","quizCorrect":"That the list contains exactly these elements, order doesn't matter","quizWrong":["That the list is sorted ascending","That the elements appear strictly in the order a, b and nothing else","That the list contains at least either a or b"],"picCap":"The assertion chain passes link by link"},"g-te-spy-vs-mock":{"q":"spy vs mock in Mockito — what's the fundamental difference and where is spy dangerous?","a":"A mock is a hollow shell: every method returns null/0, there's no real code. A spy is a wrapper around a REAL object: by default it calls the real methods, and you override only the ones you need. A spy is for partially stubbing legacy code.","d":"The spy gotcha: when(spy.foo()).thenReturn(x) will ACTUALLY call foo() during setup (it may throw / hit the DB). That's why for a spy you use doReturn(x).when(spy).foo() — it doesn't call the real method. A spy is a sign of poor design: the class does too much. In a clean hexagon a spy is almost never needed.","note":"The classic spy trap: when(spy.foo()).thenReturn(x) will ACTUALLY call foo() during stubbing (it may throw an exception or hit the DB). For a spy you need doReturn(x).when(spy).foo(). Also, a spy doesn't intercept a method called FROM another method of the same object (this.foo() bypasses the proxy).","hook":"A mock is a mannequin in a shop window (empty inside). A spy is a real employee whose mouth you taped shut for just one question.","more":"A **mock** is a synthetic stub: Mockito generates a subclass where **all methods are overridden** and return defaults (null/0/empty), and the real code never executes. You set behavior via `when(...).thenReturn(...)`.\n\nA **spy** is a wrapper around a **real object**: by default calls go to the real methods, and you stub only selected ones. Useful for partial substitution when the class is legacy and hard to break into dependencies.\n\n**The danger of spy:** `when(spy.foo())` will ACTUALLY call `foo()` (with side effects) before stubbing — you need `doReturn(x).when(spy).foo()`. Also a spy doesn't see the real object's internal self-calls. A spy is a smell: better to extract a dependency and mock it.","quizCorrect":"when() actually calls foo() during setup, doReturn doesn't","quizWrong":["doReturn runs faster at runtime","when() isn't supported for a spy at all","doReturn creates a new mock on top of the spy for each call"],"picCap":"A mock is a mannequin; a spy is a live person with a single mask"},"g-te-pact-cdc":{"q":"What is a contract test (Pact) and why bother if you have integration tests?","a":"Consumer-Driven Contract: the API consumer describes which requests it sends and which responses it expects → generates a contract (JSON). The provider runs the contract on its side and catches incompatibility BEFORE deploy, without booting both services together.","d":"The microservices problem: end-to-end tests are slow and brittle. Pact replaces them: the consumer tests against a stub built from the contract, the provider verifies the contract against its real code. Contracts are stored in a Pact Broker. If the provider changes a field — its CI goes red, because it broke the consumer's contract. This is about interface compatibility, not business logic.","hook":"Pact is a marriage contract between services: each one signed what it promises and verifies itself, with no witness at the wedding.","more":"**Consumer-Driven Contract:** a test on the **consumer** side describes \"request → expected response\" pairs. Pact spins up a mock server from those expectations, runs the client code, and serializes it all into a **contract JSON (pact file)**.\n\nThat file travels to the provider (via a Pact Broker), and the provider replays **the same** interactions against its real API. If a field was renamed/removed — the provider test goes red.\n\n**Why bother when you have integration tests:** a contract catches incompatibility **without booting both services at once** — fast, in each repo's own CI separately. Pitfall: Pact checks shape/compatibility, not business logic; it isn't meant for UI/browser scenarios.","quizCorrect":"The API consumer describes the expected requests/responses","quizWrong":["The provider dictates the format, the consumer adapts to it","The API gateway generates the contract automatically","A separate QA team writes the contract by hand"],"picCap":"The contract travels from consumer to provider — neither one is booted up","note":"Pact matches by example, not by schema, so without matchers (Matchers.like, eachLike, regex) the contract gets pinned to the exact values from the test — the provider build goes red as soon as a real id or date differs from the recorded one; always match on type/shape rather than literal, otherwise the test verifies data, not the contract."},"g-te-mutation":{"q":"What is mutation testing (PIT) and why is 100% coverage a lie?","a":"A tool (PITest) corrupts the code: it changes > to >=, true to false, deletes lines. If the tests are still GREEN after the corruption — the mutant survived, meaning the test checks nothing real. 100% coverage ≠ the tests catch bugs.","d":"The metric is the mutation score: the % of killed mutants. Coverage says \"the line was executed\", mutations say \"was the result actually checked\". You can run a line with not a single assert and get 100% line coverage but 0% killed mutants. PIT is slow (it runs the tests for each mutation), so you run it on critical business logic, not on the whole project.","note":"Mutation testing is slow (it runs the suite for each mutant), so on large modules run it incrementally (PIT with history / diff-only), otherwise CI will grind to a halt. And don't chase 100% mutation score — equivalent mutants (semantically identical code) can't be killed in principle.","hook":"Mutations are a saboteur who breaks the code: if the guard (the tests) is asleep and the alarm doesn't go off — the guard is useless.","more":"**How it works:** PITest takes the bytecode and makes tiny edits — **mutations**: `>` → `>=`, `+` → `-`, `true` → `false`, removing a call, replacing a return. For each mutant it runs your tests.\n\n**The logic:** if at least one test goes red — the mutant is **killed** (good, the test noticed the breakage). If they're all green — the mutant **survived**: your tests executed the code but didn't check its result.\n\n**Why 100% coverage is a lie:** coverage measures only that a line **executed**, not that there's a meaningful assert. A test with no checks gives 100% line coverage and kills 0 mutants. The mutation score is more honest. Pitfall: PIT is slow and noisy with \"equivalent\" mutants — run it selectively.","quizCorrect":"The code is corrupted, but all tests stayed green — the test is weak","quizWrong":["A test failed due to corrupted code — that's good for the suite on the build machine","Line coverage rose to 100%","The mutation sped up the test run"],"picCap":"A mutation is injected, then one mutant dies, another blinks — a blind spot"},"g-te-test-data-builder":{"q":"Object Mother vs Test Data Builder — how do you prepare test objects so tests don't break in bulk?","a":"Don't assemble the object by hand in every test (10 fields) — otherwise a constructor change means editing 50 tests. A builder sets valid defaults, the test changes only the needed field via .withX(). Object Mother is a factory of ready-made typical objects (anActiveClient()).","d":"Test Data Builder: aClient().withName(\"X\").build() — fluent, flexible, you see EXACTLY the field that matters for the test. Object Mother: ClientMother.vip() — less noise, but it breeds a method per case. In practice you combine them: the Mother returns a preconfigured Builder. The key is to centralize creation, so a model change = an edit in one place.","hook":"A builder is a burger assembler: bun and patty by default, you add only the onion if this test needs it.","more":"**The problem:** if you build an object with 10 fields by hand in every test, a constructor change breaks dozens of tests at once, and the point of the test drowns in setup noise.\n\n**Test Data Builder:** a builder object with **valid defaults** for every field; the test changes only the relevant one via `aClient().withStatus(BLOCKED).build()`. When the constructor changes, you fix a single builder.\n\n**Object Mother:** a factory of ready-made typical instances — `anActiveClient()`, `anExpiredOrder()`. Good for frequent scenarios, but it breeds a method per variation. In practice they're **combined**: the Mother returns a preconfigured Builder. Pitfall: make sure the defaults are consistent and don't mask bugs (e.g. don't make every field valid where the test is about validation).","quizCorrect":"A model change is fixed in one place, the test changes only the field it needs","quizWrong":["A builder forces objects to be immutable","It speeds up object creation at runtime","Without it Mockito can't mock the constructor of an immutable class in a test at all"],"picCap":"Assembly runs as a chain: defaults → one field → finished object","note":"A builder reused across tests as a field/singleton mutates shared state and creates test-ordering dependence: .withX() mutates the builder's internal field and a second build() inherits the value from the first test; make the builder immutable (each .withX() returns a copy) or create a fresh instance per test."},"g-te-awaitility":{"q":"Why Awaitility, and why is Thread.sleep(2000) in a test evil?","a":"Async code (Kafka, @Async, outbox) can't be checked with sleep: too early on a fast machine, too late on CI → flaky. Awaitility polls a condition up to a timeout: await().until(() -> repo.count()==1). Met — move on; not met within the timeout — fail.","d":"sleep either wastes time (you set 5s but it's ready in 0.1s) or flakes (you set 1s but CI is slow). Awaitility: atMost(5, SECONDS).pollInterval(100, MS).untilAsserted(() -> assertThat(...)). untilAsserted treats an AssertionError as \"not ready yet\". Useful with ignoreExceptions() if a transient error may arrive along the way.","note":"Awaitility only catches the TRANSITION into the desired state, but it doesn't catch the condition momentarily becoming true and immediately flipping back. For such cases you need await().during(...).atMost(...) or a stability check. And don't forget pollInterval — polling too often loads the CPU/DB.","hook":"Awaitility is like waiting for the kettle: you don't blindly set a 5-minute timer, you peek every 10 seconds and take it off the moment it boils.","more":"**Why sleep is evil:** async code (a Kafka consumer, `@Async`, an outbox poller) finishes in unpredictable time. `sleep(2000)` is either **too early** on a fast machine (the test fails spuriously) or **too long** always (it slows the build). The result is **flaky** tests.\n\n**Awaitility:** `await().atMost(5, SECONDS).until(() -> repo.count() == 1)` — **polls the condition** at an interval up to the timeout. Once the condition holds, it moves on **immediately**, without waiting out the timeout; if it isn't met within the timeout, the test fails with clear diagnostics.\n\n**Pitfall:** the condition must be side-effect-free and fast; don't use it to mask a forever-hanging bug with a huge timeout. For Kafka, account for commit/poll delays.","quizCorrect":"Moves on immediately when ready, doesn't flake on slow CI","quizWrong":["It completely disables multithreading in the test being run","It guarantees a fixed delay of exactly 5s","It mocks the async code instead of running it"],"picCap":"Polling every 100ms vs a dumb 5s sleep"},"g-te-line-vs-branch":{"q":"Line coverage vs branch coverage — why can 100% line coverage still miss a bug in an if?","a":"Line coverage = did the line execute. Branch coverage = were BOTH sides of a condition taken (both true and false). A single test for if(a && b) hits the line at 100% but exercises only one combination. Branch coverage requires taking every fork both ways.","d":"if(a || b) return x; — one test with a=true gives 100% line coverage, yet branch b is never exercised. JaCoCo reports both: COVEREDRATIO for LINE and BRANCH. For compound conditions (a && b), full branch coverage requires MC/DC, which JaCoCo doesn't give you directly. Takeaway: chase branch coverage on critical logic; line coverage is a deceptive green.","hook":"Line coverage means \"I was in this room.\" Branch coverage means \"I opened both doors out of it.\" You can walk in and never touch one door.","more":"**Line coverage** answers \"did the line execute at least once.\" **Branch coverage** asks \"were **both** sides of every branch taken\": both `true` and `false` of an `if`, every `case`, both outcomes of a ternary.\n\n**Why line coverage lies:** one test for `if (a && b) doX();` with `a=b=true` executes the line → **100% line**. But the branch \"condition false\" (where `doX` should NOT be called) is never exercised — and that's exactly where the bug hides.\n\nEven subtler: `a && b` is **two** decisions because of short-circuiting; full condition coverage requires both `a=false` and `b=false`. Branch coverage forces you through every fork both ways. Gotcha: even 100% branch coverage doesn't guarantee all **combinations** (that's MC/DC).","quizCorrect":"A line counts as covered even if you entered only one branch","quizWrong":["Line coverage doesn't account for executed lines at all","Branch coverage is always lower because of JaCoCo bugs","if-conditions aren't included in the line coverage count at all"],"picCap":"A green line ≠ both branches of the if were exercised","note":"JaCoCo computes branch coverage at the bytecode level, not the source level: the compiler expands a single ternary or a&&b into several conditional jumps, so the report shows 'phantom' uncovered branches on lines with no visible if (e.g., on a try's closing brace due to implicit finally/null checks), regularly breaking the coverage threshold without a real test gap."},"g-te-test-isolation":{"q":"Why do tests sometimes fail only in a certain order, and how do you fix test pollution?","a":"Tests share state: a static field, a singleton, an @MockBean in the context, writes to a shared DB without rollback. Test A corrupts the state — test B fails. Fix: no statics, reset in @BeforeEach, @DirtiesContext / transactional rollback, and independence from execution order.","d":"JUnit 5 does NOT guarantee order by default (you can set @TestMethodOrder, but that's a smell). A Mockito @Mock via @ExtendWith is reset between methods — but a manually created static mock is not. In Spring, @MockBean reuses the context: a when() setup from one test leaks into another without reset(). @Transactional on a test rolls back the DB after each method.","note":"@DirtiesContext fixes context pollution but recreates the whole Spring context — that kills suite speed, so it's a last resort. Prefer a @Transactional rollback or a manual mock reset (Mockito.reset / @MockBean is re-initialized automatically). And a transaction rollback doesn't clean up static fields at all.","hook":"Test pollution is like an unwashed frying pan: the next cook (test) fries on your burnt onions and blames themselves.","more":"**Cause:** tests share mutable state — a `static` field, a singleton bean, a cache, an `@MockBean` in a reused Spring context, rows in a shared DB without rollback. Test A mutates state and doesn't clean up after itself → test B depends on **whether A ran earlier**. Hence failures only in a certain order (**test pollution**).\n\n**Fix:**\n- no mutable `static`/shared singleton; reset state in `@BeforeEach`\n- DB — transactional rollback (`@Transactional`/`@Rollback`) or a clean schema per test\n- `@DirtiesContext` when the context is corrupted; for containers — a fixed seed\n- run in random order (JUnit `Random` order) so hidden coupling surfaces in CI rather than in prod.","quizCorrect":"Shared mutable state (static/context/DB) wasn't reset","quizWrong":["JUnit 5 runs tests strictly in alphabetical order","A @ParameterizedTest annotation is missing","Mockito doesn't support multiple tests in one class of tests"],"picCap":"Shared state leaks from A into B — hence order-dependent flakiness"},"g-te-methodsource":{"q":"@MethodSource and Arguments — how do you parameterize a test with multiple arguments and objects?","a":"@ValueSource provides a single primitive per run. When you need multiple parameters (input + expected result + object), @MethodSource references a static method returning Stream<Arguments>. Each Arguments.of(...) is one test run.","d":"@ValueSource — primitives/String/Class only. @CsvSource — a table, but rows are parsed into types (handy but fragile for complex objects). @EnumSource — all enum values. @MethodSource is the most powerful: it returns arbitrary objects, builders, edge cases. The source method name defaults to the test name; the source must be static (or use @TestInstance(PER_CLASS)).","hook":"@MethodSource is a conveyor with parts: one machine (the test), while the belt feeds it different workpieces one after another.","more":"**@MethodSource** references a static method (by name) that returns a stream of data — usually **Stream<Arguments>**, less often List/Iterable. JUnit runs the test once per element, unpacking Arguments into the method parameters in order.\n\nEach **Arguments.of(a, b, expected)** is one run. This is how you pass a combination of \"input + expected result + any object\" — something @ValueSource can't do (it gives a single primitive).\n\nGotcha: the source method **must be static** (otherwise you need @TestInstance(PER_CLASS)); the order and types in Arguments must match the test signature. Use it when there are many cases or you need objects/multiple parameters.","quizCorrect":"When a run needs multiple arguments or complex objects","quizWrong":["When the test isn't parameterized at all","@ValueSource handles multiple objects, there's no difference","@MethodSource only works with enum values"],"picCap":"The source yields argument sets one after another","note":"A @MethodSource returning a Stream cannot be reused: if the same factory feeds multiple tests, or you open Files.lines()/a DB stream, it will be exhausted/unclosed — return a freshly computed Stream (or a List/array) on each call, and reference an external @MethodSource from another class by fully qualified \"com.pkg.Class#method\"."},"g-te-strict-stubs":{"q":"UnnecessaryStubbingException and verifyNoMoreInteractions — what is Mockito's strict mode and why does it exist?","a":"Mockito 3+ is strict: if you set up when(...).thenReturn(...) but the mock was never called, the test fails with UnnecessaryStubbingException. This catches dead stubs and typos in arguments. verifyNoMoreInteractions checks that the mock wasn't called more than expected.","d":"Strictness.STRICT_STUBS (the default with @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension)) catches: an unused stub, an argument mismatch (you stubbed id=1, the code called id=2 → returned null, the test silently fails later). If a stub is genuinely optional — use lenient() on that specific stub. verifyNoMoreInteractions is a double-edged sword: it makes the test fragile to any new call; use it selectively, not on every mock.","note":"UnnecessaryStubbingException also fires on a matcher argument typo: when(mock.find(eq(1L))) didn't match because the code calls find(2L) — Mockito complains about an unused stub, even though the real cause is the argument mismatch. Read it as: the stub didn't fire, not just \"delete it.\"","hook":"Strict stubs are like a strict storekeeper: they handed you a key (the stub), and you opened no door with it — so they yell \"why did you take it?\"","more":"Since Mockito 3+, the default is **strict stubs**. Every configured `when(x).thenReturn(y)` must actually fire in the test; if the mock was never called with that argument, you get an **UnnecessaryStubbingException**. This catches dead stubs and argument typos (you stubbed for one value, the code calls another).\n\n**verifyNoMoreInteractions(mock)** is a separate check: after your explicit verify calls, the mock must have had no extra interactions.\n\nGotcha: for conditionally-needed stubs use `lenient()` or `lenient().when(...)`, don't silence the whole class. verifyNoMoreInteractions makes tests fragile — apply it selectively, where \"nothing else was called\" actually matters.","quizCorrect":"A stub was set up via when(), but the mock was never called with it","quizWrong":["The mock was called more times than allowed","The mock is missing the @Mock annotation","The stub argument contains null instead of a matcher in the call being stubbed"],"picCap":"The stub didn't fire → strict mode lights up red"},"web-idempotent":{"q":"Which HTTP methods are idempotent, and why does it matter?","a":"GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent — repeating them lands the same result; POST is not. So PUT/DELETE can be retried safely, while POST is only safe with an idempotency key.","d":"PATCH is not idempotent in the general case.","note":"Per the HTTP spec, idempotency is about the server's final state, NOT about getting the same status code back: a repeated DELETE may legitimately return 404 instead of 204. And your own PUT/DELETE implementation can be non-idempotent (a PUT with a server-side increment, a DELETE with a trigger) — the spec doesn't enforce it, the responsibility is on you.","hook":"A light switch (PUT): flip it ten times, the end state is the same. POST is \"pour me another coffee\" — every click is a fresh cup.","more":"Idempotency is a property: repeating the same request doesn't change server state beyond the first call. **GET** only reads, **PUT** replaces the whole resource (writing the same thing → same thing), **DELETE** removes it (repeat — the resource is already gone, same outcome). **POST** usually creates a new entity, so two POSTs = two records.\n\nWhy it matters: on a real network responses get lost, and clients/proxies **retry**. Idempotent methods can be repeated without fear of duplicating data.\n\nPitfall: POST is only safe to retry with an **Idempotency-Key** — the server remembers the key and on a repeat returns the original result instead of creating again. Without the key, a retried POST = duplicates.","quizCorrect":"GET, PUT, DELETE — repeating lands the same result; POST is not idempotent","quizWrong":["Every method is idempotent except GET","POST and PUT are idempotent, but DELETE is not","Idempotency depends on the server, not on the method and its implementation"],"picCap":"Idempotent ones you retry freely, POST only with a key"},"web-status":{"q":"401 vs 403 and 400 vs 422 — when do you use which?","a":"401 — not authenticated (who are you?); 403 — authenticated, but no permission. 400 — the request is syntactically malformed; 422 — syntax is fine, but business validation failed.","d":"Mixing up 401/403 is a common API mistake.","note":"Returning 403 instead of 404 for someone else's resource is a leak: you confirm the object exists. For private data, returning 404 on purpose is common. And per RFC a 401 MUST carry a WWW-Authenticate header — without it, formally it isn't a 401.","hook":"401 — you have no badge (who are you?); 403 — you have a badge, but this door is off-limits. 400 — a form with errors; 422 — filled in correctly, but the rules say no.","more":"Under the hood these are two different questions. **401 vs 403** is about access: 401 means \"I don't know who you are\" (no token / expired / invalid), and the server sends `WWW-Authenticate`; 403 means \"I know who you are, but you can't come in here\" (missing roles/permissions). Re-login fixes a 401, but not a 403.\n\n**400 vs 422** is about the request body: 400 means it can't be parsed at all (broken JSON, missing required field, wrong type). 422 means the format is correct, but it fails the **business rules** (email already taken, date in the past).\n\nPitfall: many APIs return 400 for everything — then the client can't tell \"I'll fix the syntax\" from \"argue with the business logic.\"","quizCorrect":"401 — not authenticated (who are you?); 403 — authenticated, but no permission","quizWrong":["401 — no permission, 403 — not authenticated","401 — resource not found, 403 — server crashed","401 and 403 are interchangeable, pick whichever you like in the response body itself"],"picCap":"401/403 — getting in; 400/422 — the data"},"web-pagination":{"q":"Why is cursor/keyset pagination better than offset in an API?","a":"OFFSET is slow on deep pages and \"jumps\" when rows are inserted/deleted between requests. A cursor (after=last_id) is stable and fast — independent of depth.","d":"Return the next-cursor right in the response.","note":"Keyset is stable only if you sort by a unique monotonic key; over a non-unique field (created_at with duplicates) rows at the cursor boundary get lost or duplicated — you need a composite cursor (created_at, id). And keyset is bad at jumping to an arbitrary page N — only next/prev.","hook":"Offset is recounting pages from the start of the book every time. A cursor is a bookmark: open it and read on, even if the book grew.","more":"OFFSET forces the DB to walk and **discard** all rows up to the requested offset: page 10000 → the engine reads and throws away hundreds of thousands of records, and time grows linearly with depth.\n\n**Keyset** (cursor) replaces \"skip N\" with \"give me rows AFTER this key\": `WHERE id > :last ORDER BY id LIMIT n`. Over an index that's an instant jump to the right spot — **speed doesn't depend** on depth.\n\nBonus — stability: with OFFSET an insert/delete between requests shifts the window, and rows get **duplicated or lost**. A cursor is anchored to a value, not a position. Downside: you can't jump to \"page 50\" — only page forward/backward, and you must sort by a unique, stable field.","quizCorrect":"Stable under inserts/deletes and fast — independent of page depth","quizWrong":["Lets you jump straight to an arbitrary page by its number","Runs faster by caching all pages in memory","Offset is faster on deep pages, cursor is only for sorting by one field"],"picCap":"OFFSET jumps and stalls — a cursor is stable and fast"},"web-versioning":{"q":"How do you version a REST API without breaking clients?","a":"Incompatible change → new version (URI /v2 or media type). Compatible change (a new optional field) — no version bump. Don't drop the old one abruptly — give a migration window.","d":"The golden rule: don't break the existing contract.","hook":"A new socket type? Add a separate one, don't rip out the old one right away — give people time to buy an adapter.","more":"The key idea is to split changes into **compatible** and **breaking**. Adding a new optional field to a response or an optional parameter — an old client simply ignores it, no version needed. Removing/renaming a field, changing a type, making a parameter required — that's **breaking**, you need a new version.\n\nWhere to put the version: in the URI (`/v2/...`) — simple and visible in logs; in a media type/header — cleaner per REST, but harder to test. For a team with juniors the URI is usually more practical.\n\nPitfall: you can't kill v1 right after shipping v2 — clients have their own upgrade cycle. Announce **deprecation** with a date and keep the old version running through a transition period.","quizCorrect":"Incompatible change → new version; compatible → none; retire the old one with a migration window","quizWrong":["Any API change requires a new /v2 version","You can silently drop old fields — clients will adapt on their own","No version needed: change the contract right in the current endpoint and warn the consumers afterwards"],"picCap":"Breaking change — ship /v2; don't kill the old one right away","note":"Adding a field is 'compatible' only if clients tolerate unknown fields: Jackson with FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES=true (or older consumers on a strict schema) will fail on a new field in the response, and an enum change (new value) breaks a client whose enum deserialization is strict; real breaking changes also hide in semantics (same type, changed meaning/units of a field), which no URI versioning catches."},"web-post-201":{"q":"What should you return on a successful create via POST?","a":"201 Created plus a Location header with the URI of the new resource (and a body, if it's useful to the client). 200 — plain success without a create, 204 — success with no body.","note":"Common pitfall: behind a reverse proxy the Location on create points at an internal host/scheme (http instead of https), because it's built from Host/X-Forwarded-* without the proxy headers set up correctly.","hook":"Hand a coat to the cloakroom — get a ticket (201 + Location) for where to pick it up later.","more":"Status semantics: **201 Created** — \"I created a new resource,\" and the **Location** header is required, carrying the URL of the fresh entity so the client immediately knows where to reach it. The body is often returned too — with server-assigned fields (id, createdAt) — to save an extra GET.\n\nDifferences: **200 OK** — success without a create (update, action, read); **204 No Content** — success with no body (delete, a PUT that returns nothing).\n\nPitfall: if the create runs **asynchronously** (queued, resource not ready yet) — **202 Accepted** with a link to the job status is more correct than 201, since the object at Location doesn't physically exist yet.","quizCorrect":"201 Created plus a Location header with the URI of the new resource","quizWrong":["204 No Content with no body and no headers","200 OK, mandatorily with the full resource body","301 Moved Permanently with a redirect to the resource that was created"],"picCap":"POST → 201 Created + Location to the new resource"},"web-tls":{"q":"How does TLS secure a connection (encryption with a key pair)?","a":"During the handshake, asymmetric crypto (a key pair) is used to securely agree on a shared secret and verify the server's certificate; after that, data is encrypted with a fast SYMMETRIC key. The CA-issued certificate confirms the server is the real one.","d":"Asymmetric crypto is expensive → used only to exchange the key; symmetric is cheap → used for the traffic.","note":"A certificate by itself doesn't prove the server is 'the real one' — the browser must also match the name in the SAN against the host (CN has long been ignored) and check revocation (CRL/OCSP). Validity and trust in the CA are not the same as a domain match.","hook":"TLS is a key exchange through an armored safe: you pass one shared key through it, then lock everything fast with that key.","more":"TLS uses **two cryptosystems at once**, because each has its own strength. Asymmetric crypto (a key pair) is reliable for exchanging with a stranger but slow; symmetric is fast but needs a shared secret the parties don't have yet.\n\nSo during the handshake, asymmetric crypto solves exactly two tasks: **verify the server's certificate** (that it's who it claims to be — confirmed by the CA) and **securely agree** on a shared session key. After that, all traffic is encrypted with the fast symmetric key.\n\nPitfall: the connection is only secured if the client actually **validates the certificate chain**. Turn that off (self-signed cert, ignored errors) and encryption stays, but a MITM can impersonate the server — and the channel is now \"secured\" with the attacker.","quizCorrect":"Asymmetric crypto in the handshake negotiates a shared secret, then data is encrypted symmetrically","quizWrong":["All session data is encrypted with the asymmetric key pair until the connection ends","TLS only signs data for integrity, without encrypting the content","The symmetric key is sent in the clear, protection comes only from the CA certificate of the server certificate chain"],"picCap":"Asymmetric negotiates the secret, then fast symmetric takes over"},"web-auth-schemes":{"q":"Basic vs Bearer authorization — what's the difference?","a":"Basic: base64(login:pass) in the Authorization header on EVERY request — only acceptable over HTTPS. Bearer: a token (usually a JWT) in the header, the server verifies its signature; the password is never exposed.","d":"base64 is NOT encryption, just an encoding. Without TLS, Basic = password in plain text.","note":"With Bearer, the server verifies the JWT by signature WITHOUT hitting the DB — so you can't revoke/log out such a token early, it's valid until exp. Hence a short TTL + refresh, or a blacklist. And the classic hole — accepting alg:none or confusing RS256/HS256.","hook":"Basic is shouting your password at the door every time; Bearer is flashing a stamped wristband, the password never shows.","more":"Both schemes put data in the HTTP `Authorization` header, but they differ in **what exactly they hand the server**.\n\n- **Basic** sends `base64(login:pass)` on every request. base64 is NOT encryption, just an encoding, decoded instantly. So Basic is acceptable **only over HTTPS** — otherwise the password effectively flies in plain text.\n- **Bearer** sends a token (usually a JWT). The server verifies the token's **signature** and never sees the password; the token can be issued with a short lifetime and revoked.\n\nPitfall: Basic's main risk is that the password goes out on every request, and leaking it = full access. Bearer's risk is different: the token is a \"bearer key\" — whoever steals it acts as you — so a short TTL and storing it outside JS-accessible places are critical.","quizCorrect":"Basic sends base64(login:pass) every time; Bearer — a token (often a JWT) verified by signature","quizWrong":["Basic sends the password encrypted, while Bearer sends it in the clear","Bearer sends login and password, Basic — only a session token","Basic is safer than Bearer, since it doesn't need HTTPS to send the password in the header of the request"],"picCap":"Basic sends the password every time, Bearer — a signed token"},"web-nonrest":{"q":"REST vs SOAP vs GraphQL vs gRPC — when to use which?","a":"REST: resources + HTTP methods, simple. SOAP: XML + a rigid contract (WSDL), heavy, legacy/enterprise. GraphQL: one endpoint, the client picks the fields (no over/under-fetch). gRPC: binary over HTTP/2, a protobuf contract, fast — for service-to-service.","d":"Public external API → REST/GraphQL; fast internal RPC → gRPC.","hook":"APIs are ways to order food: REST is a menu, SOAP is a notarized form, GraphQL is \"I'll build my own plate,\" gRPC is a courier hotline.","more":"All four are ways to call someone else's code over the network, with a different price for flexibility and speed. **REST** moves JSON over plain HTTP methods (GET/POST/...), understood by any browser and curl. **SOAP** wraps everything in XML and a strict WSDL contract — verbose, but it gives hard guarantees (banks, government services).\n\n**GraphQL** gives one endpoint where the client writes a query for exactly the fields it needs — over/under-fetching disappears, but caching and rate-limiting get harder. **gRPC** serializes into binary protobuf over HTTP/2 — very fast and compact.\n\n- Pitfall: gRPC doesn't talk well from a browser directly and is meant for server-to-server; GraphQL makes it easy to get hit by a heavy query bomb.","quizCorrect":"Binary protobuf over HTTP/2 — fast, for service-to-service","quizWrong":["JSON over HTTP/1.1 with a human-readable body","XML envelopes described in a WSDL","One endpoint where the client picks the query fields itself"],"picCap":"REST/SOAP/GraphQL/gRPC — which to pick when","note":"gRPC won't work directly from a browser: the browser doesn't give code full control over HTTP/2 frames (trailers, the stream), so you need a gRPC-Web proxy (Envoy's grpc_web filter) plus a client codegen — a plain fetch to a gRPC service won't connect."},"web-dns-lb":{"q":"What do DNS, a reverse proxy and a load balancer do?","a":"DNS: name → IP. Reverse proxy (nginx): one entry point, proxies to backends, takes on TLS/cache/static/routing. Load balancer: spreads requests across instances (round-robin/least-conn) + health checks.","d":"A reverse proxy and an LB are often combined (nginx, envoy).","hook":"DNS is the directory that gives you the address, the reverse proxy is the doorman at a single door, the load balancer seats guests across tables.","more":"These are three different layers on a request's path, often confused. **DNS** is a phone book: it turns `site.com` into an IP, runs once before the connection and is cached by TTL. A **reverse proxy** (nginx) sits in front of your backends as a single door: it terminates TLS, serves static, caches, routes by path/domain.\n\nA **load balancer** is a special case of a proxy whose job is to spread load across several identical instances (round-robin, least-connections) and drop dead ones by health check.\n\n- In practice nginx/Envoy combines the proxy and balancer roles. Pitfall: the DNS cache lives long, so you swap IPs via the balancer, not by editing DNS.","quizCorrect":"Resolves a domain name into an IP address","quizWrong":["Spreads requests across instances via round-robin","Terminates TLS and caches static in front of the backend","Runs health checks on servers and removes dead ones from the pool"],"picCap":"Name → IP → one entry point → spread across live instances","note":"Common trap: the JVM caches successful DNS lookups for 30 seconds by default, and forever (ttl=-1) only under a SecurityManager, which JDK 24+ no longer lets you install, so even with a short record TTL the app keeps hitting the old IP after failover — fix by setting networkaddress.cache.ttl in java.security or by balancing behind a stable VIP."},"x-web-cors-preflight":{"q":"What is CORS and why does the browser send a preflight request (OPTIONS)?","a":"CORS is a browser rule: a script from site A can't read a response from domain B unless B itself allows it. Before a \"non-simple\" request (PUT, custom headers) the browser sends a probe OPTIONS asking \"may I?\". The server replies with Access-Control-Allow-* — and only then does the real request fly.","d":"CORS protects the user in the browser, not the server — curl/Postman ignore it. The SERVER sets the headers: Access-Control-Allow-Origin (no * together with credentials), -Allow-Methods, -Allow-Headers, -Max-Age (preflight cache). Simple requests (GET/POST with a form content-type) go without preflight. A CORS error ≠ a server error: the request arrived and ran, the browser just hid the response from JS.","note":"CORS only protects reading the response in the browser — the request still reaches the server and the side effect runs (so CORS != CSRF protection). And a CORS-error response can't be read from JS: you only see 'CORS error', the real status is hidden. Wildcard '*' is incompatible with credentials.","hook":"A gatekeeper at the entrance: first \"who are you here for, and why?\" (OPTIONS), let through — come in (the real request).","more":"**CORS** is a browser security policy (the Same-Origin Policy): by default JS on a page from origin A can't read a response from another origin (domain/port/scheme). It's protection against a malicious site calling someone else's API on your behalf.\n\nFor \"non-simple\" requests (PUT/DELETE methods, custom headers, a non-standard Content-Type) the browser first sends a **preflight** — an OPTIONS request asking permission. The server replies with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin/Methods/Headers`, and only on a match does the real request go out.\n\n- Important: CORS doesn't protect the server — it's a restriction **on reading the response** in the browser; curl and the backend ignore the rules.\n- Pitfall: `Access-Control-*` must be configured on the server; you must answer OPTIONS, otherwise the request won't go through.","quizCorrect":"Ask the server for permission for a \"non-simple\" request before sending it","quizWrong":["Warm up the TCP connection so the main request runs faster and waits less for the reply","Check whether the server is reachable before sending data","Encrypt the request body before sending it to the server"],"picCap":"First we ask \"may I?\" (OPTIONS), then send the real request"},"x-web-idempotency-key":{"q":"POST isn't idempotent — how do you guard against a double payment on retry?","a":"The client puts a unique Idempotency-Key (UUID) in the request. On the first request the server performs the operation and stores the result under that key. If the same key arrives again (the client retried after a timeout) — the server doesn't make a second payment, it returns the stored response.","d":"This is about exactly-once at the application level: POST itself stays non-idempotent, the idempotency comes from the KEY. The key is stored with a TTL (say 24h) in a DB/Redis with a UNIQUE constraint. Subtleties: the first request is still in flight, the second arrives — you need a lock or an IN_PROGRESS status (return 409). Stripe and PayPal do this. Don't confuse it with Kafka deduplication — that's its own mechanism.","note":"The key must be inserted into the DB with a UNIQUE constraint in the SAME transaction as the payment, otherwise on a race two parallel retries both go through before the key is written. And you must cache the response itself: otherwise on a repeat you return a 200 with no data, or charge again.","hook":"A cloakroom ticket: the same ticket gets you the same coat back, it doesn't sew a new one.","more":"By semantics POST isn't idempotent: two identical calls create two entities (two payments). The problem shows up on retries — the client didn't get a response because of a timeout and repeated, even though the server already did the work.\n\nThe fix is an **Idempotency-Key**: the client generates a unique UUID for one logical operation and sends it in a header. The server atomically stores the key together with the result. A repeat request with the same key doesn't perform the operation again, it returns the **stored response**.\n\n- Pitfall: the key must be stored in the same transaction as the operation (otherwise a race — two parallel requests slip through). A unique constraint in the DB often helps.\n- You need a TTL on keys and a binding to the request body, so the same key with different data doesn't return someone else's result.","quizCorrect":"The client sends a unique Idempotency-Key, the server returns the stored result","quizWrong":["Replace POST with PUT, since PUT is idempotent by definition","Add a unique index in the DB and catch the rest as a 500 error with the text about a duplicate","Retry only once — repeating twice is unlikely"],"picCap":"The same key comes again → the payment isn't duplicated, we return the prior response"},"x-web-rate-limit-429":{"q":"How does rate limiting work, and what do you return when the limit is exceeded?","a":"The server counts a client's requests over a time window. Exceed it — it replies with 429 Too Many Requests and a Retry-After header: how many seconds until you can try again. A popular algorithm is the token bucket: a bucket refills with tokens at N/sec, each request takes a token; out of tokens — rejected.","d":"Why: protection from DDoS, abuse, and fair resource sharing. Token bucket allows short bursts (accumulated tokens), sliding window — more precise but pricier. You also return X-RateLimit-Limit/Remaining/Reset so the client can throttle itself. Limit by API key/IP/user. In microservices it's placed on the API gateway, not in every service. On a 429 the client should retry with backoff, not hammer.","note":"A naive fixed window gives a spike at the window boundary (2x the limit over a couple of seconds at the edge) — so you use a sliding window or a token bucket. And in a cluster a counter in an instance's local memory is useless — you need a shared one (Redis), otherwise the limit gets multiplied by the number of pods.","hook":"A subway turnstile on tokens: tokens drip in slowly, out of them — stand and wait.","more":"Rate limiting caps a client's request rate to protect the service from overload and abuse. The server keeps a counter per key (IP, token, user) over a time window; on exceed it returns **429 Too Many Requests** and a `Retry-After` header — how many seconds to wait before retrying.\n\nA common algorithm is the **token bucket**: a bucket of capacity N refills at R tokens/sec, each request takes a token; empty — rejected. This allows short bursts within the capacity while holding the average rate.\n\n- Alternatives — sliding window (more precise, but pricier on memory) and fixed window (simpler, but spikes at the window boundary).\n- Pitfall: in a cluster the counter must be shared (Redis), otherwise each instance limits separately and the real limit gets multiplied by the number of pods.","quizCorrect":"429 Too Many Requests and a Retry-After header","quizWrong":["503 Service Unavailable and a Retry-After header","403 Forbidden, since the client broke the access rules","400 Bad Request, since the request is deemed malformed"],"picCap":"A token bucket: while there are tokens — let through, empty (blinking) → 429 Retry-After"},"x-web-etag-caching":{"q":"How do ETag and Cache-Control save traffic, and why do you need a 304?","a":"The server serves a resource with an ETag — a fingerprint of its version (a hash). Next time the client sends If-None-Match: <etag>. If nothing changed, the server replies 304 Not Modified WITH NO body — the client uses its cache. Cache-Control (max-age, no-cache, private) sets how long and where caching is allowed.","d":"Two mechanisms: max-age — the cache is fresh for N seconds, no request at all; ETag/Last-Modified — conditional validation, the request goes out but the body isn't downloaded if it matched. ETag also gives optimistic locking on writes: a PUT with If-Match: <etag> fails with 412 Precondition Failed if someone changed the resource earlier — protection against lost updates. no-store — don't cache at all (payment data), no-cache — cache, but always revalidate.","note":"Pitfall: gzip/compression on a proxy changes the ETag, and weak (W/) vs strong ETags matter — range requests need a strong one. The key point — a 304 saves traffic, but NOT load on the backend: to compute the ETag you usually still hit the DB, unless you cache the ETag itself.","hook":"You ask \"did you re-wallpaper?\" — \"no\" (304), so you look at the old photo and don't drive over to see it.","more":"Under the hood two mechanisms work together. **Cache-Control** tells the browser \"keep this response and don't ask the server until max-age expires.\" When the time is up, the **ETag** kicks in — the browser sends a conditional request `If-None-Match: <etag>`, and the server just compares fingerprints.\n\nThe point of **304 Not Modified** is that the response comes with no body — you save traffic and the time to re-transmit large files, though the HTTP request itself still goes out.\n\n- `no-cache` ≠ \"don't cache\": it means \"cache, but always revalidate via ETag.\"\n- `no-store` — that's the actual ban on storing.\n- Pitfall: weak ETags (`W/`) compare by meaning, not byte for byte.","quizCorrect":"304 Not Modified with no response body","quizWrong":["200 OK with the resource body and a new ETag","204 No Content, since there is no body","412 Precondition Failed, since the ETag matched"],"picCap":"The version goes to the server → same → 304 with no body, the client uses the local copy"},"web-http-versions":{"q":"HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3 — what did each speed up?","a":"HTTP/1.1: one request on a connection waits for another (a queue). HTTP/2: many requests at once over one TCP, binary, compressed headers. HTTP/3: the same multiplexing, but over QUIC (UDP) — a lost packet doesn't stall the other streams, and the connection comes up faster.","d":"The main pain is head-of-line blocking (HOL): \"the head of the queue stalls the tail.\"\n\nHTTP/1.1: one connection = one request queue. Browsers worked around it by opening 6 TCP connections per host — expensive. Headers were text, uncompressed.\n\nHTTP/2: one TCP, but logically split into streams — many requests/responses in parallel, interleaved as frames. Binary format + HPACK (header compression). BUT HOL stayed at the TCP level: lose one packet and TCP holds ALL streams until it's retransmitted.\n\nHTTP/3: moves from TCP to QUIC over UDP. In QUIC the streams are independent at the transport level — a lost packet hits only its own stream, the rest keep moving. Plus the handshake is faster: TLS is built into QUIC, the connection comes up in 1 RTT (or 0-RTT on a repeat) instead of TCP+TLS separately. Bonus — connection migration: switching Wi-Fi→LTE doesn't drop the connection.","note":"HTTP/2 removed HOL blocking at the HTTP level, but not at the TCP level: a single lost packet stalls ALL multiplexed streams of one connection — exactly what HTTP/3 fixes over QUIC. So under packet loss h2 can be slower than h1 with multiple connections.","hook":"A checkout: 1.1 — one belt, everything stuck behind the fallen item; 2 — many belts, but one shared conveyor; 3 — separate belts, independent of each other.","more":"In **HTTP/1.1** one TCP connection handles one request at a time: until the response arrives, the next one waits (head-of-line blocking). Browsers worked around it by opening 6 connections per domain.\n\n**HTTP/2** introduced multiplexing: dozens of requests fly in parallel over one connection, the format is binary, headers are compressed (HPACK). But the trouble stayed at the TCP level — a lost packet stalls all streams.\n\n**HTTP/3** moves everything to **QUIC over UDP**: streams are independent, a lost packet hits only its own stream, and connection setup (TLS included) takes one round-trip. Pitfall: corporate firewalls sometimes block UDP.","quizCorrect":"Runs over QUIC (UDP), a lost packet doesn't stall the streams","quizWrong":["First introduced request multiplexing","Uses a text protocol instead of a binary one over the same TCP connection","Runs over a new, reliable TCP connection"],"picCap":"1.1 — a queue; 2 — parallel, but one TCP stalls everyone on loss; 3 — independent streams over QUIC"},"web-cors":{"q":"What is CORS and why does it exist?","a":"The browser itself does NOT let your JS read a response from another site (a different domain, port, or http/https). That's the \"same-origin\" rule. CORS is when that other server replies with a header saying \"this one's allowed\" and lets you in specifically. It protects the user in the browser, not the server.","d":"Origin = scheme + domain + port (https://a.com:443). If even one differs, the origin is foreign. The browser still SENDS the request, but without CORS permission it hides the response from JS (you see an error in the console). The server uses headers to say who's allowed: Access-Control-Allow-Origin (which origins), -Methods (GET/POST/...), -Headers. For \"non-simple\" requests (PUT, custom headers) the browser first sends a preflight OPTIONS asking permission. Important: CORS doesn't protect the server — curl/Postman/the backend ignore it. It's a browser policy, so a malicious site can't call someone else's API on your behalf.","note":"CORS is purely a browser protection: curl, the backend and Postman ignore it completely. And it protects the user, not the server, from having their data read by foreign JS; the request with its side effect still arrives.","hook":"Someone else's apartment: the door is the neighbor's. You come in only if THEY posted a list of \"these guests are allowed.\"","more":"The browser enforces the **same-origin** policy: JS on a page from `a.com` can't read a response from `b.com` unless the origin (scheme + domain + port) matches. This protects the user — otherwise a tab with a malicious site could read your mail through your own cookies.\n\n**CORS** is the mechanism by which the `b.com` server explicitly allows it: it replies with an `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header, and the browser permits the read.\n\n- the check is done by the **browser**, not the server — curl/the backend ignore CORS\n- for \"non-simple\" requests (PUT, custom headers) the browser first sends a **preflight** OPTIONS\n- CORS is about access to the response from JS — it's not authentication and not protection of the API itself","quizCorrect":"The server lets the browser read a response from another origin","quizWrong":["Protects the server from excess client requests","Encrypts traffic between the browser and the server on every request","A browser-side replacement for token authentication"],"picCap":"A foreign origin: without the server's permission the browser hides the response; with the header — it lets it through"},"web-authn-authz":{"q":"Authentication vs Authorization?","a":"Authentication is \"WHO are you?\": you present a login/password or a token, and the system makes sure you're you. Authorization is \"what are you ALLOWED to do?\": having recognized you, it checks your permissions/roles. First entry, then access. 401 — not logged in, 403 — logged in, but not allowed.","d":"The order is strict: you can't check permissions for someone you haven't identified yet. AuthN gives identity (who), AuthZ makes a decision on that identity (roles, scopes, owner-of-resource). HTTP codes: 401 Unauthorized = actually \"not authenticated\" (no/expired/invalid token) → the client should re-login. 403 Forbidden = the identity is known and valid, but there's no permission on the resource → re-login won't help. Common confusion: the name 401 is historical, in meaning it's about authentication, not authorization.","hook":"A club: the bouncer recognizes you (AuthN), but the VIP room requires a wristband (AuthZ).","more":"**Authentication (authn)** answers \"who are you?\": you present a login/password, token, or certificate, the system checks it and confirms your identity. The result — it's known WHO showed up.\n\n**Authorization (authz)** comes next: \"what are you allowed to do?\" — it checks the roles and permissions of the already-identified user for a specific action or resource.\n\n- the order is strict: authn first, then authz\n- **401 Unauthorized** = you haven't confirmed who you are (log in)\n- **403 Forbidden** = you're identified, but you lack permission\n- pitfall: confusing them — returning 403 to an anonymous user instead of 401, or checking only entry and forgetting permissions on the action","quizCorrect":"Authentication — \"who are you,\" authorization — \"what are you allowed to do\"","quizWrong":["Authentication — about permissions, authorization — about entry into the system","They're synonyms for the same process","Authorization always comes before authentication"],"picCap":"The token flows along the chain: AuthN (who) → AuthZ (what's allowed) → access. 401 — stopped at entry, 403 — stopped at permissions.","note":"The HTTP status name 401 — 'Unauthorized' — is a historical spec mistake: semantically it is about authentication, and RFC 7235 requires sending a WWW-Authenticate header with it (otherwise the response is technically non-compliant). Many frameworks emit 401 without it; and in public APIs, to hide a resource's existence from an anonymous user, 404 is often deliberately returned instead of 403."},"web-tcp-udp":{"q":"TCP vs UDP — when to use which?","a":"TCP is a phone call: first \"hello?\", you take turns, ask again about anything unclear, and end with \"bye.\" Reliable, but slower. UDP is shouting into a crowd and walking off: fast, but no guarantee anyone heard, or in what order. TCP — HTTP, databases. UDP — video, games, DNS.","d":"TCP establishes a connection (handshake), numbers bytes, acknowledges receipt (ACK), retransmits what's lost and reassembles the order — over unreliable IP you get a reliable stream. The price: latency for setup, ACKs and retransmits. UDP — just datagrams over IP: no handshake, no ACK, packets can be lost/arrive out of order/be duplicated. In return, minimal overhead and latency — for real-time \"a fresh frame beats a lost one.\" QUIC (the basis of HTTP/3) is built on UDP but adds back reliability and ordering in user space — flexibility without TCP's downsides.","note":"'TCP is reliable' — at the level of byte delivery, but not messages: it's a stream with no boundaries, two sends can be merged/split on recv (you need your own framing by length/delimiter). And an open TCP socket doesn't notice the peer died until you send data or enable keep-alive.","hook":"TCP is a registered letter with a return receipt; UDP is a postcard: dropped in the box, may or may not arrive.","more":"**TCP** establishes a connection (handshake), numbers bytes, acknowledges delivery, retransmits what's lost and guarantees order. Reliable, but it's extra round-trips and latency on loss — so it's slower.\n\n**UDP** just sends datagrams and forgets: no acknowledgments, no order, no delivery guarantee. In return, minimal overhead and latency.\n\n- **TCP** — where integrity matters: HTTP, databases, file transfer\n- **UDP** — where speed and freshness matter more, and a lost frame is forgivable: video, voice, games, DNS\n- pitfall: for real-time TCP is harmful — retransmitting a stale packet only adds lag","quizCorrect":"TCP is reliable and ordered, UDP is fast with no delivery guarantees","quizWrong":["UDP is more reliable than TCP, so it's used for databases and for payments","TCP doesn't guarantee packet order, while UDP does","Both guarantee delivery, the only difference is speed"],"picCap":"TCP: reliable and in order (HTTP, databases). UDP: fast, no guarantees (video, games, DNS)."},"g-web-oauth-flows":{"q":"Which OAuth2 flow to choose: authorization code + PKCE, client credentials, or (heaven forbid) password?","a":"User in a browser/SPA/mobile → authorization code + PKCE. Service-to-service with no user → client credentials. Implicit and the password grant are dead — don't use them. PKCE protects against interception of the code.","d":"Authorization code: the client sends the user to Keycloak, gets a short-lived `code`, exchanges it on the backend for access + refresh tokens. PKCE (code_verifier/code_challenge) adds a one-time secret so an intercepted code is useless — mandatory for public clients (SPA, mobile) without a client_secret. Client credentials: the backend logs in by itself with client_id + secret, no user. The password grant (ROPC) and the implicit flow are removed from OAuth 2.1 — tokens leak, no refresh rotation.","note":"Authorization code requires checking state (anti-CSRF) and an exact redirect_uri — without that the code gets hijacked via a swapped redirect. For an SPA, a refresh token in localStorage is vulnerable to XSS: safer is an httpOnly cookie + refresh token rotation.","hook":"With a person — you show your passport at the turnstile (authcode); robot-to-robot — a service badge (client creds).","more":"The flow choice is driven by **who** holds the secret and whether there's a live user.\n\n- **Authorization code + PKCE** — there's a user in a browser/SPA/mobile. A redirect goes to the provider, a short **code** comes back, which is exchanged for tokens. **PKCE** (code_verifier/challenge) binds the exchange to the initiator, so an intercepted code is useless.\n- **Client credentials** — machine-to-machine with no user, authentication by client_id/secret.\n\n**Dead:** *implicit* (token right in the URL — leaks) and the *password grant* (the app sees the user's password, no MFA/SSO). Pitfall: PKCE is needed by both public and confidential clients — use it everywhere.","quizCorrect":"Authorization code + PKCE","quizWrong":["Client credentials","Resource owner password (ROPC)","Implicit flow"],"picCap":"Has a user → authcode + PKCE; no user → client creds"},"g-web-csp":{"q":"What is Content-Security-Policy and why is it your main defense against XSS?","a":"CSP is a header allowlist: it tells the browser where scripts/styles/images may be loaded from. An injected foreign `<script>` simply won't execute. Without `unsafe-inline`, inline scripts are blocked — and that's what catches XSS.","d":"Input filtering for XSS is unreliable; CSP is a second line on the browser side. `default-src 'self'` forbids everything foreign. Dangers: `script-src 'unsafe-inline'` and `'unsafe-eval'` effectively disable the defense — use a nonce (`'nonce-abc'`) or a hash for the inlines you need. `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` + `report-uri` let you roll out a policy without breaking prod and collect violations.","note":"CSP is easy to bypass if 'unsafe-inline' is allowed or a whole CDN domain with vulnerable libraries is (a JSONP endpoint on a whitelisted domain = a hole). The modern approach is nonce/hash instead of domain whitelists, plus strict-dynamic.","hook":"A club's face control: on the list — come in, not on the list — turned away, no matter how badly you want in.","more":"**CSP** is an HTTP header policy where you set the allowed sources for each resource type as an allowlist (`script-src`, `style-src`, `img-src`…). The browser **refuses** to load/execute anything not on the list.\n\n**Why it beats XSS:** even if an attacker injected a `<script>` or an `onclick`, without an allowed source and without `unsafe-inline` the browser simply **won't run it** — a second line after escaping.\n\nPitfall: `unsafe-inline`/`unsafe-eval` nullify the defense; better to use a **nonce** or a **hash** for the inlines you need. Start with `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` + report-uri so you don't break prod, then switch to enforce.","quizCorrect":"script-src 'unsafe-inline'","quizWrong":["object-src 'none'","frame-ancestors 'none' in the header","default-src 'self'"],"picCap":"The browser lets through only from the allowlist"},"g-web-hsts":{"q":"Why HSTS if I already redirect from http to https?","a":"The first http request before the redirect can be intercepted (SSL-stripping). HSTS tells the browser: \"go to this domain ONLY over https, forever\" — it doesn't even make the http request, it upgrades by itself.","d":"`Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload`. The browser remembers the domain for max-age seconds and swaps http→https itself before sending. `preload` bakes the domain into browsers at build time (the hstspreload.org list) — but that's nearly irreversible, check all subdomains first. Gotcha: serve HSTS only over https (over http the browser ignores it), and be careful with includeSubDomains if you have http-only subdomains.","note":"HSTS doesn't protect the very first visit to a new domain (the header hasn't been received yet) — only the preload list baked into the browser covers that. And includeSubDomains + a long max-age on a domain where some subdomain has no HTTPS will take it down hard, and you can't roll back instantly.","hook":"Note in your notebook \"to this friend, only encrypted letters\" — and you never send a postcard again.","more":"An http→https redirect is **itself an http response**. The very first request goes out over http, and an active attacker (MITM / public Wi-Fi) intercepts it before the redirect — classic **SSL-stripping**: keeping the user on http and reading the traffic.\n\n**HSTS** (`Strict-Transport-Security`) is a header the browser **remembers** for `max-age`: it now upgrades any URL for this domain to https **before sending**, and the http request never happens at all.\n\nPitfall: the very first visit before the header is received is still vulnerable — fixed by the browsers' **preload list**. `includeSubDomains` and a large `max-age` are required for preload; roll it out deliberately — it's hard to undo.","quizCorrect":"SSL-stripping on the first http hop","quizWrong":["CSRF on forms","XSS via inline scripts","SQL injection in parameters of the query"],"picCap":"HSTS removes the vulnerable first http hop"},"g-web-samesite":{"q":"SameSite=Lax vs Strict vs None — and how does it relate to CSRF?","a":"SameSite decides whether the browser attaches a cookie to cross-site requests. `Lax` (the default) — not sent on cross-site POSTs, which kills classic CSRF. `Strict` — never sent from outside at all. `None` — always sent, but `Secure` is mandatory.","d":"Cookie flags: `HttpOnly` — unreadable from JS (protects against theft via XSS), `Secure` — sent over https only, `SameSite` — controls cross-site delivery. Modern browsers default to `Lax`, so a plain CSRF on POST no longer goes through — but GET side effects and embedded iframes (`None`) are still exploitable, so you still need a CSRF token. The browser rejects `SameSite=None` without `Secure`.","note":"SameSite=Lax is no silver bullet: a GET with a side effect and top-level POST navigation (form target) partially slip through, and subdomains count as same-site (an attack from a compromised subdomain gets in). So Lax doesn't replace a CSRF token for sensitive operations.","hook":"Lax — your key card works, but only in your own building; Strict — they check you even at the door; None — the card works everywhere, but only with gloves on (Secure)","more":"The **SameSite** cookie attribute controls whether the browser attaches the cookie to a request initiated by **another** site.\n\n- **Lax** (the default in modern browsers) — the cookie is sent on ordinary navigation (clicking a link, a GET), but **not** on cross-site POST/iframe/fetch. This kills classic form-based **CSRF**.\n- **Strict** — never sent on any inbound transition (not even via a link) → breaks \"followed a link and I'm already logged in\".\n- **None** — always sent (needed for embedding / cross-site APIs), but **`Secure` is mandatory**.\n\nPitfall: Lax does not protect against **top-level GETs** that change state — never mutate on a GET. For real safety, combine it with a **CSRF token**.","quizCorrect":"Secure","quizWrong":["HttpOnly","Path=/","Domain=*"],"picCap":"Lax withholds the cookie on a cross-site POST"},"g-web-idempotency-key":{"q":"How do you actually implement Idempotency-Key server-side, not just accept the header?","a":"The client sends `Idempotency-Key: uuid`. Within a transaction the server inserts the key into a table with a UNIQUE constraint. First time — it runs the operation and stores the response. A repeat of the same key — it returns the STORED response without running the operation again.","d":"The client generates the key (a UUID), one per logical operation, and reuses it on retries. A UNIQUE constraint + INSERT is an atomic guard against a race between two parallel retries: the second fails on the duplicate and waits for / reads the result. Store not just the fact but the response body + status, so you can return it identically. Put a TTL on keys (a day). This sits on top of \"POST is not idempotent\" — it's the concrete implementation.","note":"The UNIQUE insert of the key and the operation itself must be in one transaction, otherwise a race between two retries runs both. Subtlety: what to return while the first request is still RUNNING (key exists, no response yet) — usually 409 / 'in progress', not a silent duplicate.","hook":"A coat-check tag: same tag — same coat; they won't hand you a second one the second time around","more":"Just accepting the header isn't enough — you need **server-side deduplication**. The client sends `Idempotency-Key: <uuid>` on an unsafe request (usually a POST).\n\nIn **one transaction** the server does an `INSERT` of the key into a table with a **UNIQUE** constraint. If the insert succeeds — it runs the operation and **stores the response status + body** under that key. If the key already exists — it returns the **stored** response without re-running the business logic.\n\nPitfall: a race between two parallel retries — you need a row lock or an \"in-progress\" status (return 409 / wait). Scope the key to the user/endpoint, set a **TTL**, store both the code and the response payload.","quizCorrect":"A UNIQUE constraint on the key column","quizWrong":["The Idempotency-Key header on its own is enough","A SELECT before INSERT in code","Retry-After in the response"],"picCap":"The key hits a UNIQUE insert: new → run, duplicate → return the stored result"},"g-web-sse-vs-ws":{"q":"SSE or WebSocket — which to pick for server-side push?","a":"You only need a server→client stream (notifications, progress, quotes) → SSE: plain HTTP, auto-reconnect, simpler. You need two-way real time (chat, a game) → WebSocket. Don't drag in WS where SSE is enough.","d":"SSE (`text/event-stream`) is unidirectional over plain HTTP/2: the browser's `EventSource` reconnects itself and sends `Last-Event-ID` to resume. Downsides: text only, and a limit of ~6 connections per domain on HTTP/1.1 (a non-issue on HTTP/2). WebSocket is full-duplex, its own protocol after the Upgrade, and needs separate infra tuning (proxies, timeouts, backpressure). For \"the server pushed an event\" SSE is almost always cheaper.","note":"SSE over HTTP/1.1 eats the browser's ~6-connections-per-domain limit (open a few tabs and everything hangs); HTTP/2 lifts this with multiplexing. Plus corporate proxies/load balancers love to buffer or drop a long-lived SSE stream.","hook":"SSE — a radio (you only listen, it reconnects itself); WS — a walkie-talkie (you talk and listen, but you have to set it up)","more":"**SSE** (Server-Sent Events) is a long-lived **HTTP** response of `text/event-stream`: the server pushes text events one way, and the browser's `EventSource` **reconnects** on its own and remembers `Last-Event-ID`. It rides on ordinary HTTP infrastructure, proxies, HTTP/2.\n\n**WebSocket** upgrades the connection to a **full-duplex** bidirectional channel (binary/text), but it's a separate protocol: its own proxying, its own reconnect/heartbeat done by hand.\n\nRule of thumb: a stream **server→client only** (notifications, progress, quotes, LLM tokens) → **SSE**, simpler and cheaper. Need interactive two-way real time (chat, a game, collaboration) → **WS**. SSE pitfall: the per-domain connection limit on HTTP/1.1 — cured by HTTP/2.","quizCorrect":"Auto-reconnect with Last-Event-ID","quizWrong":["Two-way transfer","Binary frames","Its own protocol over TCP and its own port"],"picCap":"SSE — a stream to the client only; WS — exchange in both directions"},"g-web-content-negotiation":{"q":"How does content negotiation work and why might the server return 406?","a":"In `Accept` the client says what it's willing to take (`application/json`), the server picks a suitable format and sets `Content-Type`. If the server can't produce anything from `Accept` — it returns `406 Not Acceptable`.","d":"Negotiated: type (`Accept`/`Content-Type`), language (`Accept-Language`/`Content-Language`), encoding (`Accept-Encoding`→`Content-Encoding: gzip`). q-factors set priority: `Accept: application/json;q=0.9, text/html;q=0.5`. A critical caching gotcha: if the response depends on a request header, `Vary: Accept-Encoding` is mandatory, otherwise the CDN serves a gzipped body to a client that can't read it.","hook":"At a cafe you say \"lactose-free for me\" (Accept); there's no such thing on the menu — the waiter shrugs (406)","more":"**Content negotiation** — in the `Accept` header the client lists acceptable media types with **q** weights (`application/json;q=0.9, */*;q=0.1`), the server picks the best available variant and pins it in `Content-Type`. `Accept-Language`/`Accept-Encoding` work the same way.\n\nFor caches the server must send `Vary: Accept`, otherwise a proxy serves the wrong format.\n\nIf the server **can't produce** any type from `Accept` — the correct response is **406 Not Acceptable**. Pitfall: in practice many APIs ignore `Accept` and always send JSON instead of a 406 — convenient, but formally wrong. `*/*` means \"give me anything\" and almost always removes the 406 risk.","quizCorrect":"406 Not Acceptable","quizWrong":["415 Unsupported Media Type","400 Bad Request","204 No Content"],"picCap":"No common format found → 406"},"g-web-3xx-statuses":{"q":"301 vs 302 vs 307/308 — why does the confusion break POST?","a":"301/308 — permanent redirect, 302/307 — temporary. The key part: browsers often change 301/302 to GET, while 307/308 PRESERVE the method and body. Redirecting a POST — use 307/308, otherwise a payment turns into a GET.","d":"301 Moved Permanently (cacheable, for SEO), 302 Found, 303 See Other (explicitly \"go via GET\" — the POST-Redirect-Get pattern after a form), 307 Temporary / 308 Permanent — strictly preserve the method. A historical mess: per the RFC, 301/302 must not change the method, but browsers do, which is why 307/308 were introduced. Also, 304 Not Modified isn't a \"redirect\" — it's the response to a conditional request with ETag.","note":"The main pitfall is right in the topic: many clients (and old browsers) turn 301/302 into GET and DROP the body — a POST payment becomes a GET. To redirect a non-GET, use strictly 307/308. Also: after a 308 you must not change the method even on purpose.","hook":"301/302 — changing trains and losing your luggage (the method is lost); 307/308 — changing trains with the same suitcase","more":"Axis 1 — **permanence**: **301/308** \"the resource has moved for good\" (browsers/bots cache it, update links), **302/307** \"temporarily\".\n\nAxis 2 — **method preservation**, and here's where it bites: for historical reasons, on **301/302** clients often change the method to **GET** and lose the body. **307/308** are required to **preserve** the original method and body.\n\nSo when redirecting a **POST**, use **307** (temporary) or **308** (permanent), otherwise a payment POST turns into a GET — data lost, the retry won't execute. Pitfall: 308 is relatively new, ancient clients may not know it; for SEO moves of GET pages, 301 is still the standard.","quizCorrect":"307 Temporary Redirect","quizWrong":["302 Found","301 Moved Permanently with a body","303 See Other"],"picCap":"307/308 preserve the method and body"},"g-web-keepalive":{"q":"What is keep-alive and why does the HTTP client pool matter more than it seems?","a":"Keep-alive holds the TCP connection open for several requests instead of a handshake on each one. Without reuse (a fresh `HttpClient` per request) you pay the TCP+TLS handshake every time and run out of ports under load.","d":"HTTP/1.1 keep-alive is on by default; it saves ~1-2 RTT on the TLS handshake for every subsequent request. A practical gotcha in Java: creating a new `HttpClient`/`RestClient` per call leaks connections and `TIME_WAIT` ports. Keep one reusable client with a pool. A load balancer may close idle connections on timeout — the server's `keepAliveTimeout` must be LONGER than the client's/LB's, otherwise you hit a race and random 502s.","note":"Creating a new HttpClient per request (a classic in Java/.NET) exhausts ephemeral ports and leaves piles of TIME_WAIT under load — the client must be reused as a singleton. And a pooled keep-alive connection can go stale on the server/LB side, producing a random reset on the next request.","hook":"Don't ring the doorbell again for every word — come in once and talk through everything","more":"**Under the hood:** keep-alive sets the `Connection: keep-alive` header, and after the response the TCP socket isn't closed but returned to the client's **pool**. The next request to the same host grabs a ready socket — no TCP handshake (SYN/ACK) and no TLS handshake (certificate and key exchange).\n\n**Why it matters:** the handshake is 2–3 RTT plus expensive crypto. Per request that's tens to hundreds of ms and CPU load. The pool amortizes it.\n\n**Pitfall:** creating `new HttpClient()` on every call is an anti-pattern: the pool isn't reused, plus closed sockets linger in `TIME_WAIT` and under load you **exhaust ephemeral ports**. Keep a single client singleton for the whole application.","quizCorrect":"Otherwise the server closes the connection right as you reuse it → 502","quizWrong":["To enable HTTP/2 multiplexing between the browser and the server on the same connection","To make gzip work","Otherwise HSTS won't kick in"],"picCap":"One connection serves many requests"},"g-web-csrf-token":{"q":"If SameSite=Lax is already set, why still need a CSRF token?","a":"Lax isn't a full guarantee: a GET with a side effect, top-level POST navigation, old browsers, widgets with SameSite=None — all slip past it. A CSRF token (synchronizer/double-submit) is an independent secret the attacker doesn't have.","d":"Double-submit cookie: the server puts a random token in a cookie AND requires the same token in a header / form field. Another site can't read your cookie (Same-Origin Policy) or set the header, so it can't reproduce the pair. For stateless APIs using a Bearer token in the `Authorization` header, CSRF doesn't apply (the browser doesn't add the header automatically) — the problem is specifically with cookie sessions.","note":"The double-submit token breaks if a subdomain can set a cookie on the parent domain (the subdomain forges both the cookie and the header). Then you need a session-bound synchronizer token, not a stateless double-submit.","hook":"A passphrase on top of the key: the key-cookie might \"accidentally\" fire, but only you know the word","more":"**How Lax works:** the browser sends the cookie on cross-site requests only on **top-level GET navigation** (clicking a link). On a cross-site POST/fetch the cookie isn't sent — and that's what cuts off classic CSRF.\n\n**Why it's not enough:** holes remain — a GET with a side effect (`/delete?id=5`), a top-level POST via auto-submitted form (passes in some browsers in the first ~2 min), old browsers without Lax, and subdomains/widgets with `SameSite=None`.\n\n**A CSRF token** is an independent secret in the form/header that the attacker doesn't have (synchronizer — on the server, double-submit — comparing the cookie and the field). It's **defense in depth**: Lax is the first barrier, the token is the guarantee. Never mutate via GET.","quizCorrect":"A stateless API with a Bearer token in the Authorization header","quizWrong":["A cookie session with SameSite=Lax","Forms with POST on the same domain","Any request with an HttpOnly cookie sent from another origin to the endpoint"],"picCap":"The attacker doesn't know the token — the header won't match"},"web-http-anatomy":{"q":"What does an HTTP request and response consist of?","a":"An HTTP request: the first line (METHOD + path + version), then headers, a blank line, and the body. The response: a status line (code + text), headers, body. The server is dumb — every request from scratch, it remembers nothing of the past. For it to recognize you — you send a cookie/token.","d":"Versions: HTTP/1.1 — text, one connection sequentially; HTTP/2 — binary frames and multiplexing (many requests on one connection); HTTP/3 — over QUIC/UDP. Idempotency: GET/PUT/DELETE can be repeated with no side effects, POST can't. A cookie is just the `Cookie:` header the browser automatically attaches to every request to the same domain; the server sets them via `Set-Cookie`.","note":"A parsing/security pitfall: a mismatch between Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding across the front- and back-end allows HTTP request smuggling. And header names are case-insensitive, but values are not; duplicate headers are handled differently by different proxies.","hook":"The server is a salesman with amnesia: every time you show your receipt (the token), otherwise it's \"who are you?\"","more":"HTTP is a text \"question-answer\" protocol. A **request** = a start line (`GET /users HTTP/1.1`) + headers (`Host`, `Authorization`, `Content-Type`) + a blank line + an optional body (e.g. JSON for a POST). A **response** = a status line (`HTTP/1.1 200 OK`) + headers + a body (HTML, JSON, a file).\n\nWhy the structure: the blank line cleanly separates metadata (headers) from data (the body) — the parser knows where each ends.\n\n**The key point — HTTP is stateless:** the server doesn't remember past requests. Each request is self-contained. Pitfalls:\n- you logged in — but on the second request the server already \"doesn't recognize\" you;\n- to be remembered, you carry the state yourself: a **cookie** or a **token** in the header of every request;\n- forgot to attach the token → 401, even though you \"just logged in\".","quizCorrect":"The server doesn't remember past requests — you carry state in cookies/tokens","quizWrong":["The server keeps each client's session in memory","A request can't be sent without a body","Headers are forbidden, only the start line is allowed and the message body after it"],"picCap":"The request flies to the server, the response comes back: line + headers + body. The server starts from a clean slate every time."},"web-https-what":{"q":"What exactly does HTTPS give you (beyond just \"the padlock\")?","a":"HTTPS is HTTP in an armored envelope. It gives three things: 1) encryption (no one can peek), 2) protection against tampering in transit, 3) proof that the server is the real one (via a certificate). Without HTTPS your password flies in plaintext — anyone along the way can read it.","d":"The TLS handshake: client and server agree on a version and cipher suites, the server sends its certificate (a chain up to a root CA), the sides use asymmetric crypto (RSA/ECDHE) to agree on a shared session key, and from then on all traffic is encrypted with a fast symmetric cipher (AES/ChaCha20). The integrity of each message is verified via AEAD/MAC. The browser validates the certificate against the list of trusted root CAs in the OS/browser and against its validity/domain.","note":"HTTPS encrypts the body and the path, but does NOT hide the domain itself: the hostname goes out in plaintext in SNI during the handshake (and in DNS), so 'where you're going' is visible to an observer. Only ECH closes this, and it isn't widespread yet.","hook":"It's like a registered letter in a sealed envelope handed to a courier with an ID: can't be opened, can't be swapped, and you can see who delivered it.","more":"**HTTPS = HTTP over TLS.** Plain HTTP sends everything in plaintext — passwords, tokens, cookies are visible to anyone on the path (Wi-Fi, ISP, proxy). TLS wraps that same HTTP in a protected channel.\n\nIt gives exactly **three** things:\n- **Encryption** — the traffic is unreadable to outsiders.\n- **Integrity** — the data can't be silently tampered with in transit.\n- **Server authentication** — a certificate signed by a trusted CA confirms the server is really who it claims to be.\n\n**Pitfall:** the padlock in the browser only proves the *server's* authenticity and the channel's encryption — it does NOT mean the site is honest. A phishing site can also have a valid certificate.","quizCorrect":"That the site owner is honest (not a scammer)","quizWrong":["Encryption of traffic between the browser and the server","Protection of data against tampering in transit","Confirmation that the server is who it claims to be"],"picCap":"HTTP + TLS = encryption + tamper protection + a proven server"},"web-soap":{"q":"SOAP — what is it and why is it \"heavy\"?","a":"SOAP is a strict way of exchanging messages. Each message is placed in an XML envelope: a service header on top, the data itself in the body. The contract over the fields is rigidly described in WSDL. It's \"heavy\" because of all the XML wrapping and rules.","d":"SOAP ≠ HTTP-only: the transport can be HTTP, JMS, SMTP — the envelope is the same. WSDL generates client stubs (codegen), so you get strict typing out of the box, but any schema change breaks the contract. WS-Security provides security at the message level (signing/encrypting part of the body), not just the channel (unlike TLS), which matters when passing through intermediaries. Encoding style: document/literal (today's standard) vs rpc/encoded (deprecated).","hook":"A registered letter in a thick envelope: an itemized inventory slip (header), the enclosure (body), all per the post office's strict regulations.","more":"**How it works.** SOAP is a messaging protocol. A message is an XML **envelope**: it has a *header* (service info: security, routing) and a *body* (the payload). The contract is rigidly set by **WSDL** — a machine-readable description of methods and types. It's transport-agnostic: most often over HTTP, but can use others.\n\n**Why.** A strict contract + ready enterprise standards:\n- **WS-Security** — signing and encryption at the message level;\n- WS-* (reliable delivery, transactions).\nThat's why it took root in banks, government systems, system ↔ system integrations.\n\n**Pitfall.** Very verbose (mountains of XML), hard to debug, heavier than REST/gRPC. Today — mostly legacy and enterprise.","quizCorrect":"An XML envelope: header (service info) + body (data)","quizWrong":["A JSON object with data and meta fields","The URL path and the request's query parameters of the endpoint","A binary protobuf frame with field tags"],"picCap":"Each message = an XML envelope (header + body), shape set by WSDL","note":"SOAP's hidden pain isn't XML verbosity but stack interoperability: WS-I Basic Profile exists precisely because the same WSDL generated incompatible clients on .NET vs Java (especially rpc/encoded, xsd:choice, polymorphic types). Also, WS-Security with XML Digital Signature is extremely sensitive to canonicalization (C14N): any proxy/load balancer that reformats the XML breaks the signature even though the data is unchanged."},"web-grpc":{"q":"gRPC — how is it built and when to use it?","a":"gRPC is calling someone else's program like your own function. You describe \"which methods and fields\" in a .proto, and a generator writes the client and server for you. The data flies as compressed binary over HTTP/2 — fast. Used to connect SERVICES, not for the browser directly.","d":"protobuf encodes fields by their number (tag), not by name — that's why you can't change the numbers in a .proto; that's what gives backward compatibility: old code simply ignores unknown fields. HTTP/2 provides multiplexing (no head-of-line blocking at the request level) and server-pushed streams. Compared to alternatives: REST/JSON — human-readable, browser- and cache-friendly, but slower and without a strict contract; GraphQL — flexible field selection for the front end; gRPC — speed and a strict contract for internal service-to-service comms.","note":"gRPC doesn't work directly from the browser (no access to HTTP/2 frames and trailers from JS) — you need grpc-web + a proxy. And in protobuf, deleting/reusing a field number breaks backward compatibility: it's the numbers, not the names, that you can't touch.","hook":"A menu (.proto) for two: the cook and the waiter understand each other from it without words and send compressed orders.","more":"**How it works.** First you write the contract in a `.proto` file (the protobuf language): which methods exist, what fields and types they have. The code generator (`protoc`) produces a ready client and server in your language from this file. Calling a remote method looks like calling a local function, and the data is serialized into compact **binary** and travels over **HTTP/2** (multiplexing, one connection for many requests).\n\n**Why.** Binary is smaller and parses faster than JSON, the contract is strictly typed, and there are 4 modes:\n- **unary** — request → response;\n- **server-streaming** — one request, a stream of responses;\n- **client-streaming** — a stream of requests, one response;\n- **bidirectional** — a stream in both directions.\n\n**Pitfall.** The browser can't do gRPC directly (you need **grpc-web** + a proxy). Binary isn't human-readable — harder to debug. That's why gRPC is taken for **service-to-service** comms inside the back end, while REST is often kept facing the front end.","quizCorrect":"The browser doesn't give full access to HTTP/2 frames — you need grpc-web and a proxy","quizWrong":["gRPC works only over UDP, and the browser only does TCP for every single request it makes","protobuf is forbidden by browser security policy (CORS)","gRPC requires installing a browser plugin manually"],"picCap":"One .proto → generates client and server; they talk in compressed binary over HTTP/2"},"web-websocket-proto":{"q":"WebSocket under the hood — how does it work?","a":"First the client calls in like ordinary HTTP and says: \"Let's switch to WebSocket.\" The server: \"OK!\" And that's it — now they have an open pipe. Either side can shout into it at any moment without waiting their turn. No need to knock again every time.","d":"A WebSocket frame isn't \"naked\" text: there's an opcode (text/binary/ping/pong/close), masking of client data (protection against proxy cache poisoning), and a length. On top of it you can build sub-protocols (STOMP, GraphQL-WS). On a TCP drop the connection must be re-established from scratch — auto-reconnect is written by hand or you take a library (Socket.IO).","note":"After the upgrade, WebSocket is NOT HTTP: ordinary L7 features (REST routing, caching, status codes, header-based auth per frame) don't work, and the load balancer needs Upgrade support and stickiness. The browser doesn't send custom headers during the handshake — the token is stuffed into the query/subprotocol.","hook":"Like a phone call: you dial the number (HTTP), they pick up — and from then on you both talk at once, without calling back.","more":"**How it works:** the connection starts as an ordinary HTTP request with an `Upgrade: websocket` header. The server replies `101 Switching Protocols` — that's the **handshake**. After it, that same TCP socket stops being HTTP and becomes a persistent **full-duplex** channel.\n\n**Why:** now both the client and the server send messages (frames) **at any moment**, without new requests and headers. Ideal for real time: chat, online games, trading tickers, collaborative editing.\n\n**Pitfalls:**\n- The connection is **persistent** — it holds resources, you need ping/pong so it doesn't go stale.\n- Proxies/load balancers must support Upgrade.\n- Unlike **SSE** (server→client only, over HTTP) — WebSocket is bidirectional.","quizCorrect":"WebSocket is bidirectional, SSE — server→client only","quizWrong":["WebSocket is faster, but also server→client only","SSE is bidirectional, while WebSocket is client→server only","They're the same, SSE is the old name for WebSocket"],"picCap":"HTTP handshake → upgrade → a persistent bidirectional channel"},"web-webhook":{"q":"A webhook — what is it and why?","a":"Waiting for a letter? Don't run to the mailbox every minute (polling). Give an address — the mailman will run over and knock himself (webhook) when the letter arrives. The server sends you a POST itself, you don't poll.","d":"The receiving endpoint must reply 2xx as fast as possible (push heavy work to a queue/background), otherwise the provider considers delivery failed and starts retrying. Store processed event-ids (deduplication). The signature is usually HMAC-SHA256 of the request body with a shared secret — compare it constant-time. For local development you need a public URL (ngrok/tunnel).","hook":"Don't run to the mailbox every minute — give an address, and the mailman will knock himself when the letter arrives.","more":"**How it works.** You give the server your URL in advance. When an event happens (a payment, a new order), the server itself makes an HTTP **POST** to that URL with the event data. This is *push*: the data arrives on its own.\n\n**Why.** The alternative is *polling*: the client pokes the server \"anything new?\" over and over. That's wasted requests and latency. A webhook fires at the moment of the event — less load and near-zero latency.\n\n**Pitfalls:**\n- **Retries** — the network dropped, the server will retry delivery. Reply 200 fast.\n- **Idempotency** — because of retries one event may arrive twice; don't double the effect (check the event-id).\n- **Signature** — verify the request's HMAC signature, otherwise anyone can forge the call.","quizCorrect":"The server itself sends a POST to the client on an event (push)","quizWrong":["The client polls the server in a loop more often","It's encryption of HTTP traffic instead of HTTPS","The server opens a persistent WebSocket tunnel to the DB on the server"],"picCap":"The server pushes a POST to the client's URL on an event itself (push instead of poll)","note":"A non-obvious gap: you must verify the HMAC signature against the RAW body (raw bytes) before parsing JSON — if the framework (Spring, Quarkus, Express) deserializes and re-serializes the payload, key order/whitespace changes and the signature no longer matches. That's why in Spring you read the body via a ContentCachingRequestFilter / custom filter, not from an already-parsed DTO."},"web-graphql-deep":{"q":"GraphQL — upsides and pitfalls?","a":"One URL instead of a pile of endpoints. In the query you write yourself which fields you need — the server returns exactly those, in a single trip. Downside: caching and rate-limiting are hard, and it's easy to trigger extra trips to the DB.","d":"REST: resource = URL, the server dictates the response shape, caching and rate-limit out of the box by URL/method. GraphQL: the client dictates the shape via a typed schema, so control shifts into query validation: limiting query depth/complexity, persisted queries (pre-approved queries by hash — they also fix caching and cut attacks). DataLoader groups calls within a single event-loop tick into one batch query, eliminating N+1.","note":"The main pitfall is N+1: one nested query unfolds into an avalanche of trips to the DB, cured by DataLoader (batching). And a public GraphQL with no depth/cost limit on queries is a DoS: the client constructs a monstrous nested query itself.","hook":"A \"build your own\" menu: you say what to put on the plate — and they bring exactly that, in one trip.","more":"**How it works.** GraphQL has one endpoint (usually POST /graphql). In the request body the client describes a tree of the fields it needs, and the server returns JSON of exactly the same shape — no more, no less.\n\n**Why.** It solves two REST pains: **over-fetch** (got extra) and **under-fetch** (had to hit 3 endpoints). Here you fetch data for several entities in **one round-trip**, and the front end decides what it needs.\n\n**Pitfalls:**\n- **Caching** — everything is a POST to one URL, ordinary HTTP caching by URL doesn't work.\n- **N+1** — each field has its own resolver, easy to get an avalanche of DB queries → **DataLoader** saves you (it batches).\n- **Rate-limit** — queries vary in cost, you can't count by number of calls, you need complexity analysis.","quizCorrect":"All requests are POST to one URL, caching by URL can't tell them apart","quizWrong":["GraphQL forbids the Cache-Control header","Responses are always encrypted and uncacheable by any proxy in between on the way back","GraphQL works only over WebSocket, not HTTP"],"picCap":"One endpoint: the client specifies fields → gets exactly them in one trip; three pitfalls below"},"web-rest-maturity":{"q":"REST maturity levels (the Richardson model)?","a":"A 4-rung ladder of REST \"realness\": L0 — everything into one hole (RPC); L1 — each thing has its own address; L2 — we use HTTP verbs (GET/POST) and codes (200/404); L3 — the response itself contains links \"where to go next\". Most APIs live at L2, few reach L3.","d":"The model was proposed by Leonard Richardson (2008) and popularized by Martin Fowler. Roy Fielding himself (author of the REST dissertation) considered HATEOAS a mandatory trait of REST — without it an API is formally \"not REST\". At L3 the hypermedia formats: HAL, JSON:API, Siren. In practice L3 is justified where there are many clients that must depend loosely on hardcoded URLs (public platform APIs), but for internal microservices it's usually overkill — which is why the industry mostly sits at L2.","hook":"An elevator with no buttons (L0) → floors with addresses (L1) → proper buttons and a display (L2) → \"this way\" signs right inside the cabin (L3).","more":"**The Richardson model** — a REST API maturity scale of 4 levels (L0–L3).\n\nHow it works:\n- **L0 (the tunnel)** — one URL, everything via POST, the action is buried in the body. Essentially RPC over HTTP (SOAP, the old XML-RPC).\n- **L1 (resources)** — each entity has its own URI: `/orders/42`, `/clients/7`. But the method is almost always POST.\n- **L2 (verbs and codes)** — we properly use `GET/POST/PUT/DELETE` and status codes (`200`, `201`, `404`). This is where most \"REST\" APIs live.\n- **L3 (HATEOAS)** — the response itself contains links to the next available actions, and the client follows them.\n\nWhy: a shared vocabulary to assess how much an API uses HTTP's capabilities rather than reinventing its own.\n\nPitfalls:\n- L2 already gives 90% of the benefit; **full L3 is rare in practice** — expensive to build and to maintain hypermedia clients.\n- The levels aren't a \"quality grade\": L2 is enough for most tasks, no need to chase L3 just to tick a box.","quizCorrect":"Links to available actions right in the response body","quizWrong":["A separate URI for each resource","Use of the HTTP methods GET/POST/PUT on one resource of the API","Proper status codes like 404 and 201"],"picCap":"The REST maturity ladder: the further right, the more HTTP is used. Reality is stuck at L2."},"web-openapi":{"q":"OpenAPI / Swagger — why?","a":"OpenAPI is a \"technical passport\" of your REST API in YAML/JSON: which endpoints exist, what to send them, what comes back. From it a machine generates the docs (Swagger UI), clients and stubs. No explaining by hand — everyone reads one file and doesn't argue.","d":"OpenAPI 3.x is the format specification itself; Swagger is the toolset around it (Swagger UI, Codegen, Editor). `$ref` lets you reuse schemas and avoid duplication. In contract-first you keep the spec in the repo as the source of truth and validate the contract with contract tests (e.g. via Schemathesis / Spectral in CI), so the backend doesn't \"drift\" from the agreement.","hook":"The spec = a cafe menu: what to order, what they'll put on the plate, how much it costs. You order — the kitchen makes exactly that.","more":"**How it works.** You describe the API formally: paths (`/orders/{id}`), methods, parameters, request body, data schemas and response codes — all in a single YAML/JSON. This file is read by both humans (via Swagger UI) and tools.\n\n**Why.** From the spec you auto-generate:\n- interactive documentation;\n- a client SDK (the front end, another service);\n- server stubs and mocks (the front end works while the back end isn't there yet).\n\nTwo approaches: **contract-first** — you write the spec first, it is the agreement between teams, and you fit the code to it; **code-first** — the spec is generated from annotations in the code.\n\n**Pitfall.** With code-first the spec easily diverges from reality if you forgot an annotation. With contract-first you need the discipline not to change the contract silently. And in both cases the spec is useless if it isn't checked in CI.","quizCorrect":"Endpoints, parameters, data schemas and response codes of a REST API","quizWrong":["The server implementation source code in Java and its dependencies in the repository","The database configuration and indexes","User access rights in Keycloak"],"picCap":"One spec → docs, client and mocks generate themselves","note":"Code-first pitfall in Java: generators (springdoc, Quarkus SmallRye OpenAPI) often fail to correctly emit nullable/required, generic wrappers, and polymorphism (oneOf/discriminator) — due to type erasure and Bean Validation annotations not always mapping into the schema. The result: a generated client is valid against the spec but breaks on the real response; what saves you is a contract test against the REAL endpoint, not against the spec itself."},"web-oauth-tokens":{"q":"OAuth2: roles and token types?","a":"The user owns the data. The application (client) asks for access. The authorization server gives it a \"pass\" (access token). With the pass the application goes to the API (resource server) and fetches the data. A refresh token — to renew the pass without re-login. An ID token — who you are (OIDC). A scope — what you're allowed to touch.","d":"The access token is often a JWT (self-contained, the API verifies the signature locally) or opaque (the API asks the auth server via introspection). The ID token is always a JWT with claims (sub, email, exp). Today's flow for web apps is Authorization Code + PKCE: the client gets a short code and exchanges it for tokens on the backend; PKCE protects against code interception. The implicit flow is deprecated.","note":"A common mistake is using the access_token as proof of 'who the user is': it's for accessing the API, identity comes from the id_token (OIDC). And the resource server must validate the token's audience/issuer, otherwise a token issued for another API will pass (confused deputy).","hook":"A cinema ticket: the box office (auth server) gives you a ticket (access), the usher (API) checks it, the season pass (refresh) — renews without a new purchase.","more":"**How it works.** The user (resource owner) grants the application (client) access to their data. The application doesn't get the password — instead the authorization server issues it tokens. With the access token the application knocks on the API (resource server), which verifies the token and returns the data.\n\n**Why.** Separation of roles = security. The application never sees the user's password. The **access token** is short-lived (minutes), so a leak expires quickly. The **refresh token** is long-lived — it swaps an expired access without re-login. The **ID token** (this is already OIDC on top of OAuth2) carries info about the user: who logged in. **Scopes** limit the token's rights (e.g. read the profile only).\n\n**Pitfalls.**\n- OAuth2 is about authorization (access), not login; \"who you are\" comes from OIDC and the id token.\n- The ID token is NOT for calling an API — the access token is for that.\n- The refresh token must be kept as a secret, on the backend.","quizCorrect":"The access token","quizWrong":["The ID token — it also has user data in it","The refresh token — it's the most protected one","The user's password, passed to the application"],"picCap":"The owner grants consent → the Auth Server issues tokens to the client → the client goes to the API with the access token"},"web-oidc-keycloak":{"q":"OIDC and Keycloak — what are they and how do they relate to OAuth2?","a":"OAuth2 solves \"what you're allowed\" (access to an API). OIDC adds \"and who you even are\" on top — it issues an id_token (a JWT with info about the user). Keycloak is a ready-made gatehouse with a guard: it logs people in, holds SSO and social login, hands out tokens. Your service doesn't check passwords — it only looks at the token's signature: real or not.","d":"Under the hood it's most often the Authorization Code Flow + PKCE: the browser goes to Keycloak (`/auth`), the user logs in, a short code flies back, and the backend exchanges it for tokens (`/token`). The IdP's endpoints are described in the discovery document `/.well-known/openid-configuration`, and the public keys for verifying the JWT signature — in the JWKS. A Quarkus service just needs `quarkus.oidc.auth-server-url` and `audience` — it pulls the keys and verifies the token itself; no manual crypto needed.","hook":"Keycloak is the guard at the checkpoint: checks your passport and issues a pass. OIDC = \"who you are\", OAuth2 = \"where you're allowed in\".","more":"**OAuth2** is originally about *authorizing access*: the application gets an access_token to call the API on the user's behalf. But it has no standard for \"who this user is\". OIDC closes this gap — a thin authentication layer on top of OAuth2.\n\n**How it works:** the user logs in to Keycloak (the Identity Provider), which returns two tokens:\n- **id_token** (a JWT) — \"who you are\": sub, email, name, roles;\n- **access_token** — \"what you're allowed\": access to the API.\n\n**Why Keycloak:** one login center = SSO, social login (Google etc.), 2FA, unified user management. Your services don't store passwords — they only **verify the token's signature** against the IdP's public key (JWKS).\n\n**Pitfalls:** don't confuse authentication (OIDC/id_token) and authorization (OAuth2/access_token); verify the signature, `iss`, `aud` and the `exp` validity; don't hit the API with the id_token — the API needs the access_token.","quizCorrect":"An authentication layer: an id_token (JWT) with data about the user — \"who you are\"","quizWrong":["Encryption of traffic instead of TLS between services","Storing user passwords directly in every service","Replacing the access_token — after OIDC it's no longer needed for anything at all after the login"],"picCap":"Keycloak logs you in and hands out tokens — the service just verifies their signature."},"web-mtls":{"q":"mTLS (mutual TLS) — what is it for?","a":"Plain TLS: \"show your passport\" is asked only of the server. mTLS: BOTH show a passport — the server and the client. Each verifies the other by certificate. Services trust each other by certificates, not by passwords/tokens.","d":"mTLS provides only authentication (who you are) + channel encryption, but NOT authorization (what you're allowed) — that's solved by policies on top (e.g. an AuthorizationPolicy in Istio). In a service mesh mTLS is usually transparent: it's done by sidecar proxies (Envoy), the service code knows nothing about it. The service identity is often baked into the certificate as a SPIFFE-ID (spiffe://domain/ns/.../sa/...).","note":"mTLS shifts all the pain onto certificate management: an expired or un-rotated client cert brings the integration to a dead stop, and revocation (CRL/OCSP) in service-to-service mTLS often isn't actually checked — a revoked cert keeps working until it expires.","hook":"Not \"the guard checks the guest\", but two agents showing each other their IDs — both of them.","more":"**How it works.** In plain TLS the server sends its certificate, the client verifies it — but the server doesn't know who came to it. In **mTLS** a second step is added: the client also presents its certificate, and the server verifies it against a trusted CA. They trust only each other — no anonymous ones.\n\n**Why.** For service-to-service authentication (zero-trust, a service mesh like Istio/Linkerd). Service A proves \"I really am service A\" with a **certificate**, not a shared password/token that can be stolen and reused.\n\n**Pitfalls:**\n- certificates **expire** → you need auto-rotation (a short lifetime);\n- you need a **PKI/CA** and key distribution to all services;\n- revocation and the clocks on the hosts must be in sync.","quizCorrect":"The certificate is presented by both sides, not just the server","quizWrong":["The certificate is presented only by the client, not the server during it","Traffic isn't encrypted, only the signature is checked","Login and password are used instead of certificates"],"picCap":"mTLS: both present a certificate, both verify (two-way)"},"web-saml-oidc":{"q":"SAML vs OIDC — what's the difference?","a":"Both are for single sign-on (SSO). SAML is the old \"enterprise\" language: XML + browser redirects. OIDC is modern and lightweight: JSON/JWT, convenient for websites, mobile, APIs. Build new on OIDC; you'll run into SAML in old corporate systems.","d":"SAML carries identity inside a signed XML (an assertion), usually delivered via POST-binding to the application's ACS endpoint. OIDC splits things out: an ID Token (JWT, for authentication) and an Access Token (for API access), plus a UserInfo endpoint. OIDC reuses the OAuth2 infrastructure (scopes, refresh tokens), so it's convenient where you need both login and resource access. A bridge between the worlds is often built via Keycloak: a SAML/OIDC IdP with protocol translation.","hook":"One turnstile pass (SSO). SAML — a thick paper passport, OIDC — a QR code in your phone.","more":"**What they are.** SAML and OIDC are two single sign-on (SSO) protocols: you log in once at the provider (the IdP / Identity Provider), and applications trust its confirmation without asking for the password again.\n\n**How they work.**\n- **SAML 2.0** — the elder. Exchange of XML messages (assertions) via browser redirects/forms. Heavyweight, but battle-tested over the years.\n- **OIDC** — a layer over OAuth 2.0. It returns a compact **JWT** (ID Token) in JSON, communicating over REST/HTTPS.\n\n**Why and the pitfalls.** OIDC maps better onto SPAs, mobile and APIs — take it for anything new. You'll meet SAML in legacy: corporate portals, government services, old SSO. Pitfall: don't confuse authentication (who you are — OIDC/SAML) and authorization (what you're allowed — a pure OAuth2 ID Token ≠ API access).","quizCorrect":"OIDC returns JSON/JWT, SAML — an XML assertion","quizWrong":["OIDC uses XML, SAML — JSON","Both return an identical XML token","OIDC doesn't issue a token, only a cookie to the browser"],"picCap":"One goal (SSO) — two languages: SAML speaks XML, OIDC speaks JWT/JSON.","note":"Classic OIDC trap: to authorize an API you must validate the Access Token, not the ID Token — the ID Token is meant only for client authentication and its audience (aud) is the client_id, not the API; if a resource server validates an ID Token, any client of the same IdP gets in. SAML has an analogous trap — you must verify the assertion signature plus Audience/Recipient/NotOnOrAfter, otherwise classic XML Signature Wrapping (XSW) bypasses it."},"web-grpc-vs-rest":{"q":"gRPC vs REST — a detailed comparison?","a":"**gRPC vs REST** — two approaches to building APIs.\n\n**REST** — text (JSON) over **HTTP/1.1**. Human-readable, easy to debug with curl/Postman, responses are cacheable via standard HTTP mechanisms, and it works straight from the browser. Ideal for public and browser-facing APIs.\n\n**gRPC** — a binary format (**Protobuf**) over **HTTP/2**. A strict contract in a `.proto` file plus codegen for 10+ languages, bidirectional streaming, and multiplexing. Noticeably faster and more compact on the wire — ideal for high-throughput INTERNAL service-to-service communication.\n\nThe main limitation: browsers can't speak gRPC directly — you need a **grpc-web** proxy layer.","d":"**REST** (REpresentational State Transfer) — an architectural style: resources (`/users/42`) plus HTTP methods (GET/POST/PUT/DELETE), usually JSON. **gRPC** (gRPC Remote Procedure Calls, by Google) — an RPC framework: you call a remote method as if it were a local function, with the contract described in a Protobuf `.proto` schema.","note":"gRPC behind an L7 load balancer needs care: a single long-lived HTTP/2 connection multiplexes all RPCs, so naive per-connection round-robin sends all traffic to one backend — you need request-level balancing (gRPC client-side LB or an L7-aware proxy).","hook":"Why does Google speak binary between its own services but hand plain JSON to the outside world?","more":"**When to pick which (by context):**\n\n- **REST** → public API, mobile/browser client, when you need easy debugging and caching, or a team with no Protobuf experience. Text on the wire = easy to read in logs and DevTools.\n- **gRPC** → internal service-to-service microservices, high call frequency, when you need a strict contract and streaming (e.g. a quote feed or task progress). Binary + HTTP/2 = fewer bytes and lower latency.\n\n**Why gRPC is faster.** Protobuf serializes to a compact binary (no field names in every message, integers packed as varint), and HTTP/2 gives multiplexing (many requests over one connection without head-of-line blocking at the TCP-handshake level) plus header compression (HPACK). REST over HTTP/1.1 sends text JSON and opens a connection per stream. In practice this means **several times less traffic** and noticeably lower latency under load.\n\n**The cost of gRPC** is complexity: you need a `.proto`, code generation in the build, the binary can't be read by eye without tooling, and a grpc-web proxy is mandatory in the browser. So the rule is simple: **outward/to the browser — REST, internally between services — gRPC**. Many platforms run both: REST at the edge (API Gateway) plus gRPC inside the cluster.","quizCorrect":"The browser can't fully control HTTP/2 frames — you need a grpc-web proxy","quizWrong":["gRPC only works over UDP, while the browser sends over TCP on every call it ever makes","Protobuf is forbidden by the CORS policy in all browsers","gRPC requires JSON, which the browser can't parse"],"picCap":"REST — outward/to the browser; gRPC — internally between services (more compact traffic). The browser needs grpc-web."},"alg-bigo":{"q":"What does O(n) mean and why does it matter in practice?","a":"How time/memory grow as the input n grows. O(1) — constant; O(log n) — halving the range (binary search, B-tree); O(n) — linear; O(n log n) — good sorts; O(n²) — nested loops (bad on large n).","d":"It's about GROWTH RATE, not absolute speed on small data.","hook":"The more guests you invite, the more dishes to wash. O(n²) — everyone also clinks glasses with everyone else.","more":"Big-O describes the **growth rate**, not exact time: constants and lower-order terms are dropped, all that matters is how the algorithm behaves at large n. O(2n) and O(n) are the same O(n).\n\nFeel for the classes: **O(1)** — lookup by key/index; **O(log n)** — each step halves the data (binary search, an index tree in a DB); **O(n log n)** — the ceiling for good sorts; **O(n²)** — everyone against everyone.\n\nGotcha: on small n the quadratic one can be **faster** thanks to smaller constants — that's why the JDK sorts short arrays with insertion sort. Big-O is what decides as the data grows: O(n²) on a million elements is already minutes, not milliseconds.","quizCorrect":"Time/memory grow linearly as the input n grows","quizWrong":["The algorithm always runs in n seconds on any input","n nested loops — i.e. quadratic growth","Constant time regardless of input size"],"picCap":"Wider bar — the cost grows faster as n grows","note":"Big-O ignores constants and the real hardware model, so the 'asymptotically worse' algorithm is often faster: a linear scan over ArrayList beats 'O(1)' LinkedList due to cache locality, and HashMap degrades from O(1) to O(n) on collisions (in Java 8+, to O(log n) — a bucket becomes a tree at ≥8 elements with Comparable keys); ArrayList.add's amortized O(1) also hides a one-off O(n) resize."},"alg-structure":{"q":"HashMap vs TreeMap vs ArrayList — by operation complexity?","a":"HashMap get/put — O(1) (no order). TreeMap — O(log n), but sorted. ArrayList: indexed access O(1), but searching for an element O(n). Choosing a structure = choosing the complexity of the operations you need.","d":"First decide which operations are frequent, then pick the structure.","hook":"HashMap — coat-check lockers by tag number, instant. TreeMap — books alphabetized, a bit slower. ArrayList — finding the page you need by flipping through everything.","more":"A data structure is a trade-off: you choose which operations will be cheap. **HashMap** drops entries into a bucket by hash → get/put on average **O(1)**, but key order is arbitrary. **TreeMap** keeps keys in a balanced tree → O(log n), but is always sorted and supports range queries (from-to).\n\n**ArrayList** is an array: index O(1), but `contains`/value lookup is O(n), and insertion in the middle is O(n) too because of the shift.\n\nGotcha: HashMap degrades with a **bad hashCode** (everything in one bucket → O(n)); in Java a bucket turns into a tree on collisions, which softens this. Need order or ranges — take TreeMap; need frequent `contains` — use a HashSet instead of a list.","quizCorrect":"HashMap — O(1) unordered; TreeMap — O(log n) sorted; lookup in ArrayList — O(n)","quizWrong":["HashMap — O(log n), TreeMap — O(1), ArrayList — O(1)","All three give O(1) reads, the only difference is memory they consume in practice","HashMap is sorted in O(1), and TreeMap keeps no order"],"picCap":"Structure = choosing the complexity of the operations you need","note":"HashMap treeification (Java 8+) triggers only at TREEIFY_THRESHOLD=8 collisions IN A SINGLE bucket AND when capacity >= MIN_TREEIFY_CAPACITY=64; with fewer than 64 buckets HashMap just resizes instead of turning the bin into a tree. Also the tree needs Comparable keys: for non-comparable objects it degrades back to O(n) even after treeify, so 'the tree saves you from a bad hashCode' is a myth for arbitrary keys."},"alg-hidden-n2":{"q":"Where does a hidden O(n²) lurk?","a":"list.contains() in a loop over the list, String concatenation in a loop, or an N+1 query — each \"small\" call inside the loop gets multiplied by n and quietly turns into a quadratic.","d":"contains in a loop → HashSet O(1); String in a loop → StringBuilder.","note":"String concatenation in a loop is O(n²) in BOTH time and garbage: every '+' creates a new object and copies everything accumulated so far. The cure is StringBuilder. But javac already optimizes a single 'a+b+c' into one StringBuilder; the quadratic appears precisely when '+' is inside a loop.","hook":"Looking up a name in the guest list from scratch for every guest — a list of 100 turns into 10,000 checks.","more":"A hidden quadratic is born when, inside a loop over n, another O(n) operation hides. From the outside the code looks like a single loop, but it's really n × n.\n\nThe classics:\n- `list.contains(x)` in a loop over the list — a linear search inside a linear loop;\n- **String += in a loop** — strings are immutable, each step copies the whole accumulated text;\n- **N+1** — a separate DB/network query for each of the n entities.\n\nThe cure: replace `List.contains` with a **HashSet** (O(1)), build strings via **StringBuilder**, and fix N+1 with one JOIN or a batch `IN (...)` query. The gotcha is that on 100 elements everything flies, but in prod with 100k it suddenly grinds to a halt.","quizCorrect":"list.contains() in a loop, String concatenation in a loop, N+1 query — a call inside a loop","quizWrong":["In binary search over a sorted array","In HashMap.get() inside a loop over keys","In a single pass over a list with no nesting over a plain array with no inner loop of any kind"],"picCap":"A \"small\" call in a loop × n = a quiet quadratic"},"alg-recursion":{"q":"What makes deep recursion dangerous in Java?","a":"Each call is a frame on the stack; too deep → StackOverflowError. The JVM has no tail-call optimization (TCO), so large depths should be done iteratively or with an explicit stack.","d":"Default stack is ~512 KB; depths in the tens of thousands are already risky.","note":"StackOverflowError is triggered not by depth itself but by frame size: many locals/large parameters = a smaller permissible depth. The default ~512KB–1MB stack ≈ tens of thousands of frames; you can raise it with -Xss or a stack-size flag in new Thread(...), but that treats the symptom, not the cause.","hook":"Stacking glasses up to the ceiling — sooner or later the tower collapses (StackOverflow).","more":"Every method call pushes a frame onto the **thread stack**: parameters, local variables, the return address. The stack is bounded (usually hundreds of KB — thousands of frames), and at too great a depth you hit a **StackOverflowError**.\n\nUnlike Scala/functional languages, the JVM does **not do TCO** (tail-call optimization) — even if the recursive call is the last thing, a frame is still created. So an \"elegant\" recursion over a list of a million elements will blow up.\n\nWhen to use which: recursion is great for **logarithmic** depth (traversing a balanced tree, divide-and-conquer). For linear depth (a long list, a deep graph) rewrite it as a **loop** or use an explicit `Deque` as a stack — the memory goes onto the heap, which is far larger.","quizCorrect":"Each call pushes a frame onto the stack; too deep → StackOverflowError, and the JVM has no TCO","quizWrong":["The JVM itself turns tail recursion into a loop, the only risk is a heap memory leak on deep calls","Deep recursion overflows the heap and causes OutOfMemoryError","There's no danger: the Java compiler always optimizes recursion into iteration"],"picCap":"JVM with no TCO: do large depths iteratively"},"alg-sort":{"q":"Complexity of quicksort/mergesort, and can you sort in O(n)?","a":"Comparison sorts (quicksort, mergesort) — O(n log n) on average (you can't beat that for comparisons). O(n) is possible WITHOUT comparisons and only under conditions: counting/bucket/radix — when the keys are integers in a small known range.","d":"quicksort is O(n²) in the worst case with a bad pivot; mergesort is a steady O(n log n) but needs extra memory.","note":"Arrays.sort(int[]) is dual-pivot quicksort, which has a worst case of O(n²) and is NOT stable; Arrays.sort(Object[]) and Collections.sort are TimSort, a stable O(n log n). So for primitives you can't rely on stability, but for objects you can.","hook":"Comparing in pairs is like weighing on a balance scale: you can't go past the limit. But sorting into bins/cubbies (integers) drops everything in at once — in a single pass.","more":"**Quicksort** and **mergesort** work through pairwise comparisons of elements. For any such sort there's a **theoretical limit of O(n log n)** — because n elements can be arranged in n! ways, and each comparison yields only one bit of information; you can't resolve all the permutations faster.\n\nO(n) is reachable only if you **drop comparisons** and use the values themselves as indices:\n\n- **counting sort** — count how many times each value occurs (needs a small range).\n- **bucket sort** — scatter into range buckets.\n- **radix sort** — sort by digit.\n\nGotcha: this is no free lunch. The condition is **integer keys in a known small range**. Counting sort with a range in the billions will devour memory; on arbitrary objects compared via a comparator these algorithms don't apply.","quizCorrect":"Comparison sorts — O(n log n); O(n) only without comparisons (counting/radix) for integers in a small range","quizWrong":["Quicksort and mergesort always run in O(n), that's their guaranteed complexity","Any sort can be sped up to O(n) with a good hash function","Comparison sorting at O(n log n) is a theoretical limit, faster is impossible by any means whatsoever by any algorithm"],"picCap":"Comparisons — ceiling of O(n log n); O(n) only without comparisons"},"x-alg-amortized-arraylist":{"q":"Why is add() in ArrayList O(1), even though it sometimes copies the whole array internally?","a":"Usually add() drops the element at the end in O(1). But when the array is full — it creates a new one 1.5x larger and copies everything (O(n)). Such expensive moments are rare: the bigger the array, the rarer they are. Spread the cost of all insertions out and you get O(1) per insertion on average. That's amortized complexity.","d":"Amortized vs average: amortized is a guarantee over ANY sequence of n operations (O(n) total), not a probability. The 1.5x growth (Java ArrayList) gives a geometric series of copies: n + n/1.5 + n/2.25 + ... = O(n) total. So a single insertion may be O(n), but n insertions = O(n), meaning O(1) per item. If growth were +1 (fixed), it would come to O(n²) total — an antipattern.","hook":"Subway pass: the turnstile is free, once a month you pay for all the rides — on average pennies per entry.","more":"The key is **geometric growth**: the array grows not by +1 but by multiplication (in Java ~1.5x). Because of this, copies happen ever more rarely as it grows: after expanding to n there's once again plenty of free room.\n\nSum up the cost of all copies over N insertions and you get roughly 2N operations — i.e. a constant per insertion on average. That's **amortized O(1)**: the expensive steps are smeared across the cheap ones.\n\n- If growth were linear (+1 each time), copying would be O(n) per insertion → O(n²) total.\n- Gotcha: one specific insertion can still take O(n); if you know the size in advance — set the capacity in the constructor and remove all resizes.","quizCorrect":"Expensive copies are rare, so on average each insertion comes out to O(1) — amortization","quizWrong":["Array copying in the JVM runs in constant time O(1)","ArrayList is internally a linked list, so insertion is always O(1) with no copying of the array","The array is never copied — it expands in place"],"picCap":"A rare expensive copy is smeared across many cheap insertions","note":"Java ArrayList's actual growth is not exactly 1.5x but oldCapacity + (oldCapacity >> 1), computed in grow(); the first expansion of an empty list jumps straight to DEFAULT_CAPACITY=10. The capacity-preset gotcha: new ArrayList<>(1000) removes resizes, but add(0, x) into the head/middle is still O(n) due to the System.arraycopy shift — capacity only saves you from growth, not from non-tail insertions."},"x-alg-space-complexity":{"q":"What is space complexity and why can recursion eat memory even without collections?","a":"Space complexity — how much EXTRA memory grows along with the input n. Even without any lists, recursion eats memory: each nested call holds its own frame on the stack (parameters, locals, return address). Recursion depth n → O(n) stack memory. An iterative loop over the same data — O(1).","d":"We count ONLY the extra memory, the input doesn't count. Recursion: O(depth) of stack. Mergesort — O(n) for temp arrays + O(log n) stack. In-place quicksort — O(log n) stack. There's often a time↔memory trade-off: a HashSet gives O(1) lookup at the cost of O(n) memory. Tail calls in Java are NOT optimized by the JVM, so tail recursion still grows the stack — rewrite it as a loop.","hook":"A stack of plates: recursion puts a new one down on every call, a loop washes and reuses the same one.","more":"Memory splits into the input (which isn't counted) and the **extra** — what the algorithm creates itself. Space complexity describes the growth of exactly this addition relative to n.\n\nRecursion quietly devours the **call stack**: until the deepest call returns, all the parent frames hang in memory. Each frame is local variables, arguments, and a return address. Depth n gives O(n) on the stack, even if you didn't create a single list.\n\n- Gotcha: recursion that's too deep → **StackOverflowError**.\n- **Tail recursion** in Java is NOT optimized by the JVM, so rewriting it as a loop is about both speed and O(1) memory.","quizCorrect":"Each nested call holds its own frame on the stack: depth n → O(n)","quizWrong":["The JVM duplicates the input data on each recursive call","Recursion doesn't free memory until the program fully terminates entirely","Hidden copies of local variables are created on the heap"],"picCap":"Each call — a new slot on the stack; a loop reuses one variable","note":"The recursion depth at which you hit StackOverflowError depends not on n abstractly but on frame size and the -Xss flag (default ~512KB-1MB), i.e. typically thousands-to-tens-of-thousands of frames, not millions. Hidden trap: even without explicit recursion, a deep Stream/iterator pipeline or chain of CompletableFuture callbacks can grow the stack, and StackOverflowError is an Error — you shouldn't catch it as a normal exception in production."},"x-alg-avg-vs-worst":{"q":"Is HashMap.get() O(1) or O(n)? What's the difference between average and worst case?","a":"On average HashMap.get() is O(1): the hash points straight to the right bucket. But in the worst case all keys land in one bucket (collisions or a bad hashCode) — the lookup turns into walking a list, O(n). Since Java 8, long chains turn into a tree, and the worst case drops to O(log n). What matters in an interview: O(1) is amortized-average, not a guarantee.","d":"Big-O hides constants and talks about growth, so we separately distinguish: best / average / worst / amortized. HashMap: average O(1) with a good distribution and a normal load factor (0.75). Worst O(n) when it degrades into a list, O(log n) after treeify (threshold of 8 elements in a bucket + capacity >= 64). A hash-flooding attack — deliberate collisions to drive a server into O(n). For key safety — immutable keys with a good hashCode.","hook":"A left-luggage office: by locker number — instant. But if everything got dumped into one — you'll have to dig through the whole pile.","more":"HashMap distributes keys into buckets via `hashCode`. On **average** keys are spread evenly, 0-1 element per bucket → access in O(1). In the **worst** case all keys produced the same hash and landed in one bucket — get degenerates into a walk.\n\nSince Java 8 a long chain (8+ elements at capacity ≥64) turns into a **red-black tree**, and the worst case drops from O(n) to O(log n).\n\n- average is measured over the distribution of inputs, worst — the worst input.\n- Gotcha: a broken/constant `hashCode()` breaks everything down to O(n); and keys must be **immutable** — otherwise after a change you won't find your own object.","quizCorrect":"O(n) — all keys in one bucket, walking a list","quizWrong":["O(1) — the hash always points straight to the right bucket","O(log n) — a bucket is always stored as a tree","O(n log n) — the bucket needs re-sorting on lookup"],"picCap":"Evenly across buckets — O(1); all into one — a long O(n) chain","note":"Treeify giving O(log n) only happens if keys are Comparable; otherwise on collisions comparison falls back to a hashCode tie-break, and with equal hashes of non-comparable keys the tree degrades closer to an O(n) traversal. Also key nuance: the threshold of 8 (TREEIFY_THRESHOLD) only triggers when capacity ≥ 64 (MIN_TREEIFY_CAPACITY) — otherwise HashMap first just resizes the table instead of building a tree."},"x-alg-stream-overhead":{"q":"A Java Stream and a for loop both have O(n) — so why is a stream sometimes noticeably slower?","a":"Both are O(n) — they grow the same. But Big-O hides the CONSTANT. A stream creates lambdas on each element, boxes primitives (int→Integer), builds a chain of operation objects. This overhead is multiplied by n. On a hot path or for large n, a plain for over primitives with the same O(n) is actually several times faster. The O() is equal — the constant differs.","d":"Big-O is about asymptotics, not absolute speed. Sources of the constant in streams: autoboxing (use IntStream/mapToInt), megamorphic lambdas, allocation of intermediate objects. parallelStream changes not the O but splits the work across cores — worthwhile only on large n and a heavy operation, otherwise the split/merge overhead eats the gain. Rule: readability via streams by default, a loop only on a provably hot spot after profiling.","note":"Boxing kills not just speed but parallelism/memory too: use IntStream/LongStream instead of Stream<Integer>. And parallelStream by default shares the common ForkJoinPool.commonPool — one blocking operation in it slows the whole process.","hook":"Two couriers walk the same route, but one is in heavy boots — same path, different speed.","more":"Big-O measures how time grows with n, but hides the **constant factor** in front of n. For a stream this factor is larger: per element an operation wrapper object is created, a lambda is invoked (a megamorphic call the JIT inlines worse), and `Stream<Integer>` also **boxes** primitives.\n\nSo at equal O(n) a plain `for` over an `int[]` walks the data several times faster on a hot path.\n\n- Boxing cure: `IntStream`/`LongStream` instead of `Stream<Integer>`.\n- When a stream is justified: readability, **parallelism** (`parallelStream` on large volumes), complex pipelines.\n- Gotcha: micro-optimizing for speed in rarely-called code is a waste — profile first.","quizCorrect":"Big-O hides the constant: lambdas, boxing, chains of objects on each element","quizWrong":["A stream actually has O(n log n) complexity, not O(n)","A stream always runs in a single thread with no parallelism available to it at all","A stream re-traverses the collection on each operation"],"picCap":"Same O(n), but the constant (boxing, lambdas) is larger for the stream"},"alg-binsearch":{"q":"How does binary search work and what's its complexity?","a":"The array must be SORTED. Take the middle element. If the target is smaller — discard the right half, if larger — the left. So each step cuts the range in half. For 1000 elements ~10 steps suffice.","d":"Complexity O(log n): each step leaves half. 1024 → 512 → 256 → ... → 1 = 10 splits, because log2(1024)=10. A linear search (without sorting) would give up to 1024 checks — O(n). The price: the array must be sorted beforehand. Memory O(1) for the iterative version.","note":"A classic bug: mid = (lo+hi)/2 overflows int at large indices. Write lo + (hi-lo)/2. Java has a ready-made Arrays.binarySearch/Collections.binarySearch, but on an UNsorted array it silently returns a garbage (arbitrary) result rather than an error.","hook":"Guess a number 1-100 in 7 tries: \"higher/lower\" — always name the middle.","more":"Under the hood we keep bounds lo and hi. Take the middle mid, compare with the target: smaller — move lo past mid (discard the left half), larger — move hi (discard the right). So the **range is halved** each step, hence complexity **O(log n)**: 1000 elements — ~10 steps, a million — ~20.\n\nFor that speed we pay with a requirement: the array must be **sorted**, otherwise \"discarding half\" loses its meaning.\n\n- Gotcha: the classic overflow in mid = (lo+hi)/2 — write lo + (hi-lo)/2. And an off-by-one in the lo<=hi condition easily loops forever or skips an element.","quizCorrect":"The array is sorted; each step cuts the range in half — O(log n)","quizWrong":["The array is arbitrary; we go through all elements in turn — O(n)","The array is sorted, but the complexity is still O(n²)","Elements are unique, otherwise the complexity becomes O(n log n)"],"picCap":"Middle = 7. Target 13 is larger → discard the left half, search further right. Each step cuts the range in half."},"alg-bst":{"q":"Binary search tree (BST) — how is it built?","a":"A BST is a tree where for each node the numbers on the left are smaller and on the right larger. To find a number — at each step you go left or right, discarding half. That's why lookup is fast: O(log n). But if you insert already-sorted data one after another, the tree stretches into a chain (list) and becomes slow: O(n).","d":"The BST property: for ANY node, all keys in the left subtree < node < all keys in the right subtree. Lookup/insertion go along the tree's height. A balanced tree has height ~log n → O(log n). But balance isn't guaranteed: inserting 1,2,3,4,5 gives a right-leaning \"staircase\" of height n → O(n), i.e. an ordinary list. Cured by self-balancing trees (AVL, red-black) that keep the height ~log n automatically.","hook":"Like guessing a number 1–100: \"higher/lower?\" halves it. But if the hints all lie one way — you end up counting one by one.","more":"A BST holds an **invariant**: in any node the whole left subtree is smaller than it, the whole right one is larger. Lookup goes from the root: compare, step left or right, at each step discarding half the remaining subtree — on average **O(log n)**.\n\nThe main problem: speed depends on the tree's **height**, and the height — on the insertion order. Insert already-sorted data one after another and each new node goes right, the tree degenerates into a chain (essentially a linked list), and lookup degrades to **O(n)**.\n\n- That's why a \"bare\" BST is almost never used in prod — people take self-balancing variants (AVL, red-black) that keep the height under control.","quizCorrect":"The tree degenerates into a chain, and lookup degrades to O(n)","quizWrong":["The tree balances itself, lookup stays O(log n)","Lookup speeds up to O(1), since the data is already in order already","Insertion fails with an error: duplicate keys are forbidden"],"picCap":"Top — a fine tree (we halve). Bottom — sorted input stretched it into a list.","note":"`TreeMap`/`TreeSet` trap: being red-black trees they compare elements via `compareTo`/`Comparator`, NOT via `equals`. If `compareTo` is inconsistent with `equals` (e.g. BigDecimal scale: `new BigDecimal(\"1.0\").compareTo(\"1.00\")==0`), TreeSet treats them as one element and `contains` will disagree with HashSet."},"alg-balanced":{"q":"Why self-balancing trees (AVL, red-black)?","a":"If you insert already-sorted numbers into a plain BST, it turns into a \"list\" — lookup becomes O(n), slow. AVL and red-black perform rotations after insertion and keep the height ~log n. So lookup/insertion are always O(log n). In Java, TreeMap and \"treeified\" HashMap buckets run on red-black.","d":"A plain BST knows nothing about balance: insert 1,2,3,4,5 — each new node goes right, the tree stretches into a chain of height n, and lookup degrades from O(log n) to O(n). Self-balancing trees check the invariant on every insertion/deletion and repair it with rotations — a local restructuring of 2-3 nodes in O(1). AVL keeps strict balance (subtree height difference ≤ 1) — searches faster but rotates more often. Red-black balances more loosely (via node coloring), but restructures less often — better with frequent writes. The height is guaranteed O(log n), so all operations are O(log n). In the JDK: TreeMap/TreeSet are red-black trees; and in HashMap, when ≥8 collisions pile up in one bucket, the linked list is \"treeified\" into a red-black tree so the worst-case lookup within a bucket is O(log n) rather than O(n).","note":"HashMap \"treeifies\" a bucket into a red-black tree only at TREEIFY_THRESHOLD=8 nodes AND table capacity ≥64 (MIN_TREEIFY_CAPACITY), otherwise it resizes first. And it requires keys to be Comparable, otherwise it compares by identity hash — a tree won't save you from a bad hashCode.","hook":"Books in a pile (BST) topple into a crooked tower; a shelf with supports (balance) levels itself — everything within reach in log n.","more":"The root of the problem — a plain BST doesn't control its height: sorted input stretches it into a linear \"list\" with O(n) lookup. Balancing fixes this structurally.\n\n**AVL** and **red-black** check the local imbalance after each insertion/deletion and perform **rotations** — they re-link 2-3 nodes, leveling the height to ~log n. AVL keeps balance stricter (faster lookup), red-black — looser, but cheaper restructuring (faster writes).\n\n- In Java, **TreeMap**/TreeSet run on red-black, and in HashMap a long bucket-list is \"treeified\" into a red-black tree on collisions (threshold 8) — so the worst case is O(log n), not O(n).","quizCorrect":"Rotations keep the height ~log n, so lookup/insertion are always O(log n)","quizWrong":["They store data sorted, which a plain BST can't do","They give O(1) lookup thanks to key hashing","They save memory by removing references to child nodes from the leaves of the tree"],"picCap":"Chain O(n) → rotations keep the height ~log n → guaranteed O(log n)"},"alg-btree-ds":{"q":"B-tree — why do databases and file systems use it?","a":"A B-tree packs MANY keys into a single node. So the tree isn't tall but wide: to find the right key among millions, 3-4 steps down suffice. Each step is a disk read, and disk is slow. Few steps = few reads = fast. That's how DB indexes are built.","d":"Disk reads not byte by byte but in blocks (pages ~8-16 KB). A B-tree makes a node the size of one page, so a single read pulls in hundreds of keys. Height h ≈ log_B(N): with branching factor B=100 and 1M keys the height is ~3. A binary tree (B=2) would give a height of ~20 — many times more disk accesses. A B+tree (the DB variant) stores data only in leaves, and the leaves are linked into a list → fast range scans (BETWEEN, ORDER BY). Balancing happens via node split/merge, so the height stays even across all branches.","hook":"A thick phone book: open it at the right letter — and you're almost there, instead of flipping page by page.","more":"A B-tree is a generalization of a BST where one node holds not a single key but **many** (tens to hundreds) plus child references between them. Thanks to the high branching factor the tree comes out low and wide: millions of keys fit into **3-4 levels**.\n\nWhy exactly this way: each descent by a level is a separate **block read from disk**, and disk is orders of magnitude slower than memory. The node size is tuned to the disk page (4-8 KB) so that a single read gets the maximum keys. Few levels = few disk reads.\n\n- That's how DB indexes are built (often a B+tree — data only in leaves, leaves linked in a list for range scans) and FS metadata.","quizCorrect":"Many keys per node give a small height → few disk reads","quizWrong":["It fits entirely in the CPU cache and never touches the disk","Each node stores exactly one key, which speeds up comparison","It doesn't require keys to be sorted within nodes"],"picCap":"Node = many keys. The tree is wide, not tall → 3 disk reads instead of 20.","note":"Non-obvious: a B+tree index on a UUID/random key (or PK on a random GUID) causes page splits all over the tree and thrashes the buffer cache, whereas a monotonic key always appends into the same rightmost leaf. That's why under heavy insert load PostgreSQL/MySQL favor bigserial/ULID over `uuid_generate_v4()` for the clustering/primary key."},"alg-hashtable":{"q":"A hash table — what makes it O(1)?","a":"You run the key through a hash function — get a number, and it points straight at a cell number (a bucket). You don't search through all of them, you jump straight to the right one — that's why it's O(1) on average. If two keys produced the same number — there's a chain there, you check it.","d":"hash(key) → number, then `number % array_size` = bucket index. Array access by index = O(1), so lookup/insertion/deletion are O(1) on average.\n\nCollisions (different keys → one bucket) are resolved by:\n- chaining (a linked list in the bucket) — you walk a short list;\n- a tree (in Java's HashMap, at ≥8 elements in a bucket the list turns into a red-black tree → O(log n) instead of O(n)).\n\nDegradation: a bad hash (all keys into one bucket) → a long chain → O(n). Overflow (many elements per bucket, a high load factor ~0.75) → resize: a bigger array is created and all elements are redistributed (rehash), an expensive O(n) operation, but rare → amortized it stays O(1).","note":"O(1) holds only as long as hashCode is uniform and consistent with equals. A broken/mutating-after-put hashCode → the element \"gets lost\" in the map (can't be found by the same key). In Java 8 a long chain turns into a tree (O(log n)), which softens the worst case but doesn't lift the requirement on hashCode.","hook":"A coat-check with tags: by number, straight to your hanger, no searching through all of them. One number for two people — there's a short queue.","more":"Under the hood is an ordinary array of buckets. The key is run through a **hash function** into a number, then the remainder by array size is taken — giving the cell index. We don't scan everything, we jump straight to the right one: insertion and lookup on average **O(1)**.\n\nThe problem is **collisions**: different keys produced the same index. Resolved with chains (a list/tree in the bucket) or open addressing (looking for a nearby free cell).\n\n- Gotcha: when filled beyond the load factor (in Java ~0.75) the table does a **resize** and re-hashes everything — a one-off O(n).\n- A bad hash function or a collision attack dumps everything into one bucket, and O(1) turns into O(n).","quizCorrect":"The key's hash points straight at the bucket number — we jump to the right cell","quizWrong":["All keys are stored sorted, so binary search works","The table scans the cells in order, but very fast","It caches the last request, so repeat access is instant for the same key afterwards"],"picCap":"The key rides into the hash → jump to bucket 1 (O(1)). Bucket 3 pulses red — collisions, we walk the chain = degradation toward O(n)."},"alg-bfs":{"q":"BFS (breadth-first search) — how and when?","a":"BFS walks the graph in \"rings\" from the start: first the neighbors, then the neighbors' neighbors. We take a vertex from the QUEUE, put its unvisited neighbors at the tail, mark the visited ones. In an unweighted graph this finds the SHORTEST path (by number of edges).","d":"A queue (FIFO) holds the \"frontier\" — the current layer. Since neighbors are processed strictly in insertion order, vertices are discovered in increasing distance from the start: level 0, 1, 2… So the first time a vertex is reached = the shortest path in edges (for weighted edges you need Dijkstra). Complexity O(V+E). Memory O(V) — in the worst case the queue holds the graph's widest layer (its width), so on \"wide\" graphs the memory is larger than for DFS.","hook":"Like ripples on water from a thrown stone: the wave reaches the nearest points first.","more":"BFS traverses a graph in **layers**: first all vertices at distance 1 from the start, then at distance 2, and so on. The mechanics rest on a **queue (FIFO)**: pull a vertex, put its unvisited neighbors at the tail, mark visited so as not to loop.\n\nThe \"by layers\" order guarantees we reach a vertex by the minimum number of edges — so in an **unweighted** graph BFS gives the **shortest path**.\n\n- Use it for: shortest path by number of steps, finding everything \"within N connections\", levels in a tree.\n- Gotcha: mark visited at the moment of adding to the queue, not at extraction — otherwise one node ends up in the queue many times. Memory is O(layer width), large for dense graphs.","quizCorrect":"The shortest path by number of edges, traversing vertices via a queue","quizWrong":["The shortest path by edge weights, using a stack","Any path to the target, using recursion into the depths of the graph being searched","A minimum spanning tree, using a priority queue"],"picCap":"Take from the head of the queue → put neighbors at the tail. So the graph unfolds in layers.","note":"The \"BFS = shortest path\" rule breaks on weighted edges: even with 0/1 weights plain BFS is wrong — you need 0-1 BFS on a deque (push to front for weight 0), and for arbitrary weights Dijkstra. A common interview mistake is applying BFS to a graph whose \"edges\" have differing costs."},"alg-dfs":{"q":"DFS (depth-first search) — how and when?","a":"You walk through a maze: dive into the first corridor until you hit a dead end, then back up to the fork and try the next one. That's DFS: deep until you can't, then backtrack. A stack (or recursion) remembers where to return. Memory — roughly the length of the current path (the depth).","d":"DFS = depth-first search. The engine is a stack: recursion uses the call stack, the iterative variant uses an explicit Stack. We mark visited nodes so as not to loop. Memory O(depth) for the path plus O(V) for visited — that's a plus against BFS, where the queue holds the whole \"frontier\" (can be O(width)). Where it's used: cycle detection (a node in the current path = a cycle), topological sort (the order after leaving a node), enumerating all paths/combinations (backtracking), tree traversal. Important: DFS does NOT give the shortest path in an unweighted graph — for that, BFS. Traversal complexity O(V+E).","hook":"A maze with a thread: go deep until a dead end, then reel the thread back to the fork.","more":"DFS goes down one branch **to the end**, hits a dead end and backtracks to the nearest fork to try the next option. The \"where to return\" state is held by a **stack** — explicit or implicit via recursion (the call stack).\n\nThe key is memory: we keep only the current path, so the cost is ~**O(depth)**, not width. On wide graphs this is far thriftier than BFS.\n\n- Use it for: reachability/connectivity checks, cycle detection, topological sort, backtracking enumeration (sudoku, combinations).\n- Gotcha: deep recursion hits a **stack overflow** — on large graphs switch to an explicit stack. And DFS does NOT give the shortest path in an unweighted graph — for that, BFS.","quizCorrect":"Dives deep until a dead end, then backtracks; memory ~ the path depth","quizWrong":["Traverses vertices in rings from the start; memory ~ the level width of the tree","Goes deep, but needs memory for all the graph's vertices at once","Enumerates all paths simultaneously; memory ~ the number of edges"],"picCap":"We dive A→B→C to a dead end, backtrack to B, go into D. The stack holds only the current path.","note":"visited subtlety: to detect a cycle in a DIRECTED graph a single boolean visited is not enough — you need three colors (white/gray/black) or a separate \"on current recursion stack\" flag, otherwise an edge into an already fully-processed vertex is falsely counted as a cycle. In an undirected graph it's the opposite — you must ignore the edge back to the parent, else every edge looks like a cycle."},"alg-dp":{"q":"Dynamic programming — the essence?","a":"DP is \"don't compute twice\". A big problem is broken into small ones, and the same small ones recur. Solve one once — write down the answer, then just take the ready value. So an exponential brute force turns into a fast pass.","d":"DP works when there are 2 properties: (1) optimal substructure — the answer to a big problem is assembled from answers to subproblems; (2) overlapping subproblems — the same subproblems come up many times. Two approaches: memoization (top-down) — ordinary recursion + a cache, computing lazily on demand; tabulation (bottom-up) — filling an array from the base upward, no recursion. Naive Fibonacci: fib(n)=fib(n-1)+fib(n-2) recomputes the same thing → a call tree of ~2^n. With a cache each value is computed exactly once → O(n) time, O(n) or even O(1) memory.","hook":"Solved a problem on scratch paper — don't solve it again, peek at the answer on the scratch paper.","more":"Under the hood DP breaks the problem into **subproblems** and stores their answers in a table (or cache). Before computing it peeks: \"already computed?\" — if yes, takes the ready value, if no — computes and records. That's **memoization** (top-down) or filling a table bottom-up.\n\nIt only works when subproblems **overlap** (the same ones recur) and there's **optimal substructure** (the whole's answer is assembled from the parts' answers). Without overlap DP is pointless — it would just be ordinary recursion.\n\n- Gotcha: a wrongly chosen state blows up the table. First clearly describe what exactly you're memoizing.","quizCorrect":"Solve recurring subproblems once and reuse the answers","quizWrong":["Split the problem into independent parts and solve them in parallel","At each step take the locally best option without recomputing","Randomly try options until a suitable one is found"],"picCap":"The repeats (blinking) are computed once and put in the cache → the exponential becomes a line","note":"Memoization gotcha in Java: a HashMap cache with a composite key usually needs a key object (record/list) and primitives get boxed — on hot DP this hurts GC and speed; bottom-up over `int[][]`/`long[]` is often several times faster. Also recursive memoization can easily hit `StackOverflowError` for large n, which the tabular variant avoids."},"alg-two-ptr":{"q":"The \"two pointers\" technique — what's it for?","a":"Two fingers move along a sorted row: one on the left, the other on the right. The sum is larger than needed — move the right one left, smaller — the left one right. So in a single pass (O(n)) you find a pair, without enumerating all pairs.","d":"It works because the array is sorted: moving a pointer is guaranteed to change the sum in the right direction, so we won't miss any valid pair. Variants: two ends toward each other (a pair with a given sum, palindrome check) or \"fast/slow\" — one catching up to the other (removing duplicates, a sliding window/subarray). Replaces a nested O(n²) loop with a single O(n) pass. Memory O(1).","hook":"Two fingers along a shelf of books: you flip from the edges toward the center until they meet on the right pair.","more":"It works thanks to **being sorted**: since the row is ordered, from the pair's sum you know exactly which way to step. Too much — decrease with the right pointer, too little — increase with the left. The pointers move toward each other and meet — hence **O(n)** instead of O(n²).\n\nThe idea is that we discard whole groups of obviously useless pairs at once, rather than checking each one.\n\n- Use on **sorted** arrays/strings: a pair with a given sum, removing duplicates, reversal, palindrome check.\n- Gotcha: on unsorted data the method breaks — sort first (or use another approach, e.g. a hash set).","quizCorrect":"Moves pointers from the edges of a sorted row in a single O(n) pass","quizWrong":["Enumerates all pairs of elements with a nested loop in O(n²)","Hashes the elements and looks up each one's complement in O(1) in a map","Sorts the array at every step, so it runs in O(n log n)"],"picCap":"L and R move toward each other in a single O(n) pass; a match with target pulses green","note":"Hidden cost: \"O(n) with two pointers\" only holds if the array is already sorted — add the sort and you pay O(n log n), at which point for two-sum a hash set at pure O(n) wins. Two-pointer pays off when the sort is needed anyway (or data is pre-sorted), or when O(1) memory matters versus the hash's O(n)."},"alg-sliding":{"q":"Sliding window — when to apply it?","a":"The window [l,r] is a chunk of an array/string. You move the right edge forward and add an element. If the condition breaks (e.g. the sum > k or a letter repeated) — you move the left edge until it's fixed. Each element enters and leaves once → O(n), not O(n²).","d":"Apply it when you're looking for a contiguous substring/subarray with a condition: longest/shortest, sum = k, no repeats. Two pointers l and r. Inside the window you keep state (sum, character counts, a set). r always moves forward; l catches up only on a violation and never rolls back — hence 2n steps total. NOT suitable if order doesn't matter or elements can be taken non-contiguously.","hook":"A caterpillar crawls along a leaf: the head reaches forward, the tail pulls up — the body-window doesn't tear.","more":"A window is a **contiguous subrange** of elements. Instead of recomputing each subrange from scratch, we keep the current sum/count and update it **incrementally**: an element entered on the right — added, left on the left — subtracted.\n\nEach element crosses the window boundary at most twice (entering and leaving), so in total **O(n)**, not O(n²) as with enumerating all subranges.\n\n- Apply it when you're looking for the best/suitable **contiguous** fragment: max sum of length k, the longest substring without repeats.\n- Gotcha: if **negative** numbers or gaps are allowed — the window may not shrink correctly; then you need another technique (prefix sums).","quizCorrect":"Each element enters the window and leaves exactly once","quizWrong":["The window is a fixed size, so there are always exactly n/2 passes","It sorts the array beforehand, which speeds up checking the condition","The left edge tries every position for each right edge"],"picCap":"The window (blue) grows rightward in turn; on a violation the rule l++ flashes red — the left edge pulls up","note":"Classic sliding window only works when growing the window monotonically worsens the condition and shrinking improves it. With negative numbers this breaks: for 'minimum subarray with sum ≥ k' over negative values, adding an element can decrease the sum and the window won't shrink correctly — you need a prefix-sum array + monotonic deque (or TreeMap), not two pointers."},"alg-greedy":{"q":"Greedy algorithm — the idea and the risk?","a":"A greedy algorithm grabs whatever looks best right now at each step, without thinking about the future. It's fast and simple. But locally best doesn't always yield the best overall: greedy works only if the problem has the \"greedy-choice property\".","d":"Greedy gives a PROVABLY correct answer only when the problem has two properties: (1) the greedy-choice property — a global optimum can be assembled from locally optimal steps; (2) optimal substructure — the problem's optimum contains the subproblems' optima. Examples where greedy works: Huffman coding, MST (Kruskal/Prim), Dijkstra (no negative edges), making change in a \"canonical\" coin system. Where it breaks: making change with {1,3,4} for an amount of 6 — greedy gives 4+1+1=3 coins, but the optimum is 3+3=2 coins; the 0/1 knapsack. If the property is absent — use dynamic programming or backtracking.","note":"The main trap: greedy seems right on examples but gives a non-optimum — the classic coin change {1,3,4} for an amount of 6 (greedy 4+1+1=3 coins instead of 3+3=2). The correctness of greedy must be PROVED (matroid/exchange argument), not checked on a couple of tests.","hook":"A glutton at the buffet puts the biggest piece on first — and there's no room left for the cake.","more":"Greedy builds the answer step by step, fixing the locally best choice and **never reconsidering it**. No backtracking, no enumeration of options — hence the speed and the simplicity of the code.\n\nThis is correct only with two properties: the **greedy choice** (a local optimum leads to a global one) and optimal substructure. Making change with \"ordinary\" denominations works, but with tricky denominations greedy gives a non-optimum — there you need DP.\n\n- Gotcha: it's easy to believe greedy is correct and be wrong. **Prove** the correctness or check against counterexamples, don't rely on intuition.","quizCorrect":"Takes the locally best at each step; doesn't always yield the best overall","quizWrong":["Enumerates all options and is guaranteed to find the optimum in any task you give it","Memoizes subproblems, so it's always optimal like DP","Backtracks on an error, so there's no risk"],"picCap":"Amount 6, coins {1,3,4}. Top — greedy grabs 4 first (locally best), result 3 coins. Bottom — the true optimum, 2 coins."},"alg-heap-pq":{"q":"Heap (priority queue) — why bother?","a":"A heap is a \"shelf\" where the most important element (min or max) always sits on top. Drop something in and it bubbles up or sinks down to its place on its own. Grabbing the top is instant. It doesn't sort everything — it just keeps the \"best\" ready. Insert/remove is O(log n), peeking the top is O(1).","d":"A binary heap is a nearly complete binary tree stored in an array: element i has children at 2i+1 and 2i+2. Invariant: parent ≤ children (min-heap) or ≥ (max-heap). Insert appends at the end and bubbles up (sift-up); extracting the root moves the last element into its place and sinks it down (sift-down) — both O(log n), since the tree height is log n. Peeking the root is O(1). Why: top-K in O(n log k), priority queues, Dijkstra (pull the nearest vertex). A heap doesn't give a full ordering — only fast access to the extremum, which is why it's cheaper than a full sort when you only need the \"best\".","note":"Java's PriorityQueue is a min-heap by natural ordering; for a max-heap pass Comparator.reverseOrder(). Its iterator and toString do NOT return elements in sorted order (only peek/poll respect ordering). And it's not thread-safe — use PriorityBlockingQueue for concurrent access.","hook":"A heap is like an ER triage queue: not first-come-first-served, but whoever is in worse shape goes straight to the top.","more":"A heap is a **binary tree** laid out in an array where the parent is always no greater than (min-heap) or no less than (max-heap) its children. It's a weak ordering: the whole array isn't sorted, only the **root extremum** is guaranteed. On insert/remove an element bubbles up or sinks down to restore the property — a path along the tree height, hence **O(log n)**.\n\nWhy: when you don't need a fully sorted list, just the **most important** element right now, with the ability to keep adding more.\n\n- Use it for: top-K elements, merging streams, Dijkstra, task schedulers.\n- Gotcha: a heap does NOT give fast lookup of an arbitrary element and doesn't iterate in order.","quizCorrect":"The top element is always the most important: insert O(log n), peek O(1)","quizWrong":["Fully sorts all elements on every insert in O(n log n) of a new element being added","Gives O(1) access to any element by index","Stores elements as a sorted array, insert in O(1)"],"picCap":"The minimum always sits on top (pulsing); add bubbles up, poll sinks down — hence O(log n), peek O(1)"},"g-alg-open-vs-chain":{"q":"Open addressing vs chaining — what's the difference and where does each hurt?","a":"Chaining: each slot holds a list of collisions (like Java's HashMap). Open addressing: a collision goes into the NEXT free slot of the array itself, no lists. Open addressing is faster on the cache, but degrades once load goes past ~0.7 and suffers from clustering and deletions (you need tombstone markers).","d":"Java's HashMap uses chaining (since Java 8 a long bucket turns into a tree). Open addressing (linear/quadratic probing, double hashing) saves the memory of pointers and is cache-friendly, but it needs a low load factor and careful deletion via tombstones, otherwise lookups break.","note":"In open addressing a naive delete breaks lookups (it severs the probe chain) — you need tombstone markers, which over time clutter the table and force a rehash. That's why chaining is usually preferred when deletes are frequent.","hook":"Chaining is a coat hook with a bunch of coats. Open addressing — slot taken? go to the next locker.","more":"Both strategies resolve hash-table **collisions**. **Chaining**: a slot holds a reference to a list (or tree) of all elements with that hash — like `HashMap` in Java. **Open addressing**: everything lives in the array itself; on a collision you probe for the next free slot.\n\nOpen addressing has better **cache locality** (no nodes scattered across the heap) and uses less memory. But it's sensitive to fill level.\n\n- Open addressing degrades past **load factor > 0.7**, suffers from clustering\n- **Deletion** in open addressing needs tombstone markers, otherwise probing \"breaks\"\n- Chaining tolerates high load better, but spends memory on pointers","quizCorrect":"You can't just null out a slot — you need a tombstone, otherwise lookups break off","quizWrong":["Deletion is impossible without a full rehash","Deletion makes all other keys unreachable","Deletion converts the table back into chaining with a linked list in every bucket again"],"picCap":"chaining drags a list, open addressing probes a neighboring slot"},"g-alg-union-find":{"q":"Union-Find (DSU) — what does it solve and why is it almost O(1)?","a":"A \"sets with fast merging\" structure: it answers \"are a and b in the same group?\" and \"merge the groups of a and b\". Each element points to a parent; the root is the set's id. With path compression + union by rank it's amortized almost O(1) (inverse Ackermann α(n) ≤ 5).","d":"Classic uses: graph connected components, cycle detection (Kruskal's MST), dynamic equivalence, \"friends of friends\". Without the two optimizations the trees degenerate into chains and find becomes O(n). Both optimizations together give α(n) — practically a constant.","hook":"Clans: to find out \"are we kin?\", run up to the clan head. You remember the path so you don't have to run it again next time.","more":"**DSU** stores a partition into disjoint sets. Each element references a parent; the tree's **root** is the set's representative (id). `find(x)` climbs to the root, `union(a,b)` hangs one root under the other.\n\nTwo tricks give the speed. **Path compression**: during find, re-point the traversed nodes straight to the root. **Union by rank/size**: attach the smaller tree under the larger one — the height doesn't grow.\n\n- Amortized almost **O(1)** — inverse Ackermann α(n) ≤ 5 for any realistic n\n- Used for: connected components, **Kruskal** (MST), \"friends of friends\", cycle detection\n- Gotcha: without both tricks the tree degenerates into a list → O(n)","quizCorrect":"Re-points the traversed nodes straight to the root, flattening the tree for future finds","quizWrong":["Compresses element values to save memory and to make the structure fit into the processor cache","Removes duplicates within a set","Balances the tree like a red-black tree"],"picCap":"both point to the same root → one set","note":"Trap when combining path compression with union by rank: after compression the stored rank no longer equals the actual tree height, so it can't be used as a true height — it's just an upper-bound heuristic (hence \"rank\", not \"height\"). Also, classic recursive path compression on a chain of millions of elements causes StackOverflowError in Java — for large n write an iterative or two-pass find."},"g-alg-trie":{"q":"Trie (prefix tree) — why bother when there's HashMap<String,V>?","a":"A trie stores strings character by character: a shared prefix is a shared path. Lookup/insert is O(word length), INDEPENDENT of the number of words. It gives you what a HashMap can't: prefix autocomplete, \"all words starting with abc-\", alphabetical order via traversal.","d":"A HashMap gives exact get in O(L) on the string's hash, but can't do prefix queries. A trie pays in memory (a node per character) but offers prefix search and longest-prefix-match (IP routing, T9 dictionaries, spell checkers). The compressed variant is a radix/PATRICIA trie.","hook":"The entrance to an apartment building: everyone rides up to their floor together, then splits off into letter-apartments.","more":"A **trie** is a tree where the path from the root along characters = a string; a shared **prefix** shares a common path. A node marks \"a word ends here\". Insert/lookup walk by character in **O(word length)** and don't depend on how many words are in the structure.\n\nA HashMap gives O(1) lookup for an exact key but knows nothing about prefixes. A trie answers prefix queries cheaply.\n\n- **Autocomplete**: reach the node \"abc\" → traverse the subtree\n- Lexicographic order — a plain DFS over children\n- Gotcha: memory — lots of nodes/pointers; to save space use a compact **radix tree** or arrays instead of a child map","quizCorrect":"Quickly find all keys with a given prefix (autocomplete)","quizWrong":["Exact key lookup in O(1)","Store values of an arbitrary type in the nodes of the tree","Work with null keys"],"picCap":"shared path c-a, then a branch to t and r","note":"A naive Trie with a HashMap<Character,Node> per node is catastrophically memory-hungry (HashMap overhead plus Character autoboxing on every node) and kills cache locality due to pointer chasing, so on large dictionaries it is often slower than a HashMap even for prefixes; for production loads use a double-array trie or radix-tree, not the textbook map-of-children variant."},"g-alg-heapsort":{"q":"Heap-sort — how does a heap sort and why is it in-place but not stable?","a":"Build a max-heap from the array (O(n)), then n times: take the root (the max), swap it with the last element, shrink the heap, sift down. Guaranteed O(n log n), with no extra array (in-place). But the order of equal elements is broken by the swaps — it's not stable.","d":"Build-heap bottom-up is O(n), not O(n log n). Heap-sort beats quicksort with its guaranteed worst-case O(n log n) (no degradation to O(n²)), but loses on cache locality (jumps across indices i, 2i+1). That's why production usually uses quicksort/introsort.","hook":"A pyramid: the most important one is always at the top. Take it off — the next one rises to the throne.","more":"First, **build-heap**: in O(n) turn the array into a **max-heap** (sift-down from the end). Then n times: the root is the current maximum, **swap** it with the last element of the heap, shrink the heap by 1, and **sift** the new root down. The maximums settle at the end in descending order.\n\nGuaranteed **O(n log n)** worst case and **in-place** — the heap lives in the same array.\n\n- **Not stable**: swapping the root with the tail reorders equal elements\n- Slower in practice than quicksort due to poor cache locality (jumps across the tree)\n- Valuable as a safety net: predictable worst case with no recursion","quizCorrect":"Guaranteed worst-case O(n log n) with no degradation","quizWrong":["It's stable and preserves the order of equal elements","It's faster in practice thanks to the cache","It doesn't require comparing elements"],"picCap":"Root = max (pulsing), moves to the end, then we sift down","note":"Build-heap in O(n) is a tight bound, but the intuition 'n nodes × log n' is wrong: the work sums to ~2n precisely because exactly half the nodes are leaves (which never sift down) and deep nodes are few — linearity only holds with bottom-up sift-down, not with naive n insertions via sift-up, which genuinely cost O(n log n)."},"g-alg-dijkstra":{"q":"Dijkstra — how does it find the shortest path and where does it break?","a":"From the start vertex, greedily pull the nearest unvisited one (via a min-heap) and relax the edges of its neighbors. With a PriorityQueue it's O((V+E) log V). It breaks on NEGATIVE edges: the greedy \"the minimum is fixed\" assumption is wrong — there you need Bellman-Ford.","d":"Dijkstra = BFS weighted by cost, on a priority queue. The assumption: once a vertex is popped from the heap with minimal dist, that's final. A negative edge can later make an already-\"closed\" vertex cheaper, and the assumption collapses. Bellman-Ford is O(V·E) and also catches negative cycles.","note":"Dijkstra breaks specifically on negative edges, not on negative cycles — even a single negative edge with no cycle gives a wrong result, because a vertex is fixed as 'final' prematurely. In a PriorityQueue implementation, don't forget to discard stale entries (lazy deletion), otherwise a vertex gets processed again.","hook":"Water spreading from a tap: it fills the nearby area first. But if there's a \"flow backward\" somewhere, the model lies.","more":"Keep an array of minimal distances (start = 0, the rest ∞). At each step, **greedily** pop the unvisited vertex with the smallest distance from the **min-heap**, fix it, and **relax** its edges: if going through it reaches a neighbor more cheaply, update.\n\nWith a binary heap — **O((V+E) log V)**.\n\n- Breaks on **negative edges**: the assumption \"a popped vertex is already final\" is wrong — something cheaper could arrive later via a minus\n- For negative weights — **Bellman-Ford** (which also catches negative cycles)\n- Gotcha: use lazy-deletion (skip stale heap entries by checking visited)","quizCorrect":"A vertex popped from the heap is treated as final, but a negative edge can make it cheaper later","quizWrong":["A priority queue can't store negative numbers","A graph with negative edges is always disconnected and cannot be traversed by any shortest-path algorithm","Because of int overflow when summing weights"],"picCap":"The frontier moves S→A→B, we pull the minimum; the negative edge blinks — it breaks the greedy choice"},"g-alg-toposort":{"q":"Topological sort — what is it and how does it catch cycles?","a":"A linear ordering of a DAG's vertices where every edge u→v goes \"left to right\" (dependency before dependent). Kahn's algorithm: put vertices with in-degree 0 into a queue, remove them, decrement their neighbors' degrees. If not all are processed by the end — there's a CYCLE.","d":"Applications: module build order (Maven/Gradle), scheduling tasks with dependencies, import resolution, DB migrations. Two ways: Kahn (BFS by in-degree) or DFS with post-order reversal. A cycle = no valid topo sort = a \"circular dependency\" error.","hook":"Put socks on before shoes: ordering tasks with dependencies. If \"socks require shoes\" — it's a loop, no way to do it.","more":"It's a linear ordering of a **DAG**'s vertices where, for every edge u→v, vertex u comes before v. That's how you lay out dependencies: \"what must be done first\".\n\n**Kahn's algorithm**: compute the in-degree (number of incoming edges) of every vertex. Queue the vertices with **in-degree 0**, pop them one by one into the result and decrement their neighbors' degrees; any whose degree hits 0 goes into the queue.\n\n- If **not all** vertices made it into the result → a **cycle** remains, no topo sort exists\n- Alternative — DFS with post-order (reverse the exit stack)\n- Used for: builds (make), task schedulers, package dependency resolution","quizCorrect":"If not all vertices were processed — the remaining ones are in a cycle, none has in-degree 0","quizWrong":["It sums edge weights and compares to zero","It checks that the number of edges exceeds the number of vertices before starting the traversal","A cycle is impossible in any directed graph"],"picCap":"Dependencies are cleared in order A→B→C and pulled into a line; a cycle breaks the ordering","note":"Kahn's algorithm is non-deterministic: with several in-degree-0 vertices the order depends on the queue's structure, so the same DAG yields different valid orderings — if you need reproducibility (stable builds, deterministic migrations), use a PriorityQueue keyed on something stable rather than a plain ArrayDeque."},"g-alg-binsearch-answer":{"q":"Binary search on the answer — how to search where there's no sorted array?","a":"If the answer is a number in a range [lo,hi] and there's a MONOTONIC predicate check(x) over it (if x works, everything bigger/smaller works too), you binary-search the answer itself: O(log(range)·cost(check)). Classic: \"minimum speed/capacity to finish within K\".","d":"Telltale sign: \"minimize the maximum\" / \"maximize the minimum\" / \"smallest X for which it's feasible\". You don't sort the data — the answer axis itself is sorted by the predicate. Used for: capacity to ship, Koko eating bananas, split array largest sum.","hook":"A volume knob: you don't try every notch — you turn toward the \"quiet/loud\" boundary, halving each time.","more":"Binary search not over an array but over the **answer value** in a range [lo, hi]. It works if there's a **monotonic predicate** check(x): once x \"passes\", all larger (or all smaller) values pass too — a single T…T F…F boundary.\n\nHalve the range, calling check at each step. Complexity **O(log(range) · cost(check))**.\n\n- Classic: \"minimum banana-eating speed / minimum capacity to finish within K hours\"\n- Gotcha: the predicate MUST be **monotonic** — otherwise the method doesn't apply\n- Watch the boundary invariant (`lo<hi`, which half to drop) to avoid an off-by-one","quizCorrect":"A monotonic predicate: if x works, then all x greater (or smaller) work too","quizWrong":["The input data must be sorted in advance","The answer range must be a power of two","The predicate must return a number, not a boolean for the search to work over the range"],"picCap":"Monotonic predicate: we pulse the probe (?) and binary-search the boundary ✗→✓","note":"The classic bug is overflow and an infinite loop at the midpoint: `mid = (lo+hi)/2` overflows int for large bounds (write `lo + (hi-lo)/2`), and when searching for a minimum the choice of `mid = (lo+hi+1)/2` vs without the +1 decides which side rounds, and the wrong choice causes an infinite loop when `hi-lo==1`."},"g-alg-lru":{"q":"LRU cache — how to get O(1) get/put and what's LinkedHashMap got to do with it?","a":"You need a structure that can quickly: look up by key (HashMap) AND move the \"recently used\" item to the front (doubly-linked list). Pairing HashMap + doubly-linked list gives O(1) for everything. In Java, the ready-made option is LinkedHashMap with accessOrder=true and an overridden removeEldestEntry.","d":"A HashMap alone doesn't remember access order; a list alone can't look up in O(1). Hence the duo. LinkedHashMap internally keeps a chain of nodes; accessOrder=true moves a node to the tail on get. removeEldestEntry automatically evicts the head when capacity is exceeded.","note":"LinkedHashMap is not thread-safe, and accessOrder=true MUTATES the structure even on get() — two concurrent reads = ConcurrentModificationException/corruption. For a concurrent LRU you need external synchronization or Caffeine/Guava Cache.","hook":"A stack of plates in the kitchen: take a plate, put it back on top. Toss out the extra ones at the bottom (the old ones).","more":"You need two operations in **O(1)**: look up a value by key and mark an element \"most recent\". A **HashMap** gives the lookup, a **doubly-linked list** gives instant moving of a node to the head and removal of the tail (the LRU victim). The map stores key → list node.\n\nOn get: grab the node, move it to the head. On put past capacity: remove the **tail**.\n\n- In Java, ready-made: **LinkedHashMap(cap, 0.75f, accessOrder=true)** + override **removeEldestEntry**\n- accessOrder=true moves an element on access; without it the order is insertion-only\n- Gotcha: not thread-safe — for concurrent access you need an external lock or Caffeine","quizCorrect":"HashMap gives O(1) lookup, the list gives O(1) node move and removal","quizWrong":["To keep two copies of the data for reliability","The list sorts the keys, and the HashMap hashes them for fast lookup by their key","HashMap can't hold more than 16 elements"],"picCap":"the map finds the node, the list keeps recency order: an access moves the node toward new, old gets evicted"},"g-alg-amortized":{"q":"Amortized complexity — why is ArrayList.add \"O(1)\" even though it's sometimes O(n)?","a":"Amortization averages the expensive operations across the whole run. ArrayList sometimes copies the entire array (O(n)) when it grows, but doubling the capacity makes that RARE: over n additions it's O(n) copies total → O(1) per operation amortized. This is NOT \"average case\" — it's a guarantee over the run.","d":"The key is doubling (×2, not +1): copies are 1+2+4+…+n = 2n−1 ≈ O(n) over the whole run. If it grew by +1 each time, it would be O(n²). Amortized analysis (aggregate/banker's/potential method) gives an honest bound for a sequence, independent of the input distribution.","note":"Amortized O(1) breaks down if you ignore its precondition: every ArrayList growth copies the whole array, so when you know the size, call ensureCapacity / the capacity constructor. But add at the start/middle is O(n) on EVERY operation — amortization doesn't help there.","hook":"A gym membership: you pay for the year all at once, but per workout it's pennies a visit.","more":"**Under the hood:** ArrayList stores elements in a fixed-size array. When it's full, a new array **twice as big** is created and all elements are copied — a one-off O(n) operation.\n\n**Why O(1):** doubling makes the expensive copy **rare**. To get from 1 to n, total copies are ~2n (the geometric sum n+n/2+n/4+...). Divide by n operations → **a constant per add**.\n\n**Gotcha:** it's a guarantee over the run, not an *average case* (there's no randomness here) and not the worst case (a single add is still O(n)). If you know the size — pass `new ArrayList<>(capacity)` and avoid the extra copies.","quizCorrect":"Amortization is a guarantee over the whole run of operations, with no assumptions about input distribution","quizWrong":["It's the same thing, just different names","Amortization is about memory, average is about time, and the two are measured in completely different units","Average-case is always worse than amortized"],"picCap":"A rare expensive step (n!, pulsing) dissolves into cheap O(1) ones"},"g-alg-bit-tricks":{"q":"Bit tricks — which ones actually come up in Java production?","a":"Parity check x&1, multiply/divide by 2 via x<<1 / x>>1, power of two (x&(x-1))==0, flag masks (bit sets instead of a hand-rolled EnumSet), fast mod by 2^k via x&(n-1), clear the lowest set bit x&=x-1. They give speed and compactness, but readability suffers — add comments.","d":"Important gotcha: in Java >> is an arithmetic shift (carries the sign), >>> is logical (fills with zero). For hashes/unsigned you need >>>. n%2 for negatives gives −1, while &1 gives 1 — not interchangeable. Integer.bitCount / Long.numberOfTrailingZeros are built in, no hand-rolled loops.","note":"In Java, right shift: >> preserves the sign (arithmetic), >>> fills with zeros (logical) — for unsigned bit masks you need >>>. And the shift amount is taken modulo 32 for int / 64 for long: x<<32 == x, not 0 — a frequent surprise.","hook":"Switches in a breaker panel: each bit is a separate toggle. A shift moves the whole row of toggles.","more":"**How it works:** numbers in memory are bits, so shifts and masks = arithmetic directly on the hardware. `x<<1` shifts the bits left = multiply by 2; `x>>1` = divide. `x&(x-1)` clears the lowest set bit.\n\n**Actually used in production:**\n- parity `x&1`\n- power of two `(x&(x-1))==0`\n- fast mod by 2^k: `x&(n-1)` (that's what HashMap does)\n- flag masks in a single int instead of EnumSet\n- clear the lowest bit `x&=x-1` (popcount)\n\n**Gotcha:** works only for powers of two and unsigned shifts (Java has `>>>`). Readability drops — the modern JIT already optimizes `*2`, so for speed these tricks are rarely needed; **add comments**, otherwise a junior won't get it.","quizCorrect":">> carries the sign bit (arithmetic), >>> inserts zeros (logical)","quizWrong":[">>> works only with long, >> only with int, so they are not interchangeable","No difference, >>> is a deprecated synonym",">> shifts left, >>> shifts right"],"picCap":"clearing the lowest bit; beware the arithmetic shift"},"git-rebase":{"q":"rebase vs merge — what's the difference and where are the gotchas?","a":"merge preserves both histories and creates a merge commit (branchy history, but truthful). rebase replays your commits ON TOP OF another branch — the history is linear, but the commits get rewritten (new hashes).","d":"Never rebase a branch that has already been pushed and shared — you'll break history for everyone else.","note":"Golden rule: don't rebase pushed/shared branches — rewritten hashes force everyone else into a force-pull and breed duplicates. After rebasing your own branch, push only with --force-with-lease (not a bare --force, otherwise you'll clobber someone else's commit that landed after your fetch).","hook":"merge — two families at one wedding table; rebase — rewriting your diary as if everything went in one straight line.","more":"Both combine branches, but they rewrite history differently. **merge** takes two branches and ties them together with a new merge commit that has two parents — nothing is lost, you see the real picture of \"we worked in parallel\". **rebase**, on the other hand, lifts your commits and replays them on top of the fresh tip of another branch — the history becomes **linear**, as if you wrote everything sequentially.\n\nThe price of rebase: commits are recreated with new hashes (the old ones become \"dangling\").\n\n- Golden rule: **don't rebase already-pushed** shared branches — your teammates' history will diverge and you'll have to force-push. Rebase is great locally, before publishing.","quizCorrect":"Replayed on top of another branch, history linear, hashes new","quizWrong":["Both branches are preserved and a separate merge commit is created","Squashed into a single commit without changing hashes","Kept with the same hashes, only the order changes"],"picCap":"merge — branchy; rebase — linear, but commits rewritten"},"git-cherrypick":{"q":"What is git cherry-pick for?","a":"To move ONE specific commit into the current branch without merging the whole branch. The classic use is pulling a hotfix from one branch into another.","d":"Creates a NEW commit with the same changes (different hash).","hook":"cherry-pick — take a single cherry off someone else's cake without dragging the whole cake home.","more":"`cherry-pick` takes the changes of one specific commit (its diff) and applies them as a **new commit** on the current branch — with a new hash but the same content. Essentially \"copy a single change\" without dragging the whole branch and its history along.\n\nThe main scenario is a **hotfix**: you fixed a bug in a release branch, and with the same commit you patch the hole point-by-point in main or develop.\n\n- Gotcha: if you cherry-pick many commits back and forth, the history ends up with duplicates of the same change under different hashes — at a later merge this confuses git and breeds conflicts. For a series of changes, merge/rebase is usually better.","quizCorrect":"Moves one specific commit into the current branch","quizWrong":["Merges the entire branch with a single merge commit","Rolls back the last commit, rewriting history","Temporarily shelves uncommitted changes"],"picCap":"cherry-pick: one commit (new hash), no branch merge"},"git-bisect":{"q":"What is git bisect for?","a":"Binary search for the commit that introduced a bug. You mark a known good and a known bad commit, git halves the range and checks out the middle — you test, repeat. In log2(n) steps you find the culprit.","d":"Can be automated with a script: git bisect run ./test.sh","hook":"bisect — find the rotten berry in a basket by halving it: one half clean? then the rot is in the other.","more":"`bisect` finds the commit that broke the code by **binary search** instead of checking commits by hand. You say: things worked here (the old one) — `good`, the bug is here (the new one) — `bad`. Git checks out the middle of the range, you test and mark good/bad — it halves the remaining range again.\n\nWhy it's fast: each step **throws away half** the commits, so out of 1000 commits the culprit is found in ~10 checks (log2).\n\n- Gotcha: you need a reliable, reproducible \"bug present / absent\" test. If the check is manual and noisy, one wrong label sends the search astray. You can automate it: `git bisect run ./test.sh`.","quizCorrect":"Find the commit that introduced a bug via binary search","quizWrong":["Roll a branch back to a known working state on the remote","Move a hotfix from one branch to another","Combine several small commits into one"],"picCap":"git bisect: binary search for the buggy commit","note":"git bisect run judges a commit purely by the script's exit code: 0 = good, 1-124/126/127 = bad, while code 125 means 'commit untestable' (skip). If your test accidentally returns 127 (command not found) or 128+, bisect treats it as bad and blames the wrong commit; for builds that don't compile return exactly 125 so the commit is skipped instead of marked bad."},"git-reset-revert":{"q":"reset vs revert — when to use which?","a":"revert creates a NEW commit that undoes the changes — safe for shared branches (history isn't rewritten). reset moves the branch pointer back (--soft/--mixed/--hard) — it rewrites history, dangerous on pushed branches.","d":"On a shared branch, undo only via revert.","note":"Reverting a merge commit requires -m <parent number>, and the revert itself \"marks\" the branch as already merged — re-merging the same branch later won't bring the changes in until you revert the revert. reset --soft keeps the changes in the index, --mixed — in the working tree, --hard wipes them for good (only the reflog can save you).","hook":"revert — write \"struck out\" on a new line; reset — erase the page with an eraser, which you can't do in a shared notebook.","more":"The difference is whether you rewrite history or not. **revert** adds a **new commit** on top containing the inverse diff: the changes are undone, but the whole history stays intact — that's why it's safe for shared branches, and the push goes through without `--force`.\n\n**reset** moves the branch pointer back to a chosen commit: `--soft` leaves the changes in the index, `--mixed` (the default) — in the working tree, `--hard` wipes everything clean.\n\n- Rule: on **pushed** branches — revert only. reset is for local cleanup before publishing. Gotcha: `reset --hard` irreversibly deletes uncommitted work; only `git reflog` might save you.","quizCorrect":"Creates a new undo commit without rewriting history","quizWrong":["Moves the branch pointer back without new commits","Deletes the problematic commit from history forever everywhere","Stashes the changes until the cause is clarified"],"picCap":"revert — new commit C′; reset — branch back to B"},"git-stash":{"q":"What is git stash for?","a":"To temporarily shelve uncommitted changes so you can switch to another branch with a clean tree, then bring them back later (stash pop).","d":"Handy for an urgent hotfix in the middle of unfinished work.","note":"git stash by default does NOT shelve new (untracked) or ignored files — you need -u / -a, otherwise they'll \"leak\" when you switch branches and get in the way. On a conflict, stash pop leaves the entry in the stash (unlike a successful pop, which removes it) — easy to lose, thinking you've already applied it.","hook":"Tucked the unfinished work into a desk drawer to clear the desk for another task, then took it back out.","more":"`stash` lifts your uncommitted changes (working tree + index) onto a separate hidden \"shelf\" and returns the tree to a clean HEAD. It's a **stack**: you can put several on it (`stash@{0}`, `{1}`...). To restore — `stash pop` (apply and drop the entry) or `stash apply` (apply but keep it).\n\nWhy: to urgently switch to another branch or pull updates without making a half-baked commit.\n\n- Gotcha: it's easy to forget a stash and pile up a heap — then you won't remember what's where. By default it doesn't take new (untracked) files — you need `-u`. For long-term storage, a regular commit on a temporary branch is better.","quizCorrect":"Temporarily shelve uncommitted changes and restore them later","quizWrong":["Create a permanent commit with the current changes in the branch","Move one commit to another branch","Roll a branch back to a previous state"],"picCap":"stash hides changes → tree clean → pop restored"},"git-flow":{"q":"Git flow in a nutshell?","a":"A branching model: main — prod, develop — integration, feature/* — features, release/* — release prep, hotfix/* — urgent prod fixes. It structures which branches grow from where and merge into where.","d":"For CD teams it's often simpler: trunk-based + short-lived branches.","hook":"A house: main — the living floor, develop — the construction site, feature — separate rooms, hotfix — patch a hole urgently.","more":"Git flow is an agreement on **which branches are responsible for what**. `main` — what's in prod (every merge is tagged with a version), `develop` — the common pot where finished features collect. `feature/*` branch off develop and return there.\n\nWhen there's enough for a release — they cut a `release/*` branch for stabilization, then merge it into main and back into develop. `hotfix/*` grows straight off main for an urgent prod fix and also goes into both branches.\n\n- Gotcha: for frequent deploys (CI/CD, many times a day) this is **overkill** — two long-lived branches slow things down. Then teams use trunk-based / GitHub Flow: a single main + short feature branches.","quizCorrect":"The integration branch where features merge in","quizWrong":["The prod branch that releases are made from to users","The branch for urgent prod fixes (hotfix)","The branch for one specific feature"],"picCap":"feature→develop; release/hotfix→main","note":"The real pain of git flow in practice isn't the branches themselves but the long-lived develop: features branch off it and aren't merged for weeks, so merging release/* into main regularly produces conflicts and drift between develop and main. That, not mere 'overhead', is what pushes CD teams toward trunk-based with feature flags instead of long branches."},"x-git-reflog":{"q":"What is git reflog for? How do you recover a \"lost\" commit after a botched reset --hard or rebase?","a":"reflog is a log where Git records EVERY movement of HEAD (commit, reset, rebase, checkout). Even if a branch \"lost\" a commit, it still lives by its hash. You find the line in the reflog, take the hash, and git reset --hard <hash> or git checkout -b rescue <hash> — and the commit is yours again.","d":"Commits aren't deleted immediately: they become \"unreachable\" and physically live ~90 days (gc.reflogExpire) until git gc cleans them out. reflog is local and isn't pushed. It's your personal insurance: as long as a commit was in HEAD at least once, you can almost always retrieve it.","note":"reflog is local and short-lived: entries live ~90 days (gc.reflogExpire), and unreachable commits are cleaned out by git gc — rescue a \"lost\" one immediately. On a freshly cloned repo the reflog is empty, so you can't recover someone else's lost commits through it.","hook":"The Windows Recycle Bin: a file is 'deleted' but sits there for 90 days — pull it back by name and restore it.","more":"Commits in Git are almost never deleted right away — they become \"dangling\" (unreachable), but physically live in `.git` until garbage collection (usually ~30-90 days). **reflog** is a local log that records every position of HEAD and the branches.\n\nSo after a `reset --hard` or a botched rebase the commit isn't gone — it just lost its name. You find its hash in `git reflog` and bring it back.\n\n- `git reset --hard <hash>` — move the current branch back to that commit.\n- `git checkout -b rescue <hash>` — safer: you create a new branch without breaking anything.\n- Gotcha: the reflog is **local and isn't pushed**; on a fresh clone it doesn't exist. And it won't save untouched (never-committed) files.","quizCorrect":"Find its hash in git reflog and reset/checkout to that hash","quizWrong":["No way — reset --hard removes the commit from the repo forever","Only restore from a backup, Git stores nothing itself","Run git fsck --recover to rewrite history"],"picCap":"reflog remembers every step of HEAD"},"x-git-rebase-i":{"q":"What does interactive rebase (git rebase -i) do? Why squash/fixup before merging into main?","a":"rebase -i opens a list of commits where you rewrite them: pick (keep), squash/fixup (merge into one), reword (rename), drop (discard), reorder. Before merging into main you collapse 10 messy commits — \"wip\", \"fix typo\", \"fix again\" — into 1-2 meaningful ones, so the history is clean.","d":"squash preserves the messages of all commits (lets you edit them), fixup silently discards the message of the \"attached\" commit. Important: rebase REWRITES hashes, so do it only on your own branch before pushing (or if you're the sole owner). After that — push --force-with-lease, not just --force.","hook":"Draft letters: 5 edits into the trash, one clean letter goes to the boss.","more":"`rebase -i` takes a range of commits and replays them per your scenario from a todo list. Commands: **pick** (keep), **squash** (merge, keeping the message), **fixup** (merge, dropping the message), **reword**, **edit**, **drop**, plus you can reorder the lines.\n\nThe point before merging into main is to turn a junky development history into meaningful atomic commits: the reviewer and `git bisect` will have an easier time later.\n\n- `fixup` is ideal for tiny fixes like \"forgot a semicolon\".\n- Gotcha: rebase **rewrites hashes**, so don't do it on already-shared branches — you'll break history for your teammates. If the branch is yours alone — push via `--force-with-lease` (safer than a plain `--force`).","quizCorrect":"squash (or fixup)","quizWrong":["merge","reword changes only the message of an existing commit","amend"],"picCap":"4 messy ones collapse → 1 meaningful","note":"--force-with-lease is safe only if your local remote-tracking ref is stale: if you ran git fetch (or IDE autofetch) after someone else's push, the lease 'sees' the already-updated ref and will clobber their commits just like a plain --force. Safer is --force-with-lease=<ref>:<expected-sha> with an explicit SHA. Another trap: a rebase -i that changes anything (squash/reword) rewrites committer date and loses the original SHA — GPG signatures and tracker links to the commit become invalid."},"x-git-merge-conflict":{"q":"What is a merge conflict? How do you read it (<<<<<<< ======= >>>>>>>) and resolve it properly?","a":"A conflict is when two branches changed the SAME line differently, and Git doesn't know whose version to take. It inserts markers: between <<<<<<< HEAD and ======= is yours (the current branch), between ======= and >>>>>>> is theirs (the incoming one). You keep what you need by hand, remove all markers, git add, git commit (or rebase --continue).","d":"Git merges automatically as long as the edits are in different places in the file. A conflict = an overlap. Tools: git mergetool, git checkout --ours/--theirs (take one whole side), git merge --abort (roll everything back). Prevention: small frequent merges of main into your branch so divergence doesn't pile up.","note":"The absence of <<<<<<< markers doesn't mean \"no conflict\": a semantic conflict (both sides changed different lines but jointly break the logic) is invisible to git — it compiles but fails at runtime. git rerere can remember resolutions of recurring conflicts during long rebases.","hook":"Two people in Google Docs erased each other's line — the editor asks: whose do you keep?","more":"Git merges automatically as long as the changes are in different places. A conflict arises when **both branches touched the same region** of a file — the machine has no right to decide whose variant is correct, and hands the choice to you.\n\nThe markers read like this: between `<<<<<<< HEAD` and `=======` is **your current** version, between `=======` and `>>>>>>>` is the **incoming** one (the one you're merging in).\n\n- You keep what you need (often a combination of both), erase ALL three markers, then `git add` + `git commit` (or `git rebase --continue`).\n- Gotcha: during a rebase, \"current\" and \"incoming\" effectively swap places relative to a merge — it's easy to take the wrong side. And always re-read the file with your own eyes: a removed marker ≠ correct logic.","quizCorrect":"The current branch's version (yours)","quizWrong":["The incoming branch's version (theirs)","The common ancestor of both branches","The final version automatically chosen by Git"],"picCap":"one line — two edits"},"x-git-fetch-pull":{"q":"What's the difference between git fetch and git pull? What is fast-forward and why pull --rebase?","a":"fetch only DOWNLOADS changes from the remote into origin/main, but doesn't touch your branch — you can look around calmly. pull = fetch + immediate merge (or rebase) into the current branch. fast-forward is when your branch hasn't diverged, and Git just moves the pointer forward without a merge commit. pull --rebase replays your commits on top of theirs — the history is linear, with no extra merge nodes.","d":"git pull (merge by default) on diverged branches breeds parasitic \"Merge branch main\" commits. Commands: pull --ff-only (fail if not fast-forward — safe for CI), config pull.rebase true. fetch before you work = see what others did before you merge.","note":"A bare git pull on diverged branches does a merge and breeds \"merge branch main into main\" commits in the history — which is why many set pull.rebase=true. fetch updates origin/main but does NOT move your local main: after a fetch you have to merge/rebase it separately.","hook":"fetch = downloaded the email into your Inbox and read it. pull = downloaded it and immediately pasted it into your document.","more":"**fetch** updates only the remote-tracking branches (`origin/main`) — your working branch and files don't change, you can calmly check `git log origin/main` before merging. **pull** = fetch + immediate merge (or rebase) into the current branch.\n\n**Fast-forward** is a special case: your branch hasn't diverged from the remote, you have no new commits of your own, so Git just moves the pointer forward without a merge commit.\n\n- `pull --rebase` replays your local commits ON TOP OF theirs — the history is linear, with no \"diamonds\" from merges.\n- Gotcha: rebase changes the hashes of your commits; if they're already pushed, you'll get divergence. It's convenient to set `pull.rebase=true` for personal feature branches.","quizCorrect":"fetch only downloads into origin, pull = fetch + merge/rebase into the branch","quizWrong":["fetch downloads one branch, pull downloads all branches at once without merging them","fetch updates the working copy, pull only the remote refs","fetch requires a push after it, but pull does not"],"picCap":"fetch reaches origin, pull flows on into local"},"g-git-worktree":{"q":"What is git worktree and why use it when a branch is enough?","a":"worktree gives you a second working directory on the same .git but with a DIFFERENT branch. You can fix a hotfix in one folder without touching your unfinished feature in another — no stash and no second clone. One repository, several checkouts at once.","d":"Each worktree keeps its own HEAD and index but shares the objects and .git/. The same branch can't be checked out in two worktrees at once. `git worktree list` shows them all, `git worktree remove` deletes one. Handy for parallel builds/tests on different branches without switching and recompiling.","hook":"One fridge (.git), two kitchens: you cook two dishes in parallel, the ingredients are shared.","more":"**How it works:** `git worktree add ../hotfix main` creates a **separate folder** with working files, but `.git` (the objects, history) stays **shared** — there's one for all worktrees. Each directory keeps its own branch and its own index.\n\n**Why it beats a branch:** an ordinary branch switch changes the files in the **same** directory — an unfinished feature gets in the way. A worktree gives you a parallel checkout: you fix the hotfix alongside, without touching your current work, **no stash and no second clone** (objects aren't duplicated — saving disk space).\n\n**Gotcha:** the same branch can't be open in two worktrees at once. Delete via `git worktree remove`, not `rm -rf`, otherwise you'll leave dangling records.","quizCorrect":"Have two branches in two directories at once without stash/clone","quizWrong":["Compress the repo history and delete old objects","Create a remote copy of the repository on a server for backup purposes","Merge two branches into one without conflicts"],"picCap":"One .git → two directories, two branches"},"g-git-submodule":{"q":"What is a git submodule and why is it treacherous?","a":"A submodule is another git repository nested in yours at a FIXED commit (a gitlink), not at a branch. The parent remembers a specific SHA. After clone the folder is empty until you do submodule update. Forget it — and you build the wrong version.","d":"`clone --recurse-submodules` or later `submodule update --init --recursive`. The parent commits a pointer to the submodule's SHA; updating the dependency = a new commit in the parent. Gotchas: detached HEAD inside the submodule, a forgotten submodule push, desync among teammates. Alternatives: a monorepo, a package manager.","note":"A plain git clone doesn't pull submodule contents — you need --recurse-submodules or a later submodule update --init; forget it and you build the old version. git pull in the parent updates the submodule pointer to a new SHA but not the submodule files themselves, and these \"gitlink jumps\" are a common invisible source of CI bugs.","hook":"A book with a bookmark in another book: you remember the exact page, not \"the latest\" one.","more":"**How it works:** a submodule is a **nested standalone repository**. The parent stores not files but a special **gitlink** record — a specific SHA of the submodule's commit. So a **precise version** is pinned, not a branch.\n\n**Why it's treacherous:** after `git clone` the submodule folder is **empty** — you need `git submodule update --init`. Forget it and you'll build without the dependency or with a stale version. Updating the submodule = a new gitlink commit in the parent, easy to forget to commit.\n\n**When to use:** for tightly versioned external dependencies that change rarely. For active joint development, a regular dependency via a package manager is usually more convenient.","quizCorrect":"At a specific commit (SHA) of the nested repository","quizWrong":["At a branch of the nested repo, always the latest commit","At the latest tag of the nested repository","At a URL with no version pinning"],"picCap":"The parent points to a specific commit of the submodule"},"g-git-tag-annotated":{"q":"Lightweight vs annotated tag — what's the difference and which one for a release?","a":"Lightweight — just a label pointing at a commit, with no metadata. Annotated — a full object: author, date, message, can be GPG-signed. For releases use annotated (-a), it shows up in describe and carries info. Lightweight — for local temporary marks.","d":"Annotated is stored as a separate tag object in git's database; lightweight is just a ref. `git describe` by default considers only annotated tags. Tags are NOT pushed automatically: `git push origin v1.2.0` or `--tags`. Deleting: `git tag -d` locally + `git push origin :refs/tags/v1.2.0` on the server.","hook":"A sticker on a box (lightweight) vs a full shipping invoice with a signature and date (annotated).","more":"**How it works:** a lightweight tag is just a **named pointer** to a commit, like a branch but it doesn't move. An annotated tag (`-a`) is a **separate object** in git's database with an author, date, message, and an optional GPG signature.\n\n**Why annotated for a release:** it carries metadata (who released it and when), `git describe` picks it up, and it can be **verified by signature**. It's an auditable release point, not a nameless mark.\n\n**Gotcha:** `git tag v1.0` silently creates a lightweight tag — easy to mix up. Tags are **not pushed** automatically: you need `git push --tags` or `git push origin v1.0`. Leave lightweight tags for temporary local marks.","quizCorrect":"Annotated (-a): stores author, date, message, shows in describe","quizWrong":["Lightweight: it's lighter and is pushed automatically with the branch","Either — git doesn't technically distinguish them","Lightweight, because only it supports a GPG signature"],"picCap":"Annotated carries metadata, lightweight — a bare pointer","note":"The trap of `git checkout v1.0` and similar: a tag points at a commit, so checking it out gives a detached HEAD — new commits made 'on the tag' are lost at the next switch; also `git push` doesn't send tags by default, and an annotated tag sharing a name with a branch breaks ref resolution, so name tags unambiguously (`v1.0`, not `release`)."},"g-git-detached-head":{"q":"What is a detached HEAD and why can commits be lost here?","a":"Normally HEAD points to a branch. After `checkout <SHA>` or a tag, HEAD is \"detached\" — it points straight at a commit, with no branch. You commit — the commits belong to no one: switch away and there's no reference to them, GC will eat them. The reflog or a new branch IN TIME saves you.","d":"Common causes: checkout by SHA/tag, during a rebase, in a detached worktree, in CI. To save your work: `git switch -c new-branch` while you're on the right commit. If you already left — `git reflog` will find the orphaned SHA. `git switch -` returns you to the previous branch.","note":"The classic trap with submodules and CI: after a checkout by SHA any commit dangles in a detached HEAD and is lost on the next switch unless you create a branch. git checkout - won't bring back detached commits — only their hash from the reflog will.","hook":"A boat with no dock: you row (commit), but you didn't tie up — let go and it drifts away.","more":"**How it works:** normally `HEAD` points to a **branch name**, and the branch points to a commit. After `checkout <SHA>` or a tag, HEAD points **straight at the commit** — there's no branch between them. That's a detached HEAD.\n\n**Why commits get lost:** new commits in this state aren't referenced by any name. As soon as you switch to a branch, there's **no pointer** to them — git's garbage collector will eventually delete them.\n\n**Gotcha and the rescue:** create a branch in time — `git switch -c fix` **before** switching. If you already left — `git reflog` remembers where HEAD pointed, and you can recover the SHA from there. The state itself is fine (CI, checking out an old version); only losing the reference is dangerous.","quizCorrect":"Commits without a branch are lost after switching (GC collects them)","quizWrong":["You can't make commits at all — git forbids them","The history of the whole main branch gets automatically corrupted beyond repair","It deletes your working changes the moment you enter the mode"],"picCap":"HEAD dangles on a commit directly — no branch, easy to lose"},"g-git-revert-merge":{"q":"How do you revert an already-merged merge commit and why is -m needed?","a":"A merge commit has TWO parents, git doesn't know which side to treat as the \"norm\". `revert -m 1` means: go back to the first parent (usually main), undoing the merged branch. Without -m git complains. Gotcha: re-merging the same branch later won't work without tricks.","d":"`-m 1` — mainline (the branch you merged into), `-m 2` — the merged-in one. After a revert-merge the branch is considered already accounted for; to merge it again you'll need a revert-of-the-revert or a rebase. So in important cases it's sometimes better to use `--no-ff` as a separate feature rather than a revert.","note":"Reverting a merge commit via -m 1 undoes the merged code but does NOT roll back the history itself — git still considers the branch merged. To improve it and merge it again you'll have to reverse the revert itself (revert the revert), otherwise a re-merge won't bring the changes in.","hook":"A fork in the road: you tell the GPS \"go back via the left\" — otherwise it doesn't know where you came from.","more":"**How it works:** a merge commit has **two parents**: parent 1 — the branch you merged into (usually main), parent 2 — the merged-in feature. `revert` builds the inverse diff, but git doesn't know **relative to which parent** to compute the changes.\n\n**Why `-m 1`:** the flag specifies the \"mainline\" — `-m 1` means \"keep the state of the first parent, undo what was merged in\". Without `-m` git refuses: the choice is ambiguous.\n\n**Gotcha:** after a revert the branch is considered already merged, and git **won't merge it again** with a normal merge — you first have to revert the revert itself or rebase. That's why for revertable features people more often use a plain revert of a series of commits rather than a merge.","quizCorrect":"Specify the mainline parent: which side of the merge to return to","quizWrong":["So revert produces not a commit but immediately deletes history up to that point","To skip running the pre-commit hooks","It's a commit message flag, like -m on commit"],"picCap":"-m selects which parent to treat as the base"},"g-git-no-ff-merge":{"q":"Fast-forward vs --no-ff merge — when which and why?","a":"If main hasn't moved, git just advances the pointer (fast-forward) — there's no merge commit, the history is linear, but the fact that \"there was a feature\" is lost. `--no-ff` always creates a merge commit — the feature boundaries are visible and you can easily roll back the whole branch with a single revert.","d":"Many team policies set `--no-ff` for feature→develop so a PR leaves a merge node. `--ff-only`, conversely, forbids merge commits (protection against junk in a pull). Squash-merge is a third option: one commit with no node. The choice = a balance of readability and traceability.","hook":"ff — appended to the end of the line; no-ff — added a separate heading paragraph \"a feature was here\".","more":"**How it works:** if main **hasn't moved** since the branch point, git on merge simply **fast-forwards** main onto the feature's commits — it's a fast-forward, there's no new commit, the history is linear.\n\n**`--no-ff`:** forcibly creates a **merge commit** with two parents, even when FF is possible. The history keeps a **visible boundary** of the feature — all its commits are grouped under one merge.\n\n**When which:** FF — for small personal branches where a clean linear history matters more. `--no-ff` — for team features: you can see what was merged together, and you **roll the whole feature back with one** `git revert -m 1`. Many configure `--no-ff` by default for main.","quizCorrect":"Always creates a merge commit, preserving the merged branch's boundaries","quizWrong":["Forbids any merges, only rebase is allowed","Advances the pointer without a new commit","Squashes all the feature's commits into one squash commit in the target branch"],"picCap":"no-ff leaves a merge node, ff — a line","note":"git config merge.ff only affects a LOCAL merge: with squash- or rebase-merge via the GitHub/GitLab UI no merge node is created anyway, so 'roll back with a single revert -m 1' works only if the PR was merged using the merge-commit strategy. Also remember: reverting a merge commit with -m 1 'marks' the branch as already integrated — re-merging the same branch later won't bring the changes back; you need a revert of the revert."},"g-git-blame":{"q":"git blame: why and how to not get stuck on a cosmetic commit?","a":"blame shows who, and in which commit, changed EACH line of a file — to understand \"why it's written this way\" and find context. Gotcha: formatting/renaming spoils the picture. The flags -w (ignore whitespace) and -C (track moved code) save you.","d":"`blame -L 40,60 file` — only the lines you need. To punch through a \"tidying\" commit: `git log -L` or `git blame <SHA>^ -- file`. `git config blame.ignoreRevsFile` + `.git-blame-ignore-revs` excludes mass reformat commits from the output for good.","hook":"A detective working fingerprints: on each line — whose hand touched it last.","more":"**How it works:** `git blame` finds, for **each line** of a file, the commit that last changed it, and shows the author, date, and SHA. It helps you understand *why* the code is the way it is — through the commit you get to the PR, the ticket, the context.\n\n**Gotcha:** cosmetics break the picture — reformatting, renaming, or moving lines make the \"author\" whoever just ran the formatter, hiding the real meaning.\n\n**The rescue:**\n- `-w` ignores whitespace-only changes\n- `-C` / `-M` track the **movement and copying** of code between files\n- `git log -L` shows the history of specific lines deeper than a single layer of blame.","quizCorrect":"-w (ignore whitespace when attributing a line's authorship)","quizWrong":["-m, to choose the mainline of a merge commit during a revert","--squash, to collapse cosmetic commits","-f, to force a history rewrite"],"picCap":"Each line → its last commit and author"},"g-git-hooks":{"q":"Git hooks: what are they and why can't pre-commit be treated as protection?","a":"Hooks are scripts that git itself runs on events (pre-commit, commit-msg, pre-push). Handy for running lint/tests locally. BUT they live in .git/hooks, are NOT committed to the repo, and are easily bypassed with `--no-verify`. The real barrier is CI on the server.","d":"To share hooks across the team: `core.hooksPath` pointing at a folder in the repo, or tools like pre-commit/husky. Server-side hooks (pre-receive) are the only ones a client can't bypass. The principle: hooks = developer convenience, the gate = CI/branch protection.","note":"Hooks live in .git/hooks and aren't versioned — a new developer simply doesn't have them until they set up core.hooksPath or Husky/pre-commit. Any hook is bypassed with the --no-verify flag, so quality checks must be duplicated in server-side CI, not only in pre-commit.","hook":"A lock on your own apartment door (a hook) vs security at the office entrance (CI) — you can unlock the first one yourself.","more":"**How it works:** hooks are **executable scripts** in `.git/hooks/` that git itself runs on events: `pre-commit` (before a commit), `commit-msg` (checking the message), `pre-push` (before a push). A non-zero exit code cancels the operation. Handy for running lint/tests locally and catching errors early.\n\n**Why they're not protection:** the `.git/hooks` directory **isn't versioned** — it doesn't end up in the repo on clone, so your teammates simply don't have the hooks. And anyone bypasses them with the `git commit --no-verify` flag.\n\n**Takeaway:** hooks are a **convenience for the developer**, not a control. The real barrier is **CI checks** on the server, which can't be skipped. husky/pre-commit help distribute hooks to the team, but even they are optional.","quizCorrect":"It's local, isn't committed, and is bypassed with the --no-verify flag","quizWrong":["It runs only on the server and is invisible to the developer in their clone","Git runs it just once a day on a schedule","It blocks push but allows any commit"],"picCap":"commit passes through the hook; --no-verify bypasses it"},"g-git-gitignore-tracked":{"q":".gitignore doesn't work on an already-tracked file — why, and how to fix it?","a":".gitignore affects ONLY untracked files. If a file is already committed, adding it to ignore isn't enough — git keeps seeing its changes. You have to remove it from the index: `git rm --cached`, leaving the file on disk, and commit the removal from tracking.","d":"Subtleties: rules with a leading `/` are anchored to the root; `!pattern` un-ignores, but won't pull a file out of an already-ignored folder; `**` is recursive. Global ignore: `core.excludesFile`. For secrets: ignore doesn't erase history — you need filter-repo/BFG if the file is already in history.","note":"After git rm --cached the file stays on disk but is removed from the index — for others, on pull, it's also deleted from the working copy (often unexpectedly wiping someone's local configs). And adding a directory to .gitignore doesn't ignore the files already tracked inside it.","hook":"\"No entry\" at the gate won't throw out those already inside — you have to escort them out by hand (rm --cached).","more":"**.gitignore** works as a filter only at the stage when git decides whether to add a **new, still-untracked** path to the index. A file already in the index (committed) git considers \"its own\" and keeps tracking its changes — ignore simply doesn't touch it.\n\nWhy: ignore is a rule for `git status`/`git add` about **untracked** files, not a command to \"forget what you're tracking\". The tracking state is stored in the index, not in .gitignore.\n\nThe fix: `git rm --cached <file>` removes it from the index while leaving it on disk, then commit — git will stop seeing the file (it's now untracked and falls under ignore). Gotcha: without `--cached` the file is deleted physically; and for teammates it stays until a pull.","quizCorrect":"The file is already tracked; you need git rm --cached, ignore is only for new ones","quizWrong":["You need to restart git daemon so it re-reads the rules","The rule must be written in uppercase, otherwise the ignore doesn't work for files or folders",".gitignore applies only to files larger than 1 MB"],"picCap":"Ignore is powerless on a tracked file → rm --cached saves you"},"g-git-cherry-pick-conflict":{"q":"A conflict during cherry-pick: how to continue, abort, and avoid breeding duplicates?","a":"On a conflict cherry-pick pauses. You fix the files, `git add`, then `git cherry-pick --continue` (NOT commit again). Changed your mind — `--abort` restores the previous state, `--skip` skips this commit. Gotcha: moving a commit creates a NEW SHA — later you can easily get duplicates on a merge.","d":"Conflicts are common when the context has changed. `-x` appends \"(cherry picked from ...)\" — it helps with traceability. Move a whole range with `A..B`. Since the SHAs are new, a later merge of the same changes yields duplicate commits; for series, rebase/merge is better than a mass cherry-pick.","note":"cherry-pick creates a commit with a NEW SHA, so on a later merge of the same branch git sees a \"duplicate\" of the changes and may produce a conflict or double the edits. git cherry helps find already-moved commits; -x adds a reference to the original in the message.","hook":"Transplanted a cutting into another pot: it took root, but it's already a new plant (a new SHA), not a clone.","more":"**cherry-pick** applies the diff of the chosen commit on top of the current branch. If the context has diverged — git pauses the process with conflict markers and waits for manual resolution, keeping a \"cherry-pick in progress\" state.\n\nWhy `--continue` and not `commit`: git already remembers the original message and metadata; `--continue` finishes the current operation correctly. The exit options: `--abort` rolls everything back to the start, `--skip` drops only this commit and moves on (useful in a series).\n\nGotcha: the moved commit gets a **new SHA** (different parent/time). If you later merge the same branch, the same change can come in again — hence duplicates and extra conflicts. Better to move the minimum and not cherry-pick what will be merged in anyway.","quizCorrect":"git add the fixes, then git cherry-pick --continue","quizWrong":["git commit --amend, to rewrite the original commit","git merge --continue, since it's a kind of merge","git revert --continue, otherwise the SHA won't be preserved"],"picCap":"After fixing — continue, not a new commit"},"devops-cicd":{"q":"What is a CI/CD pipeline?","a":"CI (continuous integration): every push automatically triggers a build + tests — you catch breakage early. CD (delivery/deployment): automated delivery of the artifact to environments. Stages are described in .yml (build → test → deploy).","d":"A green pipeline is a merge requirement; a red one blocks.","hook":"A conveyor belt: for every chunk of code thrown in, a robot builds it, checks it for defects, and ships it to the shelves.","more":"This automates the path from \"code → prod\". **CI** runs a build and tests in a clean environment on every push — breakage is visible immediately, not at the demo. **CD** picks up the ready artifact and rolls it across environments: delivery goes up to prod with a manual button, deployment is fully automatic.\n\nEverything is described declaratively in `.yml` (GitLab CI, GitHub Actions): the stages `build → test → deploy` run as a pipeline, and a failed stage halts the rest.\n\n- Why: it removes manual builds and \"works on my machine\". Pitfall: slow/flaky tests kill the whole point — people start merging on a red pipeline. Keep CI fast and stable.","quizCorrect":"Auto build and tests on every push — catch breakage early","quizWrong":["Auto-delivery of the ready artifact to prod environments automatically","Manual code review before merging into main","Switching traffic between application versions"],"picCap":"CI: build+test on push. CD: deploy. Stages in .yml","note":"Classic trap: a pipeline goes green because a failed step didn't fail the build — in a shell script each command returns its own exit code, and without set -e (or set -o pipefail for pipes) a mid-script error is silently swallowed. GitHub Actions runs `run:` with bash -e by default while GitLab CI does not, so it's easy to get a 'successful' deploy of a broken artifact there."},"devops-protected":{"q":"Why protected branches and MR/PR?","a":"A protected branch (main) rejects direct pushes. Changes land only through a merge/pull request — with peer review and a green pipeline. This is quality and history control.","d":"An MR is the point of code review and running checks before reaching the prod branch.","hook":"You can't open the main warehouse door without a pass and the guard's signature — goods come in only through the receiving window.","more":"A **protected branch** forbids a direct `git push` into an important branch (`main`): the server simply rejects any commit that bypasses the process. The only entrance is through a **merge/pull request**, where the code passes peer review and mandatory checks.\n\nWhy: no single person merges into prod alone and blindly. A PR is the point where four guarantees converge: an outside pair of eyes, green CI, discussion, and a record of \"who changed this and why\".\n\n- Typical rules: a minimum of N approvals, a mandatory green pipeline, no force-push or branch deletion. Pitfall: if review is just a formality (\"approve without looking\"), the protection turns into bureaucracy with no benefit.","quizCorrect":"Forbids direct push — changes only via MR/PR with review","quizWrong":["Automatically deploys every commit to prod","Stores a backup copy of the entire repo history","Merges features without the pipeline or reviewers at all whatsoever"],"picCap":"Into main — only via MR: review + green CI","note":"Enabling a protected branch isn't enough: by default the rules usually don't apply to admins/maintainers, and a 'green CI' requirement is easy to bypass unless the status check is explicitly marked required. In GitHub you must turn on 'Do not allow bypassing the above settings' and 'Require branches to be up to date', otherwise you can merge a PR against a stale and actually-red base."},"devops-soa-msa":{"q":"SOA vs microservices (MSA) — what's the difference?","a":"SOA: services around a shared bus (ESB), often a shared DB, coarser-grained. MSA: small independent services, each with its OWN DB, deployed and scaled separately, communicating over the network. MSA is an evolution of SOA with an emphasis on independence.","d":"The price of MSA's independence is distributed-systems pain: network, consistency, observability.","hook":"SOA is a shared kitchen on the floor; microservices are each their own apartment with its own fridge.","more":"**SOA** builds the system around a shared bus, the **ESB**: it routes messages, transforms formats, and does orchestration. Services are coarse-grained, often share one DB and common data schemas. The bus becomes the center of integration — and simultaneously a bottleneck and a point of failure.\n\n**MSA** removes the \"smart\" bus: logic lives in the services, communication is simple (REST/events through a broker). The key is **independence**: each has its own DB, separate deployment and scaling.\n\n- MSA pitfalls: distributed transactions, data consistency, network failures.\n- Don't split for the sake of splitting — many tiny services = a lot of infrastructure pain.","quizCorrect":"Each microservice has its own DB; in SOA it's often shared","quizWrong":["In MSA there's one shared DB, in SOA one per service","Both mandatorily require a single shared ESB","In MSA there's no DB at all, everything is stored in memory"],"picCap":"SOA: shared bus and DB. MSA: own DB per service","note":"The most common failure in interviews and in prod is the 'distributed monolith': formally microservices with separate repos but sharing a DB or a synchronous REST chain A→B→C where one failure takes down all. The mark of true independence is a separate DB and async events: if deploying service B requires deploying A at the same time, it's still SOA-style coupling masquerading as MSA."},"devops-deploy":{"q":"Blue-green vs canary deployment?","a":"Blue-green: two identical environments; the new version is rolled out to \"green\", then all traffic is switched over at once (instant rollback to \"blue\"). Canary: the new version is rolled out to a small % of traffic, you watch the metrics, then gradually expand.","d":"Blue-green is fast rollback; canary is early problem detection on a small share.","note":"Blue-green requires DB compatibility with both versions simultaneously (they run against a shared database): an incompatible migration breaks the rollback to blue. And both canary and blue-green are useless for stateful in-memory sessions on an instance — you need sticky sessions or to externalize the session.","hook":"Blue-green — two identical halls, flip the switch and move the whole crowd at once. Canary — let in a couple of guests first and watch.","more":"**Blue-green** keeps two full environments. Traffic goes to blue, you deploy the new version to green and run it through; then the balancer **switches all traffic at once**. Rollback is instant — point the arrow back to blue. You pay for double the infrastructure.\n\n**Canary** sends the new version to a small **percentage of traffic** (1-5-25%), watches errors/latency, and expands as confidence grows.\n\n- Blue-green — abrupt, simple rollback, but a bug hits 100% immediately on switch.\n- Canary — early detection on a small audience, but you need good monitoring and compatibility of two versions against one DB.","quizCorrect":"The new version goes to a small % of traffic and you watch the metrics","quizWrong":["Keep two environments and switch all traffic at once to the new one in one go","Stop the service, update it, and start it again","Roll prod back to the previous version on error"],"picCap":"Blue-green: traffic at once. Canary: green creeps up bit by bit."},"devops-serverless":{"q":"Serverless / FaaS (e.g. AWS Lambda) — the gist and the downsides?","a":"A function runs on an event, the provider manages the infrastructure, you pay per invocation/runtime. Pros: no server to maintain, auto-scaling. Cons: cold start, time/memory limits, vendor lock-in, harder to test locally.","d":"Good for rare/spiky loads and gluing events together.","note":"Cold start hits the JVM (Quarkus/Spring) especially hard due to warmup — hence GraalVM native image for Lambda. A second non-obvious trap: Lambda scales out per event without bound, and the DB connection pool is instantly exhausted (thousands of parallel instances × connection) — you need an RDS Proxy/pooler.","hook":"A taxi instead of your own car: you pay per ride, but you wait for pickup and can't go far in someone else's.","more":"In **FaaS** you upload a function, and the provider spins up a container on an event (HTTP, queue, timer), scales to zero and back. You pay per number of invocations and runtime — **no idle costs** for an idle server.\n\nThe price of convenience is execution trade-offs.\n\n- **Cold start**: the first invocation after idling waits for the runtime to spin up.\n- Hard time and memory limits — long tasks don't fit.\n- **Vendor lock-in**: triggers and APIs are tied to the cloud; local tests and debugging are harder.\nGood for rare/spiky loads, bad for steady high RPS.","quizCorrect":"A function runs on an event, the provider holds the infrastructure; the downside is cold start and vendor lock-in","quizWrong":["You rent and configure the server yourself, just without an OS — paying a fixed monthly fee regardless of the load","Functions run forever as daemons with no time or memory limit","It's fully portable between providers without changes, no lock-in"],"picCap":"On an event: auto-scale, but cold start and limits"},"devops-linux":{"q":"Basic service diagnostics on Linux — where to look?","a":"Processes/CPU — top/ps; memory — free; disk — df/du; network — ss/curl/ping; logs — journalctl/tail -f. This set is enough to narrow \"the service went down\" to its cause.","d":"First — is there memory/disk/CPU, and is the port listening; then dig into the application logs.","hook":"A doctor at a checkup: pulse (CPU), breathing (memory), blood pressure (disk), pulse at the wrist (network), complaints (logs).","more":"Diagnostics go layer by layer over resources, each with its own utility. First check **what exactly is maxed out**: CPU, memory, disk, network, or the process itself.\n\n- **Process/CPU**: `top`/`htop`, `ps aux` — is the process alive, is it loading the core.\n- **Memory**: `free -h` — is there swap/OOM pressure.\n- **Disk**: `df -h` (space), `du -sh` (who took it).\n- **Network**: `ss -tlnp` (is the port listening), `curl`/`ping` (reachability).\n- **Logs**: `journalctl -u <svc> -f`, `tail -f`.\n\nLogic: confirm the process is running → check the port → resources → the cause in the logs. That way \"the service went down\" quickly narrows to a specific link.","quizCorrect":"CPU/processes — top/ps, memory — free, disk — df/du, network — ss/ping, logs — journalctl/tail","quizWrong":["Only in journalctl — the other metrics (CPU, memory, disk) are pointless to check without aggregation","Memory is checked via df, and disk space via free","Network connections are viewed with top, and processes with ss"],"picCap":"Service down: 5 places to look on Linux","note":"free -h misleadingly shows little 'free' memory because Linux uses unused RAM for the page cache — look at the `available` column, not `free`, for the truly usable memory, or you'll falsely diagnose memory exhaustion. And find an actual OOM-kill not in free's output (that's in the past) but in `dmesg`/`journalctl -k` under the line 'Out of memory: Killed process'."},"x-ops-container-vs-vm":{"q":"Container (Docker) vs virtual machine — what's the difference?","a":"A VM drags a whole guest OS on top of a hypervisor — heavy, boots in minutes. A container shares the host kernel and packages only the app + libraries — lightweight (MB), starts in seconds. A VM's isolation is stronger, a container's is weaker.","d":"A container = a host process isolated via namespaces (visibility) and cgroups (CPU/RAM limits). The image is layered, cached. All containers on a host share one Linux kernel, so you can't run a Windows container on a Linux kernel. A VM virtualizes the whole hardware — full isolation, but overhead per guest OS. In practice: dev environments, CI agents, microservices — containers; hard multi-tenant security isolation — VMs.","note":"A container shares the host kernel, so a Linux image won't run natively on a Windows kernel (Docker Desktop spins up a hidden Linux VM). And because of the shared kernel, a container sees the CPU/RAM of the whole host, not its own limits — old JVMs before the container-aware flags computed heap from the node's full memory and hit OOM.","hook":"A VM is a separate house with a foundation; a container is an apartment in a shared building (shared kernel-foundation)","more":"A VM virtualizes hardware: a **hypervisor** runs a full guest OS with its own kernel on top of the host. A container virtualizes the OS: processes share the **host kernel**, and isolation is achieved by kernel mechanisms (on Linux — namespaces and cgroups), packaging only the app with its dependencies.\n\nHence the difference: a VM — gigabytes and minutes to start, a container — megabytes and seconds.\n\n- The price of lightness is **weaker isolation**: a hole in the host kernel can potentially break through all containers.\n- Pitfall: a container shares the kernel, so a Linux image can't run natively on a Windows kernel (Docker Desktop quietly runs a small Linux VM).\n- When a VM: hard isolation, a different kernel/OS, untrusted workloads.","quizCorrect":"A container shares the host kernel and packages only the app, a VM drags a guest OS","quizWrong":["A container runs its own guest OS, while a VM shares the host kernel","A container and a VM are identical, the only difference is the name across vendors of the vendor","A container is heavier than a VM and starts slower due to strong isolation"],"picCap":"VM = its own floor with an OS, a container shares the kernel"},"x-ops-k8s-basics":{"q":"Kubernetes: what are a Pod, a Deployment, and a Service?","a":"A Pod — the minimal unit, 1+ containers with a shared IP, ephemeral (dies — replaced by a new one). A Deployment keeps N replicas of Pods and rolls out updates. A Service — a stable address + load balancing onto live Pods (their IPs change, the Service doesn't).","d":"Pods are mortal: on restart they get a new IP, so you can't address them directly. A Deployment via a ReplicaSet guarantees the desired number of replicas (self-healing) and does a rolling update. A Service via a label selector finds healthy Pods and gives them a single DNS name/ClusterIP. From outside the cluster — via Ingress (L7 routing by host/path) or a LoadBalancer. K8s is a control loop: controllers constantly reconcile actual state to the desired state from the manifests.","note":"A ClusterIP Service gives a stable DNS, but with long-lived TCP/gRPC connections there's effectively no per-pod balancing: the connection pins to one pod and stays. For gRPC you need an L7 balancer or a headless Service + client-side balancing.","hook":"A Pod is a taxi driver (comes and goes), a Deployment is the dispatcher (always 3 on shift), a Service is the single taxi-booking number","more":"Under the hood the hierarchy is simple: a **Pod** wraps container(s) with a shared IP and volumes. A Pod is mortal: a node failed or the image was updated — the old Pod is removed, a NEW one is born with a different IP. By itself it restarts nothing.\n\nSo you don't create Pods by hand — you describe them via a **Deployment**: \"I want 3 replicas of such a Pod\". The controller watches the fact and reconciles it to the desired state — one died, it brings up a replacement, an update rolls out gradually (rolling update).\n\n- A **Service** solves the problem of changing IPs: it gives one stable DNS address and itself balances traffic onto live Pods by their labels.\n- Pitfall: addressing a Pod by IP directly — the address disappears on the very first restart.","quizCorrect":"A Pod — a unit of 1+ containers, a Deployment keeps N replicas, a Service — a stable address","quizWrong":["A Pod — a stable address, a Deployment — one container, a Service — a data store for the cluster","A Pod is a cluster node, a Deployment is a namespace, a Service is a volume","A Deployment is the minimal unit, a Pod keeps replicas, a Service runs updates"],"picCap":"A Service balances traffic onto the Deployment's replicas"},"x-ops-observability-pillars":{"q":"The three pillars of observability: logs, metrics, traces — how do they differ?","a":"Logs — textual events of \"what happened\" (an error, a line). Metrics — numbers over time (RPS, latency, CPU) for alerts and graphs. Traces — the path of a single request through all the microservices, you see where the bottleneck is. Logs are expensive, metrics are cheap, traces show the connections.","d":"Metrics (Prometheus): aggregated numbers, cheap to store, ideal for dashboards and alerts, but they don't explain a specific failure. Logs (ELK/Loki): detailed, but voluminous and expensive — you need sampling. Distributed tracing (OpenTelemetry, Jaeger): a request gets a trace_id, each service adds a span; you see the full chain and where the milliseconds were lost. The key is correlation: a single trace_id is threaded through logs, metrics, and traces. This is diagnosing \"why it's slow\", not just \"everything went down\".","hook":"Logs — entries in a journal, metrics — a dashboard, traces — the GPS route of a parcel through all the warehouses","more":"The three signals answer different questions about the same system. **Logs** — discrete events of \"what exactly happened\" (an exception, a query line). Rich in detail, but voluminous and expensive to store/search.\n\n**Metrics** are aggregates, numbers over time (latency, RPS, CPU). Cheap, because they don't store every request but compute sums/histograms — ideal for dashboards and alerts, but they won't tell you WHY it went down.\n\n- **Traces** stitch a single request through all the services by a shared trace-id, showing exactly where the bottleneck is in the chain.\n- The combo in practice: a metric spotted a spike → a trace found the guilty service → a log explained the cause. Pitfall — traces break without propagating the context (trace-id) between services.","quizCorrect":"Logs — textual events, metrics — numbers over time, traces — the request's path across services","quizWrong":["Logs — numbers for graphs, metrics — the request's path, traces — textual events","All three are the same thing, just different names in different monitoring systems and vendors of tooling","Metrics — error text, traces — CPU counters, logs — connections between services"],"picCap":"Logs=what, metrics=how much, traces=where"},"x-ops-horizontal-scaling-lb":{"q":"Horizontal vs vertical scaling and the role of the load balancer?","a":"Vertically — give one machine more CPU/RAM (simple, but there's a ceiling and a single point of failure). Horizontally — add more instances, with a load balancer in front spreading the requests. Horizontal requires a stateless application (push the session out, into Redis).","d":"Scale up (vertical) runs into the physics of hardware and gives no fault tolerance — one server down, everything's down. Scale out (horizontal) is nearly linear and resilient, but the app must be stateless: no state in an instance's memory, sessions in Redis/JWT. The load balancer (Nginx, HAProxy, L4/L7): algorithms round-robin, least-connections; health-checks throw out dead nodes; sticky sessions if you really need affinity. In the cloud, autoscaling itself adds/removes instances by a CPU/RPS metric.","note":"Stateless isn't only about the session: a local cache, in-memory counters, background timers, and writing to a local disk also break with N instances. A classic bug — a cron/scheduler inside the application that, after scaling, fires on every instance at once.","hook":"Vertical — we bulk up one loader; horizontal — we hire a crew, and the foreman (LB) hands out the boxes","more":"**Vertically** (scale up) — the same machine, but beefier: more CPU/RAM. Simple, you don't touch the code, but you hit the hardware ceiling, pay non-linearly dearly, and it's still ONE point of failure.\n\n**Horizontally** (scale out) — many identical instances, with a **load balancer** in front spreading the requests. Scale is nearly limitless, the failure of one instance doesn't bring down the service.\n\n- The price of horizontal is that the app must be **stateless**: you can't store a session/files in an instance's memory, or the next request lands on a different node and loses the state.\n- The fix: externalize state — sessions in Redis, files in S3/MinIO, data in the DB.","quizCorrect":"Horizontally — add instances behind a balancer, vertically — give the machine more CPU/RAM","quizWrong":["Horizontally — give the machine more CPU/RAM, vertically — add instances","Horizontal doesn't require a balancer and works only with stateful apps","Vertical scaling has no ceiling and eliminates the single point of failure of the whole system"],"picCap":"The balancer pours traffic onto live instances, the dead one dropped off"},"p-strangler-cutover":{"q":"Why Strangler Fig instead of rewriting a service \"from scratch all at once\" (big-bang rewrite)?","a":"Big-bang — long, expensive, a huge chance of regressions and a long feature freeze. Strangler: you bring the new service up IN PARALLEL behind the same entry points (URL, the same consumer group, the same Zeebe job type), sync the data ahead of time, switch traffic with a proxy. The old one stands as a rollback target.","d":"Conditions: identical entry points, data synced before cutover (bulk copy + delta poll by modified_date), the old stack untouched. Downsides: a period of double infrastructure upkeep + data backfill + in-flight requests during the switch require consumer idempotency. Rollback = revert the NGINX upstream in minutes, while the old Postgres is still alive.","hook":"The strangler fig wraps a tree, intercepts its sap, and the old one dies off on its own — without a jerk and without a freeze.","more":"Strangler Fig grows the new service **behind the same facade**: identical URLs, the same consumer group, the same Zeebe job type. The proxy/router gradually hands part of the traffic to the new code, while the old one keeps running as a **rollback target** — switching is instant in both directions.\n\nThis works because there's no \"D-day\": you sync the data ahead of time, migrate piece by piece (one endpoint, one task type), and compare the old and new behavior in prod. Regression risk drops from \"the whole service\" to \"one function\".\n\n- Big-bang is bad because the branch lives for months, accumulates conflicts and a **feature freeze**, and you can only verify the whole thing at the very end.\n- Strangler pitfall: you need real switching infrastructure and parallel writing/reconciliation of data — otherwise it's just a long branch under a different name.","quizCorrect":"The new service runs in parallel, traffic is switched gradually, the old one is the rollback","quizWrong":["The rewrite goes faster thanks to a clean start without legacy","No need to sync data between the old and new service","The old service is switched off right away, freeing up resources on the cluster immediately for the new one"],"picCap":"Switched the upstream — traffic goes to NEW, rollback in minutes","note":"The most common Strangler pitfall isn't the code but the data: while traffic hits both stacks, both versions write the DB, and delta-poll on modified_date misses deletes (a DELETE doesn't bump modified_date) and is vulnerable to clock skew between nodes. You need CDC/triggers for deletes or tombstone flags; otherwise a rollback to the old Postgres resurrects \"zombie\" records."},"p-obs-metric-cardinality":{"q":"Why is adding user_id or order_id to a Prometheus metric label a way to take down monitoring?","a":"Each unique combination of labels = a separate time series in Prometheus's memory. Set label=order_id → a million orders = a million series for ONE metric → a cardinality explosion, monitoring OOM. A label is only for LOW-cardinality dimensions (status, type, endpoint, method). High-cardinality (user_id, trace_id, document_id) belongs in logs and traces, not in metrics.","d":"Rule: the number of series ≈ the product of the cardinalities of all labels. status(5)×method(4)×endpoint(20)=400 series — fine. Added user_id(1e6) → ×1e6 = catastrophe. If you need a breakdown by a specific entity — that's the job of structured logs (filter by a field) or distributed tracing (search by trace_id), not metrics. For metrics under high traffic, also remember trace sampling (e.g. 10%) so you don't drown the backend.","hook":"Shelves in a store for EVERY receipt: 'milk' — one shelf, 'milk receipt #48213' — a shelf per receipt, the warehouse burst.","more":"Under the hood: Prometheus stores a **separate time series** for each unique combination of the metric name and label values, and keeps active series in memory. Cardinality = the number of such combinations.\n\nA label like `order_id` or `user_id` multiplies series by the number of entities: a million orders → a million series for one metric. Memory explodes, scrapes and queries slow down, and it ends in a monitoring **OOM**.\n\n- Labels are only for low-cardinality dimensions: `status`, `method`, `endpoint` (the route, not the full URL with an id!), document type.\n- High cardinality (id, trace_id, a path with parameters) — write to **logs and traces**: that's where it's needed to analyze a specific case, and leave the metrics for aggregates.","quizCorrect":"Each unique value is a new series, a cardinality explosion and OOM","quizWrong":["Prometheus doesn't support string label values at all","Labels with an ID are encrypted and heavily load the CPU on write of every sample","An ID in a label is automatically sent to all alerts as spam"],"picCap":"Series = the product of the labels' cardinalities"},"p-obs-cardinality-labels":{"q":"Why is adding client_id or order_id as a label to a Prometheus metric a ticking time bomb?","a":"Each unique combination of labels = a separate time series in memory. client_id/order_id yield millions of values → millions of series → Prometheus eats up RAM and falls over (OOM). Labels are only for bounded sets: route, status, doc_type. And an ID goes into logs and into a trace span.","d":"Cardinality is the number of unique values of a field. High cardinality (user_id, trace_id) is invaluable for debugging, but in a metrics system each new set of labels spawns a new time series, and memory grows linearly. Rule: metrics = low cardinality (aggregates), logs/spans = high (the context of a specific request). A counter with route+status — fine; with client_id — an explosion.","hook":"An ID in a label = giving each customer a personal shelf in the warehouse. There aren't enough shelves — the warehouse collapses.","more":"Under the hood: the pairing of the metric name + the set of labels forms a **unique time series**, and Prometheus keeps active series in RAM. A `client_id`/`order_id` label adds a series per entity — millions of values yield millions of series for one metric.\n\nIt's a ticking time bomb because at the start there are few entities and everything works; the load grows gradually until Prometheus hits its memory ceiling and catches an **OOM**.\n\n- Labels are only for bounded sets: `route`, `status`, `doc_type`, method.\n- Identifiers (client_id, order_id, trace_id) go into logs and into a trace span; there they give a pinpoint incident analysis, and you leave the metrics for trends and alerts.","quizCorrect":"Into logs and into a trace span","quizWrong":["Into a separate gauge-type metric with the same name","Into the annotations of Prometheus alerts","Into recording rules for pre-aggregation"],"picCap":"One ID-in-a-label = a time series explosion","note":"The 'time bomb' nature is the real catch: in dev/staging there are few entities, the series fit in RAM, and the problem is invisible until production hits real volume. Defense — don't rely on review: enforce server-side limits (sample_limit in scrape_config, --query.max-samples) and alert on prometheus_tsdb_head_series; in client libraries Micrometer offers MeterFilter.maximumAllowableTags, which caps the explosion before exposition."},"p-k8s-liveness-cheap":{"q":"Why must a liveness probe be \"cheap\" and check only the process itself, not the DB and neighboring services?","a":"On failure, liveness RESTARTS the container. If you stuff a DB check into it — and the DB blinks — k8s will simultaneously restart ALL the pods (a restart storm), even though the app was alive. Amplifying the outage instead of curing it. Check dependencies in readiness — it merely removes the pod from balancing, without a restart.","d":"Liveness answers the question \"is the process hung/deadlocked?\" → fail = restart container. Readiness — \"can I serve traffic right now?\" → fail = remove from the Service's endpoints (without a restart). So dependencies (DB, downstream) belong in readiness. Liveness that pings a shared DB turns a local DB failure into a cascading restart of the whole fleet = an outage amplifier.","hook":"Don't call an ambulance (restart) for everyone in the building when the shared power was cut — fix the power (readiness).","more":"Under the hood: **liveness** answers the question \"is the process hung?\" — on failure the kubelet **restarts the container**. **readiness** answers \"ready to accept traffic?\" — on failure the pod is merely removed from the service's endpoints, without a restart.\n\nIf you stuff a DB check into liveness, then during a brief DB failure the probes fail across all pods at once — and k8s simultaneously restarts the whole deployment. You get a **restart storm**: amplifying the outage instead of curing it, since the apps themselves were alive.\n\n- Keep liveness cheap: check only the process itself (the event loop isn't hung, no deadlocks), without network or external dependencies.\n- Check dependencies (DB, cache, neighboring services) in readiness — the pod simply drops out of balancing and returns when the dependency comes back to life.","quizCorrect":"A liveness failure restarts the container — a blinking DB gives a restart storm","quizWrong":["Liveness is called too rarely to catch a DB failure","A DB check in liveness violates Kubernetes network policies in a default cluster","Liveness has no access to env vars with the DB address"],"picCap":"A DB check — in readiness (drop from LB), not in liveness (restart)","note":"A common mistake is a single shared /health for both liveness and readiness: if it touches the DB, you get exactly the restart-storm you were avoiding. Another trap: with a slow-starting JVM, liveness fires before warm-up and spins the pod into CrashLoopBackOff — that's what a separate startupProbe is for (k8s 1.16+, GA in 1.20), which disables liveness/readiness until the app is up."},"p-obs-burn-rate":{"q":"What is burn rate and why is waiting for an \"SLO breach at month's end\" too late?","a":"Error budget = the allowed share of bad (1 − SLO). Burn rate — how fast you're spending it relative to plan. Burn rate >1× = the budget runs out ahead of schedule. You alert on a fast burn (e.g. >14.4× over an hour = 2% of the monthly budget in an hour), not on the mere fact of an SLO breach — otherwise you find out when the budget is already burned.","d":"An SLO is the target reliability level (99.5% of requests successful and fast over a window). The signal to act is the SPEED of budget burn, measured over a short window, not an SLO violation after the fact. <1× — healthy, dev ships features; 1–14× — investigate during work hours, freeze risky deploys; >14× — wake the on-call, roll back the release. A classic example: a memory leak burned the budget to −566%, traditional monitoring never fired — you needed burn rate hours earlier.","hook":"Watch the fuel-consumption gauge, not the empty tank — on an empty tank you're already stranded.","more":"Under the hood: the **error budget** is the allowed share of bad requests over a period, `1 − SLO` (for 99.9% the budget = 0.1%). **Burn rate** shows how many times faster than plan you're spending that budget: 1× — exactly over the whole period, 14.4× — you'll burn 2% of the monthly budget in an hour.\n\nWhy this way: an alert on the mere fact of an SLO breach fires when the budget is already burned — too late to react. An alert on a high burn rate catches the problem while there's still budget left.\n\n- Google SRE practice: multi-window — a fast burn (e.g. >14.4× over 1h) for acute incidents and a slow one (>3× over 6h) for quiet degradations.\n- This removes both false positives and late alerts: you alert by spend speed, not by the final fact.","quizCorrect":"How fast the error budget is being spent relative to plan","quizWrong":["What percentage of the SLO has already been finally violated for the month","The mean time to recover a service after an incident","The share of requests that hit the cache instead of the backend"],"picCap":"React to the speed, not the fact","note":"A subtle point: a burn-rate alert must combine short and long windows with an AND condition (e.g. 1h AND 5min), otherwise the short window false-fires on a single spike and the long window is slow to clear the alert after a fix (long tail). And mind MTTR: an alert of '2% in an hour at 14.4×' is useless if on-call can't react within that time — the window threshold must be matched to the team's real reaction time."},"p-k8s-cpu-mem-qos":{"q":"How does CPU differ from memory when a limit is exceeded, and how does the QoS class decide which pod gets killed first?","a":"CPU is compressible: exceed the limit — you get throttled, slowed down, but not killed. Memory is incompressible: exceed the limit — OOM-kill, the pod dies. QoS by req/limit: BestEffort (no req/lim) killed first → Burstable (req<lim) → Guaranteed (req==lim) killed last. So for critical services set req==lim.","d":"requests — the guaranteed reservation for the scheduler and the QoS floor; limits — the hard ceiling. CPU at the ceiling is throttled (it can be \"taken away\" and returned), memory can't be taken away — so the kernel's only option under a RAM shortage is to kill the process. Under node pressure the kubelet evicts in QoS order. Didn't set requests → the scheduler over-packs the node and evicts the pod under load; didn't set limits → one noisy pod starves the whole node. For the JVM keep heap ≤ memory limit, or you get an OOM-kill instead of an OutOfMemoryError.","hook":"CPU — like a queue at the checkout (you'll wait). Memory — like space in an elevator: the extras just get thrown out.","more":"Under the hood the difference is in the physics of the resource. CPU is **compressible**: through CFS quotas the kernel simply doesn't give the pod more cycles — it slows down, but lives. Memory is **incompressible**: you can't take back occupied pages, so under a shortage the kernel invokes the **OOM-killer** and the process is killed.\n\nQoS is the survival priority under a node memory shortage. **BestEffort** (no requests/limits) is evicted first, **Burstable** (request < limit) — next, **Guaranteed** (request == limit on cpu and mem) — last.\n\n- Pitfall: you set a high memory limit but a low request — the pod is Burstable and gets killed under the neighbors' load. For critical services set req == lim to land in Guaranteed.","quizCorrect":"OOM-kill: memory is incompressible, the pod dies","quizWrong":["The pod is throttled and slowed down, but keeps running","Kubernetes automatically raises the limit to the next value","The pod goes to Pending until memory is freed on the node"],"picCap":"CPU gets throttled, memory gets killed","note":"JVM subtlety: before Java 10 (UseContainerSupport, backported to 8u191) the JVM saw the whole node's memory/CPU rather than the cgroup limit and sized Xmx by the host → instant OOM-kill in the container. Even now the heap isn't the whole process: metaspace, thread stacks, direct buffers and code cache live outside -Xmx, so the container needs a limit with headroom above the heap (or set -XX:MaxRAMPercentage ~75%), otherwise RSS exceeds the limit and you get an OOM-kill with no OutOfMemoryError."},"p-k8s-sigterm-drain":{"q":"What happens between \"the pod is deleted\" and SIGKILL, and why do requests break without a preStop and SIGTERM handling?","a":"On shutdown: the pod is removed from endpoints → preStop hook → SIGTERM → grace period (30s by default) → SIGKILL. Removal from balancing and SIGTERM are NOT atomic: traffic still reaches the already-shutting-down pod. A preStop (sleep 5) lets the LB update. And the application MUST catch SIGTERM: stop taking new work, finish in-flight, close connections, exit 0. Otherwise — broken requests.","d":"Most \"k8s flakiness\" is an unclosed drain: either no health probes, or SIGTERM ignored. The race is that endpoint-removal propagates across the cluster asynchronously, while SIGTERM arrives immediately — a short preStop sleep covers the window while the balancer is still sending traffic. PID 1 will get SIGTERM only if it's the process itself (a shell wrapper swallows the signal — you need the exec form or dumb-init). Didn't fit within terminationGracePeriodSeconds — SIGKILL arrives and severs the unfinished work. For Kafka consumers: on SIGTERM stop the poll, commit the offsets.","hook":"Before closing the store — a \"closing\" sign, and you see out the current customers, not kill the lights on people.","more":"When a pod is deleted, two processes run **in parallel**: the kubelet sends preStop → SIGTERM → waits the grace period (30s) → SIGKILL, while the endpoint-controller removes the pod from Endpoints and updates the kube-proxy rules on all nodes. The latter takes time and isn't synced with the former.\n\nThe result: the pod has already received SIGTERM, but the balancers are still sending traffic to it — hence the ragged 502/connection refused during a deploy.\n\n- **preStop sleep 5** doesn't fix the pod, it just buys time while the balancing rules propagate across the cluster.\n- The application MUST catch SIGTERM: stop taking new work, finish in-flight, close pools. Otherwise graceful shutdown doesn't work.","quizCorrect":"Give the balancer time to update endpoints while traffic still reaches the pod","quizWrong":["Flush the application logs to disk before terminating","Guarantee the atomicity of endpoint removal and SIGTERM","Wait until Kubernetes detaches the PersistentVolume from the pod completely from the node"],"picCap":"preStop closes the race with the balancer — the stages go in order","note":"Hidden PID 1 trap: the shell form of ENTRYPOINT (`CMD java -jar app.jar`) runs the process under /bin/sh, which does NOT forward SIGTERM to the child — the app never sees the signal and always dies by SIGKILL. You need the exec form (`CMD [\"java\",...]`) or an init like tini/dumb-init. Also order matters: the preStop sleep must be LONGER than the endpoint-removal propagation time through kube-proxy, otherwise the sleep ends before the LB stops sending traffic."},"ops-maven":{"q":"Maven build lifecycle — the main phases?","a":"Maven builds the project in step-phases in a fixed order: validate → compile → test → package → verify → install → deploy. Run any phase — all the ones before it execute. `mvn package` runs everything up to and including building the jar/war.","d":"This is the default lifecycle. Key phases: validate (check the project), compile (compile into target/classes), test (unit tests), package (package into a jar/war), verify (integration checks), install (a copy of the artifact into the local cache ~/.m2/repository — available to your other projects), deploy (upload to a remote repository, e.g. Nexus/Artifactory, for the team/CI). You can't run a phase \"bypassing\" the previous ones: `mvn install` itself runs test and package. You can skip tests with the -DskipTests flag, but the test phase formally still \"passes\".","note":"`mvn install` without an explicit `test` still runs the tests (the test phase comes before package) — you can only skip with `-DskipTests` (compiles, but doesn't run) or `-Dmaven.test.skip=true` (doesn't even compile). And install puts the artifact into the local ~/.m2, not into a shared Nexus — that's deploy; the confusion breaks sharing between developers.","hook":"A factory conveyor: the part passes all the machines in order — you can't package it without building it.","more":"Maven's **lifecycle** is an ordered chain of phases: `validate → compile → test → package → verify → install → deploy`. Each phase is bound to plugins that do the real work (compilation, tests, packaging).\n\nThe key rule: by running a phase, you execute **all the previous ones** in that cycle. So `mvn package` itself runs validate, compile, test and then builds the jar/war.\n\n- `install` puts the artifact into the local repository `~/.m2`, `deploy` — into a remote one\n- there are actually three cycles: default (build), clean, site\n- pitfall: `mvn clean package` cleans and builds; you can skip tests with `-DskipTests`, but don't disable them out of habit","quizCorrect":"All phases up to and including package will execute","quizWrong":["Only the package phase will execute, without compile and test","The jar will be built, but the tests will be skipped","The install and deploy phases will run too"],"picCap":"Any phase pulls along all the previous ones to its left — we go down the chain"},"g-ops-docker-layer-cache":{"q":"Why does COPY of the whole project BEFORE mvn dependency kill the layer cache, and how is it fixed by ordering in the Dockerfile?","a":"Docker caches layers in order: if a layer changed — it and all the following ones are rebuilt. If you FIRST COPY all the code, then any code edit invalidates the layer and the dependencies are downloaded anew every build. Fix: first COPY pom.xml + download the dependencies (they change rarely), THEN COPY the code. Then the dependency cache survives between builds.","d":"A layer's cache lives as long as these are unchanged: the instruction, the content of the copied files, and all the previous layers. mvn dependency:go-offline in a separate layer = dependencies are downloaded only when pom.xml changes. This cuts build time from minutes to seconds. The same trick — package.json before COPY . in Node.","hook":"First you put the heavy and constant stuff in the suitcase (chargers), on top — what you change every day (socks).","more":"Docker builds an image in layers top to bottom and caches each. A layer's cache key depends on the instruction and the content. As soon as a layer changes — it and **all the layers below** are invalidated and rebuilt.\n\nWhy `COPY . .` at the start is a disaster: any code edit changes an early layer, so the dependency-download step below it loses its cache and downloads anew every build — slow and traffic-heavy.\n\nIt's cured by **ordering**: first `COPY pom.xml`, then `mvn dependency:go-offline` (dependencies change rarely → the layer survives most builds), and only THEN `COPY src` + build. That way frequent code changes touch only the last layers. Same for npm: first package.json + install, then the code.","quizCorrect":"The dependency layer is cached and not rebuilt on a code edit","quizWrong":["Maven reads only pom.xml and ignores src without a separate COPY","Docker compresses pom.xml more than the sources","This runs the dependency download in parallel with compilation"],"picCap":"Stable — to the bottom of the stack, volatile — to the top","note":"COPY ordering is only half the battle: in CI the layer cache is EMPTY by default on each runner (fresh Docker daemon), so dependency:go-offline re-downloads everything anyway until you wire up --cache-from / registry cache (BuildKit) or a persistent cache. And Maven's go-offline is notorious for not fetching all plugins/dependencies — some get pulled during the build phase, breaking the layer's offline isolation."},"g-ops-latest-tag":{"q":"Why is deploying an image by the :latest tag a landmine, and what replaces it in prod?","a":":latest isn't a version, just \"the last one pushed\". Two nodes can pull DIFFERENT images under one :latest, rollback is impossible (roll back to what?), and imagePullPolicy may take an old one from the cache. In prod they tag with an immutable tag: git-sha or semver — app:1.4.2 / app:a1b2c3d.","d":"The problem is that :latest is a mutable reference: today it points at one thing, tomorrow at another. Reproducibility is lost. Best practice: tag = commit SHA (exact build↔code traceability) or semver for releases. In k8s set imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent with an immutable tag — it saves the pull and guarantees the same image.","hook":"\"Give me the latest newspaper\" — but over a week there are five different ones. \"The June 19 newspaper\" — always the same one.","more":"**:latest** isn't version semantics, just a label on the last pushed image. It's **mutable**: the same tag points at different digests at different times.\n\nWhy it's dangerous: two nodes pulling at different moments get **different** images under one tag → no reproducibility. There's nowhere to roll back to — a \"previous version\" as a stable point doesn't exist. Plus `imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent` may keep a stale image from the node's cache.\n\nIn prod they tag **immutably**: semver (`app:1.4.2`) or git-sha (`app:a1b2c3d`). One tag = one digest forever → a deterministic deploy, a clear rollback, and an honest audit of \"what exactly is running\".","quizCorrect":"The tag is mutable: nodes can get different images, rollback is ambiguous","quizWrong":["latest always pulls slower due to a signature check of the image in the registry on every pull","Docker forbids :latest in production mode","latest takes up twice the space in the registry"],"picCap":"latest floats, semver/sha is pinned","note":"The sneaky part: with imagePullPolicy, :latest defaults to Always while a concrete tag defaults to IfNotPresent, so an immutable tag without a digest is still unsafe if someone re-pushes that tag (registries allow this, except e.g. ECR immutable mode). True reproducibility only comes from pinning by digest (app@sha256:...), not by a text tag."},"g-ops-semver-breaking":{"q":"In MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH, which number do you bump on a breaking API change, and why is 1.0.0 a special threshold?","a":"A breaking change (an incompatible change to the public API) = bump MAJOR: 1.4.2 → 2.0.0, and MINOR/PATCH reset to 0. MINOR is a new feature without breaking compatibility (1.4.2→1.5.0). PATCH is a bugfix (1.4.2→1.4.3). Below 1.0.0 (zero versions) the rule doesn't apply: anything may break at any time.","d":"The SemVer contract: from the version, a consumer can tell whether it's safe to upgrade. ^1.4.2 in dependencies = \"take any 1.x\" (you trust that MINOR won't break things). The cardinal mistake: silently breaking the API in a MINOR/PATCH — every consumer's build/runtime fails on a supposedly \"safe\" update. 0.x.y is a public signal that \"the API isn't stable yet\".","hook":"A floor of a house (MAJOR) — knock out a wall and everyone rebuilds. A room (MINOR) — you added furniture. A light bulb (PATCH) — you swapped it and nobody noticed.","more":"**SemVer** encodes public-API compatibility in three numbers. A **breaking change** (removed/renamed a field, changed the contract) → bump **MAJOR**, and MINOR and PATCH reset to zero: `1.4.2 → 2.0.0`. The reset signals \"everything below is no longer relevant\".\n\n**MINOR** — a backward-compatible addition of functionality (`1.4.2 → 1.5.0`). **PATCH** — a backward-compatible bugfix with no new features (`1.4.2 → 1.4.3`).\n\nWhy **1.0.0** is a threshold: `0.y.z` versions are declared unstable — you may break things in any release there, the API hasn't \"set\" yet. Releasing 1.0.0 is a public promise to keep compatibility by the rules. Gotcha: don't conflate \"behavior visible to the consumer\" (that *is* the API) with internal refactoring, which doesn't move the version.","quizCorrect":"MAJOR (e.g. 1.4.2 → 2.0.0)","quizWrong":["MINOR, since it's an API change","PATCH, because it's a code edit","Nothing — a removal doesn't count as a version change"],"picCap":"Break the contract — turn the left digit","note":"Range subtlety: for 0.x.y npm treats ^0.4.1 as >=0.4.1 <0.5.0 (caret behaves like tilde), so 0.5.0 is NOT pulled in automatically — a common 'why didn't it update' cause. And SemVer only covers the visible API: changing a transitive dependency or runtime behavior (log format, defaults) is formally not breaking, yet really breaks consumers."},"g-ops-ci-secrets":{"q":"Why is a secret that ends up in `echo $TOKEN` or in a Docker ARG considered leaked, even if the pipeline is green?","a":"Secrets in CI must not be printed and must not be baked into image layers. echo prints the token to the job log (visible to everyone with access). Docker ARG/ENV are baked into the image history — docker history will pull them out even after \"deletion\". The right way: masked secret variables from a store (Vault/CI secrets), a --secret mount at build time, and rotation on any suspicion of a leak.","d":"Log masking isn't a cure-all: a token in a URL, in a child process's ENV, or in an artifact will leak anyway. BuildKit --mount=type=secret provides the file only during a RUN, without saving it into a layer. The core principle: a secret that COULD have been seen is already compromised — you rotate it, you don't \"hope nobody noticed\".","hook":"A password you once said out loud in a crowded elevator is no longer a password — even if \"seemingly nobody was listening\".","more":"A secret is \"leaked\" the moment it becomes **readable from artifacts that outlive the job**, even with a green pipeline.\n\nTwo typical channels: `echo $TOKEN` writes the value to the **job log** — seen by everyone with CI access, and logs are often retained for a long time. `ARG`/`ENV` in Docker are **baked into the image's layers/metadata**: `docker history` and inspection will extract the value even after the variable is \"deleted\" in a later layer — the layer stays in the history.\n\nThe right way: store it in **masked** CI secret variables or an external Vault; at build time pass it via a `--secret` mount (BuildKit), which doesn't settle into layers; mask it in logs. And if a secret ever flashed in a log/layer — treat it as compromised and **rotate** it, don't \"clean it up\".","quizCorrect":"The value is baked into the layer history and retrievable via docker history","quizWrong":["ARG only works at runtime, not at build","ARG is limited to 256 characters and will truncate the token passed into the build","Docker automatically logs all ARGs to the registry"],"picCap":"Landed in a log/layer — consider it leaked","note":"Log masking only catches exact string matches: a base64/URL-encoded or split-up secret (e.g. inside JSON output) passes into the log unmasked. And BuildKit --mount=type=secret only prevents it from landing in a layer — if inside RUN you echo the secret or write it to a file you later COPY, it still ends up baked into the image."},"g-ops-terraform-state":{"q":"Why does Terraform need remote state with locks, and what breaks with a local terraform.tfstate in a team?","a":"tfstate is a map of \"what Terraform created in the cloud\". A local file = everyone has their own version of reality: two people apply at the same time and overwrite each other's resources — drift, duplicates. A remote backend (S3+DynamoDB lock, or Terraform Cloud) holds a single shared state and blocks concurrent applies. Without it, a team quickly tears its infrastructure apart.","d":"State is the source of truth for the diff: Terraform compares state ↔ config ↔ the real cloud. A lock (state locking) prevents two applies from running at once. Also: state contains secrets in cleartext (DB passwords) — so you encrypt the S3 bucket and never commit tfstate to git. Drift is caught with terraform plan/refresh.","hook":"A shared warehouse ledger: if everyone keeps their own copy, an item is \"in stock\" and \"out of stock\" at the same time. You need one ledger and a lock while someone is writing.","more":"**tfstate** is a snapshot of the correspondence \"resource in code ↔ real object in the cloud\" (id, attributes). Terraform compares the desired state against the state file rather than polling the entire cloud.\n\nA local file = each engineer has **their own version of the truth**. Two people run `apply` at the same time → a race: one overwrites the other's changes, producing drift, duplicates, or the deletion of someone else's resources. Plus the file can contain secrets and is lost with the laptop.\n\nThe solution is a **remote backend** (S3 + DynamoDB lock, GCS, Terraform Cloud): a single shared state and a **lock** for the duration of an operation, so applies run strictly one at a time. Gotcha: state is sensitive data — never commit it to git; edit it by hand (`terraform state`) carefully and from a backup.","quizCorrect":"It prevents concurrent applies so two runs don't overwrite the infrastructure","quizWrong":["It encrypts passwords inside the state file","It speeds up terraform plan via a cache","It versions the configuration instead of git and keeps its own history of every change"],"picCap":"One state, one writer at a time — the lock lets you through in turn","note":"Trap: the S3+DynamoDB lock everyone cites as canonical is legacy as of Terraform 1.10 (Nov 2024) — the S3 backend now supports a native lockfile via `use_lockfile = true`, and the separate DynamoDB table (`dynamodb_table`) is deprecated and being removed in Terraform 1.11+. Copying an old tutorial onto a new version produces warnings and a redundant resource; and if you already run a DynamoDB lock you can't drop it in one commit, or teammates on older binaries lose locking."},"g-ops-feature-flag-debt":{"q":"How is a feature flag better than a separate long-lived branch, and why is a \"forgotten\" flag tech debt and a risk?","a":"A feature flag is if(flags.on(\"x\")) in the code: the feature is merged into main turned off and enabled via runtime config, without a redeploy. This removes the merge hell of long-lived branches and decouples deploy from release. But every flag is a branch in the code. A forgotten enabled flag = a dead else-path that nobody tests, and one day someone will trip it.","d":"Flags come in kinds: release (gradual rollout), ops (kill-switch), experiment (A/B), permission. The key discipline is TTL: a release flag has a lifetime, and after full rollout it's removed along with the old branch of code. Accumulating flags = a combinatorial explosion of states that are impossible to test. That's not a feature, it's debt with interest.","note":"A flag read in several places within a request can cause inconsistency if you toggle it mid-processing — snapshot the flag value once per request. And flags for business logic (not release flags) live forever — they can't be cleaned up, which blurs the meaning of a \"temporary flag\".","hook":"A temporary patch on a pipe: it saves you today, but if you never remove it, in a year everyone forgets about it and it bursts at the worst possible moment.","more":"A **feature flag** is a runtime condition `if (flags.on(\"x\"))`: the feature's code is merged into main **turned off** and enabled via config/rollout percentage without a redeploy. This **decouples deploy from release** and removes the pain of long-lived branches — there's no accumulated divergent diff and no merge-conflict hell, integration is continuous.\n\nA long-lived branch, by contrast, accumulates divergence over weeks and explodes at merge time.\n\nThe cost: every flag is a **branch** in the execution paths, growing the test matrix. A forgotten flag = a dead or untested else-path; someone will toggle it one day and run code that hasn't executed in months. That's why flags are short-lived: roll out → stabilize → **remove the flag and the dead branch**.","quizCorrect":"An untested else branch remains, which someone will activate one day","quizWrong":["Flags slow down the JVM due to extra ifs on every single request path","Every flag requires its own deploy","Flags can't be removed once enabled"],"picCap":"A flag without a TTL turns into debt — the forgotten path keeps hanging around"},"g-ops-rollback-migration":{"q":"Why doesn't \"just roll the image back to the previous version\" always save you if there was an ALTER TABLE DROP COLUMN?","a":"Rolling back the code ≠ rolling back the DB. If the new version ran a DROP COLUMN migration and you roll the image back — the old code expects a column that no longer exists → crash. The DB schema moves \"forward in time\", the code goes back. That's why migrations are made backward-compatible (expand/contract): first you add, deploy, and only then separately remove the old thing.","d":"The expand-and-contract pattern: (1) expand — add the new column, code writes to both; (2) deploy and backfill; (3) contract — drop the old column in a separate release, once the old code is no longer needed. This way, at every step the N and N±1 code versions are compatible with the schema. Roll-forward (fix forward) is often safer than rollback once the DB has already changed.","hook":"You tore down the old bridge and built a new one — cars following \"revert to the old route\" on the map have nowhere to go. First build the new one alongside, demolish the old one later.","more":"Rolling back the image reverts the **code**, but not the **DB schema** — these are two independent directions of time. If the release ran `ALTER TABLE DROP COLUMN`, the data and the column are already gone; after rolling the image back, you get old code that still `SELECT`s a nonexistent column → runtime errors.\n\nWhy: migrations are usually **irreversible or destructive**, the DB moves \"forward\", and you can't simply roll it back to a snapshot in prod without losing fresh data.\n\nThe cure is **expand/contract**: first you only add the new thing (column/table), deploy code that handles both old and new; clean up data; and only in a separate later release, once the old code is guaranteed not to return, do you `DROP`. This way, at every step adjacent code versions are compatible with the schema.","quizCorrect":"The old code expects the dropped column — the DB schema didn't roll back with the image","quizWrong":["Docker blocks rollback if there was a DB migration","Rolling back the image automatically runs the down migration and restores the previous schema","DROP COLUMN is always reversible by rolling the image back"],"picCap":"The schema doesn't roll back as easily as the image","note":"Non-obvious: expand/contract saves you from DROP COLUMN but silently breaks on type-narrowing and NOT NULL — e.g. `ALTER ... TYPE varchar(50)` or adding `NOT NULL` without a DEFAULT is not backward-compatible, and on large tables in Postgres such an ALTER takes an `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock and takes the service down before any rollback. That's why you add a new NOT NULL column as nullable + backfill + a separate constraint later, rather than assuming 'I only added a column, so it's safe.'"},"g-ops-prometheus-pull":{"q":"Prometheus fetches metrics itself (pull) rather than the service sending them (push) — why, and what do you do with short-lived jobs?","a":"Prometheus periodically scrapes /metrics on its targets (the pull model). Pros: Prometheus knows the list of targets (service discovery), it's easy to tell \"is the target alive\" (up=0 if it didn't respond), and there's no flood from a push avalanche. Con: short batch jobs die between scrapes and their metrics are lost. For those there's the Pushgateway — a buffer the job pushes metrics into before dying.","d":"Pull gives \"health as a side effect\": if a scrape fails, the target is down and the alert fires by itself. Service discovery (k8s, Consul) gives Prometheus an up-to-date list of pods. The Pushgateway is the exception, not the rule: it is NOT used for long-lived services (metrics \"stick\" there and don't reflect that the job already died). For those, pull from /metrics only.","note":"A scrape gives a metric only at the moment of polling (usually 15-60s) — a short error spike between scrapes is invisible to Prometheus. And a counter reset by a pod restart is only computed correctly via rate()/increase(), which detect resets; a raw difference yields negative garbage.","hook":"The teacher walks the desks and checks notebooks (pull). Whoever's absent is immediately obvious. He doesn't wait for the students to run up and hand them in (push).","more":"**Prometheus** works by **pull**: on a timer (`scrape_interval`) it visits each target's `/metrics` itself and grabs the current values. It learns the target list via **service discovery** (k8s, consul, static config).\n\nWhy pull is convenient: the server centrally controls who to poll and how often; the healthcheck is free — the `up=0` metric if a target didn't respond; there's no risk of a client avalanche flooding the server with pushes; it's easy to hit `/metrics` manually for debugging.\n\nGotcha: **short-lived** batch/cron jobs die between scrapes — Prometheus simply doesn't get to poll them and the metrics are lost. For those there's the **Pushgateway**: the job pushes metrics there before finishing, and Prometheus scrapes the gateway instead. It's important to then clean up stale entries in the gateway, otherwise it serves \"ghost\" values of dead jobs.","quizCorrect":"A target's unavailability is immediately visible as up=0 — health for free","quizWrong":["Services don't need to expose a /metrics endpoint — the server collects it itself","Push is impossible to implement in Kubernetes","Pull encrypts metrics while push does not"],"picCap":"The server pulls /metrics itself; no answer means down"},"g-ops-structured-logs":{"q":"Why do prod logs get written as JSON lines with a trace_id, rather than \"pretty\" multi-line text with a stack trace?","a":"In ELK/Loki, the logs of all pods merge into a single stream. A multi-line human-readable log gets torn into pieces (each stack-trace line = a separate record), context is lost, and you can't filter on it. A structured JSON log = one record with fields (level, trace_id, order_id) — Elasticsearch indexes and searches by field, and the trace_id stitches a request across all services.","d":"Keys: (1) one event = one JSON line (a multiline stack trace is glued into a message field); (2) the trace_id from MDC ties one request's logs across different services to the traces; (3) levels and fields instead of parsing text with regexes. stdout/stderr is the only \"transport\" (12-factor); an agent (Filebeat/Fluentd) collects them itself. Don't write secrets/PII to the log.","hook":"A form with labeled fields (name, date, phone) versus a sheet of solid text: with the form you find everyone \"from Almaty\" in a second; with the text you'll read every line.","more":"Under the hood, an agent collects each pod's stdout **line by line** (Promtail/Fluent Bit): one line = one record. A multi-line human-readable log with a stack trace gets torn apart — each trace line becomes a separate event with no context, and parsing \"pretty\" text with regexes is fragile.\n\nA JSON log is **one record with typed fields** (level, trace_id, order_id, msg). Elasticsearch/Loki index the fields, so you can filter (`level=ERROR AND order_id=123`) and aggregate. The **trace_id** stitches one request across all services.\n\n- Gotcha: wrap the stack trace into a single field; watch field cardinality (Loki).\n- Locally a pretty output is handier; in prod — JSON.","quizCorrect":"Search/filter by fields and stitching one request's logs across services","quizWrong":["JSON logs take up less disk space","Elasticsearch can't ingest text logs","JSON automatically hides secrets in logs before they are shipped anywhere"],"picCap":"Fields get indexed, text doesn't"},"g-ops-gradle-vs-maven-incremental":{"q":"What makes Gradle usually faster than Maven on repeat builds, and what's the price?","a":"Gradle keeps an incremental build + build cache + an in-memory daemon: it rebuilds only the changed tasks and reuses outputs (even across branches/machines). Maven by default runs phases linearly every time. The price of Gradle: the build is an imperative Groovy/Kotlin script (flexible, but harder, \"magic\"); Maven has a declarative rigid XML lifecycle (predictable, boring, easy to read).","d":"The tradeoff for a team with juniors: Maven wins on transparency — phases are fixed, the pom is declarative, fewer ways to shoot yourself in the foot. Gradle wins on speed on large multi-module projects and on the flexibility of custom tasks. Choose by context: not \"Gradle is newer = better\", but what matters more — speed/flexibility or predictability/a low barrier to entry.","note":"Gradle's build cache is treacherous: a \"dirty\" input (not declared as a task input) yields a false cache hit and a stale artifact — hence the habit of `--no-build-cache`/`clean` on mysterious bugs. The daemon also accumulates state between builds and sometimes needs `--stop`.","hook":"Gradle is a chef's kitchen (fast, but you have to know how). Maven is an IKEA manual: step by step, boring, but it'll assemble anything.","more":"Gradle builds a **task graph** and computes an input/output hash for each. If inputs haven't changed, the task is `UP-TO-DATE` or pulled from the **build cache** (even from another branch/machine). Plus the **daemon** keeps the JVM and a warmed cache in memory between runs. Maven by default runs phases linearly and rebuilds the whole module.\n\nThe price is the build model: a Gradle script is **imperative** (Groovy/Kotlin) — flexible, but \"magic\" creeps in and debugging is harder. Maven is **declarative XML** with a rigid lifecycle: boring and verbose, but predictable and readable by any junior.\n\n- Gradle gotcha: invalid inputs/outputs → a false cache, dirty builds.\n- Maven speeds up via `-T` and `-o`.","quizCorrect":"Incremental build and build cache reuse the outputs of unchanged tasks","quizWrong":["Gradle compiles Java to native code, bypassing the JVM","Gradle skips downloading dependencies entirely","Maven always recreates the JVM per phase while Gradle doesn't, keeping a daemon"],"picCap":"Speed/flexibility vs transparency"},"arch-layered-hex":{"q":"Layered vs hexagonal/Clean — what's the difference?","a":"Layered: UI → service → data, everything flows top-down, and the domain DEPENDS on the DB. Hexagonal/Clean: the domain sits at the center and never reaches outward — it talks through port interfaces, while the DB/API plug in from outside (dependencies point inward). Hex is easier to test and to swap infrastructure.","d":"The key idea is the direction of dependencies. In n-tier the arrow goes UI→Service→DAO→DB, so swapping the DB touches the domain. In Clean the domain declares a port (e.g. `OrderRepository` as an interface), and an adapter (JDBC/REST) implements it from the outside — that's Dependency Inversion. The domain knows nothing about Postgres/Kafka, so in tests the port is replaced with a fake, without spinning up infrastructure. The cost is more interfaces and mapping, justified for a long-lived, complex domain.","hook":"Layered is like the floors of a building: the upper one presses down on the lower. Hexagonal is like a power socket: you plug the device (DB) into a standard port, the core itself stays untouched.","more":"**Layered**: dependencies flow top-down — UI depends on the service, the service on the data layer. The domain ends up importing DB/ORM classes, and it can't be built or tested without infrastructure.\n\n**Hexagonal/Clean** flips this with one rule: dependencies point INWARD, toward the domain. The domain declares **ports** (interfaces), while the DB/API/queues are **adapters** that implement those ports from outside. The domain doesn't know about them.\n\n- **Upside:** you swap infrastructure (Postgres→Mongo) without touching the logic; in tests you replace the port with a mock.\n- **Pitfall:** for a CRUD service these are extra layers — only justified where the domain is complex and long-lived.","quizCorrect":"Outside-in: infrastructure depends on the domain through ports","quizWrong":["Top-down: the domain depends on the DB access layer","Inside-out: the domain calls Postgres and Kafka directly","No dependencies, all layers are fully isolated from each other completely"],"picCap":"Left — dependencies flow downward, the domain bottoms out at the DB. Right — the domain is at the center, everything outside hooks onto it through ports (arrows pointing inward).","note":"Common mistake when implementing the hexagon on JPA: the domain model is annotated @Entity and drags in lazy associations, EntityManager and cascades — so the domain is coupled to the ORM again despite the port. A true hexagon needs a separate persistence entity and manual mapping in the adapter (MapStruct), otherwise you get LazyInitializationException and infrastructure leaking into the domain."},"arch-mono-micro":{"q":"Monolith vs modular monolith vs microservices?","a":"Monolith — all the code in one application, one deployment. Simple to start with, but it grows heavy over time and everything gets tangled. Modular monolith — the same single deployment, but internally there are clear modules with boundaries (often the best starting point). Microservices — many separate services, each with its own DB: teams are independent and you can scale pieces selectively, but you take on the pain of a distributed system (network, consistency).","d":"A boundary = the cost of a call. Monolith: in-process calls are cheap, but boundaries blur easily and refactoring slows down. Modular monolith: the same cheap calls, but module discipline (separate packages/schemas, communication through interfaces) keeps order and gives an easy path to extracting services later. Microservices: a boundary = the network → you need fault tolerance, eventual consistency, distributed transactions/saga, observability, API versioning. Choose microservices for a real need (independent deploy/team scaling), not \"for the future.\"","hook":"A home: a monolith is a studio (everything in one room), a modular monolith is an apartment with rooms, microservices are separate houses each with its own meters.","more":"**Monolith** — one deployment, one process. Quick to start, simple to run/transact, but without discipline the modules grow into each other and releases become risky.\n\n**Modular monolith** — the same single deployment, but with hard module boundaries inside (talk through public APIs, no reaching into each other's tables). Often the best default: you get isolation without the network pain.\n\n**Microservices** — separate processes, a DB per service, independent releases and scaling. The cost: the network is unreliable, consistency is now distributed (sagas, retries), you need DevOps. Take it only when teams/load truly hit a wall.","quizCorrect":"Clear modules, but still one deployment","quizWrong":["Each module has its own separate DB and network","Each module deploys independently","It's just a monolith without any boundaries"],"picCap":"From a single deployment — to independent services each with its own DB","note":"A modular monolith usually fails not because of code but because of a shared DB: modules reach into each other via JOINs on foreign tables, and the boundary exists only on paper. Real isolation requires a schema-per-module and a ban on cross-schema FK/JOIN (or ArchUnit rules on packages) — otherwise when you extract a service you'll find the data is inseparable."},"arch-event-driven":{"q":"Event-driven architecture — when and why?","a":"Services don't call each other directly; instead they throw an \"event\" into a broker (like Kafka): \"order created!\" Whoever cares listens and reacts. The producer doesn't know who reads it. Want a new listener? Just subscribe it — you don't touch the producer.","d":"Coupling is through the fact of \"what happened,\" not the address of \"whom to call.\" This gives loose coupling and asynchrony: the producer doesn't wait for a response and doesn't fail if a subscriber is down. The downsides are real: the end-to-end flow is smeared across services — hard to debug (you need correlation-ids, tracing) and to keep consistent (instead of transactions — eventual consistency, idempotency, handling duplicates and ordering). Use it when there are many subscribers and they change; for a strict synchronous request-response transaction a direct call is simpler.","note":"An event is not \"a call over a queue\": the producer loses control and doesn't know whether it was processed. The main pitfalls are delivery guarantees (at-least-once → duplicates, you need idempotency) and ordering: in Kafka it's guaranteed only within a partition, and people pick the wrong partition key and break the sequence for a single aggregate.","hook":"It's like a PA announcement at an airport: the announcer doesn't call everyone individually — whoever needs it hears it and goes.","more":"The producer writes an **event** (\"OrderCreated\") into a broker (Kafka/RabbitMQ) and forgets about it. Consumers are subscribed and react on their own — the producer doesn't know who or how many. This is **loose coupling** and asynchrony.\n\nYou add a new consumer by subscribing it, without changing the producer. Good for reactive flows, integrations, fan-out.\n\n- **Pitfall:** eventual consistency (data converges, not instantly), harder debugging/tracing, you need idempotency — events may arrive again or out of order.","quizCorrect":"New subscribers are added without changing the producer","quizWrong":["Guarantees instant strict data consistency","Simplifies debugging the end-to-end request flow","The producer always waits for a response from all subscribers"],"picCap":"The fact goes to the broker (runs along the arrow) → each subscriber fires in turn, independently"},"arch-12factor":{"q":"12-factor app — the key principles?","a":"12 rules to let an app live in the cloud without pain. The main ones: config in environment variables (not in code), the app remembers nothing between requests (stateless), it streams logs to stdout. Then it's easy to copy, scale, and move.","d":"The key ones out of the 12: 1) one codebase, many deploys; 2) explicitly declared dependencies (pom/package.json, not \"it's installed on the machine\"); 3) config in env, not in code — the same image rides to dev/stage/prod, only the variables change; 4) backing services (DB, queue, cache) are attachable resources addressed by URL; 5) stateless processes: state lives in the DB/Redis, not in instance memory → you can kill one and bring up a new one; 6) logs as an event stream to stdout, the environment handles aggregation; 7) dev/prod parity — minimal differences between environments. This is what enables horizontal scaling: run N identical copies behind a load balancer.","hook":"Like fast-food workers: all interchangeable, instructions on the wall (env), they carry no memory with them — everything's in the shared storeroom (DB).","more":"A set of rules for apps that are easy to run in the cloud and scale horizontally. The key ones:\n\n- **Config in the environment** (env vars), not in code/files — one image for dev/prod.\n- **Stateless processes:** they store nothing between requests, state lives in an external DB/cache. That's why an instance can be killed and a new one brought up.\n- **Logs to stdout** as an event stream — the platform collects them.\n\nAlso: explicit dependencies, dev/prod parity, fast startup/shutdown (disposability). The gist: the app is a replaceable, copyable process with no hidden local state.","quizCorrect":"One image rides to dev/prod, only the variables change","quizWrong":["This makes the code run faster in prod","Environment variables are encrypted automatically by the platform","It reduces the size of the built image"],"picCap":"Config from env → identical stateless copies → logs streamed to stdout. State is pushed out to the DB.","note":"Stateless refers to process memory, but the HTTP session is often hidden state: the default in-memory HttpSession in Spring pins a user to one instance and breaks on scaling/restart. You need either externalized sessions (Spring Session + Redis) or stateless JWT — otherwise sticky sessions defeat horizontal scaling."},"arch-api-gateway":{"q":"API Gateway — why?","a":"An API Gateway is a single door in front of a bunch of microservices. The client knocks only on it, and it decides: check the entrant (authorization), is it knocking too often (rate limit), where to route the request (routing), and sometimes it assembles a response from several services at once. The client doesn't know the internal services' addresses. Downside: if the door breaks — everything stops.","d":"What a gateway typically does: 1) routing (/orders → order-service); 2) authentication/authorization in one place (validating JWT/OIDC), services trust the gateway; 3) rate limiting and throttling; 4) aggregation — assemble a response from 2-3 services into one (the BFF pattern); 5) API versioning (/v1, /v2) without changing the client; 6) TLS termination, logs, metrics. The main risk is a single point of failure and a bottleneck: treated by scaling the gateway horizontally (it's stateless) + health checks. Don't turn the gateway into a monolith: business logic lives in the services, the gateway is only about cross-cutting concerns. Examples: Kong, Spring Cloud Gateway, Nginx, AWS API Gateway.","note":"A gateway easily turns into a God Object: people drag business logic, aggregation, and transformations into it, and it becomes a bottleneck and a point of failure. Keep it thin (auth, routing, rate limit), and push orchestration out to a BFF/services.","hook":"A gateway is the reception desk in an office: visitors don't wander into the rooms, security checks the pass and walks you to the right person.","more":"A single entry point in front of a swarm of microservices. The client knows only its address; the gateway handles **cross-cutting concerns**: authentication/authorization, rate limiting, path-based routing, TLS termination, sometimes aggregating responses from several services.\n\nIt pulls shared logic out of each service and hides the internal topology — services can be reshuffled and the client won't notice.\n\n- **Pitfall:** it's a single point of failure and a potential bottleneck — you need HA and scaling. Don't stuff business logic into the gateway, or it bloats into a new monolith.","quizCorrect":"It can become a bottleneck and a point of failure for the whole system","quizWrong":["The client has to know the addresses of all internal services in advance","Microservices can't talk to each other directly","API versioning through the gateway is impossible"],"picCap":"The request travels from the client to the gateway → the gateway validates and routes it onward to the internal services"},"arch-bff":{"q":"Backend for Frontend (BFF) — the idea?","a":"A BFF is a separate backend for each type of client. Web screens need one set of data, mobile needs another. Instead of one \"one-size-fits-all\" API, we build a dedicated layer for web and for mobile: each assembles exactly what its screens need. The client doesn't pull excess and doesn't make 10 requests.","d":"A single shared API is always a compromise: it either returns excess fields (over-fetch), or the client hits several endpoints and stitches them together itself (under-fetch). A BFF inverts the dependency: the backend knows the client's screens and tailors the response to them, aggregating calls to downstream services. The downside — duplicated logic across BFFs and a growing number of services; a BFF is owned by the frontend team. An alternative to the same problem is GraphQL, where the client picks the fields itself.","hook":"Like a waiter per room: one room has a kids' menu, another a banquet one; the kitchen is the same, the serving differs.","more":"A separate backend layer per client type: one BFF for web, another for mobile (and so on). Each assembles and formats exactly the data its screens need, calling the internal services.\n\nWhy: web and mobile have different needs (mobile cares about traffic and few requests). A shared \"API for everyone\" gets covered in conditionals and compromises — a BFF removes that, and the frontend gets a ready response in a single call.\n\n- **Pitfall:** duplicated logic across BFFs; they're usually owned by the frontend team. Not needed if there's a single client — then it's just an extra layer.","quizCorrect":"Each one assembles data exactly for its client's screens","quizWrong":["So clients don't know the backend addresses (security) from clients","To swap the database on each client","So one service serves all clients identically"],"picCap":"Each client → its own BFF → shared services. The response is tailored to the screen.","note":"A BFF easily degenerates into a distributed God-object: business logic creeps into the aggregation layer and the BFF becomes a de-facto monolithic facade over all services. Keep the BFF thin (only screen-shaped aggregation/transformation) and leave domain decisions in downstream services — otherwise the frontend team unwittingly owns business rules."},"arch-service-discovery":{"q":"Service discovery — why?","a":"On startup, services register themselves in a shared \"phone book\" (a registry). A client asks: \"where's payment-service?\" — the registry hands back live addresses. No need to change IPs: instances die and come back with new addresses, while the name stays the same.","d":"Eureka/Consul keep a list of instances and send heartbeats: an instance dies — it drops out of the results. Discovery comes in client-side (the client picks the instance itself) and server-side (the choice is made by a load balancer/proxy). In Kubernetes the registry's role is played by built-in DNS + Service: you address by the service name, and kube-proxy/endpoints resolve the live pods.","hook":"Like calling directory assistance: you don't remember the pizzeria's number — you give the name, they give you the current number. They moved — the directory already knows the new one.","more":"On startup an instance registers in a **registry** (Consul, Eureka, etcd), and the client asks the registry \"where's payment-service?\" and gets a list of live addresses. The registry kicks out dead ones via health checks.\n\nIt solves the problem of dynamism: in the cloud/k8s instances constantly die and come back with new IPs — you can't hardcode addresses. The name is stable, the addresses change.\n\n- **Client-side**: the client picks the instance itself (balances). **Server-side**: a load balancer/DNS does it for you. In Kubernetes discovery is built in via Service + DNS, a separate registry is often not needed.","quizCorrect":"Instance addresses change dynamically, so you need lookup by name","quizWrong":["To encrypt traffic between services","To store services' data instead of a database of their own behind them","To speed up query execution inside a service"],"picCap":"The client asks the registry for a name — it returns only the live instances","note":"Client-side discovery with a registry cache creates a staleness window: an instance is already dead, but the client keeps hitting the old address for a few seconds until the cache/heartbeat TTL expires. So discovery does not replace retry + circuit breaker; in Eureka this is worsened by self-preservation mode, which during network issues deliberately does NOT evict 'dead' instances."},"arch-service-mesh":{"q":"Service mesh / sidecar — what does it solve?","a":"A small proxy \"helper\" (sidecar) is placed next to each service. All network traffic goes through it: it encrypts (mTLS), retries on failure, sets timeouts, splits load, and collects traces. You don't touch the service's code — the network is now the proxy's concern.","d":"A sidecar is a separate container in the same pod as the service (the \"sidecar\" pattern). All sidecars together = the data plane; they're managed by the control plane (Istio Pilot/istiod), which distributes config and certificates. The service talks to localhost — Envoy intercepts the traffic and decides where and how to send it. Upsides: a single security and observability policy, zero code changes, any language. Downsides: +latency per hop, increased CPU/RAM usage (a proxy per pod), operational complexity. The 2024+ trend is ambient mesh (Istio) without sidecars, to remove the overhead.","hook":"A sidecar is like a personal bodyguard next to each person: it encrypts the correspondence itself, calls back if it couldn't get through, and you just talk.","more":"A **sidecar** proxy (Envoy) is placed next to each service, and all network traffic goes through it. The proxy takes on networking concerns: **mTLS** encryption, retries, timeouts, circuit breaker, load balancing, tracing, and metrics.\n\nThe logic is moved out of code into infrastructure — uniform behavior for services in any language, with no edits or rebuilds. Managed centrally (control plane, e.g. Istio).\n\n- **Pitfall:** noticeable complexity and operational overhead, +1 hop of latency. Justified with dozens of services; for a couple it's overkill.","quizCorrect":"Move network logic (mTLS, retries, timeouts) out of the service's code","quizWrong":["Store the service's data instead of a separate DB","Replace the service's business code with something off-the-shelf from the shelf","Compile the service into a single binary for deployment"],"picCap":"Traffic goes code → sidecar → network → sidecar → code. All the mTLS/retries live in the proxy, not in the code.","note":"The sidecar adds a non-obvious startup race in the pod: the app container can start before Envoy is ready, so the first outbound requests fail (and on shutdown the app dies while the sidecar still holds connections). Fixed via holdApplicationUntilProxyStarts / native sidecar containers in Kubernetes 1.28+ and proper SIGTERM ordering — otherwise you get flaky errors only during deploys."},"arch-db-per-service":{"q":"Database per service vs a shared database?","a":"In microservices each service has its own DB. Another service touches the data only through its API, not directly in the tables. This gives autonomy: you can change the schema and deploy independently without breaking anyone. A shared DB for everyone is hidden coupling: change a column — the neighbors fall over. For microservices it's an anti-pattern.","d":"The pattern is called Database per Service. Another service doesn't know its neighbor's tables — only its contract (REST/gRPC/events). Upsides: independent deploy, a DB technology suited to the task, isolation of failures and load, clear data ownership boundaries. Downside: the data is spread out, so there are no distributed transactions and no JOINs across services — consistency is achieved via Saga/events (eventual consistency), and aggregate queries via API composition or a CQRS read model. A Shared Database is acceptable only as a temporary step when carving up a monolith, but that's no longer true microservice autonomy.","note":"In practice \"own DB\" breaks down on reports and joins across service boundaries: cross-service queries are done via API composition or a CQRS read-model, not via a direct join. And the most common hidden anti-pattern is a shared DB with different schemas but one instance: formally separate, actually coupled through resources and migrations.","hook":"Your own DB is like a personal fridge: you don't reach into the neighbor's shelf, you knock on the door (API). A shared fridge — someone rearranged the shelves, and everything fell out for everyone.","more":"Each service owns its DB, and the outside reaches it only through its API — not directly into the tables. This gives **autonomy**: you change the schema and deploy without breaking neighbors, and you can pick the right DB type for the task.\n\nA **shared DB** for everyone is hidden coupling: another service is tied to your columns, any schema change risks breaking everyone, and independent deploy dies. For microservices it's an anti-pattern.\n\n- **The cost of your own DB:** no joins or ACID transactions across services — you need sagas, data replication, eventual consistency.","quizCorrect":"One service changes the schema — everyone who read those tables directly breaks","quizWrong":["A shared DB physically can't handle the load of several services at the same time","You can't write to one DB from two applications at the same time","SQL queries to a shared DB run slower than to separate ones"],"picCap":"Each has its own DB + access only through the API. A shared DB = hidden coupling."},"arch-saga-orch-choreo":{"q":"Saga: orchestration vs choreography?","a":"A saga is a long transaction made of steps, each with a \"rollback\" (compensation). Orchestration: one conductor commands who does what and what to roll back — the whole flow is visible, but it's a single point of failure and everything is tied to it. Choreography: services react to each other's events on their own — no center, loose coupling, but the overall flow is smeared and harder to trace.","d":"A saga solves the distributed-transaction problem without 2PC: instead of one ACID commit — a chain of local transactions, and on failure at step N the compensations of steps N-1…1 run (a semantic undo, not a rollback).\n\nOrchestration: a separate orchestrator service (often on a BPMN engine — Camunda/Zeebe, as in orders-service) holds the saga's state and explicitly invokes participants (commands) and their compensations. Upsides: the flow logic is in one place, easy to add a step, clear timeouts/retries. Downsides: the orchestrator knows about everyone (coupling), it's one more service to operate.\n\nChoreography: participants communicate via events in a broker (Kafka). Service A publishes \"OrderCreated\" → B reacts, does its thing, publishes \"PaymentDone\" → C, and so on. Upsides: zero coupling, no center. Downsides: there's no single place where the whole scenario is visible; risk of event loops; debugging is saved only by correlation-ids and tracing.\n\nIn practice: short sagas of 2-3 steps — choreography; a complex multi-step business process with branching — orchestration.","note":"The main pitfall of a saga is that compensations don't undo like a DB rollback: the side effects already happened (the email went out, the money was debited), the compensation only performs the inverse action. Plus you need idempotent steps and a semantic lock — otherwise during parallel sagas, data in an intermediate state gets read as valid.","hook":"Orchestration = a conductor waves the baton at each musician. Choreography = a round dance: the neighbor moved — you moved, no conductor.","more":"A **saga** replaces a distributed transaction with a chain of local steps; each has a **compensation** (undo) in case of a failure further down the chain.\n\n**Orchestration:** a central coordinator commands the steps and triggers compensations. The flow is visible in one place, easier to debug — but it's a single point of failure and the services are tied to the orchestrator.\n\n**Choreography:** services react to each other's events, no center — coupling is looser and there's no single point of failure, but the overall flow is smeared across services, hard to see and to trace.\n\n- **Rule:** few steps → choreography; a complex flow with branching → orchestration.","quizCorrect":"Orchestration — a central coordinator; choreography — reacting to events","quizWrong":["Orchestration without compensations, choreography with compensations only","Orchestration is only synchronous, choreography only via the DB","Orchestration doesn't need a broker, choreography requires 2PC"],"picCap":"Top: a center commands A/B/C. Bottom: events pass down the chain without a center."},"arch-event-sourcing":{"q":"Event sourcing — what and why?","a":"We don't store \"how much is in the account now.\" We store a list of facts: +100, -30, +50. The current balance = sum all the facts from the start. Upside — you see the whole history and can \"rewind\" back. Downside — it's more complex and slower, so sometimes a checkpoint is saved.","d":"The source of truth is an append-only event log (OrderCreated, ItemAdded, OrderPaid); events can't be changed or deleted. State is a derivative: replay the events through a reducer. Speed: a periodic snapshot (state at version N) + replay only the tail after it. The pain is schema evolution: old events stay in the log forever, so events are versioned and upcasters are written (migrating the old format to the new on the fly). It often goes together with CQRS: we write events, but read from separate projections (a read-model). Don't confuse event sourcing (state FROM events) with ordinary publishing of events to Kafka (events as a side effect on top of state). Suited where audit and history matter (finance, orders), overkill for simple CRUD.","note":"The main pain is event versioning: old events in the store can't be changed, while the schema evolves, so you need upcasters. And never trigger external side effects during replay (sending an email, a payment) — replay will run them again; side effects go into projections/a process manager.","hook":"Like a bank statement: what matters isn't the balance figure but the lines of operations — the balance can always be recomputed from them.","more":"**Under the hood:** state isn't stored directly. **Events** are written to a log (append-only) — immutable facts of \"what happened\": `Deposited 100`, `Withdrew 30`. The current state = a **fold** of all the events in order. To avoid recomputing from scratch, **snapshots** (checkpoints) are saved and only the tail is replayed.\n\n**Why so:** you get a full audit log, time-travel (state at any moment), and a convenient source for projections/CQRS.\n\n**Pitfall:** the event schema is forever (you need versioning), you can't \"just fix\" a row, the log volume grows. Used where history matters: finance, audit, orders.","quizCorrect":"A sequence of events; state is computed by replaying them","quizWrong":["The current state in a table; events are just logs for debugging","A snapshot of state; events are deleted after being applied","A read-model in a cache; events are generated from it"],"picCap":"We store events, not the total. We get the current state by replaying the events in order."},"arch-replication":{"q":"Replication: synchronous vs asynchronous?","a":"Data has copies on other servers (replicas). Synchronous: the master waits for \"written!\" from the replica — reliable but slower. Asynchronous: the master doesn't wait — fast, but the replica lags a bit, and you can read stale data from it. Replicas take on reads, offloading the master.","d":"Semi-synchronous is a compromise: the master waits for confirmation from at least one replica, not all of them. With synchronous replication, if a replica stalls or goes down, the master itself can hang. With asynchronous, there's a risk of losing the latest transactions if the master dies before they reach the replica. Reading from a replica = \"eventual consistency\": the data will converge, but not this very second (replication lag).","note":"An async replica gives a read-your-writes anomaly: a user writes and immediately reads the old value from the slave. Treated by routing critical reads to the master or by sticky reads. Another pitfall — replication lag grows under load unnoticed, until someone reads stale data.","hook":"Synchronous — you call the courier and wait for \"delivered.\" Asynchronous — you toss the parcel and walk on, it'll get there on its own.","more":"**Under the hood:** the master's changes are propagated to the replicas. **Synchronous** — the master waits for confirmation from at least one replica before answering the client: data isn't lost on master failure, but latency grows and with an unavailable replica writes block. **Asynchronous** — the master answers immediately, the replicas catch up later.\n\n**Why so:** it's a reliability vs speed trade-off. Often people take semi-synchronous: wait for one replica, the rest asynchronously.\n\n**Pitfall:** with asynchronous — **replication lag**: reading from a replica returns stale data, and on master failure un-replicated commits are lost.","quizCorrect":"You can read stale data from a replica (it lags behind)","quizWrong":["The master runs slower than with synchronous","Replicas can't be used for reads","Data on replicas is always instantly up to date after a write"],"picCap":"Data runs from the master to the replicas. Synchronous waits for OK; asynchronous doesn't wait and lags behind."},"arch-sharding":{"q":"Sharding — how and why?","a":"When there's too much data for one server, you split the table by a key (for example, by client_id) into pieces — shards. Each shard is a separate server with its own portion of the data. Writes and volume are divided among them → you can grow out, adding servers, instead of one giant one.","d":"The shard key is critical: it's used to compute which shard a row lives on (a hash of the key or a range). A bad key → skew (one shard is full, others empty) and hot spots. The pain: rebalancing when adding a shard (consistent hashing softens it), and cross-shard queries/JOINs — the data is on different nodes, so you either denormalize or assemble the result at the application level. Sharding scales WRITES and volume; for scaling READS replicas are usually enough.","note":"The pain isn't sharding itself but the choice of key: a bad key gives a hot shard (everything lands on one) and cross-shard queries/joins that are nearly impossible. Resharding a live system is a separate hell, so the key is chosen with headroom upfront and often with consistent hashing.","hook":"A cabinet with folders A–H, I–P, Q–Z: you search by the first letter. Fast, until you need to merge folders from different drawers.","more":"**Under the hood:** data is split by a **shard key** (e.g. `client_id`). Strategies: by range, by **hash** of the key (more even distribution), or by a lookup table. A router uses the key to determine the right shard — each holds its own piece of data and its own load.\n\n**Why so:** one server hits a disk/RAM/IOPS wall. Sharding gives **horizontal** scaling: more data and writes — you just add servers.\n\n**Pitfall:** queries and JOINs across shards are expensive, transactions get harder. A bad key → **hot shard** (skew). Resharding is painful. Key choice is critical.","quizCorrect":"Writes and data volume","quizWrong":["Read speed only","Reliability through copies of data","The complexity of cross-shard JOINs"],"picCap":"The key's hash is computed and routes the request (id=42) straight to its shard"},"arch-read-write-split":{"q":"Read/write splitting?","a":"You can only write to the master. And you read from its copies (replicas). That way the master doesn't drown in read requests. But the copies update a bit later: you write — and right away on a replica you may still see old data.","d":"The master replicates changes to the replicas asynchronously (replication), hence the lag — that's eventual consistency. Reads scale horizontally: add a replica — read capacity grows. Writes don't scale this way, there's one master. If you need to read exactly what you just wrote — read from the master (read-your-writes) or wait for sync.","hook":"The cashier (master) takes the money, while their assistants issue receipts from a copy of the ledger — but the write into the copy doesn't arrive instantly.","more":"**Under the hood:** all writes go to the **master**, it replicates the data to replicas; the app routes SELECTs to the replicas. That's how reads scale — add replicas, offload the master. Often a proxy does this (ProxySQL, pgpool).\n\n**Why so:** in typical apps reads vastly outnumber writes, and one master can't handle them.\n\n**Pitfall:** **replication lag** breaks \"read-your-writes\" — you write and immediately read stale. Treated by: critical reads — from the master, or \"sticking\" to the master for N seconds after a write. It doesn't scale writes themselves — for that you need sharding.","quizCorrect":"To take the read load off the master and scale reads","quizWrong":["So data on replicas is always fresher than the master","So writes become strictly consistent with no delays","To remove the master and write straight to all replicas"],"picCap":"The write goes to the master → the copy spreads across the replicas → reads come from the replicas (with lag)","note":"Pinning to the master for N seconds after a write does not help when reads come from a different app instance or after a redeploy — the read-your-writes marker must live in the client session, not in a node's memory; in Postgres it is more robust to compare LSN: remember pg_current_wal_lsn() after the write and read from a replica only once its pg_last_wal_replay_lsn() has caught up."},"arch-caching-strategies":{"q":"Caching strategies — what are they?","a":"A cache is a fast box in front of the DB. 4 ways to keep it fresh: cache-aside (the app fills it on a miss), read-through (the cache pulls from the DB itself), write-through (write to the cache and DB at once — fresh but slow), write-behind (to the cache now, to the DB later — fast but a loss risk).","d":"Reads: cache-aside and read-through. In cache-aside the logic \"on a miss, go to the DB and fill the cache\" is written by the app itself — the cache knows nothing about the DB. In read-through the cache itself can load (via a provider/loader), the app always asks only the cache. Writes: write-through keeps the cache and DB in sync (consistency), but you pay latency on every write. Write-behind (a.k.a. write-back) acknowledges the write immediately and flushes to the DB in a batch/on a timer — high write speed, but if it crashes before the flush the data is lost, plus a window of inconsistency. They're often combined: read-through + write-behind.","note":"Cache-aside classically catches a race condition on invalidation: one thread reads the old value from the DB and puts it in the cache right after another thread updated the DB and deleted the key — the cache freezes with stale data. From the same root come the thundering herd on mass TTL expiry and the cache stampede on a popular key.","hook":"Cache-aside — you run to the warehouse for the item yourself. Write-through — you put it on the shelf AND into the books at once. Write-behind — on the shelf now, into the books in the evening.","more":"**Under the hood:** the cache (Redis/Memcached) is a fast layer in front of the DB. **Cache-aside**: on a miss the app reads from the DB itself and fills the cache. **Read-through**: reads go through the cache, it loads on its own. **Write-through**: write synchronously to the cache and DB — fresh, but slower on writes. **Write-behind**: write to the cache, to the DB asynchronously — fast, but on a crash the data can be lost.\n\n**Why so:** a different balance of freshness/speed/risk.\n\n**Pitfall:** invalidation and **stale data**, you need a TTL, protection against a **cache stampede** on mass expiry. Most often cache-aside is taken as the default.","quizCorrect":"Write-behind (write-back)","quizWrong":["Write-through on every write to the cache","Read-through","Cache-aside"],"picCap":"A read runs App → Cache → (miss) DB. A write goes through the cache: straight to the DB (through) or deferred (behind)."},"arch-cdn":{"q":"CDN — why?","a":"A CDN is a network of servers around the world. Images, js, css, video live not only on your main server (origin) but are copied to nodes close to the user. A person in Almaty downloads from a server in Almaty, not from the US. The result: it loads faster and the load on the origin drops.","d":"An edge node caches the origin's response for a TTL and serves it itself for repeat requests — the origin is touched only on a cache miss or expiry. The benefit: lower latency (a shorter path to the user), offloading the origin and the channel, resilience to traffic spikes. Good for static/immutable assets (versioned files, a hash in the name); dynamic and private data is usually not cached or Cache-Control is configured carefully.","hook":"A CDN is a network of \"corner stores\": bread from the central plant is distributed to the districts, and you go to the nearest one.","more":"**Under the hood:** static assets (images, JS, CSS, video) are served by **edge nodes** around the world. DNS/anycast routes the user to the nearest node. If there's a cache miss there — the node pulls the file from the **origin**, caches it per the TTL, and serves it to the next ones. Freshness is governed by the `Cache-Control`/`ETag` headers.\n\n**Why so:** a shorter path to the user → less **latency**; most requests are absorbed at the edge → offloading the origin and protection against spikes/DDoS.\n\n**Pitfall:** invalidation on release (you need **cache busting** — a hash in the file name); dynamic and private data can't be cached without care; you pay for traffic.","quizCorrect":"Serve static assets from a node near the user: less latency and less load on the origin","quizWrong":["Encrypt traffic between client and server instead of TLS between the two endpoints entirely","Balance writes to the main DB across regions","Keep a single copy of files on one powerful server"],"picCap":"Content flows from the origin → spreads to the edge nodes → is served to nearby users","note":"Vary: Cookie or a stray Set-Cookie header on static assets destroys CDN efficiency — the edge keys on cookies, so they either shatter the cache to zero or (worse) the CDN caches a private response with someone's session and serves it to everyone; for assets strip cookies and Vary aggressively, and mark private content with Cache-Control: private/no-store."},"arch-load-balancing":{"q":"Load balancing — how does it distribute?","a":"A load balancer sits in front of a group of identical servers and decides who gets each request. Round-robin — in turn, in a circle. Least-connections — to whoever is least busy right now. Hash/sticky — the same client always to its own server (so the session isn't lost). And a health-check constantly pings the servers and removes the dead ones from the list.","d":"Round-robin is simple and good when requests are roughly equal in weight. Least-connections is more accurate with varying request durations (some fast, some hanging). Sticky/hash balancing is needed when session state lives on a specific instance; the ideal is to make instances stateless (sessions in Redis), then no affinity is needed and any request can go anywhere. Health-checks come in passive (watch errors on real traffic) and active (separately ping /health); a failed instance is taken out of rotation and returned after several successful checks.","hook":"A restaurant hostess: seats guests at tables in turn or with a free waiter, and removes a broken table.","more":"**Under the hood:** the LB (L4 — by TCP, or L7 — by HTTP) sits in front of a pool of identical servers and distributes requests. **Round-robin** — in a circle; **least-connections** — to the least loaded (better for requests of varying length); **hash/sticky** — a client always to one server (sessions). A **health-check** polls the backends and takes failed ones out of rotation.\n\n**Why so:** even load + fault tolerance with no downtime.\n\n**Pitfall:** sticky breaks evenness and hinders draining nodes; the LB itself is a point of failure (you need a pair + failover); session state is better pushed out (Redis) so servers are stateless.","quizCorrect":"So one client always lands on the same instance (for the session)","quizWrong":["So requests go strictly in turn, in a circle","To send the request to the least loaded server","To automatically take failed instances out of rotation by a health check"],"picCap":"The LB spreads requests across the live instances; the failed app3 was taken out of rotation by the health-check","note":"Behind an L7 load balancer (nginx/ALB) the backends see the LB's own IP, not the client's — without X-Forwarded-For and a trusted-proxy list, geo, rate-limiting and audit break; and since XFF is forgeable by the client, you may only trust the value appended by your own LB, not whatever arrived from outside."},"arch-rate-limiting":{"q":"Rate limiting: token bucket vs leaky bucket?","a":"Both limit request frequency, protecting the server from overload and abuse. Token bucket: tokens accumulate — a short BURST is let through while there are tokens. Leaky bucket: requests leak out at EXACTLY a fixed rate — bursts are smoothed into an even stream.","d":"Token bucket: a capacity of B tokens, refill of R tokens/sec. A request takes a token; no tokens — rejection (429). Allows a burst up to B at once. Leaky bucket: a fixed-size queue, processing strictly at R/sec; queue overflow — rejection. TB matters when the AVERAGE rate + an allowed burst matter (more popular in APIs: nginx, Stripe). LB matters when an EVEN output rate matters (traffic shaping, protecting downstream from spikes). In essence TB is an inbound limit with a burst buffer, LB is output stabilization.","note":"A naive fixed-window limiter lets through 2x the limit at the window boundary (a burst at the end of one window + the start of the next) — so in prod people take a sliding window or token bucket. In a distributed system the counter must be atomic (Redis INCR/Lua), otherwise a race zeroes out the limit.","hook":"Token bucket — a piggy bank of tokens at a turnstile: saved up — you pass with the crowd at once. Leaky bucket — a bucket with a hole: no matter how much you pour, it drips out one drop at a time.","more":"**Under the hood:** **Token bucket** — tokens drip in at a constant rate, a request spends a token; accumulated tokens allow a short **burst**, an empty bucket = rejection/wait. **Leaky bucket** — requests in a queue \"leak out\" at a fixed pace, evening out the stream; queue overflow = drop.\n\n**Why so:** token bucket is more flexible (tolerates bursts while limiting the average rate), leaky bucket gives a **smooth** even output to downstream.\n\n**Pitfall:** in a distributed system you need a shared counter (Redis) — otherwise the limit is multiplied across instances; mind the time unit and what to return to the client (`429` + `Retry-After`).","quizCorrect":"Token bucket allows short bursts, leaky bucket emits an even stream","quizWrong":["Token bucket is faster because it stores no state between requests at all","Leaky bucket allows bursts, token bucket doesn't","They're identical, differing only in name"],"picCap":"TB spends accumulated tokens and lets a burst through in a batch; LB releases an even stream through the hole"},"arch-consistency-models":{"q":"Strong vs eventual consistency?","a":"Strong: you write — and everyone immediately reads the new value, but the system waits for all to agree (slower, costlier). Eventual: you write — and someone still sees the old value for a few moments, but it's fast and works even during failures. You choose by what matters more: accuracy or speed/availability.","d":"The link to CAP/PACELC: during a network partition strong consistency sacrifices availability (CP), eventual chooses availability (AP). Even without failures (Else) strong pays latency for coordination (quorums, Raft/Paxos consensus), eventual returns an answer from the nearest replica. Money/account balances → strong. Likes, view counters, a feed → eventual is fine.","note":"\"Eventual\" doesn't mean \"soon\" — there's no guarantee on convergence time, and without read-your-writes/monotonic-reads a user sees now the new, now the old value, jumping back and forth. Strong consistency under CAP sacrifices availability during a partition — under a partition the nodes simply refuse the write.","hook":"Strong — all waiters carry one menu at once. Eventual — the new menu reaches the rooms one by one.","more":"**Under the hood:** **strong** consistency — after a write any read sees the new value; for that the nodes synchronize (quorum/locks), which adds latency. **Eventual** — replicas converge \"over time,\" for some window they return the old value, conflicts are resolved (last-write-wins, versions/CRDT).\n\n**Why so:** under **CAP**, during a network partition you choose between consistency and availability — eventual sacrifices freshness for speed and operation during failures.\n\n**Pitfall:** strong — costlier and vulnerable to a network partition; eventual — the app must tolerate a stale read. Money/balances → strong; likes, feeds, carts → eventual.","quizCorrect":"Replicas converge over time, a reader may temporarily see the old value","quizWrong":["Data is never synchronized between replicas","All readers always see the latest write instantly","A write is acknowledged only after all nodes agree among themselves on the value"],"picCap":"One write, two read models: strong — all replicas already agree; eventual — one lags but will catch up."},"arch-consensus":{"q":"Consensus (Raft) — how do nodes agree?","a":"The nodes elect one chief — a LEADER. It records changes and broadcasts them to the rest. A change is \"accepted\" only when a MAJORITY of nodes have recorded it (a quorum). So even if some nodes go down, everyone sees the same truth.","d":"A quorum = more than half. Out of 5 nodes you need 3 — we survive 2 going down. The leader went silent — the nodes start an election on timeout and vote for a new one (only a node whose log is no older gives a vote). A majority prevents two leaders from appearing and doesn't lose a confirmed write. Nodes are taken in odd counts (3,5,7): 4 tolerates the same failures as 3 but requires more agreement.","hook":"A board of directors: a decision stands when a majority voted \"yes\" — a couple of absentees don't block it.","more":"**Under the hood:** **Raft** divides time into terms and elects a **leader**; client writes go only to it. The leader appends the entry to its log and replicates it to followers; the entry **commits** when a **majority** (quorum) has confirmed it. On leader silence a follower initiates a new election on timeout.\n\n**Why so:** a quorum guarantees a single source of truth and survival of a minority of nodes failing — everyone sees the same ordered log.\n\n**Pitfall:** you need an **odd** number of nodes (3/5), otherwise there's a risk of **split-brain**; on loss of quorum the cluster is unavailable for writes; the leader is a write bottleneck. It's about consistency, not read scaling.","quizCorrect":"When a majority of nodes have confirmed it (a quorum)","quizWrong":["When the leader has written it to its disk","When ALL nodes without exception have confirmed it it in writing","When at least any one node has confirmed it"],"picCap":"The leader sends the log; the entry is accepted when a majority confirmed it (3 of 4) — even with a node down.","note":"A linearizable read cannot just be taken from the leader: an old leader may have been deposed yet still believe it is leader (stale read) — Raft requires the leader to confirm leadership via a fresh heartbeat-quorum round (ReadIndex) or wait for a no-op entry of its own term to commit before answering; otherwise the client reads stale data during a network partition."},"arch-distributed-lock":{"q":"Distributed lock — what are the pitfalls?","a":"One of many processes grabs a \"key\" in Redis/ZooKeeper to touch a resource alone. A TTL is set so the key releases itself if the process dies. But if the work runs longer than the TTL, the key expires, a second process also enters, and now there are two of them. It's often simpler to make the operation itself idempotent: a repeat doesn't break anything.","d":"Pitfalls: (1) the TTL expired while the process was still working (GC pause, slow I/O) → the lock got \"stolen\", two writers run at once. (2) Split-brain: the network partitioned, a Redis node thinks the lock is free and hands it to another. Cures: a fencing token (a monotonic number, the resource rejects an \"old\" lock), Redlock with caveats, but the honest fix is idempotency: a dedup key / INSERT ... ON CONFLICT / UPSERT, so a repeat is safe and a race doesn't corrupt data. The lock lowers the probability, idempotency removes the consequences.","note":"A Redis lock on a single instance isn't safe under failover: the lock replicates asynchronously, the master dies before replication — and a second process takes the same lock. Redlock is also criticized (Kleppmann): a GC pause or network delay longer than the TTL → the process thinks it holds the lock, but it has already been handed off. What saves you isn't the lock, but a fencing token + idempotency.","hook":"A single toilet stall with a timed key: the key drops out by itself after 5 min — and you're still inside. The door is opened for the next person. It's safer to make sure that \"two entered at once\" doesn't break anything.","more":"**Under the hood:** the process does an atomic `SET key val NX PX <ttl>` in Redis (or an ephemeral node in ZooKeeper). NX = \"only if the key doesn't exist\", so there's exactly one winner. The TTL is needed so the lock releases itself if the owner dies and never gets to run DEL.\n\n**Pitfalls:** the TTL is a bet on how long the work takes. If the operation drags on longer than the TTL, the key expires, a second process enters — and now there are two of them (mutual exclusion violated). Extending the TTL (a watchdog) partially helps, but not against GC/network pauses.\n\n**When to use:** better not to rely on the lock as a correctness guarantee, but to make the operation itself **idempotent** (fencing token, unique key) — then a double entry doesn't corrupt data.","quizCorrect":"The work can run longer than the TTL — the lock expires and a second process enters","quizWrong":["A TTL always guarantees a single writer, races never happen under any clock skew whatsoever","Redis can't store keys with expiration","Without internet a lock works more reliably than with it"],"picCap":"The lock expired during the work → two in the resource. Idempotency saves you from the consequences."},"arch-2pc-vs-saga":{"q":"2PC vs Saga — for a distributed transaction?","a":"2PC: one coordinator asks everyone \"ready?\", everyone freezes and waits — atomic, but if the coordinator dies, everyone stalls with locked resources. Saga: you do steps one at a time, each commits immediately; if something breaks — you roll back by hand (compensations). It doesn't block, but others see a half-finished state.","d":"2PC (two-phase commit): phase 1 prepare — the coordinator asks all participants to reserve and say \"ready\"; phase 2 commit/abort — if all said \"yes\", the command to commit. While the vote is in progress, rows/locks are held → it scales poorly, and a coordinator failure between phases leaves resources locked (in-doubt). Saga: a business transaction = a sequence of local transactions T1..Tn, each commits immediately; on a failure of step k, compensations C(k-1)..C1 run. There's no isolation — you need protection against dirty read/lost update (semantic locks, versions, idempotency). Coordination: orchestration (a central saga orchestrator) or choreography (events). In microservices Saga is usually chosen precisely because of the absence of locks between services.","note":"2PC locks resources for the whole protocol and doesn't survive a coordinator failure in the commit phase (in-doubt transactions hang with locks) — that's why it's almost never used in microservices. Saga instead sacrifices isolation: intermediate states are visible to others, and you need semantic locks and idempotent compensations.","hook":"2PC — the waiter waits for \"everyone ready?\", the table is frozen. Saga — you bring dishes one at a time, wrong one — you take it back.","more":"**2PC:** the coordinator sends everyone prepare (\"ready to commit?\"), participants lock resources and answer yes/no, then commit/abort. Atomic and consistent, but **synchronous and blocking**: if the coordinator dies between phases, participants hang with locked rows.\n\n**Saga:** the transaction is split into local steps, each commits immediately. On failure, **compensations** run in reverse order (cancel the booking, refund the money). Nothing is blocked for long, it scales.\n\n**Pitfalls/choice:** Saga gives only eventual consistency — the intermediate state is visible to others, you need idempotency and well-thought-out compensations. Take 2PC for short, strict ACID; saga — for long distributed processes in microservices.","quizCorrect":"Doesn't block resources, but rolls back errors with compensations","quizWrong":["Guarantees the same isolation as 2PC, but faster","Needs no rollback — steps always succeed","Requires a single coordinator holding locks until the end of the whole flow"],"picCap":"2PC: everyone is locked and waiting. Saga: steps commit immediately, on failure — compensations."},"g-arch-bounded-context":{"q":"Bounded Context in DDD — what is it and why the boundary?","a":"A boundary within which a term means ONE thing. \"Customer\" in billing and in support are different models. Inside the context — its own single model and language (ubiquitous language). Outside — you translate via contracts. You don't split by tables — you split by meaning.","d":"A Bounded Context often = a microservice/module boundary. A Context Map shows the relationships between them: Shared Kernel, Customer/Supplier, Conformist, Anti-Corruption Layer, Published Language. The main mistake is one anemic God-model \"Customer\" for the whole company: it drags in conflicting requirements and breaks on any change.","hook":"The word \"bow\": for a violinist, an archer, and on a ship — three different things. Context decides.","more":"A Bounded Context is a **boundary of meaning** within which one term = one model. \"Customer\" in billing (invoices, limits) and in support (tickets, contacts) are different entities, even if they look similar in the DB. Inside the boundary the team speaks a single **ubiquitous language**, and the code echoes those words.\n\nWhy: a single model for the whole company inevitably bloats into a contradictory monster where every field is \"for someone\". The boundary localizes changes and vocabulary.\n\n- You split by **meaning/language**, not by tables or layers.\n- At the seams between contexts — explicit contracts and translation (see ACL).\n- Often 1 context ≈ 1 service, but not necessarily.","quizCorrect":"To bound the area where a model term has a single meaning","quizWrong":["To speed up queries through table partitioning","To encrypt data inside a single service","To guarantee strong consistency between services of one domain"],"picCap":"One word — different meaning in different boundaries","note":"Subtlety people confuse in interviews: a Bounded Context is a boundary of LANGUAGE/model, not of a team or a deployment unit — so one microservice can legitimately host two contexts, and one context can be deployed across several services. A common mistake is equating context with subdomain: a subdomain is part of the problem space (what exists in the business), while a context is a solution in the code space, and the mapping is not always 1:1."},"g-arch-aggregate":{"q":"Aggregate and Aggregate Root — why and what are the rules?","a":"An Aggregate is a cluster of objects that changes as a single whole and holds invariants. From outside you touch only the Root by id. A transaction = one aggregate. Between aggregates you reference by id, not by object, and the link is via eventual consistency.","d":"Vernon's rules: 1) protect true invariants within the boundary; 2) keep aggregates small; 3) reference other aggregates by identity; 4) update other aggregates asynchronously (events). A large aggregate = locks and optimistic-lock conflicts. Example invariant: the sum of order line items = the order total.","note":"The main mistake is a bloated aggregate: half the domain is pulled into one root for \"convenient\" navigation, and then every write locks a huge chunk and hits conflicts under concurrent access. The rule \"one transaction = one aggregate\" gets violated by changing two aggregates in one transaction instead of eventual consistency via events.","hook":"A box of parts: you take the whole box, you don't reach inside with your hands. Integrity is kept behind the lid.","more":"An Aggregate is a cluster of entities and value objects that changes **as a single whole** and holds invariants (e.g. the sum of line items ≤ the limit). From outside it's accessible only through the **Aggregate Root** by its id — you don't expose internal objects or modify them directly.\n\nRule: **one transaction = one aggregate**. This is the boundary of consistency and concurrent access (optimistic locking by the root's version). Between aggregates you hold a **reference by id**, not the object.\n\n- Links between aggregates — via **eventual consistency** (events), not a shared transaction.\n- Pitfalls: too large an aggregate → locks and conflicts; too small — the invariant doesn't hold.","quizCorrect":"One — update the rest asynchronously via events","quizWrong":["All related ones at once, to keep strong consistency","At least two, to avoid races","As many as you like — aggregate boundaries aren't about transactions"],"picCap":"From outside — only the Root; inside — invariants"},"g-arch-value-object":{"q":"Value Object vs Entity — what's the difference and why a VO?","a":"An Entity has identity (id), lives over time, two with equal fields are different. A Value Object has no id, equality is by value, immutable. Money(100,KZT) == Money(100,KZT). A VO captures rules right in the type: no \"bare\" BigDecimal without a currency.","d":"A VO gives you: self-validation in the constructor, no bugs from mutation (it can be shared), meaningful equals/hashCode, a fight against Primitive Obsession. In Java 17+, a record is perfect for a VO. Replacing email:String with an Email VO removes a class of bugs \"an invalid email leaked into the domain\".","note":"A VO must be immutable, otherwise when one instance is shared between entities, a mutation in one place silently changes data in another. A common pitfall in Java is forgetting to override equals/hashCode, and the VO starts comparing by reference, breaking lookups in collections and comparison by value.","hook":"A 1000 KZT note: any one equals any other (VO). But your passport is unique by its number (Entity).","more":"An Entity has **identity (id)** and lives over time: two objects with identical fields are different entities (two namesake customers). A Value Object **has no id**, equality is **by value**, and it's **immutable**: to change it you create a new one.\n\nWhy a VO: it encapsulates **rules right in the type**. `Money(100, KZT)` carries the currency and won't let you add tenge to dollars; there's no \"bare\" `BigDecimal` where the meaning is lost. Fewer invalid states, simpler tests.\n\n- VO for: money, date periods, coordinates, email, percentages.\n- Pitfalls: don't slap an id where only the value matters; immutability simplifies thread-safety and caching.","quizCorrect":"No identity, equality by value, immutable","quizWrong":["A VO is always stored in a separate table with an id","A VO is mutable, while an Entity is immutable","A VO can't be used inside an aggregate"],"picCap":"Has an id → Entity; no id → Value Object"},"g-arch-domain-event":{"q":"Domain Event — what is it and how does it differ from an integration event?","a":"A Domain Event is the fact that \"something significant happened in the domain\" in past tense: OrderPlaced, PaymentReceived. Inside the service — synchronous handlers. An Integration Event is the same thing outward for other services, via a broker. Don't confuse a command (a request) with an event (an accomplished fact).","d":"A domain event lives inside a bounded context, often published after the aggregate's transaction commits (or via an outbox). An integration event is a public contract, versioned, and must not drag the internal model along. A typical mistake is to send a domain event outward with internal fields, coupling other services to your schema.","hook":"An internal memo within a department vs a press release to the outside: one event, but the wording and audience are different.","more":"A Domain Event is a **fact from the domain in past tense**: `OrderPlaced`, `PaymentReceived`. It's immutable and describes what already happened. Inside the service it's consumed by **synchronous handlers** (in the same transaction/process) — side effects, projections, invariants of neighboring aggregates.\n\nAn Integration Event is the same fact, but **outward**, for other services, via a broker (Kafka). It has a stable contract/schema, versioning, at-least-once delivery.\n\n- Don't confuse a **command** (`PlaceOrder` — a request, can be rejected) with an **event** (it happened, can't be undone).\n- Pitfalls: don't publish internal domain events outward as-is — map them to an integration contract, otherwise the internal model leaks.","quizCorrect":"An accomplished fact in past tense (OrderPlaced)","quizWrong":["A command-request in imperative mood (PlaceOrder)","A SQL query to read a projection","A synonym for a REST POST endpoint"],"picCap":"An internal fact ≠ a public contract","note":"Catch: 'synchronous handlers in the same transaction' is convenient, but this is exactly where the anti-pattern is born — when one domain-event handler modifies ANOTHER aggregate in the same transaction, violating the 'one aggregate per transaction' rule and producing deadlocks/bloated consistency boundaries. The canonical DDD advice: do side effects on neighboring aggregates asynchronously (eventual consistency), and synchronously within the transaction touch only the same aggregate."},"g-arch-cqrs-readmodel":{"q":"CQRS read model — why a separate model for reads?","a":"Writes and reads have different needs: a write stores invariants (normalized), a read wants to quickly serve a ready-made screen (denormalized). CQRS separates them: a write model (aggregates) and a read model (flat projections tailored to queries), updated from events.","d":"A read model is a projection tailored to a specific screen/report: no JOINs across 5 tables at runtime. You pay with eventual consistency (the projection lags by fractions of a second). Don't introduce CQRS everywhere — it's complexity; take it where the read and write models genuinely diverge or reads heavily outnumber writes.","hook":"The kitchen cooks from raw ingredients (write), and on the display case — ready-made dishes to order (read).","more":"Writes and reads pull the model in different directions: a write preserves invariants and is usually **normalized** (minimal duplication, integrity), while a read wants to **serve a ready-made screen in a single query** — i.e. denormalization tailored to a specific UI.\n\nCQRS separates them: the **write model** = aggregates and business rules; the **read model** = flat projections/views for queries, updated from write events. The read side can go into a separate store (Elasticsearch, Redis), scaled independently.\n\n- The price: projections are **eventually consistent** — a lag between the write and its appearance in the read.\n- Pitfalls: don't drag CQRS into every CRUD; it's justified under load asymmetry/complex queries.","quizCorrect":"Eventual consistency — the projection lags behind the write","quizWrong":["Data loss on every service restart","Inability to horizontally scale reads","A mandatory two-phase commit on every query to the read side"],"picCap":"One model writes, another (flat) one reads","note":"Implementation trap: a read model updated from events must be idempotent and resilient to reordering — with at-least-once delivery (Kafka) the same event arrives twice, and events from different partitions arrive out of order, so a naive projector double-inserts or overwrites a row with stale data. Fix it with an aggregate version/seqno in the projection (apply only if version > current) or an upsert by key, not a blind INSERT."},"g-arch-idempotent-consumer":{"q":"Idempotent consumer — why and how to build one?","a":"Brokers give at-least-once: one message can arrive twice (retry, rebalance). The consumer must produce the same result on a repeat. Solution: store processed messageIds in a table and check them before processing, in the same transaction as the business logic.","d":"Options: 1) an inbox table (processed_messages with PK = messageId) — atomically \"marked + did it\"; 2) natural idempotency (UPSERT by business key); 3) a conditional update (WHERE status='NEW'). Just \"check then write\" without a transaction/PK is a race. Dedup by messageId is more reliable than by content.","note":"The messageId check and the business write itself must be in ONE DB transaction — otherwise on a crash between them you either lose the processing or duplicate it. And the dedup table grows forever: it needs TTL-based cleanup, but the retention window must cover the broker's maximum retry lag.","hook":"A ticket inspector stamps the ticket: a second time with the same ticket won't get you in.","more":"Brokers guarantee **at-least-once**: after a retry, a partition rebalance, or an ack timeout, one message will arrive again. So the handler must produce **the same result on a repeat**, otherwise a double debit/duplicate order.\n\nHow: take a stable **messageId** (or business key), store processed ids in a table, and in **the same transaction** as the business logic: checked absence → executed → recorded the id. A unique constraint on the id catches the race.\n\n- Important: the check and the id write must be atomic with the effect, otherwise there's a window for a duplicate.\n- Pitfalls: clean the processed table by TTL; for external side effects you need idempotency on their side too (an idempotency key).","quizCorrect":"Brokers give at-least-once — a message can arrive again","quizWrong":["Otherwise you can't scale producers","Kafka guarantees exactly-once, but only for Java and Scala clients","So that messages get compressed on a resend"],"picCap":"Seen the messageId — I skip the repeat"},"g-arch-outbox-vs-cdc":{"q":"Transactional Outbox vs CDC — how to reliably send events?","a":"The dual-write problem: you can't atomically write to the DB AND send to Kafka. Outbox: you write the event to an outbox table in the same transaction, a separate process reads it and sends it to the broker. CDC (Debezium) reads the DB's WAL/binlog and publishes the changes itself — without an outbox table.","d":"Outbox: explicit control over WHAT and in what format gets published; you need a relay/poller. CDC: zero code in the application, but it publishes the table structure (you have to map it to events, the schema leaks outward). Often a combo: write to an outbox table, and CDC streams it (Outbox+Debezium) — the best of both.","note":"The outbox relayer gives at-least-once — the same event can go to the broker twice (crashed after sending, before marking it in the DB), so the consumer must be idempotent. CDC, in turn, requires not doing an UPDATE-then-DELETE of the outbox row without an INSERT, otherwise Debezium won't see it, and you hit problems with the initial snapshot and ordering across multiple partitions.","hook":"You drop a letter into your own mailbox (one operation). The postman (relay/CDC) picks it up and delivers it himself.","more":"The **dual-write** problem: you can't atomically \"write to the DB AND send to Kafka\" — a failure is possible between them, and the states diverge.\n\n**Outbox**: in the same transaction as the business data you write the event to an `outbox` table. A separate relay process reads it (polling) and publishes it to the broker, marking the sent ones. Simple and explicit in code. **CDC (Debezium)** instead of a table reads the DB's **WAL/binlog** and publishes the changes itself — the application knows nothing about the broker at all.\n\n- Outbox: more code, but you control the event schema; you need a relay and an idempotent consumer.\n- CDC: less code, but it's tied to a specific DB's log, and the events = \"row changes\", which you often have to map to domain ones.","quizCorrect":"Dual-write: atomicity of the DB write and the event publication","quizWrong":["Deduplication of incoming messages on the consumer side before handling","Encryption of event payloads in Kafka","Load balancing across partitions"],"picCap":"The event goes into the DB in one transaction → delivery happens separately"},"g-arch-acl":{"q":"Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL) — why a translator layer?","a":"An ACL is a layer that translates a foreign/legacy model into yours, so their concepts don't \"leak\" into your domain. All calls to the legacy go through a translator adapter. Your model stays clean; when the external API changes — you fix only the ACL.","d":"Without an ACL, foreign DTOs, broken enums, and bugs spread across the code. ACL = adapter + mapper + (optionally) facade. A classic with Strangler Fig: the new service talks to the legacy only through an ACL. In practice these are ports like TaxReportPort/OrderDataPort — a port + adapter hiding JDBC/legacy SP.","hook":"An interpreter at negotiations: inside you speak your own language, outward he translates. The grime of the foreign language doesn't reach you.","more":"An Anti-Corruption Layer is a **translator layer** between your context and a foreign/legacy one. All outward calls go through an adapter that maps foreign DTOs/terms into **your** domain model and back. Their broken names, nullable fields, and weird status codes **don't leak** inside.\n\nWhy: without an ACL the foreign model spreads across the code, and when the external API changes the whole domain breaks. With an ACL there's a single point of change.\n\n- Inside the core — clean types; the \"grime\" of the external contract lives only in the ACL.\n- Pitfalls: the ACL isn't a thin one-to-one mapper, but a **semantic translation**; keep it thin on logic but honest on meaning, and cover it with translation tests.","quizCorrect":"Your domain model from a foreign/legacy model leaking in","quizWrong":["The database from SQL injections","The service from a DDoS at the gateway level of the cluster","Kafka messages from duplication"],"picCap":"The foreign model doesn't leak — the ACL translates it","note":"The main ACL trap is that it lives inside YOUR bounded context (on your side), not in the legacy: teams often push the translation into a shared module or into the legacy service itself, and then the foreign model leaks again to multiple consumers. Second pitfall: an ACL is not free in latency/CPU (mapping plus sometimes extra enrichment calls), so for a performance-critical path you must profile it rather than assume it's 'thin'."},"g-arch-strangler":{"q":"Strangler Fig — how to safely replace legacy?","a":"Don't rewrite everything at once (big-bang is risky), but gradually: you put a facade/router in front of the legacy and carve out functions into the new service piece by piece, redirecting traffic to it. The legacy is \"strangled\" piece by piece until nothing is left, and then you delete it.","d":"Steps: 1) a facade in front of the legacy; 2) carve out one piece of functionality into the new one; 3) route that path to the new one, the rest — to the old one; 4) repeat; 5) shut down the legacy. You need: an ACL to the old DB, feature flags/routing, a parallel run (shadow) for comparison. This is how teams migrate print-service → document-service.","note":"The most underrated part is data: while old and new code write to the same DB, you need two-way synchronization or a single source of truth, otherwise the state diverges. And the facade-router can't be removed while even one piece still goes to the legacy — it lives for the entire migration, which lasts for months.","hook":"A strangler fig wraps a tree and gradually takes its place — the host doesn't fall all at once.","more":"**Under the hood:** in front of the legacy system you put a **proxy/router (facade)**. Initially it drives all traffic to the old system. You implement one piece of functionality in the new service and switch that endpoint's route to the new code. You repeat until the legacy is emptied — then you delete both it and the router.\n\n**Why so:** a big-bang rewrite is risky — you can't test everything at once, and a rollback means losing months. The incremental path gives a fast rollback at each step (just point the route back) and continuous delivery of value.\n\n**Pitfalls:** you need a data compatibility layer — old and new code often share one DB, it's easy to catch a desync. The metaphor from the strangler fig: it wraps the tree until it dies off.","quizCorrect":"Gradual replacement of legacy piece by piece via a facade-router","quizWrong":["A full halt of the system and a rewrite in a single release of the product","Duplicating the DB for a hot standby","Caching legacy responses on a CDN"],"picCap":"Piece by piece, traffic moves off the old onto the new"},"g-arch-multitenancy":{"q":"Multi-tenancy — what are the data isolation models?","a":"One instance serves many clients (tenants). Three models: 1) shared DB, shared schema + a tenant_id in every row (cheap, but a leak risk); 2) schema per tenant; 3) DB per tenant (max isolation, expensive). The choice is a balance of cost, isolation, and the number of tenants.","d":"Shared schema: a tenant_id is mandatory in ALL queries — otherwise a leak of others' data; Postgres RLS (Row-Level Security) helps. Schema-per-tenant: cleaner, but migrations across hundreds of schemas are painful. DB-per-tenant: for regulation/large clients. The main gotcha — forgetting the tenant_id filter in even one query.","note":"The \"shared schema + tenant_id\" model catches data leaks on ANY forgotten WHERE tenant_id — one missed filter in a query shows others' data. That's why the filter is pushed below the code level (RLS in Postgres, a Hibernate filter), rather than left to the developer's discipline.","hook":"A dorm (shared kitchen, rooms by number), a communal flat (separate rooms), separate apartments — rising isolation and cost.","more":"**Under the hood:** one running application instance serves many clients (**tenants**); the code, by the `tenant_id` from the token/context, picks whose data to show. The models of **storage isolation** differ:\n\n- **Shared DB, shared schema** — a `tenant_id` in every row. Cheap, easy to scale to thousands of tenants, but filtering is on the code's conscience (forgot the WHERE — a leak).\n- **Schema-per-tenant** — a shared DB, its own schema per tenant. A balance of isolation and cost.\n- **DB-per-tenant** — maximum isolation, simple per-client backup/deletion, but expensive and heavy as the number of tenants grows.\n\n**When:** banks/enterprise with strict compliance gravitate toward separate DBs; mass SaaS — toward a shared one. The shared-schema pitfall — mandatory cross-cutting filtering (RLS in Postgres greatly reduces the risk).","quizCorrect":"A forgotten tenant_id filter in a query will leak others' data","quizWrong":["Inability to make a backup of the data","A mandatory two-phase commit on every query","A total loss of CPU isolation between tenants, always and everywhere"],"picCap":"Cheap+risky ← → expensive+isolated"},"ap-ambassador":{"q":"What is an Ambassador and how does it differ from a Sidecar?","a":"An Ambassador is an out-of-process proxy helper placed next to a service so it takes on the networking hassle of outgoing calls: retries, timeouts, TLS, circuit breaker, metrics. The service talks to localhost, and the ambassador goes out into the external world and suffers the network for it. It solves the pain of \"I don't want to hardwire network resilience into every service's code in every language.\" The price — an extra network hop and one more process to deploy and monitor.","d":"A Sidecar is a general umbrella: any auxiliary process next to the application (logs, config, metrics). An Ambassador is a special case of a sidecar, tailored specifically as a proxy for outgoing/incoming network calls. That is, \"every ambassador is a sidecar, but not every sidecar is an ambassador.\"","note":"⚠️ An extra network hop and a second process per instance is +latency and +operations. For a couple of calls it's simpler to use a regular library (Resilience4j) right in the code. An Ambassador pays off in a polyglot zoo and when you want to change network policy without redeploying the service.","hook":"A personal ambassador: you talk to your own ambassador in your native language (localhost), and he negotiates with a foreign country — knows their protocol, tolerates rudeness, and tries again.","more":"**Why move it out:** network resilience (retry, timeout, circuit breaker, mTLS, tracing) is written once in the proxy, rather than duplicated in every service in Java, Go, Python.\n- **Polyglot win:** services don't need resilience libraries for their language.\n- **Where it lives:** in the same Pod/host, talks via localhost — the hop is cheap, but not zero.\n- **Relation to service mesh:** Envoy in Istio/Linkerd is essentially an ambassador-proxy on steroids, grown into a full-blown mesh.\n- **Difference from a Sidecar:** a sidecar is the general category \"a helper process next to it\", an ambassador is the narrow role \"a proxy for network calls\".","quizCorrect":"An Ambassador is a special case of a sidecar-proxy for network calls (retries, TLS, metrics)","quizWrong":["An Ambassador runs inside the application process as a library","An Ambassador is a load balancer for the whole cluster's inbound traffic and never touches outbound","Ambassador and Sidecar are two names for the same thing, no difference"],"picCap":"The app calls localhost, the ambassador takes on retries/TLS/metrics and goes out into the external world"},"ap-claim-check":{"q":"What is the Claim Check pattern and when is it needed?","a":"Claim Check solves the problem of large payloads in messages: instead of pushing a megabyte file through the broker, we put it in storage (S3/MinIO/DB) and write only a reference — a \"claim check\" — into the message. The recipient uses the claim check to fetch the body from the store itself. The pain — brokers (Kafka/RabbitMQ) don't like fat messages: limits, clogged memory, slow replication. The price — an extra round-trip for the body and the care for object lifetime/cleanup in the store.","d":"The name comes from a baggage claim ticket: you check your suitcase into a left-luggage office and carry only a slip with a number, not the suitcase itself. The slip gets you the suitcase back.","note":"⚠️ The main pitfall is the object's lifecycle. Delete the body before the consumer (or a DLQ retry) has read it — and you get a broken reference. And conversely: forgot the TTL — the store swells with old payloads. There's also an eventual-consistency window: the reference is already in the topic, but the object hasn't reached the store yet (write to the store BEFORE sending the message).","hook":"A left-luggage office at the station: you check the suitcase, carry only the slip with a number — and that's what gets the suitcase back.","more":"**When to use:** the payload is larger than the broker's comfortable limit (Kafka ~1 MB by default, RabbitMQ also dislikes fat), or when not all consumers need the body at all.\n- **Pros:** lightweight messages, fast replication/rebalance, less broker memory.\n- **Cons:** extra infrastructure (the store) and a network round-trip for the body.\n- **Cleanup:** you need a TTL/lifecycle policy in the store, otherwise garbage piles up forever.\n- **In practice:** PDF print forms make sense to send via MinIO + a reference in Kafka, not as bytea in the message.","quizCorrect":"The body is put in storage, only a reference — the claim check — is passed in the message","quizWrong":["The message is encrypted and split into parts for reliable delivery over the network in transit","Every message requires a check confirmation before processing","The broker itself compresses large messages and caches them"],"picCap":"The body goes to the store, a lightweight claim check goes to the broker, the consumer fetches the body by the reference"},"ap-scatter-gather":{"q":"Explain Scatter-Gather and why the timeout is there.","a":"Scatter-Gather is \"fan out and collect\": one request fans out to N workers (scatter), then their responses are aggregated/merged into a single result (gather). The classic — meta-search: ask 5 price providers and show the best. The pain it solves: query many sources in parallel instead of a sequential walk. The price — you need a timeout for stragglers, otherwise the slowest worker holds up the whole response (tail latency).","d":"Gather strategies: wait for all (all), wait for the first valid (first wins), wait for a quorum, or take whatever arrived by the deadline. The choice depends on whether you need completeness or speed.","note":"⚠️ Without a timeout and a concurrency limit, scatter-gather turns into a failure amplifier: one stuck provider holds all the requests, the thread/connection pool burns out, latency soars to the worst case. A per-call timeout, an overall deadline, and a partial-response strategy are mandatory. And remember: waiting for \"all\" = your latency equals the latency of the slowest one.","hook":"A tender with a deadline: you sent the spec to five contractors, on Friday you collected the offers and picked the best — whoever didn't make it, we don't wait for.","more":"**The gist:** parallelize the polling of many sources and stitch the result together.\n- **Scatter:** fan-out of the request to N recipients (REST/queues/gRPC).\n- **Gather:** collection and aggregation — best price, merging lists, voting.\n- **Timeout is mandatory:** the overall response must not depend on the slowest link; we work with whatever arrived.\n- **Degradation:** on partial responses we decide — return an incomplete result or an error (depends on the business).\n- **Relation:** often a pair with MapReduce (scatter=map, gather=reduce) and with request-reply over queues.","quizCorrect":"The request is fanned out to N workers in parallel, the responses are aggregated, the timeout cuts off the stragglers","quizWrong":["The requests go to workers strictly in turn until someone answers","One worker splits the task and collects the result inside itself","Messages are broadcast to all topic subscribers with no response collection from any of them afterwards being expected"],"picCap":"A request fanned out to N workers, a merge of the arrived responses, the straggler P3 is dropped by timeout"},"ap-competing-consumers":{"q":"What is Competing Consumers and at what cost does the scaling come?","a":"Competing Consumers — several consumers sit on one queue, and each message goes to exactly one of them (they \"compete\" for messages). This is horizontal scaling of processing: load grows — you add consumers, they clear it faster. The pain — one consumer can't keep up draining the queue. The price — global order is lost: messages are processed in parallel and may finish out of the order they arrived in.","d":"In Kafka this is solved with partitions: order is preserved within a partition, and the competing effect is given by a consumer group — one partition is read by only one consumer in the group. Parallelism is limited by the number of partitions, but order by key is held.","note":"⚠️ A common mistake is to scale consumers where the business requires strict order (e.g. events for one account: \"opened → funded → closed\"). Parallel workers will reorder them and break the invariant. Cured by partitioning by entity key (all events of account X → one partition → one consumer), but then parallelism hits the number of partitions.","hook":"A supermarket checkout: one queue of shoppers, several tills — whoever frees up takes the next; the overall order \"who entered the store after whom\" is no longer kept.","more":"**Why:** throughput — you add consumers and clear the queue in parallel.\n- **Delivery:** one message — to one consumer (competing), not to all.\n- **The price — order:** with parallel processing messages finish out of sync; global FIFO collapses.\n- **How to recover partial order:** key-partitioning (Kafka) or a message group (SQS FIFO / ActiveMQ) — order is held within a key, not globally.\n- **Idempotency:** with retries and at-least-once one message can arrive twice — the handler must be idempotent.","quizCorrect":"Several consumers share one queue, each message goes to one — scale at the cost of losing global order","quizWrong":["Each consumer gets a copy of every message for reliability","Global message order is strictly preserved for any number of consumers and costs nothing at all to maintain","It's a pattern for broadcasting one message to all subscribers at once"],"picCap":"Several consumers share one queue: a msg goes to one of them, global order isn't guaranteed"},"ap-event-carried-state":{"q":"What is Event-Carried State Transfer and what pain does it cure?","a":"Event-Carried State Transfer is when the event carries the needed data within it (\"customer changed\" + the new address, name, status itself), rather than just the fact \"customer changed, go ask the source.\" The consumer puts this data into its own local read copy and works without synchronous pokes to the source. The pain — to remove runtime coupling and the cascade of synchronous calls between services. The price — data duplication across services and eventual consistency.","d":"The opposite is a \"thin\" notification event (just the ID), after which the consumer makes a synchronous callback to the source for details. That's simpler on storage, but brings back temporal coupling: the source is down — the consumer stalls too.","note":"⚠️ Duplicated data = a desync risk: a missed/reordered event leaves the consumer with a stale copy. You need idempotency, versions/timestamps to discard old updates, and a re-sync mechanism (snapshot/replay). And don't stuff the whole aggregate into the event \"just in case\" — a bloated schema becomes a fragile public contract that's painful to change.","hook":"A letter with an attachment vs a notification letter: instead of \"our document has been updated, come download it\", they send the document itself — the recipient doesn't have to go anywhere.","more":"**Why:** to break runtime coupling. The consumer is autonomous — it answers queries from its own read model even when the source is unavailable.\n- **Plus to availability:** the source going down doesn't take consumers with it.\n- **Plus to latency:** no synchronous round-trip for details.\n- **Minus — duplication:** the same data lies as copies in several services.\n- **Minus — consistency:** the copies are eventually consistent, there's a divergence window.\n- **Schema evolution:** the event contract becomes a public API — change carefully, version it.\n- **Relation:** often implemented via a CQRS read model and Outbox for reliable publication.","quizCorrect":"The event carries the needed data with it so the consumer doesn't poke the source synchronously","quizWrong":["The event contains only the ID, and the consumer always pulls the data from the source in a separate request","State is stored centrally and shared via a common DB","Every event requires a synchronous confirmation from the source"],"picCap":"The event carries the data with it; the consumer updates a local copy and doesn't poke the source synchronously"},"ap-p2p-vs-pubsub":{"q":"What's the difference between a Point-to-point queue and a Pub/Sub topic?","a":"Point-to-point (a queue) — a message is delivered to exactly one consumer: you dropped a task, one of them took it and processed it. Pub/Sub (a topic) — ALL subscribers receive the message: one event, N independent reactions. Different pains: a queue is about distributing work (1 task = 1 worker), a topic is about notifying many (1 fact → many interested parties). The price of pub/sub — loose coupling is good, but it's harder to track who processed the event and how.","d":"In Kafka the line is thinner: a topic + one consumer group behaves like a queue (competing consumers, a msg to one in the group), while several different groups on the same topic give pub/sub (each group gets all messages). So \"p2p vs pub/sub\" is about the delivery model, not always about a separate product.","note":"⚠️ The pitfall — mismatching the model to the task. You send a \"debit money\" command to a pub/sub topic with several subscribers — it gets debited several times. And conversely: you pour a domain event into a p2p queue — one consumer eats it, the rest of the interested parties never learn of it. Rule: a command (do it) → point-to-point to one owner; an event (it happened) → pub/sub to everyone interested.","hook":"A queue — a support ticket: one operator takes the ticket. A topic — a company-wide mailing: the letter lands in every employee's inbox at once.","more":"**Point-to-point (Queue):**\n- 1 message → 1 consumer (even if there are several — competing consumers).\n- The \"task/work distribution\" model, load balancing.\n- Examples: SQS, RabbitMQ queue, ActiveMQ queue.\n\n**Publish/Subscribe (Topic):**\n- 1 message → all subscribers, each with its own logic.\n- The \"event broadcast\" model, loose coupling of publisher and subscribers.\n- Examples: SNS, RabbitMQ fanout exchange, a Kafka topic with several consumer groups.\n\n**Kafka hybrid:** topic + 1 group ≈ a queue; topic + N groups ≈ pub/sub. It holds both modes in a single abstraction.","quizCorrect":"A queue (P2P) — a message to one consumer; a topic (Pub/Sub) — to all subscribers","quizWrong":["A queue broadcasts to all, while a topic delivers to one chosen consumer out of the group","The difference is only in speed: a topic is faster than a queue","In both cases a message is always deleted after the first read"],"picCap":"Queue: one message to one consumer. Topic: one message to all subscribers"},"ap-bulkhead":{"q":"One slow downstream hung the whole service — all threads are stuck on it. How to isolate it?","a":"Bulkhead — each dependency gets its own separate thread/connection pool with a limit. If payment-service hangs, only its pool gets clogged, while calls to other services and the health-check keep working. The price: more threads/memory and tuning the pool sizes per dependency.","d":"Where the name comes from: on a ship the hull is split into compartments — bulkheads. A breach in one compartment floods it, but doesn't sink the whole ship.","note":"⚠️ Too many small pools = fragmentation: a lot of threads are allocated in total, but each pool is small and hits its limit during a spike, while neighboring ones sit idle. And a semaphore bulkhead does NOT save you from stuck blocking calls — it limits the number of incoming ones, but the stuck thread itself goes nowhere; you need the thread-pool variant + a timeout.","hook":"Compartments on a ship: one is breached — it floods, but the vessel stays afloat.","more":"**Bulkhead = resource isolation.** Without it all requests share one common thread/connection pool. One hung downstream holds the threads (they wait for the timeout), the pool is exhausted — and the service stops serving ALL requests, even to healthy dependencies.\n- **Thread-pool bulkhead** — a separate thread pool per dependency (isolates blocking calls too).\n- **Semaphore bulkhead** — just a counter of concurrent calls, cheaper, no thread switching.\n- The pool size is computed as: `pool = target_throughput × p99_latency` (Little's law).\n- Works in a pair with **Timeout** — without a timeout a thread in a clogged pool hangs forever.","quizCorrect":"Give each dependency a separate thread/connection pool with a limit, so its failure doesn't exhaust the resources of the whole service","quizWrong":["Repeat the failed call until success with an exponential backoff","Break the circuit after N errors and temporarily stop calling the downstream for a while, letting it recover before traffic is allowed through again","Cache the downstream's responses, to avoid going to it again"],"picCap":"A separate thread pool per dependency: a hung payment doesn't eat inventory's threads"},"ap-resilience-stack":{"q":"Timeout, Retry, Circuit Breaker, Bulkhead, Fallback — in what ORDER do they wrap a call and why does the order matter?","a":"These are wrapper layers around a single call, in order from outside in: Fallback → Retry → CircuitBreaker → Bulkhead → Timeout → the call itself. Each layer has its own job: the timeout keeps it from hanging, the bulkhead isolates the resource, the CB cuts off a dead downstream, the retry repeats, the fallback returns a backup response. The cost of an ordering mistake — a retry INSIDE the CB (under the circuit) hammers a dead downstream with a batch of repeats and stops the circuit from opening in time, which is why the retry is placed ABOVE the CB.","d":"In Resilience4j the order is set by decorators; the recommended one is: Bulkhead( Retry( CircuitBreaker( TimeLimiter( call )))) , and Fallback is hung on the outermost result.","note":"⚠️ The main trap — Retry + Circuit Breaker without coordination. If the retry is aggressive and the CB is configured on an error percentage, retries can \"pad\" the successes and prevent the circuit from opening, continuing to hammer a failing downstream. And never retry when the CB is already open — it's pointless load; place retry above the CB, so it immediately sees open and doesn't repeat.","hook":"An onion of protection: the request breaks through the layers down to the core call, and an error bubbles back outward.","more":"**The layers aren't interchangeable — each has its own pain:**\n- **Timeout** — keep the call from hanging forever (otherwise the bulkhead/pool clogs).\n- **Bulkhead** — isolate the resource, limit concurrency to the downstream.\n- **CircuitBreaker** — stop calling an obviously dead service, give it a chance to recover.\n- **Retry** — finish off rare transient failures (the network blinked).\n- **Fallback** — give the client at least some response (cache, a stub), not a 500.\n\n**Why Retry above the CB:** a retry inside the circuit makes several physical calls per one circuit decision — the CB counts them separately and either opens too early or the retries mask the degradation. A retry on the outside = \"one attempt with its repeats\" for the circuit.","quizCorrect":"Retry must be ABOVE the Circuit Breaker: one logical call with repeats = one event for the circuit, otherwise retries hide failures and prevent the circuit from opening","quizWrong":["Retry must be under the Circuit Breaker, so each repeat opens the circuit separately and the downstream is relieved of the repeated load far sooner than it otherwise would be","Fallback is hung inside, closest to the call, before Timeout","Timeout is placed as the outermost layer, on top of Retry"],"picCap":"An onion of layers: the request enters from the top through all the wrappers down to the call, the response/error bubbles back out"},"ap-failfast-vs-degrade":{"q":"Fail-fast or graceful degradation — when is it better to fail instantly vs. return a reduced response?","a":"Fail-fast — return an error immediately instead of spending resources on a call that's doomed anyway (CB open, queue full, required data missing). Graceful degradation — serve a partial/stale result instead of failing entirely when a non-essential feature is unavailable. Rule: fail fast when the data is critical for correctness; degrade when the feature is secondary and there's an acceptable fallback. The price of degradation is the risk of serving an inaccurate/stale response.","d":"Fail-fast is about resources (don't tie up a thread on a dead call), graceful degradation is about UX (show the feed without the 'recommendations' block if the recommender is down).","note":"⚠️ The danger of 'silent degradation': you serve a stale/incomplete response, but the client doesn't know it and makes decisions on wrong data (old price, outdated balance). Degradation must be observable — a metric/flag in the response/log — otherwise you're masking an incident and learning about it from users.","hook":"Engine dead — pull over immediately (fail-fast). AC dead — keep flying without it (degrade)","more":"**These are different axes, and they don't conflict — they often work together.**\n- **Fail-fast** saves resources and gives fast feedback: an instant 503 beats 30s of waiting followed by an error anyway. An open Circuit Breaker is fail-fast.\n- **Graceful degradation** preserves partial functionality: the home page loads without the 'related items' block, search runs on a stale index, the price shows from cache with a marker.\n\n**The combo:** fail-fast on a non-essential call + fallback = degradation. The service quickly figures out 'recommender is dead' (fail-fast via open CB) and immediately serves the page without recommendations (degrade). You can't degrade a critical payment that way — there you do an honest fail-fast with an error to the user.","quizCorrect":"Fail-fast — when the call is doomed anyway or the data is critical for correctness; degradation — when the feature is secondary and an acceptable fallback exists","quizWrong":["Fail-fast is always better than degradation because it never serves inaccurate data","Graceful degradation means retrying the call more slowly until it succeeds","The choice between them is determined only by the timeout size, not by the criticality of the data being served, which is why degradation is acceptable in reports only"],"picCap":"A fork: critical data → fail fast; secondary feature → degrade to a fallback response"},"ap-load-shedding":{"q":"Load has exceeded capacity, latency is climbing, the service is about to go down. What do you do at the ENTRY point?","a":"Load shedding — drop excess requests at the entry point (immediate 429/503) before the system gets overloaded, serving only as much as you can actually handle. Cheaply rejecting early beats accepting everything, degrading on latency, and going down entirely (metastable failure). The price: some clients get rejected — but deliberately and quickly, instead of everyone getting a timeout.","d":"Difference from rate limiting: rate limit is about fairness/quotas for a specific client (a predefined limit); load shedding is about the service's self-preservation under actual overload (adaptive to load/latency/queue depth).","note":"⚠️ Load shedding is useless if the shed clients immediately retry without backoff — you rejected them, but the load didn't drop. So it works ONLY paired with client-side discipline (jitter backoff, retry budget) and shedding BEFORE acquiring expensive resources. Shedding after a DB round-trip is already pointless — the resource is spent.","hook":"A club bouncer: lets in as many as fit, the rest get stopped at the door — no crush inside","more":"**Load shedding protects against metastable failure.** Under overload without shedding: queues grow → latency grows → clients hit timeouts and retry → load grows even more → the service can't pull out of the spin even after incoming traffic subsides.\n- You must shed **early and cheaply** — before the request has acquired expensive resources (a thread, a DB connection).\n- **Prioritization:** drop low-priority/background traffic first, protect critical traffic (payments, health checks).\n- **Overload signals:** queue depth, in-flight count, CPU, p99 latency.\n- Respond honestly: `503` + `Retry-After`, so the client backs off instead of hammering.","quizCorrect":"Drop excess requests at the entry point (429/503) under overload, serving exactly the load the system can handle","quizWrong":["Put excess requests into an unbounded queue and process them as capacity allows frees up, so that nothing is ever lost","Automatically retry the dropped requests on the server side","Slow down every request evenly so the total fits within capacity"],"picCap":"Excess requests are dropped at the entry point with 503 to preserve capacity for the requests being served"},"ap-retry-budget":{"q":"A downstream starts slowing down, all clients retry in unison — and finish it off completely. How do you cap retries globally?","a":"Retry budget — a global cap on the SHARE of retries relative to the primary traffic (via a token bucket): a retry is allowed only if the bucket has a token. Each request refills the bucket, each retry spends a token; on an error spike the tokens run out and retries shut off. This solves the retry storm: retries won't exceed, say, 10% of the load and won't trigger a downstream avalanche. The price: under mass failures, retries stop helping individual victims.","d":"This beats a fixed '3 attempts per request': under a general failure, 3×N requests triple the load exactly when the downstream is already dying. The budget looks at the aggregate, not at each request individually.","note":"⚠️ A per-request retry count (3 attempts) looks safe, but it's exactly the main mechanism of retry storms and cascading failure: on a failure, 1000 requests instantly become 3000. The real defense is precisely an aggregate budget + jitter. And you can only retry idempotent operations, otherwise a repeat double-charges a payment/order.","hook":"A global cap on retries like a casino cage with chips: out of chips — no retrying, downstream survives","more":"**The retry storm is a classic incident amplifier.** A per-request limit (e.g., 3 attempts) is dangerous because under a general failure it multiplies the load by 3 at the worst moment, turning a downstream blip into a full collapse (cascading failure).\n- **Retry budget** counts the aggregate: retries must not exceed a share (usually 10–20%) of the primary traffic.\n- Implementation — a **token bucket**: successful requests refill, retries draw down. A stream of errors → empty bucket → retries shut off automatically, with no manual flag.\n- `minRetriesPerSecond` — a floor, so that single transient failures still get retried under low traffic.\n- Must be complemented with **jitter backoff**, so retries don't synchronize into waves.","quizCorrect":"A token bucket caps retries as a share of the primary traffic globally, so under mass failures the bucket empties and retries shut off on their own","quizWrong":["A fixed 3 attempts per request is more reliable because the number is predictable in advance and never depends on the traffic volume at all it is under","Retry budget increases the delay between attempts exponentially for each request","A retry budget replaces the Circuit Breaker and timeouts"],"picCap":"A token bucket limits the share of retries; during a failure storm it empties and retries shut off automatically"},"ap-sidecar-ambassador-adapter":{"q":"Sidecar, Ambassador, Adapter — what's the difference between these three 'helper container' patterns?","a":"All three are a helper container alongside the main application (in the same pod) that handles an infrastructure concern on its behalf. Sidecar — the general case: something useful alongside (logs, metrics, config). Ambassador — a special case: a proxy for OUTBOUND calls (retry, TLS, service discovery on behalf of the app). Adapter — a special case: normalizes an INBOUND/outbound interface to a standard form (e.g., turns a custom metrics format into Prometheus). The price — an extra container, a network hop, and debugging complexity.","d":"Ambassador and Adapter are specializations of Sidecar. A service mesh (Envoy in Istio) is essentially an Ambassador sidecar raised to the platform level.","note":"⚠️ An extra container per pod doubles the number of processes, adds latency on a hop, and brings debugging pain ('where did it break — in the app or the sidecar?'). Don't bolt on a sidecar for a single feature that's easier to embed as a library. A sidecar is justified when the concern is shared across MANY services in different languages — then one container beats N libraries.","hook":"Sidecar — a motorcycle sidecar (just alongside). Ambassador — a diplomat who travels and negotiates OUTWARD on your behalf. Adapter — a plug converter in the socket: it makes a foreign plug fit.","more":"**All three live in the same pod/host as the application and share the network (localhost) — that's the key.**\n- **Sidecar** — an umbrella term: any companion container that offloads a cross-cutting concern (log collection, config sync, a metrics agent).\n- **Ambassador** — a sidecar that represents the application to the outside world: the app talks to `localhost`, and the ambassador does retry, circuit breaking, encryption, load balancing. This way the business code knows nothing about networking details.\n- **Adapter** — a sidecar that brings a heterogeneous interface to a uniform one: e.g., a legacy service exposes metrics in its own format, the adapter serves them as `/metrics` for Prometheus.\n- A service mesh (Istio + Envoy) is the industrial implementation of the Ambassador pattern at the platform level, not by hand.","quizCorrect":"Ambassador proxies the application's outbound calls (retry, TLS, discovery), Adapter normalizes the interface to a standard form","quizWrong":["Ambassador and Adapter are just different names for the same Sidecar pattern with no difference between them in deployment or in purpose","Adapter proxies outbound calls, while Ambassador accepts inbound traffic from the internet","A Sidecar must run in a separate pod on a different node for isolation"],"picCap":"Ambassador=outbound proxy, Adapter=interface normalizer, Sidecar=general helper alongside"},"ap-service-discovery":{"q":"Service discovery: client-side vs server-side — who resolves the address of the target instance, and where?","a":"Services in the cloud constantly change IPs and instance counts, so you can't hardcode an address — you need a lookup mechanism. Client-side: the client itself queries the registry (Eureka/Consul), gets the list of live instances, and picks one itself (client-side load balancing). Server-side: the client hits one stable address (a load balancer/proxy, e.g., AWS ELB or a k8s Service), which resolves and distributes. The price of client-side — discovery logic leaks into every client; the price of server-side — an extra hop and the load balancer as a point of failure.","d":"A k8s Service is server-side discovery (kube-proxy/iptables). A service mesh shifts client-side balancing into a sidecar, removing the code from the application.","note":"⚠️ The main pain of both is a stale registry: an instance died but is still listed as alive, and traffic flies into a corpse. Health checks and TTL/heartbeat help, but there's an error window between the death and its detection — the client must have a retry to another instance. Client-side also ties you to a stack (an Eureka client isn't available everywhere), so in k8s server-side is the default.","hook":"Client-side — you call directory assistance yourself, get all the numbers, and decide who to dial. Server-side — you call a single call center, and the operator routes you to an available specialist.","more":"**The problem: in a dynamic environment IPs and instance counts change (autoscaling, restarts, deploys), a static config is impossible.**\n- **Client-side discovery** — the client itself queries the service registry (Netflix Eureka, Consul), gets the current list of healthy instances, and applies a balancing algorithm itself (round-robin, least-conn). Pro: fewer hops, flexible balancing. Con: discovery+LB logic is duplicated in every client in every language.\n- **Server-side discovery** — the client sends the request to a single stable endpoint (AWS ELB, NGINX, Kubernetes Service). The router queries the registry itself and distributes. Pro: the client is simple, unaware of discovery. Con: an extra network hop, and the load balancer must be made fault-tolerant.\n- The **service registry** in both cases is a DB of live instances; instances register themselves (self-registration) or are registered by the platform (third-party registration via health checks).","quizCorrect":"Client-side: the client queries the registry itself and picks an instance; server-side: the client hits a load balancer that resolves on its behalf","quizWrong":["Client-side: the load balancer picks the instance; server-side: the client reads the registry itself and decides for itself where to go for the instance it needs","The only difference is that server-side works over HTTP and client-side over gRPC","Client-side needs no registry at all — the client scans the network looking for live instances"],"picCap":"client-side: the client queries the registry and picks itself; server-side: a load balancer resolves on the client's behalf"},"ap-saga-orchestration-vs-choreography":{"q":"Saga: orchestration vs choreography — how does a central conductor differ from an event chain, and when do you pick which?","a":"Saga — a way to keep consistency in a distributed transaction without 2PC: a business operation is split into local transactions, each with a compensation (an undo action). Orchestration — there's a central coordinator (saga orchestrator) that explicitly commands the steps and, on failure, runs compensations; the logic is in one place, easy to observe, but the coordinator is a bottleneck. Choreography — no coordinator: each service listens to events and reacts with its step, emitting a new event; no single point of failure, but the logic is smeared out and the whole flow is hard to grasp. The price of saga overall — no isolation (intermediate states are visible) and complex compensations.","d":"Compensation is not a perfect rollback: you don't 'undo' a money debit, you do a reverse operation (a refund), which may have its own side effects.","note":"⚠️ Saga's main trap is the lack of isolation: between steps, the intermediate state is VISIBLE to others (money already debited, goods not yet reserved) — these are anomalies like dirty reads, mitigated with semantic locks and 'in progress' flags. And compensations must be idempotent and almost always succeed: if the refund itself fails, you have no 'compensation for the compensation' — you need manual investigation and an alert.","hook":"An orchestra with a conductor — one waves the baton, everyone plays on cue. A round dance — no conductor, each watches their neighbor and joins on a signal. Both make music, but fail differently.","more":"**Saga solves the pain of distributed transactions: 2PC (two-phase commit) locks resources and doesn't scale, so consistency is made eventual via a chain of local transactions with compensations.**\n- **Orchestration** — the saga orchestrator (often on Camunda/Zeebe) knows the whole scenario, invokes services in turn, and on failure runs compensations in reverse order itself. Pros: logic is centralized, the flow is visible, easy to add a step, easier to test and monitor. Cons: the coordinator is a coupling point and a potential bottleneck, with the risk of turning it into a god-class.\n- **Choreography** — each service subscribes to events and publishes its own; nobody owns the scenario as a whole. Pros: loose coupling, no single point of failure, easy to add a new subscriber. Cons: the end-to-end flow isn't written down explicitly anywhere — hard to understand and debug, easy to get cycles and implicit dependencies.\n- **Selection rule:** few steps and loose coupling — choreography; many steps, complex rollback logic, observability needed — orchestration.","quizCorrect":"Orchestration — a central coordinator explicitly commands the steps and compensations; choreography — services react to events without a coordinator","quizWrong":["Orchestration uses events, while choreography uses direct synchronous calls to a coordinator made by each service in turn along the chain of the business process","Choreography guarantees ACID isolation, while orchestration does not","Saga is needed only for read operations; for writes you always use 2PC"],"picCap":"Orchestration: a center commands the steps; choreography: services react to events in a chain"},"jvm-stackheap":{"q":"Where do local primitives, references, and the objects themselves live?","a":"Local primitives and reference variables live on the method's stack frame (alive while the method runs). The objects themselves always live on the heap and are reclaimed by the GC.","d":"That's why an object with no references from the stack or statics is a candidate for collection.","hook":"The stack is a pocket: leave the room and you empty it all out. The heap is a shared warehouse where the objects sit until they're hauled away.","more":"Every method call has its own **stack frame** in the thread. It holds local primitives (`int`, `boolean`, etc.) and **reference variables** — the \"pointer\" itself, not the object. The frame disappears when the method returns.\n\nThe objects themselves (everything created with `new`, including arrays) always live on the **heap** — shared across all threads and managed by the GC. A reference on the stack just points to an object on the heap.\n\n- So an object stays alive as long as there's at least one reachable reference to it.\n- Deep recursion overflows the stack (`StackOverflowError`); too many objects overflow the heap (`OutOfMemoryError`).","quizCorrect":"Primitives and references on the stack frame, objects always on the heap","quizWrong":["All local variables and objects live on the heap; the stack is only for calls","Primitives on the heap, objects on the stack for faster access","Objects on the stack while small, then move to the heap as they grow"],"picCap":"Primitives and references on the stack; objects on the heap","note":"Trap: \"objects always live on the heap\" isn't absolute. The JIT, via Escape Analysis, can decompose an object that never escapes the method into scalar fields right on the stack/registers (scalar replacement), so the new may allocate nothing on the heap at all. That's why microbenchmarks with local objects lie unless you disable EA or let the object escape."},"jvm-stw":{"q":"What is stop-the-world, and why is calling System.gc() evil?","a":"STW is the moment when the GC freezes ALL application threads. System.gc() forcibly triggers a heavy full collection and a long pause where you don't need one.","d":"Modern GCs (G1, ZGC) minimize STW. Leave collection to them.","note":"System.gc() can be disabled with the -XX:+DisableExplicitGC flag (often set in prod), and then the call is silently ignored. But be careful: some libraries (e.g. off-heap DirectByteBuffer/Netty) rely on System.gc() to release native memory in time — with GC disabled you can hit a 'Direct buffer memory' OOM.","hook":"The cleaner switches off the lights for everyone at once. System.gc() is you calling a deep clean in the middle of the workday.","more":"**Stop-the-world** is the phase when the garbage collector **freezes all** application threads so it can safely walk the object graph. The application is halted during this time; the more live objects there are, the longer the pause.\n\n`System.gc()` is a request to run a **full** collection right now. It triggers a heavy STW pause where the JVM wouldn't have done one, and breaks the collector's own fine-tuning.\n\n- In production this shows up as sudden lag/timeouts.\n- Trust the GC's automation; if needed it can be disabled with the `-XX:+DisableExplicitGC` flag.","quizCorrect":"STW freezes all threads; System.gc() triggers a heavy collection and pause","quizWrong":["STW only stalls the GC thread, and System.gc() speeds up memory cleanup quite noticeably","System.gc() is guaranteed to free memory immediately, with no downsides","STW is an application deadlock, and System.gc() actually fixes it"],"picCap":"System.gc() = manual Full GC, an unnecessary long STW pause"},"jvm-g1":{"q":"How does G1 work in simple terms, and what is it for?","a":"G1 splits the heap into many small regions and collects the ones with the most garbage first (\"garbage first\"), staying within a target pause time. This keeps pauses predictable even on a large heap.","d":"-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis. Default since Java 9.","hook":"You clean the dirtiest room first — and still make it within the allotted time before the guests arrive.","more":"G1 (Garbage-First) splits the heap not into a couple of big zones but into **many small regions** of equal size, each dynamically playing the role of Eden/Survivor/Old. It continuously tracks where the most garbage has piled up.\n\nWhen collecting, G1 takes the **dirtiest** regions first (hence the name) and collects as many as it can fit within your **target pause** (`-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis`). This keeps pauses predictable even on a heap of tens of gigabytes.\n\n- The default collector since Java 9 — a good throughput/latency balance.\n- The pause target is a goal, not a hard guarantee; too small a target cuts throughput.","quizCorrect":"Splits the heap into regions, collects the garbagiest ones, holds the target pause","quizWrong":["Collects the whole heap in one long pause for reliability","Never does stop-the-world, runs fully in parallel","Splits the heap into two regions, young and old, and cleans only old ones during a pause"],"picCap":"Garbage First: clean the dirtiest regions — pauses on target"},"jvm-metaspace":{"q":"Where is class metadata stored, and why isn't it on the heap?","a":"In metaspace — separate native memory outside the heap. Class structures are loaded there. A leak here = uncontrolled generation of classes/classloaders.","d":"Before Java 8 this was PermGen. OOM: Metaspace ≠ OOM: heap.","note":"By default metaspace is unbounded and grows as long as there's native memory — people forget to set -XX:MaxMetaspaceSize. The classic leak: hot-redeploy in an app server, or dynamic generation of proxies/classes (CGLIB, lambdas via reflection), when old ClassLoaders aren't collected because of dangling references.","hook":"Building blueprints aren't kept inside the apartments themselves but in a separate archive; keep churning out blueprints endlessly and the archive bursts.","more":"When loading a class, the JVM creates its internal description: fields, methods, bytecode, the constant pool, a reference to the loader. These structures go into **metaspace** — a region of the OS's **native memory**, not the heap where ordinary objects live. Before Java 8 this was PermGen inside the heap with a fixed size.\n\nIt was moved out because class metadata follows different rules: its amount depends not on the number of objects but on the number of loaded classes and classloaders. Metaspace grows dynamically (bounded by `-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize`).\n\n- A leak here = uncontrolled class generation: dynamic proxies, frequent redeploys, CGLIB, scripting engines.\n- The symptom is `OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace`, while the heap is free.","quizCorrect":"In metaspace — native memory outside the heap; class structures are loaded there","quizWrong":["In the heap's young generation, so it's cleaned on every minor GC","On the stack of the thread that loaded the class first","In PermGen inside the heap — metaspace is just its new name in the heap after the rename"],"picCap":"Class metadata is in metaspace (native), not the heap"},"jvm-leak":{"q":"If Java has a GC, can there still be a memory leak?","a":"Yes. The GC only removes things that have NO live references. Static collections, unremoved listeners, and growing caches hold references — the objects live forever and memory leaks.","d":"Classics: a static Map used as a cache with no eviction; a ThreadLocal in a thread pool.","hook":"The garbage collector won't touch what someone is holding in their hands. A forgotten list holds the item forever — and it keeps piling up.","more":"The GC works by reachability: an object is alive as long as there's a chain of **live references** to it from GC roots (thread stacks, statics). No references — it gets collected. A leak in Java isn't \"lost\" memory but objects you **accidentally keep a reference to** so they can never be reached for cleanup.\n\nThat's exactly why the GC isn't a cure-all: it can't tell \"still needed\" from \"forgot to release.\"\n\n- Classics: a growing `static Map` cache with no eviction, listeners/callbacks you never unregistered, a ThreadLocal in a thread pool, unclosed resources.\n- Cured with weak references (WeakHashMap), bounded caches (Caffeine with eviction), and explicit removal from collections.","quizCorrect":"Yes: the GC won't touch objects with live references (statics, caches, listeners)","quizWrong":["No, by definition the GC rules out any memory leaks","A leak is only possible with manual memory allocation via native code through JNI calls","A leak only happens when the GC is disabled with the -XX:-UseGC flag"],"picCap":"Yes: the GC only cleans the unreferenced — a reference holds it, memory leaks","note":"Trap: a ThreadLocal in a thread pool leaks even though the key is a WeakReference — only the reference to the ThreadLocal object itself is weak, while the value is held by a strong reference in the live pool thread's ThreadLocalMap and isn't cleared while the thread is alive. Always call remove() in a finally block instead of relying on the ThreadLocal's \"weakness\"."},"x-jvm-generations":{"q":"What are heap generations (Young/Old), and why does collection run faster because of them?","a":"The heap is split into Young (Eden + 2 Survivors) and Old. Almost all objects die young (the \"weak generational hypothesis\"). Minor GC cleans only Young — fast. Survivors accumulate, and after surviving N times move to Old. Old is cleaned by the rare and expensive Major/Full GC.","d":"An object is born in Eden. Minor GC copies the live ones into a Survivor (S0/S1, a copying scheme — survivors move, the dead simply aren't copied, no fragmentation). The age counter grows; on reaching MaxTenuringThreshold (default up to 15) the object is \"promoted\" (tenuring) to Old. The idea: separate by age so you don't scan the whole heap every time. Large objects (humongous in G1) may go straight to Old. In G1 the generations are logical — sets of regions, not contiguous zones.","hook":"The nursery (Eden) is noisy and cleaned often; the pensioners (Old) are quiet — cleaning there is rare","more":"The heap is split into **Young** (Eden + two Survivors, S0/S1) and **Old**. New objects are born in Eden. When it fills up, a **Minor GC** fires: the live ones are copied into a Survivor, the dead are simply discarded. Those surviving several cycles (the age counter) are \"promoted\" to Old.\n\nWhy it's fast: the **weak generational hypothesis** holds — the vast majority of objects die young. Minor GC scans only the small Young and copies just the few survivors, not the whole heap. That's cheap.\n\n- Gotcha: what lives long ends up in Old, which is cleaned by the rare **Major/Full GC** — expensive, with long pauses. Leaks and premature promotion bloat Old and make heavy collections more frequent.","quizCorrect":"Almost all objects die young, and Minor GC cleans only the small Young","quizWrong":["Old is always smaller than Young, so Full GC completes instantly and pauses nothing","Young needs no thread pausing, and Old is cleaned without pauses","Objects in Old aren't scanned by the GC at all, saving time"],"picCap":"the young are born in Eden, survivors move along the arrow to Old"},"x-jvm-references":{"q":"Strong / Soft / Weak / Phantom references — what's the difference, and why do non-strong references exist?","a":"Strong (the usual one) — while it exists, the object is alive. Soft — the GC collects only under memory pressure (cache). Weak — collected on the very first GC if there's no strong reference (WeakHashMap). Phantom — to control the moment of finalization/resource cleanup instead of finalize().","d":"Reachability via the strongest reference decides an object's fate. SoftReference is good for memory-sensitive caches: the JVM keeps it while heap is sufficient and drops it before OOM. WeakReference is for canonicalizing maps and metadata tied to an object (WeakHashMap clears the entry when the key dies). PhantomReference can't be get() (always null) — it's enqueued in a ReferenceQueue AFTER the object is already unreachable, to safely release native resources (a replacement for the unreliable finalize/Cleaner). All three non-strong kinds can be registered with a ReferenceQueue.","note":"SoftReference is NOT a cure for cache leaks: the GC clears them only under memory pressure and often too late — long Full GCs start right before OOM. finalize() is deprecated and unreliable (no guarantee of being called or of timing) — for resource cleanup use PhantomReference + Cleaner or try-with-resources.","hook":"Strong — you're holding it in your hands; soft — in the closet until cleaning day; weak — on the nightstand, swept away at once","more":"A reference's strength dictates when the GC may collect the object. **Strong** — the ordinary reference: while it exists, the object is untouchable. **Soft** — the object stays alive as long as there's enough memory; the GC discards it only just before `OutOfMemoryError`. Perfect for **caches**.\n\n**Weak** — collected on the very first GC if there are no strong references to the object; `WeakHashMap` is built on this, where an entry vanishes as soon as the key is no longer needed by anyone. **Phantom** — the weakest: you can't retrieve the value through it; it's there to learn the moment the object is definitely unreachable and to release resources cleanly.\n\n- Why non-strong: to break the retention of objects in memory and replace the unreliable `finalize()` with cleanup managed via a `ReferenceQueue`.","quizCorrect":"Only under memory pressure — which is why Soft is handy for caches","quizWrong":["On the very first GC, like a WeakReference","Never, as long as the thread that created the reference is alive and holds it","Right after exiting the method where the reference was created"],"picCap":"reference strength = object survivability"},"x-jvm-tlab-escape":{"q":"What are TLAB and escape analysis — how can the JIT avoid allocating an object on the heap at all?","a":"TLAB is a slice of Eden private to a thread: allocation = bumping a pointer, no locks. Escape analysis — the JIT checks whether an object \"escapes\" the method; if it doesn't, it can break it into fields in registers/stack (scalar replacement), and the new never hits the heap.","d":"TLAB (Thread-Local Allocation Buffer) removes contention: each thread pours new objects into its own buffer via bump-the-pointer, and synchronization is needed only when the buffer runs out. Escape analysis — a compile-time analysis in C2/JIT: NoEscape → the object is local → scalar replacement (fields live as ordinary local variables, the GC doesn't know about it); it also enables lock elision (removing unneeded synchronization). Important caveat: this is a JIT optimization, it's not guaranteed and only works after warmup; in the interpreter the object is still on the heap. So an \"object on the stack\" is a consequence of scalar replacement, not a separate language entity.","note":"Escape analysis and scalar replacement only work in C2 after warmup and break over trivia: the object 'escapes' if you pass it to a non-inlined method, store it in a field/array, or synchronize on it. You can't count on 'the JIT will remove it all' — in benchmarks this masks real allocations (you need JMH with Blackhole).","hook":"Every cook has their own tray (TLAB); and if the dish was eaten on the spot — it was never put on display (escape analysis)","more":"**TLAB** (Thread-Local Allocation Buffer) is a private slice of Eden per thread. Allocation = a simple pointer bump (`bump-the-pointer`), no synchronization, because the buffer is exclusively owned. When it runs out, the thread grabs a new TLAB. This makes object creation nearly free and scalable across cores.\n\n**Escape analysis** — a JIT analysis: does the reference \"escape\" beyond the method (into a field, another thread, the return value)? If the object doesn't escape, it can avoid heap allocation entirely and, via **scalar replacement**, decompose it into individual fields in registers/stack.\n\n- Effect: a `new` in a hot method can vanish as an allocation — zero GC load. But it's a JIT optimization: it breaks on escape (storing into a collection, passing to another thread), and it isn't present in cold/interpreted code.","quizCorrect":"Decompose it into fields on the stack/registers (scalar replacement), bypassing the heap","quizWrong":["Move it straight to Old, bypassing Young and Minor GC","Cache it in a neighboring thread's TLAB for reuse","Replace new with a reference from the JVM's object pool kept alive for the whole run of the application"],"picCap":"each thread pours objects into its own Eden lane — no locks"},"x-jvm-oom-types":{"q":"What kinds of OutOfMemoryError exist, and how do you tell them apart (Java heap space, Metaspace, GC overhead, direct buffer)?","a":"\"Java heap space\" — the heap ran out (too small -Xmx or a leak). \"Metaspace\" — too many loaded classes. \"GC overhead limit exceeded\" — the GC spins >98% of the time, freeing <2%. \"Direct buffer memory\" — off-heap space for ByteBuffer/Netty is exhausted.","d":"OOME is an Error, not an Exception: you usually can't catch it and continue. Tell them apart by the message text: heap space → take a heap dump (-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError) and look at dominators/retained size in Eclipse MAT; Metaspace → a classloader leak (frequent redeploy, dynamic proxy/class generation); GC overhead → a symptom of an imminent heap OOM, the heap is nearly full; \"unable to create native thread\" → an OS limit/too many threads, not heap; direct buffer memory → off-heap (-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize), a DirectByteBuffer is freed only when its wrapper is GC'd. The fix: first find the cause with a dump, not just bump -Xmx.","note":"OutOfMemoryError is an Error, not an Exception; catching it with catch(Exception) is pointless, and after it the JVM is often in an inconsistent state. 'GC overhead limit exceeded' is deceptive: the heap is formally not full, but the application is essentially dead. For post-mortem analysis set -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError.","hook":"Don't put out the fire with a -Xmx canister: take a dump and find exactly where it's leaking","more":"`OutOfMemoryError` is a family, and the text after the colon points to the cause. **Java heap space** — the main heap ran out: either `-Xmx` is too small or there's a **leak** (objects held by strong references). **Metaspace** — the class metadata region overflowed: too many loaded classes, often due to classloader leaks (hot redeploy, proxy generation).\n\n**GC overhead limit exceeded** — the JVM spends >98% of the time on GC, freeing <2%: the heap is nearly full, collections run in vain — a precursor to heap space. **Direct buffer memory** — **off-heap** memory for `ByteBuffer.allocateDirect`/Netty is exhausted; it isn't bounded by `-Xmx` but by `-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize`.\n\n- Diagnosis: heap/overhead — take a **heap dump** and look for dominators; Metaspace — count classes/classloaders; direct — check limits and native buffer leaks.","quizCorrect":"The GC spends >98% of the time freeing <2% of memory","quizWrong":["The limit on the number of concurrently running GC threads is exceeded","Off-heap memory for direct ByteBuffers is exhausted","Too many classes are loaded, Metaspace overflowed"],"picCap":"different sources — different diagnosis and fix"},"jvm-jre-jdk":{"q":"JVM vs JRE vs JDK — what's the difference?","a":"The JVM is the engine that runs your Java code (bytecode). JRE = engine + ready-made parts (libraries) to RUN a program. JDK = JRE + the javac machine and tools to BUILD and DEVELOP a program. Writing code → you need the JDK. Just running → the JRE is enough.","d":"Nesting: JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM. The JVM executes platform-independent bytecode (.class), hence \"write once, run anywhere.\" javac (in the JDK) turns .java → .class; java (in the JRE) starts the JVM and loads .class. Other JDK tools: jar, javadoc, jdb, jshell. Since Java 11 the separate public JRE was removed — people usually install the full JDK.","hook":"Russian nesting dolls: the JVM is the engine inside, the JRE is the car to drive, the JDK is the garage with tools to build it.","more":"**The JVM** is the virtual machine: it reads platform-independent **bytecode** (.class), interprets it, and JIT-compiles it into machine code for the specific OS. This is what gives \"write once, run anywhere.\"\n\n**The JRE** = JVM + standard libraries (java.lang, collections, etc.) — the minimum to **run** a finished application. **The JDK** = JRE + developer tools: the javac compiler, the jdb debugger, jar, javadoc — everything for **building**.\n\n- In practice: since Java 11 Oracle no longer ships a separate JRE — you install the JDK and trim a runtime with jlink if needed.\n- Gotcha: \"no javac\" almost always means only the JRE is installed, not the JDK.","quizCorrect":"JDK — includes the javac compiler and development tools","quizWrong":["JRE — it has libraries and a compiler","JVM — it compiles sources itself","Only the JVM; the JDK is only needed for running the code"],"picCap":"JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM: each larger one includes the smaller","note":"The 'write once, run anywhere' slogan is about bytecode, but the class major version is hard-bound: a class compiled by JDK 17 (class file version 61) fails with UnsupportedClassVersionError on JVM 11. That's why in prod you set the target via --release (not just -target); otherwise you can accidentally use an API newer than the runtime and get a NoSuchMethodError at startup."},"g-jvm-jit-tiers":{"q":"Why does HotSpot have two compilers, C1 and C2, and what is \"warmup\"?","a":"Code first goes through the interpreter (slow, but immediate). Frequently called methods are JIT-compiled: C1 — fast and rough, C2 — slow but heavily optimized. Warmup = until the counters accumulate statistics, the code isn't optimized and the first requests are slow.","d":"Tiered compilation: levels 0 (interpreter) → 1-3 (C1 with profiling) → 4 (C2). Thresholds: -XX:CompileThreshold, -XX:TierStopAtLevel. That's why benchmarks without warmup lie — you need JMH. C2 uses the profile from C1 for speculative optimizations.","note":"Because of warmup, the first thousands of requests after startup are slow — critical for short jobs, serverless/FaaS, and load tests without warmup (you'll get understated numbers). Solutions: -XX:+TieredCompilation (default), AppCDS, or AOT/JIT cache (Project Leyden / GraalVM native-image) to remove warmup.","hook":"A car in winter: you drive slowly at first, then once it's warmed up you fly.","more":"**Under the hood:** the JVM first **interprets** bytecode — instant startup, but slow. Each method/loop has invocation and iteration counters. Once a threshold is exceeded, the JIT kicks in. **C1** (client) compiles fast, with light optimizations and embedded profiling counters. **C2** (server) kicks in for \"hot\" methods: it compiles slowly but aggressively (inlining, escape analysis).\n\n**Why two:** the \"fast response vs peak performance\" tradeoff — tiered compilation gives you both.\n\n**The \"warmup\" gotcha:** until the profile is gathered, hot code isn't optimized yet and the first thousands of requests are slow. That's why benchmarks warm up the JVM (JMH), and p99 latency at startup is always worse.","quizCorrect":"C2 compiles longer but produces more heavily optimized code","quizWrong":["C2 interprets bytecode, while C1 compiles","C1 is for servers, C2 only for desktop clients and laptops only","C2 works without a profile, C1 requires warmup"],"picCap":"cold code warms up to C2"},"g-jvm-deopt":{"q":"What is deoptimization, and why does an already-compiled method suddenly fall back to the interpreter?","a":"C2 makes speculative bets: \"this type is always this,\" \"this branch never runs.\" If a bet doesn't pan out (a new class was loaded, a rare branch was taken) — the JVM discards the compiled code, returns to the interpreter, and then recompiles.","d":"Triggers: an uncommon trap (rare branch), class loading breaking the CHA-based inlining of a monomorphic call, a wrong type profile. On graphs you see a temporary perf drop after a deopt. Too-frequent deopts = the JIT can't stabilize (megamorphic call sites).","note":"A frequent performance gotcha is megamorphic call sites: if 3+ different types arrive at a single call site, the JIT can't devirtualize/inline and leaves an expensive virtual call. Constant deopts show up in -XX:+PrintCompilation as 'made not entrant'.","hook":"You took a shortcut along the path — hit a fence, went back to the road.","more":"**Under the hood:** C2 compiles based on the profile and makes **speculative assumptions**: \"here it's always type `X`,\" \"this branch/exception never runs,\" \"this class is effectively final.\" Cheap checks (uncommon traps) are embedded in the code. If an assumption is violated — the trap fires.\n\n**Why the rollback:** the compiled code is correct only under those assumptions. The JVM discards it, transfers execution (on-stack replacement) back to the interpreter, gathers more profile, and later recompiles taking the new fact into account.\n\n**Triggers:** loading a new class (breaks the call's monomorphism), the first pass into a rare branch, a null/class cast that \"shouldn't have\" happened. Gotcha: a burst of deopts = a latency dip; visible in `-XX:+PrintCompilation`.","quizCorrect":"A wrong JIT speculation (a new type/class, a rare branch)","quizWrong":["A heap overflow and the GC firing","An application's call to System.gc()","A second method reaching CompileThreshold during the same run"],"picCap":"the bet didn't pan out → rollback"},"g-jvm-inlining":{"q":"What is inlining, and why is it the \"mother of all optimizations\" in the JIT?","a":"The JIT inserts the callee's body right at the call site — removing the call overhead and enabling other optimizations (escape analysis, constant folding) across the method boundary. Without inlining, most other optimizations don't work.","d":"Limits: -XX:MaxInlineSize (default 35 bytes), FreqInlineSize (325). Megamorphic calls (>2 implementations per call site) aren't inlined — that's why long chains of virtual calls are slow. Small getters are inlined almost always.","hook":"You don't call a courier for the salt — you just take it off your own shelf.","more":"**Under the hood:** instead of a call instruction, the JIT copies the **callee's body** straight to the call site. The overhead vanishes: creating a stack frame, passing arguments, the jump.\n\n**Why the \"mother of optimizations\":** most analyses work within a single method. After inlining the boundaries disappear and the optimizer sees everything at once — **escape analysis** becomes possible (the object doesn't escape → stack/scalarization), along with constant folding, dead code elimination, and check removal across the call chain.\n\n**Gotcha:** inlining is bounded by a budget — large methods (`-XX:MaxInlineSize`, `FreqInlineSize`) and megamorphic calls (many interface implementations) aren't inlined. Hence the rule: keep hot methods small, avoid megamorphism.","quizCorrect":"It enables the other optimizations across the method boundary","quizWrong":["It shrinks the class file size on disk","It replaces the interpreter and removes warmup","It guarantees the object won't reach the Old gen of the heap during collection"],"picCap":"the call drives off, the body of f() lights up in its place","note":"C2 inlining is speculative: at a bimorphic call-site (2 implementations) the JIT inlines both branches behind a type-guard, but if a third class is later loaded the call-site becomes megamorphic and the code is deoptimized back to the interpreter. So adding a new interface implementation can silently slow down long-running hot code; diagnose via -XX:+PrintInlining and deopt logs (-XX:+TraceDeoptimization)."},"g-jvm-zgc-shenandoah":{"q":"How do ZGC and Shenandoah fundamentally differ from G1, and what are they for?","a":"G1 still does noticeable STW pauses on large heaps. ZGC and Shenandoah relocate (evacuate) objects and fix up references concurrently — alongside the application. Pauses are sub-millisecond almost regardless of heap size (even terabytes).","d":"Achieved via load/store barriers and colored pointers (ZGC) / Brooks forwarding (old Shenandoah). The price: slightly higher throughput overhead and memory. Generational ZGC — an option since JDK 21 (-XX:+ZGenerational), default since JDK 23. The choice: need predictable low latency on a big heap → ZGC; throughput batch → Parallel.","hook":"Road repair at night on one lane — traffic never stops.","more":"**Under the hood:** G1 does evacuation (relocating live objects) in **stop-the-world**: the bigger the heap and the live data, the longer the pause. ZGC and Shenandoah relocate objects and fix up references to them **concurrently** with the running application. ZGC uses **colored pointers** + a load barrier; Shenandoah — a **Brooks/forwarding pointer** + barriers — so that an access to a relocating object always leads to the current copy.\n\n**Why:** for services with a large heap (tens of GB–TB), p99 latency matters more than throughput.\n\n**Result/gotcha:** pauses are sub-millisecond almost regardless of heap size. The price: higher barrier overhead (CPU), slightly less throughput, and more memory. For small heaps G1/Parallel is often more efficient.","quizCorrect":"Concurrent compaction: pauses ~sub-millisecond on any heap","quizWrong":["No garbage collection at all","Maximum throughput for batch jobs","They don't use generations or barriers of any kind at all in their design"],"picCap":"ZGC: pause near zero","note":"ZGC/Shenandoah sub-millisecond pauses don't mean sub-millisecond application latency: under a high allocation rate the collector can't free memory concurrently fast enough and an allocation stall kicks in — the thread requesting memory blocks until GC catches up. The fix isn't \"enable ZGC\" but raising heap/concurrent GC threads (-XX:ConcGCThreads), otherwise p99 latency spikes despite \"<1ms pauses\"."},"g-jvm-classloader-delegation":{"q":"How does parent-delegation work in class loaders, and why is it needed?","a":"Before loading a class itself, a loader asks its parent (upward: app → platform → bootstrap). The class is loaded by the first one that can. This prevents replacing java.lang.String with your own version and guarantees a single instance of the core classes.","d":"Hierarchy (JDK 9+): Bootstrap → Platform → Application. Class identity = (name + loading loader): one class loaded by two loaders is two different types, a ClassCastException. Tomcat/OSGi break delegation (child-first) to isolate applications.","note":"Parent-delegation breaks down where an application must see its own version of a class: web containers (Tomcat) and OSGi deliberately invert delegation (child-first). Hence the classic ClassCastException 'Foo cannot be cast to Foo' — the same class loaded by two different loaders is treated as different types.","hook":"You ask your boss before deciding yourself — the most senior one goes first.","more":"**Under the hood:** when a class is requested, a loader first **delegates to its parent** up the chain (Application → Platform → Bootstrap), and only if no one higher loaded it does it try itself. It's loaded by the **first one that can**, and the result is cached.\n\n**Why:** this guarantees that core classes (`java.lang.*`) are always loaded by Bootstrap, and can't be replaced with your own version from the classpath. Plus uniqueness: the same class won't load twice, and identity (the `Class` object) is unique within loader+name.\n\n**Gotcha:** class identity = (name + loading loader). One class loaded by two loaders is **different** types, hence the `ClassCastException` \"X cannot be cast to X.\" Containers/plugins sometimes break delegation (child-first) for version isolation.","quizCorrect":"The fully qualified class name plus the ClassLoader that loaded it","quizWrong":["Only the fully qualified class name (package + name) with no loader involved","The hash of the .class file's bytecode","The load sequence number in Metaspace"],"picCap":"the request travels upward — we ask the parent first"},"g-jvm-happens-before":{"q":"What is happens-before in the JMM, and why without it does one thread not see another's write?","a":"Without synchronization, the JVM/CPU may reorder and cache writes — a thread may see a stale value forever. happens-before is a visibility guarantee: if A hb B, then everything written before A is visible after B. It's provided by volatile, synchronized, final, join/start.","d":"Classics: a volatile write hb a volatile read of the same field; unlock hb lock; Thread.start() hb the thread's body; the thread's end hb join. volatile does NOT provide atomicity of a compound op (i++), only visibility + a ban on reordering around it. For counters — AtomicInteger.","note":"A common mistake is double-checked locking without volatile: without it another thread may see a reference to a not-fully-constructed object. And conversely: volatile on a REFERENCE to a collection doesn't make the collection itself thread-safe — happens-before covers only the write/read of the volatile field itself.","hook":"Your note will be seen only if you put it on the shared board, not in your own pocket.","more":"**Under the hood:** without synchronization, the JVM, compiler, and CPU are free to **reorder** instructions and keep values in registers/cache. So a reader thread may indefinitely see a stale value of a field written by another thread.\n\n**What it is:** **happens-before** is a partial-order relation from the JMM. If action A *hb* B, then everything written before A is guaranteed visible (and not reordered past B) for B.\n\n**What provides it:** a `volatile` write/read, entering/exiting `synchronized` (unlock hb a subsequent lock of the same monitor), `Thread.start`/`join`, initialization of `final` fields. Gotcha: hb provides **visibility and ordering, but not atomicity** — `count++` is still a race; and an object published incorrectly through a non-final field can be seen \"half-built.\"","quizCorrect":"Visibility of a write to other threads and a ban on reordering","quizWrong":["Atomicity of i++ for all threads","Mutual exclusion, like a synchronized block","That the field will never end up in the CPU cache of any core at any point"],"picCap":"T1's write reaches T2's read only along the hb edge"},"g-jvm-string-intern-dedup":{"q":"How does String interning differ from G1 String Deduplication?","a":"intern() — your explicit call: it puts the string into a shared pool and returns the canonical object, so == works. String dedup — G1's background work: it finds different String objects with identical content and makes them share one char[]/byte[]. The objects stay different, only the array is saved.","d":"intern: the pool is in native memory (not PermGen since JDK 7+), abuse = growth and contention. Dedup: -XX:+UseStringDeduplication (G1/some only), transparent, doesn't change identity, helps when there are many duplicate strings (JSON, DB). Don't confuse: dedup does not make == true.","note":"intern() on mass dynamic strings is an antipattern: before Java 7 it leaked into PermGen, now the pool is in the heap, but the strings are practically never collected and the pool table is fixed-size — frequent intern() degrades performance. String dedup (-XX:+UseStringDeduplication) is more beneficial and free for most applications.","hook":"intern — one shared key for everyone; dedup — everyone has their own keychain, but one blank key.","more":"**Under the hood:** `intern()` is an **explicit** call in your code: the string is put into the shared String pool, and the method returns the canonical instance; for identical literals after interning `==` works (the same object).\n\n**String Deduplication** is **background** work by G1 during GC: it scans live `String`s, finds objects with identical content, and switches their internal arrays to **one shared** `byte[]/char[]`.\n\n**The key difference:** with dedup the `String` objects themselves stay **different** (`==` is still false), only the backing array is saved. With intern the object becomes **one**. Gotcha: intern is manual and risks overflowing the pool and slowing things down; dedup is automatic and safer, enabled with `-XX:+UseStringDeduplication`, but only pays off with real duplicates.","quizCorrect":"Makes identical strings share one backing array","quizWrong":["Makes == true for equal strings, like intern()","Removes string duplicates from the heap entirely","Places all strings into the native String pool"],"picCap":"dedup: objects different, array one"},"g-jvm-heap-flags-ergonomics":{"q":"What are the key heap flags, and why is -Xmx==-Xms often set in production?","a":"-Xms — the heap's initial size, -Xmx — the maximum. If they differ, the JVM grows the heap gradually and pauses to resize/commit memory. People set -Xms == -Xmx to fix the heap up front, remove resizes, and remove latency instability.","d":"In containers: JVM 10+ reads cgroup limits; instead of -Xmx, -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75 is often used. GC metrics: -Xlog:gc* (JDK 9+), -verbose:gc (old). Handy: -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=... Don't forget that heap != the whole process (Metaspace, threads, direct, code cache on top).","note":"-Xmx == -Xms by itself doesn't physically commit memory — pages are allocated lazily on first access; to actually pin it and remove page faults you need -XX:+AlwaysPreTouch (at the cost of a longer startup). A separate gotcha in containers: on older JVMs -Xmx didn't account for the cgroup limit — set -XX:MaxRAMPercentage.","hook":"You rented the apartment for the whole term at once — no dragging furniture back and forth.","more":"**Under the hood:** the JVM reserves virtual address space for the heap but actually commits (hands to the OS) pages as it grows. When occupancy approaches the current size, the JVM expands the heap toward **-Xmx** — that's a memory commit + often a full GC to decide whether to grow.\n\n**Why -Xms == -Xmx:** it fixes the heap at startup. No step-wise resizes, no sudden pauses for a commit/Full GC to expand, and the memory is already \"warmed up.\"\n\n- **Gotcha:** on a server/container with dedicated resources this is the norm; on a laptop where RAM is shared with other processes, a hard -Xms grabs it all at once. Don't confuse it with GC tuning — the flags set the bounds, not the algorithm.","quizCorrect":"Fix the heap and remove pauses/instability from resizing","quizWrong":["This disables garbage collection in the Old generation","This makes the JVM ignore the container's cgroup limits entirely","This enables ZGC instead of G1 automatically"],"picCap":"fixing it removes resize pauses; a growing heap flashes on every expansion"},"g-jvm-safepoint":{"q":"What is a safepoint, and why can a long loop with no calls \"freeze\" the whole JVM?","a":"To do an STW (GC, deopt, dump), the JVM must stop all threads at safe points — safepoints. A thread in a hot counted loop with no safepoint check doesn't stop, and all the other threads wait for it — that's \"time to safepoint.\"","d":"The JIT inserts safepoint polls at method back-edges and in ordinary loops, but C2 may remove them from a \"counted loop\" (int counter). A long such loop → high TTSP, pauses look like GC even though GC has nothing to do with it. Diagnosis: -Xlog:safepoint. Fix: a long counter, split the loop.","note":"The JIT doesn't insert a safepoint check into a 'counted loop' with an int counter — so a long int loop can block reaching a safepoint for a long time and freeze all threads ('time to safepoint'). Replacing the counter with long or enabling -XX:+UseCountedLoopSafepoints fixes it. Diagnosis: -XX:+PrintSafepointStatistics / safepoint logs.","hook":"The bus won't move until the last passenger reaches their seat.","more":"**Under the hood:** for an STW operation (GC, deoptimization, thread dump) the JVM can't touch a live thread's stack. It raises an \"everyone to a safepoint\" flag and waits. A thread checks this flag at pre-inserted points: at loop back-edges, at method calls, at allocations.\n\n**Why it hangs:** for \"counted\" loops with an int counter, the JIT may remove the safepoint check as an optimization. The thread spins with no check points → doesn't see the flag → **everyone else waits for it** until the loop ends. That's the high \"time to safepoint.\"\n\n- **Gotcha/diagnosis:** enable `-XX:+PrintSafepointStatistics`/`safepoint` logs. Cured by switching to a long counter (loop strip mining) or by newer JVMs where such loops get a safepoint.","quizCorrect":"A thread in a counted loop with no safepoint poll doesn't stop","quizWrong":["The heap is full and the GC can't keep up cleaning it up in time","Too many threads are waiting on one monitor","Metaspace is exhausted and classes aren't unloaded"],"picCap":"one didn't reach the safepoint — everyone is stopped"},"qk2-buildtime-di":{"q":"Why does Quarkus start in milliseconds while Spring Boot takes seconds? What is fundamentally different about DI?","a":"Spring builds the bean graph at runtime: on startup it scans the classpath via reflection, reads annotations, and builds the context — that is the main startup cost. Quarkus (the ARC engine) does all of this work at BUILD time: it generates ready-made bytecode that wires the beans. At runtime almost nothing is scanned — hence the ms startup and lower RAM (no reflection metadata sitting in the heap).","d":"ARC = the Quarkus CDI container. It doesn't \"discover\" beans at startup — it already knows them, because the code that creates them is generated ahead of time.","note":"\"Build-time\" doesn't mean \"you can't configure at runtime\". @ConfigProperty values are read at startup; only the STRUCTURE of the bean graph is fixed at build time.","hook":"Spring assembles the furniture every morning when you walk into the house; Quarkus had it delivered pre-assembled from the factory.","more":"**Build-time DI** is the core idea of Quarkus. During the Maven build, the ARC extension processes annotations, validates injections, resolves qualifiers, and **generates Java bytecode** that creates and wires the beans.\n\n- No classpath scanning at runtime — there's nothing to scan, everything is already known.\n- Less metadata in the heap → less RAM.\n- Wiring errors (missing bean, ambiguity) are caught at **build time**, not in production.\n- This is also what makes native image (GraalVM) feasible, where reflection is expensive and undesirable.","quizCorrect":"Quarkus moves classpath scanning and bean-graph construction to build time, generating ready-made bytecode","quizWrong":["Quarkus uses a faster version of reflection at runtime","Quarkus caches the bean graph to a file on first startup and reads it afterward","Quarkus runs the Spring context on a separate thread so it doesn't block startup from the classpath during augmentation"],"picCap":"Spring spins up reflection at startup; Quarkus did everything at build time"},"qk2-cdi-scopes":{"q":"What's the difference between @ApplicationScoped, @Singleton, @RequestScoped and @Dependent in CDI/Quarkus?","a":"@ApplicationScoped — one instance per application, but accessed through a lazy client proxy (created on first access). @Singleton — also one per application, but WITHOUT a proxy, created eagerly/directly. @RequestScoped — its own instance per HTTP request. @Dependent — no scope of its own: a new instance per injection point, living as long as its owner.","d":"By default, a bean with no explicit scope in CDI gets @Dependent.","note":"You can't inject @RequestScoped into @Singleton directly without a proxy — you'd get a stale/wrong instance. @ApplicationScoped (with a proxy) doesn't have this problem, which is why it's the default scope for CDI beans in Quarkus.","hook":"ApplicationScoped is the shared service elevator for the whole building; RequestScoped is a disposable cup for each guest.","more":"**Scope = the rule for lifecycle and number of instances.**\n\n- **@ApplicationScoped** — a normal scope → a proxy is injected into the client. The real object is created lazily on the first method call. Safe to inject into @Singleton.\n- **@Singleton** — a pseudo-scope; a direct reference to the object is injected. Faster (no delegation through a proxy), but doesn't support client-proxy-dependent behavior.\n- **@RequestScoped** — lives within an HTTP request / Kafka message; ideal for request-bound data.\n- **@Dependent** — unbound: one instance per injection point.","quizCorrect":"@ApplicationScoped — one instance per application via a lazy client proxy","quizWrong":["@ApplicationScoped creates a new instance per application thread","@Singleton and @ApplicationScoped are completely identical, only the name differs","@RequestScoped creates one instance for the whole application, just like @Singleton"],"picCap":"How many instances and how long the bean lives in each scope"},"qk2-client-proxy":{"q":"What does \"@ApplicationScoped is injected through a client proxy\" mean, and why is it needed?","a":"When you inject an @ApplicationScoped bean, the field holds not the object itself but a generated proxy subclass. On every method call the proxy delegates to the real contextual instance. This gives laziness (the object is created on first access) and decouples lifecycles: a long-scoped bean can hold a reference to a short-scoped one without \"freezing\" a stale instance.","d":"That's why an @ApplicationScoped class can't be final and must have a no-arg constructor (otherwise the proxy can't be generated).","note":"The proxy requires a non-final class with an accessible no-arg constructor. A final class or only a parameterized constructor → an ARC build error. Final methods are not intercepted by the proxy.","hook":"A proxy is like a secretary: you always call the same number, and they figure out where the boss is sitting right now.","more":"A **client proxy** is an ARC-generated subclass of the bean that is substituted into the injection point in place of the real object.\n\n- **Laziness:** the real instance isn't created until the first method call — faster startup.\n- **Scope decoupling:** a @Singleton can hold a proxy to a @RequestScoped bean and always get the current request's instance.\n- **Injection stability:** the proxy is stable and doesn't depend on the target being recreated.\n\nThe cost is a small delegation overhead on each call (@Singleton has none).","quizCorrect":"The proxy delegates calls to the real contextual instance, providing laziness and lifecycle decoupling","quizWrong":["The proxy caches bean method-call results to speed things up","The proxy is only needed for AOP interception and isn't used with @ApplicationScoped in a normal application","The proxy creates a new bean instance on every method call"],"picCap":"The proxy intermediary delegates calls to the real bean"},"qk2-inject-vs-autowired":{"q":"How does @Inject in Quarkus differ from @Autowired in Spring? How does ARC resolve a bean at build time?","a":"@Inject is the CDI/Jakarta standard (not Spring's homegrown @Autowired annotation). The main difference is the MOMENT of resolution: Spring picks a candidate by type/qualifier at runtime via reflection. ARC does this at BUILD time — it analyzes types and qualifiers, selects the single matching bean, and generates a direct constructor/setter call. Ambiguity or a missing bean is a compile-time error, not a runtime one.","d":"Ambiguity is resolved via @Named or a custom qualifier, just like in CDI; the @Primary equivalent in CDI is @io.quarkus.arc.DefaultBean / alternatives.","note":"In Quarkus you can also use Spring annotations (@Autowired, @Component) via the quarkus-spring-di extension, but under the hood it's still ARC working at build time — it's a compatibility layer, not real Spring.","hook":"Spring fumbles for the right part at runtime; ARC already labeled at the factory which part screws in where.","more":"**At build time ARC builds a map: bean type (+ qualifiers) → concrete class.**\n\n- For each @Inject point it finds the single matching candidate by required type and qualifiers.\n- **0 candidates** → UnsatisfiedResolutionException at build time.\n- **>1 candidate** → AmbiguousResolutionException at build time (unless resolved by a qualifier/alternative).\n- The result is generated bytecode that directly calls the constructor / sets the field. No reflection at runtime.\n\nConstructor injection in CDI works without @Inject if there's a single constructor.","quizCorrect":"@Inject is the CDI standard, and ARC resolves the candidate and validates ambiguity at build time","quizWrong":["@Inject and @Autowired are the same annotation from a shared library shipped with the framework under a different name","ARC defers resolution until the first HTTP request to save memory","In Quarkus bean ambiguity is resolved by random choice at runtime"],"picCap":"ARC picks the bean and validates ambiguity already at build time"},"qk2-extension":{"q":"What is a Quarkus extension and why is its work split into deployment and runtime modules?","a":"An extension is a way to integrate a library into Quarkus so that as much work as possible moves to build time. It's split into two modules: deployment (runs at BUILD time — registers beans, classes for reflection, generates bytecode) and runtime (thin code that runs in production). The build processor produces BuildItems via @BuildStep, from which Quarkus assembles an optimized application.","d":"It's the deployment module that resolves reflection/proxying for you ahead of time, which keeps the runtime lightweight and native-friendly.","note":"The deployment module does NOT end up on the application's runtime classpath — a common point of confusion. Only the runtime artifact ships.","hook":"An extension is the factory (deployment) and its finished product (runtime): the heavy work happens at the factory, the customer receives a lightweight part.","more":"**An extension is the unit of integration in Quarkus.** Architecturally, almost every extension has two parts:\n\n- **deployment** — contains build processors with @BuildStep methods. They run on the JVM during the MAVEN BUILD and exchange data via typed BuildItems (e.g. ReflectiveClassBuildItem, AdditionalBeanBuildItem).\n- **runtime** — what actually ships to production: recorders, config, minimal code.\n\nThis is exactly why Quarkus is so fast: the extension \"chews through\" reflection, proxying, and wiring ahead of time and leaves the runtime ready-made bytecode.","quizCorrect":"The deployment module does the work at build time (@BuildStep → BuildItem); the runtime module stays as thin production code","quizWrong":["Both modules run at runtime; deployment is just for tests","An extension is a plugin that Quarkus loads via reflection at application startup","The deployment module ends up in the final artifact and runs in production alongside the runtime part of the extension itself"],"picCap":"deployment runs at build time, runtime is lightweight code in production"},"qk2-reflection-enemy":{"q":"Why is reflection the enemy of fast startup and native image, and how does ARC eliminate it?","a":"Runtime reflection is slow (looking up classes/methods, access checks) and opaque to GraalVM: when building a native image, static analysis can't see what will be invoked via reflection, and without explicit registration that code is simply stripped (closed-world). ARC eliminates this by replacing runtime reflection with generated direct bean-wiring bytecode — constructor and setter calls are known statically.","d":"Where reflection is still needed (Jackson, JPA entities), extensions register classes via ReflectiveClassBuildItem at build time.","note":"In native, an unexpected NoSuchMethodException/ClassNotFoundException — the prime suspect is a class not registered for reflection. Fix it with @RegisterForReflection or the appropriate extension.","hook":"Reflection is rummaging blindly through a drawer on every call; ARC labeled it ahead of time and grabs what's needed instantly.","more":"**Why reflection gets in the way.**\n\n- **Speed:** a reflective call is more expensive than a direct one (lookup, access checks, no inlining/JIT optimizations), and class metadata is kept in memory.\n- **Native (GraalVM):** operates on the **closed-world** principle — at build time EVERYTHING that will run must be known. Reflection is dynamic → the analyzer can't see it → classes are stripped as \"unreachable\".\n\n**How Quarkus fixes it:**\n- ARC generates direct bean-wiring bytecode — reflection in DI isn't needed at all.\n- For libraries that objectively need reflection, extensions register the classes (ReflectiveClassBuildItem) at build time, and GraalVM includes them explicitly.","quizCorrect":"ARC replaces runtime reflection with generated direct wiring bytecode, which speeds up startup and is compatible with native closed-world","quizWrong":["ARC bans reflection entirely across the whole application, including Jackson and JPA — which is exactly why startup ends up fast even on an ordinary JVM","Reflection is fast, but Quarkus disables it only to reduce jar size","GraalVM automatically finds all reflective calls on its own without registration"],"picCap":"ARC replaces runtime reflection with direct calls in bytecode"},"qk2-jvm-vs-native":{"q":"JVM mode vs native mode in Quarkus — what's the key tradeoff?","a":"Native (GraalVM) gives instant startup (tens of ms) and low RAM, because everything is already compiled to machine code and there's no JIT/warmup. JVM mode starts slower and eats more memory, but the JIT warms up over time and delivers higher peak throughput under sustained load. Roughly: native wins on startup and RAM, JVM wins on peak performance.","d":"↳ In native there are no profile-based JIT optimizations of hot code, so steady-state throughput is often lower than a warmed-up JVM.","note":"A native build is slow (minutes) and consumes a lot of RAM on the build machine — this hits CI time, not runtime.","hook":"Native is a sprinter off the blocks; a warmed-up JVM is a marathon runner over the distance.","more":"**JVM mode:** bytecode is executed by the JVM, and the JIT (C2) profiles hot paths and optimizes them at runtime. The cost is slow startup (seconds), large RAM (class metadata, JIT cache), and a warmup phase.\n\n**Native:** GraalVM/Mandrel compiles the application ahead of time (AOT) into a single executable. Startup is tens of milliseconds, RAM is 2–5x lower, no warmup.\n\nWhere to use which:\n- **Native** — serverless/FaaS, CLI, k8s with aggressive autoscaling, where cold start and pod density matter.\n- **JVM** — long-lived services under steady high load, where the JIT has time to warm up.","quizCorrect":"Native starts faster and uses less RAM, but the JVM with JIT gives higher peak throughput","quizWrong":["Native is always faster than the JVM on every metric, including peak throughput","The JVM starts faster than native but loses on memory consumption","Native and JVM differ only in the size of the final artifact; their speed is the same entirely"],"picCap":"Native is shorter on startup and RAM, the JVM is higher on peak throughput"},"qk2-closed-world":{"q":"What is the closed-world assumption in GraalVM native, and why does it break reflection?","a":"The closed-world assumption is the premise that during AOT compilation ALL code that could possibly run is visible. Native image performs static reachability analysis and discards everything not explicitly called. Reflection, dynamic class loading, and dynamic proxies access classes by string names at runtime — the analyzer can't see them, considers them dead, and strips them, so they fail in native.","d":"↳ That's why native has no lazy \"on-demand\" class loading: whatever isn't in the image at build time has nowhere to come from at runtime.","note":"In Quarkus most such cases are already covered by extensions at build time — your own reflection-using code you have to cover manually.","hook":"Native is a suitcase packer: it only packs what you named out loud; whisper a name at runtime and the item didn't make the trip.","more":"**Closed-world** is the foundation of native image: the compiler builds a reachability graph from the entry points and keeps only what can actually be reached statically. Everything else (classes, methods, fields) is removed for the sake of small size and fast startup.\n\nWhat breaks, because resolution goes by string at runtime:\n- **Reflection** — `Class.forName`, `getDeclaredMethod`, field access.\n- **Dynamic proxy** — `java.lang.reflect.Proxy`.\n- **Dynamic loading** of classes and resources.\n- **Serialization** (Jackson/JSON-B via reflection over getters).\n\nThe solution is to declare it ahead of time in config (reflection-config / `@RegisterForReflection`) so the analyzer includes the classes in the image.","quizCorrect":"AOT sees all code ahead of time and strips what's statically unreachable, while reflection accesses classes by name at runtime","quizWrong":["GraalVM bans reflection for security reasons, not because of reachability analysis of the whole program at build time rather than at runtime","Reflection works but is 100x slower in native due to the absence of a JIT","Closed-world means the application can't make outbound network calls"],"picCap":"The analyzer keeps reachable code and strips the blinking cell (reachable only via reflection)"},"qk2-register-for-reflection":{"q":"Why are @RegisterForReflection and reflection-config.json needed in a native build?","a":"They tell native image: \"these classes are used via reflection — don't strip them and keep metadata about their fields/methods/constructors\". Without this, static analysis considers the class unreachable (or strips its members), and runtime reflection fails with ClassNotFound/NoSuchMethod. `@RegisterForReflection` is a Quarkus annotation, `reflect-config.json` is a low-level GraalVM file with the same effect.","d":"↳ `@RegisterForReflection(targets = {...})` lets you register third-party classes (from libraries) that you can't annotate directly.","note":"The annotation registers only the class itself. If its fields hold nested types that also go through reflection, register them separately — otherwise you'll hit a failure on the nested object.","hook":"@RegisterForReflection is reserving a spot in the suitcase: you explicitly said \"I need this item\", and the packer won't throw it out.","more":"**When you need it:** your own DTOs for JSON (de)serialization, classes from libraries accessed via reflection/proxy, and anything selected \"by name\" at runtime.\n\n**Registration options:**\n- `@RegisterForReflection` on your own class.\n- `@RegisterForReflection(targets = {...})` on a marker class — for third-party classes without editing their sources.\n- `reflect-config.json` in `META-INF/native-image/` — the low-level GraalVM format, describing fields/methods precisely.\n- In Quarkus 3 a lot is covered by extensions' build-time processors, so for typical code (REST, Panache, Jackson from an extension) you don't need to do it by hand.\n\n**Symptom that you forgot:** works in JVM mode, fails only in native with ClassNotFound/NoSuchMethodException.","quizCorrect":"It keeps the class and its metadata in the native image so reflection doesn't fail at runtime","quizWrong":["It speeds up reflection by caching getDeclaredMethod results at build time of the native image","It marks the class as serializable, replacing the Serializable interface","It forces GraalVM to compile the class with JIT instead of AOT"],"picCap":"The annotation forces the class and its metadata into the image — reflection works at runtime"},"qk2-when-not-native":{"q":"When should you NOT go native and keep plain JVM mode?","a":"When the service is long-lived and runs under steady high load — there the warmed-up JIT delivers higher peak throughput than native, and the slow startup is amortized (started once a week — doesn't matter). Also skip it if the code heavily uses reflection/dynamics that are expensive to cover, or if a slow native build breaks CI. Cold start doesn't matter there, but losing peak performance does.","d":"↳ The JIT (C2) optimizes against the real profile: inlines hot methods, speculatively devirtualizes — native lacks this runtime optimization.","note":"Interview trap: \"native is always faster\" — false. Native is faster ONLY at startup; on steady-state throughput a warmed-up JVM is usually ahead.","hook":"A long-lived service under load is a marathon: let the JVM warm up and it overtakes native at the finish.","more":"**Native wins** where process life is short or starts are frequent: FaaS/serverless, CLI, scale-to-zero, high pod density (little RAM per pod).\n\n**JVM wins** on long-lived services under load:\n- **JIT (C2)** profiles hot code and optimizes more aggressively than static AOT.\n- **GC** on the JVM is more mature (G1, ZGC, Shenandoah) — in native only Serial GC was available for a long time in community builds; G1 and advanced GCs come with Oracle GraalVM / a subscription.\n- A slow startup once a week bothers no one.\n\nOther reasons to stay on the JVM: heavy reflection/agent bytecode manipulation, a slow and resource-heavy native build in CI, the need for full profiling/debugging with standard JVM tooling.","quizCorrect":"Long-lived services under steadily high load, where a warmed-up JIT gives higher peak throughput","quizWrong":["Small CLI utilities that start and immediately exit","Serverless functions where cold start is critical","Services on k8s with aggressive autoscaling and frequent pod recreation on every deploy of the whole fleet"],"picCap":"Over the long haul a warmed-up JIT overtakes native's flat plateau"},"qk2-aot-vs-jit":{"q":"AOT compilation in native image vs JIT — which is faster when?","a":"AOT (native image) compiles EVERYTHING ahead of time, so the code is fast from the very first millisecond — it wins on startup and on short-lived processes. JIT compiles lazily at runtime and first interprets/warms up, but then optimizes hot code against the real profile and overtakes AOT over the long haul. In short: AOT is faster at the start, JIT after warmup.","d":"↳ The JIT can do what AOT can't: speculative optimizations with deoptimization, inlining based on actual calls, profile-guided decisions — because it sees real behavior.","note":"GraalVM can also do PGO (profile-guided optimization) for native: gather a profile from an instrumented run and rebuild the image — this narrows the gap with JIT, but the feature is from Oracle GraalVM, not the free community build.","hook":"AOT is a pre-printed book: open it and read; JIT is a translator who stumbles at first, then runs more fluently than print.","more":"**AOT (native image):**\n- Full compilation at build time, closed-world.\n- Zero warmup, instant peak performance from the first request.\n- Optimizations are static — no data about real runtime behavior.\n\n**JIT:**\n- Starts via interpretation/C1, then C2 compiles hot methods.\n- Profile-guided: inlining, devirtualization, speculation with deoptimization on a miss.\n- Over the long haul delivers a higher throughput plateau.\n\n**Bottom line:** short process / frequent restarts / cold start → AOT. Long steady load → JIT. This follows directly from the earlier cards on startup-vs-throughput and closed-world.","quizCorrect":"AOT is fast right from startup; JIT is slower during warmup but afterward overtakes thanks to profile-based optimizations","quizWrong":["AOT is always faster than JIT because machine code is inherently faster than bytecode under every possible workload profile","JIT is faster from startup, and AOT only catches up under peak load","AOT and JIT give the same speed; the only difference is artifact size"],"picCap":"AOT leads at startup; after warmup JIT reaches a higher plateau"},"qk2-uni-vs-multi":{"q":"Mutiny has Uni and Multi. What's the difference and when do you use each?","a":"Uni — an async result that arrives exactly once (0 or 1 element) and completes: a DB response, a REST call, a file write. Multi — a stream of 0..N elements over time: Kafka events, rows from a stream, server-sent events. Roughly: Uni ≈ an async single-shot CompletableFuture, Multi ≈ an async Publisher (a stream).","d":"Both are lazy: nothing runs until something subscribes. In Quarkus the framework usually does the subscription itself when you return a Uni/Multi from an endpoint.","note":"Uni doesn't mean \"fast/synchronous\". Uni is still asynchronous — there's just a single result. What people confuse is the element-count semantics, not whether it blocks.","hook":"Uni is a parcel with a single order; Multi is a conveyor belt with a stream of parcels.","more":"**Uni** is a promise of a single result. It either emits one item or an error, and that's it. Ideal for request-response: a REST endpoint, a DB fetch, a call to another service.\n\n**Multi** is a stream of events. Items arrive over time, and there can be zero, one, or infinitely many. Suited for a Kafka consumer, SSE, reading a large file in chunks.\n\nThe key property of both is **laziness**: a chain of operators (`onItem`, `onFailure`, `transform`) only describes a plan. The real work starts only on `subscribe()`. In Quarkus the runtime usually subscribes for you.","quizCorrect":"Uni emits at most one element and completes; Multi is a stream of 0..N elements","quizWrong":["Uni is synchronous and blocking, Multi is asynchronous and non-blocking by their nature","Uni is for GET requests, Multi only for POST requests","Uni emits N elements, Multi exactly one (the reverse)"],"picCap":"Uni: one item and completion. Multi: a stream of items over time."},"qk2-imperative-vs-reactive":{"q":"RESTEasy Reactive: how does an imperative endpoint differ from a reactive one in terms of thread handling?","a":"A reactive endpoint (returns Uni/Multi) runs directly on the event-loop (I/O) thread — there are only a few of them (≈ the number of cores). An imperative endpoint (returns a plain object) Quarkus automatically moves to the worker pool, so that blocking code doesn't stall the event loop. The reactive path gives maximum throughput, provided the code never blocks anywhere.","d":"RESTEasy Reactive decides by the method signature: Uni/Multi → event-loop; a plain type → worker thread. This is what distinguishes it from the old RESTEasy Classic.","note":"Reactive != automatically faster. If inside a Uni you call blocking JDBC, you block the event loop and crash the throughput of the whole instance, not just one request.","hook":"The event loop is a single waiter for the whole room: it never sits down to wait for the kitchen, or the room comes to a halt.","more":"RESTEasy Reactive in Quarkus 3 is reactive by default. It looks at the method **signature**:\n\n- Return **Uni** or **Multi** → the method runs on the **event-loop** (I/O) thread. There are only a handful of them — roughly the number of CPU cores. This is very cheap on memory and fast.\n- Return a **plain type** (User, String) → Quarkus assumes the code may be blocking and dispatches it to the **worker pool** itself, so as not to freeze the event loop.\n\nThe key idea: on the event loop **you must not block** — one blocked thread stalls thousands of concurrent requests. So the reactive path requires the entire chain to be non-blocking (a reactive DB driver, a reactive HTTP client).","quizCorrect":"Returning Uni/Multi runs on the event loop; returning a plain object Quarkus moves to the worker pool","quizWrong":["A reactive and an imperative endpoint always run on the same thread pool","An imperative endpoint runs on the event loop, and a reactive one on the worker pool of the container that serves it","Quarkus picks the thread by HTTP method: GET on the event loop, POST on the worker"],"picCap":"Uni/Multi runs on the few event-loop threads; a plain return goes to the worker pool."},"qk2-blocking-annotation":{"q":"Why is @Blocking needed on a reactive route if the method returns Uni/Multi?","a":"Because, by the Uni/Multi signature, Quarkus considers the method non-blocking and puts it on the event loop. If inside it you do a blocking operation (JDBC, a legacy call, heavy CPU), @Blocking explicitly says: \"move me to the worker pool, don't run me on the event loop\". This saves the event loop from freezing.","d":"The inverse annotation is @NonBlocking: force a method onto the event loop that would otherwise go to the worker pool by its return type.","note":"The most common prod bug: returning a Uni and forgetting @Blocking when calling a blocking dependency. Under load the instance degrades not pointwise but entirely — all event-loop threads are busy.","hook":"@Blocking is a sign saying \"I'll be in the kitchen a while\", so the event-loop waiter is pulled away and you're seated at a separate worker table.","more":"Quarkus determines the thread type by signature, and here's the trap: the method **returns a Uni**, so Quarkus thinks \"non-blocking\" and puts it on the **event loop**. But inside you might call blocking code — old JDBC, the file system, a synchronous HTTP client, a heavy CPU computation.\n\nOn the event loop this is a catastrophe: one thread serves thousands of connections, and while it hangs in a blocking call — all those requests stall.\n\n**@Blocking** overrides the decision: \"run this method on the worker pool\". There blocking is safe — there are many threads and that's what they're for.\n\nThe paired annotation **@NonBlocking** does the opposite — it forces execution on the event loop for a method that would otherwise go to the worker.","quizCorrect":"@Blocking moves the method's execution off the event loop to the worker pool, saving the event loop from blocking","quizWrong":["@Blocking makes the method synchronous and removes the asynchrony of Uni","@Blocking adds a lock to the method for thread safety","@Blocking speeds up the method by running it on several threads in parallel and merging their results back together afterwards"],"picCap":"Without @Blocking, blocking code stalls the event loop; with @Blocking it goes to the worker."},"qk2-reactive-messaging-kafka":{"q":"How do you read/write Kafka in Quarkus via SmallRye Reactive Messaging? What do @Incoming and @Outgoing do?","a":"@Incoming(\"channel\") marks a method as a consumer of messages from a channel; @Outgoing(\"channel\") — as a producer. A channel maps to a Kafka topic in application.properties. A method with both annotations is a processor (reads from one channel, writes to another). SmallRye handles serialization, offsets, and the broker connection for you.","d":"A channel is an abstraction; in config you set connector=smallrye-kafka and topic. You can work with the payload directly or with Message<T> for access to metadata and manual ack.","note":"By default the ack strategy may commit the offset before processing completes. If you need an \"processed → committed\" guarantee, manage ack manually via Message or configure the commit/failure strategy, otherwise you'll lose a message on a crash.","hook":"@Incoming/@Outgoing are the \"in\" and \"out\" pipes, and your method is the filter in the middle of the conveyor.","more":"**SmallRye Reactive Messaging** is the Quarkus implementation of MicroProfile Reactive Messaging. Core concepts:\n\n- **Channel** — a named message pipe. A method connects to it via `@Incoming(\"name\")` (reads) or `@Outgoing(\"name\")` (writes).\n- **Connector** — the bridge between a channel and a broker. For Kafka it's `smallrye-kafka`, set in `application.properties`.\n- **Processor** — a method with both annotations: reads from one channel, returns the result into another.\n\nYou can work with the **payload** directly (just `Order`) or with **Message<Order>** — then you get access to metadata (key, partition, headers) and manual `ack()/nack()`.\n\nThe method can return `Uni`/`Multi` for async processing, or a plain type — then mind blocking just as on REST routes.","quizCorrect":"@Incoming makes a method a channel consumer, @Outgoing a producer; channels map to Kafka topics in config","quizWrong":["@Incoming and @Outgoing define REST endpoints for inbound and outbound HTTP requests handled by the framework","@Incoming reads from the DB, @Outgoing writes to the DB via Panache","@Incoming and @Outgoing are transaction directions: read and write in one topic"],"picCap":"@Incoming pulls from a topic, the method processes, @Outgoing writes to another topic."},"qk2-virtual-threads":{"q":"@RunOnVirtualThread — how is this an alternative to the reactive style? What's the gain?","a":"Virtual threads (Java 21, Project Loom) let you write plain blocking code that reads linearly but scales almost like reactive. @RunOnVirtualThread runs the endpoint/method on a virtual thread: a blocking call doesn't hold an expensive OS thread but parks a cheap virtual one. You get high throughput without Mutiny callback chains.","d":"Under the hood, on a blocking I/O call the JVM suspends the virtual thread and frees the carrier platform thread. Requires Java 21 and genuinely non-blocking points (no synchronized/JNI pinning).","note":"Pinning: if inside a virtual thread you hit a synchronized block or native code during a blocking call, the thread gets \"pinned\" to the carrier and loses the advantage. Use ReentrantLock instead of synchronized on hot paths.","hook":"A virtual thread is like hiring a thousand interns for the price of one: the intern blocks, while the expensive OS thread stays free.","more":"**Virtual threads** (Project Loom, stable in Java 21) are lightweight threads managed by the JVM, not the OS. You can create them by the millions.\n\nThe idea: on a blocking I/O call the JVM **parks** the virtual thread and frees the carrier platform thread for other work. So a blocking call no longer \"eats\" an expensive OS resource.\n\nFor Quarkus this means: you write **plain imperative code** (`var u = repo.find(id)`), mark it `@RunOnVirtualThread`, and get scalability close to reactive but **without** Mutiny chains like `onItem().transform().chain()`.\n\nWhen to choose which:\n- **Virtual threads** — if readability matters, the team dislikes reactive, the stack is blocking (JDBC).\n- **Reactive/Mutiny** — if you need streams, backpressure, or extreme throughput on minimal memory.","quizCorrect":"@RunOnVirtualThread runs blocking linear code on a virtual thread, giving scale without Mutiny chains","quizWrong":["@RunOnVirtualThread automatically turns blocking code into reactive Uni/Multi","Virtual threads are just an enlarged worker pool of regular OS threads","@RunOnVirtualThread runs the method on the event loop without the risk of blocking it of the application"],"picCap":"Reactive — chains of operators; virtual threads — linear blocking code on cheap threads."},"qk2-backpressure-multi":{"q":"What is backpressure in Multi, and what's the nuance when the consumer is overloaded?","a":"Backpressure is a mechanism where the consumer itself signals how many elements it's ready to accept (request(n)), and the producer doesn't send more. This protects a slow consumer from being flooded by a fast producer. The nuance: if the source is \"hot\" and can't slow down (a timer, a Kafka stream without pause) and the consumer can't keep up — Mutiny applies an overflow strategy: buffer, drop, or a MissingBackpressureException error.","d":"Multi implements the Reactive Streams Publisher, where Subscription.request(n) is the backpressure channel itself. You control it via onOverflow(): buffer(n), drop(), dropPrevious(), error().","note":"An unbounded buffer by default is a path to OutOfMemoryError under load. On hot sources always explicitly set onOverflow with a limit, rather than hoping it \"holds back on its own\".","hook":"Backpressure is the cashier telling the queue \"one at a time\", so the counter isn't buried in goods there's nowhere to put.","more":"**Backpressure** lives at the core of Reactive Streams: `Multi` is a `Publisher`, and the subscriber says via `Subscription.request(n)` \"give me n elements, no more\". The producer must respect this. That's how a slow consumer doesn't choke on a fast producer.\n\nThe problem arises with **hot sources** that can't slow down: a system timer, market ticks, an event stream. They emit on their own schedule, ignoring request(n).\n\nWhen the buffer is full, Mutiny applies an **overflow strategy** (via `onOverflow()`):\n- `buffer(n)` — accumulate up to n, then fail with an error;\n- `drop()` / `dropPreviousItems()` — discard the excess;\n- `error()` — immediately `MissingBackpressureException` / `BackPressureFailure`.\n\nThe strategy choice is a business decision: which matters more, not losing data (buffer, but OOM risk) or not crashing (drop, but lose elements).","quizCorrect":"Backpressure — the consumer requests (request n) as much as it can handle; on overload an overflow strategy is applied (buffer/drop/error)","quizWrong":["Backpressure is automatic horizontal scaling of consumers under load","Backpressure forces the producer to always buffer all elements without loss","Backpressure is the retransmission (retry) of elements the consumer didn't accept until every one of them has finally been accepted downstream"],"picCap":"The consumer throttles the producer via request(n); on overflow — buffer/drop/error."},"qk2-panache-ar-vs-repo":{"q":"Panache active record (entity extends PanacheEntity) vs repository — what's the difference and what's the risk for clean architecture?","a":"Active record: the entity knows how to save and find itself — methods like persist(), listAll(), find() hang right on the entity class. Repository: the same methods in a separate repository class, while the entity stays a plain POJO. The active-record risk is that persistence leaks into the domain: your business object is hard-wired to Hibernate and the DB, and can't be carried into a clean hexagonal core.","d":"In the reference audit-service the domain is pure Java without JPA annotations, so active record is forbidden there — only a repository behind a port.","note":"Active record's static methods (Order.findById) are hard to mock in unit tests — you end up dragging in a heavy integration test with a real DB.","hook":"Active record is an employee who runs to the archive themselves; a repository is a separate archivist, and the employee just does their job.","more":"**Active record** is convenient for quick CRUD apps: less boilerplate, everything in one class. **Repository** is preferable when:\n- you need a clean/hexagonal architecture (the domain with no ORM dependency)\n- you want to mock data access in unit tests\n- a single entity is used in different contexts\n\nBoth variants in Quarkus are sugar over Hibernate ORM; performance is the same. The difference is purely in design and coupling.","quizCorrect":"Active record hangs DB-access methods right on the entity, which makes persistence leak into the domain","quizWrong":["Active record works only reactively, while repository works only blocking and they cannot be mixed in one project","Repository is faster because it doesn't use Hibernate under the hood","Active record doesn't support transactions, so a repository is needed"],"picCap":"At the top a leak pulses red: active record drags the DB into the domain. At the bottom a repository separates them."},"qk2-hibernate-reactive-vs-blocking":{"q":"Hibernate Reactive vs classic blocking Hibernate ORM — when to use each and what's the fundamental difference?","a":"Blocking ORM: the thread hangs on a JDBC call and waits for the DB response, occupying a worker thread. Reactive: the query is non-blocking, methods return Uni/Mutiny, the thread is freed and works on other requests while the DB thinks. Reactive scales to thousands of connections with few threads, but the entire stack must be reactive — a single blocking operation breaks it all.","d":"Reactive uses a Vert.x DB driver (e.g. vertx-pg-client), not JDBC — JDBC is blocking by its very nature.","note":"You can't mix them thoughtlessly: a blocking JDBC call inside a reactive event-loop thread will hang the entire event loop. Quarkus even throws a BlockingOperationNotAllowedException.","hook":"Blocking — the waiter stands at the stove waiting for your dish; reactive — the waiter takes the order and goes to serve ten more tables.","more":"**When reactive:** very high concurrency under I/O-bound load (thousands of simultaneous connections), you need to economize on threads, the whole stack is already reactive (REST on Mutiny, reactive Kafka).\n\n**When blocking (the default for most):**\n- ordinary CRUD load\n- it's easier for the team to read imperative code (juniors)\n- blocking libraries are used that can't be avoided\n\nQuarkus works great in both modes. Don't pick reactive for the hype — it's harder to debug (stack traces, debugging) and drags reactivity throughout the code.","quizCorrect":"In reactive the thread doesn't block on a DB query — methods return a Uni and the thread is freed for other tasks","quizWrong":["Hibernate Reactive uses the same JDBC driver, just wrapping it in a Uni","Reactive is always faster on a single query because it doesn't open a transaction and skips the flush entirely before returning","Classic ORM can't work in Quarkus; only Reactive is needed"],"picCap":"Blocking keeps the thread busy waiting; reactive releases the thread during the query."},"qk2-transactional-narayana":{"q":"How does @Transactional work in Quarkus, and what's the catch with reactive/Uni?","a":"@Transactional (JTA, the Narayana manager) wraps a method in a transaction: opened on entry, commit on success, rollback on a RuntimeException. The catch: in reactive code @Transactional on a method returning a Uni doesn't work correctly — the transaction is bound to the thread, while the Uni runs asynchronously on a different thread. For reactive you use Panache.withTransaction(() -> ...) or @WithTransaction.","d":"By default JTA rolls back only on unchecked exceptions; for checked ones you need @Transactional(rollbackOn = ...).","note":"Self-invocation: if a @Transactional method is called from another method of the SAME bean directly (this.save()), the interceptor doesn't fire and there will be no transaction — a classic prod gotcha.","hook":"@Transactional keeps the transaction in the current thread's pocket; the Uni jumps to another thread — and the pocket is empty.","more":"**Narayana** is the JTA transaction manager built into Quarkus. **@Transactional** works via a CDI interceptor around the method.\n\nKey points:\n- by default rollback only on **RuntimeException/Error**, not on checked\n- attributes as in JTA: REQUIRED (default), REQUIRES_NEW, MANDATORY, etc.\n- self-invocation (calling a method within the same bean) is **not intercepted** — the transaction won't open\n\nIn the **reactive** world the transaction can't live in a ThreadLocal, so Quarkus provides **Panache.withTransaction()** and the **@WithTransaction** annotation (Hibernate Reactive), which carry the transaction through the Mutiny context.","quizCorrect":"@Transactional is bound to the thread, while a Uni runs asynchronously on another — you need Panache.withTransaction / @WithTransaction","quizWrong":["@Transactional isn't supported in Quarkus at all; you always use only @WithTransaction","@Transactional rolls back the transaction on any exception, including checked, with no configuration needed on the annotation itself or anywhere else","In reactive @Transactional works but requires a manual commit() call at the end of the Uni"],"picCap":"@Transactional lives on one thread; the Uni runs off to another and loses the transaction."},"qk2-dev-services":{"q":"What are Dev Services in Quarkus, and why do they remove the need to spin up a database by hand?","a":"Dev Services — in dev and test mode Quarkus automatically spins up dependencies (PostgreSQL, Kafka, Redis, Keycloak) in Docker via Testcontainers if you haven't set their URL in config. It sees a datasource with no URL, starts a DB container, and wires in the address automatically. Tests and local runs work out of the box with no docker-compose and no manual setup.","d":"The container is reused across runs if you enable quarkus.devservices.reuse and the testcontainers reuse flag in ~/.testcontainers.properties.","note":"Requires a running Docker/Podman. On CI agents without Docker-in-Docker, Dev Services will fail — there you usually supply real URLs via environment variables.","hook":"Dev Services are an auto-chef: you didn't pick a restaurant, so it warms up a database in a container for you while you write code.","more":"**Dev Services** activate automatically when:\n- the dependency is on the classpath (e.g. quarkus-jdbc-postgresql)\n- its connection parameters are **not set** explicitly\n- Docker (or Podman) is available\n\nSupported: databases, Kafka, AMQP, Redis, MongoDB, Keycloak, Vault, and more.\n\nUpside: zero configuration, an identical environment for everyone on the team, CI that runs without external services. In **production** Dev Services are **disabled** — a real URL is mandatory there. This is strictly a dev/test convenience, not a runtime mechanism.","quizCorrect":"In dev/test Quarkus spins up the dependency in Docker via Testcontainers when its URL isn't set in config","quizWrong":["Dev Services are a built-in in-memory H2 database that replaces Postgres in tests","Dev Services also work in production, automatically spinning up containers in prod","Dev Services require a separate docker-compose.yml that Quarkus generates at build time and leaves in the project root"],"picCap":"No URL in config → Quarkus starts the DB container itself and injects the address."},"qk2-flyway-liquibase":{"q":"Flyway vs Liquibase in Quarkus — how do migrations get wired up, and how do the approaches differ?","a":"Quarkus has quarkus-flyway and quarkus-liquibase extensions that run schema migrations at application startup. Flyway: versioned SQL scripts like V1__init.sql, simple linear ordering. Liquibase: a changelog (XML/YAML/JSON/SQL) with changeSets, abstracted from the DB dialect and capable of rollbacks. Enabled with the migrate-at-start=true flag.","d":"Unlike hibernate.hbm2ddl.auto=update, migrations are a versioned, reproducible, and prod-safe way to evolve the schema.","note":"In a cluster of N instances they all start at once and try to migrate — both Flyway and Liquibase take a lock on a service table so exactly one pod runs the migration. But a long migration will still delay the startup of all of them.","hook":"Migrations are git for your DB schema: every step is numbered, and the server knows which commit it has already applied.","more":"**Flyway** — versioned migrations, usually plain SQL (V<version>__<description>.sql). Simple, transparent, linear. Ideal when the team is confident writing SQL.\n\n**Liquibase** — a changelog of changeSets in XML/YAML/JSON/SQL:\n- abstraction over DB dialects (one changelog → different databases)\n- built-in **rollback**\n- contexts and labels for conditional application\n\nBoth are integrated into Quarkus as extensions, both support **migrate-at-start**, and both work in native builds. The choice is about team preference: Flyway for simplicity, Liquibase for flexibility and cross-DB portability.","quizCorrect":"The Quarkus extension applies the missing versioned migrations at startup, tracking applied ones in a history service table","quizWrong":["Flyway and Liquibase in Quarkus only work in dev mode and are ignored in production","Liquibase supports only SQL scripts, while Flyway supports only XML changelogs","Migrations are applied automatically by Hibernate via hbm2ddl.auto, and the extension merely logs what it has already applied"],"picCap":"Migrations are versioned; at startup only the not-yet-applied ones are run."},"qk2-mpconfig-sources":{"q":"Where does Quarkus get the value for @ConfigProperty, and which source overrides which?","a":"Quarkus implements MicroProfile Config: a single property can come from several sources, and the source with the higher ordinal (priority) wins. By default: system properties (-D, ordinal 400) > environment variables (300) > .env (295) > application.properties (250/100). So an env variable overrides what sits in application.properties without touching the code.","d":"↳ A property name maps to an ENV var by the rule: dots and dashes → underscores, uppercase (quarkus.http.port → QUARKUS_HTTP_PORT).","note":"If a property is required (no defaultValue and the type isn't Optional) and it's nowhere to be found, the application fails at startup with a DeploymentException rather than returning null.","hook":"Config is like a stack of transparent sheets: you look from the top, and the top sheet covers the ones below.","more":"**MicroProfile Config** — the standard Quarkus implements via SmallRye Config.\n\n- Each **ConfigSource** has a numeric **ordinal**; on conflict the source with the higher ordinal wins.\n- Default priorities: sys props `400` > env `300` > `.env` `295` > `application.properties` `250`.\n- You can add your own sources (e.g. from Vault/Consul) with the ordinal you need.\n- `@ConfigProperty` injects the value; `Optional<T>` or `defaultValue` saves you from a startup failure when the property is absent.","quizCorrect":"System properties (-D) override environment variables, which in turn override application.properties","quizWrong":["application.properties always has the highest priority and can't be overridden by anything and cannot be extended","Environment variables override system properties (-D)","Priority is determined by the order properties are declared in the file, not by the source"],"picCap":"Sources top to bottom by decreasing priority; the top one wins"},"qk2-profiles":{"q":"How do the %dev/%test/%prod profiles work, and which one is active by default?","a":"A profile is a prefix on a key: `%dev.quarkus.http.port=8081`. Quarkus picks the profile itself: `dev` for `quarkus:dev`, `test` during tests, `prod` in all other cases (a packaged jar/native). A property prefixed with the active profile overrides the same property without a prefix.","d":"↳ Change the profile manually with `-Dquarkus.profile=staging` or the `QUARKUS_PROFILE` env var. You can also define your own profiles, not just the three built-in ones.","note":"%test is read only from application.properties on the test classpath; in prod a file with %test keys is harmless — they simply don't activate.","hook":"A profile is like a washing-machine mode: one drum, but the wash program changes the temperature to fit the situation.","more":"**Profiles** let you keep config for all environments in a single file.\n\n- Built-in: `%dev`, `%test`, `%prod`. The active one is chosen automatically by how you launch.\n- You can define an arbitrary profile (`%staging.…`) and activate it via `quarkus.profile=staging`.\n- A profile applies to **any** ConfigSource, not just properties — e.g. the env variable `_DEV_QUARKUS_...`.\n- `prod` isn't \"the production server\" but simply \"everything that isn't dev or test\"; a packaged application always starts in prod.","quizCorrect":"In a packaged jar/native the prod profile is active by default","quizWrong":["In a packaged jar the dev profile is active by default","A profile must always be set manually via -Dquarkus.profile, otherwise none is active","The test profile is active everywhere except when -Dquarkus.profile=prod is set explicitly"],"picCap":"The launch command determines the active profile; a %-key overrides the base one"},"qk2-buildtime-runtime":{"q":"How do build-time properties differ from runtime ones, and why can part of the config not be changed after the build?","a":"Quarkus does as much work as possible at build time: some properties get \"baked\" into the artifact and can't be changed without a rebuild (build-time fixed). Runtime properties are read at startup — they can be overridden via env/sys props. This is the price of fast startup and of native, where reflection and dynamism aren't available: anything that can be decided ahead of time is decided at build.","d":"↳ If you set a build-time property via env in prod, Quarkus logs a warning and ignores it, keeping the value fixed at build time.","note":"A common trap: people override `quarkus.datasource.db-kind` via env in Docker and are surprised it doesn't work — it's a build-time property, baked in at `mvn package`.","hook":"Build-time is like a mold for concrete: while the concrete is wet, shape it however you like; once it's set (built), you can't reshape the mold without a new pour.","more":"**The build-time / runtime split** is a key idea in Quarkus.\n\n- At build (`augmentation`) the framework builds a metamodel: which beans, which endpoints, which database — and drops everything unnecessary. That gives fast startup and low memory.\n- **Build-time fixed** properties (e.g. `db-kind`, the set of extensions, `http.root-path`) end up in the artifact; their env/sys props won't change them at runtime.\n- **Runtime** properties (URL, passwords, port) are read at startup — those are the ones to pass via env/secrets.\n- In **native** this is critical: GraalVM compiles AOT, reflection and dynamism are limited, so most decisions are made ahead of time.\n- In the docs each property is marked `RUNTIME` or `BUILD TIME`.","quizCorrect":"Build-time properties are fixed in the artifact and require a rebuild to change","quizWrong":["Any Quarkus property can be overridden by an env variable without a rebuild","Build-time properties exist only in native and don't exist in JVM mode","Runtime properties can't be changed after startup, while build-time ones can be changed on the fly"],"picCap":"Build-time is baked into the artifact; runtime is read on every startup"},"qk2-devmode-livereload":{"q":"How are dev mode and live reload built, and when exactly does recompilation happen?","a":"`quarkus dev` (or `mvn quarkus:dev`) brings up the application with hot reload. Recompilation is lazy: the code is rebuilt not when you save a file, but on the next HTTP request. Quarkus compares hashes of the changed files, reloads only those, and serves the request with the new code already — no JVM restart needed.","d":"↳ Under the hood there's a Dev UI (`/q/dev`), and there's also a remote dev mode for an application running in a container.","note":"If an edit breaks compilation, the error lands right in the browser as a nice exception page on the next request — that's normal for dev mode.","hook":"Live reload is like a waiter who runs to the kitchen only when you place an order, not every time the cook chops something.","more":"**Dev mode** is the headline feature for development speed.\n\n- Launch: `quarkus dev` / `mvn quarkus:dev` / `gradle quarkusDev`.\n- **Lazy live reload**: on an HTTP request Quarkus checks the changed sources, recompiles the affected ones, and restarts the application \"inside\" the same JVM (a fast context restart) rather than spinning up a new one.\n- **Dev UI** (`/q/dev`) shows extensions, config, beans, and provides tools (migrations, cache, etc.).\n- **Dev Services**: for dev/test Quarkus itself spins up dependencies (Postgres, Kafka) in Testcontainers — no need to specify a URL.\n- There's **remote dev** — you edit locally and it's applied to the process in the container.","quizCorrect":"Recompilation happens lazily — on the next incoming request, not when you save a file","quizWrong":["Quarkus recompiles and restarts the JVM on every file save","Live reload requires manually restarting quarkus:dev after edits to the source files you have made","Changes are picked up only after a full rebuild via mvn package"],"picCap":"A request triggers a hash check and lazy recompilation of the changed classes"},"qk2-continuous-testing":{"q":"What is continuous testing in Quarkus, and how does it differ from a regular test run?","a":"Continuous testing is a mode where tests run automatically in the background right after you change code, while you work. Quarkus runs only the tests affected by the change (impacted tests) and shows the result right in the dev console / Dev UI. No need to invoke `mvn test` separately — feedback is almost instant.","d":"↳ Enabled in dev mode with the `r` key in the terminal; `o` toggles test output, `v` opens results in the Dev UI.","note":"Continuous testing uses the test profile (%test) and Dev Services — behavior may differ from prod if the config is heavily tied to the profile.","hook":"Continuous testing is like a smoke detector in the kitchen: you don't wait until you decide to check — it beeps the moment something starts smoking.","more":"**Continuous testing** lives inside dev mode and relies on the same hot reload.\n\n- After an edit Quarkus determines which tests are **affected** by the change and runs only those — fast.\n- Controls from the terminal: `r` — start/restart, `f` — failures only, `b` — break-on-failure, `o` — output, `q` — quit.\n- Results are also visible in the **Dev UI**.\n- This shrinks the \"wrote it — checked it\" cycle to seconds and encourages a TDD style without manually running Maven/Gradle.","quizCorrect":"Tests run automatically in the background after changes, executing only the affected tests","quizWrong":["It's just an alias for mvn test that runs all tests on a schedule once a minute in the background","Tests run only on a git commit via a pre-commit hook","Continuous testing runs the entire test suite on every save"],"picCap":"A code edit runs only the affected tests in the background and gives a verdict immediately"},"qk2-packaging":{"q":"fast-jar, uber-jar, native — what's the difference, and which to choose?","a":"fast-jar (default) — the application is split into layers (`quarkus-app/`: lib, app, quarkus): fast startup and convenient Docker layer caching. uber-jar — everything in one self-contained jar, handy to ship as a single file, but bigger and worse at caching. native — a GraalVM AOT binary for the target OS: startup in milliseconds and minimal RAM, but slow builds and no JIT.","d":"↳ The type is set via `quarkus.package.jar.type=fast-jar|uber-jar` (formerly `quarkus.package.type`); native via `-Dnative` / `quarkus.native.enabled=true`.","note":"The property was renamed: the current one is `quarkus.package.jar.type`, the old `quarkus.package.type` is marked deprecated in Quarkus 3.x — easy to slip up with the outdated key in an interview.","hook":"fast-jar is IKEA furniture in boxes (easy to deliver), uber-jar is an assembled wardrobe (one piece, but heavy), native is built-in cabinetry (instant, but you can't redo it).","more":"**Packaging formats** are chosen by deployment method.\n\n- **fast-jar** (default): a `quarkus-app/` directory with `lib/`, `app/`, `quarkus/` layers. Dependencies separate from code → Docker caches the library layer, the image builds faster, startup is optimal. Run: `quarkus-run.jar`.\n- **uber-jar**: a single self-contained jar (`quarkus.package.jar.type=uber-jar`). Plus — ship as one file; minus — bigger, weaker caching, possible conflicts during shading.\n- **native**: GraalVM/Mandrel compiles AOT into a binary for a specific OS/architecture. Startup in milliseconds, low RAM usage — ideal for serverless/k8s scale-to-zero. Downsides: slow and memory-hungry builds, no JIT (worse under long heavy load), reflection limitations.\n- Under the hood native relies on that same build-time config split.","quizCorrect":"fast-jar is the default format: split into layers for fast startup and Docker layer caching","quizWrong":["uber-jar is the default format because it's the most compact of the three","A native binary keeps JIT compilation and therefore warms up on hot code better than the JVM","fast-jar and uber-jar require GraalVM to build, just like native"],"picCap":"Three packaging formats; native starts an order of magnitude faster, but without JIT"},"qk2-fault-tolerance":{"q":"What do the SmallRye Fault Tolerance annotations do: @Retry, @CircuitBreaker, @Timeout, @Bulkhead, @Fallback?","a":"These are declarative call protections. @Retry repeats a failed call N times. @Timeout aborts a hung call on timeout. @CircuitBreaker \"opens the circuit\" after a series of errors and quickly rejects requests instead of hitting a dead service. @Bulkhead limits the number of concurrent calls (isolation like bulkheads in a ship). @Fallback provides a backup response when everything has failed.","d":"↳ This is the MicroProfile Fault Tolerance implementation on top of the SmallRye library; annotations can be combined on a single method, and their order of execution is strictly defined by the spec.","note":"@Retry on a non-idempotent call (e.g. POST \"create order\") can duplicate the operation. It's safe to retry only idempotent calls, or to guard them with an idempotency key.","hook":"It's a first-aid kit for a call: a bandage retries, a tourniquet shuts it off, and the spare parachute is the fallback.","more":"**SmallRye Fault Tolerance** is resilience to failures declaratively, without hand-written try/catch and counters.\n- **@Retry** — retry on exception (careful with non-idempotent operations).\n- **@Timeout** — guarantees a hung downstream won't tie up your thread forever.\n- **@CircuitBreaker** — states CLOSED → OPEN → HALF_OPEN: after an error threshold it opens and blocks requests for a while, letting the service recover.\n- **@Bulkhead** — a pool of N concurrent calls, so one slow downstream doesn't eat all of the application's threads.\n- **@Fallback** — a backup method (e.g. cache or a default) when the protections are exhausted.","quizCorrect":"@Bulkhead limits the number of concurrent calls, isolating the load","quizWrong":["@Bulkhead retries a failed call a fixed number of times","@Bulkhead caches a method's successful response between calls to the same arguments","@Bulkhead logs all of a method's exceptions to OpenTelemetry"],"picCap":"A chain of protections: timeout → retries → an open breaker falls through to fallback"},"qk2-health-probes":{"q":"What's the difference between @Liveness and @Readiness in SmallRye Health, and how do they relate to Kubernetes probes?","a":"@Liveness answers \"is the application alive or hung?\" — if the probe fails, Kubernetes restarts the pod. @Readiness answers \"is it ready to take traffic?\" — if it fails, the pod is removed from load balancing but NOT restarted. Liveness maps to livenessProbe, Readiness to readinessProbe.","d":"↳ Quarkus endpoints: /q/health/live and /q/health/ready (with a combined /q/health). In Quarkus 3 the smallrye-health extension exposes them automatically.","note":"A classic mistake is putting an external DB check in @Liveness. Then, when the DB is unavailable, Kubernetes will endlessly restart a healthy pod. External dependencies belong only in @Readiness.","hook":"Liveness is the patient's pulse (gone — resuscitation), Readiness is the \"open\" sign on the door (closed — we don't let customers in, but the store is still standing).","more":"**SmallRye Health** implements MicroProfile Health. You implement `HealthCheck` and annotate the bean.\n- **@Liveness** → `/q/health/live` → `livenessProbe`. Should only check \"I'm not dead-hung,\" without external dependencies. Including a DB check here means a downed DB causes an endless restart loop.\n- **@Readiness** → `/q/health/ready` → `readinessProbe`. External dependencies (DB, Kafka) are appropriate here: while they're absent — no traffic.\n- **@Startup** (Quarkus 3) → `startupProbe` for slow startup.\n- Many extensions (datasource, kafka, redis) add readiness checks automatically.","quizCorrect":"A Readiness failure removes the pod from load balancing but doesn't restart it","quizWrong":["A Readiness failure always leads to an immediate pod restart","A Liveness failure removes the pod from load balancing without a restart of the container","Liveness and Readiness are synonyms and return the same result"],"picCap":"Liveness failure = pod restart; Readiness failure = removed from load balancing without a restart"},"qk2-micrometer-otel":{"q":"How are metrics (Micrometer) and distributed tracing (OpenTelemetry) built in Quarkus, and how does a metric differ from a trace?","a":"A metric is a number over time (RPS, latency, pool size): cheap, aggregated, answers \"what and how much.\" A trace is the path of ONE request through all services as a tree of spans: answers \"where exactly is the bottleneck.\" In Quarkus 3 metrics come via quarkus-micrometer (usually with a Prometheus registry), and tracing via quarkus-opentelemetry (exported over OTLP).","d":"↳ The trace context (traceId/spanId) is propagated between services via the W3C traceparent header; Quarkus instruments REST, gRPC, JDBC, and Kafka automatically.","note":"Full trace sampling in prod is expensive: 100% tracing under load hurts latency and storage cost. In prod you enable sampling (e.g. 5–10% of requests).","hook":"Metrics are the car's speedometer and tachometer; a trace is the dashcam recording of the whole trip from door to door.","more":"**Observability = metrics + traces + logs.**\n- **Micrometer** in Quarkus is a facade over metrics; most often you plug in a Prometheus registry, and metrics go to `/q/metrics`. HTTP, JVM, pools, and datasource are collected automatically.\n- **OpenTelemetry (OTel)** is the tracing standard; Quarkus exports spans over the **OTLP** protocol to a collector/Jaeger/Tempo. The `traceId` is end-to-end — it links spans from different services into one tree.\n- **The combination**: from a metric you see that p95 latency spiked; from a trace in the same time window you find the specific slow span (e.g. a SQL query).\n- In Quarkus 3 OTel tracing is `quarkus-opentelemetry` (the old `quarkus-smallrye-opentracing` is deprecated).","quizCorrect":"A metric is an aggregated number over time, a trace is the path of one request through services","quizWrong":["A metric and a trace are the same thing, just different export formats","A trace is an aggregated number over time, while a metric is the path of one request through the system","Micrometer handles tracing, while OpenTelemetry handles only metrics in Quarkus"],"picCap":"A metric answers \"how much/how often,\" a trace answers \"where's the bottleneck in the chain of spans\""},"qk2-openapi-swagger":{"q":"Why SmallRye OpenAPI and Swagger UI in Quarkus, and where does the spec come from?","a":"SmallRye OpenAPI generates the OpenAPI spec (the API contract in YAML/JSON) from your JAX-RS/REST endpoints at build time — it's served at /q/openapi. Swagger UI is an interactive web page on top of that spec at /q/swagger-ui, where you can view the endpoints and call them right from the browser.","d":"↳ The spec can be enriched with @Operation, @Schema, @APIResponse annotations; Swagger UI in Quarkus is available by default only in dev mode and is enabled in prod with a separate property.","note":"By default Swagger UI is available only in dev mode. To open it in prod you need to explicitly set `quarkus.swagger-ui.always-include=true` — and to consider whether you're exposing an internal API to the outside.","hook":"OpenAPI is a restaurant menu generated from the kitchen itself, and Swagger UI is the waiter who lets you poke at that menu.","more":"**SmallRye OpenAPI** implements MicroProfile OpenAPI.\n- The spec is generated **from the code** (code-first): it scans JAX-RS annotations and DTO types.\n- Endpoints: the spec at **/q/openapi** (supports `?format=json`), the UI at **/q/swagger-ui**.\n- The annotations `@Operation`, `@APIResponse`, `@Schema`, `@Tag` enrich the spec with descriptions, examples, and response codes.\n- The alternative is **contract-first**: drop in a ready `openapi.yaml` and generate clients/server interfaces.\n- The spec is useful not only for people: client SDKs and contract tests are generated from it.","quizCorrect":"SmallRye OpenAPI generates the spec from REST endpoints, and Swagger UI displays it","quizWrong":["Swagger UI generates endpoint code from a manually written spec","SmallRye OpenAPI is a runtime proxy that validates requests against the schema on every call","The OpenAPI spec is served at /q/health, and Swagger UI at /q/metrics"],"picCap":"Endpoint code → auto-generated OpenAPI spec → Swagger UI on top of it"},"qk2-vs-springboot":{"q":"Quarkus vs Spring Boot — when to choose which? (consider more than just startup speed)","a":"Quarkus is stronger where fast startup and low memory matter: serverless, frequent scaling, native images (GraalVM). Spring Boot wins on maturity and breadth of ecosystem, a huge community, and the fact that almost any Java developer already knows it. The choice isn't a startup benchmark but a balance: the team, the ecosystem of needed integrations, risk, and support timelines.","d":"↳ Quarkus pushes work to build time (build-time DI and indexing), hence fast startup and native; Spring historically does more at runtime via reflection, though Spring Boot 3 + AOT/native has narrowed that gap.","note":"Quarkus's main argument — fast cold start — barely matters for a long-lived service that restarts once a week. It's critical in serverless/frequent scaling, not everywhere across the board.","hook":"Quarkus is a sports car: light and fast off the line, but fewer spare parts. Spring Boot is a road-tested wagon: heavier, but there's a service shop on every corner.","more":"**The decision is about context, not hype.**\n- **Pick Quarkus** if: serverless/FaaS, aggressive autoscaling (many small pods), you need native for minimal RAM, the project is new and the team is ready to learn.\n- **Pick Spring Boot** if: the team is already deep in Spring, you need a rare integration (a huge selection of starters), predictable hiring matters, you need long-term support, or there's legacy on Spring.\n- **The maturity nuance**: Spring has a broader set of ready libraries, more StackOverflow answers, easier hiring. This often outweighs Quarkus's cold-start advantage — especially for long-running services that start once a week.\n- **Native ≠ free**: a GraalVM build is slower, reflection needs configuration, debugging is harder.","quizCorrect":"The choice considers ecosystem maturity and team experience, not just startup speed","quizWrong":["Quarkus is always the best choice because it starts faster in every scenario","Spring Boot can't do native images, so it's unsuitable for cloud-native","Quarkus and Spring Boot are incompatible by Java version, and that alone drives the choice"],"picCap":"Quarkus — startup and memory; Spring — maturity and team familiarity"},"qk2-oidc-keycloak":{"q":"How does Quarkus Security work with OIDC/Keycloak, and what is bearer mode in a broker-like stack?","a":"Quarkus integrates with Keycloak as an OIDC provider via the quarkus-oidc extension. In a microservice architecture, services run in service (bearer) mode: the client has already obtained a JWT access token, puts it in the Authorization: Bearer header, and the service validates the token's signature against Keycloak's public keys (JWKS) and extracts the roles. No sessions and no login redirects — the service only verifies the token.","d":"↳ Roles from the token map onto @RolesAllowed; for UI applications there's a web-app mode with code flow and a redirect to Keycloak, and for API services — bearer-only.","note":"In bearer mode the service trusts the signature rather than calling Keycloak on every request — that's a speed win, but an expired/revoked token stays valid until its exp. For instant revocation you need a short token TTL or introspection.","hook":"Keycloak is the passport office that issued the JWT passport; the service doesn't phone the office on every request but checks the watermark (the signature) against the public sample.","more":"**Quarkus + OIDC/Keycloak** is the standard scheme for securing microservices in a broker-like stack (Keycloak is listed as Auth across all platform services).\n- **Two modes** via `quarkus.oidc.application-type`:\n  - **service (bearer)** — for APIs: the token already exists, the service only validates. Sessionless, stateless.\n  - **web-app** — for UI: authorization code flow, redirect to Keycloak, a cookie session.\n- **JWT validation**: the service downloads the realm's public keys (JWKS) and verifies the token signature locally — it doesn't hit Keycloak on every request (fast and resilient).\n- **Authorization**: the roles claim from the token → `@RolesAllowed(\"...\")`, plus you can secure by path via `quarkus.http.auth.permission.*`.\n- **Token propagation**: when calling downstream services the token can be forwarded onward (`quarkus-oidc-token-propagation`).","quizCorrect":"In service mode the service validates the Bearer JWT against Keycloak's keys without a login redirect","quizWrong":["The service redirects the user to the Keycloak login page on every request","The service stores user passwords and verifies them against Keycloak over the network itself","The Bearer token is decrypted with the service's secret key rather than having its signature verified with a public key"],"picCap":"The client sends a Bearer JWT; the service validates the signature against Keycloak's keys and maps the roles"},"sd-interview-flow":{"q":"You're told \"design TinyURL.\" Where do you start, and in what order do you proceed?","a":"Don't jump straight to drawing boxes. First gather requirements (functional + non-functional: QPS, latency, data volume), then back-of-envelope estimates, then a high-level diagram (client→LB→service→DB/cache), and only after that look for bottlenecks and dig into them. Order: clarify → estimate → sketch big-picture → drill into the bottleneck.","d":"The classic junior mistake is diving into details (which Postgres index?) before agreeing on scale. Without a QPS number you don't even know whether you need a cache or sharding at all.","note":"⚠️ Don't go silent while doing math in your head. Voice your assumptions (\"let's say 100M records, 10:1 read/write\") — otherwise the interviewer can't tell where your numbers came from and assumes you guessed.","hook":"First ask \"where are we headed,\" then estimate the fuel, then draw the route — and only then think about where the traffic jam is","more":"The skeleton interviewers expect:\n- **Requirements**: functional (what it does) + non-functional (QPS, p99 latency, availability, consistency).\n- **Estimation**: back-of-envelope — QPS, traffic, data volume over 5 years.\n- **High-level design**: client → LB → app → cache → DB, plus a queue if you write in batches.\n- **Deep dive**: the interviewer pokes at a bottleneck (hot key, table growth) — you dig into exactly that one.\nThink out loud about your assumptions — the interview is about your reasoning, not the one true answer.","quizCorrect":"Clarify requirements and scale → estimate load → high-level diagram → bottleneck","quizWrong":["Immediately pick a database and draw an ER diagram of the tables","Start by choosing the language and framework for the service","Jump straight into sharding and replication without knowing the QPS or the data volume"],"picCap":"The interview funnel: from words to numbers, from numbers to a diagram, from the diagram to the weak spot"},"sd-back-of-envelope":{"q":"What is back-of-envelope estimation, and why round to powers of 10?","a":"It's a rough \"on a napkin\" estimate: order of magnitude, not an exact number. You round to 10^n so you can multiply quickly in your head and figure out whether this is thousands of RPS or millions. The goal isn't precision but grasping the scale of the problem: do you need a cache, sharding, a CDN.","d":"Keep handy constants in your head: a day has ~86400 ≈ 10^5 seconds; 1M records of 1KB = 1GB; reading from memory ~100ns, from SSD ~100µs, network within a datacenter ~0.5ms, cross-region ~50-150ms.","hook":"A napkin estimate: not \"exactly how much\" but \"thousands or millions\" — that's enough to decide whether you need a cache","more":"Why round and reason in orders of magnitude:\n- **Speed**: 5M/10^5 = 50 in your head — that's average QPS. The exact 86400 just gets in the way.\n- **Decisions driven by scale**: 50 QPS — a single instance handles it; 50000 QPS — you need sharding and a cache.\n- **Memorized constants**: 1KB×1M=1GB, 1KB×1B=1TB — a ruler for sizing storage.\n- **Peak ≠ average**: multiply average QPS by 2-3 for peak load, and size capacity by peak.\nThe interviewer wants to see that you're not afraid of numbers and have a feel for scale.","quizCorrect":"A day has roughly 10^5 seconds (86400)","quizWrong":["A day has roughly 10^4 seconds and a year 10^6","A day has roughly 10^6 seconds","A day has roughly 10^7 seconds"],"picCap":"Anchor numbers: you compute the order of magnitude, not the exact value","note":"Where the estimate really lies: average QPS hides the tail — at 50k QPS mean, latency is set by p99/p999, not the average, and Little's Law (concurrency = throughput × latency) shows that rising latency inflates the number of concurrent connections/threads and exhausts the pool before CPU does. So size capacity by peak QPS AND tail latency, not by the mean."},"sd-qps-readwrite":{"q":"How do you turn \"100M active users\" into QPS, and why do you care about the read/write ratio?","a":"Take actions per user per day, multiply by users, divide by ~10^5 seconds = average QPS. Peak = average ×2-3. The read/write ratio tells you where the pain is: at 100:1 the pain is on reads → cache and replicas; if it's write-heavy the pain is on writes → sharding and buffering through a queue.","hook":"QPS = people × actions ÷ 100k seconds. And the read/write ratio is where the system hurts","more":"Why read/write is the key number:\n- **Read-heavy (social network, feed)**: you scale reads — cache (Redis), read replicas, CDN. Writes are rare, the master copes.\n- **Write-heavy (metrics, logs, IoT)**: you scale writes — shard by key, batch through Kafka, LSM engines (Cassandra).\n- **Peak matters more than average**: plan capacity for peak, otherwise you fall over at rush hour.\n- **QPS per instance**: knowing one instance holds ~1-5k RPS, you can figure the number of machines behind the LB.\nThis ratio drives almost all of the remaining architecture.","quizCorrect":"Average QPS ≈ (actions × users) / 10^5, peak ≈ average × 2-3","quizWrong":["QPS = number of users directly, without actions or time of day at all","Peak QPS always equals the average","QPS is computed by dividing by the number of servers"],"picCap":"Read/write dictates what to optimize: reads (cache) or writes (shard)","note":"Trap of dividing by 10^5: traffic isn't spread evenly over the day — for most products the daily peak sits on top of an already uneven profile, and real peak QPS can be 5-10x, not ×2-3, over the average (classic cases: Twitter fan-out to a celebrity, or a flash-sale). Size capacity by the worst-case pattern, not by an averaged ×3, or you'll go down at exactly the moment the system was built for."},"sd-latency-budget":{"q":"You're given an SLA: p99 API response ≤ 200ms. How do you split that budget across layers?","a":"Divide 200ms among the participants in the chain: round-trip network, load balancer, business logic, DB/cache calls, serialization. Each layer gets its share, and the sum must fit the budget with headroom. If a DB query eats 150ms, you have 50ms left for everything else — so the DB is the bottleneck.","d":"Reason in p99, not the average: with 3 sequential DB calls the tails add up. If each call is p99=20ms, three in a row easily produce 60ms+ on the tail. Fewer sequential hops, more stable tail.","note":"⚠️ Average latency lies. A system with avg=50ms can have p99=2s — and those 1% of users complain the loudest. Always measure and promise in percentiles.","hook":"A latency budget is an envelope of cash: hand it out to every layer, and the sum has to fit. Whoever takes the most is the problem","more":"How a latency budget gets cut:\n- **Tail latency**: SLAs are set on p99/p999, not the average. One slow step breaks the tail.\n- **Sequential vs parallel**: sequential hops add up, parallel ones take the max. Parallelize independent calls.\n- **Fan-out problem**: a request to 100 shards waits for the slowest one. One shard's p99 becomes almost guaranteed on every request.\n- **Where to save**: a cache removes the DB trip (150ms→1ms), a connection pool removes the handshake.\nReference points: memory ~100ns, SSD ~100µs, disk seek ~10ms, cross-region RTT ~50-150ms.","quizCorrect":"The p99 budget is split across layers; you must reason in p99 because tails of sequential calls add up","quizWrong":["The budget is computed from average latency — it's more accurate that way than percentiles on the whole path","Network latency can be ignored, it's negligible","Parallel calls add up latency the same way sequential ones do"],"picCap":"The 200ms budget is sliced across layers; the DB eats the most — that's where we look"},"sd-availability-nines":{"q":"What does \"four nines\" of availability mean, and why is each additional nine several times more expensive?","a":"99.99% = no more than ~52 minutes of downtime per year. Each nine divides the allowed downtime by 10: three nines ~8.7h/year, four ~52min, five ~5min. It's more expensive because removing the last minutes requires multi-AZ, auto-failover, replication, and eliminating single points of failure — complexity and cost spike sharply.","note":"⚠️ The availability of a sequential chain is a PRODUCT, not the minimum. Five services at 99.9% each already give you ~99.5%. The more hops, the lower the overall availability.","hook":"Nines are like steps: each next one is 10× less downtime, but several times more expensive in hardware and nerves","more":"The cost of nines and the traps:\n- **Sequential chain**: if a request passes through 3 services at 99.9% each, overall availability = 0.999³ ≈ 99.7% — worse than any single link.\n- **Redundancy raises it**: two parallel links at 99% each give 1−(0.01)² = 99.99%. Redundancy is the main lever.\n- **What you buy with nines**: multi-AZ/region, auto-failover, health checks, no SPOF, graceful degradation.\n- **Common sense**: not everyone needs 5 nines. An internal admin service is fine at 99.9%; a payment gateway is not.\nFirst ask the business how much downtime is actually acceptable — it's money.","quizCorrect":"99.99% ≈ 52 minutes of downtime per year","quizWrong":["99.99% ≈ 8.7 hours of downtime per year of downtime","99.99% ≈ 5 minutes of downtime per year","99.99% ≈ 3.65 days of downtime per year"],"picCap":"Each nine shrinks downtime 10× — and costs several times more"},"sd-cap-theorem":{"q":"CAP theorem: what do you actually have to choose, and why is \"pick 2 of 3\" an oversimplification?","a":"In a distributed system the network eventually partitions (P is inevitable), so the real choice is between C and A only during a network partition. CP: during a partition you refuse to answer rather than serve stale data (bank, balance). AP: you always answer, but possibly with stale data (feed, cart). Outside a partition a system can be both consistent and available.","d":"P (partition tolerance) isn't optional in a distributed system — the network can always partition. So the honest phrasing is: \"during a network partition, choose C or A.\" Real databases tune along a spectrum, not strictly into one corner.","hook":"The network will partition anyway. The only question is: during a partition, lie-but-answer (AP) or stay-silent-but-honest (CP)","more":"CAP in practice:\n- **You don't choose P** — it's a given of distributed systems. The C/A choice only kicks in during a partition.\n- **CP cases**: account balance, stock on hand, seat reservation — better an error than a double charge.\n- **AP cases**: news feed, likes, cart, cache — better a slightly stale answer than a 500.\n- **PACELC** — an extension: even without a partition (Else) there's a Latency vs Consistency choice. Strong consistency costs latency.\n- **Tuning, not a corner**: Cassandra/DynamoDB set consistency per request (quorum), sliding between AP and CP.\nIn an interview, tie the choice to the business case rather than quoting the theorem.","quizCorrect":"During a network partition you choose C (refuse, for freshness) or A (answer, risking stale data)","quizWrong":["You can permanently give up P and get perfect C and A","CAP forbids having both consistency and availability even without network partitions in the network","An AP system always serves strictly fresh data"],"picCap":"P is inevitable; during a partition you choose: correctness (CP) or availability (AP)","note":"Common interview confusion: the 'C' in CAP is linearizability (strict single-object consistency), NOT the ACID 'Consistency' from transactions and not serializable isolation. So saying 'MongoDB/Postgres is CP' is meaningless without naming the level: the same DB slides along the spectrum via settings (write concern, read concern, sync replicas), and the real guarantee is set by config, not by a marketing line."},"sd-sql-vs-nosql":{"q":"SQL or NoSQL for a specific case — by what signals do you decide, rather than by fashion?","a":"Pick SQL when you need ACID transactions, relationships, and complex queries/joins, and the schema is stable (money, orders, accounting). Pick NoSQL when you need horizontal write scaling, a flexible/changing schema, simple key access, and can live with eventual consistency (feed, sessions, metrics, catalog). The data access pattern decides, not hype.","d":"NoSQL isn't \"faster in general\" — it's faster on its pattern (key access, no joins). Modern Postgres with JSONB and partitioning covers many \"NoSQL cases\" while staying transactional. By default, start with SQL.","note":"⚠️ \"NoSQL = schemaless\" is a myth. The schema exists, it just moves into the application code. Without discipline this turns into a dump of heterogeneous documents that's impossible to migrate.","hook":"SQL is an accounting ledger with strict rules. NoSQL is a pile of sticky notes: you slap them down and grab them by key fast, but order isn't guaranteed","more":"How to justify the choice:\n- **SQL (Postgres/MySQL)**: transactions, FK, aggregates, ad-hoc queries, reporting. The price — vertical scaling and the pain of sharding.\n- **Document (Mongo)**: nested documents, flexible schema, access by id.\n- **Wide-column (Cassandra)**: write-heavy, time series, queries known in advance, write scaling.\n- **KV (Redis/DynamoDB)**: cache, sessions, counters, simplest key access, low latency.\n- **Default**: take SQL until a concrete bottleneck (write scale/schema) proves you need NoSQL. Polyglot persistence is fine: SQL for money + Redis for cache.\nNever answer \"NoSQL because it's more modern.\"","quizCorrect":"SQL — for ACID, relationships and joins; NoSQL — for horizontal write scaling and a flexible schema","quizWrong":["NoSQL is always faster than SQL on any query","SQL can't be scaled horizontally under any circumstances","The choice is made by which database is popular this year in engineering blogs and conference talks that year"],"picCap":"Choose by access pattern and transactional requirements, not by novelty"},"sd-sharding-key":{"q":"When is it time to shard, and how do you pick a shard key so you don't shoot yourself in the foot?","a":"You shard when a single node physically can't keep up: data won't fit on disk/in RAM, or writes hit the ceiling of one master. Pick the key so load spreads evenly and frequent queries land on a single shard. A bad key → hot shards and cross-shard joins that kill all the benefit.","d":"Hash sharding distributes evenly but kills range queries. Range sharding is good for ranges but creates hot spots (fresh data by time all pours into one shard). Consistent hashing reduces resharding when adding nodes.","note":"⚠️ The most common mistake is a shard key with low cardinality (e.g. country or status). All traffic funnels into a couple of shards, and you get a distributed system running at the speed of one node.","hook":"A shard key is like a seating chart: seat guests badly and one table is overflowing while the rest sit empty","more":"Sharding done right:\n- **Alternatives first**: read replicas, cache, vertical upgrade, partitioning within a single DB. Sharding is a last resort — it complicates everything.\n- **Key criteria**: high cardinality, evenness, and a frequent query landing on a single shard (locality).\n- **Hash vs range**: hash — even, but no range scans; range — has ranges, but hot spots on \"fresh\" data.\n- **The pain of sharding**: cross-shard joins/transactions, rebalancing, ID uniqueness (you need Snowflake-like generators).\n- **Consistent hashing**: adding a node moves only a fraction of keys, not the whole map.\nDon't shard prematurely — it's expensive to operate.","quizCorrect":"You shard when a single node can't handle the volume/writes; you pick the key for evenness and query locality","quizWrong":["You should shard from day one of any project for reliability, no matter how small the data set is at the start","The best key is an auto-increment ID, it's always even","Sharding always speeds up joins between tables"],"picCap":"A good key spreads evenly; a bad one collects traffic into a single hot shard"},"sd-replication":{"q":"Synchronous vs asynchronous replication — what do you pay in each case, and how does consistency come into it?","a":"Sync: the master waits for replica acknowledgment before responding to the client — no data loss if the master dies, but higher write latency and the risk of stalling if a replica lags. Async: the master responds immediately, replicas catch up later — fast, but if the master dies you lose the last unreplicated commits (replication lag).","d":"Reading from an async replica gives a \"read-your-writes\" problem: you write and then can't see your own write because the replica lagged. Fixed by routing fresh reads to the master or by semi-sync (wait for at least 1 replica).","note":"⚠️ Read replicas scale READS, not writes — all writes still go through one master. If you've hit the write ceiling, replicas won't save you; you need sharding.","hook":"Sync — you mail a package and wait for the recipient's signature. Async — you drop it in the mailbox and walk off: fast, but no guarantee it arrives","more":"Replication and consistency:\n- **Sync**: durability at the cost of latency. If a replica is unavailable, the write can stall. People use semi-sync (wait for at least one of N).\n- **Async**: low latency, but replication lag → eventual consistency on replicas.\n- **Read-your-writes**: a user doesn't see their own write due to lag. Fixes: sticky-routing fresh reads to the master, or read versions/tokens.\n- **Failover**: when the master dies, async can lose the \"tail\" of commits; you need a mechanism to elect a new master (Raft/Paxos, sentinel).\n- **Quorum (Dynamo-style)**: W+R>N gives strong consistency without a single master.\nThe choice is an explicit durability ↔ latency trade-off; spell it out in the interview.","quizCorrect":"Sync — no data loss but higher latency; async — faster, but if the master dies you lose unreplicated commits","quizWrong":["Async replication guarantees data won't be lost if the master dies: the lagging replica catches up by itself afterwards","Sync replication is always faster than async on write latency","Replicas always exactly match the master at every moment in time"],"picCap":"Sync waits for the replica (reliable/slow); async doesn't wait (fast/risk of loss on failure)"},"sd-rate-limiter":{"q":"Design a distributed rate limiter. How does token bucket in Redis differ from sliding window, and what do you pick?","a":"Token bucket: per key we store tokens and a timestamp; each request takes a token, tokens refill at a fixed rate — allows a burst up to the bucket size. Sliding window log/counter limits \"no more than N per window\" more precisely but costs more memory. In a distributed setup we count centrally in Redis (a shared counter for all instances), and we do the check itself atomically via a Lua script — otherwise there's a race between GET and SET.","d":"Sliding window counter is the compromise: two adjacent fixed windows with weighted interpolation. Cheap (2 counters) and almost no burst at the window boundary, unlike fixed window where you can let through 2×N at the edge.","note":"⚠️ Fixed window lets through up to 2× the limit at the window boundary: N requests at the end of a minute + N at the start of the next. For strict SLAs use sliding window.","hook":"A bucket of water: it drips in (refill), each request is a glass of water. Run dry — wait.","more":"**Why Lua and not INCR+EXPIRE separately:** between the commands another instance squeezes in and the limit leaks. A Lua script runs atomically in Redis — read-modify-write in a single operation.\n- **Fixed window** — just INCR a key `rl:{user}:{minute}` with EXPIRE 60, but gives a 2×N burst at the minute boundary.\n- **Sliding window counter** — take the current and previous window with weights: `prev*overlap + curr`.\n- **Token bucket** — the best choice for an API: smooth rate + controlled burst.\n- Multi-region usually rate-limits locally (per-region); global synchronization is expensive and unnecessary.","quizCorrect":"Token bucket allows a short burst up to the bucket size, sliding window log doesn't","quizWrong":["Token bucket is more precise than sliding window because it stores every request it sees","Sliding window is cheaper on memory than token bucket","Both algorithms guarantee a perfectly even interval between requests"],"picCap":"Each request takes a token; empty — 429. Refill at a fixed rate gives burst headroom."},"sd-url-shortener-id":{"q":"URL shortener: how do you generate a short id so there are no collisions and the next one can't be guessed?","a":"Two paths. (1) Counter + base62: encode an auto-increment id in base62 (a-zA-Z0-9) — guaranteed unique, short, but sequential and guessable. (2) Random 7-character base62 (~3.5 trillion variants) with a uniqueness check on insert. At scale you hand out id ranges to services via a range allocator (like Twitter Snowflake/Zookeeper) so you don't hit a shared counter on every request.","d":"Hashing the URL (MD5/SHA → first 7 base62) is tempting, but gives collisions for different long URLs and the same short for the same URL — which sometimes you don't want (privacy). Counter+base62 is more predictable in behavior and easier to debug.","note":"⚠️ Don't use a UUID as the short code — 36 characters, no \"shortness\" at all. A UUID is fine only as an internal PK, not as a public short code.","hook":"A coat-check ticket: handed out in order (counter), but printed in a compact font (base62).","more":"**Counter vs random — the tradeoff:**\n- **Counter+base62**: 0 collisions, minimal length, but ids are sequential → you can enumerate others' links (enumeration). Fix: salt/bit-shuffle, or a separate namespace.\n- **Random base62**: unpredictable, but needs a duplicate check on INSERT (unique constraint + retry).\n- **Range allocator**: each instance grabs a range [1..1000], hands them out locally, and rarely goes for a new one — removes contention on the global counter.\n- Storage: a KV store (short → long), key = short. Reads heavily dominate writes.","quizCorrect":"7 base62 characters give ~3.5 trillion combinations (62^7)","quizWrong":["7 base62 characters give about 10 million combinations","base62 encoding reduces the number of possible ids compared to base10","Hashing the URL into a short code guarantees no collisions"],"picCap":"A numeric id → base62 gives a compact alphanumeric short code."},"sd-url-shortener-redirect":{"q":"Redirect in a shortener: 301 or 302? And how do you cache hot links?","a":"302 (or 307) — temporary: the browser asks the server every time, you see clicks (analytics) and can change the target. 301 — permanent: the browser caches it forever, clicks after the first don't reach you, but it's faster and lower load. Hot links go into Redis/in-memory LRU in front of the DB: the short code is read on every click, and the click distribution is Zipf (a few links produce almost all the traffic).","d":"If the business metric is clicks and you need to revoke/reassign a link, take 302. If the link is eternal and analytics isn't critical, 301 saves infrastructure. In practice most shorteners take 302 for the analytics.","note":"⚠️ Once you set 301, you CAN'T properly count clicks or reassign the link — browsers and CDNs cache the redirect for a long time. Rolling back is hard.","hook":"301 — moved for good (the post office stops writing), 302 — temporarily staying with neighbors (they check each time whether you're home).","more":"**Caching under Zipf:** clicks per link are highly uneven — the top 1% of links produce the bulk of traffic. An LRU cache (Redis/Caffeine) with a TTL covers the hot codes, the DB is offloaded.\n- **301 (Moved Permanently)** — the browser remembers it, no repeat requests → fast, cheap, but clicks are invisible and you can't change the target.\n- **302/307 (Found/Temporary)** — every click hits the server → analytics, A/B, link revocation.\n- **404** for a nonexistent code; optionally a fallback page.\n- Protection: rate-limit on creation, check the target for malware/loops.","quizCorrect":"302 gives click analytics because the browser goes to the server every time","quizWrong":["301 gives better analytics because it's considered more reliable by search engines","301 and 302 are cached the same way by the browser","302 can't be used for redirects to external URLs"],"picCap":"Hot codes come from cache; a 302 response gives click analytics, 301 doesn't."},"sd-feed-fanout":{"q":"News feed: fan-out on write or on read? Explain the tradeoff and where it breaks.","a":"Fan-out on write (push): when a post is published we drop a copy into every follower's feed ahead of time — reading the feed is instant (read a ready-made list), but writing is expensive, especially for celebrities with millions of followers. Fan-out on read (pull): the feed is assembled at request time from the posts of those you follow — writing is cheap, but reading is slow (merging many sources). The choice depends on the read/write ratio and the follower distribution.","d":"The push problem — a \"celebrity\" with 50M followers: one post = 50M writes (fan-out storm). The pull problem — an active user with 5000 follows, every feed open is a merge of 5000 timelines. That's why in reality people take a hybrid.","note":"⚠️ Pure push on a celebrity = a \"fan-out storm\": one tweet spawns tens of millions of writes and lag. That's why celebrities are always moved to the pull branch.","hook":"Push — a paperboy puts a copy in every mailbox ahead of time. Pull — you walk the kiosks yourself on the way out. Nobody delivers the celebrity — there's a queue for them.","more":"**Why a hybrid (like Twitter/Instagram):**\n- **Push (write)** is good for most: reading the feed is one read of a ready list from cache (Redis list/timeline). But publishing a celebrity = a write storm.\n- **Pull (read)** is good for celebrities: their post isn't duplicated, it's pulled in rarely relative to the follower count.\n- **Threshold (STAR_THRESHOLD)** — e.g. >100k followers: those we don't push.\n- **Merge** on read: precomputed feed + celebrity posts, sorted by time/ranking.\n- Feed storage: Redis sorted set (score = timestamp), trimmed to the last N.","quizCorrect":"Hybrid: push regular authors into feeds, pull celebrities on read","quizWrong":["It's most efficient to push celebrities so their posts are in feeds instantly","Fan-out on read is always faster because it doesn't copy data","Fan-out on write is cheaper on writes than on read"],"picCap":"Push regular authors into feeds ahead of time; pull celebrities on read — a hybrid."},"sd-notifications":{"q":"Notification system: how do you ensure reliable delivery — retries, dedup, templates?","a":"We put the incoming event into a queue (Kafka/SQS), workers pick it up and send to channels (push/email/SMS). A provider fails — retry with exponential backoff and a dead-letter queue after N attempts. Dedup — by idempotency key (event_id): we store \"already sent\" in Redis/DB and drop the repeat. We don't hardcode content — templates (Mustache/Handlebars) with data substitution, kept separate from the delivery code.","d":"At-least-once delivery from a queue means duplicates — so dedup is mandatory on the sending side, otherwise a user gets 3 identical pushes. An idempotency key makes reprocessing safe.","note":"⚠️ Without jitter in the backoff, all workers retry in sync and create a \"thundering herd\" on the provider the moment it recovers. Add random spread.","hook":"Mail sorting: letters in the queue, each stamped with an id (don't send twice), a template form, and the undeliverable goes into the \"returns\" box (DLQ).","more":"**Key properties of a reliable notification system:**\n- **Queue buffer**: decouples event ingestion from delivery, survives spikes and provider outages.\n- **Retries with exponential backoff + jitter**: don't hammer a downed provider, spread attempts over time.\n- **DLQ**: after N failures the message goes to dead-letter for manual review, rather than getting lost or spinning forever.\n- **Dedup**: SETNX/a unique key on event_id — protection against at-least-once duplicates.\n- **Templates**: content separate from logic, versioned, supports locales.\n- **User preferences/quiet hours**: filtering before sending.","quizCorrect":"Dedup by idempotency key is needed because the queue gives at-least-once and duplicates","quizWrong":["Queues guarantee exactly-once, so dedup isn't needed","Retries are better done without backoff, to deliver faster to the device in the end to the handset","Templates slow down delivery and are avoided in production"],"picCap":"Queue → worker: dedup by key, render the template, retries with backoff and a DLQ."},"sd-counter-leaderboard":{"q":"Distributed counter and leaderboard: how do you avoid a hot key and get the top-N in milliseconds?","a":"A single shared counter on a popular object = a hot key, all increments fight over one row/key. The fix is sharded counters: split into K sub-counters (counter:id:0..K-1), write to a random shard, read by summing. For the leaderboard take a Redis Sorted Set (ZSET): ZADD writes the score, ZREVRANGE gives the top-N in O(log N), ZREVRANK gives a player's position without a full scan.","d":"The exact global sum across shards reads a bit more expensively (K reads), but writes scale linearly. If precision isn't critical (views) — sharded counters are ideal; if you need rank — the ZSET maintains order itself.","note":"⚠️ ZINCRBY on a single key for a very popular object also creates a hot key in Redis — it'll hit one shard/thread. For extreme write loads combine sharded counters + periodic flush of the aggregate into a ZSET.","hook":"A sharded counter — several checkouts instead of one line. A ZSET — a tournament table, always sorted, your rank visible instantly.","more":"**Two different problems — two tools:**\n- **Sharded counters** solve the write-side hot key: split the counter into K keys, INCR a random shard, total = the sum. Writes scale, reads are a bit more expensive.\n- **Sorted Set (ZSET)** solves the leaderboard: a skip-list inside, sorted by score. `ZREVRANGE 0 9` — top-10, `ZREVRANK` — a player's position, both ~O(log N).\n- **A huge leaderboard** (millions): shard the ZSET by ranges/regions, assemble the global top by merging the tops.\n- **Precision vs speed**: for \"likes/views\" an approximate sum is acceptable; for money/rating — an exact count.","quizCorrect":"A Sorted Set gives the top-N and a player's rank in O(log N) without a full scan","quizWrong":["To get the top-N you must sort all players in O(N log N) on every request that comes in","Sharded counters make reads faster and writes slower","A ZSET stores only the score with no way to get an element's rank"],"picCap":"Writes scattered across shards (no hot key); a ZSET keeps the sorted top-N."},"sd-payment-ledger":{"q":"Design a payment ledger: why double-entry, how do you guarantee exactly-once debit and an audit trail?","a":"A ledger is an immutable journal of entries (append-only), not a table with a mutable balance. Each transaction is two entries (debit one account, credit another), and their sum is always 0 — an invariant that catches bugs. We do the debit atomically in a single DB transaction together with inserting the entries; idempotency — by a client-supplied request_id (UNIQUE), so a retry doesn't charge twice.","d":"An account balance = the sum of all entries (you can materialize it into a snapshot table for speed, but the source of truth is the journal). You can't modify past entries — you fix a mistake with a compensating (reversal) entry, so the audit sees the full history.","note":"⚠️ Idempotency by \"amount+account+time\" is a trap: two legitimate identical payments collapse into one. The key must be generated by the client ahead of time (on the button/in the form), not by the server.","hook":"An accounting ledger: every dollar came from somewhere and went somewhere — otherwise the books don't balance.","more":"**Double-entry** — every operation touches at least two accounts with opposite signs, and the \"sum = 0\" invariant automatically catches lost/duplicated money.\n- **Idempotency**: the client supplies a unique request_id; a UNIQUE constraint turns a repeat retry into a no-op (ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING / catch the violation and return the previous result).\n- **Exactly-once debit** = at-least-once delivery (retries) + an idempotent receiver. Pure exactly-once doesn't exist over a network.\n- **Audit**: an append-only journal, fixes only via a reversal entry. No UPDATE/DELETE on history.\n- The balance is read from a materialized snapshot, but recomputed from the journal — the journal is the source of truth.","quizCorrect":"Each transaction = a pair of entries (debit+credit), their sum = 0, the journal is immutable","quizWrong":["Store only the current balance and overwrite it on every debit","Idempotency isn't needed if the DB supports transactions","An erroneous entry is deleted from the journal and the balance recomputed from scratch every time"],"picCap":"Two mirrored entries per transfer; their sum is always zero."},"sd-chat-delivery":{"q":"Design a messenger: how do you guarantee delivery and message order if the recipient is offline?","a":"The message is first persisted on the server (write-ahead), then distributed. Online clients get a push over WebSocket; offline ones pull from a mailbox/inbox on reconnect by last_seen_seq. Order is guaranteed by a monotonic per-conversation seq_id assigned by the server, not by client time (clocks are out of sync).","d":"Delivery is at-least-once: the client sends an ACK, the server retries until the ACK; on the client, dedup by message_id. Double checkmarks = sent (on the server), delivered (device ACK), read (read receipt).","note":"⚠️ Global ordering across the whole messenger isn't needed and doesn't scale — order matters only within a single conversation. Don't build one global counter bottleneck.","hook":"A post office: the letter first goes into the cubby (durable), and then the addressee is either home (push) or picks it up themselves (pull on reconnect).","more":"**Persist-then-fanout**: a durable write before distribution — otherwise if a node dies the message vanishes.\n- **Order**: a monotonic per-conversation seq_id (Redis INCR / sequencer), not global and not by client time.\n- **Offline**: a per-user mailbox or sync cursor; on reconnect the client sends last_seen_seq and pulls the tail.\n- **At-least-once delivery**: the server retries until the ACK, the client dedups by message_id (sender's UUID).\n- **Presence** is usually separate: a TTL key in Redis, a heartbeat every N sec; on disconnect the key expires → offline.","quizCorrect":"The server assigns a monotonic per-conversation seq and persists the message before distributing it","quizWrong":["Order is determined by the sender device's timestamp","Offline messages are lost, the client must be online","Exactly-once is achieved without an ACK and without client-side dedup on the receiving side at all whatsoever"],"picCap":"The server assigns a seq and persists BEFORE distributing; offline catches up by seq."},"sd-chat-presence":{"q":"How do you do presence (online/offline, typing…) in a messenger for millions of users?","a":"Presence is ephemeral state, you do NOT store it in the main DB. The client sends a heartbeat every ~30s, the server puts a key in Redis with a TTL slightly larger than the interval. No heartbeat → key expires → user offline. \"Typing…\" is a fire-and-forget event with no persistence, with a short TTL on the client.","d":"Fan out presence only to subscribers (friends/chat participants), not to everyone — otherwise O(N²) traffic. On a WS disconnect you can send an explicit offline, but the TTL is the safety net against lost connections.","hook":"A light bulb with a timer: as long as you press the button every 30 sec it stays lit; stop and after 45 sec it goes out on its own.","more":"**Ephemerality**: presence changes constantly and is irrelevant after a crash — its place is in an in-memory store (Redis), not a durable DB.\n- **TTL safety net**: an explicit offline on WebSocket close is unreliable (crash, network loss), so the TTL decides: beat 30s, ttl 45s.\n- **Targeted fan-out**: presence updates only to those who actually see the user (open chat, friends list), otherwise an event storm.\n- **Typing indicator** — the best example of fire-and-forget: losing the event is non-critical, no persistence needed.\n- At large scale presence is sharded by userId and aggregated, so you don't hit Redis on every list render.","quizCorrect":"Heartbeat + a Redis key with a TTL slightly larger than the interval; no beat → key expires → offline","quizWrong":["The online status is stored in the main DB and updated on every request from the client application the user makes","Presence is broadcast to all users in the system on any change","Offline is set only on an explicit logout, no TTL needed"],"picCap":"A TTL key + heartbeat: signal lost — the status goes out on its own, no explicit disconnect.","note":"The hidden pain of presence isn't the TTL itself but fan-out on 'heavy' users: one person with a million followers going online/offline generates a million notify events, and with a flapping connection this becomes a storm. So presence updates are debounced (don't emit until N seconds of stable state) and read via a pull model on demand, rather than pushing every online flicker."},"sd-object-storage-upload":{"q":"Design large-file upload to object storage: why pre-signed URLs and chunking?","a":"The client does NOT upload the file through your backend — that would kill throughput. The backend issues a pre-signed URL (a temporary signature for a PUT to S3/MinIO), and the client streams the bytes straight to storage. Large files are cut into chunks (multipart upload): parts upload in parallel, a failed one is re-uploaded separately, and at the end CompleteMultipartUpload assembles them into an object.","d":"Serving is the reverse: a pre-signed GET or a public object behind a CDN — static content is cached at the edge, the origin isn't loaded. Metadata (owner, size, status) — in your DB, the bytes themselves — in storage.","note":"⚠️ A pre-signed URL = a capability: whoever gets the link can write. Use a short TTL and narrow rights (only PUT, only this key), otherwise a leaked link = writing into someone else's space.","hook":"A warehouse pass: security (the backend) issues a temporary pass, and from there the truck drives to the warehouse itself, bypassing the office.","more":"**A pre-signed URL** offloads the backend: it doesn't proxy terabytes, it only authorizes and signs a short-lived link (minutes), bound to bucket+key+method.\n- **Multipart/chunking**: parallel part upload + retry only the failed part, not the whole file; mandatory for gigabyte uploads.\n- **Separation**: bytes in object storage (S3/MinIO), metadata in the DB; the 'uploaded' status is set via a completion callback or verify.\n- **CDN on serving**: an edge cache serves popular objects close to the user, the origin (storage) is touched rarely.\n- In document-service it's exactly this: PDFs in MinIO, metadata in Postgres.","quizCorrect":"The backend issues a temporary pre-signed URL, the client streams bytes straight to storage, large files use multipart","quizWrong":["The file always passes through the backend, which writes it to the DB as bytea and then streams it back to the client on every read","A pre-signed URL is issued indefinitely for convenient re-upload","Multipart is only needed for images, videos are uploaded in a single request"],"picCap":"The backend only signs; chunks fly straight to storage, serving via a CDN."},"sd-autocomplete-search":{"q":"Design autocomplete and search: where do you use a trie, where an inverted index, and what's the difference?","a":"These are different problems. Autocomplete is prefix-based: \"how to continue a started word\" — that's a trie (prefix tree), where nodes store the top-K popular completions. Full-text search is \"find documents containing words\" — that's an inverted index: word → list of documents where it occurs. Prefixes are fast because they're precomputed, not computed on the fly.","d":"Often a trie is replaced by a ready-made engine (Elasticsearch completion suggester / edge n-grams) — it also gives ranking and typo handling. A bare trie is good for understanding, but in production people rarely write one by hand.","note":"⚠️ A trie in memory for the whole query corpus is huge. In production you shard it by first letter/prefix hash and keep only the top-K in nodes, not all variants.","hook":"A trie is a table of contents by first letters, leading to the continuation. An inverted index is the subject index at the back of a book: word → pages.","more":"**Trie (prefix tree)**: the path from the root = the prefix; to avoid scanning the whole subtree on every keystroke, each node caches the top-K popular completions — an answer in O(prefix length).\n- **Inverted index**: for each term — a sorted posting list of doc_id; a multi-word query = intersection of lists (AND) or union (OR).\n- **Ranking**: popularity/frequency for suggestions; TF-IDF or BM25 for full-text.\n- **Typos/fuzziness**: edge n-grams, fuzzy (Levenshtein distance) — usually served by Elasticsearch, not a bare trie.\n- In practice both structures more often come from an engine (ES/OpenSearch), and a hand-written trie is for interviews and narrow cases.","quizCorrect":"Trie — prefix autocomplete with precomputed top-K; inverted index — document search by words via intersection of posting lists","quizWrong":["A trie and an inverted index are the same thing, the difference is only in the name","An inverted index stores word prefixes in a tree","Autocomplete always computes the top-K on the fly with a full scan of the query base on every keystroke the user makes while typing"],"picCap":"A trie answers \"how to continue a prefix\"; an inverted index — \"which documents contain a word\"."},"sd-distributed-cache":{"q":"Design a distributed cache: why consistent hashing, and how do you deal with cache stampede and invalidation?","a":"Keys are spread across nodes via consistent hashing: adding or removing a node remaps only ~1/N of the keys instead of the whole cache (as a plain mod N would). Invalidation is usually TTL plus an explicit delete on DB writes (or write-through). Cache stampede (thousands of misses on a hot key hitting the DB at once) is tamed with a single-flight recompute lock and/or an early refresh before the TTL expires.","d":"Virtual nodes (vnodes) even out the load on the ring; without them one node ends up owning a disproportionate share of keys. Cache-to-DB consistency is always eventual — pick your strategy (cache-aside / write-through) based on how much staleness you can tolerate.","note":"⚠️ A common invalidation mistake is updating the cache AFTER writing to the DB (set instead of delete): if two updates race, the cache can end up holding the old value. delete-on-write is safer — let the next read repopulate it.","hook":"A coat-check ring: hang a new hook and only a handful of nearby coats move, not the whole wardrobe. And one runner fetches the coat, not a whole crowd at once.","more":"**Consistent hashing**: nodes and keys are placed on a single hash ring, and a key goes to the nearest node clockwise; when membership changes, only a segment of the ring moves (~1/N of the keys).\n- **Vnodes**: each physical node maps to many points on the ring → even load and smooth rebalancing.\n- **Stampede (thundering herd)**: when a hot key's TTL expires, an avalanche of requests slams the DB. Fixes: single-flight (one recompute, the rest wait or serve stale) + TTL jitter + early/probabilistic refresh.\n- **Invalidation**: cache-aside (delete-on-write) or write-through; consistency is eventual — budget for acceptable staleness.\n- Mind the hot key: even with the ring, a single popular key lives on one node — you need a local cache or key replication.","quizCorrect":"Consistent hashing moves ~1/N of the keys on a reshard; stampede is contained with single-flight/early refresh; invalidation is TTL plus explicit delete","quizWrong":["Adding a node makes consistent hashing redistribute all keys evenly","Cache stampede is solved by raising the TTL to infinity","Invalidation isn't needed: cache and DB are always strictly consistent with each other, because every write goes through both of them in one transaction on the write path"],"picCap":"The ring moves only part of the keys on a reshard; single-flight absorbs the miss avalanche."},"grpc-codegen-idl":{"q":"What is a .proto file and why is gRPC called contract-first?","a":"A `.proto` file is an IDL (interface definition language): in a single file you declaratively describe messages and services, and the `protoc` compiler generates client and server code for any language. The contract comes first: schema first, then implementation — that's why it's contract-first. A server and client written in different languages are guaranteed to be compatible, because both were generated from the same file.","d":"In REST the contract is more often described after the fact (OpenAPI generated from already-written code), and the two can easily drift apart. In gRPC the `.proto` is the single source of truth, and divergence is impossible at the type level.","hook":"One blueprint for a part — a machinist on any lathe turns out the exact same thing","more":"An **IDL (Interface Definition Language)** is a platform-independent language. `protoc` with plugins (grpc-java, grpc-go, etc.) turns a `.proto` into:\n- **message classes** — type-safe DTOs with serialization;\n- **stubs** — a client-side proxy whose methods look like local calls;\n- **service base classes** — the server just overrides the business logic.\n\nPlus **contract-first**: reviewing the API boils down to reviewing a single `.proto`, and schema versioning becomes versioning that file.","quizCorrect":"protoc generates client and server code from a .proto for different languages","quizWrong":[".proto compiles to a JSON schema that the runtime reads","The contract is written by hand in each language separately and then reconciled",".proto is only needed by the server; the client works via reflection"],"picCap":"One contract → generated code for every language","note":"Contract-first doesn't save you from semantic incompatibility: proto3 removed required and made ALL scalar fields default-valued (0, \"\", false) with no presence info — so until you add optional (proto3 presence, reintroduced in protoc 3.15) you cannot distinguish 'field not sent' from 'sent 0/empty string'. This routinely breaks PATCH semantics and validation: the server sees amount=0 and can't tell whether it's a deliberate zero or a missing field."},"grpc-http2":{"q":"Why is gRPC built on HTTP/2 rather than HTTP/1.1?","a":"HTTP/2 provides multiplexing: dozens of RPCs fly in parallel over a single TCP connection without application-level head-of-line blocking. Plus binary framing (instead of text) and HPACK header compression — cheap and fast. And HTTP/2 supports streams in both directions, which is what makes gRPC's streaming calls possible.","d":"In HTTP/1.1 a connection handles one request at a time; parallelism comes from a connection pool. HTTP/2 instead runs many logical streams over one connection — each RPC is a single stream.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: browsers don't let code directly control HTTP/2 frames and trailers, so pure gRPC from the browser is impossible — you need gRPC-Web plus a proxy (Envoy).","hook":"HTTP/1.1 — one checkout serving in turn; HTTP/2 — many cashiers in one hall","more":"Key HTTP/2 properties gRPC relies on:\n- **Multiplexing** — many RPCs over one TCP connection, without opening a connection pool;\n- **Binary framing** — data is split into binary frames (DATA, HEADERS), cheaper to parse than text;\n- **HPACK** — compression of repeated headers;\n- **Server-initiated streams** — the server can push frames to the client on its own → streaming RPCs.\n\n**HOL caveat:** HTTP/2 removes head-of-line blocking at the HTTP level, but not at the TCP level — losing a single packet stalls all streams. HTTP/3 (QUIC over UDP) fixes this.","quizCorrect":"Multiplexing streams over one connection plus binary framing","quizWrong":["HTTP/2 encrypts traffic, which HTTP/1.1 fundamentally cannot at all","HTTP/2 runs over UDP, so it's faster","Only HTTP/2 supports JSON, which Protobuf needs"],"picCap":"One TCP connection, many parallel streams"},"grpc-call-types":{"q":"What are the 4 call types in gRPC and how do they differ?","a":"Unary — a plain request-response (one to one). Server-streaming — one request, a stream of responses (subscriptions, dumping a list). Client-streaming — a stream of requests, one response (chunked upload, aggregation). Bidirectional — both sides stream independently over one connection (chat, realtime). All of this is possible thanks to HTTP/2's bidirectional streams.","hook":"One-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-one, many-to-many — like four shapes of dialogue","more":"Where each is used:\n- **Unary** — 90% of calls, classic RPC;\n- **Server-streaming** — deliver a large list as a stream rather than all at once; subscribe to events (quotes, progress);\n- **Client-streaming** — upload a file/metrics in chunks, the server aggregates and replies once;\n- **Bidirectional** — independent streams both ways: chat, an order book, telemetry.\n\nWithin a single direction message order is guaranteed (an HTTP/2 stream is ordered), but client and server send independently — neither waits for the other.","quizCorrect":"unary, server-streaming, client-streaming, bidirectional","quizWrong":["GET, POST, PUT, DELETE — like REST, just binary over HTTP/2","sync, async, batch, polling","request, response, push, pull"],"picCap":"Arrows = direction and number of messages","note":"Ordering is guaranteed only within ONE HTTP/2 stream (a single call); in a bidirectional stream there's no automatic request-to-response correlation — if you send several requests without waiting for replies, the server may answer in any order and you must correlate yourself (via an id in the message). Also a classic trap: gRPC streams keep the HTTP/2 connection open, and without an explicit deadline/keepalive a 'stuck' server-streaming call (quotes, subscription) can silently die on an intermediate L4 balancer/idle-timeout while the client waits forever."},"grpc-proto-fields":{"q":"Why are Protobuf fields numbered, and why does the number matter more than the name?","a":"In the binary format what goes on the wire is not the field name but its number (field tag) plus type. So compatibility rests on the numbers: you can rename a field — nothing breaks — but changing the number breaks the format. Numbers 1–15 are encoded in a single byte, so they're reserved for frequently used fields.","d":"The decoder uses the number to know which field to assign the value to. An unknown number is simply skipped (unknown field) — that's the foundation of forward compatibility.","hook":"A field number is like a seat number on a plane: the passenger may change their name, but the seat stays the same","more":"Encoding: each field = a **varint tag** `(number << 3 | wire_type)` plus the value. From this:\n- **1–15** → the tag fits in 1 byte, give these to hot fields;\n- **16+** → 2 bytes;\n- **19000–19999** are reserved by the protocol and must not be used.\n\nField names are entirely absent from the binary payload (unlike JSON). So renaming a field changes only the source, not the format. But two different fields sharing one number is a disaster: the decoder will mix up the data.","quizCorrect":"The field number goes on the wire, so renaming is safe but changing the number is not","quizWrong":["Numbers are only needed to sort fields when printing","The field name goes on the wire; the number is just for documentation and readability of the schema","Field numbers must run consecutively with no gaps"],"picCap":"The name is for humans, the number is for the bytes","note":"Deleting a field by just removing it from the .proto is a landmine: the number can later be reused for a different type, and old serialized data/clients decode corrupted; that's exactly why you need reserved (for both numbers and names), which protoc enforces to prevent reuse. Also: changing a field's type while keeping its number is compatible ONLY within the same wire_type group (int32/int64/uint32/bool/enum are interchangeable as varint, but int32↔string breaks decoding) — the number matches but the data reads as garbage."},"grpc-schema-evolution":{"q":"How do you safely evolve a .proto, and why do you need reserved?","a":"The rule: add new fields with new numbers — old clients simply ignore them (forward-compat), new ones see them (backward-compat). Never reuse the number of a deleted field: mark it `reserved` so the compiler won't let you accidentally take the old tag and read someone else's data as your own. You can delete fields, but you must not change their type or number.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: proto3 has no `required`, and by default you can't tell \"the field wasn't sent\" from \"the default was sent (0/\\\"\\\")\". If the difference matters, wrap it in `optional` (proto3) or use wrapper types.","hook":"reserved is like a welded-shut hotel-room door: no new guests get checked in there","more":"**Compatibility:**\n- **Backward** — new code reads old data (new fields take their defaults);\n- **Forward** — old code reads new data (unknown fields are preserved as unknown fields, not lost on re-serialization).\n\nSafe changes: add a field, delete a field (with reserved), rename a field. Dangerous: change the number, change the type (`int32`→`string`), or make a singular field `repeated` without understanding wire compatibility.\n\n**reserved** blocks both the number and the name at compile time — protection against someone a year later taking tag 2 for a different field and starting to read old bytes as new.","quizCorrect":"Add fields with new numbers; mark deleted numbers reserved and never reuse them","quizWrong":["You can change a field's number if you update client and server in sync","reserved is for hiding a field from serialization, but the number stays free for reuse","You can never delete fields, only mark them deprecated"],"picCap":"reserved \"locks\" the number forever; a new field takes the next one"},"grpc-vs-rest":{"q":"gRPC vs REST: where does gRPC really win, and where does it lose?","a":"gRPC wins on performance (binary + HTTP/2 + multiplexing), on a strict contract (codegen from `.proto`, type safety), and on streaming. It loses on debugging (binary can't be read by eye like JSON) and in the browser (needs gRPC-Web plus a proxy). Roughly: internal service-to-service — gRPC; public APIs for browsers/partners — REST/JSON.","hook":"gRPC — the service elevator between floors; REST — the front entrance for guests","more":"Breakdown by axis:\n- **Contract** — gRPC is strict (types from `.proto`, generation), REST is conventional (OpenAPI on top of HTTP semantics);\n- **Performance** — Protobuf is more compact and parses faster than JSON, HTTP/2 multiplexes; for high-RPS internal traffic this is tangible;\n- **Browser** — there's no native gRPC from the browser, you need gRPC-Web plus Envoy/a proxy; REST/JSON works out of the box;\n- **Debugging** — REST is readable with `curl` and logs; gRPC needs `grpcurl` and reflection, and the payload is binary.\n\nBottom line: microservices among themselves and mobile clients — gRPC; public/browser APIs — REST. They often coexist via a gRPC-gateway (a REST facade over gRPC).","quizCorrect":"gRPC: performance/contract/streaming; REST: debugging and native browser support","quizWrong":["gRPC is always faster and more convenient; REST is obsolete in every way that matters","REST supports streaming, while gRPC is request-response only","gRPC works natively in the browser; REST requires a proxy"],"picCap":"gRPC — inward into the system; REST — outward to the browser","note":"gRPC's default message size limit is 4 MB (maxInboundMessageSize), and with large payloads you suddenly hit RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED instead of a clear error; for big data you must either raise the limit or switch to streaming, because a single gRPC message is always buffered fully in memory rather than streamed byte-by-byte within the message itself."},"grpc-deadlines-status":{"q":"How do deadlines, cancellation, interceptors, and error codes work in gRPC?","a":"A deadline is an absolute \"no later than\" time that the client attaches to a call; it propagates down the call chain through the context, and each service trims its own budget. Cancellation (client cancel or an expired deadline) propagates to the server and onward — everyone raises `CANCELLED`/`DEADLINE_EXCEEDED`. Interceptors are middleware around an RPC (auth, logging, metrics). Errors are not HTTP codes but gRPC status codes (`OK`, `NOT_FOUND`, `UNAVAILABLE`...).","d":"Important: the client sets a deadline (a point in time), not a timeout duration — so when propagated through 5 services there's one deadline for the whole chain, not a fresh one reset at each hop.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: if you set no deadline at all, a call can hang forever — gRPC does NOT impose a default timeout. Always set `withDeadlineAfter` on the client.","hook":"A deadline is the 18:00 train across all your transfers, not \"10 minutes per station\"","more":"**Deadline propagation:** the client sets a deadline, and gRPC puts the remaining budget into the metadata of every outgoing call. Service A, calling B, passes the remainder along — if the budget is spent, B doesn't even start. This cuts cascading hangs.\n\n**Cancellation:** the client closed the call or the deadline expired → the server context is marked cancelled, and the handler must check this and stop working (especially in streaming).\n\n**Interceptors:** wrap an RPC like middleware — auth, tracing, metrics, retries; there are client and server, unary and streaming variants.\n\n**Status codes:** their own set (`OK=0`, `INVALID_ARGUMENT`, `NOT_FOUND`, `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED`, `UNAVAILABLE`, `UNAUTHENTICATED`...). `UNAVAILABLE` is usually retryable, `INVALID_ARGUMENT` is not.","quizCorrect":"A deadline is an absolute point in time, propagated along the chain; cancellation reaches everyone; errors are gRPC status codes","quizWrong":["Each service in the chain gets a fresh timeout again","gRPC uses HTTP codes 404/500 like REST","An interceptor can change the .proto contract on the fly during a call, so the client and the server can disagree about the schema without any error at all"],"picCap":"One deadline for the whole chain; cancellation propagates"},"bld-lifecycle-phases":{"q":"What are Maven lifecycle phases, and why does `mvn package` also run compile and test?","a":"A lifecycle is an ordered chain of phases: validate → compile → test → package → verify → install → deploy. When you invoke any phase, Maven runs ALL the preceding ones in order, not just the one you named. That's why `mvn package` compiles and tests first, and only then assembles the artifact.","d":"There are technically more phases (process-resources, prepare-package, etc.), but these 7 are the main anchor points. Plugin goals are bound to each phase, and the goals do the actual work.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: `install` puts the artifact into your LOCAL cache `~/.m2/repository`, while `deploy` pushes it to a REMOTE repository. Mixing them up in CI is classic — people assume install \"published\" it somewhere.","hook":"Escalator: you can step on at any step — but you got there from the very bottom, you can't skip ahead","more":"**Lifecycle** is the build template, not the actions themselves. The real work is done by plugin **goals** bound to phases.\n- **validate** — check the project is correct\n- **compile** — compile src/main/java\n- **test** — run unit tests (surefire)\n- **package** — assemble the jar/war\n- **install** — put the artifact into the local `~/.m2`\n- **deploy** — upload to a remote repo (Nexus/Artifactory)\nThere are three separate lifecycles: `default` (build), `clean`, `site`. They're independent, so `mvn clean package` is two chains run back to back.","quizCorrect":"Maven runs every phase up to and including the named one, in order","quizWrong":["Maven runs only the named phase; the rest you invoke manually afterwards","Maven runs the named phase and everything after it","Phase order is defined in pom.xml and can be anything"],"picCap":"You invoked package — every phase to its left ran too"},"bld-dependency-scopes":{"q":"How do the compile, provided, runtime and test scopes differ? Where does provided get provided?","a":"A scope controls at which stages (compile/test/runtime) a dependency is available and whether it lands in the final artifact. `compile` — everywhere and in the package. `provided` — present at compile time, but at runtime the container/server supplies it. `runtime` — absent at compile time, present at runtime (drivers). `test` — tests only.","d":"The default scope is compile. `provided` and `test` are NOT transitive and do NOT end up in the fat-jar. The classic provided case is the Servlet API: the servlet container (Tomcat) supplies it.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: `provided` is NOT transitive. If your lib declares something as provided, consumers of your lib won't get it — they must supply it themselves. A frequent source of NoClassDefFoundError downstream.","hook":"provided = \"the tool is already at the workplace\": you need it in your head to build, but you don't pack it into the product box — the factory already has it","more":"**compile** (default) — on every classpath, transitive, in the artifact.\n**provided** — present at compile/test, but at runtime it's expected from outside (Servlet API, Lombok partly, container libs). Not transitive, not in the fat-jar.\n**runtime** — absent at compile time (you don't write code against it), but needed to run. Example: a JDBC driver — you code against the JDBC API, and the driver is loaded via reflection.\n**test** — JUnit, Mockito. Only when compiling/running tests.\nThere's also **system** (path to a local jar, a deprecated practice) and **import** (only in dependencyManagement for a BOM).","quizCorrect":"provided is available at compile time, but at runtime the container supplies it and it isn't packaged into the artifact","quizWrong":["provided is available only at runtime; it's absent at compile time and therefore cannot be used in the code you compile against it","provided always ends up in the fat-jar like compile","provided makes a dependency optional and transitive"],"picCap":"Which dependency is visible at which classpath stage"},"bld-version-mediation":{"q":"Two transitive dependencies pull different versions of the same library. Which one does Maven pick?","a":"Maven uses the \"nearest wins\" rule — the version closest to the root of the dependency tree (fewer transitivity levels) wins. At equal depth, the one declared earlier in the pom wins. The loser is marked \"omitted for conflict\".","d":"This is NOT \"highest version wins\" (Gradle's default). Maven looks at distance in the tree, not the version number. So a nearer 1.0 beats a farther 2.0.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: nearest-wins can silently downgrade a version to an old/vulnerable one just because it happened to be nearer. It's safer not to rely on mediation but to pin versions in dependencyManagement.","hook":"Not \"who's older\" but \"who's closer to the boss\": the near relative inherits, the distant one misses out","more":"**Mediation** resolves version conflicts in the dependency graph.\n- **Nearest wins**: minimal transitivity depth from the root.\n- **At equal depth**: the one encountered earlier while traversing the pom wins (declaration order).\nIn `dependency:tree -Dverbose` the losing branch is marked **(omitted for conflict with X)**.\nIf nearest-wins picked an inconvenient version — you fix it via **dependencyManagement** (hard-pins the version regardless of depth) or via `<exclusions>`.\nGradle, by contrast, uses \"highest version wins\" — a common point of confusion in interviews when switching build tools.","quizCorrect":"The version nearest the tree root wins; at a tie, the one declared earlier","quizWrong":["The highest version always wins (highest wins)","The last one declared in the pom always wins","Maven fails and demands you specify the version explicitly in the pom of the module"],"picCap":"Nearest wins: closer to the root — 1.0 beats 2.0 even though 2.0 is newer"},"bld-dependency-management-bom":{"q":"Why dependencyManagement if you can specify the version right in the dependency? And what is a BOM?","a":"`dependencyManagement` centrally PINS versions (plus scope/exclusions) but doesn't add the dependency itself — modules declare it without a version and inherit the pinned one. A BOM (Bill of Materials) is a special pom that, via `<scope>import</scope>`, pulls in a whole set of consistent versions (e.g. the Quarkus BOM).","d":"dependencyManagement overrides nearest-wins: its version applies to ANY transitive dependency, regardless of depth. A BOM is imported into the dependencyManagement section.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: dependencyManagement overrides EVEN transitive versions. That's its superpower and its trap at once — you can accidentally force the version of a deep transitive lib and break compatibility.","hook":"BOM = the warehouse price list: each shop orders a part by name and takes the price (version) from a single list, it doesn't make up its own","more":"**dependencyManagement** — a section where versions/scope/exclusions are set, but NO linking happens. A module that declares a dependency without `<version>` gets the pinned one.\nWhy: a single place to bump versions, no drift between modules, and override of transitive versions (stronger than nearest-wins).\n**BOM** — a pom artifact consisting mostly of dependencyManagement. Imported via `<type>pom</type><scope>import</scope>`. Platforms (Spring Boot, Quarkus) ship a BOM with a guaranteed-compatible version set — you just add starters without versions.\nDifference from a parent pom: you can import several BOMs, but you can have only one parent.","quizCorrect":"dependencyManagement pins versions centrally, and dependencies are added by modules without specifying a version","quizWrong":["dependencyManagement adds dependencies to all modules automatically of the project, so declaring them again is unnecessary","A BOM is a fat-jar with all the libraries inside","dependencyManagement works only within a single module"],"picCap":"A BOM is imported into dependencyManagement → modules take versions with no duplication"},"bld-multimodule-reactor":{"q":"What is the reactor in a multi-module build, and how does it figure out the module build order?","a":"The reactor is the Maven mechanism that builds all modules of a multi-module project in a single run. It builds a graph of inter-module dependencies and topologically sorts them: the ones others depend on are built first. The order in `<modules>` is just the starting point — the graph decides the real order.","d":"If module-app depends on module-core, the reactor builds core before app, even if they're listed in the opposite order in `<modules>`. The `-pl` (project list) and `-am` (also make) flags control partial builds.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: with `-pl app` and no `-am`, the reactor takes core from `~/.m2` (possibly stale!) instead of rebuilding it. Forget `-am` — and you build against an old core.","hook":"Reactor = the site foreman: foundation first (core), then walls (app) — logic dictates the order, not the list in the estimate","more":"**Reactor** kicks in when the parent pom has `<packaging>pom</packaging>` and a `<modules>` section. It:\n- builds the inter-module dependency graph;\n- topologically sorts it (dependency order);\n- builds all modules in one pass, passing artifacts through the reactor cache (not through `~/.m2`).\nHandy flags: **-pl** (--projects) build specific modules; **-am** (--also-make) add their dependencies; **-amd** (--also-make-dependents) add those depending on them; **-T 1C** parallel build per core; **-rf** (--resume-from) continue from the failed module.\nThis is the CI backbone for monorepos: rebuild only the affected module and its dependents.","quizCorrect":"The reactor topologically sorts modules by their mutual dependencies and builds dependents later","quizWrong":["The reactor builds modules strictly in the order of the <modules> tags in the pom file itself of the parent","The reactor builds all modules in parallel ignoring dependencies","The reactor is a plugin you have to add separately"],"picCap":"The reactor sorts modules by dependencies, not by pom order"},"bld-plugin-goal":{"q":"What's the difference between a plugin and a goal, and how is a goal tied to a lifecycle phase?","a":"A plugin is a set of related tasks (e.g. maven-surefire-plugin). A goal is a specific task inside a plugin (e.g. surefire:test). It's the goals that do the actual work: a lifecycle phase does nothing by itself — plugin goals are just BOUND to it and those goals execute.","d":"You can invoke a goal directly with `mvn compiler:compile` (plugin:goal) or via the phase `mvn compile` that the goal is bound to by default. The binding is set in `<executions>` or via packaging defaults.","hook":"Plugin = toolbox, goal = a specific tool. A phase is the \"moment on the schedule\" when the tool is pulled out","more":"**Plugin** — an artifact (its own groupId:artifactId) containing one or more **Mojo**s — these are the goals (Maven plain Old Java Object).\nInvocation: `mvn plugin-prefix:goal` (e.g. `mvn dependency:tree`) or via a phase.\n**Binding a goal to a phase** (plugin binding) comes in two forms:\n- default — packaging `jar` automatically binds compiler:compile to compile, surefire:test to test, jar:jar to package;\n- explicit — via `<executions>` in the pom you specify `<phase>` and `<goals>`.\nFlexibility: the same shade-plugin is bound to package, flyway:migrate can be bound to any phase or invoked by hand. Goals outside the lifecycle (e.g. `dependency:tree`) are utilities you invoke directly.","quizCorrect":"A goal is a specific plugin task; it's bound to a phase and runs when the build reaches it","quizWrong":["Plugin and goal are synonyms, no difference","A phase does the work itself, and plugins only configure it, so a phase can be executed on its own","A goal can be invoked only via a phase, never directly"],"picCap":"A plugin contains goals; a goal is bound to a phase and runs there"},"bld-profiles":{"q":"What are Maven profiles, and how does activation via `-P` differ from `activeByDefault`?","a":"A profile is a named set of overrides (dependencies, plugins, properties) enabled by a condition: explicitly via `-Pname`, by a property, by JDK, by OS, or by a missing file. It lets you tailor the build per environment (dev/prod) without touching the main pom.","d":"Trap: `activeByDefault` switches off the moment ANY other profile from that pom is activated via `-P` — which is unintuitive and breaks builds.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: `activeByDefault` is treacherous — it works ONLY while no other profile from the same pom is activated via -P. Activate any other -P and the default silently switches off. Better to activate by a property with a default value.","hook":"Profile = the mode switch on a washing machine: same drum (pom), but \"delicate/quick\" changes the program to fit the situation","more":"**Profile** lives in pom.xml (or settings.xml — there for secrets/repos) and overrides: dependencies, plugins, properties, repositories, modules.\nActivation methods:\n- **-Pname** — explicitly from the CLI;\n- **`<activation>`** — by `<property>`, `<jdk>`, `<os>`, presence/absence of a file, or `<activeByDefault>true</activeByDefault>`;\nTypical use: dev/staging/prod configs, enabling integration tests, different repos, Quarkus native build (`-Pnative`).\nCheck the active ones: `mvn help:active-profiles`.","quizCorrect":"A profile is a set of build overrides activated explicitly (-P) or by a condition (property/JDK/OS)","quizWrong":["A profile is a separate pom file per environment","Profiles are always active at once and stack in order","A profile can change only properties, not dependencies and plugins, which are always shared by every profile"],"picCap":"One pom — different builds depending on the active profile"},"bld-fatjar-shade-relocation":{"q":"How does shade differ from assembly when building a fat-jar, and why is relocation needed?","a":"Both shade and assembly build a fat-jar (uber-jar) — a single jar with all dependencies inside. assembly simply unpacks the dependency classes side by side. shade is smarter: it correctly merges files from META-INF/services (SPI) and can do relocation — renaming a dependency's packages to avoid version conflicts. Relocation saves you from \"dependency hell\" when two libs pull different versions of a shared one.","d":"Without merging SPI descriptors a fat-jar often breaks (ServiceLoader providers get lost). That's why shade is preferred over assembly for a shadow jar.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: relocation rewrites package names in the bytecode, but does NOT fix string references in reflection/configs (Class.forName(\"com.google...\")). After shading such code silently fails at runtime.","hook":"Relocation = re-labeling: two identical cans (guava) on the shelf — you rename one so the cook doesn't grab the wrong one","more":"**Fat-jar (uber-jar)** — a self-contained jar with all dependencies; run with `java -jar`.\n**maven-assembly-plugin** — a general packager (`jar-with-dependencies`) that just unpacks dependency classes into one jar. Doesn't merge SPI descriptors correctly → breaks ServiceLoader.\n**maven-shade-plugin** — specialized for the uber-jar:\n- **Transformers** merge `META-INF/services`, `spring.handlers`, the manifest;\n- **Relocation** renames packages (shading) so your dependency's version doesn't conflict with the host app's version or another lib's. Especially important for libraries embedded into someone else's applications.\nQuarkus/Spring Boot use their own format (nested jars + a custom loader) instead of shade.","quizCorrect":"shade merges META-INF/services and can relocate packages; assembly just dumps the classes together","quizWrong":["assembly can do relocation, shade cannot","relocation removes duplicate dependencies from the jar","shade and assembly are identical, only the config syntax differs and the plugin coordinates in the pom"],"picCap":"Relocation renames the conflicting version's package — both coexist"},"bld-impl-vs-api":{"q":"In Gradle: how does `implementation` differ from `api`, and why does `api` \"leak\"?","a":"`implementation` — the dependency is visible only inside the module: consumers of your module do NOT see it on their compile classpath. `api` — the dependency leaks onward: it ends up on the compile classpath of everyone who depends on you. `api` \"leaks\" because it transitively imposes its library (and its version) on everyone up the chain.","d":"Rule: use `api` only if a type from that dependency actually shows up in your public API (in a public method signature, return type, parameter). Everything else — `implementation`. This trims consumers' classpaths and speeds up incremental rebuilds: change an internal dependency and only your module rebuilds, not half the world.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: `compile` is deprecated and removed in Gradle 7+. Old guides using `compile` effectively mean `api` (with the leak), which is exactly what caused bloated classpaths. When migrating, default to `implementation`, and switch to `api` only where truly needed.","hook":"api is a window to the outside (everyone sees), implementation is the back kitchen (locked behind a door)","more":"Gradle configurations describe the **visibility graph** of dependencies, not just their presence.\n- **api** — the dependency lands on both the compile and runtime classpath of consumers. Use it when the library's type is visible in the public signature.\n- **implementation** — the dependency is on your compile classpath and the consumer's runtime, but NOT on the consumer's compile classpath. The consumer can't accidentally use your transitive dep.\n- **Benefit of implementation:** fewer API leaks, fewer rebuilds (an ABI change in an internal dependency doesn't touch consumers), a cleaner graph.\nThe `java-library` plugin provides `api`; the plain `java` plugin doesn't — there you only have `implementation`.","quizCorrect":"`api` places the dependency on the compile classpath of the module's consumers, `implementation` does not","quizWrong":["`api` is for prod dependencies, `implementation` only for tests","There's no visibility difference, only classpath order","`implementation` leaks to consumers, while `api` isolates the dependency inside the module from its consumers"],"picCap":"api leaks into the consumer's compile classpath, implementation doesn't"},"bld-version-resolution":{"q":"Two transitive deps pull different versions of the same lib. Which does Gradle take, and how do you pin it?","a":"By default Gradle picks the HIGHEST of the requested versions (highest-version-wins), not the first found. To pin it hard, use `constraints` or a `dependency` with `strictly`. This differs from Maven, which takes the \"nearest in the tree\" (nearest-wins).","d":"`constraints` declare a version policy without adding the dependency itself to the classpath: the version applies only if that lib is actually pulled in transitively by someone. `strictly` is already a hard requirement: if someone requests an incompatible version, the build fails with a conflict instead of silently \"smoothing it over\".","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: `force` (the old way) bluntly overrides a version with no stated reason and combines poorly. The modern path is `constraints` + `strictly` — they give clear conflict messages and rich versions (ranges, reject).","hook":"Gradle takes the \"highest by version\", Maven takes the \"closest by kinship\"","more":"Version resolution in Gradle is about **picking one version per module** in a single graph.\n- **Default strategy:** highest-version-wins among all requested versions.\n- **constraints** — a recommendation/policy: doesn't pull the lib itself, but dictates the version if it's pulled transitively. Ideal for BOM-like management.\n- **strictly** — a hard requirement: downgrades the version even against highest-wins and fails the build on incompatibility.\n- **failOnVersionConflict()** in `resolutionStrategy` — forces a failure on any divergence, so versions are explicit.\nThere's also `platform()` for importing a BOM (like Maven's dependencyManagement).","quizCorrect":"By default Gradle picks the highest of the requested versions","quizWrong":["Gradle always takes the first version by order in build.gradle","Gradle takes the nearest in the dependency tree (like Maven)","Gradle fails on any version divergence"],"picCap":"Gradle: the highest version wins (Maven — the nearest)"},"bld-build-cache":{"q":"What are the build cache and incrementality in Gradle? Why is \"a clean build every time\" an anti-pattern?","a":"Gradle caches task RESULTS by the hash of their inputs (sources, classpath, flags). If the inputs haven't changed, the task is marked UP-TO-DATE and doesn't run (incrementality). The build cache goes further: it reuses a result even after `clean` or on another machine/CI under the same key. `clean build` every time throws away all those savings.","d":"The local cache lives in `~/.gradle/caches`; the remote (shared) one is shared across the team/CI so that one builder caches a result and the rest download the ready one. For correctness a task must be \"properly declared\": all inputs/outputs declared, otherwise the cache hands back a stale or wrong result.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: the habit of running `clean` \"just in case\" kills incrementality and the build cache. `clean` is rarely needed — when changing Gradle/JDK or suspecting corrupted state, not as a ritual before every build.","hook":"The cache is the memory of \"I already computed this\": same question → same ready answer from the drawer","more":"Gradle saves time at several levels.\n- **Incremental (UP-TO-DATE):** a task is skipped if its inputs and outputs haven't changed since the last run in this project.\n- **Build cache (FROM-CACHE):** the result is taken from the cache by a hash key, survives `clean`, and works across machines via a remote cache.\n- **Configuration cache:** caches the configuration phase itself (the task graph), speeding up startup.\n**Correctness condition:** a task must fully declare its inputs (`@InputFiles`) and outputs (`@OutputDirectory`). An undeclared input = a \"dirty\" cache and bugs like \"it built the old thing for me\".","quizCorrect":"Gradle reuses a task's result if the hash of its inputs matches one already computed","quizWrong":["The cache stores ready artifacts by last-build time, not by input hash","Incrementality works only within one session and resets on exit","The build cache caches downloaded dependencies, not compilation results of the modules"],"picCap":"Input hash → cache hit = work skipped"},"bld-gradle-vs-maven":{"q":"Gradle vs Maven — when to choose which, and what's the fundamental difference in model?","a":"Maven is declarative XML with a rigid, fixed lifecycle (phases compile→test→package), predictable and uniform. Gradle is a programmable task graph (Groovy/Kotlin DSL) with incrementality and a build cache, faster on large multi-module and custom builds. Take Maven for simplicity and conventions; Gradle for speed, flexibility, and complex pipelines.","d":"The key difference is version resolution: Maven — nearest-wins (nearest in the tree), Gradle — highest-wins (highest) plus rich versions and constraints. Maven is clearer to a junior and its XML is trivially read by tooling; Gradle is more powerful, but the script easily turns into unreadable code.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: \"Gradle is faster\" holds only with caching/incremental enabled and tasks declared correctly. A badly written build.gradle with side effects in the configuration phase can be slower than Maven.","hook":"Maven is a train on rails (phases are fixed), Gradle is a car with a navigator (drive as you like, but you can get lost)","more":"The choice is a tradeoff between convention and flexibility.\n- **Maven:** a rigid lifecycle, everything via plugins and XML, minimal magic, any developer reads a pom the same way. Downside — verbosity and weak incrementality.\n- **Gradle:** a task graph with dependencies, build cache, configuration cache, Kotlin DSL with autocompletion. Downside — the script-is-code, easy to get an unreadable build and harder to onboard.\n**In practice:** for a typical service with a team that has juniors, Maven often wins on maintainability. For monorepos/Android/custom pipelines, Gradle is justified by speed.","quizCorrect":"Maven uses a fixed lifecycle of phases, Gradle a programmable task graph with incrementality","quizWrong":["Gradle is slower than Maven on large projects due to lack of a cache","Maven and Gradle use the same version resolution strategy (highest-wins) for the classpath they assemble","Gradle doesn't support multi-module projects, Maven does"],"picCap":"Maven = conventions and predictability; Gradle = task graph and speed"},"bld-dependency-tree":{"q":"`NoSuchMethodError` at runtime. How do you find who pulled in which version transitively?","a":"Build the dependency tree: `mvn dependency:tree` or `./gradlew :module:dependencies`. Find where the conflicting lib comes from and which version won (Maven marks `omitted for conflict`, Gradle shows an arrow `2.15 -> 2.17`). Pinpoint it with `mvn dependency:tree -Dincludes=group:artifact` or `./gradlew dependencyInsight --dependency artifact`.","d":"`NoSuchMethodError`/`ClassNotFoundException` almost always means: one version is on the classpath while the code was compiled against another (a method appeared/disappeared between versions). Fixed by pinning the version (constraints/dependencyManagement) or excluding the transitive dep via `exclude`.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: check the RUNTIME classpath specifically, not compile. Often `dependency:tree` without a scope is misleading — add `-Dscope=runtime`, otherwise you may fix the wrong version.","hook":"dependency:tree is an X-ray of the classpath: you see all the bones and where the version fracture is","more":"Diagnosing conflicts is reading the graph, not guessing.\n- **`mvn dependency:tree`** — the full tree; `-Dverbose` shows even the discarded branches with the reason (`omitted for conflict with X`).\n- **`gradle dependencies`** — the tree by configuration; `dependencyInsight --dependency X` explains why a specific version was chosen.\n- **Typical cause of runtime errors:** code built against API version N, while the classpath has version M where the signature changed.\n**Fix:** pin the version (Maven `dependencyManagement`, Gradle `constraints`), or `exclude` the unwanted transitive dep from the culprit.","quizCorrect":"`mvn dependency:tree` / `gradle dependencies` show transitive dependencies and which version was chosen","quizWrong":["`NoSuchMethodError` always means a typo in a method name in your code","The dependency tree shows only the direct dependencies from the pom/build file","A version conflict is impossible if all dependencies are declared explicitly in pom.xml of the project of the build"],"picCap":"The tree shows who pulls the lib and which version won"},"bld-semver-classpath":{"q":"What is semver, which versions break compatibility, and why does \"works on my machine\" come from the classpath?","a":"Semver = MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH: MAJOR breaks backward compatibility (breaking), MINOR adds features without breakage, PATCH is bug fixes. \"Works on my machine\" arises when two machines end up with different effective transitive-dep versions on the classpath (different lock, cache, resolution order) — same code, different bytecode dependency.","d":"Protection against version drift is lock files: Maven via explicit `dependencyManagement`/`maven-enforcer`, Gradle via `dependency locking` (`gradle.lockfile`). They pin the EXACT versions of the whole graph so CI, prod, and local build an identical classpath instead of \"whatever resolved today\".","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: semver is a promise, not a guarantee. Libraries break it (breaking on a MINOR). So versions alone aren't enough — you need lock files and tests that catch the real classpath, not faith in the version number.","hook":"MAJOR — moving to another house (the keys don't fit), MINOR — new furniture, PATCH — fixed the faucet","more":"Semver is a contract between the lib author and you.\n- **MAJOR** (1.x → 2.x): backward incompatibility, removed/changed signatures. Upgrading — read the changelog.\n- **MINOR** (1.4 → 1.5): new functionality, the old still works.\n- **PATCH** (1.4.1 → 1.4.2): fixes without API changes.\n**Why \"works on my machine\":** without pinned versions, highest-wins/nearest-wins may resolve different transitive deps on different machines (different cache, different set of direct dependencies). The fix — **dependency locking** (Gradle) or hard `dependencyManagement` + enforcer (Maven): the same classpath everywhere = a reproducible build.","quizCorrect":"A MAJOR version bump allows breaking changes; lock files pin the exact versions of the graph","quizWrong":["Breaking changes are allowed on a PATCH bump; MAJOR only changes documentation and examples in the release","Lock files speed up the build but don't affect version reproducibility","In semver a MINOR version is required to break backward compatibility"],"picCap":"MAJOR breaks; without a lock file the classpath drifts between machines"},"rx-streams-api":{"q":"What does Reactive Streams consist of? Name the 4 interfaces and their contract.","a":"A standard of 4 interfaces: Publisher (the source), Subscriber (the consumer), Subscription (the link between them, carrying request/cancel) and Processor (Subscriber and Publisher at once). The contract is strict: a Subscriber sees onSubscribe → 0..N onNext → exactly one onComplete OR onError, and nothing after that. Until the Subscriber calls subscription.request(n), the Publisher is not allowed to emit onNext — that is exactly what backpressure is.","d":"In Java the standard lives as java.util.concurrent.Flow (Java 9+) — the same 4 interfaces, byte-for-byte compatible with Reactive Streams. Reactor (Mono/Flux) and RxJava are implementations on top of this contract.","hook":"Publisher is the waiter, Subscriber is the guest. The guest says \"bring me 3 dishes\" (request 3); the waiter doesn't dump the whole menu on the table at once.","more":"**4 interfaces:** Publisher, Subscriber, Subscription, Processor.\n- **Publisher** — `subscribe(Subscriber)`, kicks off the stream.\n- **Subscriber** — 4 callbacks: `onSubscribe`, `onNext`, `onError`, `onComplete`.\n- **Subscription** — the bridge: `request(n)` and `cancel()`. This is where demand is implemented.\n- **Processor** — a stage in the middle (transformation); it is both a Subscriber and a Publisher.\n\n**Termination:** `onComplete` or `onError` exactly once, and mutually exclusive. After either — silence. Violating this contract is a bug in the implementation, not in your code.","quizCorrect":"The Publisher emits no onNext until the Subscriber has called request(n)","quizWrong":["The Publisher emits elements right after subscribe; request(n) only speeds it up","The Subscriber must call onComplete itself once it's done processing","Processor is just another name for Subscription"],"picCap":"The Subscriber drives the pace through the Subscription","note":"A contract subtlety people flunk: onSubscribe/onNext/onComplete/onError of a single Subscriber must be invoked serially (happens-before, as if from one thread) — a Publisher may NOT emit onNext concurrently from two threads even if the total request(n) allows it. That's exactly why you almost never hand-write a Publisher: in Reactor/RxJava operators like `serialize()` and the factories themselves (`Flux.create`) handle serialization and correct delivery."},"rx-backpressure":{"q":"What is backpressure and why do you need it? What happens without it?","a":"Backpressure is the mechanism a slow consumer uses to tell a fast source \"slow down, I can't keep up\". Without it a fast Publisher overruns the slow Subscriber's buffer/memory → OutOfMemory, or a growing queue and rising latency. In Reactive Streams it's built in: the source never sends more than the Subscriber asked for via request(n).","d":"Backpressure only helps when the source can actually be slowed down (a pull source: DB, file). If the source is push-only (mouse clicks, live exchange quotes) you can't slow it, so you apply strategies instead: buffer/drop/latest/error.","note":"⚠️ Backpressure ≠ \"nothing will ever blow up magically\". If the source is push-only and you chose buffer with no limit, you get the same OOM — just later. Always bound the buffer.","hook":"Backpressure is the valve on a fire hose. Leave it open and you drown. request(n) is the hand on that valve.","more":"**Why:** in an async pipeline, stages run at different speeds. If you don't reconcile the pace, the slowest stage piles up data in memory.\n\n**Where it's built in:** `request(n)` on the Subscription. The Subscriber itself decides how much it \"can chew\". Reactor/RxJava typically request in batches (e.g. 256) and replenish as they process.\n\n**When it doesn't save you:** a source you physically can't slow down (external events). Then you either buffer or drop — see overflow strategies.","quizCorrect":"A signal from consumer to source: \"don't send me more than I requested\"","quizWrong":["Compressing the data in the stream to save memory on intermediate buffers","Resending elements after a network error","Prioritizing streams by message importance"],"picCap":"Without demand, the queue between fast and slow overflows"},"rx-overflow-strategies":{"q":"The source can't be slowed (push-only). Which overflow strategies exist and when do you use each?","a":"Four basic ones: BUFFER (queue them up — you risk memory), DROP (discard new elements when there's no demand), LATEST (keep only the most recent, overwrite the rest), ERROR (fail immediately with MissingBackpressureException). The choice depends on what matters most: completeness, freshness, or protecting memory.","hook":"A checkout line with no room: BUFFER — line everyone up in the corridor, DROP — let no new ones in, LATEST — admit only the last one, ERROR — close the store.","more":"**BUFFER** — you need the full history (logs, transactions). Dangerous without a limit: `onBackpressureBuffer(maxSize)` is mandatory.\n- **DROP** — loss is acceptable, processing something is what counts (telemetry).\n- **LATEST** — only the current value matters (stock price, cursor position). Ideal for UIs and dashboards.\n- **ERROR** — fail-fast: better to crash and learn about the overload than to silently lose data.\n\nIn Reactor the strategy is set in `Flux.create(..., OverflowStrategy)` or via the `onBackpressure*` operators.","quizCorrect":"LATEST keeps only the freshest element, overwriting previous unread ones","quizWrong":["LATEST keeps the last N elements in a ring buffer and discards the oldest","DROP discards old elements, keeping the new ones","ERROR pauses the stream until the buffer frees up"],"picCap":"What to do with elements when there's no demand","note":"Pitfall: `onBackpressure*` operators control only the boundary BETWEEN specific stages, not the source — if you have a hot source without backpressure (timer, network push), real protection comes from the strategy at the emission itself (`Flux.create(sink, OverflowStrategy)`), otherwise MissingBackpressureException fires further up the chain. And DROP/LATEST drop silently — without `doOnDiscard`/metrics you won't see data being lost and will hunt for 'missing' events in the consumer's logs."},"rx-push-pull":{"q":"How does reactive differ from plain pull (Iterator) and from pure push? What does request(n) give you?","a":"Iterator is pure pull: the consumer pulls next() itself, the source is passive — but it's all synchronous and blocking. Pure push: the source emits on its own, the consumer can't control the pace (risk of overload). Reactive Streams is a hybrid, \"push by permission\": data arrives asynchronously (push), but only up to request(n) (pull-driven demand).","d":"That's why the model is called dynamic push-pull. When the Subscriber is fast and requests a lot, it behaves as efficiently as push. When it's slow and requests a little at a time, it degrades into safe pull. One contract covers both cases.","hook":"Pull — you walk to the well with a bucket yourself. Push — they pour on you whether you asked or not. Reactive — you say \"pour me 3 liters\" and you get exactly 3.","more":"**Pull (Iterator/Stream):** the consumer drives each step. Simple, but synchronous and blocks the thread on a slow source (e.g. a network call).\n\n**Push (observer without backpressure):** the source dictates the pace. Asynchronous, but the consumer can be overwhelmed.\n\n**Reactive (Streams):** `request(n)` is the \"pull demand signal\", while onNext delivery is the \"push\". The source never exceeds the total requested. This is what solves the classic producer-consumer problem without blocking threads.","quizCorrect":"Reactive is push, but bounded by demand via request(n); it's a dynamic push-pull","quizWrong":["Reactive is just an async version of Iterator, always pull and never push in any case","Reactive is pure push with no feedback from the consumer","request(n) blocks the thread until n elements arrive"],"picCap":"Reactive = push within the requested demand","note":"request(n) is additive (two request(1) calls = demand of 2), not overwriting; per Reactive Streams spec §3.17 the cumulative demand must not overflow Long.MAX_VALUE, and request(Long.MAX_VALUE) is treated as 'unbounded' — effectively disabling backpressure and turning the stream into pure push. Many operators (e.g. flatMap with default concurrency 256, or onBackpressureBuffer) silently buffer, so the OutOfMemoryError surfaces not at the slow consumer but in an unbounded buffer upstream."},"rx-hot-cold":{"q":"Hot vs cold publisher — what's the difference? Give examples of each.","a":"A cold publisher starts its work fresh for each subscriber and replays the whole stream from the beginning (an HTTP request, reading a file, a DB query). A hot publisher emits independently of subscribers, and a new subscriber only sees what's flowing right now, missing the past (exchange quotes, mouse clicks, a live Kafka topic). Cold = \"a private session for everyone\", hot = \"a shared broadcast\".","d":"In Reactor a cold Flux can be made hot via .share()/.publish().refCount() or ConnectableFlux. Sinks.many().multicast() is a typical hot source.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: subscribe twice to a cold WebClient Flux and you fire two HTTP requests, even though you thought it was one. If you need a single call shared across several subscribers — `.cache()` or `.share()`.","hook":"Cold is Netflix: hit play and the movie starts from the beginning, just for you. Hot is a live broadcast: join late and you missed the start.","more":"**Cold** (lazy, unicast by default): the source \"comes alive\" on each subscribe and replays the full sequence. Examples: a `WebClient` request, `Flux.range`, reading a file. Idempotent per subscriber.\n\n**Hot** (lives on its own, multicast): emission is independent of subscription. Examples: market quotes, UI events, `Sinks.many().multicast()`, an event bus. A new subscriber joins \"mid-movie\".\n\n**Conversion:** cold → hot via `share()`, `publish().refCount()`, `ConnectableFlux`. The other way — by caching (`cache()`/`replay()`), so late subscribers get the history.","quizCorrect":"Cold starts fresh for each subscriber; hot emits independently, and a late subscriber misses the past","quizWrong":["Hot caches all elements and hands them to any new subscriber from the very beginning, losing nothing that was sent","Cold works with one subscriber only, hot with several","The difference is that hot is faster than cold in latency"],"picCap":"Cold replays from scratch, hot broadcasts \"live\""},"rx-vs-imperative-loom":{"q":"Reactive vs imperative and vs virtual threads (Loom) — what do you really gain and when do you pick which?","a":"Reactive (vs blocking imperative) gives you high concurrency on few threads without blocking, plus built-in backpressure and a rich set of async composition operators. The price is convoluted, non-linear code and painful debugging. Virtual threads (Java 21) give you the same cheap concurrency while keeping the code plain and synchronous — but with no backpressure and no operator pipeline out of the box.","d":"The 2024+ reality: for most IO-bound services on Java 21, virtual threads are simpler and good enough. Reactive earns its keep when you genuinely need backpressure, streaming event processing, complex async composition (merge/zip/retry/window), or you already have a reactive stack (WebFlux/Reactor).","note":"⚠️ The main reactive trap is a hidden blocking call (JDBC, `Thread.sleep`, a sync client) on an event-loop thread: throughput collapses. Virtual threads don't have this problem — blocking is fine there.","hook":"Loom is an automatic transmission: you just drive. Reactive is a sports car with a manual: faster in the data-flow race, but you shift gears yourself and can easily stall.","more":"**What reactive gives over imperative:**\n- non-blocking IO → thousands of connections on a handful of threads;\n- **backpressure** out of the box;\n- declarative async composition: `flatMap`, `zip`, `merge`, `retry`, `window`, `timeout`.\nThe price: callback-/operator-style code, hard debugging (fragmented stack traces), easy to slip a blocking call into a non-blocking pool.\n\n**What virtual threads change (Project Loom, Java 21 GA):** concurrency is cheap again with ordinary synchronous code — blocking a VT doesn't pin an OS thread. They remove the main reason to go reactive \"for scale\".\n\n**What VTs lack:** backpressure and a streaming operator pipeline. You'd have to build those by hand.\n\n**The choice:** an ordinary IO-bound service/CRUD on Java 21 → virtual threads (easier to maintain). Need streaming event processing, pace reconciliation, complex async logic, or already on WebFlux → reactive.","quizCorrect":"VTs give cheap concurrency in synchronous code, but no backpressure and no async operators","quizWrong":["Virtual threads fully replace reactive, backpressure included","Reactive is always faster than VTs in throughput under any load and on any hardware you can measure","VTs are OS threads, you just get to create lots of them"],"picCap":"Loom solved \"cheap concurrency\"; reactive adds streaming and pressure on top"},"rx-mono-flux-uni-multi":{"q":"What's the difference between Mono and Flux in Reactor? And are Uni and Multi in Mutiny the same idea?","a":"Mono is a stream of 0 or 1 element (like an Optional, but async and lazy); Flux is 0..N elements (like an async Stream). In Mutiny it's exactly the same dichotomy: Uni is 0..1, Multi is 0..N. Mono ⇄ Uni, Flux ⇄ Multi.","d":"Mono is used for single-answer requests (find by id, save, count), Flux for streams (a list, server-sent events, paginated output). Mutiny's Uni is semantically closer to CompletionStage: one result or an error.","hook":"Mono = Optional over the wire, Flux = Stream over the wire. Mutiny just renamed them: Uni and Multi.","more":"**The key is cardinality:**\n- **Mono/Uni** — a promise of at most one value. Handy as a return type wherever you used to have `Optional<T>` or `CompletableFuture<T>`.\n- **Flux/Multi** — a stream of arbitrary length, including infinite (intervals, events).\n\n**Conversion is cheap:** `flux.next()` → Mono (the first element), `mono.flux()` → Flux. In Mutiny: `multi.toUni()`, `uni.toMulti()`.\n\n**Why two types and not one:** the type in the signature immediately tells the reader whether to expect one value or many — it's part of the API contract, not an implementation detail.","quizCorrect":"Mono is 0..1 element, Flux is 0..N; in Mutiny they map to Uni and Multi","quizWrong":["Mono is always exactly 1 element, Flux is exactly 2 or more","Mono is synchronous, Flux is asynchronous; Mutiny has no such split","Uni is 0..N, Multi is 0..1 (in Mutiny the order is reversed vs Reactor) itself"],"picCap":"Mono/Uni — at most one element; Flux/Multi — a stream of many","note":"Mono<Void> and an empty Mono differ in practice: then()/thenEmpty() discard values, but a Mono that never emits onComplete (e.g. a forgotten sink.success()) silently hangs the whole chain with no error and no timeout. Another trap: a Mono may legitimately complete with no onNext at all (like an empty Optional), so code like mono.map(...) on an empty Mono simply won't run — you need defaultIfEmpty/switchIfEmpty rather than assuming a value will arrive."},"rx-map-flatmap-concatmap":{"q":"map vs flatMap vs concatMap — when do you use each? Why is flatMap risky for ordering?","a":"map is a synchronous 1→1 value transform (T→R). flatMap creates an inner publisher per element and subscribes to many at once: concurrent, order NOT guaranteed. concatMap does the same but strictly one at a time, sequentially: order preserved, but no parallelism.","d":"If the inner operation is async (a service call, a DB query), you need flat/concat, because the result is itself wrapped in a Mono/Flux and map would leave you with Flux<Mono<R>>. Between them: order matters and you want to cap load → concatMap; speed matters and order doesn't → flatMap.","note":"⚠️ Classic mistake: using `map` for an async call that returns `Mono<R>`. You get `Flux<Mono<R>>` — a nested publisher nobody subscribes to. You need `flatMap`/`concatMap`.","hook":"map — repack inside the box; flatMap — open N parcels at once, whoever's unpacked first ships first; concatMap — a queue, one at a time.","more":"**The signature drives the choice:**\n- **map** `T -> R` — the function returns a plain value. A pure synchronous transform.\n- **flatMap** `T -> Publisher<R>` — the function returns another stream. Reactor subscribes to several inner streams at once (default concurrency=256) and merges results as they're ready → **order not guaranteed**.\n- **concatMap** `T -> Publisher<R>` — same, but waits for the previous inner stream to complete before subscribing to the next → **order preserved, no concurrency**.\n\n**In Mutiny** the equivalent: `multi.onItem().transformToUniAndMerge()` (like flatMap) vs `...transformToUniAndConcatenate()` (like concatMap).\n\n**There's also flatMapSequential** — subscribes concurrently (fast) but reorders the results back on output. A compromise between flatMap's speed and concatMap's ordering.","quizCorrect":"flatMap subscribes to inner publishers concurrently and doesn't guarantee order; concatMap is sequential and preserves order","quizWrong":["map and flatMap are identical, only the name differs","concatMap runs concurrently while flatMap runs strictly one at a time, which is why order is preserved only by the former of the two","flatMap preserves order while map loses it"],"picCap":"flatMap drives inner subscriptions in parallel (order is lost), concatMap goes strictly in turn"},"rx-zip-merge-combine":{"q":"zip, merge and combineLatest — how do they differ when combining streams?","a":"merge folds several streams into one as elements arrive, no pairing, cross-stream order not guaranteed. zip waits for one element from EACH source and assembles a tuple (paired, by index). combineLatest emits, on every new element from any source, a combination of the latest values from all of them.","d":"zip is handy for \"wait for both A and B, then assemble the answer\" (like parallel calls to two services). merge is for fusing homogeneous events from different sources. combineLatest is for reactive forms/state where the \"last known\" value of each input matters.","hook":"zip — a zipper: left tooth + right tooth = one link. merge — dump two bags into one basket. combineLatest — a board showing the latest rate of each currency.","more":"**Three different combining semantics:**\n- **merge** — N streams → 1, elements pass through as they arrive (interleaving). Subscribes to all sources at once. If you need strict source ordering — use `concat` instead of merge.\n- **zip** — synchronizes by index: waits for the i-th element from each source, emits a tuple. Result length = length of the shortest source. Ideal for \"glue together the results of parallel calls\".\n- **combineLatest** — reactive \"always fresh\": whenever any input changes, it recomputes the result from the latest values of all inputs. The first emit happens only once every source has at least one element.\n\n**In Mutiny:** `Uni.combine().all().unis(u1, u2).asTuple()` (zip-equivalent for Uni), `Multi.createBy().merging()`, `Multi.createBy().combining().latest()`.","quizCorrect":"zip waits for one element from each source and assembles a tuple; merge folds elements in as they arrive, no pairing","quizWrong":["merge assembles tuples by index, while zip just interleaves elements","zip and merge are identical, both assemble pairs","combineLatest emits only the first element of each source, not the latest that each of the sources has produced so far"],"picCap":"zip assembles tuples in pairs and waits for both; merge just interleaves elements as they arrive","note":"zip with sources of different length silently TRUNCATES the result to the shortest one and cancels the rest — extra elements of the longer stream are lost silently, which is especially dangerous if that longer stream has side effects (writes, acks). combineLatest, in turn, can 'swallow' the very first element of a fast source while the slow one hasn't emitted yet, and produces stale combinations when input rates differ — for racing to a final value prefer zip/Uni.combine over combineLatest."},"rx-error-handling":{"q":"onErrorReturn vs onErrorResume vs retry vs retryWhen — when do you use which?","a":"onErrorReturn — substitute the error with a static fallback value. onErrorResume — switch to another publisher (e.g. a backup source). retry(n) — resubscribe to the source from scratch, up to n times, immediately. retryWhen — flexible retry with control over delays/backoff/condition via a companion stream.","d":"Important: in reactive an error is a terminal signal that kills the stream. A try/catch around the chain won't catch an async error — those are handled only by operators. And retry RESUBSCRIBES (replays everything from the start of the source) rather than continuing from where it failed.","note":"⚠️ retry resubscribes to the ENTIRE source — if the source has side effects (a POST request, an increment), they repeat. For non-idempotent operations, retry is dangerous.","hook":"retry — \"try again from the start\". onErrorResume — \"that failed, take the backup route\". onErrorReturn — \"no way at all, here's a stub\".","more":"**An error is a terminal signal** that kills the stream. Handled only by operators, not try/catch.\n\n- **onErrorReturn(value)** — replace the error with a fixed value. Can be predicated on the exception type.\n- **onErrorResume(fn)** — a function of the error returns a **new publisher** (a backup service, a cache, an empty stream). The most flexible \"catch\".\n- **onErrorMap(fn)** — repackage the exception into a domain one (e.g. your own `ServiceException`).\n- **retry(n)** — on error, resubscribe to the source up to n times immediately.\n- **retryWhen(Retry.backoff(...))** — advanced retry: exponential backoff, jitter, filter by error type, a limit. Under the hood — a companion Flux of errors.\n\n**Mutiny:** `onFailure().recoverWithItem(...)`, `onFailure().recoverWithUni(...)`, `onFailure().retry().atMost(n)` / `.withBackOff(...)`.","quizCorrect":"onErrorReturn yields a static value, onErrorResume switches to another publisher, retryWhen controls retries with backoff","quizWrong":["onErrorResume returns one static value, while onErrorReturn switches to another stream of elements instead to the subscriber","retry continues the stream from the failure point without resubscribing","an async error in the chain can be caught with a plain try/catch around subscribe"],"picCap":"First retryWhen retries; if that fails — onErrorResume to a backup stream; the last line of defense is onErrorReturn"},"rx-cold-nothing-without-subscribe":{"q":"\"Nothing happens until you subscribe\" — what does that mean and why cold?","a":"A Publisher (Mono/Flux/Uni/Multi) is a recipe, not a result. Until someone calls subscribe(), the operator chain does NOT run: not map, not the DB query, not the HTTP call. That's why such streams are called cold — each subscriber starts the execution anew, from scratch.","d":"This is laziness. A cold stream \"replays\" its data afresh for each subscriber (a new HTTP request per subscribe). A hot stream (e.g. via .share()/Sink) broadcasts a single source to everyone and doesn't restart. Forgetting to subscribe = silently doing nothing — a common mistake.","note":"⚠️ The most common beginner mistake: call `repo.save(x)` and not subscribe — the data isn't saved, no error, just silence. Any Mono/Uni from IO must either be returned up the stack or subscribed.","hook":"A Publisher is a recipe in a book, not a cooked dish. subscribe() = turn on the stove. No subscribe — you sit hungry over the book.","more":"**Lazy / cold by default:**\n- Creating a `Flux`/`Mono` merely **describes** the pipeline. The operators build the chain but don't execute it.\n- **subscribe()** initiates the subscription: an onSubscribe signal travels up to the source, then data flows down. Only here do the real effects happen (requests, computations).\n- **Cold** — every new subscriber gets its own independent execution (a new HTTP request, a new query). **Hot** (via `share()`, `publish().refCount()`, `Sinks`) — one source broadcasts to all, latecomers miss past elements.\n\n**On the web** subscribe is usually called by the framework: return a `Mono`/`Uni` from a controller and Spring WebFlux / Quarkus subscribes for you. Subscribing by hand inside a service is almost always a smell (you lose context, errors, cancellation).\n\n**Mutiny** is lazy the same way: `Uni`/`Multi` do nothing until subscribed.","quizCorrect":"Without subscribe() the chain doesn't run — it's a lazy cold stream, and each subscriber starts execution anew","quizWrong":["The operator chain runs immediately on Flux creation; subscribe just reads the result that was already computed","A cold stream runs once and caches the result for all subscribers","map and flatMap run at chain-assembly time, and subscribe is only needed for onError"],"picCap":"Without subscribe the chain is a dormant recipe; subscribe() kicks off all emission"},"rx-schedulers-blocking":{"q":"subscribeOn vs publishOn — which thread does the code run on? And why does a blocking call in the chain take down the service?","a":"By default the whole chain runs on the thread that called subscribe(). subscribeOn sets the thread of the SOURCE (it affects upstream); publishOn switches the thread for the operators BELOW it. This is critical because of blocking: the event loop holds ≈ core-count threads, and a blocking call (JDBC, Thread.sleep, .block()) on it freezes the entire service. Move blocking work onto boundedElastic.","d":"subscribeOn — one per chain, usually close to the source (multiple = a bug, the first wins). publishOn can be placed many times — each is a new thread boundary. Wrap blocking code in Mono.fromCallable(...).subscribeOn(boundedElastic()). BlockHound in tests catches blocking on a non-blocking scheduler; in Quarkus there's @Blocking.","note":"⚠️ `parallel()` is NOT for blocking IO: its pool is core-count sized, you'll saturate it instantly and tank throughput just the same. For blocking, use only `boundedElastic` (Reactor) or a worker pool (Quarkus). And several `subscribeOn` in a row is usually a bug.","hook":"subscribeOn — where the factory is (the source). publishOn — where goods are transferred to another truck. The event loop is one waiter for the whole hall: make him brew coffee (blocking) and the hall starves; hand it to the kitchen (boundedElastic).","more":"**Threading model:** by default the whole chain runs on the thread that called `subscribe()`.\n- **subscribeOn(scheduler)** — the thread for subscription/start of source emission; effect is upstream. Multiple subscribeOn are pointless — the one closest to the source wins.\n- **publishOn(scheduler)** — changes the thread for operators below; can be placed many times, each a new boundary.\n- **Schedulers:** `parallel()` — CPU (= cores), `boundedElastic()` — blocking/IO (a bounded pool, the replacement for the deprecated `elastic()`), `single()`, `immediate()`.\n\n**Why blocking hurts:** a non-blocking runtime (Netty/Vert.x) holds a tiny pool of event-loop threads (≈ cores). A blocking call (JDBC, classic JPA, `RestTemplate`, `Thread.sleep`, `.block()`) \"pins\" the thread → throughput drops several-fold.\n\n**The right way:** wrap in `Mono.fromCallable(...).subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic())`; in **Quarkus** — `@Blocking` (worker pool) or reactive drivers (Hibernate Reactive). **BlockHound** in tests throws on blocking from a non-blocking thread.\n\n**Mutiny:** `runSubscriptionOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool())` or `@Blocking`.","quizCorrect":"subscribeOn sets the source thread (upstream), publishOn the operators below it; blocking on the event loop freezes the service, so isolate via subscribeOn(boundedElastic)","quizWrong":["publishOn affects the source, subscribeOn the operators below; blocking in map is safe as long as the pipeline itself was created on a bounded elastic scheduler beforehand by the framework","The problem is only .block(); Thread.sleep and JDBC in the chain are harmless","Isolate blocking via publishOn(Schedulers.parallel()) — that's the IO pool"],"picCap":"Blocking on the event loop freezes other requests; on boundedElastic it's isolated"},"crypto-aes-modes":{"q":"Symmetric encryption: what is AES, why do you need a mode (GCM/CBC), and where do IV/nonce fit in?","a":"AES is a block cipher: the same key both encrypts and decrypts. On its own it can only encrypt exactly one 16-byte block, so you need a mode to chain blocks into a stream. The IV/nonce is a fresh starting value for each message, so that the same plaintext under the same key produces different ciphertext every time.","d":"↳ GCM (AEAD) = encryption plus a built-in integrity tag: the receiver immediately knows if the ciphertext was tampered with. CBC gives you confidentiality only — integrity has to be bolted on with a separate MAC. That's why the default today is AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: reusing a nonce in AES-GCM under the same key is a catastrophe, not just 'sloppy': an attacker can recover the authentication key and forge tags. Under high throughput a 96-bit counter overflows easily — for random nonces the safe limit is roughly 2^32 messages per key.","hook":"AES is a safe with a single key: you lock it and unlock it with the same key. The mode is the rule for chaining many little safes together, and the nonce is a random tag so that two identical parcels look different on the wire.","more":"- **Symmetric** = one key for both operations. Fast (hardware AES-NI), but you still have to deliver that key to both sides securely — which is what asymmetric crypto / key exchange solves.\n- **The mode** turns a block cipher into a stream. ECB is forbidden: identical blocks → identical ciphertext, leaking the structure of the data (the classic 'penguin image').\n- **IV vs nonce**: CBC needs an unpredictable random IV; GCM needs a unique nonce (never repeat it under the same key — a repeat breaks GCM's security entirely).\n- **AEAD** (GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305) is the gold standard: confidentiality + integrity + authenticity in one call, plus AAD for metadata that stays in the clear but is still authenticated.","quizCorrect":"AES-GCM encrypts and produces an integrity tag in one step; the nonce must be unique per message under a given key","quizWrong":["The nonce must be kept secret, just like the key","AES in CBC mode verifies on its own that the ciphertext wasn't tampered with and rejects it if it was on decryption","The same nonce can safely be reused — it just speeds things up"],"picCap":"Key + a fresh nonce per message → ciphertext + integrity tag"},"crypto-hash-properties":{"q":"What is a cryptographic hash function, what are its properties, and what does 'collision' mean?","a":"A hash function takes any amount of data and produces a fixed-length string (e.g. SHA-256 → 32 bytes). It's a one-way operation: you can't recover the input from the hash. A collision is when two different inputs map to the same hash; for a strong function, finding one should be practically infeasible.","d":"↳ Three properties: preimage resistance (can't find an input from a hash), second-preimage resistance (given an input, can't find a second one with the same hash), and collision resistance (can't find any colliding pair at all). Avalanche effect: flipping one input bit changes about half the bits of the hash.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: the 'birthday paradox' halves collision resistance in bits — SHA-256 gives about 128 bits of collision resistance, not 256. For signatures that's still plenty of headroom, but keep it in mind when choosing a hash length.","hook":"A hash is a meat grinder: turning a steak into mince is instant, but reconstructing the steak from the mince — never. And if you change even a single fiber in the steak, the mince comes out looking completely different.","more":"- **Fixed length**: 1 byte and 1 GB both produce a hash of the same size. That means collisions always exist mathematically (the input space is infinite, the output finite) — security lies in the fact that you can't *find* them.\n- **SHA-2** (SHA-256/384/512) is today's workhorse. SHA-3 (Keccak) rests on a different mathematical foundation, kept as a fallback in case SHA-2 is ever attacked.\n- **MD5 and SHA-1 are dead** for security: collisions can be found in reasonable time (SHA-1 was broken in 2017, SHAttered). Off-limits for signatures/certificates. Fine for non-crypto uses (checksums, sharding).\n- **Don't confuse it with encryption**: a hash has no key and no decryption. They're different tools for different jobs.","quizCorrect":"A hash is irreversible, has a fixed length on any input, and finding a collision should be computationally infeasible","quizWrong":["A good hash function should let you recover the original data from the hash","The hash length grows in proportion to the size of the input data","MD5 and SHA-1 are still considered safe today for signatures and certificates, because no practical collision has been demonstrated"],"picCap":"One changed character → a completely different hash (avalanche effect)"},"crypto-password-hashing":{"q":"How to store passwords correctly: why salt, and why bcrypt/argon2/scrypt instead of SHA-256?","a":"A password is never stored in plaintext and never encrypted — it's hashed with a dedicated slow password algorithm. The salt is a random, per-user value mixed in before hashing. SHA-256 is the wrong choice because it's designed to be fast, which means a GPU can brute-force passwords by the billions per second.","d":"↳ The salt kills rainbow tables and makes identical passwords hash differently in the database. bcrypt/scrypt/argon2 have a work factor — a tunable 'cost' you raise as hardware gets faster. argon2 is also memory-hard: it eats RAM, which neutralizes GPU/ASIC farms.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: bcrypt silently truncates the password to 72 bytes — long passphrases and passwords with multi-byte UTF-8 characters can collide. If long passwords matter, use argon2, or pre-hash with SHA-512 before bcrypt (which has its own subtleties).","hook":"SHA is a race car — a million hashes a second — and for passwords that's bad: the attacker runs his brute-force on the very same engine. A password algorithm is a cement mixer: deliberately slow and heavy, so that every batch costs you and the attacker dearly.","more":"- **The salt is unique per user** and isn't secret — it's stored next to the hash (bcrypt/argon2 embed it right into the output string). Its purpose isn't to hide anything, but to defeat precomputed tables and split identical passwords apart.\n- **The work factor** is tunable: bcrypt cost 12+, argon2 with 19–64 MB of memory and several iterations. Bump it every couple of years as hardware improves.\n- **argon2id** is the current OWASP recommendation (memory-hard, GPU-resistant). bcrypt is the time-tested default (but max 72 bytes of input). scrypt sits between them.\n- **Pepper** is optional: an application-wide secret added to all passwords and stored separately from the DB (in a KMS/config), an extra layer if the database leaks.","quizCorrect":"Passwords are hashed with argon2/bcrypt/scrypt and a unique per-user salt; the slowness is a feature against brute force","quizWrong":["Running a password through SHA-256 twice is enough to make it secure against brute force with rainbow tables built in advance","The salt must be kept secret, separately from the password database","The faster the password hashing algorithm, the better for security"],"picCap":"Per-user salt + a deliberately slow algorithm = brute force isn't worth it"},"crypto-hmac":{"q":"What is HMAC, and why can't you just concatenate the key with the data and run SHA-256?","a":"HMAC is a hash-based MAC (message authentication code): it takes the data plus a secret key and produces a tag that proves both integrity (not tampered with) and authenticity (the sender knows the key). The naive hash(key + message) is vulnerable to a length-extension attack on SHA-2: knowing the hash, you can append data and recompute the tag without knowing the key. HMAC's nested double-hashing construction exists specifically to close that hole.","d":"↳ HMAC(K, m) = H((K⊕opad) ‖ H((K⊕ipad) ‖ m)). The outer hash over the inner one kills length-extension. The tag must be verified with a constant-time comparison, or it leaks through timing.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: comparing tags with a plain `Arrays.equals`/`String.equals` leaks timing — an attacker guesses the tag byte by byte from the response delay. Always use constant-time (`MessageDigest.isEqual` in Java). And don't confuse HMAC (needs a shared secret) with a signature (private/public key pair).","hook":"A hash proves the data wasn't corrupted (integrity), but anyone can recompute it. HMAC is a seal on the envelope that only someone holding the secret signet ring can make: you can see it wasn't opened, and that it came from the right person.","more":"- **HMAC ≠ encryption**: it doesn't hide the data, it proves it's authentic. The message stays readable, with the tag alongside it.\n- **Symmetric**: the same secret key on both sender and receiver. If you need public verifiability (anyone can verify, but only one party can sign), that's a digital signature (RSA/ECDSA), not HMAC.\n- **Length-extension**: bare SHA-256(key‖msg) lets an attacker append data to a known hash and forge a tag. HMAC's nested hashing closes this — which is why you use it instead of a homemade concatenation.\n- **Where it lives**: JWT (HS256 = HMAC-SHA256), API webhook signatures (Stripe, GitHub), integrity checks on cookies/tokens.","quizCorrect":"HMAC provides integrity and authenticity via a secret key, and its construction is protected against length-extension attacks","quizWrong":["HMAC encrypts the message, so its contents can't be read without the key","hash(key + message) on SHA-256 is just as secure as HMAC, just shorter to write and no weaker in any practical scenario you can construct","An HMAC tag can be compared with a plain equals — timing doesn't matter"],"picCap":"Only someone who knows the key can create and verify the tag → tampering is visible"},"crypto-at-rest-in-transit":{"q":"Encryption at-rest vs in-transit: what's the difference and why do you need both?","a":"In-transit is encrypting data while it travels over the network (TLS/HTTPS between services, client and server). At-rest is encrypting data sitting on disk (databases, backups, files in S3/MinIO). They guard against different threats: in-transit protects against interception on the wire, at-rest against someone walking off with a disk, a dump, or gaining access to storage.","d":"↳ They're orthogonal and don't substitute for each other. TLS won't help if an attacker reads the DB files directly; disk encryption won't help against a man-in-the-middle on the network. There's also a third, in-use (encryption in memory/enclave), but that's still niche.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: volume-level at-rest encryption only protects against physical theft of the disk — on a running server with access rights the data is decrypted 'transparently'. Disk encryption won't save you from SQL injection or a compromised application; for that you also need app-level encryption of sensitive fields.","hook":"In-transit is the armored cash-in-transit van while the money drives across town. At-rest is the vault in the bank where it sits. A van without a vault, or a vault without a van, is pointless — you get robbed in both places.","more":"- **In-transit**: TLS 1.2/1.3 everywhere — client↔service, service↔service, service↔DB. mTLS for mutual authentication inside the cluster. Without it, tokens and data are readable at any intermediate hop.\n- **At-rest**: volume encryption (LUKS/EBS), TDE at the database level, server-side encryption in S3/MinIO. Keys live in a KMS/Vault, not in code and not in the same database.\n- **The key point**: these are different threat models. One doesn't cover the other. Compliance (PCI DSS, GDPR, Kazakhstan banking requirements) typically demands both.\n- **What's often forgotten**: backups, logs with PII, Kafka queues, temp files, DB replicas — these need at-rest encryption too.","quizCorrect":"In-transit protects data on the network (TLS), at-rest protects it on disk; they cover different threats and you need both","quizWrong":["If TLS is on, disk encryption is no longer needed — the data is already protected on its way and at rest alike on the server","At-rest and in-transit are two names for the same mechanism","Disk encryption protects against interception of traffic between services"],"picCap":"Two independent lines of defense: the wire (TLS) and the disk (encryption at-rest)"},"crypto-encrypt-vs-hash-passwords":{"q":"Common mistake: 'let's encrypt the passwords.' Why are passwords NOT encrypted but hashed?","a":"Encryption is reversible: with the key you get the plaintext back. So if you hold a key to decrypt passwords, then an attacker who grabs the database along with the key (and the key usually sits right there — in config, a KMS, the code) gets every password in plaintext. Hashing is irreversible: at login you hash the entered password and compare it against the stored one — you never need the original, so there's nothing to store.","d":"↳ You never need to 'recover' a user's password — only to check that it matches. So the right tool is a one-way password hash (argon2/bcrypt). Encryption is for data you must READ back later (card number, passport), not for passwords.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: in an interview this is a marker question. Answering 'we'll encrypt passwords with AES' is a red flag for a senior role. The correct phrasing: passwords are *hashed* with a slow salted algorithm; *encryption* is for reversible data you need to read back.","hook":"Encrypting a password means locking it with a key you carry around right next to it. Hashing means burning the original and keeping only the ash-print: you can check a new password against the ash, but no one — including you — can reconstruct the original.","more":"- **The tell-tale sign of the mistake**: if a system can be made to *show* a user their current password, passwords are stored wrong (encrypted or in the clear). Sound systems can only reset a password, never reveal it.\n- **Why the key doesn't save you**: when a server is compromised, the attacker usually gets both the database and the key (they live in the same infrastructure). A hash has no 'master key' — each password has to be cracked individually by brute force.\n- **When encryption is appropriate**: data you must return in its original form — card PANs, passport details, access tokens. A password isn't on that list.\n- **The right stack for passwords**: argon2id/bcrypt + a unique salt + (optionally) a pepper. No bare SHA, no AES.","quizCorrect":"Passwords are hashed because you only need to verify them, not read them; encryption is reversible and the key leaks along with the database","quizWrong":["Passwords are encrypted with AES so the user can be shown their old password if needed","Encryption is more secure than hashing, which is why it's applied to passwords","There's no difference: encryption and hashing protect passwords equally well when the DB leaks, so the choice between them is purely a matter of taste"],"picCap":"Encryption can be undone with a key; a hash is a one-way street"},"crypto-asymmetric":{"q":"Why do you need two different keys if symmetric AES is faster and simpler?","a":"With symmetric crypto the same key encrypts and decrypts — which means both sides must somehow exchange it in advance over a secure channel, and that channel is exactly what you don't have. Asymmetric crypto gives you a pair: the public key you can hand out to everyone, the private key you keep to yourself. What's encrypted with the public key can only be decrypted with the private one, and vice versa. This solves the initial key-exchange problem without a secret channel.","d":"↳ Asymmetric is slow (RSA is hundreds of times slower than AES), so in practice you don't encrypt the data with it — only a small symmetric session key, and then everything runs over AES. This is called hybrid encryption, and it's exactly how TLS works.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: 'encrypt with the private key' is common shorthand for signing, but it's a confusion. An RSA signature isn't encryption with the private key — it's a separate operation (sign/verify). Don't mix up Cipher and Signature in the API: for signing, use the Signature class.","hook":"The public key is an open padlock you've handed out to everyone. Anyone can snap it shut on a box, but only you hold the key (the private one) that opens it.","more":"- **RSA** rests on the hardness of factoring large numbers; a sane key size today is **2048 or 3072 bits**, with 1024 already considered weak.\n- **ECC** (elliptic curves) gives the same strength with a smaller key: a **256-bit ECC ≈ 3072-bit RSA**, so ECC is faster and lighter, and is increasingly preferred on mobile and in TLS.\n- Asymmetric crypto is almost never used to encrypt large data directly — only to transport a symmetric session key (the hybrid scheme).\n- The same key pair underpins digital signatures, just with the roles swapped: you sign with the private key, verify with the public one.","quizCorrect":"You encrypt with the public key; only the holder of the private key can decrypt","quizWrong":["Both keys can encrypt and decrypt symmetrically","You encrypt with the private key and decrypt the data with the public one afterwards","Asymmetric is faster than symmetric, so all traffic is encrypted with it"],"picCap":"The public key encrypts (anyone can snap the padlock shut), the private key decrypts (only the owner can open it)"},"crypto-signature":{"q":"Is a digital signature encryption? And what exactly does it guarantee?","a":"No, it's a separate operation. You compute the hash of the message and transform it with your private key — that's the signature. Anyone holding your public key verifies it: they recompute the message hash and compare it with what's embedded in the signature. A match means it was signed by the holder of the private key (authenticity) and the message wasn't altered (integrity).","d":"↳ You sign the hash, not the message itself, for two reasons: the asymmetric operation is expensive (the hash is short and fixed-size), and this works for data of any size. So the signature's strength also depends on the hash function — MD5/SHA-1 are already broken for signatures, you need at least SHA-256.","hook":"A signature is like a wax seal on a letter. Only the one holding your personal signet ring (the private key) can press the impression, but recognizing your seal by sight (the public key) — anyone can.","more":"- A signature gives you **authenticity** (signed by the key holder), **integrity** (the text wasn't altered), and **non-repudiation** (you can't deny it — only you hold the private key).\n- A signature does NOT give you **confidentiality**: the message stays in the clear, anyone can read it. If you want secrecy too, encrypt separately.\n- The hash is what's signed, so a weak hash function breaks the whole scheme: a hash collision = a forged signature. Use **SHA-256 or higher**.\n- In the ECC world the counterpart is **ECDSA** (or EdDSA/Ed25519): the same sign/verify, but shorter and faster than RSA.","quizCorrect":"You sign with the private key and verify with the public one; it guarantees integrity and authenticity","quizWrong":["You sign with the public key and verify with the private one — that's more secure for the recipient of the message","The signature encrypts the contents, so an outsider can't read the message","The signature guarantees only secrecy, not authorship"],"picCap":"I sign the hash with the private key; the verifier checks the hash with the public key — integrity plus sender authenticity","note":"A common trap is conflating RSA signing with 'encrypting with the private key': real schemes (RSASSA-PSS, even the old PKCS#1 v1.5) include padding and DigestInfo encoding, not a raw modular exponentiation of the hash; a naive 'encrypt(hash, privateKey)' is vulnerable to forgery (Bleichenbacher, e=3 attacks), so always use a vetted library and prefer PSS over hand-rolled RSA."},"crypto-dh-fs":{"q":"How do two parties agree on a shared secret over an open channel where everything is eavesdropped?","a":"Diffie-Hellman: each party generates its own secret, sends the other a public derivative of it, and both independently compute the same shared key. The trick is in the math — recovering the secret from the intercepted public values is computationally infeasible (the discrete logarithm). The secret key itself is never sent over the channel — it's born simultaneously on both sides.","d":"↳ Forward secrecy comes from generating a fresh ephemeral DH pair for each session (ECDHE) and discarding it afterward. Then even if the server's long-term private key is stolen later, previously recorded traffic can't be decrypted, because the ephemeral secrets are already gone. Without ephemerality (the old RSA key exchange), a key leak cracks the entire intercepted archive.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: DH on its own doesn't protect against MITM — it's just key agreement without authentication. Authenticity comes from a signature/certificate layered on top. 'DH = secure' without verifying the peer's identity is a false sense of safety.","hook":"Diffie-Hellman is like mixing paint. Each picks a secret shade, publicly exchanges a mix built on a common base, then stirs in their own secret again — both end up with the same color. An eavesdropper holding both mixes can't separate them back into the original shades.","more":"- Bare DH is vulnerable to **man-in-the-middle**: the channel isn't authenticated, so someone in the middle can swap the public values. That's why in TLS the DH exchange is signed with the server's private key (its certificate).\n- **Forward Secrecy (PFS)** is the key property of ephemeral DH: compromising the server's long-term key doesn't expose past sessions.\n- In practice you use **ECDHE** (Elliptic Curve DH Ephemeral) — faster than classic DH and mandatory in TLS 1.3.\n- In **TLS 1.3** the static RSA key exchange is removed entirely: only ephemeral (EC)DHE, meaning forward secrecy is now the default for everyone.","quizCorrect":"The parties exchange public derivatives, each computes the shared secret itself, and it's never sent over the channel","quizWrong":["One party generates the key and sends it to the other in encrypted form","The shared secret is sent over the channel, but encrypted with the public key of the recipient before it is sent over the wire","Forward secrecy means old traffic can be decrypted with the server's key"],"picCap":"Only g^a and g^b are exchanged; each computes the shared secret g^ab locally, it never travels the channel"},"crypto-pki-chain":{"q":"I received the server's public key. How do I know it's genuine and not planted by an attacker?","a":"A public key on its own proves nothing — anyone could have generated it. Trust is built through PKI: the key is wrapped in a certificate signed by a certificate authority (CA) you already trust. The browser verifies the chain of signatures from the server's certificate to an intermediate CA and on to a root CA that sits in the OS trust store. If the chain is valid and ends at a known root, the key can be trusted.","d":"↳ Root CAs are self-signed and pre-installed in the OS/browser — that's the anchor of trust. Intermediate CAs exist so the root's private key can be kept offline (a bunker, an HSM): the root signs rarely, and the actual certificates are issued by the intermediates. If the root is compromised, everything collapses, so it's guarded as tightly as possible.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: a classic mistake in Java/services is the server returning only the leaf without the intermediate certificate. It works in a browser (which pulls the intermediate from cache), but a strict client like curl/Java fails with 'unable to verify'. Always serve the full chain (fullchain.pem), minus the root.","hook":"A certificate is like a passport. The piece of paper means nothing on its own, but it's trusted because it was issued by a state (the CA) that people trust. And the state is trusted because the international community recognizes it (the root store in your OS).","more":"- **A CA (Certificate Authority)** is a trusted party that signs certificates, attesting that 'this public key belongs to this domain.'\n- **The root CA** is self-signed and sits in the OS/browser trust store ahead of time — it's the **trust anchor**, the end of the chain.\n- **Intermediate CAs** exist so the root's private key can be kept offline in an HSM and never exposed; compromise of an intermediate is contained by revocation rather than being a catastrophe.\n- Revoking compromised certificates goes through **CRL** or **OCSP** (and in modern browsers often OCSP stapling), plus short lifetimes (Let's Encrypt — 90 days).","quizCorrect":"The certificate is signed by a CA; the browser verifies the chain of signatures up to a trusted root CA in the OS store","quizWrong":["A public key on its own proves the server's authenticity","The server sends the root certificate over the network along with the rest","Trust is built because the certificate is encrypted, not because it's signed by an authority the browser already trusts out of the box"],"picCap":"Chain of trust: the leaf is signed by an intermediate, the intermediate by the root, the root is self-signed and pre-installed"},"crypto-tls-handshake":{"q":"Walk through the TLS handshake step by step: what gets encrypted with what, and when?","a":"First ClientHello/ServerHello — the sides negotiate the version and ciphers and exchange randoms and ephemeral ECDHE public keys (all in plaintext). The server sends its certificate and signs its DH contribution with its private key — the client verifies the signature and the CA chain. Then both independently derive the shared secret via ECDHE and from it the symmetric session keys. From that point on, all traffic (application data) is encrypted with fast AES.","d":"↳ The key point: asymmetric crypto (certificate + signature) is used only to authenticate the server and agree on a key, while the actual data is encrypted with symmetric AES-GCM. In TLS 1.3 the handshake is cut to 1-RTT: the client sends its ECDHE contribution already in the ClientHello, and part of the handshake after ServerHello is encrypted too. The RSA key exchange is removed — ephemeral only, for forward secrecy.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: a common misconception is 'TLS encrypts the data with the server's RSA key.' No: the server's RSA/ECDSA key only signs/authenticates. The data is always encrypted with the derived symmetric key. If the data went through RSA, the connection would be wildly slow and have no forward secrecy.","hook":"The handshake is like two people through a glass door inventing a shared cipher for their notes. First they agree on the rules out loud (in the open), check each other's passport (the certificate), then switch to a secret language (AES) they derived together.","more":"- The handshake solves three problems: **authenticating** the server (certificate + signature), **agreeing on a key** (ECDHE), **switching to symmetric** (AES-GCM/ChaCha20).\n- In **TLS 1.2** it was 2-RTT, in **TLS 1.3** it's 1-RTT (plus 0-RTT for resumed connections, but 0-RTT is vulnerable to replay, so be careful).\n- **CertificateVerify** — the server signs a hash of the handshake with its private key, proving it actually owns the key in the certificate rather than just relaying someone else's.\n- The symmetric keys are derived from the shared secret via **HKDF**, with separate keys for each direction (client→server, server→client).","quizCorrect":"Asymmetric crypto authenticates the server and agrees on a key, then the data is encrypted with symmetric AES","quizWrong":["All TLS traffic is encrypted directly with the server's public key (RSA)","The server generates the symmetric key and sends it to the client over the open channel before encryption begins","The certificate encrypts the data and the signature decrypts it"],"picCap":"Asymmetric crypto for authentication and key exchange; the data is encrypted with the derived symmetric AES"},"crypto-jwt-sign":{"q":"How does HS256 differ from RS256 in JWT, and why is verifying the signature on the server critical?","a":"HS256 is a symmetric signature on HMAC: one shared secret both signs and verifies. RS256 is asymmetric: you sign with a private key (held only by the issuer) and verify with a public key that can be safely handed to every service. The signature protects the payload from tampering: the JWT body is just base64, not encryption — it's visible and can be edited. Without signature verification, an attacker simply writes role=admin for themselves and walks right in.","d":"↳ HS256 is good when the same service signs and verifies (the shared secret never leaves the perimeter). RS256 is needed when one party issues tokens (the auth server) and many independent services verify them — you hand them only the public key and never expose the private one. In microservices it's almost always RS256/ES256 via a JWKS endpoint.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: never trust the alg field from the token itself. The infamous attack — a client changes alg to none, or from RS256 to HS256, and a buggy library verifies the HMAC using the public key (which is common knowledge) — so the signature 'checks out'. Hard-code the allowed algorithms in the verification code.","hook":"A JWT payload isn't a safe, it's an envelope made of clear film: the contents are visible to everyone. The signature is the wax seal on it: if someone opened it and rewrote it, the seal won't match. Don't check the seal and you'll accept any forgery as genuine.","more":"- A JWT is `header.payload.signature`, all base64url. **The payload isn't encrypted** — anyone can decode it; secrecy comes only from the transport (HTTPS), and integrity from the signature.\n- **HS256 (HMAC-SHA256)**: symmetric, one secret. Simple, but the secret has to be known by everyone who verifies — scales poorly across many services.\n- **RS256 (RSA-SHA256)** / **ES256 (ECDSA)**: asymmetric. The auth server signs with the private key, the rest pull the public key via a **JWKS** endpoint and only verify.\n- The classic hole — **alg:none** and **RS256-to-HS256 substitution**: if the library naively takes alg from the token, the attacker signs with HMAC using the public RSA key as the secret. Always pin the expected algorithm on the verifying side.","quizCorrect":"HS256 — one shared secret for sign and verify; RS256 — the private key signs, the public key is verified by all services","quizWrong":["HS256 encrypts the payload, so its contents can't be read","RS256 uses a single secret, just with a longer hash","The JWT signature can be skipped if the token arrived over HTTPS, because the transport already guarantees the token was not modified"],"picCap":"HS256 — one shared secret; RS256 — the private key signs, the public key is verified by all. The payload is open; only the signature protects it"},"ds-trie":{"q":"Why a Trie when there's HashMap<String, Value>? Where does it actually win?","a":"A Trie (prefix tree) stores strings character by character: a shared prefix is a single shared path from the root. A HashMap only answers \"is this exact key present\", while a Trie cheaply answers \"which keys start with cat\" — that's autocomplete, dictionaries, prefix search. Lookup/insert of a single word is O(L), where L is the string length, independent of dictionary size.","d":"↳ Each node is a transition on one character (a children array/map). A word ends where the isEnd flag is set. A prefix traversal = descend along the prefix, then DFS downward and collect all isEnd nodes.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: a Trie doesn't find a substring in the middle (\"ats\" in \"cats\") — it's only about prefixes. For arbitrary substrings you need a suffix trie/suffix automaton or just an index. And don't make children an array of 65536 for Unicode — it'll blow up memory; use a HashMap.","hook":"It's like folders in a file explorer: \"Documents/\" is one shared path, and inside it branches into Work, Photos, Music. You don't repeat \"Documents\" in every file name.","more":"**When to reach for a Trie:**\n- autocomplete in search/IDE: \"type cat → suggest cat, catalog, catch\".\n- a dictionary with prefix checking, T9, filters by the start of a string.\n- prefix-based routing (IP routing is a radix trie).\n\n**The cost:** on sparse data a Trie with a 26/256-child array eats memory. Fix it with path compression — a **radix tree (compressed trie)**, where a chain of single-child nodes is collapsed into one node holding a substring.\n\n**Complexity:** insert/search/startsWith — O(L). It barely depends on the number of words in the tree, and that's its main edge over a tree/map.","quizCorrect":"Finding all keys with a given prefix — O(prefix length) plus a subtree traversal, with no scan of the whole dictionary","quizWrong":["A Trie always uses less memory than a HashMap, on any data","A Trie guarantees O(1) insert regardless of string length","A Trie can find a substring in the middle of a word as fast as by prefix, because it indexes every suffix of the stored words too"],"picCap":"Shared path c-a, then a branch to t (cat) and r (car). * = isEnd."},"ds-balanced-bst":{"q":"Why balance a BST? What's wrong with a plain binary search tree?","a":"A plain BST degenerates: if you insert sorted data (1,2,3,4...), the tree turns into a linked list and lookups fall from O(log n) to O(n). Balancing (red-black tree, AVL) guarantees the height stays O(log n) for any insertion order via rotations and node recoloring. A red-black tree is a self-balancing BST, and Java's TreeMap/TreeSet are built on it.","d":"↳ A red-black tree maintains 5 invariants (the root is black, a red node's children are black, every path from a node to its leaves has the same number of black nodes, etc.). These rules guarantee: the longest path is at most twice the shortest → height O(log n).","note":"⚠️ Caveat: if you don't need ordered operations (ranges, ceiling/floor, sorted traversal) — use a HashMap, it's O(1) versus O(log n). A balanced BST is justified precisely for ordering, not for the speed of a point lookup.","hook":"It's like a stack of books versus a library shelf. Pile the books into one column — to find the bottom one you go through them all. Spread them across shelves — any one is a couple of steps away.","more":"**Why balance matters in practice:**\n- a worst-case O(log n) guarantee — important when data arrives sorted or adversarial.\n- ordered queries: \"nearest key >= X\", range [a, b], min/max — things a HashMap doesn't give.\n\n**RB vs AVL:**\n- AVL keeps balance more strictly (faster lookups), but does more rotations on insert/delete.\n- A red-black tree balances more loosely, but modifications are cheaper. That's why libraries (Java TreeMap, C++ std::map, the Linux kernel) chose RB — a compromise for mixed workloads.\n\n**Where in Java:** TreeMap/TreeSet — RB tree. A HashMap bucket also turns into an RB tree once a single bucket has more than 8 collisions (treeification).","quizCorrect":"So that for any insertion order the height stays O(log n) and operations don't degrade to O(n)","quizWrong":["So the tree uses less memory than an unbalanced one and fits into the processor cache better of the machine","So an in-order traversal becomes faster than O(n)","To remove the need to compare keys during lookup"],"picCap":"Left — degenerated into a list. Right — the height stays logarithmic."},"ds-union-find":{"q":"Union-Find / DSU: how is \"are two nodes in the same group\" checked in almost O(1)?","a":"DSU (Disjoint Set Union) stores elements as a forest of trees: each node has a pointer to its parent, and the tree's root is the group's \"representative\". find(x) climbs to the root; two elements are in the same group if they share a root. union(a,b) hangs one root under another. With the optimizations, find/union run in almost a constant — the inverse Ackermann function α(n), which in practice is <= 4.","d":"↳ Two optimizations are mandatory. Union by rank: hang the smaller tree under the larger one so the height doesn't grow. Path compression: during find, re-hang all traversed nodes straight to the root, \"flattening\" the path for the future.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: a recursive find with path compression on a chain of millions of nodes can hit StackOverflow before the first compression. In production with large n, use an iterative two-pass find or path halving (parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]).","hook":"It's like the question \"are you and I from the same clan?\". Everyone knows their chief through a chain of mentors. And path compression is \"let's all just remember the chief directly so we don't run through the whole chain next time\".","more":"**Where DSU is used:**\n- Kruskal's algorithm for a minimum spanning tree — checking \"will this edge create a cycle\".\n- graph connected components, dynamic merging of groups (friends/networks, clusters).\n- problems like \"how many separate islands / groups\".\n\n**Why almost O(1):**\n- with both optimizations the amortized cost per operation is **O(α(n))**, where α is inverse Ackermann. For any realistic n (even 2^65536) α(n) <= 4. In practice it's treated as a constant.\n- with one optimization it's worse: path compression alone or rank alone gives O(log n).\n\n**What DSU can't do:** un-union. It's a \"merge-only\" structure — a standard DSU cannot remove an edge and split a group back apart.","quizCorrect":"Path compression re-hangs the path's nodes directly to the root, and union by rank keeps trees from growing in height — together they give almost O(1)","quizWrong":["DSU stores a separate connectivity flag for each pair of elements in a matrix, which is why a query about two elements is answered in constant time without any traversal at all","find runs in O(1) because the root is cached in every node on union","Balance is held by red-black coloring of nodes, as in TreeMap"],"picCap":"find(d) flattens the path: b, c, d now point straight at the root a."},"ds-fenwick-segtree":{"q":"You need a range sum over [l, r] with frequent updates. Why not a prefix-sum array?","a":"A prefix-sum array gives a range sum in O(1), but any element update invalidates all prefixes after it — O(n) per update. If the data changes often, that's lethal. A Fenwick tree (BIT) and a segment tree give both the range query and the update in O(log n) each. The BIT is more compact and simpler for sums; the segment tree is more flexible — it handles min/max/gcd and range updates.","d":"↳ A Fenwick tree stores partial sums cleverly by bits: index i is responsible for a block of length (i & -i) — the lowest set bit. So walking up/down the tree is just jumps i += i & -i and i -= i & -i, O(log n) steps total.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: a classic Fenwick tree is for sum/xor (invertible operations: range = prefix(r) - prefix(l-1)). For min/max over an arbitrary range, subtraction doesn't work — there you need a segment tree. Don't try to \"subtract minimums\".","hook":"It's like counters on warehouse floors. To know \"how many boxes on floors 1–5\" you don't recount each one — you add up a few aggregated counters. A box arrives — you only adjust the counters that cover it.","more":"**When to use what:**\n- static data, sum queries only — **prefix-sum array**: O(n) preprocessing, O(1) query, no updates.\n- frequent updates + sum/xor queries — **Fenwick (BIT)**: compact, ~10 lines, O(log n) for everything.\n- need min/max/gcd over a range, or **a whole-range update** (lazy propagation) — **segment tree**: more flexible, but 2-4x more memory and code.\n\n**The bit trick:** `i & -i` isolates the lowest set bit — that's the size of the block the BIT node is responsible for. Hence the name \"binary indexed\".\n\n**Memory:** BIT — an array of size n+1. Segment tree — usually 2n..4n. For the segment tree's flexibility you pay in memory and complexity.","quizCorrect":"Fenwick/segment tree give both the range query and a point update in O(log n), whereas a prefix-sum array is O(n) per update","quizWrong":["A prefix-sum array also updates in O(log n), it just needs more memory","Fenwick is faster than a prefix-sum array on a sum query — its query is O(1) regardless of the range being summed by the query","A segment tree can't do range queries, only point values"],"picCap":"Range query and update — both O(log n) thanks to bit jumps."},"ds-lru-cache":{"q":"Design an LRU cache with O(1) get and put. Why isn't one HashMap enough?","a":"A HashMap gives O(1) access, but doesn't remember usage order — and an LRU has to evict the least-recently-used element, which it must know in O(1). The solution: a HashMap (key → node) plus a doubly-linked list, where the head is the freshest and the tail is the eviction candidate. get/put move a node to the head; on overflow we remove the tail. Together the two structures give O(1) for everything.","d":"↳ The linked list must be doubly-linked: to remove a node from the middle in O(1) you need to know its left and right neighbors. The HashMap stores a reference straight to the list node, so \"find and splice out\" is a constant.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: a bare LinkedHashMap is NOT thread-safe — under concurrent access reordering corrupts the structure. Either wrap it in synchronized/Collections.synchronizedMap or use Caffeine. And don't forget accessOrder=true: by default LinkedHashMap keeps INSERTION order, not access order — you'd get FIFO instead of LRU.","hook":"It's like a stack of plates at a serving line. Take a plate, put a clean one on top. No room — remove the bottom one, the one nobody has reached for in a while. The top is always what was just used.","more":"**Why exactly this pair:**\n- **HashMap** → find a node by key in O(1).\n- **doubly-linked list** → move a node to the head and splice out the tail in O(1) (you need both prev/next references).\n- on its own neither structure gives both operations in a constant.\n\n**In Java in practice:**\n- `LinkedHashMap(cap, 0.75f, true)` — the `accessOrder=true` flag moves an element to the end on every get/put; override `removeEldestEntry` for auto-eviction. 10 lines instead of a hand-rolled list.\n- for concurrent load — **Caffeine** (or Guava Cache): thread-safe, with TTL, and internally smarter than LRU (the W-TinyLFU algorithm).\n\n**LRU vs LFU:** LRU evicts \"not touched in a while\", LFU — \"touched least often\". LFU keeps \"hot but rare\" keys better, but is harder to implement.","quizCorrect":"A HashMap doesn't store usage order, while a doubly-linked list gives O(1) to move a node to the head and remove the tail","quizWrong":["A singly-linked list is enough: you can remove a middle node in O(1) without a back-reference to the previous node in the list","You need a TreeMap to keep elements sorted by access time","A HashMap by itself evicts old keys when the load factor is reached"],"picCap":"Fresh node 3 to the head, the stale node 2 at the tail — it gets evicted on overflow."},"ds-bloom-filter":{"q":"What is a Bloom filter and why can it lie \"yes\" but never lie \"no\"?","a":"A Bloom filter is a bit array + k hash functions. On add(x) it sets k bits to 1; on contains(x) it checks those same k bits. If even one bit = 0 — the element is definitely absent (no false negatives). If all k bits = 1 — the element is \"possibly present\", but the bits could have been set by other elements (a false positive).","d":"↳ The FP probability grows with fill level: p ≈ (1 - e^(-kn/m))^k, where m is bits, n is elements, k is hashes. The optimum is k = (m/n)·ln2. You can't remove an element (clearing a bit would break others); for that you need a Counting Bloom (counters instead of bits).","note":"⚠️ Caveat: you can't exceed the declared expectedInsertions — past overflow the FP rate degrades non-linearly and quickly approaches ~100%. The size is fixed at creation, you can't \"grow\" it — only recreate (or use a Scalable Bloom).","hook":"A bouncer with a VIP list by height and jacket color: \"no such person\" — trust it completely; \"seems like they were here\" — might mix them up with a lookalike.","more":"**Why:** to save memory and network/disk requests. Instead of \"go to the DB and check if a record exists\", first ask the Bloom filter: if it says \"no\" — it really isn't there, skip the request.\n- **Where it's used:** LSM trees (Cassandra, RocksDB, HBase) — to avoid touching an SSTable where the key definitely isn't; CDNs/caches — \"one-hit\" protection; anti-spam/checking compromised passwords.\n- **Tuning:** you set n (expected number of elements) and p (acceptable FP) — the library computes m and k itself. Underestimate n and the FP rate spikes.\n- **Not a fit** if you need deletion, enumeration, or an exact answer. Then use a Counting Bloom, a Cuckoo filter, or a regular set.","quizCorrect":"A Bloom filter gives false positives (says \"present\" when it isn't), but never false negatives","quizWrong":["A Bloom filter gives false negatives, but never false positives","A Bloom filter is always exact, no errors — it's just a compact HashSet","A Bloom filter stores the elements themselves and lets you enumerate and delete them one by one"],"picCap":"A single zero bit = a guaranteed \"no\". All ones = \"possibly\"."},"ds-skip-list":{"q":"Skip list — how does a sorted linked list give O(log n) lookup without balancing a tree?","a":"On top of an ordinary sorted list, \"express levels\" are layered — sparse links that jump over nodes. The search goes top-down: run along the upper level until the next node would overshoot the target, then drop one level down. A node's height is chosen randomly (a coin flip: with probability 1/2 promote it one more level), so on average there are ~log n levels and lookup is O(log n).","d":"↳ There's no balancing like an AVL/RB tree — the structure holds up probabilistically, not via rotations. This greatly simplifies a concurrent implementation: an insert touches local links and can easily be made lock-free. The worst case is O(n), but the probability is vanishingly small.","hook":"A subway with an express and a local line: you ride the express past stations, get off near the right neighborhood, then transfer to the local — you don't crawl past every stop.","more":"**The idea:** replace expensive deterministic balancing with cheap randomization. Implementation and debugging are simpler than an RB tree, while the average characteristics are the same.\n- **Concurrency:** the main selling point. ConcurrentSkipListMap/Set in the JDK is the primary way to get a thread-safe sorted map; inserts/deletes are local and scale well without a global lock.\n- **Where it shows up:** Redis sorted set (ZSET) is built on a skip list + hash table; LevelDB memtable; many in-memory indexes.\n- **Memory:** a bit more than a tree, due to the extra levels of pointers (on average ~2 references per node).","quizCorrect":"Node heights are set randomly (by a coin flip), which gives O(log n) on average with no balancing operations","quizWrong":["Node heights are strictly fixed at log2(n) and recomputed on every insert","A skip list guarantees O(log n) worst case via rotations, like a red-black tree on every insertion of a node into the list","A skip list stores elements unordered and is therefore faster than a tree"],"picCap":"Upper levels are express links that jump over nodes; descending leads to the target in ~log n steps.","note":"ConcurrentSkipListMap does NOT keep a real size field — its size() walks the whole list in O(n) and isn't atomic under concurrent modifications, so frequent size()/isEmpty() calls on a hot path are a hidden performance killer; for emptiness checks use isEmpty() (it only inspects the first node), not size()==0."},"ds-heap-priority-queue":{"q":"What is a binary heap, what operations does it have, and how fast are they?","a":"A binary heap is a nearly complete binary tree stored in an array, with the invariant: in a min-heap the parent ≤ its children (in a max-heap, the reverse). peek (the min/max) — O(1), it's the root. insert and extractMin/poll — O(log n): an element \"bubbles up\" (sift-up) or \"sinks down\" (sift-down) until the invariant is restored. Building a heap from a whole array — O(n).","d":"↳ Stored in an array without pointers: for index i the children are 2i+1 and 2i+2, the parent is (i-1)/2. That's what gives compactness and cache locality. Important: a heap is NOT fully sorted — only the root is guaranteed. Iterating a heap doesn't yield a sorted sequence.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: PriorityQueue in Java is NOT thread-safe and does NOT guarantee FIFO for equal priorities (the order of equals is undefined). Need concurrency — PriorityBlockingQueue; need a FIFO tiebreak — add a monotonic seq number to the comparator.","hook":"An ER triage desk: you always instantly know who's in the worst shape (the top), but you don't keep a fully sorted list of all patients — it's not needed.","more":"**Why a priority queue:** when you repeatedly need to pull the \"most important\" element from a changing set. Dijkstra/A* (the nearest vertex), task schedulers, top-K (see below), merging K sorted lists, rate limiting/timers.\n- **Top-K in O(n log k):** keep a min-heap of size k; for each element — if it's greater than the root, poll+offer. The k largest remain in the heap, without sorting the whole array.\n- **heapify O(n):** building a heap from a ready array is cheaper than n inserts at O(log n) each — because most nodes are near the bottom and sink only a little.\n- **Don't confuse with TreeMap:** a heap gives a fast min/max, but not range queries or floor/ceiling — for those you need a balanced tree or a skip list.","quizCorrect":"peek of the minimum — O(1), insert and extractMin — O(log n); the heap is sorted only at the root","quizWrong":["All operations, including insert and extractMin, run in O(1)","The heap keeps elements fully sorted, so a traversal yields a sort of the whole list at once in sorted order","extractMin runs in O(n) because it has to scan the whole array"],"picCap":"The tree lives in an array; parent ≤ children; the root is the minimum."},"ds-consistent-hashing":{"q":"Why do you need consistent hashing and how is it better than plain hash(key) % N?","a":"With sharding via hash(key) % N, adding/removing a single node changes N, and almost all keys move — a catastrophe for caches and distributed stores. Consistent hashing places both nodes and keys on an imaginary ring (the hash space 0..2^32). A key belongs to the first node clockwise. When a node leaves, only its keys move (to the next node), the rest stay put — on average ~1/N of the keys.","d":"↳ The problem: with a small N the nodes land on the ring unevenly → load skew. Fixed with virtual nodes (vnodes): each physical node is represented by hundreds of points on the ring (hash(node + \"#0\"), hash(node + \"#1\"), ...). The more vnodes, the more even the distribution and the smoother the migration when membership changes.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: consistent hashing solves \"minimal migration\", but does NOT solve hot keys. If one key gets 50% of the traffic, vnodes won't help — it's still on a single node. For hot keys you need a separate mechanism (key replication, a cache in front of the shard).","hook":"A round table of waiters: each serves a sector clockwise. One leaves — the neighbor on the right takes their tables, the other sectors are untouched. mod N is reseating absolutely everyone.","more":"**Where it's used:** distributed caches (memcached clients, Ketama), Cassandra/DynamoDB/Riak (partitioning + replication), sharded stores, load balancers with sticky sessions.\n- **Replication:** a key is often written not to one node but to the next R nodes around the ring — that's how you get replicas without a separate placement scheme.\n- **vnodes are not an option but a necessity** in practice: without them load variance is large, and the rebalance when adding a node is uneven. Cassandra defaults to hundreds of tokens per node.\n- **Alternative:** rendezvous (HRW) hashing — for each key compute hash(key, node) over all nodes and take the maximum. Also minimal migration, simpler with no ring, but O(N) per lookup.","quizCorrect":"When the number of nodes changes, consistent hashing moves on average ~1/N of the keys, not almost all","quizWrong":["Consistent hashing redistributes all keys when adding a node, just like mod N, so the cache has to be warmed again","Virtual nodes exist to encrypt keys, not to even out load","Consistent hashing guarantees perfectly equal shards without any vnodes"],"picCap":"The ring: a node leaving touches only its sector; vnodes even out the load."},"ds-cardinality-sketches":{"q":"How do HyperLogLog and Count-Min Sketch differ, and what does each one approximate?","a":"Both are probabilistic structures with a fixed, tiny memory footprint. HyperLogLog estimates cardinality — the number of distinct elements (how many different user_ids you've seen). Count-Min Sketch estimates frequency — how many times a specific element occurred (top-K, heavy hitters). HLL answers \"how many distinct things total\", CMS answers \"how often this exact one\".","d":"↳ HLL: tracks the longest run of leading zeros in the hashes of elements — more distinct values means a longer zero-run is likely to appear; averaging across registers yields the estimate. ~12-16 KB can estimate billions of distinct items with ~1-2% error. CMS: a d×w counter matrix plus d hash functions; count(x) is the minimum across the d cells. It can only overestimate frequency (collisions add counts), never underestimate.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: both are unreliable at the tails. On small cardinalities (tens of elements) HLL applies corrections / linear counting, otherwise the error blows up. CMS heavily overestimates rare elements under load — for rare frequencies the result is nearly useless; accuracy holds only for frequent items (heavy hitters).","hook":"At a festival gate: HLL tells you \"how many different people came in today\" (without remembering anyone). CMS tells you \"how many times this particular regular showed up\". Different questions, different instruments.","more":"**Why:** exact distinct/frequency counting over large streams needs O(n) memory (storing the whole set/map). These sketches give acceptable accuracy in kilobytes and merge easily across shards/windows.\n- **HLL is used for:** unique visitors/IPs/queries per window, GROUP BY with COUNT(DISTINCT) in analytics (Redis PFCOUNT, BigQuery APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT, Presto). Merging unions is O(registers).\n- **CMS is used for:** heavy hitters (the most frequent URLs/keys/words), approximate rate limiting, network monitoring. Often paired with a min-heap for top-K.\n- **Common trait:** the CMS error is one-sided (upward only) and the HLL error is relative (depends on the register count, not on data volume) — which is why memory stays constant.","quizCorrect":"HyperLogLog estimates the number of distinct elements; Count-Min Sketch estimates the frequency of a specific element and can only overestimate it","quizWrong":["Both count the same thing — the number of distinct elements — HLL is just more accurate and needs far less memory to do it at the same time for the same accuracy","Count-Min Sketch can both under- and overestimate an element's frequency","HyperLogLog stores the distinct elements themselves and lets you enumerate them"],"picCap":"HLL — the count of distinct items as a single number; CMS — frequency as the minimum across the matrix rows."},"mq-exchanges":{"q":"In RabbitMQ a producer sends not to a queue but to an exchange. Why this layer, and what are the 4 types?","a":"A producer knows nothing about queues, it publishes to an exchange with a routing key, and the exchange fans copies out to queues by rules (bindings). That decouples sender and receivers. Types: direct (exact routing-key match), topic (mask match with *.# ), fanout (to all bound queues, the key is ignored), headers (match on headers instead of the key).","d":"A binding key is set at queue.bind, a routing key at publish. For direct they must match exactly; for topic a routing key like `order.eu.created` matches a binding `order.*.created` (* = one word, # = zero or more words).","note":"Subtlety: a single message bound by N bindings to the SAME queue still lands in it once (RabbitMQ dedups per queue). But independent copies go to DIFFERENT queues, each with its own ack.","hook":"An exchange is a mail sorting hub. You drop a letter with a zip code (routing key), and the sorter, by its table (bindings), puts copies into the right boxes (queues). The sender never sees the boxes.","more":"- **direct**, for pointed routing on a fixed key (logs by severity: `error`, `info`).\n- **topic**, hierarchical keys with a mask: `*` = exactly one word, `#` = zero or more words. The most flexible.\n- **fanout**, broadcast: every bound queue gets a copy, the routing key is ignored. For pub/sub notifications.\n- **headers**, match on headers (`x-match=all|any`) instead of a string key; slower, rarely used.\n- There's also the **default exchange** (empty name, type direct): a publish with routing key = queue name lands straight in it, which is what tutorials use.","quizCorrect":"topic with binding `order.#` catches both `order.created` and `order.eu.created`","quizWrong":["fanout matches the routing key against a mask with * and #","a RabbitMQ producer publishes straight to a queue, bypassing the exchange and its routing","a direct exchange sends a copy to all bound queues"],"picCap":"The exchange routes copies by binding key; the producer doesn't know the queues"},"mq-ack-prefetch":{"q":"How does ack differ from nack/reject, why prefetch (QoS), and what happens with autoAck=true if the consumer crashes?","a":"ack means 'handled, delete from the queue'. nack/reject means 'couldn't'; with requeue=true the message returns to the queue, with false it's dropped or goes to a DLX. With autoAck=true RabbitMQ counts it delivered the moment it's sent, so if the consumer crashes mid-processing the message is lost. prefetch (basicQos) caps the number of unacknowledged in-flight messages per consumer, otherwise a fast broker floods a slow consumer, memory bloats, and balancing breaks.","d":"reject handles exactly one message; nack supports multiple=true (ack/nack a batch up to this deliveryTag). requeue returns the message to the HEAD of the queue, so a poison message loops forever unless you cap retries or route it to a DLX.","note":"Subtlety: requeue=true on a permanently failing message = an infinite hot loop eating CPU. Fix it with x-delivery-limit (quorum queues) or a retry counter + DLX.","hook":"A waitress (prefetch=2) carries at most 2 orders. Until she delivers (acks) at least one, she won't carry a third. autoAck is 'count the order done the moment you pick it off the counter': drop the tray and the guest goes hungry while the kitchen thinks they were fed.","more":"- **Manual ack** (autoAck=false), required wherever processing guarantees matter. ack only AFTER successful work.\n- **prefetch=1**, fair balancing for long/uneven tasks: each takes the next only after finishing the current. Downside, a round-trip per message, lower throughput.\n- **prefetch 20-100**, a throughput compromise for short tasks.\n- **nack multiple=true**, ack/nack everything up to and including deliveryTag (a batch).\n- An unacknowledged message 'hangs' behind the consumer; if the connection/channel dies, RabbitMQ automatically requeues it to another consumer.","quizCorrect":"With autoAck=true, a consumer crash mid-processing means the message is lost","quizWrong":["nack with requeue=false always returns the message to the queue","prefetch caps the total queue size in bytes","ack and reject are synonyms, both delete the message and require no processing"],"picCap":"ack deletes, nack requeues/drops; prefetch holds the unacknowledged window"},"mq-rabbit-vs-kafka":{"q":"RabbitMQ vs Kafka in one phrase: how does the 'queue' model differ from the 'log', and when do you pick which?","a":"RabbitMQ is a smart queue: the broker pushes a message to a consumer, and after ack it's DELETED, you can't re-read it. Kafka is an append-only log: the consumer pulls by offset, the message stays until retention regardless of reads, and you can rewind the offset and replay history. Take RabbitMQ for task/RPC distribution and complex routing; Kafka for event streams, many independent readers, and replay.","d":"In Kafka parallelism = the number of partitions (one partition is read by one consumer in a group), and ordering is guaranteed within a partition. In RabbitMQ parallelism = the number of consumers on a queue, and they compete for messages (competing consumers), with no global ordering between them.","note":"Subtlety: 'RabbitMQ can't be re-read' is about classic queues. RabbitMQ Streams (since 3.9) are a log structure with offsets, closer to Kafka. Don't confuse classic/quorum queues with streams.","hook":"RabbitMQ is a checkout conveyor: take your order, carry it off, and it's no longer on the belt. Kafka is a recording of the shift: watch from any minute, rewind, and five different people watch their own part independently. The recording is erased on a timer (retention), not when someone has watched it.","more":"- **Storage semantics**: Rabbit is transient (gone after ack), Kafka is a durable log with retention by time/size (or compaction by key).\n- **Delivery**: Rabbit push (the broker pushes, prefetch regulates), Kafka pull (the consumer fetches batches itself, the broker keeps no per-message state).\n- **Replay**: Kafka only, `seek(offset)` or a new consumer group reads from the start. In Rabbit a repeat needs the producer to publish again.\n- **Routing**: Rabbit is rich (exchanges/bindings/topic masks), Kafka is simple (topic + key->partition).\n- **Throughput**: Kafka is higher on streams (sequential disk, batches, zero-copy). Rabbit is more flexible on per-message logic and priorities.\n- **Take Kafka** when several different consumers must read an event in their own way, or you need replay or event sourcing. **Take Rabbit** for command/task queues, RPC, complex routing, priorities, TTL.","quizCorrect":"In Kafka a message stays after a read and can be replayed by rewinding the offset","quizWrong":["In RabbitMQ you can re-read a message after ack by rewinding the offset","Kafka pushes messages to consumers and deletes them right after ack","In RabbitMQ parallelism is hard-capped by the queue's number of partitions it was created with"],"picCap":"Queue: push + delete. Log: pull by offset, the data stays"},"mq-dlx":{"q":"What is a dead-letter exchange/queue, and which 3 events send a message to a DLX?","a":"A DLX is an ordinary exchange to which RabbitMQ automatically re-routes 'dead' messages, and a bound DLQ accumulates them for review/retries. A message becomes dead-lettered when: it's reject/nack'd with requeue=false; the message or queue TTL expires; or the queue's max-length is exceeded (overflow). This saves you from loss and from a poison loop.","d":"A DLX is configured on the source queue via the arguments x-dead-letter-exchange (and optionally x-dead-letter-routing-key). RabbitMQ adds an x-death header with the history: a counter, the reason (rejected/expired/maxlen), and the original queue, which is used to build retry with backoff.","note":"Subtlety: a queue's TTL only expires for the message AT THE HEAD of the queue, RabbitMQ checks expiry when trying to deliver. A message in the middle can 'outlive' its TTL until the read order reaches it.","hook":"A DLQ is the 'returned mail' bin at the sorting office. A letter wasn't delivered (rejected), went stale (TTL), or the box is full, and instead of throwing it out they put it in a special bin marked with why. Then a human sorts it out, rather than losing it silently.","more":"- **A DLX is not a special type** but a role: an ordinary direct/topic/fanout exchange designated as the receiver of dead messages.\n- **Three dead-letter reasons**: `rejected` (nack/reject, requeue=false), `expired` (message/queue TTL), `maxlen` (overflow on x-max-length / x-max-length-bytes).\n- **The x-death header** holds the failure history: count, reason, queue, time, the basis for counting retries.\n- **Retry with backoff**: the classic pattern is a DLQ with its own TTL, after which the message goes back through a DLX into the working queue (delayed retry). Or the rabbitmq-delayed-message-exchange plugin.\n- **Parking lot**: after N retries the message settles in a final 'penalty' queue for manual review, so it doesn't loop forever.","quizCorrect":"A message goes to a DLX on nack/reject with requeue=false, TTL expiry, or exceeding max-length","quizWrong":["A DLX is a special exchange type, distinct from direct/topic/fanout declared separately in the broker","A message lands in the DLQ right after a successful consumer ack","A DLQ automatically re-routes messages back every 5 seconds"],"picCap":"Reject/TTL/overflow -> DLX -> DLQ for review and controlled retries"},"mq-sqs":{"q":"AWS SQS: what is the visibility timeout, why at-least-once, and how does FIFO differ from standard?","a":"After receiving a message, the consumer doesn't delete it but makes it invisible for the visibility timeout, others don't see it while you process. Finished in time, you send DeleteMessage; ran out of time or crashed, the timeout expires and the message becomes visible again and goes to someone else. Hence at-least-once: the same message can arrive twice (crashed after processing but before delete), so the consumer must be idempotent. Standard is near-unlimited throughput, best-effort ordering, and possible duplicates; FIFO is strict ordering and exactly-once processing within a MessageGroupId, but capped at ~3000 msg/s with batching.","d":"If processing runs longer than the visibility timeout, the message 'resurfaces' and a second consumer picks it up, giving you double processing. Fix it with ChangeMessageVisibility (extend it like a heartbeat) or by tuning the timeout. FIFO dedups by MessageDeduplicationId within a 5-minute window.","note":"Subtlety: even FIFO gives exactly-once on delivery-into-the-queue and on processing via dedup, but that's no magic shield against a double side-effect if a failure lands between processing and delete. Idempotency is still useful.","hook":"The visibility timeout is like checking a book out of the library 'for an hour'. While it's with you, the catalog marks it 'on loan', no one else takes it. Return it (delete) and it's gone from the desk. The hour passes and you haven't returned it, the system decides you're lost and re-shelves the book. So two people can get a copy.","more":"- **At-least-once (standard)**: at least one delivery is guaranteed, with possible duplicates and reordering, so you need consumer-side idempotency.\n- **DLQ via redrive policy**: after maxReceiveCount unsuccessful receives the message goes to the designated DLQ (poison protection, manual review).\n- **FIFO**: strict ordering and dedup within a **MessageGroupId**; different groups are processed in parallel. Dedup by MessageDeduplicationId / content-based within a 5-min window.\n- **Long polling** (WaitTimeSeconds up to 20) cuts empty responses and cost versus short polling.\n- **Limits**: standard is near-unlimited; FIFO is 300 API calls/s, up to 3000 msg/s with batches of 10.\n- Unlike Rabbit/Kafka, **SQS has no push of its own and no exchange routing**: just pull + visibility; routing/fan-out is added via SNS->SQS.","quizCorrect":"If the consumer didn't call DeleteMessage before the visibility timeout ended, the message becomes visible again and is redelivered","quizWrong":["SQS guarantees exactly-once in a standard queue, duplicates are impossible","A FIFO queue gives unlimited throughput, just like standard","The visibility timeout is the time after which a message is deleted forever from the queue without ever being processed by anyone on the queue"],"picCap":"A message is hidden for the visibility timeout; no delete means it resurfaces (at-least-once)"},"mq-ordering":{"q":"Why does message ordering break the moment you have more than one consumer? How do you preserve order where it matters?","a":"A queue is FIFO only on dispatch. As soon as two consumers pull in parallel, m1 and m2 go to different workers, and which is processed first depends on speed, network, and retries. Plus nack-requeue returns a message to the queue behind its neighbors. You preserve order by routing related messages into ONE processing stream: a partitioning key (Kafka partition by key, SQS MessageGroupId), or a separate queue per entity, or a single consumer with prefetch=1.","d":"Global ordering and horizontal scaling are mutually exclusive. What you actually need is only ordering PER KEY (all events of one order/client in a row). Shard by key: one key -> one partition/group -> one handler at a time. Across keys, parallelism is free.","note":"Subtlety: 'we need ordering' almost always means 'ordering within an aggregate', not global. A requirement for global total ordering through a broker is a design red flag, it kills scaling.","hook":"The checkout line is fair while there's one register. Open a second and someone who was behind can jump ahead if the front is slow. To always serve a client in order, route all the 'same-surname' people to ONE register (the partition key). Different surnames can go in parallel.","more":"- **The ordering guarantee is local**: Kafka within a partition, SQS FIFO within a MessageGroupId, RabbitMQ on one queue with ONE consumer.\n- **The partitioning key** is the main trick: hash by entityId and all its events land in one ordered stream. Parallelism across keys is preserved.\n- **prefetch=1 + a single consumer** gives strict ordering in RabbitMQ but kills throughput, fine for low-rate critical flows.\n- **Retries break order**: requeue/redelivery puts a message back behind. If ordering is critical, process synchronously within a key or buffer.\n- **Idempotency + version/sequence** in the payload is insurance: the consumer drops a stale event by version number itself, even if it arrives out of order.","quizCorrect":"You preserve per-key ordering by routing all messages of one key to one partition/group/queue and one handler","quizWrong":["RabbitMQ guarantees global ordering with any number of parallel consumers","Adding more consumers improves message ordering","Kafka preserves global ordering across the whole topic regardless of partitions and consumer count in the group"],"picCap":"Two consumers = a race. Order is held by sharding by key into one handler"},"mq-competing-consumers":{"q":"The competing-consumers pattern: what is it, how does it scale, and what must you watch so it doesn't shoot you in the foot?","a":"Several consumers listen on ONE queue and compete for messages, the broker gives each message to exactly one of them. Add workers and throughput grows and you gain fault tolerance (one dies, the rest clear the backlog). The price: ordering between messages is lost, processing must be idempotent (redelivery is possible), and load is balanced via prefetch.","d":"In RabbitMQ this is natural: N consumers on a queue, prefetch regulates how many each has in flight. The Kafka analog is a consumer group: partitions are split among group members, but you can't have more consumers in a group than partitions (the extras sit idle). That's competing consumers at the partition level, not per individual message.","note":"Subtlety: adding consumers beyond the number of partitions (Kafka) doesn't speed things up, the extras just sit idle. In RabbitMQ the limit is different: sensible prefetch and the downstream's (DB, API) ability to take the load, otherwise you just push the bottleneck further down.","hook":"One long order list and three cooks at one counter. Whoever frees up grabs the next ticket. Three times faster, and if a cook goes down two carry on. Downside: dishes don't come out in strict order, and if a ticket is returned a different cook may take it.","more":"- **Goal**: horizontal throughput scaling + fault tolerance. The broker distributes the work itself, no separate dispatcher needed.\n- **Competing vs pub/sub**: here ONE consumer gets the message (a work queue, 'fanout' in the load sense), not all of them. Broadcast is a separate queue per consumer via a fanout exchange.\n- **Balancing**: prefetch=1 is fair for uneven tasks; a larger prefetch is higher throughput but risks a fast worker idling while a slow one sits on a batch.\n- **Idempotency is mandatory**: on a failure/retry a message may be redelivered or go to another consumer.\n- **Kafka subtlety**: group parallelism is capped by the partition count, scaling consumers requires partitions planned in advance.\n- **Autoscaling**: the consumer count can be driven by queue depth as a metric.","quizCorrect":"Each message from the shared queue goes to exactly one of the competing consumers","quizWrong":["Each consumer gets a copy of every message from the queue (broadcast)","Competing consumers guarantees strict message processing order","In a Kafka consumer group the number of active consumers can exceed the number of partitions"],"picCap":"N consumers on one queue split the load; each message goes to one"},"mq-queue-vs-stream":{"q":"The decisive design question: when do you take a queue vs a log-stream? Give a criterion, not 'it depends'.","a":"A queue, when a message is a TASK, has one executor, isn't needed after it's done, and what matters is load decoupling/retries (send an email, generate a PDF, process a payment). A log-stream, when a message is a FACT/event read independently by several consumers in their own way, you need replay, audit, or state reconstruction (order created -> billing, analytics, notifications, and search all listen). The criterion: 'one consumer, then throw it away' -> queue; 'many readers and/or you need history' -> stream.","d":"A queue is optimized for competing consumers and deletion after ack; replay isn't provided. A log keeps data by retention regardless of reads, and offsets let new/crashed consumers catch up on history, at the cost of cleanup being by time, not by processing. Hybrid: commands (do X) -> queue, events (X happened) -> stream.","note":"Subtlety: trying to bolt 'replay' onto an ordinary queue (re-reading processed messages) is an antipattern, you'd have to store copies and reinvent the wheel. Needing replay is a sign you needed a log from the start, not a queue.","hook":"A queue is the to-do list on the fridge: do an item, cross it out, forget it. A log is a diary: write down a fact, and different people read it (the accountant, the doctor, you a year later), each with their own bookmark, and you can re-read the past. To-dos get thrown away, the diary is kept.","more":"- **Command vs Event** is the best litmus test: 'do X' (an addressed command, one executor) -> queue; 'X happened' (a fact with no addressee, broadcast) -> stream.\n- **How many readers**: one logical consumer -> queue; several independent ones, each at its own pace -> stream.\n- **Do you need replay/audit/state reconstruction** (event sourcing, regenerating projections, a new service catching up on history) -> stream only.\n- **Data lifecycle**: 'handled, thrown away' -> queue; 'kept by retention, read by many' -> log.\n- **Throughput profile**: a very high stream of uniform sequential events -> a log (Kafka) is more efficient; complex routing/priorities/TTL on small volumes -> a queue broker (Rabbit).\n- **Not dogma**: one system often uses both, tasks via RabbitMQ/SQS, domain events via Kafka.","quizCorrect":"You choose a log-stream when several consumers must read an event independently and/or you need to replay history","quizWrong":["You choose a queue when several consumers must independently replay one event","A log-stream is optimal for tasks with one executor that are thrown away after ack","The choice between queue and stream is determined solely by the consumer's programming language and its client library"],"picCap":"A task with one executor -> queue. A fact for many readers/replay -> log"},"api-richardson-maturity":{"q":"How does \"real REST\" differ from \"JSON over HTTP\", and what does the Richardson Maturity Model measure?","a":"Most \"REST APIs\" are really RPC wrapped in HTTP. The Richardson model breaks maturity into 4 levels: L0 — a single endpoint, everything through POST (a tunnel); L1 — there are resources (/orders/42) but only one verb; L2 — you use HTTP verbs meaningfully (GET/POST/PUT/DELETE) and status codes; L3 — HATEOAS, where the response carries links to the next available actions. Real-world production lives almost entirely at L2 — and that's fine.","d":"↳ L2 is the \"sweet spot\": resources as nouns, verbs as actions, codes as outcomes. L3 gives self-description, but the cost in client complexity rarely pays off outside hypermedia APIs (like PayPal, or GitHub partially).","note":"⚠️ Caveat: chasing L3 without a real need is over-engineering. HATEOAS complicates both client and server, and most front-ends hardcode URLs anyway. Adopt L3 only if your clients are genuinely loosely coupled (a public API with long-term evolution).","hook":"REST maturity is like levels of driving. L0: you push the car by hand. L1: there's a steering wheel but only one gear. L2: a full gearbox and turn signals — where almost everyone drives. L3: autopilot that even suggests the turns.","more":"**Richardson** devised the model to explain how far an API has \"grown into\" Fielding's ideas.\n- **L0 (POX)** — Plain Old XML/JSON: one URL, POST for everything, HTTP as mere transport.\n- **L1** — noun-resources appear, but often still a single verb.\n- **L2** — proper verbs (idempotent GET/PUT/DELETE) plus meaningful codes; caching and uniformity.\n- **L3** — hypermedia (HATEOAS): the server dictates the available transitions to the client via links.\nFielding considered L3 mandatory for \"true REST\", but in the industry L2 is the de-facto standard.","quizCorrect":"L2 — correct use of HTTP verbs and status codes","quizWrong":["L2 — having at least one resource with a URI of its own","L2 — mandatory HATEOAS links in the response","L2 — tunneling all operations through POST"],"picCap":"The maturity ladder: production lives at L2"},"api-status-codes":{"q":"When do you return 201 vs 202 vs 204, and what's the fundamental difference between 409, 422, and 400?","a":"The code family is a contract: 2xx \"it worked\", 4xx \"you messed up\", 5xx \"I messed up\". 201 Created — the resource was created synchronously (return a Location). 202 Accepted — we took it but will process it asynchronously (no result yet). 204 No Content — success with no body (typical for DELETE or a PUT update). 400 — a syntactically malformed request; 422 — the syntax is fine but the semantics are invalid (a business rule); 409 — a conflict with the resource's current state (a duplicate, a version race).","note":"⚠️ Caveat: 422 formally comes from WebDAV (RFC 4918) but caught on in REST for validation. Some teams deliberately use only 400 for simplicity — that's acceptable; what matters is consistency within a single API, not \"400 here, 422 there at random\".","hook":"Status codes are like a waiter's reply. 2xx \"bringing your dish\". 4xx \"you ordered something not on the menu — that's on you\". 5xx \"the kitchen's on fire — that's on us\". Mixing up the classes is like blaming the guest for a kitchen fire.","more":"The key is separating the \"class\" (first digit) from the \"detail\".\n- **201 vs 200**: 201 is strictly about creating a resource, with a Location header pointing to it.\n- **202**: a \"we accepted it, no result yet\" reply — for async processing, return a link to a status job.\n- **204**: success, no body — ideal for DELETE and idempotent PUT where there's nothing to return.\n- **400 vs 422**: 400 — the parser couldn't make sense of it; 422 — it parsed, but the values violate the rules (a negative quantity).\n- **409**: a conflict with state — a duplicate unique key, a lost optimistic-lock race.","quizCorrect":"422 — the request is syntactically correct but violates a business rule (semantics)","quizWrong":["422 — the request contains a syntax error in the JSON that has to be fixed by the client","422 — the resource was not found on the server","422 — an internal server error while processing"],"picCap":"The code class = whose fault it is; the digits = the detail"},"api-idempotency":{"q":"Why are PUT and DELETE idempotent, POST is not, and how do you make POST safe on retries (idempotency-key)?","a":"Idempotency = repeating a request N times = the same effect as one. PUT puts a resource into a specific state (repeating doesn't change the result), DELETE removes it once and subsequent calls just mean \"already gone\" (the same final effect). POST \"create a new one\" is inherently non-idempotent: two POSTs = two orders. To protect POST from double-charging on retries/timeouts, the client sends a unique Idempotency-Key, the server remembers key→result, and on a repeat returns the stored response without performing the operation again.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: store under the key not \"the fact that it was processed\" but the actual result+status, and handle the race (two parallel requests with the same key) via a unique index/lock, otherwise both go through. And always put a TTL on keys — otherwise the table will bloat.","hook":"An Idempotency-Key is like a coat-check tag. You hand over a jacket and get tag #7. Come back a second time with the same #7 — they give you the same jacket, not a new one. By the key the server recognizes \"this is the same operation\" and doesn't charge twice.","more":"Distinguish **idempotent** from **safe**: GET is safe (doesn't change state) and idempotent; PUT/DELETE are idempotent but not safe; POST is neither.\n- **PUT** — \"a full replacement at a known URI\" → a repeat yields the same state.\n- **DELETE** — the \"resource is gone\" effect is identical after 1 and N calls (though the 2nd may return 404 — that's about the response code, not the effect).\n- **POST + Idempotency-Key** — the Stripe/PayPal pattern: the client generates the key (a UUID) for one business operation, and the server deduplicates in a DB/Redis with a TTL.\n- The key risk without this: a network timeout → the client retries → a double charge.","quizCorrect":"The server stores a key → result mapping and on a repeat returns the stored response without re-executing","quizWrong":["The server simply ignores all repeated POSTs with any body","The client just doesn't send the request a second time, so no key is needed on the backend at all on the server","Idempotency-Key makes POST idempotent automatically with no server-side logic"],"picCap":"An Idempotency-Key turns POST into something safe to retry"},"api-versioning":{"q":"Three ways to version an API (URI / header / content-type) — pros, cons, and which to choose?","a":"URI version (/v1/orders) — the simplest and most visible: easy to test from a browser/curl, explicit in logs, but it \"pollutes\" the URL and formally violates the idea \"URI = resource\". Header version (Api-Version: 2) — a clean URL, but invisible, harder to debug and cache. Content-type / media-type version (Accept: application/vnd.acme.order.v2+json) — the most \"correct\" by REST (you version the representation, not the resource), but harder for clients and teams. In practice, for internal/corporate APIs the URI almost always wins — for its transparency and low maintenance cost.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: with header/media-type versioning, don't forget `Vary: Api-Version` (or Vary: Accept), otherwise a CDN/proxy will serve a cached response of the wrong version. This is a classic trap that gets caught in production.","hook":"Versioning is like a store's address. URI: a new branch at a new address — everyone sees it at once. Header: the same address, but you whisper to the guard \"take me to the renovated hall\". Media-type: the same address, and you ask for the item \"in the 2024 packaging\". The subtler the hint, the easier it is for the courier to get lost.","more":"The main debate: **pragmatism vs REST purity**.\n- **URI**: early GitHub, Twilio, most corporate APIs. Pros — cacheable, trivially routable, visible in logs. Cons — the same resource under two URLs.\n- **Header**: conceptually cleaner, but caches ignore it by default (you need Vary), and you can't drop a link in chat saying \"open this\".\n- **Media-type**: GitHub v3 (Accept: vnd.github.v3+json), academically correct — but hard for clients and juniors, increasing coupling.\n- General advice: **version only major breaking changes**; do minor ones backward-compatibly without a new version.","quizCorrect":"Media-type versioning is considered the most RESTful, because it versions the resource's representation rather than its URI","quizWrong":["URI versioning is considered the most RESTful because the URL is always unique and visible in the logs and in every proxy cache along the way","Header versioning is forbidden by the HTTP spec","All three approaches are equivalent and have no tradeoffs"],"picCap":"The more correct by REST — the more expensive to maintain"},"api-problem-json":{"q":"What is RFC 7807 problem+json and why is \"{ \\\"error\\\": \\\"...\\\" }\" in every service a pain?","a":"RFC 7807 (updated by RFC 9457) — a standard format for an HTTP API error body, so that all services respond to errors the same way. Content-Type: application/problem+json, with fields: type (a URI for the error type), title (a short human-readable summary), status (duplicates the HTTP code), detail (specifics of this case), instance (a URI for this occurrence). You can extend it with your own fields (e.g. errors[] for validation). Without a standard, every microservice cobbles together its own structure — and the client/front-end has to parse ten different formats.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: don't put a raw stack trace or internal DB messages in detail — that's a leak for an attacker. detail must be safe for the client; technical specifics go to logs keyed by traceId/instance.","hook":"problem+json is like a single complaint form. Each department used to write its refusal on its own scrap of paper however it pleased, and the lawyer went mad sorting it out. Introduce one form with fields for \"type/title/status/detail\" — and any department fills it in the same way, the client reads it instantly.","more":"The goal — **machine-readable + human-readable** errors without inventing a format in every service.\n- **type** — a URI, a stable identifier for the error class (the client branches its logic on it), and it need not resolve.\n- **title** — constant text for the type; **detail** — about this specific case.\n- **status** — duplicates the HTTP code in the body (handy for logging/proxies).\n- **instance** — a URI for the specific incident (can be a correlation-id).\n- **Extensions** — add your own fields (errors[] for per-field validation, traceId).\n- **RFC 9457** (2023) superseded 7807; the format is the same, the details clarified.","quizCorrect":"application/problem+json with fields type, title, status, detail, instance","quizWrong":["application/json with a single mandatory error field and no status code in the payload","text/plain with the exception's stack trace","application/error+xml with a code and a message"],"picCap":"One error standard instead of a zoo of formats"},"api-resources-verbs":{"q":"How to design resources and URIs correctly: nouns vs verbs, nesting, and non-CRUD actions?","a":"A resource is a noun (an entity), and the action is expressed by the HTTP verb, not the URL. Bad: POST /createOrder, GET /getOrders. Good: POST /orders, GET /orders. Collections — plural (/orders, /orders/42/items). Nesting shows ownership, but don't go deeper than 1–2 levels — beyond that, prefer a flat resource with a filter. For actions that don't fit CRUD (cancel, confirm), either model a state sub-resource or pragmatically do POST /orders/42/cancel — that's an acceptable compromise.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: don't try to cram EVERYTHING into pure CRUD for dogma's sake. Business actions like \"confirm a payment\" or \"cancel an order\" are often cleaner as POST /resource/{id}/action than as contortions with a PATCH on state. Dogmatism here hurts readability.","hook":"URIs are like warehouse shelves, verbs are the operations on them. The shelf is called \"orders\", not \"take-order\". You don't make a \"delete-orders\" shelf — you take the \"orders\" shelf and apply the DELETE action to it. A verb in the shelf's name is a mess on the warehouse floor.","more":"Basic rules they expect in an interview:\n- **Nouns, plural**: /orders, /clients — the collection, and the element /orders/42.\n- **Verbs in the URI = code smell** (RPC): a sign of Richardson L0/L1.\n- **Filtering/pagination/sorting — query parameters**: ?status=open&page=2&sort=-createdAt, not separate endpoints.\n- **Nesting ≤ 2 levels**: /orders/42/items is fine; deeper — make it a standalone resource.\n- **Non-CRUD actions**: either model the state (PATCH with status) or use an action sub-resource POST /orders/42/cancel — pragmatic and readable.","quizCorrect":"POST /orders — a noun-resource, with the action expressed by the HTTP verb","quizWrong":["POST /createOrder — putting the action in the URI makes the API more explicit","GET /getOrders?delete=true — reusing GET for deletion","Resource names are always singular: /order, /item"],"picCap":"Nouns in the URI, actions as verbs"},"api-pagination-cursor":{"q":"Why does offset pagination (?page=5000) fall apart on large tables, while cursor/keyset doesn't?","a":"OFFSET N forces the DB to physically scan and discard N rows before returning the wanted page — on page 5000 that's millions of discarded rows per request. Keyset, instead of \"skip N\", says \"give me the rows AFTER this value\" (WHERE id > :last_id), and the index jumps straight to the right spot in O(log n). Bonus: with inserts/deletes the offset \"slides\" and shows duplicates or gaps, while a cursor is anchored to a specific row and stays stable.","d":"The cursor is usually encoded in base64 (e.g. the last (created_at, id)) so the client doesn't depend on the internal schema and can't forge the sort. Keyset's downside: you can't jump to an arbitrary \"page 100\" — only forward/back from the current position.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: keyset out of the box only gives you the next/previous page. For bidirectional navigation you need two cursors (before/after) and careful boundary handling. And remember the tie-breaker — without a unique tail in ORDER BY, the cursor will lose or duplicate rows.","hook":"Offset is \"count 5000 people from the front of the line and throw them out\". Keyset is \"stand right behind that redhead\". The deeper you go, the more it hurts the first one.","more":"- **Offset is fine** for small tables and UIs with numbered pages, where you need a jump \"to page 50\" and a total count.\n- **Keyset/cursor is the default for infinite scroll, feeds, and APIs**: stability under inserts matters more than random access.\n- The cursor must include **all sort fields**: if you sort by created_at and it's not unique — add a tie-breaker (id), otherwise you'll skip rows with the same timestamp.\n- **A total count for keyset is usually not returned** — it's expensive; if needed, it's estimated or done in a separate query.\n- Don't let the client change the sort between pages with the same cursor — the cursor is tied to a specific ORDER BY.","quizCorrect":"OFFSET forces the DB to scan and discard all preceding rows, which is why deep pages are slow","quizWrong":["OFFSET caches worse, while keyset is always served from the page cache","keyset is faster because it returns less data in one response","OFFSET doesn't use indexes at all, while keyset necessarily builds them on the fly for the query"],"picCap":"offset degrades linearly with depth, keyset stays constant"},"api-rate-limit-headers":{"q":"A client got a 429. Which headers tell it when and how to retry, and how does Retry-After differ from X-RateLimit-*?","a":"429 Too Many Requests means \"you exceeded the limit\". Retry-After says specifically \"don't poke for N seconds\" (or until a date) — it's a command for how long to wait before retrying. X-RateLimit-Limit/Remaining/Reset is an info dashboard: how much is allowed in the window total, how much is left, and when the window resets (usually a Unix timestamp). A good client reads Remaining ahead of time and slows itself down before hitting a 429.","d":"The IETF standard is moving toward RateLimit-Limit/RateLimit-Remaining/RateLimit-Reset (without the X-), but in production X-RateLimit-* still dominates. Retry-After works not only with 429 but also with 503 (Service Unavailable) and 3xx redirects.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: rate-limit headers aren't rigidly standardized — GitHub, Stripe, and Twitter return them differently (X-RateLimit- vs RateLimit- vs their own). Never hardcode names for a single API; read Retry-After as the primary source of truth, treat the rest as best-effort.","hook":"X-RateLimit-Remaining is your speedometer and fuel gauge: you see ahead of time that you're running low. Retry-After after a 429 is a \"no entry\" sign with a timer: stand still for exactly that long.","more":"- **Retry-After** takes two formats: a number of seconds (`Retry-After: 30`) or an HTTP date (`Retry-After: Wed, 24 Jun 2026 12:00:00 GMT`). The client must handle both.\n- **Don't retry blindly**: after a 429 use exponential backoff with **jitter** (random spread), otherwise all clients hit at the same moment after Reset — a \"thundering herd\".\n- **X-RateLimit-Reset** is most often a Unix timestamp in seconds, but some APIs return \"seconds until reset\" — read the docs, don't guess.\n- Limits are often **multi-tier**: per-second, per-minute, per-day at the same time. The headers may describe only one — the most pressing.\n- 429 is a \"slow down\" signal, 403 is \"you're not allowed at all\". Don't confuse them: retrying a 403 is pointless.","quizCorrect":"Retry-After specifies how long to wait before retrying; X-RateLimit-* show the current limit state ahead of time","quizWrong":["Retry-After and X-RateLimit-Reset are the same field under different names, so sending either one of them is enough","X-RateLimit-Remaining is only returned together with a 429 status","Retry-After is always given in milliseconds per the HTTP standard"],"picCap":"the Remaining counter drops to 0, then 429 and a command to wait"},"api-content-negotiation":{"q":"What is content negotiation, and why is the API version sometimes stuffed into the Accept header rather than the URL?","a":"Content negotiation is when the client, via headers (Accept, Accept-Language, Accept-Encoding), says \"I want this format\", and the server returns the best available variant of the same resource. Accept: application/json vs application/xml — one URL, different representations. The version goes into Accept (`application/vnd.api.v2+json`) to keep the URL a clean pointer to the resource, with the version being a detail of the representation — but it's a contentious practice; many prefer /v2/ in the path for simplicity and cacheability.","d":"Based on Accept the server picks a representation and MUST return a Content-Type matching what it actually sent, plus a Vary: Accept header — otherwise caches and CDNs will mix up versions between clients. If no variant fits — 406 Not Acceptable.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: versioning via Accept (media-type versioning) looks elegant on paper but breaks simple testing from a browser/curl and confuses proxies. For a corporate service maintained partly by juniors, /v2/ in the URL is almost always more practical — pick Accept versioning only if you have strict HATEOAS/hypermedia discipline.","hook":"The same kebab, but Accept is \"mine on a flatbread, Russian-style, not spicy\". The kitchen (the URL) is one, the serving differs. Vary: Accept — so the waiter doesn't bring someone else's order from memory.","more":"- **Accept ≠ Content-Type**: Accept — what the client WANTS in the response; Content-Type — what's actually in the body (of the request or response).\n- **406 vs 415**: 406 — the server can't serve the requested Accept; 415 — the server doesn't understand the Content-Type of the sent body. A common mix-up in interviews.\n- **q-factors** rank preferences: `Accept: application/json;q=0.9, application/xml;q=0.5` — JSON is preferred.\n- **The version in the URL (/v2/) wins on simplicity**: visible in logs, easy to cache, easy to route. The version in Accept is \"more correct\" by REST theory, but costlier to operate — harder for a junior to debug.\n- Forget **Vary: Accept** — and the CDN will serve an XML client the previously cached JSON. A classic trap.","quizCorrect":"The server picks a representation by Accept and must set Vary: Accept so the cache doesn't mix versions","quizWrong":["Content negotiation only works for choosing between JSON and XML; language isn't part of it","On an Accept mismatch the server must return 415 Unsupported Media Type","Vary isn't needed because the response's Content-Type already uniquely identifies the variant for the cache"],"picCap":"one resource, different representations; the server picks by Accept"},"api-backward-compat":{"q":"What can you change in an API without breaking clients, and what's a breaking change? And what is expand-contract?","a":"Safe (additive): add a new optional parameter, a new field in the response, a new endpoint. A client that doesn't know about them just ignores them. Breaking: remove/rename a field, make an optional one required, change the type or semantics, tighten validation. Expand-contract (parallel change) is how you migrate without downtime: first you ADD the new alongside the old (expand), let clients move over, and only then REMOVE the old (contract).","d":"The key principle is the Robustness Principle (Postel): \"be strict in what you send, and tolerant in what you accept\". Clients should ignore unknown fields (tolerant reader), so adding fields doesn't break them. The server must not rely on the client sending exactly the expected set.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: \"adding a field is safe\" breaks if the client has a strict JSON schema with additionalProperties:false or a strict deserializer (e.g. old Jackson with FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES). Then even a new field = a 500 on the client. Always verify that consumers are tolerant readers before considering an additive change safe.","hook":"You're changing the rails under a moving train: first you lay a second track alongside (expand), switch the trains onto it, and only then remove the old one (contract). Cutting the old track right away — a wreck.","more":"- **Breaking almost always**: removing a field, renaming, changing the type (string→int), narrowing an enum, a new required field in the request, tightening validation, changing the response code/error semantics.\n- **Safe**: new optional fields in the response, new optional request parameters, new endpoints, new enum values in the RESPONSE (if the client is ready for unknowns).\n- **A new enum value — a gray area**: safe in a response only if clients don't do an exhaustive switch and have a default branch. Otherwise it breaks.\n- **A tolerant reader** on the client side is half the battle: don't fail on extra fields, don't require a strict order.\n- **Deprecation, not deletion**: mark a field deprecated (the `Deprecation`, `Sunset` headers), log who still uses it, and delete only when the traffic has dropped to zero.","quizCorrect":"Adding an optional field to the response is backward-compatible, provided clients ignore unknown fields","quizWrong":["Renaming a field is safe if the old name is mentioned in the documentation","Any change to the response schema is always breaking and requires a new major version of the API for consumers","Expand-contract means first removing the old field, then adding the new one"],"picCap":"expand: added alongside; pause for migration; contract: removed the old"},"api-rest-rpc-grpc-graphql":{"q":"REST, RPC, gRPC, GraphQL — what's the essence of each and when to choose which in practice?","a":"REST — resource-oriented: you operate on entities (/orders/42) with HTTP verbs; simple, cacheable, familiar to everyone. RPC — you call a remote procedure like a function (createOrder(...)); thinking in \"actions\" rather than \"resources\". gRPC — RPC over HTTP/2 with Protobuf: binary, fast, a typed contract, streaming — ideal for internal service-to-service. GraphQL — the client itself describes exactly which fields and relations it needs, in one request without over/under-fetching — strong for flexible front-ends with heterogeneous screens.","d":"A rough rule: REST — public/CRUD APIs and anything where cacheability and simplicity matter; gRPC — internal microservice communication with an emphasis on speed and a strict contract; GraphQL — when you have many front-ends needing different data slices and you're tired of multiplying endpoints. RPC \"in general\" is a family; gRPC is its specific, popular implementation.","note":"⚠️ Caveat: GraphQL easily turns into a performance trap — one \"pretty\" nested query spawns N+1 DB hits and bypasses the usual HTTP caches/rate limits. You need DataLoader batching, query depth/complexity limits, and well-thought-out field-level authorization. It's not \"REST, just nicer\" — it's a different class of operational problems.","hook":"REST — a cafe menu: you take a dish whole. RPC/gRPC — calling the kitchen \"do this action\". GraphQL — a buffet: you put on your plate exactly the fields you need, no more, no less.","more":"- **REST pros**: cacheability (HTTP cache, CDN), familiar to everyone, great for public APIs. Cons: over/under-fetching, N endpoints for N screens.\n- **gRPC pros**: binary protobuf (compact/fast), a strict contract and code generation, bidirectional streaming. Cons: lives poorly in the browser without grpc-web/a proxy, harder to debug by eye, no HTTP cache.\n- **GraphQL pros**: one request — exactly the fields you need, great for mobile and complex dashboards. Cons: caching is hard, N+1 on resolvers, authorization and rate-limiting at the field level — a separate pain.\n- **A practical approach**: between microservices — REST/gRPC depending on load; outward to clients — REST; introduce GraphQL only if you genuinely have a heterogeneous-front-end problem, not \"because it's trendy\".\n- **Don't choose by hype**: GraphQL and gRPC add infrastructure complexity (schema, codegen, gateway). If it's CRUD and a couple of clients — REST does the job and a junior can maintain it.","quizCorrect":"gRPC is RPC over HTTP/2 with Protobuf, usually for fast typed communication between internal services","quizWrong":["GraphQL replaces REST in all cases because it's always faster and caches better than any of the alternatives","REST uses binary Protobuf and HTTP/2, while gRPC runs JSON over HTTP/1.1","RPC and REST are the same thing; gRPC is just Google's new name for REST"],"picCap":"on the axis: public/cache ← REST … gRPC → speed/internal; GraphQL — front-end flexibility"},"gap-sc-structured-task-scope":{"q":"You fired off 3 parallel tasks via an ExecutorService; one of them fails — the other two keep grinding away for nothing. How does Structured Concurrency (Java 21 preview) fix this \"leaky\" scenario?","a":"StructuredTaskScope ties child tasks into a single tree: they start inside one scope and never outlive it. If one fails (ShutdownOnFailure) or one finishes first (ShutdownOnSuccess), the scope cancels the rest via interrupt. join() blocks the parent until every child completes, so no orphaned tasks are left behind.","d":"↳ Under the hood it's just a pool of virtual threads plus a cancellation tree. fork() spins up a vthread, join() waits for all of them, and scope.close() guarantees no child outlives the parent (like try-with-resources, but for concurrency).","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: after `scope.fork()` you MUST call `scope.join()` (or `joinUntil`) before reading results via `subtask.get()` — otherwise `get()` throws `IllegalStateException`, because the result isn't committed yet. And a scope can't be shared across threads: it's bound to the thread that created it (confinement). Children are cancelled via interrupt, so child tasks must react to InterruptedException properly, or ShutdownOnSuccess/Failure won't be able to wind them down quickly.","hook":"A team of climbers on a single rope: roped into one party, moving together. One slips — the belay yanks the rest, and the group doesn't scatter across the mountain alone. The leader (parent) doesn't leave the route until the whole party is gathered.","more":"- **StructuredTaskScope** — the core API: `fork()` spawns a child task (a subtask on a virtual thread), `join()` blocks the parent until all children finish, and `close()` (via try-with-resources) guarantees no child outlives the scope.\n- **ShutdownOnFailure** — \"all or nothing\": the first child to fail triggers cancellation of the rest; `throwIfFailed()` propagates the exception to the parent.\n- **ShutdownOnSuccess** — \"first one wins\": the first successful result ends the scope and cancels the competitors (the classic hedged-requests / replica-racing pattern).\n- **ScopedValue** — the ThreadLocal replacement for virtual threads: the value is **immutable**, set via `ScopedValue.where(KEY, val).run(...)` only for the duration of the block, and automatically **inherited** by the scope's child tasks.\n- ScopedValue is **cheap across millions of vthreads**: no per-thread map to clean up, no memory leaks like ThreadLocal — the value is bound to a dynamic scope, not to a thread.\n- This is a **preview** in Java 21 (needs `--enable-preview`); the API kept maturing in 22-23 and signatures changed by the final version — in prod, budget for adjustments.","quizCorrect":"ShutdownOnFailure cancels the remaining tasks and propagates the error; ShutdownOnSuccess ends the scope on the first success, cancelling the rest","quizWrong":["ShutdownOnFailure restarts the failed task, while ShutdownOnSuccess launches duplicates for reliability","Both policies wait for ALL tasks to finish and only then decide whether to cancel anything","ShutdownOnSuccess cancels a task on error, while ShutdownOnFailure does so on success (the names are inverted), so the two policies must be swapped in code"],"picCap":"ShutdownOnFailure: one child failing cancels the whole tree"},"gap-jwt-alg-none":{"q":"A client sent a JWT with the header alg=\"none\" and no signature. Why might a naive server accept it and let the request through under someone else's user_id?","a":"If the server picks the verification algorithm from the token itself (from its header), an attacker simply writes alg=\"none\", drops the signature — and the server \"verifies\" it under the rules of none, i.e. checks nothing. The second variant of the attack: the header is switched from RS256 to HS256 and the token is signed with HMAC, where the secret is the public RSA key (which isn't secret — it's publicly available). The server thinks \"RS256 → I'll verify with the public key,\" while the library decides from the header \"HS256 → I'll use that same key as the HMAC secret\" — and the signature checks out. The root cause is the same in both cases: the server trusts the alg field, which is controlled by the attacker.","d":"↳ Deeper: this is a special case of the confused deputy. Trusted code (the JWT validator) performs an action (choosing the algorithm/key) on the orders of an untrusted party (the token header) without being entitled to. The fix is to take the lever away from the attacker: the expected algorithm is set on the server by config, not read from the token.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: \"I'm on the latest jjwt, so I'm safe\" — not always. Parsing a token and verifying its signature are separate steps. If somewhere in the code there's `Jwts.parser().build().parse(token)` without `verifyWith(key)` (the old `parseClaimsJws` vs `parseClaimsJwt`), you can read the payload of an unsigned token and accidentally trust it further down the line. The signature must be verified explicitly, and its absence is an error, not an \"empty algorithm.\"","hook":"It's like a guard who admits people by their passes, but the way to check the pass is dictated by the person walking in. The guest says \"my pass doesn't need checking\" — and the guard dutifully doesn't check it. Fixed by one rule: the security team decides how to verify, not the guest.","more":"Why this is still around:\n- **alg=none** exists legitimately in the JWT standard (RFC 7519) — for already-secured channels. The trouble is that old libraries accepted it on verify by default.\n- **RS256→HS256 confusion** works because a single verify function took both the key and the token and chose the operation type from the header. The public RSA key isn't secret — the attacker grabs it from jwks.json and signs an HMAC with it.\n- **Defense 1:** on verification, pin a specific algorithm (`Algorithm.RSA256(pub, null)`), not \"whatever's in the token.\"\n- **Defense 2:** separate keys by purpose — a key meant for RS256 must never end up on a path where it can be used as an HMAC secret.\n- **Defense 3:** update your libraries — modern ones (java-jwt, jjwt, nimbus) no longer let none slip through and require an explicit algorithm.\n- **Also check** iss, aud, exp — verifying the signature is necessary but not sufficient.","quizCorrect":"Pin the expected algorithm in the server config and don't read the alg field from the token","quizWrong":["Extend the token's lifetime so it has to be reissued less often by the authorisation server itself","Encrypt the payload so the attacker can't see the user_id","Trust the alg from the header, but additionally log every login"],"picCap":"Top: the server trusts the alg from the token and lets the forgery through. Bottom: the algorithm and key are pinned on the server — the attack fails."},"gap-alg-floyd-cycle":{"q":"How do you find a cycle in a linked list (or in a sequence x→f(x)) using O(1) memory, without a HashSet of visited nodes?","a":"You launch two pointers: the slow one steps by 1, the fast one by 2. If a cycle exists, the fast pointer will sooner or later catch the slow one from inside the loop (they meet). If there's no cycle, the fast one simply hits null. O(1) memory, O(n) time.","d":"↳ To find the START of the cycle: after they meet, put one pointer back at head, leave the other at the meeting point, and move both by 1 step — they'll meet exactly at the cycle entrance. This follows from the fact that the distance from head to the entrance equals the distance from the meeting point to the entrance (modulo the cycle length).","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: the loop condition `while (fast != null && fast.next != null)` — the order of the checks matters. If you check `fast.next.next` without first checking `fast.next != null`, you'll get an NPE on an odd-length list with no cycle. And both pointers must start from head (not slow=head, fast=head.next), otherwise the cycle-start formula breaks.","hook":"Two runners on a circular track at different speeds: the fast one inevitably laps the slow one and catches up from behind.","more":"**Why the different speeds.** The hare's speed relative to the tortoise inside the cycle is exactly 1 step per iteration. So the gap between them shrinks by 1 every step and is bound to reach 0 — they can't skip past each other, because the step is discrete.\n\n- **Finding the start (theorem).** Let the distance from head to the cycle entrance be `m`, and the cycle length be `L`. By the time they meet, slow has walked `m + a` and fast has walked `2(m + a)`, with `m + a` trailing by a whole number of laps. From this it follows: if you walk from head and from the meeting point by 1 step each, you'll meet at the entrance.\n- **Cycle length.** After the first meeting, hold one pointer in place and walk the other around the cycle, counting steps until it returns — that gives you `L`.\n- **Where it's used.** Detecting a duplicate in a `1..n` array (LeetCode 287, where `nums[i]` is treated as an edge), spotting cycles in hash functions, detecting looping links/redirects.","quizCorrect":"The fast pointer laps the slow one and they meet at one of the nodes","quizWrong":["The pointers meet exactly at the cycle's entry node on their very first meeting","You need a HashSet of visited nodes, otherwise you can't detect the cycle in O(1) memory","If a cycle exists, the fast pointer hits null before the slow one"],"picCap":"The hare is twice as fast — it catches the tortoise inside the loop."},"gap-alg-quickselect":{"q":"You need to find the k-th order element (say, the median) in an unsorted array. Why bother with quickselect when you could just sort in O(n log n) and take nums[k]?","a":"Sorting does extra work — it orders the whole array, even though you only need one position. Quickselect uses partition like quicksort, but recurses ONLY into the half where k lies. This gives an average of O(n) instead of O(n log n), because the size of the part being processed drops geometrically: n + n/2 + n/4 + ... ≈ 2n.","d":"↳ After partition, the pivot lands on its final position `p`. If `p == k`, you've found it. If `k < p`, the answer is on the left, so you go left only. If `k > p`, you go right only. You throw away one of the two halves entirely, unlike quicksort, which sorts both.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: quickselect REORDERS the original array (partition mutates the input). If the array mustn't be touched, copy it up front, or you'll hand the caller a bug. And second: the k index is zero-based — the \"median\" is `k = n/2` (for odd n), whereas \"the k-th counting from one\" is `k-1`; off-by-one confusion here is a classic in interviews.","hook":"You're looking for a particular book on a shelf by its height: you grab a random one, split the shelf into \"shorter\" and \"taller,\" and dig only through the pile where yours could be — you never touch the other one.","more":"**Where the average O(n) comes from.** With a random pivot, the expected size of the remaining part is ≈ half. Total work: `n + n/2 + n/4 + ... = 2n = O(n)`. Sorting, by contrast, pays a `log n` factor for each of its levels, because it processes BOTH halves.\n\n- **Worst case O(n²).** If the pivot is the worst possible every time (a sorted array plus a naive choice of the boundary element), you trim 1 element at a time: `n + (n-1) + ... = O(n²)`. Cured by randomizing the pivot — then the worst case is unlikely.\n- **Median-of-medians (BFPRT).** A deterministic pivot choice that guarantees O(n) in the worst case: split into groups of 5, take the median of medians as the pivot. Rarely needed in practice — the constant is large, and randomization is usually enough.\n- **In-place.** Works in place, swapping array elements; extra memory is O(1) (iterative version) or O(log n) on the stack (recursive).\n- **Built-in in Java.** For medians/percentiles in prod it's often simpler to use `Arrays.sort` on small data, and streaming structures or a library `Quantiles` (Guava) on large data. A hand-rolled quickselect is for interviews and hot spots.","quizCorrect":"Quickselect recurses only into the half of the partition where the target k lies, which gives an average of O(n)","quizWrong":["Quickselect is always faster than sorting and guarantees O(n) even in the worst case for any input distribution it sees","Quickselect first fully sorts the array and then takes the element at index k","Quickselect needs O(n) extra memory for both halves after partition"],"picCap":"Recursion goes into just one half — that's why it's O(n) on average."},"gap-rx-context":{"q":"In a reactive chain the thread hops between operators. So how do you carry a trace_id or security context from subscribe() all the way to the source, if ThreadLocal doesn't work here?","a":"Through the Reactor Context — an immutable key-value store bound not to a thread but to the subscription itself (Subscription). It flows BOTTOM-UP: from subscribe() to the source. You put a value in via .contextWrite(...), and read it higher up the chain via Mono.deferContextual / Flux.deferContextual. ThreadLocal won't do, because an operator may run on a different thread, where the ThreadLocal value simply won't be visible.","d":"↳ \"Bottom-up\" — because in Reactor the chain is assembled top-down, but the actual subscription propagates from the bottom (from the final subscribe) up to the source. The Context is part of the Subscription, so it's visible to operators ABOVE the point where contextWrite was done, and not visible below it.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: the classic trap is putting .contextWrite() at the very end of the chain, hoping the operators written above it will see the value. That actually works (above = visible). But the reverse mistake is fatal: if deferContextual sits BELOW contextWrite (closer to subscribe), it WON'T see the value and will blow up on ctx.get() with NoSuchElementException. Rule: contextWrite must be BELOW (closer to subscribe) than the deferContextual that reads it.","hook":"It's like a relay run backwards: the anchor (subscribe) grabs the Context baton first and passes it back down the lane — to the runner at the start (the source). Whoever stands HIGHER up the lane gets the baton; whoever's below the handoff point doesn't.","more":"Key properties of the Reactor Context:\n- **Immutable**: each contextWrite creates a NEW Context, the old one is unchanged — hence the \"bottom-up\" direction and the isolation between subscriptions.\n- **Bound to the subscription, not the thread**: it survives a thread switch on publishOn/subscribeOn, unlike ThreadLocal.\n- **Read only via deferContextual** (or the deprecated Mono.subscriberContext): you can't \"fetch\" the Context synchronously outside a reactive operator.\n- **Visibility**: contextWrite affects operators ABOVE itself in the chain; anything below the write won't see that value.\n- For bridging with ThreadLocal (MDC, SecurityContext), Reactor 3.5+ has **ContextPropagation** plus the micrometer-context-propagation library — it automatically carries selected Context keys into ThreadLocal at operator boundaries.","quizCorrect":"The Context flows bottom-up (from subscribe to the source) and is read via deferContextual above the contextWrite point","quizWrong":["The Context flows top-down, like the regular data flow, and is visible to all operators below contextWrite","The Context is stored in a ThreadLocal and automatically carried between threads by the scheduler","The Context can be mutated via ctx.set(), and the change is immediately visible to all operators in the chain below it as well"],"picCap":"contextWrite puts a key in; the Context flows from subscribe() up to the source, so it's visible to operators ABOVE the write point."},"gap-ap-apt-rounds":{"q":"Lombok, MapStruct, Dagger all \"generate code.\" At what stage and how exactly does javac let them hook into the compilation?","a":"They're annotation processors (APT). Before javac turns your code into bytecode, it runs processing rounds: it parses the sources into a tree (AST), hands the processors the annotations they've \"declared,\" and in response the processors write NEW .java files via the Filer. The generated files trigger the next round — until nobody produces anything new. Only then does the actual compilation of everything together happen. That's why MapStruct hands you a ready-made MapperImpl, not runtime magic.","d":"A processor's contract: the class implements javax.annotation.processing.Processor (usually via AbstractProcessor), is annotated with @SupportedAnnotationTypes, and is registered in META-INF/services/javax.annotation.processing.Processor (or via @AutoService). javac finds it via the ServiceLoader on the classpath/processorpath.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: Lombok is the odd one out here. Standard APT WRITES new files, whereas Lombok EDITS the existing AST of your class (it inserts getters/setters/toString right into the User tree). There's no official API for mutating the AST in javac — Lombok reaches into the internal com.sun.tools.javac.* classes. That's why on new JDKs it regularly needs --add-opens/--add-exports to keep working, and in the IDE you need a separate plugin so the editor can see methods that physically aren't in the source.","hook":"A processor is a contractor that the foreman (javac) lets onto the site before the concrete is poured: it draws in the missing blueprints (sources), and only once there's nothing left to draw does everything get poured at once.","more":"Key constraints and pitfalls of APT:\n- A processor works at the level of the **model** (Element, TypeMirror) and the **AST** — it almost always only READS the existing code and WRITES new files, rather than editing old ones. This is by design: the standard API doesn't let you append to someone else's sources.\n- **Processor ordering** is non-deterministic: javac doesn't guarantee the order in which different processors run within a single round. If MapStruct needs to see Lombok's output (the getters Lombok \"draws in\"), you need `lombok-mapstruct-binding` — otherwise MapStruct won't yet see getName() in its round.\n- **Claimed annotations**: if Processor.process() returns true, it \"claims\" its annotations and other processors no longer see them. Almost always you should return false.\n- Several generations → several rounds, and in each you have access to a `RoundEnvironment` (what appeared in this round, whether it's the last one).\n- In Maven, processors are wired in automatically from the classpath, but in large projects they're moved into `annotationProcessorPaths`, so the processor isn't dragged into the runtime dependencies.","quizCorrect":"Processors run in rounds before compilation, generate new sources via the Filer, and the generated code triggers the next round","quizWrong":["Processors patch the bytecode after javac, reading annotations via reflection at runtime","javac calls a processor once and can't handle the files it generates","Annotations with RetentionPolicy.SOURCE are processed only by the classloader at JVM startup rather than by the compiler during the build"],"picCap":"APT spins through generation rounds BEFORE the actual compilation; an empty round = stop"},"gap-oh-direct-memory":{"q":"Where does memory allocated by ByteBuffer.allocateDirect live, and why isn't its release instantaneous?","a":"allocateDirect takes memory straight from the OS, OUTSIDE the Java heap: the GC neither moves nor scans it. The ByteBuffer object itself (a small wrapper) sits on the heap, with the large native buffer behind it. Release is deferred: the native memory is returned to the OS only once the GC collects the wrapper and its Cleaner/PhantomReference fires. No references to the wrapper and no GC — and the memory just hangs around.","d":"↳ Why off-heap at all? Zero-copy: for read/write through a socket or file, the kernel needs a buffer at a fixed address. The GC can move a heap buffer, so the JDK first copies it into a hidden direct buffer. allocateDirect removes this extra copy — the data is already where the native call expects it. That's why NIO, Netty, and Kafka run on direct buffers.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: the classic leak is a short-lived direct buffer inside a long-lived object. The wrapper outlives its actual usefulness, the GC never gets to it, and the native memory quietly grows. The process's RSS swells while the heap dump is clean — monitor direct memory specifically (BufferPoolMXBean / NMT), not just the heap. And don't allocateDirect for small/frequent buffers: allocation is pricier than heap and cleanup is non-deterministic — pool them or use heap.","hook":"A direct buffer is like a storage locker outside your apartment. In the apartment (heap) only the tag-key hangs. As long as the key sits in your pocket, nobody frees the locker — even if you stopped needing the junk inside long ago.","more":"Key pitfalls of direct memory.\n- **Separate limit:** the size of direct memory is bounded by the flag **-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize**. By default it's roughly equal to **-Xmx**, but it's not the heap — it's a second, independent budget.\n- **Its own OOM:** exceeding the limit throws **OutOfMemoryError: Direct buffer memory**, not the usual heap OOM. The heap dump in that case is often nearly empty — the buffer isn't on the heap.\n- **Release tied to GC:** native memory is returned only once the collector reaps the wrapper. If the old gen isn't under pressure, a Full GC isn't called for a long time, and direct memory piles up even though the heap is free.\n- **System.gc() and -XX:+DisableExplicitGC:** as a last resort, the direct-buffer allocator triggers System.gc() to provoke the Cleaner. If **-XX:+DisableExplicitGC** is set, that lifeline is off — the OOM arrives sooner.\n- **Java 9+:** the Cleaner based on sun.misc.Cleaner was replaced with java.lang.ref.Cleaner (PhantomReference + a background thread), but the essence is the same — allocateDirect has no deterministic free().\n- **That's why they pool:** Netty and others reuse direct buffers (PooledByteBufAllocator) instead of constant allocate/free, to avoid running into the deferred cleanup.","quizCorrect":"Native memory outside the Java heap; the GC doesn't move it and releases it lazily via the Cleaner, once it collects the ByteBuffer wrapper.","quizWrong":["Memory in the Java heap in a separate generation; free() is called immediately when the buffer goes out of scope. Nothing else is required from you at all.","Memory on the thread's stack; released instantly on method return, like local variables.","Memory in Metaspace together with classes; cleaned only on full classloader unload."],"picCap":"A small wrapper on the heap holds a large native buffer off-heap; it's freed via the Cleaner once the GC collects the wrapper."},"gap-oom-oomkilled-vs-javaoom":{"q":"A pod in Kubernetes dies with exit code 137 and status OOMKilled, but there's not a single OutOfMemoryError in the application logs. Who killed whom, and why didn't the JVM \"notice\"?","a":"The container was killed by the OS kernel (cgroup memory limit), not by the JVM. The kernel saw the process exceed the memory.limit set in the cgroup and sent SIGKILL (kill -9) — hence exit code 128+9=137 and the OOMKilled status. Java OOM has nothing to do with it: this is death from outside the process, and the JVM had no chance to throw an exception or write a log.","d":"↳ Before Java 10, the JVM didn't read the cgroup limit at all: Runtime.maxMemory() and the ergonomics took the RAM of the whole host (say 64 GB), not the container limit (say 512 MB). The JVM happily grew the heap up to \"its\" boundary, exceeded the cgroup limit — and caught a kill -9 before any Java OOM.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: `MaxRAMPercentage` controls ONLY the heap. If you set it to 90-100%, the heap eats almost the whole limit, while metaspace, thread stacks (about ~1 MB per thread!), and direct memory go on top of that — and the kernel hands out another 137, even though Java OOM never happens. The real trap: an app with a pool of 200 threads quietly spends ~200 MB outside the heap. Keep MaxRAMPercentage around 70-75% and monitor the container's RSS, not just heap usage.","hook":"It's like a bouncer at a club with a fire-safety capacity limit: when there are more people than allowed, he doesn't ask and doesn't warn — he just throws you out the door. Inside the club nobody announced \"we're full\" (no Java OOM), the decision was made outside (the kernel).","more":"**OOMKilled (137) vs OutOfMemoryError** — different worlds:\n- **OOMKilled** — the kernel sent SIGKILL to a process that exceeded the cgroup `memory.limit`. Exit code 137, the status shows in `kubectl describe`, and the app logs are silent. Cured by memory limits, not try/catch.\n- **OutOfMemoryError** — the JVM itself failed to allocate an object in the heap (or metaspace), throws `java.lang.OutOfMemoryError`, with a stack trace and (if configured) a heap dump. This is an event inside the process.\n- **Fix as of Java 10+:** `-XX:+UseContainerSupport` is on by default — the JVM reads the cgroup limit. Instead of a fixed `-Xmx`, use `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75.0`: the heap scales from the container limit, and when limits change you don't have to edit flags.\n- **Don't forget non-heap:** the process RSS = heap + metaspace + thread stacks (each thread ~1 MB) + direct/native ByteBuffer + JIT code. MaxRAMPercentage limits only the heap, which is why you can't set 100% — leave headroom for non-heap, or you'll catch another 137 with a formally \"not overflowing\" heap.","quizCorrect":"Exit code 137 = SIGKILL from the kernel over the cgroup limit; this is the process dying from outside, so there's no OutOfMemoryError in the logs","quizWrong":["137 is exactly the Java OutOfMemoryError code, the stack trace just got lost when the container restarted and was never written into the log file at all","OOMKilled means the heap overflowed, and you just need to raise -Xmx — it has no effect on the cgroup","Code 137 is emitted by Quarkus itself on graceful shutdown; the kernel has nothing to do with it"],"picCap":"The kernel kills the process from outside over the cgroup limit — the JVM doesn't throw OutOfMemoryError"},"gap-kms-envelope-encryption":{"q":"You have gigabytes of encrypted data. Tomorrow you need to rotate the master key. Do you really have to decrypt it all and encrypt it again?","a":"No, and that's exactly why envelope encryption exists. The data is encrypted with a fast data-key, and the data-key itself is encrypted with a master key in the KMS. On master rotation you re-encrypt only the tiny data-key, leaving the gigabytes of data untouched. The secrets and the master key live in a KMS/Vault/HSM, not in the code and not in git.","d":"↳ The encrypted data-key is stored next to the data (in the same record/file). To decrypt the data, the application sends the encrypted data-key to the KMS, the KMS returns the decrypted data-key in memory — it's used right away and discarded, never written to disk. This is the \"sealed envelope\" pattern: inside is the letter (the data-key), outside is the KMS lock.","note":"⚠️ Gotcha: the decrypted (plaintext) data-key must not be logged, cached for long, or written to disk — that defeats the whole scheme. Use it in memory and null the reference right away. And the second trap: when rotating the master, don't delete the old key version in the KMS right away — as long as even one data-key is sealed by it, deleting that version = irreversible data loss.","hook":"A safe in a bank: inside is your apartment key (data-key), outside is the bank's lock (the master in the KMS). Changing the bank's lock is easy, and you don't have to re-key every apartment.","more":"- **Why two levels of keys.** Symmetrically encrypting gigabytes with the master directly is awkward: the master sits in the KMS, and routing all the traffic through it is expensive and slow. So the **data-key** encrypts the data locally (fast), and the KMS encrypts only the data-key itself.\n- **Key versions.** The KMS doesn't delete the old master on rotation — it keeps **versions**. An encrypted data-key remembers which version sealed it, and on decrypt the KMS picks the right one itself. So new data is encrypted with the new version, while old data is still readable with the old one.\n- **Regular rotation.** The master is rotated on a schedule (say once a year) or per incident. Envelope encryption makes this cheap — only the thin data-key layer is rewritten, and with lazy re-wrapping even that can be done gradually.\n- **HSM.** A Hardware Security Module stores the key so that it physically **can't be extracted** — you send data into the device, it encrypts/decrypts inside and returns the result. The key never leaves the chip. Under the hood, a KMS often relies on exactly this — an HSM.\n- **Secrets not in git.** Neither the master key, nor passwords, nor the data-key in plaintext are ever committed. In the code there's only a call to the KMS/Vault by a key identifier (alias/path); the key itself stays behind the perimeter.","quizCorrect":"Re-encrypt only the data-key with the new master key — the data itself is left untouched","quizWrong":["Decrypt all the data with the old key and encrypt it again with the new one in place afterwards","Copy the old master key out of the KMS into the code and swap it there","Nothing — rotating the master automatically re-signs all the data on disk"],"picCap":"Master rotation = re-encrypt the tiny data-key, not the gigabytes of data"},"de-server-lifecycle":{"q":"What physically happens on a server when an HTTP request arrives?","a":"The request is accepted on the listening socket (accept), then picked up by a thread from the pool (thread-per-request) or by an event loop, parsed; business logic runs; the response is serialized and written back to the socket. A server = a process listening on a port; \"scaling the server\" = adding processes/machines behind a load balancer.","d":"A stateless server (state in external storage/token) scales horizontally without sticky sessions.","note":"People confuse \"server\" with \"machine\". One machine runs many server processes; one logical service spans many machines. The bottleneck is usually not CPU but the number of threads/connections (stack memory + context switches) — hence event loops for c10k. Keep state in process memory (HttpSession on heap) and you lose horizontal scaling: a request must hit \"its\" instance (sticky), and if that instance dies, the session is gone.","hook":"A server is a waiter at the door (the port). The bell (request) rings → the waiter (a thread from the pool) takes it → the kitchen (logic) → brings the dish (response). More guests — more waiters and dining halls (machines).","more":"**A server is a process listening on a port**, not \"hardware\". Request flow: TCP handshake → `accept()` on the listening socket → request handed to a **thread from the pool** (Tomcat model: thread-per-request) or to an **event loop** (Netty/Vert.x, one thread driving thousands of connections) → parse → business logic → serialize → write response → keep-alive holds the connection for the next request.\n\n- The bottleneck is usually not CPU but the **number of threads/connections**: thread-per-request tops out around a couple thousand threads, so high concurrency uses an event loop.\n- **Stateless** = state lives not in process memory but in external storage/JWT → any instance behind the balancer serves any request → horizontal scaling.","quizCorrect":"A process listening on a port; the request is accepted on the socket and handled by a thread/event loop","quizWrong":["A physical machine that can't be duplicated","An object in the JVM created fresh for each request and destroyed as soon as the response has been sent back","A database holding the state of all clients"],"picCap":"Socket → accept → thread from pool → handler → response; state outside = stateless"},"inf-autoscale-mechanics":{"q":"How does autoscaling work, and on what metric?","a":"A controller (e.g. K8s HPA) periodically compares the observed metric to the target and computes the desired replica count: desired = ceil(replicas × current/target). The metric can be CPU, RPS, or an application one (queue depth). Over target — it adds pods; below — it removes them (slower, with a stabilization window against flapping).","d":"Reactive autoscaling always lags by the \"metric→decision→pod start\" delay; for predictable spikes use scheduled/predictive.","note":"CPU as a metric lies for I/O-bound services: the thread is parked on a DB response, CPU is low — HPA won't add pods even though latency is already rising. Scale on RPS or queue depth instead. And always account for the lag: the metric is averaged (~15s) + decision + pod cold start = tens of seconds; a short spike can pass before pods come up — which is why you keep baseline replicas with headroom.","hook":"A thermostat: hot (metric↑) — turn on another AC unit (pod); cooled down — turn it off, but carefully so you don't toggle back and forth (flapping).","more":"**HPA formula**: `desired = ceil(currentReplicas × currentMetric / targetMetric)`. The metrics-server reports values; the controller recomputes every ~15s.\n\n- **The metric matters**: CPU is convenient but lies for I/O-bound services (a thread waits on the DB — CPU is low while latency climbs). For those, use **RPS** or **queue depth** (custom/external metric).\n- **Asymmetry**: scale **out** aggressively (load is burning now), scale **in** cautiously with a stabilization window (`stabilizationWindowSeconds`), otherwise it flaps.\n- Reactive HPA lags; for known spikes (market open, a blast email) use **scheduled** (a CronJob on replicas) or **predictive**.","quizCorrect":"desired = ceil(replicas × observed/target), compared to the target periodically","quizWrong":["Adds exactly one pod every second under load","Scales by the application's disk usage","Knows future load in advance and pre-provisions pods ahead of the forecast peak arriving"],"picCap":"HPA: desired = ceil(replicas × current/target); up fast, down cautiously"},"inf-autoscale-coldstart":{"q":"Why can scaling up under load make things worse (cold start, thundering herd)?","a":"A new instance isn't ready instantly: container startup + JVM/JIT warmup + cache filling + connection-pool ramp. While it's \"cold\", its p99 is high, yet the LB already pours traffic at it. On top of that, a burst of new pods hits the DB/cache all at once (thundering herd) and can topple the downstream.","d":"Fixed with a readiness probe + warmup traffic + a scale-rate limit + warm baseline replicas.","note":"The readiness probe is your main safeguard: while a pod isn't warm, don't mark it Ready, and the balancer won't route traffic to it. On the JVM, cold start hurts especially (interpret → C1 → C2), which is why Quarkus native / GraalVM AOT is no hype but a real answer for autoscaling: startup in ms instead of seconds of warmup. Liveness ≠ readiness: liveness will restart a live-but-cold pod and only make it worse.","hook":"A cold engine: start it and floor it in a race — it stalls. Without warmup, new \"helpers\" only get in the way: slow themselves and choke the DB.","more":"**Pod cold start**: the container starts → the JVM loads classes → code runs interpreted, then C1, then C2-compiled (peak form is seconds/minutes away) → caches are empty (cache-miss storm) → the connection pool fills lazily. During this window p99 is high.\n\n- **Readiness probe** is the key safeguard: until Ready, the LB sends no traffic.\n- **Thundering herd**: HPA spins up 10 pods at once → 10× new connections and cache misses against the DB/Redis → the downstream can fall over. Fixed by limiting scale-up rate and by warmup.\n- **The JVM pain is solved by native**: Quarkus native / GraalVM AOT start in milliseconds at peak form — a direct argument for native under autoscaling/serverless.","quizCorrect":"A new pod is cold (JIT/caches/pools) and immediately catches traffic + a burst of pods hits the downstream","quizWrong":["Kubernetes always starts pods instantly and warm","Cold start affects only serverless, not pods","A new pod automatically gets a copy of a neighbor's cache so the first requests are served at full speed right away"],"picCap":"A cold pod = high p99 + a burst on the downstream; saved by readiness + warmup"},"de-estimate-latency":{"q":"The orders of magnitude of latency you should remember for back-of-the-envelope math?","a":"L1 ~1ns, RAM ~100ns, SSD random read ~100µs, round-trip within a DC ~0.5ms, read 1MB from SSD ~1ms, HDD seek ~10ms, cross-continent round-trip ~100-150ms. What matters is the relative scale: RAM is ~1000× faster than SSD, cross-region network is ~1000× slower than within a DC.","d":"Don't memorize exact numbers — keep the orders and ratios, that's enough for estimation.","note":"These numbers are a design tool, not interview trivia. The main takeaway: one cross-region round-trip costs more than hundreds of local operations, so an N+1 over the network between regions kills latency, while an N+1 to a local DB is merely unpleasant. And mind the tail: the average lies — the client feels p99, which under a fan-out into N services becomes worst-of-N and grows.","hook":"A ladder of scales: ns (cache) → µs (RAM/SSD) → ms (disk/DC network) → 100ms (another continent). Each rung ×~1000 — and it's instantly clear what's expensive.","more":"The classic — \"Latency numbers every programmer should know\" (Jeff Dean). Why keep them in your head: to see where the bottleneck is **before** writing code.\n\nKey orders: L1 ~1ns · branch mispredict ~5ns · mutex lock/unlock ~25ns · RAM ~100ns · compress 1KB ~3µs · SSD random ~100µs · round-trip in a DC ~0.5ms · read 1MB from RAM ~100µs, from SSD ~1ms · HDD seek ~10ms · CA↔Netherlands round-trip ~150ms.\n\nDesign consequences: cache in RAM ≈ ×1000 vs disk → cache the hot path; a cross-region round-trip ≈ 300 local disk reads → don't make a chatty protocol between regions; sequential disk reads are far faster than random → hence LSM trees and batching.","quizCorrect":"RAM ~100ns, SSD ~100µs, round-trip in a DC ~0.5ms, cross-continent ~100ms","quizWrong":["SSD is faster than RAM on random reads","Round-trip within a DC is ~100ms","Reading from RAM and from an HDD take comparable time on modern hardware these days"],"picCap":"A latency ladder: ns→µs→ms→100ms, each rung ×~1000"},"de-estimate-qps":{"q":"How do you estimate QPS and storage for a service on the back of an envelope?","a":"QPS ≈ DAU × actions_per_user / 86400 (seconds per day). Peak ×2–5 of the average. Storage = object_count × object_size × retention (+ replication ×3, + indexes). Count in powers of two/ten and round — you need the right order of magnitude, not precision.","d":"86400 ≈ 10⁵ — handy: 1 million actions per day ≈ 12 QPS average.","note":"The goal of the estimate is not an exact number but the architectural call \"one box or a data center\". A common mistake: estimate by the average QPS and forget the peak factor (evening traffic, a blast, a flash mob) — the system falls over precisely at the peak, which is several times the average. Always: average → peak factor → headroom. And estimate bandwidth separately (QPS × response size) — sometimes you hit the network, not CPU/disk.","hook":"Divide daily actions by ~100k seconds — the average QPS. Multiply by the peak factor — that's the real load, the one the system actually falls over at.","more":"**Example**: 100M DAU, 10 actions/user → 1B requests/day. ÷86400 ≈ **11.6k average QPS**, peak ×3 ≈ **35k QPS**. Storage: 1B records × 1KB = 1TB/period, ×3 replication = 3TB, + indexes.\n\n- Count in **powers of two**: 1KB=2¹⁰, 1M=2²⁰, 1B≈2³⁰; and remember 86400 ≈ 10⁵.\n- **Why**: to check whether it fits one machine or needs sharding/CDN/cache. Precision isn't the goal — the \"one server or a cluster\" decision is.\n- **Read/write asymmetry**: reads are usually 10–100× writes → hence read replicas and cache.","quizCorrect":"QPS ≈ DAU×actions/86400, peak ×2–5; storage = objects×size×retention×replication","quizWrong":["QPS = DAU directly, without dividing by time or the peak-to-average ratio of the traffic","Storage doesn't depend on retention","Peak QPS always equals the average"],"picCap":"DAU×actions ÷86400 = average QPS; ×peak factor; storage separately"},"di-leader-raft":{"q":"How does leader election work in Raft?","a":"Time is split into terms. A follower that gets no heartbeat from the leader within a randomized election timeout becomes a candidate, increments the term, and sends RequestVote. Whoever collects a majority (quorum N/2+1) becomes leader and sends heartbeats. The randomized timeout spreads candidates out in time so there's no perpetual split-vote.","d":"A majority guarantees a unique leader per term: two candidates can't both reach quorum at once.","note":"Majority quorum dictates an **odd** cluster size: 3 nodes survive 1 failure, 5 survive 2. An even number (4) gives the same fault tolerance as 3 but costs more and risks a tie. The key property: a leader doesn't commit without replicating to a majority — so on a network split the minority freezes (a CP choice under CAP) rather than producing a second leader. A vote is granted only to a not-behind log — otherwise you could lose committed entries.","hook":"An election by alarm clock: whoever wakes first (a randomized timer) asks for votes; got more than half — president for a term, sending \"I'm alive\" heartbeats.","more":"**Raft election**: `term` is a logical clock (monotonically increasing). A follower with no heartbeat within the timeout → candidate, `term++`, votes for itself, sends `RequestVote`. A node grants its vote if it hasn't voted yet this term and the candidate's log is **not behind** (safety: a node with a stale log won't be elected).\n\n- Got a **majority** → leader, sends `AppendEntries` (which doubles as the heartbeat).\n- **Randomized timeout** (150–300ms) spreads candidates apart → split-vote is rare; if it happens — a new term and re-election.\n- etcd, Consul use Raft; ZooKeeper uses the related ZAB. This is how a leader is elected in Kafka KRaft, DB shards.","quizCorrect":"A candidate collects a majority of votes (N/2+1) for the term; the randomized timeout dampens split-vote","quizWrong":["The leader is the node with the highest IP address","All nodes vote unanimously, or there's no election","The leader is chosen by an external coordinator forever, and it never changes for the lifetime of the cluster"],"picCap":"Raft: timeout→candidate→RequestVote→majority→leader→heartbeats"},"di-split-brain-fencing":{"q":"What is split-brain, and how does a fencing token protect against an old leader?","a":"Split-brain is a network partition where two parts of the cluster each consider themselves the leader and both write → data diverges. It's fixed with a quorum (only a majority may write) + a fencing token: the leader gets a monotonically increasing number, and storage rejects a write whose token is below the highest already seen. A \"resurrected\" old leader with a stale token is rejected.","d":"A time lease without fencing doesn't save you: a GC pause / VM freeze can outlive the lease unnoticed by the leader itself.","note":"The main trap is trusting time: \"I hold a 10s lease, I'm the leader\" → the process stalls in a 15s GC pause, the lease expires, a new leader is elected; the old one wakes, still \"the leader\", and writes — but its token is stale and rejected. Clocks and timeouts are unreliable (NTP jumps, pauses, VM freezes) — trust only a monotonic fencing counter on the resource side. The quorum prevents two active leaders, fencing finishes off a zombie that still thinks it's alive.","hook":"Two captains on a ship — disaster. We hand out numbered orders (the token): the warehouse executes only an order with a number higher than the last; the old captain with order #7 after a new #8 is rejected.","more":"**Split-brain**: on a network partition both halves \"think\" they're the leader and both write → data diverges, the conflict can't be resolved.\n\n- A **quorum** prevents two nodes being active leader at once (only one majority exists).\n- A **fencing token** (Kleppmann, DDIA) is protection **on the resource side**: a lock service (ZooKeeper `zxid`) issues a monotonic number; storage keeps the max seen and **rejects** a write with a smaller one. The classic case: a leader goes into a **15s GC pause**, the lease expires, a new one is elected; the old wakes, still \"leader\", and writes — but its token is stale and rejected.\n- The hardware analog is **STONITH** (Shoot The Other Node In The Head) in HA clusters.","quizCorrect":"Two nodes each think they're the leader and write; a fencing token (monotonic) — storage rejects a write with an older number","quizWrong":["Split-brain is when the leader uses two CPU cores, and the two halves of the cluster keep serving writes without ever noticing each other at all","A fencing token is a cluster access password","A time lease is enough, fencing isn't needed"],"picCap":"Fencing token: storage rejects a write with an older number — the zombie leader is dropped"},"di-batch-vs-stream":{"q":"Batch vs stream processing of big data — what's the difference, and what are Lambda/Kappa?","a":"Batch (MapReduce/Spark) — accumulate data and compute in large chunks: high throughput, latency in minutes/hours, easy to recompute. Stream (Flink/Kafka Streams) — process event by event in near real time: low latency, but harder state/correctness. Lambda architecture keeps both layers (batch for accuracy + stream for freshness); Kappa keeps only stream with log replay.","d":"Kappa is simpler to operate (one code path) but requires being able to replay the whole needed volume from the log.","note":"Don't confuse the **Lambda architecture** (batch+stream layers) with **AWS Lambda** (FaaS) — same name, different things, a classic interview trap. The main stream nuance is **event time vs processing time**: an event happened at 10:00 but arrived at 10:05; correct windowed aggregates use event-time with a watermark, otherwise late events are lost or fall into the wrong window. And remember: a \"real-time\" stream is costlier to operate (state, exactly-once) — reach for it only when freshness is truly needed.","hook":"Batch — a full load of laundry once a day (a lot, but you wait). Stream — washing each item right under the tap (fast, but small and harder).","more":"**Batch**: MapReduce (map → shuffle → reduce), Spark (in-memory DAG, RDD/DataFrame). High throughput, easy to recompute a period, latency minutes/hours.\n\n**Stream**: Flink/Kafka Streams — event by event. Subtleties: **event-time vs processing-time**, **watermarks** for late events, **exactly-once** via state checkpoints.\n\n**Architectures**:\n- **Lambda** (Marz): a batch layer (accurate, slow) + a speed layer (approximate, fresh) + a serving layer that merges them. Downside — two code paths.\n- **Kappa** (Kreps): stream only, history = replaying the log (Kafka). Simpler, but you need enough retention/volume for replay.","quizCorrect":"Batch — large chunks, high throughput, latency in minutes; stream — event by event, low latency","quizWrong":["Stream is always more accurate and cheaper than batch for any kind of aggregation you might want to run","Batch processes one event at a time in real time","Lambda architecture is about AWS Lambda functions"],"picCap":"Batch — in chunks (throughput); stream — event by event (low latency)"},"di-oltp-vs-olap":{"q":"OLTP vs OLAP, data lake vs warehouse, and why columnar storage?","a":"OLTP — many small transactions (insert an order, update a balance), row storage, normalization, low latency on writes/point reads. OLAP — analytics over millions of rows (sum/group by columns), columnar storage (read only the needed columns and compress them). A warehouse holds cleaned structured data (schema-on-write); a lake holds raw data of any format (schema-on-read).","d":"A columnar format (Parquet/ORC) reads an order of magnitude less from disk for analytics: only the requested columns.","note":"A common mistake is running heavy analytics (`GROUP BY` over a whole table) straight against the production OLTP database: it locks, bloats buffers, and hurts transaction latency. Move analytics to OLAP/a replica/a warehouse via CDC or ETL. The reverse is also true: you can't run a columnar DB as OLTP — an `UPDATE` of a single row in columnar is brutally expensive. Rule: OLTP writes, OLAP reads aggregates; between them a pipeline, not one instance for everything.","hook":"OLTP — the cash register (quickly ring up one receipt). OLAP — accounting at month end (reconcile millions of receipts by category). The register works by rows, accounting by columns.","more":"**OLTP** (Postgres/MySQL): a row store — a row sits together contiguously → fast to insert/read one record. Normalization, ACID, indexes for point access.\n\n**OLAP** (ClickHouse, Parquet, BigQuery): a **column store** — values of one column sit contiguously → analytics reads only the needed columns and **compresses better** (similar data adjacent). But a point insert/update of one row is expensive.\n\n**Stores**:\n- **Warehouse** — structured, cleaned data for analytics, **schema-on-write** (ETL).\n- **Lake** — raw data of any format (S3/HDFS), **schema-on-read** (ELT).\n- **Lakehouse** (Delta/Iceberg) — a hybrid: a cheap lake + the transactions/schema of a warehouse.","quizCorrect":"OLTP — small transactions, rows; OLAP — analytics by columns, columnar storage","quizWrong":["OLAP is optimized for frequent point inserts","Columnar storage is faster for writing one row and for point lookups by key as well","A data lake holds only cleaned structured data"],"picCap":"OLTP — small transactions over rows; OLAP — analytics over columns"},"de-sysdesign-framework":{"q":"How do you approach any System Design problem in an interview (a framework)?","a":"1) Clarify requirements: functional + non-functional (scale, latency, consistency, availability). 2) Estimate on the envelope (DAU→QPS, storage). 3) Describe the API. 4) Data model and storage choice. 5) High-level diagram (clients→LB→services→DB/cache/queue). 6) Deep-dive into 1–2 bottlenecks. 7) Talk through trade-offs and failure points. Don't jump into details before scoping.","d":"The senior signal — requirements and estimation first, solution second; and explicit trade-offs, not a silver bullet.","note":"Two main interview mistakes: (1) jumping into technologies before requirements — \"let's use Kafka and Cassandra\" before the scale is clear; (2) not voicing trade-offs — a senior always says \"I chose X, the downside is Y, because Z matters more for us\". A silent \"perfect\" design reads as junior. Think out loud, keep a dialogue with the interviewer, and name your design's weak spots yourself before they do.","hook":"Don't build a house without a spec: ask → estimate → sketch the frame → dig into the foundation → name the risks. Order, not a chaos of technologies.","more":"**The framework** (RESHADED/analog):\n1. **Requirements** — functional (\"what it does\") + **non-functional** (scale, p99, consistency, availability, budget). Always ask for numbers: scale changes the design.\n2. **Back-of-the-envelope** — QPS, storage, bandwidth → one machine or a cluster.\n3. **API** — endpoints/contracts.\n4. **Data model + storage choice** (SQL/NoSQL, and why).\n5. **High-level** — clients → LB → services → DB/cache/queue/CDN.\n6. **Deep dive** into 1–2 **bottlenecks** (sharding, cache, queue, hot key).\n7. **Trade-offs + SPOF** — what you chose, what it costs, where the failure points are.\n\nThe interviewer evaluates your **structure of thinking**, not a single \"right\" answer.","quizCorrect":"Requirements → load estimation → API/data → high-level → deep-dive into bottlenecks → trade-offs","quizWrong":["Drawing microservices and databases right away without clarifying requirements with the interviewer","Picking the trendiest stack and justifying everything with it","Listing every technology you know without a diagram"],"picCap":"Requirements→estimate→API→data→diagram→deep-dive→trade-offs"},"jc-pass-by-value":{"q":"Java — is it pass-by-value or pass-by-reference? Why does a method that reassigns an object argument NOT change the outer variable, while calling a setter DOES?","a":"Java is always strictly pass-by-value, no exceptions. For reference types, the value of the reference (the address-pointer to the heap object) is copied — not the object itself. Reassigning `arg = new X()` inside the method changes only the local copy of the reference; the outer variable still points at the old object. But `arg.setField(...)` follows the copied reference to the same heap object and mutates its state — so the change is visible outside.","d":"Primitives are passed as a direct copy of the value; the 'object' is never passed at all — only a copy of its 32/64-bit reference is.","note":"⚠️ Real trap: a method taking a `List`/`Map`/array and calling `param.clear()` or `param.add()` silently mutates the caller's collection — a frequent bug source in legacy code (especially when the collection is shared across threads or cached). Defense: pass `List.copyOf(...)`/a defensive copy, or accept an immutable collection. Conversely: if you think `param = Collections.emptyList()` inside the method will 'clear' the outer list — no, it's a no-op for the caller.","hook":"You gave a friend a photocopy of your apartment key. He can come in and rearrange the furniture (setter — you see it). But if he throws away the copy and buys a key to a different apartment (reassign), your apartment and your key are untouched.","more":"**Precise model.** In Java an argument is always a copy of the bits of the variable's value. For `int` it's a copy of the number; for an object it's a copy of the reference (essentially a copy of the pointer to a heap cell). The object itself is never placed on the call stack.\n\n**Why setter works but reassign doesn't:**\n- `void f(StringBuilder sb) { sb.append(\"x\"); }` — the copy of `sb` points at the same object; `append` mutates the shared heap structure → visible outside.\n- `void f(StringBuilder sb) { sb = new StringBuilder(); }` — only the local copy of the reference is redirected; the outer reference is unchanged.\n\n**Classic gotcha — swap.** A `swap(a, b)` method that swaps via parameter assignment simply cannot work in Java — it only swaps the local copies of the references. You can only swap *contents* (e.g. via an array/wrapper), never the outer variables themselves.\n\n**Subtlety with `String` and wrappers.** `String`, `Integer`, `Long` are immutable — they have no setters; any 'change' creates a new object and rebinds the local copy. So a string passed into a method can't be changed from outside — often mistaken as 'proof of pass-by-reference in reverse', though the cause is immutability, not passing semantics.\n\n**Formal terminology (Java Language Specification, §8.4.1):** parameters are local variables initialized from argument values via assignment conversion. The JLS has no notion of 'passing a variable'.","quizCorrect":"Always pass-by-value: for objects the value of the reference is copied, so mutating the shared object is visible, but reassigning the copy is not","quizWrong":["Pass-by-reference for objects and pass-by-value for primitives — so objects can be reassigned outward","Pass-by-value, but a copy of the object itself is passed (deep copy), so a setter isn't visible outside of the method that made the change to it to the field","Depends on the modifier: final arguments are passed by value, ordinary ones by reference"],"picCap":"The reference is copied, not the object: a setter reaches the shared object, while reassign breaks only the local copy"},"jc-mutable-key":{"q":"You put an object into a HashMap as a key, then changed a field that participates in hashCode/equals. Why does get() now return null, even though equals and hashCode are overridden correctly?","a":"On put() HashMap computes the key's hashCode and stores the Node in the bucket at index `(n-1) & hash`. After the field is mutated the hashCode changes, so get() searches a DIFFERENT bucket — it never reaches the old Node, and equals is never even called. The entry is orphaned: it physically remains in the table but is unreachable by the new key state. The HashMap contract requires keys to be effectively immutable in the fields used by hashCode/equals.","d":"The hash isn't recomputed on the fly: HashMap caches the `hash` field inside the Node at insertion time, so the original bucket still remembers the old hash while the lookup index is derived from the new one — desync is guaranteed.","note":"⚠️ The nastiest form: even HashSet.remove(theSameObject) won't delete a mutated element — remove searches the new hash in a different bucket and finds nothing. The element stays in the set forever (visible via size()) but is unreachable — a real memory leak in long-lived collections. Especially dangerous with JPA entities in a Set: equals/hashCode over mutable fields like an id assigned by the DB after persist breaks the Set right after flush.","hook":"The key is the address written on an envelope. You drop the letter into slot 'house 7', then scratch the address to 'house 3' right on the envelope — the postman goes to house 3, finds it empty, while the letter sits in slot 7 with no way left to retrieve it.","more":"**Step by step:**\n- `put(k, v)`: HashMap computes `hash = hash(k.hashCode())`, caches it in `Node.hash`, bucket index = `(n-1) & hash`. The Node lands in, say, bucket[7].\n- You change a field used by hashCode (`k.setId(...)`). The object k is the same by reference, but its hashCode is now different.\n- `get(k)`: HashMap recomputes the hash from k's CURRENT state, gets a different index — bucket[3]. There it's empty or holds other keys. The search never reaches bucket[7] → `null`.\n\n**The key subtlety — equals is irrelevant here.** Many think the problem is in comparison, but equals is called ONLY inside an already-located bucket. If the bucket is wrong, comparison never runs.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:**\n```java\nSet<Point> set = new HashSet<>();\nPoint p = new Point(1, 2);\nset.add(p);\np.setX(100);              // mutated a field from hashCode\nset.contains(p);          // false! the object itself is 'lost'\nset.remove(p);            // won't remove — wrong bucket\nfor (Point x : set) {}    // yet it's THERE in iteration\n```\nThe object simultaneously 'exists' (visible in iterator/size) and 'doesn't exist' (unreachable by key). Memory leak + logic bugs.\n\n**The right way:**\n- Make keys immutable: `final` fields, no setters (String, Integer, UUID, a record with immutable components).\n- If a field truly must change — `remove()` BEFORE the mutation, change it, then `put()` back.\n- Java 16+ records give correct equals/hashCode, but a record with a mutable field (array, mutable object) inside is the same trap.","quizCorrect":"get() computes the bucket index from the new hashCode and lands in a different bucket where the old Node isn't — equals never even gets to compare","quizWrong":["HashMap auto-rehashes all keys on mutation, but does it lazily, so the first get() returns null","equals() starts returning false due to the changed field, and the Node in the correct bucket isn't matched","Mutation breaks the equals/hashCode contract, and HashMap throws ConcurrentModificationException instead of returning the value to the caller immediately"],"picCap":"Mutating the key sends the lookup to bucket[3], while the Node is locked in bucket[7] forever"},"jc-chm-internals":{"q":"How is ConcurrentHashMap structured internally in Java 8+ (no segments)? Why does a write lock only one bucket, why are null keys/values forbidden, and why is size() approximate?","a":"In Java 8+ the segments (the ReentrantLock-based Segments from Java 7) are gone: the structure is a single bucket array `Node[] table`, and locking happens per-bucket via `synchronized` on that bucket's head `Node`. If the bucket is empty, insertion happens with no lock at all — via CAS (`tabAt`/`casTabAt` on Unsafe/VarHandle); collisions are stored either as a linked list or as a red-black tree (`TreeBin`) when a bucket reaches ≥8 entries and the table is ≥64. `null` keys/values are forbidden because in a concurrent setting a `get` returning `null` would be indistinguishable between 'key absent' vs 'value present but null' — and without locking you can't do an atomic `containsKey`+`get` to resolve it. `size()` is approximate because the counter is spread across a `CounterCell[]` array (like `LongAdder`) and summed without a global lock, so concurrent modifications make the result slightly imprecise.","d":"On a write to a non-empty bucket the thread holds `synchronized` only on that head Node, so writes to different buckets run in parallel; resize is cooperative — multiple threads migrate buckets at once, and a thread hitting a bucket under migration sees a `ForwardingNode` (hash = MOVED = -1) and helps with the transfer.","note":"⚠️ Inside `computeIfAbsent`/`compute`/`merge` you must not modify the same `ConcurrentHashMap` — the lambda runs under `synchronized` on the bucket's head Node. In Java 8 a recursive `computeIfAbsent` on a key in the same bucket caused a CPU-spinning hang/deadlock with no exception (the well-known JDK-8071667); since Java 9+ direct recursion on the same key throws `IllegalStateException: Recursive update`, but via other buckets you can still silently get blocked. And remember: atomicity is guaranteed only for a single operation — a `get` then `put` pair is NOT atomic, you need `putIfAbsent`/`compute`.","hook":"Java 7 is an apartment block with 16 shared per-entrance locks (segments): enter an entrance and you lock out everyone sharing it. Java 8 gives each apartment (bucket) its own lock, and you can just slip into an empty apartment (CAS) without a key at all. And the doorman counting residents doesn't tally heads at once — he adds up notes from separate logs (CounterCell); while he's adding, someone already walked in, so the number is approximate.","more":"**What actually changed in Java 8:**\n- The inner class `Segment extends ReentrantLock` from Java 7 was removed. Previously concurrency was capped by `concurrencyLevel` (default 16) — at most 16 parallel writers. Now lock granularity = number of buckets, and it grows on resize.\n- Structure: `transient volatile Node<K,V>[] table`. Cell access goes through `tabAt`/`casTabAt`/`setTabAt` (originally `sun.misc.Unsafe`, in newer versions on top of acquire/release semantics).\n- **Treeification:** a bucket's list becomes a red-black tree (`TreeBin`) when `TREEIFY_THRESHOLD = 8` AND `table.length >= MIN_TREEIFY_CAPACITY = 64`. If the table is smaller than 64, a resize is done instead of treeifying. It reverts to a list at `UNTREEIFY_THRESHOLD = 6`.\n\n**Why null is forbidden (and how it differs from HashMap):**\n- `HashMap` allows one `null` key and `null` values because it's single-threaded — you can distinguish 'no key' from 'value is null' via `containsKey`.\n- In `ConcurrentHashMap`, `get` takes no lock. If `map.get(k)` returned `null`, you couldn't atomically check `containsKey(k)`: between the two calls another thread could remove/insert the key. Doug Lea frames this as 'an ambiguity that can't be tolerated without locking'. So `null` is forbidden on both sides — a deliberate design choice, not a bug.\n\n**Why size() is approximate:**\n- The counter is `baseCount` (volatile long) + a `CounterCell[]` array, the same pattern as `LongAdder`: under contention on the `baseCount` CAS a thread writes into its own cell. `size()` sums `baseCount` + all cells with no global lock, so it reflects a 'snapshot in motion'.\n- `size()` also returns an `int` (clamped to `Integer.MAX_VALUE`); for large maps there's `mappingCount()` → `long`.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** `computeIfAbsent` / `compute` hold `synchronized` on the bucket's head Node for the entire duration of your lambda. If inside the lambda you touch the same `ConcurrentHashMap` (especially a key that lands in the same bucket) you can get a deadlock or (in Java 8) a silent corruption/hang. Java 9+ added a fail-fast `IllegalStateException: Recursive update` for direct recursive `computeIfAbsent` on the same key.","quizCorrect":"Locking is done via synchronized on the head Node of the specific bucket; an empty bucket is filled via CAS without locking","quizWrong":["Each bucket is guarded by its own ReentrantLock, and the array is split into 16 segments by default","The entire table is locked with a single synchronized on the ConcurrentHashMap object itself on any write to the map that arrives","Writes lock the bucket via a StampedLock in optimistic mode, retrying on conflict"],"picCap":"One bucket array: empty bucket — CAS, occupied — synchronized only on head; the counter is spread across CounterCell[]."},"jc-iterator-removal":{"q":"How do you safely remove elements while iterating a collection? How does Iterator differ from ListIterator, and why do iterator.remove()/removeIf NOT throw ConcurrentModificationException while list.remove() inside a for-each does?","a":"Remove safely via the iterator itself: iterator.remove() or collection.removeIf(predicate). ArrayList keeps a modCount field (count of structural changes); on creation the iterator copies it into expectedModCount and re-checks on every next()/hasNext(). list.remove() bumps modCount directly while the iterator (the one for-each uses under the hood) never updates its expectedModCount — so the next next() sees the mismatch and throws ConcurrentModificationException (fail-fast). iterator.remove() removes the element and immediately re-syncs expectedModCount = modCount, so no exception. ListIterator extends Iterator and works only on List: it traverses both directions (hasPrevious/previous), knows the index (nextIndex/previousIndex), and on top of remove() adds set(e) and add(e).","d":"CME is a fail-fast heuristic, not a guarantee: due to null fields or races it can be 'missed', so never build logic on catching CME. For concurrent iteration without CME use CopyOnWriteArrayList (snapshot; iterator.remove() throws UnsupportedOperationException) or ConcurrentHashMap (weakly consistent iterator).","note":"⚠️ Classic trap: on an ArrayList of exactly 2 elements, a for-each that removes the second-to-last element will NOT throw CME — it silently skips the last one. Reason is the subtle ordering in ArrayList$Itr: hasNext() is just `cursor != size`, and checkForComodification lives inside next(), not hasNext(). After remove(), size=1 and cursor=1 → hasNext() returns false, the loop exits, and the modCount-checking next() is never called. So fail-fast fires unreliably and depends on element position — you cannot assume every violation will be caught.","hook":"The iterator is an employee with two notebooks: a shared one (modCount, edited by the whole collection) and a personal one (expectedModCount). Each step it cross-checks: 'mine says 3 but the shared one already says 4 — someone went around me!' → CME. But if it removed the element via its own remove(), it honestly rewrites the personal notebook too — and walks on calmly.","more":"**Fail-fast mechanics (ArrayList):**\n- `modCount` — counter of structural changes (add/remove that alter size), declared in `AbstractList`.\n- On creation `Itr` copies `expectedModCount = modCount`.\n- `Itr.next()` calls `checkForComodification()`: `if (modCount != expectedModCount) throw new ConcurrentModificationException()`.\n- `Itr.remove()` calls `ArrayList.this.remove(lastRet)`, then `expectedModCount = modCount` — re-syncing its notebook.\n\n**Why for-each throws but an explicit iterator with remove() doesn't:**\n- `for-each` over `Iterable` compiles to `Iterator it = list.iterator(); while(it.hasNext()) it.next();`. Calling `list.remove(...)` inside mutates `modCount` but not the iterator's `expectedModCount` → CME on the next `next()`.\n- `removeIf` walks the collection itself, managing modCount/expectedModCount consistently (in ArrayList it's a single-pass batch removal) — so it's safe and faster than a remove-in-loop.\n\n**Correct ways to remove:**\n- `it.remove()` after `it.next()` (not twice in a row without next, not before the first next → IllegalStateException).\n- `list.removeIf(predicate)` — preferred, O(n) and readable.\n- classic index-based `for (int i = ...)` with manual index adjustment (often `i--` after remove).\n\n**ListIterator vs Iterator:**\n- `Iterator`: hasNext / next / remove — forward-only, for any Collection.\n- `ListIterator extends Iterator`: + hasPrevious/previous, nextIndex/previousIndex, set(e) (replace last returned), add(e) (insert before cursor). List-only.","quizCorrect":"The iterator stores expectedModCount and compares it with the collection's modCount in next(); list.remove() changes modCount without touching expectedModCount → mismatch → CME. iterator.remove() re-syncs expectedModCount = modCount after removing.","quizWrong":["ArrayList is synchronized, and a concurrent list.remove() from another thread is always detected via a volatile flag, so CME is guaranteed on any modification. That is exactly why the iterator is unnecessary here in the first place. There is nothing else to do.","iterator.remove() works through reflection and bypasses the modCount check, while list.remove() takes the normal path and therefore always throws CME.","CME is thrown because for-each creates an immutable copy of the list, and changing the original breaks it via the hash."],"picCap":"On the left both counters are equal. list.remove() bumps only modCount → next() detects the mismatch and throws CME. it.remove() immediately syncs expectedModCount — no exception."},"gap-co-sync-vs-reentrantlock":{"q":"synchronized vs ReentrantLock vs ReadWriteLock — what does ReentrantLock provide that synchronized lacks (tryLock, fairness, interruptibility), and when do you actually need a ReadWriteLock?","a":"synchronized is the JVM's built-in monitor: blocking, non-fair, non-interruptible acquisition, scoped strictly to a method/block (lock/unlock are implicit). ReentrantLock adds what the monitor lacks: tryLock() (non-blocking acquire and tryLock with timeout), lockInterruptibly() (a waiting thread can be interrupted), optional fairness via new ReentrantLock(true) (FIFO queue, starvation protection), and Condition objects instead of a single wait/notify. ReentrantReadWriteLock fits a read-heavy pattern: many concurrent readers, rare writers — the readLock is held by several threads at once, the writeLock is exclusive; on write-heavy load it loses to a plain lock due to overhead.","d":"Since Java 8, for counters/accumulators LongAdder/Atomic is often better, and instead of ReadWriteLock consider StampedLock with optimistic reads (tryOptimisticRead), which doesn't block writers at all.","note":"⚠️ ReentrantReadWriteLock does NOT support upgrading a readLock to a writeLock — trying to acquire the writeLock while holding the readLock causes an instant deadlock (the thread waits on itself). Downgrade (write → read) is allowed. Also, under write-heavy load the RWLock is often slower than a plain ReentrantLock due to its complex state bookkeeping, and writers can starve under a stream of readers (in non-fair mode). For optimistic reading use StampedLock instead — but it is NOT reentrant, which is the second trap.","hook":"synchronized is a subway turnstile: one pass, you wait silently, no options. ReentrantLock is a barrier with buttons: 'I'll try and leave' (tryLock), 'cancel me' (interrupt), 'strict queue' (fair). ReadWriteLock is a reading room: many can read at once, but rearranging the shelves is solo and only when the room is empty.","more":"**What ReentrantLock gives beyond synchronized:**\n- `tryLock()` — instant non-blocking attempt; `tryLock(timeout, unit)` — with timeout. This is the only standard way to build deadlock avoidance (grab both locks or back off).\n- `lockInterruptibly()` — a waiting thread responds to `Thread.interrupt()`; plain `lock()` and `synchronized` do not.\n- Fairness: `new ReentrantLock(true)` — FIFO, no starvation, but lower throughput. Default (false) is faster but allows barging.\n- Multiple `Condition` objects per lock (`newCondition()`) vs the monitor's single wait-set — you can separate 'not empty'/'not full'.\n- Metrics: `isLocked()`, `getHoldCount()`, `getQueueLength()`.\n\n**Both are reentrant** (the same thread re-enters) — that's about reentrancy, not an advantage.\n\n**When ReadWriteLock:** strictly read-heavy, where the read genuinely lasts longer than acquiring the lock. On short sections the RW-lock overhead eats the gain.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** forgetting `unlock()` in `finally`. With `synchronized` the monitor is released automatically even on exception; with `ReentrantLock` a missed `unlock()` = permanent deadlock. The canonical form:\n```java\nlock.lock();\ntry { /* ... */ } finally { lock.unlock(); }\n```","quizCorrect":"ReentrantLock offers tryLock (incl. with timeout), lockInterruptibly, and optional fairness — none of which synchronized has.","quizWrong":["ReentrantLock is reentrant whereas synchronized is not, which is its main advantage.","ReentrantLock releases the lock automatically on exception without a finally block, unlike synchronized. which requires one for it","ReentrantLock works without the happens-before guarantee, making it faster than a synchronized monitor."],"picCap":"The monitor is option-poor; ReentrantLock adds control; the RWLock admits many readers but a single writer is exclusive."},"gap-co-interruption-cancellation":{"q":"How do you correctly cancel a running task/thread via interrupt()? What does the interrupt flag mean, why must you never swallow InterruptedException, and why was Thread.stop() removed?","a":"interrupt() does not 'kill' a thread — it merely sets an internal boolean interrupt flag and serves as a cooperative 'time to stop' signal. Blocking methods (Thread.sleep, Object.wait, BlockingQueue.take, Future.get) throw InterruptedException and CLEAR the flag on throw; therefore in the catch block you must either restore the flag via Thread.currentThread().interrupt() or rethrow the exception — otherwise upstream code (a pool, while(!isInterrupted())) never learns about the cancellation and hangs. Long-running computations without blocking calls must poll Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted() in a loop themselves. Thread.stop() is deprecated and removed (deprecated since 1.2, throws UnsupportedOperationException in Java 20), because it asynchronously threw ThreadDeath at an arbitrary point, releasing monitors and leaving objects in a half-updated, corrupted state with no chance for proper cleanup.","d":"isInterrupted() only reads the flag, while the static Thread.interrupted() reads AND clears it — this trips people up during manual status checks.","note":"⚠️ The most common mistake: catch (InterruptedException e) { log.error(e); } without restoring the flag. After the exception is thrown the flag is ALREADY CLEARED, and if you don't restore it via Thread.currentThread().interrupt(), upstream code in while(!Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) loops forever, and ExecutorService.shutdownNow() can't stop the workers. Never swallow InterruptedException — either restore the flag or rethrow the exception.","hook":"interrupt() is a polite knock on the door 'hey, wrap it up', not a bullet to the head (that was Thread.stop()). InterruptedException is a note slipped under the door; if you swallow it and don't pass it on, the boss never learns the task should be cancelled and the worker waits forever.","more":"**Flag mechanics:**\n- `interrupt()` — sets the flag (for a live thread) OR wakes a blocking call with `InterruptedException`.\n- `isInterrupted()` (instance) — reads the flag, does NOT clear it.\n- `Thread.interrupted()` (static) — reads AND clears the current thread's flag. Common trap: calling it twice and losing the status.\n\n**Two code scenarios:**\n- Blocking code: catch `InterruptedException`. Since the flag is already cleared on throw, do `Thread.currentThread().interrupt()` (restore) OR rethrow.\n- Computational code: periodically check `if (Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) return;` yourself — otherwise interrupt() does nothing.\n\n**Why `stop()` is deadly dangerous:**\n- It threw `ThreadDeath` asynchronously at ANY instruction.\n- It released all held monitors (`synchronized`) but did NOT roll back partial changes — other threads saw the object in a 'broken' state (broken invariants).\n- No `finally`-guaranteed invariant restoration.\n- Deprecated since Java 1.2; in Java 20+ it throws `UnsupportedOperationException`. Likewise `suspend()`/`resume()` — removed due to deadlocks.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** in a thread pool (`ExecutorService`) a swallowed `InterruptedException` means `shutdownNow()` (which sends interrupt to all workers) won't work — the thread keeps blocking in `queue.take()` and the application won't terminate. The JVM will hang on shutdown.","quizCorrect":"interrupt() sets the interrupt flag; blocking methods throw InterruptedException and clear the flag, so in the catch block you must restore it or rethrow the exception","quizWrong":["interrupt() immediately stops the thread by throwing ThreadDeath in it, just like the deprecated Thread.stop()","InterruptedException can be safely swallowed in catch — the JVM restores the interrupt flag itself on exit from the block and the caller above will notice it anyway in any case","Long-running CPU computations without blocking calls are interrupted automatically without checking isInterrupted()"],"picCap":"interrupt() = cooperative signal; erasing the flag or swallowing InterruptedException = losing the cancellation. stop() tears the thread apart at an arbitrary point."},"gap-co-safe-publication-final":{"q":"What is safe publication of an object between threads, and what guarantees do final fields give in an immutable class? Why can an object 'leak' half-constructed?","a":"Safe publication means publishing a reference so that a reading thread is guaranteed to see all the object's fields in their as-of-publication state (not a pre-construction state). The JMM does not guarantee that writing the reference and writing the object's fields become visible to another thread atomically or in program order: without happens-before, the compiler/CPU may reorder the assignments, and a reader can see a non-null reference but fields still at defaults (0/null) — that is the half-constructed leak. Correct publication establishes happens-before: writing to a volatile/AtomicReference, under a shared lock, via a final field (JLS §17.5: after the constructor returns all final fields are seen correctly without synchronization, provided this did not escape the constructor), plus static initializers and thread-safe collections.","d":"The final guarantee also extends to objects reachable through a chain of final fields (e.g., elements of a final array or fields in a nested immutable object) — this is the constructor-end 'freeze action' forming a happens-before to reads of those fields.","note":"⚠️ final does NOT make a field 'forever visible in all scenarios' — the guarantee is tied to constructor completion and to this not escaping early. If in the constructor you did EventBus.register(this), executor.submit(this), or assigned this to a static field before the constructor returned — the freeze hasn't happened yet, and another thread may read your final fields as 0/null. Also, a final reference to a mutable object (e.g. a final List that is later mutated) safely publishes only the list's initial state, NOT its later changes — those need separate synchronization.","hook":"The courier dropped the box on the doorstep and left (ref published), but inside the box is still empty — the goods 'arrive' later. A final field is a seal: until the constructor 'sealed' the box (freeze) it isn't handed over; the recipient always sees the full parcel.","more":"**Root of the problem.** `obj = new Holder(42)` is not one operation but at least three: allocate memory, write fields into that region, write the reference into `obj`. The JMM (JLS §17.4) lets the compiler and CPU reorder these steps as long as the single-thread result is unchanged. Another thread reading `obj` without happens-before may see a non-null reference but fields still at defaults (`int`=0, `Object`=null). The classic bug is DCL (double-checked locking) without `volatile`.\n\n**What counts as safe publication (Goetz, JCiP §3.5.3):**\n- initialization from a static initializer (`static { ... }`);\n- writing the reference to a `volatile` field or `AtomicReference`;\n- writing to a field protected by `final` (for immutable objects);\n- writing under a shared `lock`/`synchronized`;\n- placing into a thread-safe collection (`ConcurrentHashMap`, `CopyOnWriteArrayList`, `BlockingQueue`, etc.).\n\n**The final guarantee (JLS §17.5).** At the end of the constructor a *freeze action* runs for all final fields. Any thread that obtains the reference through a data race still sees correctly initialized final fields and everything reachable through them. This makes truly immutable classes thread-safe without synchronized — `String`, `Integer`, immutable value objects.\n\n**Concrete gotcha.** The final guarantee breaks if `this` escapes the constructor BEFORE it completes: registering a listener (`registry.add(this)`), starting a thread (`new Thread(this).start()`), passing `this` into a callback. Then another thread gets the reference before the freeze — the final guarantee does not apply, and fields may still be at defaults.","quizCorrect":"final fields are seen correctly after the constructor returns without extra synchronization — provided this did not escape the constructor","quizWrong":["final guarantees atomicity and visibility of any subsequent runtime writes to that field","final fields are protected by an automatically inserted synchronized block around every read of such a field by any thread at all that touches it","Safe publication is achievable only via volatile; final has no effect on visibility"],"picCap":"Without happens-before the reader sees the reference before the fields (field=0). final/volatile insert a freeze/barrier — fields become visible correctly."},"gap-co-wait-notify-spurious":{"q":"Why is wait() always called inside a while loop rather than an if? What is a spurious wakeup, and what is the difference between notify() and notifyAll()?","a":"wait() is wrapped in a while because the condition must be re-checked after waking up: the thread may have woken yet the condition is already false again. Three reasons: spurious wakeup (a wakeup with no notify, permitted by the JLS and real on POSIX systems due to interrupted futex/condvar waits); a race — between notify and reacquiring the monitor another thread changed the state; and notifyAll waking several waiters for a single event. notify() wakes exactly one arbitrary thread from the wait-set, notifyAll() wakes all; notify() is safe only when all threads wait on the same condition and one event is meant for one thread, otherwise use notifyAll to avoid a lost wakeup.","d":"All three methods (wait/notify/notifyAll) require holding the object's monitor, otherwise IllegalMonitorStateException; wait() atomically releases the monitor and sleeps, then reacquires it on wakeup.","note":"⚠️ Replacing notifyAll() with notify() 'for optimization' is a classic source of hung systems: with two different conditions on one monitor, notify may wake the 'wrong' thread, which checks its condition, goes back to sleep — and the signal is lost forever (lost wakeup), leaving the rest hung indefinitely. The bug is non-reproducible under dev-load and only fires in prod. If you really need notify(), put each condition on its own monitor object or use separate Conditions on one ReentrantLock.","hook":"wait() in an if is like waking to an alarm and driving straight to work without checking it's 3 a.m. (spurious). while means 'rub your eyes and re-check the clock'. notify = nudge one sleeper at random; notifyAll = turn the lights on for everyone.","more":"**Why while and not if:**\n- **Spurious wakeup** — JLS §17.2.1 explicitly permits a wakeup with no notify/notifyAll/interrupt. In practice it comes from POSIX `pthread_cond_wait`, which may return on an OS signal/futex interrupt. The `Object.wait()` Javadoc explicitly mandates a loop.\n- **Race after notify** — the woken thread does not own the monitor immediately: it queues to re-enter the monitor. While it waits for the lock, a third thread may grab the monitor and consume the state again (e.g. take the queue element). By the time it enters, the condition is false again.\n- **notifyAll on a shared monitor** — if different conditions wait on one object (producer waits 'not full', consumer 'not empty'), notifyAll wakes everyone, but the woken thread must check its own condition.\n\n**notify vs notifyAll:**\n- `notify()` — wakes one **arbitrary** thread from the wait-set (no FIFO guarantee). Cheap but dangerous: if it does not match the condition, the signal is lost (**lost wakeup**) and the rest sleep forever.\n- `notifyAll()` — wakes all; they contend for the monitor, the extra ones go back to sleep in the while. More expensive (thundering herd) but safe by default.\n- Rule: `notify()` is acceptable only if (1) all wait on the same condition and (2) one notification is meant for exactly one thread. When in doubt — `notifyAll()`.\n\n**Canonical pattern:**\n```java\nsynchronized (lock) {\n    while (!condition) {\n        lock.wait();\n    }\n    // condition is true AND the monitor is held\n}\n```\nThe modern alternative is `java.util.concurrent.locks.Condition` (`await`/`signal`/`signalAll`) on a `ReentrantLock`: same while-loop rule, but allows multiple Conditions on one lock (separate queues for producer/consumer).","quizCorrect":"A thread can wake when the condition is false again (spurious wakeup or a race after notify), so the condition must be re-checked","quizWrong":["if works, but while is faster due to JIT compiler optimization","while is needed because wait() does not release the monitor and must be called repeatedly until the condition finally becomes true","There is no difference; while is just a legacy convention from Java 1.0"],"picCap":"while re-checks the condition after wakeup; notify wakes one thread, notifyAll wakes all."},"gap-jvm-gc-roots":{"q":"What are GC roots and how does the collector decide an object is garbage? Why do two objects referencing each other still get collected?","a":"GC roots are the set of inherently-live 'root' references: references on thread stacks (local variables, parameters), static fields of loaded classes, JNI references (local/global), active monitors (synchronized), plus JVM-internal references (e.g. objects in finalization). Modern collectors (G1, ZGC, Parallel) use tracing/mark-and-sweep, NOT reference counting: they start from GC roots and walk the reachability graph, marking everything reachable; anything unmarked is garbage. Therefore two mutually-referencing objects A&harr;B that are unreachable from any root form an isolated 'island' and get collected — mutual references don't keep them alive; what matters is reachability from roots, not the presence of incoming references.","d":"This is the key difference between a tracing GC (HotSpot) and reference counting (like CPython): RC breaks on cycles, while tracing handles them naturally because it considers alive only what's reachable from roots.","note":"⚠️ A `static` field IS a GC root, and people constantly forget this. Putting an object into a `static Map`/`static List` (a cache, a listener registry, a ThreadLocal without remove) makes it forever reachable from a root — it will NOT be collected even if nothing else references it. This is the classic 'memory leak in a managed language': the GC works perfectly, the object is simply formally alive. Fixed with `WeakHashMap`, explicit removal, or `WeakReference`; this is exactly how reloadable classloaders leak in app servers (a static reference from an 'eternal' class pins the whole old ClassLoader and all its classes).","hook":"The GC doesn't 'count friends', it 'calls down from the boss'. The boss (root) phones acquaintances down the chain; whoever picks up stays alive. Two pals A and B who only call each other but whom the boss never calls get laid off, no matter how much they keep ringing each other.","more":"**Core point:** HotSpot collectors (Serial, Parallel, G1, ZGC, Shenandoah) are based on **reachability from GC roots**, not reference counting. The algorithm is tracing (mark-sweep / mark-compact / evacuation).\n\n**What counts as GC roots:**\n- Local variables and parameters in active stack frames of all threads (determined via the OopMap for the given safepoint)\n- `static` fields of classes loaded by live classloaders\n- JNI local/global references\n- Objects held as monitors (`synchronized`)\n- JVM-internal references: class objects of the system classloader, objects in the finalization queue, temporarily `Thread` objects\n\n**Why a cycle gets collected:** the mark phase starts strictly from roots. If there's no path from any root to the A&harr;B island, both objects stay unmarked and sweep/evacuation reclaims them. References inside the island are invisible to the GC as a liveness signal.\n\n**Concrete gotcha — Reference objects break the naive 'reachable = alive' model:**\n- `WeakReference`/`SoftReference`/`PhantomReference` provide *weak* reachability. An object reachable ONLY through a `WeakReference` is treated as garbage, collected, and the referent is nulled.\n- So 'reachability' is a hierarchy: strong &gt; soft &gt; weak &gt; phantom. An object is alive only if reachable via **strong** references from roots.\n- Classic leak: a `static` Map (it's a GC root!) with strong keys/values — objects never become unreachable. Fix with `WeakHashMap` or manual cleanup.\n\n**Why know about safepoints:** stack tracing requires threads to stop at a safepoint — otherwise the OopMap isn't consistent. Long counted loops without a safepoint poll delay the STW pause (time-to-safepoint).","quizCorrect":"The collector traces the graph from GC roots; an unreachable A&harr;B cycle is left unmarked and collected","quizWrong":["A and B won't be collected as long as they have at least one incoming reference to each other from anywhere","HotSpot uses reference counting, so a cycle with counter&gt;0 lives forever (memory leak)","Cycles are collected only by a Full GC, because young-GC can't handle circular references"],"picCap":"Tracing from GC roots: the reachable lives, the isolated A&harr;B cycle is collected."},"gap-jvm-write-barrier-cardtable":{"q":"During a minor GC only the young generation is scanned, but a young object may be referenced by an object in the old gen. How does the collector find such old→young references without scanning the entire old gen?","a":"On every reference store into an object field the JIT inserts a write barrier — a short code stub that marks the corresponding card in the card table as dirty. The card table is a byte array where one byte covers a heap region (512 bytes in HotSpot). During minor GC the collector scans only the dirty cards of the old gen (plus roots), finds the old objects in them that reference young, and adds those references to the young GC roots. Thus the cost of finding old→young references is proportional to the number of mutated cards, not to the size of the whole old gen.","d":"Serial/Parallel/CMS use exactly the card table; G1, on top of the card table, adds a per-region remembered set (RSet), because regions are collected individually and you must know the incoming references for each specific region, not just old→young.","note":"⚠️ The card-marking barrier runs on EVERY reference store, not only old→young — the JIT doesn't know the target's generation at store time, so it marks unconditionally (unconditional barrier in Parallel GC). This means even a young→young reference store still triggers the barrier and dirties a card. Two senior takeaways: (1) allocation-heavy code pays a hidden tax on every reference-field store; (2) when profiling long young pauses, look not only at young size but at the volume of dirty cards — write-heavy mutation of long-lived collections (a large cache that's constantly overwritten) inflates the minor GC scan phase even though young itself is small.","hook":"The card table is a 'notice board' by blocks of the old gen. Every time an old object 'adopts' a young one (writes a reference to it), the guard at the gate (write barrier) puts a red mark on that block. The young collector doesn't walk the whole city — it goes only to the blocks with red marks.","more":"**Why this is needed at all.** A minor GC promises to be cheap: it scans only young. But correctness requires knowing ALL references to young objects, including those coming from old. Fully scanning old would kill the minor GC benefit. The solution is to offload the bookkeeping to mutation time.\n\n**Mechanics step by step:**\n- **Write barrier** — code inserted by the JIT after a reference store into a field (`obj.field = ref`). In HotSpot for young collection it's a card-marking barrier: `card_table[(address >> 9)] = dirty`. The shift by 9 = division by 512 (the card size).\n- **Card table** — a `byte[]`, one byte per 512 bytes of heap. A dirty byte = 'someone in this old region wrote a reference, possibly to young'.\n- **Minor GC** — linearly walks the card table; for each dirty card it scans the objects of that old region, adds the found old→young references to roots, then clears the card.\n\n**Why a byte, not a bit:** a byte write is atomic and needs no read-modify-write (a bit would need CAS/masking), so the barrier stays 2-3 instructions with no synchronization.\n\n**Concrete gotcha — imprecise marking:** a card is marked dirty as a whole, even if a single field of a single object in a 512-byte region changed. The collector then re-scans ALL objects of that card. So many small mutations in one area of old create 'false' work for minor GC — a real source of longer young pauses under write-heavy load on long-lived structures.\n\n**G1 difference:** G1 collects regions individually (not 'all of old vs young'), so a single card table is not enough — it needs a **per-region remembered set (RSet)**, which stores which cards from OTHER regions point into a given region. Maintaining the RSet is more expensive: the G1 barrier is two-part (including enqueue into a dirty card queue + concurrent refinement threads that asynchronously update the RSet).","quizCorrect":"On a reference store the write barrier marks a card in the card table dirty; minor GC scans only the dirty cards of the old gen","quizWrong":["On every minor GC the JVM scans the whole old gen, but fast — old barely changes","A read barrier on field reads checks the object's generation and registers the reference in the remembered set of that generation","Each young object keeps a list of incoming references from old (back-pointers)"],"picCap":"The write barrier marks a card dirty on a reference store; minor GC scans only the dirty cards, not the whole old gen."},"gap-jvm-object-header-coops":{"q":"How many bytes does an empty object weigh and what makes up its header (mark word, klass pointer)? What does -XX:+UseCompressedOops do, and why does memory usage suddenly grow once the heap exceeds 32 GB?","a":"On 64-bit HotSpot the object header = mark word (8 bytes: hash, GC age bits, lock bits / monitor pointer) + klass pointer (4 bytes with compressed pointers, otherwise 8). An empty `Object` weighs 16 bytes: 12 bytes of header + 4 bytes of padding to the 8-byte alignment; an array adds another 4 bytes for length. `-XX:+UseCompressedOops` (on by default when heap < 32 GB) stores references in 4 bytes instead of 8, encoding a 35-bit range via a 3-bit shift (relying on the 8-byte object alignment).","d":"Just above 32 GB the JVM can no longer address the heap with 32-bit oops even with the shift, so it disables CompressedOops (and CompressedClassPointers) — all references and klass pointers go back to 8 bytes, so a ~33 GB heap actually holds fewer objects than a 31 GB one.","note":"⚠️ Bumping the heap from 31 GB to, say, 36 GB can give you LESS usable memory: reference bloat eats the gain, and the app may start GC-thrashing worse than at 31 GB. Practical rule — either keep `-Xmx` below ~32 GB (the threshold depends on alignment and JVM version, sometimes ~30–31 GB), or jump straight to 40+ GB to outgrow the dip. `-XX:ObjectAlignmentInBytes=16` raises the threshold to ~64 GB at the cost of more padding.","hook":"The header is the object's 'passport': page one (mark word) is who it is and its state (hash, age, lock), page two (klass pointer) is its 'citizenship' (class). CompressedOops is like a short ZIP code: while the city is under 32 GB the short code suffices; cross the line and you must write the full 8-byte address on every envelope, and all letters get fatter at once.","more":"**What the mark word holds (64 bits):**\n- Normal state: identity hashCode (if computed), GC age bits, lock flags.\n- Under biased/thin/fat lock — a pointer to a stack lock record or to an ObjectMonitor. (Biased locking was disabled by default in JDK 15 and removed in JDK 18.)\n- During GC (mark-compact) a forwarding pointer is temporarily stored here.\n\n**Empty-object arithmetic (compressed oops, alignment=8):**\n- mark word: 8 bytes\n- klass pointer: 4 bytes (compressed)\n- 12 bytes total → padded to 16 bytes.\n- `new Object()` = **16 bytes**. For an array: +4 bytes for `length` → 16 bytes header minimum.\n\n**How oop encoding works:**\n- Objects are 8-byte aligned, so the low 3 bits of an address are always zero.\n- A reference is stored as `realAddr >> 3`, dereferenced as `compressedOop << 3` (+ heap base if base is non-zero).\n- 32 bits × 8 = a 35-bit range = **32 GB** of addressable heap.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:**\n- With `-Xmx32g` the JVM often will NOT enable zero-based compressed oops and may disable compressed oops entirely — check `-XX:+PrintFlagsFinal | grep UseCompressedOops` and the `-Xlog:gc+heap+coops=info` logs. The real compressed-oops ceiling is usually ~30–31 GB, not exactly 32.\n- A list that took N at a 28 GB heap will take almost twice as much per reference field at a 34 GB heap — this hurts cache locality and actual capacity.","quizCorrect":"16 bytes: 8 (mark word) + 4 (compressed klass pointer) + 4 padding to the 8-byte alignment","quizWrong":["8 bytes: just the mark word, the klass pointer is stored in the class itself","Exactly 12 bytes, no padding, since the header sum is already a multiple of 4 on any platform","24 bytes: mark word 8 + klass pointer 8 + 8 bytes reserved for hashCode"],"picCap":"Object header (16 bytes) and the capacity dip when the heap crosses 32 GB: compressed references switch off, and every reference and klass pointer goes back to 8 bytes."},"gap-jvm-classloader-leak":{"q":"After several hot-redeploys of an app in Tomcat/WildFly, Metaspace runs out even though the app is 'undeployed' each time. What keeps the old ClassLoader alive and why does an entire class hierarchy leak?","a":"A ClassLoader is unloaded from Metaspace only if it itself becomes unreachable — which requires ALL classes it loaded and ALL their instances to become unreachable too (every object reaches its loader via getClass().getClassLoader()). A single live strong reference from long-lived code OUTSIDE the webapp to any class/instance from the app keeps the whole hierarchy alive via the chain `instance → Class → defining ClassLoader → all other classes`. Classic culprits: a ThreadLocal whose value-class comes from the webapp on a thread from a shared pool (no remove()), JDK/container statics (java.sql.DriverManager, java.beans.Introspector), an app-started thread that was never stopped, a JNDI/MBean/shutdown-hook registered at JVM level. The old loader is never GC'd → its Metaspace region is never freed → after N redeploys you get OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace.","d":"Find the holder: heap dump → Eclipse MAT → 'Path to GC Roots' for the WebappClassLoader object, excluding weak/soft/phantom refs — you'll see the exact strong chain to a GC root.","note":"⚠️ A ThreadLocal on pool threads is the most common, insidious trap: the thread is reused across requests and redeploys, so a set() without remove() in a finally leaves the value alive forever. Even if your ThreadLocal key is a static field in a library outside the webapp, the value object of a webapp class holds the whole loader. A WeakReference on the key won't help — it's the value that leaks, not the key. After each redeploy another 'dead' WebappClassLoader with all its classes piles up in Metaspace.","hook":"A ClassLoader is a 'ship's anchor': as long as one sailor (object) still grips the rope (a ThreadLocal on a foreign thread), the whole ship (the entire class hierarchy in Metaspace) cannot sink to the GC seabed.","more":"**Why the whole hierarchy leaks, not just one class**\n\nJVM reachability model:\n- Every object knows its `Class` (object header), and a `Class` knows its **defining ClassLoader** (`Class.getClassLoader()`).\n- A ClassLoader holds strong references to **all** classes it defined (an internal table). So one live object drags in the whole loader and all webapp classes at once.\n- A class is unloaded from Metaspace **only** when its ClassLoader becomes unreachable and is GC'd. One loader = the atomic unit of unloading.\n\n**Typical holders (GC roots) that live OUTSIDE the webapp and thus survive undeploy:**\n- **ThreadLocal** with a value whose class is loaded by the webapp loader, on a thread from a container/shared pool (HTTP worker, ForkJoinPool.commonPool). The thread itself is a GC root.\n- **java.sql.DriverManager** — a JDBC driver registered from the webapp (Tomcat tries to deregister, but not always; a third-party driver leaks).\n- **java.beans.Introspector** caches `BeanInfo` → holds classes. Fixed via `Introspector.flushCaches()`.\n- **java.util.logging / log4j** — an appender/level holds the category class.\n- Threads **started by the app and not stopped** (Timer, your own ExecutorService, a library background thread) — every live Thread is a GC root and its `contextClassLoader` = the webapp loader.\n- **Shutdown hooks**, MBeans in the platform MBeanServer, JNDI bindings, RMI.\n\n**The concrete ThreadLocal gotcha:** even though the key (`ThreadLocal`) is held via a WeakReference, the **value is held strongly**. As long as the thread lives and the ThreadLocalMap isn't cleared (and a pool doesn't recreate threads), value→Class→ClassLoader stay alive. So `remove()` in finally is mandatory — it won't 'clean itself up via the weak key'.\n\n**Diagnostics:**\n- `-Xlog:class+unload` (JDK 9+) / `-verbose:class` — shows whether classes are unloaded.\n- Heap dump (`jmap`/`jcmd GC.heap_dump`) → Eclipse MAT → find all `WebappClassLoader` instances, for each 'Merge Shortest Paths to GC Roots → exclude weak/soft' → the strong holder.\n- `jcmd <pid> VM.metaspace` / `VM.classloader_stats` — growth in the number of live loaders after redeploys.","quizCorrect":"A live strong reference from long-lived code (a ThreadLocal on a shared-pool thread, a JDK static) to a webapp instance/class → via getClass().getClassLoader() the whole loader and its classes are pinned in Metaspace","quizWrong":["Tomcat doesn't call System.gc() after undeploy, so the old loader just waits for the next full GC and goes away on its own","Metaspace has no limit by default, and classes in it are physically never unloaded even once the loader is collected","Each redeploy spawns a new JVM process, and the old process's Metaspace isn't released by the OS until the container restarts, which is why nothing in the application itself can possibly be holding on to the old classes."],"picCap":"A single ThreadLocal reference from a shared pool holds the instance &rarr; its Class &rarr; the entire loader &rarr; the whole class hierarchy in Metaspace."},"gap-jvm-invokedynamic-lambda":{"q":"What does a lambda compile to at the bytecode level? Why is it invokedynamic + LambdaMetafactory rather than a compiler-generated anonymous class, and what does that buy you?","a":"javac puts the lambda body into a private synthetic static method of the same class (e.g. lambda$main$0) and, at the use site, emits an invokedynamic instruction whose bootstrap method is LambdaMetafactory.metafactory(...). On the first execution of that indy site the bootstrap runs exactly once, returns the functional-interface implementation via a CallSite (the class is spun at runtime by the internal InnerClassLambdaMetafactory as a hidden class), and the result is constantly linked into the CallSite — subsequent calls are just a direct invoke with no regeneration. The carrier class for the lambda is NOT created by the compiler: the decision of HOW to materialize the lambda is deferred to runtime.","d":"Stateless (non-capturing) lambdas — those that use neither this nor local variables — are cached by LambdaMetafactory as a singleton, so such a lambda does not allocate an object on every call, unlike an anonymous class where new always creates an instance.","note":"⚠️ Method references to an instance method actually capture the receiver: list::add carries list, so it is capturing and allocates an object on every pass through the indy site — people wrongly treat method references as 'free' and drop them into hot loops. The second common surprise: LambdaMetafactory generates the class lazily on the FIRST execution of the site, so the 'cold' first lambda pays for the bootstrap (spinning a hidden class) — this hurts startup/warmup latency and shows up in first-request benchmarks.","hook":"A lambda is an 'IOU', not goods: javac writes an invokedynamic note, and the LambdaMetafactory factory floor only opens on the first buyer, after which it forever hands out a ready object through the CallSite window.","more":"**Full lambda materialization chain:**\n\n- **Compile-time (javac):** the lambda body is moved into a synthetic private method — `static` if there is no `this` capture, otherwise a `private` instance method. At the use site there is an `invokedynamic` instruction whose bootstrap (BSM) points at `java.lang.invoke.LambdaMetafactory.metafactory(...)` (or `altMetafactory` for serializable/intersection types). The interface-implementation class is **absent** from the `.class` file.\n- **Link-time (first execution of the indy site):** the JVM invokes the BSM exactly once. Inside, `InnerClassLambdaMetafactory` runs — it spins the functional-interface implementation class. Since Java 15 this is a **hidden class** (`Unsafe.defineHiddenClass`/`Lookup.defineHiddenClass`); earlier it was `Unsafe.defineAnonymousClass`. The BSM returns a `ConstantCallSite`.\n- **Steady-state:** the `CallSite` is constantly linked — further calls are a direct `linkToTargetMethod`, with no re-bootstrap and no reflection.\n\n**What this buys you over an anonymous class:**\n- **No class-per-lambda on disk** — an anonymous class is always a separate `Outer$N.class` bloating the JAR and metaspace; a lambda spawns no files.\n- **Deferred strategy** — the JVM (not the compiler) decides how to materialize; in the future it could be value-based with no allocation.\n- **Singleton cache for non-capturing** — a lambda without capture is created once and reused.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** a non-capturing lambda is reused as a singleton, but a **capturing** lambda (capturing a variable/`this`) allocates a **new** object on every pass through the indy site — `() -> x` in a hot loop is an allocation per iteration, no better than an anonymous class. So on the hot path you check whether it captures or not.","quizCorrect":"The lambda body is compiled into a private static method, and at the use site there is an invokedynamic with a bootstrap method LambdaMetafactory, which on the first call generates the implementation class and links it into a CallSite.","quizWrong":["javac generates an ordinary anonymous class Outer$1 at compile time, just as for new Runnable(){...}, merely hiding it inside the bytecode. The bootstrap method is never involved at any point during the whole process. No indy call site is ever created.","A lambda compiles into a MethodHandle object that the JVM invokes directly via invokevirtual on every access.","Each lambda call goes through the LambdaMetafactory bootstrap method again, so the implementation class is generated on every execution."],"picCap":"javac hoists the body into a static method and emits invokedynamic; the implementation class is born at runtime once and linked into a CallSite."},"jc-optional-misuse":{"q":"How should you NOT use Optional? Why are opt.get() without a check, isPresent()+get() instead of map/orElse, and Optional as a field/parameter/collection element all anti-patterns?","a":"Optional was designed by Brian Goetz strictly as a method return type to explicitly signal 'a result may be absent'. opt.get() without a check throws NoSuchElementException and is no better than an NPE — it merely masks the same problem. The if(isPresent()) get() combo is an imperative restatement of what map/filter/orElse/orElseGet/ifPresent do declaratively, only more verbose and prone to forgetting the else branch. Optional as a field/parameter/collection element is an anti-pattern: the class is not Serializable (extra memory and GC overhead), a parameter gains three states (value / empty / null) instead of two, and in collections you should use an empty list or an absent key instead.","d":"Since Java 10 there is opt.orElseThrow() with no argument — it reads clearer than get(); and get() is often flagged in modern reviews as 'forbidden, use orElseThrow'.","note":"⚠️ orElse(value) evaluates its argument eagerly — ALWAYS, even when the Optional is non-empty. So optionalUser.orElse(repository.createDefault()) hits the DB on every call, including when the user was found. For an expensive or side-effecting fallback always use the lazy orElseGet(() -> repository.createDefault()). This is a classic hidden source of extra queries and production bugs.","hook":"Optional is a 'yellow envelope marked may be empty' that the courier (the method) hands you on the way out. Tearing it open with get() without looking is like opening the envelope without checking whether there's a letter inside: it's empty — and you're back on the floor with the same NPE, only now it's called NoSuchElementException.","more":"**Why each point is an anti-pattern:**\n\n- **`get()` without a check.** Throws `NoSuchElementException` — the same fail-fast as an NPE, just 'hidden'. If you check with an `if` anyway, `get()` adds nothing over working with the reference directly. Replace with `orElse`, `orElseGet`, `orElseThrow(() -> new BusinessException(...))`.\n- **`isPresent()` + `get()`.** Imperative code where Optional offers a declarative API: `map`, `flatMap`, `filter`, `ifPresent`, `ifPresentOrElse` (Java 9). The chain reads linearly and leaves no 'forgotten' else branch.\n- **Class field.** `Optional` does not implement `Serializable` — it breaks serializable DTOs/entities. Plus an extra wrapper object per field (memory + GC). A nullable reference or an explicit default value is enough for a field.\n- **Method parameter.** Creates three states: value, `Optional.empty()`, and `null` (the Optional reference itself). The caller still has to null-check the parameter — zero benefit. Prefer a method overload or a nullable argument.\n- **Collection element.** `List<Optional<T>>`, `Map<K, Optional<V>>` are almost always a mistake. Express an empty collection with an empty list, and absence with `containsKey`/`getOrDefault`.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** `Optional.of(x)` throws NPE if `x == null` — for a possibly-null value use `Optional.ofNullable(x)`. And `opt.orElse(heavyCompute())` **always** evaluates its argument, even when the Optional is non-empty; for an expensive fallback use the lazy `orElseGet(() -> heavyCompute())`.","quizCorrect":"Optional is designed as a method return type; get() without a check merely masks an NPE as NoSuchElementException, and isPresent()+get() is a verbose imperative restatement of map/orElse","quizWrong":["Optional should be used as entity field types instead of nullable references — it makes the domain explicit and safe","opt.get() is always safe: on an empty Optional it returns null rather than throwing","Optional in method parameters is a recommended practice because it forces the caller to explicitly pass empty instead of null and makes the absent case impossible to overlook at the call site"],"picCap":"Optional is only for method return; get() and isPresent() chains mask the same NPE"},"jc-optional-orelse":{"q":"orElse vs orElseGet vs orElseThrow — what's the difference? Why is orElse(expensiveCall()) evaluated ALWAYS, even when a value is present?","a":"orElse(T other) takes an already-computed value, so its argument is evaluated BEFORE the method is even entered — Java evaluates arguments before passing them (eager evaluation), and expensiveCall() runs every time, even if the Optional is non-empty and the result is discarded. orElseGet(Supplier) takes a lambda and invokes it LAZILY — only when the value is actually absent (isPresent() == false). orElseThrow(Supplier) is also lazy: it throws the given exception only on an empty Optional, and the no-arg form (Java 10+) throws NoSuchElementException.","d":"Rule of thumb: orElse for cheap constants/literals; orElseGet when the fallback is expensive (DB query, new object, network call) or has side-effects.","note":"⚠️ The most painful trap isn't the 'wasted computation' — it's the hidden side-effect: repo.findById(id).orElse(repo.save(newDefault())) will write the default to the DB on EVERY call, even when the record already exists (the eager argument always runs). A senior must reflexively use orElseGet(() -> repo.save(...)) for anything that allocates, hits the DB/network, or has a side-effect — otherwise you get duplicates, extra INSERTs, and latency regressions under load.","hook":"orElse is a 'just-in-case set table': the chef cooks the dish ALWAYS, even if the guest is already full. orElseGet is 'cook to order': the chef (Supplier) steps up to the stove only when the plate is genuinely empty.","more":"**Root cause — Java's evaluation order.** Method arguments are evaluated BEFORE entering the method (strict/eager evaluation). So in `opt.orElse(expensiveCall())`, `expensiveCall()` runs first, and only then is its result passed into `orElse` — where it may simply be discarded if `opt` is non-empty.\n\n**Signatures (java.util.Optional):**\n- `T orElse(T other)` — takes a value `T`, eager.\n- `T orElseGet(Supplier<? extends T> supplier)` — takes a lambda, lazy (calls `supplier.get()` only when empty).\n- `T orElseThrow(Supplier<? extends X> exSupplier)` — lazy, throws a custom exception.\n- `T orElseThrow()` — Java 10+, no-arg, throws `NoSuchElementException(\"No value present\")`.\n\n**Concrete gotcha — double side-effect and wasted load:**\n- `userRepo.findById(id).orElse(saveDefaultUser())` — `saveDefaultUser()` writes to the DB ALWAYS, even when the user is found. An extra row/INSERT on every successful request.\n- `cache.get(k).orElse(new BigObject())` in a hot loop — `new BigObject()` is created on every iteration, even on a cache hit. GC pressure and latency degradation.\n\n**Correct choice:**\n- Cheap constant / already-computed value / literal &rarr; `orElse`.\n- Expensive call, allocation, DB/network access, side-effect &rarr; `orElseGet` / `orElseThrow`.\n\n**Subtlety:** `orElse(null)` is a legitimate idiom to unwrap `Optional<T>` into a nullable `T`. But `orElseGet(() -> null)` is almost always a design mistake.","quizCorrect":"orElse receives an already-computed value (eager) — the argument is evaluated before the method call; orElseGet receives a Supplier and invokes it lazily only when the Optional is empty","quizWrong":["orElse and orElseGet are semantically identical, differing only in lambda-vs-value syntax","orElse is lazy and orElseGet always pre-computes its argument — so for expensive calls you use orElse and orElseGet only when the default value is already computed and cheap and needs no supplier","orElseGet caches the Supplier's result between calls, while orElse recreates the value each time"],"picCap":"With a non-empty Optional, orElse still executes its argument (eager), while the Supplier in orElseGet/orElseThrow is never touched (lazy)."},"gap-serial-version-uid":{"q":"What is serialVersionUID, and what happens on deserialization if you add a field to the class but leave the UID unchanged — versus if you change it?","a":"serialVersionUID is a long version identifier of a Serializable class that ObjectInputStream compares between the incoming stream and the current class. If you add a field but keep the same UID, deserialization succeeds: the new field gets its default value (null/0/false) — this is a compatible change per the JVM Serialization Spec. If you change the UID, the JVM treats the classes as incompatible and throws java.io.InvalidClassException (\"local class incompatible: stream classdesc serialVersionUID = ... ; local class serialVersionUID = ...\"), even if the field structure is identical.","d":"If the UID is not declared explicitly, the compiler/runtime computes it automatically from the class signature (name, fields, methods, interfaces) via SHA-1, so even adding a private method or changing a modifier can silently break compatibility between builds.","note":"⚠️ The most common trap: not declaring serialVersionUID at all. Then the compiler generates it from the class signature, and any 'harmless' change (a new private method, a lambda, a different javac version) changes the hash → old objects in Redis/Kafka/session/DB stop deserializing with InvalidClassException in production. And the reverse: don't change the UID by hand if you just added a field — you'd be breaking backward compatibility with all previously stored data yourself.","hook":"serialVersionUID is the class's 'version passport' at the deserialization border control. Add a field but keep the same passport — you're let through (the newcomer just gets a blank field). Change the passport — the border officer (ObjectInputStream) turns you away shouting InvalidClassException, even if the face is the same.","more":"**Comparison mechanics.** On `readObject()` the stream carries an `ObjectStreamClass` with the recorded `serialVersionUID`. `ObjectInputStream` takes the local class, computes (or reads the declared) UID, and compares the two. Match — it proceeds to map fields by name and type; mismatch — immediate `InvalidClassException`, before any fields are read.\n\n**What counts as compatible (compatible changes) per the Java Serialization Spec:**\n- adding new fields — the old stream doesn't contain them, so they get defaults on read;\n- removing fields — stream data for them is silently discarded;\n- adding/removing methods, constructors, implemented interfaces (other than `Serializable`/`Externalizable`).\n\n**What is incompatible:**\n- changing the type of an existing field;\n- removing `Serializable` from the class;\n- switching a field `static`/`non-static` or `transient`/`non-transient`;\n- changing the `serialVersionUID` itself.\n\n**Concrete gotcha.** If the UID is **not** declared explicitly, the JVM generates it from a SHA-1 hash of the entire class signature. Building on different javac versions or adding a harmless `private` method yields a **different** auto-generated UID → previously serialized data (in a cache, queue, or DB) suddenly fails to read. That's why you put `private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;` by hand in any Serializable class.\n\n- IDE inspections and `serialver` help generate the UID.\n- Change the UID by hand only when you want to **deliberately** block reading of old data.","quizCorrect":"Same UID + new field → deserialization succeeds, the field gets its default value; changed UID → InvalidClassException.","quizWrong":["Same UID + new field → InvalidClassException, because the field structure changed; changed UID → everything works.","In both cases deserialization fails with NotSerializableException.","Same UID → the new field is initialized from the default constructor; changed UID → the field is simply ignored without error."],"picCap":"Same UID + new field = OK (default); different UID = InvalidClassException."},"gap-serial-deser-security":{"q":"Why is native Java serialization considered dangerous, and how is RCE achieved from deserializing an untrusted stream (gadget chains)?","a":"The danger is that `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` rebuilds the object graph BEFORE any application-level type validation: the attacker controls the bytes, hence which classes get instantiated and which of their magic methods (`readObject`, `readResolve`, `readExternal`, and for proxies/maps `hashCode`/`equals`/`compareTo`) execute during reconstruction. A gadget chain is a sequence of already-present, seemingly-harmless classpath classes (gadgets) where one such callback reflectively invokes the next, and the final link reaches `Runtime.exec()` / `Method.invoke()` / building a `TemplatesImpl` with attacker bytecode. Classic source gadgets: `AnnotationInvocationHandler` + `LazyMap`/`TransformerChain` from commons-collections 3.1, `BadAttributeValueExpException`, JDK7/8 `Templates` chains — all fire by themselves during `readObject`, with no application code invoked.","d":"Key subtlety: the mere act of `readObject` over foreign bytes is vulnerable — you don't need 'dangerous' application classes nor to call the deserialized object; the right gadgets on the classpath suffice, which is why ysoserial generates payloads tailored to specific libraries.","note":"⚠️ Main trap: people put a guard INSIDE their custom `readObject()` or validate the object AFTER deserialization — useless, because the gadget chain fires during graph reconstruction, before the top-level `readObject()` even returns. The only solid barrier is `ObjectInputFilter` (JEP 290, Java 9+, backported to 8u121) with an allowlist by class/depth/size, installed BEFORE reading. And remember: a filter is risk reduction; the proper senior answer is to not accept native serialization from untrusted sources at all, replacing it with JSON/protobuf with an explicit schema.","hook":"A serialized stream isn't 'data' — it's a constructor program: you're not loading an object, you're executing someone else's build script. Handing `readObject()` foreign bytes is like running an .exe mailed to you 'just to see what's inside'. And a gadget chain is dominoes already standing on your classpath — the attacker merely flicks the first one.","more":"**Why the mechanism is dangerous by nature:**\n- `readObject()` creates instances via `sun.reflect.ReflectionFactory` bypassing constructors — normal class invariants don't protect you.\n- Graph reconstruction **itself invokes** callback methods: `readObject`/`readResolve`/`readExternal`, and when rebuilding `HashMap`/`HashSet` also `hashCode()`/`equals()`, when rebuilding `TreeMap` also `compareTo()`. These are the trigger entry points for the chain.\n- The object type comes from the stream (`TC_OBJECT` + class descriptor), not from app expectations — `(MyDto) ois.readObject()` casts AFTER the graph is built and callbacks have already run.\n\n**Anatomy of the classic chain (commons-collections 3.1):**\n- Source: `sun.reflect.annotation.AnnotationInvocationHandler.readObject()` iterates a `Map` and calls `entry.setValue()`.\n- Trigger: `LazyMap` invokes its `Transformer` on access.\n- Chain: `ChainedTransformer` of `ConstantTransformer` + `InvokerTransformer` reflectively builds `Runtime.getRuntime().exec(cmd)`.\n- Sink: `Runtime.exec()` → RCE.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** even if YOU don't use commons-collections, it arrives transitively (Spring, Hibernate, Jenkins historically). `ysoserial` ships dozens of payloads (`CommonsCollections1-7`, `Spring1/2`, `Hibernate1/2`, `JRMPClient`, `URLDNS` for blind detection). The attack surface is more than explicit `readObject`: RMI/JMX, JNDI/LDAP (the same deserialization fed Log4Shell), JMS `ObjectMessage`, Java-based session storage, any endpoint with `Content-Type: application/x-java-serialized-object`.\n\n**Defenses by decreasing reliability:**\n- Don't accept native serialization over untrusted channels at all; move to JSON/protobuf/Avro with a schema.\n- `ObjectInputFilter` (JEP 290): allowlist packages, limits `maxdepth`/`maxrefs`/`maxbytes`; globally `jdk.serialFilter` or a filter factory (JEP 415, Java 17).\n- `serialVersionUID` and `final` fields are about compatibility and invariants, NOT security.","quizCorrect":"The gadget chain executes inside readObject() during graph reconstruction — via magic callback methods (readObject/readResolve, and for collections hashCode/equals/compareTo) of classes already on the classpath, reaching Runtime.exec() via reflection","quizWrong":["RCE is only possible if the stream carries a class implementing Runnable and the application itself calls its run() after deserialization","The vulnerability exists only when SecurityManager is disabled; with SecurityManager on, native deserialization of untrusted data is fully safe, because the sandbox forbids calling Runtime.exec() and blocks every access to the file system and to processes","It's enough to check serialVersionUID and make fields transient — then readObject() cannot instantiate dangerous gadget classes"],"picCap":"The stream controls which classes and callbacks run; the chain of classpath gadgets reaches exec(). The barrier is a class filter installed BEFORE readObject."},"gap-exc-interrupted":{"q":"An InterruptedException was thrown — why can't you just swallow it, and what does it mean to 'restore the interrupt flag' (Thread.currentThread().interrupt())?","a":"When a blocking method (sleep, wait, join, Lock.lockInterruptibly, BlockingQueue.take) throws InterruptedException, the JVM CLEARS the thread's interrupt status back to false. If you swallow the exception in catch, you lose the cancellation signal: the calling code (thread pool, worker loop) no longer knows the thread was asked to stop and keeps running. So in catch you either rethrow the exception, or — if you can't — call Thread.currentThread().interrupt() to set the flag again and give upper layers a chance to shut down cleanly.","d":"Interruption in Java is cooperative: interrupt() doesn't kill the thread, it only sets a flag / wakes a blocking call — the code itself must react to it, otherwise the signal is simply lost.","note":"⚠️ Real-world trap: catch (InterruptedException e) { throw new RuntimeException(e); } is a common 'compromise', but it's often worse than swallowing. The interrupt flag is NOT restored, and the RuntimeException propagates up where it may crash a thread pool or trigger needless retries when the intent was to quietly stop. Correct: first Thread.currentThread().interrupt();, and only then, if you really must abort, throw your own exception.","hook":"An interrupt is a 'time to leave' note stuck on the thread's forehead. When the thread catches InterruptedException, the JVM rips the note off (flag = false). If you silently toss it in the trash (catch {}), the boss above assumes you never got the memo — and keeps you working. interrupt() = sticking the note back on so whoever's above can read it.","more":"**Why swallowing InterruptedException is dangerous**\n\nInterruption in Java is a *cooperative* mechanism. interrupt() doesn't forcibly stop a thread, it only:\n- sets the internal flag interrupted = true;\n- wakes a thread blocked in sleep/wait/join/take/lockInterruptibly by throwing InterruptedException **while clearing the flag back to false**.\n\n**Two correct ways to react in catch:**\n- **Rethrow** — best option if the signature allows (throws InterruptedException). Don't 'wrap' it in a RuntimeException and lose the type.\n- **Restore the flag** — Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); — when the method (e.g. Runnable.run()) can't throw a checked exception. Then upper code checking Thread.interrupted() / isInterrupted(), or the pool itself, will see the signal.\n\n**Concrete gotcha — ExecutorService:**\n- Future.get() itself throws InterruptedException — don't swallow it either.\n- ThreadPoolExecutor on shutdownNow() calls interrupt() on its workers. If your Runnable swallows IE, the thread *won't stop*, and awaitTermination hangs until timeout. With the flag restored, the worker loop (while (!Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted())) exits cleanly.\n\n**Anti-patterns:**\n- catch (InterruptedException e) { /* ignore */ } — the classic bug.\n- catch (InterruptedException e) { log.error(...); } without restoring the flag — also a lost signal.","quizCorrect":"When InterruptedException is thrown the JVM clears the interrupt status, so swallowing loses the cancellation signal; interrupt() sets the flag again for upper layers","quizWrong":["InterruptedException means a fatal thread failure and must always be terminated via System.exit(); the flag is irrelevant here once the exception has been observed by the handler","Thread.currentThread().interrupt() immediately and forcibly stops the current thread, like the deprecated Thread.stop()","Restoring the flag is only needed for logging: the interrupt status has no real effect on pools and loops"],"picCap":"InterruptedException clears the interrupt flag — we restore it so the cancellation signal isn't lost."},"sp-tx-rollback-only":{"q":"An outer @Transactional (REQUIRED) called an inner method, which threw and the caller caught the exception — yet the outer commit fails with UnexpectedRollbackException. Why is the transaction 'marked rollback-only' and how do you fix it properly?","a":"The inner method is also @Transactional with REQUIRED, so it did not start a new physical transaction but joined the existing one. When a RuntimeException escaped it, Spring's interceptor (TransactionInterceptor/TransactionAspectSupport) flagged the single shared physical transaction via setRollbackOnly() — a global rollbackOnly flag in the resource holder. The outer try/catch swallowed the exception, but the flag is already set, so at outer commit AbstractPlatformTransactionManager sees rollbackOnly=true, performs a rollback instead of commit, and throws UnexpectedRollbackException. Proper fix: don't swallow silently — either let the exception propagate, or isolate the inner method as Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW (a separate physical transaction whose rollback doesn't taint the parent), or if the inner rollback is unacceptable, don't annotate it @Transactional / catch the exception INSIDE it before leaving the proxy.","d":"The rollbackOnly flag sticks to the physical transaction, while logical nested REQUIRED transactions merely share it — so a flag set by any participant dooms the whole set to rollback ('participating transaction failed').","note":"⚠️ The main trap: people slap REQUIRES_NEW on the inner method and are surprised the rollback-only flag still sticks — because the method is called via this.inner() within the same bean, and the AOP proxy does NOT intercept self-invocation, so no new transaction is created. The annotation only works when invoked through the proxy (from another bean or via self-injection). Second trap: noRollbackFor helps only if you control the annotation of the method that marks rollback-only — and checked exceptions by default do NOT trigger rollback (only RuntimeException and Error do), which separately confuses people.","hook":"A transaction is like a group bank transfer: one participant shouts 'cancel!' (setRollbackOnly), and the teller at the exit rolls back the WHOLE batch — even if you muffled the shouter with a try/catch. Want his cancellation to be private? Put him in a separate booth (REQUIRES_NEW).","more":"**Under the hood**\n\n- With REQUIRED, Spring distinguishes *logical* from *physical* transactions. The outer method opens the physical tx; the inner REQUIRED merely *participates* in it (logical nesting), creating no physical tx of its own.\n- When the inner method throws an unchecked exception, `TransactionAspectSupport.completeTransactionAfterThrowing()` for a *participating* transaction does not roll back immediately — it sets the global flag via `setRollbackOnly()` on the `DefaultTransactionStatus` / resource holder (for JPA: `JpaTransactionManager` → `EntityTransaction.setRollbackOnly()`).\n- The outer `try/catch` swallows the exception, control reaches the outer commit. `AbstractPlatformTransactionManager.commit()` checks `isGlobalRollbackOnly()` → sees `true` → calls `processRollback()` and, with `failEarlyOnGlobalRollbackOnly`, throws `UnexpectedRollbackException` ('Transaction silently rolled back because it has been marked as rollback-only').\n\n**Fix options (by context)**\n\n- **REQUIRES_NEW** on the inner method — it gets a *separate* physical transaction (the parent is suspended). Its rollback never touches the parent's flag. Cost: genuinely two connections/two transactions, possible deadlocks, and the outer tx 'can't see' what the inner committed until its own end.\n- **Let the exception propagate** — the most honest path: if the rollback is logically mandatory, don't swallow it.\n- **Transaction boundary outside the catch** — run the 'optional' operation through a separate service/method so its failure never enters the current tx's proxy.\n- `@Transactional(noRollbackFor = ...)` marks that this exception should NOT trigger rollback — then no flag is set and the outer commit succeeds.\n\n**Concrete gotcha**\n\n- Self-invocation: if the 'inner' method is called via `this.inner()` in the same bean, the proxy never fires — `@Transactional(REQUIRES_NEW)` is silently ignored and you're back in a single transaction. Use self-injection / extract into a separate bean.","quizCorrect":"The inner REQUIRED joined the same physical transaction; its RuntimeException triggered setRollbackOnly(), and the outer commit saw the flag — you need REQUIRES_NEW or to propagate the exception","quizWrong":["Spring creates a new transaction per @Transactional, and they conflict at commit due to a deadlock on the same row","The outer try/catch rolls back only the inner savepoint, while the outer commit fails from an optimistic-lock version mismatch raised on the entity by another transaction that committed in between","UnexpectedRollbackException always means a transaction timeout — increase @Transactional(timeout) on the outer method"],"picCap":"One rollbackOnly on the shared physical tx dooms the outer commit to rollback; REQUIRES_NEW isolates it."},"sp-aspect-order":{"q":"A single method carries @Transactional, @Cacheable, @Retryable, and a custom @Aspect. In what order are they applied and how do you control it? Why is retry 'inside' the transaction and cache 'outside' a matter of @Order?","a":"Order is set by the advisor chain sorted by `Ordered.getOrder()` / `@Order`: the LOWER the value, the more OUTSIDE it sits (enters the proxy first, returns last). Defaults: `@Cacheable` (CacheInterceptor) = `Ordered.LOWEST_PRECEDENCE`, `@Transactional` also `LOWEST_PRECEDENCE`, `@Retryable` (RetryInterceptor) = `LOWEST_PRECEDENCE - 1`, i.e. retry sits slightly more 'inside'. With equal order the actual ordering is non-deterministic, so you set it explicitly: `@EnableTransactionManagement(order = ...)`, `@EnableCaching(order = ...)`, `@Order` on your aspect. You want cache OUTSIDE (a cache hit opens no transaction and runs no retry), while retry INSIDE a transaction is dangerous: a re-attempt reuses an already rolled-back / marked-rollback transaction.","d":"Actually, for 'retry with a fresh transaction per attempt' you need the opposite — @Retryable OUTSIDE @Transactional, so each attempt gets a fresh `REQUIRES_NEW` context; otherwise after the first rollback the transaction is marked rollback-only and breaks every retry.","note":"⚠️ The classic trap: @Retryable INSIDE @Transactional (retry closer to the method). After the first failed attempt the current transaction is marked `rollback-only`, and all subsequent retries run in that same dead transaction — yielding an `UnexpectedRollbackException` at commit even though 'retry succeeded'. If you need a real retry that rolls back the DB, put @Retryable OUTSIDE and inside call a separate bean method with `@Transactional(propagation = REQUIRES_NEW)`, so each attempt gets a fresh transaction. And remember: self-invocation within the same bean bypasses the proxy — the annotations simply won't fire.","hook":"The proxy is an onion: @Order is the layer number. A small number = the outer skin (outside, cut first). Cache is the outermost skin: if it's dry (a hit), the knife never reaches the transaction core. And you can't shove retry into an already-cut (rollback-only) onion.","more":"**Mechanics.** The AOP proxy wraps the target method in a chain of `MethodInterceptor`s inside `ReflectiveMethodInvocation`. The chain is built from advisors sorted by `AnnotationAwareOrderComparator` on `Ordered.getOrder()` (or `@Order`). Rule: **lower value = higher precedence = closer to the caller (outside)**. `HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE = Integer.MIN_VALUE`, `LOWEST_PRECEDENCE = Integer.MAX_VALUE`.\n\n**Default order of built-in interceptors:**\n- `CacheInterceptor` (`@EnableCaching`) &rarr; `LOWEST_PRECEDENCE`\n- transactional advisor (`@EnableTransactionManagement`) &rarr; `LOWEST_PRECEDENCE`\n- `@Retryable` (Spring Retry, `@EnableRetry`) &rarr; `LOWEST_PRECEDENCE - 1` (slightly more 'inside' than the transaction)\n\n**How to control it explicitly:**\n- `@EnableTransactionManagement(order = 200)`\n- `@EnableCaching(order = 100)`\n- `@EnableRetry(order = 300)`\n- `@Order(...)` / `implements Ordered` on your `@Aspect`\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** with EQUAL order (and Cache and Transaction both default to `LOWEST_PRECEDENCE`!) Spring does NOT guarantee the ordering — it depends on bean registration order / classpath. So you can't rely on 'the default just works right': if it matters that cache sits outside the transaction, give cache a strictly lower order. Otherwise on one environment cache is outside and on another it's inside, and the transaction opens even on a cache hit.","quizCorrect":"The lower the @Order value, the more 'outside' the advice; with equal order the ordering is non-deterministic, so it's set via the order attribute of @EnableCaching/@EnableTransactionManagement","quizWrong":["Order is determined by the alphabetical names of the annotations; @Order on advice is ignored by the framework","The higher the @Order value, the more 'outside' the advice; HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE = the most inner one","All built-in interceptors share the same order and always run strictly in the order the annotations are declared on the method regardless of what any @Order annotation on the aspects might say in the class"],"picCap":"A lower @Order means the outer advice. Cache outside short-circuits the call on a hit; retry inside a TX is dangerous."},"sp-config-props":{"q":"@ConfigurationProperties vs @Value: why is binding a whole prefix better than point injection, what is relaxed binding, and how do you add validation (@Validated) to config?","a":"@ConfigurationProperties binds an entire prefix to a typed POJO as one object: type-safe, supports nested structures, List/Map/Duration/DataSize, IDE metadata, and crucially relaxed binding. @Value('${...}') injects a single property as a string via SpEL — no relaxed binding, no nesting, requires the exact key name. Relaxed binding maps a field name to different key formats: server.port matches serverPort, server-port, SERVER_PORT (env), server.PORT — Spring normalizes via ConfigurationPropertyName. Validation: put @Validated on the @ConfigurationProperties class and JSR-380 annotations (@NotNull, @Min, @Email) on fields — errors are caught at context startup (BindValidationException), not at runtime.","d":"The POJO is registered via @EnableConfigurationProperties(MyProps.class) or @ConfigurationPropertiesScan; with constructor binding (@ConstructorBinding / record) fields become immutable and don't require @Component.","note":"⚠️ For a nested object inside @ConfigurationProperties, JSR-380 validation does NOT cascade automatically: without @Valid on the nested-type field, the annotations (@NotBlank etc.) inside it are ignored — a config with a broken nested block silently passes startup. Second common trap: if Hibernate Validator isn't on the classpath (forgot spring-boot-starter-validation), @Validated and all JSR-380 annotations simply do nothing — with no warning at all.","hook":"@Value is a pipette: you draw one drop at a time, strictly by name. @ConfigurationProperties is a bucket under the 'app.mail.*' tap: you scoop the whole prefix at once, and relaxed binding is an adapter that doesn't care how the tap is spelled (dot, camelCase, or SCREAMING_ENV).","more":"**When to use which:**\n- **@ConfigurationProperties** — for a group of related settings (a whole prefix), complex structures (nested objects, List, Map, Duration, DataSize), when you need validation and IDE autocompletion from spring-boot-configuration-processor.\n- **@Value** — for one or two scattered keys or when you need SpEL (@Value('#{systemProperties[...]}'), expressions, defaults ${key:default}). SpEL @Value supports neither relaxed binding nor validation.\n\n**Binding styles:**\n- Setter-based (mutable POJO with getters/setters) — needs a default constructor.\n- Constructor-based — a record or @ConstructorBinding (with Spring Boot 3 the annotation isn't needed on a record), fields are immutable, **setters are ignored**.\n\n**Relevant gotcha — invisible binding:**\n- If a POJO field has **no setter** (with setter binding) and isn't supplied via the constructor — Spring **silently skips** it, the field stays null/default. No error. So migrating to immutable can easily drop a value.\n- Relaxed binding works **only** for @ConfigurationProperties, **not** for @Value — @Value('${MY_VAR}') requires an exact match with the property name (env vars are picked up as a property source, but without kebab/camel normalization).\n\n**Validation details:**\n- @Validated on the class + JSR-380 (@NotNull, @Min, @Email, @Valid for nested) → on failure Spring throws BindValidationException (wrapped in ConfigurationPropertiesBindException) **at startup**, the app won't come up.\n- You need a **Bean Validation provider** on the classpath (spring-boot-starter-validation / Hibernate Validator), otherwise the annotations are simply ignored without error.","quizCorrect":"@ConfigurationProperties binds a whole prefix to a typed POJO with relaxed binding and @Validated support; @Value injects a single property as a string via SpEL without relaxed binding","quizWrong":["@Value supports relaxed binding and validation via @Validated, while @ConfigurationProperties does not; the difference is only syntax between the two of them and nothing more between the two approaches","Both are equivalent in capabilities, but @ConfigurationProperties works only with .properties and @Value only with .yml","@ConfigurationProperties is validated at runtime on every field access, while @Value is validated at context startup"],"picCap":"One prefix &rarr; typed POJO; relaxed binding maps 4 key formats; @Validated fails the context at startup"},"h-l1-persistence-context":{"q":"What is the persistence context (first-level cache), and why do two find() calls by the same id within one transaction return the SAME object and issue only one SELECT?","a":"The persistence context is the set of managed entities bound to the EntityManager lifecycle (in Spring with @Transactional, one per transaction). Inside it operates the first-level cache: a Map keyed by EntityKey (entity type + id) holding the single instance of each object. The first find() runs a SELECT and stores the entity in that Map; the second find() by the same id finds it in the Map and returns the same reference without hitting the DB. This provides the identity guarantee: within a persistence context exactly one object exists per id (== identity, not just equals).","d":"The L1 cache cannot be disabled — it's built into the EntityManager itself; you can only bypass it via em.clear()/detach() or a native SQL query, which flushes on execution but whose returned managed entities are still taken from L1 by their id.","note":"⚠️ The L1 cache does NOT hand back fresh DB data. If someone modifies a row inside the transaction bypassing your EntityManager (another connection, a native UPDATE, a trigger), a repeated find()/JPQL by that id returns the STALE managed object from the context — Hibernate won't re-read the columns. To get the current state you need em.refresh(entity) (or em.clear()/detach()). This regularly bites in long transactions and in tests where data is patched directly in the DB.","hook":"The persistence context is the transaction's coat check: bring a token (id) the first time and they fetch the item from the warehouse (SELECT); bring the same token again and they hand you the exact same item from the shelf behind the counter, without going back to the warehouse.","more":"**Internals (Hibernate):**\n- The persistence context physically lives in `org.hibernate.engine.spi.PersistenceContext` (impl `StatefulPersistenceContext`) inside the `Session`/`EntityManager`.\n- The key is `EntityKey` = (`id` + `EntityPersister`/type). So `User#7` and `Order#7` are different keys — no collision.\n- This is the **identity guarantee**: within one context `a == b` (not just `a.equals(b)`), which is required for correct dirty checking and cascades.\n\n**L1 vs L2 (`@Cacheable` / Ehcache, Infinispan):**\n- L1 — per EntityManager/transaction, cannot be disabled, short-lived.\n- L2 — shared across the SessionFactory, optional, survives transactions, stores **disassembled (hydrated) state**, not the object itself.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** L1 caches only loads by **primary key** (`find`, `getReference`, association navigation). A JPQL query `SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.id = 7` **always hits the DB** (L1 isn't an index over arbitrary fields), BUT once rows come back, Hibernate reuses the existing managed instance from L1 by id for each — so the reference stays the same, while the fresh column values from the DB are **ignored** in favor of the context's version (the classic 'I changed the DB by hand — JPQL returned the old data' trap).","quizCorrect":"The entity is stored in a Map keyed by (type + id); the second find() locates it there and returns the same reference without a SELECT","quizWrong":["Hibernate caches the SQL query result in the query cache, so the repeated SELECT never reaches the DB a second time within the same session","The second find() still issues a SELECT, but the DB serves it from its page cache, so it's fast","JPA compares objects via equals()/hashCode() and reuses the first instance on a match"],"picCap":"The first find() loads from the DB and stores it in L1 by EntityKey; the second takes the same reference from L1 without a SELECT."},"h-l2-cache-query-cache":{"q":"How does the second-level cache (L2) differ from L1, why does it live at the SessionFactory level, and when does it actually help versus only hurt (plus what is the query cache)?","a":"The L1 cache (persistence context) lives within a single Session/EntityManager and is always on: it guarantees identity and dirty checking, and dies with the transaction. L2 lives at the SessionFactory level and is shared across all the application's sessions, so it stores entity data (dehydrated state as a CacheEntry, not managed objects) and survives transaction close — you must enable it explicitly (hibernate.cache.use_second_level_cache + @Cacheable/@Cache with a provider like Ehcache/Infinispan/Caffeine). It really helps on read-mostly reference data with a high read/write ratio, accessed by PK or via @OneToOne/@ManyToOne; it hurts on frequently changing data (constant invalidations), in a cluster without a distributed cache (node divergence), and with NONSTRICT_READ_WRITE, where dirty reads are possible. The query cache (hibernate.cache.use_query_cache + setCacheable(true)) caches only the list of result identifiers and pulls the entities themselves from L2, so without L2 enabled on the entity it is nearly useless and easily produces N+1 and an invalidation avalanche.","d":"The query cache is invalidated via UpdateTimestampsCache: any write to a table involved in the query stamps its timestamp and invalidates ALL cached queries over that table — so on frequently written tables it nets out negative.","note":"⚠️ The most common trap: L2 caches an entity but NOT its collections. Caching a @OneToMany requires a separate @Cache annotation on the collection itself — otherwise, on an entity hit, the collection is still loaded from the DB. And the collection cache stores only the ids of the related entities: for the collection to actually be read from cache, the child entity must also be @Cacheable + @Cache. Miss one of the three parts and the 'cached' aggregate still hits the DB, and you sit there wondering why the SQL queries didn't disappear.","hook":"L1 is your personal shopping basket (one trip, everyone has their own). L2 is the store's shared warehouse: goods sit there for all shoppers and don't vanish when you leave. The query cache isn't goods at all — just a list of barcodes ('what to grab'); with the barcodes you walk to the same warehouse (L2). If the warehouse is empty, the barcode list is useless.","more":"**Key L1 vs L2 differences:**\n- **L1 (persistence context):** always on, scope = Session/EntityManager, holds managed objects (identity guarantee, dirty checking), cleared on `clear()`/`close()`/end of tx. This is not an 'optimization' but a correctness mechanism of the ORM.\n- **L2:** scope = SessionFactory, opt-in. Stores not objects but dehydrated state (`CacheEntry` — value array + version), because one object cannot be shared across threads. On a hit Hibernate `hydrate`s a fresh managed instance into the current Session.\n\n**Concurrency strategies (`@Cache(usage=...)`):**\n- `READ_ONLY` — for immutable reference data, fastest.\n- `NONSTRICT_READ_WRITE` — cache invalidated AFTER commit, a dirty-read window; acceptable only if updates are rare and divergence is tolerable.\n- `READ_WRITE` — soft-lock on write, near-transactional, but no XA.\n- `TRANSACTIONAL` — only with JTA and a transaction-capable cache (Infinispan).\n\n**Concrete query cache gotcha:**\nYou set `setCacheable(true)` but forgot to make the entity `@Cacheable`. The query cache stores **only the list of ids**. For each entity miss Hibernate goes to the DB one by one — you get N+1 on top of a 'cached' query, which is slower than a plain `JOIN FETCH`.\n\n**Cluster:**\nA local L2 (Caffeine/Ehcache local) across multiple pods = desync: pod A updated and invalidated its copy, pod B holds a stale one. You need a distributed/replicated cache (Infinispan, Hazelcast) or an invalidation mode.","quizCorrect":"L1 lives within a single Session and is always on; L2 is shared across sessions at the SessionFactory level, stores dehydrated entity state (CacheEntry), and survives transaction close, but must be explicitly enabled","quizWrong":["L2 is just a faster version of L1 at the same Session level; the only difference is the provider (Ehcache vs the built-in one), and the visibility scope is the same for both of them in every single case and for every entity type","L1 stores SQL query results while L2 stores managed entities; both are on by default and require no annotations","L2 caches data at the level of an individual transaction and is automatically cleared on commit, so dirty reads between sessions are impossible"],"picCap":"L1 is private to a Session; L2 is shared at the SessionFactory. Helps on read-mostly by PK, hurts on frequent UPDATEs. The query cache holds only ids, pulling entity bodies from L2."},"h-entity-equals-hashcode":{"q":"How do you correctly write equals()/hashCode() for a JPA entity, and why does a generated @Id break a HashSet if you put a not-yet-persisted entity into it?","a":"Before flush a transient entity has id == null; after the INSERT the database assigns a value. A HashSet places the object into a bucket by hashCode() at the moment of add(); if hashCode depends on id, then after flush it changes, the object stays in the old bucket — and contains()/remove() can no longer find it. Correct approach: hashCode() returns a CONSTANT (usually based on getClass()), while equals() compares by a business key or by id with a null check; you cannot rely on the raw id.","d":"Vlad Mihalcea's canon: equals/hashCode by the natural (business) key; if there is none — equals by id, and hashCode = constant (e.g. getClass().hashCode()) so it stays stable across the entire entity lifecycle.","note":"⚠️ Lombok @Data / @EqualsAndHashCode on a JPA entity is a classic landmine. @Data generates hashCode over ALL fields, including lazy @OneToMany collections: touching them triggers a load (or LazyInitializationException outside a session), and the hashCode itself becomes mutable and recursive (entity ↔ collection). Never put @Data/default @EqualsAndHashCode on an entity — use only an explicit business key or the 'id-based equals + constant hashCode' pattern.","hook":"You put the entity into a HashSet — it registers in 'building #null'. Then the DB gives it id=42, it 'moves to building 42', but physically stayed in the old one — so the mailman contains() knocks on 42 and finds nobody.","more":"**Root cause — a mutable hashCode on a managed entity.**\n\n- `HashSet`/`HashMap` compute the bucket = `hash & (n-1)` **at the moment of add/put** and never recompute it on their own.\n- If `hashCode()` is tied to a `@GeneratedValue` id, then a transient entity has one value (id=null → e.g. 0), and after `flush()` the DB assigns the id and `hashCode()` returns a different value. The object physically stayed in the old bucket → `contains`, `remove`, uniqueness iteration break.\n\n**Three valid strategies (per Vlad Mihalcea):**\n\n- **Business key (best):** there is a natural key (ISIN, email, contract number) — equals/hashCode by it. Always stable.\n- **id + constant hashCode:** `equals()` by id (with `id != null` and a `getClass()`/Hibernate.getClass-for-proxy check), `hashCode()` = `getClass().hashCode()` — a constant. Note: `@EqualsAndHashCode(of = \"id\")` does NOT do this — Lombok will compute hashCode from id, which is exactly the trap.\n- **UUID assigned in code before persist** (`@Id UUID`, not DB-generated) — id is stable from `new` onward, so equals/hashCode by id are safe and ideal.\n\n**Concrete proxy gotcha:** in equals you must not write `this.getClass() == obj.getClass()` — for a lazy Hibernate proxy the class is `Entity$HibernateProxy$xxx`, so the entity won't equal its own proxy. Use `Hibernate.getClass(this) == Hibernate.getClass(obj)` or compare via unproxy.","quizCorrect":"hashCode() must return a constant (based on getClass()), while equals() compares by a business key or id with a null check — so that hashCode does not change after the id is assigned on flush","quizWrong":["It's enough to generate equals/hashCode by @Id via the IDE — Hibernate itself guarantees id stability","hashCode() should be computed over all entity fields, including @OneToMany collections, for maximum precision even when those collections have not been initialised yet and remain uninitialised proxies at that moment","There is no problem: on flush the HashSet automatically rehashes all elements by their new hashCodes"],"picCap":"id appears only after flush → hashCode jumps → the object is 'lost' in the HashSet; a constant hashCode fixes it"},"g-db-normalization-forms":{"q":"Explain 1NF / 2NF / 3NF on a single Orders-table example. What exactly is violated at each step, and why normalize a schema at all?","a":"Take a 'flat' table Orders(order_id, product_id, product_list, qty, customer_id, customer_city, city_region) with PK (order_id, product_id). **1NF** requires atomic values: a product_list column with comma-separated items violates 1NF — split each product into its own row. **2NF** forbids a non-key attribute depending on PART of a composite key: customer_id depends only on order_id (part of the key), not on the whole (order_id, product_id) — a 2NF violation, move it to Orders. **3NF** forbids transitive dependencies of non-key attributes: city_region depends on customer_city, not directly on the key (order_id → customer_city → city_region) — move it to a City lookup. We normalize to eliminate insert/update/delete anomalies and duplication, not 'for elegance'.","d":"Each higher form strictly includes the previous: a 3NF schema is automatically in 2NF and 1NF; 2NF and 3NF only matter with a COMPOSITE key or a transitive chain — with a simple single-column PK no partial dependency can exist, so the table is at least in 2NF right away.","note":"⚠️ Normalization is NOT always the right endpoint. In OLTP systems you aim for 3NF/BCNF, but in analytics/reporting and under heavy read load you deliberately **denormalize** (star/snowflake, duplicating city_region into the order row) to kill expensive JOINs. A classic junior trap is 'everything always in 3NF'; a senior knows normalization optimizes integrity and writes while denormalization optimizes read speed, and chooses consciously per workload. Another trap: 3NF is not BCNF — interviewers love asking about overlapping composite candidate keys, where a schema is in 3NF but NOT in BCNF.","hook":"1-DON'T cut, 2-DON'T halve, 3-DON'T leapfrog. 1NF: cut lists into atoms. 2NF: a non-key attribute must not depend on HALF of a composite key. 3NF: an attribute must not depend on the key THROUGH another attribute (no middleman jump). The oath: 'every non-key attribute depends on the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key'.","more":"**Walkthrough on the Orders table:**\n\n- **1NF (atomicity):** violated if a cell holds a list (`'A,B'`) or there are repeating column groups (`product1, product2, product3`). Fix — one row per product. This makes the PK composite: `(order_id, product_id)`.\n- **2NF (no partial dependencies):** only relevant with a composite key. `customer_id`, `customer_city` depend only on `order_id` — part of the key, not the pair. Move them to an `Order` table with PK `order_id`. What remains is `OrderItem(order_id, product_id, qty)`.\n- **3NF (no transitive dependencies):** `city_region` functionally depends on `customer_city`, which depends on the key. The chain `order_id → city → region` is transitive. Move it to a `City(city, region)` lookup.\n\n**The concrete gotcha — anomalies are the 'why':**\n\n- **Update anomaly:** if a city's region is stored in every order row and the city is renamed/re-assigned, you must update thousands of rows; miss one and you get inconsistency.\n- **Insert anomaly:** you can't register a new city-region until there is at least one order for it (without NULL hacks).\n- **Delete anomaly:** deleting the last order in a city loses the fact that the city and its region ever existed.\n\n**Interview subtlety:** 3NF doesn't catch every anomaly — there's **BCNF** (stricter than 3NF: every determinant of a functional dependency must be a superkey). 3NF can miss the case where a non-key attribute determines part of the key (overlapping candidate keys). Beyond that come 4NF (multivalued dependencies) and 5NF — but in practice you stop at 3NF/BCNF.","quizCorrect":"2NF: a non-key attribute must not depend on PART of a composite key (partial dependency)","quizWrong":["2NF: all column values must be atomic, no lists or repeating groups","2NF: a non-key attribute must not transitively depend on the key via another non-key attribute","2NF: every table must have a surrogate integer primary key"],"picCap":"One order splits into 4 tables: each form removes one type of dependency (atomicity &rarr; partial &rarr; transitive)."},"g-db-denormalization":{"q":"The schema is normalized to 3NF, but read queries with lots of JOINs are slow. When do you deliberately denormalize, what do you pay for it, and how do you keep the data consistent?","a":"You denormalize when reads dominate and JOINs over large tables have become a bottleneck that indexes (including covering indexes) and query rewriting can no longer fix. The price is write anomalies: the same data now lives in several places, so every write must update all copies atomically or they drift apart (the update/insert/delete anomalies that 3NF existed to prevent). Consistency is held either synchronously in the same transaction (triggers, updating duplicates in one UPDATE) or asynchronously via materialized views with REFRESH, CDC/outbox, or scheduled recompute — accepting eventual consistency. The core principle: denormalization is a deliberate trade of read speed for write complexity, not a sign you 'forgot to normalize'.","d":"Before duplicating columns, exhaust the cheap options: covering/partial indexes, query rewriting, materialized views (in Postgres they don't auto-refresh — you need REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY), and denormalizing aggregates (counters, sums) rather than raw rows.","note":"⚠️ The most common trap is to duplicate data but forget the update path: you add a `customer_name` column to `orders` for speed, then when the customer renames themselves in `customers`, old orders keep the stale name and nobody notices for months. If you denormalize, decide up front WHO and HOW synchronizes the copy (trigger, CDC, job), and plan for reconciliation. A separate landmine: `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW` without `CONCURRENTLY` takes an `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock and blocks reads of the view during the recompute.","hook":"3NF is a warehouse with one master copy of each part: fix it in one place. Denormalization is handing out copies of your passport to every branch office: getting a certificate is faster, but when you change your surname you must visit ALL branches, or some keep the old one.","more":"**When denormalization is justified**\n- Read:write is heavily skewed toward reads, and data changes rarely (reference data, historical snapshots).\n- The JOIN is over tables that don't fit in memory/cache, and `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` shows an expensive hash/merge join, not just a missing index.\n- Analytics/reports where a data lag is acceptable (eventual consistency is fine).\n\n**What exactly you pay (the write anomalies normal forms exist to prevent)**\n- **Update anomaly** — a value must be changed in every copy; miss one and you get drift.\n- **Insert anomaly** — you can't add a fact without duplicating context.\n- **Delete anomaly** — deleting a row loses data that lived only in it.\n- Larger size and heavier writes (more bytes, more indexes to update).\n\n**How to hold consistency (strongest to weakest)**\n- Synchronously in the same transaction: one `UPDATE` touching both original and duplicate; or `AFTER UPDATE` triggers.\n- Generated/computed columns — the DB maintains the derived value (`GENERATED ALWAYS AS ... STORED`).\n- Materialized view + `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY` (requires a unique index on the view).\n- Asynchronously: CDC (Debezium) / transactional outbox / Kafka → a projector updates the denormalized read model (essentially CQRS).\n\n**A concrete gotcha**\nDenormalize *aggregates and redundant columns deliberately*, but don't turn it into a second copy of the schema. The classic case is a `comments_count` counter on `posts`: under concurrent load `UPDATE posts SET comments_count = comments_count + 1` creates a hot row and serializes all comment inserts onto one row (lock contention). Fixes are buffering/sharding the counter or periodic recompute — i.e. denormalization spawned a new write-side performance problem.","quizCorrect":"Reads dominate, JOINs are the bottleneck and indexes can't fix it; you pay with write anomalies and must explicitly sync duplicates (triggers/CDC/materialized view), accepting eventual consistency","quizWrong":["Denormalization is always better for performance, so production drops 3NF by default and normalizes only in analytics where read speed matters less than storage where the extra storage cost is considered acceptable in exchange","Just declare a FOREIGN KEY with ON UPDATE CASCADE — the DBMS will sync all duplicated columns across tables itself","Denormalization speeds up both reads and writes at once and needs no extra sync logic as long as ACID is enabled"],"picCap":"We duplicate name into orders for fast reads, but must keep the copy fresh synchronously/asynchronously — otherwise it drifts."},"g-db-clustered-index":{"q":"Clustered vs non-clustered index — what's the difference, and why does Postgres essentially have no 'clustered index like in MySQL/SQL Server' (the PK does not order the heap)?","a":"A clustered index defines the physical on-disk order of rows: its B-tree leaves hold the actual rows (InnoDB always clusters by PK, so the table IS the PK index). A non-clustered (secondary) index stores the key plus a pointer to the row, adding an extra hop. In Postgres all tables are heaps (unordered piles), and every index — including the PRIMARY KEY — is always secondary: a B-tree leaf stores the key plus the physical address ctid (page, offset). The CLUSTER command only physically re-sorts the heap by an index once and does not maintain that order on subsequent INSERT/UPDATE.","d":"Consequence: in InnoDB a secondary index stores the PK value (not a pointer), so a lookup through it does a double traversal — secondary B-tree, then PK B-tree (bookmark lookup); in Postgres all indexes are symmetric and lead to a ctid.","note":"⚠️ In InnoDB a 'fat' or random PK (e.g. UUIDv4) is double trouble: (1) it is copied into EVERY secondary index, bloating them, and (2) random values cause page splits when inserting into the clustered index. Postgres avoids this (secondary indexes store ctid, not the PK), but in exchange the PK gives no 'free' ordered access — range locality must be paid for manually (CLUSTER/VACUUM/BRIN). Changing your key strategy without understanding the target DB's storage model is a classic MySQL↔Postgres migration mistake.","hook":"A Postgres table is a pile of stuff dumped in a warehouse, and an index is a catalog with shelf coordinates (ctid). MySQL/InnoDB is a book sorted alphabetically: the book itself is the PK index, and any other index first finds the 'page number' (PK), then you flip to it.","more":"**Core difference — where the row data lives:**\n\n- **Clustered index (InnoDB, SQL Server CLUSTERED):** the B-tree leaf = the row itself. The table is physically sorted by the clustering key. So an InnoDB table without an explicit PK still clusters by the first UNIQUE NOT NULL column or a hidden 6-byte `DB_ROW_ID`.\n- **Non-clustered / secondary index:** the leaf = key + reference to the row. In InnoDB the reference is the **PK value** (so a fat PK bloats every secondary index). In SQL Server it is a RID (if heap) or the clustering key. In Postgres it is the `ctid`.\n\n**Why Postgres has no 'clustered' index:**\n\n- Storage is a **heap**: rows sit in insertion/space-reuse order (after VACUUM), not by key.\n- **All** indexes (including PRIMARY KEY) are secondary and equal: each leaf stores the indexed key + `ctid`. The PK index gets no physical-order privilege.\n- `CLUSTER table USING idx` is a **one-shot** operation: it rewrites the heap in index order but does NOT keep it ordered afterward. New rows land in arbitrary free space. You must re-run it periodically (and it takes an `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock).\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** a developer with a MySQL background expects a range scan over the PK in Postgres to be nearly free (like a clustered scan). But in a heap, rows adjacent by PK are physically scattered → each index tuple points to a random heap page → lots of random I/O and low `correlation` in `pg_stats`. Fixed with `CLUSTER` or by maintaining order via BRIN/ordered inserts — but that is manual work, not automatic as in InnoDB.","quizCorrect":"In Postgres every index (including the PK) is secondary and points to a ctid; CLUSTER only re-sorts the heap once and does not maintain order.","quizWrong":["In Postgres the PRIMARY KEY automatically clusters the table just like InnoDB, but only for integer keys. Physical row order changes along with it.","Postgres keeps the heap permanently sorted by the PK, which is why it has no separate CLUSTER command.","A non-clustered index in InnoDB stores a raw row pointer (RID), so it needs no PK lookup."],"picCap":"InnoDB: PK index leaf = the row, secondary goes via the PK. Postgres: all indexes &rarr; ctid &rarr; a shared heap."},"g-db-ranking-functions":{"q":"ROW_NUMBER() vs RANK() vs DENSE_RANK() — how do they differ on ties, and how do you use them to take the 'top-1 trade per client' or deduplicate rows?","a":"All three are ranking window functions computing a row's position within a PARTITION BY section ordered by ORDER BY. ROW_NUMBER() always yields a unique sequential number (1,2,3,4), breaking ties arbitrarily; RANK() assigns the same rank to equal values and leaves gaps (1,1,3,4); DENSE_RANK() also gives ties the same rank but with no gaps (1,1,2,3). For 'top-1 trade per client' use ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY client_id ORDER BY amount DESC) and filter = 1 in an outer query. For deduplication — the same ROW_NUMBER() over the business key in PARTITION BY, then drop/keep rows with number > 1.","d":"A window function can't sit in WHERE/HAVING of the same SELECT (it's evaluated after them), so the rn = 1 filter is wrapped in a subquery/CTE or done via QUALIFY (BigQuery, Snowflake, ClickHouse).","note":"⚠️ ROW_NUMBER() without a fully deterministic ORDER BY (with a unique tie-breaker like id) returns a NON-DETERMINISTIC result: on an amount tie the engine may assign '1' to different rows across runs. In production this gives a 'jumping top-1' and non-reproducible deduplication — always add a unique column (PK) at the end of ORDER BY.","hook":"A sports podium: two runners finished second. ROW_NUMBER is the perfectionist judge — still assigns 2 and 3 (by photo finish). RANK follows Olympic rules: both get silver (2,2), no bronze, next is 4th. DENSE_RANK is the kind judge: both 2nd, but bronze isn't lost, next is 3rd.","more":"**Tie behavior (ORDER BY amount DESC, values 100, 90, 90, 80):**\n\n- **ROW_NUMBER()** → 1, 2, 3, 4 — always unique, ties broken non-deterministically (if ORDER BY has no tie-breaker).\n- **RANK()** → 1, 2, 2, 4 — equal values share a rank, next rank = position (a 'gap' at 3).\n- **DENSE_RANK()** → 1, 2, 2, 3 — equal values share a rank, next rank with no gap.\n\n**Top-1 per client:**\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM (\n  SELECT t.*, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n    PARTITION BY client_id ORDER BY amount DESC, id DESC) AS rn\n  FROM trades t\n) s WHERE rn = 1;\n```\n\n**Deduplication (keep 1 row per business key):**\n```sql\nDELETE FROM trades WHERE id IN (\n  SELECT id FROM (\n    SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY client_id, isin, trade_date\n      ORDER BY created_at DESC) AS rn\n    FROM trades\n  ) s WHERE rn > 1\n);\n```\n\n**Main gotcha:** if you need ALL rows with the max value (several clients tied on top amount), ROW_NUMBER() keeps only one random row — here you need RANK() = 1 or DENSE_RANK() = 1, otherwise you silently lose data.","quizCorrect":"ROW_NUMBER() yields unique numbers and breaks ties arbitrarily; RANK() leaves gaps after equal ranks, DENSE_RANK() does not","quizWrong":["RANK() yields unique numbers, while ROW_NUMBER() and DENSE_RANK() assign the same rank on ties within the same partition of the window","All three behave identically on equal values; the only difference is the returned number type","DENSE_RANK() leaves gaps after a tie, while RANK() removes them — that's their only difference"],"picCap":"On a tie (90=90) ROW_NUMBER breaks it arbitrarily, RANK gives the same rank and skips the next, DENSE_RANK has no skips."},"g-db-gap-next-key-locks":{"q":"By what exact mechanism does a database prevent phantom reads at Repeatable Read / Serializable — what are gap locks and next-key locks (InnoDB) and predicate locks (PG SSI)?","a":"InnoDB locks not just existing rows but the 'gaps' between index values. A gap lock locks the open interval between two adjacent index keys (e.g. (10,20)), forbidding inserts into it; a next-key lock = a record lock on the row PLUS a gap lock on the gap before it — this is the default mode on REPEATABLE READ, and it's what kills phantoms. PostgreSQL takes a different route: at SERIALIZABLE it uses SSI (Serializable Snapshot Isolation) with predicate locks (SIREAD locks) — these are non-blocking 'tracking' markers on read rows/pages/index ranges; the engine detects dangerous cycles of rw-conflict dependencies and rolls back one transaction with serialization_failure (SQLSTATE 40001).","d":"The key difference: InnoDB prevents phantoms pessimistically (locks the gap, the insert waits), while PG SSI is optimistic (blocks no one on reads, but may roll back at commit).","note":"⚠️ Gap locks are taken ONLY when the predicate runs over an index. If `WHERE non_indexed_col = 5` isn't covered by an index, InnoDB scans and next-key-locks effectively the whole row range (up to the entire table), not one 'logical' row — this destroys concurrency and breeds deadlocks. Gap locks are also NOT taken for unique-index equality lookups (`WHERE pk = 5`) — a record lock suffices there. Many assume 'the row matching the condition' is locked, when in fact an index gap is.","hook":"InnoDB puts a 'barrier on the empty road' between cars (the gap) — no one can drive in; PG SSI just photographs who drove where and only at the finish line disqualifies whoever caused the crash (rollback).","more":"**InnoDB — mechanics:**\n- **Record lock** — locks the index record of an existing row.\n- **Gap lock** — locks an open interval between keys; on its own it only forbids inserts, and gap locks from different transactions on the same gap are **compatible** (don't conflict with each other).\n- **Next-key lock** — record + gap before the record; the default mode on REPEATABLE READ. Because of it, `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` over a range blocks inserts into that range.\n- On **READ COMMITTED**, gap locks are largely disabled (kept only for FK/duplicate checks) — so RC gives higher concurrency but allows phantoms.\n\n**PostgreSQL — SSI:**\n- Works only at the **SERIALIZABLE** level. At REPEATABLE READ, PG uses plain snapshot isolation — which already prevents SQL-standard phantoms but allows write-skew anomalies.\n- SIREAD locks (predicate locks) live in shared memory, have tuple/page/relation granularity, and can 'escalate' under memory pressure, raising the rate of false serialization failures.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** on InnoDB REPEATABLE READ, `DELETE FROM t WHERE id BETWEEN 10 AND 20` takes next-key locks over the whole range — two such transactions touching rows in opposite order easily hit a **deadlock**, even if the deleted row sets don't physically overlap (they conflict on the gaps).","quizCorrect":"A next-key lock = a record lock on the row + a gap lock on the gap before it; PG SSI uses non-blocking SIREAD predicates and rolls back a transaction on a dangerous rw-dependency cycle","quizWrong":["Both InnoDB and PostgreSQL prevent phantoms purely via MVCC snapshots, with no gap locking whatsoever","A gap lock locks an entire existing row, while a next-key lock locks only the table's primary key","On SERIALIZABLE, PostgreSQL takes true predicate locks and blocks competing INSERTs exactly like InnoDB, which makes the two engines behave identically under concurrent inserts on the same rows"],"picCap":"InnoDB locks the gap (insert waits) · PG SSI marks reads and rolls back at commit"},"de-dynamic-dispatch":{"q":"Static vs dynamic binding: why is overload resolution done at compile time while overriding is resolved at runtime? What does a call to an overloaded method print if the argument is declared as Object but is actually a String?","a":"Overloading is resolved at compile time by the STATIC (declared) type of the argument — the compiler picks a concrete signature and embeds a symbolic reference via invokevirtual/invokestatic. Overriding is resolved at runtime by the ACTUAL object type through virtual dispatch (vtable): invokevirtual finds the real implementation in the object's class. So with Object x = \"hello\"; p(x) selects the p(Object) overload and prints \"Object: hello\", because x's static type is Object, not String.","d":"To hit p(String) you must change the static type: either declare String x, or cast explicitly — p((String) x); the cast changes what the compiler sees when choosing the signature.","note":"⚠️ Classic null pain: p(null) makes the compiler pick the most specific overload (p(String) is more specific than p(Object)), but with two unrelated same-level overloads — p(String) and p(Integer) — you get a compile error 'reference to p is ambiguous'. And equals(MyType) instead of equals(Object) is a silent bug: it compiles, but collections (HashMap/HashSet) call equals(Object) via the override contract, so your overload is never invoked and objects aren't found in the map.","hook":"Overloading looks at the variable's PASSPORT (what the declaration says); overriding looks at the object's FACE (who actually showed up). The passport is checked at the gate (compile time), the face only once inside (runtime).","more":"**Why the JVM works this way:**\n\n- **Overloading** means DIFFERENT methods with the same name but different signatures. During method resolution (JLS §15.12) the compiler picks the most specific applicable signature strictly by the static argument types and embeds it in bytecode as a symbolic reference (`p(Ljava/lang/Object;)V`). At runtime the choice is already made — nothing left to decide.\n- **Overriding** means ONE signature, different implementations across the hierarchy. `invokevirtual` at runtime takes the object pointer, finds its class, and dispatches through the method table (vtable) to the actual implementation.\n\n**Concrete gotcha — `equals`:**\n```java\nObject key = \"abc\";\n// HashMap calls key.equals(other) — this is an override, works by the real String type\n// BUT if you write boolean equals(MyType o) — that's an OVERLOAD, not an override!\n@Override\npublic boolean equals(Object o) { ... } // correct\npublic boolean equals(MyType o) { ... }  // trap: collections call equals(Object), your method never runs\n```\n\n**Invocation opcodes:** `invokevirtual` (virtual methods, override), `invokestatic` (static — always by static type), `invokespecial` (private/constructors/super — no virtual dispatch), `invokeinterface`, `invokedynamic` (lambdas). Static methods are NOT overridden — they are hidden (hiding) and also resolved by static type.","quizCorrect":"p(Object) — chosen by static type at compile time, prints \"Object: hello\"","quizWrong":["p(String) — at runtime the JVM sees the real String type and picks the exact overload","p(String), because overloading is virtual too and dispatches via the vtable","Compile error: the call is ambiguous between p(Object) and p(String)"],"picCap":"Overloading is decided by the compiler from the declared type (Object); overriding by the JVM from the actual type."},"de-chain-of-responsibility":{"q":"The Chain of Responsibility pattern — what does it solve? Why is a chain of handlers better than a big if/else, and where do you already work with it without calling it by name (Servlet/OncePerRequestFilter, Netty pipeline, interceptors)?","a":"Chain of Responsibility breaks the rigid coupling between sender and a concrete handler: the request travels down a chain of independent handlers, each deciding to handle it, delegate further, or stop. Unlike a monolithic if/else, the chain is Open/Closed — a new step is added as a separate class without touching existing ones, order is declared (config/@Order), and each handler is tested and reused in isolation. In practice this is javax.servlet.Filter/FilterChain and Spring OncePerRequestFilter, Netty ChannelPipeline with ChannelHandlers, Spring MVC HandlerInterceptor, OkHttp/Spring ClientHttpRequestInterceptor, and Spring Security SecurityFilterChain.","d":"Key difference between implementations: the 'pure' GoF variant passes the request only to the next link (next.handle()), while pipeline variants (Filter, Netty, interceptors) give a two-way traversal — code before chain.doFilter() is the 'descent' (pre-processing), after it the 'ascent' (post-processing), which lets one filter both measure latency and mutate the response.","note":"⚠️ In a Servlet/Spring Filter, forgetting to call chain.doFilter(req, res) silently breaks the chain: the request never reaches the controller, later filters and the DispatcherServlet itself don't run, and the client gets an empty 200 with no body and no explicit error. Symmetrically dangerous: calling doFilter twice, or continuing to write to the response after delegating — that's an IllegalStateException/corrupted response. And remember: filter order (@Order, FilterRegistrationBean.setOrder) is part of the contract; a CORS/auth filter placed in the wrong spot breaks security.","hook":"The chain is airport border control: passport, screening, gate. Each officer either lets you through or turns you back (early return), and no officer knows the 'whole' procedure — only the neighbor to their right.","more":"**What the pattern actually solves:**\n- Decouples sender and receiver — the sender doesn't know which or how many handlers will process the request.\n- Replaces an if/else if/else 'wall' with a list of objects where the condition lives inside each handler.\n- Gives **Open/Closed**: adding/removing/reordering a step = adding a class, not editing an existing method.\n\n**Two chain modes (important for interviews):**\n- **Stop-on-first** (classic GoF): the first matching handler processes and stops the chain — log levels, command parsing, fail-fast validators.\n- **Pass-through pipeline** (Filter/Netty/interceptors): the request passes through ALL links, each may enrich the context — auth, CORS, gzip, tracing, rate-limit.\n\n**Concrete gotcha — Netty ChannelPipeline:**\nIn Netty, order and direction depend on the handler type. ChannelInboundHandlers fire in insertion order (head → tail), while ChannelOutboundHandlers fire in **reverse** (tail → head). Put an encoder/decoder in the wrong order relative to the business handler and you get either a ClassCastException on an Object that wasn't decoded yet, or a silently 'swallowed' write because the event never reached the socket. Here the chain is also bidirectional, which reliably trips up newcomers.\n\n**When NOT to use it:** if there are always 2-3 steps and they never change, a plain if/else is more honest and readable. The pattern pays off when links are dynamic, reusable, or configured externally.","quizCorrect":"The request travels down a chain of independent handlers; each decides to handle, delegate further, or stop — this decouples the sender from the concrete handler and gives Open/Closed","quizWrong":["It's a way to guarantee that EXACTLY one handler out of all registered ones必ず processes every request","The handler chain always executes all links in parallel on separate threads to speed up processing","The pattern centralizes all branching logic in a single dispatcher that picks the right handler via a switch, which keeps every branch of the decision visible in one place for a future reader"],"picCap":"Monolithic if/else vs. a chain of independent links: each handler passes on or stops (early-return); the pipeline variant adds a descent (pre) and ascent (post)."},"de-service-locator":{"q":"Service Locator vs Dependency Injection — both 'fetch' a dependency from the outside. Why is Service Locator considered an antipattern while DI is preferred? How exactly does it hide dependencies and break testability?","a":"With DI, dependencies arrive through the constructor — they're explicit in the signature, the compiler guarantees the object can't be built without them, and in tests you just pass mocks. Service Locator instead pulls the dependency from inside a method (`locator.get(Foo.class)`), so it's invisible in the class's public API: you can't tell from the constructor what the class actually needs. That's the 'hidden dependency' — it only surfaces at runtime as an `NPE`/'not registered' exception, not at compile time. Testability breaks because to test you must configure a global/static locator (often a singleton), which is shared mutable state that leaks between tests and requires teardown.","d":"Key nuance: Service Locator shifts the coupling from 'class → concrete dependency' to 'class → the locator itself', meaning EVERY consumer is coupled to the locator infrastructure — a global coupling point you can't remove locally.","note":"⚠️ Main trap: in Spring/Quarkus people think 'we have a DI container, so we have no Service Locator.' Then the code contains `applicationContext.getBean(X.class)`, `CDI.current().select(X.class).get()`, or a dependency pulled from a service registry — that's a full-blown Service Locator inside a DI framework, with all its downsides. On code review the red flag is any manual `getBean`/`CDI.current()` in a business class (not in a factory/composition root): it hides the dependency and makes the class untestable without a running context.","hook":"DI is the ingredient list on the package: you see the contents; no purchase, no cooking. Service Locator is 'go to the pantry and find it yourself': the recipe says nothing about ingredients, and you learn the salt is missing only when the dish is already on the stove (runtime).","more":"**The essence of the antipattern (Martin Fowler, Mark Seemann 'DI in .NET'):** both patterns are about Inversion of Control but differ in the *visibility* of dependencies. DI makes them **explicit** (push: the container/caller 'pushes' dependencies into the constructor). Service Locator makes them **implicit** (pull: the class itself 'pulls' from a registry).\n\n**Why it's an antipattern:**\n- **Hidden dependencies.** From the signature `new OrderService()` you can't tell it needs `Repo`, `Clock`, `Notifier` inside. That surfaces only when the method calls `locator.get(...)` — at runtime. The compile-time guarantee 'you can't build it without the dependency' is lost.\n- **Global coupling.** Every class now depends on the `ServiceLocator` type (often a static singleton). Replacing the locator infrastructure means touching every class. With DI a class depends only on its domain ports.\n- **Fragile tests.** To substitute a dependency you must `Locator.register(mock)` in the global registry before the test and clean it up after. Forget teardown → state **leaks** between tests, creating order-dependence and flaky tests.\n\n**Concrete gotcha — this is NOT about `@Inject`/`@Autowired`.** People often confuse: 'a container is also a locator.' No. Container DI (Spring/Quarkus CDI) injects via the **constructor** — dependencies are visible and testable with plain `new`. But `applicationContext.getBean(Foo.class)` inside code or static `CDI.current().select(Foo.class)` IS Service Locator inside a DI framework, and carries exactly the same problems.\n\n```java\n// Service Locator — bad: dependency hidden\nclass OrderService {\n  void place(Order o) {\n    var repo = CDI.current().select(OrderRepo.class).get(); // pull, invisible\n    repo.save(o);\n  }\n}\n// DI — good: dependency explicit\nclass OrderService {\n  private final OrderRepo repo;\n  OrderService(OrderRepo repo) { this.repo = repo; } // push, visible\n}\n```\n\n**When SL is still justified:** at the very composition boundary (composition root / factories, plugin systems where the set of types is unknown at compile time) — there the locator IS the infrastructure. Inside domain/business logic — never.","quizCorrect":"Service Locator hides dependencies (you can't see them in the constructor/signature) and introduces a global coupling point — the locator; errors surface only at runtime, and tests require configuring shared mutable state","quizWrong":["Service Locator is slower than DI because reflection in `get()` is invoked on every request, whereas DI resolves the graph once at startup and stores the finished object graph in fields ready for use by the container before the first request arrives","Service Locator can't be used with interfaces and abstractions, it works only with concrete classes — so it breaks polymorphism","Service Locator creates circular dependencies that a DI container always detects at compile time, while the locator does not"],"picCap":"DI: dependencies in the constructor (visible, caught by the compiler). Service Locator: dependency pulled from inside — invisible in the API, the error surfaces only at runtime, the test requires global setup."},"de-tell-dont-ask":{"q":"Tell-Don't-Ask and Command-Query Separation (CQS): why is 'pull data out via getters and decide outside' a code smell, and how does a command (mutates state, returns nothing) differ from a query (returns a value, mutates nothing)?","a":"Tell-Don't-Ask: don't extract an object's state via getters to decide outside — hand the decision to the object that owns the data (`account.withdraw(sum)` instead of `if (account.getBalance() >= sum) account.setBalance(...)`). 'Ask-and-decide' outside is feature envy and an anemic model: logic and invariants leak into callers, and the object becomes a bag of data with no protection. CQS (Bertrand Meyer): a command mutates state and returns `void`; a query returns a value with no side effects — so a query can be called any number of times in any order, letting you reason about code without fear of hidden mutations. They are different axes: Tell-Don't-Ask is about WHERE the decision lives, CQS is about one method not doing both.","d":"Tell-Don't-Ask isn't absolute: for DTOs, read-only value objects, and reporting, getters are legitimate — the principle targets objects with invariants, not data carriers.","note":"⚠️ Dogmatic Tell-Don't-Ask easily flips into its opposite — a God Object: if you cram ALL behavior into the data-owning object, it bloats and takes over orchestration, coordination, presentation. TDA means the decision should live near the data, not that one object must do everything. Cross-aggregate coordination, UI formatting, report queries are legitimately external via services/DTOs. In an interview, the strong answer names the boundary: TDA protects the invariants of a single aggregate, it doesn't abolish the service layer.","hook":"'Don't interrogate — command.' You don't quiz the waiter about the kitchen's ingredients to cook the steak yourself (ASK); you say 'make the steak' (TELL) — the cook knows their own invariants. And CQS is 'either bring it (command) or show the menu (query),' but not 'bring a menu that also changes the order.'","more":"**Why ASK-style smells:**\n- **Feature envy:** the caller's method is more interested in another object's data than its own — logic belongs where the data is.\n- **Anemic model (Fowler):** an object with only getters/setters and no behavior; business rules get duplicated in every service that 'asked.'\n- **Leaky invariants:** if anyone can mutate balance via `getBalance()`+`setBalance()`, the object can't guarantee `balance >= 0`. Encapsulation is illusory.\n\n**CQS in practice:**\n- **Command** → `void` (or void-like), mutates state. **Query** → returns a value, idempotent w.r.t. state, referentially transparent.\n- Payoff: queries can freely be cached, reordered, repeated in logs/tests without risk.\n\n**Concrete gotcha — legal CQS violations:**\n- `stack.pop()` mutates state AND returns the element. `queue.poll()`, `iterator.next()`, `AtomicInteger.getAndIncrement()`, `map.putIfAbsent()` — same thing. These are deliberate exceptions: the 'mutate-and-return' atomicity can't be safely split into two calls (race condition between `peek()` and `pop()`).\n- So CQS is a guideline, not a law: CQRS (its system-level relative) splits commands and queries at the architecture level, but at the method level pragmatic exceptions are fine.\n\n**Don't conflate TDA and CQS:** you can honor Tell-Don't-Ask and violate CQS (a rich object with a method that mutates and returns a result), and vice versa.","quizCorrect":"A command mutates state and returns void; a query returns a value with no side effects — so queries can be safely repeated and reordered.","quizWrong":["A command returns a value to confirm success, while a query returns nothing and writes its result into a field of the object. for the caller to read","CQS requires all methods to be void; any return type counts as a violation of encapsulation.","A command must be synchronous and a query asynchronous; the split is about threads of execution, not side effects."],"picCap":"ASK pulls data and decides outside; TELL hands the decision to the owner. CQS separates command (void) and query (value); a mixed method is a smell."},"gap-ds-kafka-transactional-producer":{"q":"You already have enable.idempotence on. So why also transactional.id, initTransactions() and commitTransaction()? What does a transactional producer give you that an idempotent one doesn't, and how do consume-process-produce and zombie-producer fencing fit in?","a":"Idempotence gives exactly-once only within a single producer session to a single partition (the broker dedups by PID + sequence number). Transactions add atomicity across multiple partitions/topics plus atomic commit of consumer offsets together with the produce — that's the consume-process-produce (read-process-write) pattern: via sendOffsetsToTransaction() the input offsets are committed in the same transaction as the output records, so it's all-or-nothing. transactional.id provides a stable identity across restarts: on initTransactions() the transaction coordinator bumps the producer epoch, and an old 'zombie' instance with the same transactional.id but a lower epoch gets a ProducerFencedException — that's zombie fencing. Consumers see the result only with isolation.level=read_committed.","d":"Idempotence is PID + a monotonic sequence per (producer, partition); transactions add the transaction coordinator, the transaction log (__transaction_state), COMMIT/ABORT markers in partitions, and the epoch used for fencing.","note":"⚠️ transactional.id MUST be stable and unique per logical task/input-partition — never generate it randomly (a UUID at startup) and never share one across parallel instances. A random id kills fencing: after a restart the coordinator can't link the new producer to the old one, the predecessor's in-flight transaction isn't aborted, and read_committed consumers stall on it (the LSO won't advance) until transaction.timeout.ms expires. A shared id across N instances makes them constantly fence each other with ProducerFencedException. Kafka Streams handles this automatically (id = applicationId + taskId), but in manual consume-process-produce you must build the id→partition mapping yourself.","hook":"Idempotence is 'don't double-ring a receipt at one till'. A transaction is 'either the whole shopping trip is recorded (all receipts + the entry deduction) or none of it', and transactional.id with epoch is the turnstile that won't let your own zombie twin in on an expired pass.","more":"**What each level gives you:**\n\n- **enable.idempotence=true** — the broker assigns the producer a PID and numbers records with a sequence number per partition. Retried duplicates are dropped, order is preserved. But this only holds while a single producer instance is alive and only within its partitions — it is NOT atomicity across partitions.\n- **transactional.id** — a stable logical producer identity. By it the transaction coordinator finds/recovers transaction state and assigns the producer epoch.\n- **initTransactions()** — registers the producer with the coordinator, bumps the epoch (fencing previous instances), and **cleans up/aborts any unfinished transaction left by a crashed predecessor** before starting work.\n- **beginTransaction() / sendOffsetsToTransaction() / commitTransaction()** — the atomic unit: output records + input offsets are committed together.\n\n**Concrete gotcha (consume-process-produce):** if you commit offsets with a plain `consumer.commitSync()` instead of `producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(offsets, groupMetadata)`, you lose atomicity — you can write the output and crash before committing the offset (duplicate), or commit the offset and crash before flushing the output (loss). Offsets MUST go inside the producer's transaction. And the downstream consumer must be on `isolation.level=read_committed`, otherwise it reads data from aborted transactions and the whole EOS falls apart.","quizCorrect":"Atomic writes across multiple partitions/topics together with committing consumer offsets, plus fencing of zombie instances by producer epoch","quizWrong":["Deduplication of retries within a single partition by sequence number — the same as idempotence, just faster and requires nothing beyond it on top","A guarantee of strict message ordering within a partition that an idempotent producer lacks","Batch compression and reduced network traffic between producer and broker via the transactional protocol"],"picCap":"Idempotent = dedup by PID+seq within one partition. Transaction = atomicity across N partitions + offsets, and the epoch fences the zombie."},"gap-ds-auto-offset-reset":{"q":"You connect a BRAND-NEW consumer group to a topic with a month of history — and it either reads everything from scratch or silently ignores the old data. What does auto.offset.reset (earliest vs latest) decide, when does it actually trigger, and why does a 'vanished' committed offset (offsets.retention) suddenly replay the same scenario in prod?","a":"auto.offset.reset governs ONLY the case where the group has NO valid committed offset for a partition (new group) OR the stored offset is out of the current log range (OffsetOutOfRangeException, e.g. older than the log's retention.ms). earliest → read from the start of the available log (log-start-offset), latest → read only new messages after connecting (high watermark); the default is latest. When a valid offset EXISTS, the parameter never triggers — the consumer resumes from the committed position. The prod trap: __consumer_offsets has its own retention (offsets.retention.minutes, default 7 days since Kafka 2.0; previously 24 hours) — if a group is idle longer than that, the broker deletes its committed offset, so on the next connect the group looks 'new' → auto.offset.reset fires, and earliest suddenly replays the whole topic.","d":"The 'none' value forbids both scenarios: on a missing/invalid offset it throws an exception instead of silently resetting — useful to surface the problem explicitly rather than quietly re-reading or losing data.","note":"⚠️ offsets.retention is NOT the same as topic retention. Even if the topic keeps data for months, a group's committed offset lives in __consumer_offsets for a limited time (7 days by default, historically just 24 hours on Kafka before 2.0). A consumer stopped over a weekend/holiday can come back as a 'new group' and replay the whole topic from earliest, producing a flood of duplicates. Since Kafka 2.1+ (KIP-211) the offset-expiry timer only starts once the group becomes empty — this saves running groups with slow traffic, but not a genuinely stopped service.","hook":"auto.offset.reset is your Plan B for 'I lost my bookmark in the book'. If the bookmark is in place, no Plan B is needed — you read on. But the bookmarked book sits on the __consumer_offsets shelf, which has its own shelf-life (7 days): leave it too long and the broker-janitor tosses the bookmark, so you're a 'new reader' again → either you re-read the volume from page one (earliest) or open it at the last page (latest).","more":"**When the parameter TRIGGERS (exactly two cases):**\n- There is no committed offset in `__consumer_offsets` for (group, topic-partition) — a new group or the offset was deleted.\n- The committed offset is out of the partition log's range → `OffsetOutOfRangeException` (e.g. the log was cleaned by `retention.ms`/`retention.bytes` while the group lagged). Then `latest`/`earliest` auto-'fixes' the position.\n\n**Values:**\n- `latest` (default) — position = high watermark, read only what arrives AFTER connecting. Risk: silently skip history.\n- `earliest` — position = log-start-offset (not necessarily 0! it's the start after cleanup). Risk: replay the whole topic and cause duplicates.\n- `none` — throw an exception, don't guess. Forces explicit handling.\n\n**Concrete gotcha (offsets.retention vs topic retention):**\nThese are TWO different retentions. The topic log may keep data for a month (`retention.ms`), but a group's committed offsets are stored in `__consumer_offsets` with their OWN lifetime — `offsets.retention.minutes` (default 7 days since Kafka 2.0; in 0.10–1.x it was only 24 hours). Scenario: Friday evening the consumer service is shut down, Monday 3 days later it starts — on Kafka 1.x the offsets are already gone, the group is 'new', `earliest` replays everything → a storm of duplicates. Since Kafka 2.1+ the retention countdown for offsets only starts WHEN the group becomes empty (KIP-211), not from the last commit — this greatly reduces the issue but doesn't remove it: a long-idle stopped group still expires.\n\n**Practice:** for critical pipelines use `none` + explicit offset initialization, or monitor lag and never let the group stay empty longer than `offsets.retention`. `earliest` is the default on streaming jobs (Kafka Streams, Flink) where replaying state is actually desired.","quizCorrect":"The parameter triggers only when there is no valid committed offset (new group or OffsetOutOfRange); earliest = log-start, latest = high watermark; the offset can expire via offsets.retention.","quizWrong":["auto.offset.reset is applied on EVERY poll() and constantly resets the position to the start or end of the topic regardless of the committed offset.","earliest always means offset 0, and committed offsets are kept exactly as long as the topic data (one shared retention.ms).","The latest value makes the consumer read all history up to the high watermark, while earliest connects only to new messages — the names reflect read order. rather than position in the log in the partition"],"picCap":"Reset fires ONLY on the red branch: no valid offset — either a new group or expired by offsets.retention."},"gap-ds-kafka-streams-ktable":{"q":"Kafka Streams: how does KStream differ from KTable (stream-table duality), where does state live during aggregation/join, and why the changelog topic and RocksDB? Why is this fundamentally better than 'I just read with a consumer and store into Postgres'?","a":"A KStream is an unbounded stream of independent events (insert semantics, each record is a fact), while a KTable is a materialized 'latest value per key' view (upsert/changelog semantics, null = tombstone/delete). Stream-table duality: any changelog stream can be folded into a table (by latest value per key), and table changes can be unfolded back into a stream of deltas. On aggregate/join/reduce, state lives locally in the app instance in a state store (RocksDB on disk by default, off the JVM heap), and for fault tolerance every store change is written to a compacted changelog topic in Kafka — on crash/rebalance the store is restored from it (or from a standby replica). This beats 'consumer + Postgres' because state is sharded together with partitions (co-partitioning, local lookups with no network round-trip to a DB), and the aggregate plus state plus offset are committed in one Kafka exactly-once transaction (consume-process-produce), instead of 'read → crash before the Postgres write → duplicate/loss'.","d":"A GlobalKTable is a full (non-sharded) copy of a topic on every instance: it allows joins on a non-key field without co-partitioning, at the cost of fully duplicating the data and eventual consistency (it updates asynchronously, without event-time alignment with the main stream).","note":"⚠️ Silent loss/duplicates with EOS disabled. By default (processing.guarantee=at_least_once) the state store, the offset and the output topic are NOT committed atomically: crash between writing to the store and committing the offset and, after restart, you reprocess the same events and the aggregate inflates (double-counting). Set processing.guarantee=exactly_once_v2 for an atomic consume-process-produce transaction. Second trap: RocksDB state lives on the pod's LOCAL disk — in Kubernetes without a PersistentVolume every pod restart wipes state.dir, and the whole store fully replays from the changelog topic on startup. For large stores that's minutes of downtime on every restart/rebalance; fix with a StatefulSet + PVC and/or standby replicas.","hook":"A KStream is a bank statement (a list of every transaction, nothing erased), while a KTable is the current account balance (one number per key, the new value overwrites the old). RocksDB is your local 'balance notebook' at hand, and the changelog is a copy of that notebook in the bank's vault: notebook burned — restore it from the vault.","more":"**Stream-table duality in practice.** `KStream.toTable()` folds a stream into a table (the latest value per key is kept), and `KTable.toStream()` unfolds it back into a stream of delta-changes. This is exactly why `aggregate()` takes a KStream and returns a KTable: each input event updates a record in the store and emits a new changelog record.\n\n**Where state physically lives:**\n- **RocksDB** — an embedded LSM-tree key-value store on the instance's local disk (off-heap, so large state causes no GC pauses). Stored under `state.dir`.\n- **changelog topic** — `<app-id>-<store-name>-changelog`, compacted (`cleanup.policy=compact`), created automatically. Every put into the store is mirrored there. On restart/rebalance the store is re-read from the changelog from scratch (or from a standby replica if `num.standby.replicas > 0` — then failover is seconds, not minutes).\n\n**Why co-partitioning is critical for KStream-KStream/KTable joins.** To join locally, both inputs must have: the same number of partitions, the same key, and the same partitioning strategy. Otherwise the 'needed' record ends up in another instance. A GlobalKTable removes this requirement at the cost of a full copy on every node.\n\n**Concrete gotcha — `aggregate()` emits on every event, not a 'final total'.** Downstream sees a stream of intermediate results (10, then 30, then 35...), not a single final number. If you then write to a DB or external system, you get many updates. To get 'batch totals' you need windowed aggregates + **suppress()** with `untilWindowCloses` (and then you must configure a grace period for late events), otherwise every increment flows out.","quizCorrect":"KStream is a stream of independent events (insert), KTable is a materialized latest-value-per-key (upsert/changelog); aggregation state lives locally in RocksDB and is backed up to a compacted changelog topic","quizWrong":["KStream and KTable are semantically identical, a KTable just retains data longer; all state always lives in the Kafka broker, not in the app instance","A KTable stores all versions of an event as a stream while a KStream stores only the latest value; aggregation state is written directly to Postgres via a JDBC sink work identically in either case anyway, whichever you pick","KStream is for writing to Kafka, KTable is read-only; the state store always lives in the JVM heap, with RocksDB used only as an optional cache"],"picCap":"A stream of facts (KStream) folds into a table of latest values (KTable); aggregates live in RocksDB and are backed up to a compacted changelog topic, whereas 'consumer + Postgres' loses atomicity."},"gap-ds-clock-skew-ordering":{"q":"Why must you never order events or build timeouts/leases on System.currentTimeMillis() (wall-clock) across nodes? What are clock skew and NTP drift, how do monotonic/logical clocks help, and why is time the worst way to decide 'who was last'?","a":"System.currentTimeMillis() returns each node's wall-clock, and node clocks diverge: clock skew is the instantaneous difference between two nodes (tens of ms with NTP, seconds without), while drift is the rate the crystal wanders (~10–100 ppm). NTP also jumps: after a step correction the clock can go backwards, so 't2 > t1' on different nodes proves nothing about ordering, and even on one node (t2 − t1) can turn negative or spike. For timeouts/leases and measuring durations use a monotonic clock (System.nanoTime() / CLOCK_MONOTONIC) — it only increases and isn't adjusted by NTP; for causal ordering across nodes use logical clocks (Lamport timestamps, vector clocks). 'Who was last' by time is the worst criterion: LWW (last-write-wins) on wall-clock silently loses data under skew, so use versions/CRDTs/quorums, not timestamps.","d":"Google Spanner doesn't even pretend time is exact: TrueTime returns an interval [earliest, latest] with an uncertainty bound ε and does a commit-wait — it waits until ε has elapsed to guarantee external consistency; an honest admission that there is no absolute 'now' across nodes.","note":"⚠️ Classic prod incident: measuring a duration/timeout via currentTimeMillis(). NTP does a backward step (or a jump on a leap second / DST change), and elapsed = end − start comes out negative or huge — the timeout fires instantly or 'hangs' forever, a TTL cache suddenly expires all at once. Any code like `long start = System.currentTimeMillis(); ... ; if (System.currentTimeMillis() - start > timeout)` is a bug; for durations always use System.nanoTime(). Separate trap: nanoTime() is monotonic but its absolute value is meaningless and incomparable across JVMs/nodes — never send it over the wire.","hook":"Node clocks are like wristwatches in a crowd with no shared radio signal: everyone has their own 'noon', some run fast, some slow, and NTP is when someone yanks one watch backwards mid-conversation. Asking 'whose time is bigger?' to decide who spoke last is a fight over whose watch lies more elegantly. A monotonic clock is a stopwatch: it doesn't know the time of day, but it honestly measures how much elapsed.","more":"**Three different notions of time — don't conflate:**\n- **Wall-clock (`System.currentTimeMillis`, `CLOCK_REALTIME`):** 'what time it is'. Synced by NTP, can jump forward AND backward, shifts on DST/leap second. Only good for display and logs, NOT for measurements and NOT for cross-node ordering.\n- **Monotonic clock (`System.nanoTime`, `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`):** only increases, not adjusted by NTP, counts from an arbitrary origin. For timeouts, latency, deadlines, leases — within a single process/node.\n- **Logical clocks:** not time at all, but counters for causality. Lamport clocks give a total order consistent with happens-before (a→b ⇒ L(a)<L(b)), but not the converse. Vector clocks can distinguish causal relation from concurrency (detect conflicts).\n\n**Why skew and drift are unavoidable:**\n- Each node has its own crystal, drift ~10–100 ppm ⇒ up to ~8 seconds a day without correction.\n- NTP adjusts either by slew (gently tweaking frequency) or step (a sharp jump when divergence > 128 ms) — step is the source of 'time going backwards'.\n- Real skew in a datacenter with good NTP is single-digit to tens of ms; in cloud / on VM pauses (VM freeze, GC stop-the-world, live migration) — hundreds of ms or more.\n\n**Concrete gotcha — LWW on wall-clock loses writes:**\nNode A writes a key at t=100 (its clock is 50 ms fast ⇒ real time 50). Node B writes the same key LATER in real time (60) but its clock is slow ⇒ stamps timestamp 55. With last-write-wins A's write wins (100 > 55), even though B was physically last. B's write **silently vanishes** — no error, just lost data. That's why Cassandra LWW is famous for 'resurrecting' deleted rows and losing updates when clocks drift. Fixes: versions (`@Version` / vector clocks), CRDTs, quorum revisions (like `modifyIndex` in etcd/Consul), Spanner TrueTime.\n\n**Interview rule:** 'For duration — nanoTime. For ordering across nodes — logical/vector clocks or an external source of truth (Kafka offset, DB sequence, Raft log index), never wall-clock. If you truly need physical ordering — only with an explicit uncertainty bound (TrueTime/HLC).'","quizCorrect":"System.nanoTime() / CLOCK_MONOTONIC — for durations and timeouts; Lamport or vector clocks — for causal ordering of events across nodes","quizWrong":["System.currentTimeMillis() on all nodes + NTP sync to 1 ms accuracy fully solves the ordering problem","Just take time from one 'master' node and broadcast it to the rest once a second — then wall-clock becomes consistent to every participant","Replace milliseconds with nanoseconds in the currentTimeMillis logic — higher resolution removes skew between nodes"],"picCap":"Top: wall-clock jumps backward on NTP-step, skew between nodes ⇒ LWW silently loses write B. Bottom: monotonic clock only increases (durations), logical clock — causal order."},"gap-ds-group-coordinator-offsets":{"q":"Where does Kafka physically store committed consumer-group offsets, and who orchestrates partition assignment? What are the group coordinator and the __consumer_offsets topic, and why are offsets NO longer kept in ZooKeeper?","a":"Since 0.9, committed offsets live not in ZooKeeper but in an internal compacted topic __consumer_offsets (50 partitions by default, RF=3). An offset commit is just a produced record keyed by (group.id, topic, partition) with value (offset, metadata); log compaction keeps only the latest value per key. The group coordinator is the specific broker that leads the __consumer_offsets partition the group maps to via abs(hash(group.id)) % offsets.topic.num.partitions; it also runs the JoinGroup/SyncGroup protocol, tracks heartbeats and triggers rebalances. ZooKeeper was dropped because it couldn't handle frequent commits and became a bottleneck; in KRaft (KIP-500) ZK is gone entirely.","d":"Key point: the coordinator does NOT assign partitions itself — it merely elects one consumer as the group leader, and that client computes the assignment via the chosen assignor (RangeAssignor/CooperativeStickyAssignor) and returns it to the coordinator in SyncGroup.","note":"⚠️ The most common prod surprise: offsets can be 'lost' not because of a broker, but because of offsets.retention.minutes. If a group is idle longer than retention (7 days by default) with no active consumers at that moment, its committed offsets in __consumer_offsets are compacted away → on the next start the group falls back to auto.offset.reset (often latest) and silently skips accumulated messages, or (with earliest) reprocesses everything. Seasonal/rarely-running consumers must monitor this and/or raise retention.","hook":"The coordinator isn't a dispatcher, it's the doorman of one specific building entrance (a __consumer_offsets partition). A group is 'registered' to one entrance by hash(group.id); the leader-broker of that entrance keeps the offset ledger and calls residents to a meeting (rebalance), but the actual partition-allocation plan is written not by it, but by an elected senior-resident consumer.","more":"**What physically happens on an offset commit:**\n- The consumer sends an `OffsetCommit` RPC to the coordinator, it does not write to ZK.\n- The coordinator does an ordinary append to its `__consumer_offsets` partition: key = `(group.id, topic, partition)`, value = `(committed offset, leader epoch, metadata, timestamp)`.\n- The topic is **compacted** plus has retention (`offsets.retention.minutes`, 7 days by default) — old versions of the key collapse, only the last offset remains.\n\n**Who the group coordinator concretely is:**\n- It is the broker that **leads** the `__consumer_offsets` partition the group maps to: `abs(hash(group.id)) % offsets.topic.num.partitions`.\n- One broker can be coordinator for many groups, but for a single group there is exactly one coordinator.\n- When a broker dies, leadership of that `__consumer_offsets` partition moves to an ISR replica — the coordinator 'moves' there too, and group state is restored from the log.\n\n**Role separation (classic interview gotcha):**\n- **Coordinator (broker)** — runs membership, heartbeat, rebalance, stores offsets.\n- **Group leader (one of the consumers)** — computes partition assignment via a `PartitionAssignor`.\n- The coordinator does NOT compute the assignment — it only relays metadata between consumers across the JoinGroup → SyncGroup phases.\n\n**Why they moved off ZooKeeper:**\n- ZK was not built for high-frequency writes (a commit every `auto.commit.interval.ms` = 5s per consumer) — it became a bottleneck and a degradation point.\n- There was a transitional dual-commit mode (`offsets.storage`/`dual.commit.enabled`), then ZK storage was removed.\n- KIP-500 / KRaft removes ZooKeeper entirely (cluster metadata lives in its own raft log), while offsets stay where they were — in `__consumer_offsets`.","quizCorrect":"In the internal compacted topic __consumer_offsets, on the partition led by the group's coordinator broker","quizWrong":["In ZooKeeper, under the /consumers/<group>/offsets znode path","In a local RocksDB state store on each consumer instance","In a dedicated relational metadata store managed by the controller broker rather than in a topic of its own"],"picCap":"A group maps to a __consumer_offsets partition; that partition's leader broker = the group coordinator. An offset commit is a produced record, rebalance goes through JoinGroup/SyncGroup. ZooKeeper is out of the picture."},"g-te-classicist-mockist":{"q":"Classicist (Chicago/Detroit) vs Mockist (London) school of TDD — what is the core difference, and why does over-mocking make tests brittle and coupled to implementation?","a":"The Classicist (Chicago, aka state-based) school tests behavior via state: real objects, doubles only for slow/non-deterministic collaborators (DB, network), asserting the result with assertEquals. The Mockist (London, interaction-based) school fully isolates each class, replacing all collaborators with mocks and checking interactions via verify(mock).method() — an outside-in, role-driven design. Over-mocking couples the test to the exact sequence and signatures of calls (verify(repo).save(x); verify(notifier).send(...)), so any refactoring of the internal implementation (reordered calls, merged calls, added cache) breaks green tests even though observable behavior is unchanged — the test asserts 'how', not 'what'.","d":"Classicist is blamed for large 'blast radius' (one bug in a shared object reds dozens of tests); Mockist for over-specification and missing integration bugs (mocks lie about real contracts — hence the need for contract/integration tests on top).","note":"⚠️ The main trap is mocking what you don't own: when(externalSdkClient.call()).thenReturn(...). Your mock describes the third-party contract as YOU understood it, not as it really behaves. When the vendor changes behavior (response format, new exception, retry), your tests stay green while prod breaks. Rule: only mock your own ports/adapters (what you own), and cover integration with third-party SDKs via contract/integration tests (Pact, WireMock, Testcontainers). A second over-mocking symptom: MockitoExtension with STRICT_STUBS starts throwing UnnecessaryStubbingException on every refactor.","hook":"The Classicist judges the dish by tasting the finished plate (state); the Mockist stands behind the chef checking every knife move (verify). Change the cutting technique and the dish is identical, but the London inspector yells 'not per the recipe!'.","more":"**The core distinction (Fowler, 'Mocks Aren't Stubs'):**\n- **State verification (Classicist):** after the action, assert the state of the SUT and real collaborators. Test doubles only for 'awkward' dependencies (DB, time, external API). Dominated by `assertThat(account.getBalance()).isEqualTo(...)`.\n- **Behavior verification (Mockist):** assert that the SUT made the right calls to collaborators: `verify(gateway).charge(amount)`. Each unit is fully isolated.\n\n**Why over-mocking = brittleness (test coupled to implementation):**\n- `verify(mock)` pins down the **signature, arguments and sometimes order** (`InOrder`). It's white-box — the test knows the internal mechanics.\n- A behavior-preserving refactor (two `save()` calls merged into one `saveAll()`, a cache added, order changed) **breaks** green tests &rarr; refactoring becomes expensive, trust in tests drops.\n- `when(...).thenReturn(...)` duplicates the dependency's contract inside the test. When the real class's contract changes, the mock keeps lying, the test stays green, prod breaks.\n\n**Concrete gotcha — Mockito strict stubs:**\nWith Mockito 2+, `strictness = STRICT_STUBS` (default in `MockitoExtension`) throws `UnnecessaryStubbingException` if a stubbed call never happens. This catches dead stubs, but also means the slightest change in the execution path breaks the mock setup — a classic over-mocking symptom.\n\n**Practical synthesis (industry today):**\n- Mock only **architectural boundaries / ports** (repository, gateway, bus), not every internal object.\n- Don't mock what you don't own (types from third-party libraries) — wrap them in your own adapter and mock that.\n- Don't mock value objects and pure functions — create them for real.\n- Keep **contract/integration tests** (Testcontainers, Pact) on top of unit tests to catch mocks' lies.","quizCorrect":"A Mockist test verifies interactions (verify of calls) and therefore breaks on refactoring of the implementation, even when observable behavior is unchanged","quizWrong":["The Classicist school always uses mocks while the Mockist uses real objects, which is why London-style tests are brittle end up far more brittle than the other kind","Mockist tests are brittle because mocks are slower than real objects and time out more often","The difference is only the library: Chicago uses Mockito, London uses EasyMock, otherwise the approaches are identical"],"picCap":"State (what) is robust to refactoring; verifying interactions (how) is brittle"},"g-te-fake-vs-stub":{"q":"Full test-double taxonomy: dummy / stub / fake / mock / spy — how does a fake (in-memory implementation of a port/repository) differ from a stub, and when is it better than a mock?","a":"Meszaros' taxonomy: **dummy** — placeholder that is passed but never used (just fills a parameter); **stub** — returns canned answers to calls (state-based, feeds the SUT data); **spy** — records facts about calls for later assertion; **mock** — pre-programmed with expectations and fails by itself when they're violated (behavior verification); **fake** — a working but simplified implementation (in-memory Map instead of a JDBC repository). Key difference fake vs stub: a stub returns hardcoded answers and keeps no state across calls, whereas a fake is a full **behavioral** implementation of the port's contract (save→findById returns what was saved). A fake beats a mock when you test several interactions through one port or want to assert on resulting state rather than on call facts — mock chains `when().thenReturn()` are brittle and over-engineered, while a fake is realistic and reusable.","d":"Fowler splits doubles into two verification philosophies: stub/fake → **state verification** (assert the result), mock → **behavior verification** (assert the right method was called with the right args); a spy is a 'post-hoc mock' (Mockito spy wraps a real object).","note":"⚠️ In Mockito, `mock()` and `spy()` are technical implementations, NOT taxonomy roles from Meszaros. `Mockito.mock()` with `when().thenReturn()` and no `verify()` is actually a **stub**, not a mock (there's no behavior verification). And `Mockito.spy()` wraps a real object and by default calls the real methods — it's easy to trigger a real side effect (hitting the DB, sending a mail) while thinking the object is 'stubbed'. Classic trap: `when(spy.foo()).thenReturn(x)` on a spy WILL really invoke `foo()` — for a spy you must write `doReturn(x).when(spy).foo()`, otherwise the real method runs during stubbing.","hook":"A stub is an answering machine ('always says the same thing'), a fake is a mini-fridge (it actually stores what you put in and gives it back), a mock is a strict examiner (knows the correct answers in advance and gives a 'fail' if the SUT asks the wrong thing).","more":"**Where the stub vs fake line is (the main interview confusion):**\n- **stub** has no logic and no memory: `when(repo.findById(1)).thenReturn(user)` — for any other id it returns null/a placeholder, and `save()` does nothing.\n- **fake** implements the contract: `InMemoryUserRepository implements UserRepository` with a `Map<Long,User>` — `save()` stores, `findById()` retrieves, `delete()` removes. The test becomes scenario-driven, not 'pre-programmed'.\n\n**When a fake beats a mock:**\n- you test several steps through one port (create → update → read);\n- you want to avoid brittle `verify()` assertions coupled to the implementation;\n- one fake is reused across dozens of tests (especially for repositories/gateways in a hexagon).\n\n**When a mock is still needed:** to verify the *interaction* itself — that an email was actually sent, that an event was published to Kafka, that a call happened exactly once. This is a side effect with no observable state, so state verification is impossible here.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** a fake duplicates the contract's logic. If the real adapter's behavior and the fake diverge (e.g., the real JDBC throws on a unique conflict while the fake silently overwrites), the tests stay green and prod breaks. The cure is **contract tests** — the same test suite run against both the fake and the real port implementation.","quizCorrect":"A stub returns canned answers without keeping state, while a fake is a full behavioral implementation of the port's contract that stores state (save→findById returns it).","quizWrong":["A stub verifies that methods were called with the right arguments, while a fake only returns hardcoded values., which is why a fake is only ever suitable for the very simplest of cases","A fake is always slower because it hits a real database, while a stub works in memory.","There is no difference — 'fake' and 'stub' are just two names for the same Mockito mechanism."],"picCap":"5 doubles: a stub feeds data, a fake stores state, a mock verifies the calls"},"g-te-mockstatic-smell":{"q":"How do you mock static and final methods in Mockito (mockStatic, inline mock-maker), and why is the need to do so usually a sign of bad design?","a":"Since Mockito 2 you need the `mockito-inline` mock-maker (default since 5.x — `mock-maker-inline` via ByteBuddy + the Instrumentation API, no PowerMock). `Mockito.mockStatic(Foo.class)` returns a `MockedStatic<Foo>`, which is AutoCloseable and MUST be closed in a try-with-resources, otherwise the static mock leaks across tests and breaks neighboring ones. final classes and final methods are mocked transparently by the inline maker. Needing to mock statics is usually a smell because a static call (`LocalDateTime.now()`, `Utils.calc()`) is a hard, hidden dependency that can't be swapped via the constructor — a DIP violation and a sign the type isn't testable in isolation.","d":"The proper cure for the smell: extract the static behind a port/interface (e.g. `Clock` instead of `LocalDateTime.now()`, a factory/provider instead of `new`/static factory) and inject it — then a plain `mock()` is enough.","note":"⚠️ A `MockedStatic` lives only in the thread that created it and is thread-local by default: if the code under test makes the static call from another thread (executor, `@Async`, parallel stream), the mock will NOT apply there — you'll hit the real method. And if you forget try-with-resources/close(), the static stub leaks into the next tests on the same thread, producing 'flaky' failures that can't be reproduced by running a single test in isolation.","hook":"A static method is a nail driven straight into concrete: you can't swap it, you can only drill it out with a jackhammer (the inline mock-maker via Instrumentation). If you have to drill concrete just to test, the wall was built wrong. And a `MockedStatic` without try-with-resources is an unclosed tap that floods the neighbors below (the neighboring tests).","more":"**How the inline mock-maker works**\n- Before Mockito 2, static/final could only be mocked by PowerMock via a custom classloader and bytecode manipulation — heavy and conflicts with agents.\n- `mockito-inline` (a separate artifact in 2.x–4.x) and the default `mock-maker-inline` in **Mockito 5+** use ByteBuddy + the Java Instrumentation API to retransform classes at runtime — no separate classloader.\n- `mockStatic` and `mockConstruction` arrived in **Mockito 3.4.0**.\n\n**Canonical no-leak pattern:**\n```java\ntry (MockedStatic<LocalDateTime> mocked = Mockito.mockStatic(LocalDateTime.class)) {\n    mocked.when(LocalDateTime::now).thenReturn(fixed);\n    // ... test\n} // close() removes the mock here\n```\n\n**Why it's a smell — point by point:**\n- A static call can't be swapped via DI → the dependency is hidden and hard-wired (Dependency Inversion violation).\n- The test becomes brittle: it knows internal implementation (that `Foo.bar()` is called inside).\n- It often hides a responsibility that should have been extracted into a separate injectable service.\n\n**Conceptual trap:** mocking the static of YOUR OWN code is almost always 'treating the symptom.' The exception is mocking SOMEONE ELSE'S static you can't wrap (e.g. `System.currentTimeMillis()`), but even there it's cleaner to introduce a `Clock`/wrapper and mock it the normal way.","quizCorrect":"`MockedStatic` is AutoCloseable, must be closed (try-with-resources), and is thread-local by default: the mock isn't visible in other threads","quizWrong":["`mockStatic` applies globally across all threads and is removed automatically after each test via a JUnit extension, so nothing is left behind and so no cleanup is ever needed","To mock statics in Mockito 5 you need to add PowerMock and a custom classloader","Mockito fundamentally can't mock final methods — only statics via `mockStatic`"],"picCap":"Smell: a hard static dependency 'fixed' with a brittle MockedStatic. Cure: extract behind a Clock port and mock with a plain mock()."},"g-te-doreturn-void":{"q":"when().thenReturn() vs doReturn()/doThrow()/doNothing() — why can't void methods and spies be stubbed via when(...), and when is the do* syntax mandatory?","a":"when(mock.method()) ACTUALLY invokes method() first: Mockito intercepts the call through its proxy and records the invocation into OngoingStubbing. For void this is syntactically impossible (when() expects a value argument), and on a spy the real method runs with its side effects/exceptions before you can set up the stub. The do* syntax (doReturn/doThrow/doNothing/doAnswer/doCallRealMethod) defines behavior BEFORE the call — when(mock).method() passes the mock itself, not the call result, so the real code never executes.","d":"The price for bypassing the real call is loss of type-safety: doReturn(Object) is not checked by the compiler against the method's return type, so a ClassCastException surfaces only at runtime during stubbing.","note":"⚠️ Loss of compile-time type checking: doReturn(\"text\").when(mock).getCount() where getCount() returns int — the compiler stays silent (the argument is Object), but at stub time you get WrongTypeOfReturnValue/ClassCastException. With when(mock.getCount()).thenReturn(\"text\") the compiler catches it. So use do* ONLY when there's no alternative (void/spy), not as a default style.","hook":"when() is 'jump first, check the parachute later' — the method has ALREADY flown off into real code. do*() is 'put on the parachute (behavior) first, then jump (invoke)'.","more":"**Mechanics of the two syntaxes**\n\n- `when(mock.foo()).thenReturn(x)` — the argument to `when()` is the *result* of `mock.foo()`. The JVM must evaluate `foo()` BEFORE entering `when()`. On a plain mock this is harmless (the stub returns a default), but:\n  - **void**: `when(mock.foo())` won't compile — you can't pass `void` as an argument.\n  - **spy**: `spy.foo()` runs the REAL method. If it throws, hits the DB, or mutates state, that happens during stub setup.\n\n- `doReturn(x).when(mock).foo()` — `when(mock)` returns the proxy itself in stubbing mode, and `.foo()` merely *records* which method is being stubbed without executing its body.\n\n**Full do\\* family:** `doReturn`, `doThrow`, `doNothing`, `doAnswer`, `doCallRealMethod`.\n\n**Gotcha:** on a spy `when(spy.getList().get(0)).thenReturn(\"x\")` fails with `IndexOutOfBoundsException`, because the real `get(0)` runs on an empty list.","quizCorrect":"when(mock.foo()) actually executes foo() — for void this is syntactically impossible, and on a spy it runs the real code with side effects before stubbing; do* defines behavior before the call","quizWrong":["when() works only with interfaces and do* only with classes, so a spy of a concrete class requires do*","do* is faster because it doesn't create an OngoingStubbing object, and on void methods when() is just slower","The difference is purely stylistic: both bypass the real call identically, do* was added only for test readabilityuse, and the two forms can be swapped freely anywhere in a test suite in any suite"],"picCap":"when(...) invokes the real method before stubbing; do* defines behavior in advance, bypassing the call"},"g-te-mockbean-context":{"q":"@MockBean / @SpyBean in Spring Boot — how do they replace a bean in the context, and why does overusing them destroy the TestContext cache and slow down the whole test suite?","a":"@MockBean/@SpyBean are handled by MockitoContextCustomizer, which via MockitoPostProcessor (a BeanFactoryPostProcessor + BeanPostProcessor) either replaces an existing BeanDefinition with a mock, registers a new one, or wraps the real bean in a Mockito spy. The key point: the set of these fields feeds into MergedContextConfiguration through ContextCustomizer.equals()/hashCode(), so every unique combination of mocks produces a NEW TestContext cache key — the context isn't reused and is refreshed from scratch. Across a large suite that means dozens of extra ApplicationContext refreshes plus overflow of the default cache (32 contexts) with LRU eviction and re-creation — hence the multiplied runtime.","d":"@TestPropertySource with inline values and dynamic customizers cause the same effect: any detail affecting MergedContextConfiguration.equals() fragments the cache just like a unique @MockBean.","note":"⚠️ Classic trap: developers assume @MockBean is reused if 'the type is the same', but the cache is sensitive to bean NAME and qualifiers, and crucially — adding a mock in just ONE class breaks context sharing for the whole group of similar tests. In Spring Boot 3.4 @MockBean is deprecated in favor of the core @MockitoBean from spring-test — on migration it's a different package (org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito), the cache semantics are identical, and migration does NOT fix the problem.","hook":"Each new @MockBean is a new 'fingerprint' for the context: the fingerprint doesn't match → the guard (TestContext cache) won't let you in on the old badge and spins up a fresh context from scratch.","more":"**Replacement mechanics (Spring Boot 3.x, before the move to `@MockitoBean` in 3.4):**\n- `MockitoContextCustomizerFactory` scans the test class and collects via `DefinitionsParser` a list of `MockDefinition`/`SpyDefinition`.\n- `MockitoPostProcessor` as a `BeanFactoryPostProcessor` locates the target `BeanDefinition` by type/name and **replaces** it with a mock (registerSingleton/replace); for `@SpyBean` it wraps the already-created real bean via `Mockito.spy(...)` in the `BeanPostProcessor` phase.\n- If the bean is absent, the mock is simply registered as a new singleton.\n\n**Why the cache suffers:**\n- The cache key is `MergedContextConfiguration`, which includes the `ContextCustomizer`s. `MockitoContextCustomizer.equals()` compares the **set** of mock definitions. Different sets → different keys → cache miss.\n- Default cache size is **32** (`spring.test.context.cache.maxSize`). With more unique configs, LRU eviction kicks in and evicted contexts are **rebuilt** within the same run.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** two classes with the *same* set of mocks, one declaring `@MockBean A; @MockBean B` and the other `@MockBean B; @MockBean A` — order doesn't matter (it's a `Set`), the cache is reused. But adding one 'harmless' `@MockBean Clock` for a single test breaks sharing for that whole branch and spawns an extra context.\n\n**Remedies:**\n- Hoist the common mock set into a shared base test class / shared `@TestConfiguration` so the config is identical.\n- Mock via constructor/`@Mock` in pure unit tests where no Spring context is needed at all.\n- For slices use `@WebMvcTest`/`@DataJpaTest` with a minimal set, not `@SpringBootTest` plus a pile of `@MockBean`.","quizCorrect":"Every unique combination of @MockBean/@SpyBean changes the MergedContextConfiguration key, so the context cache misses and the ApplicationContext is refreshed from scratch.","quizWrong":["@MockBean always triggers @DirtiesContext, which physically destroys the context after each test method.","Mockito mocks can't be cached because they aren't serializable, and Spring forbids storing them between tests.ests, which is why every class gets a fresh context of its own own","@MockBean switches the whole context to prototype scope, so beans are recreated on every @Test."],"picCap":"Each unique mock combination = new cache key = new context refresh; if it matches, the context is reused."},"g-web-session-vs-jwt-revocation":{"q":"Stateful session (cookie + server store) vs stateless JWT — which to choose, and how do you revoke an issued JWT on logout/ban if it's valid until expiry?","a":"A session gives revocation for free: the token is just an opaque ID, delete the record in Redis/DB and the session is instantly dead; the cost is a round-trip to the store on every request and a stateful server. A JWT is stateless and scales horizontally well, but is fundamentally non-revocable until exp because it's validated by signature without contacting the server. There's no clean way to revoke a JWT — you must bring state back: either a short access-token TTL (5–15 min) plus a long refresh token that lives server-side and is actually revoked, or a denylist (the token's jti in Redis with TTL = remaining time to exp), or a per-user version/epoch check. In practice, for most web apps that need logout and ban, a session or a hybrid (short JWT + server-side refresh) is the right call rather than a 'pure' long-lived JWT.","d":"Logging out a 'pure' JWT on the client (removing the token from localStorage) is security theater: a copy of the token stays valid until exp and the server will still accept it.","note":"⚠️ Don't store a JWT in localStorage — it's readable by any JS, and a single XSS = theft of a token you cannot revoke until exp. Keep it in an HttpOnly+Secure+SameSite cookie (then you need CSRF protection). And don't use alg:none / don't trust the alg from the token header — the classic algorithm-confusion attack: hard-pin the expected algorithm (e.g. RS256) on the validator side.","hook":"A JWT is a printed cinema ticket with the showtime on it: the usher admits you by the signature, without calling the box office. You can't cancel an already-printed ticket — you can only make sessions short (short TTL) and keep a blacklist of stolen ticket numbers at the door (denylist by jti).","more":"**Three working ways to 'revoke' a JWT (all bring state back to the server):**\n\n- **Short-lived access + long-lived refresh.** The access JWT lives 5–15 min and is checked by signature only (fast, stateless). The refresh token lives server-side (Redis/DB) and is deleted on logout/ban — once access expires, no new one is issued. Compromise window = access-token TTL.\n- **Denylist (blacklist) by `jti`.** The JWT carries a unique `jti` claim. On logout, `jti` is written to Redis with `TTL = exp − now`. Every request checks whether `jti` is on the denylist. Downside: you're back to a lookup, partially losing the stateless advantage — but only for revoked tokens.\n- **User version / token epoch.** The token carries a `tv` (token version) claim. The user has a `token_version` field in the DB. Ban/password change → increment. On each request you compare token `tv` with the DB; mismatch → reject. This revokes ALL of a user's tokens at once (handy for 'log out of all devices').\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** many set the access-token TTL to hours/days 'so the user doesn't re-login' — and then a ban takes effect not immediately but after those hours. If you need instant revocation, either use a short TTL or a mandatory lookup (denylist/version), and then admit honestly: you've built stateful auth, just on top of JWT. 'Stateless with instant revoke' are mutually exclusive requirements.\n\n**Where a session still beats JWT:** a classic server-rendered web app on a single domain that doesn't need cross-service identity propagation — sessions are simpler, safer (the token isn't in a JS-accessible place, HttpOnly+Secure+SameSite cookie), and revoke is free.","quizCorrect":"A JWT is validated by signature without contacting the server, so until exp it's non-revocable — for instant revocation you must bring state back (short TTL + server refresh, jti denylist, or token version).","quizWrong":["A JWT can be instantly revoked the standard way — just remove the token from localStorage on the client at logout.nd it stops being accepted by every service in the system straight away within a second or two","Sessions and JWTs are equivalent for revocation: both require a DB hit on every request, no difference in revoke.","To revoke a JWT you rotate the signing secret on the server — this safely invalidates only the one target token."],"picCap":"A session is revoked by deleting the record; a JWT stays valid until exp — state is brought back via short TTL + server-side refresh + jti denylist."},"g-web-refresh-token-rotation":{"q":"Why do you need an access + refresh token pair, how does refresh token rotation work, and how does reuse detection function?","a":"The access token is made short-lived (5-15 min) and stateless (usually a JWT) so the resource server can validate it locally by signature without hitting a database; the refresh token is long-lived (days/weeks), stored securely (HttpOnly Secure cookie), and exchanged for a new pair only at the auth server. Rotation means each exchange invalidates the old refresh token and issues a new one — tokens become single-use. Reuse detection: if an already-used (revoked) refresh token arrives, the server treats it as a sign of theft and invalidates the whole token family, forcing a re-login. This mechanism is described in the OAuth 2.0 Security BCP (RFC 9700) as mandatory protection for public clients (SPA, mobile).","d":"Tokens in a family are linked via a shared family_id and a counter/previous-jti in the auth server's DB — refresh rotation and reuse detection cannot be done correctly without server-side state.","note":"⚠️ If you store the refresh token in localStorage (instead of an HttpOnly Secure cookie), any XSS reads it instantly — and rotation does NOT save you here: the thief just rotates the chain faster than the victim, and in the worst case reuse detection kicks out the legitimate user while leaving the session to the thief. Rotation protects against an ALREADY-STOLEN token after the fact, but does not replace protection against theft (HttpOnly, CSP, sender-constrained DPoP/mTLS tokens from RFC 9700).","hook":"A refresh token is like a single-use ticket with a seat number: present it, get the next ticket, the old one burns. If the conductor sees TWO tickets with the same number (yours and the thief's), they realize someone made a copy and shut down the whole route.","more":"**Why split into two roles at all:**\n- **Access token** — short and stateless: the resource server validates the signature (RS256/ES256) locally, with no network call. The trade-off price is that it's valid until expiry — it can't be revoked instantly (hence the short TTL).\n- **Refresh token** — long-lived but used rarely and only at the auth server, so it can be kept stateful (in a DB) and revoked at any moment.\n\n**How rotation + reuse detection work together:**\n- Each refresh token belongs to a **token family** (a shared `family_id` created at first login).\n- On exchange the server marks the old token `used`/`revoked` and issues a new one in the same family.\n- If a token that is already marked `used` arrives — it's either a thief using a stolen copy, or the legitimate client using a copy the thief already stole. They're indistinguishable, so the **whole family is killed** — both the legitimate user and the thief get sent to re-login. Security over UX.\n\n**A concrete gotcha — race on parallel requests:**\nWhen the access token expires, an SPA often fires 3-4 parallel requests, each catches a 401 and independently hits refresh with the same RT. With a naive implementation the second request brings an 'already-used' token and falsely triggers reuse detection — kicking the user out for no reason. Fix it with:\n- **single-flight / mutex** on the client — only one refresh in flight, the rest wait for its result;\n- a **grace period** on the server (a short ~10-30 sec window during which the just-rotated token is still accepted);\n- idempotency: return the same new token for concurrent exchanges of the same RT.","quizCorrect":"On exchange a new refresh token is issued and the old one is invalidated; re-presenting the old one is treated as theft and invalidates the entire token chain","quizWrong":["The refresh token is re-signed with the same value but a new expiry; reuse detection catches expired access tokens","The access token renews itself on the resource server without contacting the auth server, and refresh is only needed on logout needed only for the very first login","Rotation changes the signing key (kid) on every request, and reuse detection compares the client's IP address"],"picCap":"Rotation: every refresh is single-use; a revoked RT2 arriving = theft signal &rarr; the whole token family is invalidated"},"g-web-put-vs-patch":{"q":"PUT vs PATCH vs POST for updating a resource — how do they differ semantically, which is idempotent, and why is PATCH usually NOT idempotent?","a":"PUT is a full replacement of the resource at a known URI: the client sends the complete new representation and the server makes it the current state. PUT is idempotent by definition in RFC 7231 — resending the same body yields the same final state. PATCH (RFC 5789) is a partial modification: the body describes not the result but a set of instructions/a delta, and the method is neither required to be idempotent nor safe. POST is unsafe and non-idempotent by contract, typically creating a subordinate resource (server picks the URI) or triggering loosely-defined processing; repeating a POST creates a duplicate.","d":"Idempotency is a property of the method's protocol contract, not a guarantee of a given implementation: even PUT can be implemented non-idempotently (e.g. an increment), but that violates HTTP semantics that proxy/client retries rely on.","note":"⚠️ A classic prod trap: PUT with a partial body silently wipes fields. A client sends PUT /user/42 with {\"name\":\"Ivan\"}, forgetting email — by strict PUT semantics this means 'make the whole resource look like this', so the server must null out email. Many implement PUT as a merge and think they're doing PUT, when in fact it's PATCH; this breaks the contract and confuses integrators. If you need a partial edit, it's PATCH, not a 'convenient' PUT.","hook":"PUT is 'overwrite the whole file' (save twice — same file). PATCH is 'a command to a counter': balance += 10 on a double-click credits 20. POST is 'submit the form': click twice — two orders.","more":"**Semantics of the three methods (RFC 7231 / RFC 5789):**\n\n- **PUT** — 'make the state of the resource at this URI equal to the request body'. The body is a *complete representation*. If the resource didn't exist it may create it (upsert); if it existed it replaces it entirely. Idempotent and **not** safe.\n- **PATCH** — 'apply the set of changes from the body to the resource'. The body is a *patch document* (e.g. application/json-patch+json per RFC 6902 or application/merge-patch+json per RFC 7386). NOT idempotent and NOT safe by contract.\n- **POST** — 'process this however you see fit'. The most general method. Not safe, not idempotent.\n\n**Why PATCH usually is NOT idempotent — concretely:**\n\n- **Relative operations.** JSON Patch allows {\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/items/-\",\"value\":x} — append to the end of an array. A repeat appends the element twice.\n- **Test-dependent patches.** The test operation in RFC 6902 fails on the second application (412/409) because the state has already changed — the result differs from the first call.\n- **BUT:** a merge-patch with *scalar* fields ({\"status\":\"PAID\"}) is effectively idempotent — a repeat yields the same value. So PATCH *can* be idempotent; the spec just doesn't guarantee it, and infrastructure (retries) doesn't rely on it.\n\n**Practical takeaway:** safe automatic retries on network errors are allowed only for idempotent methods (GET, PUT, DELETE). For POST/PATCH you need an explicit dedup mechanism — Idempotency-Key (Stripe pattern), a unique DB constraint, or a conditional request with If-Match/ETag.","quizCorrect":"PUT is idempotent (full replacement → same final state), PATCH often isn't, because the body may describe a relative operation (e.g. an increment), and repeating it accumulates the effect","quizWrong":["PATCH is always idempotent because it changes only part of the resource, while PUT isn't because it replaces the whole objectaces the whole thing on every single call it receives that arrives","Both are idempotent: PUT and PATCH both modify an existing resource, unlike POST which creates a new one","Idempotency depends solely on the method in the spec and cannot be violated by the server implementation"],"picCap":"PUT sets the whole state (a repeat is safe); a PATCH delta accumulates on retry; POST spawns resources."},"g-web-tcp-handshake-lifecycle":{"q":"What happens when establishing a TCP connection (3-way handshake), why is setup 'expensive', and how do head-of-line blocking and keep-alive relate to it?","a":"3-way handshake: the client sends SYN (with its initial ISN and options — MSS, window scale, SACK-permitted), the server replies SYN-ACK (its own ISN + ACK of the client's), the client sends ACK — only then is the connection ESTABLISHED. It's 'expensive' because of at least 1 full RTT of latency before any data can be sent (and with TLS, another 1–2 RTTs on top), plus the server holds an entry in the SYN queue and spends memory/resources per connection. Keep-alive (HTTP persistent connection / Connection: keep-alive) reuses an already-established TCP connection for multiple requests, amortizing the handshake and TLS cost. Head-of-line blocking arises because TCP guarantees strict byte ordering: losing one segment blocks delivery of all subsequent bytes to the application until the packet is retransmitted — on that single connection all multiplexed HTTP/2 streams suffer.","d":"QUIC/HTTP3 fixes TCP HOL by moving ordering and loss to independent streams over UDP, and merges the transport + TLS handshake into 1 RTT (0-RTT on resumption).","note":"⚠️ HTTP/2 does NOT eliminate head-of-line blocking — it merely moves it from the HTTP layer to the TCP layer. Multiplexing streams over one TCP connection means a single lost TCP segment stalls ALL streams at once (on lossy networks HTTP/2 can be slower than HTTP/1.1 with a connection pool). Only HTTP/3 over QUIC truly solves it. A second common mistake: setting a very large server keep-alive timeout without accounting for the file-descriptor/thread limit — idle connections eat resources and cause failures under load.","hook":"A handshake is like shaking hands before a deal: 'Hi, I'm ready' (SYN) → 'I'm ready too, hi' (SYN-ACK) → 'Great, let's start' (ACK). Only after the triple nod can business begin. And TCP is a queue at a single door: one stuck person (a lost packet) holds up the whole line, even if those behind are headed to different offices.","more":"**States and handshake cost:**\n- **SYN** — client: `SYN_SENT`. Carries the initial sequence number (ISN, randomized against spoofing) and TCP options: MSS, window scale, SACK-permitted, timestamp.\n- **SYN-ACK** — server: `SYN_RECEIVED`. The entry is held in the **SYN queue** (incomplete connection queue).\n- **ACK** — both sides: `ESTABLISHED`, the connection moves to the accept queue and `accept()` picks it up.\n\n**Why expensive:**\n- At least **1 RTT** before the first data byte; +TLS 1.2 = 2 more RTTs, TLS 1.3 = 1 RTT (0-RTT on resumption).\n- Memory per connection (buffers, control block), CPU load during the TLS handshake.\n- **TIME_WAIT** after close holds the port for 2×MSL (typically 60s) — with thousands of short connections, ports/resources get exhausted.\n\n**Keep-alive (two different things — don't confuse):**\n- **HTTP keep-alive** (`Connection: keep-alive`, default in HTTP/1.1) — reusing a TCP connection for multiple HTTP requests. Amortizes handshake + TLS.\n- **TCP keepalive** (SO_KEEPALIVE) — a separate probing mechanism to detect dead peers, defaults to ~2 hours, unrelated to performance.\n\n**Head-of-line blocking by layer:**\n- HTTP/1.1: app-level HOL — one slow response blocks the pipeline, hence the pool of 6 connections per host.\n- HTTP/2: removed app-level HOL via multiplexing, but **created TCP-level HOL** — one lost segment stalls all streams.\n- HTTP/3 (QUIC/UDP): streams are transport-independent — loss in one stream doesn't block others.\n\n**Gotcha:** SYN flood — an attack filling the SYN queue with half-open connections; defense is **SYN cookies** (the server keeps no state, encoding it in the response ISN).","quizCorrect":"Loss of a single TCP segment blocks delivery of all subsequent bytes (and all multiplexed HTTP/2 streams) until retransmission, because TCP guarantees strict ordering","quizWrong":["The server can't process requests fast enough due to thread-pool exhaustion, so they queue up in the accept backlog until a worker finally frees up to take them to take","The browser caps parallel connections to one host at six, so the seventh request waits","The TLS handshake blocks the channel until certificate exchange completes across all streams"],"picCap":"SYN / SYN-ACK / ACK = 1 RTT before data. In HTTP/2 a single lost TCP segment blocks all multiplexed streams (HOL)."},"g-web-token-storage-cookie-vs-header":{"q":"Where to store the token on the client: httpOnly cookie vs Authorization header (localStorage) — how does the choice affect XSS, CSRF, and server-side logic?","a":"An httpOnly+Secure+SameSite cookie is unreadable from JS, so XSS cannot steal the token — but the browser sends it automatically on every request to the domain, which opens up CSRF (you need SameSite=Lax/Strict plus an anti-CSRF token or double-submit). A token in localStorage/Authorization header is immune to classic CSRF (your JS attaches it, not the browser), but any XSS reads and exfiltrates it, and it isn't sent on sub-requests automatically. Server-side, the cookie approach usually relies on a session/stateful logic plus a CSRF filter (Spring Security CsrfFilter), while the header approach uses stateless Bearer JWT validation (OncePerRequestFilter / Quarkus SmallRye JWT), with no CSRF defense but a mandatory CORS policy.","d":"Today's best-practice hybrid: short-lived access token in a JS in-memory variable (not localStorage) + long-lived refresh token in an httpOnly+SameSite=Strict cookie scoped to a narrow /auth/refresh path — so XSS can't reach the refresh token and SameSite neutralizes CSRF.","note":"⚠️ SameSite=Lax does NOT block CSRF on top-level GET navigation (clicking a link, GET form) and offers no protection if subdomains aren't isolated — a `Domain=.example.com` cookie is sent to evil.example.com on a hostile subdomain. And remember: localStorage is vulnerable not only to direct XSS reads — any third-party JS library (supply-chain) bundled in your app silently reads the token; an httpOnly cookie protects against this, localStorage fundamentally cannot.","hook":"A cookie is 'a butler who carries the letter to the recipient himself' (XSS can't open the envelope, but anyone can shout 'deliver it!' — CSRF). localStorage is 'you keep the letter in your pocket and hand it over yourself' (no stranger can force you to give it up — but a pickpocket-XSS lifts it).","more":"**Threat matrix:**\n\n- **XSS:** httpOnly cookie — inaccessible via `document.cookie`, token is protected. localStorage/sessionStorage/JS variable — readable by any script running on the page, token leaks. This is the main argument against localStorage.\n- **CSRF:** localStorage/header — immune (the browser doesn't attach the header itself, your fetch/axios sets it). Cookie — vulnerable, since the browser sends it automatically; protection required.\n\n**Protecting a cookie from CSRF (in order of strength):**\n\n- `SameSite=Strict` — cookie isn't sent on any cross-site request (but breaks navigation from external links into the logged-in area).\n- `SameSite=Lax` (default in modern browsers) — sent only on safe top-level navigation (GET). Does not fully close CSRF.\n- Anti-CSRF token (synchronizer token) or **double-submit cookie** — mandatory for `SameSite=None` and as defense-in-depth.\n\n**Server-side logic differs:**\n\n- Cookie → typically stateful session (`HttpSession`, Redis session store), requires a `CsrfFilter`. Load balancing needs sticky sessions or a shared store.\n- Header/Bearer → stateless: `OncePerRequestFilter` validates the JWT signature, stores nothing. Scales horizontally without a session store, but token revocation is harder — you need a blocklist or a short TTL.\n\n**Concrete gotcha:** many put a JWT in localStorage 'because it's stateless and convenient for SPAs', but forget that a **JWT can't be revoked instantly** — a token stolen via XSS stays valid until exp. With an httpOnly refresh cookie you can invalidate the session server-side.","quizCorrect":"An httpOnly cookie protects against token theft via XSS, but requires CSRF defense (SameSite + anti-CSRF token), since the browser sends it automatically","quizWrong":["localStorage is safer than a cookie in every way: it's protected from both XSS and CSRF simultaneously","An httpOnly cookie completely eliminates both XSS and CSRF without additional measures","An Authorization header from localStorage is vulnerable to CSRF because the browser attaches it automaticallyally to any cross-site request the page makes"],"picCap":"Mirror tradeoff: the cookie closes XSS, opens CSRF; the header — the opposite. The hybrid takes the best of both."},"web-owasp-top10":{"q":"What is the OWASP Top-10 and which categories are must-know for a backend developer?","a":"The OWASP Top-10 is a consensus checklist of the most critical web security risks (2021 edition), not a list of specific CVEs. It's a ranking of risk categories used in threat modeling, security review, and as a baseline for requirements. The backend core: A01 Broken Access Control (#1 — horizontal/vertical escalation, IDOR), A03 Injection (SQL/NoSQL/OS — fixed by parameterization), A02 Cryptographic Failures (storing/transmitting sensitive data), A07 Identification & Auth Failures, and A08 Software/Data Integrity (insecure deserialization, CI/CD supply chain). Plus A04 Insecure Design, A05 Misconfiguration, A06 Vulnerable Components, A09 Logging Failures, A10 SSRF.","d":"The OWASP Top-10 is revised roughly every 4 years. In 2021 there was a shift: Broken Access Control rose from #5 to #1 (present in 94% of tested apps), Injection dropped from #1 to #3, and new categories appeared — Insecure Design (A04) and Software & Data Integrity Failures (A08, which absorbed Insecure Deserialization). 8 of 10 categories are ranked by data (frequency, exploitability, impact), and 2 by community survey. Note: it's not a certification standard (OWASP ASVS exists for verification) but an awareness document. Each category aggregates many CWEs.","note":"⚠️ The OWASP Top-10 is an awareness baseline, NOT a 'pass = secure' criterion. Covering all 10 categories does not equal 'the app is secure': it's coverage of the most common risks, not verification. For real security validation use OWASP ASVS (Application Security Verification Standard) with levels L1–L3, and keep the Top-10 as a starting reference in reviews, not as a final stamp of quality.","hook":"It's like a pilot's pre-flight checklist: not a description of every possible aircraft failure, but the 10 items that statistically kill most often. Skip A01 'are the cockpit doors locked' and a first-class passenger walks into someone else's account.","more":"**Concrete gotcha — A01 and IDOR via a 'safe' REST endpoint**\n\nThe most common 2021 flaw isn't exotic — it's plain CRUD. Example:\n\n```java\n@GET @Path(\"/orders/{id}\")\npublic Order getOrder(@PathParam(\"id\") Long id) {\n    return orderRepo.findById(id); // ❌ no owner check\n}\n```\n\nThe endpoint is authenticated (valid JWT present), so many treat it as 'protected'. But authorization ≠ authentication: user A changes the `id` in the URL to someone else's and reads user B's order (**IDOR**, classic Broken Access Control). Fix — always bind the resource to the subject:\n\n```java\nreturn orderRepo.findByIdAndOwner(id, currentUser.getId())\n        .orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException()); // 404, not 403 — don't leak existence\n```\n\n**Why it's #1:** access control can't be delegated to a single framework filter — it's tied to the business logic of each resource, so it breaks most often. Deny by default, enforce on the server (not in the UI), and write integration tests for 'other user → 404'.","quizCorrect":"OWASP Top-10 is a checklist of risk categories for awareness and threat modeling, where #1 in 2021 is Broken Access Control","quizWrong":["OWASP Top-10 is an exhaustive list of specific CVE vulnerabilities, updated weekly","OWASP Top-10 is a certification standard whose passing guarantees an application is secure and is required for the audit too","OWASP Top-10 hasn't changed since 2017, and Injection still holds #1"],"picCap":"OWASP Top-10 2017&rarr;2021 shifts: Access Control moved to #1, A04 and A08 added"},"web-supply-chain":{"q":"Supply-chain attack: the vulnerability is not in your code but in a transitive dependency (Log4Shell, CVE-2021-44228). How do you defend against it at the development-process level?","a":"Log4Shell is an RCE in log4j2: when logging a string containing ${jndi:ldap://...}, the library performed a JNDI lookup and loaded a remote class, executing attacker code. Defense isn't a single patch but a process: SBOM (a full inventory of all dependencies including transitive ones), automated CVE scanners (OWASP Dependency-Check / Snyk / Trivy) in CI, version pinning and artifact signature verification, and a well-drilled fast patch process to ship a fix in hours, not weeks. The core pain: the vulnerability arrives transitively — you never even declared it in pom.xml.","d":"A senior must understand: you can't pre-empt a 0-day in someone else's library, but you can shrink MTTR (mean time to remediate). That comes from visibility (SBOM knows log4j exists at all and where), automated detection (the scanner fails CI on a known CVE), and patch readiness. Log4Shell proved the hard part wasn't 'how to patch' but 'which of our 500 services even contain a vulnerable log4j, and what version' — without an SBOM, companies hunted that by hand for weeks.","note":"⚠️ The system flag -Dlog4j2.formatMsgNoLookups=true (and env var) is NOT a full fix: it doesn't close every vector and doesn't work for versions < 2.10. The only reliable fix is upgrading the artifact itself to 2.17.1+. And don't forget shaded copies of log4j bundled inside other dependencies' fat-jars — dependency:tree won't see them; you need a content scanner (Trivy/grype).","hook":"It's like a contaminated ingredient inside a meal kit: you didn't add the spoiled sauce yourself, it came pre-packed. Without an invoice (SBOM) you don't even know which dishes contain it, and by the time you find out the whole restaurant is poisoned.","more":"**Concrete gotcha — 'patched, but not really'.**\n\nThe first fix, 2.15.0, closed the main vector, but a bypass was found (CVE-2021-45046, DoS + limited RCE in non-default configs), then a DoS in 2.16.0 (CVE-2021-45105) and a JDBC Appender issue (CVE-2021-44832). Teams that settled on 'we're on 2.15, we're safe' stayed exposed. The safe version is **2.17.1+** (for Java 8).\n\n```\n# temporary mitigation BEFORE patching (older versions)\n-Dlog4j2.formatMsgNoLookups=true   # only works 2.10..2.14.1, does NOT help in <2.10\n```\n\nAnother trap: formatMsgNoLookups does NOT fully close the hole (lookups could fire via other paths, e.g. Thread Context Map). The only reliable route is to upgrade the artifact, not flip a flag. And remember: a vulnerable log4j copy can arrive **shaded/fat-jarred inside another library** — a plain `mvn dependency:tree` won't show it; you need a content scan (Trivy/grype read nested jars too).","quizCorrect":"The substring ${jndi:ldap://...} in logged text made log4j2 perform a JNDI lookup and load an arbitrary class from a remote server — that's the RCE","quizWrong":["The vulnerability was in the JDK itself: deserialization of an untrusted object via ObjectInputStream during logginging a normal request to the service","The attacker overwrote log4j2.xml via path traversal and swapped appenders for their own","A buffer overflow in log4j's native code when logging very long strings"],"picCap":"A transitive dependency turns a log string into RCE; defense is visibility + auto-scan + fast patch"},"web-idor":{"q":"Broken Access Control / IDOR: the endpoint `/api/orders/123` takes the id from the URL and returns the resource. An attacker changes 123 to 124 and reads someone else's order. Why does this happen and how do you fix it correctly?","a":"IDOR is the absence of a permission check on the SPECIFIC object: the code verifies 'the user is logged in' (authentication) but not 'this order belongs to this user' (object-level authorization). The correct fix is to verify ownership/access to the object on the server for every request, e.g. `WHERE id = :id AND owner_id = :currentUser`, and to operate deny-by-default. You must not trust any identifiers or flags coming from the client; using a UUID instead of an incremental id only makes guessing harder (security by obscurity), it does not replace the access check.","d":"IDOR is #1 in OWASP Top 10 2021 (Broken Access Control). The key distinction: 401/authentication ≠ 403/authorization. Authentication says 'who you are', authorization says 'what you're allowed'. IDOR is a failure of the latter, at the level of an individual object. The protection should be enforced centrally (filter/interceptor/policy layer), not via an `if` scattered across every controller — otherwise one forgotten endpoint = a hole.","note":"⚠️ Authentication ≠ authorization. A valid JWT/session proves only WHO you are, not that you may touch THIS object. Thousands of IDOR vulnerabilities live in code where the developer saw `@Authenticated` and assumed access was locked down — while there is no object-ownership check. And never trust `owner_id`/`role`/`tenant_id` sent in the request body: take them from the server-side authentication context.","hook":"It's like a coat check where they don't verify the ticket: you handed in your №123 but asked for coat №124 — and the rack silently gives you someone else's coat because 'the person came with a ticket, didn't they'. They check that you're a customer at all (you entered the building), but not that THIS particular coat is yours.","more":"## A concrete gotcha\n\nThe most common hole is **'checked it on one endpoint, forgot it on the neighbor'**. The classic:\n\n```\nGET    /api/orders/{id}        // here we added AND owner_id = :user ✅\nGET    /api/orders/{id}/items  // but here we forgot — items fetched by orderId without an owner check ❌\nPUT    /api/orders/{id}        // also forgot here — you can edit someone else's order\nDELETE /api/orders/{id}        // and here\n```\n\nThat's why the check must not be smeared by hand across controllers — it's enforced **centrally**: a policy layer / `@PreAuthorize` with an ownership check / an interceptor that for any `:id` calls a single `accessControl.assertCanAccess(user, resource)`. Coverage must be **deny-by-default**: a new endpoint is closed by default until explicitly allowed.\n\n**Mass assignment as a sibling hole:** even with a correct ownership check, if you bind the whole JSON onto the entity, a user will send `{\"ownerId\": \"victim\", \"isAdmin\": true}` and reassign owner/role. Accept only whitelisted fields (a DTO), and set `owner_id` from the authentication context, NOT from the payload.\n\n**Why UUID is not a fix:** ids leak constantly — in URLs in logs, in the Referer, in shared links, in other APIs' responses, in email. A UUID reduces mass enumeration, but one leaked id + no check = access. UUID is defense in depth, not the defense itself.","quizCorrect":"On the server, for every request, verify that the object belongs to / is accessible by the current user (object-level authorization, deny-by-default)","quizWrong":["Replace incremental ids with random UUIDs — then nobody can guess another user's id","Move the id into the request body instead of the URL and check permissions only on the frontend before the request is ever sent to the server to the server","Enable HTTPS and JWT authentication — that's enough to stop anyone from reaching others' data"],"picCap":"Without an object-level check, swapping the id returns someone else's resource; the fix is owner_id in the condition on the server"},"web-secrets":{"q":"Where must you NOT store secrets, and how to do it right?","a":"Secrets must never live in code, git history, Docker images (layers persist forever), or plain env/config files without encryption. The right way: a centralized secret manager (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager) as the single source of truth, least-privilege access (policies scoped to a specific path), automatic rotation, and short-lived credentials (dynamic secrets / STS instead of eternal passwords). Always scan git history with gitleaks/trufflehog in CI and pre-commit — a leaked secret is compromised forever and must be rotated, not just deleted from history.","d":"A senior must separate 'where the app reads the secret from' versus 'where the secret is stored'. A plain env var isn't evil per se — the evil is its value sitting in git/the image/CI logs. Vault gives three things a static store cannot: dynamic secrets (a DB password that lives 1 hour and is bound to a specific instance), an audit trail of every access, and rotation without redeploy. The key mindset shift is from 'secret as a constant' to 'secret as a short-lived token'.","note":"⚠️ git filter-branch/BFG and force-push do NOT remove a secret: forks, local clones, CI caches, GitHub PR snapshots, and mirrors all keep the old commits. After any leak you must immediately rotate (revoke the key) — 'cleaning the history' is cosmetic, not protection.","hook":"A secret in git history is like a key under the doormat: you 'removed' it (git rm), but the neighbor who peeked once already made a copy. The only real fix is changing the lock (rotation), not hiding the mat better.","more":"**The Docker-layer gotcha.** Many assume `ENV SECRET=...` or `ARG SECRET` in a Dockerfile is 'temporary'. In reality each instruction creates a layer, and the value stays in the image history — anyone running `docker history` or unpacking layers sees it, even if you 'deleted' it in a later layer via `unset`/`rm`. Build-time secrets must go through `--secret` (BuildKit), which mounts them in RAM and never writes them to a layer.\n\n**Vault dynamic secrets — the main win.** Instead of 'here's an eternal Postgres password', Vault creates a DB user on the fly with a TTL (e.g. 1 hour) and drops it on expiry. A leak of such a credential is nearly useless — it'll expire. That's the long-lived to short-lived shift.\n\n**Practical team minimum:** gitleaks in a pre-commit hook + in CI on every PR, secrets only from Vault/Secrets Manager via a sidecar or CSI driver, access policies scoped to a specific path (not `*`), and audit logging enabled.","quizCorrect":"A secret leaked into git is compromised forever — it must be rotated, not just removed from history","quizWrong":["A git rebase/filter-branch and force-push is enough — the secret physically disappears for everyone with no trace","If the repo is private, secrets in code are safe and rotation isn't needed","gpg-encrypting the .env file fully replaces a secret manager and rotation"],"picCap":"Forbidden stores (leaked = forever) versus Vault with rotation and TTL"},"web-security-headers":{"q":"List the key security headers and explain why a CORS misconfiguration is dangerous.","a":"Core set: CSP (whitelist of script/style sources — the main XSS barrier), HSTS (forces HTTPS, defends against downgrade/SSL-strip), X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff (blocks MIME-sniffing), and X-Frame-Options or CSP frame-ancestors (anti-clickjacking). A CORS misconfig is dangerous when the server returns Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * together with Allow-Credentials: true (the browser blocks that combo, but an echoed Origin without a whitelist check is NOT blocked), or reflects the incoming Origin without validation — then a malicious site can read private responses sent with the victim's cookies. Key point: CORS does not protect a resource, it relaxes the Same-Origin Policy by letting foreign origins read the response.","d":"CSP is a response header listing allowed sources (default-src/script-src etc.); it blocks inline scripts without a nonce/hash, which is the primary XSS mitigation. HSTS (Strict-Transport-Security) with max-age forces the browser to use HTTPS only; includeSubDomains and preload harden it further. nosniff stops the browser from guessing Content-Type and executing text as script. frame-ancestors is the modern replacement for X-Frame-Options and supports multiple sources. CORS works at the browser level: SOP forbids JS from reading a cross-origin response, and CORS headers selectively permit it — so a mistake in their config is a read-access hole for sensitive data, not a protection.","note":"⚠️ The browser blocks the combo Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * + Allow-Credentials: true, and many wrongly treat that as 'safe by default'. But if the server reflects the incoming Origin instead of using * (or validates it via contains/endsWith), the block is bypassed — a full data-theft vulnerability. CORS headers govern reading the response, not access to the resource: they do NOT replace authorization and do NOT protect against CSRF.","hook":"Security headers are locks on your apartment doors: CSP keeps out foreign scripts, HSTS forbids entering without a reservation (HTTP). A CORS misconfig is a doorman who lets in anyone who states any name: you log 'who came' instead of checking against the residents list — so a burglar just states his name and walks in with your keys (cookies).","more":"## Concrete gotcha\n\nThe most common mistake is the 'dynamic whitelist': the server takes `Origin` from the request and **reflects** it back into `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, adding `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. This is equivalent to `ACAO: *`, but bypasses the browser's ban on `*` + credentials. Any attacker site sends `fetch(url, { credentials: 'include' })`, the browser attaches the victim's cookies, and the private response becomes readable from the attacker's JS.\n\n```http\n# request from the evil site\nOrigin: evil.example\n# vulnerable response — server blindly echoed the incoming Origin\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: evil.example\nAccess-Control-Allow-Credentials: true\n```\n\nRelated traps:\n- **Suffix check** `origin.endsWith(\"mybank.com\")` is bypassed via `evilmybank.com`; substring `contains(\"mybank.com\")` via `mybank.com.evil.com`.\n- **`null` Origin** in a whitelist is dangerous: it's sent by `file://`, sandboxed iframes, and redirects — an attacker can easily forge it.\n- **CSP without `frame-ancestors`** does not close clickjacking; the old `X-Frame-Options` cannot list multiple domains.\n- **HSTS** has no effect on the very first HTTP visit before the site is in the preload list — hence you need `preload`.","quizCorrect":"Reflecting the incoming Origin into Access-Control-Allow-Origin without a whitelist check + Allow-Credentials: true — a foreign site reads private responses with the victim's cookies","quizWrong":["Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * fully opens reading of cookie-bearing responses to any site that asks for them, which is why it is the recommended default in production in every deployment","CORS headers protect the server from CSRF, so their absence is the main hole","CSP and CORS are synonyms; both restrict script sources"],"picCap":"4 headers strengthen defense; a CORS misconfig (echoed Origin + credentials) breaks it"},"web-rate-limit":{"q":"How do you protect authentication against brute-force and account enumeration? Which levels of limiting to apply, and where is the lockout-vs-DoS tradeoff?","a":"Limit on several axes at once: per IP, per username/account, and per endpoint — using token bucket (allows bursts) or sliding window (exact count per window). On failed logins apply exponential backoff and captcha on suspicious activity. Against enumeration return the SAME response and the SAME timing for 'no such user' and 'wrong password' (single generic error, constant-time). A hard account lockout by username becomes a DoS vector — an attacker locks out other people's accounts — so prefer throttle + captcha, and combine per-IP limits with per-account limits.","d":"Senior-level: know that a pure per-IP limit is bypassed by a botnet/proxies (distributed slow brute-force), while a pure per-username lockout is a ready-made DoS. So you need multi-axis defense (IP + account + global) plus soft measures (backoff, captcha) instead of a hard block. User enumeration leaks not only via the error text but also via timing (bcrypt runs only when the user exists), HTTP codes, and the behavior of reset-password and signup forms.","note":"⚠️ Identical error TEXT does not stop enumeration if the timing differs: for a missing user the code often skips bcrypt/argon2 and replies in milliseconds, while for an existing user it computes the hash for hundreds of ms. The attacker enumerates accounts by response time. Always run the password check against a dummy hash even when the user is not found (constant-time path).","hook":"It's like bank security. One turnstile at the entrance (per-IP limit) — a swarm of bots walks in through a hundred proxy doors. So you also put a counter on each specific deposit box (per-account limit). But if 'three wrong PINs and the box is welded shut forever', any troublemaker deliberately enters three wrong PINs on SOMEONE ELSE'S box and locks out its owner — that's your DoS. So instead of welding, first ask to 'wait and solve a captcha'. And crucially: the guard must answer 'no such client' and 'wrong password' identically and in exactly the same time, otherwise the robber reads from his hesitation which boxes actually exist.","more":"## Gotcha: enumeration via timing, not error text\n\nThe team honestly made an **identical message** 'Invalid email or password' for both cases — and it still leaked. The cause is timing:\n\n```java\n// VULNERABLE: bcrypt runs only when the user is found\nUser u = repo.findByEmail(email);     // fast\nif (u == null) return UNAUTHORIZED;   // replies in ~5 ms\nreturn passwordEncoder.matches(raw, u.getHash()) // bcrypt ~250 ms\n        ? OK : UNAUTHORIZED;          // replies in ~250 ms\n```\n\nThe attacker measures response time: 5 ms → no user, 250 ms → exists. That is **timing-based user enumeration**, bypassing the 'identical text'.\n\n**Fix — always do the same work:**\n```java\nUser u = repo.findByEmail(email);\nString hash = (u != null) ? u.getHash() : DUMMY_BCRYPT_HASH;\nboolean ok = passwordEncoder.matches(raw, hash); // bcrypt ALWAYS runs\nreturn (u != null && ok) ? OK : UNAUTHORIZED;\n```\n\nAlso worth knowing:\n- **Multi-axis limiting**: per-IP only is bypassed by a botnet (distributed brute-force, 1 attempt from each of thousands of addresses); per-account only is a DoS. You need both axes plus a global anomaly counter.\n- **token bucket vs sliding window**: a bucket lets through a burst up to the bucket size and refills at the refill rate (good for spiky APIs); a sliding window enforces an exact limit of N requests per window T without the 'double-burst' at window boundaries that fixed windows suffer.\n- **Enumeration leaks beyond login**: reset-password ('email sent' vs 'no such email'), signup ('already taken'), differing HTTP codes/Location/response length.\n- **Counter storage**: an in-memory counter in each instance is a hole under horizontal scaling; you need a shared Redis (INCR + EXPIRE) or Bucket4j on a shared backend.\n- **Backoff** must grow per the failures of a specific account/IP, not globally, otherwise one attack slows everyone down.","quizCorrect":"A hard account lockout by username creates a DoS vector: the attacker deliberately locks out other people's accounts with wrong attempts, so throttle + captcha is preferred","quizWrong":["A per-IP rate limit alone fully stops brute-force, since the attacker always has a single address","Different messages 'no such user' / 'wrong password' are better for UX and security, since they help the legitimate user work out which half of the pair they got wrong in on the login form","A token bucket forbids any traffic bursts, whereas a sliding window allows them"],"picCap":"Multi-axis limiting + backoff/captcha; identical response against enumeration; a hard lockout = DoS."},"jvm-thread-dump":{"q":"How do you find a deadlock and contention from a thread dump in production?","a":"Capture the dump: `jstack <pid>` or `kill -3 <pid>` (output goes to the process's stdout). The JVM detects deadlock itself and prints a 'Found one Java-level deadlock' block with a cycle of threads, each holding a monitor the next one needs. Contention shows up as a batch of threads in BLOCKED state waiting on the same lock (`- waiting to lock <0x...>`) while one RUNNABLE thread owns it. Many RUNNABLE threads on the same stack frame = hot path / CPU bottleneck.","d":"Thread states in a dump: RUNNABLE (executing or in I/O — the JVM doesn't distinguish), BLOCKED (waiting to enter a synchronized monitor), WAITING (Object.wait/park with no timeout), TIMED_WAITING (with timeout). The key to spotting contention is grouping BLOCKED threads by lock address: the same `<0x...>` across many threads = a hot spot. To see a trend, take 3-5 dumps ~1-2s apart: if the same threads are stuck on the same frame, it's real blocking, not a momentary snapshot. ReentrantLock and java.util.concurrent are NOT caught by the JVM's built-in deadlock detector (only intrinsic monitors and owned Locks via ThreadMXBean).","note":"⚠️ The JVM's built-in deadlock detector finds deadlocks on intrinsic monitors (`synchronized`) and on ownable locks from `java.util.concurrent.locks` (via `ThreadMXBean.findDeadlockedThreads()`), but it does NOT see deadlocks on semaphores, CountDownLatch, message exchange, or external resources (JDBC connection pool). So the absence of a 'Found one Java-level deadlock' line does NOT guarantee the system isn't in a live lock-up — visually-stuck threads parked on park()/wait() must be analyzed by hand across several dumps.","hook":"A thread dump is a freeze-frame of a traffic jam at an intersection. Deadlock: four cars drove into the center and each waits for the one on its left to back up first — nobody moves (the JVM posts a 'jam detected' sign). Contention: one lane, one barrier, and a huge queue behind it — everyone's alive, but barely crawling.","more":"### Concrete gotcha: RUNNABLE ≠ 'using CPU'\n\nThe JVM marks a thread RUNNABLE both when it's actually computing and when it's parked on blocking native I/O (`socketRead0`, `read` from a DB driver). This is the most common diagnostic mistake: you see 200 RUNNABLE threads and think 'CPU bottleneck', but they're all hanging on a slow downstream service.\n\n```\n\"http-pool-42\" #142 RUNNABLE\n  java.net.SocketInputStream.socketRead0(Native Method)   // NOT CPU — waiting on network!\n  at oracle.jdbc.driver.T4CSocketInputStreamWrapper.read(...)\n```\n\nHow to tell them apart: correlate top CPU threads (`top -H -p <pid>`, convert nid to hex) with the dump. If a RUNNABLE thread burns 0% CPU but sits on `socketRead0`, it's not a hot path, it's I/O wait.\n\n### Contention: group by lock address\n```\n\"worker-5\" BLOCKED\n  - waiting to lock <0x000000071ab2c3d0> (a c.acme.OrderCache)\n\"worker-6\" BLOCKED\n  - waiting to lock <0x000000071ab2c3d0> (a c.acme.OrderCache)  // same address!\n\"worker-1\" RUNNABLE\n  - locked <0x000000071ab2c3d0> (a c.acme.OrderCache)           // owner\n```\nThe same `0x...71ab2c3d0` across many BLOCKED threads + one `locked` = a hot monitor. A candidate for lock splitting or swapping to a concurrent structure.\n\n### 3-5 dumps in a row\nOne dump is a snapshot — a thread may have just briefly entered a synchronized block. Take a series 1-2s apart: consistently stuck on the same frame = a real problem.","quizCorrect":"The JVM detects deadlock itself and prints 'Found one Java-level deadlock' with a cycle of threads; contention shows as many BLOCKED threads on the same lock address while one thread owns it","quizWrong":["Deadlock appears as all threads in WAITING state, and contention as threads in TIMED_WAITING on different locks","The JVM's built-in deadlock detector finds any blocking, including ReentrantLock and semaphores from java.util.concurrent.util.concurrent, so no manual analysis is ever required by a human at all","A RUNNABLE thread is guaranteed to burn CPU, so many RUNNABLE threads always mean a deadlock"],"picCap":"Deadlock = cycle of mutual waits (JVM detects it). Contention = a queue of BLOCKED threads on one lock with a single owner."},"jvm-heap-dump":{"q":"A Java prod service slowly eats memory and hits OutOfMemoryError. How do you find the leak from a heap dump using Eclipse MAT and the dominator tree? Explain the role of retained heap and the GC root.","a":"Capture the dump: either pre-arm with -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError (.hprof is written at the OOM moment), or manually run jmap -dump:live,format=b,file=heap.hprof <pid>. Open the .hprof in Eclipse MAT, look at Leak Suspects and the dominator tree sorted by retained heap — the bytes actually freed if the object is collected (its exclusive subtree in the dominator graph). Find the object with an abnormally large retained size (usually a collection/cache) and via 'Path to GC Roots' (excluding weak/soft) see which live GC root holds it — that is the leak source.","d":"A Java leak = objects logically no longer needed but still reachable from a GC root (statics, threads, stack, JNI), so GC won't collect them. Shallow heap is the object's own size; retained heap is everything that becomes unreachable if it's removed, so retained shows the leak's true 'price'. Dominator tree: A dominates B if every path from a GC root to B goes through A, hence retained(A) = sum of its subtree. Classics: static Map/cache without eviction, ThreadLocal without remove(), unclosed resources, listeners never unsubscribed, an ever-growing ArrayList. jmap -dump:live triggers a full GC and keeps only live objects, so garbage doesn't mask the picture.","note":"⚠️ Taking a heap dump is stop-the-world: for an 8–16 GB heap the JVM freezes for tens of seconds, and the .hprof is as big as the live heap (needs disk space — and the OOM flag writes there too). Don't run jmap -dump blindly on a peak prod instance — you'll push it past health-check timeouts and it gets restarted. Dump on a replica/canary or in a low-load window.","hook":"It's like finding who flooded the apartment below. Shallow heap is the size of one puddle. Retained heap is how much water drains if you shut off one specific tap (the object). The dominator tree shows the main pipe feeding the whole floor, and Path to GC Roots reveals who forgot to close the valve (a static, a ThreadLocal) so the water never drains.","more":"## Gotcha: live dump vs as-is snapshot\n\n`jmap -dump:live,...` forces a **full GC before capture** and writes only reachable objects — handy, garbage doesn't add noise. But if you're hunting a **leak through soft/weak references** or want to see what GC *can't keep up with* under load, `live` may hide it — dump without `live`. And remember: capturing the dump is **stop-the-world** for seconds-to-minutes on a multi-GB heap — a visible pause on hot prod, don't do it blindly at peak.\n\n## In MAT\n- Sort the dominator tree by **retained**, not shallow — otherwise you see a million tiny Strings instead of the one Map holding them.\n- In 'Path to GC Roots' pick **'exclude weak/soft/phantom references'**, or the path drowns in caches and won't reveal the real anchor.\n- Group the histogram **by class** and check 'duplicate strings' / big `char[]`, `byte[]` — a common tell.\n- Compare **two dumps** (spaced apart under load): a growing retained for one class = the smoking gun. One dump shows state, two show the trend.\n\n## Common sources\n`static` collection/cache without eviction, `ThreadLocal` without `remove()` (especially in a Tomcat/Quarkus thread pool — the thread lives long), unclosed resources, listeners never unsubscribed, an unbounded in-memory log.","quizCorrect":"Retained heap — how much memory is freed when the object is collected (its exclusive subtree in the dominator tree); that's what you use to find the leak","quizWrong":["Retained heap — the size of the object itself with no references; it's a synonym for shallow heap under a different name in the tooling used by the analysis tools","Retained heap — memory the object already returned to the collector and that can't be reclaimed","Retained heap is only computed for objects reachable via soft/weak references"],"picCap":"From OOM to hprof: in MAT sort by retained, the abnormal object &rarr; path to the GC root that holds it."},"jvm-profiling":{"q":"JFR vs async-profiler — how to profile CPU and allocations in production, and what is the fundamental difference in sample quality?","a":"JFR (JDK Flight Recorder) is built into the JVM, has extremely low overhead (~1%), uses an event-based model, and is officially production-safe; you enable it via a flag or `jcmd JFR.start`, and it writes a .jfr stream. async-profiler samples via perf_events / AsyncGetCallTrace, so it does NOT suffer from safepoint-bias (the stack is captured at any point, not only at a safepoint, unlike a jstack-loop or most Java profilers), producing honest flame graphs and supporting CPU, alloc, lock, wall-clock, and cache-misses. In practice: JFR runs continuously as a low-cost 'black box' recorder; async-profiler is used surgically for a flame graph when you need max accuracy on hot methods and native code.","d":"Safepoint-bias is the key idea for a senior. Safepoint-based profilers (jstack in a loop, many commercial JVMTI GetAllStackTraces agents) can only capture a thread's stack when the thread has reached a safepoint. A hot tight-loop with no method calls and no allocations may have no safepoint inside at all — the JIT elides it — so such code is 'invisible' or distorted: the profiler attributes the time to the wrong method. async-profiler uses AsyncGetCallTrace + a signal (SIGPROF from perf) to interrupt the thread at an arbitrary point, bypassing the safepoint mechanism, so the sample distribution reflects real CPU time. For CPU method sampling, JFR was historically also tied to safepoint sampling natively, but its overhead/safety is better; and for alloc profiling JFR writes TLAB events directly — this is not stack sampling but real allocation events, giving an exact picture of what is allocated and where. Conclusion: for 'who honestly burns CPU' → async-profiler flame graph; for 'always-on cheap audit + GC/alloc/IO/locks/JIT events in one stream' → JFR. On modern JDKs (17/21) they're often combined: async-profiler can write the .jfr format and you view it in the same JMC.","note":"⚠️ In Docker/Kubernetes `async-profiler -e cpu` silently degrades or fails if `perf_events` is unavailable inside the container (`kernel.perf_event_paranoid > 1` or no `CAP_SYS_ADMIN`/`CAP_PERFMON`). The symptom is an empty or distorted flame graph. Fix: either lower `perf_event_paranoid`, or run with `-e itimer` (works without privileges but is less accurate for native frames). And without `-XX:+DebugNonSafepoints` you lose accuracy even with async-profiler — by default the JVM doesn't emit debug info outside safepoints.","hook":"JFR is an aircraft's flight recorder: always spinning, lightweight, weighs nothing, and after an incident you pull the .jfr and reconstruct what happened. async-profiler is a thermal camera in a mechanic's hand: aim it precisely and see which part is actually overheating. A safepoint profiler is a guard who logs visitors only at the front gate: whoever slipped through a window (a tight-loop with no safepoint) never makes it into the log.","more":"**Concrete gotcha — safepoint-bias in practice.** Classic example: an accumulator method in a tight-loop (`for(...) sum += arr[i]`) with no calls and no allocations. The JIT compiles it without a polling safepoint inside the loop. A safepoint-based profiler (jstack in a loop, hprof, many APM agents) physically cannot interrupt the thread inside this loop — the stack is only captured on exit. Result: the hot spot is attributed to the caller or lost entirely, and you 'optimize' the wrong code.\n\n**Commands.**\n```\n# JFR: continuous recording, dump on demand\njcmd <pid> JFR.start name=rec maxsize=512m maxage=1h\njcmd <pid> JFR.dump name=rec filename=app.jfr\n\n# async-profiler: 30 s CPU flame graph\nasprof -d 30 -e cpu -f flame.html <pid>\n# allocations (TLAB sampling)\nasprof -d 30 -e alloc -f alloc.html <pid>\n# wall-clock — for I/O-bound code where the thread sleeps\nasprof -d 30 -e wall -f wall.html <pid>\n```\n\n**async-profiler pitfalls:** `-e cpu` requires `perf_events` → in Docker/k8s you need `--cap-add SYS_ADMIN` or `kernel.perf_event_paranoid <= 1`. If perf is unavailable — fall back to `-e itimer` (less accurate but unprivileged). And always add `-XX:+UnlockDiagnosticVMOptions -XX:+DebugNonSafepoints`, otherwise even async-profiler loses inline-frame accuracy.\n\n**When to use which:** always-on audit + GC/JIT/IO/lock in one — JFR. Surgical hunt for a hot method and native code (JNI, malloc, GC threads) — async-profiler. On JDK 17/21 combine them: async-profiler writes `.jfr` and it opens in JMC.","quizCorrect":"async-profiler captures the stack via a signal (AsyncGetCallTrace) at an arbitrary point and does not suffer from safepoint-bias","quizWrong":["JFR works only in OpenJDK, while async-profiler works only in Oracle JDK","async-profiler requires stopping the JVM (stop-the-world) on every sample it takes from the running application from the running process","JFR cannot be used in production due to 30-40% overhead"],"picCap":"Safepoint-bias: a jstack-loop distorts a hot loop, while async-profiler captures the stack via a signal at any point"},"sp-jpa-projection":{"q":"DTO / constructor projections — why bother if you already have the entity?","a":"Loading a full @Entity just to read it drags in ALL columns (including @Lob and lazy associations once accessed) and puts the object into the persistence context: it becomes managed and dirty-tracked, so on flush Hibernate compares a snapshot of every field. A projection (JPQL constructor expression `new Dto(...)`, Spring Data interface- or class-projection) selects only the needed columns, returns a detached read-only object, and pays nothing for dirty tracking or snapshots. For lists, reports and API responses this is multiple times cheaper in memory, CPU and DB traffic. Rule of thumb: command → entity, query/read → projection.","d":"There are two distinct axes of gain here. First, the DB: `SELECT u.id, u.name FROM User u` instead of `SELECT *` reduces pages read and network traffic, especially when the table has fat TEXT/JSONB/BLOB columns. Second, the persistence context: a managed entity keeps a snapshot of all fields for dirty checking, occupies the session's L1 cache and participates in flush; a projection result is detached, so none of that happens. Spring Data supports three kinds of projection: interface-based (closed — getters map to columns, open — @Value SpEL, but that already loads the whole entity and defeats the optimization), class-based (DTO with a constructor) and dynamic (`<T> T findBy...(..., Class<T> type)`). The JPQL constructor expression is the most explicit and predictable: `SELECT new com.app.UserDto(u.id, u.name) FROM User u`. The main risk is N+1 and partial data: if a getter of a nested association sneaks into the projection, Hibernate may either fetch it with a separate query or hand back a proxy.","note":"⚠️ An open interface projection with `@Value(\"#{target...}\")` (SpEL over target) secretly loads the FULL entity and applies the expression in memory — `SELECT *` plus a managed object, so all the projection savings are gone. For a narrow SELECT you need a closed projection: only simple getters mapping 1:1 to columns, no SpEL.","hook":"Hauling the whole wardrobe for one T-shirt. An entity is moving house with all the furniture (truck, movers, an inventory of every item in case it changed), but all you need is the name on the tag. A projection is snapping a photo of the tag and leaving: nothing to carry, nothing to guard.","more":"## Concrete gotcha: open projection + SpEL kills all the savings\n\nInterface projections come in **closed** and **open** flavors. Closed — getters map exactly to columns, Hibernate builds a narrow `SELECT id, name`:\n\n```java\ninterface UserView {        // closed — optimal\n    Long getId();\n    String getName();\n}\n```\n\nAn open projection with `@Value` and SpEL looks harmless but breaks the optimization:\n\n```java\ninterface UserView {        // open — trap\n    @Value(\"#{target.firstName + ' ' + target.lastName}\")\n    String getFullName();\n}\n```\n\n`#{target...}` means \"give me target\" → Spring Data **loads the full entity**, then applies the SpEL in memory. So you think you took a projection, but you actually got `SELECT *` + a managed entity. Zero savings.\n\n## Second gotcha: constructor expression and FQN\n\nIn JPQL, `new` requires the **fully-qualified class name** (`new com.app.dto.UserDto(...)`), otherwise you get an `IllegalArgumentException`. And the DTO constructor must match the selected expressions **exactly** in type/order — `u.id` is `Long`, not `long`, otherwise \"no matching constructor\". In Hibernate 6 this is stricter than in 5.\n\n## When a projection is NOT needed\n\nIf you load an object to modify and save it right away — take the entity. A projection is detached, you can't do a dirty-update; trying to \"project, then merge\" = an extra `SELECT` before the `UPDATE`. Projection is for the read path (CQRS-style read side), entity is for commands.","quizCorrect":"A projection selects only the needed columns and returns a detached read-only object — no dirty checking and no field snapshot in the persistence context","quizWrong":["A projection is always faster because Hibernate automatically caches the DTO in the second-level cache","An entity cannot be used for reading — JPA forbids SELECT over managed classes without a transaction","A projection loads the same columns but just hides the extra fields at the Java level, so the saving is only in JVM memoryn the wire and never in the database itself"],"picCap":"An entity drags everything in and lands in the persistence context; a projection — only the needed fields, detached"},"ops-correlation-id":{"q":"Correlation/trace-id propagation and structured logging?","a":"A single trace-id is created at the edge (or accepted from the W3C `traceparent` header) and propagated through every service — over HTTP/Kafka in headers, and inside the JVM via MDC (which must be carried manually across thread/reactive boundaries). In Java this is provided by Micrometer Tracing / OpenTelemetry, which put traceId/spanId into the MDC. Logs are written as structured JSON (fields traceId, service, level), not free text, so the aggregator (ELK/Loki) can parse and stitch together the logs of one request across services and correlate them with traces by the shared id. PII and secrets must never go into logs.","d":"A trace-id is Ariadne's thread through a distributed system: without it the logs of 10 microservices become unlinkable noise and an incident can't be investigated. W3C Trace Context (`traceparent: 00-{trace-id}-{span-id}-{flags}`) is the standard that OTel, Micrometer, proxies and clouds agree on, so propagation works cross-vendor. Structured JSON logs are needed so search is by field (`traceId=abc`), not a grep over strings; log-to-trace correlation is built on the shared traceId. The main pain is losing context on async boundaries (new thread, @Async, reactive, Kafka consumer): MDC is bound to a ThreadLocal and does not travel by itself.","note":"⚠️ MDC lives in a ThreadLocal — on every async/reactive/Kafka boundary the traceId is lost unless the context is carried explicitly (TaskDecorator, context-propagation, record headers). And the mirror problem: a thread pool reuses threads, so a forgotten `MDC.clear()` drags a previous request's traceId into the next one — logs get glued to the wrong trace.","hook":"A trace-id is the cloakroom ticket attached to each request. As long as the slip travels with the coat from hall to hall, you can always find your item among a thousand others. The moment an attendant (async thread) forgets to pass the ticket along, your coat (the request's logs) dissolves into the common pile.","more":"**Gotcha — losing MDC on an async boundary.** MDC sits on top of `ThreadLocal`, so switching threads does not carry the context:\n\n```java\n// traceId exists on the current thread\nexecutor.submit(() -> {\n    log.info(\"processing\"); // traceId == null, orphaned log\n});\n```\n\nFixes: `MDC.getCopyOfContextMap()` + restore inside the task, or wrappers (`TaskDecorator` in Spring, `ContextExecutorService`/Context Propagation in Micrometer/Reactor, Vertx/Mutiny context in Quarkus). For Kafka: the traceId must be explicitly put into / read from **record headers**, not assumed to survive in a ThreadLocal between producer and consumer.\n\n**Also:** the real leak is rarely the `traceparent` header — it's `log.info(\"req={}\", request)` serializing a body with a national ID or token. Mask at the layout/serializer level, not 'I'll remember later'.","quizCorrect":"The traceId is propagated between services in a header (W3C traceparent), lives inside the JVM in the MDC, and logs are written as structured JSON for correlation by id","quizWrong":["The traceId is regenerated in each service, and logs are linked by timestamp and service name","Writing traceId into a plain text log is enough — the aggregator will infer the link via regex","MDC is automatically copied into any new thread and reactive chain, so manual context transfer isn't neededed anywhere in the application code under any circumstances at all"],"picCap":"One traceId from traceparent flows through services via MDC into JSON logs; on an async boundary the ThreadLocal is lost."},"jc-init-order":{"q":"In what order is a Java object initialized (statics, fields, instance blocks, super-chain), and why does a virtual call from the parent constructor see the child field as null?","a":"On first use of a class: parent static fields/blocks, then child's — once per class, in textual order. On new: the whole super() chain runs first (down to Object); only AFTER super() returns do the child's field initializers and instance blocks run (textual order), then the rest of its constructor. So if the parent ctor calls a virtual method overridden in the child that reads a child field, the field is still at its default (null/0) because its initializer hasn't run. KEY: this trap fires only if the field is actually read. If it's a constant variable (a final initialized by a constant expression, e.g. `final String x = \"x\"`), the compiler inlines the literal (ldc), the field is never read — so you see the literal, not null.","d":"super() always executes first in the constructor body (explicitly or implicitly), before the child's field initializers. A final field assigned in the constructor BODY (blank final) or via a method call (`final String n = compute()`) is NOT a constant variable, is read from memory, and is still default at the moment super() runs. Effective Java Item 19: never call overridable methods from a constructor.","note":"⚠️ Common explanation bug: `class Child { final String name = \"x\"; }` + a virtual getName() from Parent's constructor does NOT print null — it prints \"x\". `final String name = \"x\"` is a constant variable (JLS 4.12.4 — applies to instance fields too); the compiler constant-folds it: getName()'s bytecode is `ldc \"x\"; areturn`, the field is never read. You get null only with a NON-constant final: a blank final (`final String name;` + `this.name = \"x\"` in the body) or a method initializer (`final String name = compute()`). Both verified to print null.","hook":"super() runs to the end — while the child's fields are still empty","more":"## Full init order\n\n**Once per class (on first active use):**\n1. Parent static fields + static blocks — textual order\n2. Child static fields + static blocks — textual order\n\n**Per `new`:**\n3. `super(...)` — recursively the entire parent constructor (up to `Object`)\n4. Child field initializers + instance blocks — textual order (AFTER super returns)\n5. Rest of the child constructor body\n\n## The virtual-call-from-constructor trap\n\n```java\nclass Parent {\n    Parent() { System.out.println(getName()); } // virtual call\n    String getName() { return \"parent\"; }\n}\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    final String name;            // blank final — NOT a constant\n    Child() { this.name = \"x\"; }  // assigned in body, AFTER super()\n    @Override String getName() { return name; }\n}\nnew Child(); // prints null\n```\n`super()` (step 3) calls `getName()`, dispatches to `Child.getName()`, but `this.name` isn't assigned yet (step 5) → `null`.\n\n## Why `final String name = \"x\"` prints \"x\", not null\n\n`\"x\"` is a constant expression, so `name` is a **constant variable** (JLS 4.12.4 — applies to instance fields too). The compiler inlines the literal: `Child.getName()`'s bytecode is `ldc \"x\"; areturn` — the field itself is never read. So the virtual call from the parent ctor sees `\"x\"`.\n\nTo reproduce `null` you need a **non-constant** final:\n- a blank final assigned in the constructor body;\n- `final String name = someMethod();` (a method call is not a constant expression).\n\n## Takeaway\nNever call overridable methods from a constructor (EJ Item 19). If the parent ctor must let the subclass configure something, do it via an explicit method after construction completes.","quizCorrect":"`final String name = \"x\"` is a constant variable; the compiler inlines the literal (ldc), the field is never read → you see \"x\"","quizWrong":["The field is read from memory, but the JVM returns the default value for final fields, so it's always null until the constructor finishes","A virtual call from a constructor is forbidden in Java and won't compile","getName() here dispatches statically to Parent.getName() and prints \"parent\""],"picCap":"super() runs to completion; the child field is still at its default (null) — but only if the final is NOT constant, otherwise the literal is inlined"},"jc-final-finally-finalize":{"q":"final vs finally vs finalize() — three unrelated things with confusingly similar names. What does each do, and what is the actual state of finalize() in a modern JVM?","a":"Three UNRELATED things. **final** — a modifier: variable/field = assign exactly once (final fields also get JMM safe publication, JLS 17.5); method = can't override; class = can't extend (records are implicitly final). **finally** — a try/finally block: runs almost always (even on return/throw in try), but does NOT run on System.exit()/Runtime.halt(), a JVM crash, kill -9, or if a daemon thread is killed at JVM exit. **finalize()** — a protected Object method the GC called before reclaiming an object. Object.finalize() is @Deprecated(forRemoval=true, since=\"9\"); JEP 421 (Java 18) deprecated the whole finalization mechanism for removal and added the --finalization=disabled flag. IMPORTANT: finalization is STILL ENABLED BY DEFAULT — only that flag turns it off, and removal is merely planned. Replacement: try-with-resources / Cleaner / PhantomReference.","d":"finally gotcha: a return or throw inside finally swallows the return/exception coming from try (antipattern). finalize() is also dangerous via the finalizer attack: if a constructor throws, the GC still calls finalize() on the partially-constructed object — so validating classes make themselves final or declare an empty final finalize(). Cleaner/PhantomReference are safer: they cannot resurrect the object and don't block its collection.","note":"⚠️ The big myth: \"finalize() is already disabled in new JDKs.\" No — by default it STILL runs. It is disabled only by the explicit `--finalization=disabled` flag (JEP 421, Java 18). And the timeline confusion: `forRemoval=true` on `Object.finalize()` itself is **since=\"9\"** (method level), while JEP 421 in Java 18 deprecated the whole mechanism for removal and added the disable flag. Removal is a plan, not a fact.","hook":"final, finally, finalize — written alike, doing completely different things.","more":"## The three \"final-\" things\n\n| Name | What | Essence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `final` | modifier | variable/field — assign once; method — no override; class — no extends |\n| `finally` | block | post-processing after try/catch |\n| `finalize()` | Object method | GC hook before reclamation (dying mechanism) |\n\n### final — three uses\n- **variable/field:** exactly one assignment. For **final fields** the JMM (JLS 17.5) guarantees safe publication: after a properly-constructed object is published, another thread sees the initialized final fields without extra synchronization.\n- **method:** cannot be overridden.\n- **class:** cannot be extended. **Records are implicitly final** (`Modifier.isFinal` == true).\n- effectively final — not marked final but never reassigned → can be captured by a lambda/anonymous class.\n\n### finally — when it WON'T run\n- `System.exit()` / `Runtime.getRuntime().halt()`\n- JVM crash (segfault) / `kill -9`\n- a daemon thread killed at JVM shutdown\n- an infinite loop/block inside try\n- Antipattern: `return`/`throw` inside `finally` swallows the original return/exception from try.\n\n### finalize() — the precise timeline\n- **Java 9:** `Object.finalize()` marked `@Deprecated(forRemoval=true, since=\"9\")` — at the METHOD level.\n- **Java 18 (JEP 421):** deprecated the finalization MECHANISM for removal + added the `--finalization=disabled` flag.\n- **Today:** finalization is **still enabled by default** (verified on JDK 25: 200k objects → ~200k finalize() calls). With `--finalization=disabled` → 0 calls. Removal is planned, not done.\n\n**Why it's dead:** non-deterministic timing, may never be called, slows the GC, finalizer attack on a partially-constructed object, ability to resurrect the object.\n\n**Replacement:** `try-with-resources` (AutoCloseable) → `Cleaner` → `PhantomReference`.","quizCorrect":"finalize() is STILL ENABLED BY DEFAULT in the JVM; it's turned off by the --finalization=disabled flag, and full removal is only planned (JEP 421)","quizWrong":["finalize() is already disabled by default in modern JDKs and is no longer invoked","finalize() was removed from Object starting in Java 18 (JEP 421)","finalize() is invoked deterministically and immediately when an object goes out of scope, exactly like a destructor in C++ does leaves the enclosing scope"],"picCap":"Three different things: final (modifier), finally (block), finalize() (a dead GC method that nonetheless still runs by default)"},"jc-override-overload":{"q":"Override vs overload: how do their dispatch rules differ, and at which moment (compile-time / runtime) is the method chosen?","a":"Override — same descriptor (name + params + return) in a subclass; the method is chosen at RUNTIME by the object's actual type via invokevirtual (virtual method table). Overload — several methods with the same name but different parameter lists; the COMPILER (javac) picks the most specific applicable one at compile-time from the static argument types and bakes a fixed descriptor into the call instruction. static methods are NOT overridden but HIDDEN: resolved by the reference type, not the object.","d":"Overloading does NOT distinguish methods by return type: you cannot declare two methods with the same parameter signature but different returns — javac selects an overload only by the number and types of arguments. The return type is part of the JVM descriptor but plays no role in overload resolution. Override, however, requires a compatible return: covariant for references (Number → Integer), while any change for primitives (int → long) breaks the override.","note":"⚠️ A common mistake is attributing overload selection to runtime / invokevirtual. In fact the overload signature is chosen by javac at compile-time and a fixed descriptor is baked into the bytecode; invokevirtual is the OVERRIDE mechanism (dynamic dispatch via the vtable), not overload. Also: overloads cannot be distinguished by return type — the return type is in the JVM descriptor but is not used in overload resolution.","hook":"Override = the object decides at runtime (invokevirtual). Overload = javac decides at compile-time and bakes the descriptor. Return type does NOT distinguish overloads.","more":"## Override vs Overload — precise rules\n\n### Override (dynamic dispatch)\n- Same signature (name + parameter types) in the subclass, compatible return.\n- Method chosen at **runtime** by the object's actual class via `invokevirtual` (lookup in the virtual method table).\n- Covariant return for **reference** types: `Number make()` may be overridden as `Integer make()`.\n- No covariance for **primitives**: `int make()` → `long make()` is a compile error `return type long is not compatible with int`.\n\n### Overload (static resolution)\n- Several methods, one name, **different** parameter lists.\n- **javac picks at compile-time** from the static (declared) argument types: it finds the most specific applicable method and **bakes a fixed descriptor** straight into the `invokestatic`/`invokevirtual`. The JVM does no signature selection at runtime.\n- `f(\"x\")` → `f(String)`; `f((Object)\"x\")` → `f(Object)` — different call instructions in the bytecode.\n- **Return type does not distinguish overloads**: two methods with identical parameters but different returns won't compile.\n\n### static — hiding, not override\n- `static` methods are not overridden, they are **hidden**: resolved by the **reference** type, not the object.\n- `Parent p = new Child(); Parent.stat()` → the `Parent` version.\n\n### Verified on JDK 25 (javac/java + javap)\n```\np.who()             -> Child            // invokevirtual #20 Method Parent.who, vtable -> Child\nParent.stat()       -> Parent.stat      // hiding by reference type\nf(\"x\")              -> f(String)        // invokestatic f:(Ljava/lang/String;)...\nf((Object)\"x\")      -> f(Object)        // invokestatic f:(Ljava/lang/Object;)... (different instruction!)\nf(null)+f(String)+f(Integer) -> \"reference to f is ambiguous\"  // compile error\n```\n\n### Effective Java\n- Item 52 'Use overloading judiciously': overload selection is static and easy to confuse — especially `f(null)` and widening types. Prefer distinct names.","quizCorrect":"Override is resolved at runtime by the object's actual type (invokevirtual/vtable), while overload is resolved by the compiler at compile-time from the static argument types","quizWrong":["Both override and overload are resolved at runtime by the object's type via invokevirtual, so the declared type of the variable never matters for either of them for either of them","Overloads can be distinguished by return type, just like override via covariance","invokevirtual performs overload signature selection at runtime based on argument types"],"picCap":"Override is resolved at runtime (invokevirtual/vtable); overload is resolved by the compiler from static types, with the descriptor already baked into the bytecode."},"jc-hashmap-treeify":{"q":"When a HashMap bin becomes a red-black tree, how does find() locate a key on equal hashes, and why can the tree still degrade to O(n)?","a":"A bin treeifies at TREEIFY_THRESHOLD=8 nodes AND capacity≥MIN_TREEIFY_CAPACITY=64 (otherwise it resizes first). In TreeNode.find(h, k, kc) the descent compares hashes; on an EQUAL hash the order is: (1) equals() check — return on match; (2) if keys are Comparable — pick a direction via compareComparables (compareTo), O(log n); (3) if keys are NOT Comparable — there is no deterministic direction, so find recurses into BOTH subtrees (pr.find(...), then walk pl). Hence many keys sharing the same full hashCode with non-Comparable keys make the tree degrade toward O(n). Crucially: System.identityHashCode/tieBreakOrder play NO role in lookup — they run only at INSERTION (treeify/putTreeVal) to set a stable placement order.","d":"Untreeify back to a list happens at UNTREEIFY_THRESHOLD=6 during a resize (hysteresis 8 vs 6 prevents thrashing). treeifyBin with capacity<64 calls resize() instead of building a tree. Probability of 8 collisions in one bin ~ Poisson(λ=0.5): P(8)≈6·10⁻⁸ for a good hashCode — trees are a defense against collision attacks, not the normal mode.","note":"⚠️ Common myth: 'find() picks the subtree by identityHashCode'. No. On equal hashes find() first uses equals(), then — for Comparable keys — compareTo; for non-Comparable keys there is no deterministic direction, so find searches BOTH subtrees (→ O(n)). System.identityHashCode/tieBreakOrder are called ONLY at insertion (treeify/putTreeVal) for stable placement and take NO part in lookup.","hook":"The tree only rescues you from O(n) when the keys are Comparable; otherwise get() walks both subtrees.","more":"## Treeification mechanics\n\n**Thresholds (OpenJDK HashMap):**\n- `TREEIFY_THRESHOLD = 8` — nodes in a bin to switch to a tree\n- `UNTREEIFY_THRESHOLD = 6` — back to a list (on resize); 8↔6 hysteresis prevents border thrashing\n- `MIN_TREEIFY_CAPACITY = 64` — `treeifyBin` with `cap < 64` does **resize()**, not a tree\n\n**Lookup — `TreeNode.find(h, k, kc)`:** descend by `hash`. On an **equal** hash, in order:\n```\n1) (pk == k || k.equals(pk))   -> return p      // equals wins\n2) Comparable? compareComparables(kc,k,pk) != 0 -> p = (dir<0)?pl:pr   // one branch\n3) else: q = pr.find(...) ; if null -> p = pl   // BOTH subtrees\n```\nStep 3 is the source of O(n) degradation: without Comparable, find has no deterministic direction, so it checks both subtrees.\n\n**`identityHashCode` — insertion only.** `tieBreakOrder(a,b)` first compares `getClass().getName()`, and on a tie `System.identityHashCode`. It is called **only** from `treeify` and `putTreeVal` to give a stable node-placement order for equal-hash, non-Comparable keys. It is **not** used in `find` — that was exactly the bug fixed from the previous version of this card.\n\n**Why a tree at all:** with a good `hashCode`, the chance of 8 collisions in a bin ~ Poisson(λ=0.5), `P(8) ≈ 6·10⁻⁸`. Trees defend against hash-flooding attacks, not the normal path. The best defense is a correct `hashCode` plus (for collision-prone keys) implementing `Comparable`.","quizCorrect":"In a treeified bin with equal hashes and NON-Comparable keys, find() recurses into both subtrees, degrading toward O(n)","quizWrong":["find() compares keys by System.identityHashCode to choose a subtree on equal hashes of the bin it lands in that it falls into","A bin always becomes a tree at 8 nodes, regardless of the table's capacity","tieBreakOrder is called on every get() to choose the descent direction in the tree"],"picCap":"find on equal hashes: equals &rarr; Comparable &rarr; (no Comparable) both subtrees. identityHashCode — only at insertion."},"jc-immutable-collections":{"q":"What's the difference between List.of(), Collections.unmodifiableList(), and Arrays.asList()? Which is truly immutable, which is a live view, and which is fixed-size writing through to the array?","a":"Three different things people conflate. List.of() (Set.of/Map.of, Java 9) is truly immutable: rejects null at creation time (NPE), any mutation → UnsupportedOperationException, backed by ImmutableCollections (List12 for 1–2 elements, ListN for 0 and 3+). Collections.unmodifiableList() is only a wrapper VIEW over the source list: mutating through the wrapper → UOE, but changes to the underlying backing list ARE visible through the wrapper (it's not a copy). Arrays.asList() is a FIXED-SIZE list over an array: set() writes through to the array (and vice-versa), add()/remove() → UOE, null allowed. For a real defensive copy use List.copyOf(src): it makes an immutable copy of a mutable source, but short-circuits (returns the same instance) when the source is already a List.of-immutable.","d":"List.of/Set.of/Map.of strictly reject null at creation and on contains(null) (NPE); Set.of with a duplicate and Map.of with a duplicate key → IllegalArgumentException. Size classes: List12 (java.util.ImmutableCollections$List12) holds 1–2 elements, ListN holds empty and 3+. unmodifiableList gives a wrapper (UnmodifiableList) that delegates reads to the backing and blocks writes. Arrays.asList → java.util.Arrays$ArrayList, fixed-size, two-way bound to the array.","note":"⚠️ Collections.unmodifiableList(src) does NOT make a copy — it's a live view: whoever holds a reference to src can mutate it, and the change shows through the wrapper. For a real defensive copy use List.copyOf(src): it makes an immutable copy of a mutable source, BUT short-circuits — if the source is already a List.of-immutable it returns the SAME instance (List.copyOf(List.of(1,2,3)) == List.of(1,2,3) → true), no copy happens. Another trap: Arrays.asList(new int[]{1,2,3}) yields a List<int[]> of size 1 (a primitive array isn't spread into varargs), and Arrays.asList(arr).set(i,v) silently writes into the original array.","hook":"\"unmodifiable\" locks the door, not the walls: the source list can still change, and the view shows it.","more":"## Three \"immutables\" — different semantics\n\n| Factory | Null | Mutate through it | Tie to source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `List.of(...)` (Java 9) | rejected at creation → NPE | `add/set/remove` → UOE | no source, self-contained |\n| `Collections.unmodifiableList(src)` | as in backing | → UOE | **live view**: `src.add()` shows through |\n| `Arrays.asList(arr)` | allowed | `add/remove` → UOE, `set` OK | two-way bound to the array |\n\n```java\n// 1. unmodifiableList is a VIEW, not a copy\nList<Integer> src  = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1,2,3));\nList<Integer> view = Collections.unmodifiableList(src);\nsrc.add(4);\nview.get(3);          // 4  — leaked through the wrapper\n\n// 2. Arrays.asList writes through to the array\nInteger[] arr = {1,2,3};\nList<Integer> al = Arrays.asList(arr);\nal.set(0, 99);        // arr[0] is now 99\narr[1] = 88;          // al.get(1) is now 88\nal.add(4);            // UnsupportedOperationException\n\n// 3. List.copyOf — a real defensive copy...\nList<Integer> mut  = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1,2,3));\nList<Integer> safe = List.copyOf(mut);  // decoupled immutable copy\nmut.add(99);          // safe unchanged\n\n// ...but it short-circuits if the source is already immutable\nList<Integer> imm = List.of(1,2,3);\nList.copyOf(imm) == imm;   // true — same instance, no copy\n```\n\n**Internal classes (JDK 25):** `List.of(1,2)` → `ImmutableCollections$List12` (size 1–2), `List.of()` and `List.of(1,2,3)` → `ImmutableCollections$ListN` (0 and 3+). `Arrays.asList(...)` → `Arrays$ArrayList`, `Map.of(...)` → `ImmutableCollections$MapN`.\n\n**Why it matters:** defensive copies in constructors/getters. `List.copyOf(arg)` is safer than `Collections.unmodifiableList(arg)` — the latter leaves the caller a reference to the backing and the ability to mutate your \"immutable\" list from outside.","quizCorrect":"List.copyOf(src) makes an immutable copy of a mutable src, but returns the same instance if src is already a List.of-immutable (short-circuit)","quizWrong":["List.copyOf always allocates a new array and copies, even when src is already immutable","Collections.unmodifiableList makes a deep copy, so changes to the backing list aren't visible through the wrapper at all once it has been wrapped","Arrays.asList returns a truly immutable list, just like List.of"],"picCap":"Three \"immutable\" lists with different semantics + copyOf short-circuiting for an already-immutable source"},"gap-co-cf-default-executor":{"q":"Which thread runs a CompletableFuture callback (thenApply vs thenApplyAsync), which Executor is the default, and why is blocking on it dangerous — especially in a 1-vCPU container?","a":"Without -Async, a callback (thenApply/thenAccept) runs on whatever thread COMPLETED the previous stage, or synchronously on the caller thread if the stage was already complete when you attached it. thenApplyAsync(fn) with no explicit Executor goes to the shared ForkJoinPool.commonPool() (parallelism = cores-1, daemon threads). The danger: commonPool is JVM-wide (shared with parallel streams and other CompletableFutures). Block its workers on I/O and work piles up in a queue. On 1 vCPU there are only 1-2 workers, so such tasks just serialize and accumulate, risking timeouts. Fix it by passing your own sized Executor for blocking I/O.","d":"commonPool never degenerates into thread-per-task or synchronous execution: at parallelism=0 it is forced up to a minimum of 2 workers (asyncCommonPool 'ensures minimal parallelism'); at parallelism=1 all tasks run on ONE worker in sequence. It is always a bounded ForkJoinPool, never a ThreadPerTaskExecutor. ForkJoinPool is built for CPU-bound work-stealing; for blocking there is ManagedBlocker (temporarily expands parallelism). For blocking I/O, prefer your own Executor with a sensible size.","note":"⚠️ Common myth: 'at 1 vCPU commonPool spins a thread per task (ThreadPerTaskExecutor) or degenerates to synchronous execution.' Wrong. CompletableFuture's default Executor is ForkJoinPool.commonPool() (via asyncCommonPool), and it is always a bounded pool: at parallelism=0 it is forced up to 2 workers, at parallelism=1 it runs all tasks sequentially on one worker. No thread-per-task spawning. The real pain is the opposite — TOO FEW workers, so blocking async chains clog the queue and stall other commonPool tasks (parallel streams, other CFs). Same conclusion: use your own Executor for blocking.","hook":"commonPool is JVM-wide: block its workers and you stall everyone else's tasks too.","more":"## Where the callback runs\n\nCompletableFuture has two attach modes:\n\n- **`thenApply` / `thenAccept` / `thenCompose` (no `Async`)** — the callback runs on the thread that COMPLETED the previous stage. If the stage was already complete when you attached the callback, it runs **synchronously on the caller thread**.\n- **`thenApplyAsync(fn)` with no `Executor`** — task goes to **`ForkJoinPool.commonPool()`**.\n- **`thenApplyAsync(fn, myExecutor)`** — to your pool.\n\n## commonPool parameters\n\n- Default `parallelism` = `availableProcessors() - 1`.\n- Threads are **daemon** → they don't keep the JVM alive.\n- The pool is **JVM-wide**: shared with parallel streams, `Arrays.parallelSort`, other `CompletableFuture`s.\n\n## Why you must not block commonPool\n\nThere are few workers (`cores - 1`). Put a blocking call (HTTP, JDBC, `get()` on another future) there and a worker stalls instead of working. A handful of those and the whole shared pool idles while other tasks queue.\n\n## 1-vCPU container (the key nuance)\n\nEasy to state nonsense here. Facts (verified on a JVM):\n\n| parallelism | CompletableFuture behavior |\n|---|---|\n| `0` (forced via flag) | bumped up to a **minimum of 2** workers (`asyncCommonPool`) |\n| `1` | all tasks on **ONE** worker, sequentially (serialized) |\n\nIt is **always a bounded `ForkJoinPool`**, NOT a `ThreadPerTaskExecutor` and NOT synchronous-on-caller. The 1-vCPU risk is precisely worker scarcity: blocking async chains queue up and hit timeouts.\n\n## How to do it right\n\n```java\nExecutorService io = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(32); // for blocking I/O\nCompletableFuture\n  .supplyAsync(() -> callRemote(), io)   // blocking -> your pool\n  .thenApply(this::transform)            // cheap CPU -> wherever\n  .thenApplyAsync(this::heavyCpu);       // CPU-bound -> commonPool is fine\n```\n\n- Blocking I/O → your own sized Executor (or virtual threads in Java 21).\n- CPU-bound work-stealing → ForkJoinPool/commonPool.\n- If blocking inside an FJP is unavoidable → `ForkJoinPool.ManagedBlocker` (temporarily widens pool parallelism).","quizCorrect":"thenApplyAsync(fn) with no Executor submits the task to the shared ForkJoinPool.commonPool()","quizWrong":["thenApplyAsync(fn) with no Executor creates a new thread per task via ThreadPerTaskExecutor of its own","At 1 vCPU parallelism degenerates into synchronous execution on the caller thread","thenApply (without Async) always runs in commonPool, just like thenApplyAsync"],"picCap":"thenApply → completing/caller thread; thenApplyAsync with no Executor → commonPool. Blocking on it clogs the shared pool, it does NOT spawn a thread per task."},"jc-stream-infinite":{"q":"Infinite stream: which operations hang it and which don't? How does sorted() differ from distinct()?","a":"An infinite source (Stream.iterate/generate) terminates only if something downstream short-circuits the pull of elements: limit, takeWhile, findFirst, anyMatch/allMatch/noneMatch. sorted() is a true full barrier: it must buffer the ENTIRE input before emitting anything, so it hangs forever even with a limit below it. But distinct() is stateful yet NOT a barrier: it emits element-by-element, so a downstream limit(5) stops the pull at the 5th (returns [1,2,3,4,5]). The real risk of distinct() is different: on an infinite source with NO downstream short-circuit, its seen-set grows without bound.","d":"sorted()/count() (on a non-SIZED source) traverse the whole input and hang on an infinite one. distinct()/limit()/takeWhile() are pull-based, element by element.","note":"⚠️ Common misconception: \"distinct() is a barrier like sorted(), so iterate(1, n->n+1).distinct().limit(5) will hang/OOM.\" False — verified on JDK: that pipeline completes with [1,2,3,4,5], pulling exactly 5 elements, because the limit below distinct() short-circuits the pull. The barriers are sorted() (and count() on a non-SIZED source): they traverse all input and hang even with a limit BELOW them. The real danger of distinct() on an infinite source is the growing seen-set, and only when NOTHING downstream short-circuits the pull; a downstream limit rescues it. Rule: put limit BEFORE sorted() (a barrier); after distinct() it already stops the pipeline.","hook":"The conveyor pulls parts one at a time from the end. distinct() hands them over one by one, and the collector shouts \"enough, I've got five\" — the belt stops. But sorted() first demands the WHOLE infinite belt in a pile, and by then shouting is too late: it has already stopped forever.","more":"**Infinite sources:** `Stream.iterate(seed, next)` (2-arg) and `Stream.generate(supplier)` produce an endless stream. Only a downstream **short-circuit** that stops pulling elements can end it:\n\n- `limit(n)` (Java 8) — takes n and stops the pull;\n- `takeWhile(pred)` (Java 9) — while the predicate is true;\n- `findFirst` / `findAny`, `anyMatch` / `allMatch` / `noneMatch` — exit as soon as the answer is known.\n\n**Barrier vs stateful-but-pull:**\n\n- `sorted()` is a FULL barrier (stateful + consumes the whole input): emits nothing until it has everything. On an infinite source it hangs even with a `limit(10)` AFTER it. Fixed by placing `limit` BEFORE `sorted()`: `iterate(...).limit(10).sorted()` completes.\n- `distinct()` is stateful (holds a seen-set) but NOT a barrier: it emits an element as soon as it confirms it's new. So `iterate(1, n -> n+1).distinct().limit(5)` returns `[1,2,3,4,5]`, pulling exactly 5 elements.\n\n**3-arg `iterate(seed, hasNext, next)`** (Java 9) is already FINITE (like a for-loop) and ends on its own.\n\n`count()` on an infinite source hangs; the SIZED-flag short-circuit doesn't kick in here because `iterate` isn't SIZED.","quizCorrect":"distinct() emits elements one at a time, so a limit(5) below it stops the pull — the stream completes; sorted() must buffer the whole input and so it hangs","quizWrong":["Both sorted() and distinct() are full barriers, so both hang on an infinite source even with a limit beloww them in the pipeline placed further down the chain","sorted() completes with a limit below it, while distinct() hangs because it stores a set of seen elements","Both complete: a limit always stops any pipeline no matter where it sits"],"picCap":"sorted() hangs even with limit below it; distinct()+limit completes in 5 elements"},"jc-groupingby-downstream":{"q":"What does the downstream collector in groupingBy do, what type does counting() return, and how does toMap behave on duplicate keys?","a":"groupingBy(classifier) buckets elements into a List per key; the second argument is a downstream collector that folds each group (counting() → Long, summingInt → Integer, mapping(fn, toSet()) → Set, etc.). A 3-arg overload adds a mapFactory (TreeMap::new, EnumMap::new) to choose the Map type. Key trap: counting() returns Long, not int — so `int n = map.get(k)` does NOT compile (Long can't widen to int implicitly). toMap(key, value) with no merge function throws IllegalStateException \"Duplicate key…\" on collisions; the 3-arg overload takes a mergeFunction to resolve them. toMap also throws NPE on a null value (inside Map.merge) even with unique keys — groupingBy does not.","d":"downstream is a nested Collector inside groupingBy: groupingBy(classifier, downstream) and groupingBy(classifier, mapFactory, downstream). Common downstreams: counting()→Long, summingInt/Long→Integer/Long, averagingDouble→Double, mapping(f, dc), filtering(p, dc), reducing(...), toSet(). toMap is separate: 2-arg throws IllegalStateException on key collision (via Map.merge), 3-arg takes a BinaryOperator merge, 4-arg also a Map supplier (LinkedHashMap::new). Unlike HashMap.put, Map.merge throws NPE on a null value, so toMap can't carry nulls.","note":"⚠️ counting() returns Long, not int — `int n = map.get(k)` won't compile (no implicit Long→int; use `long n = …` or `.intValue()`). The popular == myth does NOT apply the way people think: `map.get(k) == 200` (int literal) is TRUE even for 200 (outside the −128..127 cache), because the Long is unboxed to long and compared numerically. The boxing cache only breaks comparison of TWO boxed references: `Long a = 200L, b = 200L; a == b` → false. So compare downstream Longs with .equals() or unbox explicitly (`map.get(k).longValue() == 200`), don't assume \"== is always broken by the cache.\"","hook":"groupingBy(byFirst, counting()) just handed you a Map<..., Long>. Why is `int n = map.get(k)` red in your IDE?","more":"## groupingBy and downstream collectors\n\n```java\n// 1-arg: value is a List\nMap<Character, List<String>> g1 =\n    words.stream().collect(groupingBy(w -> w.charAt(0)));\n\n// 2-arg: downstream folds each group\nMap<Character, Long> g2 =\n    words.stream().collect(groupingBy(w -> w.charAt(0), counting())); // Long!\n\n// 3-arg: mapFactory picks the Map type\nTreeMap<Character, Long> g3 = words.stream()\n    .collect(groupingBy(w -> w.charAt(0), TreeMap::new, counting()));\n```\n\n**Downstream value types:**\n\n| downstream | value type |\n|---|---|\n| `counting()` | `Long` |\n| `summingInt` / `summingLong` | `Integer` / `Long` |\n| `averagingInt/Double` | `Double` |\n| `mapping(fn, toSet())` | `Set<...>` |\n\n**toMap vs groupingBy on duplicates:**\n\n```java\n// IllegalStateException: Duplicate key a ...\nwords.stream().collect(toMap(w -> w.charAt(0), w -> w));\n\n// OK — 3-arg merge resolves it\nwords.stream().collect(toMap(w -> w.charAt(0), w -> w, (a, b) -> a + \"|\" + b));\n```\n\n**Why == with boxing is its own story (see note):** counting() yields `Long`. `map.get(k) == 200` (int literal) is safe — the Long unboxes to `long`, numeric compare. Only `Long == Long` for two boxed values breaks outside the `−128..127` cache.","quizCorrect":"counting() returns Long, so assigning to int requires an explicit unbox/cast","quizWrong":["counting() returns a long primitive that can't be assigned to int without data loss","groupingBy always returns a Map with Object values, so the type is lost","int n = map.get(k) compiles but throws ClassCastException at runtime"],"picCap":"groupingBy folds each group via a downstream (counting()→Long); == with an int literal unboxes and compares numerically, while Long==Long breaks outside the −128..127 cache"},"gap-serial-externalizable":{"q":"How does Externalizable differ from Serializable, and what exactly is NOT written to the stream with Externalizable? Plus: how is each object reconstructed on deserialization?","a":"Serializable is a marker: the JVM writes fields reflectively and, on deserialization, does NOT call the serializable class's constructor (only the no-arg constructor of the first non-serializable ancestor runs); fields are restored natively/via Unsafe. Externalizable extends Serializable, but you write/read state yourself in writeExternal/readExternal. In an Externalizable stream the class descriptor (class name + serialVersionUID) IS still present; what is NOT written is the FIELD metadata (names/types) and the fields' values automatically — hence a more compact, faster format. On deserialization the JVM calls the public no-arg constructor, then readExternal; no such constructor -> InvalidClassException (\"no valid constructor\").","d":"Externalizable ignores transient (you manage everything manually). writeReplace/readResolve work in both cases for object substitution (e.g. preserving a singleton's identity via readResolve). serialVersionUID is written and checked in both cases.","note":"⚠️ Don't confuse it: with Externalizable the stream loses FIELD metadata (names/types) and automatic value writing — but the class descriptor (name + serialVersionUID) is still written. Saying \"class metadata is not written\" is wrong. Also: in normal Serializable deserialization the serializable class's constructor is NOT called (constructor invariants may be skipped) — whereas Externalizable always invokes the public no-arg constructor before readExternal.","hook":"\"Field metadata isn't written\" — not \"class metadata\": the class name and serialVersionUID are still in the stream.","more":"# Serializable vs Externalizable\n\n## Serializable (marker)\n- The JVM serializes all non-transient fields reflectively.\n- On **deserialization the serializable class's constructor is NOT called**. The JVM finds the **first non-serializable ancestor** and runs only ITS no-arg constructor; fields are then restored natively (via `Unsafe`/JNI), bypassing any constructor logic.\n- `transient` fields are skipped (get default values).\n- Fine-tuning via private `writeObject`/`readObject` (invoked reflectively; usually call `defaultWriteObject()`/`defaultReadObject()` inside).\n\n## Externalizable\n- `interface Externalizable extends Serializable` + two public methods: `writeExternal(ObjectOutput)` and `readExternal(ObjectInput)`.\n- The stream contains the **class descriptor** — class name and `serialVersionUID` (via `writeClassDescriptor`). It does NOT contain **field metadata** (names/types) or field values automatically — hence a smaller, faster format.\n- On deserialization: the **public no-arg constructor** is invoked, then `readExternal`. No public no-arg constructor -> `InvalidClassException: ... no valid constructor`.\n- `transient` is **ignored** — you decide what to write.\n\n## writeReplace / readResolve\n- `writeReplace()` substitutes the object before writing.\n- `readResolve()` substitutes after reading (classic: return the singleton/enum instance to preserve identity).\n\n## When to use which\n- Default to `Serializable` (less room for error).\n- Use `Externalizable` when you need full control over format/performance and are willing to maintain compatibility by hand.","quizCorrect":"Externalizable doesn't write field metadata or values automatically (but the class descriptor — name + serialVersionUID — stays in the stream); the class writes/reads its state itself in writeExternal/readExternal","quizWrong":["Externalizable writes no class descriptor at all — neither class name nor serialVersionUID ends up in the stream stream, so the two sides need no agreement about the class at all beyond the field order itself and nothing more","Externalizable, like Serializable, reconstructs the object without calling any constructor, via Unsafe","Externalizable requires private writeObject/readObject instead of public writeExternal/readExternal"],"picCap":"Externalizable: the class descriptor stays, but the class writes the fields and their metadata itself"},"gap-exc-chaining-cause":{"q":"How do you correctly wrap an exception while keeping the original cause, and where does the cause get lost (in code and in SLF4J logs)?","a":"Set the cause chain via the constructor `new RuntimeException(\"wrap\", e)` or `initCause(e)` (callable only once — a second call throws `IllegalStateException: Can't overwrite cause`). `getCause()` returns it and `printStackTrace()` recursively prints it under `Caused by:`. The cause is LOST if you wrap only the text — `new RuntimeException(\"re: \" + e.getMessage())`: then `getCause()==null` and the original type and stacktrace vanish. In logs the rule differs: SLF4J prints the stacktrace when the Throwable is the LAST argument (even with a `{}` present); the trace is lost only when the Throwable is NOT last and a placeholder consumes it via `toString()`.","d":"`initCause` returns `Throwable`, enabling the idiom `throw (RuntimeException) new RuntimeException(\"wrap\").initCause(e);`. The original message CAN be null — e.g. from a no-arg constructor `new IllegalStateException()` or `new RuntimeException((String) null)`; then `\"\" + e.getMessage()` yields the string `\"null\"`. Note: a dereference NPE is no longer such an example — since JDK 14 (JEP 358, on by default since JDK 15) it carries a helpful message like \"Cannot invoke ... because ... is null\", i.e. non-null.","note":"⚠️ The SLF4J stacktrace is NOT lost because of `{}`. `log.error(\"msg {}\", e)` prints the full trace — `{}` stays a literal. The trace disappears only when the Throwable is NOT the last argument (another param follows) and a placeholder swallows it via `toString()`: `log.error(\"x={}\", e, \"tail\")` → `x=java.lang.RuntimeException: boom` with no trace. Simple rule: keep the throwable as the last argument.","hook":"Wrapping and placing-next-to are two different things: a cause must be PASSED, not glued to a string.","more":"## Cause-chain mechanics\n\n```java\n// 1) Constructor with cause — canonical\nthrow new RuntimeException(\"order processing failed\", e);\n\n// 2) initCause — when the class lacks a (msg, cause) constructor.\n//    It returns Throwable, so cast-and-throw in one line:\nthrow (RuntimeException) new RuntimeException(\"wrap\").initCause(e);\n```\n\n`initCause` may be called **only once** (and only if the cause was not already set by the constructor); a second call → `IllegalStateException: Can't overwrite cause`.\n\n### Where the cause is lost (in code)\n```java\ncatch (IOException e) {\n    // BAD: the IOException type and the whole stacktrace are gone,\n    // only the message text remains, getCause()==null\n    throw new RuntimeException(\"re: \" + e.getMessage());\n}\n```\n`printStackTrace()` recursively prints the chain via `Caused by:` — but only if the cause is actually set.\n\n### Where it is lost (in SLF4J logs)\n`MessageFormatter` rule: the **last** argument is treated as the `Throwable` and its stacktrace is printed when there are more args than placeholders consume. The presence/absence of `{}` is irrelevant.\n\n| Call | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `log.error(\"msg\", e)` | message + full stacktrace |\n| `log.error(\"msg {}\", e)` | `msg {}` (literal brace) + **full stacktrace** |\n| `log.error(\"x={} y={}\", 1, e)` | `x=1 y={}` + **stacktrace** (extra throwable) |\n| `log.error(\"x={}\", e, \"tail\")` | `x=<e.toString()>` — **stacktrace LOST** (e is not last, a `{}` ate it) |\n\nTakeaway: to reliably see the stacktrace, keep the exception as the **last** argument and never feed it into a `{}`.","quizCorrect":"log.error(\"msg {}\", e) prints the stacktrace: SLF4J treats the last argument as the Throwable regardless of whether a {} is present","quizWrong":["log.error(\"msg {}\", e) does NOT print the stacktrace because {} substitutes e via toString()","initCause(e) can be called any number of times, the last call wins","new RuntimeException(\"re: \"+e.getMessage()) preserves the original type and stacktrace via cause of the original failure that was thrown"],"picCap":"The cause chain is kept by the constructor/initCause and lost when wrapping with bare text. In SLF4J the trace prints when the Throwable is the last argument."},"gap-io-byte-char-charset":{"q":"How does a byte stream (InputStream/OutputStream) differ from a character stream (Reader/Writer), and why are `new String(bytes)`, `getBytes()`, `FileReader`, `InputStreamReader`, and `Scanner` without an explicit charset a time bomb?","a":"Byte streams (InputStream/OutputStream) handle raw `byte`; character streams (Reader/Writer) handle `char` (UTF-16 code units), and the bridge between them is always a charset. These APIs with no explicit charset use the \"default charset\", so text gets silently decoded/encoded with the wrong bytes → mojibake. Before Java 18 the default was the platform's `file.encoding` (windows-1251 on RU-Windows, UTF-8 on Linux), so the result depended on where the code ran. JEP 400 (Java 18) made UTF-8 the default for `java.io`/`java.nio` regardless of platform, but `System.console()`, logs, and external processes can still use the native encoding. The fix: always pass `StandardCharsets.UTF_8` explicitly (`new String(b, UTF_8)`, `getBytes(UTF_8)`, `new InputStreamReader(in, UTF_8)`, and `new FileReader(f, UTF_8)` is available since Java 11).","d":"The default decoder uses `CodingErrorAction.REPLACE`: invalid bytes don't throw — they become U+FFFD (�). Corruption is silent, with no crash. That's why the bug shows up in prod/Docker with a different locale, not in dev (UTF-8 locale).","note":"⚠️ JEP 400 (Java 18) made UTF-8 the default only for the JVM itself (`java.io`/`java.nio`). It does NOT fix: (1) `System.console()`/`Console.charset()`, which still use the console's native encoding; (2) files actually written in cp1251 by other software; (3) running on older JVMs (≤17), where the default is the platform locale. And crucially: invalid bytes don't fail — they silently become `�` (U+FFFD) due to `CodingErrorAction.REPLACE`. Always pass `StandardCharsets.UTF_8` explicitly; never rely on the default.","hook":"Bytes don't know their own encoding — the reader always picks it. Don't set a charset explicitly, and the platform default is picked for you.","more":"## The point\n\n**Two stream families:**\n- `InputStream`/`OutputStream` — raw `byte` (images, zip, binary). `read()` returns an `int` 0..255.\n- `Reader`/`Writer` — `char` (UTF-16 code units). Text.\n- The **bridge** is `InputStreamReader`/`OutputStreamWriter`, and it **always** needs a charset (explicit or default).\n\n**Where the default charset hides (traps):**\n\n| API | What it does without a charset |\n|---|---|\n| `new String(byte[])` | decodes with default |\n| `String.getBytes()` | encodes with default |\n| `new FileReader(f)` / `FileWriter` | default (charset ctor since Java 11) |\n| `new InputStreamReader(in)` | default |\n| `new Scanner(in)` / `new Scanner(file)` | default |\n| `new PrintWriter(file)` | default |\n\n**Evolution of the default:**\n- Before Java 18: `file.encoding` = the OS's native encoding (Windows-1251 on RU-Windows, UTF-8 on a typical Linux). Same code, different result on different machines.\n- **JEP 400 (Java 18):** UTF-8 is the default for `java.io`/`java.nio`/`java.util`. `file.encoding` now reads as `UTF-8`. But `System.console()` and `Console.charset()` stay on `stdout.encoding`/native — console output may differ.\n- `Charset.defaultCharset()` now returns UTF-8 (on 18+), but you still can't rely on \"the default\": compatibility with older JVMs, foreign processes, files from Windows.\n\n**Concretely for 'П' (U+041F):**\n- UTF-8: `D0 9F` (2 bytes) → decode as UTF-8 → `П` ✅\n- Those same 2 bytes as **cp1251** → `Рџ` (Р = `D0`, џ = `9F`) — two characters\n- Those same 2 bytes as **cp1252** → `ÐŸ` (Ð = `D0`, Ÿ = `9F`) — two characters\n\n**The right way:**\n```java\nimport static java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets.UTF_8;\n\nbyte[] b = s.getBytes(UTF_8);\nString s2 = new String(b, UTF_8);\nvar r = new InputStreamReader(in, UTF_8);\nvar fr = new FileReader(file, UTF_8); // Java 11+\nFiles.readString(path);               // UTF-8 by contract\n```\n\n**Silent corruption:** the default `CharsetDecoder` is configured with `CodingErrorAction.REPLACE` → a broken byte turns into `�` (U+FFFD), no exception. To catch it, build the decoder by hand with `onMalformedInput(CodingErrorAction.REPORT)`.","quizCorrect":"The default decoder uses CodingErrorAction.REPLACE: invalid bytes are silently replaced with U+FFFD, with no exception","quizWrong":["new String(bytes) without a charset always throws UnsupportedEncodingException on invalid data in the input bytes given","Since Java 11 you can't pass a charset to FileReader — it's hard-wired to UTF-8","Reader/Writer work with raw bytes, while InputStream/OutputStream work with char"],"picCap":"The same bytes D0 9F: correct only under UTF-8 (П). cp1251 gives 'Рџ', cp1252 gives 'ÐŸ' — two glyphs, not one; no byte is lost."},"sp-tx-nested-savepoint":{"q":"Propagation.NESTED sets a SAVEPOINT while REQUIRES_NEW opens a brand-new transaction. What's the difference, and which transaction managers actually support NESTED?","a":"REQUIRES_NEW suspends the outer transaction and opens a physically NEW one (its own commit, generally its own connection); rolling it back does not affect the outer transaction. NESTED runs INSIDE the same physical transaction and the same connection: Spring sets a JDBC Savepoint at the start of the nested block and, on error, rolls back only to that savepoint, leaving the outer transaction alive — but if the OUTER rolls back, the nested work rolls back too. NESTED works only when the PlatformTransactionManager supports savepoints AND has nestedTransactionAllowed=true. DataSourceTransactionManager has that flag true by default. JpaTransactionManager also supports savepoints but defaults nestedTransactionAllowed to false — you can flip it on manually, and then the savepoint is set at the JDBC level. JtaTransactionManager does not support NESTED at all and throws NestedTransactionNotSupportedException.","d":"NESTED is not a second transaction but a rollback point (savepoint) inside the first; REQUIRES_NEW is a full second physical transaction that suspends the outer one.","note":"⚠️ Don't read NESTED as 'REQUIRES_NEW but cheaper'. With REQUIRES_NEW the inner tx commits separately: if the OUTER later fails, the already-committed inner data is NOT rolled back. With NESTED everything is one physical transaction, so an outer rollback also wipes the nested work. Also: NESTED is disabled by default on JpaTransactionManager (nestedTransactionAllowed=false) — this is NOT 'JPA can't do savepoints' (it can); you just have to enable the flag. JtaTransactionManager, by contrast, doesn't support savepoints at all.","hook":"REQUIRES_NEW = new transaction (outer suspended). NESTED = savepoint inside the same transaction.","more":"## REQUIRES_NEW vs NESTED\n\n| | REQUIRES_NEW | NESTED |\n|---|---|---|\n| Physical tx | new, outer **suspended** | same, same commit |\n| Mechanism | separate `begin/commit` | JDBC `Savepoint` |\n| Inner rollback | does NOT touch outer | rolls back to savepoint, outer alive |\n| Outer rollback | inner already committed, NOT undone | rolls nested back too |\n| Connection | generally a different one | the same one |\n\n## When NESTED actually works\n\nNESTED is handled by `AbstractPlatformTransactionManager`. Two conditions must hold:\n\n1. the manager supports savepoints (`Connection.setSavepoint()` available);\n2. `nestedTransactionAllowed == true`.\n\n| Transaction manager | nestedTransactionAllowed | NESTED |\n|---|---|---|\n| `DataSourceTransactionManager` | `true` (default) | works (driver must support savepoints) |\n| `JpaTransactionManager` | `false` (default) | enable the flag → savepoint at the JDBC level |\n| `JtaTransactionManager` | — | **unsupported**, `NestedTransactionNotSupportedException` |\n\nFrom the `JpaTransactionManager` javadoc: *\"This transaction manager supports nested transactions via JDBC Savepoints. The nestedTransactionAllowed flag defaults to false ...\"*\n\n## The JPA caveat\n\nEven with `nestedTransactionAllowed=true`, the savepoint on `JpaTransactionManager` works at the **JDBC level** (rolls back SQL to the point). The `EntityManager` / persistence context does NOT semantically rewind to the savepoint: managed entities and the first-level cache are not reverted automatically. So on pure JPA, NESTED is a rare and fragile technique; the typical case is `DataSourceTransactionManager` + JDBC/MyBatis.","quizCorrect":"NESTED works on any savepoint-capable manager with nestedTransactionAllowed=true: DataSourceTransactionManager has it true by default, JpaTransactionManager false (but enableable), while JtaTransactionManager throws NestedTransactionNotSupportedException","quizWrong":["NESTED is supported only by DataSourceTransactionManager; JpaTransactionManager always throws NestedTransactionNotSupportedExceptionactionNotSupportedException, so the only way to get a savepoint under JPA is to open a brand new transaction instead of a savepoint","NESTED and REQUIRES_NEW are identical: both set a savepoint inside the current transaction","NESTED opens a new physical transaction on a separate connection and commits it independently of the outer one"],"picCap":"NESTED rolls back to a savepoint inside one tx; REQUIRES_NEW commits a separate tx"},"sp-tx-readonly":{"q":"What does @Transactional(readOnly=true) actually do in Spring + Hibernate — and which 'optimization' does it NOT give you?","a":"The one thing Spring does on the Hibernate side: HibernateJpaDialect.beginTransaction() switches the Session to FlushMode.MANUAL (FlushMode.NEVER in older versions). This removes the auto-flush and the dirty-checking pass before queries and commit → it saves CPU, not memory. Spring does NOT call session.setDefaultReadOnly(true), so loaded entities still get a full EntityEntry with loadedState (the hydrated snapshot) — the snapshot copies stay in memory. To actually avoid holding snapshots (loadedState=null) you need the org.hibernate.readOnly hint: Query.setReadOnly(true), session.setDefaultReadOnly(true), or @QueryHint — none of which the Spring flag enables. Additionally readOnly may propagate to Connection.setReadOnly(true) (via DataSourceUtils, driver/pool dependent) and is used to route to a read replica.","d":"readOnly=true ≠ 'entities are read-only'. It is only a transaction hint: MANUAL flush (no dirty-check → less CPU) plus the isCurrentTransactionReadOnly() flag used for replica routing and optionally pushed into JDBC Connection.setReadOnly. It does NOT save memory on snapshot copies — that requires the org.hibernate.readOnly hint.","note":"⚠️ Under readOnly=true Hibernate is in FlushMode.MANUAL: if you accidentally mutate a managed entity, NO UPDATE is emitted — flush never runs and the change is silently lost (not an exception!). And remember: readOnly is honored only on the OUTERMOST transaction (REQUIRED). If an inner readOnly=true method merely joins an already-open readOnly=false transaction, the flag is ignored — no new MANUAL-flush transaction is started.","hook":"readOnly saves CPU (no dirty-check), not memory — snapshots stay until you use org.hibernate.readOnly.","more":"## What `@Transactional(readOnly=true)` actually turns on\n\nSpring passes `readOnly` into the `TransactionDefinition`. Then `HibernateJpaDialect.beginTransaction()` does essentially one thing:\n\n```\nsession.setHibernateFlushMode(FlushMode.MANUAL); // FlushMode.NEVER in older Hibernate\n```\n\nEffects:\n- **MANUAL flush** → no auto-flush before JPQL/Criteria and before commit → **no dirty-checking pass** (comparing current state against the snapshot). This is a **CPU** saving, especially on large reads.\n- The flag is exposed via `TransactionSynchronizationManager.isCurrentTransactionReadOnly()` → used to route reads to a replica.\n- `DataSourceUtils.prepareConnectionForTransaction()` **may** call `Connection.setReadOnly(true)` — but that depends on the driver/pool (often a no-op or just an optimizer hint).\n\n## What readOnly does NOT do (the main trap)\n\nSpring does **not** call `session.setDefaultReadOnly(true)`. So every loaded entity is placed into the persistence context as usual, with an `EntityEntry` whose `loadedState` is the hydrated snapshot array. **Snapshot copies stay in memory.**\n\nThe real no-snapshot optimization (`loadedState = null` → less memory) is enabled separately:\n\n```java\n// any of these — and this is NOT Spring's readOnly\nsession.setDefaultReadOnly(true);\nquery.setReadOnly(true);\n// JPA:\n@QueryHint(name = \"org.hibernate.readOnly\", value = \"true\")\n```\n\n## Interview checklist\n| Claim | True? |\n|---|---|\n| readOnly → FlushMode.MANUAL | yes |\n| readOnly saves CPU (no dirty-check) | yes |\n| readOnly saves memory (no snapshot) | **no** |\n| readOnly = `setDefaultReadOnly(true)` | **no** |\n| readOnly can propagate to JDBC `setReadOnly` | yes (driver-dependent) |\n| readOnly helps route to a replica | yes |\n\nThe classic write-up is by Vlad Mihalcea: Spring `readOnly` ≠ Hibernate read-only entities.","quizCorrect":"Switches the Hibernate Session to FlushMode.MANUAL — removes auto-flush and dirty-checking (CPU saving), but the entities' snapshot copies stay in memory","quizWrong":["Calls session.setDefaultReadOnly(true) so entities load without loadedState — saving memory on snapshot copiesreation for the whole session for the whole run","Makes entities immutable: any UPDATE on a managed entity throws during flush","Opens a connection to a read-only replica at the driver level and physically forbids any INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE"],"picCap":"Spring readOnly = only MANUAL flush (CPU down). Snapshot copies remain — memory is saved only by the separate org.hibernate.readOnly hint."},"h-entity-states-merge-persist":{"q":"JPA entity lifecycle: how do transient / persistent / detached / removed differ, and why do persist() vs merge() behave differently on a detached entity from a REST payload?","a":"Four states: transient (new, no id, outside the persistence context), persistent/managed (in the PC, dirty checking works), detached (was managed but the PC was closed/cleared), removed (marked for deletion). persist(detached) throws EntityExistsException (at persist or later at flush/commit) — you can't reattach a detached instance this way. merge(detached) does NOT change the passed argument: it stays detached, and the method RETURNS a new managed copy. merge with id==null behaves like persist (INSERT); merge with id!=null not in the PC does a SELECT, copies the fields and returns the managed instance. So a REST DTO is almost always merge, and its return value is what you use afterwards.","d":"remove() is allowed ONLY on a persistent (managed) entity. em.remove(detached) throws IllegalArgumentException per JPA 3.2.2. So removed is reachable only from persistent, not 'further down the chain' after detached. Spring Data SimpleJpaRepository.save() calls em.merge() for a non-new entity and returns the managed result — ignoring it means working with a detached copy.","note":"⚠️ merge(detached) does NOT make the argument managed — it stays detached, and managed is ONLY the returned value. Classic trap: `repo.save(dto); return dto;` returns the detached object (with stale/null id and without merged-in fields). You need `dto = repo.save(dto)`. Second trap: a partially-populated graph from REST + orphanRemoval=true — merge sees the 'empty' children collection and deletes the rows in the DB.","hook":"merge doesn't 'attach' your object — it hands back a new one. Use the argument afterwards and you're working with a detached ghost.","more":"## JPA state transitions (spec 3.2)\n\n| From | Operation | To |\n|---|---|---|\n| transient | `persist()` | persistent |\n| persistent | `remove()` | removed |\n| persistent | close/clear PC, `detach()`, `evict()` | detached |\n| detached | `merge()` | **returns** a managed copy (argument stays detached) |\n| removed | `persist()` | persistent (un-delete before flush) |\n\n### persist vs merge on detached\n- `persist(detached)` &rarr; `EntityExistsException` (wrapped in `PersistenceException`) — at the call or deferred to flush/commit.\n- `merge(null-id)` &rarr; behaves like `persist` (INSERT of a new row).\n- `merge(id!=null, not in PC)` &rarr; SELECT by id, copies the detached instance's fields into the loaded managed entity, returns managed.\n\n### Why remove is not from detached\n`em.remove(e)` requires a managed `e`. On a detached one &rarr; `IllegalArgumentException` (JPA 3.2.2). To delete a detached entity: first `e = em.merge(detached)`, then `em.remove(e)`.\n\n### Spring Data\n`SimpleJpaRepository.save()`: if `isNew()` &rarr; `persist`, else &rarr; `merge` and **returns the merge result**. So always `entity = repo.save(entity)`.","quizCorrect":"em.remove() on a detached entity throws IllegalArgumentException — removed is reachable only from persistent","quizWrong":["em.remove() moves a detached entity to removed, the same as a persistent one in the same context that manages it","merge() mutates the passed argument to managed and returns void","persist() on a detached entity silently does an UPDATE of the existing row"],"picCap":"remove() branches off persistent, not off detached: remove(detached) &rarr; IllegalArgumentException. merge returns a NEW managed copy."},"h-flush-vs-commit":{"q":"flush() vs commit() in Hibernate — what's the difference, and why is flush dangerous before the commit?","a":"flush() only syncs the L1 cache with the DB: it sends the accumulated INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE over the already-open JDBC connection within the same transaction — without a COMMIT. The changes are NOT durable and aren't visible to other transactions (except under READ UNCOMMITTED). commit() = auto-flush first, then COMMIT: only now is the data persisted and visible. The key trap: constraints, triggers, and IDENTITY key generation fire at flush time — i.e. BEFORE the commit.","d":"FlushModeType.AUTO (the JPA default) triggers an auto-flush before a JPQL/Criteria/native query and before commit. FlushModeType.COMMIT flushes only at commit — then a JPQL SELECT can return stale data, while find()-by-id still returns the dirty entity from the L1 cache. setHibernateFlushMode(MANUAL) disables auto-flush entirely.","note":"⚠️ A huge batch + the default FlushMode.AUTO = degradation. A loop over N entities triggers an auto-flush on every intermediate query, and each flush is a full dirty-checking pass over ALL managed entities in the context — i.e. O(n²) snapshot-comparison work (not O(n²) emitted UPDATEs — already-flushed rows aren't re-sent). Fix it with periodic flush()+clear() in chunks and FlushMode.MANUAL for the loop. And remember: with GenerationType.IDENTITY, JDBC batch-insert doesn't work at all — an INSERT fires immediately on each persist().","hook":"flush is like dropping your draft on the printing press: the sheets are already printed and sitting on the machine, but the run isn't signed off yet. commit is the signature: only now does the issue go on sale, and you can't recall it.","more":"**flush() ≠ commit().** Two independent points in time.\n\n- **flush()** — Hibernate runs dirty checking over managed entities and, via the `ActionQueue`, sends the accumulated `INSERT`/`UPDATE`/`DELETE` over the already-open JDBC connection. The transaction **stays open**: the changes are not committed (`COMMIT` hasn't been called), not durable, and not visible to other transactions — except under the exotic `READ UNCOMMITTED`.\n- **commit()** — an implicit flush first (if anything is pending), then the `COMMIT` itself. Only now is the data durable and visible.\n\n### When auto-flush fires\n`FlushModeType.AUTO` is the JPA default. Auto-flush fires:\n- before running a JPQL/Criteria/native query (so the query sees its own changes);\n- before the transaction commit.\n\n`FlushModeType.COMMIT` defers flush to commit only — then a JPQL `SELECT` may return stale data because the dirty changes aren't in the DB yet; but `find()`-by-id still returns the dirty entity straight from the L1 cache. `session.setHibernateFlushMode(MANUAL)` turns off auto-flush completely — flush only by hand.\n\n### Why it matters in production\nSince flush sends SQL **before** the commit, at flush time the following already fire:\n- `NOT NULL`/`UNIQUE`/FK constraint checks and DB triggers;\n- for `GenerationType.IDENTITY`, Hibernate must run the `INSERT` immediately on `persist()` to obtain the generated key. Side effect: **JDBC batch-insert is disabled for IDENTITY** (you can't batch what must run one-by-one to return the id).\n- the `ActionQueue` orders the actions (INSERT before DELETE, etc.) rather than by call order — hence the surprising SQL order in the logs.","quizCorrect":"flush sends the accumulated SQL over the open connection within the transaction but does NOT commit — the data isn't durable and is visible to others only under READ UNCOMMITTED","quizWrong":["flush does a COMMIT right away, so the changes are instantly durable and visible to all transactions that are running at that moment concurrently, with no further action required from the application","flush and commit are synonyms in Hibernate; there's no timing difference between them","flush only recomputes dirty checking in memory and doesn't send any SQL to the DB until commit"],"picCap":"flush sends SQL into the open transaction without committing; commit does an auto-flush, then COMMIT — only then durable"},"h-lazy-proxy-getreference":{"q":"em.getReference() / repository.getReferenceById() returns a lazy Hibernate proxy. How does it differ from find(), and which type-check operator breaks on it — instanceof or getClass()?","a":"getReference() (Hibernate load()) returns an uninitialized proxy — a ByteBuddy-generated subclass of the entity with only the PK populated; the real SELECT is deferred until the first non-id access. find()/get() runs the SELECT immediately and returns the object or null. The operator that breaks is getClass(): proxy.getClass() is Order$HibernateProxy$xxxx, so getClass() == Order.class is false. But proxy instanceof Order is TRUE, because the proxy is a SUBCLASS of Order — instanceof is the safe operator. Correct approach: use instanceof for the type check and Hibernate.getClass(entity) to get the real class (it unwraps the proxy).","d":"getReference() on a missing row does not fail immediately: the proxy is created, but on first access to a non-id field it throws EntityNotFoundException (or LazyInitializationException if the session is already closed). Direct access to a proxy's private field returns null — the subclass overrides only the getters, not the fields themselves.","note":"⚠️ Common inverted mistake: assuming proxy instanceof Order returns false. It doesn't — the proxy is a subclass, so instanceof is TRUE. What actually breaks is getClass() == Order.class (returns false, since the class is Order$HibernateProxy$xxx). So never write getClass() == o.getClass() in equals()/hashCode() of JPA entities — a proxy and the managed instance of the same row would compare as unequal. Use instanceof for the type and Hibernate.getClass() for the real class.","hook":"instanceof survives a proxy, getClass() doesn't","more":"## getReference() vs find()\n\n| | find() / get() | getReference() / load() |\n|---|---|---|\n| SELECT | immediate | deferred until first non-id access |\n| Missing row | null | proxy, then EntityNotFoundException |\n| Result type | the entity | proxy subclass (if not already in context) |\n\n## Proxy and type checks\n\nThe proxy is generated by ByteBuddy (default since Hibernate 5.3; Javassist before, CGLIB earlier) as a SUBCLASS of your entity:\n\n```java\nOrder proxy = em.getReference(Order.class, id);\nproxy instanceof Order;            // true  — proxy EXTENDS Order\nproxy.getClass() == Order.class;   // false — it's Order$HibernateProxy$abc123\nHibernate.getClass(proxy);         // Order.class — correct way\n```\n\n- Use instanceof for the type check (safe on proxies).\n- Use Hibernate.getClass(entity), not getClass(), for the real class.\n- Never use getClass() == o.getClass() in entity equals()/hashCode() — it breaks comparison between a proxy and the real entity of the same row.\n\n## The one genuine instanceof gotcha\n\nPolymorphism + inheritance: if the declared type is a base Animal but the row is really a Dog, the proxy is built against Animal, so proxy instanceof Dog is false even though the DB row is a Dog. There you need initialization + Hibernate.unproxy(). In the ordinary (non-polymorphic) case, instanceof Order is always true.","quizCorrect":"proxy instanceof Order returns true (the proxy is a subclass of Order), and what breaks is getClass() == Order.class, which returns false","quizWrong":["proxy instanceof Order returns false, because Order$Proxy ≠ Order","Both instanceof and getClass() return true — the proxy is indistinguishable from the entity","getReference() runs the SELECT immediately, so the proxy and the comparisons behave like a normal entity from the very first access onwards"],"picCap":"instanceof on a proxy = true (safe); getClass() breaks; Hibernate.getClass() fixes it"},"sp-jpa-batch":{"q":"You save 10 000 entities in a loop via a JPA repository with `@GeneratedValue(strategy = IDENTITY)`, set `hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50` — yet the logs still show 10 000 separate INSERTs. Why doesn't batching work, and how do you fix it?","a":"`IDENTITY` silently disables JDBC batching: Hibernate must execute every INSERT immediately to fetch the DB-generated key back — there's nothing to batch. Fix it by switching to `SEQUENCE` with a pooled optimizer (`allocationSize` > 1): Hibernate then hands out ids from a pre-allocated range with no per-row round-trip and can accumulate INSERTs into one JDBC batch. Add `order_inserts`/`order_updates`, periodic `flush()`+`clear()`, and (on Postgres) `reWriteBatchedInserts=true`.","d":"**Why IDENTITY breaks batching.** With `IDENTITY` the database generates the key at INSERT time (auto_increment/serial). Hibernate needs that id immediately (it's part of the entity's identity in the persistence context), so it runs each INSERT one at a time and reads `getGeneratedKeys()`. The JDBC batch API can't reliably return a per-row key, so Hibernate just disables batching for IDENTITY — silently, no error. Any `batch_size` is ignored.\n\n**The SEQUENCE + pooled fix.** `@GeneratedValue(strategy = SEQUENCE, generator = \"g\")` + `@SequenceGenerator(name=\"g\", sequenceName=\"my_seq\", allocationSize=50)`. The pooled optimizer pulls one value from the sequence and hands out `allocationSize` ids locally → no per-row round-trip → Hibernate accumulates INSERTs and ships them as one batch.\n\n**Also enable:**\n- `hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50` — the batch size.\n- `hibernate.order_inserts=true`, `hibernate.order_updates=true` — group statements by table so the driver actually assembles a batch (without sorting, interleaved INSERTs into different tables break the batch).\n- Periodic `flush()` + `clear()` every N entities — otherwise the persistence context grows, dirty-checking becomes O(n), memory leaks.\n\n**Postgres:** `reWriteBatchedInserts=true` in the JDBC URL — pgjdbc collapses the N-INSERT batch into a single multi-row `INSERT ... VALUES (..),(..),...`, a real on-the-wire speedup.","note":"⚠️ `reWriteBatchedInserts` (camelCase) is a PostgreSQL driver (pgjdbc) parameter — meaningless for other databases. MySQL has no sequence objects at all (it's a Postgres feature, absent from every MySQL version including 8.0) — use a `TABLE` generator for batch inserts or enable `rewriteBatchedStatements=true` in the Connector/J JDBC URL (different name, different driver).","hook":"IDENTITY says \"give me the key right now\"; batching says \"I'll collect and send in bulk.\" Incompatible by design.","more":"## Why IDENTITY specifically is incompatible with batching\n\n`IDENTITY` means \"the DB generates the key at insert time\" (`AUTO_INCREMENT`, `SERIAL`). Hibernate stores the entity in the persistence context by its id, so it needs the id right after the INSERT:\n\n```java\n@Id\n@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY) // batching killed\nLong id;\n```\n\nEvery `persist()` → a separate INSERT + a `getGeneratedKeys()` read. The JDBC `addBatch()`/`executeBatch()` path can't reliably map generated keys to rows, so Hibernate **silently** disables batching for IDENTITY — `batch_size` simply has no effect.\n\n## The right generator\n\n```java\n@Id\n@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = \"g\")\n@SequenceGenerator(name = \"g\", sequenceName = \"my_seq\", allocationSize = 50)\nLong id;\n```\n\nThe pooled optimizer (enabled when `allocationSize > 1`) hits the sequence once and hands out 50 ids locally. No per-key round-trip → Hibernate accumulates INSERTs and sends them in one `executeBatch()`.\n\n## Settings\n\n```properties\nspring.jpa.properties.hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50\nspring.jpa.properties.hibernate.order_inserts=true\nspring.jpa.properties.hibernate.order_updates=true\n```\n\n`order_inserts/order_updates` re-sort statements by table. Without it, interleaved INSERTs into different tables split the batch into pieces.\n\n## Don't flood the persistence context\n\n```java\nfor (int i = 0; i < entities.size(); i++) {\n    em.persist(entities.get(i));\n    if (i % 50 == 0) { em.flush(); em.clear(); }\n}\n```\n\n`flush()` ships what's accumulated, `clear()` detaches managed entities — otherwise dirty-checking degrades to O(n) and the context eats memory.\n\n## Postgres bonus\n\n`reWriteBatchedInserts=true` in the JDBC URL (a pgjdbc-specific parameter) rewrites N INSERTs into one multi-row `INSERT ... VALUES (..),(..),...` — a speedup at the network/parsing level, on top of the JDBC batch.","quizCorrect":"With IDENTITY, Hibernate must obtain the generated key right after each INSERT, so batching is disabled — INSERTs go one at a time","quizWrong":["batch_size=50 is too small, the driver ignores values below 100","Spring Data JPA doesn't support JDBC batching at all, you need JdbcTemplate","Batching works, Hibernate just logs each INSERT on a separate line while it's one statement on the wire to the database in a single trip"],"picCap":"IDENTITY: a round-trip for the key on every row — the batch is dead. SEQUENCE+pooled: ids are handed out locally from a range, INSERTs accumulate into one JDBC batch."},"sp-jpa-flush":{"q":"Inside @Transactional(readOnly=true) the code calls repo.save(entity) and mutates a managed entity. Which of these actually reaches the DB at commit — and which silently vanishes?","a":"readOnly=true puts the Hibernate Session into FlushMode.MANUAL (Spring does this in HibernateJpaDialect when the tx starts). MANUAL disables auto-flush both BEFORE queries and AT commit — only a manual flush remains. So: a dirty change to a managed entity and repo.save()/em.merge() merely queue an INSERT/UPDATE in the action queue, but no flush happens → at commit the changes are SILENTLY discarded and nothing hits the DB (Spring Data save() does not flush; only saveAndFlush() does). What does NOT depend on auto-flush bypasses MANUAL and really writes: an explicit entityManager.flush(), and JPQL/native DML (UPDATE/DELETE via Query.executeUpdate(), which sends SQL directly). Bottom line: MANUAL is not a DB-level write ban — it just turns off auto-flush; ordinary ORM writes via save/merge/dirty-checking silently get lost, while an explicit flush and DML do not.","d":"readOnly=true → FlushMode.MANUAL: save()/merge()/dirty UPDATE get scheduled but, with no auto-flush at commit, are silently discarded. Only an explicit em.flush() and JPQL/native DML actually write — they don't rely on auto-flush.","note":"⚠️ Mirror trap: expecting a dirty-checking UPDATE INSIDE a readOnly method — it won't fire (MANUAL kills auto-flush at commit). And conversely: treating readOnly as a write-guard is dangerous — JPQL/native UPDATE/DELETE via executeUpdate() and any explicit entityManager.flush() write to the DB anyway, bypassing MANUAL.","hook":"readOnly doesn't make data read-only — it just turns off auto-flush. So save() silently evaporates, while a manual flush() and an UPDATE query still write.","more":"## The FlushMode.MANUAL mechanism\n\n`@Transactional(readOnly=true)` with Hibernate leads to `session.setHibernateFlushMode(FlushMode.MANUAL)` (via `HibernateJpaDialect`; older versions set `NEVER`). MANUAL means **flush happens only on an explicit** `flush()` call — no auto-flush before queries, none at commit.\n\n### What SILENTLY vanishes (never reaches the DB)\n\n- **`repo.save(entity)` / `em.persist()` / `em.merge()`** — Spring Data `save()` does NOT flush (only `saveAndFlush()` does), and under MANUAL there's no auto-flush before a query or at commit → the write stays in the action queue and is dropped.\n- **Mutating a managed entity (dirty checking)** — the diff is computed, the UPDATE is scheduled, but with no flush at commit it never goes out.\n\n> This is the fix versus the earlier (incorrect) version of the card: `save()`/`merge()` do **NOT** belong in the \"really writes\" list — they rely on exactly the flush that MANUAL disables.\n\n### What still writes (bypasses MANUAL)\n\n```java\n@Transactional(readOnly = true)\nvoid demo() {\n    em.merge(detached);                 // scheduled → DISCARDED at commit\n    order.setStatus(DONE);              // dirty → also DISCARDED\n\n    em.flush();                         // explicit flush → WRITES (merge+dirty go out now)\n\n    em.createQuery(\"update Order o set o.status=:s where o.id=:id\")\n      .setParameter(\"s\", DONE).setParameter(\"id\", id)\n      .executeUpdate();                 // JPQL DML → WRITES directly, bypassing auto-flush\n}\n```\n\n- **Explicit `em.flush()`** — the manual flush MANUAL exists for.\n- **JPQL / native DML (`executeUpdate()`)** — runs SQL directly without relying on persistence-context auto-flush (and JPQL DML even bypasses the persistence context, leaving the L1 cache out of sync).\n\n### Senior nuance\n\n`readOnly=true` is a **hint**, not a DB-level write-guard: besides MANUAL flush, the `isCurrentTransactionReadOnly()` flag drives read-replica routing (`AbstractRoutingDataSource`) and may propagate into `Connection.setReadOnly(true)`. But if the connection still hits the master and you do an explicit flush/DML, the write physically happens. Don't rely on `readOnly` as protection against mutations.","quizCorrect":"Both are silently discarded: under FlushMode.MANUAL there is no auto-flush at commit, so save() and the dirty UPDATE are never flushed.","quizWrong":["Both are written: commit always flushes pending changes regardless of FlushMode.","save() is written but the dirty change is discarded, because save() flushes immediately. and never re-reads the entity afterwards at all","Neither is written and an exception is thrown, because readOnly forbids any write call."],"picCap":"Under FlushMode.MANUAL: save()/merge()/dirty are scheduled then discarded at commit; only explicit flush() and JPQL/native DML write."},"ops-three-pillars":{"q":"Your dashboard shows avg latency = 50ms, all green, yet customers complain. Why does the mean lie, and why can't you average the p99 of two instances?","a":"The mean drowns the tail: 99 fast responses hide one 2-second response. You need percentiles (p95/p99/p99.9) — they show what the worst N% of users actually feel. The key senior trap: a percentile is NOT additive — you cannot average or sum it across instances. avg(p99_A, p99_B) ≠ p99 of the merged stream. That's why Prometheus Summary (quantile computed client-side) is useless for cross-pod aggregation; you need a Histogram: add cumulative _bucket{le} via sum by(le)(rate(..._bucket[5m])) and compute histogram_quantile at query time, across all instances.","d":"Mean and percentiles answer different questions. Mean = 'on average how much', dragged by both large and small values, and the long latency tail vanishes. A percentile pX = 'the value below which X% of requests fall': p99=1s means 1 in 100 users waits ≥ 1s. A percentile is an order statistic — nonlinear, so averaging or summing precomputed pX across instances/time is mathematically wrong. A Prometheus Histogram stores cumulative per-bucket counters (_bucket{le}, plus _sum/_count); the quantile is computed at query time (histogram_quantile) and therefore aggregates correctly across all pods via sum by(le). A Summary computes the quantile inside the app — those numbers can no longer be recombined over a pool.","note":"⚠️ Don't confuse Histogram and Summary in Prometheus. Summary gives a ready-made {quantile=\"0.99\"} computed in the app — you CANNOT aggregate it across instances (you can't avg/sum quantiles). Only a Histogram (_bucket{le}) lets you sum by(le) + histogram_quantile over a pool of pods. Also: p99 accuracy is bounded by bucket boundaries — histogram_quantile only interpolates within a bucket, so coarse le values give a coarse percentile.","hook":"'Average across the ward': the mean hides whoever is dying in the tail. p99 is about the worst of a hundred — and you can't average it.","more":"## Why the mean lies and percentiles don't\n\n**The mean sinks into the tail.** 99 requests at 10ms plus 1 request at 2000ms gives avg ≈ 30ms — 'green', though every hundredth client waits 2 seconds. The percentile shows exactly the tail: `p99 = 2000ms`.\n\n**You cannot average a percentile.** It's an order statistic, nonlinear by nature:\n```text\ninst A: p99 = 20ms\ninst B: p99 = 20ms\navg(p99) = 20ms          # A LIE\np99(A ∪ B) may be 1000ms  # if both tails line up in time\n```\nSame trap over time: you can't average p99 over 5-minute windows to get the hourly p99.\n\n**Hence Histogram, not Summary, in Prometheus:**\n```promql\n# Histogram: buckets are cumulative, quantile computed at query time\nhistogram_quantile(\n  0.99,\n  sum by (le) (rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m]))\n)\n```\n- `Histogram` exposes `_bucket{le=\"...\"}` (cumulative counters) + `_sum` + `_count`. Add buckets with `sum by(le)` across all pods, THEN take the quantile — mathematically correct.\n- `Summary` computes the quantile **inside the app** (`{quantile=\"0.99\"}`). Those numbers can't be recombined over a pool — the official Prometheus docs forbid aggregating them.\n\n**Histogram accuracy is bounded by bucket boundaries.** `histogram_quantile` linearly interpolates *within* the bucket the quantile lands in. Coarse `le` values (0.1, 0.5, 1, 5s) make p99 jump between boundaries — buckets must match your SLO. Native histograms (Prometheus 2.40+) fix this with exponential buckets.\n\n**Tail-at-scale (Dean & Barroso).** If one user request fans out to N backends, the chance of hitting someone's tail is `1 − (p_fast)^N`. With each service at p99 and N=100: `1 − 0.99^100 ≈ 0.63` — two-thirds of requests hit somebody's p99. That's why distributed systems chase p99.9 and cut the tail (hedged requests, timeouts) instead of celebrating a 'good average'.","quizCorrect":"A percentile is an order statistic, not additive: avg(p99_A, p99_B) ≠ p99 of the merged stream; you must aggregate histogram buckets and compute histogram_quantile at query time","quizWrong":["You can just average each instance's p99 — Prometheus does this automatically via avg()","The mean is more accurate than percentiles because it uses all requests, while p99 discards 99% of the data","Prometheus Summary aggregates across instances better than Histogram because the quantile is already computeduted on the instance itself before it is scraped by the collector itself"],"picCap":"You can't average precomputed p99s across instances; add the histogram buckets and compute the quantile at query time. At fan-out N=100 the p99 tail hits ~63% of requests."},"jc-stream-peek":{"q":"Why does `Stream.of(\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\",\"e\").peek(System.out::println).count()` print nothing in Java 9+ — and which operations bring peek back to life?","a":"On a SIZED source `count()` (since Java 9) reads the exact size directly via `getExactSizeIfKnown()` and never traverses the elements, so `peek` (any `Consumer`) is not invoked. The size stays \"known\" as long as the pipeline keeps the SIZED characteristic. `peek` runs again only if an intermediate op ERASES the exact size: `filter`, `distinct`, `flatMap`. But `map`, `limit`, `skip`, `sorted`, `boxed` PRESERVE the size — after them `count()` still short-circuits and `peek` stays silent.","d":"This is a `java.util.stream` library optimization (not the JIT): the terminal `count()` asks the pipeline for an exact size and, if SIZED, returns it without traversal. Senior trap: people assume `.limit(3)` is \"dynamic\" and revives peek — it doesn't. `limit`/`skip` over a SIZED source yield a computable exact size (SLICE preserves it), so `count()` short-circuits again and peek stays silent. The size is lost only if it was ALREADY lost upstream: `filter(...).limit(3)` runs peek because `filter` already cleared SIZED, not because of `limit`. Empirically (OpenJDK 25): peek+count→0, peek+map+count→0, peek+limit(3)+count→0, peek+skip(2)+count→0, peek+filter+count→5, peek+distinct+count→5, peek+flatMap+count→5, peek+filter+limit+count→3.","note":"⚠️ Don't \"fix\" this with `.limit()` — on a SIZED source limit/skip preserve the exact size and peek stays just as silent. Only filter/distinct/flatMap erase SIZED. Separately: in `parallelStream()` peek is invoked on several ForkJoinPool worker threads at once (measured: 9 distinct threads over 1000 elements) — any side effect inside peek must be thread-safe, and call order is undefined.","hook":"peek isn't silent because of laziness — count() just read the size off the label without opening the box.","more":"## What actually happens\n\nSince Java 9 (JDK-8067969) `count()` first asks for the exact size via the internal `getExactSizeIfKnown()`. If the source and the whole pipeline are **SIZED**, the size is returned without traversal — the terminal pass never runs, so no intermediate `Consumer` (including `peek`) fires.\n\n```java\n// peek stays silent — count() reads the size directly\nStream.of(\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\",\"e\")\n      .peek(System.out::println)   // prints nothing\n      .count();                    // 5\n```\n\n## Who PRESERVES the exact size (peek still silent)\n\n`map`, `limit`, `skip`, `sorted`, `boxed`, `asLongStream` all keep the SIZED characteristic. `limit`/`skip` are SLICE operations: over a SIZED source their result has a **computable exact size**, so count() short-circuits again.\n\n```java\n.peek(...).map(x->x).count();    // peek silent\n.peek(...).limit(3).count();     // peek silent, count=3\n.peek(...).skip(2).count();      // peek silent, count=3\n```\n\n## Who ERASES the exact size (peek wakes up)\n\n`filter`, `distinct`, `flatMap` — after them the size is unknown ahead of time, so count() must actually traverse:\n\n```java\n.peek(...).filter(x->true).count();    // peek runs 5x\n.peek(...).distinct().count();         // peek runs\n.peek(...).flatMap(Stream::of).count();// peek runs\n```\n\n## The ordering subtlety\n\n`limit` itself does NOT erase the size. But if an unsizing op already sits above it, peek runs because of that op, not because of limit:\n\n```java\n.peek(...).filter(x->true).limit(3).count(); // peek runs 3x\n//          ^ filter erased SIZED, limit is irrelevant\n```\n\n## How to force peek to run\n\nUse `forEach` (or collect into a collection) instead of `count()` — terminal ops that must visit every element always invoke peek.","quizCorrect":"map, limit and skip preserve SIZED, so count() reads the size directly and peek is not invoked","quizWrong":["limit makes the stream unsized, so after it peek runs again before count() as the developer intended","peek is never invoked on count() at all, even after filter","it's a JIT optimization that inlines the empty Consumer away"],"picCap":"map/limit/skip preserve SIZED → count() short-circuits, peek silent. Only filter/distinct/flatMap erase it."},"g-te-dont-mock-dont-own":{"q":"Why 'don't mock what you don't own' — and what does Mockito return by default if you do mock a third-party value object / collection?","a":"'Don't mock what you don't own' (GOOS, Freeman & Pryce): mock only your own abstraction interfaces, not third-party types — value objects, DTOs, JDK collections, library types. Build those for real (`new`, builders, factories) instead of faking them. A mock of a foreign type freezes your guess about its contract; when the library changes, the mock lies while tests stay green. For collections/DTOs a mock is also more useless than the real object. If you do mock anyway, Mockito's default (`RETURNS_DEFAULTS` = `ReturnsEmptyValues`) gives: empty `List`/`Set`/`Map`, `Optional.empty()`, empty `Stream`, `0`/`0.0`/`false` for primitives, and `null` for any reference type.","d":"The principle draws the mocking boundary: fake the roles/ports you own and whose contract you define, but use foreign concrete types for real. Mockito's defaults aren't a gift here: a mock `Repository` with a forgotten stub silently returns an empty list or `Optional.empty()` — the test passes for the wrong reason instead of failing on NPE. These values come from `ReturnsEmptyValues` (a.k.a. `RETURNS_DEFAULTS`), and collections/`Optional` arrive empty, not `null`.","note":"⚠️ Mockito returns **empty objects, not `null`** for collections and `Optional` by default — `repo.findAll()` on an unstubbed mock returns an empty `List`, and `repo.findById()` returns `Optional.empty()`. A test with a forgotten stub won't NPE; it silently passes for the wrong reason. Use `RETURNS_SMART_NULLS` or strict stubbing if you want unstubbed calls to fail loudly. And note: `BigDecimal` is not `final` (it's mockable), but it's still a foreign value object — build it, don't mock it.","hook":"Mocked a DTO — green test, wrong reason.","more":"## The principle\n\n'Don't mock what you don't own' comes from *Growing Object-Oriented Software, Guided by Tests* (Freeman & Pryce). A mock encodes **your assumption** about a type's contract. For your own interface that's fine — you author it. For a foreign type (JDK, library, value object) your assumption can drift from reality: the library updates, behavior shifts, the mock keeps lying with the old contract → tests green, prod broken.\n\n```java\n// BAD: mocking a foreign value object\nBigDecimal price = mock(BigDecimal.class);\nwhen(price.add(any())).thenReturn(...); // freezing a guess about arithmetic\n\n// GOOD: build for real\nBigDecimal price = new BigDecimal(\"100.50\");\n\n// GOOD: mock your own port\nPriceRepository repo = mock(PriceRepository.class); // your interface\nwhen(repo.findPrice(id)).thenReturn(new BigDecimal(\"100.50\"));\n```\n\n## Mockito default values (`RETURNS_DEFAULTS`)\n\nAn unstubbed method is served by `ReturnsEmptyValues` (synonym of `Answers.RETURNS_DEFAULTS`). Verified empirically on Mockito 4.11:\n\n| Return type | Default |\n|---|---|\n| `List` / `Set` / `Map` | **empty** collection (not `null`) |\n| `Optional<T>` | `Optional.empty()` (not `null`) |\n| `Stream<T>` | empty stream |\n| `int`/`long`/`double`/`boolean` | `0` / `0L` / `0.0` / `false` |\n| any reference type | `null` |\n\nThat's exactly why a forgotten collection/`Optional` stub **doesn't NPE** — it silently passes, the nastiest class of false-green tests.\n\n## Historical note: final and the mock-maker\n\nBefore Mockito 2 you couldn't mock `final` classes or `final` methods (the regular proxy/subclass mock-maker couldn't override them). And `String`, `LocalDate`, `record` (a record is implicitly `final`), `Integer`, `UUID` are all `final`, so mocking them out of the box was impossible.\n\n⚠️ Important: `java.math.BigDecimal` is **NOT** `final` (it `extends Number`), so it was always technically mockable. But you still shouldn't mock it — it's precisely the foreign value object you should build for real. 'Can be mocked' ≠ 'should be'.\n\nFrom Mockito 2 the `InlineByteBuddyMockMaker` arrived (bytecode instrumentation via the Byte Buddy agent), able to mock `final`. In Mockito 5.x the **inline mock-maker became the default**. But 'now you technically can mock `final`' still doesn't mean 'you should': the don't-mock-foreign principle still holds.","quizCorrect":"Mockito returns an empty List and Optional.empty() (not null) by default, and null for a reference type","quizWrong":["Mockito throws UnsupportedOperationException on an unstubbed method by default that has not been configured","An unstubbed method returns null for collections and Optional too","Mockito returns a really constructed empty object via the default constructor"],"picCap":"Mock your own ports; build foreign types for real. Mockito defaults: collections/Optional empty, references null."},"web-ssrf":{"q":"What is SSRF and why is it especially dangerous in the cloud? How do you defend against it?","a":"SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery) makes the server send an HTTP request to a URL the attacker controls, on the server's behalf. In the cloud this is critical: a request to the metadata endpoint 169.254.169.254 can steal the instance's temporary IAM credentials. Defenses: an allowlist of hosts and schemes, blocking internal/link-local ranges, controlling redirects, and IMDSv2 (which requires a session token).","d":"The key subtlety is redirects: validating only the initial URL is bypassed by a 302 redirect to an internal address. You must validate the FINAL URL after each redirect (or forbid them).","note":"⚠️ A hostname-based allowlist is vulnerable to DNS rebinding: at check time the name resolves to a public IP, but at request time (TTL=0) to an internal one. Resolve the name yourself, check the resulting IP against a denylist of private ranges, and connect to THAT exact IP. On redirects: `java.net.http.HttpClient` does NOT follow them by default (`Redirect.NEVER`), whereas `RestTemplate`/Apache HttpClient often do — there, validate the final URL.","hook":"The server is your courier with a pass into the internal network. SSRF is 'go fetch from this address': the attacker sends the courier to an internal vault (metadata) that's unreachable from outside.","more":"**SSRF** = make the server fetch a URL the attacker controls. The server often has access where the attacker doesn't: internal services, DB admin panels, and above all **cloud metadata**.\n\n**Cloud vector:** `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/` (AWS) returns the instance role's temporary IAM credentials. One SSRF → hijack of the whole instance's privileges. This is how the Capital One breach (2019) happened.\n\n**Defenses (in order):**\n- **IMDSv2** — requires a PUT for a session token before reading metadata; a plain GET via SSRF won't pass.\n- **Allowlist** of hosts AND schemes (only `https`, not `file://`/`gopher://`).\n- **Block private ranges** on the resolved IP: `10/8`, `172.16/12`, `192.168/16`, `127/8`, `169.254/16`, `::1`, `fc00::/7`.\n- **Redirects**: forbid them, or re-validate the final URL after EACH hop.\n\n**Common SSRF sinks:** webhooks, URL-based PDF/preview generation, import-by-link, image proxies.","quizCorrect":"The attacker makes the server request their URL; in the cloud — stealing IAM credentials via metadata at 169.254.169.254","quizWrong":["SSRF is a SQL injection via a forged request to the database","SSRF is dangerous only if the server returns the response directly to the client over the network over the open network to the requesting client","The defense against SSRF is HTML-escaping the server's response"],"picCap":"SSRF: the server follows the attacker's URL to cloud metadata → IAM credential theft; defense — allowlist + block private + IMDSv2"},"sp-autoconfig-imports":{"q":"What changed in registering auto-configurations in Spring Boot 2.7 / 3 (spring.factories → AutoConfiguration.imports)?","a":"Before Boot 2.7, auto-configurations were registered in `META-INF/spring.factories` under the `EnableAutoConfiguration` key. As of 2.7 that's deprecated, and the canonical place is the file `META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports` (one class FQN per line). In Boot 3, support for `spring.factories` specifically for auto-configs was REMOVED — a starter must use the new file, or its auto-config silently won't be picked up.","d":"The class itself is annotated with `@AutoConfiguration` (instead of `@Configuration`), and ordering is set via its `before`/`after` attributes instead of separate `@AutoConfigureBefore/After`.","note":"⚠️ A common confusion: `spring.factories` is NOT removed entirely in Boot 3 — it lives on for other extensions (`ApplicationListener`, `ApplicationContextInitializer`, `FailureAnalyzer`, `EnvironmentPostProcessor`). Only auto-config registration via the `EnableAutoConfiguration` key was removed. If you upgrade your own starter to Boot 3 and forget to create `AutoConfiguration.imports`, the auto-config beans simply won't appear — with no startup error.","hook":"Starters used to 'introduce themselves' via the shared spring.factories directory. Boot 3 added a separate door just for auto-configs — knock on the old one and you're not let in.","more":"**Why the change:** parsing `spring.factories` loads and instantiates entries for all keys at once; splitting auto-configs into their own file speeds up startup and simplifies processing.\n\n**New format** (Boot 2.7+):\n```\n# META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports\ncom.acme.MyAutoConfiguration\ncom.acme.OtherAutoConfiguration\n```\nThe class:\n```java\n@AutoConfiguration(after = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)\npublic class MyAutoConfiguration { ... }\n```\n`@AutoConfiguration` is a meta-annotation over `@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods=false)` with `before`/`after`/`beforeName`/`afterName` attributes for ordering.\n\n**Boot 2.7** — both mechanisms work (the old one with a warning). **Boot 3** — the old `EnableAutoConfiguration` key in `spring.factories` is no longer read.","quizCorrect":"As of 2.7 — the AutoConfiguration.imports file (one FQN per line); in Boot 3 auto-config registration via spring.factories was removed","quizWrong":["In Boot 3 auto-configs are registered via @ComponentScan over the starter's package rather than through an imports file on the classpath","spring.factories is fully removed from Spring Boot 3 for all factory types","Nothing changed — spring.factories is still canonical in Boot 3"],"picCap":"spring.factories (removed for auto-configs in Boot 3) → AutoConfiguration.imports; @Configuration → @AutoConfiguration"},"kc-realm-client-roles":{"q":"Keycloak: realm, client, roles (realm vs client) and groups — how is the access model structured?","a":"A realm is an isolated tenant: its own users, clients, signing keys and token settings; one Keycloak hosts many realms. A client is an application registered in a realm (public / confidential / bearer-only). Roles are either realm roles (global within the realm) or client roles (bound to a specific client); they are assigned to a user directly or via groups (a bundle of roles + attributes). A composite role includes other roles. In the token, realm roles live under realm_access.roles, while client roles live under resource_access.<clientId>.roles.","d":"The 'realm → client → role/group' model is Keycloak's RBAC core. A realm gives hard isolation (prod/dev, separate tenants) down to its own signing keys, so a token from one realm is invalid in another. A client describes HOW an app obtains tokens: public (SPA, no secret, PKCE required), confidential (backend with client_secret), bearer-only (only validates incoming tokens, never initiates login). Splitting realm vs client roles keeps an 'admin' role from leaking across apps: client roles scope permissions per application, realm roles are cross-application. Groups are a convenient way to attach a bundle of roles and attributes to many users without assigning each one by hand.","note":"⚠️ Deleting or renaming a client (its clientId) invalidates all assigned client roles and the resource_access.<clientId> path in tokens — old sessions/tokens reference a vanished client, and downstream services that match roles against a hard-coded resource_access.api.roles silently stop authorizing. When changing a clientId, update protocol mappers, audience and role mapping in consumers in lockstep.","hook":"A realm is a separate apartment building with its own lock on the entrance (signing keys): tenants of one building can't enter another with the same keys. A client is a specific apartment-application. A realm role is a pass to the whole building, a client role is the key to one apartment. A group is a key-ring handed to a new family at once, so you don't have to cut each key by hand.","more":"## Where roles sit in the token and why it's a trap\n\nA common mistake is looking for **client roles** in `realm_access`. They won't appear there: realm roles are kept separate from client roles.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"realm_access\": { \"roles\": [\"trader\", \"offline_access\"] },\n  \"resource_access\": {\n    \"api\":    { \"roles\": [\"read\", \"write\"] },\n    \"account\":{ \"roles\": [\"view-profile\"] }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Gotcha: Spring Security doesn't see roles out of the box\n\nKeycloak puts roles in `realm_access.roles` / `resource_access`, but `JwtAuthenticationConverter` reads the `scope`/`scp` claim by default. Without a custom converter you get authentication with zero `ROLE_*`:\n\n```java\nvar conv = new JwtAuthenticationConverter();\nconv.setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter(jwt -> {\n  Map<String,Object> ra = jwt.getClaim(\"realm_access\");\n  List<String> roles = ra == null ? List.of()\n      : (List<String>) ra.getOrDefault(\"roles\", List.of());\n  return roles.stream()\n      .map(r -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority(\"ROLE_\" + r))\n      .collect(Collectors.toList());\n});\n```\n\n## Client roles don't reach the token automatically\n\nFor `resource_access.<otherClient>` to land in the access token, you must either request that client as an audience or add a **User Client Role** protocol mapper. By default the token carries client roles only for clients present in `aud`.\n\n## composite role\n\nA `composite` is an aggregate role: assign a user `manager` and it 'expands' into `read` + `write` + realm role `trader`. Convenient, but the token shows the already-**expanded** flat list of roles — you'll have to debug the nesting in the admin console.","quizCorrect":"Client roles scope permissions per application and land in the token under resource_access.<clientId>.roles, while realm roles are global within the realm and live in realm_access.roles","quizWrong":["Realm roles and client roles are merged into one shared top-level roles array in the token, with no per-application separationpplication separation at all anywhere in the issued token that the client receives","A client is a group of users inside a realm, and roles can only be assigned directly to a user, bypassing groups","One Keycloak hosts exactly one realm, and tenant isolation is done with separate Keycloak instances"],"picCap":"One realm: 3 client types; a realm role lands in realm_access, a client role in resource_access.<clientId>"},"kc-public-confidential":{"q":"Public vs confidential vs bearer-only client in Keycloak — when to use which?","a":"**Confidential** — has a client secret, for backends we trust to keep it: the authorization code flow exchanges the code for tokens by calling the token endpoint with the secret. **Public** — NO secret (SPA/mobile, where the secret is visible in the browser/binary anyway), so it MUST use PKCE so an intercepted authorization code can't be exchanged. **Bearer-only** — a pure resource server: it only validates incoming Bearer access tokens, never initiates a redirect login and never calls the token endpoint. Core rule: client type = 'who can be trusted with a secret'.","d":"This is about OAuth2/OIDC client authentication, not roles. Decision axes: (1) can the client keep a secret safely → confidential vs public; (2) does it initiate a login flow at all → if it only checks tokens, it's bearer-only. SPAs historically were public+implicit flow; the standard now is public + authorization code + PKCE (implicit is deprecated). Confidential supports extra client authentication methods (client_secret_basic/post, private_key_jwt, mTLS). In modern Keycloak, instead of a 'bearer-only' checkbox you set 'Client authentication: ON' and disable all auth flows (Standard/Direct) — the client stays a token validator.","note":"⚠️ The old 'Bearer-Only' checkbox is gone from Keycloak's new client model. If a backend resource is mistakenly left as a plain confidential client with Standard Flow + service accounts enabled, it can initiate logins and obtain tokens — extra attack surface. For a pure resource server disable Standard Flow, Direct Access Grants and Service Accounts: let it ONLY validate the incoming Bearer token. And remember: a bearer-only client can't be used for login at all — it has no flow, and Keycloak returns an error if you try to start an authorization request with it.","hook":"Like keys to a vault. The backend (confidential) is a clerk behind an armored door: you can hand it a secret key. The SPA/mobile (public) is a courier on the street whose bag is in plain sight, so you don't give it a secret key — instead you put a one-time tamper seal (PKCE) on the delivery note so an intercepted note can't be substituted. Bearer-only is the gatekeeper: it lets no one in and hands out no keys, it just checks already-issued passes at the door.","more":"## Gotcha: an SPA with a confidential secret\n\nThe temptation to 'make the SPA confidential to be safer' is an anti-pattern. A secret in a JS bundle is visible to anyone via DevTools → Sources and is extracted in seconds. Anyone who has the secret can impersonate your client. For a browser/mobile app **the secret provides no protection — PKCE does** (a dynamic `code_verifier` that never leaves the client).\n\nCorrect SPA client config (Keycloak ≥ 22, new model):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"clientId\": \"spa-frontend\",\n  \"publicClient\": true,\n  \"standardFlowEnabled\": true,\n  \"implicitFlowEnabled\": false,\n  \"attributes\": {\n    \"pkce.code.challenge.method\": \"S256\"\n  },\n  \"redirectUris\": [\"https://app.example.kz/*\"],\n  \"webOrigins\": [\"+\"]\n}\n```\n\nBackend (confidential) that exchanges the code:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"clientId\": \"orders-bff\",\n  \"publicClient\": false,\n  \"clientAuthenticatorType\": \"client-secret\",\n  \"standardFlowEnabled\": true\n}\n```\n\nBearer-only resource server (only validates tokens, no login):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"clientId\": \"orders-api\",\n  \"publicClient\": false,\n  \"standardFlowEnabled\": false,\n  \"directAccessGrantsEnabled\": false,\n  \"serviceAccountsEnabled\": false\n}\n```\n\nRoles in the access token are read from `realm_access.roles` (realm roles) and `resource_access.<clientId>.roles` (client roles) — in Quarkus these map into `SecurityIdentity`/`@RolesAllowed`.","quizCorrect":"A public client holds no secret (SPA/mobile) and must use PKCE; confidential holds a secret and calls the token endpoint; bearer-only only validates incoming tokens","quizWrong":["public is safer than confidential because it never sends a secret over the wire — so it's the one to use for backends","bearer-only is a client that itself initiates login via authorization code flow, just without a secret","the difference is only in Keycloak's UI: at runtime all three types behave identically and any of them can exchange a code for tokensn hold a client secret safely too"],"picCap":"Client type = who can be trusted with a secret; a public client compensates for the missing secret with PKCE."},"kc-roles-to-authorities":{"q":"Roles from a Keycloak token don't work in @PreAuthorize(\"hasRole('ADMIN')\") — why, and how do you map them into Spring authorities?","a":"Keycloak puts roles in the realm_access.roles claim (realm roles) and resource_access.{clientId}.roles (client roles), NOT in scope. Spring's default JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter only reads scope/scp and prefixes them with SCOPE_, so it never finds the roles — hasRole fails. Fix: write a custom Converter that pulls realm_access.roles (and/or client roles) and wraps each in a SimpleGrantedAuthority with a ROLE_ prefix. The prefix is mandatory because hasRole itself prepends ROLE_, and the comparison is done on the full authority string.","d":"Spring Security 6 Resource Server: a JwtAuthenticationConverter bean with setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter overridden. hasRole('ADMIN') checks for the authority ROLE_ADMIN; hasAuthority('ADMIN') checks exactly ADMIN with no prefix. Realm roles are global to the whole realm, client roles are scoped to a specific clientId — pick the source deliberately.","note":"⚠️ Client roles appear in the access token ONLY if the client has a role mapper enabled and the role lands in audience/resource_access. By default, client A's roles won't show up in a token issued for client B — even if the user has them. If you expect a client role in resource_access and it's missing, check Client Scopes / mappers in Keycloak, not your code.","hook":"The roles sit in a separate drawer (realm_access.roles), but the Spring guard, out of habit, only looks in the scope drawer — and keeps saying \"not on the list.\" Until you point him at the right drawer and stick a ROLE_ badge on the name, he lets no one through.","more":"## Gotcha: double prefix ROLE_ROLE_\n\nThe most common mistake after the fix is sticking `ROLE_` in the converter AND also using `hasAuthority('ROLE_ADMIN')` somewhere else, or configuring the Keycloak role already as `ROLE_ADMIN`. Then `hasRole('ADMIN')` looks for `ROLE_ADMIN` while the authority holds `ROLE_ROLE_ADMIN` — 403 again. Rule: store roles in Keycloak WITHOUT a prefix (`ADMIN`); only the converter adds the prefix.\n\n```java\n@Bean\nJwtAuthenticationConverter jwtAuthConverter() {\n    var conv = new JwtAuthenticationConverter();\n    conv.setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter(jwt -> {\n        var realm = jwt.getClaimAsMap(\"realm_access\");\n        if (realm == null) return List.of();\n        var roles = (Collection<String>) realm.get(\"roles\");\n        return roles.stream()\n            .map(r -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority(\"ROLE_\" + r))\n            .collect(Collectors.toList());\n    });\n    return conv;\n}\n```\n\nClaim structure in the token:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"scope\": \"openid profile email\",\n  \"realm_access\": { \"roles\": [\"ADMIN\", \"USER\"] },\n  \"resource_access\": {\n    \"my-client\": { \"roles\": [\"order:read\"] }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor a client role the source is `resource_access.my-client.roles` (scoped to a specific `clientId`); for a global one it's `realm_access.roles`. If you need both scopes and roles, compose the default `JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter` with yours — don't throw the first one away.","quizCorrect":"The default converter reads scope/scp (SCOPE_ prefix), but Keycloak roles live in realm_access.roles — you need a custom converter that maps them into an authority with a ROLE_ prefix","quizWrong":["Keycloak doesn't put roles in the access token at all — you have to fetch them separately via the userinfo endpoint on the authorisation server, and then map them onto authorities in a filter of your own writing","hasRole doesn't work with JWT tokens at all; for them only hasAuthority is available","Roles are in scope, but Spring loses them because the token isn't signed with the RS256 algorithm"],"picCap":"The default converter reads scope (&rarr; SCOPE_) and ignores realm_access.roles; a custom converter maps roles to ROLE_*, and then hasRole finds a match."},"kc-token-types":{"q":"Access token vs ID token vs refresh token in Keycloak — which goes where, and what is an offline token?","a":"Access token is for AUTHORIZATION: you send it to the resource API via `Authorization: Bearer ...`; it carries roles (`realm_access.roles`, `resource_access.<client>.roles`), `scope`, `aud`; it is short-lived (minutes). ID token is the OIDC artifact for AUTHENTICATION: the CLIENT consumes it to learn who logged in (`sub`, `email`, `name`); you do NOT send it to the resource API. Refresh token is not for APIs at all: you exchange it at the token endpoint for a fresh access token. Offline token is a special long-lived refresh (requested with `scope=offline_access`) that survives logout/SSO-session close and lets background daemons/batches work without a live browser session.","d":"The three tokens solve three different problems, and the senior trap is conflating the authentication layer (who you are — ID token at the client) with the authorization layer (what you may do — access token in the API). The resource server validates the access token specifically (signature via JWKS, `iss`, `exp`, `aud`), and an ID token makes no sense in the API — wrong audience and wrong claim set. The offline token is a separate story about working with no user at the wheel.","note":"⚠️ The ID token is NOT meant for authorizing against a resource API: its `aud` = client_id, not the resource server, and it lacks `realm_access.roles`/`scope`. Sending it to the backend instead of the access token either gets rejected on audience or silently breaks the role model. Only the access token goes to the API.","hook":"It's like a bank access system: the photo ID badge tells security WHO you are (ID token — to the client), while the key card opens SPECIFIC doors and vaults (access token — to the API). Feed the photo badge into the vault's card reader and nothing opens — wrong object. And the offline token is the night cash-courier's service pass: it works even when the main entrance is closed and no staff are around.","more":"## Pitfall: ID token instead of access token in the API\n\nThe most common integration mistake — the frontend grabs `id_token` (it sits right there in the response) and sends it to the backend. The resource server's resolver will either reject it on `aud` (the ID token's audience = client_id, not the resource server), or accept it but find no `realm_access.roles` / `scope` — and role-based authorization quietly collapses.\n\nWhich token holds what (Keycloak):\n\n```json\n// access_token (to the API): authorization\n{\n  \"iss\": \"https://kc/realms/acme\",\n  \"aud\": [\"account\", \"orders-api\"],\n  \"scope\": \"openid profile orders:read\",\n  \"realm_access\":    { \"roles\": [\"trader\"] },\n  \"resource_access\": { \"orders-api\": { \"roles\": [\"read\"] } },\n  \"typ\": \"Bearer\", \"exp\": 1718000300\n}\n// id_token (at the client): authentication — NOT to the API\n{ \"iss\": \"...\", \"aud\": \"frontend-spa\",\n  \"sub\": \"u-123\", \"email\": \"a@b.kz\", \"name\": \"Asel\", \"typ\": \"ID\" }\n```\n\nRequesting an offline token (daemon/batch):\n\n```http\nPOST /realms/acme/protocol/openid-connect/token\ngrant_type=password&client_id=batch&scope=offline_access&...\n```\n\nKey point: the offline token is not tied to the SSO session's lifetime and does not expire on `SSO Session Idle`. Its lifetime is governed by `Offline Session Idle/Max` and by whether you exercise it periodically. Every offline token is visible in the Account Console and revocable there / via admin REST `logout`.\n\nSenior nuance: on the resource server validate `aud` explicitly. If you have several APIs behind one realm — configure an Audience mapper so the access token carries the right `aud`, otherwise one service will accept a token issued for another.","quizCorrect":"Access token is sent to the resource API (Authorization: Bearer); the ID token is consumed by the client to determine identity and is not sent to the API","quizWrong":["The ID token is sent to the API, while the access token stays only on the client to display the user's nameo endpoint on every single request that is made to it","The refresh token must be sent on every API request alongside the access token for a double check","The offline token is a short-lived access token for fast internal service-to-service calls"],"picCap":"Each token to its own endpoint; an ID token in the resource API is the red zone."},"kc-audience-issuer":{"q":"Why must a resource server validate iss and aud, not just the token signature?","a":"A valid signature only proves the token was issued by some IdP whose key you trust — not that it was meant for YOU. Validating iss ensures the token was issued by the expected realm (otherwise a token from another realm/IdP, legitimately signed for it, would pass). Validating aud blocks the confused-deputy problem: a token issued for client A must not be presented to service B. You also check exp/nbf with an allowance for clock skew.","d":"In multi-tenant / multi-realm setups, one JWKS cache or shared trust may cover several issuers — without an iss pin any of their signed tokens 'passes'. aud protects against forwarding an access token from one microservice to another (token replay across services). In Keycloak the access token's aud may by default NOT contain your client_id — you need an audience-mapper or an audience-scope, or strict aud validation will break the integration.","note":"⚠️ If a single shared JWKS/trust resolves signatures for multiple realms (or you accept multiple issuers), without a hard iss pin a token from a 'neighbouring' realm — legitimately signed for it — will pass validation. Signature valid, wrong realm. Always pin the expected issuer explicitly.","hook":"The seal on the envelope is genuine — but the letter is addressed to your neighbour. Signature = the mail is real; iss/aud = your address and your name are actually on the envelope, not someone else's.","more":"**Gotcha:** in Keycloak the access token's `aud` is often missing by default or equals `account`, not your client_id. Enable strict aud validation without configuring a mapper and every request 401s. Add an **Audience mapper** on the client (or a client scope with `Included Client Audience`).\n\nMinimal claims to validate:\n```json\n{\n  \"iss\": \"https://auth.example.com/realms/invest\",\n  \"aud\": [\"document-service\"],\n  \"azp\": \"frontend-spa\",\n  \"exp\": 1718800000,\n  \"nbf\": 1718796400,\n  \"realm_access\": { \"roles\": [\"doc-reader\"] }\n}\n```\n\nQuarkus does this declaratively — don't parse the token by hand:\n```properties\nquarkus.oidc.auth-server-url=https://auth.example.com/realms/invest\nquarkus.oidc.token.issuer=https://auth.example.com/realms/invest\nquarkus.oidc.token.audience=document-service\n```\nWithout `token.audience` Quarkus does NOT check aud (default). `azp` (authorized party) ≠ `aud`: azp is who requested the token, aud is who it is intended for.","quizCorrect":"A valid signature only proves issuer authenticity; pinning iss rejects tokens from another realm, and aud rejects tokens issued for a different client","quizWrong":["The signature already cryptographically embeds iss and aud, so checking them separately is redundant and adds nothing to security beyond what it already has","aud verifies the token's lifetime while iss verifies the user's roles in realm_access.roles","Checking exp alone is enough because Keycloak guarantees the correct audience itself"],"picCap":"Signature is valid, but iss/aud decide whether the token was meant for you"},"ops-red-use-golden":{"q":"RED, USE and the Four Golden Signals — three monitoring methods: what to measure and when?","a":"RED (for services/requests): Rate — RPS, Errors — error ratio, Duration — latency (p50/p95/p99). USE (for resources/hardware): Utilization — load, Saturation — queue/backlog, Errors — failures. Four Golden Signals (Google SRE): Latency, Traffic, Errors, Saturation. You measure RED at the service boundary, USE on resources (CPU/disk/pool).","d":"RED answers 'is the user hurting'; USE answers 'why' (which resource hit the wall). Combined: alert on the RED symptom, localize the cause with USE.","picCap":"RED — symptom at the service; USE — cause at the resource; Golden Signals — the generalization (Saturation leads)","hook":"RED is the patient's vitals (pulse, temperature, pain); USE is the organ labs (which one is overloaded). First you see WHAT hurts, then WHY.","quizCorrect":"RED = Rate/Errors/Duration for services; USE = Utilization/Saturation/Errors for resources","quizWrong":["RED and USE are two names for the same set of metrics","RED is measured on CPU/disk, USE on HTTP endpoints","The Four Golden Signals include only Latency and Errors and nothing else besides in the set"],"more":"**RED** (Tom Wilkie) — per service/endpoint: **R**ate (requests/s), **E**rrors (share of failures), **D**uration (latency distribution, p95/p99). Answers 'how does the service feel from the user's side'.\n\n**USE** (Brendan Gregg) — per resource (CPU, disk, network, connection pool): **U**tilization (% busy), **S**aturation (over-load, queue depth), **E**rrors. Answers 'which resource is the bottleneck'.\n\n**Four Golden Signals** (Google SRE book): Latency, Traffic, Errors, Saturation — a generalization of both.\n\n**Together:** alert on **RED/Golden** (symptom, user-facing) → diagnose with **USE** (which resource is saturated). Saturation is the leading signal: the queue grows BEFORE failure.","note":"⚠️ Alert on the SYMPTOM (RED/latency/errors — what the user feels), not the cause (CPU 90%). High CPU alone isn't an incident if latency is fine; but p99 rising with normal CPU is a real problem (waiting on I/O, a lock, GC). USE metrics are for diagnosis AFTER a symptom alert fires, not for alerting."},"db-tsdb-compression":{"q":"How do time-series DBs (Prometheus, VictoriaMetrics) compress metrics so tightly — delta-of-delta and Gorilla?","a":"A metric is (timestamp, float) at a regular step with a slowly changing value, so it compresses extremely well. Timestamps: delta-of-delta — store not the time but the difference of differences (with an even step it's almost always 0 → a couple of bits). Values: Gorilla compression (Facebook) — XOR neighboring float64s; close values share many bits, only the changing chunk is stored. Result — often ~1-2 bytes per point instead of 16.","d":"This works precisely on regular series; chaotic values (high entropy) compress poorly.","picCap":"Timestamps — difference of differences (even step → 0); values — XOR of neighboring floats; 16 B → ~1-2 B per point","hook":"We don't write '10:00:00, 10:00:15, 10:00:30' — we write 'step 15, step 0, step 0'. And not the whole number, just how it differs from its neighbor. Regularity = mostly zeros = tiny size.","quizCorrect":"delta-of-delta for timestamps (difference of differences ≈ 0) + XOR compression of floats (Gorilla) for values","quizWrong":["Each point is gzipped independently","Values are stored as strings and deduplicated","Compression only works if you delete old points from the series first, which is why retention has to be short up"],"more":"**Timestamps — delta-of-delta:** at a fixed scrape interval the delta between points is constant (e.g. 15s), so the delta of deltas is 0 → stored in 1 bit. Small jitter → a few bits.\n\n**Values — Gorilla** (Facebook, 2015): the current float64 is XORed with the previous one. A slowly changing gauge/counter → nearly identical bits → the XOR leaves a handful of meaningful bits (leading/trailing zeros dropped). Result — ~1-2 bytes per point instead of 16 (8 timestamp + 8 value).\n\nThat's why a TSDB holds billions of points cheaply. VictoriaMetrics adds per-block type-aware compression on top.","note":"⚠️ Practical takeaway: do NOT store high-entropy values in metrics (random ids, exact amounts to the cent, timestamps-as-value) — they kill Gorilla, and a point balloons from ~1-2 bytes to 8+. And a regular scrape interval is critical: ragged timestamps break delta-of-delta. Chaos in values/step = disk explosion even with few points."},"db-tsdb-downsampling":{"q":"Downsampling and retention tiers in metrics — why thin out history?","a":"Fresh data is needed at full resolution (per-second — for incident forensics), old data is not: a point every 5 minutes/hour is enough. Downsampling = aggregate old points into coarse ones (min/max/avg/sum over a window); retention tiers = different retention for different resolutions (raw 15d → 5m rollup 90d → 1h rollup 2 years). A year-long chart is built from ready rollups, not from billions of raw points.","d":"Without downsampling a 'last year' query drags raw data → slow and expensive; with it, it reads a coarse tier.","picCap":"Fresh — raw and dense; old — thinned into 5m/1h rollups; a long query reads the coarse tier","hook":"Yesterday's seconds matter (incident review), last year's don't: hourly points are enough. We squeeze the past into a coarse grid — history stays light, trends stay intact.","quizCorrect":"Old points are aggregated to a coarse resolution (rollup) + kept for different durations (tiers): raw short, rollup long","quizWrong":["Downsampling deletes all old data without aggregating, which is why the history is always lost after the retention window","Downsampling increases the resolution of old points","Retention tiers are about replication, not resolution"],"more":"**Idea:** fresh data (last days) at full resolution for incident forensics; older data thinned. Downsampling computes windowed aggregates (min/max/avg/count/sum) and stores them instead of raw.\n\n**Retention tiers:** raw 15d → 5m rollup 90d → 1h rollup 2 years. The query planner picks the coarsest tier covering the range: 'last year' reads 1h points (thousands), not raw (billions). VictoriaMetrics, Thanos, M3 do this.\n\n**Trade-off:** you lose spike detail in history — a one-second spike dissolves into an hourly avg.","note":"⚠️ Downsampling loses spikes: a one-second latency spike dissolves into an hourly average. So store not just avg in the rollup but max/min/p99 too — otherwise you'll never see an old incident after the fact. And compute quantiles correctly: you can't average p99 over time (an average of percentiles ≠ a percentile) — for histograms aggregate the buckets, not the finished quantile."},"ops-recording-alerting-rules":{"q":"Recording rules vs alerting rules in Prometheus — why each, and how do they differ?","a":"A recording rule periodically evaluates an expensive PromQL expression and STORES the result as a new metric; the dashboard then reads the ready value instead of recomputing over raw data on every refresh. An alerting rule evaluates a condition (e.g. error rate > 5% for 5 minutes) and, when it fires, sends an alert to Alertmanager. The first is about query speed, the second about notifications.","d":"Recording rules materialize aggregates (like a materialized view for metrics); alerting rules are threshold triggers.","picCap":"Recording — computes the expensive thing and stores it (dashboard reads ready data); alerting — threshold → Alertmanager","hook":"Recording — precompute and write it down (so the dashboard doesn't lag); alerting — watch a threshold and wake the on-call. One for speed, one for the alarm.","quizCorrect":"A recording rule stores the result of an expensive expression as a metric; an alerting rule sends an alert on a condition","quizWrong":["Both send notifications, they only differ by interval","A recording rule deletes old metrics on a schedule","An alerting rule speeds up dashboards by caching queries and has nothing to do with firing notifications at all to anybody"],"more":"**Recording rule** runs on `evaluation_interval` and writes the result as a new series: `record: job:http_errors:rate5m`. Dashboards and other rules reuse the ready metric — cheap and consistent (everyone computes it the same way).\n\n**Alerting rule:** `alert: HighErrorRate, expr: ..., for: 5m` — `for` requires the condition to hold for N minutes before firing (anti-flapping). The alert goes to **Alertmanager**, which deduplicates, groups and routes notifications.\n\n**Best practice:** alert on a recording-rule metric (error-budget burn rate) — stable and fast.","note":"⚠️ `for:` in an alerting rule is anti-flapping: the condition must hold for N minutes, otherwise a one-off spike won't needlessly wake the on-call. And alert on burn rate (a recording rule over the error budget), not raw error rate: multi-window burn rate (a fast + a slow window) catches both sharp outages and slow degradation without spamming. A raw 'errors>1%' threshold either stays silent or screams."},"sd-metrics-tsdb-scale":{"q":"How do you scale a metrics store (VictoriaMetrics/Thanos): splitting ingest/store/query and remote_write?","a":"A single Prometheus hits disk/memory limits as the number of series grows. The fix — split the roles: the ingester (vminsert) shards incoming series by hash across storage nodes; storage (vmstorage) holds and merges parts, compresses; query (vmselect) reads shards in parallel and deduplicates. Prometheus ships data out via remote_write into such a clustered long-term store. Write, storage and read then scale independently.","d":"Role separation = independent scaling: a write peak → add insert nodes; heavy queries → add select nodes.","picCap":"insert shards by series → storage×N holds/compresses → select reads all shards and deduplicates","hook":"A single Prometheus is one cashier for the whole supermarket. Split it: receivers spread goods across warehouses (shards), warehouses store and compress, readers assemble the order from all warehouses in parallel. Each role scales on its own.","quizCorrect":"Roles are split: insert shards by series hash, storage holds/compresses, select reads shards and deduplicates; Prometheus feeds via remote_write","quizWrong":["One storage node holds all metrics, insert/select are just caches in front of a single shared relational table underneath it that stores every series","remote_write replicates the whole Prometheus to a second server","Sharding is by time, not by series"],"more":"A single Prometheus doesn't scale horizontally for storage; `remote_write` pushes samples into a scalable backend.\n\n**VictoriaMetrics cluster:**\n- **vminsert** (stateless) — hashes each time series → routes it to the right vmstorage shard.\n- **vmstorage** (stateful) — LSM-like parts + background merges + Gorilla compression + retention.\n- **vmselect** (stateless) — fan-out across shards, merge + dedup of replicas.\n\nThanos does similar (sidecar + store gateway + querier over object storage). Sharding by **series** (not time) keeps a series local for fast reads; a replication factor gives HA.","note":"⚠️ Sharding is by series (by label hash), NOT by time — otherwise one 'hot' moment in time hammers a single node, and reading a series over a period fans out to all nodes. And dedup is mandatory: two HA Prometheus replicas send near-identical points offset in time; vmselect collapses them, otherwise rate() doubles. remote_write is async with a buffer — if the backend is down Prometheus accumulates in the WAL, but an overflow = loss of fresh points."},"tr-finally-return":{"q":"What does a method return if try has return 1 and finally has return 2? And where does the exception from try go?","a":"It returns 2. A return (or even a thrown exception) in finally OVERRIDES the return/exception from try — the try result is simply lost. So return and throw inside finally are almost always a bug: they silently swallow both the normal answer and the error.","d":"finally always runs (except System.exit / killing the JVM), so its return has the last word.","picCap":"finally has the last word: its return/throw overrides everything from try","hook":"finally is like the last signature on a document: whatever's written above, the last one wins. Put a return there and you erase both the answer and the complaint (exception) from try.","quizCorrect":"It returns 2 — a return/throw in finally overrides the result and exception from try","quizWrong":["It returns 1 — try runs first and its return wins","Compile error — two returns in one method","It returns 1, and the try exception still propagates to the caller unchanged afterwards"],"more":"`finally` always runs after `try`/`catch` — for releasing resources. But if it itself does `return` or throws, that becomes the **final result of the method**: a pending return from try is discarded, a pending exception is **swallowed**.\n\nSo `finally` must not contain `return`/`throw`. Prefer releasing resources with **try-with-resources**, not by hand in finally.","note":"⚠️ A classic code-review trap: `return`/`throw` in `finally` silently eats the exception from `try` — the sneakiest way to hide a bug (the error 'vanishes' without a trace). Linters (SpotBugs) flag it. In `finally` — resource cleanup only, no exits from the method."},"tr-list-remove-overload":{"q":"For List<Integer>, does list.remove(1) remove element 1 or the element at index 1?","a":"By INDEX 1 — not the value! There are two overloads: remove(int index) and remove(Object o). The literal 1 is an int, so remove-by-index is called. To remove by value, pass an object: remove(Integer.valueOf(1)) or remove((Integer)1).","d":"For List<String> there's no problem — only remove(Object) exists. The trap is specific to List<Integer>.","picCap":"remove(int) — index; remove(Object) — value. The compiler picks by argument type","hook":"Two doors with the same 'remove' sign: a bare number goes through 'by seat number', a wrapped one through 'by content'. Easy to take the wrong door.","quizCorrect":"remove(1) calls remove(int index) → removes the element at index 1, not the value 1","quizWrong":["It removes the value 1, since the list is of Integer and not of primitive int at all","Compile error — ambiguous overload","It removes both index 1 and value 1"],"more":"`List` has two `remove`s: `remove(int index)` and `remove(Object o)`. For `remove(1)` the compiler sees the primitive `int` and picks the **by-index overload** — no autoboxing to `Integer` happens (an exact type match wins).\n\nTo remove by value from `List<Integer>`: `remove(Integer.valueOf(1))` or `remove((Integer)1)`. With `List<String>` there's no ambiguity.","note":"⚠️ Sneaky because on `List<String>` the code works 'by value', and after a refactor to `List<Integer>` the same `remove(x)` line suddenly removes by index → a silent bug + possible IndexOutOfBounds. Always wrap explicitly: `remove(Integer.valueOf(x))`."},"tr-incr-atomic":{"q":"Why does count++ from several threads lose increments, even though it's 'one line'?","a":"count++ is THREE operations: read, add, write (read-modify-write). Another thread slips in between them, both read the same old value and overwrite each other's increment. You need AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet() or synchronized — 'one line' of code ≠ atomicity.","d":"volatile doesn't help here: it gives visibility, not atomicity of read-modify-write.","picCap":"read-modify-write isn't atomic: two threads read 5, both write 6 — one increment lost","hook":"Two people topping up a shared counter: both peeked '5', both wrote '6'. One top-up vanished. The 'peek-add-write' must be one indivisible move.","quizCorrect":"count++ is a 3-step read-modify-write; threads interleave between the steps and overwrite increments","quizWrong":["count++ is atomic, losses are impossible even under heavy contention between threads between the threads","Making count volatile fully solves it","The problem only occurs on 32-bit JVMs"],"more":"`count++` isn't atomic: **read → modify → write**. The scheduler can switch threads between these steps. Two threads read the same value, increment locally, and write — the result loses some increments (lost update).\n\n`volatile` gives **visibility** (a read is always fresh) but not atomicity of a compound operation. Fixes: `AtomicInteger`/`LongAdder` (via CAS), or `synchronized`/`Lock` around the whole operation.","note":"⚠️ Trap: `volatile int count` looks 'thread-safe', but `count++` still loses data — volatile is NOT about read-modify-write atomicity. For counters use `AtomicInteger`/`LongAdder`; `volatile` is only for flags (one write, many reads)."},"tr-hashmap-mt":{"q":"What happens if you write to a plain HashMap from several threads?","a":"Data corruption: lost writes, 'sudden' nulls, and on old JVMs (before 8) a resize could spin into an infinite loop in a bucket — the thread hung at 100% CPU. HashMap is NOT thread-safe. For concurrent access use ConcurrentHashMap (not Collections.synchronizedMap, which locks the whole thing).","d":"ConcurrentHashMap locks per segment/bin — parallel writes to different buckets don't block each other.","picCap":"HashMap collapses under concurrency; ConcurrentHashMap locks per bin, not the whole map","hook":"A shared reference notebook with no queue rules: two people write at once — pages get muddled, and on a 'move' (resize) entries loop. ConcurrentHashMap is a notebook with locks on separate sections.","quizCorrect":"HashMap isn't thread-safe → lost data/nulls, and before Java 8 an infinite loop on resize; use ConcurrentHashMap","quizWrong":["HashMap is thread-safe by default","Declaring the map volatile is enough","synchronizedMap is faster than ConcurrentHashMap under load because it locks the whole map only once per call each"],"more":"`HashMap` isn't synchronized. Parallel `put`s during a **resize** (rehashing) on Java 7 could loop a bucket's linked list → the thread spins at 100% CPU. Java 8+ has no infinite loop, but lost writes and a torn structure remain.\n\n`Collections.synchronizedMap` wraps every method in one lock (slow). `ConcurrentHashMap` locks **per bin** (CAS + narrow locks) — parallel writes to different buckets don't contend.","note":"⚠️ Sneaky: the bug reproduces rarely and 'floats' (depends on timing and resize) — in prod under load, silent in tests. Rule: any Map shared between threads — ConcurrentHashMap only. And don't confuse its atomic methods (`compute`, `merge`) with the non-atomic `get`+`put` combo."},"tr-float-money":{"q":"Why is 0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3, and what should you use for money?","a":"double/float are binary floating point, and 0.1, 0.2, 0.3 aren't represented exactly. The sum is 0.30000000000000004. For money and exact amounts use BigDecimal (built from a STRING, not from a double!) or count in minimal units — cents — as long integers.","d":"new BigDecimal(0.1) is also inexact — use new BigDecimal(\"0.1\").","picCap":"double stores 0.1 inexactly; money — BigDecimal from a string or integer minimal units","hook":"Like writing 1/3 in decimals — 0.333… gets cut off. Likewise binary can't store 0.1 exactly — a 'tail' remains. For money you can't lose cents.","quizCorrect":"double stores 0.1/0.2 inexactly (binary fraction) → sum ≈0.3000..04; for money use BigDecimal from a string or integer cents","quizWrong":["It's a bug of a specific JVM, on another it'll be 0.3 with no rounding error at all in the result on any correctly configured runtime","== for double is reliable if you round both to int","new BigDecimal(0.1) fully solves it"],"more":"`double`/`float` are IEEE-754 with a binary mantissa. Numbers like 0.1 are an infinite repeating fraction in binary, stored rounded → error accumulates.\n\nConsequences: don't compare `double` with `==` (use an epsilon); for money/exact decimals use `BigDecimal`, building it **from a string** (`new BigDecimal(\"0.1\")`), since `new BigDecimal(0.1)` inherits the same double inexactness. Alternative — store the amount in **cents** as a `long`.","note":"⚠️ A double trap: (1) people 'fix' money with `double` and lose cents in reports; (2) even switching to BigDecimal, they write `new BigDecimal(0.1)` — carrying the same error. Only `new BigDecimal(\"0.1\")` or `BigDecimal.valueOf(0.1)`. And set `scale`+`RoundingMode` explicitly."},"tr-fixed-pool-oom":{"q":"What's the trap with Executors.newFixedThreadPool during a task spike?","a":"It has an UNBOUNDED queue (a LinkedBlockingQueue with no limit). If tasks arrive faster than they're processed, the queue grows without bound → OutOfMemoryError, not rejection. The right way — build a ThreadPoolExecutor manually with a BOUNDED queue and a deliberate rejection policy (RejectedExecutionHandler).","d":"newCachedThreadPool is the opposite — it spawns threads without limit. Both factory methods are dangerous in prod.","picCap":"An unbounded queue piles up tasks until OOM; a bounded one honestly rejects the excess","hook":"An endless kitchen order rail: guests pour in, cooks can't keep up — the rail grows to the ceiling and crushes the kitchen. Better a short rail: full → politely refuse, don't collapse.","quizCorrect":"newFixedThreadPool has an unbounded queue → under overload it grows to OutOfMemoryError","quizWrong":["It creates a new thread per task and fails on threads rather than on the queue of the pool","It drops excess tasks by default","The problem only occurs with a pool of size 1"],"more":"`Executors.newFixedThreadPool(n)` is internally a `ThreadPoolExecutor` with an **unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue`**. Under overload, tasks pile up in the queue without limit → heap growth → `OutOfMemoryError`. `newCachedThreadPool` is the other extreme: an unbounded number of threads.\n\nProd rule: construct `ThreadPoolExecutor` explicitly — set `corePoolSize`/`maxPoolSize`, a **bounded** `ArrayBlockingQueue`, and a `RejectedExecutionHandler` (e.g. `CallerRunsPolicy` — backpressure on the caller).","note":"⚠️ Sneaky because on tests and low load everything is perfect, and degradation arrives at the peak — the queue quietly piles up until OOM strikes. Never take `Executors.*` factories to prod unquestioned; set the queue limit and rejection policy deliberately."},"tr-static-hiding":{"q":"Is a static method overridden in a subclass? What gets called via a parent reference?","a":"It is NOT overridden — static methods aren't polymorphic, they're 'hidden' (method hiding). Which one runs is decided at compile time by the REFERENCE TYPE, not the actual object. Parent p = new Child(); p.stat() calls Parent's method, not Child's.","d":"For instance methods it'd be the opposite — dynamic dispatch by the object.","picCap":"static — hiding (by reference type); instance — override (by the actual object)","hook":"For a normal method you ask 'who are you really?' (the object). For a static one — 'how are you labeled on paper?' (the reference type). Different questions — different answers.","quizCorrect":"static isn't overridden (hiding); the call resolves by reference type at compile time → Parent","quizWrong":["Child is called — normal polymorphism works","Compile error — you can't declare a same-named static method in a subclass at all of the parent class","Child is called, because the object is really Child"],"more":"Polymorphism (dynamic dispatch) works for **instance** methods: the call goes by the object's real class. `static` methods aren't tied to an instance — they're **hiding**, not overriding: the compiler picks the method by the **declared type** of the reference (or the class) at compile time.\n\nSo `@Override` on a static won't compile. Call statics by class name (`Parent.who()`), not through a reference — that makes the intent explicit.","note":"⚠️ The trap is worsened by `p.who()` through a reference compiling and 'looking polymorphic', while silently taking the version by reference type. Never call a static through an instance variable; always `ClassName.method()`. And don't expect overriding from static — there is none."},"tr-ternary-promotion":{"q":"What does System.out.println(true ? Integer.valueOf(1) : Double.valueOf(2)) print?","a":"1.0, not 1! The ternary operator promotes both branches to a COMMON numeric type. Integer and Double → both to double, so 1 becomes 1.0. And if one branch is a wrapper holding null and auto-unboxing happens, the ternary can suddenly throw NullPointerException.","d":"A classic: cond ? intValue : (Integer)null — when false it tries to unbox null → NPE.","picCap":"The ternary reduces branches to a common numeric type (Integer+Double→double); a null branch → NPE on unboxing","hook":"The ternary requires both branches to 'speak one language': mix Integer and Double and it converts both to double (1→1.0). And an empty (null) branch crashes when converting to a primitive.","quizCorrect":"Both branches are promoted to a common type double → it prints 1.0; and a null branch with unboxing gives NPE","quizWrong":["It prints 1 — the Integer branch is taken as-is with no unboxing happening anywhere in the expression at all here","Compile error — branches of different types","It prints 2.0 — the second branch is taken"],"more":"The ternary `?:` computes a **single result type** from both branches (binary numeric promotion). If one branch is `Integer` and the other `Double`, both are promoted to `double`, and the integer branch prints as `1.0`.\n\nA separate trap is **auto-unboxing null**: `flag ? 0 : boxedNull` has type `int`, so `boxedNull` tries to unbox to a primitive → `NullPointerException`, though no explicit `.intValue()` is visible.","note":"⚠️ A double trap: (1) the silent `1 → 1.0` breaks formatting/comparison; (2) `cond ? primitive : boxedNullable` gives an NPE 'out of nowhere'. Don't mix a wrapper and a primitive/different numeric types in the ternary branches — convert explicitly or use if/else."},"tr-integer-cache":{"q":"Why is Integer a=127,b=127; a==b true, but false at 128?","a":"== compares REFERENCES, not values. Autoboxing goes through Integer.valueOf(), which caches objects for -128..127. So 127 and 127 are the same object (==true), while 128 makes two new objects (==false). Compare values only with .equals() or use primitive int.","d":"The cache upper bound can be raised with -XX:AutoBoxCacheMax, but never rely on it.","picCap":"127 — shared cached object (==true); 128 — two new objects (==false)","hook":"127 — two tickets for one seat in a cached row (the same object); 128 — two fresh tickets in different seats. == looks at the 'seat', not the number.","quizCorrect":"== compares references; Integer caches -128..127 → 127==127, but 128 makes new objects","quizWrong":["You can't compare Integer with == — it's a compile error no matter how large the numbers involved happen to be","Both 127 and 128 give true, since the values are equal","== on Integer compares values, not references"],"more":"Autoboxing `int→Integer` compiles to `Integer.valueOf(int)`, which for the range **-128..127** returns an object from the internal `IntegerCache`. So two `127`s are physically one object → `==` is true. Outside the range a new object is made each time → `==` is false.\n\n`new Integer(128)` (deprecated) is always a new object, even for 127. `Byte/Short/Long` cache -128..127 too, `Character` 0..127, `Boolean`.\n\nTakeaway: compare boxed wrappers with `.equals()`; for `==` use primitives.","note":"⚠️ The trap: the bug is NOT visible in tests with small numbers (passes because of the cache) and fires in prod on large ids/amounts. Rule: boxed wrappers — `.equals()` only; better yet keep a primitive `int/long` if you don't need null."},"tr-new-string":{"q":"How many objects does String s = new String(\"hello\") create, and why is new String almost always wrong?","a":"Potentially TWO: the literal \"hello\" goes into the string pool (if not already there), and new String forces an extra separate copy on the heap. So new String duplicates what's already in the pool. Just write String s = \"hello\" — then there's one object, from the pool, reused.","d":"Because of the separate copy, new String(\"x\") != \"x\" by ==, though .equals() is true.","picCap":"A literal is one pooled object; new String is a redundant heap copy","hook":"A literal is like borrowing a book from a shared library (one for all). new String makes your own photocopy of the same text: same content, but a different physical copy.","quizCorrect":"The literal goes to the pool (reused); new String makes a redundant separate heap copy","quizWrong":["new String is more efficient — it saves pool memory and both of them end up in the string pool automatically","Both ways give the same pooled object","new String is always needed, literals are obsolete"],"more":"String literals are **interned** — stored in the string pool and reused: two identical literals are one object. `new String(\"x\")` explicitly says 'create a new object', ignoring the pool → a redundant copy and `==` stops working.\n\nAlmost always the right thing is just a literal `String s = \"x\"`. `new String` is justified very rarely (e.g. forcing a substring copy). You can return a string to the pool with `.intern()`.","note":"⚠️ The trap: people habitually write `new String(...)` 'to make sure a string is created' — and get a redundant object + broken `==`. Always compare strings with `.equals()` (or `equalsIgnoreCase`), not `==` — otherwise the bug shows exactly where the string didn't come from a literal."},"tr-cme":{"q":"Why does removing an element inside a for-each throw ConcurrentModificationException?","a":"for-each uses an iterator that remembers the collection's modCount. Changing the list directly (list.remove) in the loop bumps modCount → on the next step the iterator sees the mismatch and throws ConcurrentModificationException (even in a single thread!). Remove via iterator.remove() or removeIf().","d":"The name is misleading: 'Concurrent', but usually it's a single-thread structural-modification-during-iteration error.","picCap":"A direct remove bumps modCount → the iterator catches the mismatch and throws CME","hook":"You're counting boxes down a list and tossing a box out of the row at the same time. The list shifted, the 'seal' counter doesn't match → stop. Remove through the walker itself, not around it.","quizCorrect":"for-each goes through an iterator with modCount; a direct list.remove bumps modCount → ConcurrentModificationException","quizWrong":["CME only happens with access from two threads","You need to declare the list volatile","for-each doesn't use an iterator, it's about indices, because the collection is only checked for structural changes when the loop finally finishes running"],"more":"`for-each` compiles to an `Iterator`. The `ArrayList` iterator holds an expected `modCount`; any **structural modification** (`add`/`remove` directly) increments the collection's `modCount`. On the next `next()` the iterator compares the counters, sees the mismatch, and throws `ConcurrentModificationException` — this is **fail-fast** behavior.\n\nCorrect: `list.removeIf(pred)`, `Iterator.remove()`, or collect items to remove into a separate list and `removeAll` after the loop.","note":"⚠️ The name `ConcurrentModificationException` misleads: it's usually a **single-thread** bug — modification during iteration. Conversely, absence of CME doesn't guarantee thread safety (fail-fast is best-effort, not a contract). Remove only via `iterator.remove()`/`removeIf()`."},"tr-dcl-volatile":{"q":"Why is classic double-checked locking for a singleton broken without volatile?","a":"Because of reordering: instance = new Foo() is allocate memory, run the constructor, and assign the reference, and the JIT/CPU may REORDER them. Another thread sees a non-null reference to a not-yet-built object and uses it. The field must be volatile — it forbids such reordering and gives visibility.","d":"Simpler and safer — a singleton via a static holder (lazy class init) or an enum.","picCap":"Without volatile the reference assignment can outrun the constructor → another thread grabs a 'half-built' object","hook":"You hung the 'open' sign before arranging the furniture: a visitor walks into an unfinished store. volatile guarantees 'finish building first, then hang the sign'.","quizCorrect":"The reference assignment may reorder before the constructor finishes → another thread sees a half-built object; volatile fixes it","quizWrong":["Double-checking alone is sufficient, volatile isn't needed","The problem is that synchronized is slow","You need synchronized on the whole method, volatile is pointless, so a second thread can never observe a partially constructed object under any circumstances at all"],"more":"`inst = new S()` isn't atomic: (1) allocate memory, (2) run the constructor, (3) write the reference to the field. The JMM allows reordering (2) and (3). Then a thread outside `synchronized` in the first check sees a **non-null reference to a not-yet-initialized** object.\n\n`volatile` introduces happens-before and forbids such reordering → the other thread sees either `null` or a fully built object. Even simpler: **initialization-on-demand holder** (`static class Holder { static final S I = new S(); }`) or an `enum` singleton — no manual DCL.","note":"⚠️ Sneaky: without `volatile` the code passes all tests (the bug shows only under a race on multicore with a specific reorder) and 'works for years' until it fires in prod. If you must DCL — the field is strictly `volatile`; but 99% of the time prefer the holder idiom or an enum."},"njava-core":{"q":"You replaced a DTO class with a record. What does the compiler write for you, and where will a record let you down?","a":"A record is an immutable data carrier: the compiler generates private final fields, the canonical constructor, accessors without get, and equals/hashCode/toString over all components.","d":"A record is implicitly final and already extends java.lang.Record: you can neither subclass it nor have it extend a class (interfaces are fine), you cannot add an ordinary field, and validation lives in the compact constructor.","note":"Immutability is shallow: if a component is a List or an array, callers can freely mutate the contents (copy it in the compact constructor). And you cannot make a JPA entity out of a record — that needs a no-arg constructor and mutability.","hook":"A passport: the data is written once when it is issued and never rewritten, and two passports with identical contents are treated as the same person.","more":"The compiler marks the class with a `Record` attribute listing the components and extends `java.lang.Record` — `Class.isRecord()` checks exactly this, and `getRecordComponents()` returns the names and types. Component names are always present in the class file — unlike ordinary constructor parameter names, which collapse to `arg0` without `-parameters`.\n\n`equals`, `hashCode` and `toString` are not expanded in the bytecode: there is a single `invokedynamic` to `java.lang.runtime.ObjectMethods.bootstrap` that assembles the implementation from the accessors. Hence something non-obvious: `hashCode` is unspecified and may change between JDK builds — never build sharding or a cache key on it. And `double` and `float` are compared as `Double.compare(...) == 0`, so `NaN` equals `NaN` while `0.0` does not equal `-0.0` — exactly the opposite of `==`. Otherwise the copy contract would break: a record rebuilt from its own accessors must be equal to the original.\n\nImmutability applies to references only: `record Order(String id, List<Item> items)` hands out the very same list that was passed to the constructor, the caller mutates it — and your \"immutable\" object changes. Copy in the compact constructor: `items = List.copyOf(items)`.\n\nProduction detail: Jackson understands records from jackson-databind 2.12 onward — components are read from the `Record` attribute, and `-parameters` is not needed for them (in maven-compiler-plugin `<parameters>` defaults to `false`, and an ordinary DTO does require it). On 2.11 the first incoming JSON gives `InvalidDefinitionException: Cannot construct instance of ... (no Creators, like default constructor, exist)`, and you notice it not in the logs but as a spike of `http_server_requests_seconds_count{status=\"400\"}` on a single endpoint.\n\nWhere it breaks: someone tries to make a record a JPA entity. Hibernate instantiates objects through a no-arg constructor and proxies non-final classes — you get `org.hibernate.InstantiationException: No default constructor for entity`. The fix is to separate the roles: the entity is an ordinary class, the record is a DTO and a projection — `select new com.x.OrderDto(o.id, o.sum)` in JPQL.","quizCorrect":"The compiler writes final fields, the canonical constructor, accessors and equals/hashCode/toString over the components","quizWrong":["It is pure syntax sugar: equals and hashCode stay reference-based, so you still have to write both of them by hand exactly as you did for an ordinary DTO class","A record can extend an abstract class so that fields can be reused","Record fields are mutable, the setters are simply generated package-private"],"picCap":"record Trade: the compiler generates 4 members; extends and your own field — no, an interface — yes"},"njava-boxing":{"q":"int count = map.get(\"key\"); — an NPE landed on the line where you \"just assigned a value\". Where did it come from?","a":"map.get returned null, and assigning to a primitive makes the compiler insert a hidden null.intValue() — hence the NPE, even though no call is visible in the code.","d":"The same trap appears in conditions (an if over a Boolean field), in arithmetic over Integer, and in a ternary that unboxes both branches.","note":"Fix it with getOrDefault(k, 0) or an explicit check. And do not store Long/Boolean where a value is always present: a primitive will not let itself be fooled.","hook":"You pulled emptiness out of the drawer and put it on the scales — scales cannot weigh \"nothing\".","more":"At the point of assignment javac emits `invokevirtual java/lang/Integer.intValue()`. There is no such call in the source, yet the stack trace points at exactly that line — hence the feeling of an \"NPE out of nowhere\". Since JDK 15 helpful messages are on by default (`-XX:+ShowCodeDetailsInExceptionMessages`), and the text reads directly: `Cannot invoke \"java.lang.Integer.intValue()\" because the return value of \"java.util.Map.get(Object)\" is null`.\n\nThe ternary breaks for a different reason. JLS 15.25: if one branch is `int` and the other is `Integer`, the type of the whole expression is promoted to `int` by binary numeric promotion, so the chosen branch is unboxed before the assignment even happens — even when the result goes back into an `Integer`. The classic: `Integer x = flag ? 1 : mayBeNull` fails when `flag == false`, although not a single cast is written in the code.\n\nThe flip side of autoboxing is the `Integer.valueOf` cache over the range −128..127; the upper bound is raised with `-XX:AutoBoxCacheMax`, 127 by default. Comparing two `Integer`s with `==` \"works\" on small numbers in tests and falls apart at 128 in production. In hot loops boxing also generates garbage: `Long sum` instead of `long sum` in an accumulator means millions of short-lived objects, visible in the allocation rate and `jvm_gc_pause_seconds`.\n\nWhere it breaks: an `is_active boolean NULL` column maps to a `Boolean` field, the code does `if (client.getIsActive())` for years and survives until it hits a row with `NULL` left by an old migration — an NPE on a single client, not reproducible locally. The fix has three parts: `NOT NULL DEFAULT false` in the schema, `Boolean.TRUE.equals(x)` instead of a direct dereference, and `map.getOrDefault(key, 0)` or `Objects.requireNonNullElse` wherever a value may be absent.","quizCorrect":"Auto-unboxing inserted intValue() on a null reference","quizWrong":["HashMap itself throws an NPE whenever the requested key is missing","int does not accept null, so the JVM throws a ClassCastException","Unboxing turns null into 0, so the NPE came from somewhere else"],"picCap":"int c = map.get(k): javac appended .intValue(), null → NPE on the assignment line"},"njava-core-2":{"q":"You stored the trade time in a LocalDateTime. The app moved into a UTC container — and the reports drifted by several hours. What should you have used?","a":"LocalDateTime is a label on a calendar without a zone; it does not identify a moment in time. A moment is an Instant (or OffsetDateTime/ZonedDateTime when the zone matters).","d":"The rule: store and transfer the moment in UTC (Instant / timestamptz), and convert to local time only for display, knowing the user's zone.","note":"LocalDateTime.now() silently takes the JVM zone, and that differs between your laptop and a container. And in ZonedDateTime plusDays(1) is not plusHours(24): on a DST switch day a day is not 24 hours long.","hook":"\"See you at 9:00\" without naming a city is not a time, it is a wish.","more":"Inside, `Instant` is an `epochSecond` plus `nano` — a single point on the time axis. `LocalDateTime` holds calendar fields and no offset at all, so no direct conversion between them exists: you need `toInstant(ZoneOffset)` or `atZone(ZoneId)`. The value itself carries no hint: from `2026-08-14T10:00` there is no way to tell whose 10:00 it is.\n\nThe JVM takes the default zone from the `user.timezone` system property, and when that is empty, from the OS — that is, from the container's `/etc/localtime`. A base image is almost always UTC, a laptop is `Asia/Almaty`, and `LocalDateTime.now()` in those two environments means different moments for identical text.\n\nIn the database the split is just as hard: `timestamp` is a label without a zone, `timestamptz` is a moment; PostgreSQL converts the input to UTC using the session `TimeZone` parameter and converts back on read. In Hibernate this is governed by `hibernate.jdbc.time_zone`, which has no default: without it the driver writes the `Timestamp` in the JVM zone, and the same field from two pods with different `TZ` lands in the database differently.\n\nProduction detail: a bare `new ObjectMapper()` with `JavaTimeModule` has `WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS` enabled, so an `Instant` reaches the client as `1786665600.000000000`, the frontend reads it as milliseconds and renders 1970; Quarkus (`quarkus.jackson.write-dates-as-timestamps`, `false` by default) and Spring Boot switch it off in favour of ISO-8601 — which is why a hand-rolled mapper in one place produces a format that differs from the rest of the API. On alpine without the `tzdata` package `ZoneId.of(\"Asia/Almaty\")` fails with `ZoneRulesException: Unknown time-zone ID: Asia/Almaty`, and on tzdata older than 2024a Almaty is still UTC+6 instead of UTC+5.\n\nWhere it breaks: slicing data into days. Boundaries computed in UTC cover 05:00 to 05:00 in Almaty, so trades between midnight and five in the morning never make it into the daily report — a discrepancy you only catch by reconciling trade counts against the source. The fix is to store `Instant`, compute explicit boundaries with `date.atStartOfDay(ZoneId.of(\"Asia/Almaty\")).toInstant()`, and inject a `Clock` through DI so tests can cover it.","quizCorrect":"LocalDateTime is a date-time without a zone; the moment is Instant/OffsetDateTime, stored in UTC","quizWrong":["It is enough to set TimeZone.setDefault at startup, the type does not need changing and nothing has to be rewritten","LocalDateTime is always in UTC, the formatter was to blame","You should go back to java.util.Date — it stores the zone inside"],"picCap":"10:00 with no zone: 05:00Z on the laptop, 10:00Z in a UTC container — the report drifted 5 h"},"njava-generics":{"q":"Object[] arr = new String[3]; arr[0] = 42; — compiles and fails at runtime. Why is this allowed for arrays, while List<Object> = List<String> will not even build?","a":"Arrays are covariant: String[] counts as a subtype of Object[], so the element type is checked on every write — hence ArrayStoreException at runtime. Generics are invariant, and the compiler catches the same mistake.","d":"Array covariance is a compromise from Java 1.0, when generics did not exist and general-purpose methods such as sort(Object[]) had to be written somehow.","note":"This is where the rule \"do not mix arrays and generics\" comes from: new T[] is forbidden precisely because an array's runtime check knows nothing about the erased type.","hook":"The box is labelled \"anything\", but it is really a shoebox — you learn about the kettle only when you try to put it in.","more":"An array is a reified type: its klass carries the element type, and the `aastore` instruction checks the value's type against it on every write. A miss gives `ArrayStoreException: java.lang.Integer`. Generics have no such information at runtime: after erasure `List<String>` and `List<Long>` are the same `java.util.List`, there is nothing to check against on write, so the check is moved into the compiler. Invariance is the price for a check that is free and fires earlier.\n\nWildcards compensate for it. `List<? extends Number>` gives covariance for reading and forbids writing (except `null`), because the concrete subtype inside is unknown; `List<? super Integer>` is the mirror image. That is PECS, and it does exactly what arrays do at runtime, only without the crashes.\n\nErasure leaks the other way too: `Collection.toArray(T[])` performs the same `aastore` internally, so `list.toArray(new String[0])` on a list holding a `Long` gives `ArrayStoreException`; generic varargs create a covariant array — hence the heap pollution warning and the `@SafeVarargs` annotation.\n\nProduction detail: raw types only produce the note `uses unchecked or unsafe operations; recompile with -Xlint:unchecked` — `-Xlint:unchecked` itself is off by default, the details are invisible in the build, and nobody reads such a note in CI. At runtime it surfaces as `ClassCastException: class java.lang.Long cannot be cast to class java.lang.String`, and on a line where no cast is written: javac inserted the `checkcast` when reading from the collection.\n\nWhere it breaks: a legacy DAO returns a raw `List`, `Long`s are put into it, and the caller declared `List<String>`. What fails is neither the DAO nor the moment of writing, but three layers up, in the first `for (String s : list)` — and a day goes into the investigation. The fix is `-Xlint:unchecked -Werror` on the module, parameterised DAO signatures, and `Collections.checkedList(...)` in tests so the exception arrives at the point of writing.","quizCorrect":"Arrays are covariant and check the element type at runtime → ArrayStoreException, while generics are invariant and caught by the compiler","quizWrong":["Because of type erasure an array carries no element type at runtime either, so neither the array nor the generic collection performs any check and both failures come from the cast inserted by javac","Arrays are invariant, which is why it fails, whereas List is in fact covariant","The compiler simply missed it — at runtime a cast from Object to String failed"],"picCap":"a[0]=42 compiles and fails with ArrayStoreException; List<Object>=List<String> does not build"},"njava-numbers":{"q":"What does Integer.MAX_VALUE + 1 return? And why is binary search written as low + (high - low) / 2?","a":"It returns Integer.MIN_VALUE: an int overflows silently, wrapping around, with no exception. That is why (low + high) goes negative at large indices and yields a negative index.","d":"Math.addExact/multiplyExact throw ArithmeticException instead of overflowing quietly; keep counters and money in long or BigDecimal.","note":"long ms = 24*60*60*1000*365; overflows BEFORE the assignment — the right-hand side is computed as ints, and 24L*… saves it. And Math.abs(Integer.MIN_VALUE) returns MIN_VALUE itself, that is, a negative number.","hook":"An odometer at 999999: drive one more kilometre and it shows zeros, not \"the car is broken\".","more":"`iadd` is addition modulo 2^32 in two's complement, and the JVM never looks at the overflow flag; JLS 15.18.2 states outright that integer overflow throws no exceptions. The same arithmetic gives `Math.abs(Integer.MIN_VALUE) == Integer.MIN_VALUE`: the absolute value of the minimum does not fit into the `int` range.\n\n`(low + high) / 2` breaks once the sum passes 2^31−1. With `low = 2^30` and `high = 2^31−1` the sum becomes −1073741825, the division gives −536870912, and the next `arr[mid]` throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`. This bug lived for nine years inside `Arrays.binarySearch` in the JDK itself and was fixed in Java 6: it now uses `(low + high) >>> 1` — an unsigned shift treats the overflowed bit as the high-order bit and restores the correct index. The `low + (high - low) / 2` variant does the same thing and reads more honestly.\n\n`Math.addExact` and `multiplyExact` are intrinsics: the same addition, but with a flag check and `ArithmeticException: integer overflow`. Neither `int` nor `double` is fit for money: `new BigDecimal(0.1)` gives `0.1000000000000000055511151231257827…`, so use only `BigDecimal.valueOf(...)` or the string constructor, with an explicit `scale` and `RoundingMode`.\n\nProduction detail: an `id integer` column with a sequence at 2147483647 returns `ERROR: nextval: reached maximum value of sequence \"orders_id_seq\" (2147483647)`, and inserts stop entirely, with no graceful degradation. The alert goes not on the exception but on the ratio of `last_value` to 2147483647 from `pg_sequences`, with a threshold of 0.8.\n\nWhere it breaks: a portfolio total in tiyn accumulated in an `int` — past 21.4 million tenge the accumulator goes negative, the `if (total > 0)` check lets a negative balance through, and the discrepancy is only found in the monthly report. The fix is `long` or `BigDecimal` in accumulators, `Math.addExact` where overflow is unacceptable, and `bigint` in the schema from day one.","quizCorrect":"It silently overflows to Integer.MIN_VALUE — which is why low+high can go negative","quizWrong":["An ArithmeticException is thrown, because the JVM checks the overflow flag after every integer addition and refuses to wrap silently","int is automatically widened to long, so overflow does not exist in Java","The result \"sticks\" at Integer.MAX_VALUE"],"picCap":"low=2^30, high=2^31−1: (low+high)/2 = −536870912 → a negative index"},"njava-strings":{"q":"\"a.b.c\".split(\".\") returned an empty array, and \"a,b,,\".split(\",\") lost the trailing empty values. What is going on?","a":"split takes a regex, not a literal: \".\" means \"any character\". And without a limit the method drops trailing empty strings — you need split(\",\", -1).","d":"For a literal separator use Pattern.quote(\".\") or escape it as \"\\\\.\"; the same pair exists as replaceAll (regex) versus replace (literal).","note":"In replaceAll the replacement string has special meaning too: $ and \\ are control characters there, so \"$5\" in a price turns into a group substitution or an exception.","hook":"You said \"cut along the dot\", and the knife heard \"cut everywhere\".","more":"`String.split(regex)` delegates to `Pattern.compile(regex).split(...)`, but there is a fast path: if the separator is a single character and is not one of `.$|()[{^?*+\\`, no regex is compiled at all. That is why `split(\",\")` is cheap while `split(\".\")` takes the full path and matches every character: only empty strings remain between matches, all of them trailing, all of them dropped — the result is an array of length 0, not `[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"]`.\n\nThe limit rule: `0` applies the pattern as many times as possible and removes trailing empty strings; a negative value applies it the same number of times but keeps trailing empties; a positive `n` applies it at most `n−1` times and puts the whole remainder into the last element, which makes `split(\":\", 2)` the normal way to cut a key from a value. The leading empty string follows a separate rule: since Java 8 a zero-width match at the start produces no leading `\"\"`, so `\"abc\".split(\"\")` gives `[a, b, c]`, whereas Java 7 gave `[\"\", a, b, c]`.\n\nProduction detail: `split(\"|\")` is an alternation of two empty branches, a zero-width match at every position, giving a character-by-character array; `split(\"*\")` fails immediately with `PatternSyntaxException: Dangling meta character '*' near index 0`. In `replaceAll` a `$` in the replacement string already gives `IllegalArgumentException: Illegal group reference` — cured by `Matcher.quoteReplacement`. Compiling a regex on every line of a file shows up as rising CPU and p99 in parsing; hoist it into a `private static final Pattern`.\n\nWhere it breaks: a CSV with empty trailing columns. `\"1,IIN,,\".split(\",\")` returns two elements, the parser reads `parts[3]` and gets `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 3 out of bounds for length 2` — and only on some records, which is how the bug calmly reaches production. The fix is `split(\",\", -1)`, and with quotes or newlines inside fields, moving to a real CSV reader.","quizCorrect":"split takes a regex (\".\" = any character), and trailing empties are dropped without limit = -1","quizWrong":["split cuts on a literal separator, and Java always discards every empty element it produces, which is why both results came back shorter than expected","The dot is a Java control character, writing split(\".\", -1) is enough","Only the dot behaves this way — it is the decimal separator"],"picCap":"split(\".\") → 0 elements; \"a,b,,\".split(\",\") → 2, and with limit −1 → 4"},"njava-arrays":{"q":"You compared two arrays with the same elements using equals — false. You printed an array to the log and got [Ljava.lang.String;@6d06d69c. Why?","a":"An array is an ordinary object that overrides neither equals nor toString: comparison is by reference, and what gets printed is the type plus a hash. Contents are handled by Arrays.equals and Arrays.toString.","d":"For nested arrays use Arrays.deepEquals/deepToString; an array's hashCode is identity-based too, so an array used as a HashMap key is never found.","note":"Arrays.asList(intArray) gives a List<int[]> of one element, not a list of numbers — for primitives you need Arrays.stream(...).boxed(). And in an SLF4J log an array is also printed as a hash unless you wrap it in Arrays.toString.","hook":"Two identical boxes of chocolates, but you are checking the inventory numbers on the boxes rather than the chocolates.","more":"The array class is synthesised by the JVM itself: it extends `Object` and adds only the `length` field, the `clone()` method and the `Cloneable`/`Serializable` markers. There is nobody there to override `equals`/`hashCode`/`toString`, so the `Object` defaults apply: `equals` is `this == obj`, `hashCode` is the identity hash, `toString` is `getClass().getName() + \"@\" + Integer.toHexString(hashCode())`.\n\n`[Ljava.lang.String;@6d06d69c` is the JVM type descriptor: each `[` adds a dimension, `L…;` is a reference type, primitives are encoded by a letter (`[I` is `int[]`, `[[B` is `byte[][]`). The identity hash is not an address: in HotSpot it is computed lazily on first access (the default `-XX:hashCode=5` is a thread-local xorshift) and stored in the mark word — stable for the object's lifetime and random between runs. `Arrays.equals` goes element by element (primitives via `==`, references via `equals`), `Arrays.hashCode` is a 31-based fold. `deepToString` is recursive and prints a self-reference as `[...]`, while the behaviour of `deepEquals` on an array containing itself is undefined per the javadoc.\n\nIn production this surfaces in three ways. `assertEquals(expected, actual)` on arrays compares references and fails as `expected: <[Ljava.lang.String;@6d06d69c> but was: <[Ljava.lang.String;@7a81197d>` — you need `assertArrayEquals`. `log.info(\"ids={}\", ids)` with a `String[]` binds to the `Object...` overload, the array is spread into the argument list, and only the first element reaches the log. Lombok's `@ToString` substitutes `Arrays.deepToString` for object arrays and `Arrays.toString` for primitive ones; a hand-written `toString` almost never does.\n\nWhere it breaks: `HashMap<byte[], Session>` — put and get with identical contents always miss, the cache hit ratio stays near zero, and the map grows until OOM (a rising sawtooth on `jvm_memory_used_bytes{area=\"heap\"}` and a linearly growing collection size). The fix is a wrapper key: `String`, `ByteBuffer.wrap`, `List<Byte>`, or a record with explicit `Arrays.hashCode`/`Arrays.equals`.","quizCorrect":"An array overrides neither equals nor toString — comparison is by reference; you need Arrays.equals/Arrays.toString","quizWrong":["equals on arrays compares only the length, which is why two arrays of the same size are reported as equal and the contents are never examined at all","Cast it to Object[] — then equals will compare the contents","Primitive arrays do not support equals, while object arrays work fine"],"picCap":"Same contents, different references: equals=false, Arrays.equals=true, println gives type@hash"},"njava-equals-hashcod":{"q":"The base class compares via instanceof, the subclass added a field and its own equals. Why does parent.equals(child) give true while child.equals(parent) gives false, and what does that do to collections?","a":"instanceof lets the subclass through, so the parent considers the objects equal while the subclass does not: the symmetry of the equals contract is broken.","d":"Hence \"the object is in the list, but remove/contains cannot find it\" — collections rely on symmetry and call equals in either order.","note":"getClass() instead of instanceof restores symmetry but breaks on a Hibernate proxy (which is a subclass). Lombok works around it with canEqual, but @EqualsAndHashCode defaults to callSuper = false — the parent's fields simply do not take part in the comparison.","hook":"The boss thinks you are colleagues, while the colleague thinks you are an intern — \"equal\" stopped working in both directions.","more":"The `equals` contract from the `Object` javadoc: reflexivity, symmetry, transitivity, consistency, and `x.equals(null) == false`. A check via `instanceof` in the base class breaks symmetry specifically — `o instanceof Point` accepts a `ColorPoint`, while `ColorPoint.equals` additionally demands a matching `color`.\n\nThe order of the call is decided by the collection, and it differs. `ArrayList.indexOf(o)` calls `o.equals(elementData[i])`, that is, equals on the argument; `HashMap.getNode` calls `key.equals(k)`, where `key` is the key being looked up and `k` is the one sitting in the bucket. Hence \"the object is in the list, but `contains` returns false\": the outcome depends on who is stored in the collection and who arrives as the query.\n\nThere are two clean solutions: `getClass() != o.getClass()` — symmetric, but it cuts against Liskov substitution and trips over proxies; or composition instead of inheritance. Lombok takes a third route and generates `canEqual(Object)`: on `ColorPoint` it returns false for a `Point`, so both sides answer false. From Java 16 the topic is closed by records — they are final, and no subclass can appear.\n\nProduction detail: `@EqualsAndHashCode` defaults to `callSuper = false`, the compiler emits `Generating equals/hashCode implementation but without a call to superclass, even though this class does not extend java.lang.Object`, and the parent's fields take no part in the comparison at all — fixed by the line `lombok.equalsAndHashCode.callSuper = call` in `lombok.config`. Under Hibernate the `getClass()` variant trips over the lazy proxy `Order$HibernateProxy$aB1x`: comparing a loaded entity with a proxy gives false, so you need `Hibernate.getClass(o)` or a business key.\n\nWhere it breaks: deduplicating payments in a `HashSet<Payment>` with a `Payment`/`CardPayment` hierarchy — equality works in one direction only, a duplicate passes the filter, `list.remove(payment)` returns false and removes nothing, and the retry is sent again. Visible as a divergence between the counter of sent messages and the number of unique `externalId`s. The fix is equality strictly on a business key inside a single final class or a record.","quizCorrect":"instanceof lets the subclass through: the parent says \"equal\", the subclass says \"no\" — symmetry is broken","quizWrong":["The problem is hashCode: the subclass computes a different one, and that is the reason equals returned false in the second direction","equals cannot be overridden in a subclass — the compiler forbids it","Calling super.equals is enough, symmetry then restores itself"],"picCap":"p.equals(cp)=true, cp.equals(p)=false — remove/contains find the object one way only"},"nsyst-ekspluataciya":{"q":"You need to rename a column and put NOT NULL on it, while the service is going out as a rolling update. How do you avoid taking production down?","a":"During the rollout the old and the new version of the code are alive at the same time, so the schema has to work with both. Do expand-contract: add the new column as nullable → write to both → backfill in batches → switch reads over → and only in the next release drop the old one and set NOT NULL.","d":"A rename is always \"add + copy + drop\", never an in-place RENAME: RENAME instantly breaks the old version of the code, which is still taking traffic.","note":"ALTER waits for an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock, and a queue builds up behind it that includes plain SELECTs — set a lock_timeout and retry. Run the backfill in batches: a single UPDATE over 50 million rows is a long lock and a bloated WAL.","hook":"You do not demolish the bridge before building the new one: you put up a second one, move the traffic across, and only then take the old one apart.","more":"A rolling update keeps both ReplicaSets in Endpoints (`maxSurge`/`maxUnavailable` are 25% each by default), while the migration runs exactly once — usually on the start of the first new pod (`quarkus.flyway.migrate-at-start`, `false` by default; in production it is more often moved into an initContainer or a Job). Between the moment of the migration and the eviction of the last old pod, both versions of the code see the schema, and all of the traffic falls into that window.\n\n`ALTER TABLE … RENAME COLUMN` is cheap in Postgres — it only edits the catalogue — and that is precisely what makes it treacherous: it does not slow anything down, it instantly breaks the old pods. The log shows `ERROR: column \"client_name\" does not exist`, which through Hibernate surfaces as `SQLGrammarException: could not extract ResultSet`. The flip side: `ADD COLUMN … NULL` without a default never rewrote the table, and since PG 11 `ADD COLUMN … DEFAULT` does not rewrite it either (the value lives in `pg_attribute.attmissingval`) — the expand step is practically free.\n\nThe expensive step is `SET NOT NULL`: ACCESS EXCLUSIVE plus a full scan. The safe path is `ADD CONSTRAINT … CHECK (full_name IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID`, then `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT` under SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE (reads and writes are not blocked), and only then `SET NOT NULL` — since PG 12 it sees the valid CHECK and skips the scan. `lock_timeout` defaults to `0`, meaning wait forever; migrations set `SET lock_timeout = '3s'` with a retry, otherwise a pending ACCESS EXCLUSIVE queues every SELECT behind it: in `pg_stat_activity` this is `wait_event_type = 'Lock'`, and in the application it is an exhausted pool (`quarkus.datasource.jdbc.max-size`, 20 by default). Backfills run in batches of 5–10 thousand rows: one large UPDATE holds a long transaction, bloats the WAL and prevents autovacuum from cleaning dead row versions.\n\nWhere it breaks: the release is rolled back, but the migration has already dropped the old column — the old image will not start at all, and rollback stops being a rescue. The fix is the rule \"contract only in the next release, after the previous one is declared stable\": every migration stays compatible one code version back.","quizCorrect":"Expand-contract: new nullable column, dual writes, backfill, switch reads, drop the old one next release","quizWrong":["Run ALTER TABLE RENAME COLUMN inside the deployment transaction — DDL is transactional in Postgres, so it will roll back together with everything else","Ship the migration first and the code second: the old code simply will not notice the new column","Easier to take five minutes of downtime at night — more reliable than any compatibility scheme"],"picCap":"RENAME kills a still-live v1 pod: column does not exist; expand-contract holds both versions"},"nweb-webhooks":{"q":"A webhook arrives from the payment provider saying \"payment succeeded\". What must the endpoint do beyond writing the body into the database?","a":"Verify the HMAC signature over the raw body (constant-time comparison) and the freshness of the timestamp — otherwise anyone can send \"payment succeeded\", and an old valid request can be replayed. And return 2xx quickly, handing the processing off to the background.","d":"The signature is computed over the RAW bytes: parse the JSON and serialise it back and the field order and whitespace shift, so the signature no longer matches.","note":"Delivery is at-least-once and ordering is not guaranteed: dedup by event id is mandatory, and the handler should look at state rather than assume that \"paid\" arriving means \"created\" already happened. When money is involved, verify the amount and status with a request to the sender's API instead of trusting the webhook body.","hook":"A courier at the door: first you look through the peephole and at the seal on the package, rather than signing straight away and carrying it inside.","more":"The signature is computed not over \"the body\" but over a specific string. Stripe sends `Stripe-Signature: t=1717…,v1=<hex>` and signs `t + \".\" + rawBody`; GitHub sends `X-Hub-Signature-256: sha256=<hex>` over the raw body. Any normalisation before verification — parsing the JSON and serialising it back, a shifted key order, collapsed whitespace, changed Unicode escaping, a proxy repacking gzip — changes the bytes and voids the HMAC. In JAX-RS/Quarkus that means accepting `byte[]` or `InputStream` (or a buffering `ContainerRequestFilter`), and in Spring a `ContentCachingRequestWrapper`, because after `@RequestBody` the stream has already been consumed.\n\nComparison must use `MessageDigest.isEqual(a, b)`, not `Arrays.equals` or `String.equals`: the latter return on the first difference, and the signature can be guessed byte by byte from the response time. Stripe's freshness window is a `tolerance` of 300 seconds by default; outside it you get `SignatureVerificationException: Timestamp outside the tolerance zone`, and on a hash mismatch `No signatures found matching the expected signature for payload`.\n\nDelivery is always at-least-once: Stripe retries with exponential backoff for up to three days, so the event identifier goes into an inbox table with a UNIQUE index and `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` — that is what idempotency means here, not \"check whether such an order already exists\". Observability: the endpoint's p99 and the share of non-2xx responses on the provider's dashboard; the delivery timeout is short and hard (GitHub allows 10 seconds), and after a run of failures the endpoint is simply disabled.\n\nWhere it breaks: the processing was made synchronous inside the request — under load the response misses the delivery timeout, the provider counts the attempt as failed and repeats the webhook, but the payment has already gone through and the client is credited twice. The fix is to store the raw body and signature in an inbox, return 2xx immediately, and run a separate worker that is idempotent on `event_id`.","quizCorrect":"Verify the HMAC over the raw body, cut off replays by timestamp, and return 2xx quickly","quizWrong":["Process the event synchronously and return 200 only once it has fully succeeded — that is more reliable for the sender and removes the need for any inbox table","It is enough to check that the sender's IP is in their subnet — the signature is redundant","Verify the signature after parsing the JSON — more convenient, same result"],"picCap":"HMAC over raw bytes matches; after re-serialising the JSON it does not; replays cut off by t"},"ninfr-k8s--limits--j":{"q":"You set limits.memory=1Gi on the pod and -Xmx512m on the app, yet the pod keeps restarting with exit code 137 (OOMKilled), and there is not one line about OutOfMemoryError in the logs. Where does the memory go?","a":"-Xmx bounds the heap only, and on top of it the JVM takes metaspace, code cache, thread stacks, GC structures and native/direct buffers — another few hundred megabytes. Once the container's total RSS passes the limit, the kernel kills the process through the cgroup: no stack trace, no heap dump, no chance to log anything.","d":"So set the heap as a share of the container limit rather than an absolute (-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75 with UseContainerSupport on), bound -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize, and alert on container_memory_working_set_bytes rather than on jvm_memory_used_bytes.","note":"The catch: OOMKilled is NOT a Java OOM, you cannot catch it in code, and the log cuts off mid-sentence (look for reason: OOMKilled in kubectl describe pod). Right next to it is a second mine — limits.cpu: it does not mean \"runs slower\" but CFS throttling, where the pod is frozen until the end of the 100 ms period, and p99 jumps while average utilisation sits at 30%.","hook":"Xmx is the size of the suitcase, while the pod limit is the allowed weight of all your luggage: the suitcase fits, but the wheels, the handle and you yourself are already over.","more":"`-Xmx` bounds only the Java heap. Real RSS is heap + metaspace and compressed class space (`MaxMetaspaceSize` is unbounded by default) + code cache (`ReservedCodeCacheSize` 240 MB) + thread stacks (`-Xss`, 1 MB by default on x64 Linux) + GC structures (G1's remembered sets and card table are a few percent of the heap) + direct/mapped NIO and Netty buffers + native memory of drivers and glibc arenas. The only way to break it down is `-XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary` plus `jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory summary`.\n\nWhat kills you is not the JVM but the kernel: the cgroup hits `memory.max`, the OOM killer fires, the process receives SIGKILL, and the container exits with code 137 (128 + 9). SIGKILL has no handlers, so neither `OutOfMemoryError` nor `-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError` will run — the application log is silent, and the fact is visible in `kubectl describe pod` (`Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled, Exit Code: 137`) and in dmesg as `Memory cgroup out of memory: Killed process 1 (java)`.\n\n`UseContainerSupport` has been on by default since JDK 10, but `MaxRAMPercentage` defaults to 25 — without explicit flags the JVM gives the heap a quarter of the limit and starts grinding Full GCs while memory is still free. A working combination: `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75 -XX:MaxMetaspaceSize=256m -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize=128m` plus `MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2`, otherwise glibc creates up to `8 × ncores` arenas and inflates RSS. The alert goes on `container_memory_working_set_bytes` relative to the limit (threshold 0.9): `jvm_memory_used_bytes` shows the heap only and looks perfectly healthy at the moment of the OOMKill.\n\nWhere it breaks: a slow direct-memory leak — an HTTP client not releasing `ByteBuf`, the heap graph flat, RSS creeping for a week, the pod dying at night during a peak, and the retries landing on neighbouring replicas. The fix is a `MaxDirectMemorySize` ceiling (instead of a mute SIGKILL you get `OutOfMemoryError: Direct buffer memory` with the culprit's stack trace) and a diff against an NMT baseline.","quizCorrect":"Xmx covers the heap only; metaspace, stacks, code cache and direct/native memory sit on top, and the cgroup kills on total RSS","quizWrong":["The JVM always reserves exactly Xmx, so the kernel must have counted the page cache — raising requests.memory is enough to make the restarts stop","This is an ordinary Java OutOfMemoryError, it should be caught in code for graceful degradation","Xmx does not work in a container; set -Xms equal to the pod limit instead"],"picCap":"heap 512m + non-heap ~630m = RSS 1.1Gi against a 1Gi limit → exit 137 with no OutOfMemoryError"},"ninfr-k8s--graceful-":{"q":"Every deploy hits users with a burst of 502s, even though there are five replicas and a rolling update. What physically happens at the moment a pod is killed?","a":"The SIGTERM to the container and the removal of the pod from Endpoints happen in parallel, and the rules reach every kube-proxy/ingress asynchronously — traffic is still flying at a pod that has already started shutting down. The fix is ordering: on SIGTERM first fail readiness and wait a few seconds (preStop sleep), and only then close the server, letting in-flight requests finish.","d":"terminationGracePeriodSeconds is a hard deadline: fail to close in time and SIGKILL arrives, so long operations must either fit inside it or checkpoint.","note":"Separately about consumers: on SIGTERM you must leave the poll loop and call consumer.close() — otherwise the group waits out session.timeout, the rebalance is delayed, and an uncommitted batch is reprocessed. And do not hang long work like \"drain the queue\" on a shutdown hook — the grace period will cut it off halfway.","hook":"When closing the till you first take down the \"open\" sign and serve whoever is already in the queue, rather than switching off the lights with people still inside.","more":"Deleting a pod starts two independent chains. First: the kubelet runs `preStop`, then sends SIGTERM, counts down `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` (30 by default) and finishes with SIGKILL. Second: the endpoint controller removes the pod from the EndpointSlice, after which kube-proxy on every node rewrites iptables/ipvs and the ingress controller updates its upstreams. There is no ordering between the chains — on a busy cluster the rules drift apart by hundreds of milliseconds, and for all that time the balancer sends requests to a pod that has already closed its listening socket: the client gets connection refused and the ingress returns 502.\n\nA readinessProbe alone does not save you: with `periodSeconds = 10` and `failureThreshold = 3` the pod is marked not-ready up to 30 seconds after the fact. Hence the `preStop: exec sleep 5` trick — the hook runs BEFORE SIGTERM, the port stays open throughout, and the rules have time to arrive; since Kubernetes 1.30 there is a built-in `preStop.sleep.seconds` action that does not require a `sleep` binary in the image. The hook's time counts against the grace period and must be budgeted into `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`.\n\nThe application must also finish its in-flight work. In Spring Boot `server.shutdown` defaults to `immediate` — you need `graceful` together with `spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase` (30s by default); in Quarkus it is `quarkus.shutdown.timeout`, which has no default and must be set explicitly. A separate detail is keep-alive: nginx reuses a connection to the upstream even after the endpoint is removed, so during shutdown the server must answer `Connection: close`, and for HTTP/2 send GOAWAY. On the graphs this appears as a spike of `nginx_ingress_controller_requests{status=\"502\"}` lining up exactly with the deploy windows.\n\nWhere it breaks: a pod is running a long task — a 60-second report generation against a grace of 30 — SIGKILL lands in the middle, the transaction rolls back, the Kafka offset is not committed, and after the restart the message is processed again. The fix is a longer grace period plus idempotency and checkpoints, and better still, moving long tasks to a worker outside the rolling update.","quizCorrect":"Endpoint removal and SIGTERM run in parallel and propagate asynchronously — fail readiness and wait first, close the server second","quizWrong":["Kubernetes guarantees that traffic stops before SIGTERM arrives, so a burst of 502s can only mean the liveness probe is far too aggressive","You need to raise maxUnavailable — then old pods hold their connections longer","It is enough to catch SIGKILL and close the connection pools cleanly"],"picCap":"Port closed at t=0.1 s, rules arrived at t=2 s — the 502 window; preStop sleep 5 removes it"},"ninfr-redis--single-":{"q":"You ran KEYS session:* on production, just to see how many there are. The whole service went down for several seconds. How did a single read request take everyone out?","a":"Redis executes commands on a single thread from one shared queue: while KEYS walks the entire keyspace in O(N), nobody else is served — clients sit and run into timeouts. For iteration there is the cursor-based SCAN: it returns a little at a time and releases the thread between iterations.","d":"The same mine sits in any O(N) command over a fat object — SMEMBERS/HGETALL/LRANGE 0 -1 over a million elements, DEL of a huge key (cured by UNLINK, which frees memory in the background) and long Lua scripts that block the whole instance.","note":"The catch: \"single-threaded\" does not mean \"slow\" — it is precisely the single thread that makes commands atomic without locks, which is why INCR and Lua are safe by design (the multithreading in Redis 6 covers network I/O only, not command execution). And SCAN gives weak guarantees: keys that lived through the whole iteration you will see, while keys added or removed along the way are down to luck, and duplicates are possible. Shots like this are caught by the slowlog.","hook":"Redis is one very fast window at the clinic: while one person sorts out a year's worth of certificates, the entire queue stands still.","more":"Commands are executed by a single thread from one shared queue: since 6.0 there is `io-threads` (1 by default, plus `io-threads-do-reads no`), but those only parallelise socket reads/writes and protocol parsing — execution itself stays single-threaded. While `KEYS` walks the keyspace hash table linearly, the event loop does not return to other clients: on 10M keys that is several seconds of total deafness.\n\nThe `SCAN` cursor is not an offset but a hash-table bucket number, traversed by a reverse-binary increment, which is why the iteration survives a rehash as the database grows. `COUNT` defaults to 10: a naive full walk over 10M keys is about a million round trips, so in practice it is set to 500–1000. The guarantee is weaker than KEYS: a key that lived from the start to the end of the walk will be returned at least once (duplicates are possible), while keys added or removed along the way are down to luck.\n\nProduction detail: `slowlog-log-slower-than` defaults to 10000 microseconds and `slowlog-max-len` to 128 — after the incident `SLOWLOG GET` will show KEYS with a time in the millions of microseconds, and `INFO commandstats` the line `cmdstat_keys:calls=1,usec=4200000`. On the client side this is `JedisConnectionException: Read timed out`, or with Lettuce `RedisCommandTimeoutException: Command timed out after 1 minute(s)` (60 s by default). `latency-monitor-threshold` defaults to 0, that is, disabled; set to 100 the event surfaces in `LATENCY LATEST`. Prevention is to forbid the command outright: `rename-command KEYS \"\"` or an ACL `-keys` for the application user.\n\nWhere it breaks: `DEL` of a 5M-element list frees memory synchronously on the same thread and hangs the instance for seconds — cured by `UNLINK` and the flags `lazyfree-lazy-user-del`, `lazyfree-lazy-expire`, `lazyfree-lazy-eviction` (all `no` by default). A stuck Lua script has the same effect: after `busy-reply-threshold` (formerly `lua-time-limit`, 5000 ms) everyone else gets `BUSY`, but the script is not removed — `SCRIPT KILL` helps, and if it has already written, only `SHUTDOWN NOSAVE` does.","quizCorrect":"Redis runs commands on one thread, so an O(N) command blocks every client; use the cursor-based SCAN instead of KEYS","quizWrong":["KEYS takes a global write lock while readers are left unblocked, so what actually went down was replication to the replica and the clients simply carried on","The problem is the network: the KEYS response is too large, raise client-output-buffer-limit","Since Redis 6 commands run multithreaded, so KEYS is not to blame — it was LRU eviction"],"picCap":"KEYS: 10M keys in 4200 ms, the whole queue stalls; SCAN walks by cursor and releases the thread"},"ndist-retry--amplifi":{"q":"One slow service deep in the chain — and the whole contour went down, even though the retries were \"only 3 times\" at each level. How did one failure turn into x27?","a":"Retries do not add up, they multiply across layers: 3 at the gateway × 3 at the BFF × 3 at the service = 27 requests per user request, and the dying downstream gets a multiplied spike exactly when it is at its worst. Retry at one level only, with backoff + jitter and a retry budget — \"no more than N% of total traffic may be retries\".","d":"The second multiplier is timeouts: if the outer timeout is shorter than the total time of the inner attempts, the client is already gone while the system burns resources on a response nobody is waiting for — which is why the deadline is passed down the chain and decremented at each hop.","note":"The catch: you may only retry idempotent operations and only retryable errors. Retrying a POST on timeout without an idempotency key is a silent duplicate payment, and retrying a 400/422 is simply burned budget. And a circuit breaker is no substitute here: it damps a storm that has already begun, it does not stop you from creating one.","hook":"Every floor calls back three times just in case — and the person whose phone died gets 27 calls instead of one.","more":"The multiplication arises because each layer sees only its immediate downstream and does not know that its own inbound call is already somebody's attempt №3. In Resilience4j `maxAttempts` defaults to 3 and `waitDuration` to 500 ms, the interval is fixed and there is no jitter — attempts from different clients also clump into one front. Istio, without explicit configuration, attaches its own `retries.attempts=2` to every VirtualService, so two invisible retry layers appear without a single line of code.\n\nThree mechanisms work against this. A retry budget: in Linkerd `retryRatio: 0.2` with `minRetriesPerSecond: 10` — retries never exceed 20% of base traffic, beyond which requests fail without an attempt. Exponential backoff with full jitter, `sleep = random(0, base * 2^n)`. And a deadline instead of a timeout: gRPC carries `grpc-timeout` in a header, each hop subtracts what it spent, and on exhaustion answers `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED` immediately without going down.\n\nProduction detail: what you watch is the ratio of outbound to inbound requests. Under amplification the user-facing RPS stays flat while the downstream's `http_client_requests_seconds_count` grows 3–27 times; above it the logs show `504 upstream request timeout` from Envoy, below it `context deadline exceeded`. Only idempotent calls may be retried: a POST without an `Idempotency-Key` creates a third payment on the third attempt, because the first one did arrive — it was the response that did not.\n\nWhere it breaks: the downstream degrades to a p99 of 2 s, the retries multiply the load and prevent it from recovering even after the original cause is removed — the contour sticks in a metastable failure where the retry traffic itself has become the overload. It does not resolve from the inside: the cure is a circuit breaker in front of the call, a retry budget and disabling retries at every level except the one closest to the caller, plus shedding traffic in the moment.","quizCorrect":"Retries multiply across layers (3×3×3) and finish off the failing service; fix with retries at one level plus a retry budget and an end-to-end deadline","quizWrong":["Retries add up rather than multiply (3+3+3=9), and the whole problem is the missing jitter, which on its own completely removes the question of amplification once attempts stop arriving as one front","The circuit breaker is to blame, it should have opened by itself: lowering its threshold is enough","Raise the timeouts at the upper levels so attempts have time to finish before cancellation"],"picCap":"3×3×3 = 27 requests into a dying service, 26 responses nobody awaits — the 2 s timeout expired"},"ndist-consistent-has":{"q":"You shard the cache by hash(key) % N. You added one node to ten — and almost the whole cache missed, taking the database down. Why did more than 1/11 of the keys miss, and what is used instead?","a":"With hash % N, changing N changes the remainder for almost every key — it is not at its new place, and nobody looks at the old one any more, so nearly everything moves rather than 1/N. Consistent hashing puts both nodes and keys on one hash ring (a key goes to the first node clockwise), so adding a node moves only its segment — roughly 1/N of the keys.","d":"A bare ring is skewed, so each physical node is placed on it as one or two hundred virtual nodes (vnodes): the load spreads evenly, and a failed node's data is smeared across all the survivors instead of dropping onto a single neighbour.","note":"The catch: consistent hashing does not save you from a hot key — one popular key still flies to a single node, and for that you need key replicas or suffixes. Where you meet it: Cassandra and DynamoDB (a token ring), Redis Cluster (16384 hash slots — not a ring, but the same idea: slots move one at a time).","hook":"Someone brought one more chair — you shift a single row rather than reseating the whole cinema.","more":"A key survives the move from 10 nodes to 11 only if `hash % 10 == hash % 11`. Both remainders are determined by `hash % 110`, and exactly 10 of those 110 remainders qualify — so 1/11 of the keys survive and about 91% move. Consistent hashing breaks the link between a key's position and the number of nodes: the hash space is closed into a ring (2^32 in ketama), the nodes get points on it, and a key goes to the first point clockwise. A new node takes over one arc segment; for all other keys the \"first point clockwise\" has not changed at all.\n\nOne point per node gives a skew of up to ±40% by volume, which is why virtual nodes are used: ketama in libmemcached uses 160 points per server, and in Cassandra this is `num_tokens`, 256 before 4.0 and 16 from then on. The side effect matters more than the evenness: when a node dies, its segments are spread across all the survivors instead of landing entirely on one ring neighbour, which would promptly die next. Redis Cluster solves the same problem differently — 16384 fixed slots, `CRC16(key) mod 16384`, and resharding moves slots while the formula never changes.\n\nProduction detail: it shows immediately in the hit ratio, `keyspace_hits / (keyspace_hits + keyspace_misses)` from `INFO stats` — a drop from 0.98 to 0.1 with a simultaneous multiple spike of QPS on the database replica. The mode is chosen on the client, not the server: in spymemcached it is `KetamaConnectionFactory` versus the default `ArrayModNodeLocator`, and in libmemcached the configuration string `--DISTRIBUTION=consistent`.\n\nWhere it breaks: even with a ring, 9% misses at peak means thousands of simultaneous trips past the cache for one hot key (a cache stampede), and the database goes down from those alone. The cure is coalescing requests by key (singleflight: one thread goes to the database, the rest wait for its result), adding nodes one at a time outside peak hours, and `stale-while-revalidate` — serving the expired value while one thread refreshes it.","quizCorrect":"hash % N remaps almost every key when N changes; a consistent-hashing ring moves only ~1/N, and vnodes even out the skew","quizWrong":["hash % N moves exactly 1/N of the keys — so the real cause was the TTL, and every key happened to expire at the same moment","You need a cryptographic hash function (SHA-256): then the distribution over % N becomes stable against a change of N","Consistent hashing guarantees that not a single key moves when a node is added"],"picCap":"hash%N: going 10→11 misses 91% of keys, the ring moves one segment — 9%"},"ndist-kafka--partiti":{"q":"Consumers were falling behind — you raised the partition count from 3 to 6. The lag went away, but accounting is complaining: events for one client are now applied out of order. What broke?","a":"The partition is chosen as hash(key) % partition_count, so after N changed, the same client key went to a different partition while its old messages still sit in the previous one. For a while two consumers work through one client's events from different partitions in parallel — the \"ordering within a key\" guarantee rested entirely on N never changing.","d":"Kafka does not let you reduce the partition count at all (there is nowhere to put the data and offsets), so partitions are provisioned with headroom in advance rather than tuned under load.","note":"How to live with it: grow partitions in a window where the lag is zero (the old data is already consumed), or copy into a new topic with the desired N, or stop relying on ordering and make processing idempotent by entity version. For compacted topics it hurts twice over: a key's history is smeared across two partitions, and compaction no longer collapses it into one place.","hook":"You rearranged the shelves in the archive: new papers go in by the new scheme, old papers on the same case stay under the old one — and two people read one case from opposite ends.","more":"The partition is computed by the producer: the default partitioner takes `Utils.toPositive(murmur2(keyBytes)) % numPartitions`. It learns the partition count from metadata, so a key does not move at the moment of `--alter` but as metadata refreshes on each instance — `metadata.max.age.ms` defaults to 300000 ms. That is minutes during which different pods write the same client into different partitions. The arithmetic is unforgiving: if `hash % 3 == 1`, then after the expansion the key goes to P1 or P4, while its old messages stay in P1.\n\nOrdering within a partition does not hold by itself either: `max.in.flight.requests.per.connection` defaults to 5, and on a batch retry a later batch could overtake an earlier one — you are saved from that by `enable.idempotence=true` (the default since 3.0), where the broker numbers batches by producer id and rejects reordering. On the consumer side ordering is broken by hand: `max.poll.records` of 500 handed out to a thread pool loses intra-partition ordering without any resharding at all.\n\nProduction detail: `kafka-topics.sh --alter --partitions 3` on a topic with six answers `InvalidPartitionsException: Topic currently has 6 partitions, which is higher than the requested 3`. The symptom of desynchronisation is not lag but a spike of optimistic-locking conflicts and \"negative balances\" in reports; what you watch is `kafka_consumergroup_lag` per partition and the timestamp of the last event for a specific key.\n\nWhere it breaks: partitions were grown under load, and for about five minutes one client's events travel two paths — a debit is applied before a credit, the balance goes negative, and it cannot be replayed because the offsets are already committed. The cure is drain-and-switch: stop the producers, wait for lag=0 on every partition, and only then `--alter`; or a new topic with generous partitioning and a consumer cutover.","quizCorrect":"A key maps as hash(key) % N: when N changes it moves to another partition while old messages stay in the previous one — per-key ordering temporarily breaks","quizWrong":["Kafka redistributes already-written messages across the new partitions by itself, so all that was needed was to wait for the migration to finish before reading","Ordering was broken by a consumer rebalance — a cooperative-sticky assignor would have helped","You should have raised the replication factor along with the partitions, then ordering would have held"],"picCap":"client42 moved from P1 to P4: the tail e2,e3 is still in P1, e4 overtakes; 6→3 is forbidden"},"ndist-virtual-thread":{"q":"You moved to Java 21 and virtual threads: thread-per-request is now \"free\". A load test — and instead of higher RPS the service falls over while the database throws connection timeouts. What happened?","a":"The platform thread pool was an implicit load limiter: 200 threads meant at most 200 simultaneous trips to the database. Virtual threads removed that ceiling, tens of thousands of concurrent tasks come in and all fight over 20 pool connections — instead of back pressure you got an unbounded queue inside your own process.","d":"The limiter is now set explicitly: a Semaphore or a concurrency limit around every external call — one for the database, one per downstream, that is, a bulkhead by hand rather than by accident from a pool size.","hook":"You widened the shop door to stadium gates while keeping a single till — a crowd inside, buying no faster.","note":"The second trap sits right next to it — pinning: under synchronized (and in native/JNI frames) a virtual thread could not unmount from its carrier platform thread, so a blocking call inside synchronized ate a thread from the shared pool; in 21/22 the cure was replacing synchronized with ReentrantLock, in 24 monitor pinning was removed, but native frames remain. And do not create a \"pool of virtual threads\" — they are single-use, created per task.","more":"A virtual thread is mounted onto a carrier from a ForkJoinPool whose size is set by `jdk.virtualThreadScheduler.parallelism` (the core count by default, with a `maxPoolSize` ceiling of 256). On a blocking call the VT unmounts and the carrier is immediately free — which is exactly why the limit that `server.tomcat.threads.max=200` used to provide for free disappears. In Spring Boot this is switched on by the single line `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true`, and the application loses its only concurrency regulator.\n\nA limit survives only where the resource is physically finite: HikariCP's `maximumPoolSize` defaults to 10 and `connectionTimeout` to 30000 ms. From there Little's law applies: 20 connections at 10 ms per query caps you at 2000 rps, everything above that piles up in the pool queue, and every waiting task holds its request's object graph in the heap. The result is an unbounded in-process queue: latency grows linearly up to the timeout, RPS does not grow at all, and the GC burns CPU on the live objects of waiting requests.\n\nProduction detail: `HikariPool-1 - Connection is not available, request timed out after 30000ms`, the `hikaricp_connections_pending` metric in the hundreds instead of zero, and the p99 of `hikaricp_connections_acquire_seconds` pinned to connectionTimeout. The explicit limiter is a `Semaphore` with `tryAcquire(50, MILLISECONDS)` or a Resilience4j Bulkhead (`maxConcurrentCalls` 25, `maxWaitDuration` 0 by default) — one per external resource, not one shared. The second source of surprises is pinning: before JDK 24 blocking inside `synchronized` does not release the carrier (`-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=short`), and pgjdbc moved from `synchronized` to `ReentrantLock` only in 42.5.1.\n\nWhere it breaks: under load the health check reaches for a connection from the same exhausted pool, `/health` times out, Kubernetes restarts the pod, its traffic moves to the neighbours and takes them down one by one. The cure is a semaphore in front of every external call, a fail-fast timeout comfortably below the client deadline, and a separate two-connection datasource for liveness/readiness.","quizCorrect":"The thread pool size was an implicit concurrency limit; virtual threads removed it — bound parallelism explicitly (semaphore/bulkhead) per external resource","quizWrong":["Virtual threads cannot do blocking JDBC — only a reactive driver will do, so moving to them means rewriting the entire data access layer from scratch","The right fix is a fixed-size pool of virtual threads: the concurrency limit then reappears on its own, exactly the way it used to work with a bounded pool of platform threads","It is the GC: millions of virtual threads live in the heap, so raising Xmx is enough"],"picCap":"20,000 virtual threads against Hikari max 20: a queue and timeout 30000ms instead of higher RPS"},"ninfr-metrics--cardi":{"q":"You added a user_id label to the http_requests_total metric — easier to debug. An hour later Prometheus ate all the memory and stopped scraping. Why did one label take everything down?","a":"In Prometheus every unique combination of labels is a separate time series with its own index and in-memory buffer, and user_id turns one metric into a million series (cardinality explosion). Labels may hold only values from a small finite set — method, status, endpoint template — while identifiers and arbitrary strings belong in logs and traces.","d":"The same explosion comes from an untemplated path: /order/123 instead of /order/{id} gives a series per order, which is why URLs are normalised to the routing template right in the instrumentation.","note":"The catch: removing the label does not bring instant relief — the old series live in the index and the head block until the end of the retention. And count cardinality as a product: two \"harmless\" labels with 50 values each are already 2500 series per metric, and that is per pod.","hook":"A metric is a counter on the wall, not a visitors' log: giving every person their own counter would take more walls than you have.","more":"In the TSDB a series is defined by its full label set: `http_requests_total{method=\"GET\",status=\"200\",uri=\"/order/{id}\"}` is a separate memSeries in the head block, with its own open chunk of 120 samples and entries in the inverted index. The cost is a few kilobytes of RAM per active series, and it is not released immediately: the head is cut every two hours, but the series stays in the index until compaction, and every new series is also written to the WAL. A million new series in an hour means gigabytes and an OOMKill.\n\nWhat is expensive is not the number of label values as such but the product: `user_id` multiplies the existing 1200 combinations of method × status × route by the number of unique users in the window. An untemplated path does the same — `/order/123` gives a new series per order, which is why the URI is normalised to the routing template in the instrumentation rather than in recording rules.\n\nProduction detail: the key graph is `prometheus_tsdb_head_series`, where a vertical line appears right after the release; the culprit is found with `topk(10, count by (__name__)({__name__=~\".+\"}))` or on the `/tsdb-status` page under Head Cardinality Stats. On the scrape side you set a fuse, `sample_limit` in scrape_config (0 by default, that is, no limit) — on exceeding it the target fails with `sample limit exceeded`. In Micrometer the ceiling is set by `MeterFilter.maximumAllowableTags(...)`; Spring Boot 2.x did it with the property `management.metrics.web.server.max-uri-tags`, defaulting to 100, and logged `Reached the maximum number of URI tags for 'http.server.requests'`.\n\nWhere it breaks: Prometheus dies exactly during the incident the release caused, and the alerts and dashboards go with it — you end up fixing things blind. The quick cure is not an application rollback but `metric_relabel_configs` with `action: labeldrop, regex: user_id` on the scrape plus a Prometheus restart; the identifier itself goes back into the log and into the trace as an exemplar.","quizCorrect":"Every unique label combination is a separate time series; high-cardinality values (id, email, raw URL) blow up memory and belong in logs and traces","quizWrong":["Prometheus stores labels as strings inside a single series, so the problem is the length of the value and hashing user_id down to a short digest is enough to fix it","You need to scrape less often: raising scrape_interval removes the cardinality problem","You should have used a Histogram instead of a Counter — histograms collapse labels"],"picCap":"1200 series × 500k user_id = 600M series and OOM; /order/123 versus /order/{id} — 1 series"},"nspri-tx":{"q":"Inside an @Transactional method we call an external REST service that takes 3 seconds. Load grew — and the application stalled with \"connection is not available, request timed out\". What has HTTP got to do with it?","a":"The transaction holds a pool connection until the commit, so the whole time you wait for someone else's service the connection sits there occupied. Ten Hikari connections serve ten requests, and the rest queue up and fail on timeout.","d":"The rule: the network lives outside the transaction. A short read transaction → the HTTP call → a second short write transaction.","note":"It hurts most when the HTTP client has no timeouts set (by default it has none): the transaction hangs indefinitely, \"idle in transaction\" sessions pile up in Postgres, locks are held and autovacuum cannot remove dead rows.","hook":"You occupied the only till and stand there phoning a friend: the till is technically \"open\", but the queue is not moving.","more":"Hibernate acquires a connection lazily — on the first SQL statement inside the transaction — and holds it until commit/rollback: the `DELAYED_ACQUISITION_AND_RELEASE_AFTER_TRANSACTION` mode. `DataSourceTransactionManager` is worse: it grabs the connection right at BEGIN. Hence the consequence: a REST call before the first query does not touch the pool, while after it the connection sits occupied for all 3 seconds while the thread waits on a socket.\n\nFrom there Little's law applies: the ceiling is pool size / hold time. `spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size` defaults to **10** and the hold is about 3.05 s → roughly **3 rps per instance**, no matter how many threads Tomcat has (`server.tomcat.threads.max=200`). Two hundred requests share ten connections, and the rest wait in Hikari's queue.\n\nIn production it looks like this: `spring.datasource.hikari.connection-timeout` defaults to 30000 ms, and once it expires you get `HikariPool-1 - Connection is not available, request timed out after 30001ms`. Before the collapse `hikaricp_connections_pending` rises (any value above 0 is already trouble) along with `hikaricp_connections_usage_seconds`: if usage matches the external API's latency, the diagnosis is made. `leak-detection-threshold: 20000` additionally prints the stack trace of whoever is holding the connection.\n\nWhere it breaks: the external service degrades from 200 ms to 3 s — it is still alive and returning 200 OK, but your pool burns out, and requests that never touch that API go down with it. The cure is moving HTTP outside the transaction boundary (a short read tx → the call → a short write tx via `TransactionTemplate`) plus mandatory connect/read timeouts on the client: a bare `RestTemplate` has none and will wait as long as the network lets it.","quizCorrect":"The transaction holds a pool connection for the whole method, including the HTTP wait — the pool runs out","quizWrong":["The REST call opens its own transaction with REQUIRES_NEW and the nesting eats up the connection pool entirely within a dozen requests","The HTTP client and Hikari share one thread pool and compete for it","Spring raises the isolation level to SERIALIZABLE for the duration of the external call"],"picCap":"The connection is held all 3 s of the REST call: 10/10 Hikari busy, ceiling ~3 rps, then timeouts"},"nspri-virtual-thread":{"q":"You switched on spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true in Boot 3.2+ — RPS barely moved and in places got worse. What actually changed, and where is the bottleneck now?","a":"Every HTTP request is served by its own virtual thread instead of one from the Tomcat pool — threads are now unlimited and cheap. But the database connection pool and the limits of external systems are unchanged: the queue simply moved from Tomcat to Hikari.","d":"Virtual threads cure a shortage of threads on blocking I/O — if you were limited by CPU or by the connection pool, there is no gain to be had.","note":"You also lose free back pressure: concurrency used to be bounded by the Tomcat pool, and now everything gets in at once. And caching \"an expensive object in a ThreadLocal\" turns from an optimisation into a leak — there are millions of threads now, not 200.","hook":"You widened the shop entrance from one door to a hundred while keeping a single till — the crowd now stands inside.","more":"`spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` (Boot 3.2+, Java 21) switches the Tomcat protocol handler to a virtual-thread executor, replaces `applicationTaskExecutor` with a `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor` backed by virtual threads, and moves the scheduler and listener containers onto them too. `server.tomcat.threads.max=200` stops limiting anything: each request creates a new virtual thread costing ~1 KB of heap instead of a ~1 MB platform stack. The only remaining ceiling at the entrance is `server.tomcat.max-connections=8192`.\n\nUnderneath sits the same ForkJoinPool of carrier threads, with `jdk.virtualThreadScheduler.parallelism` equal to the core count, so CPU work is not accelerated at all. A thread unmounts from its carrier only on I/O that has been rewritten for virtual threads; before JDK 24 `synchronized` pinned the carrier entirely (lifted by JEP 491) — caught by the JFR event `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned` or `-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full` on 21.\n\nThe database pool has not moved meanwhile: `maximum-pool-size` is still 10. The metric picture is characteristic — `tomcat_threads_busy_threads` is no longer meaningful (there is no pool), while `hikaricp_connections_pending` climbs into the tens, `hikaricp_connections_acquire_seconds` stretches towards 30 s, and the tail of `http_server_requests_seconds` spreads out. The queue has merely changed address.\n\nWhere it breaks: the implicit protection is gone — 200 Tomcat threads used to act as a rate limit on the downstream, and now thousands of virtual threads hit someone else's API at once and take it down, with no back pressure anywhere. The cure is an explicit concurrency limit (a semaphore, a Resilience4j bulkhead, a limit on the HTTP client) and sizing the database pool to real concurrency rather than to the thread count.","quizCorrect":"Requests are served by virtual threads, but the bottleneck moves to the database pool and external services","quizWrong":["@Transactional stops working: it relies on a ThreadLocal, which a virtual thread does not have, so every transactional boundary silently disappears","Virtual threads spread work across cores, so CPU-bound code speeds up","Tomcat switches into non-blocking mode and the code has to be rewritten as reactive"],"picCap":"10,000 threads now, but Hikari max=10 unchanged — the queue moved from Tomcat to the database"},"nspri-data":{"q":"You ran a bulk update via @Modifying @Query(\"update Order o set o.status = :s where ...\"), yet in the same transaction order.getStatus() still returns the old value. Why?","a":"A JPQL update goes straight to the database, bypassing the persistence context. Entities already loaded know nothing about it, and find() hands you exactly those — from the L1 cache.","d":"Fixed with @Modifying(flushAutomatically = true, clearAutomatically = true): first flush your own changes, then clear the context so the next read goes to the database.","note":"clearAutomatically detaches EVERY entity in the context, so without flushAutomatically you silently lose unwritten dirty changes. And a bulk update fires no @PreUpdate and does not move @Version — optimistic locking passes it by.","hook":"You rewrote the document in the archive while an old printout lies on your desk — and that is the one you are reading.","more":"`@Modifying @Query(\"update ...\")` becomes a single SQL `UPDATE` and goes straight to the database. Hibernate does not know which rows were affected and leaves the persistence context alone: `Order#42` keeps its old snapshot, and `em.find(Order.class, 42L)` returns the object from the identity map without touching the database at all.\n\nIt gets more dangerous from there. Before running the query Hibernate auto-flushes only on an overlap of query spaces, and a dirty entity left in the context will be written on commit on top of the bulk update — your own `UPDATE` overwrites the result. That is why the order in `@Modifying(flushAutomatically = true, clearAutomatically = true)` is exactly this: flush what has accumulated first, then clear. Two adjacent mines: a bulk query does not increment `@Version` (in HQL you must ask explicitly — `update versioned Order ...`), which blinds optimistic locking, and it does not update the L2 cache row by row — `BulkOperationCleanupAction` simply invalidates the regions of the affected tables.\n\nA production classic is forgetting `@Transactional`: you get `TransactionRequiredException: Executing an update/delete query`, wrapped by Spring into `InvalidDataAccessApiUsageException`. Forgetting `@Modifying` itself sends the query down the ordinary select path and fails with `Not supported for DML operations`. Worth checking are the returned `int` (the number of rows actually changed) and the `logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG` output.\n\nWhere it breaks: `clearAutomatically = true` detaches everything indiscriminately, including entities the calling code is holding — followed by `LazyInitializationException` on their collections or silently lost edits. The cure is to keep the bulk update in its own narrow repository method, call it in its own transaction, and re-read whatever you need afterwards.","quizCorrect":"Bulk JPQL writes straight to the database, bypassing the persistence context — loaded entities stay stale","quizWrong":["The second-level cache is to blame, mark the entity @Cache(NONSTRICT_READ_WRITE) and everything will work exactly as you expect","The update has not been committed yet, the value becomes current after commit","@Modifying requires REQUIRES_NEW, otherwise the changes are not applied"],"picCap":"The UPDATE reached the database (status=NEW), while order.getStatus() reads the L1 cache and returns OLD"},"nspri-security":{"q":"A method under @Async sends a notification on behalf of the user, but SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication() inside it is null. Where did the user go?","a":"The SecurityContext lives in a ThreadLocal of the thread that served the request. @Async runs the method on a different thread from a pool — and there the ThreadLocal is empty.","d":"The context has to be carried across explicitly: DelegatingSecurityContextExecutor or your own TaskDecorator on the executor.","note":"MODE_INHERITABLETHREADLOCAL looks like a cure, but with a pool it is worse than an empty context: the thread is reused and drags along the user from a previous request. In exactly the same way, MDC/traceId and the transaction do not travel into a background thread — all of them are ThreadLocals.","hook":"Your pass stayed in your own jacket pocket — the colleague you sent in your place arrives at the turnstile empty-handed.","more":"`SecurityContextHolder` runs in the `MODE_THREADLOCAL` strategy by default (switched by the `spring.security.strategy` system property), so the context lives in a `ThreadLocal` of the `http-nio-8080-exec-N` thread. `@Async`, through `AsyncExecutionInterceptor`, submits the call into a different pool where that `ThreadLocal` is empty. Worse still, `SecurityContextHolderFilter` clears the context in a `finally` block right after the response is sent — so even if the thread did match, by the time the async task started there would be nothing left to take.\n\nThe same mechanism loses the MDC: `traceId` disappears from the logs of the async branch, because it is also a `ThreadLocal`. The cure is shared — wrap the executor: `DelegatingSecurityContextAsyncTaskExecutor` or your own `TaskDecorator` that captures the context on the calling thread and installs it inside the task, always clearing it in a `finally`, otherwise the context stays hanging on a reused thread.\n\nThe defaults that matter here: without a bean of its own, `@Async` goes to `applicationTaskExecutor` with `spring.task.execution.pool.core-size=8`, while `max-size` and `queue-capacity` are `Integer.MAX_VALUE`, so max is never reached — tasks simply pile up in the queue. In the logs the symptom looks like `AuthenticationCredentialsNotFoundException: An Authentication object was not found in the SecurityContext` or an NPE on `getAuthentication().getName()`. Watch `executor_queued_tasks` and `executor_active_threads`.\n\nWhere it breaks: scarier than null turns out to be `MODE_INHERITABLETHREADLOCAL` — a pool thread inherits the context of whoever created it, is then reused and executes tasks under someone else's identity; the audit trail fills with actions by the wrong user. The cure is passing the user identifier in the task payload itself and restoring the context at the start of processing.","quizCorrect":"The SecurityContext is kept in a ThreadLocal, and the @Async thread is a different one — the context is not carried over","quizWrong":["@Async creates a separate ApplicationContext in which the Security beans are never started at all, so the context there is empty","@Async and @PreAuthorize are both proxies and they mutually disable each other","The token simply expires by the time the background task starts"],"picCap":"The context sits in the ThreadLocal of exec-1, thread task-1 is empty: getAuthentication() = null"},"nspri-validation":{"q":"@Valid on a @RequestBody gives a tidy 400 with a list of fields, while the same @Valid on a service method parameter does nothing at all. Why?","a":"In the controller the validation is triggered by Spring MVC itself while binding the argument (a MethodArgumentNotValidException → 400). On a bean it is the job of an AOP proxy, switched on by @Validated on the class — without it @Valid is simply ignored.","d":"On a service the violation arrives as a ConstraintViolationException and, without your own @ExceptionHandler, turns into a 500 rather than a 400.","note":"Two finishing blows: validation descends into a nested object only where that nested field carries @Valid, and on a service the ordinary proxy disease applies — a call through this validates nothing.","hook":"There is face control at the club entrance, but at the bar inside you are checked only if you hired a guard yourself.","more":"In MVC the validation is triggered by `AbstractMessageConverterMethodArgumentResolver.validateIfApplicable()`: it fires on `@Valid` or on any annotation whose name starts with `Valid` (including `@Validated` with groups). The result is placed into a `BindingResult`, and if no such parameter exists in the method signature, a `MethodArgumentNotValidException` is thrown, which `DefaultHandlerExceptionResolver` turns into a 400 broken down by field.\n\nOn an ordinary bean there is no binding at all. There the work is done by `MethodValidationPostProcessor` (raised by autoconfiguration), which proxies only classes marked `@Validated` and attaches a `MethodValidationInterceptor` calling `ExecutableValidator.validateParameters()`. Hence the two proxy limitations: a method called from inside the same bean is not validated, and a final class or final method cannot be wrapped by CGLIB.\n\nThe default that burns people most often: without the `spring-boot-starter-validation` dependency (hibernate-validator) there is no `Validator` bean in the context and `@Valid` quietly becomes a no-op — no error, no log, and rubbish reaches the database. If everything is wired up, the service throws `jakarta.validation.ConstraintViolationException: createOrder.arg0.amount: must be greater than 0` — the name `arg0` instead of `amount` means the build lacks the `-parameters` compiler flag. Without your own handler that is a 500, visible as `http_server_requests_seconds_count{status=\"500\"}` on a specific endpoint.\n\nWhere it breaks: the checks were moved from the controller into the service \"so the rules live in one place\", `@Validated` was forgotten — validation switched off entirely and silently, while the happy-path tests stayed green. The cure is `@Validated` on the class, an `@ExceptionHandler(ConstraintViolationException.class)` returning 400, and a test that asserts the rejection of deliberately bad input.","quizCorrect":"In the controller MVC validates during binding; on a bean it is an AOP proxy enabled by @Validated","quizWrong":["It is the namespace: the controller uses jakarta.validation while a service needs the older javax.validation generation","A service needs @Valid on the class, and @Validated is its deprecated synonym","Validation on a bean does not work without Spring MVC on the classpath"],"picCap":"@Valid in MVC gives 400; on a bean without @Validated there is no check, with it a 500 instead of 400"},"nspri-jpa":{"q":"\"Let's make DTOs and entities records, they are immutable after all.\" Where will a record work, and where will Hibernate refuse?","a":"For DTOs and constructor projections (select new com.x.OrderView(...)) a record is ideal. But it cannot be an @Entity: JPA needs a no-arg constructor, a non-final class and mutable fields in order to populate the object and slip in a lazy proxy.","d":"Immutability and dirty checking contradict each other in principle: Hibernate lives by watching a managed object change, and a record has nothing to change.","note":"The restriction applies specifically to @Entity: Hibernate 6 happily takes a record as an @Embeddable, and Spring Data JDBC and JdbcClient work with them throughout. And in a hexagonal domain model (Money, Isin, OrderId) a record is the best candidate for a value object.","hook":"A record is a photograph: convenient to look at, impossible to paint over. An entity has to be a canvas.","more":"JPA demands three things of an entity: a public or protected no-argument constructor, a non-final class and non-final getters. Hibernate instantiates the object by reflection and then fills the fields, and for `getReference()` and lazy associations it generates a subclass through ByteBuddy (`hibernate.bytecode.provider=bytebuddy` by default). A record violates all of it at once: the class is final, the fields are final, the canonical constructor is mandatory. At startup you get `org.hibernate.InstantiationException: No default constructor for entity`, while a merely final class gives a quiet `HHH000305: Could not create proxy factory for ...`, after which laziness is silently disabled.\n\nThe deeper contradiction sits in dirty checking: `EntityEntry` holds the loaded state — a snapshot of the values at load time — and on flush Hibernate compares it field by field with the object's current state. In a record the \"current state\" cannot be changed; any edit produces a new instance, which as far as the context is concerned is a different entity.\n\nWhere a record works as intended: constructor projections `select new com.x.OrderView(o.id, o.status)`, Spring Data DTO projections (a repository method returning `List<OrderView>`, mapped through the canonical constructor), and from Hibernate 6.2 also `@Embeddable` components read by the same constructor.\n\nWhere it breaks: the entity turns out to be final (a common case is a Kotlin data class without the allOpen plugin) — no proxy is created, every `@ManyToOne` reference is loaded eagerly, and in production that is a sudden N+1 on list screens. Visible through `hibernate.generate_statistics=true` and the number of entities loaded per request. The cure is an ordinary class with a protected no-arg constructor, keeping immutability in the DTO layer.","quizCorrect":"A record suits DTOs and projections, but not @Entity: that needs a no-arg constructor, a non-final class and mutable fields","quizWrong":["A record can be made an @Entity as long as you add a Lombok @NoArgsConstructor, which generates the missing constructor Hibernate is looking for","Hibernate 6 supports record entities, the restriction now applies only to @Id","A record cannot be used even in projections — Hibernate supports interface projections only"],"picCap":"record OrderView in select new — fine; a record as @Entity — no no-arg ctor, class is final"},"njvm-containers":{"q":"The pod was given a CPU limit of 1 and 512 MB. The service came up, but everything got slower: parallelStream barely crawls and GC pauses grew. What did the JVM \"see\" in the container?","a":"The JVM reads the cgroup quota (UseContainerSupport): availableProcessors became 1, so the common ForkJoinPool has no workers left — parallelStream computes right on the calling thread. And the ergonomics of a \"non server-class\" machine (under 2 CPUs or ~1792 MB) silently chose SerialGC instead of G1.","d":"What you check is not docker inspect but the JVM itself: java -XX:+PrintFlagsFinal -version | grep -E 'UseSerialGC|UseG1GC|MaxHeapSize' and Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors() right inside the pod.","note":"The flip side: pools sized as availableProcessors()*2 turn into a couple of threads inside a pod — while you were expecting thirty-two. Set pool sizes explicitly, and -XX:ActiveProcessorCount where needed, instead of relying on \"however many cores are there\".","hook":"You drove a lorry onto a single-lane road: same vehicle, nowhere to overtake — everything \"parallel\" lines up in one file.","more":"`-XX:+UseContainerSupport` has been on by default since 8u191/JDK 10: at startup the JVM reads not `/proc/cpuinfo` but the cgroup — `cpu.max` in v2 (a line like `100000 100000`) or the `cpu.cfs_quota_us`/`cpu.cfs_period_us` pair in v1 — and takes `ceil(quota / period)`. At a limit of 1, `Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors()` returns 1 on any node, and almost all of the runtime's autotuning is derived from that single number.\n\n`ForkJoinPool.commonPool()` takes parallelism = `availableProcessors() - 1`, that is, 0. A pool with zero parallelism starts no workers and runs in caller-runs mode: `parallelStream()` is computed on the calling thread but with the full cost of split and merge — slower than a plain `stream()`. Separately, `os::is_server_class_machine()` kicks in: it wants ≥2 CPUs and 2 GB of memory minus a 256 MB allowance, hence the **1792 MB** threshold; below it you get the client profile with `SerialGC` instead of `G1`.\n\nThe second default hits the same spot: `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` is **25.0**, so out of a 512 MB limit the heap gets 128 MB and the rest goes to metaspace, stacks, code cache and native buffers. The pod slides into OOMKilled (exit code 137) with no `OutOfMemoryError` in the log at all. Watch `jvm_memory_max_bytes{area=\"heap\"}` against `container_memory_working_set_bytes`, `container_cpu_cfs_throttled_seconds_total` and `jvm_gc_pause_seconds`: under SerialGC the last one reads `Copy`/`MarkSweepCompact` rather than `G1 Young Generation`.\n\nWhere it breaks: the CPU quota is enforced in 100 ms CFS windows, and at limit=1 the GC thread itself gets throttled — a STW pause that took 40 ms on four cores stretches into seconds, a liveness probe with a 1 s timeout goes unanswered, the kubelet restarts the pod, and after the restart it all repeats. The cure is explicit flags instead of ergonomics: `-XX:+UseG1GC`, `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75`, `-XX:ActiveProcessorCount=N`, a CPU limit of at least two cores (or requests only), and parallel computation in your own pool of an explicit size rather than the common FJP.","quizCorrect":"The JVM takes the core count from the cgroup quota: 1 CPU → a common pool with no workers and SerialGC by ergonomics","quizWrong":["A CPU limit does not affect the JVM at all — only the memory limit and MaxRAMPercentage matter, and those are what you configure at startup","The JVM always sees the host's cores and spawns extra GC threads that fight over the quota","Under a hard CPU limit the JVM disables the JIT and runs in the interpreter"],"picCap":"cgroup limit=1 → availableProcessors 1 → FJP parallelism 0 and SerialGC instead of G1"},"ndb-sqljoin":{"q":"A join of two large tables takes 40 seconds, and EXPLAIN shows a Nested Loop with a million iterations. What join strategies does the planner actually have, and why did it choose this one?","a":"There are three: Nested Loop (for each row of the outer table look up matches in the inner one — good when the outer side is tiny and the inner has an index), Hash Join (build a hash over the smaller side and scan the larger one once) and Merge Join (both sides arrive already sorted). The planner picks a Nested Loop when it expects a dozen rows from the outer side — and a million turned up.","d":"In EXPLAIN ANALYZE compare rows= (the estimate) with actual rows: a discrepancy of tens of times is cured by ANALYZE and extended statistics for correlated columns, not by forbidding Nested Loop.","note":"A Nested Loop is not evil in itself: \"small outer + an index on the inner\" is the fastest and most memory-frugal option there is. A Hash Join pays with memory: exceed work_mem and it spills to disk in batches (visible in the plan as Batches: 8, Disk Usage). A Merge Join is nearly free when the data already arrives sorted from an index, otherwise you pay for the sort.","hook":"Finding people from a list: for three names it is easier to walk the hall, for a thousand it pays to write everyone into a notebook once.","more":"The planner costs a Nested Loop as `outer_rows × cost(inner scan)`: with 12 rows on the outside and an index scan inside, that is a few dozen random accesses, cheaper than any alternative — the choice is correct as long as the estimate is correct. A Hash Join builds the hash over the smaller side in `work_mem` (default **4MB**) and scans the larger side once linearly; if it does not fit, the executor splits it into batches with temporary files, shown in the plan as `Buckets: 65536 Batches: 8 Memory Usage: 4096kB`. A Merge Join wants sorted input from both sides: free when both come from an index, otherwise it pays for two Sorts.\n\nThe estimate comes from `pg_statistic`: a histogram and a list of most-common values of depth `default_statistics_target` (default **100**). The classic source of a miss is column correlation: selectivities are multiplied as if independent, so `WHERE city='Almaty' AND region='Almaty region'` yields a number tens of times below reality. That is fixed by `CREATE STATISTICS ... (dependencies, ndistinct) ON city, region FROM ...`, not by `enable_nestloop = off`.\n\nThe second cause is stale statistics: autovacuum runs ANALYZE at `autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor` = **0.1** of the table size plus a threshold of 50, so after a nightly load the plan lives on old numbers for hours. Diagnostics: `rows=` versus `actual rows=... loops=` per node, `Buffers: shared hit=4823901` under the Nested Loop, `n_mod_since_analyze` and `last_analyze` in `pg_stat_user_tables`, and a rise of `mean_exec_time` in `pg_stat_statements` without a rise in `calls`.\n\nWhere it breaks: a batch job inserts 2 million rows, ANALYZE does not keep up, at the next planning the outer-side estimate is still `rows=1`, and the Nested Loop becomes 2 million index scans — the query takes 40 seconds instead of one, the connection pool fills up, and the whole application degrades behind it. The cure is an explicit `ANALYZE` at the end of the load, extended statistics on correlated columns and `ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN ... SET STATISTICS 1000` on the problem column; a global `enable_nestloop=off` is contraindicated — OLTP queries with LIMIT need the Nested Loop.","quizCorrect":"A Nested Loop is chosen when the outer side is estimated as small — and that estimate was far too low","quizWrong":["There are not enough indexes on both tables, and without them a Hash Join cannot be built at all by the planner","A Nested Loop is always slower and should be disabled in the configuration","A Merge Join is impossible without ORDER BY in the query, so a Nested Loop remained"],"picCap":"Estimate rows=12 against actual 1.2M: the Nested Loop runs 1.2M lookups — 40 s instead of 1 s"},"ndb-ddllocks":{"q":"You applied an ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN migration — an \"instant\" operation — and production stopped answering for 5 minutes. What happened?","a":"ALTER TABLE takes an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock, incompatible even with SELECT, and queues behind a long-running query (a report, a forgotten transaction). And behind it every new query to the table joins the same queue: the ALTER itself takes milliseconds, while the outage equals the length of the longest query ahead of it.","d":"The cure is not \"do it at night\" but SET lock_timeout = '2s' before the DDL plus a retry — a failed migration beats a downed production.","note":"ADD COLUMN ... DEFAULT does not rewrite the table in Postgres 11+, but ADD CONSTRAINT (CHECK, FK) scans the whole table under the lock — do it in two steps: ADD CONSTRAINT ... NOT VALID, then VALIDATE CONSTRAINT (which takes a weak lock). Hence the same expand/contract pattern: add the column and write to both, then switch reads, and only then drop the old one.","hook":"One person with boxes stopped in a narrow corridor, waiting for a porter to pass — and the whole office is already queued behind them.","more":"ACCESS EXCLUSIVE conflicts with all eight table lock modes, including the ACCESS SHARE that any `SELECT` takes. The key point is not the conflict itself but the fair waiting queue: a waiting writer blocks everyone who arrives after it, even those whose mode is compatible with the current holder. That is why \"instant\" DDL halts the table for the length of the longest query that started before it, plus the time to drain the tail.\n\n`ADD COLUMN` itself really does not rewrite the table since PostgreSQL 11, even with a DEFAULT — the value is stored in `pg_attribute.attmissingval` and substituted on read, but only if the expression is not volatile. `ALTER TYPE`, `ADD PRIMARY KEY`, `SET NOT NULL` before PG 12, and a column with `DEFAULT random()` take the same lock and hold it for a full scan or a file rewrite — that is no longer milliseconds.\n\nThe defaults that decide everything: `lock_timeout` = **0** (wait forever), `statement_timeout` = 0, `deadlock_timeout` = 1s, `log_lock_waits` = **off**. With `log_lock_waits` on, the log shows `process 12345 still waiting for AccessExclusiveLock on relation 16483 of database 16401 after 1000.123 ms`, and with a timeout set the migration fails predictably: `ERROR: canceling statement due to lock timeout`. Live, this is visible in `pg_locks` as rows with `granted = false` and in `pg_stat_activity` as `wait_event_type = 'Lock'`.\n\nWhere it breaks: while the ALTER waits in the queue the application keeps opening connections — they queue too and are never released — and within a minute `max_connections` (default **100**) is exhausted. Then comes `FATAL: sorry, too many clients already`, and things that never touch that table fall over too; after the ALTER is cancelled the database spends several more minutes digesting the avalanche. The cure is `SET lock_timeout = '2s'` in the DDL transaction plus a retry with backoff, `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` (SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE) instead of the plain form, splitting heavy ALTERs into steps, and killing long queries with `pg_terminate_backend()` before the rollout.","quizCorrect":"The ALTER waits for ACCESS EXCLUSIVE behind a long query, and everyone else queues up behind it","quizWrong":["ADD COLUMN physically rewrites the entire table in Postgres 11+, and the rewrite is what took those five minutes","Postgres locks the whole database for the duration of any DDL","The migration rebuilds every index on the table"],"picCap":"The ALTER waits for ACCESS EXCLUSIVE behind a 5-minute SELECT, and all new traffic queues behind it"},"ndb-vacuumxmin-hor":{"q":"Autovacuum is on and honestly doing its rounds, yet the table keeps swelling and queries keep slowing down. pg_stat_activity shows a connection \"idle in transaction\" for two hours. Are they connected?","a":"Directly: VACUUM removes only those dead row versions that no live transaction can still see. An open transaction holds a snapshot (the xmin horizon), so all the garbage accumulated after it started is skipped — the table and indexes grow, and scans read more and more nearly empty pages.","d":"The same effect comes from a long query on a replica with hot_standby_feedback=on, a forgotten replication slot, or a stuck prepared transaction.","note":"The main source is the code: open a transaction, go off to an external REST/Kafka call for 30 seconds, then commit. Set idle_in_transaction_session_timeout and statement_timeout, and move external calls OUTSIDE the transaction boundary. One-query diagnosis: SELECT max(age(backend_xmin)) FROM pg_stat_activity.","hook":"The cleaner cannot throw out the boxes while someone sits in the room saying \"I still want to look at them\".","more":"Every row version carries an `xmin`/`xmax`. VACUUM may free a version only if its `xmax` is older than the oldest snapshot that could still see it. That minimum is the xmin horizon: computed from backends' `backend_xmin`, replica xids, `pg_replication_slots.xmin` and hung prepared transactions. A transaction sitting `idle in transaction` for two hours holds the horizon in two ways: under REPEATABLE READ the snapshot lives for the whole transaction, and under READ COMMITTED it is enough that it has already written something — its own xid stays in the active list.\n\nThe consequence is not only file size. Pages with unremovable dead versions are not marked all-visible in the visibility map, so an index-only scan has to visit the heap, and a seq scan reads pages that are 80% garbage. Autovacuum runs normally and writes to the log: `DETAIL: 4200000 dead row versions cannot be removed yet, oldest xmin: 34512` — not an error but a diagnosis.\n\nDefaults: `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` = **0** (never kill; production sets 30s–5min), `autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor` = 0.2 plus a threshold of 50, `hot_standby_feedback` = off, though it gets switched on for the sake of replicas, thereby moving their horizon onto the primary. Monitor: `n_dead_tup` in `pg_stat_user_tables`, `max(now() - xact_start)` over `pg_stat_activity` where `state = 'idle in transaction'`, `age(backend_xmin)`, and inactive `pg_replication_slots`.\n\nWhere it breaks: hold the horizon long enough and the question moves from bloat to wraparound. When the database age reaches `autovacuum_freeze_max_age` (default **200 million** transactions), an anti-wraparound vacuum starts that also cannot freeze anything, and when **3 million** transaction IDs remain before overflow the cluster stops issuing new xids: `database is not accepting commands to avoid wraparound data loss`. Digging out means stopping the load and running VACUUM by hand (single-user mode on older versions). Prevention: an idle-in-transaction timeout, an alert on the age of the oldest snapshot and on inactive slots; a bloated table is cured by `pg_repack`.","quizCorrect":"An open transaction holds a snapshot, and VACUUM cannot remove garbage newer than it","quizWrong":["Such garbage is only cleaned by VACUUM FULL, and autovacuum never touches it under any circumstances at all","idle in transaction locks the whole table against writes","Autovacuum does not start while even one active connection exists"],"picCap":"VACUUM: 0 removed, 4.2M dead not yet removable — the idle-in-tx snapshot holds the xmin horizon"},"ndb-pkuuid":{"q":"You took a UUID (random, v4) as the primary key instead of a bigint. Six months later inserts sagged, indexes swelled and the WAL grew. Why is randomness to blame, and what does UUIDv7 change?","a":"A random key lands in an arbitrary B-tree page: every insert hits its own page — cache misses, page splits, extra full-page writes in the WAL, plus 16 bytes instead of 8 in every index. UUIDv7 keeps the timestamp in the high bits, so values grow monotonically and inserts go to the right edge of the tree, just like a bigint.","d":"A UUID is justified when the id is generated by the client or another service BEFORE the write (idempotency, sharding, no round trip for a sequence) — and then take v7, not v4.","note":"Do not store a UUID in a varchar(36): that is extra bytes and character-by-character comparison instead of the uuid type (16 bytes). UUID.randomUUID() in the JDK is v4 only; v7 comes from a library or is generated in the database. The flip side of v7 is that it reveals the record's creation time — for public ids that is a leak.","hook":"Shelving books alphabetically as they arrive in random order: every time you reach into the middle of the shelf and push the row apart. By date, you just put them at the end.","more":"A B-tree leaf is an 8 kB page, and the index `fillfactor` defaults to **90**. For a monotonic key nbtree has a fastpath for rightmost inserts: the value goes into the rightmost page, and when it overflows the page is split by fillfactor (90/10 rather than in half), one page stays dirty, and that same page is always in the buffer cache. A random v4 picks a page uniformly out of millions: a random read from disk, a 50/50 split, two dirty pages plus a write to the parent node.\n\nThen the WAL joins in. `full_page_writes` is on by default: the first change to a page after a checkpoint writes the entire page into the journal. With a monotonic key that is the same page for the whole `checkpoint_timeout` (default **5min**), with a random one nearly every insert drags along its own 8 kB. It shows directly in the plan: `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, WAL) INSERT ...` gives `WAL: records=4 fpi=4 bytes=33kB` versus `fpi=0`. Plus size: a uuid is stored in 16 bytes (37 if it was mistakenly put in a `varchar(36)`), and those 16 against a bigint's 8 are paid in every secondary index, because the PK is present in each of them.\n\nMonitor: `wal_fpi` and `wal_bytes` in `pg_stat_wal`, insert rows/s, `idx_blks_read` versus `idx_blks_hit` in `pg_statio_user_indexes`, and the size of `pg_wal`. UUIDv7 is standardised by RFC 9562 (48 bits of unix milliseconds in the high bits); PostgreSQL 18 has a built-in `uuidv7()`, and before that the application generates the value.\n\nWhere it breaks: a bulk load of tens of millions of rows with v4 — at some point the index stops fitting into `shared_buffers` (default **128MB**), every insert becomes a random disk read, throughput falls from tens of thousands of rows/s to a few thousand, WAL volume triples, `archive_command` cannot keep up and fills the volume under `pg_wal`, after which the cluster halts with `PANIC: could not write to file ...: No space left on device`. The cure is moving to v7 (or a bigint PK plus a separate unique uuid column for the outside world) and `REINDEX CONCURRENTLY` for indexes that are already bloated.","quizCorrect":"Random keys are inserted into random index pages — splits, cache misses and WAL growth","quizWrong":["It is only about the 36 characters — with the uuid type there is no difference from a bigint whatsoever, neither in space nor in speed","A UUID cannot be indexed by a B-tree, it needs a hash index","Generation is the slow part: SecureRandom is invoked on every insert"],"picCap":"v4 hits random pages: splits and fpi on every insert; v7 and bigint go to the right edge"},"ntest-integrationtx":{"q":"An integration test with @Transactional and a rollback at the end is green, yet in production the same code fails on a constraint and on LazyInitializationException. What does a transactional test hide?","a":"The test shares its transaction with the code and rolls it back at the end: with no commit you see neither flush errors, nor deferred constraints, nor triggers. And the session stays open throughout, so lazy associations load happily — while in production the very same spot throws LazyInitializationException.","d":"The rule: a test must not share its transaction with the code under test — let the service open its own, and verify after flush/clear or through a separate EntityManager.","note":"The flip side of dropping @Transactional is a dirty database between tests: clean it explicitly (TRUNCATE, @Sql, recreating the schema) rather than by rollback. And remember: a rollback test fundamentally cannot check deadlocks, races or unique indexes under concurrency — those need real commits and several threads.","hook":"Testing a car without leaving the garage: the engine starts, but you never found out how it drives.","more":"In Spring, `@Transactional` on a test is handled by `TransactionalTestExecutionListener`: the transaction starts before the method and, with `defaultRollback = true` (the default), is rolled back afterwards. A service with `Propagation.REQUIRED` joins it rather than opening its own. Everything follows from that: `persist()` puts the entity into the persistence context, the insert reaches the database on flush, and the flush happens at commit or before a query (`FlushMode.AUTO`) — no commit means no check of DEFERRABLE constraints, no COMMIT triggers, no FK errors.\n\nA subtlety with the id generator: `GenerationType.IDENTITY` forces Hibernate to execute the insert immediately (the persistence context needs the id), while `SEQUENCE` allows batching until flush — which is why the same defect surfaces at once in one project and only at commit, which the test never reaches, in another.\n\nProduction specifics: `spring.jpa.open-in-view` defaults to **true**, so lazy loading succeeds on an HTTP thread but no longer does inside `@Async`, `@Scheduled` or a Kafka listener. The failure texts: `org.hibernate.LazyInitializationException: could not initialize proxy [Order#42] - no Session` and `ERROR: duplicate key value violates unique constraint \"uk_order_number\"` (SQLSTATE 23505), wrapped into a `DataIntegrityViolationException` and thrown out of the commit — beyond the service's `try/catch`. It is kept under control by counting SQL statements per scenario: `hibernate.generate_statistics=true` catches both N+1 and misplaced transaction boundaries.\n\nWhere it breaks: the release passes a green CI and fails on the first real request — the service gets 23505 on flush and rolls back the whole business operation, including calls already made to external systems. The cure is a test that does not share the transaction with the code: the service is invoked outside the test tx (`TransactionTemplate` or `TestTransaction.flagForCommit()` in Spring, `QuarkusTransaction.requiringNew()` instead of `@TestTransaction` in Quarkus), the result is checked after `em.flush(); em.clear()` or from a separate EntityManager, and the test database is a real one through Testcontainers rather than H2 with different constraint semantics.","quizCorrect":"The test shares the transaction with the code: without a commit you see no flush errors, constraints or lazy loading","quizWrong":["A transactional test always runs on H2, and the difference in constraint semantics between H2 and the production database is where all of these failures come from","@Transactional in a test disables Hibernate validation","The rollback in the test prevents Mockito mocks from firing"],"picCap":"The test shares the tx and rolls back → PASS; in production the commit gives 23505 and LazyInit"},"ntest-testcontainers":{"q":"You brought in Testcontainers — the build went from 4 to 40 minutes, and Postgres starts up in every test class. How should it be done, and why is a static @Container not enough here?","a":"A static @Container means one container per CLASS; a hundred classes is a hundred starts. You need one container for the whole JVM: a singleton (started statically in a shared base class, never calling stop() — Ryuk will finish it off) or .withReuse(true) locally, while you clean the data between tests yourself.","d":"The second multiplier is the Spring context cache: an identical configuration reuses the context, while every unique @MockBean / @TestPropertySource / @DirtiesContext raises a new one.","note":"Spring Boot 3.1+ has @ServiceConnection — it fills in the url/user/password itself instead of a hand-written @DynamicPropertySource; Dev Services do the same in Quarkus. And pin the image by tag (better by digest): \"postgres:latest\" will one day arrive as a new major and the build falls apart without a single commit to the code.","hook":"Do not switch the engine off at every traffic light: start it once and drive all day.","more":"`@Testcontainers` plus a `static @Container` gives one start per CLASS: JUnit 5 raises the container in `beforeAll` and stops it in `afterAll`. A hundred classes means a hundred create/start/stop cycles, and each is not only a `docker run` but also the wait strategy (`Wait.forListeningPort()`), database initialisation and a Flyway migration run: 10–20 seconds per class, which is where the 40 minutes come from.\n\nA singleton is a static block in an abstract base class: the container starts once when the class is initialised and lives until the JVM ends, with the URL passed through `@DynamicPropertySource`. There is no need to call `stop()` — Testcontainers installs a shutdown hook, and anything orphaned is finished off by Ryuk (the `testcontainers/ryuk` sidecar, disabled by `TESTCONTAINERS_RYUK_DISABLED=true`). `.withReuse(true)` requires `testcontainers.reuse.enable=true` in `~/.testcontainers.properties`: on a runner that file does not exist, the flag is silently ignored, and containers marked for reuse are not touched by Ryuk at all.\n\nThe second multiplier is the Spring context cache: `spring.test.context.cache.maxSize`, 32 by default, with LRU eviction. The cache key is built from classes, active profiles, property sources, initialisers and the set of `@MockBean`s; one new mock is a new key, a new context, a new Hikari pool and another Flyway run, while `@DirtiesContext` discards the context outright. Visible at DEBUG on `org.springframework.test.context.cache`: `... size = 32, maxSize = 32, hitCount = 40, missCount = 61` — a missCount comparable to the number of test classes means the cache is not working at all.\n\nWhere it breaks: with reuse the container outlives the run along with its data, and a test that inserted a row yesterday hits `duplicate key value violates unique constraint \"users_email_key\"` today; cured by having the test clean its own state — TRUNCATE of all tables with RESTART IDENTITY in `@AfterEach`, not a container restart.","quizCorrect":"Keep one container for the whole JVM (singleton/reuse) and clean the data between tests","quizWrong":["Put @DirtiesContext on every class so that the container gets reused across all of the test classes in the suite","Replace Postgres with H2 — containers are not needed in CI at all","Start the container in @BeforeEach so that it has no time to grow"],"picCap":"100 classes × a Postgres start = 40 min; one singleton container per JVM = 4 min"},"ncryp-sluchaynost-i-":{"q":"Password reset tokens and one-time codes are generated with new Random(System.currentTimeMillis()) — \"it is random anyway\". Why is that a hole, and how is SecureRandom different?","a":"java.util.Random is a predictable generator (linear congruential): knowing the seed, or a couple of issued values, the next ones are computed exactly, and a time-based seed is found by brute-forcing seconds. SecureRandom draws entropy from the OS and is cryptographically unpredictable — that is what generates tokens, salts, IVs/nonces and keys.","d":"Unpredictability means a strong generator AND sufficient length: a 6-digit OTP falls to a million attempts, so what protects it is not entropy but rate limiting and a short TTL.","note":"In Java: new SecureRandom(), 32 bytes → Base64url; there is no need to seed it by hand. UUID.randomUUID() already uses SecureRandom internally and works as a token, while Math.random()/ThreadLocalRandom never do. The scare stories about \"SecureRandom hanging\" concern old JVMs and /dev/random; on Linux today it does not block.","hook":"A lock with a \"random\" code that changes strictly by the clock: the code is new every time, but the neighbour with the same alarm clock knows it.","more":"`java.util.Random` is a 48-bit linear congruential generator: `seed = (seed * 0x5DEECE66D + 0xB) mod 2^48`, with the high bits of the state handed out. A single `nextInt()` is enough to brute-force the 2^16 missing low bits, a second output filters the candidates — and from there the sequence is computable both forwards and backwards. An explicit time-based seed kills even that: `System.currentTimeMillis()` gives 86,400,000 variants per day, while the real window narrows to a couple of seconds around the timestamp of the email containing the link, that is, to a few thousand checks. `Math.random()` is the same `Random`; `ThreadLocalRandom` is built on SplitMix64, with better statistics, but the function is invertible and the state is likewise recoverable from outputs.\n\nOn Linux `SecureRandom` resolves to the SUN provider's `NativePRNG`: `nextBytes()` reads the non-blocking `/dev/urandom`, while `generateSeed()` uses the source from `securerandom.source=file:/dev/random` in `java.security`. A separate trap is `getInstanceStrong()`: it follows `securerandom.strongAlgorithms=NativePRNGBlocking:SUN` and, in a container with an empty entropy pool, hangs in `NativePRNG$RandomIO.readFully` — and `-Djava.security.egd=file:/dev/./urandom` does NOT fix it, since that property only substitutes `securerandom.source`. Cured by overriding `strongAlgorithms` or by haveged. A reset token is 32 bytes of `nextBytes` in Base64url (43 characters), and the database stores its hash rather than the token itself.\n\nWhere it breaks: the generator was replaced with a strong one while the 6-digit OTP was left unrestricted — 10^6 variants fall to a botnet within hours. In the metrics this is a spike of 4xx on `/verify` with the number of successful confirmations unchanged; cured by an attempt counter per code (five misses and the code burns), a 5-minute TTL, single use and a per-account limit, rather than by making the code longer.","quizCorrect":"Random is predictable from its seed and previous values — secrets are generated with SecureRandom","quizWrong":["Random is not thread-safe, which is where the predictable duplicate tokens come from when several are issued in parallel","SecureRandom differs only in speed, the strength is the same","There is no difference if you seed Random from nanoTime"],"picCap":"The seed window is 4,000 ms around the email time; two Random outputs and the reset token is computed"},"ncryp-ataki-na-reali":{"q":"You verify a webhook signature with computed.equals(headerSignature). The cryptography is all correct, yet the security engineer rejects the review. What is wrong with the comparison?","a":"String.equals and Arrays.equals return at the first differing byte — so the response time depends on how many characters of the signature the attacker has guessed, and it can be recovered character by character in thousands of measurements instead of brute-forcing the whole space. Secrets are compared in constant time: MessageDigest.isEqual(a, b).","d":"The rule is broader than one method: any comparison of a secret (HMAC, token, API key, OTP) must be constant-time, while public data may be compared however you like.","note":"Beware of early exits around it: if (sig.length() != 64) return false before the comparison already leaks the length. And if no constant-time function is at hand, there is a trick: run both sides through an HMAC with a random one-off key and compare the results — then there is no timing left to leak.","hook":"A combination lock that clicks louder on a correct digit: instead of a million attempts you need six lots of ten.","more":"`String.equals()` checks the length and walks the array to the first difference — in HotSpot it is also the `_String_equals` intrinsic with vectorisation, so \"0 characters matched\" and \"20 matched\" differ reliably in time. The attacker does not brute-force the whole signature (16^64 ≈ 2^256 for a hex HMAC-SHA256) but assembles it character by character: 16 variants × 64 positions ≈ 1024 series, each with hundreds of repeats and a minimum over the sample to subtract network noise. The per-character difference is fractions of a nanosecond, but statistically ~100 ns is already distinguishable over a LAN (Crosby, 2009); over the internet you simply need more measurements.\n\n`MessageDigest.isEqual(byte[], byte[])` performs no early exit: it XORs every byte into a `result` accumulator and compares with zero at the end. Only the length leaks, so what you compare should be decoded `byte[]` of fixed size rather than hex strings. Without a constant-time primitive, double HMAC works: run both sides through an HMAC with a random key living in process memory and compare the results however you like.\n\nTwo mandatory points sit alongside. The HMAC is computed over the raw body bytes before JSON parsing: a `@RequestBody Map` with re-serialisation changes the bytes, and the signature stops matching on whitespace and key order. And the timestamp from the header is checked — Stripe sends `Stripe-Signature: t=...,v1=...` with a 300-second window, otherwise one valid request can be replayed forever.\n\nWhere it breaks: while debugging, both signatures get logged (`expected={}, got={}`) — constant time then loses all meaning and the secret travels into Kibana in full; the log should carry only the `key_id` and the fact of a mismatch, while the guessing attack is caught by a counter of 401s on the webhook endpoint broken down by IP: a spike while the number of successful deliveries stays flat.","quizCorrect":"equals stops at the first mismatching byte — the response time reveals the guessed prefix","quizWrong":["You must compare hashes of the signatures, because equals does not work with raw byte arrays in any meaningful way at all","equals compares references, so the signature will never match","It is an encoding problem: the comparison must be done in hex, not Base64"],"picCap":"equals() stops at byte 3, then at byte 4: 1024 measurement series and the signature is assembled"},"nbuil-maven":{"q":"Unit tests are run by surefire in the test phase. Where do integration tests with Testcontainers belong, and why not the same place?","a":"On failsafe: it runs `*IT` in the integration-test phase and, unlike surefire, does not fail the build immediately — it first lets post-integration-test run (shutting the containers down) and fails at verify instead.","d":"Hence the rule for CI: call `mvn verify`, not `mvn test` (integration tests simply will not run) and not `mvn install` (wasted time and rubbish in the runner's ~/.m2).","note":"⚠️ The plugins are separated by naming: surefire takes `*Test` by default, failsafe takes `*IT`/`IT*`. And they are split not for elegance but by the cost of feedback: unit tests are milliseconds on every push, ITs are minutes on a merge. Hang Testcontainers on the `test` phase and developers stop running tests locally at all.","hook":"Surefire is testing a part on the bench, failsafe is starting the whole car; either way you have to switch the engine off, even if the trip went badly.","more":"The plugins differ not in what they run but in when and how they fail. Surefire is bound to the `test` phase, with default includes `**/Test*.java`, `**/*Test.java`, `**/*Tests.java`, `**/*TestCase.java`; on a failure it throws a `MojoFailureException` and nothing further in the lifecycle executes. Failsafe binds two goals: `integration-test` to the phase of the same name (includes `**/IT*.java`, `**/*IT.java`, `**/*ITCase.java`) and `verify` to the `verify` phase. The first throws no exception at all and writes the outcome into `target/failsafe-reports/failsafe-summary.xml`; the second reads that file and fails the build. In between, `post-integration-test` gets to run, shutting down containers and releasing ports; were failsafe to fail immediately like surefire, containers and occupied ports would hang around until the end of the runner.\n\nThe phase order: `test` → `package` → `pre-integration-test` → `integration-test` → `post-integration-test` → `verify` → `install`. `mvn test` stops at the first, so the `*IT` classes physically never ran: the console shows only the surefire summary, and a green build is easily mistaken for a verified one. The flags differ too: `-DskipTests` skips both plugins, `-DskipITs` only failsafe, and `-Dmaven.test.skip=true` does not even compile the tests.\n\nWhere it breaks: failsafe was declared in `<executions>` with only the `integration-test` goal, forgetting `verify` — integration tests fail, nobody reads `failsafe-summary.xml`, and the pipeline stays green for months. The tell in the log: failsafe's `Tests run: ..., Failures: 1` line is present while the final `There are test failures. Please refer to target/failsafe-reports for the individual test results.` is not; cured by both goals in one execution and by calling `mvn -B verify` in CI instead of `mvn test`.","quizCorrect":"In failsafe — it runs *IT at integration-test and fails only at verify, after the environment is stopped","quizWrong":["The same place, in surefire — it is one and the same plugin, the only difference being the name of the file","In a separate profile on the package phase, so as not to interfere with building the jar","In surefire, but with `<skipTests>` in the prod profile"],"picCap":"mvn test does not see *IT; at verify failsafe first stops the containers, then fails the build"},"nbuil-vosproizvodimo":{"q":"Yesterday the build was green, today it is red, and git has no new commits. Where do you start?","a":"Look for unpinned inputs: a SNAPSHOT dependency, a version range (`[1.0,2.0)`), a floating base image tag, a different Maven/Gradle or JDK version on the runner — the code is the same but new artifacts have arrived.","d":"Reproducibility means EVERY input is pinned: release versions instead of SNAPSHOTs, a base image by digest, a wrapper (mvnw/gradlew) in the repository, a fixed JDK.","note":"⚠️ A SNAPSHOT is refreshed on a schedule (once a day by default) — which is why it breaks \"in the morning\"; that is not mysticism but a stale cache in ~/.m2. The `-U` flag forces an update, but the real cure is different: SNAPSHOTs have no place in a release build.","hook":"The recipe is the same — \"flour, water, yeast\" — but the yeast comes from a different shelf every day: sometimes the bread rises, sometimes it does not.","more":"A build is deterministic exactly as far as its inputs are pinned, and there are four classes of them: dependencies, toolchain, base image and the builder itself.\n\nA SNAPSHOT in Maven is not a version but a pointer: the remote repository holds `lib-1.4.0-20260814.101533-117.jar`, and which one arrives is decided by the repository's `<updatePolicy>` — `daily` by default for snapshots, with `-U` forcing it immediately. Hence the classic \"green locally, red on the runner\": `Failure to find com.x:lib:jar:1.4.0-SNAPSHOT ... was cached in the local repository, resolution will not be reattempted until the update interval of nexus has elapsed` — the local `~/.m2` is holding yesterday's copy. A range like `[1.0,2.0)` is resolved at build time, so somebody else's release into Nexus changes your artifact without a single commit. A Docker tag is the same kind of pointer: `eclipse-temurin:17` was 17.0.11 yesterday and is 17.0.12 today, and only `image@sha256:...` saves you. Maven and the JDK on the runner are pinned by the wrapper (`.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties` in the repository) and by `toolchains.xml`.\n\nByte-level reproducibility is the next level: `<project.build.outputTimestamp>` normalises times and entry order in the jar (maven-archiver 3.4.0+, jar-plugin 3.2.0+), and the result is verified with `mvn verify artifact:compare`. In Gradle the same intent is served by dependency locking: `gradle.lockfile` and `--write-locks`.\n\nWhere it breaks: a base image on a floating tag was rebuilt with different tzdata or glibc — tests on date formatting and string sorting fail while `git log` is empty; cured by pinning the digest and by saving `mvn dependency:list` as an artifact of every build, so that yesterday's and today's dependency sets can be diffed in a minute.","quizCorrect":"Unpinned inputs — SNAPSHOTs, version ranges, a floating base image tag — pulled in new artifacts","quizWrong":["That is how transitive resolution works: Maven always takes the freshest available version of every dependency it finds","The local cache went stale, running `mvn clean` before every build helps","The problem is the CI runner, restarting the job is enough"],"picCap":"Commit a1b2c3d is the same, but the SNAPSHOT became -117 and temurin:17 is now 17.0.12: red build"},"ndevo-ci-cd":{"q":"The pipeline deploys with `kubectl apply` from CI. Why are teams moving away from that to GitOps (Argo CD / Flux)?","a":"With a push deploy, the cluster's real state is known only to the last job: someone edits it by hand and nobody notices. In GitOps the desired state lives in git, an agent inside the cluster continuously compares reality against it and clears the drift itself; a rollback is a git revert.","d":"A bonus is access: CI no longer needs credentials for the production cluster (only repo read and image push), and the connection goes from inside the cluster outwards rather than from outside in.","note":"⚠️ GitOps does not solve secrets: what goes into git is not the secret but an encrypted form (SOPS/Sealed Secrets) or a reference to an external store. And \"we never touch it by hand\" only works when direct cluster access is genuinely closed — otherwise the drift comes back.","hook":"Not \"I told you once how it should be\", but a thermostat: it keeps chasing the set temperature by itself, continuously.","more":"In the push model `kubectl apply` is a one-off event: the declared state settles into the `kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration` annotation (or into `managedFields` with `--server-side`), and from that moment nobody compares against it. A manual `kubectl scale`, a hotfix through the dashboard or an image rollback live in the cluster until the next deploy, which silently overwrites them along with somebody's meaningful fix — and git carries no trace of either.\n\nThe pull model is a controller inside the cluster running a loop. Argo CD re-reads the repository on `timeout.reconciliation` (the `argocd-cm` configmap, 180s by default; a GitHub webhook makes it instant), renders the manifests, diffs them against live objects and sets `Synced`/`OutOfSync` plus `Healthy`/`Degraded`. Returning to git is enabled separately: `syncPolicy.automated.selfHeal: true` and `prune: true`, both false by default — without them Argo only shows the drift and does not repair it. Flux does the same through `spec.interval: 1m` and `prune: true` on a Kustomization.\n\nDrift is measured by the `argocd_app_info{sync_status=\"OutOfSync\"}` metric with an alert on \"pending longer than 15 minutes\"; in Flux it is `gotk_reconcile_condition{type=\"Ready\",status=\"False\"}`. Access is inverted: CI needs only an image push to the registry and a tag commit into the manifest repo, the production kubeconfig is not needed at all, and the connection runs from inside the cluster outwards.\n\nWhere it breaks: `selfHeal` was switched on while `replicas` stayed in the manifest alongside a live HPA — Argo restores 3, the HPA sets 7 again, pods flap every three minutes and the application is permanently `OutOfSync`; cured by removing `replicas` from the manifest or by `ignoreDifferences` with `jsonPointers: [/spec/replicas]`.","quizCorrect":"The desired state is described in git, and an agent in the cluster continuously brings reality to it and repairs manual drift","quizWrong":["The agent triggers the deploy from a webhook on push, and everything else stays exactly as it was before with kubectl apply from the pipeline itself","GitOps just means keeping manifests in git instead of a wiki","GitOps replaces CI: builds and tests move into the cluster too"],"picCap":"A manual scale=1 goes unnoticed with push; the agent compares against git every 180 s and restores 3"},"narhi-granicy-i-kont":{"q":"You agreed: the domain module must not depend on Spring or JPA. Six months later it does. How do you catch that in CI rather than at review?","a":"With an architecture test (ArchUnit): rules like \"the domain package does not import org.springframework or jakarta.persistence\" and \"adapters do not reach into each other\" are an ordinary JUnit test that goes red together with the build.","d":"The first line of defence is cheaper — split into separate maven/gradle modules: the domain simply does not see Spring on its classpath; ArchUnit then covers what happens inside a single module.","note":"⚠️ On legacy, enable the rule with an explicit list of exceptions (freeze the current violations), otherwise the first run produces 300 reds and the rule is simply switched off. An ADR is its partner here: the test says \"not allowed\", the ADR explains why it was decided that way.","hook":"A fence instead of a \"keep off the grass\" sign.","more":"ArchUnit analyses bytecode rather than sources: `ClassFileImporter` reads `target/classes` and builds a dependency graph from the constant pool — imports, field types, annotations, calls in method bodies. The rule `noClasses().that().resideInAPackage(\"..domain..\").should().dependOnClassesThat().resideInAnyPackage(\"org.springframework..\")` is an `ArchRule` object, and a field annotated `@ArchTest` inside a class with `@AnalyzeClasses(packages = \"kz.bcc\", importOptions = ImportOption.DoNotIncludeTests.class)` turns it into an ordinary JUnit test that fails with the build.\n\nFor layers there are ready-made `layeredArchitecture()` and `onionArchitecture()`: one call describes the domain, the adapters and the adapter→adapter prohibition. For inherited code there is `FreezingArchRule.freeze(rule)`: current violations are written into the `archunit_store` directory and do not fail the build, while any new one does. Settings live in `archunit.properties` on the classpath: `archunit.freeze.store.default.allowStoreCreation` is `false` by default (the first run is done with `-D...allowStoreCreation=true`), and `archunit.resolveMissingDependenciesFromClassPath` is `true` — switching it off on large modules cuts the run time several-fold. A CI failure looks like this: `Architecture Violation [Priority: MEDIUM] - Rule 'no classes that reside in a package '..domain..' should depend on classes that reside in any package ['org.springframework..']' was violated (7 times)` followed by the list of classes. Worth watching are the number of violation lines and the duration of the arch test.\n\nWhere it breaks: ArchUnit only sees static references in the bytecode, so `Class.forName(\"org.springframework...\")`, configuration by string names and everything Spring slips in through runtime proxies pass the rule by; and while Spring is on the domain's classpath at all, the \"wrong\" code compiles happily. Cured by ordering: the domain is a separate maven module without Spring and Hibernate among its dependencies plus `maven-enforcer-plugin` with `bannedDependencies`, leaving ArchUnit for what happens inside a single module.","quizCorrect":"Write an ArchUnit test forbidding the imports and run it in CI as an ordinary unit test","quizWrong":["Split the modules into separate repositories — then the dependency physically will not compile under any circumstances","Put the rule into a code review checklist and remind everyone at standup","Record the decision in an ADR: once it is written down, nobody will break it"],"picCap":"domain imported jakarta.persistence: review let it through, ArchUnit fails the build"},"nquar-testy":{"q":"You wrote a test with @QuarkusTest, and a colleague says \"that is not an integration test\". What is the difference from @QuarkusIntegrationTest?","a":"@QuarkusTest raises the application in the same JVM as the test — you can @Inject beans and replace them via @InjectMock. @QuarkusIntegrationTest launches the already-built artifact (fast-jar, native, container) from the outside: there is no reaching inside, only HTTP.","d":"@TestProfile changes the configuration and the set of beans for a group of tests, but every new profile is an application restart, so there must be few profiles or the run time spreads out.","note":"@InjectMock requires a normal-scoped bean: on @Singleton it fails with \"not a normal scoped bean\" (cured by QuarkusMock). And only @QuarkusIntegrationTest can prove a native build — @QuarkusTest knows nothing about it.","hook":"One is an inspection with the bonnet open, the other is a test drive of an already locked car.","more":"`@QuarkusTest` raises the application once for the whole run through `QuarkusTestExtension`. The application lives in a separate runtime classloader, and the test class is reloaded by the same loader — which is why `@Inject` on a test field yields a real CDI bean from the running context. `@InjectMock` replaces not the bean itself but what the client proxy sees, hence the limitation: normal-scoped beans (`@ApplicationScoped`, `@RequestScoped`) can be mocked while `@Singleton` cannot, and you get `Invalid use of io.quarkus.test.junit.mockito.@InjectMock - beans with singleton scope are not supported`; cured by `@InjectMock(convertScopes = true)` or by changing the scope.\n\n`@QuarkusIntegrationTest` raises nothing in its own JVM. It looks for the build output — `target/quarkus-app/quarkus-run.jar`, a native binary, or an image if `quarkus.container-image.build=true` — and launches it as a separate process or container. Hence all the restrictions: no `@Inject`, no `@InjectMock`, no access to the application's statics; only REST Assured, the default port `quarkus.http.test-port=8081`, and the base address arriving through `@TestHTTPResource` or the `test.url` system property. The class must be named `*IT` and be run by failsafe in the `verify` phase, otherwise the artifact is not on disk yet and the test fails before starting.\n\nEvery unique `@TestProfile`, like `@WithTestResource`, causes a full application restart. You count them in the log: the line `Installed features: [cdi, ...]` is printed exactly as many times as there were restarts, and each one costs seconds of augmentation plus raising Dev Services again.\n\nWhere it breaks: the team introduces a profile per test class, a three-minute run becomes twenty, and in CI parallel Dev Services additionally fight over ports and the docker socket. Cured by one or two shared profiles, targeted `getConfigOverrides()` instead of new profiles, and reducing `@QuarkusIntegrationTest` to two or three HTTP smoke checks against the built artifact.","quizCorrect":"@QuarkusTest raises the app in the test JVM and lets you inject and mock beans, @QuarkusIntegrationTest hits the built artifact from outside","quizWrong":["The difference is only in start-up speed, the API is completely identical: both raise the application in the same JVM and let the test inject beans","@QuarkusTest does not open an HTTP port, so endpoints cannot be checked with it — controllers are always tested exclusively with @QuarkusIntegrationTest","@QuarkusIntegrationTest is the same @QuarkusTest, only with a database raised"],"picCap":"@QuarkusTest — one JVM and @InjectMock; @QuarkusIT — the artifact from outside, HTTP only"},"nquar-rest-client":{"q":"A service needs to call a neighbouring service over HTTP. Why does nobody in Quarkus write an HttpClient by hand?","a":"There is the MicroProfile REST Client: you describe an interface with JAX-RS annotations and @RegisterRestClient, the build generates the implementation, and the baseUri and timeouts live in configuration rather than in code.","d":"That same interface is a ready-made place for @Retry/@Timeout/@CircuitBreaker and for propagating the token and trace headers; a non-2xx arrives as a WebApplicationException, and a human-readable exception is made with a ResponseExceptionMapper.","note":"The production trap: no connect/read timeout set — threads hang on a dead neighbour, the pool is eaten up, and the one that falls over is you, not them. The timeout is set immediately, not \"later\".","hook":"You do not explain the route to the courier every time — the address is written into the contract, and they take it from there.","more":"An interface with `@RegisterRestClient` is processed at augmentation time: the extension generates the implementation and registers a CDI bean with the `@RestClient` qualifier, so at runtime there is neither reflection nor dynamic proxies — everything is visible to a native build. The address and timeouts live in configuration: `quarkus.rest-client.<config-key>.url`, `.connect-timeout` (15000 ms by default), `.read-timeout` (30000 ms by default), `.scope`; the key is set as `@RegisterRestClient(configKey = \"order-api\")`, otherwise the key becomes the interface's fully qualified name.\n\nErrors: the default MicroProfile mapper is registered with priority `Integer.MAX_VALUE` and throws a `WebApplicationException` on any status of 400 or above, from which the log receives a bare `HTTP 409 Conflict` with no body. Your own `ResponseExceptionMapper<T>` with a lower `@Priority` intercepts earlier, reads the body and turns it into a domain exception. Headers are propagated through `@RegisterClientHeaders` with a `ClientHeadersFactory` or the `org.eclipse.microprofile.rest.client.propagateHeaders=Authorization` property, while `traceparent` is added by `quarkus-opentelemetry` itself. The same interface carries `@Timeout`, `@Retry` and `@CircuitBreaker` from SmallRye Fault Tolerance.\n\nProduction details: the default read timeout of 30 seconds is longer than almost any caller's own timeout, so it is set explicitly. Everything is visible through the `http.client.requests` metrics with `clientName` and `status` tags and through `ft.circuitbreaker.calls.total{circuitBreakerResult=\"circuitBreakerOpen\"}`.\n\nWhere it breaks: the neighbour degrades to 10-second responses, retries multiply the load, connections pile up in the pool, and the 30-second read timeout keeps them alive — the service eats through its workers and starts returning 503 before the neighbour returns anything at all. The second common case is a blocking call of a reactive client on an IO thread: `You have attempted to perform a blocking operation on an IO thread`. Cured by timeouts shorter than the caller's SLA, `@CircuitBreaker` with bounded retries, and deliberate placement of `@Blocking`.","quizCorrect":"You describe an interface with @RegisterRestClient, the build generates the implementation, and the url and timeouts come from configuration","quizWrong":["The implementation is created by reflection at runtime on the first call, which is why the first request is always noticeably slower than the rest","The REST Client works only in reactive mode and requires Mutiny","The URL is baked into the annotation and cannot change without a rebuild"],"picCap":"Left: HttpClient with a hardcoded URL and no timeout; right: OrderApi with config, @Retry and a mapper"},"ngit-force-push":{"q":"After a rebase you push with --force, but meanwhile a colleague pushed their own commit to the same branch. What happens, and how do you avoid erasing their work?","a":"--force bluntly overwrites the remote branch with yours, and the colleague's commit drops out of it. --force-with-lease first checks that the remote is where you last saw it, and rejects the push on any divergence.","d":"The lease is checked against your remote-tracking ref (origin/feature), so a fresh git fetch — including an IDE autofetch — \"renews the receipt\" and the protection evaporates; --force-if-includes or an explicit --force-with-lease=refs/heads/feature:<sha> is safer.","note":"Overwritten commits are usually still alive — in the reflog and locally on your colleague's machine — but the branch and the MR are broken. The rule: force-push only into your own branch, never into main/develop.","hook":"It is \"re-upload the document only if nobody edited it after me\" instead of \"silently paint over someone else's work\".","more":"A push is a request to \"move a ref from one value to another\". An ordinary push requires a fast-forward: the old value must be an ancestor of the new one. `--force` sends the same command without that check, and the server simply rewrites the ref. The colleague's commit is not physically deleted — it becomes unreachable and sits in the object database until `gc` cleans it: `gc.reflogExpireUnreachable` is 30 days, `gc.pruneExpire` is 2 weeks for genuinely dangling objects. On GitHub/GitLab the SHA is still visible in events and the API, and locally it is found through `git fsck --lost-found`.\n\n`--force-with-lease` without an argument takes the \"expected\" value not from the server but from your own remote-tracking ref `refs/remotes/origin/feature`. On divergence the push is rejected with `! [rejected] feature -> feature (stale info)`. The hole is precisely in the data source: any `git fetch` — including IntelliJ's autofetch, which by default polls the remote every few minutes — advances `origin/feature` to the colleague's commit, and the lease starts confirming someone else's work as the last one known to you. `--force-if-includes` (Git 2.30+) closes this differently: it checks that the tip of the remote branch is reachable from your local branch's reflog, that is, that you really saw it and rebased onto it. Enabled once: `git config --global push.useForceIfIncludes true` (`false` by default), after which `--force-with-lease` is automatically strengthened.\n\nWhere it breaks: a shared branch with no protection, `receive.denyNonFastForwards` on the server defaulting to `false`, the colleague pushes at 15:00, you fetch at 15:02 and force at 15:03 — their commit disappears from the branch, and it is discovered a day later when the feature \"rolled itself back\". Cured by a protected branch on develop/master and `push.useForceIfIncludes=true` in the shared config; recovery is simple while the colleague still has C locally — a `git rebase origin/feature` and an ordinary push — and without a local copy the SHA is dug out of `git reflog origin/feature` or the server's events, as long as gc has not collected the object.","quizCorrect":"--force-with-lease rejects the push if the remote branch has moved from the position you saw","quizWrong":["--force-with-lease merges the other commits into your rewritten history by itself, so nothing can ever be lost with it","There is no difference, --force-with-lease is an alias with friendlier output","--force-with-lease blocks any force-push into protected branches"],"picCap":"--force erases commit C; the lease checks origin/feature, but a git fetch renews the receipt"},"ngit-internals":{"q":"Does a git commit store a diff or a full snapshot of the project? And why then can a rebase not physically \"change\" an existing commit?","a":"A commit is a snapshot of the file tree (a tree) plus a reference to its parent and metadata, while git computes diffs on the fly by comparing two snapshots. The SHA is a hash of all of it, so changing the content or the parent means getting a DIFFERENT commit.","d":"Everything else follows: rebase and amend create new commits and move the branch pointer (the old ones live in the reflog until gc), and editing an old commit rewrites every SHA after it — which is exactly why a shared branch must not be rewritten.","hook":"Git is not a log of edits but a shelf of photographs: you work out the difference between frames when you look at them.","note":"Space is saved not by deltas in the model but in storage: identical files are not duplicated (a blob is addressed by its hash), and inside a packfile objects are additionally delta-compressed — while the model stays snapshot-based. And cherry-pick does not \"move\" a commit: it applies a patch and creates a new SHA.","more":"Every object in `.git/objects` is addressed by a hash of the string `<type> <size>\\0<content>`. For a commit the content is plain text: a `tree <sha>` line, zero or more `parent <sha>` lines, `author` and `committer` with timestamps and time zones, a blank line, and the message. `git cat-file -p HEAD` prints it as is. The direct consequence: the hash depends on the tree, the parent, the author and the time — change anything and you get a different object, while the old one stays untouched.\n\nA tree is a list of \"mode, name, sha\" entries. A file that has not changed references the same blob in the new commit, so \"full snapshot\" does not mean \"a copy of the repository\": deduplication happens by content. Delta compression exists, but at the packing level rather than in the data model: `pack.window` = 10, `pack.depth` = 50, `core.bigFileThreshold` = 512m. Renames do not exist in the model at all — `git log --follow` and status infer them heuristically at diff time, governed by `diff.renames` = true and `diff.renameLimit` = 1000; above that limit renames simply stop being detected and the file looks like a delete plus an add.\n\nA rebase therefore works as a re-apply: the diff of a commit against its parent is taken, applied onto the new base, a new commit object with a new SHA is created, and the branch is moved onto it. The old chain stays reachable from the reflog, with `gc.reflogExpire` = 90 days and `gc.reflogExpireUnreachable` = 30 days. `git count-objects -vH` shows loose objects growing after heavy rewriting, until `gc.auto` fires at its threshold of 6700.\n\nWhere it breaks: a branch was rewritten after somebody had already based work on it — on the next `git pull` without rebase the merge drags in both versions, the history gains pairs of commits with identical messages and different SHAs, and the conflicts have to be resolved twice. Cured by discipline: only `revert` on shared branches, rewriting on personal ones, and undoing a bad rebase with `git reset --hard <sha from git reflog>`.","quizCorrect":"A commit stores a full snapshot of the tree, and the diff is computed when you look at it","quizWrong":["A commit stores a diff against the previous one, and that is precisely why the history takes up so little space on disk","A commit stores a diff, and a full snapshot is only made for tags and releases","The SHA is computed from the commit message alone, so the content can change without the hash changing"],"picCap":"Commit = tree + parent; README reuses the same blob, rebase produces a new SHA"},"nalgo-sorting":{"q":"Production fails on list.sort() with \"Comparison method violates its general contract!\". What is wrong with the comparator?","a":"The comparator contradicts itself: it says a < b and b < c, yet also c < a. TimSort only catches this while merging runs — which is why everything \"worked\" on lists shorter than 32 elements, where it uses insertion sort and merges nothing.","d":"The classics are comparison by subtraction (a.count - b.count) with int overflow, subtracting doubles with NaN, or a compare that answers differently for \"equal\" elements depending on argument order.","hook":"Three judges said: Anna beats Boris, Boris beats Vera, Vera beats Anna. You cannot build a league table out of that.","note":"Cured by Integer.compare / Double.compare and Comparator.comparing(...).thenComparing(...), not by arithmetic. The -Djava.util.Arrays.useLegacyMergeSort=true flag does not fix it but silences the check: the ordering will still be rubbish.","more":"`Collections.sort` and `List.sort` end up in `Arrays.sort(Object[], Comparator)`, that is, in TimSort. The constant `MIN_MERGE = 32`: when `nRemaining < MIN_MERGE` a single `binarySort` is run over the whole array and the method returns — there are no merges, and a contradictory comparator quietly produces \"some\" order. From exactly 32 elements the main path kicks in: `minRunLength(32)` returns 16, the array is split into two runs, and they are merged. `mergeLo`/`mergeHi` in galloping mode rely on transitivity; when it is violated, the counter of remaining run elements hits zero early, an explicit check fires and `java.lang.IllegalArgumentException: Comparison method violates its general contract!` is thrown with a stack through `java.util.TimSort.mergeHi`. The same input with `-Djava.util.Arrays.useLegacyMergeSort=true` sorts without an exception — the old merge sort performs no such check — but the ordering stays wrong, so it is a diagnostic rather than a fix.\n\nThe sources of contradiction are almost always the same: `(int)(a.count - b.count)` overflowing on values of opposite sign, subtraction of `double` where `NaN` breaks any ordering and `-0.0` versus `0.0` yields zero instead of a difference, a cascade of `if`s over booleans where the \"both true\" branch returns `1` instead of `0`, and a comparator over a field that changes during the sort — a mutable object, a lazily loaded Hibernate proxy, a `System.currentTimeMillis()` inside `compare`.\n\nWhere it breaks: the tests use a list of 20 elements and production has 5000 — it fails only in production and only on data containing a cycle, and the exception flies out of the middle of a business operation, taking down the whole request rather than merely spoiling the order. In the logs it is a rare spike of `IllegalArgumentException` with an identical `TimSort.mergeHi` frame. Cured by replacing subtraction with `Integer.compare`/`Double.compare` and by building a total order with `Comparator.comparingLong(...).thenComparing(Order::getId)`, where a unique tie-breaker guarantees consistency, plus snapshotting the sorted fields before calling sort.","quizCorrect":"The comparator is intransitive or self-contradictory, and TimSort detects it while merging","quizWrong":["The elements do not override equals, which makes comparing them impossible and the sort throws for that reason","The comparator returned a value outside the -1/0/1 range","A null ended up in the collection and the comparator threw an NPE"],"picCap":"Cycle a→b→c→a: silent at 31 elements, at 32 mergeHi throws violates general contract"},"nalgo-sorting-2":{"q":"You need to sort a 100 GB file with 4 GB of memory. How?","a":"External sorting: cut the file into chunks that fit in memory, sort each one and write it to disk as its own file. Then merge all the chunks in a single pass through a min-heap, always taking the smallest of the heads.","d":"A heap of size k (the number of chunks) makes the merge O(n log k), and memory holds one record from each chunk — the footprint does not depend on the data volume.","note":"The same trick is \"merge k sorted lists\" at an interview and compaction in LSM engines (RocksDB, Cassandra). With too many chunks you run into the file descriptor limit — then merge in several passes.","hook":"A thousand-card deck on a small table: you lay them out fifty at a time, sort each pile, then collect them by taking the lowest card off the tops.","more":"This is mergesort with the splitting phase moved to disk. The first pass is **run generation**: read exactly as much as fits in the heap, sort it in memory (`Arrays.sort` — dual-pivot quicksort for primitives, TimSort for objects) and write the finished run as its own file. 100 GB in 2 GB chunks gives 50 files. The second pass is the **k-way merge**: a `PriorityQueue` of k elements, `poll()` returns the minimum, the next record is read from that same run and `offer()` returns it to the heap. Every record passes through the heap exactly once — hence O(n log k).\n\nThe chunk size is not the heap size. `-Xmx4g` does not mean \"4 GB of data will fit\": a `String` of 20 Latin characters is ~64 bytes (24 for the object plus 40 for the `byte[]`), plus the array reference, plus a copy during garbage collection. A working chunk is taken as 30–50% of `-Xmx`, otherwise full GC starts consuming more time than the I/O itself.\n\nProduction: in GNU `sort` the number of files merged at once is set by `--batch-size`, default 16 — with 50 runs the merge takes two passes instead of one, that is, an extra ~200 GB of reads and writes. The Hadoop analogue is `mapreduce.task.io.sort.factor`, default 10. What to watch is not CPU but iowait and the write speed into the temp directory: a `BufferedReader` with its default 8192 characters pokes the disk in small portions and drops throughput several-fold, whereas a 1 MB buffer for each of 50 runs costs only 50 MB.\n\n**Where it breaks:** on the temp directory. External sorting needs free space of at least the data volume — all runs live until they are merged — and by default they go into `/tmp` (`java.io.tmpdir` in Java, `-T` in GNU sort). The classic is `sort: write failed: /tmp/sortAbc123: No space left on device` an hour in, halfway through the job. Cured by a dedicated temp partition on a large disk, a `File.getUsableSpace()` check before starting, and merging in a single pass (k = the number of runs) so intermediate results are not rewritten a second time.","quizCorrect":"Sort chunks in memory, then merge the chunks with a k-way merge through a min-heap","quizWrong":["Map the file through a MappedByteBuffer and sort it in place — the OS will page out whatever does not fit on its own","Use quicksort — it is in-place, so no memory is needed","Raise -Xmx above the file size, there is no other option"],"picCap":"100 GB at once → OOM; 50 chunks of 2 GB + a min-heap of 50 heads → a single-pass merge"},"nalgo-hashing":{"q":"You spread keys across 16 shards with key.hashCode() % 16. What breaks in production?","a":"A hashCode can be negative — and % then returns a negative index, giving you an ArrayIndexOutOfBounds. And for an object without its own hashCode the value comes from the identity hash, so after a JVM restart the same key moves to a different shard.","d":"Only the hashCodes fixed by the specification are stable across runs: String, Integer, Long and collections of them — for sharding take a string key or an explicit hash (Kafka computes murmur2 over the key bytes).","note":"Math.abs(h) does not save you: abs(Integer.MIN_VALUE) is negative again. The right way is Math.floorMod(h, n), or h & (n-1) for a power of two; & (n-1) is exactly what HashMap uses internally. And an enum's hashCode is identity too — different between restarts.","hook":"You filed documents on shelves by cover colour, and after the move the printer changed the colours — now there is nowhere to look.","more":"`Object.hashCode()` is an identity hash: HotSpot generates it lazily on the first call and stores it in the object header. The algorithm is selected by the `-XX:hashCode` flag, default 5 — a thread-local xorshift, so the number depends on the order of calls in a particular JVM run and has nothing to do with the object's content. `enum` falls in the same bucket: `Enum.hashCode()` is final and also identity, so you cannot shard by it.\n\nThe sign. `%` in Java is a remainder, not a modulus: the sign of the result equals the sign of the dividend, so `\"ORD-2026-0814\".hashCode()` = -1304264694 gives `% 16 = -6`. `Math.abs()` does not help — `Math.abs(Integer.MIN_VALUE)` returns `Integer.MIN_VALUE` itself. The correct forms are `Math.floorMod(h, n)` or `(h & 0x7fffffff) % n`; for a power of two, `h & (n-1)`, as in `HashMap`, which additionally mixes the high bits first: `h ^ (h >>> 16)`.\n\nProduction: Kafka's partitioner does not touch `hashCode` at all — it computes `Utils.murmur2(keyBytes)` over the key bytes and takes `toPositive(hash) % numPartitions`, where `toPositive` is `& 0x7fffffff`. Meanwhile librdkafka (Go, Python, C#) defaults to `consistent_random`, that is, CRC32, and the same key lands in a different partition until you set `partitioner=murmur2_random`. A home-grown implementation fails as `java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index -6 out of bounds for length 16`, and not immediately — only on the first key with a negative hash. The second symptom is quieter: skew, visible in a \"requests per shard\" metric — if one of 16 shards holds 30–40%, the hash is poor or the key is low-cardinality.\n\n**Where it breaks:** after a restart or a redeploy. A key written to shard 3 is looked up in shard 11: a 100% cache miss, duplicates in the database, \"the record exists but the service cannot see it\", and not a single error in the logs. Cured by sharding on a stable byte representation (`key.getBytes(UTF_8)` → murmur3 or CRC32), `floorMod` instead of `%`, and a constant test: a fixed key yields a fixed shard number, failing in CI rather than in production.","quizCorrect":"A hashCode can be negative, and for objects without an override it also changes after a JVM restart","quizWrong":["% only works correctly with powers of two, and 16 is one of them, so there is no problem and the division is fast","hashCode() is always non-negative, and the only problem is collisions","The only problem is speed: % is slower than bitwise operations"],"picCap":"hashCode % 16 = −6 → AIOOBE; identity hash: after a restart the key moved from shard 3 to 11"},"nconc-vthreads":{"q":"You moved the service from a 200-thread pool to virtual threads — and the database (or a neighbouring service) went down. What happened?","a":"The pool was not only an executor but also a limiter: physically no more than 200 simultaneous requests could leave. Virtual threads removed that ceiling — now as many fly out as come in.","d":"Virtual threads are not pooled, they are created per task; the limit must be explicit and tied to the resource — a Semaphore around the call or the connection pool size (HikariCP), not the number of threads.","note":"A second effect: the waiting queue became invisible. It used to live in the ThreadPoolExecutor and show up in metrics; now thousands of vthreads simply wait for a connection — so hang your saturation metric on the semaphore and the connection pool, or you will notice the overload only through timeouts.","hook":"You removed the turnstile at the entrance so nobody would crowd — the crowd just moved inside, to the single till.","more":"A thread pool doubles as a semaphore: a `ThreadPoolExecutor` with 200 threads lets no more than 200 simultaneous calls out, and the rest wait in a `LinkedBlockingQueue`. That was the back pressure — implicit but real. `Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` creates a thread per task and contains no limit whatsoever, so concurrency becomes equal to the incoming traffic.\n\nVirtual threads execute on carriers from a ForkJoinPool: `jdk.virtualThreadScheduler.parallelism` defaults to the core count and `maxPoolSize` to 256. A blocking socket in the JDBC driver parks the virtual thread and frees the carrier, so 20,000 requests really do reach the database at once. Before JDK 24 blocking inside `synchronized` pins the carrier (`-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full`) — and then you hit exactly those 256.\n\nProduction: in Spring Boot 3.2+ `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` switches Tomcat to a virtual-thread executor, and `server.tomcat.threads.max` (default 200) stops limiting anything — the ceiling becomes `server.tomcat.max-connections`, default 8192. HikariCP stays with `maximumPoolSize` = 10 and `connectionTimeout` = 30000 ms, which is why it is the first to fall: `java.sql.SQLTransientConnectionException: HikariPool-1 - Connection is not available, request timed out after 30001ms`. Visible in `hikaricp_connections_pending`: the number of waiters takes off, `hikaricp_connections_acquire` pins to 30 seconds, and the CPU sits idle.\n\n**Where it breaks:** on someone else's resource. Postgres with its default `max_connections = 100` answers `FATAL: sorry, too many clients already`, and it is not only your service that goes down but everyone using that database. With an HTTP neighbour it is the same: they get a multiplied RPS, return 503, and retries finish building the avalanche. Cured by limiting the resource rather than the threads: a `Semaphore` with `tryAcquire(timeout)` (not `acquire()`) around every external call, a sensible connection pool size, hard timeouts and a circuit breaker so the queue of waiters cannot grow forever.","quizCorrect":"The thread pool bounded concurrency; vthreads removed it — you need an explicit semaphore or connection pool limit","quizWrong":["Each vthread opens its own database connection, so the pool stops applying and the limit you configured is ignored entirely","Virtual threads do not work with JDBC, only R2DBC will do","It is pinning: synchronized in the driver occupied all the carrier threads"],"picCap":"The 200-thread pool was the limit; 20,000 vthreads → Hikari max 10 → \"not available after 30000ms\""},"gp2-load-what-to-measure":{"q":"What should a load test measure beyond \"did it hold or not\"?","a":"Three quantities together: throughput (requests per second), latency percentiles (p50/p95/p99) and the error rate. Separately — resource saturation: CPU, memory, connection pool, queue.","d":"One number means nothing: 1000 rps with 30% errors is not 1000 rps. Look for the point where latency rises while throughput no longer does — that is the system's limit.","hook":"A marathon: what matters is not only the speed but the heart rate and the number who dropped out. One result without the other two tells you nothing.","note":"Percentiles from different instances cannot be averaged: a cluster p99 is computed from histogram buckets, not from the mean of the pods' p99s.","more":"The three quantities are linked and each lies on its own. Throughput without the error rate is the rate at which you serve 500s. Mean latency hides exactly the tail people complain about: with a mean of 50 ms the p99 can be two seconds.\n\nThe graph is read by its inflection point. While the system is not saturated, more load raises throughput and latency barely moves. After saturation throughput flattens and latency climbs — that is the limit, and it gets written down as a number rather than as an impression.\n\nCapture resource saturation separately: the queue for the connection pool, the task queue length, collector pauses. The limit usually rests not on the CPU but on the pool — and then \"add more pods\" does not help.\n\nAt an interview you will be asked how p99 differs from the mean and why percentiles from several instances cannot be averaged.","quizCorrect":"Throughput, latency percentiles and the error rate together","quizWrong":["Mean response time: it is more stable than percentiles and easier to explain to the business on a chart","Only the maximum requests per second — everything else can be derived from it","CPU utilisation: if it is not maxed out, the system is handling the load fine"],"picCap":"The limit is where rps flattens and p99 climbs. The mean hides the tail."},"gp2-load-mistakes":{"q":"Typical load-test mistakes that make the result lie","a":"The test hits one row and everything sits in cache; the load generator itself became the bottleneck; the run is shorter than JIT warm-up and pool filling; the load is flat while production comes in spikes.","d":"A trap of its own is coordinated omission: if the client waits for a response before the next request, it will not send load at the moment the service is struggling, and the percentiles come out prettier than reality.","hook":"A test drive around an empty car park. Excellent car — until you take it onto a real road at rush hour.","note":"Tools that hold a set rate regardless of responses (wrk2, Gatling) cure coordinated omission; ab and plain wrk do not.","more":"The most common mistake is unrealistic data. The test hits the same identifier, everything sits in the database cache and the application cache, and the number comes out three times better than the real one. The second most common miss is the load generator on the same host as the service: they share the CPU, and what you measure is their sum.\n\nA short run lies too: the JIT has not compiled the hot code, connection pools are not filled, caches are cold. A minute of warm-up and only then the measurement is the usual rule.\n\nCoordinated omission is a subtle trap. If the client sends the next request only after a response, then at the moment the service stalls the load drops by itself. As a result the worst latencies never enter the sample. Tools like wrk2 and Gatling can hold a set rate regardless of responses — and that is the cure.\n\nWhere it breaks: the test passes with a flat stream while production falls over at the morning peak, because real load arrives in bursts.","quizCorrect":"The test warms the same key, so everything answers from cache","quizWrong":["Too long a run: after the JIT has warmed up the results become optimistically inflated","Different data in every request — because of it the result cannot be reproduced a second time","Load in spikes instead of a flat stream: a flat one reflects production behaviour more accurately"],"picCap":"Cache, a shared host and waiting for responses — three ways to get a beautiful untruth."},"gp2-scoped-value":{"q":"ScopedValue instead of ThreadLocal — what changes?","a":"The value is visible only inside the block where it was bound, and cannot be reassigned from within. When the block ends it disappears on its own, so there is nothing to forget to clean up.","d":"ThreadLocal is designed for a long-lived pooled thread: there it must be cleared by hand, otherwise the value leaks into the next task. With a million virtual threads that model is also expensive in memory.","hook":"A visitor badge at the entrance: valid while you are inside and taken back on the way out by itself. An employee pass you have to remember to hand in.","note":"Child tasks inside a StructuredTaskScope see the bound value automatically — there is no need to pass it separately.","more":"ThreadLocal assumes the model \"a thread lives long and is reused\": the value is set at the start of a task and must be removed at the end. Forget, and the next task from the pool reads someone else's data — a classic source of leaks and confused user identities.\n\nScopedValue inverts the control: the value is bound for the duration of a block, cannot be reassigned inside it, and disappears on exit without any finally. Child tasks inside a StructuredTaskScope see it automatically.\n\nThe practical difference shows at scale: a million virtual threads with a ThreadLocal means a million separate value maps, whereas ScopedValue is built as an immutable stack of bindings and is shared between threads.\n\nWhere it breaks: a library reads a ThreadLocal internally while you moved the call onto another thread — the context never arrived, and the failure looks random.","quizCorrect":"The value lives only inside the block and needs no manual cleanup","quizWrong":["It is automatically carried into any thread you create, including pools of platform threads","ScopedValue can be reassigned from inside the block, which is what makes it handier than a ThreadLocal","There is no real difference: it is a renamed ThreadLocal with shorter syntax"],"picCap":"Bound to a block — gone on exit. Nothing to clean, because there is nothing to forget."},"gp2-record-patterns":{"q":"What do record patterns give you, and what is deconstruction for?","a":"They take a record apart right inside the type check: `if (o instanceof Point(int x, int y))` immediately gives you x and y with no getters. Patterns nest inside one another.","d":"It works only with records: the compiler knows their canonical component order. An ordinary class has no such decomposition — there you would have to guess which getters count as components.","hook":"You do not carry the parcel around whole: you open it and take out what is inside, without asking the courier about every item.","note":"Add a component to a record and every pattern with a fixed number of variables stops compiling. That is a good thing: it shows you every place that takes it apart.","more":"Deconstruction works because a record has a canonical component order known to the compiler. It is the compiler that substitutes the `x()` and `y()` calls for you, and checks the types at compile time while it is at it.\n\nPatterns nest: `case Line(Point(var x1, var y1), Point p2)` takes a nested structure apart in one step. That noticeably shortens code that parses events and expression trees — where there used to be a cascade of instanceof plus casts.\n\nAn important limitation: an ordinary class has no such decomposition. The compiler has no way of knowing which getters count as components or in what order, so the syntax is available to records only.\n\nAt an interview you will be asked what happens if a component is added to a record — old patterns with a fixed number of variables stop compiling, and that is a good thing.","quizCorrect":"They take a record apart into components right inside the type check","quizWrong":["They work with any class that has getters: the compiler matches them to components by itself","They replace equals and hashCode, comparing objects component by component on every match","They exist only to shorten the code and have no effect whatsoever on the compiled bytecode"],"picCap":"The compiler knows the record's component order and substitutes the getters itself."},"gp2-switch-exhaustive":{"q":"When must a switch over types be exhaustive, and what does when give you?","a":"For a sealed hierarchy the compiler requires every variant to be covered, or a default to be written. A branch like `case Circle c when c.r() > 10` adds a condition on top of the type.","d":"Exhaustiveness is protection for the future: add a new subtype to a sealed hierarchy and every switch without a default stops compiling, showing the places that need updating. That is exactly why a default is often harmful here.","hook":"A to-do list where every item must have a tick. Add an item and the list itself reminds you it is empty.","note":"null in a switch over types matches no pattern and gives an NPE: you need an explicit case null branch.","more":"Exhaustiveness of a switch over types rests on sealed: the compiler knows the complete list of subtypes and can verify that all of them are covered. If even one is not covered and there is no default, it is a compile error.\n\nThat is precisely why a default in such a switch is more often harmful than useful. With one, adding a new subtype passes silently and the new variant quietly falls into the catch-all branch. Without one, the compiler itself shows every place that needs updating — a cheap way to forget nothing.\n\nA `when` branch attaches a condition on top of an already-matched type: first the match, then the predicate. If the condition fails, the search continues down the following branches, so the order of branches with `when` matters.\n\nWhere it breaks: null in a switch over types matches no pattern and throws an NPE unless you write an explicit `case null`.","quizCorrect":"For a sealed hierarchy the compiler requires every variant to be covered","quizWrong":["A default is always mandatory, otherwise a switch over types will not compile in Java at all","The when branch is checked before the type, so the condition may reference the variable before the cast","Exhaustiveness is checked at runtime: an uncovered type throws a MatchException on the call"],"picCap":"Without a default the compiler shows every place that forgot the new subtype."},"gp2-sql-window-rank":{"q":"row_number, rank and dense_rank — how do they differ on equal values?","a":"row_number always gives consecutive numbers. rank gives the same number for ties and then skips the next one. dense_rank also ties them but skips nothing.","d":"Hence the practical rule: a \"top 3 by sales\" through row_number cuts exactly three rows, while through rank it may return four if two sellers share third place.","hook":"A podium with two second places. rank leaves third empty, dense_rank does not, and row_number simply hands out 1-2-3.","note":"The order within ties is undefined for row_number — for stable pagination a unique key is added to the ORDER BY.","more":"The difference shows precisely on duplicates. Take three sellers with totals of 100, 90, 90. row_number gives 1, 2, 3 — and the order within the tie is undefined and may change between runs. rank gives 1, 2, 2, and the next one gets 4. dense_rank gives 1, 2, 2, and the next one gets 3.\n\nHence the choice follows the meaning of the task. Need exactly N rows for a page — row_number, but with a stable sort on an extra key, otherwise the pagination jumps. Need an honest sports table with gaps in the places — rank. Need levels with no holes — dense_rank.\n\nSeparately, remember ntile: it cuts into equal buckets and distributes the remainder in a non-obvious way on small result sets.\n\nAt an interview you will be asked why a \"top 3\" through rank can return four rows.","quizCorrect":"rank skips numbers after ties, dense_rank does not","quizWrong":["row_number gives the same number for equal values and then skips the following number entirely","All three behave identically and differ only in which databases support them","dense_rank numbers consecutively while row_number ties rows with equal values"],"picCap":"On duplicates rank skips a number, dense_rank does not, row_number never notices them."},"gp2-sql-window-frame":{"q":"What is a window frame, and how does ROWS differ from RANGE?","a":"The frame defines which rows inside the group take part in the calculation. ROWS counts by row numbers, RANGE by the sort values, so equal values fall into the frame as a whole.","d":"The default frame for aggregates with ORDER BY is RANGE up to and including the current row, and on duplicates it gives something other than what people expect. A moving average is almost always asked for with ROWS.","hook":"A carriage window: ROWS is \"five seats in a row\", RANGE is \"everyone who got off at this station\", and there may be ten of them.","note":"ROWS 6 PRECEDING is seven rows, not seven days: with gaps in the data a calendar window cannot be computed that way.","more":"The frame answers the question \"which rows take part in the calculation for the current row\". Without an ORDER BY inside the window the frame is the whole group, and the aggregate is identical for every row. With an ORDER BY the default becomes RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW — a running total.\n\nThe trap is that RANGE works on the sort values. If several rows share a date, all of them count as \"the current row\" and enter the frame as a whole: the running total on those rows will be identical rather than stepwise.\n\nROWS counts by position and produces no such effect. A seven-day moving average is written as ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW — and that is seven rows, not seven calendar days; if the data has gaps, the difference matters.\n\nWhere it breaks: the report adds up on test data with no duplicates and diverges on real data.","quizCorrect":"ROWS counts by row numbers, RANGE by values","quizWrong":["The default frame is the entire group, so an ORDER BY inside the window has no effect on the result","RANGE counts by row numbers, while ROWS pulls in every row with an equal value","A frame can be set only for aggregates; rank and row_number do not support one"],"picCap":"RANGE counts by value, ROWS by position. On duplicates those are different answers."},"gp2-log-levels":{"q":"How do you choose a log level, and what must never go into a log?","a":"ERROR — something broke and a human is needed; WARN — it healed itself but looks suspicious; INFO — notable business events; DEBUG — details for an investigation. Passwords, tokens and personal data must never be written.","d":"A good sign: if nobody goes to investigate an ERROR, it is not an ERROR. Logs with the level word inside the text (\"ERROR: all fine\") break filtering — the level must be a field, not part of the string.","hook":"A fire alarm. If it screams at every piece of toast, nobody will run for a real fire.","note":"One exception logged and rethrown upwards produces three ERROR entries in different places — log where you handle it.","more":"A level is an addressee, not a volume. ERROR means \"a human is needed right now\"; if nobody comes for it, the level was chosen wrongly and the alerts lose their value. WARN means \"the system coped by itself, but it is worth a look during working hours\": a retry fired, degradation kicked in. INFO is notable domain events: an order was created, a payment went through. DEBUG is detail for an investigation, usually off in production and switched on selectively.\n\nWhat must not be in the logs: passwords, tokens, card numbers, personal data. The rule is simple — logs travel to a shared store whose access is broader than the database's.\n\nThe level must be a separate field of a structured log rather than part of the text: otherwise filtering by level stops working, and a line like \"ERROR: everything is fine\" spoils the query.\n\nWhere it breaks: an exception is logged and immediately rethrown upwards — one error becomes three ERROR entries in different places.","quizCorrect":"ERROR — it broke and a human is needed, WARN — it healed itself","quizWrong":["It is more convenient to write the level into the message text — that way it is visible even without a structured log","Leave DEBUG on in production: extra lines cost nothing and investigations go faster","Personal data in logs is acceptable as long as the log is stored inside the company perimeter"],"picCap":"A level is an addressee. If nobody comes for an ERROR, the alerts have lost their value."},"gp2-log-mdc":{"q":"What is the MDC for, and how does it behave in a thread pool?","a":"The MDC puts request context — a trace identifier, the user — into a field of every log line, so that the history of one request can be gathered. It is stored in a ThreadLocal.","d":"Because of the ThreadLocal the context does not travel by itself into another thread, a CompletableFuture or a reactive chain — it has to be carried over explicitly. And it must be cleared in a finally, otherwise the next task from the pool inherits somebody else's identifier.","hook":"A cloakroom ticket covering all your things at once. Move to another thread and the ticket does not come with you — the things belong to nobody.","note":"The MDC does not travel through CompletableFuture or reactive operators: the context is copied inside a task wrapper.","more":"The MDC is a key-value map that the logger mixes into every line. Into it go the trace identifier, the request identifier, sometimes the user. The point is to gather a request's whole history by a single value, even if it passed through a dozen classes.\n\nThe MDC is stored in a ThreadLocal, and all its limitations follow from that. Move to another thread through an ExecutorService, a CompletableFuture or a reactive operator and the context does not come along, leaving log lines without an identifier. Cured by a task wrapper that copies the map, or by the tracing library's own facilities.\n\nThe second mandatory rule is clearing it in a finally. The thread returns to the pool carrying somebody else's context, and the next request is logged under the previous one's identifier; during an incident investigation that leads you entirely astray.\n\nAt an interview you will be asked how to carry context across an asynchronous boundary.","quizCorrect":"It puts the request identifier into a field of every log line","quizWrong":["The MDC travels to another thread along with the task, because it is not stored in a ThreadLocal","It replaces distributed tracing entirely: it also shows the request's path between services","There is no need to clear the MDC — the thread pool resets the context itself when the thread comes back"],"picCap":"Across a thread boundary the context is carried by hand, and cleared in a finally without fail."},"gp2-regex-backtracking":{"q":"Why can a regular expression hang a service on a short string?","a":"Nested quantifiers of the form `(a+)+b` force the engine to try an exponential number of splits. At forty characters that is already minutes — a ReDoS attack.","d":"There are three cures: remove the nesting of quantifiers, use possessive quantifiers or atomic groups, and never let a user-supplied pattern into a regex. Limiting the input length is also worth doing.","hook":"Trying keys in someone else's door: while one fits, it is quick. When none fits, you go through the whole keyring.","note":"A pattern must be tested on strings that almost match: the catastrophe happens where there is no match.","more":"Java's regular expression engine is a backtracking one. Meeting `(a+)+b` on a string of nothing but `a`, it tries every way of splitting the string between the outer and inner quantifier. The number of splits grows as a power of two, so forty characters already give billions of steps and a hundred hangs the thread forever.\n\nThe sign of a dangerous pattern is nested quantifiers or alternation with overlapping branches: `(a|aa)+`, `(\\s*\\S*)+`. Patterns should be tested not only on matching strings but on almost-matching ones: the catastrophe happens precisely when there is no match.\n\nThere are three cures: rewrite without nesting, use a possessive quantifier `a++` or an atomic group `(?>...)`, and limit the input length. And never assemble a regex from user input.\n\nWhere it breaks: validating an email address from the internet is the classic source of ReDoS.","quizCorrect":"Nested quantifiers produce exponential backtracking","quizWrong":["The problem is the multiline flag being on: without it parsing always runs in linear time","It is the string length: Java's engine is simply not designed for strings longer than a few thousand characters","The regex is recompiled on every call, and it is the compilation that eats all the time"],"picCap":"The catastrophe happens where there is NO match: the engine tries every possible split."},"gp2-time-types":{"q":"Instant, LocalDateTime and ZonedDateTime — when do you take which?","a":"Instant is a moment on the time axis, for events and timestamps. LocalDateTime is a date and time with no zone, for \"10:00 local\". ZonedDateTime is a moment together with the rules of a specific zone.","d":"LocalDateTime does not identify a moment in time: without a zone there is no saying when it happened. Storing it in the database for an event is the most common mistake, and it surfaces when the service moves to another region.","hook":"\"At 15:00\" and \"in three hours\" are different. The first depends on where you are standing, the second does not.","note":"Duration and Period are not interchangeable: a day is not always 24 hours, and on a daylight-saving switch they diverge.","more":"The three types answer different questions. Instant is \"when did this happen\" on a shared axis, with no calendar attachment at all; that is what goes into event timestamps. LocalDateTime is \"what the clock on the wall shows\", with no zone; fine for a shop's opening hours, but it is not a moment in time. ZonedDateTime is a moment plus the rules of a specific zone, daylight-saving transitions included.\n\nIn practice: a past event is an Instant or a timestamptz in the database. A future arrangement is a local time plus a zone identifier. A duration between moments is a Duration; a difference in calendar units is a Period, and they are not interchangeable: a day is not always 24 hours.\n\nA separate subtlety is OffsetDateTime: it stores an offset but not the zone's rules, so future dates are as unreliable with it as with a bare offset.\n\nWhere it breaks: a LocalDateTime is put into the database for an event, and then the service moves to another region.","quizCorrect":"Instant is a moment, LocalDateTime is a time without a zone","quizWrong":["LocalDateTime unambiguously identifies a moment in time, because it is always interpreted as UTC","ZonedDateTime and Instant are interchangeable: both store an offset and differ only in output","Instant stores the date and time in the server's zone, so the value drifts when the service moves"],"picCap":"LocalDateTime is not a moment in time. For an event that is a bug that surfaces on a move."},"gp2-time-utc-trap":{"q":"\"Store everything in UTC\" — when does that rule do harm?","a":"For past events UTC is correct. For future meetings in local time it is not: daylight-saving rules change, and the recorded UTC drifts away from the promised \"14:00 in Almaty\".","d":"So future events are stored as a local time plus a zone identifier, and the moment is computed at display time. And the identifier is of the form Asia/Almaty, not the offset +05:00 — an offset goes stale along with the law.","hook":"A train ticket \"at 14:00 local\". Change the clocks in the country and the time on the ticket stays while the moment of departure shifts.","note":"A stale time-zone database in an old image gives the wrong time after the next reform — tzdata is updated along with the image.","more":"For the past UTC is impeccable: the moment happened, the offset is known, there is nothing to argue about. For the future a dependency on the law appears. A government moves the daylight-saving boundaries or changes the zone entirely — and a UTC value computed in advance stops matching the promised \"14:00 local\".\n\nSo future events are stored as a pair: local date-time plus a zone identifier of the form Asia/Almaty. The moment is computed at display time or when the reminder fires, using the current tzdata.\n\nThe identifier matters more than the offset. An offset of +05:00 is a snapshot that goes stale; an identifier references rules that are updated along with the library. Updating tzdata, incidentally, is part of operations too: an old image with a stale zone database gives the wrong time after the next reform.\n\nAt an interview you will be asked how to store a recurring reminder for \"every Tuesday at 9:00\".","quizCorrect":"Daylight-saving rules change retroactively","quizWrong":["UTC loses precision on future dates, because leap seconds cannot be predicted in advance","The problem is only in display: the moment is stored correctly and the user is shown the wrong zone","An offset of +05:00 is more reliable than a zone identifier, since it does not depend on the tzdata database"],"picCap":"A zone identifier references the rules, an offset of +05:00 is only a snapshot."},"gp2-chaos":{"q":"Why break production on purpose, and where do you start?","a":"To test not the code but the assumptions: that timeouts are set, retries are bounded, alerts fire, and the on-call engineer knows what to do. You start with a hypothesis and a small blast radius.","d":"An experiment with no hypothesis and no abort button is not chaos engineering but an outage. The usual first step is not in production at all: kill one pod on a staging environment and see whether monitoring noticed.","hook":"A fire drill. The point is not that something burns but to find out whether the alarm works and whether people know the way out.","note":"The first experiment is not run in production: on staging you immediately see whether monitoring notices the failure at all.","more":"Chaos engineering tests not the code but assumptions about the environment: that timeouts are set everywhere, that retries are bounded by a budget, that a fallback path engages when a dependency fails, that the alert reaches the on-call engineer and that they know what to do.\n\nAn experiment is built as a hypothesis: \"if one database replica is killed, the service keeps answering by reading from the remaining ones, and latency grows no more than twofold\". Then comes a minimal blast radius, watching the metrics, and an abort prepared in advance. Without a hypothesis and an abort button it is simply an outage.\n\nStarting in production is not required and is usually harmful: the same experiment on staging already shows whether monitoring notices the failure at all. Production is added once staging stops producing news.\n\nWhere it breaks: the failure is discovered only through a customer complaint — meaning what was tested was not the system but luck.","quizCorrect":"To test assumptions about timeouts, retries and alerts","quizWrong":["To find bugs in the code that neither unit nor integration tests can catch at all","To check performance under peak load — it is an accelerated load test run against production","To show the business that the system falls over, in order to win time for technical debt and refactoring"],"picCap":"The first step is usually not production: kill a pod on staging and see whether monitoring noticed."},"gp2-contract-compat":{"q":"Which contract changes are safe and which break the client?","a":"Safe: adding an optional field, adding a value to an enum in the response, relaxing input validation. Breaking: removing or renaming a field, making an optional field mandatory, narrowing a type.","d":"Postel's law works in both directions: the server must tolerate extra fields on input, and the client must not fall over unknown fields in a response. Without the second, even a safe addition breaks old consumers.","hook":"A power socket: adding another one next to it is fine, but rearranging the pins in the old one and every appliance in the house stops working.","note":"The worst change is the same field name with a new meaning: it is caught by neither the schema, nor the compiler, nor a contract test.","more":"Compatibility comes in two directions. Backward — a new server understands old clients. Forward — an old client does not break on a new response. API evolution needs both, and the second is provided by client discipline: ignore unknown fields.\n\nSafe changes: adding an optional field to a request, adding a field to a response, relaxing input validation, adding a new endpoint. Breaking ones: removing or renaming a field, making an optional field mandatory, narrowing a type or range, changing a field's meaning while keeping its name — the last is the worst, because neither a schema test nor the compiler catches it.\n\nAdding a value to an enum is safe on output only if clients handle unknown values; on input it is almost always safe.\n\nAt an interview you will be asked how to roll out an incompatible change — the answer is several steps plus a period during which the server understands both formats.","quizCorrect":"Adding an optional field is safe, removing one is not","quizWrong":["Renaming a field is safe if you keep the old name in the documentation and warn people in chat","Making an optional field mandatory is safe: old clients were always sending it anyway","Any change requires a new version, so the notion of a compatible change is meaningless"],"picCap":"A change of meaning under the same name is caught by neither the schema nor the compiler."},"gp2-feature-flags":{"q":"What kinds of feature flags are there, and why must each one have a deadline?","a":"A release flag lives until the rollout and then dies; an experiment flag until the measurement ends; an operational kill switch lives permanently. The first two, left without a deadline, turn into eternal forks.","d":"The price of a flag is a doubling of execution paths: every else branch needs tests and will one day be switched on by someone. Hence the practice of creating the removal ticket together with the flag itself, rather than \"sometime later\".","hook":"Scaffolding. While the building goes up it is needed; after handover it is simply in the way and one day falls on someone.","note":"A dozen flags produce a combinatorial explosion nobody checks in full: usually only the enabled path is tested.","more":"Flags differ by lifetime, and everything else follows from it. A release flag separates shipping the code from enabling the feature: it lives for days and is removed right after the rollout. An experiment flag lasts while the measurement runs and is removed along with the conclusion. An operational kill switch is permanent: turn off a heavy report under load, bypass a broken dependency.\n\nThe price of a flag is a doubling of execution paths. The else branch must be tested too, otherwise one day it gets switched on and turns out to be broken. A dozen flags produce a combinatorial explosion nobody checks in full.\n\nHence the practice: a flag has an owner, a deadline and a removal ticket created at the same time as the flag itself. Flags without a deadline are technical debt that looks like flexibility.\n\nWhere it breaks: flag configuration diverges between environments, and the bug reproduces only in production.","quizCorrect":"A release flag dies after the rollout, a kill switch lives forever","quizWrong":["All flags should be kept forever: that way the old behaviour can be restored at any moment without a deploy","An experiment flag is removed only after both branches show identical metrics for a quarter","A flag does not double the execution paths, because the disabled branch is not compiled into the bytecode"],"picCap":"The removal ticket is created together with the flag, not \"sometime later\"."},"gp2-reactive-when":{"q":"When is reactive programming genuinely justified, and when does it only get in the way?","a":"It is justified where there are many simultaneous I/O waits on a small number of threads: gateways, aggregators, streaming. It gets in the way where the load is CPU-bound or where the whole chain ends up behind a blocking driver anyway.","d":"With virtual threads the main argument for reactive — saving threads — has all but disappeared. What remains is its other half: back pressure and operators over event streams, and that is something virtual threads do not provide.","hook":"A waiter versus a conveyor belt. While the guests wait for their order the waiter serves others; but the vegetables he still chops with his own hands.","note":"A single blocking call inside the chain occupies an event-loop thread entirely and takes down the serving of every request at once.","more":"Reactive solves two different problems, and it helps to separate them. The first is not holding a thread while waiting for I/O: a thousand simultaneous requests are served by a dozen threads. The second is operators over an event stream: merging, windows, back pressure.\n\nVirtual threads closed the first problem more simply: blocking code is written as usual and a thread is cheap. So the argument \"reactive for the sake of saving threads\" is largely exhausted on Java 21, and the question becomes whether you need the other half.\n\nWhere reactive gets in the way: CPU-bound work gains nothing from it; debugging becomes harder because the stack does not reflect the execution path; a single blocking call inside the chain freezes the event loop and takes the whole service down. And if the database driver is blocking, the entire construction ends up behind it anyway.\n\nAt an interview you will be asked how back pressure differs from rate limiting.","quizCorrect":"Many simultaneous I/O waits on a small number of threads","quizWrong":["Heavy CPU computation: a reactive pipeline parallelises it across cores by itself","Any service on Java 21 or newer: virtual threads work only inside reactive chains","When strict processing order is needed — reactive guarantees it even under concurrency"],"picCap":"Saving threads is no longer the argument. The argument is operators and back pressure."},"craft-naming":{"q":"What makes a name good — and why is `List<User> list` a bad one?","a":"A name answers \"what is this by meaning\", not \"how is it built\": `activeUsers` rather than `list`, `daysSinceLogin` rather than `d`. The type is already written in the declaration; there is no point duplicating it in the name. A good name removes the need for a comment beside it.","d":"↳ A working sign of an unfinished name: beside the declaration you feel the urge to explain what it is. A second: a name that is awkward to say out loud on a call. A third: a name you would have to change if the implementation changed — `userArray` needs editing when you move to a list, `activeUsers` does not.","more":"**A name is a variable's interface.** The reader must grasp the meaning without looking into the declaration or reading twenty lines further down.\n\n**What to remove from a name:**\n- the type: `userList` → `users`, it is in the declaration anyway\n- filler words: `data`, `info`, `manager`, `helper`, `processor`\n- abbreviations, except ones standard in the domain: `cnt`, `usr`, `tmp`\n\n**What to add:** units of measurement and state. `timeout` → `timeoutMs`, `users` → `activeUsers`, `price` → `priceMinorUnits`. An order-of-magnitude error from seconds instead of milliseconds is a classic, and it is cured by a name rather than a comment.\n\n**Name length is proportional to scope.** `i` in a three-line loop is fine. `i` as a class field is not. The reverse holds too: `theUserWhoWasJustAuthenticated` inside a two-line lambda is noise.","quizCorrect":"It talks about the structure rather than the meaning: which users, and what for","quizWrong":["Generic names are cheaper to maintain: if the meaning of the collection changes, the name needs no editing anywhere in the file","`List` is an interface, and a variable name is supposed to state the concrete implementation","Hungarian notation requires a type prefix, and here the type is written as a suffix"],"picCap":"A name answers \"what by meaning\". The type is already in the declaration, units go in the name."},"craft-comments":{"q":"When is a comment appropriate, and when is it a sign of a problem?","a":"A bad one retells the code (`i++; // increment i`) — that kind goes stale first and starts lying. An appropriate one carries what is absent from the code: why this order was chosen, which invariant holds, where a magic constant came from, a link to a bug or a clause of a standard.","d":"↳ Two canons argue here, and that makes a good interview answer. Clean Code: a comment is a sign of defeat, you failed to express it in code. \"A Philosophy of Software Design\" objects — some knowledge is inexpressible in code: intent, invariants, rejected alternatives; the demand that \"code is self-documenting\" simply loses that knowledge. The boundary runs like this: what the code does — in code, why it does it that way — in words.","more":"**An argument between two canonical books, both of which sit in this library.**\n\n**Clean Code:** every comment is a small failure. You could not express it in code, so you wrote words. Comments are neither compiled nor tested, so they inevitably drift from the code and start lying.\n\n**A Philosophy of Software Design:** taken that far, it does harm. There is knowledge that is physically absent from the code — why this algorithm was chosen, which alternatives were rejected and why, which invariant must hold. Banning comments does not remove that knowledge; it throws it out of the project along with the author.\n\n**The practical boundary:**\n\n| a comment is needed | not needed |\n|---|---|\n| why this decision was made | what the line does |\n| an invariant that holds | a retelling of the method name |\n| where a constant came from | commented-out old code |\n| a link to a bug, RFC or standard clause | a `// TODO` with no date or author |\n\n**The most harmful comment** is one that was correct when written and never updated during an edit. Code can be read and checked, while such a comment is believed.","quizCorrect":"The one that explains \"why\" rather than retelling \"what\"","quizWrong":["Any comment is harmful: if the code needs explaining, it should be rewritten until the explanations become unnecessary","Only javadoc on public methods is appropriate, comments inside a body are forbidden","A comment is needed where the code is more complex than three nested conditions"],"picCap":"Two canons argue. The boundary: \"what\" in code, \"why\" in words."},"craft-function-size":{"q":"\"A function should be four lines long\" — a rule or a cargo cult?","a":"As an end in itself, a cargo cult. Splitting only pays off when the piece has a name and a meaning of its own. Otherwise you get twenty one-line methods: each is clear while the whole is not — to read one scenario you jump up and down the file.","d":"↳ Ousterhout gives the opposite criterion: a good module is deep — a simple interface over a complex implementation. A small method that cannot be understood without its neighbours is small but shallow: its interface is exactly as large as its body, and the gain is zero. The measure is not lines but how much you have to hold in your head to call it.","more":"**The metric is not length but the volume of what you must hold in your head.**\n\nOusterhout introduces the notion of depth: a good module has a simple interface and a complex implementation — it hides a lot and requires you to know little. A bad one is small and shallow: to call it you have to know almost its entire body.\n\n```\ndeep:      read(buffer)        ← a one-line interface,\n                                 inside: buffering, errors, retries\n\nshallow:   setNameField(s)     ← an interface the size of the body,\n                                 hides nothing\n```\n\n**The sign that you crossed the line** is the \"shattered logic\" effect: to understand one scenario you have to open five methods and assemble them in your head. The split reduced each piece and increased the total complexity.\n\n**How to answer at an interview.** Name both positions and the selection criterion: split when the piece acquires a name behind which you can stop thinking about the contents. If no name appears, the split is cosmetic.","quizCorrect":"Splitting is worth it only where the piece has a name and a meaning of its own","quizWrong":["Yes, it is an iron rule: a function longer than five lines almost always hides a second responsibility and must be split","No, length does not matter at all: readability is determined solely by variable names","The rule works only in languages without closures, in Java it is inapplicable"],"picCap":"Split where a name appears behind which you can stop thinking about the contents."},"craft-flag-param":{"q":"What is wrong with the signature `save(user, true)`?","a":"At the call site you cannot see what true means — you have to go to the declaration. Usually a flag means the method does two different things with an `if` on it inside. Cured by two named methods or an enum instead of a boolean.","d":"↳ The same smell squared is a second flag: `save(user, true, false)` reads like a riddle. An enum fixes the readability of the call but not the cause: if there are still two branches inside, the method has two responsibilities and its tests will have to be written in pairs.","more":"**A flag in a signature usually means two functions fused into one.**\n\n```java\n// before\nsave(user, true);          // what is true?\n\n// after — names instead of a flag\nsaveAndNotify(user);\nsaveSilently(user);\n\n// or an enum, if there are many modes\nsave(user, Notify.SKIP);\n```\n\n**Why this is not nit-picking:** flags multiply. A second mode appears, so does a second parameter, and the call turns into `save(user, true, false, null)`. Every combination needs its own test, and the meaningful combinations are usually half as many as the possible ones.\n\n**When a flag is acceptable:** when it does not switch behaviour but is data. `setVisible(boolean)` sets a value rather than choosing between two algorithms.","quizCorrect":"At the call site the meaning of true is invisible, and inside there are usually two different branches","quizWrong":["Boolean in Java is a wrapper object, so every call performs autoboxing that is noticeable on a hot path","Java cannot overload a method on boolean, there would be a signature conflict","The flag prevents the method from being static and therefore complicates testing"],"picCap":"A flag usually means two functions in one. Names instead of a boolean."},"craft-dry-real":{"q":"Is DRY \"do not copy code\"? What was actually meant","a":"DRY is about knowledge: every piece of knowledge must have a single, unambiguous representation in the system. Two identical fragments describing different rules that will change independently are not a violation. One rule written three different ways is a violation, even when the texts do not match.","d":"↳ Hence the practical step: before extracting a shared method, ask whether this is one piece of knowledge or a coincidence. A hasty merge creates coupling where there was none: the rule changes for one caller, the method is shared — and a flag parameter appears, then a second. Duplication is cheaper than the wrong abstraction: a copy is visible, while a wrong abstraction has to be untangled.","more":"**In the original:** \"Every piece of knowledge must have a single, unambiguous, authoritative representation within a system\". About knowledge, not about characters.\n\n**Not a DRY violation**, even though the code matches:\n```java\n// the discount rule for retail\nif (order.total() > 10_000) discount = 5;\n// the discount rule for wholesale — coincidentally the same\nif (order.total() > 10_000) discount = 5;\n```\nTomorrow retail changes to 7% while wholesale stays. A shared method here glues together things that must live apart.\n\n**A DRY violation**, even though the code differs: the order retention period is written as a number in the code, as a string in the config and as a picture in the documentation. One piece of knowledge, three representations, diverging on the first edit.\n\n**The check before extracting a shared method:** if this rule changes, will it change for all callers at once? Yes — extract it. No — keep the copy.\n\n**Sandi Metz's rule:** duplication is cheaper than the wrong abstraction. A copy is visible and easy to delete; a wrong abstraction has to be untangled first.","quizCorrect":"About a single representation of knowledge, not about repeated text","quizWrong":["About repeated text: any two matching fragments longer than three lines must be extracted into a shared method, or the edits will diverge","About every class having exactly one reason to change","About a ban on copying code between modules, while inside a module copying is fine"],"picCap":"DRY is about knowledge. Duplication is cheaper than the wrong abstraction."},"craft-broken-windows":{"q":"\"Broken windows\" in code — what is the metaphor about, and what do you do with it?","a":"One unrepaired ugly piece lowers the bar for the next: if it is already like that here, mine can be too. Code rots not in a leap but by accumulating small \"good enoughs\". The practice is to fix the small thing at once, while it is still small, rather than filing a ticket for later.","d":"↳ The flip side comes from the same book — the \"good enough\" program. You cannot polish forever either: there is a deadline and a user who needs it today. The difference is that \"good enough\" is a deliberate decision with a known cost, while a broken window is not a decision at all.","more":"**A metaphor from criminology:** a building with one unrepaired window acquires broken ones faster than an intact one. Not because the window matters, but because it announces that nobody is watching here.\n\nCode is the same: one four-hundred-line method with commented-out chunks and a `tmp2` inside explains to everyone who follows what the bar is around here.\n\n**What to do about it in practice:**\n- fix it the moment you notice, if it is a matter of minutes\n- do not start a \"big refactoring\" — it will never land, and the window stays\n- if there is no time to fix it, leave a trace: a ticket linking to the line, not a `// FIXME` with no date\n\n**The flip side is the \"good enough\" program.** From the same book: quality is a project parameter alongside the deadline, and it is set rather than maximised. A throwaway prototype does not have to be beautiful. The difference from a broken window is that here the decision was made and its cost is known.","quizCorrect":"Small sloppiness lowers the bar and drags the next one along","quizWrong":["That a system with accumulated defects is cheaper to rewrite from scratch than to fix: the cost of edits grows faster than the cost of new development","That bugs should be fixed in order of arrival, otherwise the queue is never cleared","That without a code style check in CI a team arrives at four different formattings within a quarter"],"picCap":"It rots not in a leap but by accumulating \"good enough\"."},"craft-debt-argue":{"q":"How do you justify time for technical debt to someone who does not write code?","a":"Not \"the code is ugly\" but in their units: how much time the team loses on every task in that area, how many incidents came from it, how much the estimates for similar tasks have grown. Then a concrete piece with a price and an effect, not \"give us a sprint for refactoring\".","d":"↳ A strong move is tying it to what has already happened: two incidents, both from the same module. Embedding it in the flow also works: not a separate sprint but the rule \"if we touch the module, we leave it a little better\" — that needs no separate approval and creates no branch living for a month.","more":"**Mistake number one is speaking in your own units.** \"There is high coupling here\" is not an argument to someone outside the code; it is a complaint about taste.\n\n**Translating into understandable units:**\n\n| instead of | say |\n|---|---|\n| \"the code is bad\" | \"tasks in this module consistently take twice the estimate\" |\n| \"we need refactoring\" | \"three incidents this quarter came from here\" |\n| \"it must be rewritten\" | \"here is a five-day piece that removes two thirds of the edits here\" |\n\n**What strengthens it:** tying it to something that already happened. An incident everyone remembers convinces better than a forecast.\n\n**What works without approval** is the boy scout rule: you touch a module for a task and leave it a little better. It needs no dedicated time, creates no month-long branch and does not compete with features in planning.\n\n**What not to do:** ask for \"a sprint for tech debt\". It looks like a pause in delivering value and is cut first, and a long refactoring branch additionally diverges from main.","quizCorrect":"Translate it into lost time, incidents and growing estimates","quizWrong":["Explain the architectural substance: once the manager understands why coupling is bad, they will find the team time to fix it themselves","Build refactoring into the estimate of every task without mentioning it separately","Collect static analysis metrics and show the trend in coverage"],"picCap":"Translate into lost time and incidents. The scout rule needs no approval."},"craft-readable-test":{"q":"What makes a test readable, and why does that matter more than brevity?","a":"One idea per test and a speaking name: from the name and the failure it must be clear what broke, without reading the body. Inside, three parts — arrange, act, assert. A test is read at the moment it goes red, and usually not by whoever wrote it.","d":"↳ Hence the dislike of clever loops and shared fixtures: they save lines and eat comprehensibility. Duplication in tests is cheaper than in production code — a test has no tests of its own, and you have to make sense of it under pressure, with the build red and a release waiting.","more":"**A test is read at the worst moment** — when it has gone red, the build is stopped and the author is on holiday. Everything else follows from that.\n\n**A name as a sentence:** `refuses_order_when_balance_is_lower_than_total` — from the red line in CI it is already clear what broke. `testOrder2` forces you into the body.\n\n**Three parts, visible to the eye:**\n```java\n// arrange\nvar user = userWith(balance(100));\n// act\nvar result = service.order(user, price(150));\n// assert\nassertThat(result).isRejected();\n```\n\n**One idea per test.** Not \"one assert\" literally — three checks of a single fact are fine. What is bad is a test checking creation, debiting and notification all at once: when it fails you cannot tell what exactly broke, and having fixed the first you learn about the second only on the next run.\n\n**Duplication in tests is more acceptable.** A test has no tests of its own. A shared fixture across twenty tests saves lines and turns every failure into an investigation.","quizCorrect":"One idea per test and a name that makes clear what broke","quizWrong":["Maximum reuse: shared fixtures and parameterisation remove duplication, which makes the test shorter and clearer","Full branch coverage: once all branches are covered, readability no longer matters","No mocks: a test with a real database is always clearer than one with stubs"],"picCap":"One idea per test. Duplication beats a shared fixture across twenty tests."}},"terms":{"Concurrency|Thread":{"term":"Thread","def":"A thread is a separate track of code execution; a program has many of them, running in parallel or taking turns.","hook":"A separate lane on a track: many runners, each in their own lane, sometimes taking turns.","more":"**Thread** — an OS scheduling unit; in the JVM (HotSpot) every Java thread was, until recently, a **native** OS thread with its own stack (`-Xss`, typically ~512K–1M) and registers, but a shared heap. The OS scheduler switches between them (preemptive multitasking).\n\n**Why:** parallelism and non-blocking I/O waits. **Pitfalls:** threads are expensive (stack memory + context switching), so you use **pools** (`ExecutorService`). For massive I/O, Java 21+ has **virtual threads** (Project Loom) — cheap and runnable by the thousands on one carrier."},"Concurrency|Race condition":{"term":"Race condition","def":"A race is when two threads hit the same data at once, and the result depends on who got there first.","hook":"Two hands reaching for the last cookie: the outcome depends on whose hand gets there first.","more":"**Race condition** — correctness depends on the **timing** of how threads interleave. Under the hood the problem is non-atomic operations: even `i++` is a read-modify-write of three steps, and two threads can clobber each other's result (lost update). Same goes for check-then-act (`if(map.get()==null) map.put()`).\n\n**Why it matters:** these bugs are flaky and don't reproduce under a debugger. **Pitfalls:** the fix is atomicity — `synchronized`, `Atomic*`, `ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent`, or immutability. `volatile` does NOT help here: it's about visibility, not atomicity."},"Concurrency|volatile":{"term":"volatile","def":"A marker on a field: a write by one thread is immediately visible to others (visibility + ordering), with no stale values.","hook":"A loud public notice board: one person posts, everyone instantly sees the latest, no stale copies.","more":"**volatile** gives two guarantees: **visibility** (a write is immediately visible to other threads, a read always comes from memory rather than a cache/register) and a **reordering barrier** around the access (via memory barriers, half-fences). A volatile write establishes happens-before with a subsequent read.\n\n**Why:** stop flags (`volatile boolean running`), double-checked locking, publishing references. **Pitfalls:** it does NOT provide **atomicity** for compound operations — a `volatile int` still loses increments; for counters you need `AtomicInteger` or `synchronized`."},"Concurrency|happens-before":{"term":"happens-before","def":"A relation from the Java memory model guaranteeing that one action's result is visible to another thread and won't be reordered across the boundary. Established by volatile, synchronized, join, etc.","hook":"A guarantee of 'this first, then that, and it's visible': without it the compiler/CPU may reorder and hide changes."},"Concurrency|synchronized":{"term":"synchronized","def":"A block/method that lets only one thread in at a time — the rest wait at the door.","hook":"An airplane lavatory: one person goes in, locks it — the rest queue at the door.","more":"**synchronized** — mutual exclusion via an object's **monitor** (intrinsic lock): on entry a thread acquires the lock (on the instance for a method, on `.class` for static, or on an explicit object in a block), and releases it on exit. Only one owner at a time; the lock is **reentrant** (the same thread can re-enter).\n\n**Why:** critical-section atomicity + happens-before (visibility) in one shot. **Pitfalls:** coarse locking reduces parallelism; acquiring two locks in different orders → **deadlock**; keep the section short and don't call foreign code while holding the lock."},"Concurrency|Lock":{"term":"Lock","def":"A lock: a thread takes it, does its work, and releases it; more flexible than synchronized (tryLock, timeouts).","hook":"The restroom key at a cafe: take it — you're in, return it — the next person goes.","more":"Under the hood, **Lock** (java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock, usually ReentrantLock) is an object with an acquisition counter and a queue of waiting threads (built on AQS). A thread calls `lock()`, does its work, then `unlock()`.\n\nWhy: more flexible than `synchronized` — it offers `tryLock()` (acquire only if free), `tryLock(timeout)`, interruptible waiting, and fair locks. **Pitfalls:** you MUST release in a `finally`, otherwise an exception leaves the lock jammed forever. If `synchronized` is enough, use it — it's simpler."},"Concurrency|Deadlock":{"term":"Deadlock","def":"A deadlock: two threads each hold a lock and wait forever for each other's lock.","hook":"Two people in a doorway: each waits for the other to go first — and both stand there forever.","more":"**Deadlock** happens when thread A holds lock X and waits for Y, while thread B holds Y and waits for X — both hang forever. All four Coffman conditions must hold (mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait).\n\n**How to avoid it:** always acquire locks in one global order; use `tryLock` with a timeout and rollback. Pitfalls: a thread dump (`jstack`) shows the deadlock explicitly — \"found 1 deadlock\". Most often it's fixed by ordering acquisitions."},"Concurrency|Atomic":{"term":"Atomic","def":"Classes like AtomicInteger change a value in one indivisible step, without locks or races.","hook":"A subway turnstile: click — instantly +1, no one can squeeze into the middle of the count.","more":"**Atomic** classes (AtomicInteger, AtomicLong, AtomicReference) rely on the hardware **CAS** (compare-and-set) instruction: \"if the value is still old — set it to new\". The JVM spins this in a loop until it succeeds — without locks (lock-free).\n\nWhy: counters, flags, accumulators are faster than locks under moderate contention. **Pitfalls:** only a single variable is atomic, not a group — for several fields you need a lock. Under heavy contention CAS stalls (many retries); then `LongAdder` is better."},"Concurrency|Thread pool":{"term":"Thread pool","def":"A thread pool: a ready crew of workers you hand tasks to, instead of creating a thread every time.","hook":"A loading crew on shift: you hand them boxes — you don't hire new people for each box.","more":"**Thread pool** keeps a fixed set of pre-created threads and a task queue. Threads loop: take a task, run it, come back for the next — the thread is reused rather than recreated.\n\nWhy: creating a thread is expensive (~1MB stack, a system call); a pool removes that overhead and caps parallelism. **Pitfalls:** a small pool + blocking tasks = everything stalls; an unbounded queue = OOM. Size it to the workload type (CPU-bound ≈ number of cores, IO-bound — more)."},"Concurrency|ExecutorService":{"term":"ExecutorService","def":"A pool manager: it accepts tasks via submit and hands them out to free threads itself.","hook":"A taxi dispatcher: you throw in an order — it assigns it to a free driver itself.","more":"**ExecutorService** — the standard interface over a pool. `submit(task)` puts a task on the queue and immediately returns a `Future`; a free pool thread picks it up. Created via `Executors` or `new ThreadPoolExecutor(...)`.\n\nWhy: it separates \"what to run\" from \"how and where\". **Pitfalls:** always `shutdown()` on stop, otherwise threads keep the JVM from exiting; `Executors.newFixedThreadPool` has an unbounded queue — prefer an explicit `ThreadPoolExecutor` with a bounded queue and a rejection policy."},"Concurrency|Future":{"term":"Future","def":"A claim ticket for a task's result that's still being computed; .get() waits and hands you the answer.","hook":"A coat-check tag: the coat isn't ready yet, but the ticket lets you collect it when it is.","more":"**Future** — a \"claim ticket\" for the result of an async task. `submit()` hands it back immediately, before the computation finishes; `get()` blocks the calling thread until the result is ready and returns it (or throws the task's exception, wrapped in ExecutionException).\n\nPitfalls: `get()` with no argument waits forever — use `get(timeout)`; `cancel()` doesn't always work. For composing callbacks and chains, **CompletableFuture** is better — a plain Future can only wait."},"Concurrency|Virtual threads":{"term":"Virtual threads","def":"Lightweight Java 21 threads: there can be millions, and the JVM parks them on blocking almost for free.","hook":"Folding cots instead of bunks: you can stack millions of sleepers, barely taking up space.","more":"**Virtual threads** (Project Loom, Java 21) — threads managed by the JVM, not the OS. On a blocking call (IO, sleep) the JVM **parks** the virtual thread, freeing the underlying platform thread (carrier) for another task.\n\nWhy: write simple blocking code while keeping millions of concurrent tasks — a replacement for reactive style. **Pitfalls:** they give no speedup for CPU-bound work; `synchronized` can \"pin\" the thread to its carrier (being fixed in recent JDKs) — for critical sections prefer Lock. No pool needed for them."},"Concurrency|ConcurrentHashMap":{"term":"ConcurrentHashMap","def":"A thread-safe Map implementation with locks per bin (not the whole map) and atomic methods (compute/merge/putIfAbsent). Scales better than synchronizedMap under concurrent writes.","hook":"A reference book with separate locks on sections: different threads write to different parts without blocking each other."},"Concurrency|Starvation":{"term":"Starvation","def":"Starvation: a thread keeps yielding to others and never gets its resource or CPU time.","hook":"A polite person in line: lets everyone go ahead and stays hungry until closing time.","more":"**Starvation** — a thread works correctly but systematically fails to get a resource: others keep overtaking it. Causes: unfair locks, low thread priority, a resource held by \"greedy\" neighbors.\n\nDifference from deadlock: here nobody is permanently stuck — one particular thread just never gets time/resource. **Fix:** fair locks (`new ReentrantLock(true)`), fair queues, capping hold time. Pitfalls: fairness lowers throughput — enable it only when starvation actually causes harm."},"Concurrency|CAS":{"term":"CAS","def":"Compare-And-Swap — an atomic CPU instruction: \"if the slot still holds the expected value, replace it with the new one, otherwise leave it alone\". The basis of all lock-free structures and Atomic classes.","hook":"Compare-and-swap: I'll change it only if no one touched anything behind my back.","more":"CAS is non-blocking: a thread doesn't sleep, it retries in a loop (**spin**). \n- Plus: no deadlock, no context switch on light contention. \n- Minus: under high contention there are many wasted cycles (busy-wait). \n- In Java: `compareAndSet` on Atomic*, `VarHandle`, inside `ConcurrentHashMap`."},"Concurrency|ABA":{"term":"ABA","def":"A CAS flaw: the value changed A→B→A in the meantime, and CAS \"passes\" as if nothing happened, even though the object's state really did change. Dangerous for reference-based structures (stacks, lists).","hook":"You come home — the door's the same, but while you were out, strangers lived in your apartment.","more":"CAS checks only the current value, not the history of changes. \n- The fix is a **version counter**: change (value+stamp) in one operation. \n- In Java: `AtomicStampedReference` (value+int stamp) and `AtomicMarkableReference` (value+boolean). \n- A common trap in hand-rolled lock-free stacks/queues."},"Concurrency|False sharing":{"term":"False sharing","def":"False sharing: two independent variables sit in the same cache line (~64 bytes), and a write to one invalidates the other core's cache. Performance drops with no logical connection at all.","hook":"Two roommates: every time one of them shifts, the other has to move out.","more":"Logically the threads don't conflict, but the **MESI** protocol bounces the cache line between cores (cache-line ping-pong). \n- The fix is padding / alignment: in Java the `@Contended` annotation (`jdk.internal.vm.annotation.Contended`, needs the `-XX:-RestrictContended` flag). \n- This is how `LongAdder` works — it spreads counters across different lines."},"Concurrency|Work-stealing":{"term":"Work-stealing","def":"A ForkJoinPool scheduling strategy: each thread has its own task deque; once its own are done, a thread \"steals\" tasks from the other end of another's queue. Balances load without a shared queue.","hook":"I take my own tasks off the top, and when I'm bored, I grab others' off the bottom so no one sits idle.","more":"A thread takes its own tasks from the head (LIFO — warm cache); stealing happens from the tail (FIFO — fewer conflicts). \n- This underlies `parallelStream`, `CompletableFuture` (the default `commonPool`), and virtual threads. \n- The trap: a blocking call inside a task \"eats up\" a carrier thread and breaks the balance — inside an FJP you need a `ManagedBlocker`."},"Concurrency|CompletableFuture":{"term":"CompletableFuture","def":"A Future you can compose without blocking: chains with `thenApply/thenCompose`, joining with `thenCombine`, error handling with `exceptionally/handle`. An async pipeline instead of `future.get()`.","hook":"I don't stand at the stove with a timer — I leave a note: \"when it's ready, do this next\".","more":"**thenApply** — transforms the result (`T→U`); **thenCompose** — flatMap, when the function itself returns a CompletableFuture (avoids the nesting `CF<CF<U>>`). \n- **thenCombine** waits for two independent futures. \n- **exceptionally** catches an error, **handle** sees both the result and the exception. \n- By default it runs in `ForkJoinPool.commonPool` — for blocking IO, pass your own executor."},"Concurrency|Semaphore":{"term":"Semaphore","def":"A permit counter: `acquire()` takes a pass (blocks if none are left), `release()` returns one. Limits the number of threads entering a section or accessing a resource simultaneously.","hook":"A bouncer with N wristbands: lets the first N in, the rest wait until someone leaves and returns a band.","more":"Unlike a Lock, a semaphore is **not tied to an owner**: a different thread can call release than the one that called acquire. \n- A semaphore with 1 permit ≈ a mutex, but without reentrancy and without a notion of owner. \n- Typical uses: a connection pool, rate-limiting, capping parallelism. \n- It can be fair (`fair`) — a FIFO queue of waiters."},"Concurrency|CountDownLatch":{"term":"CountDownLatch","def":"A one-shot wait barrier: the counter starts at N, each `countDown()` decrements it, `await()` holds threads until the counter reaches zero. Once at zero, it can't be reopened.","hook":"The start of a race: we wait until all N participants check in, and only then give the common signal.","more":"The key difference from CyclicBarrier: a latch is **one-shot** and separates roles (some count down, others wait), while a barrier is cyclic and symmetric. \n- A common case: the main thread waits for N services to initialize; or N workers wait for a start signal (latch=1). \n- For reuse, take a CyclicBarrier or Phaser."},"Concurrency|BlockingQueue":{"term":"BlockingQueue","def":"A thread-safe queue, the core of the producer-consumer pattern: `put` blocks when it's full, `take` — when it's empty. Passes data between threads without manual wait/notify.","hook":"A conveyor with a buffer: hit the ceiling — stop, belt empty — wait, it all synchronizes itself.","more":"Implementations: \n- **ArrayBlockingQueue** — a ring buffer, fixed size, a single lock. \n- **LinkedBlockingQueue** — node-based, optionally unbounded, two locks (head/tail) — higher throughput. \n- **SynchronousQueue** — capacity 0, direct hand-off (no buffer). \n- It underlies `ThreadPoolExecutor` as the task queue."},"Concurrency|Pinning":{"term":"Pinning","def":"Pinning a virtual thread (Java 21) to its platform carrier when it can't detach on blocking. The main causes are `synchronized` and native calls (JNI).","hook":"A light thread normally hops off its carrier when blocking, but inside synchronized it's pinned to it.","more":"Normally, on blocking IO a virtual thread releases its carrier — scaling to millions of threads. With pinning, the carrier is busy and doesn't serve others → the benefit is lost, and pool starvation is possible. \n- Fix: replace `synchronized` with `ReentrantLock`. \n- In Java 24 (JEP 491) pinning on synchronized is largely eliminated. \n- Diagnostics: `-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full`."},"Concurrency|ThreadLocal":{"term":"ThreadLocal","def":"A variable with a separate value per thread: one object — but each thread has its own copy. Handy for holding non-shared context (user, transaction) without passing it through parameters.","hook":"A personal locker for each employee: one key design for all, but the contents are each person's own.","more":"The main trap is **thread pools**: a thread is reused, and without `remove()` the value leaks into the next task + a memory leak (the value lingers in `Thread.threadLocals`). \n- Rule: in a pool always do `try/finally { tl.remove(); }`. \n- In virtual threads, `ScopedValue` (JEP 487) is preferable — immutable and leak-free."},"Concurrency|Livelock":{"term":"Livelock","def":"Threads aren't blocked and are actively working, but due to mutual yielding they keep changing state and make no progress. CPU is at 100%, progress is zero.","hook":"Two people in a hallway step the same way, then in sync the other way — endlessly.","more":"Differences within the family: \n- **Deadlock** — everyone is frozen, waiting on each other, 0% CPU. \n- **Livelock** — actively thrashing, but not progressing, 100% CPU. \n- **Starvation** — the thread is alive but chronically denied a resource (greedy neighbors/priorities). \n- Fixed with randomized back-offs and asymmetry (different retry orders)."},"Spring|DI":{"term":"DI","def":"Spring creates objects itself and wires them where they're needed — you don't call new by hand.","hook":"A wall socket: a lamp doesn't run a wire all the way to the power plant — you just plug it in.","more":"**DI** (Dependency Injection) is a pattern where an object doesn't create its dependencies via `new` but receives them from the outside. In Spring the container instantiates dependencies itself and supplies them through the constructor (recommended), a setter, or a field.\n\nWhy: loose coupling, easy testing (inject a mock), centralized assembly of the object graph. **Gotchas:** field injection (`@Autowired` on a field) hurts testability and hides dependencies — prefer **constructor injection**; circular dependencies signal poor design, not a reason for `@Lazy`."},"Spring|Бин (Bean)":{"term":"Bean","def":"An object managed by Spring: it creates it, keeps it in the container, and hands it out on request.","hook":"A Lego brick in Spring's box: it grabs the one you need and slots it where you ask.","more":"A **Bean** is an object whose lifecycle is managed by Spring: the container creates it, configures it (injects dependencies), stores it, and destroys it when needed. It's declared via `@Component`/`@Service`/`@Bean` or XML.\n\nWhy: a single point of control over objects and their wiring. **Gotchas:** the default scope is **singleton** — one instance per container, so a bean must be **stateless** and thread-safe; mutable state in a singleton bean = race conditions. For per-request state use `prototype`/`request` scope."},"Spring|IoC-контейнер":{"term":"IoC container","def":"Spring's box where all beans live and which decides who gets what and when.","hook":"Spring's left-luggage office: all objects sit inside and are handed out by ticket on request.","more":"The **IoC container** (ApplicationContext) is the core of Spring, implementing Inversion of Control: object creation and wiring are delegated to the framework. It scans the configuration, builds the bean graph, resolves dependencies, and hands out ready-made objects by type/name.\n\nWhy: you describe \"what you need\" rather than \"how to assemble it by hand.\" **Gotchas:** beans are created eagerly at startup by default — misconfigurations surface immediately (a plus); a heavy context slows startup. Don't drag `ApplicationContext` around your code (the Service Locator anti-pattern) — rely on injection."},"Spring|@Component":{"term":"@Component","def":"A marker on a class: \"Spring, register me as a bean and manage me yourself.\"","hook":"Like a \"hire me\" sticker on a box — Spring sees it and puts the class on the bean shelf itself.","more":"**@Component** marks a class as a **bean** candidate: during component scanning Spring finds such annotated classes, creates **one instance** (singleton by default), and puts it into the **ApplicationContext**, managing its lifecycle and dependencies.\n\n- Specializations: **@Service**, **@Repository**, **@Controller** — the same @Component but with layer semantics.\n- Gotchas: the class must fall under **@ComponentScan**; otherwise no bean is created. For third-party classes (from libraries) use **@Bean** in a @Configuration."},"Spring|@Autowired":{"term":"@Autowired","def":"A marker \"inject the right bean here\" — Spring finds it and plugs it in for you.","hook":"Like a socket labeled \"a bean goes here\" — Spring finds the plug and inserts it for you.","more":"**@Autowired** asks the container to **inject a dependency**: Spring looks for a bean of the matching type in the context and supplies it into a field, constructor, or setter.\n\n- The best option is **constructor injection**: the field can be `final`, the bean can't be created without its dependency, and it's easy to test. On a single constructor the annotation isn't even required.\n- Gotchas: if there are **several** matching beans you get an error — you need **@Qualifier** or **@Primary**. Field injection complicates testing and hides dependencies."},"Spring|Прокси (Proxy)":{"term":"Proxy","def":"A substitute wrapper object around a bean to add logic before and after a method call.","hook":"Like a secretary in front of a boss: first logs the call, then puts it through, then sees you out — and you thought you were calling the chief directly.","more":"A **Proxy** is a wrapper class generated at runtime: from the outside it looks like your bean, but every method call first passes through **extra logic** (transaction, cache, logging) and only then delegates to the real object.\n\n- Implementations: **JDK proxy** (via an interface) or **CGLIB** (a subclass of the class).\n- Gotchas: **self-invocation** (this.method() inside the same bean) bypasses the proxy — annotations like @Transactional/@Cacheable won't fire. Proxies also don't work on `private`/`final` methods."},"Spring|AOP":{"term":"AOP","def":"A way to attach shared code (logging, transactions, security) to methods without touching the methods themselves.","hook":"Like threading logging/security through all the doors at once instead of reworking each door separately.","more":"**AOP** (Aspect-Oriented Programming) extracts **cross-cutting logic** (logging, transactions, security, metrics) into separate **aspects** and weaves it automatically into the target methods via **proxies**, without cluttering business code.\n\n- Key concepts: **advice** (what to do), **pointcut** (where to apply it), **join point** (the call site).\n- Gotchas: it works only on **Spring beans** and through proxies, so the same limitations apply — self-invocations and final methods aren't intercepted."},"Spring|@Transactional":{"term":"@Transactional","def":"A marker on a method: everything inside either commits as a whole or rolls back on error.","hook":"Like a money transfer: either it all goes through or it all comes back — there's no halfway.","more":"**@Transactional** wraps a method in a **database transaction** via a proxy: a transaction opens before the method, **commits** on successful return, and **rolls back** on an uncaught exception. This makes a group of operations atomic.\n\n- By default rollback happens only on **RuntimeException/Error**; for checked exceptions you need `rollbackFor`.\n- Gotchas: it won't fire on **self-invocation** or on non-public methods. **Propagation** (REQUIRED, REQUIRES_NEW) and **isolation** matter — the wrong level causes dirty reads or excessive locking."},"Spring|JPA":{"term":"JPA","def":"A standard contract for how to put Java objects into DB tables and fetch them back.","hook":"Like a standardized contract for \"how to put things in a wardrobe and take them out\" — the rules are the same, the wardrobes can differ.","more":"**JPA** is a **specification** (a contract, a set of interfaces and annotations) for how to map Java objects to tables and work with them through the **EntityManager** and the query language **JPQL**. JPA itself does nothing — it needs an implementation.\n\n- Why: you write code against the standard, and the engine can be swapped (Hibernate, EclipseLink) without rewriting.\n- Gotchas: behind the convenient annotations hide the real SQL and the **entity lifecycle** (managed/detached). Without understanding this you easily get **N+1** or unexpected queries."},"Spring|Hibernate":{"term":"Hibernate","def":"The most popular engine that actually implements JPA: it translates objects into SQL and back.","hook":"Like a simultaneous interpreter: your objects into SQL and back, following that same standard (JPA).","more":"**Hibernate** is the most popular **JPA implementation** (an ORM engine): it builds SQL from your entities, runs it, turns rows back into objects, and tracks changes through the **persistence context** (the first-level cache).\n\n- **Dirty checking**: on commit Hibernate compares state itself and issues an UPDATE for the changed fields.\n- Gotchas: that cache lives within the scope of a session; **LazyInitializationException** after it closes, **N+1** on lazy associations, and not knowing when SQL actually fires are common sources of bugs."},"Spring|@Entity":{"term":"@Entity","def":"A marker on a class: \"this object is a row in a DB table.\"","hook":"Like a badge \"I am a table row\": the class puts it on and becomes a database record.","more":"**@Entity** declares a class a **managed entity**: JPA can persist and read its instances as table rows. By default the class name = the table name, fields = columns (adjustable via @Table/@Column).\n\n- An **@Id** (primary key) and a **no-args constructor** are required.\n- Gotchas: you need correct **equals/hashCode** (often by a business key, not by the id field, which is null before persisting). Using an entity directly as a **DTO** in an API is dangerous — it drags in lazy associations and exposes the DB structure."},"Spring|Repository":{"term":"Repository","def":"A bean for working with the DB: you give an interface and Spring writes save/find for you.","hook":"Like a storekeeper working from a request slip: you write \"give me / put this away,\" and it does save and find itself — you write no code.","more":"A **Repository** in Spring Data is an **interface** for which the framework generates a **proxy implementation** at startup: methods like `findByEmail` are parsed from their names into queries, and the basic `save/findById/delete` come from `JpaRepository`.\n\n- Custom queries are defined via **@Query** (JPQL or native SQL).\n- Gotchas: long **derived names** become unreadable — prefer @Query. `findAll()` without pagination is dangerous on large tables; for complex selections use **Specification** or projections."},"Spring|Spring Boot":{"term":"Spring Boot","def":"Spring \"out of the box\": it configures and starts itself with minimal manual config.","hook":"Like a finished car instead of a box of parts: get in and drive, the setup is already done for you.","more":"**Spring Boot** is a layer over Spring with **auto-configuration**: based on the libraries on the classpath it configures beans itself (DataSource, web server, Jackson), provides an **embedded server** (Tomcat) and launch via `main()`/jar, plus `application.yml` for settings.\n\n- **Starters** (`spring-boot-starter-*`) pull in a consistent set of dependencies.\n- Gotchas: the auto-config \"magic\" hides what actually got enabled; when debugging, check the auto-configuration report and override beans. Keep versions aligned via the **parent BOM**."},"Spring|@RestController":{"term":"@RestController","def":"A marker on a class: its methods respond to HTTP requests and return data (usually JSON).","hook":"Like a pickup window: an HTTP request comes in — you hand a box of data (JSON) over the counter.","more":"**@RestController** = **@Controller + @ResponseBody**: the class's methods handle **HTTP requests** (routes set via @GetMapping/@PostMapping etc.), and the returned object is automatically **serialized into the response body**, usually JSON via Jackson.\n\n- Unlike a plain @Controller, it doesn't return view template names — it's for **REST APIs**.\n- Gotchas: don't expose **JPA entities** outward (lazy associations, extra fields) — use **DTOs**. Centralize errors and status codes via **@ControllerAdvice** and ResponseEntity."},"Spring|Lazy loading":{"term":"Lazy loading","def":"Hibernate fetches associated data from the DB only when you actually access it.","hook":"Like a café menu: a dish is brought only when you order it, not the whole kitchen wheeled out at once.","more":"**Lazy loading** is a strategy where associated entities (`@OneToMany`, `@ManyToOne(fetch=LAZY)`) aren't loaded immediately: instead of the data Hibernate puts in a **proxy/wrapper**, and the real SQL fires only on the **first access** to the association. It saves memory and queries.\n\n- By default **collections are LAZY**, *-ToOne is EAGER (often worth making LAZY).\n- Gotchas: accessing it after the session closes → **LazyInitializationException**; iterating a list with a lazy association → the **N+1** problem. Solved via **JOIN FETCH**, EntityGraph, or DTO projections."},"Spring|BeanPostProcessor":{"term":"BeanPostProcessor","def":"A container hook that Spring invokes for every bean before and after its initialization; it's exactly the mechanism that attaches proxies (AOP, @Transactional, @Async) and resolves annotations.","hook":"Customs on the assembly line: every bean goes through inspection and may be wrapped before being released into the application.","more":"**Two methods:** postProcessBeforeInitialization and postProcessAfterInitialization (around @PostConstruct and afterPropertiesSet). The proxy for @Transactional/@Async is created precisely in the *after* phase. - That's why @PostConstruct runs on a \"raw\" bean: the proxy isn't attached yet, so @Async/@Transactional inside @PostConstruct stay silent. - AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor is also a BPP — it processes @Autowired."},"Spring|Scope (singleton/prototype)":{"term":"Scope (singleton/prototype)","def":"A bean's lifetime: singleton — one instance per container (the default), prototype — a new one on each request from the container. There's also request/session for web.","hook":"Singleton — the shared office kettle, prototype — a disposable cup for each person.","more":"**The catch:** a prototype injected as a field into a singleton is resolved ONCE when the singleton is created — from then on it's always the same instance, and the \"prototype-ness\" is lost. - Fix it with ObjectProvider, a @Lookup method, or a scoped proxy. - Spring does NOT manage the full lifecycle of a prototype: its destroy methods aren't called."},"Spring|Propagation (REQUIRED/REQUIRES_NEW)":{"term":"Propagation (REQUIRED/REQUIRES_NEW)","def":"A @Transactional rule for how a method behaves relative to an already-running transaction: REQUIRED — join or create one; REQUIRES_NEW — always suspend the current one and open a new one.","hook":"REQUIRED — you hop into a moving car; REQUIRES_NEW — you call yourself a separate taxi.","more":"**REQUIRES_NEW gotcha:** the outer tx hangs suspended — holding a connection, easily exhausting the pool when nested. - A rollback of the inner one doesn't roll back the outer (and vice versa). - It works only through the proxy: self-invocation (this.method()) ignores propagation. - NESTED makes a savepoint instead of a new transaction (driver-dependent support)."},"Spring|Isolation level":{"term":"Isolation level","def":"A transaction's isolation level — how much it sees uncommitted/changing data of its neighbors. From READ_UNCOMMITTED to SERIALIZABLE; the default is usually READ_COMMITTED.","hook":"The higher the level, the thicker the wall between transactions — but the more expensive it is to build.","more":"**Three anomalies:** a dirty read (you see someone else's uncommitted update) is prevented by READ_COMMITTED; a non-repeatable read (a row changes between two SELECTs) is prevented by REPEATABLE_READ; a phantom (new rows appear) is prevented by SERIALIZABLE. - In Postgres the default is READ_COMMITTED, and REPEATABLE_READ is implemented via an MVCC snapshot. - Higher isolation → more locking/rollbacks and lower concurrency."},"Spring|@Version (оптимистичная блокировка)":{"term":"@Version (optimistic locking)","def":"A counter field in an @Entity that Hibernate increments on every UPDATE and checks in the WHERE clause; it guards against a lost update without explicit row locks.","hook":"A coat-check tag: you come back with the old number, but the item was swapped while you were out — they won't hand it over.","more":"**Mechanism:** UPDATE ... SET version=6 WHERE id=? AND version=5. If 0 rows are affected, someone got there first and an OptimisticLockException is thrown. - Optimistic (no locking, betting on rare conflicts) vs pessimistic (SELECT FOR UPDATE, lock immediately). - The field is marked @Version, usually of type int/long or timestamp."},"Spring|N+1 select":{"term":"N+1 select","def":"An anti-pattern: you load N entities with one query, then for each lazy association a separate SELECT fires — 1+N queries in total instead of one or two.","hook":"You bring a guest list, then run to the coat check for each one's coat in turn.","more":"**Cure:** JOIN FETCH in JPQL, @EntityGraph, or Hibernate batch fetching (@BatchSize / default_batch_fetch_size) — it gathers associations in batches with IN (...). - It often shows up in logs as many identical SELECTs with different ids. - Blindly switching to EAGER doesn't solve it, it just masks it and spawns extra JOINs. Turn on show-sql during review."},"Spring|OSIV (Open Session In View)":{"term":"OSIV (Open Session In View)","def":"Spring Boot behavior where the persistence context stays open until the HTTP response is fully rendered, so lazy associations can still be fetched in the controller/template.","hook":"The water tap isn't shut off until the end of dinner — convenient, but it's tied up and dripping the whole time.","more":"**Why it's debated:** it hides LazyInitializationException, but the connection/session is held longer than needed — the pool stays busy longer, and lazy queries fire outside an explicit transaction (N+1 in the view layer). - In Boot it's on by default (spring.jpa.open-in-view=true), and a warning fires at startup. - The clean alternative: load everything needed in the service (JOIN FETCH/DTO) and disable OSIV."},"Spring|Dirty checking":{"term":"Dirty checking","def":"A Hibernate mechanism: on flush it compares a managed entity against the snapshot taken at load time and generates an UPDATE for the changed fields itself — without an explicit save().","hook":"A photographer snaps you on the way in and on the way out — he'll notice the change of clothes himself, you report nothing.","more":"**Works only for managed entities** in an open persistence context (inside a transaction). A detached object isn't tracked. - The UPDATE fires not immediately but on flush: before a query that might depend on the data, or on commit. - That's why \"forgot to save()\" often persists anyway — sometimes unexpectedly. You can disable it via session.setReadOnly / @Transactional(readOnly=true)."},"Spring|Persistence Context":{"term":"Persistence Context","def":"The first-level cache and the set of managed entities within a single session/transaction; it ensures identity (one row = one object) and dirty checking.","hook":"The session's desk: while it's open, every object pulled from the DB lies on it and Hibernate watches their edits.","more":"**Bound to the thread** via ThreadLocal (that's how @Transactional finds the \"current\" session). It closes at the end of the transaction/session — after that entities become detached. - Accessing a lazy association of a detached object → LazyInitializationException. - The L1 cache can't be turned off; the L2 cache (across sessions) is a separate, optional thing."},"Spring|FactoryBean":{"term":"FactoryBean","def":"A factory bean: when you request it by name, the container hands you not the FactoryBean object itself but the result of its getObject(). A way to create complex/proxied beans.","hook":"You ask the vending machine for a chocolate bar, not the machine itself — even though the button bears the machine's name.","more":"**Confusion:** @Autowired MyFactoryBean injects the PRODUCT, not the factory — the types don't match and you can hit \"no such type.\" - To get the factory itself, request the bean by name with a '&' prefix (getBean(\"&x\")). - This is the basis of SqlSessionFactoryBean (MyBatis), ProxyFactoryBean, and much of Spring's infrastructure."},"Spring|@ConditionalOnMissingBean":{"term":"@ConditionalOnMissingBean","def":"A Boot auto-configuration condition: the bean is created only if no such bean already exists in the context. That's how starters \"back off\" when you define your own.","hook":"A substitute takes the field only if the starter isn't in the lineup.","more":"**How to beat auto-config:** just declare your own @Bean of the same type — the auto bean with @ConditionalOnMissingBean backs off. - Order matters: auto-configs load AFTER user beans (@AutoConfiguration.after / last in the list). - Relatives: @ConditionalOnProperty, @ConditionalOnClass, @ConditionalOnBean — all the starter \"magic\" rests on them."},"Spring|@TransactionalEventListener":{"term":"@TransactionalEventListener","def":"An event listener tied to a phase of the publisher's transaction (AFTER_COMMIT by default) — it fires only after a successful commit, not immediately on publishEvent.","hook":"You announce the wedding only once the registrar's stamp is on it, not while you're still signing.","more":"**Why:** a plain @EventListener is synchronous and runs INSIDE the publisher's transaction — if the commit then fails, the listener's side effect has already happened (or its changes roll back along with it). - Phases: BEFORE_COMMIT, AFTER_COMMIT, AFTER_ROLLBACK, AFTER_COMPLETION. - If there's no transaction, by default the event is NOT delivered (fallbackExecution=true changes that). - A common case: send Kafka/email only after the commit."},"DB|Индекс":{"term":"Index","def":"A separate structure for quickly finding rows by key, so you don't have to scan the whole table.","hook":"The index at the back of a book: you jump straight to the right page instead of flipping through the whole thing.","more":"Under the hood an **index** is a separate data structure (usually a B-tree) that stores key values together with pointers (ctid/page) to table rows. The database searches the index in logarithmic time instead of doing a linear scan.\n\nWhy: speed up `WHERE`, `JOIN`, `ORDER BY`, and uniqueness checks. **Pitfall:** every index slows down `INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE` and consumes space; unused and duplicate indexes are pure overhead. Add them based on real queries, not \"just in case\"."},"DB|B-tree":{"term":"B-tree","def":"A balanced tree — the default index type; keeps keys sorted for lookups by =, <, > and ranges.","hook":"A library catalog: books sorted alphabetically across the branches — you descend to the one you need in a couple of steps.","more":"A **B-tree** is a balanced tree where all leaves are at the same depth and keys are stored **sorted**. Search, insert, and delete are O(log n); leaves are linked into a list for efficient range scans.\n\nIt covers `=`, `<`, `>`, `BETWEEN`, `ORDER BY`, and prefix `LIKE 'abc%'`. **Pitfall:** useless for conditions like `LIKE '%abc'` and for non-linear functions over a column (you need a functional index). It's the default type in PostgreSQL — and for most cases it's all you need."},"DB|Seq Scan":{"term":"Seq Scan","def":"A full sequential pass over the entire table when no suitable index exists.","hook":"Looking up a name in a logbook with no table of contents — you read every line in order to the end.","more":"A **Seq Scan** (sequential scan) means the planner reads **all** of the table's pages in order and filters rows. On a large table this is expensive, but on a small one it can beat an index (less random I/O).\n\nWhy it matters: a Seq Scan showing up in `EXPLAIN` on a large table is a signal that an index is missing or **doesn't fit** the query. **Pitfall:** it's not always bad — if the query returns a large fraction of rows, the planner will deliberately choose a Seq Scan, and that's correct."},"DB|Транзакция":{"term":"Transaction","def":"A group of operations that either executes completely or not at all (all or nothing).","hook":"A money transfer: either it's both debited AND credited, or nothing happens — there's no halfway.","more":"A **transaction** is a logical unit of work: a set of SQL operations between `BEGIN` and `COMMIT`, applied on an all-or-nothing basis. On `ROLLBACK` or failure all changes are rolled back and the database stays in a consistent state.\n\nWhy: related changes (debit one account, credit another) must not be applied partially. **Pitfall:** long transactions hold locks and interfere with vacuum; keep them short, and don't wrap external calls (HTTP, waiting on input) inside a transaction."},"DB|ACID":{"term":"ACID","def":"Transaction guarantees: atomicity, consistency, isolation, durability.","hook":"Four locks on a bank vault: the integrity of the money is guaranteed by each one.","more":"**ACID** is the four properties of reliable transactions: **Atomicity** (all or nothing), **Consistency** (invariants/constraints upheld), **Isolation** (concurrent transactions don't interfere), **Durability** (committed data survives a crash, usually via WAL).\n\nWhy: the foundation of trust in relational databases for money and accounting. **Pitfall:** in practice Isolation is relaxed by the isolation level (the default Read Committed ≠ full isolation), and Consistency here refers to database integrity, not the application's business logic."},"DB|Изоляция":{"term":"Isolation","def":"How much concurrent transactions see each other's changes; set by the isolation level.","hook":"Fitting-room stalls: while you're inside, no stranger can see your things.","more":"**Isolation** determines which uncommitted/concurrent changes a transaction sees and which **anomalies** are allowed: dirty read, non-repeatable read, phantom read. It's controlled by the level (`SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL`).\n\nWhy: a trade-off between correctness and concurrency — a stricter level yields fewer anomalies but more conflicts/rollbacks. **Pitfall:** many concurrency bugs come precisely from the wrong level; in PostgreSQL Read Uncommitted effectively behaves like Read Committed."},"DB|Read Committed":{"term":"Read Committed","def":"An isolation level (the PostgreSQL default): a transaction sees only already-committed changes made by others.","hook":"You see a chat message only after the other person has hit \"Send\".","more":"**Read Committed** is PostgreSQL's default level: each statement within a transaction sees a snapshot of data **committed as of the moment that statement starts**; others' uncommitted changes are invisible.\n\nWhy: a reasonable balance — no dirty reads, high concurrency. **Pitfall:** it allows **non-repeatable read** and **phantom read** — two identical `SELECT`s in one transaction can return different results. For read-modify-write use `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` or a stricter level."},"DB|Serializable":{"term":"Serializable","def":"The strictest level: the result is as if transactions ran one after another, with no concurrency anomalies.","hook":"A checkout line strictly one at a time: it's as if there's no concurrency at all, zero conflicts.","more":"**Serializable** is the strictest level: it guarantees that the outcome of concurrent transactions is equivalent to **some** serial execution of them. In PostgreSQL it's implemented via SSI (Serializable Snapshot Isolation), which tracks dangerous read/write intersections.\n\nWhy: it eliminates all anomalies without manual locking. **Pitfall:** a transaction can fail with `serialization_failure` (code 40001) — the application **must** be able to catch this and **retry**. Slightly higher overhead."},"DB|MVCC":{"term":"MVCC","def":"Versioning: a change creates a new version of the row, so reads and writes don't block each other.","hook":"A Google Doc with history: you edit while another person reads the old version — nobody waits on anyone.","more":"**MVCC** (Multi-Version Concurrency Control): `UPDATE`/`DELETE` don't overwrite the row but create a new version (tuple) and mark the old one obsolete via `xmin`/`xmax`. Each transaction sees its own consistent snapshot.\n\nWhy: **readers don't block writers and vice versa** — high concurrency without read locks. **Pitfall:** it accumulates \"dead\" versions (see vacuum) and bloats tables/indexes; the transaction counter is finite — hence the wraparound risk if vacuum is neglected."},"DB|Vacuum":{"term":"Vacuum","def":"Cleanup of dead (old) row versions left behind by MVCC, to reclaim space and avoid slowdowns.","hook":"Taking out the trash after cleaning: the old copies are thrown out — clean and roomy again.","more":"**Vacuum** is a background cleanup: it marks space from **dead** row versions as free for reuse and advances the visibility \"horizon\" (frozen xid), preventing transaction ID wraparound. It usually runs automatically (**autovacuum**).\n\nWhy: without it tables and indexes bloat and queries slow down. **Pitfall:** a plain `VACUUM` doesn't return space to the OS — only `VACUUM FULL` does (but it takes a heavy lock). Long transactions \"hold\" dead rows and prevent them from being cleaned up."},"DB|Dead tuple":{"term":"Dead tuple","def":"An old row version, already invisible to everyone, waiting to be removed by vacuum.","hook":"An old draft in the trash: nobody needs it, it's waiting for the moment it gets taken out.","more":"A **dead tuple** is a row version left over from `UPDATE`/`DELETE` that's **no longer visible to any** active transaction. It still physically sits on the page, waiting for vacuum to free the space.\n\nWhy it matters: their accumulation = bloat, growing size and degrading speed. **Pitfall:** a single old open transaction (or a stuck replication slot) holds a snapshot and prevents dead tuples from being removed across the whole database. Watch `n_dead_tup` in `pg_stat_user_tables`."},"DB|Блокировка (Lock)":{"term":"Lock","def":"A lock on a row or table that prevents others from modifying data at the same time.","hook":"An occupied airplane lavatory: while one person is inside, the door is locked for everyone else.","more":"A **lock** is a mechanism for managing concurrent access. PostgreSQL has **row-level** locks (e.g., `FOR UPDATE`) and **table-level** locks of various modes; only incompatible modes conflict (write/write block each other, MVCC reads usually don't).\n\nWhy: to serialize changes to a single row and protect against lost updates. **Pitfall:** a **deadlock** when lock acquisition order differs (PostgreSQL detects it and rolls back one transaction); long locks build a queue — keep transactions short."},"DB|Deadlock":{"term":"Deadlock","def":"Two transactions wait on each other's locks in a cycle and can't proceed — the DBMS kills one.","hook":"Two people in a doorway: \"after you\" — \"no, after you\", forever. A guard tosses one out.","more":"Under the hood each transaction holds locks it has already acquired and waits for others'. A **cyclic dependency** arises: T1 holds row A and wants B, T2 holds B and wants A. Via a **wait-for graph** the DBMS detects the cycle and rolls back one transaction (the victim).\n\n- cured by a **consistent order** of acquiring resources (always A→B)\n- short transactions and the right indexes help\n- pitfall: deadlock is inevitable under contention — the code must **retry** the victim, not crash"},"DB|Транзакционный лог (WAL)":{"term":"Write-Ahead Log (WAL)","def":"A log of changes is written before the data itself, so you can recover after a crash.","hook":"First you write \"going to fix the faucet\" in your diary, then you go. Memory wiped — the diary still remembers.","more":"**WAL (Write-Ahead Logging)**: before modifying data pages, a record is first written to a sequential log. The rule — the log hits disk **before** the data itself. After a crash the DBMS replays the log (redo/undo) and restores consistency.\n\n- gives durability without an fsync per page — the write is **sequential** and fast\n- the basis for replication and PITR (point-in-time recovery)\n- pitfall: the log must be archived/cleaned up, or the disk fills up; long transactions slow down its cleanup"},"DB|Covering index":{"term":"Covering index","def":"An index that contains all columns a query needs (including INCLUDE), so the answer comes straight from the index without touching the table (index-only scan).","hook":"A catalog that already has the answer written in it, rather than \"go to the shelf for the book\".","more":"In Postgres columns are added either to the key or via **INCLUDE** (they don't participate in sorting, they just sit in the leaf). A common confusion: an index-only scan may still hit the **heap** if the page isn't marked \"all-visible\" in the visibility map — that is, after bulk changes without VACUUM the benefit is lost."},"DB|Keyset pagination":{"term":"Keyset pagination","def":"Pagination via WHERE id > last_seen ORDER BY id LIMIT n instead of OFFSET — you page \"from the last key\" without flipping through discarded rows.","hook":"A bookmark in a book instead of \"count off 100000 pages from the start again\".","more":"OFFSET reads and **discards** all preceding rows — cost grows linearly with depth. Keyset hits the index and lands directly on the right position: the per-page cost is constant. Downsides: you can't jump to \"page 500\", and the sort key must be **unique and stable** (otherwise you need a composite key id+field)."},"DB|Write skew":{"term":"Write skew","def":"An anomaly: two transactions read the same state, each is individually correct, but together they violate an invariant (e.g., both doctors go off shift).","hook":"Two people both reason \"there are two of us, one can leave\" — and both leave, emptying the shift.","more":"Snapshot isolation (Postgres **REPEATABLE READ**) **doesn't save you** from write skew — the transactions write to different rows, so there's no direct write conflict. Cured by the **SERIALIZABLE** level (SSI rolls one back with error 40001) or by an explicit lock (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on the rows being read, materializing the conflict)."},"DB|Оптимистичная блокировка":{"term":"Optimistic locking","def":"Don't lock the row: read version, and on UPDATE ... WHERE version = ? check that it wasn't changed. 0 rows matched — someone beat you to it, retry.","hook":"You don't lock the door — but on the way out you check whether anyone came in while you were away.","more":"Good when conflicts are **rare**: you lock nothing, the cost is the occasional retry. Pessimistic locking (**FOR UPDATE**) is better under frequent conflicts: lock immediately, no spending on rollbacks. A common mistake — forgetting to handle \"0 rows updated\" and silently losing the update (lost update)."},"DB|N+1 запросов":{"term":"N+1 queries","def":"1 query fetches a list of N entities, then in a loop another query runs per entity — N+1 round trips to the DB instead of one.","hook":"You bring a list of 100 names, but you run to the directory separately for each one's phone number — 100 times.","more":"A classic of lazy ORM associations: calling `order.getClient()` in a loop triggers a SELECT on each iteration. Cured by **JOIN FETCH** / `@EntityGraph` / batch fetch (IN list) or by a DTO projection in a single query. The trap: on 10 test rows it's unnoticeable, but in prod on 10,000 — timeout."},"DB|SKIP LOCKED":{"term":"SKIP LOCKED","def":"A modifier for SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED: it skips rows already locked by others instead of waiting — ideal for concurrently draining a queue in a table.","hook":"You grab the first free box in the warehouse instead of queuing for the one already being carried off.","more":"A plain **FOR UPDATE** lines workers up in a jam: the second waits for the first on the same row. SKIP LOCKED hands each its own rows → linear scaling of the worker pool. Caveat: skipped rows aren't lost — the next poll picks them up; but processing order is no longer strictly FIFO."},"DB|GIN index":{"term":"GIN index","def":"An inverted index for composite values: JSONB, arrays, full-text. It breaks a document into tokens and, for each, stores the list of rows where it appears.","hook":"The subject index at the back of a book: by a word — a list of all the pages it's on.","more":"Needed for `WHERE data @> '{...}'`, `tags && ARRAY[...]`, `tsvector @@ tsquery` — where a **B-tree is powerless** (searching inside a value). Downsides: the index is larger and **more expensive to write** (one INSERT updates many keys); mitigated by the `fastupdate` parameter and periodic merging of the pending list."},"DB|BRIN index":{"term":"BRIN index","def":"Block Range INdex: instead of a pointer to each row, it stores the min/max value per range of blocks. Tiny, and effective on data physically ordered by the column.","hook":"Not a table of contents per page, but a sticker on each shelf: \"dates from March to April here\".","more":"For log tables with hundreds of millions of rows written **in ascending order** (created_at), BRIN is thousands of times more compact than a B-tree. The price of compactness is no pinpoint access but range elimination: rows within a found range are still rechecked. It breaks if the **physical order doesn't match** the column (after UPDATEs/frequent out-of-order inserts) — then ranges \"smear\" and elimination stops working."},"DB|Partial index":{"term":"Partial index","def":"An index with a WHERE condition — it indexes only a subset of rows. Smaller in size, cheaper to write, and hits exactly the hot query.","hook":"Not a catalog of the whole warehouse, but a separate list of only the \"new\" boxes that actually get handled.","more":"Ideal when a query always filters by a rare value (`status='NEW'` among 10M orders): the index is tiny and fully in memory. Also used for uniqueness over a subset (`UNIQUE ... WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`). Caveat: the query predicate must **match** the index condition, otherwise the planner won't use it."},"DB|Window function":{"term":"Window function","def":"A function with OVER (PARTITION BY ...): it computes an aggregate/rank over a group of rows but does NOT collapse rows — detail is preserved, and each row gets its aggregate attached.","hook":"Attach \"client total\" to every line of a statement without collapsing the statement into a single line.","more":"Unlike `GROUP BY` (which collapses to one row per group), a window keeps all rows. Beyond SUM/AVG it provides `ROW_NUMBER`, `RANK`, `LAG`/`LEAD`, sliding windows (`ROWS BETWEEN`). A common technique — \"top-N per group\" via `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ...)` with a filter on rn (requires a subquery — a window can't go in WHERE)."},"DB|Рекурсивный CTE":{"term":"Recursive CTE","def":"WITH RECURSIVE: a common table expression that references itself. The anchor provides starting rows, the recursive part joins the result back to the table — for traversing trees/graphs.","hook":"A nesting-doll query: you open a node, inside is a reference to the same query — and so on down to the leaves.","more":"A classic — a category tree `(id, parent_id)`: \"all descendants of a node\" in a single query. Structure: **anchor** UNION ALL **recursive part** that references the CTE's name. The danger — cycles in the data cause infinite recursion; guard against them with `UNION` (dedup), accumulating the path in an array and checking it, or `LIMIT`."},"DB|PreparedStatement":{"term":"PreparedStatement","def":"A parameterized query: SQL with placeholders (?) is sent separately from the values. Data never becomes part of the query code — so SQL injection is impossible.","hook":"A form with blank fields: the form's text is fixed, you only fill in the fields with data, you don't add new clauses.","more":"String concatenation (`\"... = '\" + input + \"'\"`) lets you slip in `' OR '1'='1` — the input is parsed as SQL. In prepared mode the planner sees the structure separately from the data, so injection is excluded by design. Bonus — **plan reuse** (but in PgBouncer transaction mode named prepared statements break — see the separate caveat)."},"DB|HikariCP":{"term":"HikariCP","def":"A connection pool: it keeps a set of open connections to the DB and reuses them, avoiding the costly opening of TCP + authentication on every query.","hook":"A fleet of taxis ready at the door — you don't buy a new car for every ride.","more":"A counterintuitive point: **more is not better**. Each Postgres connection is a process/backend run by a limited number of cores; a pool of 100 connections on 40 threads and ~8 cores causes CPU contention and lock contention, and everything gets slower. The working rule of thumb is close to the core count; key timeouts: `connectionTimeout`, `maxLifetime` (shorter than the server's idle timeout)."},"Distributed|Kafka":{"term":"Kafka","def":"A distributed message log: producers append events, consumers read them in order at their own pace.","hook":"A shared mailbox feed: some drop letters in, others read through them in turn, whenever they get to it.","more":"Kafka is a **distributed append-only log**, not a queue: events are appended to the tail and are **not deleted once read** — they live according to retention (time/size). Consumers read independently, each at its own pace, and re-reading is possible.\n\n- decouples producers from consumers and smooths out load spikes\n- great for event-driven systems, streaming, audit trails, and history **replay**\n- gotcha: it's not RabbitMQ — there's no per-message ack/delete; ordering and scaling are tied to partitions"},"Distributed|Партиция":{"term":"Partition","def":"A slice of a Kafka topic — ordering is guaranteed only within a single partition, never across them.","hook":"A single checkout line keeps strict order; across different checkout lines, there's no order at all.","more":"A topic is split into **partitions** — independent logs that can be spread across different brokers. This is the unit of **parallelism and scale**: more partitions mean more consumers working at once.\n\n- strict ordering **only within** a partition; the message key picks the partition (same key → same partition → ordered)\n- gotcha: there's no global ordering; you can easily grow the partition count but **not shrink** it, and changing it breaks the key-to-partition mapping"},"Distributed|Offset":{"term":"Offset","def":"A message's position number within a partition; the consumer remembers it to know how far it has read.","hook":"A bookmark in a book: you remember which page you stopped on and pick up from there.","more":"An **offset** is a monotonic sequence number of a record within a specific partition. The consumer tracks how far it has read (the committed offset), usually in the internal `__consumer_offsets` topic.\n\n- committing **before** processing → at-most-once (risk of loss); committing **after** → at-least-once (risk of duplicates)\n- you can rewind: seek to the beginning/end/a timestamp to replay\n- gotcha: timer-based auto-commit can confirm messages you haven't processed yet — for reliability, commit manually"},"Distributed|Consumer group":{"term":"Consumer group","def":"A set of consumers that split the partitions among themselves: each partition is read by exactly one of them.","hook":"A crew of movers splits a warehouse by aisle: each aisle is worked by one person only.","more":"A **consumer group** is a set of consumers sharing a `group.id`, across which Kafka divides the partitions: a single partition is read by **exactly one** group member. This is how reads scale horizontally.\n\n- different groups read the same topic **independently** (each with its own offsets)\n- adding/removing a consumer triggers a **rebalance** and partitions are reassigned\n- gotcha: more consumers than partitions means the extras sit idle; frequent rebalances stall processing"},"Distributed|Broker":{"term":"Broker","def":"A node in the Kafka cluster: it stores partitions, accepts writes from producers, and serves data to consumers.","hook":"A post office: it stores letters, takes them from senders, and hands them to recipients.","more":"A **broker** is an individual server (node) in a Kafka cluster. It stores its assigned partitions on disk, accepts writes from producers, and serves consumers. Each broker has a unique id.\n\n- every partition has a **leader broker** (all reads/writes go through it) and **followers** holding replicas\n- one broker acts as the **controller** (managing metadata and leadership)\n- gotcha: when a broker dies, a new leader is elected from the in-sync replicas; without replication, its data is unavailable/lost"},"Distributed|acks":{"term":"acks","def":"A producer setting for how many replicas must confirm a write (0/1/all) — the trade-off between speed and durability.","hook":"You ship a parcel: 0 — don't care, 1 — the courier nodded, all — every warehouse signed for it.","more":"**acks** controls when a producer considers a write successful: `0` — don't wait (can be lost), `1` — only the leader confirmed, `all`/`-1` — all **in-sync replicas** confirmed.\n\n- `all` + `min.insync.replicas≥2` = maximum durability (no loss if the leader fails)\n- gotcha: `acks=1` loses data if the leader dies before replicating\n- `acks=all` costs more latency; for exactly-once it's enabled together with an idempotent producer"},"Distributed|Replication factor":{"term":"Replication factor","def":"How many copies of a partition are kept on different brokers so data survives the loss of one.","hook":"Spare house keys held by three neighbors: lose one and you still get in with another.","more":"The **replication factor** is the number of copies of each partition kept on **different** brokers. RF=3 means 1 leader + 2 followers; the cluster survives broker failures without data loss.\n\n- works together with **min.insync.replicas**: how many replicas must be in sync for a write to be acknowledged\n- gotcha: RF=1 means no fault tolerance; you can't set RF higher than the number of brokers\n- RF=3 + min.insync=2 is the standard for production durability (tolerates one broker going down)"},"Distributed|Idempotency":{"term":"Idempotency","def":"A property of an operation: run it 10 times and the result is the same — duplicates don't corrupt data.","hook":"You press the elevator button 5 times — it still arrives once, not 5 times.","more":"**Idempotency** means a repeated call yields the same result and **creates no duplicates or extra side effects**. It's achieved via an operation key: a unique constraint, an upsert by id, or a dedup table of processed events.\n\n- critical with **at-least-once** delivery and retries (network, timeouts)\n- example: \"pay order X\" keyed by `orderId`, rather than blindly \"charge 100\"\n- gotcha: increments/appends aren't idempotent; you need a stable business key, not a fresh UUID per attempt"},"Distributed|Outbox":{"term":"Outbox","def":"A pattern: write the event to your own DB in the same transaction as the data, and a separate process publishes it to Kafka later.","hook":"An outgoing-mail tray you fill alongside your work; the mail carrier picks it up and delivers it.","more":"**Outbox** solves the \"dual write\" problem: you can't atomically write to the DB and send to Kafka. So in the **same transaction** as the business data you write an event row into an `outbox` table; a separate process (a poller or **CDC/Debezium**) reads it and publishes to Kafka.\n\n- guarantees the event goes out **if and only if** the data committed\n- yields at-least-once → the consumer must be **idempotent**\n- gotcha: you need to clean up the table and handle duplicates; CDC is more reliable than manual polling"},"Distributed|At-least-once":{"term":"At-least-once","def":"A delivery guarantee: the message will definitely arrive, but sometimes twice — idempotency saves you.","hook":"A registered letter is sure to arrive, but sometimes the courier brings two copies — check the number.","more":"**At-least-once** guarantees delivery, but on retries/failures (e.g. you processed a message but didn't commit the offset in time) it will arrive **again**. This is the default and most practical guarantee in Kafka.\n\n- alternatives: **at-most-once** (no duplicates but possible loss) and **exactly-once** (expensive, narrow use cases)\n- saved by **idempotency** on the consumer side or dedup by key\n- gotcha: design processing as if a duplicate is guaranteed to arrive — otherwise you get double charges/emails"},"Distributed|Exactly-once":{"term":"Exactly-once","def":"A message is processed exactly once; expensive and hard, so people usually do at-least-once + idempotency instead.","hook":"Like a single letter: never lost, never delivered twice. It's costly — so usually you send a duplicate plus 'seen it, skipping'.","more":"**Under the hood:** the system is made idempotent — each message carries a unique key (offset, messageId), and the receiver stores processed keys and discards duplicates. In Kafka this is a transactional producer + `read_committed` consumer, combining the result write and the offset commit into one transaction.\n\n**Why:** so a redelivery after a failure doesn't double a payment/order. **Gotcha:** \"true\" exactly-once doesn't exist in a distributed system — the guarantee only holds within a boundary (Kafka→Kafka). The moment you write to an external DB or call someone else's API, you need your own idempotency. In practice people use **at-least-once + dedup by key** — cheaper and clearer."},"Distributed|CAP":{"term":"CAP","def":"Under a network partition a distributed system picks either consistency (C) or availability (A); partition tolerance (P) isn't a choice.","hook":"The link drops — choose: either everyone sees the same thing (C), or everyone gets an answer (A). You can't have both at once.","more":"**Under the hood:** Brewer's theorem about distributed storage. **P (partition tolerance)** — surviving a break in connectivity between nodes — is unavoidable in a real network, so you don't \"pick\" it, you assume it. The real choice happens only at the moment of a partition: return **possibly stale** data (AP) or refuse to answer to preserve a single state (CP).\n\n**Gotcha:** CAP is about behavior during a network partition, not a permanent mode. It's not binary: PACELC adds that even without a partition there's still a latency↔consistency trade-off. Most databases are tunable (quorum levels), not strictly \"CP\" or \"AP\"."},"Distributed|Eventual consistency":{"term":"Eventual consistency","def":"Data on the nodes converges not immediately but after a while, once all updates have propagated through the system.","hook":"A rumor spreading through a village: not everyone hears it at once, but in time everyone knows the same thing.","more":"**Under the hood:** a consistency model where a write is first accepted by one node and then propagates to the rest asynchronously (replication, gossip, queues). The guarantee is weak: **if no new writes happen, all replicas will eventually reach the same value.** Stronger variants include read-your-writes and monotonic reads.\n\n**Why:** it gives high availability and low latency (the AP in CAP) — caches, DNS, Cassandra, read-only replicas. **Gotcha:** a client may read stale data or see data \"from the past\" even after its own write. You can't build invariants like \"the balance won't go negative\" on it — you need compensations or strict consistency at the critical point."},"Distributed|Saga":{"term":"Saga","def":"A long business operation as a chain of local transactions; if a step fails, compensations run to roll it back.","hook":"A restaurant order: the dish burned — so we cancel down the chain and refund the money, step by step.","more":"**Under the hood:** a distributed transaction without 2PC. The business process is split into **local ACID transactions** across different services; each step has a **compensating action** (cancel the booking, refund the money). Two styles: **orchestration** (a central coordinator conducts) and **choreography** (services react to each other's events).\n\n**Why:** it replaces the unattainable global commit across microservices. **Gotcha:** there's no isolation — intermediate states are visible to others (you need semantic locks/statuses); compensation isn't always possible (the email already went out); the system is only **eventually consistent**. Orchestration is easier to debug, choreography easily turns into an implicit web of events."},"Distributed|Consumer lag":{"term":"Consumer lag","def":"The gap between the latest offset in a partition and the offset a consumer has already processed. Shows how far the consumer is behind the message stream.","hook":"A stack of unread letters grows faster than you can work through it.","more":"**Growing lag** means the consumer can't keep up with the producer: too few instances, heavy processing, or a poison message. It's the #1 metric for Kafka alerting. **Nuance:** lag itself isn't bad (a buffer for spikes); what's bad is when it grows monotonically and never returns to zero."},"Distributed|Rebalance":{"term":"Rebalance","def":"Reassigning partitions among the consumers of one group when an instance joins, dies, or hangs. During a rebalance, processing in the group is paused.","hook":"Waiters get reassigned to tables — while they sort out the zones, nobody serves the guests.","more":"**Eager (range/round-robin)** is stop-the-world: everyone gives up all partitions and they're reassigned from scratch. **Cooperative-sticky** gives up only what's needed; the rest keep working. **Rebalance storms** during rolling restarts are tamed with `group.instance.id` (static membership)."},"Distributed|ISR":{"term":"ISR","def":"In-Sync Replicas — partition replicas that keep up with the leader and are considered current. acks=all waits for confirmation from exactly the ISR.","hook":"A quorum of in-sync understudies holding the same page as the original.","more":"**min.insync.replicas** sets the minimum number of live ISR needed to accept a write. **RF=3 + min.insync=2 + acks=all** survives the loss of 1 broker. **Trap:** min.insync=3 with RF=3 halts writes when a single broker is lost — there's no headroom."},"Distributed|Backpressure":{"term":"Backpressure","def":"A mechanism where an overloaded consumer slows the producer down, preventing the queue from growing without bound. Every queue must be bounded.","hook":"A funnel with a narrow neck: don't pour in faster than it can let through.","more":"An unbounded queue under overload eats memory and leads to **OOM** — you've merely deferred the crash. Options: block the producer, drop messages, or fail fast (load shedding). **Related:** a bounded thread pool + timeout is a special case of backpressure."},"Distributed|Circuit Breaker":{"term":"Circuit Breaker","def":"A fuse: after a streak of downstream errors it stops sending calls to that service (Open), quickly returning a fallback so hung threads don't pile up.","hook":"The breaker in your electrical panel: it trips on a short circuit so the whole wiring doesn't burn out.","more":"**Trap:** the circuit breaker counts ERRORS, but a hung slow call isn't an error — it just hangs. Without a timeout, the breaker won't save you. **Half-Open:** after a delay it lets a few probe requests through — success → Closed, failure → Open again. Always combine it with timeout and bulkhead."},"Distributed|Temporal coupling":{"term":"Temporal coupling","def":"A service works only if another service is available right now. \"Thin\" events (just an id) quietly reintroduce it.","hook":"A phone call instead of a letter: if the other party doesn't pick up right now, the work stalls.","more":"A thin notification (\"order changed, id=42\") forces the consumer to synchronously call the producer back for details → temporal coupling again. **Cure:** send \"fat\" events carrying the data, or use a log-compacted entity stream so the receiver never has to call back."},"Distributed|DLQ":{"term":"DLQ","def":"Dead Letter Queue — a separate topic/queue where messages that couldn't be processed after retries are parked, so the partition isn't blocked.","hook":"A \"deal with later\" tray: take the poison letter off the desk and keep the conveyor moving.","more":"A single poison message in a partition, if retried in-place, stalls the ENTIRE partition (ordering). **Pattern:** commit the offset immediately and move the failed message to a retry topic with a delay; once attempts are exhausted → into the DLQ. The DLQ is later handled by hand or replayed."},"Distributed|CQRS":{"term":"CQRS","def":"Command Query Responsibility Segregation — separating the write model (commands) from the read model (queries). The read model is populated asynchronously from events.","hook":"The register takes orders while the display shows the goods — two different counters.","more":"**Common confusion:** \"I wrote via a command, did a GET right away, and don't see the change\" — that's not a bug but the expected delay in populating the read model (read-your-writes isn't guaranteed). You pay with eventual consistency on reads in exchange for scalable, query-tailored projections."},"Distributed|Quorum (R+W>N)":{"term":"Quorum (R+W>N)","def":"In quorum stores there are N replicas, W must confirm a write, and R are polled on a read. When R+W>N the sets overlap → a reader always sees the latest write.","hook":"If the quorum of writers and the quorum of readers are forced to overlap, the fresh vote won't get lost.","more":"**N=3, W=2, R=2** → 2+2>3, the sets overlap, reads are consistent. A smaller W means faster writes but a risk of stale reads. This is **CAP/latency** tuning: by moving R and W you choose the balance of consistency, availability, and read/write latency."},"Distributed|Vector clock":{"term":"Vector clock","def":"Per-node event counters that establish a causal ordering and detect concurrent (conflicting) updates.","hook":"Not \"what time on the clock\" but \"who saw what before you\" — ordering by causality, not by time.","more":"**Wall-clock lies:** node clocks drift out of sync, so you can't tell from a timestamp who actually came first. A vector clock can distinguish \"A happened before B\", \"B before A\", and \"concurrent\" (a conflict). What it can't do: give the real event time — only a relative ordering."},"Distributed|2PC":{"term":"2PC","def":"Two-Phase Commit — the coordinator sends prepare to all participants, collects \"ready\", then commit. It gives atomicity for a distributed transaction, but blocks.","hook":"The wedding \"do you?\": everyone said yes and it's official; but if the officiant vanishes, the guests are left standing at the altar.","more":"**Why it's avoided in microservices:** it's a blocking protocol — if the coordinator dies after prepare, participants hold locks and wait. Plus you need a shared coordinator and XA support everywhere. **The alternative is Saga:** a chain of local transactions with compensations, no global locks."},"Distributed|Gossip":{"term":"Gossip","def":"An epidemic protocol: each node periodically picks random peers and exchanges state. That's how a cluster learns who's alive without a central registry.","hook":"A rumor through the village: each person whispers to a couple of neighbors — and by morning everyone knows.","more":"State spreads exponentially and is resilient to node loss — there's no single point of failure (unlike a central registry). Used in **Cassandra, Consul, Serf**. The cost is eventual consistency of membership: a node's death is learned not instantly but over several rounds."},"Distributed|Log compaction":{"term":"Log compaction","def":"A Kafka storage mode that keeps the latest value for each key and deletes older versions. A tombstone (value=null) marks a key as deleted.","hook":"Not a feed of every edit, but a current address book: one fresh record per key.","more":"**Difference from retention:** retention trims by time/size and deletes EVERYTHING old; compaction keeps the latest value per key forever. This is the basis of a **log-compacted entity stream**: a new service two years later reads not billions of events but the current snapshot of state by key."},"Design|SOLID":{"term":"SOLID","def":"5 principles of OO design that make code easy to change without breaking neighboring parts.","hook":"5 bricklaying rules: change one wall and the adjacent ones don't fall down.","more":"**Under the hood:** **S**RP, **O**CP, **L**SP (Liskov — a subtype substitutes for its base type without surprises), **I**SP (narrow interfaces), **D**IP — Robert Martin's set of heuristics for managing dependencies and coupling in OO code.\n\n**Why:** to lower the cost of change — so a fix in one place doesn't cascade across the whole codebase. **Pitfall:** these are **principles, not dogma**. Applying them blindly breeds needless interfaces, factories, and one-method classes — a direct path to overengineering. Reach for a principle when you feel real pain (duplication, fragility), not preemptively \"by the book\"."},"Design|SRP":{"term":"SRP","def":"Single Responsibility: a class has one reason to change, one job.","hook":"A Swiss Army knife is bad: better one knife for one job. A class has one reason to change.","more":"**Under the hood:** **\"one reason to change\"** = the class answers to a single stakeholder/aspect. A telltale violation is a God Class mixing parsing, business logic, DB access, and formatting (like the 1361-line `OrderServiceImpl`).\n\n**Why:** changes to print logic shouldn't touch calculation logic; a narrow class is easier to test and understand. **Pitfall:** \"responsibility\" is an elastic notion — easy to swing to the extreme of dozens of anemic classes. Rule of thumb: if you describe a class with \"and\" (\"computes AND saves AND sends\"), it's a split candidate. Split along the axis of change, not mechanically by line count."},"Design|OCP":{"term":"OCP","def":"Open/Closed: code is open for extension, closed for internal modification.","hook":"A wall socket: plug in a new appliance without taking the socket apart. Extend, don't tinker with the insides.","more":"**Under the hood:** new behavior is added with **new code** (a new interface implementation, strategy, subclass), not by editing what's already proven. Technically achieved via polymorphism and extension points defined up front.\n\n**Why:** not touching a tested class when adding a variant means fewer regressions. **Pitfall:** you can't make everything open to everything — that's \"for the future\" abstraction, pure YAGNI. The right path: write it concretely the first time, and **only when a second or third variant appears** do you extract an extension point. Premature OCP = useless extra interfaces."},"Design|DIP":{"term":"DIP","def":"Dependency Inversion: both high and low levels depend on an abstraction, not on a concrete class.","hook":"Lamp and socket both target one standard outlet, not each other directly.","more":"**Under the hood:** both the high-level module (business logic) and the low-level one (DB, HTTP, queue) depend on an **abstraction** owned by the upper layer. The dependency arrow is \"inverted\": the adapter implements the core's interface rather than the core importing the adapter. This is the foundation of hexagonal architecture (ports are exactly those abstractions).\n\n**Why:** the core knows nothing about Postgres/MinIO/Kafka — they can be swapped and mocked. **Pitfall:** the interface must be expressed in **domain** terms (`ClientDataPort`), not infrastructure terms (`JdbcDao`) — otherwise the inversion is fake. Don't confuse it with DI: DIP is about the direction of the dependency, DI is just a way to supply it."},"Design|DI":{"term":"DI","def":"Dependency Injection: dependencies are supplied from outside; the class doesn't create them itself.","hook":"The tool is handed to you, rather than you running off to make it yourself.","more":"**Under the hood:** an object doesn't `new` up its dependencies — it **receives them from outside** via the constructor (preferred), a setter, or a field. A container (Quarkus CDI, Spring) builds the object graph and injects them by type/qualifier.\n\n**Why:** to break the hard link to a concrete implementation → easy to swap in a mock in tests or switch adapters. It's the **mechanism** that realizes DIP. **Pitfall:** field injection hides dependencies and hampers testing — **use constructor injection**. Too many constructor parameters isn't a DI problem but a signal the class took on too much (SRP violation). A container isn't mandatory — you can inject by hand."},"Design|Strategy":{"term":"Strategy","def":"Pattern: an interchangeable algorithm behind a common interface, chosen at runtime.","hook":"Navigator: walk/drive/bike — one screen, the route is computed by the chosen mode.","more":"**Under the hood:** a family of interchangeable algorithms behind a **common interface**; the client holds a reference to the interface, and the concrete implementation is chosen and plugged in at runtime (by document type, country, tariff). It replaces a branchy `if/switch` with polymorphism.\n\n**Why:** adding a new variant = a new class, leaving the old untouched (OCP in action); each algorithm is tested in isolation. **Pitfall:** spinning up an interface + factory + DI for 2 simple branches is overengineering — a plain `switch` is more honest. It's justified when there are many variants that keep growing and are complex. In Java it often collapses to an `enum` with a strategy or a `Map<Key, Handler>`."},"Design|Factory":{"term":"Factory","def":"Pattern: a dedicated place creates objects so the code doesn't depend on new.","hook":"A coffee machine: press a button, get a drink, without knowing how it's assembled inside.","more":"**Under the hood:** object creation is pulled out of the client code into a separate method/class. Variants: **Factory Method** (a subclass decides what to create), **Abstract Factory** (a family of related objects), a simple static factory method (`of`, `valueOf`).\n\n**Why:** the client works with an interface and doesn't know the concrete class → you change the implementation in one place; you hide complex assembly and invariant validation. **Pitfall:** easy to overdo — a `UserFactory.create()` that merely wraps `new User()` is pointless. Use it when class selection is non-trivial (depends on input/config) or you need to hide complex initialization, not just \"to avoid writing new\"."},"Design|Builder":{"term":"Builder","def":"Pattern: assembles an object with lots of fields step by step, without a mess in the constructor.","hook":"A burger on a conveyor: bun→patty→cheese, step by step, no pile-up in the constructor.","more":"**Under the hood:** step-by-step object assembly via a chain of methods (`.name().date().build()`) that accumulate state, with a final `build()` that creates an **immutable** object and checks invariants. It solves the telescoping-constructor problem and the pile of `null` arguments.\n\n**Why:** readable creation of objects with many optional fields; the object is either assembled valid or not at all. **Pitfall:** for 2-3 fields it's overkill — a plain constructor/record is cleaner. Often don't hand-write it: Lombok `@Builder`, IDE generation, a Java `record` with a compact constructor. Make sure `build()` actually validates required fields — otherwise half the point is lost."},"Design|Гексагон":{"term":"Hexagonal architecture","def":"The logic core sits in the center, everything external is plugged in through ports and adapters.","hook":"Like a CPU in a socket: one core, while you bolt on plug adapters for any country from the outside.","more":"Under the hood it's dependency inversion: the **business logic** knows nothing about the DB, HTTP, or queues. **Ports and adapters** attach to it from the outside (hence the second name — Ports & Adapters). Why: the core is tested without spinning up infrastructure, and technologies change without touching the logic.\n\n- At the center — the **domain**, at the edges — the technical bits\n- Pitfall: easy to over-complicate small CRUD; hexagonal architecture pays off where the logic is genuinely rich, otherwise it's just extra layers."},"Design|Port":{"term":"Port","def":"An interface at the core's boundary: what's needed from the outside, without the how.","hook":"Like a wall socket: the shape of the holes is fixed, but it doesn't care what you plug in (a lamp, a kettle).","more":"Technically it's an **interface** the core declares: \"I need to save an order / publish an event\" — but with no implementation. They split into **driving** (inbound, who invokes the core) and **driven** (outbound, what the core invokes). Why: the core depends on an abstraction, not on Postgres or Kafka.\n\n- Port names are in the language of the domain, not the technology\n- Pitfall: a port must not \"leak\" details (`ResultSet`, `KafkaRecord`) — otherwise the adapter has fused with the core."},"Design|Adapter (гексагон)":{"term":"Adapter (hexagonal)","def":"A port's implementation for a specific technology: DB, Kafka, REST.","hook":"Like a plug adapter: one end into the port-socket, the other to a specific technology (DB, Kafka).","more":"It's a concrete implementation of a port for a technology: a **JDBC adapter**, a **Kafka adapter**, a **REST client**. The adapter translates domain calls into the external system's language and back. Why: all the integration \"dirt\" is isolated here, and the core stays clean.\n\n- One port — several adapters (prod DB and in-memory for tests)\n- Pitfall: don't drag business rules into the adapter — it's only a translator/mapper, the logic lives in the core."},"Design|DDD":{"term":"DDD","def":"Design around the business model and its language, not around DB tables.","hook":"We build the house around the family's life, not around the shape of the bricks: code around the business and its words.","more":"**Domain-Driven Design** — you design code around the business model and its **Ubiquitous Language** (a shared language of developers and domain experts), not around the table schema. Key building blocks: Entity, Value Object, Aggregate, Bounded Context.\n\n- **Tactical** (code patterns) and **Strategic** (context boundaries)\n- Why: the code mirrors real rules and is easier to change\n- Pitfall: DDD is expensive for simple CRUD; its main value is in a complex domain with non-trivial rules."},"Design|Coupling":{"term":"Coupling","def":"How tightly modules cling to one another; the less, the better.","hook":"Like earbuds in a pocket: the more tangled the wires, the more painful to untangle. Less is better.","more":"**Coupling** is the degree to which one module depends on another's internals. High coupling = a fix in one place breaks the neighbors and it's hard to test in isolation. Why reduce it: flexibility and independent deployability.\n\n- Treated with interfaces, events, **dependency inversion**\n- Pitfall: zero coupling doesn't exist — modules must communicate somehow; the goal isn't \"zero\" but loose coupling through stable contracts rather than through internals."},"Design|Cohesion":{"term":"Cohesion","def":"How much everything inside a module is about one thing; the higher, the better.","hook":"Like a 'socks only' drawer: everything inside is about one thing, nothing extraneous mixed in.","more":"**Cohesion** is how much the elements inside a module serve a single purpose. High cohesion = a class/module does one thing and does it fully. Why: clearer, easier to change, fewer reasons to \"touch\" this code.\n\n- The ideal: **high cohesion + low coupling**\n- A sign of low cohesion is a God Class with unrelated responsibilities thrown together\n- Pitfall: don't split for the sake of numbers — things related in meaning should live together."},"Java|equals()":{"term":"equals()","def":"Method that compares objects by their content rather than by reference; == compares references, equals() compares meaning.","hook":"Two passports: == checks it's the same physical document, equals() checks the data inside matches.","more":"Under the hood, `equals()` from `Object` compares references (same as `==`), which is why you **override** it to compare by fields. You need it everywhere objects are put into collections or compared by meaning (e.g. two `Money` values with the same amount are equal).\n\n**Pitfalls:** the contract is binding — reflexivity, symmetry, transitivity, consistency. Break symmetry under inheritance and you get `a.equals(b) != b.equals(a)`. Always generate it via the IDE or use a `record`, never write it by hand."},"Java|hashCode()":{"term":"hashCode()","def":"An integer fingerprint of an object; if you override equals you must override this too, or HashMap will break.","hook":"A coat-check tag: it lets you quickly find the slot. Change equals and update the tag, otherwise your item gets lost.","more":"`hashCode()` returns an `int` fingerprint that `HashMap`/`HashSet` use to pick a **bucket**. The contract: objects equal by `equals` must produce equal hashCodes (the reverse isn't required — collisions are allowed).\n\n**Pitfalls:** override `equals` but not `hashCode` and the object gets \"lost\" in a `HashMap` (you put it in, but `get` returns null). And never base hashCode on **mutable** fields if the object is already in a `Set`: the key becomes unfindable."},"Java|Дженерики (Generics)":{"term":"Generics","def":"Type parameters in <> so that List<String> holds only strings and errors are caught by the compiler, not at runtime.","hook":"A box labeled \"socks only\": it won't let you put in a mug at packing time, not when you pull it out.","more":"**Generics** — parameterization by type: `List<String>` guarantees it holds only strings, and the compiler catches errors at build time, with no manual `cast` and no `ClassCastException` at runtime.\n\nUse them in your own APIs (`Repository<T>`, `Result<T>`) to remove duplication and `Object` casting. **Pitfalls:** you can't do `new T()` or create a `T[]` array, there's no `T.class`, and you can't `instanceof List<String>` — all because of type erasure (see below)."},"Java|Type erasure":{"term":"Type erasure","def":"Generics exist only at compile time; at runtime the type is erased, so List<String> and List<Integer> are indistinguishable.","hook":"The \"socks\" label peels off as it leaves the factory: inside the box you can no longer tell what's there — all boxes look identical.","more":"**Type erasure** — the compiler checks types against generics, but in bytecode it replaces `T` with `Object` (or with the bound). At runtime `List<String>` and `List<Integer>` are just `List`, with the same class.\n\n**Why:** backward compatibility with pre-generics code. **Pitfalls:** you can't overload a method only on `List<String>` vs `List<Integer>`, and there's no `new T[]`. To get the type at runtime you pass a `Class<T>` explicitly (a token) or use a super-type token."},"Java|Wildcard ? extends":{"term":"Wildcard ? extends","def":"Type substitution: <? extends T> you read from, <? super T> you write to — the PECS rule.","hook":"From a bucket of \"fruit or below\" you only scoop out (extends); into \"fruit or above\" you only pour in (super). PECS.","more":"**Wildcard** — `?` means \"some unknown type\". The **PECS** rule (Producer Extends, Consumer Super): you can safely **read** from `<? extends T>` (a source) and safely **write** to `<? super T>` (a sink).\n\nUse it in signatures: `copy(List<? super T> dst, List<? extends T> src)` — more flexible, accepts more types. **Pitfalls:** you can't `add` to a `List<? extends T>` (except null) — the compiler doesn't know the exact subtype; mixing up extends/super is the classic beginner mistake."},"Java|Stream":{"term":"Stream","def":"A pipeline for processing a collection: filter/map/collect; lazy, it only computes once it reaches a terminal operation.","hook":"An assembly line: a filtering machine, a painting machine, packaging. The belt won't move until you hit \"pack\".","more":"**Stream** — a declarative pipeline over a source (collection, generator). Intermediate operations (`filter`, `map`) are **lazy** — they just build a plan; nothing is computed until there's a terminal operation.\n\nGreat for \"filter-map-collect\" transformations and readability. **Pitfalls:** a stream is **single-use** (reusing it throws `IllegalStateException`); don't mutate external state inside it; for a simple loop or small data a plain `for` is faster and clearer."},"Java|Terminal operation":{"term":"Terminal operation","def":"A terminating stream operation (collect, forEach, count) that triggers all the computation and returns a result.","hook":"The \"Start\" button at the end of the line: until you hit collect/count, the machines stand idle and there's no result.","more":"**Terminal operation** — an operation that \"runs\" the pipeline and consumes the stream: `collect`, `forEach`, `count`, `reduce`, `findFirst`. Before it, the intermediate steps don't execute (laziness).\n\nAfter a terminal operation the stream is **closed** — you can't reuse it. **Pitfalls:** short-circuiting operations (`findFirst`, `anyMatch`) may not traverse the whole source — don't rely on side effects across all elements. On an infinite stream without `limit`, a non-terminating operation will hang."},"Java|Optional":{"term":"Optional","def":"A wrapper container (Java 8) holding 0 or 1 value; intended as a method return type to explicitly express 'a result may be absent', not as a replacement for all nulls","hook":"A sealed envelope marked 'possibly empty': the method hands it to you on the way out, and you must open it correctly — via map/orElse, not by ripping the seal off with a blind get()"},"Java|Checked exception":{"term":"Checked exception","def":"An exception you must handle or declare in throws; the compiler forces it (IOException etc.).","hook":"A road sign: \"pothole ahead\" — you must either drive around it (catch) or pass the sign on (throws).","more":"**Checked exception** — a subclass of `Exception` (but not `RuntimeException`) that the compiler requires you to either **handle** (`try/catch`) or **declare** (`throws`): `IOException`, `SQLException`.\n\nThe idea is recoverable, expected failures of the outside world (network, file, DB). **Pitfalls:** they spread `throws` across the whole stack and don't play well with lambdas/streams. In practice they're often **wrapped** into unchecked ones (`throw new UncheckedIOException(e)`) without losing the cause; never swallow them in an empty `catch`."},"Java|Unchecked exception":{"term":"Unchecked exception","def":"A subclass of RuntimeException (NPE, IllegalArgument); the compiler doesn't force you to catch it, and it usually signals a bug in the code.","hook":"A slippery floor with no sign: nobody warned you — you fell at runtime. Usually it's your own mistake in the code.","more":"**Unchecked exception** — a subclass of `RuntimeException`: `NullPointerException`, `IllegalArgumentException`, `IllegalStateException`. The compiler does **not** force you to catch or declare them.\n\nThey usually signal a **bug in the code** or a contract violation — fixed by correcting the code, not by `catch`. Use them for argument validation (`IllegalArgumentException`) and impossible states. **Pitfalls:** don't use them as normal control flow; catch them globally at the boundary (filter/handler), not in every method."},"Java|try-with-resources":{"term":"try-with-resources","def":"A construct that closes a resource (file, connection) itself after the block, if it is AutoCloseable.","hook":"A faucet with a self-closer: leave the room and the water shuts off on its own. A file/socket closes itself after the block.","more":"**try-with-resources** — resources are declared in parentheses `try (var r = ...)` and are automatically **closed** in reverse order after the block if they implement `AutoCloseable`/`Closeable` — even on an exception.\n\nIt removes hand-written `finally { close() }` and leaks. **Pitfalls:** an exception from `close()` becomes **suppressed** (available via `getSuppressed()`) and doesn't overwrite the primary one. Multiple resources are separated by `;`. Only what is **declared** in the try is closed — don't use an object opened earlier outside it."},"Java|finally":{"term":"finally","def":"A block that always runs after try/catch (except System.exit) — for releasing resources. A return or throw inside it overrides try's result/exception, swallowing them.","hook":"The method's last word: whatever you write in finally becomes the outcome, erasing everything from try."},"Java|Immutable":{"term":"Immutable","def":"An object whose state can't be changed after creation (String): final fields plus references only to immutable things; thread-safe.","hook":"Like a photo framed under glass: once shot, you can't repaint it, only make a new one.","more":"Under the hood: **all fields are final** and set in the constructor, and reference fields point only to equally immutable objects (or to defensive copies that aren't exposed). Any \"change\" returns a **new object** — like `String.substring()`.\n\nWhy: it gives **thread safety without locks**, safe use as a `HashMap` key, and hashCode caching. Pitfalls: heavy string concatenation in a loop generates garbage — use `StringBuilder`. And remember: a `final List` is immutable by reference, but you can still change its elements — that is NOT immutable."},"Java|final":{"term":"final","def":"A ban on changing: a variable can't be reassigned, a method can't be overridden, a class can't be subclassed.","hook":"A lock on three doors: the variable can't be moved, the method can't be rewritten, the class can't be extended.","more":"Under the hood these are **different guarantees**:\n- **final variable** — assigned once (can be done lazily in the constructor), can't be reassigned afterward;\n- **final method** — can't be `@Override`-d in a subclass;\n- **final class** — can't be subclassed (`String`, all wrappers).\n\nWhy: it fixes the contract, helps JIT optimizations, and is required to capture a variable in a lambda/anonymous class (effectively final). Pitfall: **final protects the reference, not the object** — a `final List` can still be `.add()`-ed. For true immutability you need an immutable wrapper."},"Java|Autoboxing":{"term":"Autoboxing","def":"Automatic conversion of a primitive to its wrapper and back (int↔Integer); it quietly breeds NPEs and slows down loops.","hook":"Like a wrapper for candy: an int is put into an Integer and taken out automatically; in a loop — a pile of garbage.","more":"Under the hood the compiler inserts `Integer.valueOf(i)` (**boxing**) and `intValue()` (**unboxing**) itself. Values from -128 to 127 come from the `Integer` cache; the rest create a new object on the heap.\n\nPitfalls:\n- **NPE** when unboxing a `null` Integer into an `int`;\n- comparing boxed values with `==` compares references, not values (outside the cache range it returns `false`);\n- in hot loops, boxing generates garbage and loads the GC.\n\nWhen: wrappers are unavoidable for collections, but in counting loops keep primitives."},"Java|load factor":{"term":"load factor","def":"The fill threshold of a HashMap (0.75 by default): once more than this fraction of the capacity is occupied, the number of buckets doubles and all elements are redistributed (rehash).","hook":"The shelf is three-quarters full — order twice as many shelves and lay everything out again.","more":"**0.75 is a compromise** between memory and speed: lower means fewer collisions but more empty buckets. **Rehashing is expensive** O(n), so when the size is known, set `new HashMap<>(capacity)` to avoid repeated growth. Capacity is always rounded up to a power of two."},"Java|fail-fast":{"term":"fail-fast","def":"Behavior of collection iterators: if the collection is structurally modified during iteration (not through the iterator itself), a ConcurrentModificationException is thrown immediately.","hook":"The conductor memorized the passenger count at boarding — someone got off en route, and they immediately stop the train.","more":"The iterator stores `expectedModCount` and checks it against the collection's `modCount` on every `next()`. **It's not a guarantee** but a best effort — don't use it for synchronization. Safe removal in a loop is via `iterator.remove()` or `removeIf()`. The alternative is **fail-safe** collections (CopyOnWriteArrayList) that iterate over a snapshot."},"Java|ConcurrentHashMap":{"term":"ConcurrentHashMap","def":"A thread-safe Map that doesn't lock the whole structure: reads are almost always lock-free, writes lock only the individual bucket/node, so threads don't wait in a single common queue.","hook":"Not one turnstile for the whole stadium, but a separate lock on each door — the crowd doesn't pile into one.","more":"Since Java 8 it uses **synchronization at the bucket-node level** (CAS + synchronized) rather than segments. It **doesn't allow null** keys/values (unlike HashMap) — otherwise `get`/`containsKey` would be ambiguous in a multithreaded context. Its iterators are **weakly consistent**: they don't throw CME and reflect the state \"roughly now\"."},"Java|Функциональный интерфейс":{"term":"Functional interface","def":"An interface with exactly ONE abstract method (SAM) — it can be implemented with a lambda or a method reference. It may have any number of default/static methods.","hook":"A single keyhole — that's why a compact lambda \"lockpick\" fits.","more":"The `@FunctionalInterface` annotation is optional but enables a compiler check (no more than one abstract method). **Object methods** (equals, toString) don't count — which is why Comparator with two signatures is still functional. The ready-made ones in `java.util.function`: Function, Predicate, Consumer, Supplier."},"Java|default-метод":{"term":"default method","def":"An interface method with a ready implementation (the default keyword) — it lets you add methods to an interface without breaking existing implementations.","hook":"The interface's factory configuration: if you didn't override it, you drive on the stock part.","more":"Introduced in Java 8 to extend the Collection interfaces (`stream()`, `forEach`). **The \"diamond\" conflict**: if two interfaces provide the same default, the compiler requires you to override it and explicitly choose via `Interface.super.foo()`. **A class always beats an interface** — a superclass implementation takes priority over a default."},"Java|Маркерный интерфейс":{"term":"Marker interface","def":"An interface WITH NO methods (Serializable, Cloneable) — it serves as a flag/tag: code or the JVM checks via instanceof whether an object has a capability.","hook":"A \"fragile\" sticker on a box — there are no methods, but the mover behaves differently.","more":"It's an old technique — today its role is largely covered by **annotations** (which carry metadata with parameters). A marker, though, gives a type-safe check at compile time and via `instanceof`. Example of the consequences: without `Cloneable`, calling `clone()` throws `CloneNotSupportedException`."},"Java|Рефлексия (Reflection)":{"term":"Reflection","def":"Accessing classes, fields, and methods by name at runtime (Class.forName, getDeclaredMethod, setAccessible) — you can create objects and call methods without knowing the types at compile time.","hook":"The program looks in a mirror and reads its own structure to pull levers blindly.","more":"The foundation of Spring, Hibernate, Jackson (injection, proxies, mapping). **Downsides**: slower than a direct call, bypasses access checks and encapsulation, and breaks compiler code analysis (errors surface at runtime). In Java 9+, `setAccessible` on another module requires `opens`. Cache `Method`/`Field` — the lookup is expensive."},"Java|sealed":{"term":"sealed","def":"A sealed class/interface with an explicit permits list — ONLY the listed types may extend it. Gives a closed hierarchy for an exhaustive switch.","hook":"A closed guest list at the door — anyone not in permits isn't allowed to inherit.","more":"Java 17. A subclass must be `final`, `sealed`, or `non-sealed`. The main benefit is an **exhaustive switch without default**: the compiler knows all the variants and catches an uncovered case. Paired with a **record** it gives algebraic data types (sum types). An alternative to closed enums when variants need different fields."},"Java|Pattern matching":{"term":"Pattern matching","def":"Combining a type check with value extraction: instanceof or switch checks the type and binds a variable at once (instanceof String s) — without a manual cast.","hook":"One stroke: check \"is it a string?\" and immediately take it out into a labeled box — without three separate steps.","more":"`instanceof` with a variable — Java 16. **Pattern matching for switch** and **record deconstruction** (`case Point(int x, int y)`) — Java 21. The bound variable is visible only where the check is true (flow scoping). In a switch it combines with sealed types for exhaustiveness and `when` guards."},"Java|record":{"term":"record","def":"An immutable data-carrier class: record Point(int x,int y) auto-generates fields, a constructor, getters, equals/hashCode, and toString from its components.","hook":"Fill in the form of fields — and the JVM writes all the bookkeeping itself (constructor, equals, toString).","more":"Java 16. Fields are **final**, the class is implicitly final and can't be subclassed (but it can implement interfaces). A **compact constructor** for validation: `public P { if(x<0) throw... }`. equals/hashCode are based on all components. Ideal for DTOs, Map keys, and (with sealed) for deconstruction in a switch."},"Java|var":{"term":"var","def":"Local variable type inference: var x = new ArrayList<String>() — the compiler infers the type from the right-hand side. This is static typing, not Object and not dynamic.","hook":"\"Compiler, infer the type yourself from the right-hand side\" — but in bytecode the type is still hard-wired.","more":"Java 10. Only for **local** variables with an initializer, in for/try-with-resources. **Not allowed** for fields, method parameters, return types, or without initialization. The type is inferred from the expression and is fixed. Don't overuse it — readability suffers if the type isn't obvious from the right-hand side."},"Java|Ссылка на метод":{"term":"Method reference","def":"Shorthand for a lambda that just calls one existing method: String::length instead of s -> s.length(). Four kinds: static, on an object, on a type, and to a constructor.","hook":"Instead of retelling the whole recipe, just point a finger at the finished dish.","more":"Four forms: `Math::abs` (static), `str::length` (on a specific object), `String::length` (on a type — the first argument becomes the receiver), `ArrayList::new` (constructor). **Gotcha**: `obj::m` captures the current object at the moment the reference is created. The type is derived from the functional interface context."},"Java|Comparator":{"term":"Comparator","def":"An external comparison-strategy object (compare(a,b)) — it defines ordering separately from the class. Unlike Comparable (natural ordering inside the class, a single compareTo).","hook":"Comparable is a person's own character; Comparator is an outside judge who ranks however they like.","more":"Built as a chain: `comparing(A::age).thenComparing(A::name).reversed()`. The second key only kicks in when the first is equal. **The danger of `a - b`**: int overflow gives the wrong sign — use `Integer.compare(a,b)`. For null, use `nullsFirst`/`nullsLast`, otherwise you get an NPE while sorting."},"Java|intern()":{"term":"intern()","def":"Places a string into the shared pool and returns the canonical reference: strings equal by content become one object after intern() (== returns true).","hook":"You returned the book to the shared library — now everyone borrows the same copy instead of breeding copies.","more":"String **literals** are interned automatically, `new String()` is not. The pool lives on the heap (since Java 7) and its size is tunable. **The danger in production**: mass `intern()` of uncontrolled strings bloats the pool and loads the GC/table — and those strings live a long time. Since Java 9, strings are stored as `byte[]` (Compact Strings) — Latin-1 at 1 byte/char."},"Java|effectively final":{"term":"effectively final","def":"A local variable assigned once and never changed again — formally without the final keyword, but in fact immutable. Only such variables can be captured in a lambda or anonymous class.","hook":"You didn't write final, but you also never touched it after assignment — for a lambda it's the same thing.","more":"Introduced in Java 8 to avoid writing `final` before every captured variable. **Why the restriction**: a lambda captures the *value* (a copy), not a reference to the variable — changing it afterward would be ambiguous. The workaround for accumulation is a single-element array or an object field. Distinguish it from a `final` field (a class member) and a `final` method/class (a ban on overriding/inheritance)."},"Infra|Redis":{"term":"Redis","def":"Blazing-fast key-value store: keeps data in RAM for speed (can also flush a copy to disk).","hook":"Like a notepad on your desk vs. an archive in the basement: right there in memory, answers instantly.","more":"In-memory **key-value** store: data lives in RAM, hence the speed (microseconds). Single-threaded core on an event loop, but with rich structures: strings, hashes, lists, sorted sets, TTLs. Persistence via **RDB** (snapshots) and **AOF** (command log).\n\n- Used as a cache, counters, rate limiter, queues, locks\n- Pitfalls: memory isn't infinite — you need an **eviction policy** and TTLs; \"flushing to disk\" isn't a full guarantee — on a crash you can lose the most recent writes."},"Infra|Кэш":{"term":"Cache","def":"A temporary copy of data kept closer at hand, so you don't hit the slow database every time for the same thing.","hook":"Like a sticky note with the answer on the fridge: you don't dig through the cupboard again, you grab the ready answer.","more":"Under the hood it's a **temporary copy** of data in a faster layer (RAM, Redis, CDN) closer to the consumer, so you don't keep going to the slow source for the same thing. Why: lower latency and less load on the DB.\n\n- Strategies: **cache-aside**, write-through, write-back\n- Main pitfalls: **invalidation** (stale data) and **cache stampede** — when a key expires and a crowd of requests hits the database at once; mitigated with TTL + locking/warming."},"Infra|TTL":{"term":"TTL","def":"The lifetime of a cache entry: once the time is up, the entry expires on its own.","hook":"Like yogurt in the fridge: past its date — it gets tossed automatically, freeing up space.","more":"**Time To Live** — the lifetime of an entry: once it expires, the entry is automatically considered invalid and removed. Why: cached data doesn't hang around forever and refreshes itself, no manual cleanup needed.\n\n- In Redis, eviction is **lazy** (on access) plus background sampling, not precise to the second\n- Pitfalls: too short a TTL means frequent misses and load on the DB; too long means stale data. Identical TTLs across many keys cause a wave of simultaneous expirations — add **jitter**."},"Infra|RabbitMQ":{"term":"RabbitMQ","def":"A message broker: some services drop tasks into a queue, others pick them up and do the work.","hook":"Like the postal service: you drop a letter in the box, the mail carrier delivers it. The sender doesn't wait at the door.","more":"A message broker built on the **AMQP** protocol. Its key feature is the **exchange → binding → queue** model: a producer publishes to an exchange, which routes to queues based on rules. Why: decouple services, smooth out spikes, distribute tasks asynchronously.\n\n- Exchange types: direct, topic, fanout\n- Has **ack**, retries, dead-letter\n- Pitfalls: people confuse it with Kafka — RabbitMQ is a **task queue** (a message is gone once acked), not an event log you can replay history from."},"Infra|Очередь (Queue)":{"term":"Queue","def":"A list of tasks that accumulates messages until a worker picks them up one by one.","hook":"Like a checkout line: tasks stand single file, the worker takes them one at a time from the front.","more":"A buffer following the **FIFO** principle: messages pile up until a worker pulls them one by one. Why: decouple in time those who create work from those who do it; a load spike gets absorbed into the queue.\n\n- Smooths out bursts, enables retries and deferred processing\n- Pitfalls: a growing queue means the consumer can't keep up (you need lag monitoring and scaling); ordering and **exactly-once** aren't guaranteed automatically — you need acks and idempotency."},"Infra|Producer/Consumer":{"term":"Producer/Consumer","def":"The Producer puts a message into the queue, the Consumer pulls it out and processes it.","hook":"The cook places the order on the shelf (producer), the waiter takes it and carries it out (consumer).","more":"The fundamental exchange pattern: the **Producer** (publisher) builds a message and puts it into a queue/broker, the **Consumer** processes it after pulling it out. They don't know about each other — they're bound only by the message contract. Why: asynchrony and independent scaling of both sides.\n\n- Multiple consumers on one queue = parallelism (**competing consumers**)\n- Pitfalls: the consumer must be **idempotent** — on redelivery (after crash/redelivery) a single message can arrive twice."},"Infra|ACK":{"term":"ACK","def":"Confirmation that a message was processed; without it the broker will deliver it again.","hook":"Like a \"read\" receipt in a messenger app: no check mark — they'll send it again.","more":"Under the hood, an **ACK** (acknowledgement) is a signal from the consumer to the broker (Kafka, RabbitMQ): \"I've taken and processed this message, you can stop storing it and don't resend.\" Until the ACK arrives, the broker treats the message as incomplete and, after a timeout, redelivers it (to another consumer or the same one).\n\nWhy: it guarantees **at-least-once** delivery — nothing is lost if the handler crashes. Pitfalls: if you commit the ACK **before** actually processing, you'll lose the message on a crash; if **after**, a crash gives you a duplicate. That's why handlers are made **idempotent** (reprocessing doesn't corrupt data)."},"Infra|Elasticsearch":{"term":"Elasticsearch","def":"A search engine: lightning-fast full-text search and filtering over large volumes of data.","hook":"Google inside your own data: searches across millions of rows instantly.","more":"Under the hood, **Elasticsearch** is a distributed store built on top of **Apache Lucene**. Its key trick is the **inverted index**: instead of \"document → words\" it builds \"word → list of documents,\" so text search is instant even across billions of records. Data is sharded and replicated across the cluster's nodes.\n\nWhy: full-text search, filtering, aggregations, logs (the ELK stack). Pitfalls: it's **not a transactional DB** — no ACID, and search is **near real-time** (a fresh document is visible ~1s after refresh). Don't make it the source of truth; data is duplicated from the primary DB."},"Infra|Index":{"term":"Index","def":"In Elasticsearch this is a store of same-type documents, like a table in a database.","hook":"Like a drawer-folder for identical cards — thousands of documents inside.","more":"Under the hood, an **index** in Elasticsearch is a logical collection of same-type documents (JSON), physically split into **shards** (pieces of a Lucene index) that are distributed across nodes and replicated. An index has a **mapping** — a schema of fields and their types that affects how a field is indexed and searched.\n\nWhy: like a \"table\" — it groups data of one kind (e.g., all orders). Pitfalls: the number of primary shards is set at creation and **can't be changed** afterward — you'd have to reindex. The mapping of an existing field can't be redefined either, so plan your schema ahead; for log rotation, use date-based indices + an alias."},"Infra|Docker":{"term":"Docker","def":"Packages an application with everything it needs into a container, so it runs the same everywhere.","hook":"A box with the app and all its contents: wherever you open it — there it works.","more":"Under the hood, **Docker** packages an application and all its dependencies (runtime, libraries, OS layer) into a **container** — a process isolated on the host via namespaces (isolation) and cgroups (resource limits). Unlike a VM, the OS kernel is shared, so containers are lightweight and start in seconds.\n\nWhy: kills \"works on my machine\" — identical environments across dev/CI/prod. Pitfalls: containers are **ephemeral** — data inside is lost on recreation, you need volumes; don't cram several services into one container (one process = one container); watch image size and secrets."},"Infra|Image":{"term":"Image","def":"A ready-made snapshot of an application; containers are launched from it, like houses from a single blueprint.","hook":"A house blueprint: one snapshot — as many identical builds as you want.","more":"Under the hood, an **image** is an immutable template made of a stack of layers built from a Dockerfile: each layer is a filesystem diff (installing a package, copying code). Layers are cached and reused across images, saving space and build time. A container = image + a thin writable layer on top.\n\nWhy: one image → many identical containers; versioned via tags. Pitfalls: the order of instructions in the Dockerfile affects caching — put frequently changing things (COPY of code) below rarely changing ones (dependency install); the **latest** tag doesn't pin a version — in production specify a concrete tag or digest."},"Infra|Kubernetes (K8s)":{"term":"Kubernetes (K8s)","def":"A container conductor: it launches, scales, and restarts containers across a fleet of servers on its own.","hook":"An orchestra conductor: hands out parts, brings back the fallen, adds musicians.","more":"Under the hood, **Kubernetes** is an orchestrator working on the **declarative desired state** principle: you describe in YAML what you want (\"3 replicas of this pod\"), and the control plane (scheduler, controllers) continuously compares the actual state to the desired one and reconciles it — restarting what crashed, scheduling across nodes, scaling.\n\nWhy: managing containers across a cluster of tens/hundreds of machines, self-healing, rolling updates, service discovery. Pitfalls: high complexity — for a couple of containers it's overkill (docker-compose is enough); it requires well-thought-out requests/limits, probes, and an understanding of networking/RBAC, or it's pain in production."},"Infra|Pod":{"term":"Pod","def":"The smallest unit in K8s: one or more containers that live together.","hook":"A pea pod: one or a couple of pea-containers inside, together.","more":"Under the hood, a **Pod** is the atomic deployment unit in K8s: one (usually) or several containers sharing a common **network namespace** (one IP, localhost between them) and volumes. Containers in a pod are always scheduled onto the same node and live/die together.\n\nWhy: a main container + a **sidecar** (logs, service-mesh proxy, init containers). Pitfalls: a pod is **ephemeral and disposable** — on restart it gets a new IP, so you don't address it directly but through a Service. A pod doesn't self-heal on its own — it's managed by a Deployment/ReplicaSet."},"Infra|Probe":{"term":"Probe","def":"A container health check: K8s knocks and decides whether it's alive and ready for traffic.","hook":"A doctor taps with a little hammer: alive? ready to work? — decides based on the answer.","more":"Under the hood, a **probe** is a periodic check the kubelet runs against a container: an HTTP request to an endpoint, a TCP connect, or running a command inside the container. Based on the response code/success, K8s decides what to do with the pod.\n\nWhy: to automatically tell an \"alive and ready\" container from one that's \"hung/not warmed up.\" Three kinds: **liveness**, **readiness**, **startup** (for slow starts, so liveness doesn't kill the app prematurely). Pitfalls: overly aggressive timeouts/thresholds cause false restarts and cascading failures; a probe shouldn't poke heavy dependencies, or one DB lag will take down all the pods."},"Infra|Liveness/Readiness":{"term":"Liveness/Readiness","def":"Liveness checks that the pod isn't hung, Readiness checks that it's ready to accept requests.","hook":"Liveness — is there a pulse? Readiness — is the door open for guests?","more":"Under the hood these are two different probes. **Liveness** answers \"is the process not hung/deadlocked?\" — if it fails, K8s **restarts** the container. **Readiness** answers \"ready to accept traffic?\" — if it fails, the pod is **removed from the Service's endpoints** (no traffic flows), but the container is NOT restarted.\n\nWhy: readiness takes load off a pod during warm-up/overload/loss of a dependency without killing it. Pitfalls: a common mistake is hanging an external-DB check on **liveness**: during a DB lag, K8s will needlessly restart healthy pods. Dependencies → in readiness, only internal state → in liveness."},"Testing|Unit-тест":{"term":"Unit test","def":"Verifies one small piece of code (method/class) in isolation, without DB or network, very fast.","hook":"Examining a single part under a microscope — no plug, no wires, in a flash.","more":"Under the hood a **unit test** verifies the smallest unit — a method or class — in full isolation: external dependencies (DB, network, time) are replaced with **mocks/stubs**. It runs in memory, with no I/O, so thousands of tests finish in seconds.\n\nWhy: fast feedback, precise bug localization (a test fails → you know where), a safety net during refactoring. Pitfalls: it won't catch **integration** errors (how the pieces actually fit together); over-mocking → tests verify the mocks instead of the logic and break on every change. Test behavior, not implementation; verifying the wiring needs integration tests."},"Testing|Integration-тест":{"term":"Integration test","def":"Verifies how the pieces work together with a real DB, Kafka, HTTP — slower than a unit test.","hook":"Checking how the gears mesh: with a real DB and Kafka, slower.","more":"Under the hood an **integration test** runs several components together with **real** external systems — DB, Kafka, HTTP services — often spinning them up in containers (**Testcontainers**) or embedded instances. It exercises the DB schema, SQL, serialization, configs — the things mocks hide in unit tests.\n\nWhy: catches errors at the seams that unit tests miss. Pitfalls: slow (infrastructure startup, I/O), more fragile and harder to debug. That's why you keep a **test pyramid**: many fast unit tests, fewer integration ones. Isolate data between tests (clean schema/transaction), otherwise tests will flake due to shared state."},"Testing|TDD":{"term":"TDD","def":"First you write a failing test, then code to make it pass, then clean it up — the red-green-refactor cycle.","hook":"First a red test, then green, then cleanup — and around again.","more":"Under the hood **TDD** (Test-Driven Development) is the **red-green-refactor** cycle: you write a failing test for a not-yet-existing feature (**red**), write the minimum code to make it pass (**green**), then clean up the code without breaking the tests (**refactor**). The test is written BEFORE the implementation.\n\nWhy: the test becomes an executable specification, the design comes out testable and modular, regressions are caught immediately. Pitfalls: it's not a silver bullet — on exploratory code/prototypes it gets in the way; it's easy to slide into testing implementation instead of behavior. It fits when a module's requirements are clear; start from the public contract, not from private details."},"Testing|Mock":{"term":"Mock","def":"A fake object you program with expected behavior and then assert that its methods were actually called.","hook":"An actor in makeup: you write their lines yourself, then check whether they said them.","more":"Under the hood a **mock** is a generated object (via proxy/bytecode, as in Mockito) on which you describe expectations up front: which methods should be called, with which arguments, how many times. It's needed to verify **interaction** (behavior verification) rather than the result: \"the service actually called repository.save() once\".\n\n- **Pitfalls:** it's easy to slide into over-specification — the test breaks on any refactoring even though the behavior is the same.\n- **When:** you're verifying side effects, an emitted event, a call to an external port."},"Testing|Stub":{"term":"Stub","def":"A stand-in that simply returns a predefined answer and verifies nothing.","hook":"Plastic food in a shop window: the plate always shows the same thing, nobody asks \"did you eat it?\".","more":"A **stub** returns a hardcoded answer to a call and makes no assertions — it's a pure data source for the test. In Mockito it's `when(x).thenReturn(y)` with no following `verify()`. It's needed to isolate the code under test from a dependency and drive it down a specific branch (for example, the repository \"found\" or \"didn't find\" a record).\n\n- **Difference from a mock:** a stub verifies **state/result**, a mock verifies the fact of a call.\n- **Pitfalls:** don't overuse it — many stubs = the test knows too much about the internals."},"Testing|Fake":{"term":"Fake","def":"A simplified but working replacement for a dependency, e.g. an in-memory database instead of a real one.","hook":"A toy stove: it heats for pretend, but you can actually cook on it — like an in-memory DB.","more":"A **fake** is a real but lightweight implementation of an interface with real logic: an in-memory `HashMap` instead of a DB, an in-memory queue instead of Kafka. Under the hood it actually stores and returns data, so it survives several calls in a row (put → get).\n\n- **Why:** fast tests without Docker when you need working logic rather than a single stubbed answer.\n- **Pitfalls:** a fake may diverge in behavior from the production implementation (transactions, constraints, concurrency) — it's only an approximation."},"Testing|Spy":{"term":"Spy","def":"An object that records how it was called (methods, arguments) so you can verify it later; in Mockito — a real object with partial stubbing.","hook":"A spy walks alongside the real thing and jots down in a notebook who did what and how many times.","more":"A **spy** wraps an object and records the call history (methods, arguments, order) so you can later `verify`/check what was recorded. In Mockito `spy()` is a **partial mock**: a real object where most methods run for real, while individual ones can be overridden.\n\n- **Pitfalls:** with a spy use `doReturn().when(spy)` instead of `when(spy.x())` — otherwise the real method runs during setup.\n- **When:** you need to override 1–2 methods on a real object and leave the rest as is."},"Testing|JUnit":{"term":"JUnit","def":"The main framework for writing and running tests in Java (@Test, assertEquals, etc.).","hook":"A gym for code: each test is an exercise, the machine runs them all one by one.","more":"**JUnit** is the standard test engine in Java. Under the hood (JUnit 5 / Jupiter) the test runner discovers methods annotated with `@Test`, manages the lifecycle (`@BeforeEach`, `@AfterAll`), and reports the result to the build tool (Maven Surefire, Gradle, IDE) via assertions.\n\n- **Why:** a single way to write, run, and integrate tests into CI/build.\n- **Pitfalls:** don't confuse JUnit 4 (`@Before`, `@RunWith`) with JUnit 5 (`@BeforeEach`, extensions) — the annotations and API differ, and migration needs care."},"Testing|Mockito":{"term":"Mockito","def":"A library for creating mocks: mock(), when().thenReturn(), verify().","hook":"A build-it-yourself kit for fakes: mock() shapes it, when() teaches it, verify() checks it.","more":"**Mockito** generates test doubles at runtime (via ByteBuddy/proxies) and provides a DSL: `mock()` creates a double, `when().thenReturn()` sets the answer, `verify()` checks calls, `ArgumentCaptor` captures arguments. It's the most common tool for unit tests with isolated dependencies.\n\n- **Pitfalls:** you can't mock `final`/`static`/private methods without `mockito-inline`; excessive `verify` makes tests fragile.\n- **When:** you're isolating a class from its collaborators in a pure unit test."},"Testing|Testcontainers":{"term":"Testcontainers","def":"Spins up real Postgres, Kafka, MinIO in Docker for the duration of a test and tears them down itself.","hook":"Renting a real test rig for an hour: spun up Postgres in Docker, tested, shut it down.","more":"**Testcontainers** uses the Docker API to spin up real containers (Postgres, Kafka, MinIO) before a test, feeds their random ports into the config, and reliably tears them down afterward (via the ryuk reaper container). Under the hood it's a real dependency, not a fake.\n\n- **Why:** integration tests against a real DB/broker without manual provisioning and without drifting from prod.\n- **Pitfalls:** it requires a running Docker and tests are slower; use `@Container` static + reuse, otherwise every test spins up a new container."},"Testing|Assertion":{"term":"Assertion","def":"A check of the expected result; if it doesn't match, the test fails (assertEquals, AssertJ).","hook":"Scales at inspection: expected 5, the pan reads 5 — ok; otherwise an alarm.","more":"An **assertion** is a check of a fact: it compares the actual result with the expected one and, if they don't match, throws an `AssertionError`, marking the test as failed. The basic set is `assertEquals`, `assertTrue`, `assertThrows`; **AssertJ** gives a fluent style `assertThat(x).isEqualTo(y)` with readable messages.\n\n- **Pitfalls:** one check — one idea; a dozen unrelated asserts in a test make it hard to tell what exactly broke.\n- **When:** in the Assert block describe only the behavior the test was written for."},"Testing|AAA":{"term":"AAA","def":"The structure of a test: Arrange (set up), Act (invoke), Assert (verify).","hook":"A scene in three beats: set the props, perform, check the applause.","more":"**AAA** (Arrange–Act–Assert) is a structural pattern for the test body: first you prepare data and mocks (**Arrange**), then a single invocation of the action under test (**Act**), then checks of the result (**Assert**). The BDD equivalent is Given/When/Then.\n\n- **Why:** the test reads in seconds, the boundary between setup and verification is clear.\n- **Pitfalls:** if a single test has several Act blocks, it's probably several tests; split them so a failure points precisely at the cause."},"Testing|Покрытие":{"term":"Coverage","def":"The percentage of lines/branches of code touched by tests; measured by JaCoCo, but 100% isn't the goal.","hook":"Cleaning room by room: as many rooms as you entered, that many you swept; the whole flat isn't needed.","more":"**Coverage** shows what fraction of lines/branches/conditions executed under tests. **JaCoCo** instruments bytecode, counts hits, and builds a report; line-, branch-, and condition-coverage are distinguished.\n\n- **Pitfalls:** a high percentage doesn't mean \"well tested\" — code can execute without a single meaningful assert. Chasing 100% breeds pointless tests.\n- **When:** use it as an indicator of **uncovered** areas, not as an end in itself; covering critical business logic matters more."},"Testing|Fixture":{"term":"Fixture","def":"Prepared data and environment for a test, shared via @BeforeEach/@BeforeAll.","hook":"A table set before the guests arrive: data and decorations ready in advance for every test.","more":"A **fixture** is a fixed, reproducible state for a test: test objects, data in the DB, provisioned resources. In JUnit shared setup is moved into `@BeforeEach` (before each test) or `@BeforeAll` (once per class) to avoid duplicating setup.\n\n- **Pitfalls:** a shared mutable fixture between tests creates hidden dependencies and flaky behavior; `@BeforeAll` state should be immutable.\n- **When:** the same setup repeats across several tests — extract it into a fixture."},"Testing|Flaky-тест":{"term":"Flaky test","def":"A test that sometimes fails and sometimes passes with no code changes — usually due to timing or order.","hook":"A moody elevator: same code, but sometimes it moves, sometimes it doesn't — depends on its mood.","more":"A **flaky test** gives an unstable result on the same code. Under the hood the causes are: races and timing dependence (`sleep`, asynchrony), unisolated shared state, dependence on execution order, real time/time zones, network/external services.\n\n- **Pitfalls:** flaky tests undermine trust in CI — people start ignoring red builds.\n- **When to fix:** immediately. The cure is waiting on a condition (`Awaitility`) instead of `sleep`, isolating data, and pinning the `Clock`/time."},"Testing|@QuarkusTest":{"term":"@QuarkusTest","def":"A Quarkus annotation: boots the application in the JVM with CDI bean injection for tests (full e2e is provided by @QuarkusIntegrationTest).","hook":"You started a mini-copy of the app on your desk to poke at it before delivery.","more":"Under the hood **@QuarkusTest** starts the real application in the same JVM: the **CDI container** comes up, you can `@Inject` beans and replace them via `@InjectMock`. The test HTTP endpoint is reachable through RestAssured.\n\n**Why:** verify the controller→service→repository wiring without mocking everything. **Pitfalls:** this is not a native build — to verify the built artifact (jar/native) you need `@QuarkusIntegrationTest`, which runs the application as a black box in a separate process, without `@Inject`."},"Web|HTTP":{"term":"HTTP","def":"The protocol browsers and servers use to talk in requests and responses, usually over TCP.","hook":"Mail between browser and server: you send a request letter, you get a response letter back.","more":"**HTTP** is a text-based, request/response application protocol on top of TCP (over QUIC/UDP in HTTP/3). A request carries a **method, path, headers, and body**; a response carries a status, headers, and body.\n\n**Why:** one common standard for client↔server communication on the web and in APIs. **Gotchas:** the protocol is **stateless** — the server remembers nothing about prior requests, so state lives in cookies/tokens. HTTP/1.1 opens many connections; HTTP/2 multiplexes them over one. Without TLS (plain http), traffic travels in the clear."},"Web|GET/POST":{"term":"GET/POST","def":"GET fetches data, POST sends something new; there's also PUT (replace), PATCH (tweak), DELETE (remove).","hook":"GET is going to the shop for bread; POST is dropping new goods off at the warehouse.","more":"These are **HTTP methods** (verbs): **GET** reads without side effects, **POST** creates or triggers an action, **PUT** replaces a resource wholesale, **PATCH** updates some fields, **DELETE** removes it.\n\n**Why:** the method expresses intent, not the URL. **Gotchas:** GET, PUT, and DELETE should be **idempotent** (repeating them changes nothing), while POST is not — which is why a double form submit creates a duplicate. GET parameters show up in URLs and logs, so never put passwords there. A GET technically carries no body."},"Web|Коды ответа":{"term":"Status codes","def":"The numeric status: 2xx success, 3xx redirect, 4xx client error, 5xx server error.","hook":"A traffic light for the response: 2xx green, 3xx turn here, 4xx you messed up, 5xx the server fell over.","more":"The first digit sets the class: **2xx** success (200 OK, 201 Created), **3xx** redirect (301 permanent, 304 not modified), **4xx** the client's fault (400 bad request, 401 unauthenticated, 403 forbidden, 404 not found), **5xx** server failure (500, 502 bad gateway, 503 unavailable).\n\n**Why:** the code tells the client what to do next (retry, fix the request). **Gotchas:** don't return 200 with `{error:...}` in the body — it breaks error handling, caching, and monitoring; people mix up 401 (who are you?) and 403 (I know who you are, but you can't)."},"Web|REST":{"term":"REST","def":"An API style where data is resources addressed by URL and actions are expressed via HTTP methods, with no server-side state.","hook":"Every thing sits on its own labeled shelf, and the method says take it, put it, or throw it out.","more":"**REST** is an architectural style: data is modeled as **resources** (`/users/42`), actions map to HTTP methods, and the server is **stateless** (each request is self-contained, no server session).\n\n**Why:** a simple, cacheable, scalable API over plain HTTP. **Gotchas:** \"REST\" in production is usually loose — true REST requires **HATEOAS** (links to next actions in the response), which almost nobody implements. Pain points: over/under-fetching, N+1 queries, and versioning (`/v2/`)."},"Web|JSON":{"term":"JSON","def":"A simple text data format — objects, arrays, numbers, strings; in REST it carries the request and response body.","hook":"A universal box for data: labeled crates and lists, readable by both humans and machines.","more":"**JSON** is a text format borrowing JavaScript object syntax: key–value pairs `{}`, arrays `[]`, strings, numbers, `true/false/null`.\n\n**Why:** human-readable, trivially parsed everywhere — the de facto standard for REST bodies. **Gotchas:** no date type and no comments; **numbers** parse as doubles, so large `long`/`id` values lose precision (often sent as strings); keys are schemaless, so a typo goes unnoticed without validation. For bulky/binary payloads it's heavier than protobuf."},"Web|TLS":{"term":"TLS","def":"Channel encryption — the padlock in https — so traffic can't be read or tampered with.","hook":"A sealed envelope: in transit the letter can neither be opened nor swapped.","more":"**TLS** encrypts the TCP channel: during the **handshake** the parties use asymmetric crypto to agree on a shared session key, then data flows under a symmetric cipher. A certificate from a **CA** proves the server is who it claims to be.\n\n**Why:** confidentiality + integrity + server authentication (https). **Gotchas:** an expired or self-signed certificate breaks the connection; don't conflate TLS with SSL (deprecated); encrypting the channel doesn't protect data **at rest on the server** once it's decrypted."},"Web|JWT":{"term":"JWT","def":"A signed token carrying user data; the server verifies the signature and trusts it without hitting the database.","hook":"A festival wristband: security sees the stamp and lets you in without calling the box office.","more":"**JWT** is a token of three Base64 parts joined by dots: header.payload.signature. The payload holds **claims** (user id, roles, `exp`); the signature is computed with a secret (HMAC) or a private key (RSA). The server verifies the signature and trusts the contents without a DB lookup.\n\n**Why:** stateless authorization, handy across microservices. **Gotchas:** the payload is **not encrypted**, only signed — anyone can read it, so don't put secrets there; a token can't be revoked before `exp` (use a short TTL + refresh); the classic hole is accepting `alg:none`."},"Web|DNS":{"term":"DNS","def":"The internet's phone book: turns a site name into the server's IP address.","hook":"A phone book: dial the site name, get its number — the address.","more":"**DNS** is a distributed hierarchy of servers that resolves a name (`acme.com`) to an **IP**. The resolver walks the chain root→TLD→authoritative server, and answers are cached for their **TTL**.\n\n**Why:** humans remember names while the network runs on IPs; it also lets you swap servers behind a name. **Gotchas:** after you change a record, the old IP lingers in caches until the TTL expires (\"I still see the old site\"); one record type isn't enough — A/AAAA for address, CNAME for alias, MX for mail. DNS is unencrypted by default (DoH/DoT exist)."},"Web|Reverse proxy":{"term":"Reverse proxy","def":"A gatekeeper server (nginx) in front of your services: it takes requests and routes them to the right backends.","hook":"A doorman at the entrance: one person greets every guest and walks them to the right office.","more":"A **reverse proxy** (nginx, Envoy) sits in front of your backends: it accepts incoming requests and **proxies** them to the right service by rules (path, host).\n\n**Why:** a single entry point — this is where you do **TLS termination**, load balancing, caching, rate limiting, routing to microservices, and hiding the internal topology. Unlike a forward proxy, which shields the client, a reverse proxy shields the server. **Gotchas:** the client's real IP is lost — you need `X-Forwarded-For`; the proxy itself becomes a single point of failure and needs redundancy."},"Web|gRPC":{"term":"gRPC","def":"A fast binary way to call another service's methods, over protobuf and HTTP/2.","hook":"A direct internal phone call between services: short, binary, no small talk.","more":"**gRPC** is an RPC framework: calling a remote method looks like a local call. The contract lives in a **.proto** file, data is serialized as binary **protobuf**, and the transport is **HTTP/2** (multiplexing, bidirectional streaming).\n\n**Why:** fast, compact, type-safe communication **between services**, with generated client/server stubs. **Gotchas:** the binary isn't human-readable and is awkward to debug with curl; browsers can't speak it directly (you need grpc-web/a proxy); changing the .proto requires keeping compatibility. For public external APIs, REST is usually the pick."},"Web|GraphQL":{"term":"GraphQL","def":"An API with a single endpoint where the client states in the query exactly which fields it wants back.","hook":"A buffet with a custom order: one counter, but you write the bill yourself, item by item.","more":"**GraphQL** is a query language for an API behind **one endpoint** (POST `/graphql`). The client describes a tree of needed fields in the body, and the server returns exactly those, driven by a typed **schema** and **resolvers** per field.\n\n**Why:** it kills over/under-fetching and the zoo of REST endpoints; great for diverse clients (mobile/web). **Gotchas:** the backend easily hits **N+1** (DataLoader/batching saves you); HTTP caching and familiar status codes break (often 200 with an errors array); throttling deep/expensive queries is hard."},"Web|SOAP":{"term":"SOAP","def":"An old, heavy exchange protocol using XML envelopes, still alive in banks and government systems.","hook":"Like registered mail in a thick stamped envelope: heavy and formal, but banks still send it exactly this way.","more":"Under the hood, **SOAP** is a strictly specified exchange of XML messages (**Envelope → Header → Body**), usually over HTTP, with the contract described in **WSDL** and often a bundle of WS-* standards (WS-Security, WS-ReliableMessaging).\n\nYou reach for it for a **rigid contract**, typing, and built-in security and transactions — which is why it survives in banks, government, and telecom. Gotchas: verbosity, heavy XML parsing, painful debugging. Use it only when an external system demands it; for anything new, go REST/gRPC."},"Web|CORS":{"term":"CORS","def":"A browser rule: by default it blocks requests to a different domain, and the server can choose to allow them.","hook":"A guard at the gate: no entry from a stranger's yard until the homeowner personally waves this guest in.","more":"**CORS** (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a mechanism of the browser itself. Under the hood, for \"unsafe\" requests it sends a **preflight OPTIONS**, and the server answers with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin/Methods/Headers`; missing the right header, the browser cuts off the response.\n\nIt's there to protect the user from other sites hitting an API under their cookie. Gotchas: it's **browser-only** (curl/server-to-server ignore it), `*` is incompatible with `credentials`, and the error is often mistaken for a server crash — in reality the request arrived but the response was hidden."},"Web|Idempotency":{"term":"Idempotency","def":"The property where repeating the same request creates no duplicates (GET, PUT, DELETE are like this).","hook":"An elevator button: press it ten times if you like — one elevator comes, no extras summoned.","more":"**Idempotency** means repeating an operation leaves the state the same as a single call. By HTTP semantics **GET, PUT, and DELETE** are idempotent, while **POST is not**. Technically you achieve it with a deterministic key: `Idempotency-Key`, an upsert, or a UNIQUE constraint.\n\nCritical under retries, timeouts, and at-least-once delivery (Kafka, payments) — otherwise you get **duplicate charges/orders**. Gotchas: idempotent ≠ identical response (a second DELETE returns 404); the key must be stored with a TTL, and a bare retry without a key won't save you."},"Algorithms|Algorithm":{"term":"Algorithm","def":"A precise step-by-step recipe for turning input data into the result you want.","hook":"A borscht recipe: ordered steps — grab the ingredients, follow each point, get the dish.","more":"**Algorithm** — a finite, unambiguously defined sequence of steps that turns input into output. Core properties: **determinism**, finiteness (it terminates), and correctness on every valid input.\n\nIt's the foundation of any program: the same result can be reached by different algorithms with very different time/memory costs. Pitfalls: correctness isn't enough — **complexity** matters too (see Big-O), as does handling edge cases — empty input, overflow, duplicates. Get it correct first, optimize second."},"Algorithms|Big-O":{"term":"Big-O","def":"Notation showing how runtime or memory grows as the input size N increases.","hook":"A catering bill by headcount: what matters isn't the cost now, but how it climbs once a crowd shows up.","more":"**Big-O** — an asymptotic upper bound on resource growth as N → ∞. It describes the **order of growth**, dropping constants and lower-order terms: `O(2n+5)` → `O(n)`.\n\nIt lets you compare algorithms independently of hardware and language. Pitfall: it's about **growth, not absolute speed** — for small N a \"slow\" O(n^2) can beat O(n log n) thanks to constants. Alongside it live Ω (lower) and Θ (tight) bounds; in practice the worst/average case often matters too."},"Algorithms|O(1)":{"term":"O(1)","def":"Constant time: the work doesn't depend on data size — e.g. array access by index.","hook":"Pulling a book off the shelf by its slot number: a thousand books or one, you grab it instantly.","more":"**O(1)** — constant complexity: the number of operations is fixed and **independent of N**. Examples: array access by index, hash-table read/write (amortized), stack push.\n\nIt's the ideal for hot paths. Pitfalls: the \"constant\" can be large (a slow O(1) loses to a fast O(n) on small data); hash access is **amortized** O(1) — on collisions/resize a single operation can degrade to O(n)."},"Algorithms|O(n)":{"term":"O(n)","def":"Linear: walk all N elements once; time grows proportionally to the data.","hook":"Checking every guest against the list one by one: twice the people, twice the walk.","more":"**O(n)** — linear complexity: work grows **proportionally** to input size. Each of the N elements is touched a bounded number of times: scanning an array, finding the max, summing values.\n\nIt's often the lower bound for problems where you must at least look at all the data. Pitfalls: two sequential passes are still O(n) (the constant 2 is dropped), but a **nested** loop is already O(n^2); watch for a hidden linear operation lurking inside the loop, like `list.contains`."},"Algorithms|O(log n)":{"term":"O(log n)","def":"Logarithmic: each step discards half the data, as in binary search.","hook":"Looking up a word in a dictionary: open the middle, toss out half, repeat until you find it.","more":"**O(log n)** — logarithmic growth: each step **shrinks the problem by a constant factor** (usually half). Classics: binary search, operations on a balanced tree (BST, B-tree), heaps.\n\nVery scalable: ~30 steps for a billion elements. Pitfall: it has **preconditions** — binary search only works on sorted data, and sorting is O(n log n), which can outweigh the gain for a one-off lookup. The logarithm's base is irrelevant in Big-O."},"Algorithms|O(n^2)":{"term":"O(n^2)","def":"Quadratic: a nested loop over all pairs; time climbs fast for large N.","hook":"Introducing every guest to every other: 10 people means 100 handshakes, a crowd is a disaster.","more":"**O(n^2)** — quadratic complexity: typically a **nested loop over all pairs** of elements. Examples: bubble/insertion sort, naive duplicate detection comparing each with each.\n\nFine for small N, but it drags at 10^4–10^5 and is practically unusable at 10^6. Pitfall: quadratic cost often **hides** — `contains` on a list inside a loop, string concatenation in a loop, N+1 database queries. Fix it with a hash set/index to bring it down to O(n)."},"Algorithms|Time complexity":{"term":"Time complexity","def":"An estimate of how an algorithm's operation count grows as input size grows.","hook":"How many cooking steps: not minutes on the clock, but how many actions as the number of dishes climbs.","more":"**Time complexity** — an estimate of how an algorithm's **operation count** grows with input size, expressed in Big-O/Θ/Ω. It's counted by the dominant operation (comparisons, memory accesses), not in seconds.\n\nIt predicts scalability before you run anything. Pitfall: distinguish **worst, average, and amortized** cases (quicksort is O(n log n) average, O(n^2) worst); real time also depends on cache, constants, and I/O, so complement the asymptotics with a benchmark on real data."},"Algorithms|Space complexity":{"term":"Space complexity","def":"An estimate of how an algorithm's memory usage grows as input size grows.","hook":"How many suitcases for your stuff: not time but space — more data, more shelving needed.","more":"**Space complexity** — an estimate of how **memory** grows with input size: extra structures, recursion stack, buffers. Usually you count **auxiliary space** (beyond the input itself).\n\nIt matters where memory costs more than time: large data, embedded, streaming. Pitfalls: **recursion eats stack** — depth O(n) means O(n) memory and StackOverflow risk; there's often a **time–space tradeoff** (caching/memoization trade memory for speed). In-place algorithms use O(1) extra memory."},"Algorithms|Array":{"term":"Array","def":"Elements laid out contiguously in memory, with O(1) access by index.","hook":"A row of mailboxes side by side: know the number, open the right one instantly, no searching.","more":"**Array** — a contiguous block of memory holding fixed-size elements of one type; the address of the i-th = base + i·size, so index access is **O(1)** with excellent **cache locality**.\n\nIdeal for frequent indexed reads and iteration. Pitfalls: inserting/removing in the middle is **O(n)** (shifting), and the size is fixed (dynamic arrays like ArrayList/slice resize by copying, with amortized O(1) append). If you need frequent middle inserts, reach for a linked list or deque."},"Algorithms|LinkedList":{"term":"LinkedList","def":"A chain of nodes where each holds a value and a reference to the next.","hook":"A train of cars: each car only couples to its neighbor, and you can't see the end.","more":"Under the hood each node is a separate heap object holding a value and a reference to the next (in a **doubly-linked** list, to the previous too). Nodes are scattered across memory, joined into a chain.\n\n**Why:** insertion/removal is **O(1)** if you already hold the node — no shifting elements. **Pitfalls:** index access is O(n), poor cache locality (cache misses), and memory overhead for the references. If you need random access, use an ArrayList."},"Algorithms|HashMap":{"term":"HashMap","def":"A key-value store with average O(1) lookup via the key's hash.","hook":"A coat check: hand over your tag (key) and they bring your jacket instantly, no scanning every hook.","more":"Under the hood is an **array of buckets**; the bucket index = `hash(key) % capacity`. Collisions chain into a list, and in Java 8+ a long chain turns into a **tree** (O(log n)). Once it fills past the **load factor** (0.75) it resizes with a rehash.\n\n**Why:** near-instant lookup/insert by key. **Pitfalls:** O(1) is average only — a poor hash degrades it; keys must implement **equals/hashCode** correctly and be immutable; ordering isn't guaranteed; it isn't thread-safe."},"Algorithms|Stack":{"term":"Stack","def":"A LIFO pile: last in is first out, like plates.","hook":"A stack of plates: you add and take only from the top — last placed, first gone.","more":"Under the hood it's an array or linked list operated on from one end only: **push** (place on top) and **pop** (remove the top). The principle is **LIFO** — last in, first out.\n\n**Why:** function calls (the **call stack**), undo, parsing, depth-first traversal (DFS), bracket matching. **Pitfalls:** you can only reach the top — the middle is out of reach; overflow from deep recursion throws **StackOverflowError**; don't conflate the Stack abstraction with a concrete class (in Java, prefer ArrayDeque over the legacy Stack class)."},"Algorithms|Queue":{"term":"Queue","def":"A FIFO line: first in is first out, like people at a checkout.","hook":"A checkout line: whoever lined up first gets served first.","more":"Under the hood it's usually a linked list or ring buffer with two ends: **enqueue** adds at the tail, **dequeue** removes from the head. The principle is **FIFO** — first in, first out.\n\n**Why:** task buffering, breadth-first traversal (BFS), message queues, pools. **Pitfalls:** a naive array implementation with shifting is O(n) — use a ring buffer or **ArrayDeque**; multithreading needs blocking/concurrent queues. Variants: deque (both ends), priority queue (by priority, not arrival order)."},"Algorithms|Tree":{"term":"Tree","def":"A hierarchy of nodes with a root and branches — connected and cycle-free.","hook":"A family tree: one ancestor at the top, branches fanning down, and no loops.","more":"Under the hood are nodes, each referencing its children; exactly one **root**, every node has one parent, and there are no cycles (a connected acyclic graph). A binary tree has at most 2 children.\n\n**Why:** hierarchies (file system, DOM, JSON), fast O(log n) lookup in a **BST**/balanced tree, database indexes (**B-tree**). **Pitfalls:** an unbalanced BST degenerates into a list and gives O(n) — use AVL/Red-Black; recursive traversal of a deep tree risks overflowing the stack."},"Algorithms|Recursion":{"term":"Recursion","def":"When a function calls itself, breaking a problem into a smaller version of the same problem.","hook":"A matryoshka: inside each doll is the same doll, smaller, until you reach the tiniest one.","more":"Under the hood a function calls itself on **smaller** input; each call places its frame (local variables, return point) on the **call stack**, and they unwind once the bottom is reached.\n\n**Why:** natural for self-similar problems — tree/graph traversal, divide-and-conquer (sorts), combinatorics. **Pitfalls:** deep recursion means **StackOverflowError** (Java has no guaranteed tail-call optimization); recomputation kills speed — **memoization** saves it; sometimes it's cleaner rewritten as a loop."},"Algorithms|Base case":{"term":"Base case","def":"The recursion's stopping condition; without it recursion loops forever and dies with StackOverflow.","hook":"The bottom matryoshka: the smallest doll doesn't open — that's where the recursion halts.","more":"Under the hood it's the **first check** in a recursive function: on the simplest input (empty list, n==0, tree leaf) it returns a result immediately **without** a recursive call, stopping the descent.\n\n**Why:** it provides an exit — without it the calls run forever, the stack overflows, and it throws **StackOverflowError**. **Pitfalls:** the base must be **reachable** — the argument has to move monotonically toward it (always shrinking, say); a missing or wrong base case and a non-narrowing step are the most common causes of runaway recursion."},"Git|repository (repo)":{"term":"repository (repo)","def":"A project folder under Git control, with its full change history stored inside the hidden .git folder.","hook":"A time machine for a folder: it keeps every step of the project in a hidden .git chest.","more":"Under the hood it's the working tree plus a hidden **`.git`** directory holding the entire history: objects (commits, trees, blobs), branches, tags, config. Git is a distributed system: every clone has the **full** history, no always-on server required.\n\n**Why:** versioning, rollbacks, collaboration, branching. **Gotchas:** never commit secrets or large binaries — they stay in history forever (use **.gitignore**, and Git LFS for large files); deleting `.git` wipes the whole history irreversibly."},"Git|commit":{"term":"commit","def":"A snapshot of the entire project state with a unique hash, author, and message — a single point in history.","hook":"A photo of the project at a moment in time: signed, attributed, and stamped with its own hash.","more":"Under the hood a commit is an immutable object: pointers to a file tree (a snapshot, not a diff), to its **parent** commit(s), plus author, date, and message. Its **SHA hash** is computed over the whole content, so it's unique and verifies integrity.\n\n**Why:** an atomic point in history you can return to or compare against. **Gotchas:** rewriting an old commit (rebase/amend) **rewrites** its hash and every descendant; never rewrite already-pushed shared history; make small, meaningful commits with clear messages."},"Git|branch":{"term":"branch","def":"A movable pointer to a commit; lets you pursue a separate line of work without touching the main one.","hook":"A fork in the road: your own path to walk while the main highway stays intact.","more":"Under the hood a branch is just a **file containing a 40-character commit hash** (in `.git/refs/heads/`). Creating a branch is nearly free. On a new commit, the branch pointer automatically moves forward.\n\n**Why:** pursue a parallel line of work (feature, fix, experiment) without touching `main`. **Gotchas:** long-lived branches drift from main and cause painful conflicts — merge/rebase often; deleting a branch removes only the pointer, and its commits can become orphaned and get garbage-collected."},"Git|HEAD":{"term":"HEAD","def":"A pointer to the current commit or branch — where you currently are in history.","hook":"The \"YOU ARE HERE\" marker on the history map: it shows where you stand right now.","more":"Under the hood **HEAD** is a ref (`.git/HEAD`) usually pointing at the current branch (`ref: refs/heads/main`), which in turn points at a commit. It determines what's in the working directory and where the next commit will land.\n\n**Why:** Git always knows \"where you are right now.\" **Gotchas:** a **detached HEAD** is when HEAD points directly at a commit rather than a branch; commits made there aren't attached to any branch and are easily lost — create a branch before working. `HEAD~1` is the previous commit, handy for navigation."},"Git|merge":{"term":"merge","def":"Combining two branches into one; usually creates a dedicated merge commit that joins the histories.","hook":"Two rivers flowing into one: the waters mix and a new shared junction point appears.","more":"Under the hood Git finds the common ancestor of the two branches and combines the changes. If there's no divergence — **fast-forward** (it just moves the pointer). Otherwise it creates a **merge commit** with **two parents**, preserving both lines of history.\n\n**Why:** fold a finished branch back into main while preserving context. **Gotchas:** edits to the same lines in both branches = a **conflict** you must resolve by hand; the alternative is **rebase** (linear history, but it rewrites commits). Don't merge unreviewed code into main."},"Git|rebase":{"term":"rebase","def":"Replaying your commits on top of another branch: they're rewritten anew and the history becomes linear.","hook":"Like moving your books to a new shelf — the whole stack relocates on top of someone else's.","more":"Under the hood **rebase** takes your commits, rewinds the branch to its common ancestor with the target, and **reapplies each commit on top of it** — so the SHAs change, these are new objects. Used to get a **linear history** without merge commits: easier to read `git log`, smoother reviews.\n\n- The **gotcha** is rebasing an **already-pushed** branch: history diverges and others need `--force` and a re-sync. Rule: rebase only local/personal branches, leave shared `main` alone."},"Git|cherry-pick":{"term":"cherry-pick","def":"Take one specific commit from another branch and apply it to the current one.","hook":"Pick a single cherry off someone else's tree and drop it in your basket — just one commit.","more":"**cherry-pick** computes the **diff of a single commit** and applies it as a new commit on the current branch (new SHA, same change in substance). Used for targeted transfers — for example, a **hotfix** from `main` into a release branch without merging everything else.\n\n- **Gotchas:** it's easy to breed **duplicate commits** that later conflict during merge/rebase. For a series of commits or regular transfers, prefer merge/rebase; reserve pick for one-off patches."},"Git|bisect":{"term":"bisect","def":"A binary search for the commit that introduced a bug, via good/bad markers.","hook":"A \"guess the number\" game: split the commit list in half until you catch the culprit.","more":"**bisect** performs a **binary search** over history: you mark one commit `bad` and a known-good one `good`, Git checks out the midpoint, you test and say good/bad — the range halves each time. In **log2(N)** steps it pinpoints the commit where the bug appeared.\n\n- You can automate it with `git bisect run <script>` (the exit code decides good/bad). **Gotchas:** you need a reliably reproducible test for the bug and for the old commits to even build."},"Git|stash":{"term":"stash","def":"Temporarily shelves uncommitted changes so you can switch to something else, then restores them later.","hook":"Sweep the papers off your desk into a drawer, handle the urgent task, then put the papers back.","more":"**stash** takes your **uncommitted** changes (tracked files by default) and tucks them into a **stack** as special objects, restoring the working directory to HEAD. Handy when you urgently need to switch branches but don't want to commit half-finished work.\n\n- `pop` restores and removes the entry, `apply` keeps it. **Gotchas:** untracked/ignored files aren't stashed without `-u`/`-a`; forgotten stashes pile up and go stale — it's a buffer for minutes, not a storage system."},"Git|conflict":{"term":"conflict","def":"When Git can't merge changes to the same lines on its own and asks you to resolve them by hand.","hook":"Two people wrote different things on the same form line — Git throws up its hands: you decide whose to keep.","more":"**conflict** arises during merge/rebase/cherry-pick/stash when **both sides changed the same lines** (or one edited a file the other deleted). Git won't guess intent and inserts markers `<<<<<<<`, `=======`, `>>>>>>>`, flagging the file as unmerged in the index.\n\n- You resolve it manually: keep the right code, remove the markers, `git add`, continue. **Gotchas:** a forgotten marker can sneak into a commit; frequent small commits and a shared formatting convention help."},"Git|remote":{"term":"remote","def":"A remote copy of the repository (usually origin) on a server like GitHub or GitLab.","hook":"The project's cloud safe on a server — your local repo syncs with this copy.","more":"**remote** is a **named reference** to another repository (an https/ssh URL), usually `origin`. Locally Git keeps **remote-tracking branches** like `origin/main` — a snapshot of the remote's state as of the last exchange, which only moves on fetch/push.\n\n- There can be several remotes (e.g., `upstream` for a fork). **Gotchas:** `origin/main` doesn't update by itself — until you `fetch`, you're looking at a **stale** picture of the server."},"Git|push / pull":{"term":"push / pull","def":"Push sends your commits to the remote; pull fetches others' commits from the remote to you.","hook":"Mail: push — send a letter to the server, pull — grab the incoming ones for yourself.","more":"**push** uploads your local commits and updates the branch on the remote; **pull** = `fetch` (download others' commits) + `merge` or `rebase` (fold them into your branch). So pull always **changes your working branch**, while fetch touches only tracking branches.\n\n- **Gotchas:** a push is rejected (`non-fast-forward`) if the server has commits you don't — pull first. `pull --rebase` gives a linear history instead of extra merge commits."},"Git|tag":{"term":"tag","def":"A fixed marker on a commit, usually for release versions like v1.2.0.","hook":"A sticky bookmark on a book page: the v1.2.0 release is right here, you'll always find the spot.","more":"**tag** is a named pointer to a specific commit that, unlike a branch, **doesn't move**. It can be **lightweight** (just a label) or **annotated** (a separate object with author, date, message, and an optional GPG signature) — releases need the annotated kind.\n\n- **Gotchas:** tags **aren't pushed** automatically — use `git push --tags` or `push origin v1.2.0`. You shouldn't re-point an already-published tag: anyone who pulled it keeps the old version."},"Git|reset":{"term":"reset","def":"Moves the current branch to another commit, optionally discarding changes in the index and files.","hook":"A time machine for a branch: you rewind the pointer back to the commit you want.","more":"**reset** moves the current branch pointer to a given commit. The mode decides the fate of changes: `--soft` keeps edits in the **index**, `--mixed` (the default) in the working directory but not the index, `--hard` **wipes** both the index and files to match the target commit.\n\n- Used to undo local commits or rework staged changes. **Gotchas:** `--hard` **irreversibly** loses uncommitted work; don't reset a **shared** branch — to undo something published, `git revert` is safer."},"DevOps|CI/CD":{"term":"CI/CD","def":"A pipeline that automatically builds, tests, and ships your code so you don't do it by hand.","hook":"A factory conveyor belt: drop in a part and it assembles, checks, and packages the finished product on its own.","more":"**CI/CD** is a practice: **CI** (Continuous Integration) automatically builds the project and runs tests/linters on every commit so breakages are caught early; **CD** (Delivery/Deployment) then rolls the artifact out to staging or production. It's implemented with runners (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins).\n\n- The point is to remove manual steps and **human error**. **Gotchas:** slow or **flaky** tests destroy trust; auto-deploy to prod needs rollbacks and feature flags."},"DevOps|Pipeline":{"term":"Pipeline","def":"A chain of steps (build-test-deploy) that runs automatically on a commit, PR, or at the push of a button.","hook":"A subway train running the branches: a commit boards at a station and rides through the steps to the final stop, 'deploy'.","more":"A **Pipeline** is a set of **stages and steps** defined in a config (YAML, etc.) that a runner executes automatically on a trigger: push, pull request, tag, schedule, or manual run. Steps run sequentially or in parallel, passing artifacts and statuses to each other.\n\n- Typically: build → test → package → deploy, with **gates** (if a test fails, we don't proceed). **Gotchas:** keep secrets in a secure store, not in code; watch timing and cache dependencies, otherwise the pipeline slows the whole team down."},"DevOps|Артефакт":{"term":"Artifact","def":"The finished build output (jar, war, image) that's stored in a repository and later deployed.","hook":"A finished pie out of the oven: the sources are the flour, the artifact is the packaged pie on the warehouse shelf.","more":"An **Artifact** is an immutable **build output**: a jar/war, Docker image, binary, or zip. CI publishes it to a **registry/repository** (Nexus, Artifactory, GHCR) with a version, and that same file is then deployed across environments — build once, run everywhere.\n\n- The key principle is **immutability**: you test exactly what will go to prod. **Gotchas:** the `latest` tag is ambiguous (what exactly is deployed?) — version explicitly; old artifacts pile up and eat space, so you need a cleanup policy."},"DevOps|Docker":{"term":"Docker","def":"Packaging an application with all its dependencies into an image that runs the same way everywhere.","hook":"A moving box: pack your things with their wrapping and everything works the same in the new apartment.","more":"Under the hood Docker uses Linux kernel mechanisms — **namespaces** (isolation of processes, network, filesystem) and **cgroups** (CPU/memory limits). An image is built from layers according to the instructions in a **Dockerfile**.\n\n- It's needed to eliminate 'works on my machine, but not on the server' — the same environment everywhere.\n- Gotchas: on macOS/Windows a Linux VM runs underneath (slower I/O), and images bloat without multi-stage builds and a .dockerignore."},"DevOps|Контейнер":{"term":"Container","def":"A running instance of a Docker image: lightweight, isolated, fast to start and to die.","hook":"Like a pastry from a baking mold: the image is the mold, the container is the hot pastry you ate.","more":"A container is a **host process** wrapped in isolation (namespaces/cgroups), not a separate VM: it has no kernel of its own, so it starts in milliseconds with minimal overhead.\n\n- Use it for stateless services: easy to spin up copies and kill them.\n- Gotchas: a container is **ephemeral** — data inside it vanishes when it's recreated, so move state into volumes or an external DB. One container = one main process."},"DevOps|Образ (Image)":{"term":"Image","def":"A template snapshot that containers are created from, like a class that objects are made from.","hook":"Like a class and its objects: one image-snapshot, and you stamp out as many containers from it as you want.","more":"An image is an **immutable** set of filesystem layers plus metadata (start command, env, ports). Layers are reused and cached across images, saving space and bandwidth.\n\n- The analogy to a class is precise: from one image you can bring up any number of containers.\n- Gotchas: the `latest` tag doesn't pin a version — for prod, use specific tags/digests. Change an early layer and all the following ones get rebuilt."},"DevOps|Registry":{"term":"Registry","def":"A warehouse of ready-made images (Docker Hub, Nexus) that servers pull them from.","hook":"A warehouse of ready boxes: walk in, download the image you need — like an app store for servers.","more":"A registry is an HTTP service that stores images at the address `registry/repo:tag`. It can be public (**Docker Hub**) or private (**Nexus**, Harbor, GitHub/GitLab Registry, ECR).\n\n- `docker push` uploads, `docker pull` downloads; only the missing layers are transferred.\n- Gotchas: Docker Hub has **rate limits** on anonymous pulls — you'll hit them in CI; keep a private proxy/cache. Scan images for vulnerabilities and clean up old tags."},"DevOps|Kubernetes (K8s)":{"term":"Kubernetes (K8s)","def":"The most widely used container orchestrator: keeps the desired number of replicas and self-heals what falls over.","hook":"A harbor captain: a boat drifts off and they launch a new one at once, always keeping the fleet in formation.","more":"K8s is a declarative orchestrator: you describe the **desired state** (YAML manifests), and controllers bring the cluster to it via a reconcile loop. The base unit is a **Pod** (1+ containers), replicas are kept by a **Deployment**, and access is provided by a **Service**.\n\n- It restarts what crashes, scales, and performs rolling updates on its own.\n- Gotchas: a steep learning and operations curve. For a couple of services it's **overkill** — docker-compose or a managed platform will do."},"DevOps|Оркестрация":{"term":"Orchestration","def":"Managing a pile of containers: where to run them, how many replicas, what to restart on failure.","hook":"An orchestra conductor: who plays, how many violins, who to swap in if someone hits a wrong note.","more":"Orchestration solves problems a single `docker run` can't: **scheduling** (which node to place a container on by resources), maintaining the replica count, restarting crashed ones, service discovery, load balancing, and rolling out updates.\n\n- You need it once there are many containers and hosts and you can't keep track by hand.\n- Gotchas: it adds a layer of complexity and a point of failure (the control plane). For one or two services it's too early — wait until you hit real pain first."},"DevOps|Blue-Green":{"term":"Blue-Green","def":"You keep two versions side by side and instantly switch traffic to the new one, leaving the old for rollback.","hook":"Two switches: blue is live, green is new — flip the toggle and traffic is already on the new one.","more":"You keep two identical environments: **blue** (currently in prod) and **green** (the new version). Once green is warmed up, you flip the router/load balancer — traffic goes to the new one instantly, while blue stands ready for rollback.\n\n- Upside: zero downtime and an **instant rollback** with a simple switch.\n- Gotchas: you need double the infrastructure (expensive). **DB migrations** and shared state are painful — the schema must be compatible with both versions."},"DevOps|Canary":{"term":"Canary","def":"You pour the new version onto a small slice of users first, and if all is well, roll it out to everyone.","hook":"A canary in the mine: release the new build to 5% of people — they're alive, so you bring everyone in.","more":"Canary ('the canary in the mine') means the new version first gets a **small share of traffic** (1–5%); you watch the metrics/errors and gradually raise the share to 100%.\n\n- It shrinks the blast radius: a bug affects only a few, and you roll back early.\n- Gotchas: you need good **metrics and alerts**, otherwise there's no way to catch a regression. It's harder to implement (traffic weighting, sticky sessions), and both versions must coexist with one DB."},"DevOps|Rollback":{"term":"Rollback","def":"A quick revert to the last working version when the new one breaks in prod.","hook":"Ctrl+Z for prod: the new build broke — instantly bring back the previous working version.","more":"Rollback is a return to a known-good previous version (another image/tag or the previous Deployment). In K8s it's `rollout undo`; in blue-green it's switching traffic back.\n\n- It's insurance: fixing prod by rollback is faster than debugging under load.\n- Gotchas: code rolls back easily, but **DB migrations don't**. Make them backward-compatible (expand/contract), otherwise the code rollback breaks against the new schema."},"DevOps|Serverless":{"term":"Serverless","def":"You write only the function, and the cloud handles the server, scaling, and per-request billing.","hook":"A taxi instead of your own car: you ride only when you need to, pay per trip, and have no parking to deal with.","more":"Serverless (FaaS, e.g. AWS Lambda) — the cloud spins instances up/down with load down to zero and bills by the number/duration of invocations. The server exists, you just don't administer it.\n\n- Good for rare, spiky, or event-driven workloads — you don't pay for idle time.\n- Gotchas: **cold start** (latency on a cold start), time/memory limits, vendor lock-in. For steady high load it's often pricier than a regular service."},"DevOps|IaC":{"term":"IaC","def":"Infrastructure as Code: servers and networks are described in files (Terraform) instead of clicked by hand.","hook":"A house blueprint instead of building by eye: servers are described in a file — rebuild it all with one command.","more":"IaC describes infrastructure **declaratively** in code (**Terraform**, Pulumi, CloudFormation): the tool compares the desired state with the actual one (**state**) and applies the difference.\n\n- It gives you versioning in git, code review, reproducible environments, and an end to manual 'clicking'.\n- Gotchas: the **state** file is critical (store it in a remote backend with locking), and editing resources by hand causes **drift**. Change infra only through code."},"Архитектура|Ambassador (Посол)":{"term":"Ambassador","def":"An out-of-process proxy helper deployed alongside a service that takes over the network resilience of outbound calls (retries, TLS, metrics). A special case of the Sidecar pattern.","hook":"A personal ambassador: you speak to your own ambassador in your native tongue (localhost), and he handles the negotiations with the foreign country — he knows their protocol, tolerates the rudeness, and tries again."},"Архитектура|Claim Check":{"term":"Claim Check","def":"A large payload is stored in a data store, and only a reference (the claim ticket) is passed in the message; the receiver retrieves the body using it.","hook":"A luggage locker at the station: you check in the suitcase and carry only the tag with its number — that tag is what you redeem to get the suitcase back."},"Архитектура|Scatter-Gather":{"term":"Scatter-Gather","def":"Fan out a request to N workers in parallel and aggregate their responses into a single result, dropping the stragglers by timeout.","hook":"A tender with a deadline: you sent the spec to five contractors, collected the bids on Friday, and picked the best one — whoever didn't make it, we don't wait for."},"Архитектура|Competing Consumers":{"term":"Competing Consumers","def":"Multiple consumers on a single queue compete for messages; each message goes to one of them — scaling at the cost of global ordering.","hook":"A supermarket checkout: one line of shoppers, several registers — whoever frees up takes the next person; the overall order of \"who walked into the store first\" is no longer preserved."},"Архитектура|Event-Carried State Transfer":{"term":"Event-Carried State Transfer","def":"Events carry the needed data with them so the consumer can keep a local copy and not call back to the source synchronously.","hook":"An email with an attachment vs. a notification email: instead of \"our document was updated, come download it,\" they send you the document itself right away — the receiver doesn't have to go anywhere."},"Архитектура|Pub/Sub vs P2P":{"term":"Pub/Sub vs P2P","def":"Point-to-point: one message goes to one consumer (work distribution). Publish/Subscribe: one message goes to all subscribers (event broadcast).","hook":"A queue is a support ticket: one operator picks it up. A topic is a company-wide mailing: the email lands in every employee's inbox at once."},"Архитектура|Bulkhead (переборка)":{"term":"Bulkhead","def":"Resource isolation: a separate, bounded pool of threads/connections per dependency, so the failure of one doesn't exhaust the resources of the whole service.","hook":"Watertight compartments on a ship: one gets breached and floods, but the vessel stays afloat."},"Архитектура|Стена защиты (resilience stack)":{"term":"Resilience stack","def":"A composition of Timeout + Bulkhead + CircuitBreaker + Retry + Fallback as nested layers around a call; the nesting order determines the correctness of the behavior.","hook":"An onion of protection: the request punches through the layers down to the core call, and the error bubbles back out to the surface."},"Архитектура|Graceful degradation":{"term":"Graceful degradation","def":"Returning a partial, cached, or trimmed-down result when a non-essential dependency fails, instead of failing the request entirely.","hook":"Engine dies — land immediately (fail-fast). Air conditioning dies — fly without it (degrade)."},"Архитектура|Load shedding (сброс нагрузки)":{"term":"Load shedding","def":"Deliberately rejecting excess requests at the entry point under overload, to preserve capacity and prevent a full service collapse.","hook":"A bouncer at a club: lets in as many as fit, the rest get stopped at the door — rather than a crush inside."},"Архитектура|Retry budget":{"term":"Retry budget","def":"A global cap on the fraction of retries relative to the primary traffic (usually via a token bucket), preventing retries from causing a retry storm and finishing off the downstream.","hook":"A shared retry limit like a cashier's box of chips: chips run out — no more retries, the downstream won't get finished off."},"Архитектура|Sidecar":{"term":"Sidecar","def":"A helper container running next to the main application in the same pod, taking over infrastructure concerns (logs, proxy, metrics) without intruding on the business code.","hook":"Sidecar — a motorcycle sidecar (just alongside). Ambassador — an envoy who travels and negotiates ON THE OUTSIDE for you. Adapter — a plug adapter in the socket: it makes a foreign plug usable."},"Архитектура|Service registry":{"term":"Service registry","def":"A database of the current network addresses of live service instances; updated via instance registration and health checks, serving as the source of truth for discovery.","hook":"Client-side — you call directory assistance yourself, get all the numbers, and decide who to dial. Server-side — you call a single call center, and the operator routes you to a free specialist."},"Архитектура|Компенсирующая транзакция":{"term":"Compensating transaction","def":"An action that semantically undoes the effect of an already-committed saga step (e.g., a refund instead of cancelling a charge); not a real rollback, and it may have its own side effects.","hook":"An orchestra with a conductor — one waves the baton and everyone plays on his cue. A round dance — no conductor; each person watches their neighbor and joins on the signal. Both produce music, but they fail differently."},"Архитектура|CQRS":{"term":"CQRS","def":"Command Query Responsibility Segregation — separating the write model (commands, which enforce invariants) from the read model (optimized for queries).","hook":"The register takes the money, the display shows the balance — different windows for different jobs.","more":"**Not necessarily two databases.** Minimal CQRS is just separate command and query classes. **Common confusion:** CQRS ≠ event sourcing; they are often used together, but they are independent things.\n- Write side: normalized, invariants.\n- Read side: denormalized, shaped for the screen.\n- Syncing the read model is usually asynchronous → eventual consistency."},"Архитектура|Event Sourcing":{"term":"Event Sourcing","def":"State is stored not as the current snapshot but as an ordered log of facts (events); the current state is derived by replaying the events.","hook":"Not \"how much is in the account,\" but the full account statement — you recompute the balance by adding up the transactions.","more":"**Pro:** full history, audit, time-travel, you can rebuild any projection. **Con:** complexity, event versioning, snapshots for speed.\n- Events are **immutable** and append-only.\n- Current state = a fold of the log.\n- Often paired with CQRS: projections build the read model."},"Архитектура|Saga":{"term":"Saga","def":"A way to maintain consistency in a distributed transaction without 2PC: a business operation is split into local steps, each with a compensating action in case of rollback.","hook":"A chain of steps, each with an \"undo\" button — if it fails midway, you roll back what was done in reverse order.","more":"**Two styles:** orchestration (a central conductor drives the steps) and choreography (services react to each other's events). **Caveat:** a saga gives not atomicity but eventual consistency — there are intermediate inconsistent states.\n- Compensation ≠ a DB rollback; it's a business-level reverse action.\n- Each step must be idempotent."},"Архитектура|Bounded Context":{"term":"Bounded Context","def":"A boundary in DDD within which every term has exactly one meaning and its own model; outside the boundary the same term may mean something else.","hook":"\"Customer\" in billing and \"customer\" in support are different entities on the diagram, even though it's the same word.","more":"**Core of both tactical and strategic DDD.** Contexts are connected via a context map (ACL, shared kernel, conformist, etc.). **Common mistake:** one shared model for the whole system (\"god model\") — terms get blurred.\n- Within a context — a single Ubiquitous Language.\n- The boundary usually coincides with a service/team boundary."},"Архитектура|Aggregate Root":{"term":"Aggregate Root","def":"The main object of a cluster (the Aggregate), through which alone the cluster may be modified; it enforces invariants and is the single entry point from the outside.","hook":"An Order is the root — you touch its line items only through the order, so the total always adds up.","more":"**Rules:** external references point only to the root (by id); strong consistency inside the boundary, eventual consistency between aggregates. **Pitfalls:** too large an aggregate → locking and contention, too small → invariants don't hold.\n- One aggregate = one transaction.\n- Modify one aggregate at a time."},"Архитектура|Value Object":{"term":"Value Object","def":"An object with no identity, defined solely by its values; two VOs with the same fields are equal and interchangeable.","hook":"100 tenge equals any other 100 tenge — what matters is the amount, not \"which exact banknote.\"","more":"**Difference from an Entity:** an Entity has an id and exists over time (two with equal fields are still different); a VO is compared by value. **Properties:** immutability, no side effects, self-validation in the constructor.\n- Examples: Money, DateRange, Address, Email.\n- You don't change a field — you replace the whole VO with a new one."},"Архитектура|Domain Event":{"term":"Domain Event","def":"A fact that \"something significant happened in the domain,\" named in the past tense (OrderPlaced, PaymentReceived); part of the model inside the context boundary.","hook":"An entry in the domain's diary: \"this happened\" — a statement of fact, not a command.","more":"**Domain vs integration event:** a domain event lives inside the context (for model consistency); an integration event is published outward for other services (part of the contract, versioned). **Caveat:** they are often confused, and domain events get pushed straight to Kafka — that leaks the internal model.\n- Past tense in the name is mandatory.\n- It is immutable."},"Архитектура|Transactional Outbox":{"term":"Transactional Outbox","def":"A pattern for reliable event publishing: the event is written to an outbox table in the same DB transaction as the business data, and a separate process reads it and sends it to the broker.","hook":"You drop the letter in the outbox tray with the same pen you used to sign the document — the postman picks it up later.","more":"**Solves dual-write:** you can't atomically write to the DB AND to Kafka — the outbox makes it a single-transaction write. **Alternative:** CDC (Debezium) reads the WAL/binlog without an outbox table.\n- Gives at-least-once → the consumer must be idempotent.\n- The relay marks/deletes the sent rows."},"Архитектура|Idempotent Consumer":{"term":"Idempotent Consumer","def":"A consumer for which reprocessing the same message doesn't change the result — protection against duplicates under at-least-once delivery.","hook":"An elevator: press the floor button ten times — it still arrives once, at the same floor.","more":"**Why:** brokers deliver at-least-once — retries and rebalances produce duplicates. **How:** a table of processed messageIds (dedup), an upsert by key, or operations that are idempotent by nature.\n- The idempotency key should come from the producer.\n- The \"have I seen this already\" check plus the processing must be atomic."},"Архитектура|Anti-Corruption Layer":{"term":"Anti-Corruption Layer","def":"A translator layer (ACL) between your context and a foreign/legacy model that prevents their concepts from \"leaking\" into your domain.","hook":"An interpreter at a negotiation — the other party speaks their own language, but it reaches you already in yours.","more":"**Goal:** isolate a clean model from a messy external one. Inside the ACL — adapters, mapping, facades. **Where it's used:** integration with legacy, an external vendor (legacy CBS, a third-party API), during a Strangler Fig.\n- It costs money to maintain the mapping — but it protects the domain.\n- Part of the context map in DDD."},"Архитектура|Strangler Fig":{"term":"Strangler Fig","def":"A strategy for gradually replacing legacy: a router/facade is placed in front of the old system and functionality is moved piece by piece to the new one until the old is \"strangled\" down to nothing.","hook":"A strangler fig wraps around a tree and gradually takes its place — the old one dies off piece by piece.","more":"**Why:** a big-bang rewrite is risky. The router directs traffic: migrated → to the new, the rest → to the old. **Caveat:** you need a bridge to the old data (often via an ACL) during the coexistence period.\n- Rollback at every step.\n- Applicable in practice: print-service → document-service."},"Архитектура|Шардинг":{"term":"Sharding","def":"Horizontal partitioning of data by key across independent nodes (shards), for when the volume no longer fits on a single server.","hook":"A card catalog by the first letter of the surname: A–K in one cabinet, L–Z in another — you go straight to the right one.","more":"**The shard key is critical:** a bad key → skew (a hot shard). **Methods:** by hash (uniform, but range queries are poor), by range, by lookup table. **Pitfalls:** cross-shard JOINs and transactions are expensive; resharding is painful.\n- consistent hashing reduces data movement when adding a node."},"Архитектура|Raft":{"term":"Raft","def":"A consensus algorithm: nodes elect a single leader that accepts writes and replicates them to the followers' log; a write is committed once acknowledged by a majority (quorum).","hook":"A group elects a head, who keeps the shared record, and a write is considered accepted once more than half are for it.","more":"**Why:** consistent cluster state in the face of node failures. **Guarantee:** the system survives a minority failure; on quorum loss it halts writes (CP in CAP). **Where:** etcd, Consul, Kafka KRaft.\n- Term (epoch) + elections on leader timeout.\n- Easier to grasp than Paxos — that's its whole point."},"Quarkus|ARC":{"term":"ARC","def":"Quarkus CDI container that generates bean wiring as bytecode at build time","hook":"Spring assembles the furniture every morning when you walk in; Quarkus had it delivered pre-assembled from the factory."},"Quarkus|Псевдоскоуп":{"term":"Pseudo-scope","def":"A scope without a client proxy (@Singleton, @Dependent): a direct reference is injected instead of a proxy","hook":"ApplicationScoped is the shared service elevator for the whole building; RequestScoped is a disposable cup for each guest."},"Quarkus|Client Proxy":{"term":"Client Proxy","def":"A generated intermediary subclass that delegates to the real contextual instance of a bean","hook":"A proxy is a secretary: you always call the same number, and they figure out where the boss is sitting right now."},"Quarkus|Резолв (resolution)":{"term":"Resolution","def":"The container picking the single candidate bean for an injection point by type and qualifiers","hook":"Spring feels around for the right part at runtime; ARC already stamped at the factory which part screws in where."},"Quarkus|@BuildStep":{"term":"@BuildStep","def":"A build-processor method that runs at build time and produces BuildItems used to assemble the application","hook":"An extension is the factory (deployment) and its finished product (runtime): the heavy lifting happens at the factory, and only a lightweight part ships to the customer."},"Quarkus|Closed-world":{"term":"Closed-world","def":"GraalVM native principle: everything reachable must be known at build time, otherwise it gets stripped out","hook":"Reflection means rummaging blindly through a drawer on every call; ARC labeled everything in advance and grabs what's needed instantly."},"Quarkus|Native image":{"term":"Native image","def":"A self-contained executable compiled by GraalVM from the application ahead of time, without a regular JVM","hook":"Native is a sprinter off the blocks; a warmed-up JVM is a marathoner over the distance."},"Quarkus|Closed-world assumption":{"term":"Closed-world assumption","def":"The native-image assumption that all executable code is known at compile time; anything unknown is stripped out","hook":"Native is a suitcase packer: it only packs what you name out loud; whisper a name at runtime and the item never makes the trip."},"Quarkus|@RegisterForReflection":{"term":"@RegisterForReflection","def":"A Quarkus annotation that registers a class for reflective access in the native image","hook":"@RegisterForReflection is reserving a spot in the suitcase: say explicitly \"I need this item\" and the packer won't toss it out."},"Quarkus|JIT warmup":{"term":"JIT warmup","def":"The phase where the JIT profiles hot code and compiles it into optimal machine code","hook":"A long-lived service under load is a marathon: let the JVM warm up and it'll overtake native at the finish line."},"Quarkus|AOT-компиляция":{"term":"AOT compilation","def":"Ahead-of-time: translation into machine code before startup, at build time, with no runtime recompilation","hook":"AOT is a pre-printed book: open it and read; JIT is a translator that stumbles at first, then races ahead of the printed page."},"Quarkus|Mutiny":{"term":"Mutiny","def":"Quarkus reactive library with two types — Uni (single result) and Multi (stream), with an onItem/onFailure-based API","hook":"Uni is a parcel with a single order; Multi is a conveyor belt with a stream of parcels."},"Quarkus|event-loop":{"term":"event-loop","def":"The Vert.x/Netty I/O thread that handles many requests in turn; it must never be blocked","hook":"The event-loop is the single waiter for the whole dining room: never sitting down to wait on the kitchen, or the whole room stalls."},"Quarkus|@Blocking":{"term":"@Blocking","def":"A RESTEasy Reactive / SmallRye annotation: run the method on the worker pool rather than the event-loop","hook":"@Blocking is a sign saying \"I'll be in the kitchen a while,\" so the event-loop waiter moves on and you get seated at a separate worker table."},"Quarkus|канал (channel)":{"term":"channel","def":"A named message pipe in Reactive Messaging; mapped to a Kafka topic via a connector","hook":"@Incoming/@Outgoing are the \"in\" and \"out\" pipes, and your method is the filter in the middle of the conveyor."},"Quarkus|virtual thread":{"term":"virtual thread","def":"A lightweight JVM-managed thread (Loom, Java 21): on blocking it parks, freeing the OS thread","hook":"A virtual thread is like hiring a thousand interns for the price of one: the intern blocks while the expensive OS thread stays free."},"Quarkus|backpressure":{"term":"backpressure","def":"A Reactive Streams mechanism: the consumer caps the producer's pace via request(n), protecting itself from overload","hook":"Backpressure is the cashier telling the line \"one at a time\" so the counter isn't buried in goods with nowhere to put them."},"Quarkus|Active Record":{"term":"Active Record","def":"A pattern where the entity object itself holds the logic to save and load itself from the database","hook":"Active Record is an employee who runs to the archive themselves; a repository is a separate archivist, leaving the employee to just do their job."},"Quarkus|Uni":{"term":"Uni","def":"A Mutiny type representing an async result with 0 or 1 item (like CompletableFuture)","hook":"Blocking — the waiter stands at the stove waiting for your dish; reactive — the waiter takes the order and goes to serve ten more tables."},"Quarkus|Narayana":{"term":"Narayana","def":"The JTA transaction manager in Quarkus, implementing @Transactional and distributed transactions","hook":"@Transactional keeps the transaction in the current thread's pocket; Uni jumps to another thread and the pocket comes up empty."},"Quarkus|Testcontainers":{"term":"Testcontainers","def":"A library that spins up real services in Docker containers for the duration of the tests","hook":"Dev Services is an auto-chef: if you didn't specify a restaurant, it warms up a database in a container for you while you write code."},"Quarkus|Schema migration":{"term":"Schema migration","def":"Versioned, managed changes to the database structure via numbered scripts","hook":"Migrations are git for the DB schema: every step is numbered, and the server knows which commit it has already applied."},"Quarkus|ConfigSource ordinal":{"term":"ConfigSource ordinal","def":"The numeric priority of a config source; on conflict the higher one wins","hook":"Config is like a stack of transparent sheets: you look from the top, and the upper sheet covers the ones below."},"Quarkus|Config profile":{"term":"Config profile","def":"A named set of overrides (%dev/%test/%prod), activated by the run context","hook":"A profile is like a washing-machine mode: one drum, but the wash program changes the temperature to fit the situation."},"Quarkus|Augmentation":{"term":"Augmentation","def":"The Quarkus build phase where the app's config and metamodel are baked into the artifact","hook":"Build-time is like a concrete mold: while the concrete is wet you can shape anything; once it sets (the build is done) you can't reshape the mold without a new pour."},"Quarkus|Live reload":{"term":"Live reload","def":"Hot recompilation of changed code on request, without restarting the JVM","hook":"Live reload is like a waiter who runs to the kitchen only when you place an order, not every time the cook chops something."},"Quarkus|Continuous testing":{"term":"Continuous testing","def":"Background auto-run of affected tests when code changes in dev mode","hook":"Continuous testing is like a kitchen smoke detector: you don't wait until you decide to check — it beeps the moment something starts to smoke."},"Quarkus|fast-jar":{"term":"fast-jar","def":"The default Quarkus packaging format with lib/app/quarkus layers for fast startup","hook":"fast-jar is IKEA furniture in boxes (easy to ship), uber-jar is a fully assembled wardrobe (single, but heavy), native is built-in cabinetry (instant, but can't be reworked)."},"Quarkus|Circuit Breaker":{"term":"Circuit Breaker","def":"A pattern: after a run of errors it \"opens the circuit\" and fails requests fast, sparing the failing service until it recovers","hook":"It's a first-aid kit for a call: a bandage retries, a tourniquet cuts it off, and a backup parachute is the fallback."},"Quarkus|Readiness probe":{"term":"Readiness probe","def":"A Kubernetes probe for \"is the pod ready to take traffic\"; on failure the pod is removed from service endpoints but not restarted","hook":"Liveness is the patient's pulse (none — resuscitate); readiness is the \"open\" sign on the door (closed — no customers, but the shop stays put)."},"Quarkus|Span":{"term":"Span","def":"A single unit of work within a trace (a service call, SQL query, or handler); spans are assembled into a tree for one request via a shared traceId","hook":"Metrics are the car's speedometer and tachometer; a trace is the dashcam recording the whole trip from door to door."},"Quarkus|OpenAPI":{"term":"OpenAPI","def":"A machine-readable contract for a REST API (endpoints, schemas, response codes) in YAML/JSON; the basis for documentation, generated clients, and contract tests","hook":"OpenAPI is a restaurant menu generated straight from the kitchen, and Swagger UI is the waiter who lets you poke at that menu."},"Quarkus|Build-time DI":{"term":"Build-time DI","def":"Quarkus resolves dependency injection and metadata at build time rather than at runtime — hence the fast startup and suitability for native compilation","hook":"Quarkus is a sports car: light and quick off the line, but fewer spare parts. Spring Boot is a well-worn estate car: heavier, but there's a service shop on every corner."},"Quarkus|Bearer token":{"term":"Bearer token","def":"An access token (usually a JWT) that the client puts in the Authorization header; whoever presents it is authorized, so it must only be sent over TLS","hook":"Keycloak is the passport office that issued the JWT passport; the service doesn't call the office on every request — it checks the watermark (signature) against a public reference."},"JVM|Heap":{"term":"Heap","def":"The shared memory area where all objects live (everything from `new`); the GC cleans it, and it's usually the first thing to fill up.","hook":"A shared warehouse for everyone: all `new` objects land here, and it's the first to get packed to the brim.","more":"Under the hood it's a region **shared by all threads** where `new` places objects and arrays; it's split into young/old generations. The stack holds only a **reference** — the object itself lives here.\n\nWhy: dynamic memory with automatic reclamation via the **GC** — no manual `free()`. Pitfalls: size is capped by `-Xmx`; leaks (live references to garbage, caches without eviction, unclosed resources) lead to **OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space**. Diagnosis — heap dump + analysis in MAT/VisualVM."},"JVM|Stack":{"term":"Stack","def":"Per-thread memory for method calls and local variables; it grows and shrinks on its own, with no GC involved.","hook":"A stack of plates per thread: call a method — push one on; return — pop it off; it tidies itself.","more":"Under the hood there's a **separate stack per thread**: each method call pushes a **stack frame** (local variables, primitives, object references, return address), and returning pops it off. It works LIFO, with no GC involvement.\n\nWhy: ultra-fast allocation/deallocation and automatic lifetime management of local data. Pitfalls: deep or infinite recursion overflows the stack — **StackOverflowError**; frame size is set by `-Xss`. Objects themselves don't live here — only references to them (the objects live in the heap)."},"JVM|Metaspace":{"term":"Metaspace","def":"An off-heap area where the JVM stores class metadata; it grows with the number of loaded classes and can run out of space too.","hook":"A side closet for class blueprints, separate from the warehouse; cram in too many classes and it bursts.","more":"Under the hood it's native (off-heap) memory where, since Java 8, **class metadata** is stored: class structure, methods, the constant pool (it replaced PermGen). It grows **with the number of loaded classes**, not objects.\n\nWhy: moving metadata out of the heap removed PermGen's hard limit and simplified the GC. Pitfalls: with dynamic class generation (proxies, frequent redeploys, classloader leaks) you hit **OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace**. By default its growth is unbounded — set `-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize`, or it will eat all the OS memory."},"JVM|GC":{"term":"GC","def":"Garbage Collector — automatically finds and removes objects unreachable from GC roots, freeing the heap.","hook":"A cleaning robot: anything unreachable is garbage — it hauls it out and frees up the warehouse.","more":"Under the hood the **Garbage Collector** finds objects that **can't be reached from GC roots** (thread stacks, statics, JNI references) and frees their memory — typically mark-sweep with compaction. Modern collectors: G1 (default), ZGC, Shenandoah.\n\nWhy: it removes manual memory management and a whole class of bugs (use-after-free, double-free). Pitfalls: the GC **won't save you from leaks** — if a reference is live, the object won't be collected; don't call `System.gc()` by hand; watch the pauses (STW) and pick a collector for latency vs throughput."},"JVM|Young/Old":{"term":"Young/Old","def":"The heap is split into the young generation (new objects, collected often) and the old generation (survivors, collected rarely).","hook":"A nursery and a retirement home: newborns die fast and often, while old-timers live on and are rarely disturbed.","more":"Under the hood the heap is divided by the **generational hypothesis** (\"most objects die young\"):\n- **Young** (Eden + 2 Survivors) — new objects land here, collected often and quickly;\n- **Old (Tenured)** — objects that survived several Minor GCs, collected rarely and expensively.\n\nWhy: the split makes collection cheaper — most garbage is cleared in the young gen without scanning the whole heap. Pitfalls: too small a young gen pushes objects into old too early (premature promotion) and triggers frequent, heavy old-gen collections."},"JVM|Minor GC":{"term":"Minor GC","def":"A fast cleanup of the young generation only; it happens often and is almost unnoticeable.","hook":"A quick sweep of the nursery: fresh garbage gone in a flash, nobody even noticed.","more":"Under the hood it's a collection of the **young generation only**: live objects in Eden and one Survivor are copied to the other Survivor (or promoted to Old), while the dead are discarded in bulk. It's a short **stop-the-world** pause of a few milliseconds.\n\nWhy: it cheaply clears the bulk of short-lived garbage without touching the whole heap. Pitfalls: frequent Minor GCs are normal in themselves, but if survivors quickly spill into Old, that's the road to expensive Full GCs. A high frequency hints at excessive allocation (boxing, temporary objects in loops)."},"JVM|Full GC":{"term":"Full GC","def":"A heavy cleanup of the entire heap that pauses the application; frequent Full GCs are pain and a warning sign.","hook":"A full house deep-clean with a pause: long, heavy, and frequent ones are an alarm bell.","more":"Under the hood it's a collection of the **entire heap** (young + old), often with compaction and a metaspace scan; usually a full **stop-the-world** pause measured in hundreds of ms or seconds.\n\nWhy: a last resort when old is full or there's no room for promotion/large objects. Pitfalls: **frequent Full GCs** are the prime symptom of trouble: a leak (heap isn't freed), too small an `-Xmx`, or wrong generation tuning. If after a Full GC almost no memory is freed and they run back-to-back — an **OutOfMemoryError** is coming. Cure it by analyzing GC logs and a heap dump."},"JVM|Stop-the-world":{"term":"Stop-the-world","def":"The moment the GC halts all application threads so it can clean up memory undisturbed.","hook":"Freeze-frame: everyone is frozen while the cleaner calmly sweeps the memory.","more":"Under the hood it's a phase where the GC **freezes all application threads** (at a safepoint) to work with memory against a stable object graph, free of races. Both Minor and Full GC do this — just for different durations.\n\nWhy: it guarantees correctness — while the GC moves/marks objects, application code can't interfere. Pitfalls: STW = latency, visible as a freeze to the user or as API timeouts. Low-pause collectors **ZGC/Shenandoah** cut STW to sub-millisecond by doing most work concurrently. The tuning goal is short, infrequent pauses."},"JVM|Strong reference":{"term":"Strong reference","def":"An ordinary reference to an object; as long as it exists, the GC won't touch the object.","hook":"Holding the leash: while your hand grips the reference, the cleaner won't touch the object.","more":"Under the hood it's an **ordinary reference** (`Object o = new Object()`): as long as an object has a strong reference reachable from GC roots, the collector will **never remove it**, even under memory pressure (up to OOM).\n\nWhy: the default behavior for everything live and needed. Pitfalls: strong references are exactly what holds **memory leaks** — a forgotten object in a static collection/cache/listener won't be collected. When you need memory-friendliness, use the other strengths: **SoftReference** (cache, cleared under pressure), **WeakReference** (`WeakHashMap`), **PhantomReference** (post-finalization)."},"JVM|WeakReference":{"term":"WeakReference","def":"A weak reference: it doesn't keep the object alive, so with no strong references the GC removes it at the very next collection.","hook":"A sticky note held on by a thread: one gust of wind (the GC) and it's gone — nobody's holding it.","more":"Under the hood a **WeakReference** stores the pointer in a special field that the GC treats specially: during collection it checks whether the object is reachable via **strong** references. If not, the object is collected and the reference is cleared (`get()` returns `null`), even with plenty of memory free.\n\n**Why:** the canonical case is `WeakHashMap` and caches tied to the key's lifetime. **Pitfalls:** the object can vanish at any moment, so always check `get()` for `null`; if a strong reference lingers somewhere, the weak one won't help — the leak stays."},"JVM|SoftReference":{"term":"SoftReference","def":"A soft reference: the object lives as long as there's enough memory and is removed only when the heap gets tight (handy for caches).","hook":"An umbrella on the balcony: it stays while there's room; get cramped and it's the first thing tossed.","more":"A **SoftReference** works like a weak one, but the GC is required to collect it **only just before** an `OutOfMemoryError`. While the heap isn't tight, the object stays alive. The JVM accounts for recency of access (`-XX:SoftRefLRUPolicyMSPerMB`), clearing the least recently used first.\n\n**Why:** caches you don't mind losing under memory pressure. **Pitfalls:** the behavior is poorly predictable and depends on free heap; with a large `-Xmx`, soft objects pile up and provoke long full GCs. Often an explicit LRU cache (Caffeine) with a limit is more honest than relying on soft references."},"JVM|Memory leak":{"term":"Memory leak","def":"A leak: objects are no longer needed, but a reference still holds them, so the GC won't reclaim them and the heap grows.","hook":"A dripping tap: you don't need the thing, but you're still holding the string — and the heap bucket overflows.","more":"A **memory leak** in Java is a **logical** leak: an object is unreachable in meaning but reachable in the reference graph from a GC root, so it isn't collected. Classics: a collection that grows without removal, unclosed resources, `ThreadLocal` in a thread pool, listeners/callbacks without unsubscription, an unbounded cache.\n\n**Why know it:** the heap slowly grows → full GCs become frequent → eventually **OOM**. **Pitfalls:** you hunt it via a **heap dump** and dominator-tree analysis (what retains the object); static collections and ClassLoaders are the most common culprits."},"JVM|OOM":{"term":"OOM","def":"OutOfMemoryError — the JVM gives up, out of memory (usually heap or metaspace), and the application crashes.","hook":"The suitcase bursts: more stuff than fits — the zipper won't close, everything's on the floor.","more":"**OOM** (`OutOfMemoryError`) — the JVM couldn't allocate memory and throws an `Error`, not an `Exception`, so catching it is usually pointless. The subtypes matter: `Java heap space` (too small `-Xmx` or a leak), `Metaspace` (too many classes/proxies), `unable to create native thread`, `GC overhead limit`.\n\n**Why distinguish them:** the cause and the fix differ — heap is fixed with a dump and `-Xmx`, metaspace is examined by class count. **Pitfalls:** bumping `-Xmx` for a real leak is useless — it just delays the crash; you want `-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError`."},"JVM|GC root":{"term":"GC root","def":"Root points (thread stacks, static fields) from which the GC traces live objects; anything unreachable from them is removed.","hook":"An anchor at the tree: whatever hangs from the branches lives; whatever falls to the ground goes to the dump.","more":"A **GC root** is a starting point for tracing live objects: references in **thread stacks** (local variables), class **static fields**, JNI references, active threads, monitors. The collector walks the graph from them; everything **unreachable** is treated as garbage.\n\n**Why:** it's the basis of mark-and-sweep and the answer to \"why is this object alive.\" **Pitfalls:** when analyzing a leak in a heap dump you look precisely at the **path to GC root** — it shows who actually holds the object; most often the chain ends at a static collection or a thread."},"JVM|Heap dump":{"term":"Heap dump","def":"A snapshot of the entire heap to a file, used to hunt leaks and see what ate the memory and how much.","hook":"A photo of the fridge: snap everything at once, then calmly figure out at home what ate the space.","more":"A **heap dump** is a binary snapshot (HPROF format) of all heap objects, their fields and links at the moment of capture. You can take it via `jmap -dump`, `jcmd GC.heap_dump`, automatically on OOM, or from JFR/a profiler.\n\n**Why:** in Eclipse MAT / VisualVM you inspect the **histogram** (classes by size) and the **dominator tree** (who retains the memory) and trace the path-to-GC-root. **Pitfalls:** taking a live dump triggers a stop-the-world and writes a file the size of the heap (gigabytes) — do it carefully in prod; the analyzer also needs a big heap."},"JVM|Xmx/Xms":{"term":"Xmx/Xms","def":"JVM launch flags: Xmx sets the maximum heap, Xms the starting size.","hook":"A glass: Xms is how much you pour in up front, Xmx is the brim — you can't pour above it.","more":"**-Xmx** sets the maximum heap size, **-Xms** the initial (pre-allocated) size. The heap grows from Xms toward Xmx as needed; you can't go past Xmx — that's an OOM.\n\n**Why:** control over process memory and GC predictability. **Pitfalls/practice:** in containers and on servers people often set **Xms == Xmx** to avoid pauses on heap resize and to reserve memory up front. In Docker, rely on `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` rather than a hardcoded value; these flags do NOT bound metaspace."},"Reactive|Reactive Streams":{"term":"Reactive Streams","def":"A standard for async stream processing with backpressure, defined by 4 interfaces; in the JDK it lives as java.util.concurrent.Flow.","hook":"Publisher is the waiter, Subscriber is the guest. The guest says \"bring me 3 dishes\" (request 3) — the waiter doesn't dump the whole menu on the table at once."},"Reactive|Backpressure":{"term":"Backpressure","def":"The consumer paces the source via request(n) so it isn't flooded and runs out of memory.","hook":"Backpressure is the valve on a fire hose. Don't throttle it and you'll choke. request(n) is your hand on that valve."},"Reactive|Overflow strategy":{"term":"Overflow strategy","def":"How the pipeline behaves on overflow when the source can't be slowed down: buffer / drop / latest / error.","hook":"A checkout line with no room: BUFFER lines everyone up in the hallway, DROP turns newcomers away, LATEST keeps only the last one, ERROR shuts the store down."},"Reactive|Dynamic push-pull":{"term":"Dynamic push-pull","def":"The Reactive Streams model: data is pushed asynchronously, but never more than the consumer asked for via request(n).","hook":"Pull is you walking to the well with a bucket. Push is someone pouring on you uninvited. Reactive is saying \"pour me 3 liters\" and getting exactly 3."},"Reactive|Hot / Cold publisher":{"term":"Hot / Cold publisher","def":"A cold publisher replays the stream from the start for each subscriber; a hot one emits independently, so late subscribers only see what's current.","hook":"Cold is Netflix: hit play and the movie starts from the beginning, just for you. Hot is a live broadcast: tune in late and you missed the opening."},"Reactive|Virtual threads (Loom)":{"term":"Virtual threads (Loom)","def":"Lightweight JVM threads (Java 21): blocking doesn't tie up an OS thread, giving cheap concurrency without going fully reactive.","hook":"Loom is an automatic transmission — you just drive. Reactive is a manual sports car: faster in a data-stream race, but you shift gears yourself and stall easily."},"Reactive|Cardinality":{"term":"Cardinality","def":"How many elements a stream can emit: 0..1 (Mono/Uni) or 0..N (Flux/Multi).","hook":"Mono = an Optional over the wire, Flux = a Stream over the wire. Mutiny just renamed them: Uni and Multi."},"Reactive|flatMapSequential":{"term":"flatMapSequential","def":"Subscribes to the inner publishers concurrently (fast) but restores the original ordering on output — a middle ground between flatMap and concatMap.","hook":"map repacks the box; flatMap opens N parcels at once and whoever's unpacked first ships first; concatMap is a strict one-at-a-time queue."},"Reactive|combineLatest":{"term":"combineLatest","def":"An operator that, whenever any source emits, emits a combination of the latest values from all sources.","hook":"zip is a zipper: left tooth + right tooth = one link. merge dumps two bags into one basket. combineLatest is a ticker board showing the latest rate for every currency."},"Reactive|retryWhen / Retry.backoff":{"term":"retryWhen / Retry.backoff","def":"An advanced retry operator with exponential backoff, jitter, and filtering by error type; driven by a companion stream.","hook":"retry says \"try again from the top.\" onErrorResume says \"that failed, take the backup route.\" onErrorReturn says \"no way through, here's a fallback value.\""},"Reactive|Cold publisher":{"term":"Cold publisher","def":"A lazy stream that re-runs its work on every subscription and does nothing until subscribe() is called.","hook":"A Publisher is a recipe in a book, not a cooked dish. subscribe() = turning on the stove. No subscribe, and you're sitting hungry over the book."},"Reactive|boundedElastic / BlockHound":{"term":"boundedElastic / BlockHound","def":"boundedElastic is a Scheduler with a capped pool for blocking/IO work (the replacement for elastic()); BlockHound is an agent that detects blocking calls on a non-blocking thread.","hook":"subscribeOn is where the factory (source) runs. publishOn is where the goods are moved onto another truck. The event loop is one waiter for the whole room: make him brew coffee (blocking) and the room starves — hand it off to the kitchen (boundedElastic)."},"Web|IDL":{"term":"IDL","def":"Interface Definition Language — a language-agnostic contract description that code is generated from.","hook":"One blueprint for a part, and a machinist on any lathe turns out the same thing."},"Web|Мультиплексирование":{"term":"Multiplexing","def":"Many logical streams at once over a single TCP connection, without blocking one another.","hook":"HTTP/1.1 is one checkout taken in turns; HTTP/2 is many cashiers in the same hall."},"Web|Streaming RPC":{"term":"Streaming RPC","def":"A call where the request and/or response is a sequence of messages rather than a single one.","hook":"One-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-one, many-to-many — four shapes of a conversation."},"Web|Field tag":{"term":"Field tag","def":"A field number plus wire-type written on the wire instead of the field name.","hook":"A field number is like a seat number on a plane: the passenger may change their name, but the seat stays the same."},"Web|reserved":{"term":"reserved","def":"A declaration that permanently blocks a removed field's number/name from being reused.","hook":"reserved is like a welded-shut hotel room door: no new guests get checked into it."},"Web|gRPC-Web":{"term":"gRPC-Web","def":"A gRPC variant for the browser: it works through a proxy (Envoy), since browsers don't expose raw HTTP/2 gRPC.","hook":"gRPC is the service elevator between floors; REST is the front entrance for guests."},"Web|Deadline propagation":{"term":"Deadline propagation","def":"Passing the remaining time budget down the call chain so the deadline is shared across hops.","hook":"A deadline is the 18:00 train across every transfer, not \"10 minutes per station.\""},"System Design|Non-functional requirements":{"term":"Non-functional requirements","def":"Requirements about HOW the system behaves rather than WHAT it does: QPS, latency, availability, consistency, scale. These are what actually drive the architecture.","hook":"First ask «where are we headed», then figure out the fuel, then draw the route — and only then worry about where the traffic jam is"},"System Design|Back-of-the-envelope":{"term":"Back-of-the-envelope","def":"A rough order-of-magnitude estimate done in your head or on a napkin. The goal is scale (thousands vs millions), not precision to the decimal.","hook":"Napkin math: not «exactly how many» but «thousands or millions» — that's enough to decide whether you need a cache"},"System Design|QPS / RPS":{"term":"QPS / RPS","def":"Queries (Requests) Per Second — the baseline load metric. Distinguish average from peak: you size capacity for the peak.","hook":"QPS = users × actions ÷ 100k seconds. And the read/write ratio is where the system actually hurts"},"System Design|Tail latency (p99)":{"term":"Tail latency (p99)","def":"The latency of the worst requests: p99 is slower than 99% of responses. Under fan-out, the tail of a single shard becomes the typical time for the whole request.","hook":"The latency budget is an envelope of cash: you hand it out across all layers, and the total has to fit. Whoever takes the most is your problem"},"System Design|SLA / девятки":{"term":"SLA / nines","def":"Service Level Agreement — promised availability as a percentage. «Nines» is the count of 9s after the decimal point. Each extra nine divides the allowed downtime by 10.","hook":"Nines are like stairs: each step is 10x less downtime, but several times more expensive in hardware and nerves"},"System Design|CAP / PACELC":{"term":"CAP / PACELC","def":"CAP: under a network partition (P) you choose between Consistency and Availability. PACELC adds: Else (no partition) — you choose between Latency and Consistency.","hook":"The network will partition anyway. The only question: when it does, lie-but-answer (AP) or stay-silent-but-honest (CP)"},"System Design|ACID vs BASE":{"term":"ACID vs BASE","def":"ACID (SQL) — strict transactions, consistency right away. BASE (many NoSQL) — Basically Available, Soft state, Eventual consistency: availability traded for deferred consistency.","hook":"SQL is an accountant's ledger with strict rules. NoSQL is a pile of sticky notes: you slap them down and grab them by key fast, but order isn't guaranteed"},"System Design|Shard key":{"term":"Shard key","def":"The field by which data is spread across shards. It determines load evenness and query locality. Changing it later is extremely expensive.","hook":"A shard key is like a seating chart: seat the guests badly and one table is packed while the rest sit empty"},"System Design|Replication lag":{"term":"Replication lag","def":"How far a replica trails the master under async replication. It produces eventual consistency and the read-your-writes problem: a fresh write is temporarily invisible on the replica.","hook":"Sync — you ship the parcel and wait for the recipient's signature. Async — you drop it in the mailbox and walk off: fast, but no guarantee it arrives"},"System Design|Token bucket":{"term":"Token bucket","def":"A rate-limiting algorithm: tokens refill at a constant rate up to a cap, each request spends a token; allows bursts.","hook":"A bucket of water: it drips in steadily (refill), each request is a glass of water. Run dry — wait."},"System Design|base62":{"term":"base62","def":"Encoding a number with a 62-symbol alphabet [0-9a-zA-Z]; more compact than decimal and URL-safe without special characters.","hook":"A coat-check tag: handed out in order (counter), but printed in a compact font (base62)."},"System Design|Zipf-распределение":{"term":"Zipf distribution","def":"A law where an item's frequency is inversely proportional to its rank: a handful of «hot» items account for almost all the traffic.","hook":"301 — moved for good (the post office stops forwarding), 302 — temporarily at the neighbor's (they ask every time whether you're home)."},"System Design|Fan-out":{"term":"Fan-out","def":"Branching: delivering one event to many recipients. On write — pushed into feeds ahead of time, on read — assembled at request time.","hook":"Push — the paperboy drops one in every mailbox ahead of time. Pull — you walk past the kiosks yourself on your way out. Nobody delivers for a celebrity — there's a queue waiting on them."},"System Design|Dead-letter queue (DLQ)":{"term":"Dead-letter queue (DLQ)","def":"A queue for messages that couldn't be processed after N attempts; they're handled separately, neither lost nor blocking the flow.","hook":"Mail sorting: letters in a queue, each stamped with an id (don't send twice), a form template, undelivered ones go to the «returns» bin (DLQ)."},"System Design|Sorted Set (ZSET)":{"term":"Sorted Set (ZSET)","def":"A Redis structure: elements with a numeric score, always kept sorted; gives you top-N (ZREVRANGE) and rank (ZRANK) in O(log N).","hook":"Sharded counter — several tills instead of one queue. ZSET — a league table, always sorted, you see the standing instantly."},"System Design|Double-entry ledger":{"term":"Double-entry ledger","def":"A ledger where each operation is recorded twice (debit and credit) summing to zero — the invariant guards against money going missing.","hook":"An accountant's ledger: every ruble left somewhere and arrived somewhere — otherwise the books don't balance."},"System Design|Sync cursor (afterSeq)":{"term":"Sync cursor (afterSeq)","def":"A pointer to the last seq the client received; on reconnect the client requests everything greater — that's how missed data gets backfilled.","hook":"A post office: a letter first lands in a pigeonhole (durable), then the recipient is either home (push) or picks it up themselves (pull on reconnect)."},"System Design|Heartbeat + TTL":{"term":"Heartbeat + TTL","def":"The client periodically renews a presence key; an expired TTL automatically means offline, with no explicit notification.","hook":"A light bulb on a timer: press the button every 30s and it stays on; stop, and 45s later it goes out on its own."},"System Design|Pre-signed URL":{"term":"Pre-signed URL","def":"A temporarily signed link granting the client the right to perform a specific operation (PUT/GET) on an object directly against storage, bypassing the backend.","hook":"A warehouse pass: security (the backend) issues a temporary pass, then the truck drives to the warehouse on its own, skipping the office."},"System Design|Инвертированный индекс":{"term":"Inverted index","def":"A «term → list of documents containing that term» structure; intersecting the posting lists yields documents that contain all the query's words.","hook":"A Trie is a table of contents by first letters, leading to the continuation. An inverted index is the subject index at the back of the book: word → pages."},"System Design|Cache stampede":{"term":"Cache stampede","def":"A simultaneous flood of misses on a hot key that just expired, slamming the DB; tamed with single-flight and randomized TTL.","hook":"A coat-check ring: add one hanger and only a handful of nearby coats shift, not the whole wardrobe. And one runner fetches the coat, not the whole crowd at once."},"Build|Phase":{"term":"Phase","def":"A named step in the lifecycle; invoking a phase runs every preceding phase in the chain.","hook":"An escalator: you can step on at any level, but you still rode up from the bottom — you can't leap over the steps in between."},"Build|Scope":{"term":"Scope","def":"A dependency attribute that controls its visibility on the build/test/runtime classpath and whether it ends up in the artifact.","hook":"provided = the tool is already at the workplace: you need it in mind while building, but you don't put it in the box you ship — the factory already has it."},"Build|Nearest-wins":{"term":"Nearest-wins","def":"A mediation rule: on a version conflict, the version closest to the root of the dependency tree wins.","hook":"Not 'who's older' but 'who's closer to the boss': the near relative inherits, the distant one is out."},"Build|BOM":{"term":"BOM","def":"Bill of Materials — a pom containing only dependencyManagement, imported to pull in a consistent set of versions.","hook":"A BOM is the warehouse price list: every shop orders a part by name and takes the price (version) from the single list instead of inventing its own."},"Build|Reactor":{"term":"Reactor","def":"The Maven subsystem for building a multi-module project: it builds the module graph and assembles them in topological order.","hook":"The Reactor is the site foreman: foundation first (core), then walls (app) — the order is dictated by logic, not by the order in the estimate."},"Build|Goal (Mojo)":{"term":"Goal (Mojo)","def":"An atomic task inside a plugin; run directly or bound to a lifecycle phase.","hook":"A plugin is a toolbox, a goal is a specific tool. The phase is the 'point in the schedule' when you reach for that tool."},"Build|Profile":{"term":"Profile","def":"A conditional set of build overrides (deps/plugins/properties), enabled explicitly or by an activator.","hook":"A profile is the mode switch on a washing machine: same drum (pom), but 'delicate/quick' swap the program to fit the situation."},"Build|Relocation (shading)":{"term":"Relocation (shading)","def":"Renaming a dependency's packages inside a fat-jar to prevent version conflicts of one and the same library.","hook":"Relocation = re-labelling: two identical cans (guava) on the shelf — you rename one so the cook doesn't grab the wrong one."},"Build|ABI (Application Binary Interface)":{"term":"ABI (Application Binary Interface)","def":"The public binary surface of a module (its signatures). Change only that and consumers must be rebuilt; change the internal implementation and they need not.","hook":"api is the window facing out (everyone sees it), implementation is the kitchen inside (locked behind a door)."},"Build|BOM / platform":{"term":"BOM / platform","def":"Bill of Materials — a module that carries no code, only aligns the versions of a set of libraries. In Gradle it's wired in via `platform('group:bom:ver')`.","hook":"Gradle picks the 'highest version', Maven picks the 'closest by kinship'."},"Build|UP-TO-DATE":{"term":"UP-TO-DATE","def":"The status of a task Gradle skipped: its declared inputs and outputs haven't changed since the last run, so there's nothing to recompute.","hook":"A cache is the memory 'I've already worked this out': same question -> same ready-made answer pulled from the drawer."},"Build|Транзитивная зависимость":{"term":"Transitive dependency","def":"A library you never declared directly — it was pulled in by one of your direct dependencies. The source of most version conflicts.","hook":"dependency:tree is an X-ray of the classpath: you see every bone and where the version is fractured."},"Build|Dependency locking":{"term":"Dependency locking","def":"Pinning the exact versions of the whole graph into a lock file so the build is reproducible and identical on every machine and in CI.","hook":"MAJOR — moving to a different house (your keys don't fit), MINOR — new furniture, PATCH — fixed the tap."},"Crypto|AEAD":{"term":"AEAD","def":"Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data — a single primitive that gives you both confidentiality and tamper protection (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305).","hook":"AES is a safe with one key: the same key locks it and unlocks it. The mode is the rule for how you chain many little safes together, and the nonce is a random tag so two identical messages end up looking different."},"Crypto|Лавинный эффект":{"term":"Avalanche effect","def":"A hash property: flipping a single input bit changes about half the output bits, so similar inputs produce wildly different hashes.","hook":"A hash is a meat grinder: turning a steak into mince is instant, getting the steak back from the mince is impossible. And change even one sinew in the steak and the mince comes out looking completely different."},"Crypto|Memory-hard":{"term":"Memory-hard","def":"A property of algorithms like argon2 and scrypt: they demand a lot of RAM to compute, which kills cheap mass brute-forcing on GPUs/ASICs.","hook":"SHA is a race car — a million hashes a second, which is terrible for passwords because the attacker runs his brute-force on the same engine. A password algorithm is a cement mixer: deliberately slow and heavy, so every batch costs both you and the attacker dearly."},"Crypto|Length-extension":{"term":"Length-extension","def":"An attack on SHA-2: knowing H(secret‖data) and the length, you can compute H(secret‖data‖extra) without knowing the secret. HMAC is immune to it.","hook":"A hash proves the data wasn't corrupted (integrity), but anyone can recompute it. HMAC is a seal on the envelope that only someone holding the secret stamp can apply: you can see it wasn't opened, and that it came from one of your own."},"Crypto|KMS":{"term":"KMS","def":"Key Management Service — a dedicated system for storing and rotating encryption keys (AWS KMS, HashiCorp Vault), so the keys don't sit next to the data.","hook":"In-transit is the armored cash truck while the money is driving across town. At-rest is the bank vault where it sits. There's no point having the truck without the vault or the vault without the truck — you get robbed at both ends."},"Crypto|Односторонняя функция":{"term":"One-way function","def":"A function that's easy to compute forward but practically impossible to invert. The basis of password storage: you can verify, but you can't recover.","hook":"Encrypting a password means locking it with a key you carry right alongside it. Hashing means burning the original and keeping only the ash-print: you can match a new password against the ash, but nobody — including you — can bring the original back."},"Crypto|Гибридное шифрование":{"term":"Hybrid encryption","def":"Asymmetric crypto encrypts only a short session key, while the data itself is encrypted with a fast symmetric algorithm (AES). Best of both worlds.","hook":"A public key is an open padlock you've handed out to everyone. Anyone can snap it shut on a box, but only you hold the key (the private one) that opens it."},"Crypto|Неотрекаемость (non-repudiation)":{"term":"Non-repudiation","def":"A signer can't later deny having signed — only they hold the private key. This is a property of signatures specifically, not encryption.","hook":"A signature is like a wax seal on a letter. Only the holder of your personal signet ring (the private key) can press the imprint, but anyone can recognize your seal on sight (the public key)."},"Crypto|Forward Secrecy":{"term":"Forward Secrecy","def":"A property where leaking the long-term private key doesn't let an attacker decrypt previously recorded traffic, because the session keys were ephemeral and already discarded.","hook":"Diffie-Hellman is like mixing paint. Each side takes its own secret shade, publicly swaps a mix built on a shared base, then stirs in its secret again — both end up with the same color. An eavesdropper holding both mixes can't separate them back into the original shades."},"Crypto|Trust anchor (якорь доверия)":{"term":"Trust anchor","def":"A root self-signed certificate, pre-installed in a trusted store and trusted unconditionally — it's where chain validation terminates.","hook":"A certificate is like a passport. The paper means nothing on its own, but it's trusted because it was issued by a state (the CA) that people trust. And the state is trusted because the international community recognizes it (the root store in your OS)."},"Crypto|1-RTT / 0-RTT":{"term":"1-RTT / 0-RTT","def":"How many round trips you need before sending data. TLS 1.3 is 1-RTT; 0-RTT (for a known server) sends data immediately but is vulnerable to replay attacks.","hook":"A handshake is like two people through a glass door working out a shared cipher for their notes. First they agree on the rules out loud (in the clear) and check each other's passport (the certificate), then switch to a secret language (AES) they derived together."},"Crypto|JWKS":{"term":"JWKS","def":"JSON Web Key Set — an endpoint (usually /.well-known/jwks.json) where services pull the issuer's public keys to verify RS256/ES256 signatures, with key rotation supported via kid.","hook":"A JWT payload isn't a safe — it's a clear-plastic envelope: everyone can see the contents. The signature is the wax seal on it: if someone opened it and rewrote the contents, the seal won't match. Skip checking the seal and you'll accept any forgery as genuine."},"Algorithms|Radix tree":{"term":"Radix tree","def":"A compressed Trie: chains of single-child nodes are merged into one node holding a whole substring instead of a single character. Saves memory on sparse prefixes.","hook":"Like folders in a file explorer: \"Documents/\" is one shared path, and inside it Work, Photos, Music branch off. You don't repeat \"Documents\" in every file name."},"Algorithms|Treeification":{"term":"Treeification","def":"In a HashMap a bucket whose collision count reaches 8 (and when capacity >= 64) converts from a linked list into a red-black tree, so worst-case lookup is O(log n) rather than O(n).","hook":"Like a pile of books versus a library shelf. Dump books in one column and you riffle through all of them to reach the bottom one. Spread them across shelves and any book is a couple of steps away."},"Algorithms|α(n) — обратная Аккермана":{"term":"α(n) — inverse Ackermann","def":"A monstrously slow-growing function: α(n) <= 4 for any conceivable n. The amortized cost of a DSU operation with both optimizations is O(α(n)), i.e. effectively constant.","hook":"Like asking \"are you and I from the same clan?\". Everyone knows their chief through a chain of mentors. And path compression is \"let's all remember the chief directly, so next time we don't run the whole chain again\"."},"Algorithms|Lazy propagation":{"term":"Lazy propagation","def":"A segment tree optimization: when updating a whole range, don't descend to the leaves — hang a deferred tag on the node and apply it lazily on the next visit. Gives range updates in O(log n).","hook":"Like counters on warehouse floors. To know \"how many boxes on floors 1–5\", you don't recount each box — you sum a few aggregated counters. A box arrives — you only adjust the counters that cover it."},"Algorithms|accessOrder":{"term":"accessOrder","def":"A LinkedHashMap constructor flag. true — iteration and eviction in access order (a fresh get/put moves to the end) = LRU behavior. false (the default) — insertion order = FIFO.","hook":"Like a stack of plates on a serving line. Take a plate and you place a clean one on top. Out of space and you remove the bottom one nobody's reached in a while. The top is always what was just used."},"Algorithms|False positive":{"term":"False positive","def":"A false-positive answer: the structure says \"the element is present\" when it isn't. A Bloom filter can produce one; a false negative it cannot.","hook":"A bouncer with a VIP list by height and jacket color: \"not on the list\" he gets right for sure, but \"think they were here\" he might mix up with a lookalike."},"Algorithms|Skip list":{"term":"Skip list","def":"A probabilistic structure: a sorted linked list with several levels of express links. Search/insert/delete are O(log n) on average.","hook":"A metro with an express and a local line: ride the express past the stations, get off near the right district, then transfer to the local — you don't crawl through every stop."},"Algorithms|Binary heap":{"term":"Binary heap","def":"A nearly complete binary tree stored in an array with the heap invariant (parent ≤/≥ children). The basis of a priority queue: peek O(1), insert/extract O(log n).","hook":"An ER running triage: you instantly always know who's the most critical (the top), but you don't keep a full sorted list of every patient — you don't need it."},"Algorithms|Виртуальные узлы (vnodes)":{"term":"Virtual nodes (vnodes)","def":"Several points on the hash ring per physical node. They even out key distribution and make rebalancing smooth.","hook":"A round table of waiters: each serves a sector clockwise. One leaves — their tables go to the neighbor on the right, the other sectors untouched. mod N means reseating absolutely everyone."},"Algorithms|HyperLogLog":{"term":"HyperLogLog","def":"A probabilistic cardinality counter: estimates the number of unique elements in ~12-16 KB with ~1-2% error, without storing the elements themselves.","hook":"At a festival entrance: HLL answers \"how many different people came in today\" (without remembering each one). CMS answers \"how many times did this particular regular show up\". Different questions — different instruments."},"Distributed|binding":{"term":"binding","def":"a rule linking exchange→queue via a binding key; it determines which messages land in the queue","hook":"An exchange is a mail sorting hub. You drop a letter with a postcode (routing key), and the sorter, following its table (bindings), places copies into the right mailboxes (queues). The sender never sees the boxes."},"Distributed|deliveryTag":{"term":"deliveryTag","def":"a monotonic delivery id within a channel; used to ack/nack a specific message","hook":"A waitress (prefetch=2) carries at most 2 orders at a time. Until she delivers (acks) at least one, she won't carry a third. autoAck means \"counting the order done the moment it leaves the counter\": drop the tray and the guest goes hungry while the kitchen thinks they were fed."},"Distributed|offset":{"term":"offset","def":"the consumer's position in a Kafka partition log; committing the offset = \"how far I've read\", and it controls replay","hook":"RabbitMQ is a counter with a pickup belt: take the order, carry it off, and it's no longer on the belt. Kafka is a recording of the whole shift: watch from any minute, rewind, and five different people watch their own part independently. The recording is erased on a timer (retention), not when someone has watched it."},"Distributed|poison message":{"term":"poison message","def":"a message that reliably crashes processing; without a DLX or a retry limit it traps the consumer in an endless redelivery loop","hook":"A DLQ is the 'returned mail' bin at the sorting office. A letter that couldn't be delivered (rejected), went stale (TTL), or hit a full mailbox isn't thrown away — it's dropped into a special bin tagged with why. A human sorts it out later instead of it being lost silently."},"Distributed|receiptHandle":{"term":"receiptHandle","def":"a single-use token tied to one specific receive of a message; you pass it to Delete/ChangeVisibility, not the messageId","hook":"A visibility timeout is like borrowing a library book 'on loan for an hour'. While you hold it, the catalog marks it 'checked out' — nobody else can take it. Return it (delete) and it's gone from circulation. If the hour passes and you haven't returned it, the system assumes you vanished and puts the book back on the shelf — which is why two people can end up with the same copy."},"Distributed|partition key":{"term":"partition key","def":"the field that deterministically routes a message to a single partition/group; it guarantees ordering for that key","hook":"A checkout line is fair while there's only one register. Open a second and the person behind you can jump ahead if the front stalls. To keep a customer always served in order, route everyone with the 'same surname' to ONE register (the partition key). Different surnames can go in parallel."},"Distributed|consumer group":{"term":"consumer group","def":"a set of Kafka consumers sharing a topic's partitions; within the group each partition is owned by exactly one member","hook":"One long order list and three cooks at a single counter. Whoever's free grabs the next ticket. Three times faster, and if one cook drops, the other two keep going. The catch: dishes don't come out in strict order, and a ticket that's put back may be picked up by a different cook."},"Distributed|retention":{"term":"retention","def":"a log's storage policy: data lives by time/size (or by key via compaction), not until it has been read","hook":"A queue is the to-do list on the fridge: do an item, cross it off, forget it. A log is a diary: you record a fact, and different people read it (the accountant, the doctor, you a year later), each with their own bookmark, and you can reread the past. To-dos get tossed; the diary is kept."},"Web|HATEOAS":{"term":"HATEOAS","def":"Hypermedia As The Engine Of Application State — the server's response carries links to the next possible actions, so the client doesn't hardcode URLs.","hook":"REST maturity is like levels of driving. L0: you push the car by hand. L1: there's a wheel but a single gear. L2: a full gearbox and signals — where almost everyone actually drives. L3: an autopilot that even calls out the turns."},"Web|Location header":{"term":"Location header","def":"A response header with the URI of the created/target resource; mandatory companion to 201 and useful with 202/3xx.","hook":"Status codes are like a waiter's reply. 2xx \"here comes your dish.\" 4xx \"you ordered something not on the menu — that's on you.\" 5xx \"the kitchen's on fire — that's on us.\" Mixing the classes is like blaming the guest for a kitchen fire."},"Web|Idempotency-Key":{"term":"Idempotency-Key","def":"A unique client-supplied operation identifier in a header; the server uses it to dedupe repeated POSTs and return the original result.","hook":"Idempotency-Key is like a coat-check tag. You hand over a jacket and get tag #7. Come back a second time with the same #7 and they return the same jacket instead of issuing a new one. By the key the server recognizes \"this is the same operation\" and doesn't charge twice."},"Web|Content negotiation":{"term":"Content negotiation","def":"An HTTP mechanism where the client asks for the representation it wants (format/version) via Accept, and the server picks it and marks it with Vary.","hook":"Versioning is like a shop's address. URI: a new branch at a new address — everyone sees it at once. Header: same address, but you whisper to the guard \"take me to the updated hall.\" Media-type: same address, and you ask for goods \"in the 2024 packaging.\" The subtler the hint, the easier it is to confuse the courier."},"Web|problem+json":{"term":"problem+json","def":"The application/problem+json media type from RFC 7807/9457 — a standard HTTP API error body with type/title/status/detail/instance fields.","hook":"problem+json is like a single complaint form. Each department used to write its rejection on its own scrap of paper however it liked, and the lawyer went mad untangling it. Introduce one form with \"type/title/status/detail\" fields, and every department fills it in the same way, the client reads it instantly."},"Web|Resource":{"term":"Resource","def":"A named entity (a noun) addressed by a URI; you act on it with HTTP verbs, not with words in the URL.","hook":"URIs are like warehouse shelves and verbs are the operations on them. The shelf is named \"orders,\" not \"take-order.\" You don't build a \"delete-orders\" shelf — you take the \"orders\" shelf and apply DELETE to it. A verb in the shelf's name is a warehouse in disarray."},"Web|Keyset pagination":{"term":"Keyset pagination","def":"Paginating by the last item's value (WHERE col > :last) instead of skipping N rows — constant speed at any depth.","hook":"Offset is \"count 5000 people from the front of the line and toss them out.\" Keyset is \"just stand right behind that redhead.\" The deeper you go, the more it hurts the first guy."},"Web|Retry-After":{"term":"Retry-After","def":"An HTTP header (with 429/503/3xx): how many seconds, or until what date, the client should wait before retrying.","hook":"X-RateLimit-Remaining is your speedometer and fuel gauge: you see ahead that it's running out. Retry-After after a 429 is a \"no entry\" sign with a timer: wait exactly that long."},"Web|Vary":{"term":"Vary","def":"A response header telling caches \"this response depends on these request headers\" — without it a CDN will mix up different representations.","hook":"The same kebab, but Accept is \"mine in lavash, mild, the Russian way.\" One kitchen (URL), different platings. Vary: Accept keeps the waiter from serving you someone else's order from memory."},"Web|Expand-contract":{"term":"Expand-contract","def":"A safe-evolution pattern: first add the new thing alongside the old one (expand), let traffic migrate over, then drop the old one (contract).","hook":"You're re-laying the rails under a moving train: first you lay a second track next to the old one (expand), switch the trains onto it, and only then tear up the old one (contract). Ripping out the old track right away means a derailment."},"Web|Protobuf":{"term":"Protobuf","def":"A binary serialization format with a predefined schema (.proto) that gRPC uses by default — more compact and faster than JSON.","hook":"REST is ordering off a menu: you take the whole dish as plated. RPC/gRPC is calling the kitchen: \"go do this specific action.\" GraphQL is a buffet: you put exactly the fields you need on your plate, no more, no less."},"Concurrency|ScopedValue":{"term":"ScopedValue","def":"An immutable replacement for ThreadLocal aimed at virtual threads: the value is visible only inside a bounded block (where().run()), is automatically inherited by the scope's child tasks, and needs no manual cleanup — hence its cheapness across millions of vthreads.","hook":"A team of climbers on a single rope: roped into one party, moving together. One slips — the belay yanks the rest, and the group doesn't scatter across the mountain alone. The leader (parent) doesn't leave the route until the whole party is gathered.","more":""},"Web|Confused deputy":{"term":"Confused deputy","def":"A class of vulnerabilities where a trusted component performs a privileged action on the orders of a less-privileged party, with no grounds for doing so. In JWT — the validator picks the algorithm/key from the alg field, which the attacker controls.","hook":"It's like a guard who admits people by their passes, but the way to check the pass is dictated by the person walking in. The guest says \"my pass doesn't need checking\" — and the guard dutifully doesn't check it. Fixed by one rule: security decides how to verify, not the guest.","more":""},"Algorithms|Floyd's Tortoise and Hare":{"term":"Floyd's Tortoise and Hare","def":"A cycle-detection algorithm using two pointers of different speeds in O(n) time and O(1) memory; it also lets you find the cycle's entry point and its length.","hook":"Two runners on a circular track at different speeds: the fast one inevitably laps the slow one and catches up from behind.","more":""},"Algorithms|Quickselect":{"term":"Quickselect","def":"An algorithm for finding the k-th order element via partition (as in quicksort) with recursion into a single half; average O(n), worst case O(n²), deterministic O(n) via median-of-medians.","hook":"You're looking for a particular book on a shelf by its height: you grab a random one, split the shelf into \"shorter\" and \"taller,\" and dig only through the pile where yours could be — you never touch the other one.","more":""},"Reactive|Reactor Context":{"term":"Reactor Context","def":"An immutable key-value store bound to the Subscription (not to a thread), which propagates from subscribe() up to the source; written with contextWrite, read with deferContextual.","hook":"It's like a relay run backwards: the anchor (subscribe) grabs the Context baton first and passes it back down the lane — to the runner at the start (the source). Whoever stands HIGHER up the lane gets the baton; whoever's below the handoff point doesn't.","more":""},"Build|Filer":{"term":"Filer","def":"A service from the ProcessingEnvironment through which a processor creates new sources/classes/resources (createSourceFile). The only \"legal\" way to generate code in APT — it's what guarantees the new file makes it into the next round.","hook":"A processor is a contractor that the foreman (javac) lets onto the site before the concrete is poured: it draws in the missing blueprints (sources), and only once there's nothing left to draw does everything get poured at once.","more":""},"JVM|Cleaner / PhantomReference":{"term":"Cleaner / PhantomReference","def":"A deferred-cleanup mechanism: a PhantomReference is attached to the direct-buffer wrapper; when the GC declares the wrapper garbage, a background Cleaner thread triggers the release of the native memory. A replacement for finalize() without its downsides, but still non-deterministic — the moment of free() is dictated by the GC.","hook":"A direct buffer is like a storage locker outside your apartment. In the apartment (heap) only the tag-key hangs. As long as the key sits in your pocket, nobody frees the locker — even if you stopped needing the junk inside long ago.","more":""},"DevOps|cgroup memory limit":{"term":"cgroup memory limit","def":"A memory limit set by the Linux kernel for a group of processes (control group). The container runtime puts the value from resources.limits.memory here; on exceeding it, the kernel signals the OOM killer — hence OOMKilled.","hook":"It's like a bouncer at a club with a fire-safety capacity limit: when there are more people than allowed, he doesn't ask and doesn't warn — he just throws you out the door. Inside the club nobody announced \"we're full\" (no Java OOM), the decision was made outside (the kernel).","more":""},"Crypto|Envelope encryption":{"term":"Envelope encryption","def":"Two-level encryption: the data is encrypted with a fast data-key, and the data-key is encrypted with a master key in the KMS. Rotating the master requires no re-encryption of the data itself.","hook":"A safe in a bank: inside is your apartment key (data-key), outside is the bank's lock (the master in the KMS). Changing the bank's lock is easy, and you don't have to re-key every apartment.","more":""},"Design|Stateless server":{"term":"Stateless server","def":"A server that holds no client state between requests — everything lives in external storage or in a token. Any instance can serve any request → horizontal scaling without sticky sessions.","hook":"A waiter with no memory: any of them serves any guest, because the order is written on the check (token/Redis), not in the waiter's head."},"Infra|HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler)":{"term":"HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler)","def":"A Kubernetes controller that changes a Deployment's replica count by a metric (CPU/RPS/custom). desired = ceil(replicas × current/target), with a stabilization window against flapping.","hook":"A thermostat for pods: keeps the \"temperature\" (load) at the target, adding or removing AC units."},"Infra|Cold start":{"term":"Cold start","def":"The window after an instance starts when it isn't at peak form yet: the JIT hasn't compiled the hot code, caches are empty, pools aren't filled → high p99. Critical for autoscaling and serverless.","hook":"An athlete with no warmup: the first sprints are ragged; you need to warm up to working form."},"Design|Latency numbers":{"term":"Latency numbers","def":"A set of latency orders of magnitude (L1 → RAM → SSD → disk → DC network → cross-region) used to estimate on the back of an envelope where the bottleneck is and whether a call/cache/replication is worth it.","hook":"A ruler of scales: each rung ×~1000, and it's instantly clear what's expensive and what's free."},"Design|Back-of-the-envelope estimation":{"term":"Back-of-the-envelope estimation","def":"A quick estimate of load/volume in orders of magnitude (QPS, storage, bandwidth) at the design stage, to pick an architecture before coding. Precision isn't needed — the right order of magnitude is.","hook":"A calculation on a napkin: rough, but it instantly tells you — one box or a data center."},"Distributed|Quorum (кворум)":{"term":"Quorum","def":"The minimum majority of nodes (N/2+1) needed for a decision (leader election, committing a write). Any two quorums overlap → no two conflicting decisions at once.","hook":"A majority vote: two groups of \"more than half\" must overlap — you can't elect a second president."},"Distributed|Fencing token":{"term":"Fencing token","def":"A monotonically increasing number issued when leadership/a lock is acquired. The resource keeps the max seen and rejects operations with a smaller token — protection against a \"zombie\" leader after a GC pause or partition.","hook":"A queue ticket: the warehouse accepts only a ticket with a number higher than the previous one; a latecomer with an old ticket is turned away."},"Distributed|Lambda-архитектура":{"term":"Lambda architecture","def":"A big-data pattern: a batch layer gives an accurate but delayed result, a speed layer an approximate but fresh one; a serving layer merges them. Kappa simplifies it to a single stream layer with log replay.","hook":"Two cooks: one cooks perfectly but slowly (batch), the other a quick sketch (stream); you serve them together."},"Distributed|Колоночное хранение (columnar)":{"term":"Columnar storage","def":"Data on disk is laid out by columns, not rows. An analytic query reads only the needed columns and compresses them well (similar values adjacent) → OLAP is far faster, but point writes are expensive.","hook":"A card file by field, not by card: need \"everyone's age\" — grab one drawer instead of flipping through every full form."},"Design|Non-functional requirements":{"term":"Non-functional requirements","def":"Requirements not about \"what it does\" but \"how\": scale (QPS, volume), latency (p99), availability (SLA), consistency, security. They drive the architecture — the feature is often the same, but scale changes everything.","hook":"Not \"what kind of house\", but \"for how many residents and earthquakes\" — that's what the foundation depends on."},"Java|Копия ссылки (reference copy)":{"term":"Reference copy","def":"When passing an object into a method, it's not the object that's copied but the value of the reference to it — two pointers to one heap cell. Mutating the shared object is visible to both; reassigning one copy doesn't affect the other.","hook":"A photocopied key: opens the same apartment (mutation visible), but throwing away the copy doesn't lose your original key (reassign is local)."},"Java|Иммутабельность ключа (key immutability contract)":{"term":"Key immutability contract","def":"The unwritten HashMap/HashSet requirement: fields of a key used in hashCode()/equals() must not change while the object is in the collection — otherwise the entry becomes unreachable.","hook":"You can't re-cut a safe key while the safe is locked: cut it and the new key no longer fits, while the lock stays shut forever."},"Java|ForwardingNode":{"term":"ForwardingNode","def":"A special node with hash = MOVED (-1) that CHM places at the head of a bucket during resize: it signals readers to look in the new table and writers to help with migration (cooperative resize by multiple threads).","hook":"A road sign 'under repair, detour →': hit this bucket — go to the new table, and help migrate the neighbors while you're at it."},"Java|fail-fast итератор":{"term":"fail-fast iterator","def":"An iterator that, on detecting a structural modification of the collection made around it (modCount != expectedModCount), immediately throws ConcurrentModificationException instead of behaving unpredictably. A heuristic, not a guarantee.","hook":"A museum alarm: touch an exhibit off-protocol and the siren wails instantly, rather than 'we'll sort it out later'."},"Concurrency|Реентрантность (Reentrancy)":{"term":"Reentrancy","def":"A lock property where a thread already owning the lock can acquire it again without self-deadlock; the hold count increases and is released when an equal number of unlock() calls occur.","hook":"Like a hotel key-card: you're already inside — you re-enter the same door without a new card, but to leave for good you must exit as many times as you entered."},"Concurrency|Флаг прерывания (interrupt status)":{"term":"Interrupt status (flag)","def":"An internal boolean flag on every thread. interrupt() raises it; blocking methods clear it when they throw InterruptedException. Cancellation in Java is cooperative: the thread must check the flag itself or handle the exception properly.","hook":"Like the red flag on a mailbox: the neighbor raised it (interrupt), but picking up the letter and reacting is your job; nobody drags you out of the house by force."},"Concurrency|Безопасная публикация (safe publication)":{"term":"Safe publication","def":"Publishing an object reference while establishing happens-before between construction and the read, so the reading thread sees the object fully initialized rather than half-constructed.","hook":"A parcel is handed to the recipient only after it has been sealed (freeze) — you can't take a half-empty box."},"Concurrency|Spurious wakeup (ложное пробуждение)":{"term":"Spurious wakeup","def":"A return from wait() with no matching notify/notifyAll/interrupt — permitted by the JLS and genuinely happening due to how POSIX condvars/futexes work. This is exactly why the condition is checked in a while, not an if.","hook":"The alarm beeped by itself at 3 a.m. — you got up, but it's too early. Until you look at the clock (re-check the condition), don't trust the wakeup."},"JVM|Достижимость (reachability)":{"term":"Reachability","def":"The property of an object being accessible via a chain of references starting from some GC root; an unreachable object is a collection candidate regardless of internal mutual references.","hook":"A tree from the root: a branch is alive if you can reach it from the trunk (root). A severed branch falls even if its leaves cling to each other."},"JVM|Card table":{"term":"Card table","def":"A byte array where one byte covers a heap region (512 bytes in HotSpot); a write barrier marks the byte dirty on a reference store, so minor GC scans only those old regions instead of the whole old gen.","hook":"A notice board by blocks of the old gen: a red mark on a block = 'someone set up a reference here, check me', and the collector goes only to the marked blocks."},"JVM|Compressed Oops":{"term":"Compressed Oops","def":"A JVM mode that stores object references in 4 bytes instead of 8 by relying on 8-byte alignment and a 3-bit shift; it works while the heap fits within the ~32 GB addressable range.","hook":"A short ZIP code instead of a full address: it saves space on every 'envelope' reference until the 'city' (heap) outgrows 32 GB."},"JVM|Утечка ClassLoader (ClassLoader leak)":{"term":"ClassLoader leak","def":"A situation where an undeployed webapp fails to free Metaspace because its ClassLoader stays reachable via a strong reference from code outside the webapp (a thread, a JDK static, a ThreadLocal), and so isn't GC'd along with all its classes.","hook":"An anchor caught on the seabed: the ship (webapp) sailed away, but the rope (one reference) holds the whole cargo of classes in Metaspace."},"JVM|LambdaMetafactory":{"term":"LambdaMetafactory","def":"A JDK class from java.lang.invoke that acts as the bootstrap method for lambda invokedynamic sites: on the first call it spins the functional-interface implementation class and returns a CallSite that the JVM links constantly.","hook":"A factory floor that opens exactly once on the first order (indy), stamps out the implementation object, and forever hangs a CallSite sign — after that it dispenses with no retooling."},"Java|Eager vs Lazy evaluation (нетерпеливое и ленивое вычисление)":{"term":"Eager vs Lazy evaluation","def":"Eager — an expression is computed immediately when encountered (this is how Java evaluates method arguments, before the call). Lazy — computation is deferred until the result is actually needed; in Java this is achieved by wrapping it in a Supplier/lambda.","hook":"Eager — the chef cooks the dish in advance (in case someone orders). Lazy — the chef starts cooking only when the waiter actually brings the order (calls supplier.get())."},"Java|serialVersionUID":{"term":"serialVersionUID","def":"A long version constant of a Serializable class by which ObjectInputStream decides whether a serialized stream is compatible with the current class.","hook":"The version number in the class's passport: match — you're let in; mismatch — turned away at the border (InvalidClassException)."},"Java|Gadget chain":{"term":"Gadget chain","def":"A chain of already-on-classpath, harmless-looking classes whose serialization callback methods reflectively invoke one another so that during readObject() execution reaches a dangerous sink (Runtime.exec, Method.invoke, TemplatesImpl).","hook":"Dominoes ALREADY standing on your table (in your dependencies) — the attacker doesn't bring their own, they just flick the first one."},"Java|Статус прерывания (interrupt flag)":{"term":"Interrupt status (interrupt flag)","def":"An internal boolean flag on every thread. interrupt() sets it to true; blocking methods clear it back to false when they throw InterruptedException. Read via isInterrupted() (non-clearing) and the static Thread.interrupted() (clearing).","hook":"A 'time to leave' note on the thread's forehead: interrupt() sticks it on, InterruptedException rips it off — and you must stick it back."},"Spring|rollback-only (флаг глобального отката)":{"term":"rollback-only (global rollback flag)","def":"A boolean flag on the physical transaction set by setRollbackOnly(); on commit, seeing it forces a rollback and throws UnexpectedRollbackException instead of completing successfully.","hook":"A red flag on a shared track: anyone can raise it, but no one can lower it — at the finish the whole race is annulled."},"Spring|Precedence (порядок advice)":{"term":"Precedence (advice ordering)","def":"An advisor's numeric priority: a lower @Order/getOrder() value places the advice closer to the caller (outside the proxy chain), a higher one closer to the target method (inside).","hook":"Onion layers: a small number is the outer skin — the knife (the call) reaches it first and leaves it last."},"Spring|Relaxed Binding":{"term":"Relaxed Binding","def":"A Spring Boot mechanism that maps one POJO field to different key spellings (kebab-case, camelCase, SCREAMING_SNAKE from env, dotted) by normalizing into ConfigurationPropertyName. Works only for @ConfigurationProperties.","hook":"A universal power adapter: the plug-key can be any shape (mail.from / mailFrom / MAIL_FROM) — the adapter still seats it into one socket-field."},"Spring|Контекст персистентности (Persistence Context)":{"term":"Persistence Context","def":"The EntityManager-bound set of managed entities with a first-level cache (a Map keyed by EntityKey) guaranteeing one object per id, dirty checking, and deferred flush within a transaction.","hook":"The transaction's coat check: one token (id) maps to the very same item on the shelf — no second trip to the warehouse (DB)."},"Spring|Dehydrated state (CacheEntry)":{"term":"Dehydrated state (CacheEntry)","def":"The form in which L2 stores an entity: not a managed object but a flat array of field values plus the version, since one object cannot be shared across threads. On a hit Hibernate reassembles (hydrates) a new managed instance from it into the current Session.","hook":"Like flat-pack furniture in a warehouse: you don't store assembled chairs (objects), you store boxes of parts (CacheEntry), and each shopper assembles their own chair at home (in their Session)."},"Spring|Стабильный hashCode сущности":{"term":"Stable entity hashCode","def":"A contract where a JPA entity's hashCode() does not change across the whole lifecycle (transient → managed → detached), even when the DB assigns a @GeneratedValue id on flush.","hook":"Like a flat number engraved on the door at move-in: even if you're later assigned a postal code, the sign on the building must not change — otherwise visitors can't find you."},"DB|Транзитивная зависимость":{"term":"Transitive dependency","def":"When a non-key attribute depends on the key not directly but through another non-key attribute (A → B → C, where B is not a key); this is exactly what 3NF eliminates.","hook":"Broken telephone: the key whispers to the city, the city whispers to the region — the region should hear the key directly, not through a middleman."},"DB|Денормализация":{"term":"Denormalization","def":"Deliberately introducing redundancy (duplicates/aggregates) into a normalized schema to speed up reads, at the cost of harder writes and the risk of copies drifting apart.","hook":"A cache living inside the DB schema: faster to read, but someone must invalidate it or you serve stale data."},"DB|ctid":{"term":"ctid","def":"A Postgres system pseudo-column — the physical address of a row version in the heap, as (page_number, offset). Every entry of any index points to it; it changes on UPDATE/VACUUM FULL.","hook":"It's like 'shelf 7, slot 3' in a warehouse: the index stores only the coordinate, not the item itself; move the item (UPDATE) and the coordinate changes."},"DB|Оконная функция (window function)":{"term":"Window function","def":"A function computing a value over a set of rows (a 'window') related to the current row WITHOUT collapsing rows into groups (unlike GROUP BY). Defined via OVER (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ...).","hook":"Like looking out a train window: you see neighboring cars (the window of rows) but stay in your own seat — the row doesn't vanish, as it would with GROUP BY."},"DB|Next-key lock":{"term":"Next-key lock","def":"InnoDB's combined lock = a record lock on an index record + a gap lock on the gap immediately before it; the default mode on REPEATABLE READ that prevents phantom inserts into a range.","hook":"A lock not only on the door (the row) but on the corridor before it (the gap) — no one can sneak in and install a new door."},"Design|Виртуальная диспетчеризация (dynamic dispatch)":{"term":"Dynamic dispatch (virtual dispatch)","def":"The JVM mechanism where invokevirtual selects a method implementation at runtime based on the object's actual class via the vtable, not the declared type of the reference.","hook":"You dial a number (the reference) — but the call is answered by whoever is actually at the phone right now (the actual object), not the name written in your contacts."},"Design|FilterChain (цепочка фильтров)":{"term":"FilterChain","def":"The object passed into a Servlet filter; calling chain.doFilter(req, res) delegates control to the next filter, and finally to the target servlet/controller. Not calling it = aborting request processing.","hook":"A relay baton: until you hand it to the next runner (doFilter), the race is frozen in place — there's no finish line (controller)."},"Design|Спрятанная зависимость (hidden dependency)":{"term":"Hidden dependency","def":"A dependency a class gets not through its constructor/signature but by pulling it from inside (a locator/registry/singleton), so it's invisible in the public API and unchecked by the compiler — discovered only at runtime.","hook":"Like a secret ingredient in a recipe not listed in the contents: you learn about it only when the dish has already failed."},"Design|Анемичная модель (Anemic Domain Model)":{"term":"Anemic Domain Model","def":"Antipattern: domain objects hold only data (getters/setters) while all business logic is moved into services that 'ask' for data and decide outside — a direct consequence of violating Tell-Don't-Ask.","hook":"Like a mannequin in a shop window: looks like a person, but everything is done for it — it can't do anything itself."},"Distributed|Zombie fencing (огораживание зомби)":{"term":"Zombie fencing","def":"A Kafka mechanism where, on initTransactions(), the transaction coordinator raises the producer epoch for a given transactional.id, and any stale instance with the same id but a lower epoch gets a ProducerFencedException and can't write.","hook":"A turnstile with a refreshed pass: your old zombie clone, stuck in a GC pause or a network partition, taps an expired card and isn't let through."},"Distributed|auto.offset.reset":{"term":"auto.offset.reset","def":"Consumer config that sets the start position ONLY when there's no valid committed offset: latest (from the end), earliest (from the log start), none (throw an exception).","hook":"A spare bookmark: pulled out only if you lost the main one (the committed offset) or it expired."},"Distributed|Changelog-топик":{"term":"Changelog topic","def":"A compacted Kafka topic (<app-id>-<store>-changelog) into which Kafka Streams mirrors every change of the local state store; the source of truth for restoring RocksDB after a crash or rebalance.","hook":"A bank's transaction log: you can lose the local balance cache, but from the log you can always restore the exact latest state of every account."},"Distributed|Clock skew (рассинхрон часов)":{"term":"Clock skew","def":"The instantaneous difference in wall-clock readings between two nodes at the same physical moment; drift is the rate at which this difference grows due to crystal inaccuracy (~10–100 ppm). Because of skew, comparing currentTimeMillis() across nodes does not determine the real order of events.","hook":"Two people agree to meet 'at noon', but one's watch runs fast and the other's slow — they arrive at different real times, each sincerely believing they were punctual."},"Distributed|Group coordinator":{"term":"Group coordinator","def":"The broker that leads the __consumer_offsets partition a given consumer group maps to; it runs membership, heartbeat, rebalance and stores the group's offsets.","hook":"The doorman of one specific building entrance: a group is tied to one entrance (partition), and that entrance's leader-broker keeps all the records for the group."},"Testing|Тест, привязанный к реализации (implementation-coupled test)":{"term":"Implementation-coupled test","def":"A test that, through interaction verification or knowledge of internal mechanics, asserts 'how' the SUT works rather than 'what' it does, and therefore breaks on refactoring that does not change behavior.","hook":"An alarm that triggers not on a break-in (behavior) but on the owner rearranging the furniture (refactoring) — a false alarm on every move."},"Testing|Fake (in-memory реализация порта)":{"term":"Fake (in-memory port implementation)","def":"A working, simplified implementation of a contract (e.g., an InMemoryRepository backed by a HashMap) that holds state and behaves like a real adapter, but without the external infrastructure.","hook":"A mini-fridge for tests: you really store and retrieve, unlike an answering-machine stub that just repeats one phrase."},"Testing|inline mock-maker":{"term":"inline mock-maker","def":"Mockito's engine (ByteBuddy + Java Instrumentation API) that retransforms classes at runtime, letting you mock static/final without PowerMock; the default since Mockito 5.","hook":"The jackhammer used to drill out a static method cemented into concrete — it works, but the very fact you had to drill says the building was put up wrong."},"Testing|OngoingStubbing":{"term":"OngoingStubbing","def":"The object Mockito returns from when() to build the stub chain (.thenReturn/.thenThrow). Its existence requires that the method call has already happened and been intercepted.","hook":"Like a store receipt: it's issued ONLY after the purchase (method call) has already been rung up at the register."},"Testing|MergedContextConfiguration":{"term":"MergedContextConfiguration","def":"The immutable TestContext cache key: configuration classes + active profiles + property sources + the set of ContextCustomizers (including mocks). Its equals()/hashCode() decide whether an already-built ApplicationContext is reused.","hook":"It's the test's 'barcode': identical barcode — same context off the shelf; slightly different — the cashier (TestContextManager) assembles a new context from scratch."},"Web|jti (JWT ID)":{"term":"jti (JWT ID)","def":"A unique identifier for a specific token (a JWT claim). It lets you put one token on a denylist to revoke it before its exp.","hook":"The serial number on a ticket: the ticket itself is genuine and signed, but the number is blacklisted at the door — and the usher won't let you in."},"Web|Token family (семья токенов)":{"term":"Token family","def":"A chain of refresh tokens linked by a shared family_id and descending from a single login; on reuse detection it is invalidated as a whole, not token by token.","hook":"Like a family surname: catch one family member forging, and the whole family falls under suspicion — they all get 'evicted' at once."},"Web|Идемпотентность HTTP-метода":{"term":"HTTP method idempotency","def":"A property of the method's contract whereby N identical requests leave the resource in the same final state as a single request (the responses may differ, e.g. 200 vs 404). It concerns server state, not the response body.","hook":"Like the 'floor 5' button in an elevator: press it ten times — you still arrive at the fifth floor, not the fiftieth."},"Web|Head-of-line blocking":{"term":"Head-of-line blocking","def":"A situation where the first item in a strictly ordered queue (a lost TCP segment) blocks processing of all subsequent ones, even if they're already delivered and logically independent","hook":"A queue at a single door: the stuck first person holds up everyone behind, though they're headed to different offices"},"Web|SameSite (атрибут cookie)":{"term":"SameSite (cookie attribute)","def":"A cookie attribute (Strict/Lax/None) telling the browser whether to send the cookie on cross-site requests — the primary built-in CSRF defense mechanism.","hook":"It's a 'door bouncer': Strict admits only insiders, Lax admits insiders and invited guests (top-level GET), None lets everyone in (needs extra security)."},"Web|Broken Access Control (A01)":{"term":"Broken Access Control (A01)","def":"OWASP Top-10 2021 #1 category: failure of checks that restrict an authenticated user to only the resources and actions they are permitted. Includes IDOR, horizontal/vertical privilege escalation, and bypass via URL/parameter tampering.","hook":"You have a plane ticket (authentication), but it must not let you into the cockpit (authorization)."},"Web|SBOM (Software Bill of Materials)":{"term":"SBOM (Software Bill of Materials)","def":"A machine-readable full inventory of all software components and their versions, including transitive dependencies (CycloneDX, SPDX formats). Gives an instant answer to 'do we have vulnerable component X, and exactly where?'","hook":"An invoice listing every ingredient in a dish — without it, when a sauce batch is recalled you can't tell which plates it ended up on."},"Web|IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference)":{"term":"IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference)","def":"A vulnerability where the app accepts an object identifier from the client and returns/modifies the resource without verifying that the current user is allowed to access it. A subclass of Broken Access Control (OWASP #1).","hook":"A coat check that hands over a coat for any ticket, without verifying the ticket is actually yours."},"Web|Dynamic Secret":{"term":"Dynamic Secret","def":"A credential that the secret manager (Vault) generates on request with a short TTL and revokes itself on expiry — instead of a static eternal password. Bound to a specific consumer and audited.","hook":"We don't hand out an eternal pass; we print a guest badge that self-destructs in an hour."},"Web|CORS-мисконфигурация":{"term":"CORS misconfiguration","def":"A CORS setup error where the server permits cross-origin reading of the response by untrusted origins — typically by reflecting the incoming Origin without a whitelist check together with Allow-Credentials: true, letting a foreign site read the victim's private data with her cookies.","hook":"A doorman logs a guest's name instead of checking the residents list — and lets anyone in with your keys."},"Web|User enumeration":{"term":"User enumeration","def":"A class of vulnerabilities letting an attacker determine whether an account exists by differences in the server's response: error text, HTTP code, length/structure of the response, or response time. It narrows the brute-force space.","hook":"The robber doesn't smash every box in a row — first, from the guard's hesitation, he works out which ones are actually occupied."},"JVM|Thread dump":{"term":"Thread dump","def":"A snapshot of all JVM threads at a point in time: name, id, state (RUNNABLE/BLOCKED/WAITING/TIMED_WAITING), call stack, and held/awaited monitors. Captured via jstack or kill -3.","hook":"A freeze-frame of a traffic jam: you can see who stopped where and who's waiting on whom."},"JVM|Retained Heap":{"term":"Retained Heap","def":"The amount of memory that becomes unreachable (and is freed by GC) when a given object is removed — i.e. the sum of its exclusive subtree in the dominator tree. Unlike shallow heap (the object's own size), retained shows the leak's true 'cost'.","hook":"Not 'how much the box weighs' but 'how much leaves the warehouse if you take that box away'."},"JVM|Safepoint-bias":{"term":"Safepoint-bias","def":"A systematic error in profilers that capture the stack only at a JVM safepoint: hot code with no internal safepoints (tight-loop, JIT-inlined) cannot be interrupted and gets attributed to the wrong method or lost entirely.","hook":"A guard logs only those who pass through the front gate; whoever climbed in through a window is absent from the stats."},"Spring|JPA Projection":{"term":"JPA Projection","def":"A way to select only a subset of an entity's fields into a separate read-only type (a DTO via JPQL constructor expression, or a Spring Data interface/class projection), bypassing loading the full managed entity. The result is detached: no dirty checking, no snapshots, no extra columns in the SQL.","hook":"Photograph the tag instead of moving the whole wardrobe."},"DevOps|W3C Trace Context (traceparent)":{"term":"W3C Trace Context (traceparent)","def":"A standard HTTP/messaging header of the form `00-{trace-id}-{span-id}-{flags}` that carries the trace identifier between services cross-vendor (OTel, Micrometer, proxies, clouds).","hook":"An end-to-end barcode on a parcel: every sorting hub reads it the same way and knows which batch it belongs to."},"Java|Constant variable":{"term":"Constant variable","def":"A final variable (including an instance field) initialized by a constant expression (literal/constant). The compiler inlines its value at each use site (constant-folding); the field itself may never be read at runtime — JLS 4.12.4.","hook":"final + literal = inlined, not a field read"},"Java|finalize()":{"term":"finalize()","def":"A protected Object method the GC called before reclaiming an object. @Deprecated(forRemoval=true, since=9); the mechanism is deprecated for removal in JEP 421 (Java 18). Still enabled by default, disabled via --finalization=disabled. Replacement: try-with-resources / Cleaner / PhantomReference.","hook":"a dead GC hook that, by default, is still breathing"},"Java|Виртуальная диспетчеризация (override)":{"term":"Virtual dispatch (override)","def":"Choosing the overridden method at runtime by the object's actual class via invokevirtual and the vtable. Unlike overload (static choice by argument types at compile-time) and static hiding (choice by the reference type).","hook":"The object decides at runtime — not the reference, not the compiler."},"Java|Treeification (HashMap)":{"term":"Treeification (HashMap)","def":"Turning a bin's chain into a red-black tree at ≥8 nodes and capacity≥64. Lookup on equal hashes: equals → Comparable(compareTo) → otherwise search both subtrees (O(n)). identityHashCode/tieBreakOrder — insertion only.","hook":"The tree saves you from O(n) only when keys are Comparable."},"Java|List.copyOf() short-circuit":{"term":"List.copyOf() short-circuit","def":"List.copyOf(src) returns an immutable copy: for a mutable source it allocates a new collection, but for an already-immutable (List.of-style) source it returns the same instance without copying.","hook":"Copies only when there's something to copy — an immutable source passes straight through."},"Concurrency|ForkJoinPool.commonPool()":{"term":"ForkJoinPool.commonPool()","def":"The JVM-wide work-stealing pool — the default Executor for CompletableFuture.*Async without an explicit Executor, parallel streams, and parallelSort. parallelism = cores-1, daemon threads. Blocking code in it starves the shared pool.","hook":"One pool for everyone — blocking it hits all its clients at once."},"Java|Полный барьер (stateful full-barrier op)":{"term":"Full barrier (stateful full-barrier op)","def":"An intermediate operation that must receive the ENTIRE input before emitting a single element (sorted). On an infinite source it hangs even with a limit below it. Contrasted with pull-based stateful ops like distinct(), which emit elements one at a time.","hook":"A turnstile that only lets a crowd through all at once: until the last person arrives, nobody passes. On an infinite queue — nobody ever passes."},"Java|downstream-коллектор":{"term":"downstream collector","def":"A nested Collector passed as the second arg to groupingBy: it folds each group (counting()→Long, mapping(fn,toSet())→Set) instead of the default List","hook":"groupingBy(byKey, counting()) — counting() IS the downstream, and it returns Long, not int"},"Java|Externalizable":{"term":"Externalizable","def":"A sub-interface of Serializable where the class writes/reads its state itself in writeExternal/readExternal. The stream still carries the class descriptor (name + serialVersionUID), but NOT field metadata or automatic value writing; on deserialization the public no-arg constructor is called, then readExternal.","hook":"Not \"no class metadata\" but \"no field metadata\" + a mandatory no-arg constructor."},"Java|Exception chaining (cause)":{"term":"Exception chaining (cause)","def":"Linking a new exception to the original via the cause (the (msg, cause) constructor or initCause) to preserve the root cause's type and stacktrace; printStackTrace prints it under Caused by:.","hook":"Pass the cause, don't glue its text."},"Java|Default charset trap":{"term":"Default charset trap","def":"Text APIs (new String(byte[]), getBytes(), FileReader, InputStreamReader, Scanner, PrintWriter) with no explicit charset use the default encoding. Before Java 18 that was the platform's native encoding → same code, different result across machines, and silent mojibake.","hook":"A byte doesn't know its own encoding — the reader picks it. Didn't pick explicitly = the default was picked for you."},"Spring|Propagation.NESTED":{"term":"Propagation.NESTED","def":"A transaction propagation where the inner @Transactional runs in the same physical transaction via a JDBC Savepoint: rolling it back undoes only changes made after the savepoint, leaving the outer transaction intact.","hook":"a savepoint inside the transaction, not a new transaction"},"Spring|@Transactional(readOnly=true)":{"term":"@Transactional(readOnly=true)","def":"A Spring transaction hint: switches the Hibernate Session to FlushMode.MANUAL (removes auto-flush and dirty-checking → CPU saving) and sets a read-only flag for replica routing. It does NOT make entities read-only and does NOT drop snapshot copies — no memory saving.","hook":"readOnly = MANUAL flush (CPU down), not read-only entities; the snapshot stays."},"Spring|Detached entity":{"term":"Detached entity","def":"An entity that was managed, but the persistence context was closed/cleared (or detach/evict was called). It has an id, but dirty checking no longer applies; persist throws EntityExistsException, remove throws IllegalArgumentException; the only way back to managed is merge (which returns a COPY).","hook":"A managed ghost: it has an id, but nobody tracks its changes anymore."},"Spring|flush vs commit":{"term":"flush vs commit","def":"flush — syncing the L1 cache with the DB (sending SQL over the open connection without committing, not durable). commit — flush + COMMIT (persisted, durable, visible to others).","hook":"flush = a draft on the printing press, commit = signing it off 'to print'."},"Spring|Hibernate proxy (getReference)":{"term":"Hibernate proxy (getReference)","def":"A lazy proxy subclass of the entity from getReference()/load(): only the PK is populated, the real SELECT is deferred until first non-id access. instanceof works on it (the proxy is a subclass), but getClass() returns the proxy subclass, not the entity class itself.","hook":"instanceof ok, getClass() lies"},"Spring|IDENTITY vs SEQUENCE для JDBC-батчинга":{"term":"IDENTITY vs SEQUENCE for JDBC batching","def":"IDENTITY forces Hibernate to execute each INSERT immediately to fetch the DB-generated key — batching is impossible. SEQUENCE with a pooled optimizer (allocationSize>1) hands out ids from a pre-allocated range with no round-trip, letting Hibernate accumulate INSERTs into one JDBC batch.","hook":"IDENTITY = \"key right now\", batch = \"in bulk later\" — mutually exclusive."},"Spring|FlushMode.MANUAL":{"term":"FlushMode.MANUAL","def":"A Hibernate session mode where flush runs only on an explicit flush() call: auto-flush before queries and at commit is off. Spring enables it for @Transactional(readOnly=true).","hook":"MANUAL = \"flush it yourself\": save()/dirty quietly pile up and vanish at commit, while an explicit flush() and DML do write."},"DevOps|p99 (99-й перцентиль)":{"term":"p99 (99th percentile)","def":"The latency value below which 99% of requests fall: p99=1s means every hundredth request waits ≥ 1s. Unlike the mean it exposes the tail; unlike Summary quantiles it aggregates correctly across instances only via histogram buckets.","hook":"The worst of a hundred. The mean hides it, and you can't average two p99s."},"Java|SIZED short-circuit в count()":{"term":"SIZED short-circuit in count()","def":"Since Java 9 count() on a SIZED pipeline returns the size via getExactSizeIfKnown() without traversing elements; intermediate Consumers (peek) are therefore never invoked.","hook":"count() reads the size off the label without opening the box — peek inside stays silent."},"Testing|Don't mock what you don't own":{"term":"Don't mock what you don't own","def":"GOOS principle: mock only your own abstractions (ports/interfaces); use foreign concrete types — value objects, DTOs, collections, library classes — for real instead of replacing them with mocks.","hook":"Mocking a foreign contract freezes a guess that will go stale."},"Web|SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery)":{"term":"SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery)","def":"A vulnerability where the attacker, via a controlled URL, makes the server issue a request on its own behalf — to internal services or cloud metadata unreachable from outside. OWASP Top-10 A10.","hook":"Pulling chestnuts from the fire with someone else's hands: a server with internal-network access fetches the address an attacker handed it."},"Spring|AutoConfiguration.imports":{"term":"AutoConfiguration.imports","def":"The file META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports (Spring Boot 2.7+), where a starter lists the FQNs of its auto-configurations, one per line. It replaced registration via spring.factories/EnableAutoConfiguration; required in Boot 3.","hook":"A dedicated list of 'what to enable automatically' instead of the shared spring.factories dump."},"Web|Realm (Keycloak)":{"term":"Realm (Keycloak)","def":"An isolated security domain ('tenant') with its own set of users, clients, roles, groups and token-signing keys; one Keycloak serves many realms, and a token from one realm is not valid in another.","hook":"An apartment building with its own lock on the entrance: tenants (users) and apartment-apps (clients) of one building aren't let into another with the same keys."},"Web|PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange)":{"term":"PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange)","def":"An OAuth2 authorization code flow extension: the client generates a random code_verifier, sends its hash (code_challenge, S256) in the authorization request, and presents the verifier itself when exchanging the code. An intercepted authorization code is useless without the verifier, which stays on the client. Mandatory for public clients, recommended for confidential too.","hook":"A one-time tamper seal on the delivery note: intercept the paper and you still can't redeem it — the original seal lives only with the courier."},"Spring|JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter":{"term":"JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter","def":"Spring Security's default converter turning a JWT into GrantedAuthority: it reads the scope/scp claim and prefixes it with SCOPE_. It knows nothing about Keycloak's realm_access.roles — roles require a custom converter.","hook":"A turnstile that can only read one type of pass (scope); for Keycloak passes (roles) it needs a different reader head."},"Web|Offline token":{"term":"Offline token","def":"A long-lived refresh token in Keycloak, requested with scope=offline_access; it survives logout/SSO-session close and is governed by Offline Session Idle/Max. It lets background daemons/batches obtain fresh access tokens without an active user browser session.","hook":"The night cash-courier's service pass — it works when the main entrance is already closed."},"Web|Audience (aud)":{"term":"Audience (aud)","def":"A JWT claim listing the intended recipients of the token; a resource server must confirm its own identifier is present in aud, otherwise the token must be rejected.","hook":"The address line on the envelope: the letter is genuine, but if it's not your address it's not for you."},"DevOps|Saturation (насыщение)":{"term":"Saturation","def":"How 'over-full' a resource is beyond utilization — queue depth, waiting, backlog. In USE/Golden Signals it's the leading indicator: the queue grows BEFORE utilization pins at 100% and errors start.","hook":"Not 'is the register busy' (utilization) but 'how long is the line to it' (saturation) — the queue warns you earlier."},"DB|Gorilla-сжатие":{"term":"Gorilla compression","def":"XOR compression of a time series' float64 values (Facebook, 2015): the current value is XORed with the previous one, only the differing bits are stored. Slowly changing metrics → nearly identical bits → ~1-2 bytes per point.","hook":"We don't rewrite the number from scratch — we record only how it differs from its neighbor. Similar neighbors = almost nothing to write."},"DB|Downsampling (прореживание)":{"term":"Downsampling","def":"Aggregating old time-series points into a coarser resolution (e.g. per-second → 5-minute min/max/avg). Cuts volume and speeds up long queries at the cost of losing fine detail in history.","hook":"You don't need last year's chart per second — squeeze it into a sparse grid: save space, keep the trend."},"DevOps|Recording rule":{"term":"Recording rule","def":"A Prometheus rule that periodically evaluates a PromQL expression and stores the result as a new time series. Materializes expensive aggregates — dashboards and alerts read the ready value instead of recomputing over raw data.","hook":"A materialized view for metrics: compute the heavy thing once on a schedule — everyone reads it cheaply afterward."},"System Design|remote_write":{"term":"remote_write","def":"A Prometheus protocol that streams scraped samples out to an external long-lived store (VictoriaMetrics/Thanos/Cortex). Local Prometheus stays a short buffer, while scale and retention move to the clustered backend.","hook":"Prometheus collects but doesn't hoard: raw samples flow down a pipe to a big warehouse that scales on its own."},"Java|Автобоксинг":{"term":"Autoboxing","def":"Automatic conversion of a primitive to its wrapper (int→Integer) and back. Boxing goes through Integer.valueOf(), which caches -128..127 — hence the == trap.","hook":"The compiler 'packs' a number into a box-object itself — and sometimes hands back the same box from the cache."},"Java|Пул строк (String pool)":{"term":"String pool","def":"A region in the JVM where unique string literals are kept. Identical literals reuse one object; new String() makes a copy bypassing the pool.","hook":"A shared shelf of identical strings: literals take the ready one, new String prints a redundant duplicate."},"Java|Перегрузка (overloading)":{"term":"Overloading","def":"Several methods with one name but different parameter types; which to call is decided by the compiler from argument types. remove(int) vs remove(Object) is the classic trap.","hook":"One name — different doors; the compiler picks the door by the type of what you passed."},"Concurrency|Атомарность":{"term":"Atomicity","def":"The property of an operation running wholly and indivisibly — it can't be interrupted mid-way. count++ is NOT atomic (it's 3 steps), so in multithreading you need AtomicInteger or synchronized.","hook":"All or nothing: another thread can't slip into the middle of an indivisible action."},"Java|BigDecimal":{"term":"BigDecimal","def":"A class for exact arbitrary-precision decimal arithmetic. For money it's built from a string (new BigDecimal(\"0.1\")), since from a double it inherits binary floating-point inexactness.","hook":"An exact decimal calculator with no double 'tails' — but you must feed it a string, not a double."},"Java|Fail-fast итератор":{"term":"Fail-fast iterator","def":"An iterator that, on structural change to the collection during traversal, immediately throws ConcurrentModificationException (comparing modCount) instead of silently returning a broken result. Best-effort, not a guarantee.","hook":"A walker with a 'seal': change the collection around it and the seal breaks — it shouts at once instead of lying silently."},"Concurrency|RejectedExecutionHandler":{"term":"RejectedExecutionHandler","def":"A thread pool's policy when a bounded queue overflows: reject with an exception (AbortPolicy), run in the caller thread (CallerRunsPolicy — backpressure), drop the oldest, etc.","hook":"What to do when the queue is full: honestly refuse or slow the sender down — but don't grow to OOM."},"Java|Сокрытие метода (hiding)":{"term":"Method hiding","def":"A same-named static method in a subclass doesn't override but hides the parent's: the call resolves by reference type at compile time, with no dynamic dispatch. Unlike override for instance methods.","hook":"static isn't 'really overridden' — which to call is decided by the reference type, not the live object."},"Java|Числовое продвижение (promotion)":{"term":"Numeric promotion","def":"Coercion of operands to a common numeric type before an operation. In a ternary both branches reduce to one type (Integer+Double→double), and a null wrapper gets unboxed and may give an NPE.","hook":"Both branches must be one type: mix int and float and everything becomes float; mix with null and it crashes."},"DevOps|Code review":{"term":"Code review","def":"A review of your code by another developer before it gets merged into the shared branch.","hook":"Like a proofreader before a book goes to print: someone else reads your text and catches typos.","more":"**Under the hood:** another developer reads your diff in the MR/PR, leaves comments, requests changes, or approves. The goal isn't to \"catch you out,\" but to: find bugs before production, spread knowledge of the code across the team, and keep a consistent style.\n\n**Pitfalls:**\n- huge reviews (>400 lines) get rubber-stamped — split them into smaller ones;\n- review carefully yourself, argue on the merits, but don't take comments as a personal attack;\n- describe the context in the PR, otherwise the reviewer wastes time reverse-engineering your logic."},"DevOps|MR / PR":{"term":"MR / PR","def":"A request to merge your changes into the main branch, which first goes through review.","hook":"Like a request to \"let my changes into the shared project\" — first they check it, then they merge.","more":"**MR** (Merge Request, GitLab) and **PR** (Pull Request, GitHub) are the same thing: a request to \"merge my branch into the target one.\" Under the hood it's a comparison of two branches plus a trigger for **CI** (tests, linters, build) and for **code review**.\n\n**Why it matters:** quality control and an integration point for changes. Pitfall: keep the PR small and focused on a single task — a large PR takes long to review and accumulates merge conflicts. Don't merge with a red CI or without an approval."},"DevOps|Бэклог":{"term":"Backlog","def":"The shared list of tasks and features that aren't done yet and are waiting their turn.","hook":"Like a shopping list: a pile of items in a queue, worked top to bottom by priority.","more":"**Under the hood** the backlog is a prioritized queue of work: features, bugs, tech debt, ideas. It splits into the product backlog (everything) and the sprint backlog (taken into the current sprint). The owner of priorities is usually the Product Owner.\n\n**Why it matters:** a single source of truth about what to do next, so you don't keep it in your head and in chats. Pitfall: an ungroomed backlog turns into a dump of hundreds of dead tickets — it needs regular **grooming** (refinement): removing stale items and clarifying tasks."},"DevOps|Техдолг":{"term":"Tech debt","def":"The accumulated cost of quick fixes in the code: it works, but it complicates further development until you rework it.","hook":"Like a loan for speed: it \"works\" now, but you pay the interest in future rework.","more":"**Under the hood** tech debt is the gap between \"how we did it quickly\" and \"how it should have been done.\" Hacks, duplication, missing tests: the code works, but every subsequent change gets more expensive. The debt metaphor: you \"borrowed time\" now and pay \"interest\" — slowdown — later.\n\n**Pitfalls:** debt can be deliberate (you knowingly cut a corner to meet a deadline — fine, if you logged it) or accidental (from ignorance — bad). Don't let it pile up silently: record it in the backlog, otherwise in a year the system becomes scary to touch."},"DevOps|Спринт":{"term":"Sprint","def":"A short working interval (usually 2 weeks) in which the team completes a set of tasks.","hook":"Like a 2-week run: at the start a batch of tasks, at the finish a finished result.","more":"**Under the hood** a sprint is a fixed timebox (most often 2 weeks) from Scrum: the team takes a set of tasks from the backlog and commits to closing them. Inside: planning at the start, a daily every day, a demo and retro at the end.\n\n**Why it matters:** rhythm and predictability — the customer sees a result every 2 weeks, and the team doesn't drown in an endless task. Pitfall: packing the sprint to the brim — any surprise blows up the plan. Don't change the sprint goal on the fly — it breaks focus."},"DevOps|Дейли":{"term":"Daily","def":"A short daily team meeting: what you did, what you're doing, what's blocking you.","hook":"Like a morning huddle over coffee: yesterday, today, what's stuck — in 10 minutes.","more":"**Under the hood** the daily (daily standup) is a 10–15 minute meeting, done standing, where everyone answers three questions: what I did, what I'll do, what's blocking me. It's synchronization, not a report to a manager.\n\n**Pitfalls:**\n- don't turn it into a detailed report — take details and problem-solving into a discussion afterward;\n- raise blockers honestly and immediately, that's the whole point;\n- if it runs 40 minutes, the format is broken and the team is wasting time."},"DevOps|Ретро":{"term":"Retro","def":"A meeting at the end of a sprint where you discuss what went well and what to improve.","hook":"Like a post-game analysis after a match: what worked, what didn't — so next time is better.","more":"**Under the hood** a retrospective is a meeting at the end of a sprint about the process itself, not the code. You go over: what went well, what went badly, what to change. The outcome is 1–3 concrete action items with owners for the next sprint.\n\n**Why it matters:** a mechanism for continuous team improvement (kaizen). Pitfall: a retro without actions = an empty chat; you need a safe (blameless) atmosphere, otherwise people stay silent about real problems and only discuss harmless ones."},"DevOps|Эстимейт":{"term":"Estimate","def":"An estimate of how much time or effort a task will take, used to plan the sprint.","hook":"Like the \"time to home\" in a navigator — a rough guess of the time to plan the task.","more":"**Under the hood** an estimate is a forecast of effort. Often not in hours, but in **story points** (relative complexity) on the Fibonacci scale (1,2,3,5,8…) — more accurate, because people are bad at estimating absolute time but decent at comparing \"this task is twice as hard as that one.\"\n\n**Pitfalls:** an estimate is a probability, not a promise; build in uncertainty. The bigger the task, the larger the error — break large ones into smaller. Don't turn an estimate into a deadline commitment under pressure."},"DevOps|Дефинишн оф дан (DoD)":{"term":"Definition of Done (DoD)","def":"A list of conditions under which a task is considered truly done, not just \"kind of finished.\"","hook":"Like a furniture-assembly checklist: until every box is ticked it's \"not done,\" even if it's \"almost.\"","more":"**Under the hood** the DoD is a team-wide \"actually done\" checklist: code written, tests present, review passed, merged, deployed to stage, documentation updated. It applies to all tasks equally.\n\n**Why it matters:** it removes the \"but I did do it\" argument — there's an objective criterion that rules out hidden tech debt and unfinished work. Pitfall: don't confuse it with **acceptance criteria** (conditions for a specific task — what exactly must work); the DoD is about process quality, AC is about the feature's content."},"DevOps|Хотфикс":{"term":"Hotfix","def":"An urgent small fix rolled out to production out of turn to put out a bug.","hook":"Like calling an ambulance: something's on fire in production — you rush to fix it out of turn right now.","more":"**Under the hood** a hotfix is an urgent, narrow production fix that bypasses the normal cycle. In Git-flow it's a separate branch off the production tag, a minimal diff, an expedited (but not skipped) review, and a mandatory merge back into the main branch so the fix isn't lost in the next release.\n\n**Pitfalls:** under pressure it's easy to introduce a new bug — change only what's necessary. Always add a test that reproduces the problem. Frequent hotfixes are a symptom of holes in testing and the release process, not the norm."},"DevOps|Релиз":{"term":"Release","def":"Rolling out a finished version of a service to production, where real customers use it.","hook":"Like putting a book on sale: a new version heads out to real users.","more":"**Under the hood** a release is rolling a version out to production, where real customers use it. Today this is automated via a **CD** pipeline with risk-reduction strategies: blue-green, canary (part of the traffic on the new version), feature flags.\n\n**Why it matters:** to deliver value to users in a controlled way with the option to roll back. Pitfall: Friday releases before the weekend are a classic source of pain; keep versions, DB migrations, and a rollback plan ready. The smaller and more frequent the releases, the lower the risk of each one."},"DevOps|Мёрдж-конфликт":{"term":"Merge conflict","def":"When your changes and someone else's touch the same spot in a file and Git can't merge them itself — you choose manually.","hook":"Two people write different things on the same line of a notebook — you can't keep both, so you pick whose words stay.","more":"**Under the hood:** Git merges branches line by line (3-way merge): it compares both versions against a common ancestor (base). If the edits are in different places, it stitches them automatically. But if **the same lines** are changed differently in both branches, Git can't guess whose version is right, and marks the spot with markers `<<<<<<<`, `=======`, `>>>>>>>`.\n\n**Why it matters:** protection against losing code — better to stop and ask a human than to silently overwrite someone else's work.\n\n**Pitfalls:**\n- Don't leave markers in the code — the compiler/tests will fail.\n- After resolving manually — `git add` + `git commit` (or `--continue`).\n- Frequent conflicts = you rarely merge main or your branches are too large. Cured by frequent small PRs and `rebase`. You can bail out with `git merge --abort`."}},"fp":{"stack-heap":{"title":"Stack and heap","g":"Memory","hook":"The stack is a tray dispenser in a cafeteria: take and return in order. The heap is a shared warehouse, everything in a pile, cleaned up by a janitor (GC).","more":"**Stack** — per thread: call frames with local variables and references; grows and shrinks with calls, very fast, freed automatically when a method returns.\n\n**Heap** — shared across the whole JVM: this is where all objects live, managed by the GC. The stack holds only the reference, the object itself lives on the heap.\n\nWhy: this split gives speed for transient data and managed lifetime for objects. Pitfalls: deep recursion → `StackOverflowError`; too many live objects → `OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space`.","caps":["Each method has its own frame on the stack — the stack grows upward.","A local primitive sits right in the frame — the number itself.","The new Cat object itself lives on the heap — a large shared memory area.","The frame holds not the object but a REFERENCE — an arrow from the stack into the heap.","The method returned — its frame is popped, the reference from the stack is gone.","Nothing points to the object — it is unreachable, the GC will collect it 🗑."]},"metaspace":{"title":"Metaspace","g":"Memory","hook":"Metaspace is the spec sheets for all your machines (classes), stored in a separate storeroom outside the main shop floor, growing as new machines arrive.","more":"**Metaspace** stores class metadata (structure, methods, bytecode) in native OS memory rather than on the heap. It replaced PermGen in Java 8.\n\nWhy: PermGen had a fixed size and often overflowed; Metaspace grows dynamically and is freed when classloaders are unloaded.\n\nPitfalls: by default there is almost no upper bound — a classloader leak (frequent redeploys, dynamic class generation, proxies) leads to `OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace`. Cap it with `-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize`.","caps":["The heap stores application objects — this is the familiar on-heap memory.","Metaspace is a separate native JVM memory area, it is NOT part of the heap.","Every loaded class puts its metadata into metaspace.","An OOM Metaspace is not an OOM heap: a different, native region overflowed.","A metaspace leak = uncontrolled growth of classes and classloaders."]},"reference-types":{"title":"Strong / Soft / Weak references","g":"Memory","hook":"Strong — you hold its hand; Soft — you let go in a crowd when it gets crowded; Weak — already let go, blown away by the wind.","more":"**Strong** (an ordinary reference) — as long as it exists, the object is not collected. **Soft** — the GC removes it only under memory pressure, handy for caches. **Weak** — removed on the very first collection if there are no strong references (`WeakHashMap`).\n\nThere is also **Phantom** — for finalization/resource cleanup via `ReferenceQueue`, without access to the object.\n\nWhy: to control lifetime and build caches that don't leak. Pitfalls: a soft cache may unexpectedly clear under load; a weak one, conversely, may disappear too early.","caps":["An object stays alive as long as there's a reference to it from the stack — the GC leaves it alone.","Strong: an ordinary reference holds tight — the GC walks past, the object stays.","Soft: lives as long as there's enough memory — for caches; collected only under pressure.","Weak: on the next GC the object is swept away — that's how WeakHashMap cleans itself.","Phantom: the object is already collected and unreachable; the reference only signals to clean up afterward.","The weaker the reference, the weaker the hold: from \"won't give it up\" to \"cleanup after death\"."]},"string-intern":{"title":"String.intern()","g":"Memory","hook":"intern() is a shared closet: identical strings go into one slot instead of breeding copies.","more":"`intern()` puts a string into the **string pool** (on the heap) and returns the canonical reference: strings with equal content point to a single object. Literals are interned automatically, `new String()` is not.\n\nWhy: it saves memory across many duplicates and enables fast comparison by `==` instead of `equals`.\n\nPitfalls: manual `intern()` is rarely justified — it's expensive (hash lookup, synchronization), and the pool can't be cleared. Mass-interning unpredictable strings hurts performance; rely on automatic interning of literals.","caps":["Three identical \"Astana\" strings — three different objects on the heap, memory wasted.","There's one shared cabinet — the string pool, where the canonical copy lives.","s.intern() changes nothing on the heap — it RETURNS the canonical reference from the pool.","Memory is freed only if the variables point to the canonical one and the old objects are abandoned.","Bonus: after intern() identical strings are equal by == , not just equals.","Don't intern everything: the pool hash fills up, lookups slow down, GC struggles under load."]},"gc-stw":{"title":"Stop-the-world","g":"Garbage collection","hook":"Stop-the-world is the janitor turning off the office lights: everyone freezes while they sweep.","more":"**Stop-the-world** is a pause when the JVM halts all application threads so the GC can safely walk the objects in a consistent state (nobody moves references while the collector runs).\n\nWhy: some phases (for example, final marking or moving objects) require that the object graph not change.\n\nPitfalls: long STW pauses = noticeable freezes (critical for latency-sensitive services). Modern collectors (G1, ZGC, Shenandoah) minimize pauses by doing most of the work **concurrently**, in parallel with the application.","caps":["The program runs, threads spin, and garbage piles up on the heap 🗑.","The heap filled up — the JVM decides: time to call the garbage collector (GC).","⏸ Stop-the-world: ALL threads freeze for the duration of the collection.","The threads are stopped, and while they're stopped the GC walks the heap and removes the dead.","The garbage is cleared, the heap is free — the threads come back to life and run on ✓.","Don't call System.gc() manually — you'll trigger an extra STW pause."]},"gc-generational":{"title":"Young and Old generations","g":"Garbage collection","hook":"Young is a nursery with high turnover; Old is a retirement home: whoever survived lives long.","more":"The heap is split into **Young** (Eden + two Survivor spaces) and **Old**. New objects are born in Eden; those that survive a collection move to Survivor, then to Old. Collecting Young (minor GC) is fast, Old (major/full) is expensive.\n\nWhy: the **generational hypothesis** — most objects die young. Cleaning a small Young space often is cheaper than cleaning the whole heap.\n\nPitfalls: short-lived objects that make it to Old (for example, due to large size or early promotion) litter it and trigger expensive full GCs.","caps":["New objects are born in Young, in the Eden space.","Most objects die young — Eden fills up with garbage.","Minor GC is fast: it copies only the few live objects, leaving the dead untouched.","Survivors move to Survivor space and age there over several collections.","Survive the collection threshold — promoted to Old as a long-lived object.","Old grows slowly: major GC is rare but heavy and slow."]},"g1-regions":{"title":"G1: regions","g":"Garbage collection","hook":"G1 is a warehouse of identical boxes: it clears the ones with the most garbage first.","more":"**G1** divides the heap into many equal **regions** (~1–32 MB), each dynamically playing the role of Eden, Survivor, or Old. The collector keeps statistics and collects the regions with the most garbage first — hence the name Garbage-First.\n\nWhy: instead of contiguous Young/Old zones, flexible partitioning gives **predictable pauses** (the target is set with `-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis`) and works on large heaps.\n\nPitfalls: objects larger than half a region are **humongous**, placed separately and collected poorly; an abundance of them fragments the heap and triggers full GC.","caps":["The old GC sees the heap as one big slab — it cleans the whole thing at once.","G1 cuts that same heap into many small, equal regions.","Each region has its own percentage of garbage — G1 marks them during scanning.","Garbage First: we take the most garbage-filled regions first — maximum payoff.","G1 cleans exactly as many regions as fit within the target pause.","The heap is huge, yet the pauses are small and predictable — G1 keeps them steady."]},"gc-roots":{"title":"GC roots and reachability","g":"Garbage collection","hook":"GC roots are anchors: whatever the chain of threads doesn't reach gets thrown out.","more":"The GC starts from the **roots (GC roots)**: local variables on thread stacks, static fields, JNI references, active threads. From them it walks the reference graph — everything **reachable** is considered live, the rest is garbage.\n\nWhy: this is more reliable than reference counting — it correctly collects even **cycles** (A→B→A with no external references is unreachable and removed).\n\nPitfalls: leaks in Java are objects accidentally reachable from a root (a forgotten entry in a static map, an unremoved listener). Memory isn't freed as long as there's a path from a GC root.","caps":["GC roots: thread stack frames, references from static fields, JNI — the start of the traversal.","From the roots the GC follows references and marks everything reachable; whatever isn't touched is gray.","Frame a() was popped off the stack — the last reference to B is broken.","B and C reference each other, but there's no path from a root — both are garbage (not a counter!).","The reachable lives, the unreachable is freed — no matter how many references exist between them."]},"mem-leak":{"title":"Memory leak under GC","g":"Garbage collection","hook":"A leak is a forgotten box in the attic: you hold a reference, so the GC has no right to throw it out.","more":"The GC removes only **unreachable** objects (no chain of references from GC roots: stack, statics, threads). A leak is an object that's logically no longer needed but is still reachable, so the GC won't touch it.\n\nWhy it matters: the classic causes are growing static collections (`Map`/`List`), ThreadLocal without `remove()`, unclosed resources, listeners/callbacks without unsubscription, an unbounded cache.\n\nPitfalls: the symptom is steadily growing heap and increasingly frequent Full GCs, ending in `OutOfMemoryError`. You hunt them down with a heap dump and a dominator tree (MAT). Don't confuse it with growing but bounded consumption.","caps":["Heap: the GC removes only the unreachable — whatever can't be reached from a GC root.","A static field is itself a GC root: everything it holds is reachable forever.","add() is there, remove() is not — the collection swells, references hold everything.","The objects are live but unneeded — the GC is powerless, the heap creeps upward.","Same trap: a live object holds a reference, but you forgot to unsubscribe.","ThreadLocal: the pool thread is eternal, the value isn't cleared — it hangs until remove()."]},"race":{"title":"Race condition","g":"Basics","hook":"Two people grab the same notebook and write at once — the result depends on who got there first.","more":"**Race condition** — the result depends on the unpredictable order of thread execution. The classic case: `count++` is three operations (read-modify-write); two threads read the same value and one increment is lost.\n\nWhy: without synchronization, the JVM/CPU guarantee neither the atomicity of compound operations nor ordering. \"check-then-act\" is a race too.\n\nGotcha: the bug is intermittent, reproduces rarely, and never under a debugger. Fix: `synchronized`, `AtomicInteger`/CAS, immutability, or confining the data to a single thread (confinement).","caps":["A counter in shared memory equals 5 — we expect it to become 7.","Without a lock, both threads read the same value 5.","Each thread locally computes 5+1=6.","Both write 6 over the top — the second overwrites the first.","Final result is 6, but we expected 7 — one increment was lost."]},"deadlock":{"title":"Deadlock","g":"Basics","hook":"Two people in a narrow hallway: each waits for the other to step aside — and they stand there forever.","more":"**Deadlock** — threads wait for each other forever. Thread A holds lock L1 and requests L2, thread B holds L2 and requests L1 — neither releases, both are blocked permanently.\n\nCondition for it to occur: nested locks acquired in a **different order**. That's why nothing happens, there is no progress.\n\nGotcha: it shows up under load, and a thread dump (`jstack`) reveals the circular wait. Prevention: acquire locks in a single global order, use `tryLock` with a timeout, minimize lock nesting. Related troubles: livelock and starvation.","caps":["Two locks and two threads, and each one needs both locks.","A acquires lock1, B acquires lock2 — so far so good.","Now A waits for lock2, but B is holding it.","And B waits for lock1 — but A is holding it. The cycle is closed.","Both stand forever: deadlock, neither releases first.","Fix: acquire locks in the same order, or use tryLock with a timeout."]},"atomicity-visibility":{"title":"Atomicity ≠ visibility","g":"Basics","hook":"You wrote the whole note at once (atomic), but your neighbor still sees the old page (no visibility).","more":"Two distinct guarantees. **Atomicity** — the operation runs as a whole, with no interleaving (for example, `count++` is NOT atomic). **Visibility** — a change made by one thread will be seen by others.\n\nWhy they're separate: a value may be cached in a register or core cache, so another thread reads stale data. `synchronized` provides both atomicity and visibility; `volatile` — only visibility (no atomic read-modify-write).\n\nGotcha: `volatile boolean flag` is fine for a stop signal. But `volatile int` for a counter does NOT save you from a race. For counters — use `AtomicInteger` or a lock.","caps":["Two threads work with the same field in memory.","Visibility: T2 sees the fresh value, not its own cached stale copy.","Atomicity: count++ is three steps, and you can be interrupted between them.","volatile gives visibility, but two threads still lose one increment.","synchronized/Atomic: the lock makes the operation indivisible, the second thread waits.","Bottom line: volatile = visibility, synchronized/Atomic = visibility + atomicity."]},"synchronized-monitor":{"title":"synchronized and the monitor","g":"Basics","hook":"synchronized — one key to the restroom: you go in, lock the door, everyone else waits in line.","more":"Every object has a **monitor** (intrinsic lock). `synchronized(obj)` acquires the monitor on entry and releases it on exit (including via an exception); only one thread holds it at a time.\n\nWhy: it provides mutual exclusion AND memory guarantees — on entry you see others' writes, on exit you publish your own (happens-before). It's reentrant: the same thread can enter again.\n\nGotcha: locking on `this` or a public object is dangerous — external code may synchronize on it too; better to use a private `final Object lock`. Don't synchronize on a `String` or a boxed `Integer` — they are shared.","caps":["Every object has its own monitor — a built-in lock.","A thread enters synchronized and acquires the lock.","While T1 holds the lock — the other threads wait.","One inside the block — that is exactly mutual exclusion.","On exit the lock is released — a waiting thread enters.","unlock→lock provides visibility: whoever takes the lock sees the fresh x."]},"volatile":{"title":"volatile: visibility","g":"Memory model","hook":"volatile — a shared notice board: you post it, everyone sees the fresh value at once, with no cache in their head.","more":"`volatile` forbids caching the variable in a register: every read goes to shared memory, every write is immediately visible to others. It also forbids reordering operations around the access.\n\nWhy: it cheaply provides **visibility** without locking. The typical case — a `volatile boolean running` flag to stop a thread.\n\nGotcha: it does NOT provide atomicity of compound operations — `i++` on a volatile is still a race. It does not replace `synchronized` where you need a complete \"read-modify-write\". For counters and invariants spanning several fields — use Atomic classes or a lock.","caps":["Thread A writes flag=true, but the value stays with it for now.","B reads the stale false and spins in the while loop forever.","Without volatile there's no happens-before: the JIT keeps flag in B's register.","volatile: writes and reads go straight to shared memory.","Now B sees true immediately and exits the loop.","But volatile won't save i++: read and write are two steps, a race."]},"happens-before":{"title":"happens-before","g":"Memory model","hook":"happens-before — a receipt: first you put it down, then you say so — the order is guaranteed for everyone.","more":"**happens-before** — a formal rule of the JMM: if action A happens-before B, then the result of A is guaranteed to be visible in B. Without such a relationship, ordering and visibility between threads are not guaranteed.\n\nWhere the relationships come from: exit from `synchronized` → a subsequent entry; a `volatile` write → its read; `Thread.start()`; `Thread.join()`; within a single thread — program order.\n\nWhy: this is the foundation that explains why `volatile` and `synchronized` \"work\". Gotcha: without an explicit hb relationship, the compiler and CPU are free to reorder and cache — hence intermittent bugs with no visible logic.","caps":["Two threads write to shared memory — who sees what is not guaranteed.","happens-before is an arrow: if A→B, then everything from A is visible in B.","unlock happens-before lock of THE SAME lock m — the data arrives.","A volatile write happens-before a volatile read of the same field.","Thread.start happens-before the first action of the new thread.","No happens-before arrow — no guarantee: you may see the old value."]},"double-checked-locking":{"title":"Double-checked locking","g":"Memory model","hook":"You peek in the fridge: empty? — then lock the kitchen and check once more before cooking.","more":"A lazy-initialization pattern: check `instance == null` without a lock, and only if it's null do you enter `synchronized` and check again. The goal — to avoid paying for the lock after initialization.\n\nThe key gotcha: the field MUST be `volatile`. Otherwise, due to reordering, another thread may see a **non-null but not fully constructed** reference (the object isn't initialized yet) — the classic broken DCL prior to Java 5.\n\nWhen to use: rarely — usually the holder idiom is simpler (a static nested class is initialized lazily and thread-safely by the JVM itself) or an `enum` singleton.","caps":["One singleton for everyone. Two threads see at once: it doesn't exist yet (null).","First check WITHOUT a lock: if it already exists — return instantly, no slowdown.","INSTANCE is empty — take the 🔒 lock. Only one thread gets in, the second waits.","Under the lock — the SECOND check: maybe someone already created it. If not — build the object.","Without volatile, T2's first check sees the reference, but the fields are still empty — garbage 🗑.","volatile forbids reordering: the fields are ready BEFORE the reference is published ✓."]},"thread-pool":{"title":"Thread pool","g":"Threads","hook":"A crew of movers: tasks sit in a queue, the number of movers is fixed — one frees up and takes the next.","more":"A **thread pool** reuses a fixed set of threads: tasks are placed in a queue, and a free thread picks up the next one. Creating a thread is expensive (memory for the stack, a syscall), and the pool amortizes this and limits parallelism.\n\nWhy: resource control — we don't spawn thousands of threads and protect against overload. `ThreadPoolExecutor` is parameterized by core/max size, the queue, and a rejection policy.\n\nGotcha: an unbounded queue → memory growth and OOM; a pool that's too small + blocking tasks → starvation/deadlock. It's dangerous to block a pool thread on the result of a task from the same pool. Sizing: CPU-bound ≈ number of cores, IO-bound more.","caps":["A flood of work arrives — 1000 tasks, all wanting to run.","Spawning 1000 threads — each eats memory, the CPU drowns in context switches.","Solution: a pool of N threads — we use just 4 workers 🧵.","Excess tasks aren't lost — they wait their turn in the buffer.","A thread frees up — it takes the next task from the queue and is reused.","Bottom line: 4 threads chewed through 1000 tasks, the load is under control."]},"vthreads":{"title":"Virtual threads (Java 21)","g":"Threads","hook":"Freelance couriers: you can hire a million of them, one waiting for a reply sleeps for free without taking up a staff slot.","more":"**Virtual threads** (Java 21) — lightweight threads managed by the JVM, not the OS. Many virtual threads are mapped onto a few carrier platform threads. On blocking IO, a virtual thread **unmounts**, freeing the carrier.\n\nWhy: write simple blocking code but hold millions of concurrent tasks — a \"thread per request\" model without the cost of OS threads. A replacement for the complex reactive style for IO-bound work.\n\nGotcha: they don't speed up CPU-bound work. `synchronized` in older JDKs could pin the carrier thread — `ReentrantLock` is preferable. There's no need to pool them — create one per task.","caps":["Ordinary threads are heavy: the OS holds few of them, and on blocking IO they sleep.","Virtual threads are cheap: you can spawn them by the millions.","Millions of virtual threads ride on a handful of carriers (real OS threads).","On blocking IO the virtual thread unmounts from the carrier — the slot is freed.","The free carrier immediately picks up another ready virtual thread.","Bottom line: the scale of reactive, but the code is simple and blocking."]},"threadlocal-leak":{"title":"ThreadLocal in a pool","g":"Threads","hook":"A mover never took someone else's note out of his pocket — picked up a new task and mixed it up with the previous one.","more":"**ThreadLocal** stores a value in the `Thread` object itself, in its `threadLocals` map, where the key is a weak reference to the ThreadLocal itself and the value is a strong one. In a pool, threads don't die but are reused, so a stored value lives exactly as long as the worker thread lives.\n\nHence two classes of bugs: a **memory leak** (the value holds a large object and isn't collected by the GC) and **data leaking** between unrelated requests (the next task reads someone else's value).\n\nRule: always clean up via `remove()` in a `finally`, rather than relying on `set`-ting a new value. Especially critical in Tomcat / servlet pools.","caps":["The pool holds few threads and cycles them around between requests.","Alice's request landed on T1 and put her userId into a ThreadLocal.","The request finished, but remove() wasn't called — the value stayed hanging.","The same T1 picks up Bob's request — and reads Alice's userId, which isn't his.","Fix it like this: always clean the ThreadLocal in finally via remove()."]},"concurrenthashmap":{"title":"ConcurrentHashMap","g":"Threads","hook":"Lockers in a changing room: each with its own lock — everyone opens their own at once, no shared queue.","more":"**ConcurrentHashMap** provides thread safety without locking the whole map. Since Java 8 there are no segments: a write to an empty bucket goes through CAS, and on a collision only the head of that specific bucket is locked (`synchronized` on the node). Reads have no locks at all — the fields are `volatile`.\n\nWhy: read operations scale linearly, and write conflicts touch only one bucket, not the whole structure.\n\nGotcha: `null` keys/values are forbidden; compound operations (`get`+`put`) are not atomic — use `compute`/`merge`/`putIfAbsent`. The iterator is weakly consistent: it doesn't throw `ConcurrentModificationException`, but it also doesn't guarantee a snapshot.","caps":["A map is a cabinet of bucket cells, with keys spread across them.","A synchronized map: one 🔒 on the WHOLE map — threads wait in line.","A queue of one: you can't even touch different cells at the same time.","ConcurrentHashMap: into an empty bucket we put via CAS, with no 🔒 at all.","Different buckets → threads write in parallel, no shared queue.","The 🔒 is taken only on a collision — on the bucket's head node, the rest is free."]},"di-lifecycle":{"title":"Bean lifecycle","g":"DI and the container","hook":"A new hire: you onboard them, issue a badge, run an orientation, sit them down to work, and on the way out they hand back their keys.","more":"A bean goes through phases: instantiation (constructor) → dependency injection (setters/fields) → `BeanPostProcessor.before` → `@PostConstruct`/`afterPropertiesSet`/`init-method` → ready to serve → on container shutdown `@PreDestroy`/`destroy-method`.\n\nWhy this order: by the time `@PostConstruct` runs, all dependencies are already injected, so this is the safe place to initialize — not the constructor.\n\nPitfalls: `@PreDestroy` is not called on **prototype** beans (the container doesn't track them) nor on `kill -9`. Don't put heavy logic in the constructor — dependencies aren't there yet.","caps":["The container takes the class as a blueprint and molds a bean object from it.","The container injects the dependencies the bean needs into it.","BeanPostProcessor wraps @PostConstruct — final setup before going live.","The bean is ready — it sits in the container and serves requests.","On context shutdown @PreDestroy fires and the bean is destroyed.","Spring wires beans at startup; Quarkus does it ahead of time at build."]},"cglib-config":{"title":"@Configuration under CGLIB","g":"DI and the container","hook":"A factory with a single machine: no matter how many times you order the part, it hands back the same instance, not a new one each time.","more":"Spring wraps a `@Configuration` class in a **CGLIB subclass**. It intercepts calls to `@Bean` methods: when such a method is called again from within the config, it returns the already-created singleton from the container rather than a new object.\n\nWhy: so that `serviceA()` and `serviceB()`, both calling `repo()`, get the same bean. This is exactly `proxyBeanMethods = true`.\n\nPitfall: if you set `proxyBeanMethods = false` (faster startup, as in Spring Boot lite), there's no proxy — each direct call to a `@Bean` method creates a new instance. In that case wire dependencies only through method parameters.","caps":["Spring doesn't take @Configuration as-is — it wraps it in a CGLIB proxy.","Inside, @Bean foo() directly calls another @Bean bar().","Without the proxy each bar() call would mint a new object — duplicates.","The proxy intercepts bar(): the first time it creates it and puts it in the container.","After that every bar() call returns the ready bean from the container — no new.","Any caller inside the config gets the SAME singleton."]},"circular-dep":{"title":"Circular dependency","g":"DI and the container","hook":"Two people in a doorway: 'after you' — 'no, after you' — and both are stuck, nobody gets through.","more":"A circular dependency — A needs B, and B needs A. With **constructor injection** Spring can't create either one and fails with `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException`.\n\nFor **setter/field** injection a three-level cache saves the day: Spring places a \"raw\" (not yet fully initialized) instance of A as an early reference, creates B with a link to that A, then finishes building A. That's why field cycles often \"just work\" unnoticed.\n\nBetter to eliminate than to patch: a cycle is usually a sign of poor separation of responsibilities. Workarounds are `@Lazy` or extracting the shared logic into a third bean.","caps":["Bean A wants B, and bean B wants A — a loop.","Field/setter: Spring creates A and puts an early reference into the cache.","Creating B — it picks up the early reference to A from the cache.","B is injected into A — both are assembled, the loop is broken ✓.","Constructor injection: A can't be created without B and vice versa.","A closed loop of constructors → Spring fails with an error ✗."]},"bean-scopes":{"title":"singleton vs prototype","g":"DI and the container","hook":"Singleton — one shared water cooler per floor; prototype — a disposable cup for each newcomer.","more":"**singleton** (the default) — one instance per container, created at startup, alive for the whole lifecycle. **prototype** — a new object on every `getBean()`/injection request.\n\nKey trap: a prototype injected into a singleton is created exactly once — at the moment the singleton is assembled, and after that it's the same reference. To get a new one each time you need `ObjectProvider`, `@Lookup`, or a scoped proxy.\n\nAlso: for prototypes Spring does not call `@PreDestroy` and does not manage their finalization — that's entirely on you. Singleton beans must be thread-safe (stateless).","caps":["Singleton: the container creates the object once and hands everyone the same reference.","Prototype: a new object on every request from the container.","Trap: a singleton asks for a prototype once at assembly and keeps that same reference.","Call it again and again — inside it's still the very same single copy.","Fix: a scoped proxy or Provider hits the container on every call.","Result: set up correctly — a new object on every call again ✓."]},"proxy-self":{"title":"Self-invocation","g":"AOP and proxies","hook":"You call a colleague on the internal line, bypassing the secretary — and the stamp-wielding security never checked you.","more":"**Self-invocation** — calling a method of the same class via `this.method()`. Annotations like `@Transactional`, `@Cacheable`, `@Async` work through a proxy: interception happens only when the call comes from outside, through the bean wrapper.\n\nWith `this.foo()` you reach the bare object bypassing the proxy, so the aspect simply doesn't fire — the transaction/cache/async is silently ignored.\n\nFix: move the method into another bean, or self-inject (`@Autowired` of itself / `ObjectProvider`), or use `AopContext.currentProxy()`. This is a common \"silent\" bug — the code compiles, but there's no transaction.","caps":["The proxy wraps the bean from outside, like a case — everyone goes through it.","An external call goes through the proxy → @Transactional fires.","Inside, methodA calls this.methodB() — bypassing the proxy, directly.","this is the bean itself, not the proxy: the case is sidestepped.","The advice on methodB didn't fire → @Transactional is silently ignored.","Fix: call via a neighbor bean or self-inject the proxy."]},"jdk-vs-cglib":{"title":"JDK proxy vs CGLIB","g":"AOP and proxies","hook":"JDK — a mask matching a contract role; CGLIB — a look-alike heir copying the actual person.","more":"A **JDK dynamic proxy** works only through interfaces: it generates a class implementing the same interfaces and delegates calls to an `InvocationHandler`. **CGLIB** creates a subclass of the target class and overrides its methods — no interface needed.\n\nSpring chooses: if there's an interface — JDK proxy, if not — CGLIB. Since Spring Boot 2.x, CGLIB is forced by default (`proxyTargetClass=true`) for consistency.\n\nPitfalls: CGLIB can't proxy `final` classes/methods or `private` ones; the object is created without invoking a constructor. A JDK proxy can only be cast to the interface, not the concrete class — otherwise `ClassCastException`.","caps":["Spring doesn't hand back your bean directly — it slips you a proxy wrapper.","There's an interface — the JDK proxy implements the same interface and wraps the bean.","No interface — CGLIB generates a subclass of your class.","The subclass overrides the method, intercepts the call, and calls super.","But final can't be inherited — on a final class/method CGLIB fails.","Result: interface → JDK, class → CGLIB, final → no way."]},"aop-common":{"title":"One proxy mechanism","g":"AOP and proxies","hook":"The same gatekeeper at the entrance: logging, transactions, and caching alike — the mechanics of entry are one and the same.","more":"Transactions, caching, `@Async`, custom aspects, `@Validated`, security — all of these are **one proxy mechanism** of Spring AOP. A proxy (JDK or CGLIB) is built around the bean, wrapping the call in a chain of interceptors (advice).\n\nWhy understand this: since the mechanism is shared, all these features have the same limitations — self-invocation isn't caught, it doesn't work on `private`/`final`, and the aspect applies only to container beans.\n\nConsequence: if \"the transaction didn't fire,\" \"it wasn't cached,\" \"`@Async` ran synchronously\" — the cause is almost always the same: the call bypassed the proxy.","caps":["Spring doesn't call your bean directly — it hides it behind a proxy.","The proxy is a shared wrapper: this is where @Transactional @Async @Cacheable @PreAuthorize live.","An external call goes through the proxy — the wrapper fires, all good.","But this.method() is an internal call bypassing the proxy: the wrapper didn't fire.","And final also kills the proxy — it can't be inherited/wrapped.","Result: one proxy for all four — fix self-invocation and drop final."]},"tx-rollback":{"title":"@Transactional rollback rules","g":"Transactions","hook":"A store receipt: something failed unexpectedly — you get your money back; you changed your mind yourself (checked) — keep the goods.","more":"By default `@Transactional` rolls back only on **unchecked** exceptions (`RuntimeException`, `Error`). Checked exceptions do NOT trigger a rollback — the transaction commits even if the method threw an `Exception`.\n\nWhy: it's a historical EJB convention — checked is treated as an \"expected business situation,\" unchecked as a \"failure.\"\n\nPitfalls and control: `rollbackFor = Exception.class` to roll back on checked, `noRollbackFor` for the opposite. Another trap: if you catch the exception inside the method and don't rethrow it — there's no rollback; and a rollback in a nested transaction with the same proxy marks the whole transaction `rollback-only`.","caps":["Three writes happen inside one transaction of the method.","A RuntimeException was thrown — Spring rolls everything back, the DB is empty.","A checked Exception was thrown — by default Spring COMMITS.","Result: whatever was written before the exception stays in the DB. Partial data.","Fix: rollbackFor=Exception.class — rollback on ANY Exception."]},"requires-new":{"title":"REQUIRES_NEW","g":"Transactions","hook":"A separate safe: whatever collapses at your neighbor's, your lock clicked on its own.","more":"**REQUIRES_NEW** suspends the current transaction and starts a new, physically separate one. It has its own commit/rollback: the inner one commits or rolls back independently of the outer.\n\nWhy: to write an audit log or counter so that it's saved even if the main operation fails and rolls back.\n\nPitfalls: each such transaction holds a **separate connection** from the pool — two active connections on one thread. When called from the same bean via `this.method()` the proxy won't fire — you need self-injection or a call from outside. It's easy to get a deadlock if the outer and inner transactions contend for the same row.","caps":["The outer transaction is open and has written A (not yet committed).","REQUIRES_NEW pauses the outer one and takes a new connection.","The inner one writes B and commits itself — B becomes durable.","The outer one resumes on conn-1 but fails with an error.","Rolling back the outer removes A; B is already committed and stays."]},"tx-threadlocal":{"title":"A transaction lives on the thread","g":"Transactions","hook":"A transaction is a wristband on the mover's arm: switch to another thread — the wristband didn't transfer, the link is lost.","more":"Spring stores the active connection/transaction in a **ThreadLocal** (via `TransactionSynchronizationManager`). So all code running on the same thread inside `@Transactional` sees the same connection and the same transaction.\n\nWhy: to avoid dragging the Connection through every parameter — any DAO pulls it from the thread's context automatically.\n\nPitfalls: switch to **another thread** (a new `Thread`, `@Async`, parallelStream, reactive) — the transaction is not carried over there, and the code runs without one. Hence LazyInitializationException in async tasks and lost commits.","caps":["Opened a transaction — Spring puts its connection into the thread's ThreadLocal.","All code on this thread sees ONE transaction — it pulls it from the ThreadLocal.","One thread — one transaction: all operations go into a shared commit.","You launched @Async — that's ANOTHER thread, with its own empty ThreadLocal.","parallelStream splits the work across threads — the transaction does NOT reach there.","Result: the TX is bound to the thread; switch threads and it didn't transfer."]},"n-plus-1":{"title":"N+1 queries","g":"Data / JPA","hook":"You bought a box of apples, but you run to the store for each one separately — 1 list + 100 trips.","more":"**N+1** is when you load a list of N entities with one query, and then for each one the ORM fires another query for the association (a `lazy` collection). That's 1 + N queries instead of one or two.\n\nCause: lazy associations are resolved on access, one per element. For 1000 orders — 1001 queries to the DB.\n\nFix: `JOIN FETCH` in JPQL, `@EntityGraph`, batch fetching (`@BatchSize` / `hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size`), or a DTO projection. **The main thing is to see the problem**: turn on SQL logging and count the queries on a typical scenario.","caps":["We take a list of orders — that's exactly ONE SQL query to the database.","Each order has a lazy client — untouched for now.","Touch an order's client — a separate query flies off by its id.","For N orders — N client queries, plus the first: N+1 in total.","Fix: JOIN FETCH or @EntityGraph — one query with a join.","Orders and clients arrive together in one trip to the database."]},"lazy-init":{"title":"LazyInitializationException","g":"Data / JPA","hook":"The waiter went home, and only now do you decide to finish your meal — the kitchen (session) is already closed.","more":"**LazyInitializationException** flies when you access a lazy association after the Hibernate session is closed. The collection proxy wants to go to the DB, but the session/transaction is gone — there's nothing to load the data with.\n\nTypical case: you returned an entity from a `@Transactional` service, and in the controller/template you called `order.getItems()`.\n\nThe right way: load what you need inside the transaction — `JOIN FETCH`, `@EntityGraph` — or return a **DTO** instead of an entity. OSIV masks the error, but that's treating the symptom, not the cause.","caps":["Entity Order, and inside it the items list is marked LAZY — not loaded immediately.","Within the transaction the persistence context is open — the lazy association can be loaded.","The method returned Order outward — the transaction closed, the context died.","Outside we touch the lazy list — there's nothing left to load it → exception.","Fix: load what's needed while still in the transaction — JOIN FETCH or straight into a DTO.","We hand a ready DTO outward — nothing to be lazy about, no exception."]},"osiv":{"title":"Open Session In View","g":"Data / JPA","hook":"Leaving the fridge open the whole lunch so you can grab seconds without getting up — handy, but it burns a resource.","more":"**Open Session In View** keeps the Hibernate session open for the entire HTTP request — via a filter/interceptor, not just for the duration of the service-layer transaction. As a result, lazy associations get loaded even in the controller and the view.\n\nWhy it was enabled: to avoid LazyInitializationException without rewriting code. In Spring Boot it's on by default.\n\nPitfalls: lazy loading in the web layer hides **N+1**, holds a connection from the pool longer than needed (risk of pool exhaustion under load), and SQL runs outside the transaction. On serious projects OSIV is disabled (`spring.jpa.open-in-view=false`) and data is loaded explicitly.","caps":["Request: the service fetched the order, but the line items are lazy and not loaded yet.","Without OSIV: the session is closed, the view touches a lazy field and fails.","OSIV keeps the persistence context open until the response is done.","Now lazy fields in the view load silently — no error, and it's on by default.","Cost 1: a pooled connection stays held until rendering finishes.","Cost 2: each item in the view triggers its own SELECT — a hidden N+1."]},"dirty-checking":{"title":"Dirty checking","g":"Data / JPA","hook":"A sharp butler who notices you rearranged things and records the changes without being told.","more":"**Dirty checking**: when an entity is loaded, Hibernate takes a snapshot of its fields. On flush/commit it compares the current state against the snapshot and generates an UPDATE for the changed fields itself — a `setXxx()` is enough, no explicit `save()` needed.\n\nWhy: you write business logic as if working with plain objects, and the ORM syncs them to the database for you.\n\nPitfalls: editing an entity inside a transaction will hit the database even if you never called save — it's easy to trigger an \"accidental\" UPDATE. For read-only work use `@Transactional(readOnly=true)`: snapshots aren't kept, which is cheaper on memory and avoids stray updates.","caps":["Load a row: Hibernate returns a managed object and stashes a snapshot copy.","You change a field in memory — just a setter, no save() call, the DB is untouched.","On flush Hibernate compares the object against the snapshot field by field.","If they differ, Hibernate builds the UPDATE itself — you never wrote it.","Touch a managed object by accident — and you get an unplanned UPDATE in the DB.","Bottom line: dirty checking = autosave for managed rows, no save() needed."]},"autoconfig":{"title":"Autoconfiguration","g":"Boot & config","hook":"A fully furnished apartment: move in and everything's already in place — but you can still move it around.","more":"Spring Boot **autoconfiguration**: at startup it scans `AutoConfiguration` classes (from `META-INF/spring/...imports`). Each is guarded by `@Conditional` — for example `@ConditionalOnClass` (a driver is on the classpath) and `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` (you haven't declared the bean yourself).\n\nThe idea: \"convention over configuration\" — if a dependency is present, default beans appear. Add `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` and you get a DataSource, EntityManager, and transactions.\n\nPitfalls: the \"magic\" is non-obvious. To dig in, use `--debug` (the Conditions report) and `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`: your own bean overrides the auto-bean. Properties are set in `application.yml`.","caps":["Add a starter — it transitively pulls in the JAR and a list of auto-configs.","Each auto-config is a config class guarded by two conditions.","Condition #1: is the class on the classpath? No driver — the config stays dormant.","Condition #2: did you define the bean yourself? Then the auto-bean stays out.","Both conditions met — Boot creates the bean for you.","Want it your way — drop in your own bean, and Boot steps aside."]},"filter-chain":{"title":"Security filter chain","g":"Security","hook":"Airport gates one after another: passport, ticket, screening — fail one and you go no further.","more":"Spring Security is a **chain of servlet filters** (`FilterChainProxy` → `SecurityFilterChain`). A request passes through the filters in order: extracting authentication, checking authorization, handling exceptions, and so on — before it ever reaches the controller.\n\nWhy: security is decoupled from business logic and applied uniformly at the web layer for every endpoint.\n\nPitfalls: **order is critical** — the authorization filter must come after authentication. Multiple `SecurityFilterChain`s are matched by `securityMatcher`, and their priority matters. 403 vs 401: the former means no permission, the latter means not authenticated.","caps":["In front of the controller sits a filter chain — a wall of checks.","Filter 1: authentication — who are you? We verify the login and password.","Filter 2: the identity is placed in the context — the system knows who arrived.","Filter 3: authorization — are you allowed in here? We check permissions.","Passed all three — the request reaches the controller.","Failed a check — stop, it never reaches the controller."]},"jwt-validate":{"title":"JWT validation","g":"Security","hook":"A hologram on a ticket: you check the seal and the date without calling the box office to confirm.","more":"**JWT validation** on the resource server: the token is three base64 parts `header.payload.signature`. The server verifies the **signature** with the issuer's public key (JWKS from a configured URL), then `exp` (not expired), `iss`/`aud` (the expected issuer and audience).\n\nWhy: stateless verification — no round trip to Keycloak on every request, and the signature guarantees the token wasn't tampered with.\n\nPitfalls: a JWT can't be revoked before it expires — keep the lifetime short + use refresh. Keys rotate: select by `kid` from JWKS and cache with refresh. Never trust the payload without verifying the signature and `alg` (the `alg:none` attack).","caps":["Keycloak signs the token with its private key — it stamps a seal.","The service fetches the public key from JWKS once and caches it.","The client sends a request with the token — the service verifies everything locally.","We check the signature with the key and the exp/iss/aud claims — locally, no network.","Expired or forged — an immediate 401, no further access.","Token valid — we read the roles from the payload and let it through."]},"btree-leftmost":{"title":"Leftmost prefix","g":"Indexes","hook":"A phone book sorted by last name + first name: searching by first name alone is useless — you'd have to scan the whole thing.","more":"**Leftmost prefix**: a composite B-tree index `(a, b, c)` is physically sorted first by `a`, then by `b` within equal `a`, then by `c`. So it only works from the **left edge**: for `a`, `a+b`, `a+b+c`.\n\nWhy: the tree is ordered like a multi-level sort — without a value for `a`, there's no way to know where to look for `b`.\n\nGotcha: a query on `b` or `c` alone won't use the index (or only partially). `a + c` uses only the `a` part; `c` is filtered separately. That's why **column order** in the index is decisive: put first the column most often used with equality and with higher selectivity.","caps":["Index on two fields: sorted first by client_id, then by date.","client_id is primary: equal values sit next to each other in tight groups.","WHERE client_id=2 → matching rows are neighbors, the index jumps straight to the group.","But there's no global ordering by date — within the index they're interleaved.","WHERE date=07 → matches aren't on the edge, the index can't seek to them.","Rule: an index is read left to right — without the first field it doesn't work."]},"covering-index":{"title":"Covering index","g":"Indexes","hook":"Everything you need is right in the table of contents — no need to open the book (table) itself.","more":"A **covering index** contains every column the query needs right inside the index itself — via key columns plus `INCLUDE(...)`. The planner does an **index-only scan**: it reads the data straight from the B-tree and never touches the base table (heap).\n\nWhy: heap access means random disk reads, the most expensive part. Removing it speeds up SELECTs many times over, especially on wide tables.\n\nGotcha: in Postgres an index-only scan works only if the page is \"visible to all\" per the visibility map; otherwise it still hits the heap — so after bulk updates you need a fresh `VACUUM`. And don't stuff extra columns into `INCLUDE` — the index bloats and slows down writes.","caps":["The query asks for only two columns: name and city.","A regular index stores only the key and the row address.","The key is found, but city isn't in the index — a jump to the table.","Such a jump for every row is extra work.","A covering index keeps city inside itself — everything's together.","The answer comes straight from the index — index-only scan, no table."]},"keyset-pagination":{"title":"Keyset vs OFFSET","g":"Indexes","hook":"A \"continue from here\" bookmark instead of recomputing the first 10,000 pages every time.","more":"**OFFSET** forces the DB to read and discard every row up to the offset: `OFFSET 100000` scans 100k rows for nothing. On deep pages it degrades linearly.\n\n**Keyset** (the seek method) paginates by value: `WHERE (created_at, id) < (:last_ts, :last_id) ORDER BY ... LIMIT 20`. Using the index, the DB jumps straight to the right point — O(log n), with speed independent of depth.\n\nWhy: stability and speed on large lists and infinite scroll. Gotcha: you need a deterministic composite sort key (add `id` as a tie-breaker), and you can't jump to \"page N\" — only forward/backward from the cursor.","caps":["You need a page: 20 rows, but deep — after row 100,000.","OFFSET 100000 reads all 100,000 rows and THROWS them away.","The deeper you page, the more rows OFFSET reads for nothing.","Keyset remembers the last 🔑 id and jumps via the index.","The index descends to the target id — the tail isn't read.","Keyset: same cost on page 1 and on page 5000."]},"index-not-used":{"title":"Why the index isn't used","g":"Indexes","hook":"An index in a book is useless if you need half the pages — easier to just flip through the whole thing.","more":"The planner picks an index by **cost**, not \"because it exists.\" Common reasons it's skipped: a function/cast over the column (`WHERE lower(email)=...` needs an expression index), low selectivity (many rows returned → a seq scan is cheaper), type mismatch, the leading column of a composite index not in the predicate.\n\nWhy: on a large result set, a sequential heap read beats thousands of random index jumps.\n\nGotcha: stale statistics after a bulk insert — run `ANALYZE`. Check the actual plan with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`, not guesses. `LIKE '%x'` with a leading `%` can't use the index.","caps":["The query will return almost the whole table — tons of matching rows.","Via the index, that's tons of random jumps to rows on disk.","A seq scan reads everything straight through in one smooth pass — no jumps.","The planner estimates cost from statistics: jumps cost more than a pass.","Cheaper means chosen: a full scan here isn't a bug, it's a deliberate plan."]},"isolation-levels":{"title":"Isolation levels","g":"Transactions and locking","hook":"Wall thickness between neighbors: from hearing everything (cardboard) to total silence (concrete).","more":"The isolation level determines which concurrent-transaction anomalies are visible. The standard ones: **Read Committed** (you see only committed data), **Repeatable Read** (a snapshot for the whole transaction), **Serializable** (as if transactions ran one after another).\n\nWhy: a trade-off between correctness and concurrency — the stricter it is, the fewer anomalies but the more conflicts/rollbacks.\n\nGotcha: the default in Postgres is Read Committed, where lost update and non-repeatable read are possible. In Postgres, RR/Serializable use an MVCC snapshot and throw `serialization_failure` — the code **must** be able to retry the transaction. The level names match, but guarantees differ across databases.","caps":["One account is read by two queries of transaction T1 — between them someone edits it.","READ COMMITTED: 100 the first time, 120 after the other commit.","REPEATABLE READ: T1 takes a start snapshot — both reads return 100.","Row versions: T1's snapshot holds the old 100, the new 120 lives separately.","SERIALIZABLE: the outcome is as if T1 then T2 — otherwise one is rolled back.","Bottom line: higher level — fewer anomalies, but costlier concurrency."]},"write-skew":{"title":"Write skew","g":"Transactions and locking","hook":"Two doctors each left their shift, each having seen the other still on duty — and nobody was left covering.","more":"**Write skew** — two transactions read the same set of rows, each checks an invariant, sees \"all good,\" and writes to **different** rows. Individually correct, together the invariant is broken.\n\nThe classic: the rule \"at least one doctor on duty.\" Two read \"there are two of us\" at the same time, each takes themselves off — leaving zero on duty.\n\nWhy: snapshot isolation (Repeatable Read) doesn't see others' uncommitted writes and doesn't lock the rows it reads. The fix is **Serializable** (Postgres SSI rolls one back), or an explicit `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` lock / materializing the conflict through a shared lock row.","caps":["Invariant: always ≥ 1 doctor on shift. Right now there are two — all good.","Both want to leave. Each reads count = 2 and decides: allowed.","Each sees a snapshot BEFORE the other's UPDATE — so it ignores the colleague.","Each UPDATE removes ITS OWN doctor — different rows, no conflict.","Result: 0 doctors. The invariant is broken — that's write skew.","Snapshot lets both through. Only Serializable rolls one back."]},"lost-update":{"title":"Lost update","g":"Transactions and locking","hook":"Two people editing one document: whoever saves last overwrites the first one's edits.","more":"**Lost update**: transaction A reads a value, B reads the same, both compute from the old value and write — A's update is overwritten by B's. The classic read-modify-write on a counter/balance.\n\nWhy it happens: at Read Committed there's no lock on the row you read, and between read and write another transaction manages to change it.\n\nHow to avoid: an atomic update in a single statement `SET x = x + 1` (the DB locks the row itself); or pessimistic `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`; or optimistic via `version`/`WHERE x = :old`. Gotcha: read-modify-write in application code is almost always vulnerable — don't pull the value into Java to compute it there.","caps":["Two threads read the same balance = 100.","Both compute from the same old 100 — independently.","T1 writes 110, then T2 writes 95 — on top.","T1's +10 simply vanished — that's a lost update.","The cure: attach a version to the row and read it too.","T1 writes with v=7→8; T2's version is no longer 7 → rollback and retry."]},"mvcc":{"title":"MVCC","g":"Transactions and locking","hook":"A cafe with orders: everyone sees their dish as it was when ordered, the cook prepares a new one, the old one is cleared from the table later.","more":"**MVCC** (multiversion concurrency control): on UPDATE the row isn't overwritten — a new version is created; the old one lives on as long as other transactions can see it. Each version has `xmin`/`xmax`, and a transaction works with a **snapshot** — it sees the versions valid as of its moment.\n\nWhy: **readers don't block writers** and vice versa. A SELECT doesn't wait for an UPDATE — a huge concurrency win with no read locks.\n\nGotcha: dead tuples pile up → you need `VACUUM`, otherwise bloat. A long transaction holds old versions and blocks cleanup. An UPDATE is physically costlier than it looks — it's an insert plus marking the old row.","caps":["Version 1 of the row: balance=100. A reader is looking at it.","A writer does UPDATE → 120, without touching the old version.","The reader holds snapshot v1=100, while a new v2=120 already lives alongside.","The reader sees its snapshot v1=100, the writer is already on v2=120.","Nobody blocks anybody: read and write run in parallel.","The cost of MVCC: old versions pile up as dead — VACUUM cleans them."]},"optimistic-pessimistic":{"title":"Optimistic vs pessimistic","g":"Transactions and locking","hook":"The optimist grabs a spot without booking and checks at the door; the pessimist locks the stall with a key in advance.","more":"**Pessimistic** locking: you grab the row up front (`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`), and others wait. A guarantee without rollbacks, but stalls and deadlocks are possible; good when conflicts are frequent.\n\n**Optimistic**: no locks; on write you check `version`/`updated_at` (`WHERE version = :v`); if it doesn't match, someone beat you to it — you get 0 rows and retry. In JPA this is `@Version` → `OptimisticLockException`.\n\nWhen to use which: conflicts rare and transactions short → optimistic (less contention). Conflicts frequent or expensive to recompute → pessimistic. Optimistic gotcha: a retry handler is mandatory, otherwise the user hits an error.","caps":["Two transactions target the same row — someone has to yield.","Optimistic: nobody locks, the row has a version.","On commit the DB checks version: T1 matched — it writes v2.","T2 commits with v1, but it's already v2 — conflict, retry with the new version.","Pessimistic: SELECT FOR UPDATE puts a 🔒 on it immediately, others wait.","Few conflicts — optimistic; frequent fights — pessimistic."]},"vacuum-bloat":{"title":"VACUUM and bloat","g":"PostgreSQL and types","hook":"A fridge swells with expired food: VACUUM throws out the rot, otherwise the shelves are jammed with empty jars.","more":"Because of MVCC, deleted/updated rows remain as **dead versions**. `VACUUM` marks their space as free for reuse; **autovacuum** does this in the background based on thresholds. It also updates the visibility map (for index-only scans) and prevents transaction ID wraparound.\n\n**Bloat** is the swelling of tables and indexes with dead versions: disk space is occupied, scans read \"emptiness,\" and queries slow down.\n\nGotcha: a plain `VACUUM` doesn't return space to the OS — only `VACUUM FULL` does (but it takes a heavy lock). Long transactions and replicas with `hot_standby_feedback` interfere with cleanup. Frequently updated tables need a more aggressive autovacuum.","caps":["MVCC: an UPDATE creates a new row version, the old one remains.","DELETE doesn't erase the row — it just marks the version dead.","Dead versions pile up in pages — the table doesn't shrink by itself.","Bloat: few live rows, yet the file on disk swells.","VACUUM passes through and marks dead slots as free within the table.","The file stays the same size, but the slots are reused for new rows."]},"numeric-money":{"title":"Money: NUMERIC not FLOAT","g":"PostgreSQL and types","hook":"Measure money with a graduated ruler (NUMERIC), not by eye (FLOAT): the cents will leak through the cracks.","more":"`FLOAT`/`DOUBLE` is binary floating point: 0.1 isn't representable exactly, and errors accumulate when summing. For money that's unacceptable — the cents \"drift apart.\"\n\n**NUMERIC/DECIMAL** stores a number exactly in decimal form with a given precision and scale (`NUMERIC(19,4)`), with arithmetic free of rounding loss. In Java use `BigDecimal`, never `double`.\n\nWhy: financial amounts must reconcile to the cent and pass an audit. Gotcha: NUMERIC is slower and heavier than float, but for money that's the right trade-off. Set rounding explicitly (`RoundingMode`), fix the scale, and don't mix currencies in one column without a separate currency field.","caps":["0.1 is simple in decimal, but in binary it's an infinite fraction.","Float stores only a truncated fraction — exact 0.1 doesn't fit.","0.1 + 0.2 yields not 0.3 — the crumb of error is already visible.","A thousand additions in double accumulate a tail — you don't get exactly 100.00.","NUMERIC and BigDecimal compute in decimal — the cent is exact.","Rule: money — only NUMERIC in the DB and BigDecimal in Java."]},"select-star":{"title":"The harm of SELECT *","g":"PostgreSQL and types","hook":"SELECT * is \"bring everything from the pantry\" when you only need the salt.","more":"`SELECT *` pulls **all columns**, even unneeded ones: extra disk I/O, network traffic, and application-side memory. It hits especially hard on wide tables and BLOB/TEXT fields you don't even read.\n\nA separate pain is **covering indexes**: if you request only the needed columns, the DB can answer straight from the index without touching the table itself. `*` breaks that. Plus the query plan is unstable when the schema changes.\n\nGotcha: add a column and `INSERT ... SELECT` or positional ORM mapping breaks. Rule: **list explicitly** the columns you actually need.","caps":["You need 2 fields, but SELECT * pulls ALL — extra load.","Extra columns clog the network and server memory.","A query for two fields reads straight from the index — we don't touch the table.","SELECT * needs bio — the index isn't enough, we go into the table: an extra trip.","SELECT * silently pulls a new column — positional column mapping breaks.","List the columns you need — fast, stable, and the index works."]},"eav":{"title":"The EAV anti-pattern","g":"PostgreSQL and types","hook":"EAV is a closet with no shelves: everything dumped into one key-value pile, and then you spend an hour hunting for a sock.","more":"EAV (Entity-Attribute-Value) stores data not in columns but as rows of the form `(entity, attribute_name, value)`. A single table describes any property of any object — flexibility at the cost of a proper data model.\n\nWhy people reach for it: when attributes are numerous and not known in advance (product catalogs, form builders). But you pay for it: to assemble one object with N fields you need N joins or a row pivot, and you lose **types and constraints**, while indexes work poorly.\n\nPitfalls: queries turn monstrous, validation drifts into application code, and the database no longer protects your data. Use it only for the genuinely dynamic parts; keep the core of the schema in **proper columns** or use `JSONB`.","caps":["Properly: attributes are columns, each with its own type.","EAV: everything crammed into one key-value table as rows.","Everything became a string: types, NOT NULL and checks are lost.","One product is no longer a row, but a pile of joins.","JSONB: flexibility without losing the single row and its indexes."]},"kpart":{"title":"Partitions and the key","g":"Kafka","hook":"The key is the address on the envelope: one recipient always lands in the same partition-mailbox.","more":"A Kafka topic is split into **partitions** — independent, ordered logs. The producer picks a partition from the key: `hash(key) % partition_count`. Messages sharing a key always land in the same partition.\n\nWhy it matters: ordering is guaranteed **only within a partition**, and consumption parallelism is capped by their count (one consumer per partition in a group). The key is the unit of ordering and sharding — e.g. `orderId`, so that events for one order stay in sequence.\n\nGotchas: **changing the partition count after the fact hurts** — the key-to-partition mapping shifts. A bad key (too few distinct values or skew) produces hot partitions and uneven load.","caps":["A topic isn't one pipe — it's several partitions side by side.","Got a key? It gets hashed, the count divides it, one partition falls out.","A given key always flies to the same partition — all its messages stay together.","Inside a partition ordering is strict — we read 1, 2, 3 as written.","With no key, messages scatter across partitions — there's no global order.","Bottom line: order holds only within a partition, not across the whole topic."]},"acks":{"title":"acks=all","g":"Kafka","hook":"acks=all is shipping a parcel and waiting for everyone to sign, not just the courier at the door.","more":"The Kafka producer's `acks` setting decides when a write counts as successful. `acks=all` makes the leader wait for acknowledgement from every **in-sync replica (ISR)** before answering the producer. That's the strongest guarantee against data loss.\n\nWhy: with `acks=1` only the leader acknowledges, and if it dies before replication the message is lost. `acks=all` together with `min.insync.replicas>=2` guarantees the write survives a broker failure.\n\nGotchas: higher latency, lower throughput. Without `min.insync.replicas`, `acks=all` alone is weaker than it looks. For exactly-once you add `enable.idempotence=true`. You trade speed for durability.","caps":["The producer sends a message to the partition leader.","The partition has a leader and two in-sync replica copies.","acks=all: the leader copies the write to every in-sync replica.","Once all ISR confirm — only then does the leader send the ack.","Leader dies — a replica that holds the data becomes the new leader.","acks=1 is faster, but if the leader dies before the copy — data loss."]},"rebalance":{"title":"Group rebalance","g":"Kafka","hook":"A waiter walks out — the tables get reassigned: while they reshuffle, nobody serves the guests.","more":"A topic's partitions are distributed across the consumers of a single group. A **rebalance** is the reassignment of partitions when membership changes: a consumer joins or drops, a heartbeat is missed, the partition count changes.\n\nWhy: this is how the group scales and survives failures — freed partitions get picked up by the live consumers. The group coordinator runs the protocol, and during a **stop-the-world** rebalance consumption stalls.\n\nGotchas: frequent rebalances mean downtime and reprocessing. Causes — a slow `poll()` (exceeding `max.poll.interval.ms`), missed heartbeats. Fixes: the **cooperative-sticky** assignor (incremental rebalance) and careful timeouts. Don't do heavy work between polls.","caps":["The topic is broken into partitions — strips of messages.","The group splits the partitions: within a group, 1 partition → 1 consumer.","We add C4 or a consumer dies — group membership changed.","A rebalance runs: for that window processing in the group freezes.","Partitions are reassigned to the new membership, processing resumes.","Frequent rebalances = frequent pauses, lag grows — that's the pain."]},"consumer-offset":{"title":"Offset and commit","g":"Kafka","hook":"The offset is a bookmark: don't commit it and tomorrow you start the same chapter over.","more":"An offset is a message's position in a partition. The consumer tracks how far it has read by **committing the offset** to the internal `__consumer_offsets` topic. After a restart the group resumes from the committed position.\n\nThe key is the moment of commit. **At-least-once**: process, then commit (a crash can cause a replay). **At-most-once**: commit, then process (a crash can cause a loss). Auto-commit commits on a timer, which is treacherous: it can acknowledge something not yet processed.\n\nGotchas: commit before processing and you lose data; commit after and you get duplicates. So disable `enable.auto.commit`, commit manually after success, and make processing **idempotent**.","caps":["A partition is a tape of records, each with its own offset; I read from committed=2.","I take the record at the current offset and process it.","Processed — and ONLY then I commit: committed moves to 3.","Crashed BEFORE the commit — committed stayed at 2.","After restart I read from committed=2 — record 2 gets processed twice.","Rule: commit AFTER processing — a duplicate beats a loss."]},"log-compaction":{"title":"Log compaction","g":"Kafka","hook":"Compaction is a phone book: keep only the contact's latest number, erase the old entries.","more":"A regular topic is cleaned by time or size (retention). **Log compaction** instead keeps at least the **latest value for each key**, dropping older versions of the same key. The topic turns into a journal of latest states.\n\nWhy: perfect for snapshots/changelog — \"the current client profile by `clientId`\". A new consumer can re-read the topic and rebuild the current state without the full history. This is how `__consumer_offsets` and Kafka Streams state stores work.\n\nGotchas: a message with a key and `value=null` is a **tombstone** — it marks the key for deletion. A non-empty key is required; intermediate values are lost, so compaction is unfit for auditing history.","caps":["A regular topic is a tape: all versions of a key sit one after another.","One key is overwritten again and again — the stale ones pile up.","A cleaner walks the log and marks the outdated versions of a key.","Old versions of each key are dropped — the latest one remains.","Result: the topic holds the latest value per key — a key→value snapshot.","Not an event tape, but a live table of the state \"right now\"."]},"outbox":{"title":"Transactional outbox","g":"Patterns","hook":"You drop the letter in the same box as the journal entry: either both are saved, or neither is.","more":"The problem: you can't atomically write to the DB and send an event to Kafka — two different resources, no distributed transaction. **Transactional outbox**: in the same DB transaction you write both the business data and an event row into an `outbox` table.\n\nWhy: the DB transaction is atomic, so the event is saved exactly when the fact is saved. Then a separate process (a poller or **CDC/Debezium** off the WAL) reads `outbox` and publishes to the broker, marking what's been sent.\n\nGotchas: delivery is **at-least-once** — duplicates are possible, so the consumer must be idempotent. You need an index on status and table cleanup. This is exactly `archive_outbox` in document-service.","caps":["The DB and Kafka are two different systems; one transaction can't span both.","DB write committed, but the send to Kafka failed — the event is lost.","We write the data AND the event into outbox in ONE DB transaction.","A relay reads the unsent outbox rows and ships them to Kafka.","Sent — it marks the row done; on failure it retries, delivery is at-least-once.","Result: DB and Kafka stay consistent without a distributed transaction."]},"idempotent-receiver":{"title":"Idempotent receiver","g":"Patterns","hook":"A doorman with a guest list: the same guest shows up twice — he's let in once.","more":"An idempotent receiver yields the **same result** when the same message is processed again. In distributed systems delivery is almost always at-least-once, so duplicates are inevitable — you must defend on the receiving side.\n\nHow: every message carries a stable **business key / messageId**. Before processing you check whether you've seen it (a table of processed ids, `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT`, a unique constraint) and skip the repeat. Or the operation is idempotent by nature (`SET status='PAID'`).\n\nGotchas: the key must be stable and unique, and the check plus the processing must run in one transaction, otherwise a race lets a duplicate slip through. Don't tie the key to the Kafka offset.","caps":["The broker sends \"debit 120₸\" — at-least-once mode: the guarantee is \"at least once\".","The ack got lost — the broker isn't sure and sends the same message a second time.","A naive consumer debits twice — 240₸ flies off the account. Trouble.","Fix: every operation has its own idempotency key — unique 🔑.","The consumer stores processed keys — a new one arrives, it checks \"seen before?\".","No key → debit 120₸. Key present → silently ignore, the balance is intact."]},"exactly-once":{"title":"Exactly-once = idempotency","g":"Patterns","hook":"\"Exactly once\" isn't magic — it's a doorman with a list: a duplicate shows up, he throws it out.","more":"\"Exactly-once\" sounds like \"processed exactly one time\", but in a distributed system that is **honestly unachievable**: the network can drop the acknowledgement and the sender will retry. What actually works is the formula: **at-least-once delivery + an idempotent receiver** = the effect of exactly-once processing.\n\nWhy this way: you can't guarantee a single *delivery*, but you can make retries **harmless** — a duplicate doesn't change the result. That's what gets sold as exactly-once (Kafka EOS is transactions + dedup, also built on idempotency).\n\nGotchas: don't rely on \"broker magic\" across system boundaries. Anywhere there are retries, make operations idempotent by a business key — otherwise double debits and duplicates.","caps":["The network is fickle — the producer sends a message and waits for an ack that may be lost.","The ack got lost — the producer isn't sure and sends a retry, two duplicates in the log.","An idempotent producer assigns a seq — the broker cuts the duplicate by number.","Transaction: the log write and the offset advance commit together or not at all.","An idempotent consumer remembers processed ids — it skips the repeat.","The repeat doesn't change the result — and that is \"exactly once\"."]},"dead-letter":{"title":"Dead Letter Queue","g":"Patterns","hook":"An undeliverable letter goes to the post office's \"dead box\" — sorted out by hand later, not thrown away.","more":"A **Dead Letter Queue (DLQ)** is a separate queue where the broker (Kafka, RabbitMQ, SQS) parks a message that couldn't be processed after N retries or whose TTL expired. The consumer fails to ack the bad message the required number of times — and off it goes to the DLQ.\n\nWhy: one \"poison message\" must not block the whole queue and spin an endless retry. It's set aside, and the main flow keeps moving.\n\nGotchas: a DLQ without monitoring and alerts turns into a graveyard — data silently piles up there. You need root-cause analysis and a replay mechanism after the fix.","caps":["A message arrives at the consumer for processing.","Processing fails — we try again, retry after retry.","With no exit, one broken message stalls the partition.","After N retries we stop looping — we move the message to the DLQ.","The partition is freed and moves on without bottlenecks.","The broken ones sit in the DLQ — we'll sort them out later, separately and by hand."]},"cap":{"title":"CAP under a network split","g":"Theory","hook":"The link drops: you either wait for the register's answer (CP) or trade at stale prices (AP). There's no third option.","more":"**CAP**: during a network partition a distributed system can't hold Consistency and Availability at the same time — you pick one. The nodes can't see each other, and you must decide what to do with the request.\n\n**CP**: refuse to answer (error/timeout) rather than serve stale data — we pick consistency. **AP**: answer from the local replica, risking stale data — we pick availability.\n\nGotchas: CAP is about behavior specifically during a partition, not \"always\". With no network split the system gives both C and A. The choice is made per operation, not for the whole database.","caps":["Two nodes hold one value, the link is alive — both agree.","The link breaks: a partition will happen — it's inevitable, not a choice.","A writer changes N1 to 9 — but N2, behind the wall, never learned.","AP: N2 answers fast, but with a stale value.","CP: N2 refuses to answer rather than lie with a value.","Under a split the choice is only AP or CP — CA is impossible in distributed systems."]},"eventual-consistency":{"title":"Eventual consistency","g":"Theory","hook":"You sent the money — it shows up for your friend \"in a couple of seconds\". Not instantly, but it surely arrives.","more":"**Eventual consistency** — replicas/services diverge temporarily, but if writes stop, after some time they all converge on a single value. The update spreads asynchronously (replication, an event in Kafka, background sync).\n\nWhy: it buys high availability and scale — no need to block everyone for a single instantaneous state, as strong consistency requires.\n\nGotchas: the divergence window is visible to the user (\"I just wrote it — I don't see it\"). You can't build invariants that need read-your-writes without explicit techniques: versions, idempotency, reading from the leader for critical operations.","caps":["A write hits one node: it already shows 120, the neighbors still have the old 100.","The replicas have diverged: one ran ahead, the copies are still in transit.","A reader hits a lagging node — it saw the old 100.","No new writes — the nodes catch up, the convergence bar rises.","Time passes — all nodes converge on a single value, 120 ✓.","The price of convergence is availability: we always answer, sometimes with stale data."]},"idempotency-key":{"title":"Idempotency key","g":"Theory","hook":"You hit \"pay\" twice — the same receipt is charged once. The key is like a receipt number.","more":"An **idempotency key** is a unique identifier for an operation (often a client-generated UUID) that the server remembers. A repeat request with the same key doesn't run the action again — it returns the saved result of the first one.\n\nWhy: retries are inevitable over the network (timeout, lost response). Without a key, a repeat creates a duplicate — two payments, two orders. The key turns unreliable delivery into \"do it exactly once\".\n\nGotchas: the key must be stored atomically with the operation itself (one transaction / upsert on a unique index), otherwise a race between two parallel repeats. And set a storage TTL.","caps":["The client sends an operation and attaches a unique key to it.","The server runs it and stores the key together with the result.","We remember: key ab12 is already processed, it's in the journal.","The response got lost — the client sends the same key again.","The server sees ab12 in the journal — it doesn't pay a second time.","We return the prior result — the money is charged exactly once."]},"srp-godclass":{"title":"SRP and the God Class","g":"SOLID","hook":"A Swiss army knife for everything: it cuts, it saws, it uncorks — and it's a nightmare to fix. Give each tool its own handle.","more":"**SRP (Single Responsibility)** — a class should have only one reason to change, i.e. it serves a single group of stakeholders / one area of responsibility. It's not \"does one action\", it's \"changes for one reason\".\n\n**God Class** — an SRP violation: a 1000+ line class drags in validation, the database, business logic, and integrations all at once. Any change risks breaking everything, tests are heavy, and merges conflict.\n\nHow to fix: extract responsibilities into separate classes/services along the reasons to change. For example, `OrderServiceImpl` (1361 LOC) gets split into an orchestrator plus narrow services and adapters.","caps":["One class does EVERYTHING: order, fees, emails, database.","4 jobs = 4 reasons to change one class.","You tweak the emails — and the order calculation breaks.","SRP: split into use cases — each with a single responsibility.","Every class has exactly one reason to change.","Change the emails — the rest stays intact. Each owns its part."]},"ocp-switch":{"title":"OCP without an ever-growing switch","g":"SOLID","hook":"A new coin — you don't re-machine the whole machine, you plug a new acceptor into a ready-made slot.","more":"**OCP (Open/Closed)** — code is open for extension, closed for modification: new behavior is added with new code, not by editing existing code. A growing `switch`/`if-else` on type is the classic anti-pattern: every new case forces you back into the old method.\n\nThe fix: **polymorphism** — a common interface and one implementation per variant, selected via a Map<type, handler> or Strategy. A new case = a new class; the old code stays untouched.\n\nPitfall: don't spawn abstractions ahead of time (YAGNI). Polymorphism pays off when there really are many variants and they keep changing; for two stable branches a plain switch is simpler and more honest.","caps":["One switch on type — with 2 types everything's fine.","New type → back into the same switch, editing working code.","Each new type bloats the switch — and grows the risk.","OCP: one interface, each type its own class.","New format = new class. The old code stays untouched 🔒.","Open for extension, closed for modification — that's OCP."]},"lsp-rect":{"title":"LSP: Square vs Rectangle","g":"SOLID","hook":"A square \"sort of a rectangle\": you set the width — and the height jumps on its own. Swap it in, and everything breaks.","more":"**LSP (Liskov Substitution)** — a subclass object must be substitutable for the base class without breaking the logic. The classic case: `Square extends Rectangle`. A rectangle's `setWidth`/`setHeight` are independent, but a square is forced to change both sides.\n\nCode that expects a Rectangle (sets width=5, height=4, expects area 20) gets 16 with a Square — the base class invariant is broken. An \"is-a\" inheritance by geometry doesn't imply correct substitution by behavior.\n\nTakeaway: inherit by the behavioral contract, not by words. Here composition or a shared `Shape` interface without mutable independent sides is the right call.","caps":["Rectangle's contract: width and height change INDEPENDENTLY.","We expect area: 5 × 4 = 20. That's the contract's promise.","Square extends Rectangle, but it has ONE side for both.","setHeight(4) drags width → 4. The sides are coupled!","The same code expects 20 — but Square computes 4 × 4 = 16.","LSP: a subclass must substitute for its parent. Square can't."]},"dip-hexagon":{"title":"DIP and the hexagon","g":"SOLID","hook":"A standard wall socket: the lamp knows nothing about the power plant. Plug in any device — the wall doesn't change.","more":"**DIP** (Dependency Inversion): both the high level (business logic) and the low level (database, queues) depend on an **abstraction** — an interface, not on each other. In a hexagon the core declares **ports** (interfaces) and adapters implement them; the dependency direction is always from the outside in.\n\nWhy: business logic doesn't know about Postgres/Kafka, it can be tested with mocks and the infrastructure can be swapped without touching the core. That's exactly how `audit-service` is built: `audit-core` depends on `audit-api`/`audit-spi`, not on the adapters.\n\nPitfall: a port must be expressed in **domain** terms, not database terms (`save(Order)`, not `executeSql`). If the interface leaks adapter details, the inversion is a fiction.","caps":["Bad: the domain reaches into JDBC itself. Change a detail — you break the domain.","DIP: put a PORT between them — an interface abstraction.","Both sides depend on the port: both point AT the abstraction.","The adapter implements the port (arrow up to the abstraction).","Swap JDBC for REST: leave the domain and port alone, only the adapter.","Hexagon: core in the center, adapters at the edges depend on the ports."]},"strategy":{"title":"Strategy","g":"Patterns","hook":"A navigator: one \"Start\" button, but the route — on foot / by car / by bike — you pick via a swappable mode.","more":"**Strategy** — a family of interchangeable algorithms behind a common interface; the client holds a reference to the interface and swaps the implementation at runtime. Under the hood it's just polymorphism + composition instead of branching.\n\nWhy: it removes the sprawling `if/switch` on type and opens the class to extension with a new strategy without editing the old code (**OCP**). Example: different fee calculators or document formats.\n\nPitfall: don't spawn a strategy for two stable branches — that's overengineering. Use it when there are many variants that change independently or are selected dynamically.","caps":["Trouble: a switch on tariff type — every new tariff goes inside.","Idea: one \"compute the price\" interface — a shared socket for all.","The context knows no details — it holds a reference to a strategy.","Plug in tariff A — the context calls it without knowing which it is.","Change behavior at runtime — just drop in a different strategy.","New tariff C — add a class alongside, leave the old code untouched."]},"factory":{"title":"Factory","g":"Patterns","hook":"An order pickup window: you say \"burger\" and get it ready-made — you don't go into the kitchen to assemble it yourself.","more":"**Factory** encapsulates object creation: instead of `new`, the client calls a method that decides which concrete class to return and hides the assembly details. There's Factory Method (a method in a subclass) and Abstract Factory (a family of related products).\n\nWhy: the client works with an interface without knowing the specifics — the choice of implementation is centralized in one place, easy to change and test.\n\nPitfall: if creation is trivial (`new Dto()`), a factory is an extra layer. It's justified when there's a choice of implementation, complex initialization, or a dependency to hide.","caps":["No factory: the client does new for each type itself — coupled to all the specifics.","Put a factory in the middle: the client calls create(type) instead of new.","Inside the factory — picking the class by type. That's its job, not the client's.","The client gets a ready object and doesn't know which class it is.","Change the implementation inside the factory — the client stays untouched.","In a test the factory returns a stub — easy to check the client."]},"builder":{"title":"Builder","g":"Patterns","hook":"You assemble a burger layer by layer: bun, patty, cheese — and at the end \"done!\". No bun, no burger.","more":"**Builder** assembles a complex object step by step, separating construction from representation. Instead of telescoping constructors with a dozen arguments — a chain `.setX().setY().build()`, where `build()` produces an immutable object.\n\nWhy: readability (it's clear what goes where), many **optional** fields, invariant validation in one place before creation. Great for immutable objects.\n\nPitfall: for 2-3 fields it's overkill — a constructor or record will do. Make sure `build()` checks the required fields, otherwise you get a half-assembled object.","caps":["An object with lots of fields: name, age, city, phone, email.","Telescoping: a pile of constructors for different field sets.","Which one to call and what goes where — impossible to tell.","Builder: assemble step by step, each field labeled.","Set the fields you need, the rest default.",".build() → a finished immutable object, no changes allowed."]},"singleton-enum":{"title":"Singleton: enum vs DCL","g":"Patterns","hook":"An enum singleton is the tap in the wall: one for the whole apartment, can't be unscrewed or duplicated. DCL is a double lock on the door.","more":"**Singleton** guarantees a single instance per JVM. Two approaches: an **enum** with a single constant and **DCL** (double-checked locking) — a `null` check without a lock, then with a lock.\n\nEnum is preferable: the JVM itself guarantees uniqueness and thread safety, protects against reflection and serialization — with minimal code. DCL, on the other hand, requires **`volatile`** on the field, otherwise, due to instruction reordering, another thread may see a partially initialized object.\n\nPitfall: DCL is easy to get wrong (forgetting `volatile`). In modern projects it's better to let the DI container own singletons (`@ApplicationScoped`) than to roll them by hand.","caps":["A singleton — one object for everyone. The main pain: creating it safely.","DCL without volatile: thread 2 sees the reference, but the fields inside are still empty.","Cause: the JVM/CPU reorder the steps — the reference appears before the object is ready.","Fix: make the field volatile. From outside only a fully built object is visible.","Enum singleton: one INSTANCE, thread-safe out of the box, no volatile.","Bottom line: DCL works but is fragile. Enum is the most reliable path."]},"dsf-observer":{"title":"Observer","g":"Patterns","hook":"Subscribing to a YouTube channel: the author posts a video — every subscriber gets a notification, and the author doesn't know them by name.","more":"**Observer** breaks the tight coupling between \"who changes → who reacts\". The Subject keeps a list of subscribers and, on a state change, walks the list itself and calls `update()`.\n- The Subject does NOT know the concrete observer classes — only the `Observer` interface.\n- Adding a new listener = just `subscribe()`, the Subject's code stays untouched (this is OCP).\n- Built into Java: `java.util.concurrent.Flow` (Reactive Streams), `PropertyChangeListener`, Swing listeners / `ActionListener`. The old `Observable`/`Observer` (java.util) — deprecated since Java 9.\n- Risk: a notification avalanche and a memory leak if you forget `unsubscribe()`.","caps":["Problem: the data changed, and three different places need to know — but the Subject shouldn't depend on each of them.","Solution: introduce the Observer interface. Everyone who reacts implements a single update().","Subscribe: each one calls subscribe(), the Subject puts them in a list — without knowing the classes by name.","Event: the state changed — the Subject walks the list and sends update() to each.","Extension: added SMS — just subscribe(). The Subject's code didn't change by a single line (OCP).","Bottom line: one-to-many without tight coupling. The Subject knows only the interface — add and remove listeners on the fly."]},"dsf-decorator":{"title":"Decorator","g":"Patterns","hook":"Coffee: you take an espresso and wrap it in \"milk\", then \"syrup\". Each wrapper adds flavor and price, but it's still \"coffee\" — you drink it the same way.","more":"**Decorator** adds behavior to an object by wrapping it in another object of the same interface. Instead of a subclass explosion (`BufferedGzipEncryptedStream`...) you compose features in layers at runtime.\n- The decorator implements the same interface as the wrapped object and holds a reference to it — delegating the call while adding its own before/after.\n- Difference from inheritance: inheritance fixes the combination at compile time; a decorator composes dynamically and in any order.\n- Canonical in Java: `java.io` — `new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(new FileInputStream(f)))`. Also `Collections.unmodifiableList`, `HttpServletRequestWrapper`.\n- Cost: lots of small objects and long stack traces.","caps":["Problem: you need combinations of features (buffering, compression, encryption). By inheritance that's N×M classes — a combinatorial explosion.","Idea: the base object and the decorator implement ONE Stream interface. The decorator holds a reference to the nested one.","Assembly: wrap the core in layers. From outside it's still a Stream — the client sees no difference.","A write() call passes through the layers: each does its own \"before\", passes it down — all the way to the core.","Flexibility: reorder or remove a layer at runtime. No new classes — just a different assembly of wrappers.","Bottom line: behavior is composed in layers dynamically instead of a frozen subclass hierarchy. That's exactly how java.io is built."]},"dsf-state":{"title":"State","g":"Patterns","hook":"A traffic light: each color \"knows\" which one comes next. Not one big brain full of ifs, but each state deciding its own transition.","more":"**State** makes an object change behavior when its internal state changes — as if it changed class. Instead of `if (status == ...)` in every method, you have a state object and behavior is delegated to it.\n- Each state is a separate class with the same interface; a transition = swapping the current State object.\n- Cures \"status hell\": adding a new state = a new class, not editing a dozen switches all over the code.\n- Difference from Strategy: with Strategy the client sets the strategy from outside and it doesn't change; in State the object switches itself, and states know about each other.\n- Cost: many classes; for 2-3 simple statuses an enum may be simpler.","caps":["Pain: the order status is smeared across if/switch in every method. A new status — edits in a dozen places.","Idea: each state is its own class with a single State interface. The behavior lives INSIDE the state.","The context (Order) holds a reference to the current State and delegates the call to it — it doesn't branch itself.","Transition: New.pay() runs the logic itself and swaps the context's state to Paid.","State machine: transitions are explicit and verifiable. From Shipped you can no longer call pay() — that's a different state.","Bottom line: a new state = a new class, not editing switches. The transition logic is gathered and visible like a map."]},"dsf-aggregate-by-id":{"title":"Aggregate by ID","g":"Patterns","hook":"In an address book you write a friend's name, you don't glue the friend in. An order stores clientId, not the live client whole.","more":"**Aggregate** — a consistency boundary: inside it everything changes within one transaction. ANOTHER aggregate is referenced by identifier (`clientId`), not by object reference.\n- **One transaction = one aggregate.** If a transaction touches two aggregates, you hold two locks at once → conflicts, deadlocks, poor scalability.\n- A reference by ID prevents you from accidentally \"reaching in\" and changing someone else's aggregate inside your transaction — the boundary stays intact.\n- It lightens loading: you don't drag half the object graph into memory for one order.\n- Between aggregates — consistency via events (eventual consistency), not one big commit.","caps":["Temptation: Order holds a reference to a live Client object. Convenient — order.getClient().getName().","Trouble: changing the order, I accidentally edit the client too — one transaction touches TWO aggregates at once.","Two locks in one transaction = conflicts and deadlocks under load. The consistency boundaries are fused together.","Fix: Order stores only clientId — a bare identifier, not the object.","Now each transaction locks EXACTLY one aggregate. Orders and clients don't get in each other's way.","Between aggregates — consistency via events, not one giant commit. The boundaries stay intact."]},"cacheaside":{"title":"Cache-aside","g":"Cache","hook":"Cache-aside — you check the fridge first; if it's empty, you run to the store and stock the fridge for next time.","more":"**Cache-aside** (lazy loading): the application manages the cache itself. On read — it checks the cache first; on a miss → reads the DB, populates the cache, returns the value. On write — it writes to the DB and **invalidates** (deletes) the key from the cache.\n\nWhy: only data that's actually requested gets cached, the cache and DB are loosely coupled, and a cache outage doesn't break reads (just makes them slower). The most common pattern with Redis.\n\nPitfalls: there's an inconsistency window between the DB and the cache; delete the key rather than updating it (fewer races). On a cold start — a burst of misses and the risk of a stampede.","caps":["The cache sits OFF TO THE SIDE: the request goes to the DB, the cache is a branch off it.","First we check the cache. Is the key there?","Hit: key is in the cache → return it immediately, the DB is untouched.","Miss: empty → read the DB, store it in the cache with a TTL, return it.","TTL expired → the entry \"goes stale\" on its own.","Write to the DB → invalidate (delete) the stale key."]},"cache-eviction":{"title":"Eviction: TTL and LRU","g":"Cache","hook":"TTL — you toss the yogurt once it's past its expiry date. LRU — whatever has been gathering dust longest in the cupboard goes out.","more":"**Eviction** — removing entries when the cache is full or the data is stale. **TTL** deletes a key once its time-to-live expires (fighting staleness). **LRU** evicts what hasn't been used in a long time when space runs out (fighting size).\n\nWhy: memory is finite, and data goes stale. TTL gives an upper bound on freshness; LRU keeps the \"hot\" working set in the cache.\n\nPitfalls: too short a TTL — extra misses; too long — stale data. LRU performs poorly on a full scan (it flushes out the hot set). They're often combined: TTL + a memory limit with LRU/LFU.","caps":["The cache is a shelf with slots. Memory has filled up to the brim.","A new key arrives, but there's no room. Someone has to be evicted (eviction).","TTL: the key has a timer. Time's up ⏳ → the key is stale, evict it.","LRU: evicts whoever hasn't been touched the longest (Least Recently Used).","Evicted the excess → freed up slots → the new key fits.","The key metric is hit rate. High = the cache is helping, we're not evicting what's needed."]},"cache-stampede":{"title":"Cache stampede","g":"Cache","hook":"The cache expires — and the whole crowd rushes the store's single door at once; the crush takes the server down.","more":"**Cache stampede** (dogpile): a popular key expires, and dozens of concurrent requests all hit a miss at the same time and pile onto the DB for the very same value — the peak load can take the database down.\n\nFixes: **lock/single-flight** — only one thread recomputes the value, the rest wait for the result; or **early/probabilistic refresh** — refresh the key ahead of time, before the TTL expires; or serve a slightly stale value while the recomputation is in progress.\n\nPitfalls: especially dangerous on a cold start and for heavy queries. Don't give all keys the same TTL — add jitter so they don't expire all at once.","caps":["A hot key in the cache: everyone reads it, the DB is idle.","TTL expired → the key is stale, the cache is empty.","The crush: a batch misses at once and hits the DB simultaneously → a spike.","Fix 1: single-flight lock — one thread computes, the rest wait.","Fix 2: early refresh before the TTL + jitter — they don't all expire at once.","Result: lock + jitter → the DB stays calm, no spike."]},"rabbit-vs-kafka":{"title":"RabbitMQ vs Kafka","g":"Queues and search","hook":"Rabbit is a mailman: he hands over the letter and forgets it. Kafka is a journal: the entry stays put, anyone can re-read it from the start.","more":"**RabbitMQ** — a queue broker: it routes messages through an exchange into queues, and after an ack the message is **deleted**. Smart routing, per-message acknowledgments, great for tasks/RPC.\n\n**Kafka** — a distributed **log**: events are written to partitions and retained per the retention policy, readers advance their own offset and can re-read. High throughput, ordering within a partition, multiple independent consumers of the same stream — the foundation of event streaming (like `spo-pf-orders`).\n\nWhich when: Rabbit — complex routing, task queues, low latency for an individual message. Kafka — high volume, replay, event sourcing, analytics. Pitfalls: Kafka is not a task queue, and Rabbit handles replaying history poorly.","caps":["RabbitMQ: the producer sends a message to an exchange, which puts it into a queue.","The consumer acknowledged receipt — the message is deleted from the queue.","Kafka is a log: messages sit back-to-back by offset and are retained.","Each consumer reads from its own position — everyone has their own offset.","The messages stay put — the consumer rewinds the offset and re-reads.","Result: Rabbit delivers and forgets. Kafka keeps the log and lets you re-read."]},"inverted-index":{"title":"Inverted index","g":"Queues and search","hook":"An inverted index is the alphabetical index at the back of a book: word → pages, instead of leafing through the whole book.","more":"**An inverted index** — the data structure behind full-text search (Elasticsearch/Lucene). Text is broken into **terms** (tokenization, normalization), and a mapping `term → list of documents` is built for the documents it appears in (the postings list), often with positions and frequencies.\n\nWhy: instead of scanning every document, searching for a word is just grabbing a ready-made list by term; intersecting lists yields documents matching multiple words instantly. Frequencies are used for ranking (TF-IDF/BM25).\n\nPitfalls: search quality is determined by the **analyzer** (stemming, stop words, language) — the index and the query must be processed the same way. The index must be rebuilt when the analysis changes; updates in Lucene mean re-indexing segments.","caps":["Forward index: a document stores its own list of words.","Searching for \"cat\" — but you have to scan ALL documents. Slow.","We flip it: the word becomes the key, not the document.","Inverted index: each word knows its own documents.","Searching for \"cat\" — a ready-made list right away. Instant.","That's exactly how full-text search works in Elasticsearch."]},"docker-image-container":{"title":"Image vs container","g":"Containers","hook":"An image is a cake recipe in a book. A container is a cake baked from it; one recipe, many cakes.","more":"**An image** is an immutable template: filesystem layers + metadata (what to run, env, ports). It's built from a Dockerfile, each instruction = a layer. **A container** is a running instance of an image: the same read-only image plus a thin writable layer on top for runtime changes.\n\nIt's done this way for reuse and caching: one image → many identical containers, layers are shared between them. From a single image you can spin up any number of isolated processes.\n\nPitfalls: data in the writable layer disappears when the container is removed — you need volumes for state. And don't confuse \"build an image\" (build) with \"run a container\" (run).","caps":["An image — a stack of read-only layers. They can't be changed.","Run: a thin writable layer is laid on top → a container.","Writes go to its own layer; the image is untouched — copy-on-write.","From ONE image — many containers. The layers are shared.","The build caches layers: app.jar changed → everything above it is rebuilt."]},"k8s-pod":{"title":"What is a pod","g":"Containers","hook":"A pod is a boxed set: the containers inside travel together, share an address and a power outlet, and live and die together.","more":"**A pod** — the smallest deployment unit in Kubernetes: one or more containers that share a network namespace (a common IP, localhost) and can share volumes. K8s schedules and scales pods, not containers.\n\nWhy group them: tightly coupled processes (for example, the main application + a sidecar for logging/proxying) should live and die together on the same node.\n\nPitfalls: a pod is **ephemeral** — it dies, and its IP/state are lost; a new pod gets a new IP. That's why you don't talk to pods directly: you put a Deployment (restarts, replicas) and a Service (a stable address) on top.","caps":["A container — a single packaged process (the application).","A pod — a wrapper around containers, lives on ONE node.","A pod's containers share the network (one IP) and volumes.","Usually: 1 main container + a sidecar (logs, proxy).","To scale — we copy the whole pod (replicas).","Result: a pod is the unit of scaling, not a container."]},"k8s-probes":{"title":"Liveness vs readiness","g":"Containers","hook":"Liveness — is the patient breathing (if not — resuscitate). Readiness — is it ready to seat guests at the table.","more":"**Liveness** answers the question \"is the process alive?\". If the probe fails — the kubelet **restarts** the container. **Readiness** answers \"is it ready to take traffic?\". If it fails — the pod is removed from the Service (no traffic), but it is NOT restarted.\n\nThe split is needed because \"hung forever\" and \"temporarily busy\" are treated differently: the first — with a restart, the second — by pausing routing (cache warm-up, waiting on the DB).\n\nPitfalls: a heavy liveness check (one that hits the DB) causes cascading restarts when a dependency degrades. Keep liveness simple; for a slow start add a startup probe.","caps":["The pod is up. It has two sensors: \"is it alive?\" and \"is it ready to take traffic?\".","Warm-up: liveness=✓ (alive), readiness=✗ (not ready yet).","readiness=✗ → the load balancer sends no traffic, but the pod is NOT killed.","Warmed up: readiness=✓ → traffic flows, the pod is in service.","liveness ✗ → the kubelet restarts the container within the same pod.","Result: liveness=restart; readiness=let traffic in / keep it out."]},"inf2f-kafka-consumer-group":{"title":"Consumer group: dividing partitions","g":"Queues and search","hook":"Partitions are the checkout lanes in a supermarket, consumers are the cashiers. A lane is always served by exactly one cashier: add more cashiers than lanes and the extras stand idle.","more":"**A partition is the unit of parallelism in Kafka.** Within a single consumer group, each partition is read by exactly one consumer at any moment — that's how Kafka guarantees ordering within a partition and no double processing.\n\n- **Consumers = partitions** — the perfect balance, everyone is busy.\n- **Consumers > partitions** — the extras sit idle (failover reserve).\n- **Consumers < partitions** — someone handles several partitions.\n- When a consumer is added or fails, a **rebalance** happens — partitions are reassigned.\n- **Different groups** read the same topic independently, each with its own offset — that's how one event reaches several services.","caps":["A topic is split into partitions — independent, ordered streams of messages.","Within one group, each partition is pinned to exactly one consumer — ordering isn't broken.","Added a 4th consumer — but there are only 3 partitions, so the extra one just sits idle.","Removed a consumer — Kafka triggers a rebalance and reassigns its partition to the rest.","Meanwhile ANOTHER group reads the same topic in parallel — with its own offset, independent of the first.","Result: a partition = the unit of parallelism. Want more consumers — provision more partitions up front."]},"inf2f-docker-layer-cache":{"title":"Image layers and build cache","g":"Containers","hook":"A Dockerfile is a stack of transparent sheets. Change the top one — the lower ones stay. But repaint a lower one, and everything above it has to be redrawn.","more":"**Each Dockerfile instruction creates a layer**, and Docker caches them in order. A layer is taken from cache only if it and every layer ABOVE it are unchanged.\n\n- `COPY . .` before `mvn install` puts the entire source code into one early layer.\n- Change a single line of code — that layer is invalidated, and with it the whole dependency cache below.\n- The result: every build re-fetches all dependencies from the internet (minutes instead of seconds).\n- **Fix:** first `COPY pom.xml`, then `mvn dependency:go-offline` (the dependency layer), and only after that `COPY src`.\n- This way the dependency layer changes only when `pom.xml` changes, while code edits replay only the cheap last layer.","caps":["An image is a stack of layers: each Dockerfile instruction appends one layer on top.","First build: all layers are built and go into the cache. Dependencies are fetched from the network once.","Changed ONE line of code. The COPY . . layer includes the whole project — so it's invalidated.","And since the COPY layer changed, every layer BELOW it is rebuilt too — mvn re-downloads everything.","Fix: first COPY pom.xml and download dependencies as a separate layer, and only then COPY src.","Now a code edit touches only the cheap lower layers — the dep layer comes from cache. Seconds."]},"inf2f-nginx-sticky-session":{"title":"Load balancing and sticky sessions","g":"Containers","hook":"Round-robin is a coat-check clerk handing out tickets in turn. But if your coat is held by only one clerk and you're sent to another — there's no coat.","more":"**An nginx upstream spreads requests across several backends.** The default strategy is round-robin: requests go around in turn, evenly.\n\n- The problem arises when the session is stored IN THE MEMORY of a specific instance (rather than in a shared Redis).\n- Logged in — the session landed on backend-1. The next request was sent by round-robin to backend-2, which doesn't have that session — the user got logged out.\n- **Fix 1 (a crutch):** `ip_hash` or `sticky` — pin a client to one backend by IP/cookie.\n- **Fix 2 (the right one):** move the session into shared storage (Redis), and then the instances become stateless and round-robin is safe.\n- Stateless instances scale better and survive any node failure without logging users out.","caps":["One nginx, several identical backends behind it. Who gets the request?","Round-robin: requests go around in turn — 1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3. Load is even.","A user logs in — the session landed in the MEMORY of backend-1. It exists only there.","The next request goes round-robin to backend-2 — which has no session. The user got logged out.","Crutch — ip_hash/sticky: pin the client to one backend. Works, but if that node dies — no session.","The right way — move the session into a shared Redis. The backends become stateless, round-robin is safe."]},"inf2f-hpa-autoscale":{"title":"HPA: autoscaling by CPU","g":"Containers","hook":"HPA is a thermostat for pods. But a thermostat without a thermometer measures nothing: HPA without resources.requests has no basis to compute load from.","more":"**The HorizontalPodAutoscaler changes the number of pods by a metric** (usually CPU). About every 15 seconds it asks the metrics-server for current consumption and compares it against the target.\n\n- HPA computes load as a **% of requests**, NOT of the limit and not in absolute terms.\n- If a pod has no `resources.requests.cpu` set — HPA has no base for the percentage, and it won't scale (it shows `<unknown>`).\n- Formula: `desired_replicas = current × (current_metric / target)`.\n- Example: target 50%, actual 100% with 2 pods → 4 pods needed.\n- The second common reason HPA stalls — **metrics-server** isn't installed in the cluster, so there are simply no metrics.\n- HPA scales only stateless workloads; you don't grow a DB this way.","caps":["HPA is a feedback loop: measure the load, compare against the target, adjust the pod count.","To compute the %, HPA takes consumption as a fraction of the pod's resources.requests.","Load went up: 2 pods running at 100% against a 50% target. By the formula, twice as many are needed.","HPA brings replicas up to 4 — the load on each pod drops toward the 50% target. The loop is closed.","The main trap: the pod has NO requests.cpu set. HPA has no base for the percentage — the metric is unknown.","The second cause of a stall — no metrics-server. No metrics — HPA is blind. Checklist: requests + metrics-server."]},"tef-test-pyramid":{"title":"The Test Pyramid","g":"Strategy","hook":"Like a house: lots of bricks at the base, a tiny spire on top. Flip it and it collapses.","more":"**The pyramid** is about proportions, not about banning any test type.\n- **Bottom — unit:** there are MANY of them, they're fast (ms), and they hit a single class with no DB/network.\n- **Middle — integration:** fewer of them, verifying wiring (repository + a real DB via Testcontainers).\n- **Top — e2e:** FEW of them, slow and brittle, driving the whole flow through UI/HTTP.\n- **The \"ice cream cone\" anti-pattern (inverted pyramid):** a pile of slow e2e and almost no unit — the build takes 40 minutes and goes red at the slightest hiccup.\nThe idea: the more expensive and slower a test is, the fewer of them you should have.","caps":["Tests come at different \"prices\": unit is cheap and fast, e2e is expensive and slow.","A healthy ratio: a wide base of unit tests, a narrow spire of e2e.","At the bottom we run hundreds of unit tests — they catch logic in milliseconds.","Higher up, a handful of e2e: each spins up the whole app and crawls along in seconds.","Invert the ratio and you get the \"ice cream cone\": a slow, perpetually red build.","Bottom line: the right shape gives fast, stable feedback on every commit."]},"tef-tdd-cycle":{"title":"The TDD Cycle (red-green-refactor)","g":"Process","hook":"First you set up the target, then you shoot, then you clean the gun. Never the other way around.","more":"**TDD** — you write the test BEFORE the code. The three-step cycle spins on one small feature at a time.\n- **Red:** write a failing test for behavior that doesn't exist yet. Red is proof that the test actually checks something.\n- **Green:** write the MINIMUM code to make the test pass. Even a hardcoded value is fine — the only goal is green.\n- **Refactor:** now, protected by the green test, you clean up the code without fear of breaking it.\nWhy this way: a test written first shapes a convenient API and guarantees the code is covered. A test written afterward is often bent to fit the existing code and catches nothing.","caps":["Step 1 — Red: write a test for behavior that doesn't exist in the code yet.","Red is mandatory: it proves the test actually checks something rather than lying \"green\".","Step 2 — Green: write the minimum code to make the test pass. Elegance doesn't matter yet.","Step 3 — Refactor: clean up the code, and the green test catches you if you break something.","The three steps close into a loop that spins on one small feature at a time.","Bottom line: code is born already covered by tests, and the API turns out convenient \"from the inside\"."]},"tef-verify-vs-assert":{"title":"verify() vs assert: state vs interaction","g":"Mockito","hook":"assert checks \"what's in the box afterward\", verify checks \"what you did by hand along the way\".","more":"**Two different kinds of checks in a test — don't mix them up.**\n- **assert (state verification):** looks at the RESULT — what the method returned, what's in the object after the call. `assertThat(result).isEqualTo(4)`.\n- **verify (behavior verification):** looks at the INTERACTION — whether a mock's method was called, how many times, with which arguments. `verify(repo).save(order)`.\nWhy verify when you have assert: sometimes an action has no return value — e.g. `void notify()` or `repo.delete(id)`. There's no \"result\" to check, but the fact of the call to the dependency matters. That's where `verify`, `times(2)`, and `never()` come in.\nRule: assert for your own logic, verify for talking to dependencies (especially void).","caps":["The service does two things: it computes a result and calls a dependency (the repository).","assert checks the RETURN — what ends up in the result after the call.","But a void method has NO return — there's nothing for assert to check.","verify looks into the mock's call log: was it called, how many times, with what.","times(n) and never() pin down the exact number of calls — that's the whole point of verify.","Bottom line: assert is about the state of the result, verify is about the fact of talking to a dependency."]},"tef-testcontainers":{"title":"Testcontainers: a real DB in Docker for the duration of the test","g":"Integration","hook":"Disposable dishes: grab a clean plate for the test, eat, throw it away. Nobody scrubs a shared one.","more":"**Testcontainers** spins up a real dependency (Postgres, Kafka, Redis) in Docker straight from the test.\n- **The problem with H2/in-memory:** it fakes the SQL dialect. The test is green, but in production Postgres breaks on `jsonb`, `ON CONFLICT`, or types.\n- **The solution:** a container with the SAME Postgres you run in production. Full fidelity of dialect and behavior.\n- **Lifecycle:** when the test starts, the container comes up, exposes a random port (no conflicts on CI), the test connects to it, and afterward the container is destroyed along with all its data.\n- **The cost:** slower than unit (container startup is seconds), so this is the integration level, not unit. Reuse mode and a shared per-class container speed it up.","caps":["In-memory H2 is fast, but it's a FAKE Postgres — a different SQL dialect.","Testcontainers spins up a REAL Postgres in Docker — the same one you run in production.","When the test starts, the container comes up and hands out a RANDOM port — no conflicts on CI.","The test connects to a live database and runs real SQL queries.","After the test the container is destroyed along with all its data — the next start is clean.","Bottom line: production-grade fidelity at the cost of seconds — this is the integration level, not unit."]},"tef-test-pollution":{"title":"Test pollution: the neighbor test that breaks the others","g":"Reliability","hook":"One cook didn't scrub the shared pan — the next one inherits someone else's burnt crust.","more":"**Test pollution** — tests share common mutable state, and one test changes it in a way that makes another fail.\n- **Symptom:** a test is green on its own but red in the full run; it fails only in a particular ORDER.\n- **Source:** static fields, a shared list/map, an unreset mock, a write to a shared DB without rollback, a singleton config, a modified `System.setProperty`.\n- **Why it's dangerous:** the bug is flaky, depends on JUnit's order — debugging is hell.\n- **The cure:** isolation. `@BeforeEach` recreates the state, a transaction with rollback, `@DirtiesContext` in Spring, no `static` mutable fields. Tests are deliberately run in random order to flush out pollution.","caps":["Two tests share common static state — for example, a list of users.","testA adds an entry and DOESN'T clean up after itself — the state is left dirty.","testB expected an empty list but got someone else's entry — and fails.","The trap: change the run order and a different test goes red instead.","The cure is isolation: @BeforeEach recreates clean state before each test.","Bottom line: every test starts from a clean slate — order no longer matters."]},"tef-mutation-testing":{"title":"Mutation testing (PIT)","g":"Quality","hook":"To test a guard, you sneak \"saboteurs\" into the house. If he doesn't notice, the security is fake.","more":"**Mutation testing** checks the QUALITY of the tests themselves, not the code.\n- **The idea:** PIT injects small sabotages into your code — mutations (`>` → `>=`, `+` → `-`, `return true` → `return false`, removing a call).\n- **The run:** for each mutation it runs your tests. If even one test goes red, the mutant is **killed** (the tests are vigilant). If all stay green, the mutant **survives** (a hole in the tests).\n- **Why 100% coverage is a lie:** a line can be \"covered\" (executed) while no assert checks its result. Coverage shows \"executed\", mutations show \"verified\".\n- **The metric:** mutation score = killed / total. Surviving mutants are precise hints about where to add an assert.","caps":["Coverage says \"the line was executed\" — but executed ≠ verified.","PIT takes working code and injects a sabotage — for example, changing > to >=.","For each mutant PIT runs YOUR tests against the modified code.","A test goes red — the mutant is KILLED: the tests really do guard that spot.","All tests stay green under sabotage — the mutant SURVIVES: a hole here, an assert isn't checking something.","Bottom line: mutation score measures the strength of the tests, and surviving mutants are a \"add an assert\" to-do list."]},"tef-clock-injection":{"title":"Controllable time: Clock instead of LocalDate.now()","g":"Testability","hook":"Don't chase the waves out on the open sea — build a pool where you turn the water on and off yourself.","more":"**The problem:** `LocalDate.now()` straight inside a service is a hidden dependency on the system clock.\n- An \"end of month\" test passes only on the 30th, and \"yesterday\" depends on the day of the run. It's also a hexagon violation: business logic reaches into infrastructure (the clock) directly.\n- **The solution:** inject `java.time.Clock` as a dependency. In production — `Clock.systemDefaultZone()`. In the test — `Clock.fixed(...)`, frozen at the moment you need.\n- In the code, instead of `LocalDate.now()` you write `LocalDate.now(clock)`.\n- **The effect:** time becomes a controllable input. You can set December 31, February 29, midnight — and check edge cases deterministically, on any day.\nThis is a special case: the outside world (time, randomness, UUID) is pushed behind a port and swapped out in tests.","caps":["The service calls LocalDate.now() directly — it's hard-wired to the system clock.","The \"end of month\" test is green only on the 30th, and \"yesterday\" depends on the day of the run.","The solution — Clock as a dependency: the service asks the port for the time, not the OS.","In production we plug the system clock into the port — behavior is unchanged.","In the test we plug in Clock.fixed — time is frozen at the moment we need.","Bottom line: any edge-case day is checked deterministically, on any day of the run."]},"type-erasure":{"title":"Type erasure","g":"Generics & types","hook":"Generics are like a label on a jar: in the store the label reads \"pickles\", but in the warehouse every jar looks the same with no labels at all.","more":"Generics live only in the source code and are checked by the compiler. At compile time the type parameters are **erased**: `List<String>` and `List<Integer>` both collapse into a single raw `List`, and `T` is replaced by its upper bound (`Object` by default, or `Number` for `<T extends Number>`). At runtime there is no type information left.\n\nThis was done for **backward compatibility**: generics were added in Java 5, and the bytecode had to keep working with older, non-generic code. That's why the compiler inserts the casts for you.\n\nGotchas: you can't do `new T()`, `T[]`, or `instanceof List<String>`; you can't have two methods that differ only in `<...>` (their signatures are identical after erasure). The actual type is passed in via a `Class<T>` (a type token).","caps":["In source code List<String> and List<Integer> are two distinct types.","The compiler strips out the type parameter inside the angle brackets.","In bytecode both collapse into a single raw List.","Inside there's just Object — the exact type is gone.","There's no T at runtime, so the compiler forbids new T[].","instanceof can't see <String>: there's nothing to check, so it won't compile."]},"generics-pecs":{"title":"PECS: extends vs super","g":"Generics & types","hook":"Taking goods out — extends (any kind is fine). Putting goods in — super (the shelf will tolerate something broader). Producer-extends, Consumer-super.","more":"PECS = **Producer Extends, Consumer Super**. `<? extends T>` is a source: you can read from it as `T`, but you can't add to it (the compiler doesn't know the exact subtype). `<? super T>` is a sink: you can put `T` and its subtypes into it, but you can only read out as `Object`.\n\nWhy: wildcards widen the flexibility of an API. `copy(List<? super T> dst, List<? extends T> src)` accepts compatible but non-identical types — `src` produces elements and `dst` consumes them.\n\nGotchas: keep the direction straight. If a collection is both read and written as `T`, you don't need a wildcard — use the exact `List<T>`. A bare `?` (unbounded) is read-only as `Object`.","caps":["Two questions to ask about a collection: what will you READ from it and what will you PUT into it?","Producer Extends: the list HANDS OUT elements — take them as T, reading is fine.","You can't put anything into an extends-list: the exact type is unknown, so add is rejected.","Consumer Super: the list ACCEPTS T — putting in is safe.","From a super-list you read only as Object: the exact type is unknown.","The PECS rule: hands out — extends, accepts — super."]},"bounded-type":{"title":"Bounded type <T extends Number>","g":"Generics & types","hook":"Face control at the door: <T extends Number> lets in only \"our kind\" — numbers — and turns everyone else away.","more":"`<T extends Number>` bounds the type parameter from above: you can substitute only `Number` or one of its subtypes (`Integer`, `Double`). Inside the method the members of `Number` are available — for example `t.doubleValue()`. After erasure, `T` is replaced by `Number`, not by `Object`.\n\nWhy: without a bound, `T` is just `Object` and you can't do anything useful with it. A bound gives you both compile-time checking and access to that type's API.\n\nYou can have multiple bounds with `&`: `<T extends Number & Comparable<T>>` (the class comes first). The keyword is always `extends` — even for interfaces; you never write `implements` here.","caps":["<T extends Number> — T is a type parameter bounded above by Number.","Only Number and its subtypes are allowed.","String doesn't extend Number — the compiler won't allow it.","The Number bound gives access to its methods: doubleValue().","After compilation T is erased to Number — to its upper bound."]},"string-pool":{"title":"String pool vs new String","g":"Strings & numbers","hook":"The string pool is the shared kettle in the kitchen: one \"hello\" for everyone. new String is a personal thermos — separate even with the same tea.","more":"String literals are stored in the **String pool** (part of the heap). Identical literals reference the same object, so `\"a\" == \"a\"` is true. `new String(\"a\")` forcibly creates a new object on the regular heap — `==` against a literal yields `false`.\n\nWhy the pool: strings are immutable and occur in huge numbers, so reuse saves memory. `intern()` returns the canonical reference from the pool.\n\nGotchas: never compare strings with `==` — only `equals()`. `new String()` is almost always pointless (garbage plus a lost pool reference). Concatenation of literals at compile time also lands in the pool, but concatenation through a variable does not.","caps":["The literal \"hi\" goes into the shared string pool inside the heap.","A second identical literal reuses the existing pool object — it doesn't create a new one.","new String(\"hi\") creates another object on the heap, separate from the pool.","a and b point to the same object in the pool; c points to its own on the heap.","== compares references: a==b is true, a==c is false.","equals looks at the text, not the reference — use it to compare strings."]},"integer-cache":{"title":"Integer cache (-128..127)","g":"Strings & numbers","hook":"Small change −128..127 sits in the shared till (==), but big bills are printed fresh each time — compare by face value (equals).","more":"`Integer.valueOf()` (and autoboxing) caches objects for the range **-128..127**. Within those bounds, boxing returns the same object, so `Integer a=100, b=100; a==b` → `true`, while for `200` → `false` (different objects).\n\nWhy: small numbers are used most often, so the cache saves allocations. The upper bound can be raised via `-XX:AutoBoxCacheMax`.\n\nGotchas: a classic trap in interviews and in production. Compare `Integer` with `equals()` or unbox to `int`. `==` on wrappers compares references, and the cache makes the bug intermittent — it works up to 127 and breaks above it.","caps":["Autoboxing int → Integer goes through Integer.valueOf().","Values -128..127 sit in the cache as ready-made objects.","127 is taken from the cache twice — both references point to one object.","a==b compares references to the same object → true.","128 is outside the cache — a NEW object is created on the heap each time.","x==y → false (different references), but equals → true. Compare with equals."]},"float-precision":{"title":"Why 0.1+0.2 ≠ 0.3","g":"Strings & numbers","hook":"0.1 in binary is like 1/3 in decimal: the endless tail gets chopped off, and the cents don't add up.","more":"`double`/`float` are binary floating point (IEEE 754). Numbers like 0.1 and 0.2 are infinite in binary and are stored rounded, just like 1/3 in decimal. The sum of the approximations gives `0.30000000000000004`, not exactly 0.3.\n\nWhy: the format saves memory and is fast in hardware, but it represents exactly only fractions whose denominator is a power of two.\n\nGotchas: don't compare with `==` — use a threshold `Math.abs(a-b) < eps`. For money and finance, use **only `BigDecimal`** (with an explicit scale and RoundingMode), otherwise the cents will drift. Using `double` for money is a bug.","caps":["The computer only knows powers of 1/2: halves, quarters, and so on.","In binary, 0.1 is an infinite fraction, and the last bit gets cut off.","So double stores not 0.1 but the closest value — just slightly off.","Add two imprecise numbers and the errors accumulate rather than cancel.","The comparison with 0.3 fails: the tail didn't match.","For money use BigDecimal with a string — it computes in decimal, exactly."]},"equals-hashcode":{"title":"equals and hashCode","g":"Language contracts","hook":"hashCode is the locker number, equals is your key. If you move by equals, change the locker too, or your stuff gets lost.","more":"`equals()` defines logical equality of objects, `hashCode()` is the numeric hash for buckets in `HashMap`/`HashSet`. The contract: **equal objects must have equal hashCodes**; the reverse is not required (collisions are allowed).\n\nWhy together: hash collections first find the bucket by `hashCode`, then verify inside it via `equals`. Override one — override the other too, otherwise the object gets \"lost\" in the `HashMap`.\n\nGotchas: use the same fields in both; don't include mutable fields if the object is a key (otherwise you can't find it after a change). Don't confuse with `==` (reference comparison). It's convenient to generate via `Objects.equals`/`Objects.hash`.","caps":["HashMap puts the object into a bucket — hashCode computes the address.","The key went into bucket #2 and is remembered there.","With the same key: hashCode is honest, leads to bucket #2 — found it.","Contract broken: equal keys produce different hashCodes.","In the wrong bucket equals is never even called — missed.","Bottom line: equal objects must have equal hashCodes."]},"comparable-comparator":{"title":"Comparable vs Comparator","g":"Language contracts","hook":"Comparable — height is built into the person. Comparator — a separate judge with their own ruler, measuring however they like.","more":"`Comparable<T>` is the object's own natural ordering: implemented inside the class via the `compareTo` method. `Comparator<T>` is an external ordering: a separate object with `compare(a,b)` that doesn't touch the class.\n\nWhy two: `Comparable` is for when a type has one obvious order (numbers, dates). `Comparator` is for when there are many orderings or the class is third-party / can't be changed. `Comparator` is more flexible: `comparing().thenComparing().reversed()`.\n\nGotchas: the contract is to return negative/0/positive; for `int` don't subtract (`a-b` overflows) — use `Integer.compare`. Keep `compareTo` consistent with `equals`, otherwise `TreeSet` will behave unexpectedly.","caps":["The User class knows its one native order — it compares itself.","Comparable is the class's built-in natural order, and there's only one.","What if you also need to sort by name and by balance?","Comparator is an external ordering object — no need to touch the class itself.","Each Comparator gives its own order — make as many as you need.","Bottom line: Comparable — one native order inside; Comparator — many on the outside."]},"optional-usage":{"title":"Optional: why","g":"Language contracts","hook":"Optional is a box marked \"may be empty\": it honestly warns you to open it carefully, instead of silently smacking you in the face with a null.","more":"`Optional<T>` is a wrapper container that explicitly expresses \"the value may be absent\". Instead of returning `null`, the method returns an `Optional`, and the caller is forced to handle the empty case via `map`, `filter`, `orElse`, `orElseThrow`.\n\nWhy: it documents the possible absence right in the signature and nudges you not to forget the check, lowering the risk of a `NullPointerException`.\n\nGotchas: `Optional` is for **return values**, not for fields, parameters, or collections (use an empty list for emptiness). Don't call `o.get()` without a check — that's just another NPE. `Optional.of(null)` throws; for a possibly-null value use `ofNullable`.","caps":["The method returned null — and you didn't know it, calling .getName() right on the emptiness.","The User type stays silent: there might be a user inside, or null — you only find out at runtime.","Optional<User> in the type honestly says: the value may not be there.","map gets the name if present, orElse supplies a fallback — no if, no NPE.","Optional is for a method's return value. Not for fields and parameters."]},"checked-unchecked":{"title":"Checked vs unchecked","g":"Language contracts","hook":"Checked — an umbrella you're forced to grab in advance. Unchecked — you slipped on flat ground: your own fault, nobody warned you.","more":"**Checked** (subclasses of `Exception` but not `RuntimeException`) are forced by the compiler to be handled: either `try/catch` or `throws` in the signature — for example `IOException`. **Unchecked** (`RuntimeException` and its descendants, plus `Error`) are not enforced by the compiler — `NullPointerException`, `IllegalArgumentException`.\n\nThe idea: checked is for expected external failures you can recover from (file, network); unchecked is for bugs in the code and broken contracts.\n\nGotchas: don't swallow exceptions with an empty `catch`. In practice many frameworks (Spring, and the style in Quarkus) lean toward unchecked to avoid cluttering signatures. You shouldn't catch `Error` (e.g. `OutOfMemoryError`).","caps":["Throwable splits into checked and unchecked (RuntimeException and Error).","Checked — an expected failure from outside: file, network, DB can drop out.","The compiler won't let it pass: either try-catch or throws — you must handle it.","Unchecked — programmer bugs: null, index out of bounds, integer /0.","You aren't required to catch them — the compiler is silent, fix the bug in the code.","Something broke outside → catch it. You messed up → fix the code."]},"try-with-resources":{"title":"try-with-resources","g":"Language contracts","hook":"A tap declared in the try parentheses closes itself on the way out — even if you trip in the room and run out screaming.","more":"Any object implementing **AutoCloseable** that is declared in the `try (...)` parentheses is automatically closed by the JVM via `close()` when the block exits — normally or by exception. The compiler expands this into a `try/finally` under the hood.\n\nWhy: it guarantees resources (files, sockets, JDBC connections) are released without a manual `finally`, which is easy to forget or get wrong.\n\nGotchas: resources are closed in the **reverse order** of declaration. If `close()` throws, that exception becomes **suppressed** — the primary exception from the body isn't lost, and the extra one is reachable via `getSuppressed()`.","caps":["Opened resources: a file, then a socket — each must be closed.","Inside the try an exception blows up — normal code is interrupted.","try-with-resources closes everything itself in REVERSE order.","If close() also throws — we don't lose the main one, the second is attached as suppressed.","By hand in finally it's easy to forget close — the resource leaks.","Conclusion: try-with-resources is more reliable than a manual finally."]},"record-immutable":{"title":"record: immutability","g":"Language contracts","hook":"A record is a passport photo: the data is frozen forever — want different data, take a new shot.","more":"`record` generates `final` fields, a canonical constructor, getters, and `equals`/`hashCode`/`toString`. The record object itself is immutable: after creation the fields can't be reassigned.\n\nWhy: compact **value objects** (DTOs, map keys) without boilerplate; immutability gives thread safety and predictability.\n\nGotchas: the immutability is **shallow**. If a field is a `List` or array, its contents can be changed from outside. For real protection, copy collections in the compact constructor and hand out `List.copyOf()`.","caps":["An old-style class: a ton of boilerplate for a simple pair of fields.","A record is one line, and the compiler writes everything for you.","All record fields are final, set once in the constructor.","Want a different value — a new object; the old one stays intact.","An immutable value is safe to read from many threads.","Bottom line: record is perfect for DTOs and value objects."]},"tls-handshake":{"title":"TLS handshake","g":"Networking & Security","hook":"Before talking, both sides swap a secret handshake and a password — from then on they whisper, and an outsider can't follow.","more":"A TLS handshake negotiates a secure channel before any data flows. The client sends a ClientHello (supported versions, ciphers, random), and the server replies with its certificate and chosen parameters. Using **asymmetric cryptography** (the key from the certificate), the two sides safely agree on a shared **symmetric session key**, which then encrypts the traffic fast.\n\nWhy this way: asymmetric crypto is slow, so it's used only to exchange the key, while the stream itself is encrypted with a fast symmetric algorithm. The CA-signed certificate proves the server is genuine.\n\nGotchas: in **TLS 1.2** the handshake takes 2 round-trips — a noticeable delay. **TLS 1.3** cut it to 1 RTT (and 0-RTT on resumption) and dropped weak ciphers. Watch certificate validity and the chain — a frequent cause of outages.","caps":["Client knocks on the server: \"Hi, let's go encrypted\"","Server presents its passport — a certificate stamped by a CA","Client verifies the stamp with the CA — the passport is genuine ✓","A key pair: the public key is visible to all, the private one stays only on the server","They exchange in the open — and each derives the SAME secret independently","One shared fast symmetric key — the tunnel is encrypted"]},"reverse-proxy-lb":{"title":"Reverse proxy and load balancing","g":"Networking & Security","hook":"A host at the door: greets every guest and seats them with whichever waiter is free, so nobody gets overwhelmed.","more":"A **reverse proxy** is a mediator server in front of the backends: the client talks to it, and it forwards the request inward and returns the response. It's a convenient place for TLS termination, caching, compression, and URL-based routing. A **load balancer** spreads traffic across several instances (round-robin, least-connections, hash-based).\n\nWhy: a single entry point, hidden network topology, horizontal scaling, and fault tolerance — a dead instance is taken out via health checks.\n\nGotchas: you need **sticky sessions** or external session storage, otherwise the user bounces between nodes. L4 (TCP) balancing is faster, but L7 (HTTP) is smarter at routing. The balancer itself is a single point of failure — run it redundantly. Common tools: Nginx, HAProxy, Envoy.","caps":["A crowd of clients piles onto a heap of servers — chaos, no one knows where to go","Add a single entry point — nginx. Everyone hits it, and it hands work out","At the edge nginx does TLS itself 🔒, serves static 📦, and holds a cache","Requests flow evenly: each instance gets its share — load balancing","Health checks 🔎 ping every node: alive? All responded ✓","app2 is down ✗ — nginx pulls it from rotation, routes only to live nodes"]},"rest-soap-graphql-grpc":{"title":"REST / SOAP / GraphQL / gRPC","g":"Formats","hook":"REST is an à la carte menu, SOAP is a stamped form, GraphQL is build-your-own plate, gRPC is a teleport between chefs.","more":"These are API styles. **REST** — resources and HTTP verbs (GET/POST), JSON, simple and cacheable; downside is over/under-fetching and lots of endpoints. **SOAP** — strict XML with a WSDL contract, heavyweight, but gives rigid typing and security standards (lives in banks/legacy).\n\n**GraphQL** — a single endpoint where the client specifies exactly the fields it needs; solves over-fetching and is handy for frontends. Downside — harder to cache and easy to trigger heavy nested queries (N+1). **gRPC** — binary protobuf over HTTP/2, very fast, with streaming; ideal for **service-to-service** traffic, but awkward straight from the browser.\n\nChoice: REST for public APIs, gRPC inside microservices, GraphQL for flexible frontends, SOAP where a counterparty demands it.","caps":["Four ways for services to talk to each other — each different","REST: address = resource, HTTP verbs, JSON response — simple","SOAP: a strict WSDL contract and a heavy XML envelope","GraphQL: one entry point, the client picks exactly the fields it needs 🔎","gRPC: binary protobuf over HTTP/2 — fast service-to-service ⚡","Speed: gRPC is the fastest, SOAP the heaviest"]},"wbf-cors-preflight":{"title":"CORS preflight (OPTIONS)","g":"Networking & Security","hook":"The browser is a guard at the door: before a \"non-standard\" request it first sends an OPTIONS scout to ask the server \"am I allowed?\". No permission — and the real request never even leaves.","more":"**CORS** is a rule of the browser itself, not the server. The server would always receive the request; the browser, by default, forbids JS from reading a response from another origin (scheme + domain + port).\n\n- A **simple request** (GET/POST with ordinary headers) goes out right away, but the browser hides the response if it lacks `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`.\n- A **\"complex\" one** (PUT/DELETE, `Content-Type: application/json`, custom headers) triggers a **preflight** — a separate `OPTIONS` BEFORE the real request.\n- On the preflight the server replies with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin/Methods/Headers`; `Access-Control-Max-Age` caches the permission so OPTIONS isn't sent every time.\n- **Important:** CORS doesn't protect the server — it protects the user in the browser. curl and the backend ignore CORS entirely.","caps":["A page on shop.com wants to call an API on api.bank.com — a different origin. The browser gets suspicious.","The request is \"complex\": method PUT and Content-Type application/json. The browser won't let it through directly.","First the browser sends a scout itself — OPTIONS, asking \"may I do PUT with this header?\".","The server replies with permission: this origin is allowed, PUT is allowed, and remember this for 600 seconds.","Permission granted — only now does the real PUT go out, and the browser hands the response to the script.","Bottom line: preflight is the browser's \"may I?\". CORS protects not the server but the user in the browser."]},"wbf-oauth-pkce":{"title":"OAuth2 Authorization Code + PKCE","g":"Networking & Security","hook":"How to get the hotel key without trusting the courier. You first think up a secret, hand over only its \"fingerprint\", and at the end you prove the secret is yours — an intercepted code without the secret is useless.","more":"**Authorization Code + PKCE** is the standard flow for public clients (SPAs, mobile) that can't keep a client_secret.\n\n- **PKCE** = Proof Key for Code Exchange. The client generates a random `code_verifier` and computes `code_challenge = SHA256(verifier)`.\n- At the start only the **challenge** (the hash) is sent. The authorization server remembers it.\n- A short-lived **authorization code** comes back via redirect — useless on its own.\n- When exchanging the code for a token, the client sends the original **verifier**; the server checks `SHA256(verifier) == challenge`.\n- An attacker who grabs the code doesn't know the verifier → can't exchange it. That closes the authorization-code interception attack without a client_secret.\n- Tokens arrive over the **back-channel** (server-to-server at exchange time), not via the redirect URL.","caps":["An SPA wants a token, but storing a client_secret in the browser is impossible — it'd be stolen from the page source.","The client thinks up a random verifier and computes its hash — the challenge. Only the hash goes out.","The user is sent to the auth server to log in, passing only the challenge. The server remembers it.","After login a short code arrives back via redirect. On its own it's useless.","The client swaps the code for a token, attaching the original verifier. The server checks: SHA256(verifier)==challenge.","Bottom line: token issued. An attacker who grabbed the code has no verifier — the exchange fails for them."]},"wbf-http-caching-etag":{"title":"HTTP caching: ETag and 304","g":"Networking & Security","hook":"The librarian hands you a book with an inventory number (ETag). Next time you don't carry the book back — you just ask \"is the number still the same?\". If it matches, they wave \"keep yours\" and don't print a new copy.","more":"An **ETag** is a fingerprint (version) of the response. It lets you skip re-downloading the body when it hasn't changed.\n\n- The first response carries `ETag: \"v1\"` and `Cache-Control` (how long the response stays fresh).\n- While the response is **fresh** (per max-age) the browser serves it from cache with no network at all.\n- Once freshness expires, the browser makes a **conditional request**: `If-None-Match: \"v1\"`.\n- If nothing changed, the server replies **304 Not Modified** with no body. Traffic is saved and the client reuses the old copy.\n- If it changed, a **200** arrives with the new body and a new ETag.\n- `Cache-Control` governs freshness (`max-age`, `no-cache`, `private`); ETag governs **revalidation**. These are two distinct levers that work together.","caps":["The client requests /profile for the first time. The full body travels — that's costly in traffic.","The response comes with the ETag fingerprint \"v1\" and a freshness lifetime. The client caches it.","While the response is fresh per max-age, a repeat request doesn't hit the network at all — served from cache.","Freshness expired — the client doesn't re-download but asks: \"do you still have v1?\".","Nothing changed — the server sends an empty 304. No body, the client reuses the old copy.","If the data changed — a 200 arrives with a new body and a new ETag \"v2\". The cycle repeats."]},"wbf-websocket-upgrade":{"title":"WebSocket: upgrade and full-duplex","g":"Networking & Security","hook":"HTTP is a walkie-talkie: press, speak, release, wait for a reply. WebSocket is an open phone line: you dial once, and after that both sides talk whenever they want without redialing.","more":"**WebSocket** gives a persistent bidirectional channel over a single TCP connection.\n\n- It starts as an ordinary HTTP request with `Upgrade: websocket` and a `Sec-WebSocket-Key` header.\n- The server replies **101 Switching Protocols**; it's the same TCP session, but the protocol switched from HTTP to WebSocket.\n- After that data travels in **frames**, not request-response. The server can push at any moment — no polling.\n- **Full-duplex**: client and server write simultaneously and independently.\n- Unlike SSE, the channel is two-way and binary; unlike polling, there are no constant reconnects.\n- Downsides: a stateful connection (harder to balance/scale), you need `wss://` behind TLS, and reconnect handling.","caps":["Plain HTTP: every message is a new request-response. For live pushes that means polling and latency.","The client sends an ordinary HTTP request, but asks to switch protocols: Upgrade: websocket.","The server agrees: replies 101 Switching Protocols. Same TCP connection, but now it's WebSocket.","Now the exchange runs in frames. The server can push on its own without waiting for a request — no polling.","Full-duplex: both write at once over one line, independently of each other.","Bottom line: one connection — a live two-way stream. The price: a stateful connection, reconnects, wss behind TLS."]},"wbf-idempotency-key":{"title":"Idempotency-Key on the server","g":"Networking & Security","hook":"A coat-check ticket for a payment. You come back with the same ticket and get the same coat — they don't sew a second one. The network can retry the request all it wants — the money is charged once.","more":"An **idempotency-key** protects a non-idempotent POST from running twice on retries (timeout, dropped connection, client repeat).\n\n- The client generates a unique key (UUID) for **one business operation** and sends it in a header.\n- The server inserts the key atomically (e.g. `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT` / a unique index). Winning the insert means it runs the operation and **stores the response** alongside the key.\n- A repeat with the same key → the insert fails on conflict → the server **returns the stored response** without running the operation again.\n- A concurrent duplicate, while the first is still in flight, is usually held on a lock / `HTTP 409` until completion.\n- Keys **expire** (TTL), otherwise the table grows forever.\n- Accepting the header isn't enough — without dedup and a stored response there's no protection: it's the server that makes the request idempotent.","caps":["POST /payments isn't idempotent. If the response is lost, the client retries — and fears double-charging.","Fix: the client puts a unique key for this operation and sends it in a header.","The server atomically inserts K1 into a table. The insert succeeded — the key is new, safe to proceed.","The server charges the money and stores the response next to key K1 — for the future.","A retry arrives with the same K1. The insert fails on conflict — the operation is NOT repeated.","Bottom line: the server returns the stored response. The money is charged once. Accepting the header isn't enough — you need dedup."]},"hashmap-internals":{"title":"HashMap under the hood","g":"Collections","hook":"A storage room: the slot number is computed from the bag. Same number — items hang clustered on one hook.","more":"**HashMap** is an array of buckets. A key's `hashCode()` produces a bucket index (after mixing and `& (n-1)`). Collisions within a bucket form a linked list, and once a bucket holds ≥8 elements with capacity ≥64 that list converts into a **red-black tree** (O(log n) instead of O(n)).\n\nWhy: amortized O(1) for get/put. When fill exceeds the **load factor** (0.75) the array doubles and a rehash runs.\n\nGotchas: a mutable key or inconsistent `equals`/`hashCode` breaks lookups. A poor hashCode collapses everything into one bucket. HashMap is not thread-safe — concurrent writes corrupt the structure.","caps":["Key: hashCode() yields a hash, it gets mixed and the low bits are taken → bucket number.","The table is a row of buckets. The key lands in the slot at the computed number.","Two keys in one bucket → a chain. The right one is found by comparing with equals.","size crossed the 0.75×capacity threshold → resize: twice the buckets, rehash everything.","A chain reached 8 with a table ≥64 → treeify into a tree, lookup O(log n)."]},"arraylist-linkedlist":{"title":"ArrayList vs LinkedList","g":"Collections","hook":"ArrayList — numbered shelves (grab any one). LinkedList — a chain of people holding hands (search one by one).","more":"**ArrayList** is a dynamic array: elements sit contiguously in memory. Index access is O(1), insertion/removal in the middle is O(n) (shifting the tail), but appending at the end is amortized O(1).\n\n**LinkedList** is a doubly linked list of nodes with references. Insertion/removal at an already-found node is O(1), but index access is O(n) — you have to walk the references.\n\nIn practice you almost always pick **ArrayList**: cache locality makes it faster even where LinkedList has better theoretical complexity. LinkedList is rarely justified — as a Deque/queue with frequent insertions at the ends.","caps":["Two collections store the same data differently.","Index access: the array jumps straight there, the nodes walk on foot.","Inserting into the middle of the array shifts the whole tail to the right.","LinkedList: the iterator is already in place — just relink the references.","The array sits contiguously in cache — the nodes are scattered across memory.","Bottom line: pick ArrayList, it's almost always faster."]},"failfast-iter":{"title":"Fail-fast iterator","g":"Collections","hook":"You're counting guests at the table, someone gets up and leaves — you lose count, you shout \"stop!\" instead of lying on.","more":"A **fail-fast iterator** — a collection's iterator (ArrayList, HashMap) records `modCount` at creation. If the collection is structurally modified NOT through the iterator itself during traversal, the next `next()` throws `ConcurrentModificationException`.\n\nWhy: it's early bug detection, not a guarantee. Better to fail immediately than to silently return corrupted data or loop forever.\n\nGotchas: CME also catches single-threaded modification (removing inside a plain for-each). Remove via `iterator.remove()` or `removeIf`. For concurrent access — `ConcurrentHashMap`, whose iterators are weakly consistent and don't fail.","caps":["list.modCount = 3. The iterator saves a snapshot expectedModCount = 3.","Iterating via the iterator: cursor on A, snapshot expected = 3.","list.remove(B) bypassing the iterator: modCount became 4, snapshot stayed 3.","next() checks the snapshot: expected 3 ≠ modCount 4 → they diverged.","Divergence → next() throws ConcurrentModificationException.","Correct: remove via it.remove() or removeIf() — the snapshot is updated."]},"alf-binary-search":{"title":"Binary Search","g":"Searching","hook":"Guess a number from 1 to 100: always aim for the middle — the range gets cut in half each time.","more":"**Binary search** works ONLY on sorted data and runs in O(log n).\n- Keep bounds lo and hi, inspect the element at the midpoint mid.\n- If it's smaller than the target, drop the left half; otherwise drop the right.\n- Every step **halves** the candidate set: a million-element array needs only ~20 comparisons.\n- The classic trap is overflow in mid=(lo+hi)/2 — prefer lo+(hi-lo)/2.\nThat's exactly why it's log n: how many times you can halve n before you're down to one.","caps":["Given a sorted array, we're looking for 7 — a linear scan would have to walk the whole thing.","Set bounds lo and hi at the ends and aim straight for the middle.","The midpoint holds 7 — but to show the principle: if it were 9, the target is smaller, so we cut the right half.","One step, and half the array is gone — the candidate set is cut in two.","Each halving is a step down the ladder: 1M elements = only ~20 steps.","Result: found 7 in a couple of comparisons instead of scanning everything — that's O(log n)."]},"alf-bfs":{"title":"BFS — Breadth-First Search","g":"Graphs","hook":"A drop in water: the ripple spreads in rings — first all the neighbors, then the neighbors' neighbors.","more":"**BFS** traverses a graph in \"rings\" outward from the start, backed by a **queue** (FIFO).\n- Enqueue the start, mark it visited.\n- Dequeue a vertex, enqueue all of its unvisited neighbors.\n- This way you fan out from the center level by level.\nKey property: in an **unweighted** graph, BFS finds the shortest path (fewest edges), because vertices are reached strictly in increasing order of distance. Complexity O(V+E).","caps":["A graph of vertices; we start from A and want to visit everyone by \"distance in edges\".","Enqueue A and mark it visited — the queue is our \"waiting list\".","Dequeue A; all of its direct neighbors drop into the queue — that's the first ring.","Take B, then C — add their neighbors D and E: the second ring begins.","The wave spreads strictly by level: distance = ring number.","Result: BFS gives the shortest path by edge count in an unweighted graph in O(V+E)."]},"alf-dfs":{"title":"DFS — Depth-First Search","g":"Graphs","hook":"A maze with the rule \"go forward until you hit a wall, then back up to the last fork\".","more":"**DFS** dives deep along a single branch as far as it can, then backtracks.\n- Implemented with recursion or an explicit **stack** (LIFO).\n- On hitting a dead end or a visited vertex, return to the nearest fork and try another path.\nDFS underpins topological sort, finding connected components, and cycle detection. The Java hazard: **deep recursion** overflows the call stack (StackOverflowError) on long chains. Complexity O(V+E).","caps":["Same graph, but now we don't fan out in rings — we dive deep along one branch.","From A we go to B, from B straight to D — as long as there's depth, we don't turn back.","D is a dead end. Pop it off the stack and back up to the fork at B.","B has no more neighbors — back up further, to A, and try the unvisited C.","The stack grows on the dive and shrinks on the backtrack — in Java that's the recursion call stack.","Result: DFS does depth-with-backtrack, the basis for cycles and topo-sort, in O(V+E)."]},"alf-hash-collision":{"title":"Hash Collisions and Chaining","g":"Hash Tables","hook":"A coat check: slot number = hash % N. Two coats in one slot — hang them on the same hook in a chain.","more":"**A hash table** gives O(1) by turning a key into a bucket index: bucket = hash(key) % capacity.\n- Different keys can map to the **same index** — that's a collision.\n- With chaining, one bucket holds a linked list (in Java's HashMap, a tree once a bucket reaches ≥8 entries).\n- As long as collisions stay rare, get/put is O(1); if every key lands in one bucket, lookup degrades to O(n).\nThat's why a good hash function and the **load factor** matter (default 0.75): when the table fills up it resizes and rehashes entries to keep chains short.","caps":["Turn the key into a number via the hash, take it modulo the bucket count — that's the index.","Each key lands right in its own bucket — access in a single step, O(1).","Trouble: the new key \"owl\" produced the same index 1 as \"cat\" — a collision.","Don't overwrite — hang \"owl\" in a chain next to \"cat\" in the same bucket.","If all keys collapse into one bucket, lookup walks the list — O(1) turns into O(n).","The rescue: at load factor 0.75 the table grows and rehashes — chains are short again."]},"alf-dijkstra":{"title":"Dijkstra — Shortest Path","g":"Graphs","hook":"A greedy courier always drives to the nearest still-open city and from there updates the price tags for its neighbors.","more":"**Dijkstra** finds shortest paths from one source in a weighted graph.\n- Keep dist[]: 0 for the start, ∞ for everyone else.\n- Pull the vertex with the smallest known distance from a **priority queue**.\n- Do a **relaxation**: if going through it reaches a neighbor more cheaply than recorded, update the neighbor's dist.\n- Never touch a closed vertex again — its minimum is final.\nComplexity with a heap is O((V+E) log V). It breaks on **negative edges** — there greed guesses wrong, and you need Bellman-Ford.","caps":["A weighted graph: the numbers on edges are costs. We want the shortest path from A.","Start A=0, everyone else infinity — we know nothing yet.","Take the nearest vertex A and relax its neighbors: B=2, C=5.","The queue hands back the minimum — B(2). Through B, C costs only 2+1=3, less than 5: update it.","Never touch a closed vertex again — its number is finally minimal.","Result: greed over a heap yields shortest paths, but a negative edge breaks it."]},"alf-sliding-window":{"title":"Sliding Window","g":"Techniques","hook":"A train window: you don't repaint the scenery from scratch — one object leaves on the left, a new one enters on the right.","more":"**The sliding window** turns a naive O(n·k) scan of subarrays into a single O(n) pass.\n- Keep two pointers — the left and right edges of the window.\n- Move the right edge, adding the new element into the running result (sum, count).\n- When the condition breaks (window too big or invalid), move the left edge, subtracting an element.\nThe key is that you **don't recompute** the window's contents from scratch — you adjust them incrementally on each shift. Used for \"max sum of a window of size k\", \"longest substring without repeats\", and so on.","caps":["Task: the max sum of 3 consecutive numbers. Naively — recompute each window from scratch.","First window [2,1,5], compute the sum once = 8.","Shift the window right: DON'T recompute — subtract the departing 2, add the entering 1.","Another shift: 1 leaves, 3 enters — one addition and one subtraction instead of three.","The window rolls along the array like a train car — each step costs O(1), not O(k).","Result: a single pass instead of a nested scan — O(n) instead of O(n·k)."]},"alf-two-pointers":{"title":"Two Pointers","g":"Techniques","hook":"Two pointers converge from the ends of a sorted row: sum too small — left steps right; too big — right steps left.","more":"**Two pointers** replace a nested O(n²) loop with a single O(n) pass over a sorted array.\n- Put one pointer at the start, the other at the end.\n- Compute the combination (say, the pair's sum) and, based on the result, move ONE of the pointers inward.\n- Each step rules out provably useless options, so n moves are enough.\nClassics — \"find a pair with a given sum\", merging two sorted arrays, palindrome check. The key precondition: the data is sorted (or the order is meaningful in itself).","caps":["A sorted array; we want a pair summing to 10. Naively — try every pair, O(n²).","Put L at the start, R at the end, and add the ends: 1+9=10.","To show the principle on a different target: if the sum is SMALL, move the left pointer right to pick up more.","If the sum is BIG, move the right pointer left to dial it down.","The pointers move toward each other — each step shifts exactly one of them, ≤ n moves total.","Result: found the pair in a single pass — O(n) instead of O(n²), but it needs a sorted array."]},"alf-lru-cache":{"title":"LRU Cache in O(1)","g":"Data Structures","hook":"A stack of plates: take one — put it on top; out of room — toss the bottom one, untouched the longest.","more":"**An LRU cache** keeps the N most recently used items and evicts the \"least recently used\".\n- The combo: a **HashMap** gives O(1) lookup by key, a **doubly linked list** gives O(1) node movement.\n- get(key): find the node via the map, unlink it from the list, and move it to the \"head\" (freshest).\n- put(key): add at the head; if the size is exceeded, remove the node at the \"tail\" and from the map.\nThe tail is the eviction candidate, the head is the freshest. In Java the ready-made answer is **LinkedHashMap** with accessOrder=true and an overridden removeEldestEntry.","caps":["A cache of 3 elements: head — the freshest, tail — the eviction candidate.","Two structures together: the HashMap finds the node in O(1), the list moves it in O(1).","get(C): find C via the map, unlink it from the middle, and move it to the head.","put(D) on a full cache: the new node goes to the head.","Limit exceeded — evict the tail B (untouched the longest) from both the list and the map.","Result: both get and put in O(1); in Java that's LinkedHashMap(accessOrder=true) + removeEldestEntry."]},"git-rebase":{"title":"rebase vs merge","g":"Git","hook":"Merge — you tie two trails together with a fork knot. Rebase — you rewrite your path cleanly as one straight road.","more":"**Merge** creates a merge commit that joins two branches — history branches out but is preserved exactly as it was. **Rebase** replays your commits on top of someone else's tip, rewriting them (new hashes) — history becomes linear, as if you had started from a fresh main.\n\nRebase — for a clean, readable history without a web of merge commits; merge — for honestly recording the fact that a merge happened.\n\nGotcha: **never rebase already-pushed/shared branches** — the rewritten hashes break history for your colleagues. Rebase is for a local private branch before push; merge is for integrating into a shared main.","caps":["feature branched off main at commit C2","while you worked on feature, main moved ahead: C3, C4","merge: new commit M ties C4 and F2 together, history branches","rebase: F1,F2 replayed ON TOP of C4 as F1', F2' with new hashes","result: merge branches and is honest, rebase gives a straight line","rule: do NOT rebase a shared branch — others' hashes will break 🔒"]},"git-cherrypick":{"title":"cherry-pick","g":"Git","hook":"Cherry-pick — you take one cherry off someone else's cake and put it on yours, without taking the whole cake.","more":"**cherry-pick** takes one (or several) specific commit from another branch and applies it as a new commit on the current one — with a new hash but the same diff.\n\nUseful when you need only a pinpoint change, not the whole branch: backporting a hotfix into a release branch, moving a single fix without the rest of the WIP.\n\nGotcha: it easily breeds **duplicates** — the same change lives as two commits in different branches, which can cause conflicts on a later merge. If a commit depends on changes from earlier commits, picking it alone won't build. For regular transfers, prefer merge/rebase.","caps":["Two branches. feature has the commit C we need; we don't want X and Y","We only need C. Merge would drag X and Y along too — and we don't want that","cherry-pick C: copy ONLY C into the current branch main","A new commit C' (new hash) lands on main. The original C stays in feature","Result: only C moved to main as a copy. X and Y stayed in feature"]},"git-bisect":{"title":"git bisect","g":"Git","hook":"Bisect — you hunt for the broken bulb in a string of lights: split in half, test, split again — and find it fast.","more":"**git bisect** finds the commit that broke something via binary search over history. You mark a known-good and a known-bad commit — git checks out the midpoint, you test and answer good/bad, and the range is halved. In log2(N) steps you find the culprit.\n\nWhy: instead of manually checking hundreds of commits — about 10 checks for a thousand.\n\nGotcha: you need a **reliable, reproducible test** at each step. You can automate it: `git bisect run ./test.sh` and git runs it for you. Commits that don't build get in the way (mark them `skip`), and flaky checks throw off the result.","caps":["A row of commits: works on the left, broken on the right. Where's the first bad one?","Bisect checks out the MIDDLE and asks: is it already broken here?","Middle WORKS → bug is to the right. Discard the left half, check out c6.","c6 is BROKEN → bug is between c4 and c6. Only c5 left to check.","Each step throws away half — the search zone shrinks.","1000 commits — only ~10 checks: log2(n) steps to the culprit."]},"gitf-three-areas":{"title":"Three areas and staging","g":"Git","hook":"Checkout lane: the cart (working directory), the belt at the cashier (index), the receipt (commit)","more":"Git keeps a file in three places at once:\n- **Working directory** — what you actually edit on disk.\n- **Index (staging)** — the snapshot that will go into the NEXT commit. `git add` copies the file's version here.\n- **Repository (.git)** — frozen commits.\n\nKey point: `git add` captures the file's CONTENT into the index right now. If you change the file after add, the version from the index goes into the commit, not your latest edit (you need another add). `git status` shows both boundaries: staged and not staged. `git diff` — working directory vs index, `git diff --staged` — index vs the last commit.","caps":["A file lives in three states at once: on disk, in the index, and in the last commit.","You edit the file — only the working directory changes; the index and commit are still old.","git add copies the current version into the index — that's «staging for commit».","You edit the file AGAIN after add — the index still has v2, the disk already has v3.","git commit freezes what's in the index (v2). The fresh v3 is left out.","Takeaway: it commits the snapshot from the index, not «what's on disk». Changed it after add — add again."]},"gitf-reset-revert":{"title":"reset vs revert","g":"Git","hook":"reset — erase lines in the diary; revert — add a line that says «I take back the previous entry»","more":"Both «undo» a commit, but physically in different ways:\n- **reset** moves the branch pointer BACK. The commits don't vanish from the object database, but the branch no longer sees them. History is rewritten. `--soft` — keep the changes in the index, `--mixed` (default) — in the working directory, `--hard` — throw them away entirely.\n- **revert** creates a NEW commit that contains the inverse changes. History moves forward, nothing is lost.\n\nRule: on a **shared** (pushed) branch — use only `revert`, because reset rewrites history and breaks it for everyone. Locally, before push — `reset` is handier for cleanup.","caps":["History: branch main points to C, with B and A behind. We want to remove C.","reset --hard B moves the main pointer back to B. Commit C is detached.","Now history is shorter — as if C never existed. Dangerous on a shared branch.","Now the alternative — revert C. We do NOT move the pointer back.","revert creates a NEW commit C' with the inverse changes — history moves forward.","Result: reset = move the pointer (rewrite), revert = add an anti-commit (safe for a shared branch)."]},"gitf-fetch-pull":{"title":"fetch vs pull and fast-forward","g":"Git","hook":"fetch — download mail into your inbox; pull — download AND paste it straight into the diary","more":"**git fetch** downloads new commits from the server into local remote branches (`origin/main`), but does NOT touch your working branch. Safe — you can look at what arrived.\n\n**git pull** = `fetch` + `merge` (or `rebase`). It merges into the current branch right away.\n\n- **Fast-forward** — when your branch hasn't diverged, just fallen behind. Git moves the pointer forward in a straight line, with no merge commit.\n- If both sides made commits, the branches diverged — a plain `pull` creates a merge commit. `pull --rebase` instead replays your local commits ON TOP of the server's — a linear history without extra merge nodes.","caps":["Locally main is on C. On the server D, E appeared that you don't have yet.","git fetch downloads D, E into origin/main. Your working main stays put.","You did NOT make your own commits — the branch just fell behind. This is the fast-forward case.","pull here just moves the main pointer forward to E — fast-forward, no merge commit.","But if you ALSO committed (X) — the branches diverged. A plain pull joins them with merge commit M.","pull --rebase puts your X ON TOP of E — a linear history without a merge node."]},"gitf-detached-reflog":{"title":"detached HEAD and rescue via reflog","g":"Git","hook":"HEAD is a finger that usually rests on a branch's tag; when detached it points straight at a commit","more":"Normally **HEAD** points to a branch (`main`), and the branch points to a commit. When you run `git checkout <hash>` or `checkout <tag>`, HEAD starts pointing at the commit DIRECTLY — this is a **detached HEAD**.\n\nDanger: new commits here aren't attached to any branch. The moment you switch to another branch, NOTHING points to those commits, and the garbage collector will eventually delete them.\n\nThe rescue is **reflog**: Git keeps a log of where HEAD pointed at every moment. Even a «lost» commit stays in the reflog. `git reflog` → find the hash → `git branch save <hash>` creates a branch and attaches the commit back.","caps":["Normal: HEAD holds onto branch main, and main points to commit C.","git checkout <hash B> — HEAD detaches from the branch and points straight at the commit. Detached.","You make commit W — it hangs off HEAD, but no branch points to it.","You switch to main — now nothing points to W. Consider it lost.","But reflog remembers EVERY move of HEAD — the hash of W is in there.","git branch save <hash W> attaches the commit to a branch — rescued."]},"gitf-merge-conflict":{"title":"Reading a merge conflict","g":"Git","hook":"Two editors change the same line — Git doesn't choose for you, it places both versions side by side","more":"A conflict arises when two branches changed THE SAME line differently and Git can't decide which to take. It inserts markers into the file:\n- `<<<<<<< HEAD` — start of your version (current branch).\n- `=======` — separator.\n- `>>>>>>> other` — end of the other version (the branch being merged in).\n\nTo resolve = manually keep the text you want and REMOVE all three markers. Then `git add <file>` marks the conflict as resolved, and `git commit` (or `git merge --continue`) completes the merge. Important: the middle isn't an automatic «pick A or B» — you write the final text yourself; you can take pieces from both sides.","caps":["Branch main and branch feature changed the same line #3 differently.","git merge feature — Git doesn't know whose line is right, and declares a conflict.","It inserts both versions between markers in the file — yours on top, theirs below.","You manually write the final line and delete ALL three markers.","git add <file> marks the conflict as resolved.","git commit completes the merge — a merge commit M appears, history converges."]},"cicd-pipeline":{"title":"CI/CD pipeline","g":"CI/CD and deployment","hook":"CI/CD is a factory conveyor belt: code rides down the line, every station runs a test/build, and at the end it ships to prod on its own.","more":"**CI** (continuous integration) — on every push, automatically build, run tests and linters to catch breakage in the shared branch early. **CD** — the continuation: automatically deploy an artifact that passed the checks to staging/prod.\n\nA pipeline is a chain of stages (build → test → scan → deploy) where a failure in an early stage stops the rest. The goal is a fast, predictable path from commit to production without manual toil.\n\nGotchas: slow or **flaky tests** destroy trust in the pipeline, and people start ignoring it. Keep it fast, make failures unambiguous, and keep secrets out of the logs.","caps":["You push code to the repo — that hits the pipeline's \"start\" button.","The pipeline is a conveyor of three stages run in sequence.","Between test and deploy there's a gate — only green passes through.","Green tests — gate opens, we roll out to prod.","Red tests — stop: merge and deploy are blocked.","One branch point: green → prod, red → go fix it."]},"deploy-strategies":{"title":"Blue-green vs Canary","g":"CI/CD and deployment","hook":"Blue-green: two halls, move all guests into the new one at once. Canary: let a couple of guests in first — if they're fine, let everyone in.","more":"**Blue-green**: you keep two full environments — the old one (blue) and the new one (green). You deploy to green, run your checks, then switch all traffic over at once. Rollback is instant — just point routing back at blue.\n\n**Canary**: the new version first gets a small slice of traffic (1–5%), you watch metrics/errors and gradually ramp up to 100%. The blast radius is limited to a small group of users.\n\nBlue-green is simpler and gives instant rollback, but it doubles resources and switches everyone at once. Canary is safer for risky releases but requires solid monitoring and traffic management. The gotcha for both: DB migrations that are incompatible between versions.","caps":["Two strategies for rolling out a new version. The goal — to upgrade painlessly.","Blue-green: all traffic on blue (v1). Green (v2) is ready but empty.","Flip the switch all at once: all traffic moved to green v2.","Broke? Rollback is instant — flip the switch back to blue v1. ✓","Canary: send 5% to v2, the rest to v1. Watch the metrics 🔎","Metrics look good — expand v2: 5% at first, then smoothly to 50% and 100%. ✓"]},"serverless":{"title":"Serverless / FaaS","g":"CI/CD and deployment","hook":"Renting a car per trip instead of keeping your own garage: no call, no charge, and nothing to maintain.","more":"**Serverless / FaaS**: you deploy a function, and the provider spins it up on an event (HTTP, queue, timer), scales it under load, and shuts it down when idle. You pay for actual invocations and execution time, not for a server running around the clock.\n\nWhy: to drop infrastructure management and avoid paying for idle — great for irregular/spiky load and for gluing services together.\n\nGotchas: **cold start** (latency on the first call after idle), time/memory limits, **stateless** functions (state lives in external storage). Under steady high load it's often more expensive than a regular server.","caps":["A regular server runs 24/7 — you pay even when nobody's around","Serverless when idle: the function sleeps, the bill is zero","An event arrives — an HTTP request or a message from a queue","The provider spins the function up on demand — it does its job","Done — it shuts down. You pay only for that one invocation","Downside: the first \"from scratch\" start is slow — that's cold start"]},"soa-msa":{"title":"SOA vs microservices","g":"Architecture","hook":"SOA is a shared banquet hall with one kitchen; microservices are a food court where each stall has its own kitchen and register.","more":"Both split a system into services by business capability. **SOA** (classic) is usually coarser-grained and communicates through a centralized **ESB**, where the routing and orchestration logic lives. **Microservices** are smaller, each with its own DB, with lightweight communication (REST/gRPC/events) and no \"smart\" bus — the logic lives in the services, the network stays \"dumb.\"\n\nMSA emerged to enable independent deployment and scaling of teams and services; the ESB in SOA became a bottleneck and a single point of failure.\n\nMSA gotcha: distribution is expensive — networking, data consistency, observability. Splitting only makes sense when there's a real need for independence; otherwise a monolith is simpler.","caps":["Two architectures. Let's break down how they differ in plain terms.","SOA: services talk through a shared ESB and often share a single DB.","MSA: small services, each with its own DB.","Communication is direct over the network, with no central bus.","Deployment is separate: you update B — and A and C keep running.","The gist: SOA is a shared core, MSA is about independence of the pieces."]},"dof-container-vs-vm":{"title":"Container vs virtual machine","g":"Architecture","hook":"A VM is a separate house with its own foundation and walls for each resident. A container is a room in a shared house: thin walls, one foundation for all.","more":"**The key difference is where the isolation boundary runs.**\n- **VM**: a hypervisor slices the hardware into virtual machines, each with ITS OWN full guest OS and its own kernel. Heavy: gigabytes, minutes to start.\n- **Container**: processes share the SAME host kernel, and isolation comes from the kernel (namespaces + cgroups), not a second kernel. An image is just filesystem layers, with no OS inside.\n- Result: a container starts in seconds and weighs megabytes because it doesn't drag a guest OS along.\n- Cost: weaker isolation (shared kernel) and you can't run a foreign kernel (a Linux container won't run on a bare Windows kernel).\n- That's why in prod they're often combined: containers INSIDE a VM — the speed of containers plus the hard VM boundary between tenants.","caps":["The task: run three isolated applications on one server so they don't interfere with each other.","The VM path: the hypervisor gives each app ITS OWN full guest OS with its own kernel — rock-solid isolation, but heavy.","Three copies of an entire OS — that's gigabytes on disk and minutes to start each machine.","The container path: all apps share ONE host kernel, and the kernel itself provides isolation via namespaces and cgroups.","With no guest OS inside, a container weighs megabytes and starts in seconds.","Bottom line: a VM isolates with a whole second kernel (robust, heavy), a container with boundaries in a shared kernel (fast, lighter). In prod they're often stacked together."]},"dof-k8s-pod-deploy-svc":{"title":"Kubernetes: Pod, Deployment, Service","g":"Architecture","hook":"A Pod is one worker. A Deployment is the foreman who keeps exactly N workers and replaces the ones that drop. A Service is the crew's stable phone number that never changes, no matter who quits.","more":"**Three objects solve three different problems — don't mix up the levels.**\n- **Pod** — the smallest unit of execution: one (or a few tightly coupled) containers sharing an IP. A Pod is mortal: once it dies, its IP is gone forever.\n- **Deployment** — a declaration of desired state: \"I want 3 replicas of this Pod.\" The controller constantly reconciles desired against actual and fixes the difference (self-healing), plus it can do rolling updates.\n- **Service** — a stable entry point: one persistent virtual IP + DNS name fronting a changing set of Pods. It load-balances traffic across the live pods via a label selector.\n- Key idea: you do NOT talk to a Pod directly by its IP — it's ephemeral. You talk to the Service, and it finds the live pods itself.\n- That's why when a pod dies/moves, clients notice nothing: the Service address is unchanged.","caps":["A Pod is the smallest unit: container(s) sharing one IP. But a Pod is mortal — once it dies, its IP is gone forever.","To avoid babysitting that by hand, we create a Deployment: a declaration \"I want exactly 3 replicas of this Pod.\"","The controller constantly reconciles desired against actual. One pod died — it brings up a new one itself. That's self-healing.","But the new pod got a DIFFERENT IP. If a client was hitting the old IP directly — it's now knocking on the void.","The fix — a Service: one stable virtual IP and DNS name fronting the live pods via a label selector.","Bottom line: the client always calls the unchanging Service, which balances across the live pods. Pods move and die — the client never notices."]},"dof-docker-layer-cache":{"title":"Docker layer cache: Dockerfile ordering","g":"CI/CD and deployment","hook":"A build is like a stack of pancakes: they cook bottom to top. Change a lower pancake — and every pancake above it cooks again. Put what rarely changes at the bottom, what changes often at the top.","more":"**Each Dockerfile instruction = a layer, and the cache invalidates in a cascade from the top down.**\n- Docker caches the result of each layer. If a layer's inputs haven't changed — the layer is pulled from cache instantly.\n- **Cascade rule**: as soon as one layer is rebuilt, ALL layers below it (after it in the text) are rebuilt too, even if their inputs didn't change.\n- Dependencies (pom.xml + `mvn dependency:go-offline`) change rarely, while sources change on every commit. So dependencies must come BEFORE copying the code.\n- Anti-pattern: `COPY . .` at the start — editing a single line of code breaks the layer that's followed by the slow dependency download, and it re-downloads on every build.\n- Fix: first `COPY pom.xml` + download dependencies (this layer stays cached for a long time), and only THEN `COPY src` + build.","caps":["An image is a stack of layers, one per Dockerfile instruction. Docker caches each layer.","Anti-pattern: we copy the WHOLE project on the first line, and put the heavy dependency download after it.","We change one line of code. That changes the input of the COPY . . layer — and that layer's cache is invalidated.","Cascade: everything below the changed layer is rebuilt. So the heavy dependency download is re-fetched on EVERY commit.","Fix: first COPY only pom.xml and download dependencies (rarely changes), and only THEN COPY src.","Now a code edit touches only the upper layers. Dependencies stay in cache — the build is fast."]},"dof-prometheus-pull":{"title":"Prometheus: pull, not push","g":"CI/CD and deployment","hook":"It's not workers phoning the boss with reports (push) — it's the boss walking the offices on a schedule (pull). An empty office, and he immediately sees the person is gone.","more":"**Prometheus periodically polls (scrapes) the /metrics endpoint of each target itself — that's pull.**\n- A service just exposes a `/metrics` endpoint with current values. It sends them NOWHERE.\n- Prometheus walks its list of targets every N seconds and pulls the values. The mere fact of a successful scrape gives a free `up` metric — is the target alive.\n- Pull upsides: centralized control of the polling rate, easy detection of \"target not responding,\" and no risk of thousands of services flooding the collector (backpressure lives on the Prometheus side).\n- **Problem**: short-lived jobs (cron, batch) may die BEFORE Prometheus comes to poll them — the metric is lost.\n- **Solution**: Pushgateway. The job pushes the metric to Pushgateway before dying, and Prometheus pulls it from there as usual. It's a push→pull bridge for ephemeral jobs.","caps":["Push model: each service sends metrics to the collector itself. With thousands of services, the collector is easy to overwhelm.","Prometheus's pull model: the service just exposes a /metrics endpoint with current values and sends nothing anywhere itself.","Prometheus walks its list of targets itself and pulls (scrapes) the metrics every N seconds.","Bonus: a successful scrape alone yields the up metric. Empty office — Prometheus immediately sees up=0.","Problem: a short-lived cron job can die BEFORE Prometheus comes to poll it. The metric is lost.","Solution — Pushgateway: the job pushes the metric there before dying, and Prometheus pulls it from there as usual. A push→pull bridge."]},"dof-graceful-shutdown":{"title":"Graceful pod shutdown: SIGTERM, preStop, SIGKILL","g":"CI/CD and deployment","hook":"It's not a point-blank shot but a polite eviction: first \"take the sign off the door\" (pull it out of the load balancer), then \"wrap up and leave\" (SIGTERM), and only if it hangs is it carried out by force (SIGKILL).","more":"**Between \"delete the pod\" and a hard SIGKILL there's a managed window — ignore it and you tear live requests apart.**\n- When a pod is deleted, TWO things happen in parallel: it's dropped from the Service endpoints (stops receiving new traffic) and it's sent a termination signal.\n- The **preStop hook** (if set) runs FIRST, before SIGTERM. A short sleep often goes there so the load balancer actually has time to stop sending traffic (propagating the change isn't instant).\n- Then the container gets **SIGTERM** — \"finish in-flight requests and shut down.\" The application MUST catch it: send remaining responses, close DB connections, stop accepting new ones.\n- Next **terminationGracePeriodSeconds** (30s by default) ticks. Finished in time — great.\n- Didn't finish / doesn't listen for SIGTERM — **SIGKILL** arrives, the process is killed instantly, and unfinished requests are torn (clients get 5xx / dropped connections).","caps":["The pod is running and serving live requests. A command arrives to delete it — for example, during a rolling update.","Step 1: the pod is removed from the Service endpoints — it stops receiving NEW traffic. But old requests are still in flight.","Step 2: the preStop hook runs (if set) — usually a short sleep so the load balancer really has time to stop sending traffic.","Step 3: the container is sent SIGTERM — \"finish in-flight requests and shut down.\" The application MUST catch it.","terminationGracePeriodSeconds ticks (30s by default). Finished within the window — great, not a single request torn.","If the application does NOT listen for SIGTERM or has hung — once the window expires SIGKILL arrives, the process is killed instantly, and unfinished requests are torn (5xx)."]},"apf-outbox":{"title":"Transactional Outbox","g":"Reliable messaging","hook":"Drop the letter into the same mailbox as the ledger entry — either both go in, or neither does","more":"**The dual-write problem:** writing to the DB and emitting an event to Kafka are two separate systems with no shared transaction. A crash can land right in the middle: the record exists but the event was never sent (or vice versa).\n\n**The solution:** in ONE DB transaction, write both the business data and a row into the `outbox` table. The transaction is atomic — either both land or neither does. A separate process (relay/poller) reads `outbox` and publishes to the broker, marking rows as sent.\n\n- Delivery guarantee: **at-least-once** (an event may be sent twice) — the consumer must be idempotent.\n- An alternative is CDC (reading the DB's WAL), but outbox is simpler and more explicit.","caps":["A service needs to save an order to the DB and emit an event to Kafka at once — but these are two separate systems.","The DB write succeeded, then the process crashed right after — the Kafka event never went out. The systems are now out of sync.","The idea: in ONE DB transaction, write both the order and a row into the outbox table — atomically, both or neither.","A separate relay process periodically reads new outbox rows.","The relay publishes the event to Kafka and marks the row as sent.","Result: no event is ever lost. The price is at-least-once, so the consumer must be idempotent."]},"apf-circuit-breaker":{"title":"Circuit Breaker","g":"Resilience","hook":"An electrical breaker: when a neighbor shorts out, it trips the line so the whole house doesn't burn down","more":"**Why:** if a downstream is down, there's no point hammering it with requests — they'll just time out anyway, while you pile up hanging threads and finish off the struggling neighbor.\n\n**Three states:**\n- **Closed** — all good, requests flow, we count errors.\n- **Open** — error threshold exceeded: requests are rejected instantly (fail-fast), giving the downstream a breather.\n- **Half-Open** — after a timeout we let one probe request through. Success — we close; failure — back to Open.\n\nThe key is **failing fast** instead of waiting forever. Often paired with a Fallback (return a stub).","caps":["Service A calls B. B starts returning errors, but A stubbornly sends every request and waits for the timeout.","CLOSED state: requests flow, the breaker silently tracks the error rate.","Errors cross the threshold — the breaker trips to OPEN.","In OPEN, requests are rejected INSTANTLY — fail-fast. B stops being hit and gets to rest.","After a timeout — HALF-OPEN: we let one probe request through to check whether B has recovered.","Probe succeeds — we return to CLOSED. Failure — back to OPEN. This way the circuit heals itself."]},"apf-bulkhead":{"title":"Bulkhead","g":"Resilience","hook":"Watertight ship compartments: a breach floods one compartment, not the whole vessel","more":"**The problem:** one slow downstream stalls ALL of the service's threads. The shared thread pool fills up with waiting calls — healthy operations queue up too, and the whole service goes down.\n\n**The solution:** isolate resources. Each dependency call gets its own bounded pool of threads/connections. A slow neighbor only eats up its own compartment, while the rest keep working.\n\n- The name comes from shipbuilding: the hull is divided into compartments by bulkheads.\n- Often paired with Circuit Breaker: the bulkhead caps the number of concurrent calls, the breaker cuts off broken ones.","caps":["A service calls three downstreams through ONE shared thread pool.","Downstream C slows down. Threads pile up waiting for a response from C.","The pool is fully consumed waiting on C. Healthy A and B have no threads left — the whole service is down.","Bulkhead: split the shared pool into separate compartments — one pool per downstream.","Now C only floods ITS OWN compartment. The pools for A and B are untouched.","Result: the failure is contained in one compartment, the rest of the service's functions stay alive."]},"apf-saga-orch":{"title":"Saga Orchestration and Compensations","g":"Distributed transactions","hook":"An orchestra conductor: leads step by step, and if the flute hits a wrong note, signals everyone to play it back","more":"**Why:** in microservices there's no shared ACID transaction across multiple DBs. A saga is a sequence of local transactions, where each step has a **compensating** action.\n\n**Orchestration:** a single coordinator (orchestrator) explicitly invokes the steps in order and holds the state. If step N fails, the orchestrator triggers compensations for steps N-1…1 in reverse order.\n\n- A compensation isn't a rollback but a business undo (refund the money, release the reservation).\n- Pro of orchestration: the whole process is visible in one place. Con: the coordinator is a coupling point.\n- The alternative is choreography (services react to events without a conductor).","caps":["An order requires three steps across three services. There's no shared transaction over all the DBs.","The orchestrator commands the steps one by one and remembers where we are now.","Step 2 succeeded, moving on to the third — the warehouse reservation is made.","Step 3 failed: no couriers available. The saga can't complete going forward.","The orchestrator goes BACK and triggers compensations in reverse order.","Result: the system reaches a consistent state without a distributed transaction — via compensations."]},"apf-event-sourcing":{"title":"Event Sourcing","g":"Data and state","hook":"A bank statement instead of a single balance figure: we keep every transaction and compute the balance by summing them","more":"**The idea:** store not the current state but an immutable log of ALL events that led to it. Current state = a fold (replay) of events from left to right.\n\n**What it gives you:**\n- A full audit trail and the history of \"how we got here\" — for free.\n- You can reconstruct the state at any point in the past (time-travel).\n- Events are a natural source for projections (the read-model in CQRS).\n\n**The price:**\n- Replaying a long history is expensive — **snapshots** save you.\n- Changing the schema of old events is hard (you need versioning/upcasting).","caps":["The usual approach: the DB holds only the current balance, the history is overwritten.","Event Sourcing: instead of a single number we keep an immutable stream of events.","We get the current state by replaying events from left to right.","Bonus: you can stop at any event and learn the state as it was in the past.","Replaying a long history is expensive — we periodically take a snapshot and start from it.","Result: the log is the source of truth, audit and projections come for free; we pay with replay and schema evolution."]},"apf-cqrs":{"title":"CQRS: Separating Reads and Writes","g":"Data and state","hook":"Kitchen and display case: the chef cooks by a strict recipe, while the guest grabs what's ready off the case without entering the kitchen","more":"**The idea:** separate the command model (writes) from the query model (reads). They're optimized for different jobs independently.\n\n- **Write-model** — normalized, protects invariants (aggregates), accepts commands.\n- **Read-model** — denormalized for specific screens, fast SELECTs with no joins.\n- The link is events: a command changes state → event → a projection updates the read-model.\n\n**When to use it:** very different load/shape for reads vs. writes, complex reports. **When NOT to:** simple CRUD — it's needless complexity and eventual consistency between the models.","caps":["One model serves both writes and reads — a compromise that's awkward for both.","CQRS splits it in two: a separate model for commands, a separate one for queries.","A command hits the write-model: it checks invariants and changes state.","The change publishes an event — it updates the read-model (the projection).","Queries hit only the read-model: denormalized for the screen, fast SELECTs with no joins.","Result: each side scales independently. The price is lag and eventual consistency of the read-model."]},"apf-token-bucket":{"title":"Rate Limiting: Token Bucket","g":"Load management","hook":"A token turnstile: tokens drip in at a steady rate; to pass you take a token; no tokens — you wait","more":"**The task:** cap request frequency while still allowing short bursts.\n\n**Token Bucket:** there's a bucket with capacity N tokens. Tokens are refilled at a fixed rate r/sec. A request passes only if there's a token in the bucket — it takes one. Empty bucket — the request is rejected (or waits).\n\n- **Burst:** accumulated tokens let a batch through at once — up to N. That's the upside: it tolerates spikes.\n- Compare with **Leaky Bucket** — that one releases strictly evenly, smoothing a spike into a steady flow, allowing no burst.\n- Parameters: capacity (burst size) and refill rate (average rate).","caps":["We need to cap requests to an API, but without cutting off legitimate load spikes.","Set up a bucket: tokens drip into it at a steady rate r/sec.","A request arrives — it takes one token from the bucket and passes.","Accumulated tokens let a spike through — a whole batch at once, up to N.","The bucket empties — no tokens left, excess requests are rejected (429).","Result: the refill rate holds the average rate, the bucket capacity handles the spike. A leaky bucket, by contrast, emits a strictly even flow."]},"apf-strangler":{"title":"Strangler Fig","g":"Legacy migration","hook":"A strangler fig: it wraps the old tree, intercepts its sap, and when the tree dies it's left standing on its own","more":"**Why:** rewriting a large legacy system entirely and shipping it in a \"big bang\" is a huge risk. Strangler Fig replaces it GRADUALLY, piece by piece, with no long freeze.\n\n**How:**\n- Put a facade/proxy in front of the legacy, through which all traffic flows.\n- Rewrite one route at a time into a new service and switch the routing for it (the old system keeps serving the rest).\n- Once all routes have moved over — shut the legacy down.\n\n- Rollback at any step is just pointing the route back to the old system.\n- The name comes from botany: the strangler fig envelops a tree and gradually replaces it.","caps":["There's a large legacy system. Rewriting it all at once and shipping it in a \"big bang\" is too risky.","Put a facade-proxy in front of the legacy: all traffic now flows through it.","Rewrite ONE piece A into a new service and switch its route over.","Next, B moves over. The legacy gradually loses load — the fig envelops the tree.","The last route C has moved over — not a single request reaches the legacy.","Result: shut the legacy down. The migration ran with no freeze, and rollback at every step is just pointing the route back."]},"apf-sharding":{"title":"Sharding by Key","g":"Data scaling","hook":"Sorting mail by district: the postal code picks the office and the letter goes straight there, bypassing a single warehouse","more":"**Why:** when data doesn't fit / can't be handled on one node, we split it horizontally into shards, each holding its own piece.\n\n**How we find the shard:** take a shard key (e.g. user_id), compute a function → shard number. A request on that key goes straight to the right node, leaving the others untouched.\n\n- **Hash sharding:** spreads data evenly, but adjacent keys scatter apart (bad for range queries).\n- **Range sharding:** plays well with ranges, but risks hot shards.\n- Pitfalls: a request WITHOUT the shard key fans out to all shards (scatter-gather); rebalancing when adding a node is painful — consistent hashing helps.","caps":["A single DB node can no longer handle the volume and load — we've hit the ceiling.","Split the data horizontally into shards: each node holds its own slice of rows.","Pick a shard key (user_id) and a function: hash(key) % 3 = shard number.","A request on that key goes straight to its shard — the others are untouched.","Pitfall: a request WITHOUT the shard key doesn't know where to go — it fans out across ALL shards.","Result: keyed queries scale linearly. The price: key choice is critical, rebalancing is painful (consistent hashing helps)."]},"apf-scatter-gather":{"title":"Scatter-Gather","g":"Integration","hook":"Query three travel agencies at once and collect the answers — whoever misses the deadline, we don't wait for them","more":"**The idea:** send one request IN PARALLEL to several workers (scatter), then collect and aggregate their responses (gather).\n\n**Why a timeout:** the aggregator can't wait forever for the slowest one — that would kill overall latency. We set a deadline: responses that arrive in time get aggregated, latecomers are dropped (or we return a partial result).\n\n- Typical uses: search across multiple sources, price comparison, fan-out to shards.\n- Total time ≈ the slowest response WITHIN the timeout, not the sum.\n- Decide up front: is a partial answer enough, or do you need all of them.","caps":["We need the best price from three suppliers. Asking one by one is slow.","Scatter: send one request to all three IN PARALLEL.","Start a shared deadline timer: we can't wait forever for the slowest one.","P1 and P2 responded in time, P3 hung and missed the deadline.","Gather: drop the latecomer, collect the responses that arrived and aggregate them.","Result: total time ≈ the slowest one WITHIN the timeout, not the sum. Decide up front: is a partial answer enough."]},"qkf-build-time-di":{"title":"Build-time DI (ARC): why startup takes milliseconds","g":"Startup & DI","hook":"Spring assembles the furniture right in the room as you move in; Quarkus delivers it pre-assembled — you just carry it in and start living.","more":"**The key difference** is when the DI container does its work.\n- **Spring Boot:** scans the classpath and resolves the bean graph **at startup** via reflection. That's where the \"seconds\" go.\n- **Quarkus (ARC):** all bean analysis happens **at build time**. It generates ready-made bytecode that simply instantiates the objects — no scanning and almost no reflection.\n- At runtime only the already-computed plan needs to run → milliseconds to the first request.\n- Bonus: DI errors (missing bean, ambiguity) surface **at compile time**, not in production.","caps":["In plain Spring, on EVERY startup the container re-scans the classpath and rebuilds the bean graph via reflection.","Quarkus moves all of that analysis to BUILD time — where spending time is cheap.","The build output isn't \"instructions on how to assemble\" but ready-made code that directly creates the needed objects.","At startup the container only has to execute the ready-made plan — no reflection, no lookups.","Side prize: a missing or ambiguous bean shows up at compile time instead of blowing up in production."]},"qkf-native-closed-world":{"title":"Native AOT and closed-world: how GraalVM produces an .exe","g":"Native","hook":"You pack a suitcase for a trip where there are no shops: whatever you didn't pack in advance simply won't exist. Native is packing for a closed world.","more":"**Native image** compiles the application ahead of time (**AOT**) into a single executable, with no JVM at runtime.\n- **Closed-world assumption:** GraalVM sees the ENTIRE application at build time and keeps in the binary only the code that execution can actually reach (reachability). Everything else is stripped out.\n- Anything not statically proven reachable — **reflection, dynamic proxies, resources** — may be dropped from the binary.\n- That's why such cases are declared explicitly: `@RegisterForReflection`, `reflection-config.json`.\n- The trade-off: **long build times and high compiler peak-RSS**, but in return you get instant startup and low memory footprint.","caps":["In JVM mode a full JVM always rides along with the code, compiling and loading classes on the fly.","Native builds everything ahead of time (AOT): GraalVM analyzes the reachability of every piece of code.","Closed-world: anything unreachable is stripped out. Only live code makes it into the binary.","The catch: reflection resolves a class by a string at runtime — that path isn't visible statically, so the class may get dropped.","The fix is to tell GraalVM up front \"keep this\": @RegisterForReflection and reflection-config.json.","Bottom line: the build is slow and memory-hungry, but the binary starts instantly and uses little memory."]},"qkf-client-proxy":{"title":"Client proxy for @ApplicationScoped","g":"Startup & DI","hook":"Your contacts hold a single \"taxi\" number, but the car that answers is whichever one is currently on shift. The proxy is that permanent number.","more":"**Why a proxy.** An @ApplicationScoped bean is a single instance for the whole application, but injection happens while other beans are being created — and the target bean may not be ready yet, or is meant to be \"lazy\".\n- Quarkus injects not the bean itself but a **client proxy** — a lightweight wrapper of the same type.\n- Any method call on the proxy **delegates** to the real (contextual) instance, resolved at the right moment.\n- This gives you: **lazy initialization**, correct scope handling, and safe circular injection.\n- For @ApplicationScoped/@RequestScoped a proxy exists by default; for @Singleton/@Dependent there is none — a direct instance is injected there.","caps":["Service A depends on bean B with scope @ApplicationScoped and wants it injected.","The problem: at injection time the real B may not be created yet (laziness, cycles, scope).","Instead of B itself, Quarkus slips in a proxy — a wrapper of the same type B.","When A first calls a method, the proxy resolves the real contextual instance of B.","The call delegates to the real B and returns the result — A works as usual.","Caveat: @Singleton and @Dependent have no proxy — a direct instance is injected there."]},"qkf-mutiny-uni-multi":{"title":"Mutiny: Uni (one) vs Multi (stream) and backpressure","g":"Reactive","hook":"Uni is a parcel (exactly one will arrive). Multi is the airport baggage belt: a stream rolls by, and you decide how many bags to grab at a time.","more":"**Uni** is an async result that emits **0 or 1** item (or an error): one HTTP response, one DB row.\n**Multi** is a stream of **0..N** items over time: DB rows, Kafka events, SSE.\n- Mutiny is **lazy**: until someone subscribes (`subscribe`), nothing runs.\n- **Backpressure** exists only on Multi: via `request(n)` the subscriber says how many items it's ready to accept. The source isn't allowed to send more.\n- If the source is faster than the consumer and backpressure is ignored (or buffering is unbounded) — you risk **buffer overflow / OOM**. So you pick a strategy: buffer, drop, latest.","caps":["Uni promises exactly one result in the future — like waiting for a single parcel.","Multi is a stream: items arrive over time, and there can be many of them.","Mutiny is lazy: without subscribe NOTHING happens — it's just a recipe.","Backpressure: the subscriber itself requests a batch via request(n) — the source won't send more.","If the source is faster and demand is ignored, the buffer grows until it overflows and OOMs.","So you pick an overflow strategy: buffer with a limit, drop, or keep only the latest."]},"qkf-event-loop-blocking":{"title":"Event-loop vs @Blocking: which thread runs the endpoint","g":"Reactive","hook":"The event-loop is a barista who NEVER stands still and never waits. If a task requires \"standing around and waiting,\" it's taken to the kitchen (a worker) so the counter never stalls.","more":"RESTEasy Reactive in Quarkus runs on Vert.x **event-loop (I/O) threads**. There are few of them (about one per core).\n- **Golden rule:** you **must not block** on the event-loop. Block it and the whole server stalls (other requests wait).\n- If a method returns **Uni/Multi**, Quarkus treats it as non-blocking and keeps it on the event-loop.\n- If a method is **synchronous and blocking** (JDBC, files, long CPU), you need `@Blocking`: the request is offloaded to the **worker thread pool**, freeing the event-loop.\n- Quarkus partly infers this from the signature, but `@Blocking`/`@NonBlocking` give you explicit control.","caps":["Requests are served by a small pool of event-loop threads — roughly one per core.","A reactive endpoint (returns Uni) doesn't wait — it hands off the task and immediately takes the next request.","Trouble: a synchronous JDBC call on the event-loop FREEZES the thread — and everyone waits.","The fix is @Blocking: the request is offloaded to a separate worker pool where waiting is fine.","The event-loop is freed immediately, while the blocking work waits calmly on the worker.","Rule: returned Uni/Multi → event-loop; blocking synchronously → @Blocking on a worker."]},"qkf-kafka-incoming-outgoing":{"title":"Kafka via @Incoming / @Outgoing (Reactive Messaging)","g":"Messaging","hook":"It's a conveyor of pipes. @Incoming is the pipe a part rides into your method on; @Outgoing is the pipe the finished part leaves through. A channel is the name of the pipe.","more":"SmallRye Reactive Messaging describes message handling **declaratively**, through channels.\n- **@Incoming(\"channel\")** — the method receives messages from a channel. In `application.properties` the channel is bound to a source Kafka topic.\n- **@Outgoing(\"channel\")** — whatever the method returns is sent to the channel → on to a Kafka topic.\n- A method can have **both @Incoming and @Outgoing** — that's a \"processor\": it takes from one topic, transforms, and puts into another.\n- The **Message<T>** wrapper carries the payload + metadata + `ack()/nack()`. Take a bare `T` and ack is done automatically.\n- The \"channel ↔ topic ↔ (de)serializer\" wiring is set in config, leaving the code clean.","caps":["In config a channel is bound to a Kafka topic — that's the name of the \"pipe\" the code works with.","@Incoming subscribes the method to a channel: each message from the topic rides in as an argument.","@Outgoing sends whatever the method returns to its channel — and on to another topic.","A method with both annotations is a processor: it takes from one topic, transforms, and hands off to another.","Message<T> carries the payload + metadata + ack/nack to confirm processing.","Bottom line: the code describes only the logic; the topic-serializer wiring is held in config."]},"qkf-dev-live-reload":{"title":"Dev mode and live reload: when recompilation happens","g":"Dev experience","hook":"It's not \"constantly chewing\" (like a watcher) but \"compiling on the waiter's request\": no requests, silence; a request comes in, it checks whether anything changed and, if so, rebuilds.","more":"`quarkus dev` runs the application in **dev mode** with live reload.\n- The key nuance: recompilation happens **not when you save a file** but on the **next incoming request** (lazy). That's why edits show up without a restart.\n- Quarkus compares: have the sources changed since the last request? If so — a **background incremental recompile** + hot-swap of the changed classes via its own ClassLoader.\n- This is **much faster than a full restart**, because only the delta is rebuilt and build-time metadata is reused.\n- Alongside it runs **continuous testing**: the tests affected by a change are re-run automatically.","caps":["You run quarkus dev — the app is alive and waiting for requests, idling instead of churning.","You edit code and save the file — but the save itself doesn't rebuild anything yet.","The trigger is the next incoming request: that's when Quarkus wakes up.","Quarkus compares the sources with last time: if there's a delta, it rebuilds only that.","Changed classes are hot-swapped via the dev ClassLoader — no full JVM restart.","The request is served by the new code already; in parallel, continuous testing re-runs the affected tests."]},"qkf-config-ordering":{"title":"Configuration sources: who overrides whom","g":"Configuration","hook":"It's a layer cake of settings: the higher the layer, the more authority it has. ENV overrides a file like a sticker slapped over printed text.","more":"Quarkus uses **SmallRye Config** (MicroProfile Config). The same property can come from many sources — the source with the **highest priority (ordinal)** wins.\n- Roughly top to bottom: **system properties (`-D`) → environment variables (ENV) → `.env` → `application.properties` → defaults in code**.\n- ENV names are mapped: `quarkus.datasource.username` → `QUARKUS_DATASOURCE_USERNAME` (dots/dashes → underscores, uppercase).\n- **Profiles** `%dev`/`%test`/`%prod` provide a value only for the active profile; by default a normal run activates **prod**, `quarkus dev` activates **dev**, and tests activate **test**.\n- `@ConfigProperty` injects the final, already-resolved value.","caps":["A single property db.url may be set in several places at once — who wins?","Every source has a priority (ordinal): the higher the ordinal, the more authoritative the source.","ENV overrides a file: the value from the environment variable lands on top of properties.","The ENV name follows a rule: dots and dashes → underscores, everything uppercased.","A profile adds another dimension: a value with %dev/%test/%prod is taken only for the active profile.","SmallRye Config resolves all of this into one final value — and that's what @ConfigProperty injects."]},"rxf-request-backpressure":{"title":"request(n) and backpressure","g":"Stream","hook":"The waiter doesn't dump all 50 dishes on you at once — he brings exactly as many as you asked to eat.","more":"**Backpressure** is the mechanism a Subscriber uses to control the delivery rate so it doesn't get overwhelmed.\n- On subscribe, the Subscriber receives a **Subscription** and calls `request(n)` itself: \"give me n elements.\"\n- The Publisher is not allowed to emit more than the total requested — that's the Reactive Streams contract.\n- Without it, a fast source overruns a slow consumer: latency spikes, OOM, dropped items.\n- Operators like `onBackpressureBuffer/Drop/Latest` define the strategy when demand falls short of supply.\n- `request(Long.MAX_VALUE)` means \"unbounded\" — it effectively disables backpressure for that subscriber.","caps":["A fast source and a slow consumer — with no rate control, this leads straight to overflow.","On subscribe, the Subscriber receives a Subscription — its back-channel to the source.","The Subscriber explicitly asks for exactly what it can handle: request(3).","The Publisher emits no more than requested — three tokens travel to the consumer.","Done processing — it asks again. The consumer always sets the pace, not the source.","Bottom line: demand ≥ supply means no overflow. The Reactive Streams contract holds."]},"rxf-hot-vs-cold":{"title":"hot vs cold","g":"Stream","hook":"Cold is a film at the cinema: every viewer gets it from the start. Hot is a live broadcast: join late and you've missed it.","more":"A **cold** Publisher restarts its work on every subscription — data is generated for each specific subscriber.\n- Cold examples: `Flux.range`, an HTTP request inside `Mono.defer`, reading a file — every Subscriber gets the full set from scratch.\n- A **hot** Publisher emits regardless of subscribers; whoever joins later sees only what's flowing right now.\n- Hot examples: UI click events, a Kafka topic via `Sinks`, `share()`/`publish().refCount()`.\n- Cold is lazy and reproducible; hot is shared and \"live,\" but risks missing early elements.\n- You turn cold into hot with `share()`, `publish()`, or `replay()` (the last caches past elements for latecomers).","caps":["Cold: the first subscriber connects — the source starts PERSONALLY for it, from the beginning.","A second subscriber — and the source replays from the very start again, a separate run.","Hot: the source emits on its own, the stream flows independently of subscribers.","A subscriber joins at moment c — it sees only the current item onward; a and b are missed.","share()/publish() turn cold into hot: one run shared among everyone.","Bottom line: cold = replayed from scratch for each; hot = a shared \"live\" broadcast."]},"rxf-map-vs-flatmap":{"title":"map vs flatMap","g":"Stream","hook":"map repaints the box. flatMap opens the box, finds another stream of boxes inside, and merges it all onto one conveyor.","more":"**map** is a synchronous 1→1 transform: `T → R`, with no async and no unwrapping.\n- **flatMap** is `T → Publisher<R>`: each element spawns its own stream, which is immediately subscribed to and merged into the combined output.\n- You need flatMap when the transform is itself async (a REST call, a DB query) and returns a `Mono`/`Flux`.\n- flatMap **does not preserve order** (results arrive as they're ready) and runs concurrently; need order → `concatMap`, need \"latest only\" → `switchMap`.\n- Returning a `Mono` from map gives you a `Flux<Mono<R>>` — a nested stream nobody unwraps. This is the classic mistake → use flatMap.\n- flatMap has a concurrency parameter — the cap on simultaneous inner subscriptions.","caps":["map: each element is transformed 1→1; the type changes, the stream's shape doesn't.","But what if the function returns not a value, but a whole Publisher (REST, DB)?","map leaves a nested stream-within-a-stream — nobody unwraps it; that's a bug.","flatMap: each element → an inner Publisher, which we subscribe to.","The inner streams merge into one flat stream — but order is not guaranteed.","Bottom line: map = sync 1→1; flatMap = async 1→Publisher + merge. Order → concatMap."]},"rxf-subscribeon-publishon":{"title":"subscribeOn vs publishOn","g":"Stream","hook":"subscribeOn picks the shop where the whole chain runs, bottom to top. publishOn is a shift change: from here on a different crew works.","more":"**subscribeOn** affects the thread where subscription happens and emission begins — that is, the ENTIRE source up the chain.\n- The position of `subscribeOn` in the chain doesn't matter: the effect is the same — it moves the start of work onto the given Scheduler.\n- With multiple `subscribeOn`, the topmost one wins (closest to the source); the rest are ignored.\n- **publishOn** switches the thread for operators BELOW it; it takes effect from the insertion point until the next `publishOn`.\n- The position of publishOn matters: it's a thread-switch boundary. You can place several — each starts a new segment.\n- Typical pattern: `subscribeOn(boundedElastic)` for a blocking source, `publishOn(parallel)` before CPU-heavy processing.","caps":["A chain of operators. Question: which thread runs what?","subscribeOn(elastic): subscription and source startup move to elastic — the whole chain starts there.","subscribeOn's position doesn't matter — front or back, the effect is identical.","publishOn(parallel): everything BELOW the insertion point moves to parallel.","publishOn's position matters: it's a boundary. One thread before it, another after.","Bottom line: subscribeOn = where the source starts (everything up); publishOn = thread switch for everything down."]},"rxf-onerrorresume":{"title":"onErrorResume chain","g":"Stream","hook":"An error flies down the pipe as a signal; onErrorResume is the emergency gate that intercepts it and swaps in a backup stream.","more":"In reactive, **an error is a terminal `onError` signal** that travels DOWN the chain and ends the stream unless it's intercepted.\n- `onErrorResume(fn)` catches the error and switches to an alternative `Publisher` — the stream continues from the fallback.\n- `onErrorReturn(v)` is a special case: substitute a single value; `onErrorMap` re-maps the exception to another type.\n- The interception only fires for errors originating ABOVE the operator in the chain (upstream); it can't see downstream errors.\n- You can filter by type/predicate: `onErrorResume(TimeoutException.class, fn)` — react selectively.\n- After onError, no more `onNext` will come from the original source — so the fallback is a new source, not a \"continuation of the old one.\"","caps":["The stream runs normally, elements flow down the chain of operators.","An exception is thrown inside an operator — a terminal onError signal is born.","The error signal flies down, cutting off the stream — there'll be no more onNext.","onErrorResume intercepts the error signal before it reaches the subscriber.","The operator swaps in a backup Publisher — the stream continues from the fallback.","Bottom line: error = a signal downward; onErrorResume catches the upstream error and gives a new stream."]},"rxf-nothing-without-subscribe":{"title":"Nothing happens without subscribe","g":"Stream","hook":"An assembled chain is a recipe on paper. Until you hit subscribe, nobody has walked into the kitchen or turned on the stove.","more":"A reactive chain is **lazy**: the operators only describe the pipeline, they don't start any work.\n- The actual work begins only on `subscribe()` — it travels up the chain and triggers emission.\n- Without a subscription: no HTTP call, no DB query, no side effects — \"forgot to subscribe\" is the most common silent bug.\n- In Spring WebFlux the framework usually subscribes for you (returning a `Mono`/`Flux` from a controller) — you don't subscribe by hand there.\n- `block()` is also a subscription, but a synchronous, blocking one; reactive code avoids it.\n- The same cold Publisher restarts its work on every `subscribe` (see hot vs cold).","caps":["You've assembled a chain of operators — it looks like a ready, working pipeline.","But it's only a description (a recipe): not a single element has been emitted yet.","No subscribe, no calls: HTTP, DB, side-effects stay silent. A quiet bug.","subscribe() sends a signal up the chain to the source.","Now the source emits — elements actually flow down, the pipeline comes alive.","Bottom line: the chain is lazy. In WebFlux the framework subscribes; block() is a synchronous subscription."]},"grpcf-call-types":{"title":"4 call types","g":"gRPC","hook":"One phone conversation: either question-and-answer, or you dictate a list, or someone reads you a summary, or a live dialog flowing both ways at once.","more":"gRPC offers 4 forms of RPC over a single HTTP/2 stream:\n- **Unary** — one request, one response. The classic REST-like call.\n- **Server streaming** — one request, a stream of responses (subscription, export, progress).\n- **Client streaming** — a stream of requests, one response (chunked upload, aggregation).\n- **Bidirectional streaming** — both streams are independent, frames fly both ways asynchronously (chat, telemetry).\nIn proto the type is set by the **stream** keyword before the argument and/or response type. Under the hood it's all frames of one HTTP/2 stream; the only difference is who sends how many messages before half-close.","caps":["Unary: one message out, one back — the simplest case.","Server streaming: a single request spawns a stream of responses.","Client streaming: a stream of requests, and at the end — one response.","Bidirectional: two independent streams in both directions.","In proto the whole difference is the stream keyword before the type.","Bottom line: one HTTP/2 transport, four dialog forms by the number of stream sides."]},"grpcf-field-numbers":{"title":"Field numbers","g":"gRPC","hook":"Field names are for people; on the wire only numbers travel — like lockers in a left-luggage room, where the key number matters, not the label on the tag.","more":"Protobuf serializes a field as a **(number, wire-type)** pair; the name never makes it into the bytes.\n- Compatibility rests on **stable numbers**, not names: you can rename a field painlessly, but changing its number is a disaster.\n- A new field = a **new number**; old readers simply skip unknown tags (forward-compat).\n- Removing a field — via **reserved** on its number and name, so the number is never reused.\n- Numbers **1–15** take a 1-byte tag — give them to your most frequent fields; 16+ take 2 bytes.\n- You may change a field's type under the same number only between wire-compatible types, otherwise silent data corruption.","caps":["On the wire a field is a number plus a wire-type; the name is absent.","Renaming a field is safe — same number, the wire won't notice.","Changing the number breaks things: old data is read as a different field.","A new field takes a new number — an old client silently skips it.","Removed a field — pin the number via reserved so it isn't reused.","Bottom line: numbers 1-15 to hot fields, evolution rests on numbers."]},"grpcf-http2-mux":{"title":"HTTP/2 multiplexing","g":"gRPC","hook":"One cable, many strands: dozens of conversations run over a single wire at once, sliced into frames tagged with a track number.","more":"gRPC always runs over **HTTP/2** and leverages its multiplexing.\n- A single TCP connection carries **many parallel streams**; each RPC = its own stream id.\n- Data is sliced into **frames**, and frames of different streams interleave — no waiting for the previous request to finish (no HTTP/1.1 head-of-line at the application level).\n- Headers are compressed with **HPACK** and aren't duplicated on every request.\n- That's why you keep **one long-lived channel** (ManagedChannel) rather than a connection per request: fewer TCP/TLS handshakes.\n- Caution: at the **TCP** level head-of-line still exists (a lost packet stalls all streams) — that's solved only by HTTP/3/QUIC.","caps":["HTTP/1.1: one request per connection, the queue blocks.","HTTP/2: one connection, many streams in parallel.","Frames of different streams interleave in one flow — interleaving.","Headers are squeezed with HPACK — we don't resend the same bytes again.","That's why we keep one long-lived channel, not a connection per request.","Bottom line: app-level HOL is gone, but TCP-HOL remains — that's QUIC's job."]},"grpcf-deadline-propagation":{"title":"Deadline propagation down the chain","g":"gRPC","hook":"One shared alarm clock for the whole relay: the team starts with a single finish time, and each runner hands the next not \"run for 5 seconds\" but \"it all must be done by 12:00:05\".","more":"In gRPC you set not a per-hop timeout but a **deadline** — an absolute point in time by which the call must finish.\n- The deadline **propagates** down the call chain: A→B→C share one common limit.\n- At each hop you compute the **remainder** (deadline − now) and pass a trimmed value onward — downstream callees can't run longer than the caller left them.\n- When time's up, a **DEADLINE_EXCEEDED** status is returned, and cancellation via **context cancellation** flows down: useless work is killed, resources don't hang.\n- This saves you from **cascading hangs**: without a deadline a slow downstream service holds threads across the whole chain.\n- Practice: **always** set a deadline on the client and pass the incoming context onward — don't create a fresh one from scratch.","caps":["The client sets an absolute deadline, not a per-hop timeout.","Each service computes the remainder and passes a trimmed limit downward.","One limit for the whole chain — the lower service can't run longer than the upper.","Deadline expired — DEADLINE_EXCEEDED status, cancellation flows down.","Without a deadline a slow downstream holds threads across the whole chain.","Bottom line: set the deadline on the client and pass the incoming context onward."]},"sdf-rate-limiter-token-bucket":{"title":"Rate limiter: token bucket","g":"Cases","hook":"A bucket of tokens: a request arrives, pay one token; tokens drip in at a steady rate and extras don't pile up.","more":"**Token bucket** is the go-to for rate limiting because it allows short bursts while still capping the average rate.\n- **capacity** — the maximum tokens in the bucket = the allowed burst size.\n- **refill rate** — tokens added per second = the average permitted RPS.\n- A request passes if a token is available; otherwise it gets **429 Too Many Requests** or waits.\n- State (tokens, last_refill_ts) lives in **Redis** for a distributed limit; updates are atomic via a Lua script.\n- Difference from **leaky bucket**: the leaky bucket smooths output into a steady stream, while the token bucket lets a burst through.","caps":["A stream of requests hits the service unthrottled — load is unpredictable.","We put a bucket holding N tokens in front of the service.","Tokens drip in at a constant refill rate, overflow is dropped.","A request grabs a token — if one exists, it reaches the service.","Bucket empty — the request is rejected with a 429.","Result: a burst up to capacity is allowed, average RPS is bounded by the refill rate."]},"sdf-url-shortener":{"title":"URL shortener","g":"Cases","hook":"A coat-check tag: a long coat gets a short ticket, and the ticket gets the coat back.","more":"**URL shortener** — the classic exercise in short-key generation and a read-heavy profile.\n- **Key** — a short code (6-7 base62 chars ≈ 56 billion combinations) maps to the long URL.\n- Generation: **counter + base62** (predictable but guessable) or a **random code + collision check**.\n- Writes are rare, reads are massive → an aggressive **cache** (Redis) in front of a key-value DB.\n- Serve the redirect as **301** (permanent, browser-cacheable) or **302** (if you need click analytics).\n- Gotchas: **collisions**, custom aliases, TTL/expiry, protection against enumeration.","caps":["A long URL is awkward to share — you need a short form.","On write, generate a short key from a counter via base62.","Store the key→URL pair in a key-value store.","Reads are massive: check the cache first.","Cache miss — hit the DB and warm the cache.","Serve a 301 redirect to the original URL."]},"sdf-feed-fanout":{"title":"Feed fan-out","g":"Cases","hook":"Mail carrier vs. bulletin board: deliver the letter to everyone's mailbox (push), or pin a single notice and let people come read it (pull).","more":"**Fan-out** — how to populate followers' feeds when a new post lands.\n- **Fan-out on write (push)** — on write, scatter the post into every follower's feed. Reads are instant, but writes are expensive.\n- **Fan-out on read (pull)** — on read, assemble the feed from the posts of everyone you follow. Writes are cheap, reads are heavy.\n- **Celebrity problem**: a blogger with millions of followers → push blows up the write. Handle them with the pull model.\n- **Hybrid** — normal users via push, celebrities via pull, merge at read time.\n- Feeds live in a cache (Redis list/sorted set), with the tail trimmed.","caps":["A user publishes a post — it has to reach the followers' feeds.","Push: on write, copy the post into every follower's feed.","In return, reads are instant — the feed is already assembled.","Pull: the feed is assembled at read time from the authors' posts.","A celebrity with millions of followers breaks push on the write.","Result: hybrid — regular users via push, stars via pull, merge at read time."]},"sdf-payment-ledger-double-entry":{"title":"Double-entry ledger","g":"Cases","hook":"An accountant's scales: every operation is an entry in two columns, one account's debit equals another's credit, and the total always nets to zero.","more":"**Double-entry ledger** — how money moves through payment systems without leaking.\n- Each transaction = at least **two postings**: a debit on one account and a credit on another, summing to 0.\n- **Invariant**: the sum of all postings in a transaction = 0 → money is neither created nor destroyed.\n- The ledger is **append-only**: entries are never edited or deleted, and a mistake is fixed with a reversing entry (reversal).\n- An account's balance = the rollup (sum) of all its postings; often cached as a materialized balance.\n- **Idempotency** on transaction_id guards against double-charging on retries.","caps":["Transferring 100 from Alice to Bob — you can't lose or duplicate the money.","One transaction yields two postings: debit and credit.","Invariant: the postings of a transaction always sum to zero.","Append-only ledger: entries are only added, never edited.","A mistake isn't deleted — it's offset with a reversal entry.","An account's balance = the rollup of all its postings."]},"sdf-consistent-hashing-cache":{"title":"Consistent hashing","g":"Cases","hook":"A clock face: both servers and keys are placed on a ring by their hash, and a key is served by the nearest server clockwise.","more":"**Consistent hashing** — distributing keys across cache nodes so that adding/removing a node moves few keys.\n- The naive `hash(key) % N` reshuffles almost ALL keys when N changes → mass cache miss.\n- Ring: both nodes and keys are hashed onto one ring; a key goes to the **first node clockwise**.\n- Adding/removing a node moves only **~1/N of the keys**, the rest stay put.\n- **Virtual nodes** (vnodes): each physical node is spread across many ring points → even load.\n- Used in: Memcached clusters, Cassandra, Dynamo, sharded Redis.","caps":["Naive hash % N: when N changes, almost all keys relocate.","Place nodes on a ring by their hash.","A key is hashed onto the same ring and goes to the nearest node clockwise.","Add a node — only the segment in front of it relocates.","Vnodes spread each node across many points for evenness.","Result: a topology change moves only ~1/N of the keys."]},"sdf-capacity-estimation":{"title":"Capacity estimation","g":"Cases","hook":"Envelope and pencil: roughly multiply users by actions and you immediately see whether you'll hit a hardware wall.","more":"**Capacity estimation** (back-of-the-envelope) — estimating QPS, traffic, and storage before designing.\n- **QPS** = DAU × actions-per-user / 86400 sec. Peak QPS ≈ average × 2-3.\n- **Read/write ratio** — usually read >> write (e.g. 100:1), which dictates caching and replicas.\n- **Storage** = records-per-day × record-size × retention-days; don't forget indexes and replication (×3).\n- **Bandwidth** = QPS × payload size.\n- Compute in **powers of 10** and round numbers: the order of magnitude matters, not precision. 1 day ≈ 86400 ≈ 10^5 sec.","caps":["Start: 100M DAU, 10 actions per user per day.","Average QPS = actions per day / 86400 seconds.","Peak QPS = average × 2-3.","Read/write is usually skewed — that dictates caching and replicas.","Storage = records/day × size × retention days.","Result: factor in ×3 replication and indexes — leave headroom."]},"sdf-replication-sync-async":{"title":"Sync/async replication","g":"Cases","hook":"A deal witness: wait until they sign a copy (sync, reliable but slow), or hand over the receipt right away and send the copy later (async, fast but risky).","more":"**Replication** primary→replica can be synchronous or asynchronous — it's about the tradeoff between durability and latency.\n- **Sync**: the primary acks the write to the client only after an ack from the replica. No data loss if the primary dies, but higher latency, and the primary blocks if the replica lags.\n- **Async**: the primary responds immediately and the replica catches up later. Low latency, but if the primary dies you lose the not-yet-replicated tail (**replication lag**).\n- **Semi-sync** — a compromise: wait for an ack from at least one replica.\n- Async replicas yield **stale reads** (you read outdated data) → be careful with read-after-write.","caps":["The client writes to the primary, with a replica for reliability.","Sync: the primary waits for the replica's ack, only then responds to the client.","Sync is reliable: a primary failure loses no data, but latency is higher.","Async: the primary responds immediately, the replica catches up later.","Async is fast, but a primary failure loses the unreplicated tail.","Result: choose by durability-vs-latency requirements; semi-sync as a compromise."]},"sdf-sharding-by-key":{"title":"Sharding by key","g":"Cases","hook":"An alphabetized card file: data is sorted into shard drawers, and the key tells you which drawer to look in.","more":"**Sharding** — horizontally splitting data across several databases when one can't handle the volume or load.\n- A **shard key** is chosen so queries hit a single shard and load stays even (often user_id).\n- **Hash-based**: shard = hash(key) % N — even distribution, but range queries fan out across all shards.\n- **Range-based**: by key ranges — handy for range queries, but risks a **hotspot** on a hot range.\n- **Hotspot/skew** — a poor key overloads one shard; fix with a composite key or consistent hashing.\n- **Cross-shard joins and transactions are expensive** — design access so a query lives within one shard.","caps":["One DB can't handle the data volume and load.","Split the data into shards, the key determines the address.","Hash-based: shard = hash(key) % N — even distribution.","Range-based: by key ranges — handy for range queries.","Hotspot danger: a poor key overloads one shard.","Result: a good key keeps a query in one shard; cross-shard is expensive."]},"bldf-mvn-lifecycle":{"title":"The lifecycle pipeline","g":"Build","hook":"A build is a pipeline: call a downstream station and every station before it runs on its own.","more":"Maven is wired as a linear **lifecycle** of phases: `validate → compile → test → package → verify → install → deploy`. Invoking a phase runs everything that precedes it.\n- A **phase** is a slot in the pipeline — empty on its own.\n- Plugin **goals** are bound to phases: they do the actual work (`compiler:compile`, `surefire:test`, `jar:jar`).\n- `mvn package` doesn't mean \"package this\" — it means \"go as far as package, doing everything up to it along the way\".\n- That's why you can't \"just package without tests\" with a plain call — you have to say `-DskipTests` explicitly.\n- There are three separate lifecycles: `default` (the build), `clean`, and `site` — their phases don't overlap.","caps":["A lifecycle is an ordered chain of phases, not a panel of buttons.","A phase is empty on its own — the work is done by plugin goals bound to it.","Calling mvn package runs EVERY phase up to and including package.","Phases past your target don't run — install and deploy stay untouched.","Three independent lifecycles: clean and site phases don't live in default."]},"bldf-nearest-wins":{"title":"Nearest-wins","g":"Build","hook":"A version conflict isn't settled by age but by distance: the one closer to the root wins.","more":"When the same library arrives transitively via different paths with different versions, Maven picks by the **nearest-wins** rule — the one closest to the root of the dependency tree.\n- **Path depth** beats the version number: 1.0 at depth 1 beats 2.0 at depth 3.\n- On equal depth, the one **declared earlier** in the pom wins (first-declared).\n- This is NOT \"highest version\" — Gradle behaves differently; Maven takes the nearest.\n- The danger: an old version silently slips in and breaks at runtime on a method it doesn't have (`NoSuchMethodError`).\n- Fix it by declaring the version explicitly in your own pom or via `<dependencyManagement>` / a BOM.","caps":["The same library arrives transitively via two paths with different versions.","Maven measures path depth to the root, not the version number.","The nearest one wins — 1.0 beats 2.0 even though its number is lower.","On equal depth, declaration order rules — first-declared.","A silently-pulled old version breaks runtime on a method it doesn't have.","Fix: pin the version explicitly in your pom or via dependencyManagement."]},"bldf-bom-align":{"title":"BOM alignment","g":"Build","hook":"A BOM is a price list of versions: you name the product, it fills in the price — the same for everyone.","more":"A **BOM** (Bill of Materials) is a pom of type `pom`, imported into `<dependencyManagement>` with `scope=import`. It doesn't pull in dependencies — it defines a consistent set of versions.\n- Import the BOM once, and from then on you declare dependencies **without `<version>`**.\n- It solves the \"version zoo\" problem within a single family (Jackson, Quarkus, Spring).\n- `dependencyManagement` only **manages** the version — it does NOT add the dependency to the classpath; you still have to declare it in `<dependencies>`.\n- The BOM version overrides nearest-wins: the managed version applies to transitives too.\n- Quarkus and Spring Boot ship their own BOMs precisely for this alignment.","caps":["Without a BOM, versions within one family drift apart and clash.","A BOM is a pom artifact carrying a list of aligned versions.","You import the BOM into dependencyManagement with scope=import.","In dependencies you no longer write the version — the BOM supplies it.","The managed version overrides even transitive nearest-wins.","Result: one import line and the whole family is aligned on one version."]},"bldf-multi-module-reactor":{"title":"Multi-module reactor","g":"Build","hook":"The reactor is a dispatcher: it sees who waits on whom and works out the module build order itself.","more":"In a multi-module project the parent `pom` with `<packaging>pom</packaging>` aggregates the modules, while the **reactor** is the mechanism that builds them in the right order.\n- The reactor builds a **graph of inter-module dependencies** and topologically sorts it.\n- The order in `<modules>` doesn't dictate the build order — inter-module dependencies do.\n- A module is built **before** the ones that reference it (`model` before `core`, `core` before `bootstrap`).\n- `mvn install` puts the module into the local repository so the next one can find it as a dependency.\n- Handy flags: `-pl` (select a module), `-am` (also-make: build its dependencies too), `-T` (parallel across independent branches of the graph).","caps":["A parent pom with packaging=pom only aggregates the list of modules.","The reactor reads inter-module dependencies, not the order in the list.","Topological sort: a dependency is built before its dependent.","install puts the module in .m2 so the next one finds it as an artifact.","The flag -pl core -am builds core and everything it depends on.","The flag -T builds independent branches of the graph in parallel."]},"bldf-impl-vs-api":{"title":"implementation vs api","g":"Build","hook":"api is a glass door — consumers see everything; implementation is a solid door — the internals stay hidden.","more":"In Gradle (and conceptually in Maven through scopes) dependencies are split by whether they **leak** into the consumer's classpath.\n- **`api`** — the dependency lands on the consumer's public compile-classpath: they see it and can use it. It leaks outward.\n- **`implementation`** — the dependency is available only while compiling the module itself, NOT visible to consumers. Encapsulation.\n- Rule: if a library's type **appears in the signatures** of your public API, you need `api`; otherwise `implementation`.\n- The payoff of `implementation`: lower coupling and faster incremental builds — changing an internal library doesn't rebuild consumers.\n- In Maven the leakage analog is transitive `compile` scope; `runtime`/`provided` narrow visibility.","caps":["Module lib depends on guava — the question: does consumer app see it?","api: guava leaks into the consumer's compile-classpath — a glass door.","implementation: guava is hidden, absent from the consumer's classpath — a solid door.","The deciding test: does the library's type show up in a public signature?","implementation cuts coupling: change an internal library and app isn't rebuilt.","Maven analog: transitive compile leaks, provided/runtime narrow it."]},"bldf-deptree-diag":{"title":"dependency:tree","g":"Build","hook":"dependency:tree is an X-ray of the classpath: you see where a version came from and what it evicted.","more":"`mvn dependency:tree` prints the full dependency tree — the main tool for diagnosing version conflicts.\n- The tree shows the **path** of each dependency: you immediately see which module dragged in the problematic transitive.\n- `omitted for conflict with X` means that version lost nearest-wins and was replaced by X.\n- The filter `-Dincludes=group:artifact` narrows the output to the library of interest in a large tree.\n- `-Dverbose` even shows the discarded nodes annotated with the reason — without it they're invisible.\n- After diagnosing, fix it either with an `<exclusion>` on the offending path or by pinning the version in `dependencyManagement`.","caps":["A version conflict blows up at runtime — you can't spot it by eye in the pom.","mvn dependency:tree prints the full tree with dependency paths.","The flag -Dverbose shows discarded nodes with the omitted reason.","The filter -Dincludes narrows a huge tree down to a single library.","You see the culprit path — module B dragged in the old transitive.","Fix: an exclusion on the offending path or pin the version in management."]},"cryptof-aes-symmetric":{"title":"Symmetric Encryption (AES)","g":"Encryption","hook":"One safe with one key: whoever locks it is who unlocks it — and both sides must hold a copy of that same key.","more":"AES is a symmetric block cipher: the same secret key (128/192/256-bit) is used both to encrypt and to decrypt. Data is split into 16-byte blocks and run through rounds of permutation and substitution. Encryption is fast and well-suited to large volumes of data. The core problem is key delivery: both parties must somehow securely share the same secret ahead of time. That's why real-world protocols use AES to encrypt the traffic itself, while the AES key is delivered via asymmetric crypto. The mode (CBC, GCM) adds an initialization vector and, with GCM, an integrity check.","caps":["Plaintext must be sent so that an eavesdropper can't read it.","Sender and receiver share one common secret key.","AES turns the text into unreadable ciphertext using key K.","Only the ciphertext travels the open channel — useless to an interceptor.","Bob decrypts it back to the original text with the same key K.","The catch of symmetry: one key for two — how do you deliver it securely?"]},"cryptof-asymmetric-keys":{"title":"Asymmetric Crypto: Public and Private Keys","g":"Encryption","hook":"A mailbox with a slot: anyone can drop a letter in (public key), but only the owner can take it out with the small key to the door (private key).","more":"Asymmetric cryptography (RSA, ECC) uses a pair of linked keys: the public key is handed out to everyone, the private key is kept secret. Whatever is encrypted with the public key can only be decrypted with its matching private key, and vice versa. This solves the key-delivery problem of symmetric crypto: no shared secret is needed — the sender encrypts with the recipient's public key. The price is speed: asymmetric crypto is hundreds of times slower than AES, so it's used not to encrypt the traffic itself but only a short session key for AES. Security rests on computationally hard problems (factoring large numbers in RSA, the discrete log on a curve in ECC). The public key can be derived from the private key, but not the other way around.","caps":["The owner generates a pair of mathematically linked keys.","The public key can be handed out to anyone safely.","Alice encrypts the message with Bob's public key.","An interceptor has the public key but CANNOT decrypt with it.","Only Bob unlocks the message with his private key.","The catch: it's slow — so asymmetric crypto only encrypts the AES key."]},"cryptof-digital-signature":{"title":"Digital Signature","g":"Encryption","hook":"A wax seal in reverse: only the owner of the signet ring (private key) can seal it, but anyone can verify the imprint against a sample of the seal (public key).","more":"A digital signature proves authorship and integrity without encrypting the document itself. The signer takes a hash of the message and encrypts it with their private key — that's the signature. Any recipient takes the signer's public key, decrypts the signature back into a hash, and compares it with the hash they compute themselves from the received message. A match means the holder of the private key really signed it (authenticity) and the text wasn't altered in transit (integrity). This is the key pair used in reverse: encryption uses the public key, signing uses the private key. Signatures underpin certificates, JWTs, code signing, and blockchain transactions. Forging a signature without the private key is computationally infeasible.","caps":["There's a document, and we need to prove Alice is its author.","Alice computes a hash of the document — a short fingerprint.","The hash is encrypted with Alice's PRIVATE key — that's the signature.","Bob decrypts the signature with Alice's PUBLIC key → hash H.","Bob hashes the document himself and compares the two hashes.","Match — author confirmed, text untouched. No match — reject."]},"cryptof-hash-salt-password":{"title":"Hash + Salt for Passwords","g":"Encryption","hook":"We don't store the key itself but a plaster cast of it, mixing a pinch of our own sand into each cast — two identical keys yield different casts.","more":"Passwords are never stored in plaintext, nor \"encrypted\" (a cipher is reversible). They're hashed: a one-way function turns the password into a fixed-size fingerprint from which the password can't be recovered. But a bare hash is vulnerable to rainbow tables — precomputed hashes of common passwords. So each password gets a unique random salt, and the password+salt is hashed together. The salt is stored openly next to the hash. This defeats rainbow tables and ensures two users with the same password get different hashes. To verify, you take the entered password and the same salt, compute the hash, and compare. Deliberately slow algorithms (bcrypt, argon2, scrypt) are used — to make brute-forcing expensive.","caps":["Storing the password in plaintext is a no-go — a DB leak means game over.","A hash is irreversible: you can't pull the password out of the fingerprint.","A bare hash is broken by a rainbow table of ready-made hashes.","Add a unique random salt to the password and hash them together.","In the DB: salt (open) + hash. Rainbow tables are useless.","Verify: same password + same salt → hash matches. The algorithm is slow."]},"cryptof-tls-handshake":{"title":"The TLS Handshake Step by Step","g":"Encryption","hook":"Introductions before a conversation: you announce yourselves, show ID, agree on a secret word — and from then on whisper using that word.","more":"The TLS handshake is the negotiation between client and server before secure exchange begins. The client sends a ClientHello with its list of supported ciphers and a random number. The server replies with ServerHello, picks a cipher, and sends its certificate with a public key. The client validates the certificate up the chain of trust to a root CA. The two sides then agree on a shared session key — in modern TLS 1.3 via a Diffie-Hellman exchange, where the private parts are never sent over the wire. The resulting symmetric key drives fast AES, which encrypts all subsequent traffic. This ties together all the previous cards: asymmetric crypto and certificates exist only to safely birth a single symmetric key for AES. TLS 1.3 fits into a single round trip (1-RTT).","caps":["The client says hello: list of ciphers + a random number.","The server picks a cipher, replies with Hello, and sends its certificate.","The client validates the certificate up the chain to a root CA.","Both sides derive a shared session key (DH key exchange).","Done: from here all traffic is encrypted with fast AES under key K."]},"cryptof-pki-cert-chain":{"title":"PKI Certificate Chain","g":"Encryption","hook":"Vouching down a chain: the gatekeeper trusts the student because the dean vouches for them, the rector vouches for the dean, and everyone knows the rector.","more":"PKI answers the question: how does the browser know a site's public key really belongs to that site and hasn't been swapped out. Trust is built as a chain of signatures. The site presents its certificate, signed by the private key of an intermediate CA. The intermediate, in turn, is signed by a root CA. Root certificates are pre-baked into the browser and OS — that's the trust anchor. The browser walks the chain bottom-up, at each step verifying the signature with the public key of the one above, until it reaches a trusted root. Each signature is the same digital signature from the signing card. If any link doesn't check out, is expired, or is revoked — the connection is rejected. This is how signing, asymmetric crypto, and TLS tie together.","caps":["The site presents a certificate — but why should anyone trust it?","The site's certificate is signed by an intermediate CA.","The intermediate CA, in turn, is signed by a root CA.","The root certificate is already baked into the browser and OS — the trust anchor.","The browser walks the chain up, verifying each signature with the public key.","Reached a trusted root — certificate accepted. Any break → reject."]},"fp-gc-roots":{"title":"GC roots and reachability","g":"Garbage collection","hook":"The GC is a janitor with a flashlight. It does not tally who points at whom. It stands on the \"roots\" (stacks of live threads, statics, JNI) and paints with the flashlight everything it can reach by following references. Whatever stays in the dark is garbage — even if two such objects cling tightly to each other: the \"island of the dead\" is swept out anyway.","more":"**Roots (GC roots) are not \"any reference\" but a strictly defined set:**\n- local variables and parameters in stack frames of **live** threads;\n- `static` fields of classes (until the class is unloaded);\n- JNI references from native code (local/global refs);\n- active monitors (objects in `synchronized`), threads.\n\n**The key gotcha — cyclic references.** A naive reference-counting GC (like CPython/COM) will NOT collect two objects referencing each other: both counters = 1 and never reach zero — a leak. The JVM uses **mark-and-sweep**: it paints what is reachable **from the roots**, not what has a non-zero incoming-reference count. So an \"island\" of mutually referencing objects cut off from the roots is collected just fine.\n\n**The flip side:** an object is collected **only** while it is unreachable from the roots. A live `static` List or an un-removed listener keeps a chain alive — and the whole graph beneath it is immortal. That is the classic Java leak: not \"forgot to free\" but \"forgot to drop the reference from a root\".","caps":["GC roots: locals on stacks of live threads, static fields, JNI.","GC paints everything reachable from roots via references — the chain is live.","Objects with no path from roots stay unpainted — garbage candidates.","Two objects reference each other, but neither is tied to a root.","Sweep: the island is unreachable from roots — collected despite mutual refs."]},"fp-write-barrier":{"title":"Write barrier and card table","g":"Garbage collection","hook":"The card table is a \"dirty-page log\" for memory. Every reference store leaves a tick in a log cell (the write barrier), and later the minor GC reads only the marked cells of old gen instead of plowing through all of old. Cheap to write, cheap to search.","more":"**Why at all:** a minor GC collects only young. But an old object may reference a young one — that reference must not be missed, or the live young object gets collected as garbage. Scanning all of old on every minor GC is expensive, so the JVM keeps a card table.\n\n**How the barrier works:** on a store `a.field = b` the JIT inlines a few instructions — it computes the card index `card = addr >> 9` (9 = log2(512)) and writes a \"dirty\" byte into `cardTable[card]`. In classic CMS/Serial/Parallel this is an *unconditional* barrier — it always writes, even for young→young stores (false-positive cards are normal, the price of being cheap).\n\n**Granularity — gotcha:** a card is 512 bytes, not one object. A dirty card forces scanning ALL objects on that 512-byte chunk looking for old→young references. So card \"false sharing\" is real: writing one object dirties its neighbors on the same card.\n\n**G1 is more complex:** it has not just a card table but also per-region *remembered sets* plus a two-phase barrier (SATB pre-barrier for concurrent marking + a post-barrier with a dirty-card queue drained by concurrent refinement threads). G1 cards have three states (clean/dirty/young), not two.\n\n**Cost:** the write barrier is a tax on EVERY heap reference store (not on primitives, not on writes to local variables). Usually ~1-2 instructions, but noticeable in write-heavy code. It's one reason arrays of primitives are cheaper than arrays of objects.","caps":["An old object references a young one — minor GC must find this reference or it collects a live object.","Without a card table the minor GC would scan ALL of old — expensive on every cycle.","On a store a.field=b the JIT inlines a write barrier: card index = addr >> 9.","The barrier marks the corresponding card dirty — 1 byte per ~512B region.","Minor GC scans only the dirty cards of old gen, hunting old->young, skipping clean ones.","After GC the cards reset to clean — the log is ready for the next round of writes."]},"fp-invokedynamic":{"title":"Lambda → invokedynamic","g":"Bytecode & JIT","hook":"A lambda in the .class isn't a finished class — it's an empty \"socket slot\" (invokedynamic). On the first call, LambdaMetafactory acts like an electrician: it stamps out the implementation class and wires it into the CallSite. After that the socket works on its own — the electrician is never called again.","more":"**Gotcha: capture changes everything.**\n\n- **No capture** (`s -> s.isEmpty()`) — LambdaMetafactory caches a single singleton instance and hands it back on every CallSite invocation. No new object is created.\n- **With capture** (`s -> s.startsWith(prefix)`) — bootstrap builds the class once, but `invokedynamic` calls the factory method each time, passing captured values as constructor arguments → **a new object on every execution**. So a capturing lambda is not allocation-free in a hot loop.\n\nVerify it: `javap -c -p` shows `invokedynamic #N, 0` instead of `new $Lambda...`, and the lambda body lives in a `private static synthetic` method (`lambda$method$0`). There is NO anonymous `Outer$1.class` on disk — it is generated at runtime (by default via `InnerClassLambdaMetafactory`; dump it with the `jdk.internal.lambda.dumpProxyClasses` flag).\n\nSubtlety: the lambda body is compiled into an ordinary synthetic method right away — what is created lazily is the functional-interface wrapper class, not the logic itself.","caps":["Source: a lambda is passed as a Predicate into filter.","In bytecode it's NOT an anonymous class but an invokedynamic slot + a synthetic method holding the body.","First call: the slot is empty — the JVM invokes the LambdaMetafactory bootstrap.","The factory lazily stamps out the Predicate implementation class at runtime.","The class is wired into the CallSite: the slot is now linked, the lambda body is reachable.","Repeated calls go straight through — bootstrap is no longer needed."]},"fp-classloader-leak":{"title":"ClassLoader leak on hot redeploy","g":"Memory","hook":"A ClassLoader is like a box holding all your toys: to throw the box away, NOBODY outside may hold even one toy by a string. A single ThreadLocal in a foreign thread pulls one string — and the whole box, plus its Metaspace, stays on the dump forever.","more":"**Gotcha: one ThreadLocal in a thread pool sinks the whole webapp.**\n\nThreads in a `ThreadPoolExecutor` (or a Tomcat acceptor) survive a redeploy. If app code stored an instance of ITS OWN class in a `ThreadLocal`:\n\n```java\nstatic ThreadLocal&lt;MyContext&gt; CTX = new ThreadLocal&lt;&gt;();\nCTX.set(new MyContext()); // forgot remove()\n```\n\nthen the GC-reachability chain is:\n`live Thread` &rarr; `threadLocals (map)` &rarr; `value = MyContext` &rarr; `MyContext.class` &rarr; **`WebappClassLoader`** &rarr; ALL webapp classes.\n\nKey fact: **a ClassLoader holds a strong reference to every class it loaded, and each class holds its ClassLoader.** A SINGLE instance/class escaping outward keeps the whole graph (thousands of Class objects in Metaspace) alive.\n\nTypical 'strings' that pin the old ClassLoader:\n- `ThreadLocal` without `remove()` in long-lived threads;\n- `DriverManager` with an app-registered JDBC driver;\n- static caches/singletons in the JDK or a library (`java.beans.Introspector`, `sun.awt.AppContext`, loggers);\n- `Thread.setContextClassLoader(...)` on a pooled thread;\n- shutdown hooks, unclosed `Timer`/`Thread`.\n\nSymptom: **`OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace`** after the Nth redeploy, not immediately. Each redeploy adds a full copy of the classes; the old ones never GC. A heap dump shows several live `WebappClassLoader` instances — a healthy app has exactly one. Fix: `-XX:+UnlockDiagnosticVMOptions -XX:+ClassUnloadingWithConcurrentMark`, explicit `ThreadLocal.remove()` / `DriverManager.deregisterDriver()` in `contextDestroyed()`, or simply restart the JVM instead of a hot redeploy.","caps":["v1 webapp runs: its ClassLoader holds all classes, a pool thread touches the app's ThreadLocal.","Deploying v2: a new ClassLoader is created, the old one should be GC-collected.","But the thread is alive: the ThreadLocal still holds Ctx from v1 — the string outward is not released.","Reachability chain: Ctx pulls its class, the class pulls the ENTIRE ClassLoader#1.","ClassLoader#1 never dies and keeps ALL its classes in Metaspace — the whole graph leaks.","After N redeploys class copies accumulate → OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace."]},"fp-safe-publication":{"title":"Safe publication of an object","g":"Memory model","hook":"Publishing a reference is like texting a friend your home address while the floor is still being laid and the walls painted. If you don't wait until the WHOLE house is finished (the barrier), your friend walks in and falls through a hole — they see default fields. final / volatile / synchronized / a thread-safe collection are the 'handover certificate': the reference is given out only after all fields are visible.","more":"## The rot: 'saw non-null, but garbage inside'\n\nThe classic bug — a race on `getInstance()` with no barrier:\n\n```java\nclass Holder {\n    private int x;          // plain field\n    Holder() { x = 42; }    // write in constructor\n}\n\n// thread A\nshared = new Holder();      // (1) allocate (2) x=42 (3) store reference\n```\n\nThe JMM does **not** guarantee the order of (2) and (3) for another thread. Thread B may see `shared != null` yet read `x == 0`. Worse: a thread can observe a **partially constructed** object before the constructor even returns.\n\n### What builds happens-before on publication\n- **`final` field** — the JLS gives a special guarantee: if the object did NOT 'escape' the constructor (`this` not published early), any thread that sees the reference also sees the correctly written `final` fields. A freeze barrier is placed at the end of the constructor.\n- **`volatile` reference** — a `volatile` write → a later `volatile` read of the same variable gives HB; all plain writes BEFORE the volatile write are visible after the volatile read.\n- **`synchronized`** — publish under the same lock (write inside the block + read inside the block).\n- **Thread-safe collection / `AtomicReference`** — `put`/`set` and `get` already contain the needed barriers internally.\n\n### The main gotcha\n`final` saves you ONLY if `this` did not escape the constructor. Registering a listener / `this` in a shared field inside the constructor (`registry.add(this)`) breaks the guarantee — another thread gets `this` before the freeze. And `final` does not cover later mutations of the field after the constructor: for mutable state you need volatile/lock/concurrent collection.\n\nThe most reliable default: **immutable + all fields `final`**, or publish via `volatile`/`AtomicReference`/`ConcurrentHashMap`.","caps":["Thread A creates the object: storing the reference is three steps, and the order of (2) and (3) is not guaranteed for other threads.","Reordering moves step (3) ahead: the reference is published while field x is still at its default.","Thread B reads the non-null reference, steps inside — and sees x=0 instead of 42. A race on a partial object.","Safe publication: add a barrier. final / volatile / synchronized / a thread-safe collection lock the field writes BEFORE handing out the reference.","The barrier builds happens-before: A's field writes are visible before the reference is handed out, so B sees the whole object — x=42."]},"fp-tx-rollback-only":{"title":"rollback-only: why the outer commit fails","g":"Transactions","hook":"The inner method never opened its own transaction — it sits inside the same physical one. When it throws, it doesn't 'roll back locally', it crosses out the shared form with a rollback-only marker. You may swallow the exception, but the marker is already on the form — the cashier (commit) sees it and shreds the whole receipt: UnexpectedRollbackException.","more":"## One root — two REQUIRED, one physical transaction\n\n`Propagation.REQUIRED` (the default) means: 'if a transaction is open, join it; otherwise open a new one'. The outer method opened one, the inner **joined** it. There is a single shared physical transaction. The inner has no savepoint of its own, no commit/rollback of its own — it is merely a participant.\n\n## Who actually sets rollback-only\n\nWhen a `RuntimeException` escapes the inner method, the proxy `TransactionInterceptor` catches it. Because the inner method is a **participant**, not the transaction owner, the interceptor cannot roll back the physical transaction right now (its owner is the outer method). So it does the only thing available: it **marks the shared transaction as rollback-only** (`setRollbackOnly()` on the shared `TransactionStatus`).\n\n## Why try/catch doesn't save you\n\n```java\n@Transactional // REQUIRED\npublic void outer() {\n    try {\n        inner.doWork();      // throws RuntimeException\n    } catch (Exception e) {\n        log.warn(\"swallowed\", e); // flag is ALREADY set\n    }\n    // ... keep working ...\n}                            // commit -> UnexpectedRollbackException\n\n@Transactional // REQUIRED — same physical transaction\npublic void doWork() { throw new IllegalStateException(); }\n```\n\nSwallowing an exception in Java is trivial, but the `rollbackOnly` flag lives in the `TransactionStatus`, not on the call stack. The `catch` catches the exception object, but it does not clear the flag. At commit time Spring sees the flag -> rolls the physical transaction back -> and, so the caller doesn't believe data was saved, throws `UnexpectedRollbackException` ('you asked for commit, but it was actually a rollback').\n\n## Gotcha and how to fix it\n\n- **Gotcha:** the error surfaces not where it 'broke', but from `outer` at commit — debugging is misleading. The stack trace shows the proxy/`commit`, not the real cause.\n- **Fix A (semantically correct):** if the inner operation truly is optional and its rollback should not kill everything — move it into a separate bean with `@Transactional(propagation = REQUIRES_NEW)`. Then it gets its **own** physical transaction: it rolls back in isolation, the outer one stays clean. (Remember: REQUIRES_NEW is a real separate connection/suspend, not free.)\n- **Fix B:** `@Transactional(noRollbackFor = SomeException.class)` on the inner — then that exception does not set rollback-only.\n- **Self-invocation trap:** if `inner()` is called as `this.inner()` inside the same bean — the proxy is bypassed entirely and the annotation on `inner` is ignored. You need a call through an injected bean / self-proxy.\n- **Knob:** `globalRollbackOnParticipationFailure=false` (on `AbstractPlatformTransactionManager`) changes the behavior, but it's a sharp tool — don't touch it by default.","caps":["The outer REQUIRED opened one physical transaction.","The inner REQUIRED opens none — it joins the same transaction.","The inner throws a RuntimeException — being a participant, it can't roll back itself.","The interceptor doesn't roll back — it sets the rollback-only flag on the shared transaction.","The outer caught and swallowed the exception — but the flag on the transaction stayed.","outer tries commit -> flag is seen -> forced rollback and UnexpectedRollbackException."]},"fp-persistence-flush":{"title":"Dirty checking and flush","g":"Data / JPA","hook":"The persistence context is a security camera at the store entrance: when an entity is loaded it takes a photo (snapshot) of its fields. You walk around touching merchandise (changing fields) and write no receipts. But on the way out (flush) the guard compares the current shelf with the photo and issues exactly the UPDATEs that differ. No save() needed — you're already being watched.","more":"**The gist.** A managed entity inside the persistence context keeps two states: the live object and a **snapshot** (an array of the original field values captured at load/persist). On `flush()` Hibernate walks every managed entity and compares it field-by-field — that's **dirty checking**. For fields that differ it generates an `UPDATE`. An explicit `save()`/`merge()` for an already-managed entity is not needed.\n\n**When flush fires** (`AUTO` mode, the default):\n- before a JPQL/HQL/Criteria/native query that could depend on unwritten changes;\n- on transaction `commit()`;\n- on an explicit `entityManager.flush()`.\n\n**Concrete gotcha.** `FlushModeType.AUTO` does NOT always flush before native SQL: Hibernate doesn't parse raw SQL and can't know which tables it touches, so it may **not** flush before `createNativeQuery(...)`, and the native query sees stale data in the DB. Fix it with a manual `entityManager.flush()` before the native query (or `FlushModeType.ALWAYS`).\n\n**Second gotcha.** Modify a detached entity (outside the session) and you get no dirty checking — you need `merge()`. And a `@Transactional` method with no explicit save at the end still writes a managed entity's changes on commit — a classic source of \"I never saved anything, yet the DB changed.\"\n\n**Cost.** Dirty checking costs CPU to compare all fields of all managed entities. On large sessions use `@Immutable`, read-only queries (`Session.setReadOnly`/`hibernate.readOnly`), or DTO projections so no snapshot is kept at all.","caps":["Load the entity: both the object and a snapshot of its fields go into the context.","Change a field via a setter — only the object is edited, the snapshot is untouched, no save() called.","Flush happens (a query or commit) — the entity is compared against the snapshot.","Dirty checking: the name field differs — Hibernate marks it as changed.","Hibernate itself generates an UPDATE for the changed fields and sends it to the DB.","After flush the snapshot is refreshed and equals the entity — no changes, no extra UPDATE."]},"fp-gap-locks":{"title":"Gap / next-key locks vs phantoms","g":"Transactions & locks","hook":"It's like reserving not only the taken seats in a cinema row but the empty chairs between them too: while you're still there nobody can squeeze into your range — so when you look again the row is exactly the same.","more":"Under **REPEATABLE READ**, InnoDB protects a range not only via the snapshot data (MVCC) but physically — with locks. A **gap lock** locks the interval between index values (e.g. the open interval `(10, 20)`) where there is currently no record. A **next-key lock** = a record plus the gap preceding it, i.e. a half-open interval like `(10, 20]`. When `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` (or `UPDATE`/`DELETE`) scans a range over an index, next-key locks are placed on every matched record and on the gaps around it. A concurrent `INSERT` whose key falls inside a locked gap takes an insert-intention lock that conflicts with the gap lock and **waits** until the first transaction commits. That's why re-reading shows no new \"phantom\" rows.\n\n**Main gotcha:** gap locks exist **only on REPEATABLE READ** (and SERIALIZABLE). On **READ COMMITTED** InnoDB essentially doesn't take them — phantoms become possible, but you get fewer deadlocks. Second nuance: gap locks are purely **inhibiting** and don't conflict with each other — two transactions can hold a gap lock on the same interval at once, and that's the classic deadlock trap: both grab the gap lock, both try to `INSERT` into it → **deadlock**. Also: gap locks are placed during range index lookups; a query on a unique index with an exact equality (`WHERE id = 20`) locks no gaps — just the row itself.","caps":["Under REPEATABLE READ, Tx1 runs a range SELECT ... FOR UPDATE over the id index.","InnoDB locks not only row 20 but also the empty gaps between records — these are gap locks.","A row plus the gap preceding it forms a next-key lock — the half-open interval (10, 20].","Tx2 tries INSERT id=15 into the locked gap (10,20) and goes into a wait.","Tx1 re-runs the same SELECT — the insert of 15 is still pending, no new rows: no phantom appears.","After Tx1 COMMIT the locks are released — the waiting INSERT in Tx2 finally proceeds and key 15 lands in the index."]},"fp-refresh-rotation":{"title":"Refresh token rotation","g":"Networking & security","hook":"A refresh token is a one-time cinema ticket: each entry the old stub gets torn off and you get a fresh one. If someone shows up with the old, already-torn stub, the ticket was stolen — so the whole screening (the entire family) is cancelled.","more":"**Core idea:** access lives minutes (kept in memory, never revoked — it just expires). refresh lives long and is stored in DB/Redis. On every `POST /refresh` the server issues a new pair and marks the old refresh as used/rotated, tying everything to one `family_id`.\n\n**Gotcha 1 — race on parallel requests.** With two open tabs both firing `/refresh` with the SAME refresh, the second request arrives with an already-spent token; the server reads it as theft and nukes the whole family, logging out an honest user. Fix with a short grace period (old token stays valid for a few seconds) or a single-flight mutex on the client plus response dedup on the server.\n\n**Gotcha 2 — no storage, no rotation.** A stateless JWT refresh can't be revoked: to invalidate the old one and catch reuse you need server-side state (at least a `jti` + status). That's why refresh is usually an opaque token or a JWT validated against the DB.\n\n**Gotcha 3 — what exactly to kill.** Revoke the ENTIRE family (all descendants of the compromised refresh), not a single token: the thief may already have spun the chain further. Hence the `family_id`/token chain.","caps":["Login: server issues a pair — short-lived access and long-lived refresh R1, family F1.","Access expired — client sends R1 to /refresh to get a new pair.","Rotation: R1 is marked used, a new access and refresh R2 of the same family are issued.","The thief stole R1 earlier and now replays the already-spent R1.","Reuse of a spent R1 = theft signal: the server revokes the ENTIRE family F1."]},"fp-optional-orelse":{"title":"orElse vs orElseGet","g":"Language contracts","hook":"orElse is a banquet laid out just in case: the cook fries the expensive dish (a DB query) BEFORE you even say you're hungry. orElseGet is a waiter with a menu: it cooks only if you actually order (the Optional is empty). Remember: orElse(expensive) always pays, orElseGet(() -> expensive) pays on demand.","more":"### The core trap: the orElse argument is ALWAYS evaluated\n\n`orElse(x)` is an ordinary method call. By Java's rules the argument `x` is evaluated **before** entering the method, regardless of whether the Optional is empty:\n\n```java\nOptional.of(\"cached\")\n        .orElse(loadFromDb());   // loadFromDb() runs ALWAYS, even here\n```\n\nThe value is present (`\"cached\"`), yet `loadFromDb()` has already run — a wasted query, and if it contained an `INSERT` / `counter++` / logging, the side effect happened too. `orElse` simply **discards** the unneeded result.\n\n`orElseGet(Supplier)` takes a **lambda** — unexecuted code. The method first checks `isPresent()` and calls `supplier.get()` only when the Optional is empty:\n\n```java\nOptional.of(\"cached\")\n        .orElseGet(() -> loadFromDb());  // loadFromDb() is NOT called\n```\n\n### When the difference doesn't matter\nIf the default is a cheap, side-effect-free constant (`orElse(\"\")`, `orElse(0)`, `orElse(List.of())`), use `orElse`: it's shorter and more readable. The difference only matters when the default is **expensive** (DB, network, heavy computation) or **has a side effect**.\n\n### The same trap in other APIs\n- `map.getOrDefault(k, expensive())` — `expensive()` is also always evaluated; the lazy counterpart is `computeIfAbsent(k, key -> expensive())`.\n- `requireNonNullElse(a, b)` vs `requireNonNullElseGet(a, () -> b)` — exactly the same eager/lazy pair.","caps":["The Optional is non-empty — it holds \"cached\", while the default comes from an expensive DB query.","orElse(loadFromDb()): the argument is a value, computed BEFORE the method is called.","The DB query has already run even though the value is present — the result is just thrown away.","orElseGet(() -> loadFromDb()): the argument is a lambda, the code is passed unexecuted.","The method first checks isPresent(): the value exists, so the lambda never runs.","Bottom line: orElse always pays for the default, orElseGet only when the Optional is empty."]},"fp-chm-internals":{"title":"ConcurrentHashMap internals (Java 8+)","g":"Collections","hook":"It is not a bank with 16 separate teller-segments (as in Java 7), but one long row of storage lockers. An empty locker you claim silently and instantly (CAS — slap on a tag); if a locker is already taken, you lock ONLY that little door while the rest of the row keeps working. When one locker gets stuffed with too many items, the keeper swaps the stacked list for a tidy tree-shelf. And the total item counter is not a single shared one (threads would fight over it) but smeared across several mini-tables, summed on the fly.","more":"## What exactly changed in Java 8+\n\nIn Java 7 `ConcurrentHashMap` was an array of **segments** (`Segment extends ReentrantLock`), and concurrency was capped by their count (`concurrencyLevel`, default 16). In Java 8 segments were **dropped**: now it is one `Node[] table`, and lock granularity is a **single bucket**.\n\n### Three write paths in `put`\n- **Bucket empty** → `casTabAt(...)` — an atomic CAS places a new `Node` with no lock. If it fails (someone beat us), we retry the loop.\n- **Bucket occupied** → `synchronized (head)` on the **head `Node` of that specific bucket**. One bucket is locked; the others stay parallel.\n- **Resize in progress** (`hash == MOVED`, `-1`) → a writer first **helps** transfer (`helpTransfer`), then writes.\n\n### When a list becomes a tree\nTwo conditions, both required:\n- chain length in the bucket `&ge; 8` (`TREEIFY_THRESHOLD`), AND\n- `table.length &ge; 64` (`MIN_TREEIFY_CAPACITY`).\n\nIf 8 is reached but the table is smaller than 64 — **no tree, a resize instead** (growing tends to spread collisions and is cheaper than a tree on a small table). The tree is a `TreeBin` (a wrapper over a red-black tree), not a bare `TreeNode`: it needs its own tiny read/write lock during rebalancing. On shrinking back to `&le; 6` (`UNTREEIFY_THRESHOLD`) it unrolls into a list again.\n\n### Why `size()` is approximate\nA single shared counter would be a contention point. Instead it is `LongAdder`-style: `baseCount` plus a `CounterCell[]` array, across which threads spread increments (each hits its own cell). `size()` = `baseCount + sum(CounterCell[])`. Under load the result is an **estimate** — while you sum the cells, someone is already mutating the map. There is no exact snapshot; `size()`/`mappingCount()` give an \"approximately now\" view.\n\n## Gotcha\n- **`size()` must not back strict invariants** under concurrency (`if (map.size() == 0) ...` is a race). For emptiness there is `isEmpty()`, but it too is not atomic against other ops.\n- **`synchronized` on the head `Node`** — if you somehow lock an object that happens to be a bucket head, you risk surprise contention/deadlock (in practice the head is an internal `Node` that never escapes, but do not break the abstraction).\n- **`computeIfAbsent` holds the bucket `synchronized`** while the function runs: a heavy or recursive (into the same map / same bucket) lambda will block the bucket, and re-entrance into the same bucket may hang or throw. Mapping functions must be short and must not touch that same key/bucket.\n- `null` keys and values are **forbidden** — precisely so that `get()==null` unambiguously means \"absent\" without locking.","caps":["Java 7 is gone: instead of an array of 16 segment-locks there is now one shared Node[] table, with the lock at the level of a single bucket.","put into an empty bucket: an atomic CAS places the Node with no lock. Lost the race — retry the loop.","Collision: bucket taken. Take synchronized on the HEAD Node of that bucket only — other buckets are written in parallel.","Chain grew to >=8 AND table>=64 — the bucket's list is rebuilt into a red-black TreeBin.","size() is approximate: not one counter but baseCount + a CounterCell[] array (LongAdder-style), summed on the fly."]},"fp-window-ranking":{"title":"ROW_NUMBER / RANK / DENSE_RANK","g":"PostgreSQL & types","hook":"Three judges look at the same scoreboard 90, 80, 80, 70. The first hands out tickets in order — ties don't exist for him. The second fairly gives two athletes the same place, then skips an empty number. The third also honors the tie, but loses no places — he counts straight on.","more":"**The point.** All three are window functions driven by the `ORDER BY` inside `OVER (...)`. They differ only in how they treat ties.\n\nData `score` = `90, 80, 80, 70` with `ORDER BY score DESC`:\n\n| score | ROW_NUMBER | RANK | DENSE_RANK |\n|------:|:---------:|:----:|:----------:|\n| 90 | 1 | 1 | 1 |\n| 80 | 2 | **2** | **2** |\n| 80 | 3 | **2** | **2** |\n| 70 | 4 | **4** | **3** |\n\n- **ROW_NUMBER** — unique running number, ignores ties (non-deterministic for equal rows without a tie-breaker).\n- **RANK** — tie shares the rank, then a gap (after two `2`s it jumps to `4`).\n- **DENSE_RANK** — shared tie, no gap (`2`, then `3`).\n\n**The main gotcha.** The tie sits at rank **2**, not **1**. `90` alone occupies rank 1, so both `80`s are second place — `RANK` is never `1,1` here.","caps":["Given: four scores, sorted by score descending.","ROW_NUMBER: unique running numbers, ties ignored.","RANK: both 80s share rank 2, the single 90 owns rank 1.","After the 80 pair rank 3 vanishes — RANK jumps to 4.","DENSE_RANK: same tie at 2, but no gap — then 3.","Result: one dataset, three different numberings on a tie."]},"fp-kstream-ktable":{"title":"KStream vs KTable","g":"Kafka","hook":"A KStream is a roll of cash-register receipts: every receipt is its own event you cannot overwrite. A KTable is the live account-balance board: you see only the latest number per key, old ones are gone.","more":"**Core.** `KStream` is an append-only stream of facts: each `(k,v)` is independent — two records with the same key are two events, both survive. `KTable` is a changelog projection by key: it keeps only the **latest** value per key (upsert), and under the hood it's a compacted topic where old versions of a key get cleaned up by compaction.\n\n**Tombstone.** A `(k, null)` record in a KTable is a *tombstone*: it deletes key `k` from the materialized table (and marks it for removal at compaction). That's **key-deletion** semantics, not arithmetic.\n\n**⚠️ Gotcha (the bug in the previous version).**\n- Wrong: \"null in `groupByKey().reduce(...)` zeroes the aggregate.\" No — `null` in a KTable means \"this key no longer exists.\"\n- `KStream → KTable`: `stream.toTable()` or `groupByKey().reduce()/aggregate()` — records sharing a key now collapse into one current value.\n- `KTable → KStream`: `table.toStream()` — tombstones come out as records with `value == null`; filter them or you get an NPE downstream.\n- In `reduce`, an incoming `null` value for a key is treated as **removing that key's aggregate**, not \"add zero\" — conflating those was the mistake.\n\n**When to use which.** Facts/events (clicks, orders, payments) → `KStream`. State / \"latest per key\" (client profile, current price, balance) → `KTable`. A stream-table join = enriching an event with current state.","caps":["KStream is append-only: two records with key A are two distinct events, both alive.","KTable projects by key: it keeps only the latest value per key (upsert).","A new upsert A->35 overwrites key A's value; the previous 20 is gone.","Record (A,null) is a tombstone: key A is deleted from the table — NOT an aggregate reset.","Under the hood a KTable is a compacted topic: compaction purges old versions and the key's tombstone.","table.toStream() returns a stream and the tombstone surfaces as value==null — filter it or NPE."]},"fp-tcp-handshake":{"title":"TCP 3-way handshake","g":"Networking & Security","hook":"Before talking over the radio you swap three lines: \"come in — seq x\", \"got you, x+1 — my seq y\", \"got your y+1\". Now both sides know they're heard — and they talk.","more":"The TCP 3-way handshake sets up a connection before any data flows. Goal: both sides must confirm the channel is two-way and synchronize their initial sequence numbers (ISN).\n\n**Three segments:**\n1. Client &rarr; **SYN** with `seq=x` (its random ISN).\n2. Server &rarr; **SYN-ACK**: `seq=y` (its own ISN) and `ack=x+1` — acknowledging the client's SYN.\n3. Client &rarr; **ACK** with `ack=y+1` — acknowledging the server's SYN.\n\nAfter these three segments the connection is **ESTABLISHED**, and data follows immediately.\n\nKey detail: **a SYN consumes one sequence number** (so does FIN). That's why the server acknowledges `x+1`, not `x` — even though the SYN carried no data bytes. Same trick in step three: `y+1`.\n\nCost: the first data byte goes out after **1 RTT** (the client often piggybacks the third ACK on its first request). **TLS adds another RTT on top** — the encryption handshake runs over the already-established TCP (TLS 1.3 — +1 RTT, TLS 1.2 — +2). So a cold HTTPS request = TCP + TLS round-trips before the first response byte.\n\nGotchas: if the SYN is lost, retransmits back off exponentially (1s, 2s, 4s…) — that's the \"hanging on connect\" for seconds. SYN-flood (half-open connections) is mitigated with SYN cookies.","caps":["Client sends SYN with its initial number seq=x","Server replies SYN-ACK: its own seq=y and ack=x+1","The SYN consumed one number — that's why the server acks x+1","Client sends ACK with ack=y+1 — the third and final segment","All three segments done — connection ESTABLISHED, data follows","First byte costs 1 RTT; TLS adds another RTT on top"]},"fp-chain-responsibility":{"title":"Chain of Responsibility","g":"Patterns","hook":"It's like a checkpoint with several guard posts: you reach the first one, and it either handles your request itself or waves you on to the next. So on until someone handles it or the posts run out.","more":"**Core idea:** a request travels down a chain of handlers. Each handler decides: handle it itself **or** pass it on via the `next` link. The sender doesn't know which handler will process it — it only knows the start of the chain.\n\n**Correct Java/Spring examples:**\n- **Servlet Filters** (`javax.servlet.Filter`) — `chain.doFilter(req, resp)` passes the request to the next filter.\n- **Spring Security filter chain** — `AuthenticationFilter`, `AuthorizationFilter`, etc.; each link decides to pass through or abort.\n- **Middleware** (the same technique in other stacks).\n\n```java\nabstract class Handler {\n    protected Handler next;\n    Handler linkWith(Handler next) { this.next = next; return next; }\n    abstract void handle(Request r);\n    protected void passToNext(Request r) {\n        if (next != null) next.handle(r);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Gotcha (and why the previous slide was dropped):** logging levels (`java.util.logging` `Level.INFO/WARNING/SEVERE`, or log4j/slf4j) are **NOT** Chain of Responsibility. There's no chain of objects passing a request to each other via `next`; there's a single **filtering threshold** — the level's number is compared against a configured minimum, and the message either passes or doesn't. No 'pass to the next handler' — it's a plain if-threshold, not a chain of responsibility.\n\n**Second common slip:** forgetting to call `next.handle()` (the analog of `chain.doFilter()`) — the request 'falls through' and is silently lost; the chain breaks midway.","caps":["A request enters the start of the handler chain.","Auth didn't handle it — passes on via next.","Authz isn't final either — calls chain.doFilter() onward.","The App handler processes the request — chain complete.","Anti-example: log levels are a threshold, not a chain."]},"fp-app-boot":{"title":"Life of a Java app: from `java -jar` to ready","hook":"Like opening a restaurant: recipes printed in advance (javac), staff arrives (JVM), dishes taken out as needed (classes), cooks warm up (JIT), tables and links set up (DI), the 'open' sign goes up (ready).","g":"end-to-end flow","caps":["At BUILD time: javac compiles .java into platform-independent bytecode (.class) and packs it into a .jar. The source isn't needed at runtime.","Launch: the JVM starts, reads the jar manifest, finds main(). It reserves memory for the heap (objects) and stack (calls).","The ClassLoader loads .class files from the jar into Metaspace on demand, verifies bytecode and links. Classes load lazily — on first use.","First the JVM interprets bytecode. Hot (frequently called) methods are JIT-compiled to native code — then they fly. That's why warm-up matters.","The framework brings up the context: scans beans, resolves dependencies (DI), opens DB pools, starts the web server.","The app listens on its port and is ready to serve. The readiness probe returns OK — the load balancer starts routing traffic in."]},"fp-request-path":{"title":"Path of an HTTP request through the backend","hook":"Like an order in a restaurant: security at the door (filters), waiter takes the order (controller), cook prepares it (service), grabs goods from the pantry/fridge (DB/cache), orders missing items from a supplier (other service), serves the dish (response).","g":"end-to-end flow","caps":["The request hits a reverse proxy (nginx). It terminates TLS (decrypts) and routes to the right service/instance by rules.","Inside the app — a filter chain: authentication (who you are by token), authorization (what's allowed), logging, rate-limit.","The router finds the controller by URL+method. It validates input, deserializes JSON into an object and calls the business logic.","The service layer runs business rules. It usually opens a transaction for the duration of data work.","Reads/writes data: checks the cache first; on a miss goes to the DB via a connection pool. The transaction commits at the end.","Need external data — it calls another service (REST/gRPC) or emits an event to Kafka. With a timeout and circuit breaker so it won't hang.","Assembles the result, serializes to JSON, sets the status code and returns it back through the filters to the client."]},"fp-kafka-path":{"title":"Path of a message in Kafka: from producer to commit","hook":"Like a kitchen order rail: the waiter clips a ticket to the stack by type (partition by key), the ticket is copied as backup (replication), the cook takes them one by one (consumer), prepares (processing), marks 'done' (offset commit).","g":"end-to-end flow","caps":["The producer builds a message (key, value) and sends it to a topic. The partition is chosen by the key hash — one key always goes to one partition, so ordering is preserved.","The partition's leader broker appends the message to the end of the log (append-only) and assigns it an offset — a sequence number.","Replicas pull the record. With acks=all the producer waits for confirmation from all in-sync replicas — a guarantee the message won't be lost if the leader dies.","A consumer group subscribes to the topic; partitions are split among consumers (one partition — one consumer per group). Each reads its own by offset.","The consumer processes the message idempotently — in case of a redelivery. Pace heavy work, otherwise a timeout → rebalance and reprocessing.","After successful processing the consumer commits the offset — 'processed up to here'. If it dies before committing, it re-reads from the last commit (at-least-once)."]},"fp-sql-path":{"title":"Path of a SQL query inside the database","hook":"Like searching a huge archive: understand the request (parsing), pick a route — by the index or a full scan (planner), go fetch the folders (execution), some are on the desk, some from the far warehouse (buffer/disk), hand over only what you're allowed to see (MVCC).","g":"end-to-end flow","caps":["The DB receives the SQL text, parses it into a tree, checks syntax and that the tables/columns exist.","The optimizer builds a plan: use an index or a full scan? which join order? It estimates cost from table statistics.","The executor follows the plan: hits the index for the addresses of the needed rows, then fetches the rows themselves.","The needed pages are looked up in the buffer cache (RAM). If absent — read from disk (slow) and cached for later.","For each row, visibility is checked by transaction version (MVCC) — so a reader doesn't see others' uncommitted changes.","The selected rows are assembled into a result and returned. For writes, changes become visible to others only after commit."]},"fp-deploy-k8s":{"title":"Deploy to Kubernetes: from `kubectl apply` to traffic","hook":"Like swapping a shift in a store without closing: announce the new lineup (apply), assign them to floors (scheduler), hand out uniforms (pull image), check they're alive (liveness), let only ready ones to the registers (readiness), swap one at a time so the store keeps working (rolling).","g":"end-to-end flow","caps":["You apply a manifest (Deployment). The API server stores the desired state in etcd: 'I want N replicas of image v2'.","The scheduler picks nodes for the new pods based on resources (CPU/memory) and placement constraints.","On the node, kubelet pulls the Docker image from the registry (layer by layer, caching) and starts the container.","The container starts. The liveness probe watches that the process is alive; if it hangs/dies, the pod is restarted.","The readiness probe waits until the app is truly ready (warmed up, connections open). Only then is the pod added to the Service — traffic is routed to it.","Old v1 pods are shut down one by one as new v2 ones become ready — with no downtime. If something's wrong — a fast rollback to v1."]}},"prin":{"gc":{"title":"Garbage Collection","what":"GC: the broom sweeps dead objects out of the heap"},"jvm-memory":{"title":"Stack and Heap","what":"The stack pushes/pops frames; references point into the heap grid managed by the GC"},"class-loading":{"title":"Class Loading","what":"A class flies through Bootstrap to App loaders and is loaded into Metaspace"},"jit":{"title":"JIT Compilation","what":"Hot bytecode is compiled into native code (JIT)"},"thread":{"title":"How a thread works","what":"A thread carries a task from the queue onto a pulsing CPU core"},"thread-pool":{"title":"Thread pool","what":"Thread pool: tokens fly from the queue to the workers"},"lock-mutex":{"title":"Lock (mutex)","what":"A thread holds the mutex in the critical section while the others wait in the queue"},"producer-consumer":{"title":"Producer-Consumer","what":"The producer puts tokens into the queue, the consumer takes them out"},"cas":{"title":"CAS (compare-and-swap)","what":"CAS: a thread compares the expected value and swaps atomically, otherwise 🔁"},"di-wiring":{"title":"Dependency Injection","what":"The container creates beans and injects them into the nodes that depend on them"},"transactional-proxy":{"title":"@Transactional via proxy","what":"The call goes through a proxy: tx open -> method -> commit"},"request-flow":{"title":"Request flow in Spring","what":"The request travels through the Spring layers and back"},"btree-lookup":{"title":"B-tree index lookup","what":"B-tree key lookup: the token descends root → node → leaf"},"mvcc":{"title":"MVCC: row versions","what":"MVCC: row versions — reader sees v1, writer creates v2, v1 → dead"},"connection-pool":{"title":"Connection pool","what":"Connection pool: the app borrows a connection, works against the DB, returns it"},"transaction-commit":{"title":"Transaction commit","what":"Transaction: changes accumulate, then an atomic commit/rollback"},"replication":{"title":"Replication","what":"The master writes, data flows to 2 replicas"},"kafka-flow":{"title":"Kafka Flow","what":"Kafka message flow: producer → 3 partitions → consumers"},"consumer-group":{"title":"Consumer Group","what":"3 partitions → one consumer each within the group, messages streaming through"},"load-balancer":{"title":"Load Balancing","what":"Load balancing: requests hit the balancer and fan out across 3 nodes"},"circuit-breaker":{"title":"Circuit Breaker","what":"Circuit Breaker: closed→open→half-open cycle driven by request traffic"},"outbox":{"title":"Transactional Outbox","what":"Transactional Outbox: single tx → relay → Kafka"},"prd-observer":{"title":"Observer","what":"The Subject changes state and broadcasts the event as a wave to all subscribers"},"prd-decorator":{"title":"Decorator","what":"The request passes through wrapper layers inward to the core and returns enriched"},"pri-load-balancer-rr":{"title":"Load balancer — round-robin","what":"Requests are distributed across backends one after another, in a circular order"},"pri-cache-aside-hitmiss":{"title":"Cache — hit and miss","what":"A hit returns instantly from the cache; a miss goes to the database and populates the cache for next time"},"prt-test-pyramid-fill":{"title":"Test pyramid","what":"Bottom to top: many fast unit tests, fewer integration tests, a handful of e2e tests"},"prt-mock-intercept":{"title":"Mock intercepts the call","what":"The SUT calls a port — the mock intercepts the call and returns a canned response, the real DB stays untouched"},"http":{"title":"HTTP Request/Response","what":"Animated HTTP request/response cycle: client → wire → server"},"tcp-handshake":{"title":"TCP Handshake","what":"TCP handshake: SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK flying between two nodes"},"tls-handshake":{"title":"TLS Handshake","what":"Animated TLS handshake: certificate, key exchange, encrypted channel"},"oauth-flow":{"title":"OAuth2 Flow","what":"Animated OAuth2 flow: client → authz → token → resource"},"binary-search":{"title":"Binary search","what":"The pointer jumps to the middle, discards one half, and narrows in on the target"},"bfs":{"title":"Breadth-first search (BFS)","what":"A BFS wave: highlighted nodes fan out level by level from the start"},"pra-quicksort-partition":{"title":"Quicksort: partition around a pivot","what":"The pivot is fixed while two pointers converge: smaller values go left, larger ones go right, and the pivot lands in its final spot"},"pra-heap-siftdown":{"title":"Heap: sift-down","what":"The offending element at the top sinks down, swapping with the larger of its two children at each step, in O(log n)"},"prg-rebase":{"title":"git rebase","what":"The branch's commits are rewritten on top of a new base — the old ones stay dangling until garbage collection"},"prg-merge":{"title":"3-way merge","what":"Two branches converge in a merge commit with two parents — snapshots are compared against the common base"},"hashmap-put":{"title":"HashMap: put","what":"HashMap put animation: key → hash → bucket"},"string-pool":{"title":"String pool","what":"Literal references point to a single object in the string pool"},"arraylist-resize":{"title":"ArrayList: growth","what":"ArrayList growth — elements are copied into a 2x array"},"prv-cicd-pipeline":{"title":"CI/CD pipeline","what":"A commit flows through the pipeline stage by stage — each stage only kicks off once the previous one has passed"},"prv-rolling-update":{"title":"Rolling update","what":"The Deployment tears down old pods and brings up new ones one at a time, without losing capacity"},"api-gateway":{"title":"API Gateway","what":"Clients → pulse-gateway → route to microservices, token flow"},"cache-aside":{"title":"Cache-aside","what":"Cache-aside: a hit serves from cache, a miss reads from the DB and populates the cache"},"sharding":{"title":"Sharding","what":"Tokens are distributed across 3 shards by key (flow)"},"prq-buildtime-di":{"title":"Build-time DI (ARC)","what":"The bean graph is resolved at build time — at runtime the framework just pulls the ready-made wiring out of the bytecode"},"prq-aot-native":{"title":"AOT native compilation (GraalVM)","what":"Closed-world analysis walks the reachable code, strips out dead code, and compiles ahead of time into a native binary"},"prq-event-loop":{"title":"Reactive event loop","what":"A single loop thread spins without blocking, dispatching ready I/O events; blocking work is offloaded to a worker pool"},"prrx-backpressure":{"title":"Backpressure","what":"A slow consumer pulls demand itself via request(n) — the producer emits exactly that many items, no more."},"prrx-flatmap":{"title":"flatMap","what":"Each element is expanded into its own stream, and all of those streams are merged into one."},"prgrpc-bidi-stream":{"title":"gRPC bidirectional streaming","what":"A single HTTP/2 connection carrying two opposing message streams that flow independently."},"prsd-token-bucket":{"title":"Token Bucket","what":"Tokens drip into a bucket at a constant rate; each request consumes one token or is rejected."},"prsd-fanout-feed":{"title":"Fan-out on Write","what":"A single author's post is copied into the personal feeds of all their followers."},"prbld-lifecycle-conveyor":{"title":"Maven phase pipeline","what":"An artifact moves through the lifecycle phases in order: validate → compile → test → package → install."},"prbld-version-mediation":{"title":"Version mediation","what":"In the dependency tree, Maven picks the version closest to the root (nearest-wins)."},"prcrypto-digital-signature":{"title":"Digital signatures: hash, private key, verification","what":"The sender hashes the document and encrypts that hash with their private key — that's the signature. The receiver decrypts it with the sender's public key and compares it against their own hash of the document: a match proves both integrity and authorship."},"prcrypto-tls-handshake":{"title":"TLS handshake: key exchange and switching to symmetric encryption","what":"Client and server greet each other, the server sends its certificate, both sides agree on a shared secret asymmetrically and derive a symmetric session key — from then on the fast encrypted traffic runs under that single key."},"pr-gc-roots":{"title":"GC roots","what":"Reachability from roots: whatever can't be reached from GC roots is garbage — even objects that reference each other"},"pr-write-barrier":{"title":"Write barrier","what":"An old-to-young reference write marks its card dirty — minor GC scans only dirty cards, not the whole old gen"},"pr-safe-publication":{"title":"Safe Publication","what":"final/volatile establish happens-before — another thread sees the fully built object, not half-written default fields"},"pr-tcp-handshake":{"title":"TCP handshake","what":"SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK: agree on sequence numbers before any data; costs +1 RTT to the first byte"},"pr-refresh-rotation":{"title":"Refresh rotation","what":"Each refresh issues a brand-new pair and revokes the old one; replaying an old refresh token signals theft and burns the whole token family"},"pr-tell-dont-ask":{"title":"Tell-Don't-Ask","what":"Don't pull an object's data to decide outside — tell the object to do the thing, keep the logic in."},"pr-rollback-only":{"title":"rollback-only","what":"An inner failure marks the shared transaction rollback-only — the outer commit fails with UnexpectedRollbackException"},"pr-chain":{"title":"Chain of Responsibility","what":"A request travels down a chain of handlers until one of them decides to handle it"},"pr-erasure":{"title":"Type erasure","what":"List<String> and List<Integer> are the same raw List at runtime"},"pr-integer-cache":{"title":"IntegerCache","what":"valueOf caches -128..127 — so == on Integer sometimes lies"},"pr-quorum":{"title":"Quorum","what":"Any two majorities (N/2+1) overlap, so two conflicting decisions can never both pass"},"pr-escape-analysis":{"title":"Escape analysis","what":"An object whose reference never leaves the method is split into scalar fields on the stack by the JIT — new with zero heap allocation"}},"bugs":{"bug-self-tx":{"options":["save() will run WITHOUT a transaction: the call via this bypasses the proxy","Everything is correct — the transaction opens in save()","There will be a double transaction: on place() and on save()","Won't compile without @Transactional on place()"],"why":"The proxy intercepts only EXTERNAL calls. this.save() is internal, bypassing the proxy, so @Transactional doesn't kick in. Move save() into a separate bean."},"bug-eq-nohash":{"options":["No hashCode() — in HashSet/HashMap the object gets 'lost', contains returns false","Everything is fine, equals() is enough for HashSet","equals must return int, not boolean","instanceof with a pattern doesn't compile"],"why":"You overrode equals — you must override hashCode too. Otherwise different hashCodes → different buckets, and contains won't even reach equals()."},"bug-integer-eq":{"options":["== compares REFERENCES: 1000 is outside the -128..127 cache → false; you need equals()","Prints 'equal' — Integer is compared by value","Won't compile — Integer can't be compared with ==","Throws NullPointerException"],"why":"Autoboxing caches only -128..127. 1000 produces two new objects, == compares references = false. Compare wrappers with equals()."},"bug-cme":{"options":["ConcurrentModificationException — modifying the collection outside the iterator","Everything is fine, it calmly removes 'b'","Removes all elements of the list","NullPointerException on remove()"],"why":"for-each goes through an iterator that detects a change in modCount. Remove via iterator.remove() or list.removeIf()."},"bug-rollback":{"options":["The transaction will NOT roll back: a checked exception commits by default","It rolls back — @Transactional catches any exception","Won't compile — @Transactional can't be used with throws","Only debit() rolls back, the rest is committed"],"why":"By default rollback happens only on RuntimeException/Error. On a checked exception (IOException) — COMMIT. Fix: @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)."},"bug-string-loop":{"options":["O(n²): each += creates a new string and copies the old one; you need StringBuilder","Everything is fine, += for String is efficient","Won't compile — you can't use += for String","Memory leak due to the String pool"],"why":"String is immutable — each += copies the whole string. On a large list this is O(n²). Use StringBuilder."},"bug-float-money":{"options":["No: double accumulates error (0.999…); for money use BigDecimal / NUMERIC","Yes, total will be exactly 1.0","It'll be 0.0 — double resets to zero","ArithmeticException during addition"],"why":"0.1 is imprecise in binary double, the error accumulates → 0.9999999999999999. For money use BigDecimal or integer cents."},"bug-catch-order":{"options":["Won't compile: the general Exception comes before the specific IOException — the second block is unreachable","Everything is fine, IOException is handled in the second block","Both blocks execute one after another","IOException must be declared in throws"],"why":"catch goes from SPECIFIC to general. Exception before IOException makes the second catch unreachable — a compile error."},"bug-resource":{"options":["The resource is not closed (no close) — descriptor leak; use try-with-resources","All fine, the GC will close the file by itself","readLine() closes the stream automatically","r must be declared as static"],"why":"FileReader holds a file descriptor. Without close() it leaks. Wrap it in try (var r = …) — it closes itself."},"bug-nplus1":{"options":["N+1 queries: a separate SELECT for the client on each order","A single query — Hibernate loads everything at once","Always throws LazyInitializationException","Deadlock on read"],"why":"findAll is 1 query, but accessing the lazy client inside the loop triggers N more queries. Fix it with JOIN FETCH / @EntityGraph."},"bug-volatile":{"options":["volatile does not provide atomicity: count++ will lose increments; use AtomicInteger","volatile makes count++ thread-safe","It will be exactly as many as the number of increments","Won't compile — volatile is only for references"],"why":"count++ is a read-modify-write (3 steps). volatile gives visibility, but not atomicity — threads will overwrite each other. Use AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet()."},"bug-sdf":{"options":["SimpleDateFormat is NOT thread-safe — a shared static produces garbage/exceptions","All fine, format() is thread-safe","static makes it thread-safe","Marking the field volatile is enough"],"why":"SimpleDateFormat keeps mutable state inside. A shared instance across many threads = race condition. Use DateTimeFormatter (immutable)."},"bug-optional-get":{"options":["get() without a check → NoSuchElementException if empty","All fine, get() returns null if empty","Won't compile without isPresent()","Returns an empty string when absent"],"why":"Optional.get() on an empty value throws NoSuchElementException. Use map(...).orElse(...) or orElseThrow with a clear error."},"bug-lazyinit":{"options":["LazyInitializationException: lazy associations are read after the session is closed","All fine, Hibernate will lazily load it during serialization","Returns null instead of items","Double query to the DB"],"why":"The method is not transactional — the persistence context is closed by the time the lazy field is serialized. Return a DTO or load it via fetch within the transaction."},"bug2-visibility-flag":{"options":["The running field must be volatile: without it the background thread may spin in the loop forever, never seeing the running=false write from another thread (no visibility guarantee).","The stop() method must be synchronized, otherwise there will be a race condition writing the boolean, and the value will be written incorrectly.","A boolean cannot be written atomically in Java without AtomicBoolean — the running=false write may leave the field in an intermediate state.","Everything is correct: writing a plain boolean from one thread is always immediately visible to other threads in Java."],"why":"Without volatile the JMM does not guarantee the running=false write is visible to the other thread — it may read the cached true forever. Make the field volatile (boolean read/write is atomic as is)."},"bug2-check-then-act":{"options":["ConcurrentHashMap is not thread-safe for the get() method, you need to wrap the whole block in synchronized on cache.","Here is a check-then-act race: between containsKey and put two threads may simultaneously see the key is absent and both call loadUser, overwriting the value. computeIfAbsent is needed.","containsKey on a ConcurrentHashMap may throw ConcurrentModificationException during a concurrent put from another thread.","Everything is correct: ConcurrentHashMap makes the containsKey/put sequence atomic through its internal segmentation."],"why":"Each operation is atomic, but the containsKey→put sequence is not: two threads will pass the check and both load/overwrite the User. Replace it with cache.computeIfAbsent(id, this::loadUser)."},"bug2-different-monitors":{"options":["getBalance() should not be synchronized: reading an int is atomic, and the extra lock causes a deadlock with deposit().","synchronized(this) in deposit() is redundant — incrementing an int is already atomic, the lock only slows the code down.","Everything is correct: static synchronized and synchronized(this) protect the same field, there are no races.","The static synchronized method reset() locks on the class monitor (Account.class), while deposit/getBalance lock on the instance monitor (this); these are different monitors, so reset is not mutually exclusive with deposit and balance is subject to a race."],"why":"static synchronized acquires the monitor of the Class object, while non-static methods acquire the monitor of this: these are different locks, so there is no mutual exclusion between reset and deposit. reset must be synchronized on the same instance monitor / a shared object."},"bug2-dcl-no-volatile":{"options":["The outer check instance == null outside synchronized is redundant; it must be removed, otherwise two instances will be created.","Double-checked locking in Java does not work at all with any version of the JMM — you must remove both ifs and always enter synchronized.","The instance field must be volatile: without it, another thread may — due to reordering of writes — see a non-null but not yet fully constructed reference to Config.","Everything is correct: the synchronized block guarantees that instance is fully constructed before exit, volatile is not needed."],"why":"Without volatile, the write to instance may become visible before the constructor finishes (reordering), and a thread on the outer check would get a partially initialized object. The field must be volatile."},"bug2-nonatomic-increment":{"options":["long cannot be declared volatile — on 32-bit JVMs this causes a compilation error, an AtomicLong is needed.","volatile is redundant here: with multithreaded hit() it guarantees both visibility and atomicity of the increment, so it can be removed.","Everything is correct: volatile makes counter++ atomic, losing increments under concurrent hit() is impossible.","volatile provides only visibility, but counter++ is a read-modify-write of three steps; under concurrent hit() increments are lost. AtomicLong/LongAdder or synchronized is needed."],"why":"volatile guarantees visibility but not atomicity of the compound operation counter++ (read-increment-write), so under a race increments are lost. Use AtomicLong.incrementAndGet() or LongAdder."},"bug2-lock-ordering-deadlock":{"options":["Acquiring two monitors from a single thread always leads to a deadlock — you cannot hold two synchronized blocks at the same time.","Two threads doing transfer(A,B) and transfer(B,A) at the same time will acquire the locks in opposite order and deadlock; you need to order lock acquisition by a stable key (for example, by account id).","Everything is correct: nested synchronized blocks guarantee the atomicity of the transfer and a deadlock is impossible here.","The problem is that from.debit and to.credit are called inside the two locks — this causes a livelock due to reentering the monitor."],"why":"transfer(A,B) takes A→B, while a concurrent transfer(B,A) takes B→A — a classic deadlock caused by reversed lock order. Acquire the monitors in a deterministic order (for example, by System.identityHashCode or id)."},"bug2-lost-update":{"options":["find() should be replaced with getReference(), otherwise the whole entity is loaded which is slow","Read-modify-write without locking: both read 150, both pass the check and debit — the balance goes negative (lost update). A @Lock(PESSIMISTIC_WRITE) or @Version is needed","You cannot compare BigDecimal via compareTo, you must use equals — otherwise the balance check is wrong","Everything is correct: @Transactional under READ_COMMITTED guarantees that the second call will see the already debited balance"],"why":"A classic lost update: under READ_COMMITTED both transactions read the original 150 before each other commits, and both updates go through. A pessimistic @Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE) or an optimistic @Version is needed."},"bug2-func-index":{"options":["nativeQuery is incompatible with :param placeholders, you need ?1 — that is why the index is ignored","LOWER(:email) on the parameter is not evaluated in advance, which is why the planner cannot use the index","LOWER(email) is a function over the column, so the ordinary index on email does not apply; you need a functional index on LOWER(email) or to store email already in lowercase","SELECT * prevents using the index — you must list the columns, then an index-only scan will work"],"why":"The index is built on email, while the condition filters by LOWER(email) — that is a different expression, the B-tree index does not fit and a seq scan is used. You need an index on LOWER(email) (functional index) or to normalize email on write."},"bug2-offset-pagination":{"options":["OFFSET pagination over a frequently changing set: when rows are inserted/deleted between pages, some orders are duplicated or skipped. Keyset pagination is needed (WHERE created_at < :lastSeen)","LIMIT must come after OFFSET in SQL — the keyword order is violated, the query will fail","The loop is infinite: the exit condition break on empty is unreachable, because batch always contains at least one row","ORDER BY created_at is not unique — without this the results will come back in random order and process() will handle garbage"],"why":"When new ACTIVE orders are inserted at the front (DESC) between page queries, the OFFSET shifts, and rows are duplicated/lost between batches. The solution is keyset (seek) pagination by the created_at of the last processed row."},"bug2-n-plus-one":{"options":["LAZY on @ManyToOne does not work without bytecode enhancement, so client is always null and there will be an NPE","getClient().getName() outside a transaction throws LazyInitializationException — that is the only problem","N+1: for each order in the loop a separate SELECT of the client is executed. A JOIN FETCH or @EntityGraph is needed to load the clients in a single query","Everything is correct: Hibernate automatically batches lazy loads by default, there will be no extra queries"],"why":"The query loads only the orders, while accessing the lazy client in the loop triggers one SELECT per order — N+1. Fixed with JOIN FETCH o.client or @EntityGraph/@BatchSize."},"bug2-phantom-isolation":{"options":["READ_COMMITTED does not protect against phantoms: two threads simultaneously read count < CAPACITY and both insert — more seats get sold than allowed. You need SERIALIZABLE/SELECT FOR UPDATE on the parent row or a unique constraint","count(*) is slow on large tables, so the check sometimes lags behind and lets the insert through","save() must be called in a separate transaction with REQUIRES_NEW, otherwise the INSERT is not visible to the next call","Everything is correct: @Transactional serializes access to the method, so two threads cannot pass the check at the same time"],"why":"This is a write-skew/phantom problem: under READ_COMMITTED both threads see a count below the limit and both insert, exceeding CAPACITY. You need SERIALIZABLE, a lock on the event row (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE), or a constraint on the number of seats in the DB."},"bug2-autocommit-lost":{"options":["CompletableFuture.runAsync creates a new thread for each message — thread leak and OOM","With enable.auto.commit=true the offset is committed on a timer independently of the async processing: once poll() returns, the offset advances, and if the app crashes/restarts before charge() finishes, the message is lost (not reprocessed)","Everything is correct: auto-commit guarantees at-least-once, the message will be reprocessed after a failure","paymentService.charge cannot be called outside @Transactional — the data won't be saved"],"why":"Auto-commit commits the offset on an interval for already-polled records without waiting for the async processing to finish; if the application crashes after the commit but before markProcessed, the message is lost. You need a manual commit (enable.auto.commit=false) after successful synchronous processing."},"bug2-no-dedup":{"options":["account.balance is not volatile — another thread won't see the change","ack.acknowledge() must be called before save(), otherwise the offset is committed twice","Kafka guarantees exactly-once by default, dedup is not needed — the code is correct","At-least-once allows redelivery (rebalance, ack timeout, retry), and the operation is non-idempotent: a redelivered event adds the amount a second time. You need deduplication by eventId (processed ids stored in the DB) or an idempotent upsert"],"why":"Under at-least-once the same event can arrive twice; a non-idempotent +=amount doubles the balance. Fixed by deduplication on a unique eventId (a processed_events table with a PK) or an idempotent operation."},"bug2-retry-post":{"options":["@Retryable does not work on public methods — it needs protected","POST is non-idempotent: on a response timeout the payment may already have been created on the server, and the retry creates another one (double charge). You need an Idempotency-Key in the header so the server deduplicates","maxAttempts=3 is too few, you need exponential backoff — otherwise you DDoS the server","Everything is correct: the retry only fires on IOException, business errors (4xx/5xx) are not retried"],"why":"A timeout does not mean the request never arrived — the POST may have successfully created the payment, and the retry creates a duplicate. You need to send a stable Idempotency-Key, by which the server returns the same result instead of creating it again."},"bug2-partition-key":{"options":["producer.send is asynchronous — without .get() the message won't be sent","AccountEvent must be Serializable, otherwise the serializer will fail","The constructor without a partition key => events for one account are scattered across different partitions round-robin and read in parallel: the order (open->deposit->close) is broken. The key should be accountId","Everything is correct: Kafka preserves global ordering within a topic"],"why":"Without a partition key the records are spread across all 6 partitions, and events for one account lose their order during parallel consumption. You need new ProducerRecord<>(topic, e.getAccountId(), e) — then all of an account's events land in the same partition."},"bug2-no-timeout":{"options":["baseUrl must be https, otherwise the connection is not encrypted — that's the bug","RestClient is not thread-safe — you need a new instance per request","No connect/read timeout is set: when pricing-svc hangs, the calls block forever, the pool threads get exhausted and the service fails cascadingly (resource exhaustion). You need to configure timeouts on the ClientHttpRequestFactory","Everything is correct: an HTTP client has a reasonable default timeout of 30s"],"why":"By default the client has no read timeout — a hung downstream holds threads forever, the pool is exhausted, and the whole service fails. You need to explicitly set connectTimeout/readTimeout (e.g. via ClientHttpRequestFactorySettings) plus a circuit breaker."},"bug2-threadlocal-pool":{"options":["The ThreadLocal must be non-static, otherwise all threads share a single UserContext object and see each other's context","In a thread pool (Tomcat) the thread is reused, and CTX is never cleared via remove() — this is a memory leak and a leak of another user's context into the next request","ThreadLocal is not thread-safe, you need to wrap set/get in synchronized","The code is correct: ThreadLocal cleans itself up when the thread is returned to the pool"],"why":"In a thread pool the thread is long-lived and reused across requests; without CTX.remove() (usually in the filter's finally) the old value remains, causing a memory leak and the risk of serving the previous user's data. static on a ThreadLocal is actually correct — each thread has its own copy."},"bug2-finalize-resource":{"options":["finalize() must be public, otherwise the GC cannot call it and the native memory is never freed","You must call super.finalize() at the start of the method, not at the end, otherwise the resource leaks","Releasing via finalize() is unreliable: the call is not guaranteed, it delays object collection by an extra GC cycle, and under load leads to native-memory OOM — you need Cleaner/AutoCloseable","The code is correct: finalize() will reliably free the native handle during garbage collection"],"why":"finalize() is deprecated and unreliable: the timing and even the fact of the call are not guaranteed, finalizable objects survive an extra GC cycle and pile up in the finalization queue, which under load leads to native-memory OOM. The replacement is java.lang.ref.Cleaner or implementing AutoCloseable with try-with-resources."},"bug2-unbounded-cache":{"options":["computeIfAbsent is not atomic in ConcurrentHashMap — two threads can load the price twice","The cache is unbounded and nothing is ever evicted from it: with a large number of unique isins (or user-supplied keys) the Map grows without limit, up to OOM — you need a size limit/TTL (Caffeine)","BigDecimal cannot be used as a value in ConcurrentHashMap because of its mutable state","The code is correct: GC will itself remove rarely used entries from a plain HashMap"],"why":"A plain ConcurrentHashMap used as a cache has no size limit, no TTL, and no eviction; with a stream of unique keys it grows until the heap is exhausted and OOM occurs. The fix is a bounded cache (Caffeine with maximumSize/expireAfter) or explicit eviction."},"bug2-stringbuilder-loop":{"options":["StringBuilder is not thread-safe, you should use StringBuffer","result = \"\" causes an NPE on the first iteration because of autoboxing","The StringBuilder is created inside the loop and each time copies the entire accumulated result — this is the same quadratic O(n²) complexity as concatenation with +; you need to move a single StringBuilder out of the loop","The code is correct and optimal: using StringBuilder eliminates the creation of extra String objects"],"why":"The StringBuilder was placed inside the loop, and on every iteration the entire result is copied again via append(result)+toString() — this is O(n²) in volume, exactly like naive string concatenation. It is fixed by moving a single StringBuilder outside the loop and appending only p inside."},"bug2-equals-no-hashcode":{"options":["equals is written incorrectly: you should compare via getClass(), and instanceof breaks the contract","equals is overridden, but hashCode is not — two equal Money objects have different hashes, and contains in a HashSet returns false","Everything is correct: HashSet uses equals for lookup, contains returns true","cents should be a Long (object), otherwise == compares values incorrectly for large numbers"],"why":"The equals/hashCode contract is violated: equals is overridden but hashCode is not, so equal objects end up in different buckets and contains returns false. You need to add a hashCode based on cents."},"bug2-mutable-key":{"options":["An ArrayList cannot be used as a HashMap key — this will throw a ClassCastException on put","get returns 1, because it is the same reference to the key object","After mutating the key, the list's hashCode changed, the bucket no longer matches — get returns null (the entry has become unreachable)","v will be 1, because HashMap caches the original hashCode on put"],"why":"A List used as a HashMap key is mutable: after key.add(\"c\") its hashCode changes, and the entry ends up in the \"wrong\" bucket — get returns null. Keys in a HashMap must be immutable."},"bug2-autoboxing-npe":{"options":["get(op) for a missing key returns null, and unboxing into int gives a NullPointerException","The comparison count < MAX_RETRIES is always false because of Integer autoboxing","The method returns false for an unknown op, because get returns 0","Everything is correct: for a missing key count will be 0 by default"],"why":"For a missing key get returns null, and the assignment int count = null triggers auto-unboxing → NPE. You need retries.getOrDefault(op, 0)."},"bug2-integer-cache-eq":{"options":["intValue() on a Long loses precision, so sameId always returns false","Autoboxing int→Integer does not happen, primitives are compared — everything is correct","== compares Integer references: for values in the cache (-128..127) it works, but for 200 it gives false even for equal values — you need equals/intValue","r1 and r2 both return true, because the compiler optimizes autoboxing of identical values"],"why":"Integer parameters are compared via == by reference. For values from the Integer cache (-128..127) the references match, but for 200 different objects are created and == gives false. You need equals or to compare as int."},"bug2-cme-iterator":{"options":["remove(o) removes the wrong element, because List.remove(Object) treats Order as an index","Modifying the list during a for-each through the list itself breaks modCount and throws a ConcurrentModificationException on the next iteration","Everything is correct for a single thread: for-each safely removes elements from an ArrayList","There will be a memory leak, because removed Order objects remain in the iterator"],"why":"The for-each uses an iterator, and orders.remove(o) changes the list's modCount directly — on the next next() call the iterator throws a ConcurrentModificationException. You need Iterator.remove() or removeIf."},"bug2-try-with-resources-order":{"options":["ResultSet cannot be declared in try-with-resources — it does not implement AutoCloseable","st.executeQuery is called before the Statement is opened, which gives a NullPointerException","Connection is not declared in try-with-resources and is not closed — every call leaks a connection from the pool","Everything is correct: closing the Statement cascades to close the Connection too"],"why":"Only Statement and ResultSet are declared in try-with-resources, while Connection is obtained outside and is never closed — the connection is not returned to the pool (a leak). Connection must also be included in the try-with-resources."},"bug2-blocking-eventloop":{"options":["The method returns Uni, but Quarkus invokes reactive methods on the event-loop thread, and the blocking JDBC inside loadFromDb will block the loop — you need @Blocking or to offload it to a worker pool","You cannot inject ReportService without @ApplicationScoped — it will throw a NullPointerException on invocation","@QueryParam does not work with the primitive long, you need Long, otherwise parsing fails when the parameter is missing","Uni.createFrom().item(r) cannot be used with an already-computed value — you need deferred, otherwise the subscription leaks"],"why":"A method with a Uni return type is treated as non-blocking and runs on the I/O event loop; a synchronous JDBC call blocks the loop and chokes the whole server. The fix is the @Blocking annotation (or RunOnVirtualThread / an asynchronous repository)."},"bug2-transactional-self":{"options":["REQUIRES_NEW cannot be applied to a void method — the transaction won't commit without an explicit flush","The internal call to saveOne() bypasses the CDI proxy (self-invocation), so @Transactional is ignored and each persist runs without its own transaction","o.persist() requires importAll itself to be @Transactional, otherwise a PersistenceException occurs — and REQUIRES_NEW is redundant here","Everything is correct: each saveOne opens a new transaction and commits its entity independently"],"why":"saveOne is called directly via this, bypassing the CDI/interceptor proxy, so @Transactional doesn't take effect at all. You need to call the method through the injected bean proxy (self-inject) or move saveOne into a separate bean."},"bug2-buildtime-env":{"options":["@ConfigProperty cannot inject a String without Optional — if the value is missing there will be a DeploymentException at startup","quarkus.hibernate-orm.database.generation is a build-time property: it is fixed at build time, and overriding it via the env variable DB_GEN at runtime has no effect","The default ${DB_GEN:none} is syntactically incorrect — Quarkus does not support defaults in property references","FeatureGate must be @Singleton, otherwise the value of dbGen is re-read on every request and slows things down"],"why":"quarkus.hibernate-orm.database.generation is a build-time property; its value is baked in when the native/JVM artifact is built, so setting it via a runtime env variable is useless. Such a property must be changed at build time, otherwise use a runtime-configurable parameter."},"bug2-appscoped-mutable":{"options":["@Incoming requires returning a CompletionStage or Uni, otherwise messages are not acknowledged and the channel hangs","BigDecimal cannot be added to an ArrayList without a comparator — it will throw a ClassCastException when comparing in size()","An @ApplicationScoped bean is one per application, and its mutable field buffer is modified concurrently by several consumer threads without synchronization — a race condition and loss/corruption of data","Everything is correct: Quarkus guarantees that @Incoming methods of a single bean run strictly on one thread, so there is no race"],"why":"@ApplicationScoped is a singleton, and the unsafe ArrayList is mutated from concurrent onQuote calls: size, add, and clear are not atomic, which produces a race condition and loss of elements. You need to synchronize access, use a thread-safe structure, or limit the channel's concurrency."},"bug2-self-invocation-tx":{"options":["The loop calls saveOne for each order — you need a single @Transactional on process, otherwise there will be N commits instead of one","@Transactional on saveOne doesn't work: the internal call to saveOne() goes directly, bypassing the proxy, so no transaction is opened at all","publishEvent inside @Transactional will publish the event before the commit — you need @TransactionalEventListener","repository.save is already transactional on its own, the annotation on saveOne is redundant and breaks nothing"],"why":"Self-invocation (this.saveOne) bypasses the Spring proxy, so @Transactional is ignored and no transaction is created. You need to move saveOne into a separate bean or call it through a self-injected proxy."},"bug2-transactional-private":{"options":["BigDecimal.subtract does not mutate — the balance won't change, and save will write the old value","pay is not annotated with @Transactional, so save outside a transaction will throw a TransactionRequiredException","@Transactional on a private method is ignored (a CGLIB proxy cannot override it), so the transaction is not applied","Everything is correct: Spring proxies private methods within the bean, and the transaction works normally"],"why":"Spring AOP proxies work only with public methods (CGLIB cannot override private ones), so @Transactional on a private method is silently ignored. The method must be made public and called from outside."},"bug2-readonly-write":{"options":["readOnly=true optimizes reads, but a change to a managed entity will silently not be saved on flush — the lastSeen change will be lost","findById outside a separate transaction returns a detached entity, and setLastSeen will throw a LazyInitializationException","readOnly=true rolls back the transaction on an attempt to modify the entity — there will be an UnsupportedOperationException","Everything is correct: readOnly only affects the isolation level, and the lastSeen write will go through fine"],"why":"With readOnly=true Hibernate sets FlushMode.MANUAL, so dirty changes to a managed entity are not flushed and the UPDATE is not executed — the changes are lost without an error. For a write you need a normal (non-readOnly) transaction."},"bug2-prototype-in-singleton":{"options":["A prototype bean cannot be autowired — Spring will throw a NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException at startup","Handler is a singleton, so the prototype dependency is injected once at creation time: all requests share the same ctx instance","@Scope(\"prototype\") requires proxyMode=TARGET_CLASS, otherwise the bean won't be created at all","Everything is correct: on each access to ctx Spring substitutes a new prototype instance"],"why":"A prototype dependency in a singleton is resolved once at injection time, so it effectively becomes a singleton and is shared across threads. You need a scoped proxy (proxyMode=TARGET_CLASS), an ObjectProvider, or a lookup method."},"bug2-catch-swallows-rollback":{"options":["Catching the exception and continuing leaves a debit without a credit, but the transaction still commits — money is debited while the credit is never performed","After the caught RuntimeException the transaction is already marked rollback-only, so the commit on exit will throw UnexpectedRollbackException","debit and creditExternal in one transaction are automatically rolled back together on any exception inside the method","Everything is correct: catch handles the error, the transaction commits with a consistent state"],"why":"Swallowing the exception cancels the rollback: the debit commits but the credit does not, leaving an inconsistent state. Either rethrow the exception, or explicitly call TransactionAspectSupport.currentTransactionStatus().setRollbackOnly()."},"bug2-mockito-verify-no-interactions":{"options":["verify(emailSender) without a subsequent method call checks nothing — the test is green even if the email was sent. You need verify(emailSender, never()).send(any())","when(repo.findById(1L)) won't work, because Mockito requires eq(1L) for the long primitive","The test will fail with UnnecessaryStubbingException due to the unused when(...)","Optional.of(user) will throw an NPE; you need Optional.ofNullable"],"why":"verify(mock) without a method call is an incomplete verify; it does not check the absence of interactions and always passes. The correct form is: verify(emailSender, never()).send(any())."},"bug2-float-equals-assert":{"options":["calcCommission must return BigDecimal, otherwise the test won't compile","assertEquals(0.3, commission) compares doubles exactly: 0.1+0.2 == 0.30000000000000004, so the test fails. You need the overload with a delta or BigDecimal","The test is correct: JUnit 5 by default compares doubles with a delta of 1e-9","The literal 0.3 is interpreted as a float; you need 0.3d"],"why":"0.1+0.2 in double yields 0.30000000000000004, and assertEquals(double,double) without a delta compares exactly and fails. You need assertEquals(0.3, commission, 1e-9) or compare BigDecimal via compareTo."},"bug2-shared-static-state-order":{"options":["A static List is not thread-safe; the tests will fail due to a race condition when run in parallel","The field must be annotated @BeforeEach, otherwise it isn't initialized","The static field is shared between tests and is not reset: the second test that runs sees size()==2 and fails. The result depends on the run order; you need a non-static field or @BeforeEach that recreates it","JUnit 5 creates a new instance of the class for each test, so log is always empty — both tests are green"],"why":"A static field lives between tests and accumulates state: whichever test runs second, size() becomes 2 and the assert fails — the result depends on the order. It is fixed with a non-static field (JUnit 5 creates a new instance per test) or by resetting it in @BeforeEach."},"bug2-jwt-none-alg":{"options":["verify() uses setSigningKey without specifying the required algorithm — but that is not the main problem here","getUserId() reads sub directly from the unverified payload, bypassing verify() — the signature and exp are not checked, and sub can be forged","Base64.getDecoder() will fail on a JWT, since a JWT uses only standard Base64, and that is the bug","Everything is correct: parts[1] is the payload, and the signature is verified by the separate verify() call above"],"why":"getUserId parses the payload bypassing the signature check — an attacker changes sub and never calls verify(). Identity must be taken only from the Claims returned by verify(), not from a manual decode of the token."},"bug2-open-redirect":{"options":["sendRedirect must be called only after resp.setStatus(302), otherwise the redirect won't work","returnUrl is not URL-decoded, so a relative path will break on special characters","The startsWith(\"/\") check lets through values like //evil.com and /\\evil.com — the browser treats them as a protocol-relative URL and navigates to a foreign domain (open redirect)","Everything is correct: startsWith(\"/\") guarantees the redirect always stays within the current host"],"why":"//evil.com and /\\evil.com pass startsWith(\"/\"), but the browser interprets them as an absolute URL to a foreign host. You need to validate against a whitelist of paths or reject values that start with // and /\\."},"bug2-cors-wildcard-creds":{"options":["allowedMethods does not include OPTIONS, so preflight requests will be rejected and CORS won't work at all","allowedOriginPatterns(\"*\") together with allowCredentials(true) reflects any Origin into the header and permits sending cookies — effectively opening the API to any site with the user's authorization","allowedHeaders(\"*\") has no effect when allowCredentials(true) and should throw an exception at startup","Everything is correct: allowedOriginPatterns(\"*\") is safer than allowedOrigins(\"*\") and is fully compatible with credentials"],"why":"allowedOriginPatterns(\"*\") bypasses Spring's prohibition on allowedOrigins(\"*\")+credentials and reflects any Origin, allowing cross-origin requests with cookies. You need an explicit list of trusted origins."},"bug2-stacktrace-leak":{"options":["log.error(\"Unhandled\", ex) logs the exception twice and will overflow the log under load","ResponseEntity.status(500) must be replaced with HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR, otherwise the response code will be 200","The response body returns ex.getMessage() and the full stack trace to the client — this exposes internal classes, SQL, and paths, helping an attacker; only generic text and a trace-id should go out","Everything is correct: returning a stack trace in JSON is standard practice for debugging in production"],"why":"Returning getMessage() and the stackTrace in the HTTP response exposes the system's internals (DB structure, classes, paths). The client should be given a depersonalized message with a correlation-id, while the details are written only to the log."},"bug2-csrf-disabled-cookie":{"options":["sessionCreationPolicy.IF_REQUIRED does not create a session in advance, so oauth2Login cannot store state and login will break","oauth2Login requires explicitly specifying loginPage, otherwise the bean won't come up","csrf().disable() with session cookie-based authentication (oauth2Login creates a session with JSESSIONID) opens up CSRF: a foreign site will be able to send authenticated POST requests on behalf of the user","Everything is correct: when using OAuth2, CSRF protection is not needed, since tokens are passed in the Authorization header"],"why":"oauth2Login works via a session cookie (JSESSIONID), which the browser sends automatically, so disabled CSRF makes state-changing requests vulnerable. CSRF is disabled only for stateless APIs on Bearer tokens, but here it must be left enabled."}}};
